{Just as at the time of Fischer's comeback in 1992 we wondered how strong his play was then, it is also just as unclear today at what point Fischer became the strongest player of his day - certainly before1971/72. Over the New Year period of 1963/64 Fischer again won the championship of the USA - with a score of 100%.   Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This game was played in the third round of the U.S. championship 1963. Many argue that Fischer was at the peak of his strength during this time. This game and the tournament as a whole are definitely an argument for that statement. Fischer won the U.S. championship with a stunning 11/11, not conceding a single draw. This has never happened before and never been repeated since. Robert Eugene Byrne, playing with the white pieces, was one of the strongest American players at the time and also a participant in the candidates cycle later on. It adds to the incredibility that a player of such calibre loses to Fischer with White in just 21 moves. In the comments I have included Fischer's own account as written in his book "My 60 Memorable Games". Let's begin! ' So wie man bei Fischers Comeback 1992 rätselte, wie stark er da spielte, so unklar ist auch heute noch, ab wann Fischer der stärkste Spieler der damaligen Zeit war - gewiss nicht erst 1971/72. Zum Jahreswechsel 1963/64 gewann Fischer wieder einmal die USA-Meisterschaft - mit 100%.} 1. e4 e5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = D71 - Grünfeld Verteidigung-3.g3 /Gruenfeld Defence-3.g3'} 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 g5 4. h4 g4 5. Ne5 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Byrne takes a dry approach, aiming for a symmetrical pawn structure at the first opportunity. Fischer points out that 5.b3 would have kept more tension.'} Nf6 6. Bc4 d5 7. exd5 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '?!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is an unusual, and frankly, not great setup for White. By placing the pawn on e3, the dark-squared bishop cannot be developed on the c1-h6 diagonal and the light squares are weakened. The latter will play an important role in the game.'} Bg7 8. d4 Nh5 9. O-O Qxh4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'N'} 10. Qe1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' D71: Fianchetto Grünfeld: 3 g3 d5 sidelines and 5 cxd5 (without Nf3)' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer comments: "It's hard for either side to introduce an imbalance into this essentially symmetrical variation. Deadeye equality also ensues afer 10. f4 e6 11.b3 a6 12.e1 c8 13.a3 e8 14.c1 (Stahlberg-Flohr, Kemeri 1937)". Indeed, the position is equal. The main difference in the placement of the e-pawn is now emphasised by Fischer with'} Qxe1 11. Rxe1 O-O {The position is now so completely level that White becomes careless.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Stepping out of the a3-f8 diagonal and introducing the idea ...e7-e5 in the position. ' Die Stellung ist so völlig ausgeglichen, dass Weiß nun leichtsinnig wird.} 12. Nc3 $6 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'A logical move, connecting the rooks. Yet a mistake. White ignores the black idea and misses the chance to move the rooks out of diagonals of the threatening bishops.'} Nd7 $5 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 3 Games' Of course Fischer does not miss the chance to bring some tension into the game.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Suddenly Fischer is able to introduce the imbalance he was looking for. Fischer says about this moment: "I was a bit worried about weakening my queen's pawn, but felt that the tremendous activity obtained by my minor pieces would permit White no time to exploit it. 12...e6 would probably lead to a draw." Fischer's assessment is spot-on. The black piece activity more than compensates the weakening of the pawn structure. ' Die Gelegenheit Spannung in die Partie zu bringen lässt sich Fischer natürlich nicht entgehen.} 13. Nb5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Increases the pressure on d5'} c6 14. Nc7 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The pressure on the isolated pawn grows' The wrong rook. The fact that White lost this apparantly totally normal and level position after only 7 more moves is what has made this game famous. Huschenbeth,Niclas: '"Add another to those melancholy case histories entitled: The Wrong Rook." is Fischer's comment. It is understandable that Byrne wanted to break the pin of the e2-knight on the f1-a6 diagonal, but this rook is dearly needed to defend the king side, particularly the point f2. Therefore, the other rook (the right one) should have moved to d1.' Der falsche Turm. Dass Weiß diese scheinbar völlig normale und ausgeglichene Stellung in nur 7 Zügen verliert, hat die Partie berühmt gemacht.} cxd5 $1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now, the weakening of the light squares with the early e2-e3 becomes an obvious problem. The knight is deeply placed within the white position, controlling many important squares. White tries to get rid of it, but only makes matters worse. '} 15. Nxa8 $6 {[%mdl 512] Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Huschenbeth,Niclas: '?' Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''?!'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]' This move loses. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer says: "There is hardly any other defense to the threat of . .. e4." It is true that f6-e4 cannot be stopped, yet the game move loses right away. White stays afloat with e2-d4 or e2-f4.' Der Zug verliert,} (15. Bxd5 Rb8) 15... dxc4 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '!!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''!'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+!' This move may be suggested immediately by chess engines, but their reasoning is wrong.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now it becomes evident why the rook was still needed on f1. The white king is exposed and comes under a furious attack. Interestingly, Byrne and the commentators thought that this move is played out of desperation and White is clearly better! Fischer says: "The complete justification for this sac does not become apparent until White resigns!" ' Zwar wird dieser Zug a tempo von Schachprogrammen angezeigt, doch die Begründung ist falsch.} 16. Bd2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' And now Bf3 would win.'} Nxe5 17. dxe5 Bf5 18. Nc7 $8 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'After the first intrusion with the knight on d3, now the other one has landed on e3! Later Byrne wrote about this position: "As I sat pondering why Fischer would choose such a line, because it was so obviously lost for Black, there suddenly comes..."'} Bxc2 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer is not interested in regaining material by taking the rook on d1. He is going for the throat (of the king)! Once again Byrne: "This dazzling move came as the shocker... the culminating combination is of such depth that, even at the very moment at which I resigned, both grandmasters who were commenting on the play for the spectators in a separate room believed I had a won game!" '} 19. Rac1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} Bd3 $1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' The key to the sacrificial line which started with 15...xf2!. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is the punchline of the combination initiated with 15...d3xf2 and likely the move missed by Byrne and commentators alike. Black sacrifices the central pawn to open up the h1-a8 diagonal with devastating effect.' Das ist der Clou der mit 15...xf2! eingeleiteten Opfervariante. Das ist der Clou der mit 15...Sxf2! eingeleiteten Opfervariante.} 20. Nd5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} b5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer: "The king is at Black's mercy."'} 21. Bxf4 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} Rd8 $1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Threatening mate: Qh3' Source: Fischer "Meine 60 denkwürdigen Partien" (German edition of "My 60 memorable games") Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Simple, yet powerful. The strongest piece moves for the first time and the game is over - White resigned as the invasion of the queen via h3 is deadly. Fischer writes: "A bitter disappointment. I'd hoped for 22.f2 h3+ 23.g1 e1+!! 24.xe1 xd4 with mate to follow shortly." Let us take a look at this beautiful variation:' Quelle: Fischer "Meine 60 denkwürdigen Partien" (dt. Fassung)} 22. Ne7+ $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''!''} Kf8 23. Bg5 Re8 24. Nc6 $3 Ng3 25. Rcd1 $19 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''-+''} Re6 26. Nxa7 {This indeed would have been a fitting conclusion to this brilliant game by Fischer.} Bxe5 27. Nxb5 Ne2+ 28. Rxe2 Bxe2 $11 29. Rd8+ Re8 30. Rxe8+ Kxe8 $19 {After Auf} 31. Kf2 {things go as follows: Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'allows a strong deflection:' geht's so:} Bd3 32. Nc3 Kd7 $1 33. Ke3 Bh2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Overworked Piece, Deflection'} 34. Bf4 Bg1+ $19 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Black is up a pawn and exchange and will have no difficulties to convert.'} 35. Kd2 h5 36. g3 Bf2 37. Nd1 Bd4 38. Nc3 Kc6 $19 39. b4 Bf6 {or} 40. b5+ Kb7 $19 41. a4 Bd8 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'is similar to the game.'} 42. Nd5 Be4 43. Nc3 Bf3 44. Ke3 Bb6+ 45. Kd2 f6 46. Bd6 Ba5 47. Bf4 Be4 $1 $19 48. Bd6 $11 Bd3 {does not save White:} 49. Bf4 Bb4 50. Be3 h4 51. gxh4 g3 52. h5 $1 $19 {and the mate threat Bc8-h3 means White needs to give material.} {On the other hand, White could be completely satisfied after Dagegen könnte Weiß nach} g2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?'} 53. h6 f5 54. Bf2 {though the engines are counting in an advantage in material for Black. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'would give Black nothing; the position is roughly equal.' völlig zufrieden sein, während Programme schwarzen Materialvorteil errechnen.} f4 $15 55. Bg1 Ba5 $10 {Not} 56. Bh2 Bb6 57. h7 Bxh7 $11 58. Ke2 g1=Q $19 {The fork of queen and bishop decides the game right away.} {But the alternatives die Alternativen} 59. Bxg1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Bxg1 60. Nd5 Bd4 61. Nxf4 c3 $36 62. Nd3 {is certainly not a pleasant position for White, but Black does not break through immediately. Expansion on the queenside with} Bxd3+ $5 $17 {looks attractive now.} {and und} 63. Kxd3 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is relatively best.'} Kb6 64. Kc2 Ka5 {are hardly any better. können auch nicht gefallen.} 65. Kb3 Be5 66. Kc2 Kxa4 $1 0-1

