{Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'It's unlikely that anyone will want to repeat Fischer's 12.d5 as Black gets good counterplay. Fischer's idea was to prevent ...f7-f5 by going 13.g4, but this runs into 13...h5!.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Because of Keres' illness the game was played on 15.06 onstead of 13.06.  ' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Die Partie wurde wegen Krankheit von Keres erst am 15.06. statt am 13.06. gespielt. '} 1. e4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Breutigam,Martin Breutigam,Martin Die Partie wurde wegen Krankheit von Keres erst am 15.06. statt am 13.06. gespielt.} c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nge2 e5 4. Nd5 Nge7 (4... Nce7 5. Ne3 {0-1 Blatny,F-Filip,M, Prague 1963.} (5. Nec3 Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Nf6 7. Bc4 Be7 8. d3 O-O 9. O-O d6 10. f4 Nxd5 11. Bxd5 exf4 12. Bxf4 Bf6) 5... Nf6 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 6. Nc3 a6 7. a4 (7. g3 g6 8. Bg2 Bg7 9. a3 d6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer-Keres. Candidates Curacao.[7] 1962'} 10. b4 O-O 11. bxc5 dxc5 12. a4 Rb8) 7... d5 (7... g6 $6 8. Bc4 $1 $17 {[%cal Yg8g7,Yf8h8]} Bg7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 9. d3 d6 10. Bd2 $17 b6 $6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon If Black doesn't act immediately White is able to control Black's counterplay on the kingside.} 11. Qf3 O-O $6 12. h3 $1 $16 {[%cal Ye1g1,Yf3h4,Yf2f4]} {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'Keres pointed out that after''  Besser ist} Nc6 {Breutigam,Martin is better.} 13. Ncd5 {Breutigam,Martin} Nd4 14. Qd1 Bb7 (14... Ne6) 15. Nxf6+ Bxf6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '1/2 (31) Ponomariov,R (2705)-Mamedyarov,S (2752) Kemer 2007' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'is better for white'} 16. c3 {White's d-pawn is in trouble because of the threat of 17...Qb7.} b5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 17. Bd5 Nc6 18. Ng4 Rb8 $14 {0-1 (58) Hou,Y (2680)-Caruana,F (2811) Wijk aan Zee 2018 CBM 183 [Roiz,M] The game was excellently annotated by GM Roiz in CBM 183.} 19. Qf3 $5 {Interestingly, more than five weeks previously in the seventh round of the candidates tournament against the same opponent the 19 year old Bobby Fischer had played Interessanterweise hatte der 19-jährige Bobby Fischer über fünf Wochen zuvor, in der siebten Runde des Kandidatenturniers, gegen denselben Gegner} Bg5 {Breutigam,Martin - and won! Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'This was Fischer's reaction the first time he faced the Keres variation(!) 1 month before the current game.' gespielt - und gewonnen!} 20. Bxg5 Qxg5 21. Qf6 $6) 8. exd5 Nexd5 {[%cal Ya8h8,Yf7f5]} 9. Ncxd5 Nxd5 $1 10. Qh5 Be6 $19 {[%cal Yf7f5]} 11. Qxe5 Be7 $19 {In his notes to the game Keres preferred} 12. Qxg7 {(Breutigam,Martin) , maintaining the initiative on both sides of the board.} {It was also worth thinking about keeping the queens on the board with} Bf6 {Breutigam,Martin} {Black could also consider} 13. Qg3 {(Breutigam,Martin) . The text White to forestall Black's attack on the kingside by offering the exchange of queens, though admittedly the endgame is also rather poor.} Qe7 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 14. Be2 O-O-O $17) 5. Nec3 Nxd5 6. Nxd5 Be7 (6... g6 7. h4 (7. Bc4 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. d3 Na5 10. f4 exf4 11. Bxf4 Nxc4 12. dxc4 d6) 7... h6 8. Bc4 Bg7 9. h5 g5 10. d3 d6 11. Ne3 Be6 12. c3 Qd7 13. Qf3 Ne7 14. a4 O-O-O 15. a5 a6 16. Bxe6 Qxe6 17. c4) 7. g3 (7. Bc4 O-O 8. d3 Na5 (8... d6 9. O-O Bg5 10. f4 exf4 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 11. Bxf4 Ne5 $1 {[%cal Ye3h3] Exchanging a pair of rooks would make Black's initiative on the kingside a lot less lethal.} 12. Bb3 Bxf4 13. Rxf4 Ng6 14. Rf2 Be6 15. Qh5 $15 Qd7 16. Raf1 $1 Rae8) (8... Bg5 9. O-O Bxc1 {White's setup seems rather stupid but it is not easy for Black to make progress.} 10. Rxc1 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} d6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 11. Qh5 Be6 12. f4 $1 $15 exf4 13. Rxf4 Ne5 14. Rcf1 $17 Ng6 {Breutigam,Martin} 15. R4f2 Ne5 16. Ne3 Nxc4 17. dxc4 h6) 9. O-O d6 10. f4 exf4 $17 11. Bxf4 $19 {Kapnisis,Spyridon It is very difficult to suggest a plan for White. d3-f4 will soon lead to a decisive advantage for Black.} Be6 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon would prevent White from exchanging rooks with 32.Rh1:} 12. Qd2 Nxc4 $1 $19 13. dxc4 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Exchanging rooks with the help of the other rook is unadvisable since the pawn on b2 will be lost:} Re8 14. Rad1 Bf8 15. Ne3 Qf6 16. b3 Qg6 $19 17. Qd3 $19 {[%cal Yd3b2]} Bh3 18. Rf3 $19 Rxe4 $1 {[%cal Ye1e2,Ye2h2] Kapnisis,Spyridon would give White good chances of surviving.} 19. Rg3 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Bringing the knight to d3 and f4 would give black a serious attack against the white king.}) 7... d6 8. Bg2 h5 (8... O-O 9. O-O f5 10. d3 Be6 11. f4 Qd7 12. Be3 Rae8 13. Qd2 $15 Bd8 14. Rae1 exf4 15. Nxf4 $19 {[%cal Yb8h8,Yd7g4,Yh4h2]} fxe4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 16. Nxe6 Rxf1+ 17. Rxf1 $17 Qxe6 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 18. Bxe4 Bf6 19. Bg5 Bxg5 {[%cal Yh3h2]} 20. Qxg5 Ne5 $19 {[%cal Yf8a8]} 21. Bxb7 Qxa2 22. Qf5 Qf7 $19 {[%cal Yh2g2]} 23. Qxf7+ $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nxf7 24. Ra1 Re2 25. Bf3 Re7) 9. h4 Be6 10. d3 Bxd5 11. exd5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Nb8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'This choice by Keres was certainly not a surprise for Fischer since he had already faced this line a month ago in the same tournament! Although in the first game Keres was completely crushed by Fischer he persisted with his pet line that later got his name. Black's main idea is to face d5 with b6 and f5 creating counterplay on the kingside. If White keeps his centre flexible, Black wants to take on d4 twice and place his bishop on f6.'} 12. f4 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'This looks premature as Black is well placed to launch a counterattack with ... f7-f5. Fischer prevents this but in doing so weakens his kingside.'' Breutigam,Martin: 'This looks totally logical. White secures an advantage in space and in addition the knight on a5 is out of the play. Nevertheless, after this game the advance was considered premature for decades. ' Breutigam,Martin: 'Das sieht durchaus logisch aus. Weiß sichert sich Raumvorteil, außerdem steht der Springer a5 abseits. Dennoch galt der Vorstoß nach dieser Partie jahrzehntelang als verfrüht. '} Nd7 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Opens up the path for the bishop on c8 and at the same time takes aim at the d5-pawn, which is to be undermined by ...f5. ' Breutigam,Martin: 'Macht dem Läufer c8 die Sicht frei und nimmt zugleich Kontakt zum Bauern d5 auf, der mit ...f5 unterminiert werden soll. '} 13. O-O {Breutigam,Martin: '?' '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Breutigam,Martin: 'This does for the moment prevent ...f5, but it weakens the kingside too much; and in addition Black now gets rid of his bad bishop.' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer stops f5 in a rather radical way but Keres will be able to generate immediate counterplay. This must have been Fischer's preparation but it seems Black gets a clear edge.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Dies verhindert zwar bis auf Weiteres ...f5, schwächt aber zu sehr den Königsflügel; außerdem wird Schwarz nun seinen schlechten Läufer los.'} g6 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} 14. Rb1 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'Protecting the g-pawn but weakening the dark squares. Keres wastes no time in taking advantage of this.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A rather awkward retreat but there were no real choices.'} (14. f5 g5) 14... f5 15. b4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} b6 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: '!'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres exchanges his dark-squared bishop highlighting White's weakness on the dark squares. '} 16. bxc5 bxc5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres starts the procedure of getting access to the very important h file.'} 17. c4 O-O 18. Qa4 Bf6 19. Rb7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer probably felt that he was losing the battle for the h-file so he made sure that Black will not have enough fire power to organise an attack against his king. Unfortunately the exchange of queens doesn't solve White's structural problems.'} Nb6 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'This allows White to get some counterplay on the queenside.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Although Black's position remains at least pleasant I don't fully understand his decision to retreat the knight on b7 instead of placing it more aggressively on c4. My guess would be that 21.b3 a3! was probably bothering him although the knight on a3 is out of danger and actually helps Black's queenside play.'} 20. Qb5 Rf7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: ''} (20... Qc8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 21. Qc6 $1) 21. Rxf7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer hurries to play a4 before Black plays d7 and makes it impossible or very difficult to achieve this advance.'} (21. Qa6 Rxb7 $1 (21... Qc8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 22. Rxf7 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Qxa6 23. Rxf6 $5 $19 {[%cal Ya6a5,Ya5a4]} Kg7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 24. Rxd6 $1 exf4 25. Bxf4 $19) 22. Qxb7 Qc8 $16 {[%csl Re4][%cal Yh1h6]} 23. Qxc8+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nxc8 24. Bd2 Rb8 $13) 21... Kxf7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres correctly chooses to spoil his pawn structure in order to create a target (b2) and get White's bishop. In the resulting position Black will be able to create more pressure against the white pawns because the white bishop would already be exchanged.'} 22. Bd2 Rb8 23. Qc6 Nc8 (23... Rc8 $11 24. Qb7+ $5 Rc7 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 25. Qa6) 24. Re1 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'White now has some counterplay on the queenside, which makes it quite difficult for Black. He's still slightly better but only manages to score the full point after some ups and downs.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keeping the option of playing ea1 doesn't make much sense since Black will put his bishop on b5.'} Ne7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} 25. Qa4 Qc7 26. Kh2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?'} exf4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?'} (26... Rb7) 27. Bxf4 Be5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} 28. Re2 Rb6 (28... Rb4 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 29. Kh3 Ng8 30. Rxe5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer transfers his knight to g3 to protect the pawn on e4 and help White exchange a pair of rooks.'} dxe5 31. Bxe5 Qe7 (31... Qxe5 32. Qxa7+ {The passed pawn on a4 is firmly blocked and it is not easy for Black to make progress.}) 32. d6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Rxd6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A serious mistake which allows Fischer to get back in the game.'} 33. Bxd6 Qxd6 34. Bd5+ Kf8 35. Qxa7 Ne7 36. Qa8+ Kg7 37. Qb7 Kf8 38. a4 f4 39. a5 fxg3 40. a6 Qf4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres creates the threat of a5-a4 fixing the b2 pawn as an even bigger weakness.'} 41. Bf3 Nf5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'c8 is the best square for the rook in order to put the knight on d3.'} 42. Qe4 g2 (42... Qd2 $19 {[%cal Yc8h8,Yg6g5,Yd3f4] it seems rather easy for Black to put his g6 passed pawn in motion which, in cooperation with the black pieces, will create big problems for White on the kingside.} 43. Bg2 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 43. Qxf4 g1=Q 44. Be4 (44. Qb8+ $1 $11 Kf7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Activating the king may have been interesting but activating the knight looks more logical with the white king staying safe on g2.} (44... Kg7 45. Qe5+ Kh7 46. a7 Qf1+ {[%cal Yf6g5,Yg5f4]} 47. Bg2 Qf2 (47... Qxd3+ 48. Kh2 Ne3 $44 49. Qe7+ $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Kh6 50. Qg5+) 48. Kh2 $13 Qxh4+ $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} (48... Ne3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 49. Qe7+ Kh6 50. Qg5+ $13 {[%cal Yc4b2]} Kh7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 51. a8=Q $1) 49. Kg1 $1 $13 {[%cal Ya2h2] Kapnisis,Spyridon Activating the rook through the second rank provides White with enough counterplay to secure equality.}) 45. Bd5+ Kg7 (45... Kf6 46. Qh8+ Ke7 $11 47. Qe5+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 46. Qg8+ Kf6 47. Qe6+ $15 Kg7 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 48. Qf7+ Kh6 49. Qf8+) (44. Bd5 Qa1 $17 45. Qb8+ $2 Ke7 46. Qc7+ Ke8 47. a7 Qf1+ $19 {[%csl Re4][%cal Yb8f8,Yf8f4]} 48. Bg2 Qxd3+ 49. Kh2 $5) 44... Qa1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres retreats his knight to achieve a5-a4 but this means that e4 will not be under pressure for a couple of moves. '} 45. a7 (45. Bxf5 $11) (45. Qb8+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Ke7 {White will soon get the e5-pawn and the game will end up in a draw.} 46. a7 Qf1+ $11 (46... Qe1 {Black should be able to hold but he is the one who should be careful.} 47. Qe5+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Kd7 48. Bc6+ Kxc6 49. a8=Q+ Kd7 50. Qae8#) 47. Bg2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Qxd3+ 48. Kh2) 45... Qxa7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} 46. Bxf5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer immediately takes advantage of the less pressure against e4 to regroup his knight to e3 and create some counterplay against the weak c4 pawn.'} gxf5 47. Qxf5+ Kg7 48. Qg5+ Kf8 49. Qh6+ Kg8 50. Qxh5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer prefers to give up f2 in order to get c4, a questionable decision.'} Qc7 51. Qg6+ Kh8 52. Qf6+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} (52. h5 Qh2+ 53. Kg4 Qf4+) 52... Kg8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres tries to bring his rook to the kingside but he doesn't manage to do it in the best possible way.'} 53. Qe6+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer puts immediate pressure on a4 so that Keres doesn't have enough time to bring his rook to the kingside.'} Kh8 54. Qd5 (54. Kg4 Qg7+ 55. Kf4 Qf7+ 56. Ke5) 54... Qf7 55. Kg2 (55. Qxc5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} Qf3+ 56. Kh2 Qe2+ 57. Kg3 Qxd3+ 58. Kf4) 55... Qg6+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} 56. Kh3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer takes advantage of the fact that Keres has kept his rook on the a-file.'} Qf7 57. Qe5+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} Kh7 58. Kg4 Qg6+ 59. Kf4 Qh6+ 60. Kf3 Qg6 61. Qe4 Kh8 62. Ke2 Qd6 63. Qe3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Qh2+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres transfers his king to the queenside in order to control White's passed pawn. If he is given the chance, the black king could penetrate White's position through the weak dark squares. '} 64. Ke1 Qh1+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} 65. Kd2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer correctly activates the d2 which is not needed for the defence.'} Qh2+ 66. Kc3 Qxh4 67. d4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} (67. Qxc5 Qe1+ 68. Kd4 Qf2+ 69. Kd5 Qf5+ {[#] White's threat of promoting the d5-pawn is very serious and Black has nothing better than to force a draw by perpetual.} 70. Kc6 Qxd3 71. Qf8+ Kh7 72. Qe7+ $11 Kh8 73. Qe8+ Kg7 74. Qd7+ $11) 67... Kh7 {[#]} 68. d5 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A serious mistake which gives Black a winning advantage. Fischer should have sacrificed his knight to get a couple of Black's pawns and decent drawing chances.'} (68. dxc5) 68... Qf6+ $19 69. Kc2 Qd6 70. Qg5 (70. Qe8 Kg7 {[#] Black will have a new queen in the next move but it will not suffice to get the point!} 71. Qc6 $1 {[%cal Yc5b3]} Qh2+ 72. Kb3 $1 Qb8+) 70... Kh8 71. Kd2 Qb6 72. Qe5+ Kg8 73. Qe8+ Kg7 74. Qb5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Qc7 $19 75. Kc2 $5 $19 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Kf8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon leads to some very entertaining variations:} 76. Qa6 $1 {wins an important tempo, giving Black a winning advantage.} Qh2+ 77. Kb3 Qb8+ 78. Qb5 Qc7 79. Ka3 Qa7+ 80. Kb3 $19 Ke7 81. Kc2 Kd8 82. Kd2 $19 Qc7 $2 83. Qa6 Qf4+ 84. Kc2 Qe4+ 0-1

