d4 23. Nh6+ (23. Qxd4 Nc5 (23... d5)) (23. Nxd4 d5) 23... gxh6 24. Qg4+ $16 Kh8 25. Qxd7 Bd5 26. Qf5 Re5 27. Qf3 f5 28. Bf4 Re8 (28... Re7 29. Qd1 Bxe4 30. Bxe4 fxe4 31. Qxd4+ Bg7 32. Qd5) 29. Qh5 Bxe4 30. f3 Bc6 31. Rc1 (31. Rxe8 Bxe8 32. Qxf5 Bg6) 31... Bd7 32. Bxh6 (32. Bxf5 Bxf5 33. Qxf5) 32... Re6 33. Bxf8 (33. Bf4 Qe8) 33... Qxf8 34. Qh4 (34. Qxf5 Re1+) (34. Bxf5 Rh6) (34. Rc7 Qe7 35. Bxf5 Re1+ 36. Kh2 (36. Kf2 Qe3+ 37. Kg3 Qe5+) 36... Qe5+) 34... Qf6 (34... Qg7 35. Rc7 Re2 36. Qd8+ Re8 37. Rxd7 Rxd8 38. Rxd8+) 35. Qxf6+ Rxf6 36. Kf2 (36. Rc7 Rf7 (36... Be6 37. b3) 37. Ra7 (37. Bxf5 Rxf5 38. Rxd7 Rd5 39. Kf2 d3 40. Ke1 Re5+ 41. Kd1 Re2) 37... Be6 38. Rxa6 Rc7 39. Kf2 Rc1 40. Bd3) 36... Kg7 37. Rc7 Rf7 38. Ke2 (38. Ra7 Bc8) 38... f4 (38... Kf6 39. Ra7 (39. f4 Be6 40. Rc6 Rg7 41. Kf2 Rxg2+) 39... Bc8 (39... b4 40. Bd3) 40. Rxf7+ Kxf7) (38... Be6 39. Rc6) 39. Ra7 (39. Kd3 Bf5+) 39... Kf6 (39... Bc8 40. Rxf7+ Kxf7 41. Bxh7) 40. Rxa6 Re7+ 41. Kf2 (41. Kd2 Rg7) 41... Be6 (41... Ke5 42. Ra7 $16) 42. Rxd6 Ke5 43. Rc6 (43. Rb6 $16 Bc4 44. b3 $1 d3 $2 45. bxc4 bxc4) 43... Bd5 44. Rh6 $18 (44. Rc5 Kd6 45. Rxb5 Bc4 $44) (44. Rc1) 44... Rc7 45. Rh5+ Kd6 46. Rh6+ Ke5 47. Rh5+ (47. Rb6 Rc1 48. Bxh7 Bc4 $1) 47... Kd6 48. Rf5 (48. Rxh7 {Kuzmin,Alexey} Rc1 49. Bd3 Bc4) (48. b3 Rc1 49. Be4 {but after} Bxe4 {Black kept equality in a complicated deeply analysed variation.} 50. fxe4 $1) (48. Bxh7 Rc1) 48... Rc1 49. Bd3 Rd1 (49... Bc4 50. Rxf4 (50. Ke2 Rc2+) (50. Bxc4 bxc4 $14) 50... Bxd3 51. Rxd4+) 50. Ke2 Rg1 51. Kf2 Rd1 52. Ke2 Rg1 53. Rg5 Bxa2 (53... Ra1 $16 {Keeping the queens is obviously to White’s advantage.} 54. Bxh7 Bc4+ {Fischer could play} 55. Kd2 $16 {Kuzmin,Alexey and then start to lay siege to the е4-pawn. However, at that very moment he started a transposition to an advantageous ending.} Rxa2 $2 56. Kc2 $18 {Kuzmin,Alexey: 'Later Black started to prefer'} Ra1 $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey: 'In a very recent game Kobalia - Geller, Sochi 2019, White continued'}) 54. Bxb5 Rb1 55. Kd3 h6 ({Another retreat was weaker:} 55... Rxb2 $2 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 56. Kxd4 Bb1) 56. Rh5 Rxb2 57. Kxd4 Rxg2 58. Rxh6+ Ke7 59. Ke4 Rg5 60. Ba6 (60. Bf1 $14 Rg1 61. Ba6) (60. Bd3 Bf7 61. Kxf4 Rh5 $1 62. Rxh5 Bxh5 $13) 60... Bf7 (60... Bb1+ 61. Kxf4 Rf5+ 62. Kg4 $1 {Black holds the position.} Rf6 63. Rxf6 Kxf6) 61. Bc8 (61. Kxf4 $5 {is interesting but after} Rh5) 61... Rg6 (61... Rc5 62. Bg4 Rc4+ 63. Ke5) (61... Rh5 62. Rb6 (62. Rxh5 Bxh5 {Black has sufficient counterplay for maintaining equality.} {In the event of} 63. Kxf4 Bxf3 {White did not manage to activate the bishop.})) 62. Rh7 (62. Rxg6 $1 Bxg6+ 63. Kxf4 {An unexpected} Kf6 $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey was the correct decision.}) 62... Kf8 63. Bg4 Rg7 ({If Black continued to stay put -} 63... Rxg4 {Kuzmin,Alexey White would break through the defence by the pawn storm} 64. Rxf7+) (63... Rb6 64. Kxf4 Kg8) (63... Rf6 64. Bf5) 64. Rh6 Rg6 (64... Rg5 $16) 65. Rxg6 Bxg6+ 66. Kxf4 Kg7 67. Kg5 Bd3 68. f4 Be4 69. h4 (69. f5 Bxf5) 69... Bd3 70. h5 Be4 71. h6+ {Another version of the sacrifice -} Kh8 $2 {Kuzmin,Alexey} (71... Kf7 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 72. Bh5+ {Kuzmin,Alexey} Kg8 73. Bg6 {with the idea to further exchange the f-pawn for the g-pawn was another strong plan. Black would be forced to build a passive defence on the 6th rank:}) 72. Bf5 Bd5 {- would be a blunder!:} 73. Bg6 Be6 74. Kf6 Bc4 75. Kg5 Be6 76. Bh5 Kh7 77. Bg4 Bc4 (77... Bxg4 78. Kxg4 {cannot save him:} {The move} Kxh6 $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey was the only way to try and avoid the exchange of rooks.} {Taimanov should play} 79. Kf5 $5 {Kuzmin,Alexey trying to transfer the knight on to d6.}) 78. f5 $1 Bf7 79. Bh5 {with a draw.} Bc4 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 80. Bg6+ Kg8 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 81. f6 {1-0 (71) Fischer,R-Taimanov,M Vancouver 1971 CBM 196 [Kuzmin,Alexey]} (81. f6 {Kuzmin,Alexey} Bb3 (81... Kh8 {can help - in both cases White wins easily.} 82. Kf4 Bg8 83. Ke5 Ba2 $18 84. Kd6 $18 {also wins.} {After} Bg8 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 85. Ke7 {Black immediately falls into zugzwang and is forced to let the opponent’s king invade -} Bd5 86. Bh5 (86. Bf5 Bf7 {with an easy win.} {The counterplay attempt} 87. Kxf7 {Kuzmin,Alexey}) 86... Kh7 87. Bg4 Kxh6 88. Be6) 82. Kf4 Kh8 83. Ke5 Bc4 {[#]} {Now White's aim consists in forcing the exchange of the h5-pawn vs the f4-pawn. After doing this, the passed pawn on the h-file will easily decide the issue.} 84. Kd6 Bb3 85. Ke7 Bc4 {Zugzwang! The knight has to retreat.} 86. Bf7 Bd3 87. Be8 Bc4 $18 {and White wins.} {Neither} 88. Bd7 Bg8 89. Be6 Bh7 $18 {nor} 90. f7) 1-0

