{GAME N. 16 Four Queens. Fischer extracted a slight minus score from the seven games in which he was called upon to face the Caro-Kann, prompting Botvinnik to note: "Fischer's both strong and weak point lies in that he is always true to himself and plays the same way regardless of his partners or any external factor. " This variation is not only complex and critical - but perilous. After a single slip the edge passes to Black. But Petrosian overestimates his position and, somewhat recklessly, dissipates his advantage. In timepressure he misses a forced draw and Fischer regains the upper hand. From this point on, with four marauding Queens roaming the board, the play becomes "rich and strange" - resulting in a tortuous draw.  Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B11: Caro-Kann: Two Knights Variation''} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics Fischer, Ragozin'} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 {Position after} dxe4 6. Qg4 Nf6 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 Rg6 9. Qe3 b6 10. Bb2 (10. Ne2 Bb7 $19 {wins. Also on} 11. h3 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics} Nbd7 $19 12. Bb2 $2 Qe7) 10... Bb7 11. O-O-O Nbd7 {Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N''} 12. h3 Qe7 {Position after} 13. Ne2 O-O-O 14. c4 e5 15. dxe5 Nxe5 16. Rxd8+ {Position after} Kxd8 17. Nf4 $2 Rg8 $1 18. Be2 Kc8 19. Rd1 Rd8 {Black wants to secure a Q-side blockade. The b-Pawn won't run away.} 20. Rxd8+ Kxd8 $1 $17 {I already knew I'd been outplayed. Petros ian didn't even consider 20 Nxb4, opening up the lines.} 21. Qg3 Ng6 22. h4 Nxf4 23. Qxf4 Ne8 24. h5 Bc8 25. h6 Qd6 26. Qg5+ $1 Qe7 27. Qd5+ {Position after} Qd6 28. Qg5+ $2 Qe7 {Still playing with reckless abandon! Safer is 28 ..... Qd6 to prevent Qf6.} 29. Qg3 Bf5 30. Qf4 $1 Qe6 $1 {Forcing what looks like a favorable ending} 31. g4 Bg6 32. Qg5+ Qe7 33. Qd5+ Qd6 {Petrosian had been banking on the speed of this Pawn.} 34. Be5 Qxd5 35. cxd5 f6 36. Bg3 {Position after} Ke7 $2 37. Kd2 Nd6 38. Ke3 b5 $1 {A good last-ditch try. Curiously, the King will be safer in White's territory where it obtains shelter from the cluster of Pawns.} 39. Bxd6+ $2 {wins the e-Pawn. Finally on Forced, to defend against the threat of' Qxe5+. Not} Kxd6 $8 {Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!'} 40. Kd4 {I thought this was it! The two Queens are closing in for the kill.} a6 $1 41. c4 {Position after} bxc4 $1 {Slippery as an eel!} 42. Bxc4 {After the game a kibitzer asked Petrosian if he thought 42 c5 would have won for White. Petrosian, who must have analyzed it for many hours (not knowing, of course, what my sealed move was), simply replied: "I don't know."} a5 {The only move to stop mate on b2.} 43. Ba2 f5 44. gxf5 {Now White has to try to make do with the g-Pawn. Bulletin: 'g4-g5-g6-g7-g8'} Bxf5 $1 45. Bb3 Bg6 {Bulletin: 'd1'} 46. Ba4 Bf5 $1 47. Be8 {A gross oversight, but probably best anyway! White can't wIn anymore. If 47 g5 Qh4, etc. Position after Bulletin: '"An gross oversight, but probably best anyway." Fischer'} Ke7 $1 {Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: '''} 48. Ke5 $2 Bg4 $1 $17 {Bulletin: 'Petrosian accepted the offered draw. "After having fought so hard for the draw, however, Petrosian was obviously unprepared to readjust his frame of mind and start playsing for a win." Fischer'} 49. Bg6 $1 {I offered a draw, afraid that he wouldn't accept. Black certainly has the edge now. If} Bd7 {e4! OR IF} 50. Bxh7 $1 $17 {DRAW} c5 51. dxc6 $17 {After having fought so hard for the draw, however, Petrosian was obviously unprepared to readjust his frame of mind and start playing for a win. So ...} {Having overlooked Petrosian's last move, I was somewhat shaken! Not} Bxc6 $2 52. Bxe4 Bxe4 53. Kxe4 Kf6 54. f4 1/2-1/2

