1. e4 e6 2. d3 c5 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. g3 g6 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. O-O Nge7 7. c3 O-O 8. d4 d6 (8... cxd4 9. cxd4 d5 10. e5 b6) 9. dxc5 dxc5 10. Qe2 b6 11. e5 a5 (11... Qc7 12. Re1 Bb7 13. Nbd2 Rad8 14. Bf1 {it's hard for White to breakthrough. The best try might be 34.Kf3 bringing the King up the board to support the Queenside pawns with the Bishops on the board.} Rd5 15. Nc4 Rfd8 16. Bf4 h6 {threatens ...Nf4. White may be forced to meet this move with g3 pitching the h3 pawn, but seeking compensation in the Bishop pair, d-file and Black's off-side pieces.} 17. h4 {looks better.} Ba6 18. Qe4 Bxc4 19. Bxc4 {Black wants to play ...c5, but needs to get his Queen off the d-file. Besides the text, another way to prepare ...c5 - though in fact it was never played - is} R5d7 20. Qe2 Nd5 21. Bxd5 Rxd5) 12. Re1 Ba6 13. Qe4 Ra7 14. Nbd2 Bd3 15. Qh4 {GM Reshevsky used the plan of 15.Bxf6 gxf6 16.Be4 in an analogous position to try to steer for a better ending.} Nd5 16. Qxd8 Rxd8 17. a4 Rad7 $4 {Morris overlooks a tactic. Better was 17...Bf4 with complicated play.} 18. Bf1 $1 {Black has no good answer to the threats of 19.b5 and 19.Bb5.} Bxf1 19. Kxf1 $2 {Simply 19.b5, followed in most cases by 20.c6, wins material.} Nde7 ({but after} 19... h6 20. Nc4 Nce7) 20. Nc4 Nc8 21. Bg5 N6e7 22. Nfd2 h6 23. Bxe7 Rxe7 24. Ra3 Rc7 25. Rb3 Rc6 26. Ne4 Bf8 27. Ke2 Be7 28. f4 Kf8 ({White can go into a pawn endgame with} 28... h5) 29. g4 {It might look illogical to surrender the Bishop pair when the opponent had a queenside majority, but 29...Bxf3 makes some sense as after 30.gxf3 the White King has a hard time becoming active.} Ke8 30. Rf1 Rd5 31. Rf3 Rd8 {Morris stop Bobby from activating his majority with 32.a4.} 32. Rh3 Bf8 {A difficult decision as 32...Bd1 makes it hard for White to activate his queenside pawns.} 33. Nxa5 Rc7 34. Nc4 Ra7 35. Nxb6 Nxb6 36. Rxb6 Rda8 {The reduced material guarantees that sensible play will draw for Black.} 37. Nf6+ Kd8 38. Rc6 Rc7 39. Rd3+ Kc8 40. Rxc7+ Kxc7 41. Rd7+ Kc6 42. Rxf7 c4 43. Nd7 Bc5 44. Nxc5 Kxc5 45. Rc7+ Kd5 46. b4 1/2-1/2

