1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. O-O f6 6. d4 exd4 7. Nxd4 c5 8. Ne2 Qxd1 9. Rxd1 $6 {This way of handling Bobby's favorite Sozin variation is not often seen, in fact the few times it has arisin have been from a curious move order favored by GM Kupreichik (1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 d6 3.d4 cxd4 4.Nxd4 Nf6 5.Nc3 Bd7).  Practice has invariably seen White try 9.Be3 here, but 9.O-O might also be worth testing.} Bd7 $6 {Arrupe misses} 10. Nbc3 Ne7 11. Bf4 O-O-O 12. Rd2 (12. Bg3) 12... Ng6 $2 {This weakens d6 and misplaces the Knight.} 13. Bg3 Ne5 (13... Re8) 14. Bxe5 (14. b3 c4 (14... Bd6 {with an equal position: 14...Qe7+ and neither 15.Ne2 Qh4+ or 15.Qe2 Qxe2+ trouble Black.}) 15. f3) 14... fxe5 15. Rad1 c4 16. Kf1 Bc5 (16... Bd6) 17. Ng1 Bg4 (17... Rhf8) 18. Rxd8+ Rxd8 19. Rxd8+ $2 {The immediate 19.Bd5 was more to the point.} Kxd8 20. Nce2 {Realizing that e4 is weak, Fischer corrects himself. However he might have had better in} Ke7 21. Ke1 b5 22. c3 Kf6 23. h3 Bh5 24. Ng3 Bf7 25. Nf3 g6 26. Nf1 g5 27. Ke2 Bg6 28. N3d2 h5 29. Ne3 {Now Black should activate his Rook on b8 with 29...Re8. Instead he ends up with a very passive Rook which proves to be his downfall.} c6 $2 30. Kf3 Bf7 31. Ndf1 a5 32. Ke2 Be6 33. Ng3 Kg6 34. a3 Bf7 35. Ngf5 Be6 36. Kf3 Bd7 37. Kg3 Be6 38. h4 Bd7 (38... g4 39. f3) 39. hxg5 Kxg5 40. Nh4 Bg4 (40... Be6) 41. Nxg4 (41. Nf3+ Bxf3 42. Kxf3 Bxe3 43. Kxe3 $18 h4 (43... Kg4 $16 44. f3+ $1) 44. f4+ exf4+ 45. Kf3 a4 46. e5 Kf5 {is slightly better for White.} 47. e6 Kxe6 48. Kxf4 {is a little better White with his more active Rook and superior minor piece.} Kd5 49. Kf5 c5 $16 50. Kf4 Kd6 51. Kg4 Kd5 52. Kxh4 Ke4 53. g4 Kd3 $16 54. g5 $142 $14 {The move Bobby plays is strong, but he also had a good alternative in} Kc2 55. g6 Kxb2 56. g7 Kxa3 57. g8=Q Kb3 $18 58. Qg1) 41... hxg4 42. Nf5 a4 43. f3 gxf3 44. Kxf3 Bf8 45. Ne3 Kh5 46. Nf5 Bc5 1-0

