1. e4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Nagesh Havanur'} c5 2. Nf3 d6 {Spassky hopes for a transposition to Queen's Gambit Declined, an opening with which he has a lot of experience} 3. d4 {Fischer readily obliges as he has a novelty in mind.} cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. g4 d5 {The Tartakower Variation also carries the names of Soviet masters, Makogonov and Bondarevsky who contributed to it. Under Bondarevsky, his mentor's influence Boris also used it, drawing his games in 1969 World Championship Match.} 7. exd5 Nxd5 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. Qe2+ Qe7 (10... Be7 11. Nf5 $18) 11. Be3 g6 12. Bxd7+ Nxd7 13. Nb5 Ne5 14. O-O-O $5 {ChessBase: '!' '!' Furman's novelty with which he beat Geller in Moscow 1970.The idea is to discourage...Nb8-d7 supporting c5.} Bg7 $2 15. Rxd5 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' D59: Queen's Gambit Declined: Tartakower Defence: Main line (8 cxd5 Nxd5)'} O-O 16. Rhd1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 6 Games'} a6 $2 {?! Now the rook and queen defend each other and Black also threatens 17...axb5, not to mention ...c5-c4. Yet it is inadequate.} 17. Nd6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'The position is equal.' '' So far the game has followed Furman-Geller, Moscow 1970.} Qh4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N'} 18. f3 $1 {ChessBase: ' 19.b3, 19.e6' !} b5 $2 {ChessBase: '? Timman' Anxious to get away from the pin, Boris falls back on passive defence.} (18... Qh3 $18 {Timman}) 19. Bd4 {ChessBase: '!' '!'} Nc4 20. Bxg7 $1 {Konikowski: '!' Probing Black's pawn centre at its most vulnerable point.} Kxg7 $2 {? This move, opening the a2-g8 diagonal for the bishop plays into White's hands.} 21. Nxc4 {''} bxc4 22. Qxc4 {!} Qxh2 $6 23. Rd7 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '' !} Rac8 24. Rxf7+ Rxf7 (24... Kh6 25. g5+ Kxg5 26. Rd5+ {only leads to a suffocating end.} (26. Qd5+ $18 Kh6 $5 27. c3) 26... Kh6 27. Rh5+ Qxh5 28. Qf4+ Qg5 $18 {Or} 29. Rxh7+ $2) 25. Qxc8 Qf4+ 26. Kb1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Threatens to win with f6.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Threatens to win with f6.' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Threatens to win with f6.' Konikowski: '!' '+-'} Qxf3 27. Rc1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '+-'} g5 28. b3 Qe2 29. Qc3+ Kg6 30. Qh3 $1 {! %04xNh7 After this move neither the Black queen nor the knight can use g5 as a springboard.} h6 31. Rh1 {ChessBase: '!' '!'} Rh7 32. a3 Rh8 33. a4 {!} Rh7 34. Rh2 Qe1+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} 35. Ka2 Qe5 36. Qh5+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#] Strongly threatening Rh5.'} Kg7 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} 37. Rd2 $1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Rf8+! would kill now.' ! 'with the idea' Rf8} Qe7 38. Qh3 $1 $18 {!} Kg8 39. Qf3 Rf7 40. Qa8+ {ChessBase: ' 41.f7 Timman'} Kg7 41. Qxa6 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Accuracy: White = 90%, Black = 15%. '} Qe5 {!} 42. Qe2 $4 {is worse.} Qf4 $1 {Konikowski: '+-!'} 43. Rd5 1-0

