{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 38 Detective story. Occasionally one comes across a miraculous victory in which, despite intensive post-mortems, there seems to be no losing move or pattern, no blunder on the part of the vanquished. But how can that be possible? A loser must make a mistake somewhere, however infinitesimal, however it may evade detection. Is it Keres' opening novelty which leads him to disaster? Could his defense have been improved afterward? If so: where? Whatever the answer, the reader is invited to share the magnifying glass with Fischer and hunt for that elusive error. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'I think everyone has one game that first comes in mind at the simplest mention of a player's name (try it thinking about your favourite players and you will understand what I mean). I like many of Fischer's victories, but this is "that game" for me. '} 1. e4 {Quintiliano,Renato Bobby Fischer} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. g4 d5 7. exd5 Nxd5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The good old days. No computers, no Berlin, no deeply-studied Marshall Gambit... '} 8. Bb5+ Bd7 9. Nxd5 exd5 10. Qe2+ Qe7 (10... Be7 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White just brings the king to the queenside and wins, as if'} 11. Nf5) 11. Be3 g6 {Bobby Fischer: '; "Now it is not easy to fmd a satisfactory continuation for White." (KERES.) Keres' novelty, introduced on this occasion, has since become quite fashionable. I was - and still am - unimpressed. Black loses time redeveloping his knight to b6, but the K-side is weakened by its absence and it's questionable whether the Knight is not better where it stands originally. ' Quintiliano,Renato: '!?' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move rightly receives the name of Keres Variation. It failed to impress Fischer in this game, as for his notes he thought the manoeuvre f6-d7-b6 was simply a waste of time and left Black's kingside unprotected. Althought he succesfully proved his points in the present game, we can tell from the future that Keres "won" the theoretical discussion, as his idea survived the test of the time and it is actually a very interesting line, in which we can find many recent games in the database. '} 12. Bxd7+ $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: ''!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer gives an exclamation mark to this capture, probably influenced by how well it worked in the game.'} Nxd7 13. Nb5 Ne5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; This is supposed to lead to trouble. If such a natural developing move is bad, then what kind of a position is this for Black?' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The start of a wrong plan, which aims to face the manoeuvre f1-e3-d5 by defending that square with as many pieces as possible. Fischer poses the following funny question here: "If such a natural developing move is bad, what should we think about this position for Black?". Although it makes sense, we should not take such evaluation as definitive. The only possible conclusion is that Keres didn't find the best way to deal with White's plan of manoeuvring the knight to d5.'} 14. O-O-O Bg7 15. Rxd5 O-O 16. Rhd1 a6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black has apparently managed to establish firm control over d5. '} 17. Nd6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', The idea is to open lines and take advantage of Black's weakened K-side. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'But Fischer doesn't care!'} Qh4 18. f3 b5 (18... Qh3) 19. Bd4 {Bobby Fischer: ', Since his early attacking days, Keres has switched to positional-defensive chess. But this type of position is too much even for him. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'At first sight, we could think that White didn't accomplish too much with the last operation, as the central pawns were just exchanged and nothing terrible seems to be happening for Black here. But if we examine the position more closely, it will be clear that Black is the side who needs to be careful in his next moves: the c2 became very active, and his king looks dangerously abandoned on g8.'} Nc4 {Bobby Fischer: '; To defend the second rank. What else can Black do? White threatens to build up with Bf4 and Rad1, and already sacrificial themes are in the aIr.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move seemed awkward to me, and I can still remember how perplexed I was when seeing Fischer's analysis and being forced to agree with him that there is nothing better for Black. White is threatening to build annoying pressure by simple moves like f4-ad1, and some sacrificial attacking ideas are already in the air. I really like this position, especially because Fischer insists on supporting his evaluation with concrete lines. Let's check them:'} 20. Bxg7 Kxg7 {Bobby Fischer: '; The threat was 21.Ng6.'} 21. Nxc4 $1 { xd5  xd5  xd5  xd5 Bobby Fischer: 'Threatening' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Simple and strong, creating the threat of xd5 followed by e4. Instead of looking for forced sequences, Fischer just improves his position.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Instead of seeking a "violent solution," my instinct told me to strengthen the position. '} bxc4 {Bobby Fischer: '; Eliminating stock combinations against h7, but creating new weaknesses on the dark squares.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'There is no simple solution for Black. Keres prevents ideas on h7, but on the other hand weakens the dark squares around his king.'} 22. Qxc4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer immediately turns his attention to the new weakened squares on the kingside.'} Qxh2 {Bobby Fischer: '; An attempt to bring this Knight toward the embattled sector.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres struggles to bring his pieces on the queenside to more useful positions.'} 23. Rd7 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Another simple, yet strong move.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Since Black is tied up in knots, I felt sure of a patient strategical victory. '} Rac8 24. Rxf7+ $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Pinning the Knight and piling on the pressure. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'It is very nice how Fischer manages to increase the pressure on Black's camp by means of natural moves.'} Rxf7 {× e5 × e5 × e5 × e5 Bobby Fischer: '; To prevent White's Queen, in some variations, from penetrating to e5. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is basically forced to keep an eye on e5, as after a possible exchange on c4, an appearance of the White queen on e5 would be devastating.'} (24... Kh6 25. g5+ Kxg5 26. Rd5+ (26. Qd5+ Kh6 27. c3) 26... Kh6 27. Rh5+ Qxh5 28. Qf4+ Qg5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the King invades!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and again the king marches to the queenside, with an easy win.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'if' Quintiliano,Renato: 'If'} 29. Rxh7+) 25. Qxc8 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Qf4+ {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The lesser evil, but this provides other tactical motifs for White.'} 26. Kb1 Qxf3 27. Rc1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Cashing in! "Converting a spatial advantage into a material one." (See Evans' New Ideas in Chess.) ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Converting the positional advantage into a material one. The back rank becomes the main source of problems for Black now, as Fischer skilfully demonstrates from now.'} g5 28. b3 Qe2 29. Qc3+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Kg6 30. Qh3 h6 31. Rh1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Back to the old stand, angling for e5 again. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Back to the old juicy e5-square.'} Rh7 {Bobby Fischer: 'The weakness of Black's first rank has consistently proved to be his undoing throughout the mid-game. '} 32. a3 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer grabs a second pawn, which is enough to win the game.'} Rh8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Token resistance. '} 33. a4 Rh7 34. Rh2 Qe1+ 35. Ka2 Qe5 36. Qh5+ Kg7 37. Rd2 Qe7 38. Qh3 Kg8 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres probably put his hopes on this rook ending in view of his active rook, but Fischer finds a precise idea to clarify things: '} 39. Qf3 Rf7 40. Qa8+ $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is a pawn and a king down now, which means he is technically lost.'} Kg7 41. Qxa6 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns. Not only is Black a Pawn behind, but his King on the first rank is cut off as well.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'You can argue this is not the most beautiful game of Fischer and I would agree, of course. We didn't see either spectacular sacrifices or unexpected original ideas like his most famous games, but I really like the way he developed a strong initiative right from the opening and kept it until the very end, supporting even the most natural moves by concrete calculation. It's remarkable that on move 19 Black is almost running out of moves, and White's position is so superior that against any move there is a tactical refutation. I believe there is something poetic about such games as well. After all, it is a true masterpiece.'} {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres saw no reason to offer further resistance, as any Soviet master would know this endgame is beyond any chance of salvation. But let's see a possible line to make things clear:'} Qe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'A likely winning line is'} 42. Qe2 Qf4 43. Rd5 1-0

