[Event "Short & Sweet: The King's Indian Attack"]
[Site "Main Level"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Ibrahimoglu, Ismet"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C97"]
[PlyCount "79"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]
[SourceTitle "Main Level"]
[Source "Chessable.com"]

{As you can see, Fischer used the King's Indian Attack not only against the French and Sicilian but against the Caro-Kann too.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O (7... d6 8. c3) 8. c3 d6 9. h3 {In my opinion, White is already better. We were presented with a pair of Bishops for free, so I would be happy to get this position.} (9. d4) 9... Na5 10. Bc2 {Preparing e4-e5 or f2-f4. Our Queen is perfectly placed on e2.} c5 $6 {As a rule of thumb, you shouldn't open up the position when your opponent has a pair of Bishops.} 11. d4 Qc7 (11... Nd7) 12. Nbd2 {Grabbing space on the queenside and preparing a4-a5 in some cases.} Bd7 (12... Kh8) (12... Re8) (12... Rd8) (12... Nc6) (12... Bb7) (12... Nd7) (12... cxd4) 13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3 {Black's queenside is already under serious pressure.} g6 15. dxe5 (15. Bd2 Bf8 16. Rc1 (16. b4 cxb4 17. cxb4 Nc4 18. Nxc4 bxc4 19. Rc1 exd4 20. Nxd4 d5 (20... c3 21. Bb3 Nxe4 22. Rxe4 Rxe4 23. Qf3 Bc6 24. Qxc3) 21. exd5 Rxe1+ (21... Qd6) 22. Bxe1 Qd6 23. Nf3 Qxd5 (23... Nxd5 24. Be4) 24. Bc3) 16... Bg7 (16... Nc6 17. d5 Na5 (17... Nd8 18. c4) 18. b4 Nb7 19. a4) 17. b4 Nc6 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. exd5 Ne7 20. dxc5 Nxd5 (20... dxc5 21. Be3 Qd6 22. Bxc5 Qxd5 23. Bb3) 21. Bb3 Nf6 (21... Bc6 22. Bg5) 22. cxd6 Qxd6 23. Ng5 Rf8 24. Be3 Qxd1 25. Rexd1 h6 26. Ne4 Nxe4 27. Rxd7 Rac8 28. c4 Ng5 29. Bxg5 hxg5 30. c5) 15... dxe5 $6 {Weakening light squares everywhere. White is much better now. It is very educational to see how Fischer uses all of his positional trumps to destroy Black's position step by step.} 16. Nh2 Rad8 17. Qf3 Be6 (17... h5 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. exd5) 18. Nhg4 $1 {[%csl Gb5,Gd5][%cal Bb1c3] This Knight has done its job. Now, it will be relocated to d5 or b5.} Nxg4 19. hxg4 (19. Nxg4 Bxg4 20. hxg4 c4) 19... Qc6 (19... Nc4 20. Nd5 Bxd5 21. exd5 Nb6 22. Rd1 Rd6 23. a4 Red8 24. axb5 axb5 25. Be3 c4) 20. g5 (20. Qg3 f6 (20... Qc7 21. Nf5) (20... Bf6 21. g5 Bg7 22. Qh4) 21. g5 Kh8 22. b3 Rf8 23. Nd5 Bxd5 24. exd5 Qxd5 25. gxf6 Bxf6 26. Bh6) 20... Nc4 (20... Bxg5 21. Nd5 Bxc1 (21... Bxd5 22. Bxg5) 22. Nf6+ Kh8 (22... Kf8 23. Raxc1 Re7 24. Nxh7+ Kg8 25. Rcd1 Ra8 (25... Rxd1 26. Nf6+ Kg7 27. Rxd1 Nb7 28. g4) 26. Nf6+ Kg7 27. Nd5 Bxd5 28. Rxd5) 23. Raxc1 (23. Nxe8 Bg5 24. Nf6 Nc4) 23... Rf8 (23... Re7 24. Qg3 Qc7 25. Qh4) 24. Qg3 Qc7 25. Qg5 Kg7) 21. Ng4 Bxg4 22. Qxg4 {All of White's plans are connected with the light squares. Our Bishop on g2 may look dumb but it will join the game via f1.} Nb6 (22... f6 23. gxf6 Bxf6 24. a4 Nb6 25. axb5 axb5 26. Be3) 23. g3 {[%csl Gb4,Gd4] Fischer doesn't rush. He knows that Black lacks counterplay, and he can slowly build up his plans.} c4 (23... Nd7 24. a4 c4 (24... b4 25. cxb4 cxb4 26. Bb3) 25. axb5 axb5) 24. Kg2 Nd7 25. Rh1 Nf8 26. b4 $1 (26. b3 b4 27. cxb4 c3 28. a3 Ne6 29. Qh3 h5 30. gxh6 Nd4) (26. a4 b4 27. cxb4 Bxb4 28. a5 Qc7) 26... Qe6 (26... cxb3 27. Bxb3 Qxc3 28. Be3 (28. Bb2 Qxb2 29. Qf3 Ne6 30. Bxe6 Rf8 (30... fxe6 31. Rxh7 Rf8 32. Rah1 Qxf2+ 33. Qxf2 Rxf2+ 34. Kxf2 Rd2+ 35. Kf3 Bxg5 36. Rh8+)) 28... Rd7 (28... Qc8 29. Qf3 Ne6 30. Rxh7) 29. Rxh7) (26... a5 27. a3 (27. bxa5 Qa6 28. a4 Qxa5) 27... Ra8) 27. Qe2 {White will put the Bishop on b5 followed by Ra7.} a5 28. bxa5 Qa6 29. Be3 {Black's pieces are paralysed. White is positionally winning.} Qxa5 30. a4 {Fischer decided to change targets and switches his attention to the h6-square.} Ra8 (30... Qxc3 31. axb5) 31. axb5 Qxb5 (31... Qxa1 32. Rxa1 Rxa1 33. Qxc4) (31... Qxc3 32. Rac1 Ba3) 32. Rhb1 Qc6 33. Rb6 {Threatening both Bf6 and Qd5.} Qc7 34. Rba6 Rxa6 35. Rxa6 Rc8 36. Qg4 Ne6 (36... Bc5 37. Bxc5 Qxc5 38. Ba4 Ne6 (38... Rd8 39. Rc6 Qa7 40. Rxc4 Rd2 41. Qf3) 39. Bd7) 37. Ba4 {A complete triumph of White's strategy. Fischer's Bishops are monsters in this game. These types of monsters that were hiding under our beds when we were young...} Rb8 38. Rc6 Qd8 (38... Qd7 39. Kh2 (39. Rxc4 Qd3 40. Rc6 Rb1) 39... Rb1 40. Rb6 (40. Rxc4 Qd3 41. Rc8+ Kg7 42. Qh4 Bxg5 43. Bxg5 Rh1+ 44. Kxh1 Qf1+ 45. Kh2 Qxf2+) 40... Qd3 41. Rxb1 (41. Rxe6 Qf1) 41... Qxb1 42. Bd7 Nc7 (42... Nc5 43. Bc6) 43. Bc6 Qd3) 39. Rxe6 {There was no point in continuing. Black's position completely collapsed. A superb performance by the legendary American.} {The computer-like} Qc8 (39... fxe6 40. Qxe6+ Kf8 41. Qxe5) 40. Bd7 (40. Bd7 $3 {would end the game faster.} Qxd7 41. Rxg6+) 1-0

