{In this game Fischer obtains the Bishop pair early in the opening and proceeds to use them in conjunction with other positional pluses to secure the victory.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 {(Avoiding standard lines of the Caro-Kann after 2.d4 d5. White instead plays the King's Indian Reversed.)} Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. O-O f6 6. d4 Bg4 7. dxe5 Qxd1 8. Rxd1 {(Putting "the question" to the Bishop.)} fxe5 {(Black hopes to hold his own by simplification, but at the price of giving White the two Bishops.)} 9. Rd3 Bxf3 10. Rxf3 {[%cal Re4e5] (Threatening the aggressive pawn march e5-e6.)} Nf6 11. Nc3 Bb4 12. Bg5 {(A probing move typical of such positions. White hopes to induce a weakening of Black's Queenside pawns.)} Bxc3 13. bxc3 (13. Rxc3 Nxe4 14. Re3 Nxg5 15. Rxe5+ {A more popular plan here is} Kf7 {followed by} 16. Rxg5) 13... Rf8 {[%csl Ra6,Ga7,Rb5,Gb6,Rb7,Yc5,Rc6,Yd4,Ye3][%cal Rc1e3,Re3a7] (Concerned about Be3, which puts pressure on the a-pawn, Black feels compelled to play the text to block the e3-a7 diagonal. Nonetheless, this permanently weakens the light squares there. This weakness is further aggravated by the absence of the light-squared Bishop.)} 14. Bxf6 Rxf6 {[%csl Ra6,Rb5,Rb7,Rc4,Rc6,Rd5][%cal Yb3a5,Yb3c5,Yb3d4,Ye3d4,Gc5d4,Gb6a5] (Trying to limit the scope of the Nb3 and Be3, but the light squares are further weakened.)} 15. Rxf6 $1 {(Preparing to use the a-file.)} gxf6 {[%csl Rd5][%cal Yf2f4,Ye4e5] (Now d5 is permanently weak, yet without this move White could expand with f4 and e5.)} 16. Rd1 {[%csl Yb3,Yc4,Ye4,Yf3] (The Knight is maneuvered so as to press on the light squares.)} Ke7 17. Rd3 Rf8 18. Kf1 a5 {(Trying to trade off the heavy pieces to defuse White's initiative.)} 19. g4 Rg8 20. h3 $1 {[%csl Gb5] (In order to secure control of b5.)} b5 21. Ke2 Ke6 22. Kf3 Rb8 23. Ke3 c5 {(Hoping to draw by trading down.)} (23... Rg8) 24. c4 bxc4 25. Ra3 (25. Rd5 {Already White threatens}) 25... Rb5 {[%csl Ge3,Gg2] (Now after considerable maneuvers which have left Black's Queenside blockaded on the light squares, the time has come to bring the Bishops into concerted play.)} 26. Kd2 $1 Kd6 27. Rf3 {[%csl Ga1,Ga2,Ga3,Ga4,Ga5,Ga6,Ga7,Ga8] (Nailing down control of the a-file.)} Ke6 28. Kc1 Rb8 29. Ra3 Rb5 30. Rc3 $1 {(Shifting back to create threats against Black's Kingside.)} Kd6 31. a3 {(Now Bh6+ is the threat.)} Rb8 32. Rxc4 {(Still strong.)} h5 33. f3 $1 {(Threatening Bf6.)} Kc6 34. Rc3 Rd8 35. Rd3 $1 {(White's two Bishops hamstring Black's position.)} Rh8 36. Kd2 (36. c4) 36... c4 37. Re3 $1 {(Winning material.)} Kc5 38. Re2 hxg4 39. hxg4 {Black resigns.} Rh1 {For example,} (39... Rh3 40. Rf2 ({Note} 40. Ke3 Rh1 41. Rg2 Ra1) 40... Rg3) 40. Rg2 {loses to} 1-0

