1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Bd3 {John Emms: 'This line of the KIA versus the Caro-Kann is more often reached via a flank opening move order, such as that given after 1 Nf3 above.'} a6 {John Emms: 'Not the only move but a reasonable one. Black's idea is to give up the bishop pair and then erect a solid pawn centre (or at least it should be – in this game something goes awry).'} 8. O-O Ne5 9. h3 (9. f4 Neg4) 9... b5 10. f4 Nc4 $6 {John Emms: '?' John Emms: 'Why give up the centre and open the position after giving away the bishop pair?'} 11. Bxc4 Qxc4 12. Qd3 d5 (12... Bb7 13. a4 Qxd3 (13... Nxe4 14. Nxe4 Qd5 15. axb5) 14. cxd3 b4 15. Nce2 Be7 16. Ng3) 13. e5 Nd7 14. Qxc4 dxc4 $6 {John Emms: 'Preventing White from using d4, but at a cost of weakening the light squares. This wouldn't be so serious except Black got rid of the main protector on move eight.'} 15. f5 Nxe5 (15... Nc5 16. Rad1) 16. fxe6 $16 {John Emms: 'Watch this knight go!'} (16. Bf4 f6 (16... Nd7 17. fxe6 Nf6)) 16... Bxe6 $6 17. Rae1 Nd7 18. Nxe6 $1 {John Emms: 'A fine retreat, highlighting Black's light-squared problems. The b5- and d5-squares beckon.'} fxe6 19. Bd4 O-O-O 20. Rxe6 Nc5 21. Rc6+ (21. Rxf8 Nxe6) 21... Kb7 22. Rxc5 Rxd4 (22... Bxc5 23. Bxc5) 23. Rcf5 (23. Rf7+ Kb6 24. Rc8 Bc5) 23... Bd6 24. Rf7+ Kc6 25. Rxg7 b4 (25... Rd2 {and White's advantage was minimal.} 26. Ne4 Rxc2 27. Rf6 Rd8 $1 {looks okay for Black} 28. Ra7 $5 Kd5 29. Nxd6 Rxd6 30. Rxd6+ Kxd6 31. Rxa6+ Kd5) 26. Na4 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'White's domination of the light squares remain. This bishop may be redeployed actively on c4 or b5.'} Rd5 27. b3 Be5 (27... c3 28. Ra7 Kb5 29. a3 a5 30. Rff7) 28. Re7 c3 {John Emms: 'A horrible place the knight – it's totally corralled by White's light-squared bishop. Mind you, Black's other minor piece isn't having much fun either!'} 29. Re6+ $18 Bd6 30. Re4 $1 {John Emms: 'Preventing the simplifying ...Ra7 as now this would hang the bishop on d7. Black is being stretched to the limit: White's rook and light-squared bishop are doing the business on the queenside, while White's queen and remaining bishop prepare to make inroads on the other flank.'} Rf8 31. Rxf8 {John Emms: '!'} Bxf8 32. Kf2 Bd6 (32... Rd2+ 33. Re2 Bd6 $1 {(leaving the d7-square free for the other knight in case of e4-e5)} 34. Rxd2 cxd2 {(only now!)} 35. Ke2 $5 Bf4 36. Nb2) 33. Re2 Rf5+ 34. Ke3 (34. Ke1 $1 $11 {I prefer} Bg3+ {or 10...e5.} {In the later game V.Ivanchuk-A.Chernin, Lvov 1987, the Russian GM demonstrated the way for Black to play this position:} 35. Kd1 $1 Rf1+) 34... a5 35. Rf2 Re5+ 36. Kf3 Rf5+ 37. Ke2 {John Emms: '!' John Emms: 'Breaking Black's resistance and winning a pawn, the conclusion of a masterful display by Fischer.'} Re5+ 38. Kf1 h5 39. Re2 Rf5+ $5 40. Rf2 $5 1-0

