{Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'When I started to play chess in the mid 70s Bobby Fischer was a living legend. He had won the World Championship in 72 against Boris Spasskyj in such a convincing way that put him in a class of his own. But then he had stopped playing! He hadn´t played a single game of chess since he became the champ. He even refrained from defending his title in 75. Adding colour he seemed to have vanished. Nobody knew where Bobby was or what he was doing. This is how you create a myth.  I immediately fell in love with this magic guy who played chess almost like a god. When I heard stories about his rather bizarre behaviour during his career I was even more attracted. Clearly Bobby was a man who knew what he wanted. And he wanted to play chess! And so did I.  Maybe the excentric American had disappeared, but his games were still there. A lot of books appeared around the time of the 72-match, so you could easily get started. A few years later I got my copy of a book with all the games of Robert James Fischer as his full name was. To me and to everybody else he was just Bobby.  Looking at the games it was clear that from 1970 until 1972 he had reached a completely new level and showed a playing strength that none of his rivals could match. Winning 21 games in a row on the way to the World Championship speaks for itself.  In my view it all started in the famous Soviet vs. Rest of the World match in Belgrade in 1970. Fischer hadn´t played a tournament for 18 months previously, but he had trained daily, it is fair to assume, knowing the American's total dedication to chess. Already in the first game against the former World Champion Tigran Petrosian we could see a well prepared Fischer displaying his trademark style, which is crystal clear aggressive positional chess. '} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin} c6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 g6 4. Ngf3 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The Exchange Variation against the Caro-Kann was new in Fischers repertoire. As a young player he mostly went for the Two Knights Variation 1.e4 c6 2.c3 d5 3.f3, but this is the mature Fischer! The Exchange Variation suits his style very well. It is active and direct and very clear cut.'} Bg7 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Nbd7 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Petro accepts that the bishop won´t get out on g4, but he hopes to activate it on the other flank instead. This plan is also thwarted by White. '} 10. Qe2 dxe4 {Bulletin: ' b5'} 11. dxe4 $146 {[%csl Gb5,Gd3,Rd7] Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3. Bulletin: 'N!' 'N!' Bulletin: 'Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3.' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''N' Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3.' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The new move.'} Qc7 12. a4 Rad8 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Threatening b4.'} 13. Nb3 b6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Pushing the white bishop away from the dominant f4-square. '} 14. Be3 c5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '?' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'This is bad. After'} 15. a5 e5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'A depressing retreat.'} 16. Nd2 Ne8 17. axb6 axb6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '?' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Another strange decision.'} 18. Nb1 Qb7 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Now with the king on f8 this is pretty hopeless. Black´s pieces can´t coordinate in a harmonious way and he is forced to play without a real plan. Petrosian was by nature a great defender and he finds some resources later on. '} 19. Nc3 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'A nice regrouping'} Nc7 20. Nb5 Qc6 21. Nxc7 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Fischer opens lines'} Qxc7 22. Qb5 Ra8 23. c3 Rxa1 24. Rxa1 Rb8 25. Ra6 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'After the opening of the e- and f-files the Black king is feeling rather exposed on f8. Objectively White is winning, but Petro finds a good try'} Bf8 26. Bf1 Kg7 27. Qa4 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Typical Fischer who likes to force the play. There was also the strong though quiet'} Rb7 28. Bb5 Nb8 $3 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The black king hopes to escape to the queenside on the dark squares.'} 29. Ra8 Bd6 30. Qd1 Nc6 31. Qd2 (31. Bh6+ Kxh6 32. Rg8) 31... h5 $19 {F} 32. Bh6+ $1 {F/P Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The king won´t get to b8'} Kh7 33. Bg5 $1 {F} Rb8 34. Rxb8 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Opens more lines while the king is still stuck in the centre'} Nxb8 35. Bf6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Prevents ...c8. Now a check on the d-file will be lethal.'} Nc6 36. Qd5 Na7 37. Be8 Kg8 38. Bxf7+ Qxf7 39. Qxd6 1-0

