{GAME N. 26 Time will tell. The opening has always been regarded as the old warrior's weak point, and were it not for this handicap who knows how far Reshevsky might have gone toward the summit? Whatever the case, being familiar with the latest wrinkles does have the merit of saving time on the clock and, hopefully, of catching an opponent off guard. Although Reshevsky is bested in the theoretical duel (after 13 Bf3) his practical cunning enables him to extricate himself-at a great cost of time. In the end it is the clock, as much as Fischer's persistence, that causes his downfall.} 1. e4 c6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 g6 4. Ngf3 Bg7 {Allowing White the chance to get a Maroczy bind (with 5 c4). Apparently Reshevsky had booked up on this for the match. Black's idea is to dispense with an early ... d6 and possibly strive for a later ... d5 (thereby saving a tempo).} 5. g3 Nf6 (5... e5 6. Z0 $16 {Vukovic suggests} Ne7 {but}) 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 {Despite his familiarity with the Dragon Variation, I felt Reshevsky really didn't know the latest wrinkles in Alekhine's Attack.} Bxf3 9. Qxf3 {Diagram [#]} Nbd7 {I was right. This is the old (and second-rate) move.} 10. Qe2 dxe4 11. dxe4 Qc7 12. a4 Rad8 {Diagram [#]} 13. Nb3 $1 {The modern way-White maintains his centre pawn and sacs two pawns on the kings ide where Black must expose his king to get them.} b6 14. Be3 c5 15. a5 $1 e5 16. Nd2 $1 Ne8 {The best choice in a difficult position. Up to here we had both played briskly, but now Reshevsky began to consume time on the clock.} 17. axb6 axb6 {The lesser evil.} 18. Nb1 $1 Qb7 19. Nc3 Nc7 $1 {Reshevsky is putting up a firstrate defence.} 20. Nb5 Qc6 {Diagram [#] Despite his material deficit, it is obvious White has a strong attack. His problem is how to land a haymaker.} 21. Nxc7 $2 {This gives Black a little breathing space.} Qxc7 22. Qb5 Ra8 23. c3 {Overlooking his reply.} Rxa1 $1 {Reshevsky chopped it off fasthe doesn't wait to be asked twice.} 24. Rxa1 Rb8 {Black has succeeded in transferring this bishop to the kings ide and in sealing the g-file.} 25. Ra6 {So White begins operations on the h-file!} Bf8 26. Bf1 {Diagram [#]} Kg7 $2 {Anxious to simplify and ease the tension (in time-pressure) Reshevsky finally goes astray.} 27. Qa4 $1 {Now the roof caves in on Black's kingside.} Rb7 28. Bb5 $1 {That does it! Once this bishop is gone, White has a field day. "Why didn't White play 28 Nd2 ... ? So far as I can see Black can then resign. Or am I missing something?" (A. R. B. Thomas in a letter to Chess). Right, Mr. Thomas!} ({The tempting} 28. Bb5 {is refuted by} Z0 29. Bxd7 Qxd7 30. Qxd7 Rxd7 $18 {But simply} 31. Rxb6 $1 {holds, e.g.}) 28... Nb8 29. Ra8 Bd6 30. Qd1 (30. Qd1 $1 Ra7 31. Rxa7 Qxa7 32. Qxd6 $18) 30... Nc6 31. Qd2 h5 32. Bh6+ Kh7 33. Bg5 Rb8 34. Rxb8 Nxb8 35. Bf6 (35. Bf6 $18 {taxes even Reshevsky's defensive ability.} {On} Nd7 {mates in two.} 36. Bxd7 $18 Qxd7 $18 {The late Abe Turner suggested} 37. Bxe5 {but then}) 35... Nc6 36. Qd5 Na7 37. Be8 Kg8 38. Bxf7+ Qxf7 {After} 39. Qxd6 1-0

