{GAME N. 20 Theoretical scuffle. Former world champion, Dr. Max Euwe had for decades been considered one of the wor/d's leading authorities on opening theory. His Chess Archives ranks with Modern Chess Openings as an indispensable source of reference. It is no small wonder, then, when he selects a risky but playable variation. Fischer, however, just a little better versed in its intricacies, introduces a nuance on move 15 which ruffles his opponent no end. Fischer's method of dispatching his veteran adversary - on home grounds, as it were - is deceptively simple. After a mere eighteen moves the opening has become an ending and the duel is over. Euwe fights on, but to no avail.} 1. e4 {Petronijevic,Zoran Petronijevic,Zoran Donev Petronijevic,Zoran} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 {At that time I was convinced the Panov-Botvinnik attack was the sharpest} Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 $5 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'In his comments, Fischer considered the move 6...e6 to be better. According to current theory, both moves are of aproximate equal value.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'In seinem Kommentar schätzte Fischer den Zug 6...e6 als besser ein. Der Theorie nach sind beide Züge etwas gleichwertig. '} 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 Nd7 10. d4 Nb6 11. Nbd2 exd4 12. cxd4 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'This move was reccomended by Nimzowich in his book: "The praxis of my system".' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Dieser Zug wurde von Nimzowich in seinem Buch: "Die Praxis meines Systems" empfohlen.'} d5 13. Bc2 Be6 14. e5 {Position after} Qd7 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Diagram '} 15. Nb3 $1 $16 {The innovation. Months before the game I had shown this line to Benko and he suggested this innocent-looking move. Upon looking deeper I found that, horrible as White's Pawn structure may be, Black can't exploit it because he'11 be unable to develop his K -side normally. It's the little quirks like this that could nlake life difficult for a chess machine. Benko,P Petronijevic,Zoran: 'White wins the open b-file.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Weiß gewinnt den Kampf um die offene b-Linie.'} Bf5 $2 16. Bg5 Rfe8 (16... Bb4 17. Re2 Rfe8 18. Rc1 $40 {White gets a strong attack. (Evans,L-Henin,n Las Vegas op 1965} {Risky but playable. Safer is} Nc4 {On Botvinnik's old} 19. Na1 {Botvinnik,M} Bxc2 $1 20. Qxc2 Re6 21. b3 Na3 22. Qf5) 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. Rc1 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer does not hurry: he can take a7 later.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer hat keine Eile: den a7 kann er später schlagen.'} Nb4 (18... Bxc2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} 19. Qxc2 Nc4 (19... Nb4 20. Qd2 a5 21. a3 Nc6 22. Qg5) 20. Nc5 Qe8 21. Ng5) (18... Nc4 19. Nc5 Qc8 (19... Nxb2 20. Qe2) 20. b3 $11 {½-½ Karpov,A-Kramnik,V/Linares 1993/CBM 34 (99)} {on} Nb6 21. Re2) 19. Nc5 $1 {To swap Black's only active piece. To swap Black's only active piece. To swap Black's only active piece. Donev: 'Der Abtausch des passiven Turms h1 gegen den aktiven auf d7 ist die stellungsgemäße Methode, den Vorteil zu bewahren.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer follows a logical positional plan: he exchanges the only active black piece.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer folgt einem logischen positionellen Plan: er tauscht die einzige aktive Figur von Schwarz. '} Bxc2 (19... Qc8 20. Bxf5 $18 {wins, according to Evans) Evans,L} Qxf5 21. Qb3 Nc6 22. Nxa6 $16 {gives White good play against Black's isolated d-Pawn and a-Pawn)}) 20. Qd2 (20. Rxc2 $16 {with a clear advantage} Qf5) 20... Qe8 {Position after} 21. Qxb4 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer pins the black bishop to his rook.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer fesselt den schwarzen Läufer mit seinem Turm.'} a5 22. Qc3 Bg6 {Striving to untangle the K -side.} (22... Bf5 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'for instance,' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'z. B.'} 23. Nh4 Bc8 24. f4) 23. Nh4 Na4 (23... b4 24. Qg3 $11 {1-0 Thomas,I-Soueid,M/GER 1996/EXT 2001 (34). (33)} Nd7 $5 {[%cal Ye7f7]} {An alternative is Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Nowadays the move' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Heute gilt der Zug'} 25. f4 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'is considered to be better.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'als besser.'}) 24. Qb3 (24. Nxa4 bxa4 25. f4 Be4) 24... Nxc5 25. Rxc5 c6 26. Rec1 {Still trying to decide how to squeeze the most out of it.} Re6 27. f4 f5 (27... Be4 28. f5 $16 {with an enduring pull} Rh6 $5 {Petronijevic,Zoran} {after} 29. Qg3 Kh8 30. f6 gxf6 $14 {gives White a slight edge.} {The move Der Zug} 31. Rxc6 {Petronijevic,Zoran leads to an equal play. führt zu gleichem Spiel.} (31. Qf4)) 28. a4 bxa4 (28... b4 29. Qc2 Ra6 30. g4) 29. Qxa4 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!' Donev: 'Der a-Bauer strebt unaufhaltsam vorwärts.'} Rb8 30. Qa3 $1 Qd8 31. Nxg6 (31. Rxa5 $5 $16 Qb6 $2 32. Rac5 Qxb2 33. Qxb2 $13 Rxb2 $6 {is unnecessary. White can win the a-Pawn at his leisure} {also difficult is} 34. Rxc6 Rxc6 35. Rxc6 $1 Rd2) 31... hxg6 {Position after} 32. Rxc6 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!'} Rxc6 33. Rxc6 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!' Donev: 'Weiß strebt den Turmtausch an.'} Qh4 34. Rxg6 Kh7 (34... Qxf4 $18 35. Qe7 Qc1+ 36. Kh2 Qf4+ 37. Rg3 Qh6 $16 {[%emt 0:00:22] would be hard to win. Now the threat is c5} 38. Qd7 $5 $16 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Rxb2 {Donev} 39. Qxd5+) 35. Rg5 Rb4 (35... Qxf4 36. Qe7 Qe3+ $13 {The black pieces are very active. Die schwarzen Figuren stehen sehr aktiv.} 37. Kh2 $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Qf4+ $18 38. Rg3 $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran}) 36. Qf3 {Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!'} {After the game Euwe showed me a cute trap he might have played for - and almost fainted when I fell into it! The line arises after} Kh6 37. g3 Qxh3 {and he asked, "What do you do now?" I looked a few seconds and played} 38. Qxd5 $2 {whereupon he uncorked} (38. Qxd5 $1 $11 {which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '=!!'} Rxb2 $18 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Donev: '+-!' Donev: ' 35.a7' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer'} 39. Qe6+ $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Kh7 $11 {Donev: '=' Donev: ' 35...xf3'} 40. Qg6+ {Petronijevic,Zoran} Kg8 41. Qxg7#) 1-0

