{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 47 The Indian sign? Bisguier is the one Grandmaster who consistently obtains decent positions against Fischer, only to throw them away for no apparent reason. Out of something like a dozen encounters, he has squeezed but a single draw. Here is the only game in which Bisguier is outplayed from the start. Still, he does catch Fischer napping and nearly escapes. Describing his sensations before the game, Bisguier wrote: "For the first time I was really in doubt as to what Bobby would play against me as White... I was hoping to play the Black side of the King's Gambit or the Two Knights' Defense, but he 'surprised' me with the Ruy Lopez... taken by surprise I was not so prepared or comfortable as I would like to have been. Now that Bobby has added psychology to his arsenal of weapons he is a much more dangerous opponent than ever before." '} 1. e4 {Bobby Fischer} c6 2. d4 (2. Nc3) 2... d5 3. Nc3 (3. exd5) 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 (4... Bf5) 5. Nf3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 5. Qe2 $2 Ndf6 (5... Ngf6 6. Nd6#) {Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 6. Z0) (5. Bc4 Ngf6 6. Ng5 $1 Nd5 7. N1f3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'White's better.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'This idea bankrupts Black's strategy. The slight weakening of the K-side is inconsequential, but Black's loss of time with his KN is. Actually the sharpest continuation is'} h6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'as I played against Eliskases at Mar del Plata, 1960 (I simply forgot to interpolate it here)'} 8. Ne4 N7b6 9. Bb3 (9. Bd3 Nb4 10. O-O Nxd3 11. Qxd3 e6 12. Ne5) 9... Bf5 $1 10. Ng3 Bh7 $1 11. O-O e6) 5... Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ (6. Ng3 $146 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has more than enough for the Pawn.'}) 6... Nxf6 7. Bc4 Bf5 8. Qe2 e6 9. Bg5 Bg4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bronstein's'} 9... Be7 {Bronstein,D-Reshevsky,S Zürich ct 1953} {Bobby Fischer: 'and Weinstein's'} 10. O-O-O {Weinstein,R Bobby Fischer: 'are time-consuming and hence weaker. When I told Bronstein (at Mar del Plata 1960) that the text was a tremendous improvement over his game with Reshevsky, he replied: "Of course. After seven years one must find an improvement."'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Simply'} h6 { Bobby Fischer: 'may be best. Then on'} 11. Bh4 Ne4 12. g4 Bh7 13. Bg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is bad owing to'} Nxg3 14. fxg3 Qc7 15. Ne5 Bd6 16. h4) 10. O-O-O Be7 11. h3 (11. Kb1 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now Eliskases recommends'} Nd5 {Eliskases,E Bobby Fischer: 'but I fail to see how this improves matters since'} 12. Bc1 O-O (12... Bxf3 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'is answered by'} 13. Qxf3 Bg5 14. Qg3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is similar to the quoted game with Eliskases.'}) 13. Bd3) 11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nd5 13. Bxe7 {Rauser,V Bobby Fischer: ', The Rauzer Attack. White gives up the center in order to exploit Black's weakened squares on d5 and f5. '} (13. Bd2 Bg5) 13... Qxe7 14. Kb1 Rd8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Risky.'} (14... O-O-O) 15. Qe4 b5 {Bobby Fischer: '; An old line rehabilitated by Reshevsky against Bronstein at Zurich 1953.'} 16. Bd3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Bronstein's move. - after first interpolating 16.a4 Rb8.'} a5 17. c3 $1 (17. a3 b4) 17... Qd6 (17... a4 18. a3 $1) 18. g3 b4 {Bobby Fischer: '; Probably best.'} 19. c4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Of course! Chess is a matter of timing. Given another move or two Black would be able not only to defend hilnself against this invasion, but even try for the initiative. '} Nf6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Prudent.'} 20. Qe5 (20. Qe2 O-O $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'penetrates decisively.'} ({Bobby Fischer: '"Winning" the Pawn would allow White's Bishop-pair to enter the game with powerful effect: e.g.,'} 20... Qxd4 21. Bxh7) 21. Bc2 c5 {× d5 Bobby Fischer: 'the Knight is ready to pounce on d5, especially after Black's Rook has been deflected from the d-file.'}) 20... c5 $16 { Bobby Fischer: '; White has the two Bishops - or a "half point" advantage. '} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I had expected'} 20... Qxe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon'} 21. dxe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'is even more devastating than before.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'More accurate is'} Nd7 { Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 22. f4 $2 Nc5) (20... Qxd4 21. Qxa5) 21. Qg5 { h2-...-d5 Bobby Fischer: ', This Knight is also bound for d5. '} h6 22. Qxc5 (22. Qxg7 $17 { g8; fxe3 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening 29...Rg8 as well as 29...fxe3 regains the piece advantageously.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Ke7 23. dxc5 Qc6 $2 (23... Qxc5 24. Rhe1 Rdg8 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'} 25. Rxe6+ { Bobby Fischer: 'is better.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Kxe6 26. Re1+ $132 {Bobby Fischer: 'White is in trouble!'}) 24. Bg6 Rdf8 25. Bxf7 Qe4+ 26. Ka1 $1 Rh7) 22... Qxc5 23. dxc5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Winning the second "minor exchange."'} Ke7 (23... Rc8 $2) 24. c6 Rd6 25. Rhe1 {Bobby Fischer: '?'} Rxc6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '; Alert.'} 26. Re5 $1 Ra8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Forced - eventually.'} 27. Be4 (27. Rb5 Ra7) 27... Rd6 $1 (27... Nxe4 28. Rxe4 Rc5 $36 { c5 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening a winning bind with 34.Bc5.'} 29. Red4 $2 Ra7) 28. Bxa8 $1 (28. Rxd6 Kxd6 29. Rxe6+ fxe6 30. Bxa8 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Exchanging a pair of Rooks would make it more difficult, but White still maintains his grip after'} Kc5 {} 31. b3 Nd7 32. Kc2 Kd4) 28... Rxd1+ 29. Kc2 Rf1 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Now the advanced soldier must fall.'} (29... Rd8 30. Rxa5) 30. Rxa5 Rxf2+ 31. Kb3 Rh2 {Bobby Fischer: '; A useless excursion, but there was no good defense. Strangely enough, Black's difficulty stems from his third move and its consequent weakening on b6. If the pawn were still on a 7 (preventing a later Bb6) he might well hold.'} 32. c5 Kd8 (32... Rxh3 33. Ra7+ Kd8 $18 {× d3 Bobby Fischer: 'picks up the d-pawn at leisure.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 34. Rxf7 Rxg3+ 35. Kxb4) 33. Rb5 (33. Ra7 Nd7 34. c6 Nb6) 33... Rxh3 (33... Nd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 34. c6) 34. Rb8+ Kc7 {Bobby Fischer: '; Black is braced to give up his Queen for two Rooks and keep control of the crucial d-file. '} (34... Ke7 35. Kxb4 (35. c6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} Nd5 $2) 35... Rxg3 36. a4) 35. Rb7+ $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns. A devastating X-ray.'} Kc6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'After'} (35... Kc8 {Bobby Fischer: 'it's just a matter of time. E.g.,'} 36. Rxf7 Rxg3+ $18 { h3-g4-f5 Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Kg4-f5, etc.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 37. Kxb4) 36. Kc4 1-0

