{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If you asked me to pick the most instructive game ever played in the theme of demonstrating a territorial advantage, this one would be number one on my list. '} 1. e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer reaffirms his loyalty to his “best by test”, favourite first move. '} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'En garde! The problem with being the best is that everyone else is happy to hold you to a draw. In this game, Gheorghiu made his intention to draw the game clear from the start. '} 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'There are people who (probably correctly) believe that those who play Petroff's Defence are tired of life itself. I dare you to look at this position and be entertained. This is the passive/super-solid, “I refuse to weaken at any cost, will build a fortress and draw you” version of the Petroff.'} 6. Re1 b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Let's back off and cede White territory.'} 7. Bb3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer violates one principle – Don't make non-developing moves in the opening without good reason – to give precedence to another: Prevent swaps when you are in possession of a territorial advantage. In this case White's last move disallows ...Bg4, which means that Black's c8-bishop will be denied natural posts on both g4 and f5. '} d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More space gained. '} Na5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A novelty at the time and not a great one.'} 10. Bc2 c5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This seems a little inconsistent with his no weaknesses plan. Black wants to tuck his bishop away with on f8 without fear of Bg5. The problem with pushing the h-pawn is that it creates a future juicy sacrificial target for White on h6. '} 11. d4 Nc6 12. Nbd2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer deliberately violates the principle about not swapping pieces when we own more space for two reasons: 1. White wants his bishop developed on the aggressive f4-square, rather than passively on e3. 2. After the swap on e8, Black's queen will lose time when White places his other rook on e1.'} Qc7 13. dxc5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A superior square over e3, since Fischer intends Qd2, Re1 and d4-d5, when the f4-bishop prevents Black from responding with the freeing move ...Ne5. '} dxc5 14. Nf1 $16 Be6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending another swap, via ...Bf5. '} 15. Ne3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer clears the way for Nd4, which in turn controls f5, denying Black ...Bf5. '} Rad8 16. Qe2 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The same no-swap-when-you-own-more-space principle is again violated!'} c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu gets another swap, yet is not outplaying Fischer, since Black loses a little ground each time.'} 17. Nf5 (17. Ng5 h6 $18 {The engines prefer to take the b-pawn:} 18. Nxe6 fxe6) 17... Rfe8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu's create-no-weakness strategy continues.'} 18. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Preventing ...Bf5. '} Nd7 19. Bxe7 Nxe7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu finally summons the nerve to push a pawn past his own third rank, preventing b2-b4. '} 20. Ng5 h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): Black threatens the unravelling plans ...Qf6, followed by ...Re8, as well as ...Be7 followed by ...Bg5. How can White prevent Black's dream of freedom? ANSWER: Go after the black king with a direct attack by lifting the rook to the third rank. '} (20... Nf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is no fun for Black either.'}) 21. Nxe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Steinitz pointed out the principle: The side with more territory must take action and attack, otherwise the advantage is in danger of dissipating via apathy. This simple yet powerful move pushes Black closer and closer to the edge of tolerance levels. '} fxe6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} 22. Ne3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Bxh6. '} Ng6 (22... Nc5 $4 23. Ng4 {forces mate in two} Nc6) 23. g3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer unearths a hidden fallibility in Black's seemingly solid position. Threat: Qd4, followed by Bxh6. '} Nf6 24. Red1 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The threat is stronger than the execution.'} Rxd1+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The things we humans do, just to survive. Up until now, Gheorghiu tenderly nourished his pristine formation with the morality level of the Dalai Lama, Mother Teresa and Mr. Rogers, combined. Here he is forced to do that which he feared most: to sustain heavy damage to his own structure.'} 25. Rxd1 Rd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Our offended eyes hurt. How's that “I refuse to weaken my structure” strategy working out for you? '} 26. Rxd8+ $1 Qxd8 27. b3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The ominously creeping reverse motion of White's rook bears watching. This move displays a remarkable depth of understanding of the position's requirements. Black's rook is his most active piece. So Fischer either eliminates it (after which Black's h-pawn falls, as shown in the notes below) or forces Black to back off and cede the e-file. '} cxb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu looks for play on the queenside with a potential ...b6-b5 break.'} 28. Bxb3 Nf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Otherwise it's just passive defence for Black. '} ({or} 28... Nxe4 29. Qg4 {, when}) 29. c4 Qd7 (29... Nxe4 $2 {gets mated by} 30. cxb5 axb5 31. Nf5 $1 {(threats: Rh8+ and Rxf8)} Nc5 32. Qxe5 {and Rxh6.}) 30. Qc2 Qb7 31. cxb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} axb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Did Fischer blunder? After all, his bishop on f4 hangs and, if eliminated, then his knight on h6 is loose as well. EXERCISE (combination alert): White to play and not lose material. ANSWER #1: Shift the rook over to g3, threatening mate on g8. '} 32. Ng4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It's not easy to refuse a “request” this definite.'} N6d7 ({ANSWER #2: The engine's alien “mind” found the more mathematical solution} 32... Nxg4 $3 {. This geometric anomaly really lords it over Black, who appears to be winning a piece, but isn't:} 33. hxg4 Qb6 $3 {and if} 34. Qc8 Kf7 {, Black must block with ...Bg4, handing back the extra piece, since}) 33. Qd3 Qc6 (33... Nc5 $18 34. Qd6 Nxb3 {White's win is trivially easy from this stage, and Capa, Fischer or Magnus-like technique isn't required.} 35. axb3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'allows both'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} Qxe4 {# # Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'} 36. Nxe5) 34. Qe3 Kf7 (34... Qd6) 35. Nxe5+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. The pinned knight is lost.'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A mistake in a lost position, as'} Nxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'doesn't save the game either: after'} 36. Qf4+ 1-0

