1. e4 {Bobby used Larsen's opening to defeat Tukmakov at Buenos Aires and Mecking at Palma de Mallorca in 1970.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One inherent flaw with the b3/c4 mix is that, in normal 1 c4 English formations, White often plays for an immediate b2-b4. In this case, even if we play the move later, it comes with tempo loss. '} Bb4 4. e5 c5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's early...Nf6 hinders ...f7-f5 but in this case, he doesn't play for ...f7-f5 at all. QUESTION: What other plan is there for Black? ANSWER: In this formation Black plays for a central build-up with ...c7-c6 and ...d6-d5. '} 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. Nf3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Probably White's best formation in the b2-b3 English versus KID. QUESTION: What do you like about White's set-up? ANSWER: I like its inherent flexibility. White can play for d2-d4, or else d2-d3 and later challenging Black's centre with f2-f4. '} Bd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hindering his own ...d6-d5 pawn break. It may be better to delay his b8-knight's development to d7, since it weakens his grip over the key d5-square.'} 8. a4 Qa5 9. Bd2 Nbc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Where is he going with the knight? ANSWER: Black's last move is dual purpose: 1. He removes his knight from d7, possibly to reinstate the ...c6-c6, ...d6-d5 plan. 2. Black may play for kingside light squares and a kingside attack with a future ...h7-h5 and ...Nh7-g5 plan.'} 10. Be2 c4 11. h4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White expands on the queenside but, as mentioned earlier, it took him two moves to achieve b2-b3-b4 – an inherent flaw in our b3/c4 systems. QUESTION: Is there any way to fix this issue? ANSWER: I don't see one, other than beginning with the 1 c4 move order, but then the game is no longer a Nimzo-Larsen Attack. Perhaps we should open the game with the Orang-utan – maybe it should be renamed the Accelerated Nimzo-Larsen Attack! – 1 b4!? to eliminate this problem! '} f6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This looks passive and unnecessary.'} 12. h5 fxe5 13. h6 gxh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Getting off the c-file and clearly demonstrating that his earlier ...Qc7?! was a pure waste of energy. '} 14. Nxe5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is the purpose of White's last move? ANSWER: He wants to avoid an exchange of light-squared bishops and prepares to sidestep the coming ...Bh3 with Bh1. ' Bobby often went out of his way to save his Bishops (..Re8 and ...Bh8 as Black and Re1 and Bh1 here).} Nxe5 15. dxe5 O-O-O 16. Rxh6 Ng6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): By means of what expedient can White win material or greatly improve his position?    ANSWER: Step 1: Threaten a fork on c7. '} 17. Bg5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} (17. f4) 17... Qxc3+ $2 {Black overeacts to the threat of Nc7/Rc7. Instead of the text which drops a pawn, Black should have played 17...Re7 maintaining the balance.} 18. Kf1 Rdf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black begins to unravel, dropping another pawn.'} 19. Bf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Rxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Resigning himself to the loss of pawn number two;'} 20. exf6 Qxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It isn't too late to retrace his steps with'} 21. Ra3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Time for Black to resign, right? He is two pawns down with an inferior position – and against Bobby Fischer! The only factor on which Black places some hope is that it's a simultaneous game, where bone-headed decisions from great players are commonplace occurrences. '} Bc6 (21... Qg7 $3 {(the bishop inserts itself, an unwanted parenthesis in an otherwise flowing sentence, effectively choking off the white queen's passageway along the first rank, all the while calmly leaving the h3-bishop en prise – now White's queen simmers in resentment from her new downgraded status)} 22. Rah3 Nf4 $1 23. Rh2 Nxg2 {with deadly threats on both g1 and e1. White's penitent king looks upon his punishment as reward. Defeat is the great eradicator of vanity, but not if the defeat merely hides within the analysis! Fischer's best games appear as close to the absolute as the human mind is capable – but most certainly not in this game, even if it was simul! SUMMARY: When Black plays an early ...Nf6 against our b3/c4/g3 English set-up, he plans ...c7-c6 and an eventual ...d6-d5 central challenge.} 24. Qd2 Rg8) 22. Rah3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Why deviate a single inch if the path you follow is the true one?'} Qg7 23. Bg4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} Kb8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black seizes his chance to disrupt. At last Peinado abandons his deference to his famous opponent and, for the first time in the game, engages in a hostile gesture. '} {.} 24. g3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} Rf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} 25. Rxh7 Qf6 26. Qe1 d4 27. R3h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer plays the latter part of the game in remarkably un-Fischer-like fashion. Now Black is allowed a miraculous save, reminiscent of one of those commercials with a guy dying of thirst on a desert island, suddenly rescued by a bikini-clad beauty, carrying a frosty mug of beer.'} d3 28. cxd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After a vexing mistake, what often destroys our position isn't the mistake itself but, instead, our loss of composure from having made the mistake in the first place. The d3-pawn may have been expendable but the loss of initiative clearly isn't. Fischer grossly underestimates Black's potential in the position.'} cxd3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 29. Rxg6 $2 {The right square, but the wrong piece! Correct is} (29. Bh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(denial gets harder and harder when rudely confronted with an inescapable truth: Black's attackers, supple as eels navigating seaweed, weave their way past all obstacles, and White is completely busted)'} Qd4 30. Rxg6 d2 31. Qe2 {wins the Queen which cannot abandon the backrank.} d1=Q+ 32. Qxd1 Qxd1+) 29... Qxg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Covering against back rank tricks. Suddenly, Black may be winning! He threatens ...Qf3!. '} 30. Qe5+ $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A rash gesture which leaves Fischer no room to retreat from what he just set in motion.' White had to relieve the pressure on the backrank with 30.f4, but Black is already doing well. For example:} Ka8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A move clearly demonstrating hostile sentiments toward White's queen, who by now is almost out of wiggle room on the first rank. Houdini's evaluation: -9.34 – the equivalent of an extra queen for Black! '} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Fischer would have been slaughtered had Black found'} 31. Rh8 Qe8 $6 32. Rxf8 Qxf8 33. Qd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. EXERCISE (combination alert): Black just agreed to a draw in a completely winning position. What did he miss? ' Again 33...Bd2! was a winner.} a6 $1 {is crushing.} 34. Qxd3 1/2-1/2

