{B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack Garry Kasparov: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack''} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation.  ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation. '''} 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. O-O f6 6. d4 {''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi). ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: ''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi).'''} exd4 (6... Bg4) 7. Nxd4 c5 8. Nb3 (8. Ne2 {Filipowicz-Plater, Warsaw 1964}) 8... Qxd1 9. Rxd1 Bd6 ({or} 9... Bd7 10. Bf4 O-O-O 11. Nc3 {, and 'Black has a difficult position'}) (9... Bg4 10. f3 {.} {not} Be6) 10. Na5 {'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation.'''} b5 {Garry Kasparov: '!''A strong novelty. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'A strong novelty.'''} (10... Bg4 11. f3 O-O-O 12. e5) (10... b6 11. Nc4 $1 Be7 12. Bf4 Bd8 13. Nc3 Ne7 14. Rd2 Be6 15. Ne3 $1) 11. c4 (11. a4 $1 Ne7 12. Na3 $1 (12. Nc3 Rb8 {with equality} 13. axb5 axb5 $1) 12... Bd7 13. Nb7 Nc8 14. Be3 Bc6 15. Nxc5) 11... Ne7 (11... b4 12. Nd2 Kf7 13. Ndb3 {or} f5 $1 {with easy equality.} {and after} 14. exf5 Nf6 15. Be3 Ng4 {'White's position is slightly better';} 16. Bxc5 {but} Bxh2+ $5 17. Kf1 Be5 {(Korchnoi) is interesting} {or} 18. f3) 12. Be3 {Garry Kasparov: '0-1 (33) Fischer,R-Kortschnoj,V Curacao 1962 [ChessCalisthenics]'} ({or} 12. Nc3 Ng6 13. a4 b4 {or} 14. Ne2 {.} {Non-routine moves demand non-routine replies to refute them,' writes Korchnoi, who recommends} O-O {. Here are some sample variations:} 15. Be3 $1 Re8) 12... f5 {Garry Kasparov: 'As a result of the slow manoeuvre Nf3-d2-b3 a favourable version for Black of the Dragon Variation has arisen. '} (12... Rb8 13. Nd2 Be6 14. Rac1 Kf7 15. Ndb3 $1 {.} (15. f4) {However, Korchnoi's recommendation} 15... Rhd8 $1 {is better;} {or} 16. cxb5 {is also unclear. Indeed, 'it is hard to suggest how Black can maintain his advantage.'} {and in the event of} axb5 $6 17. Nxc5) (12... Kf7 13. Nc3 (13. Nd2 {with sharp play.} Re8 14. Ndb3 $6 {'This soon creates serious difficulties for White.} Ng6 15. f3 {Defending against the threat of ...a5-a4-a3. 0-1 (32) Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V 9. Candidates Tournament, Curaçao 1962 My Great Predecessors V [Garry Kasparov]} {The alternatives were unfavourable:} Re5) 13... Rb8 14. e5 Bxe5 (14... fxe5 $1 {while if} 15. Ne4 {, then}) 15. Bxc5 Bxc3 16. bxc3 Ng6 {, Black does not have sufficient compensation for the pawn} 17. Nc6 $2) (12... Ng6 13. Nc3 Kf7 $1 14. a4 $6 b4 15. Nd5 Re8 $1 {(Korchnoi)} 16. f3 Ne5 17. Rac1 g5 {and wins;} 18. Nb3 Nd7 19. a5 Rb8) 13. Nc3 $146 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'A standard attacking move, but here it runs into a powerful counter.'} f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (13... O-O 14. f3 (14. e5 Bxe5 15. Bxc5 Re8 16. Re1 Ng6 17. Bd4 $1) 14... Ng6 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Nb7 Be5 17. Nxc5 bxc4 {Hindle,O-Penrose,J/Whitby 1962/EXT 1999/0-1 (32)} {or} 18. Bd4) (13... Kf7 14. f4 $1 {or} fxe4 15. Nxe4) 14. e5 (14. Bd2 b4 (14... c6 15. Ne2 O-O 16. f3) 15. Nd5 {or} Ng6 {(Korchnoi), Black repels the attack and should be able to convert his exchange advantage.} 16. f3 $2 Be6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Practice has shown that White does not even have equal chances after''Practice has shown that White does not even have equal chances after ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Practice has shown that White does not even have equal chances after'''} 17. Be1 Ne5 18. Nb7 Nxc4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' '!''ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: '!'''} 19. Nxd6+ cxd6 20. Nxf4) (14. Rxd6 fxe3 (14... cxd6 15. Bxf4 Be6 16. Bxd6) 15. Rdd1 exf2+ 16. Kxf2 Be6) 14... Bxe5 (14... fxe3 15. exd6 exf2+ 16. Kxf2 O-O+ 17. Kg1 cxd6 18. Rxd6 Bf5 $1 19. Re1) 15. Bxc5 Bxc3 16. bxc3 Ng6 (16... Kf7 17. Bxe7 $1 ({or} 17. a4 bxa4 {, stealing up on the a4-pawn;} 18. Rxa4 {After} Re8 $6 19. Raa1 Ng6) 17... Kxe7 18. Rd5 $1 Be6 19. Rc5) 17. Nc6 Be6 {Garry Kasparov: ''For the moment White is alright materially, but his pieces are badly placed, and loss of material cannot be avoided.' (Korchnoi) '} ({if} 17... Bd7 18. Re1+ ({then simply} 18. Na7 $1 bxc4 19. Rd4) (18. cxb5 axb5 {, winning} {Hopeless alternatives are} 19. Na7 Rf8 {or} 20. Bxf8 Nxf8 21. Rxd7 Kxd7 22. Rd1+ Ke7 23. Nxb5 c6 {and Black wins.} 24. Nd4 c5) 18... Kf7 {, in the hope of} 19. Ne5+ Nxe5 20. Rxe5 $4 Rhe8 $3 {, when White wins}) 18. cxb5 axb5 19. Na7 Rb8 (19... Bc4 20. a4 bxa4 {.} 21. Rxa4 $5 {or} Be6 22. Ra6 Kf7 23. Rc6) 20. Rdb1 Kf7 (20... Bd7 $19 {Garry Kasparov: 'with drawing chances in view of the bad position of the black queen:''with drawing chances in view of the bad position of the black queen: ChessCalisthenics: '-+' ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'with drawing chances in view of the bad position of the black queen:'''} 21. a4) 21. Nxb5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'The decisive error.'} Rhd8 (21... Ne5 22. Ba7 Rbc8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' '!''ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: '!'''} 23. a4) 22. Rb4 (22. a4 {, according to Korchnoi, the 'cynical' move} Rd5 $1 {would have won.} {Garry Kasparov: 'As shown by Korchnoi, it was essential to play''As shown by Korchnoi, it was essential to play ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'As shown by Korchnoi, it was essential to play'''} 23. Bd4 $142 {Garry Kasparov: 'ChessCalisthenics: '''} Rc8) 22... Bxa2 23. Nxc7 (