{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 13 Something new.  During the mid-fifties, Gligorich, Reshevsky, and Najdorf were considered the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters. Within a few years Fischer managed to surpass them. However, in so doing, he succeeded in beating Gligorich only once - up to 1966 (see game 56). On the occasion of this first win, Fischer employs a novel attacking system (13 Qe2) against the Dragon Variation. Gligorich fails to react vigorously enough and makes the mistake of castling too soon, thereby exposing himself to the same type of sacrificial combination that demolished Larsen in game 2. '} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bobby Fischer Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 (4. Nf3) 4... Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O {Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation.''} (5... c5 $2 {(Bobby Fischer) (correct is} 6. dxc5 $1 (6. Bb5+ Bd7) 6... Qa5 7. Bd3 Qxc5 8. Qe2 Bg4 9. Be3 Qa5 10. O-O {Black resigns. 1-0; Fischer,R-Amateur} O-O 11. h3 ({So for a game appearing on this Dragons site this game does feature a pretty weird move order but it is sort of plausible that the same middlegame position could be reached via slightly normal means e.g.} 11. Rad1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} Nc6 12. Bc4 Nh5) 11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nc6 13. Ne2 $1 Nd7 $8 {(Bobby Fischer) NOT Bulletin: '' '' ''} {Of course} 14. c3 {blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now! Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now!'} Nde5 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 11. Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959'} {Most popular here is} 15. fxe5 {which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as'} Nxe5 16. Qg3 Nxd3 {isn't great for the pawn structure!}) 6. Be2 {Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square.''} (6. Bd3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'winning the Queen - that was another skittles game!)' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''}) 6... c5 7. dxc5 Qa5 {As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack! Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack!''} 8. O-O Qxc5+ 9. Kh1 {The downside of Black's last move is that it released the pressure on White's centrally posted steed and thus allowed White's dark-squared bishop to venture further afield. On White's part, Fischer seems determined to eek out the ...h6 that creates the odd weakness in the defensive black pawn shield. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'The downside of Black's last move is that it released the pressure on White's centrally posted steed and thus allowed White's dark-squared bishop to venture further afield. On White's part, Fischer seems determined to eek out the ...h6 that creates the odd weakness in the defensive black pawn shield.''} Nc6 10. Nd2 (10. Bd3 $1 {, especially bad in the case of Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!' Chris Ward: ', especially bad in the case of''}) (10. Qe1 $4) 10... a5 {Essentially a fork in the road for Black. The decision made effectively takes Bh6 away from White's options whereas Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Essentially a fork in the road for Black. The decision made effectively takes Bh6 away from White's options whereas''} (10... Be6 {Bobby Fischer} 11. Nb3 Qb6 12. Bf3 (12. g4) 12... a5 $16 13. Re1 $2 a4 14. Be3 $2 Qa6 $1 {A useful retreat waiting for Black to commit himself. Although} 15. Nd4 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} a3 {is a plausible sequence, certainly} 16. b3 {is well met by} Bg4 $1 {and frequently against such set ups Fisher favoured challenging Black's structure through f4-f5.} 17. Nxc6 {(Bobby Fischer) is met by} Qxc6 $1 {On} 18. Nd5 {Bobby Fischer} Bxf3 19. Nxe7+ $2 Kh8 20. gxf3 Qd7 21. Nd5 Nxd5 $19 {with a winning attack Also strong is Gligoric,S (GLIGORICH) Bulletin: '; Gligoric,S'} 22. Qxd5 {Bobby Fischer} Bxa1 23. Rxa1 $1 Qh3 24. Qd1 b5 25. c3 $1 $16) (10... Nd4 {Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap Bobby Fischer: 'Recently in a skittles game someone tried' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap''} 11. Nb3 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '!?' Bobby Fischer: 'against me. The game continued:' Bulletin: '!?' '!?' '!?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '?''} Nxb3 12. axb3) 11. a4 (11. Nb3 {seems to offer both sides chances although the Soltis like 15...h5!? stopping White's h-pawn in its tracks looks quite appealing too.} Qb6 {(Bobby Fischer) locks it up.} {This isn't unreasonable but given Black has delayed castling up to this point, he could hang back further with Bobby Fischer: 'Reminiscent of game 12, it is now Gligorich who castles into it! At Mar del Plata 1960, Marini played against Ine the stronger' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'This isn't unreasonable but given Black has delayed castling up to this point, he could hang back further with''} 12. a4 $5 {This is a very useful move when White's dark-squared bishop has been removed and specifically now ... e5 is a piece winning threat. As it happens that actually occurred the following year with Bobby Fischer: '(threatening -e5' Bulletin: ';  e7-e5 Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'This is a very useful move when White's dark-squared bishop has been removed and specifically now ... e5 is a piece winning threat. As it happens that actually occurred the following year with''} (12. Nd5 Nxd5 {very sensibly seeing Black look to edge forward. However after Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'very sensibly seeing Black look to edge forward. However after''} 13. exd5 {Black should have either finally castled or else continued with some queenside expansion via 16...b5 but instead lost his way through Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black should have either finally castled or else continued with some queenside expansion via 16...b5 but instead lost his way through''} a4 $6 {when Bobby Fischer: '?' Bobby Fischer: '(better is' Bulletin: '?' '?' '?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '?!' Chris Ward: 'when''} 14. dxc6 (14. Nd2 Nb4 15. Nc4 Qc5) 14... axb3 15. cxb7 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with a powerful attack. Fischer, R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960'} Rxa2 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 16. bxc8=Q Rxc8 17. Bd2 {was all one way traffic in Fischer,R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960} {Note} Bxb2 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'}) (12. a3 a4 13. Nd2 $1 Qc5 (13... Qd4 {is simply a bad ending.}))) (11. Nc4 {(Bobby Fischer) or Bulletin: ''} Bg4 {(Bobby Fischer) >= Bulletin: ''} (11... Ng4 {is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is''} 12. Nd5 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: 'White still keeps control.' Bulletin: '' '' ''} (12. Bxg4 Bxg4 {was in Ciocaltea,V-Gligoric,S Belgrade 1962 objectively a little better for White given the idea of e4-e5.} {Upon the immediate Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon the immediate''} 13. Qxg4 {Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in''} Qxc4 $5 {Instead White puts paid to that option. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'Instead White puts paid to that option.''}) 12... Nf2+ {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 13. Rxf2 Qxf2 14. Be3 Qh4 15. Ncb6 (15. g3 {(trying to put the c8-rook to good use through the threat of ...c4)} Qh3) 15... Rb8 16. Nxc8 Rfxc8 (16... Rbxc8) 17. g3 Qh6 18. f5 g5 {with chances for both sides in Amonatov,F (2636)-Li,C  (2732) Riadh 2017.} {The obvious 8...Bg7 sees us most likely headed for a 9 Bc4 Yugoslav Attack but intriguing is that Gligoric (back then considered one of the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters) reached this position three years later and on that occasion preferred} 19. Qd2 {when Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) when'} (19. h4 Bxb2) 19... Bxb2 {(justified as Black usually wants to recapture with the b-pawn but this might render ...Rc8 a waste of time.)} (19... Bf6 20. h4) 20. Bxg5 Qg7 21. Rf1) {Kotov recommends} 12. Be3 {Bobby Fischer Bulletin: 'Kotov,A'} Qh5 {(he gives only} 13. Bxg4 Nxg4 14. h3 $11 {and Black's all right. So best is probably Bulletin: '=' '=' '='} (14. Bg1 {and then either Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'and then either''} b5 {or 14 g4 with the sort of advantage that we've been used to seeing White get in the 'Anti-Soltis' variations. Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) or 14 g4 with the sort of advantage that we've been used to seeing White get in the 'Anti-Soltis' variations.'} 15. Nb6 {(Bobby Fischer) was the usual, and good, procedure. The text pennits Black to capture what was considered, then, to be the more important of the White Bishops.} {Bobby Fischer: 'A concession. But on'} Bd4 {wouldn't have incurred the same weaknesses. However regards for example Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'wouldn't have incurred the same weaknesses. However regards for example''} 16. h3 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: 'followed by h4-h5 produces a strong and almost mechanical attack.' Bulletin: '' '' '' Bulletin: ' h2-h4-h5'} Bxg1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 17. Nxa8 {I would add that although an exchange sac on c3 wouldn't be as effective with the knight on a5 rather than e5 as it obstructs the a-pawn (e.g. following 12...Rc8 13 h4 Rxc3 14 bxc3 Qc7) an alternative solution presents itself in the form of} Nf2+ $5 18. Rxf2 Qxd1 {when Black definitely has reasonable play to compensate for his backward d6-pawn.} {Bobby Fischer: 'Releasing the central tension this way is wrong. Correct is'} 19. Rxd1 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '>=' Bulletin: ' Chris Ward: '!?''} Bxf2) 14... b5 $2 (14... Nxe3 $1 15. Qxh5 {(after} gxh5 16. Nxe3 (16. Z0 $13 {but Black's two Bishops may offset the weakness of the d-Pawn. Bulletin: '× d6; '})) ({NOT} 14... Bxc3 $2 {Bobby Fischer} 15. bxc3 {Keeping things simple is Bobby Fischer: 'A totally new idea at the time.' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Keeping things simple is''} Nf6 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''})) 11... Nb4 {Obviously useful although a quick reminder that castling is no longer possible whilst the h6-pawn hangs. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Obviously useful although a quick reminder that castling is no longer possible whilst the h6-pawn hangs.''} 12. Nb3 Qb6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} 13. g4 {Given my not exactly secretive affiliation to the Dragon bishop (and hence its opposite number too), it won't come as a big surprise that I'm not a fan of this move! Though not yet of course the player he was to become, Fischer was pretty nifty at the age of 16. Apparently Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he awarded the move with a '!!' but I'm simply going to offer the respect that one of the greatest ever players is due by not awarding it with a '?!'! Bobby Fischer: '!?' Bulletin: '!?' '!?' '!?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Given my not exactly secretive affiliation to the Dragon bishop (and hence its opposite number too), it won't come as a big surprise that I'm not a fan of this move! Though not yet of course the player he was to become, Fischer was pretty nifty at the age of 16. Apparently Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he awarded the move with a '!!' but I'm simply going to offer the respect that one of the greatest ever players is due by not awarding it with a '?!'!''} (13. f5 d5 14. e5 Ne4 15. Nxe4 $132 {, etc.} {(if} dxe4 16. fxg6 hxg6 17. Bc4 Be6 $2 18. Bxe6 $1 $19 {wins for Black)} Qxe6 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 19. Bf4 Rac8 20. Nd4 Qd5 21. c3 {doesn't remotely cut it and so instead Black tries another approach.} {With the idea of f4-f5 still on White's mind, this is calm and strong although actually} Nd3 $5 {blocking out the Dragon bishop, taking advantage of the d-file pin and vacating the e4-square for the knight held a certain amount of appeal too! Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) blocking out the Dragon bishop, taking advantage of the d-file pin and vacating the e4-square for the knight held a certain amount of appeal too!'} {An important preparatory move. On the immediate} 22. Qg4 $5 {Bobby Fischer} Nxe5 23. Bxe5 Qxe5 $1 $132 {gives Black good play.} {On} 24. Rae1 {Bobby Fischer} Qd5 25. Re3 e5 $40 {continues the Pawn stampede.} 26. Nb5 $1 {was the best defence both protecting d6 and anticipating g4-g5. Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) was the best defence both protecting d6 and anticipating g4-g5.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds out after'} Rfd8 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} 27. b3 Qxb3) (13. Bf3 Be6 {is surely most direct. White aims for f4-f5 and if Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is surely most direct. White aims for f4-f5 and if''} 14. Nd4 {then Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) then'} Bc4 $1 15. Nce2 (15. Re1 $132 {(Bobby Fischer) (threatening e5 Bulletin: ' e6-e5'} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Ng4) 15... e5 16. Nb5 d5 {is cute. Bad things are going to happen on the h-file!}) (13. Ra3 Rd8 14. Bf3 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: '-f5 gives White a strong game.' Bulletin: '' '' '' Bulletin: ' f4-f5'} d5 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 15. e5 Ne4 {at first may seem promising because the Dragon bishop has some life. Unfortunately} 16. Nd4 f6) (13. Nd2 $1 Bd7 (13... d5 14. e5 Ne4 $1 15. Ncxe4 dxe4 {is suddenly a mega initiative for White with the threat to d6 and} 16. c3 Nd3 {highlighting the danger to Black.} {Or} 17. Nxe4 Nxc1 $1 {bringing into play both Qxg6+ and Nf5 i.e. a massive attack!} ({Remember} 17... Nxb2 18. Qd4 $1) 18. Qxc1 $5 {looks best and after Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) looks best and after'} Bf5 19. Ng3 $5 {brings the concept of ...a4 into play. It still seems fine to me.}) (13... Be6 14. f5 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'}) (13... Rd8)) 13... Bxg4 14. Bxg4 Nxg4 15. Qxg4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Timing is important. ON'} Nxc2 {Unless Black is serious about sacing the exchange on c3, this isn't the right move here. Instead again Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Unless Black is serious about sacing the exchange on c3, this isn't the right move here. Instead again''} 16. Nb5 (16. Nd5 $18 {releases an avalanche.} Qxb3 17. Nxe7+ Kh8 18. f5 $18 Nxa1 19. f6 Bxf6 20. Rxf6 $18 {wins a piece)} {We all know it's not always easy to deliver mate when Black has a Dragon bishop and White no equivalent dark-squared bishop to challenge it. Nevertheless I was very surprised not to see the tempting} Rfe8 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 21. Nd5 Qc4 {given how strong the threat of f4-f5 is.} {Several Yugoslav chess journalists scurried forward toward the analysis room, where Matanovich was explaining the game on a demonstration board. Apparently the feeling was that I had just blundered. The more obvious} 22. Nc3 {(Bobby Fischer) looks good, but Black still has defensive resources with} (22. Bh6 Rxe4) 22... Nb3 23. Bh6 Nc5 (23... Kg8)) 16... Nxa1 17. Nxa1 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} Qc6 18. f5 (18. Qf3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} Rfc8 19. Be3 (19. Kg2 Qc4 20. b3 Qxc1 $1) (19. Nb3 Qc4 (19... Z0)) 19... Bxb2 20. Rb1 (20. Na7 {Bobby Fischer} Qxa4 $18 {wins the exchange. Finally on}) (20. Nb3 {Bobby Fischer} Qc4 $1) 20... Bf6 21. Na7 Rxa7 {and essentially Black is getting mated.} 22. Bxa7 Qxa4 {is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim. Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing. Or if' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim.''}) 18... Qc4 19. Qf3 Qxa4 20. Nc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Qxa1 21. Nd5 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} (21. Nxa8 Rxa8 {There can be no running as Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'There can be no running as''} 22. fxg6 fxg6 23. Qb3+ ({OR} 23. Qf7+ Kh8 24. Qxe7 Qa4 {mate)} (24... Qb1 {Indeed Bobby Fischer: 'The threat against the d-Pawn is awkward to meet. On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Indeed''} 25. Qxb7 Re8 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} 26. Re1 Qd3 {might see the neat line Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'might see the neat line''}) {Upon Bobby Fischer: 'On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon''} 25. Be3 {simplest is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'simplest is''} Re8 {The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn. Bobby Fischer: '+-!' Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.' Bulletin: '+-!' '+-!' '+-!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn.''} {Not} 26. Bd4 {Bobby Fischer}) 23... Kh8 24. Qxb7 Rg8 {demonstrates. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'demonstrates.''} (24... Rf8 25. Rxf8+ Bxf8 26. Qc8 {mate)}) 25. Qb3 {Bobby Fischer} (25. Qxe7) 25... Qb1 $1 26. Qc4) 21... Rae8 22. Bg5 {However White of course isn't interested in a queen swap. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'However White of course isn't interested in a queen swap.''} ({Or} 22. Bh6 Qxb2 {Or} 23. Nxe7+ Rxe7 24. Bxg7 f6) 22... Qxb2 23. Bxe7 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} (23. Nxe7+ Rxe7 24. Bxe7 Be5) 23... Be5 {Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn.''} 24. Rf2 (24. Qh3 Qe2) 24... Qc1+ {Bobby Fischer: 'This is what the crowd thought I had overlooked.'} 25. Rf1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Qh6 26. h3 $1 {Just as strong as ever here. Bobby Fischer: 'I've made this sacrifice so often, I feel like applying for a patent!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Just as strong as ever here.''} (26. Qf2 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds out for a while.'} {Keeping the king trapped is logical although actually} a4 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 27. Bxf8 Rxf8 $1 {would have done the business too i. e.} 28. fxg6 hxg6 29. Ne7+ Kh8) 26... gxf5 27. Bxf8 Rxf8 28. Ne7+ {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} (28. exf5 a4 29. Ne3 $1 $18 {Bronstein,D} {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Qh4 {would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through''}) 28... Kh8 29. Nxf5 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} Qe6 30. Rg1 $1 (30. Qa3 {simply picks up the rook.} {White still retains a winning attack after} Rg8 {Bobby Fischer} 31. Qe3 Qa2) 30... a4 31. Rg4 {Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7.''} (31. Rf1) 31... Qb3 32. Qf1 (32. Qxb3 axb3 33. Rg1) {Bobby Fischer: 'Again a mistake!'} 32... a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'mates more quickly. Black resigns' Bulletin: ''} 33. Rg3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} Qxg3 (33... Qxg3 34. Nxg3 {equally does the job although Black resigns now anyway as Rf1+ will soon be mating.} {The threat was Qh7+ followed by g7+ whilst} a2 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'}) 1-0

[Event "Short & Sweet: The King's Indian Attack"]
[Site "Main Level"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Myagmarsuren, Lhamsuren"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C12"]
[PlyCount "63"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]
[SourceTitle "Main Level"]
[Source "Chessable.com"]

{Crushing victories like this have convinced me to start employing the King's Indian Attack myself.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Bb4 (4... Be7) (4... dxe4) 5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 (6. exf6 hxg5 7. fxg7 Rg8) 6... Bxc3 7. bxc3 (7. Bxc3 Ne4 8. Ba5 (8. Bb4 c5 9. dxc5 Nxf2 10. Kxf2 Qh4+) 8... O-O (8... b6 9. Bb4 c5 10. Ba3 cxd4 11. Qxd4 Nc6 12. Bb5) 9. Bd3 Nc6 10. Bc3 Nxc3 11. bxc3 f6) 7... Ne4 8. Qg4 g6 (8... Kf8 9. Nf3 (9. h4 c5 10. Rh3) 9... c5 10. Bd3 Nxd2 11. Kxd2 Nc6 12. Qf4 Qe7) 9. Bd3 Nxd2 10. Kxd2 c5 (10... Qg5+ 11. Qxg5 hxg5 12. g4) 11. Nf3 (11. Qf4 cxd4 12. cxd4 Qa5+ 13. Ke3 b6 14. Ne2 Nd7 15. Rhc1 Ba6) 11... Nc6 (11... Qc7 12. Qf4 f5) (11... Bd7 12. dxc5) 12. Qf4 (12. Rab1) 12... Qc7 (12... Qa5 13. Rab1 (13. Rhb1 b6 14. a4 Ba6 15. Bb5 Rc8 16. dxc5 bxc5 17. Bxc6+ Rxc6 18. Rb8+ Rc8) 13... b6 14. dxc5 Qxc5 15. Nd4 Nxd4 16. cxd4 Qa5+) (12... g5 13. Qf6 Qxf6 14. exf6 g4 15. Ne5 cxd4 16. cxd4 Nxd4 17. h3) 13. h4 $1 {It was the first game that featured this move. White wants to avoid *...a4-a3* from Black. It would let Black weaken our queenside structure because *b2-b3 *would be forced. Black can then proceed with ideas like ...Nd4 to claim more dark squares on the queenside.} (13. Qf6 Rg8 14. h4 Qa5 (14... h5) 15. h5 gxh5 16. Rxh5 cxd4 17. Rah1) 13... f5 {It looks correct to open up the queenside.} 14. g4 cxd4 15. cxd4 {Fischer decided that he can reach the g4-square through the more active e3.} Ne7 (15... Bd7 16. gxf5 gxf5 (16... exf5 17. Qg3 Ne7 18. e6 Qa5+ 19. c3 Bxe6 20. Rhe1) 17. Rhg1 O-O-O 18. Rg6) 16. gxf5 $6 exf5 17. Bb5+ Kf8 (17... Kd8 18. Bd3 Be6) (17... Nc6 18. Bxc6+ bxc6 (18... Qxc6 19. e6 Bxe6 20. Ne5 Qd6 21. Nxg6 Qxf4+ 22. Nxf4) 19. Rhg1) (17... Bd7 18. Bxd7+ Qxd7 19. e6) 18. Bd3 Be6 19. Ng1 Kf7 20. Nh3 Rac8 (20... Rag8) 21. Rhg1 {White slowly brings his pieces to Black's King.} b6 (21... Qc3+ 22. Ke3 Nc6 23. Rab1 Nb4 24. Rxb4 Qxb4 25. h5 Rcg8 26. hxg6+ Rxg6 27. Rxg6 Qe1+ 28. Kf3 Kxg6 29. Qh4 Qd1+ 30. Kg2 Rg8 31. Kh2) 22. h5 Qc3+ 23. Ke2 $1 {[%csl Rg7] Setting up a deadly net which will strangle Black's King. Qg5 is an immediate threat.} Nc6 (23... g5 24. Nxg5+ hxg5 25. Qxg5 Ke8 26. Qf6 Kd7 27. Bb5+ Rc6 28. Qxh8 Qxc2+ 29. Kf1) (23... Rcg8 24. hxg6+ Rxg6 (24... Nxg6 25. Qf3) 25. Qh4) 24. hxg6+ $1 {[%cal Ge4f6,Ge4g5,Ge4d6] Bringing more pieces into play. If Black takes on e4 we recapture with the Rook and can consider Rg4 ideas.} Kg7 (24... Ke7 25. Qh4+ Kd7 26. Rad1 Qxd4 (26... Nxd4+ 27. Qxd4) 27. Nf4) 25. Rad1 {[%cal Bh4h5]} Nxd4+ (25... Qxd4 {seems more thematic.} {White sees that Black reduced control over the e6-square by removing his Bishop from c8 but} 26. Kf1 {followed by Qh5 was simple and strong. White's attack would be almost impossible to parry without material losses.} {Nowadays, it is considered best to start with} Qxe5 {.} 27. Re1 Qxf4 28. Nxf4 Bd7 29. Nh5+) 26. Kf1 Rhe8 27. Rg3 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Be4h4,Gh4h8] Very instructive. White uses his h-pawn to force more weaknesses while preparing Rh4. This is what we call a multipurpose move!} (27. Qh4 Nf3 {25.Bf5! exf5 26.Re7! may be even stronger. It is a line that the one and only Kingscrusher suggested to me!} 28. Qf6+ Kg8 29. Bxf5 Nh2+ 30. Kg2 Qf3+ {I am sure Fischer saw that when he decided on 23. Bf6!.} 31. Kxh2 $1 {with the familiar idea of Ng4 was simple and strong.} Qxf5 {right away was possible and probably stronger.}) 27... Nc6 28. Qh4 Nxe5 (28... Qxe5 29. Nf4) 29. Nf4 $3 {What an amazing move! White decides that he needs the light-squared Bishop to finish Black.} Ng4 30. Nxe6+ $1 {[%csl Rg7]} Rxe6 31. Bxf5 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Bh5g6,Bg2e4]} {Black resigned. It is a shame because he didn't let Fischer demonstrate a beautiful idea} Qc4+ 32. Kg1 (32. Kg1 Nxf2 33. Qxc4 {.} {Black's last chance was} Rxc4 {but after} 34. Kxf2 Rf4+ 35. Rf3 {followed by Nd6 White's attack would be very strong.}) 1-0

