1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Larsen u.a. Ivkov,Borislav} e6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A minor surprise, coming from Larsen. The King of the Hypermoderns meets the premiere Classicist on his own turf.''} 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer, having played the White side of the Lopez all his life, understood the terrain like his own backyard.''} Nf6 4. g3 c5 5. Bg2 Nc6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I'm not sure if this was a surprise for Fischer, since Larsen had just written a pamphlet on the Open Ruy Lopez at the time this game was played. Larsen writes: "I thought that ordinary chessplayers ought to play such openings instead of following the latest fashion by playing, for instance, the slow positional manoeuvres of the Closed Variation." I completely agree with Larsen's sentiment. I involuntarily wince when some of my students frantically attempt to memorize some Komodo/Houdini-checked incomprehensible yet super-fashionable opening variation played by Carlsen and Nakamura (which only they understand!); I'm reminded of zoo chimps who imitate human behaviour (I say this out of love, at the grave risk of facing the reader's wrath: the truth will set us free!), without understanding what it means to be human. I just wonder why the Open Lopez isn't played more often today, since it isn't so easy for White to extract an edge against it, and it feels every bit as sound as the Closed lines and offshoots such as the Marshall Gambit. ''} 6. Ngf3 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. e5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why not take on e5 with the knight, which frees White's queen to swoop into the kingside later on?''} Nd7 9. Re1 b5 10. Nf1 (10. Qe2 b4 $1 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer isn't interested in a dynamically balanced ending after''} 11. h4 a5 12. Nf1 $10 {Larsen Larsen Larsen u.a.: '='} a4 {Larsen u.a.} 13. a3 {.} {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'However, he might have abandoned the queenside in favour of the attack with ''} bxa3 $1 { 29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2  29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2 Larsen u.a.: '  29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2 Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?''} 14. bxa3 Na5 $1 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and if ''} 15. Ne3 Ba6 $1 16. Bh3 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'then ''} d4 17. Nf1 Nb6 $18 { 33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis  33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis Larsen u.a.: ' 33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', intending Rf5, Rxg5+ and wins (as suggested by GM Mednis), although Black's defence can certainly be improved: for example, 30...Qc4 or 29...d4 both seem to end in a draw with best play.''} {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or''} 18. Ng5 Nd5 $18 {33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# 33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# Larsen u.a.: '33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# Cyrus Lakdawala: ', followed by''} 19. Bd2 {Larsen u.a.} Bxg5 $1 20. Bxg5 Qd7 {etc}) 10... b4 11. h4 a5 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '?!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Larsen plays the move he advocates in his book.''} 12. Bf4 a4 13. a3 bxa3 14. bxa3 Na5 (14... Nd4) 15. Ne3 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!'} Ba6 16. Bh3 d4 $5 {? Mednis ? Mednis Larsen u.a.: '?Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Sometimes a move need not be strong (or what a computer considers strong), if it contains the property of agitating the opponent's mind with false dreams.   QUESTION: This looks suicidal. Why did Larsen puncture all the dark squares around his king?   ANSWER: It does indeed appear as if the leaking dark squares will prove to be the genesis of Black's future misery. Larsen's move is made even more radical by the fact that he doesn't own a dark-squared bishop! I think he played 16...g6 for two reasons: 1. The alternative, 16...Nf8, entails a retreat, which isn't in Larsen's never-back-down chess character. 2. Larsen wasn't yet terrified of Fischer (remember, this game was played well before the 0-6 drubbing) and decided to provoke him into an attack. ''} 17. Nf1 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '"I don't place faith in a post-death paradise," muses the bishop, who decides to retool and dedicate his life to selfish, temporal happiness. ''} Nb6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More weirdness.''} 18. Ng5 { d2} Nd5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The only move. ''} 19. Bd2 {Ivkov,Borislav: ''} Bxg5 20. Bxg5 $5 Qd7 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?''} 21. Qh5 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why back up to f1?   ANSWER: It's a strange move. Fischer wants to play g2-g3 without allowing ...Qh3. ''} Rfc8 (21... Bb7) 22. Nd2 (22. Nh2) 22... Nc3 (22... c4 23. Bg2 cxd3) 23. Bf6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer may be overestimating his chances. We sense counterfeit enthusiasm and by this point I'm not so certain Fischer retained 100% faith in his attack.''} Qe8 24. Ne4 (24. Bxg7 Kxg7 25. Qg5+ (25. Ne4 $6 Nxe4 26. Rxe4 Kh8) 25... Kf8 $14 {Larsen}) 24... g6 (24... Nb3) 25. Qg5 (25. Qh6 Qf8) 25... Nxe4 26. Rxe4 c4 (26... Bb7) 27. h5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '?' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This looks really ominous. Three white attackers encircle Black's king. The reality, according to the computers (and Larsen!) is that Black has adequate resources to defend. ''} (27. Rxd4 cxd3 28. Rg4 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '='} (28. cxd3 $1 $19 {Mednis Mednis Larsen u.a.: 'Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and White drops a piece due to the pin on the rook.''} Nb3) (28. Qh6 Qf8) {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Line 'b' is no better: ''} 28... Rc7 $2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??''}) 27... cxd3 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Very annoying. The queen pins the e3-rook and attacks b2.''} (27... Rc7 28. Qh6 Qf8 {is a probable draw.}) 28. Rh4 (28. Qh6 Qf8) (28. hxg6 fxg6 29. Be7 (29. Qh6 Ra7) 29... Rc6) 28... Ra7 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '-+!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attack and counterattack mutually develop along distinctly separate evolutionary tracks. Larsen plays like a White House press secretary, declaring a public position while all the time realizing that secret measures are in the works, hidden from the public's view.   EXERCISE (critical decision): How should White meet Black's last move?  We must condense, into a single choice, the plots and schemes of the last dozen moves. Pick carefully from the following options: a) 29 Qh3, going for the king, intending Qh6, Bf6 and (after forcing ...Ne6) mate by Qxh7+, Rh3+ and Rh8. b) 29 cxb4, simply liquidating the problem. c) 29 f3, agreeing to an ending, having decided that lines 'a' and 'b' both fail. ''} (28... dxc2 {Larsen u.a.} 29. hxg6) 29. Bg2 $4 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fiery élan fails to make up for a move's fundamental unsoundness. Sometimes we place faith in things whose nature is unstable and ultimately unreliable. If White's attack is a banquet, then Fischer's last move is the dish of steamed kale. Externally, it appears as if Black's king is barely alive, as his watchful hospital nurses continue to ply him with oxygen, morphine and lime Jello, but the injection of 28 b3 b4! has reinvigorated him completely. Fischer normally possessed a deadly accurate pattern recognition algorithm in complex positions, but not this time! When the devil very cleverly disguised as a serpent in the Garden of Eden, encouraged the not-terribly-bright Eve to defy God and eat an apple – I would be tempted by a mango or a slice of watermelon. But an apple? Big deal! – a realization should have struck her: "Hey! This could be a con job: Snakes don't talk!" Here we see a rare example of Fischer, in Eve-like fashion, falling for the devil's tricks by failing to read the fine print in the contract.''} dxc2 (29... Qf8 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(it appears as if powerful hands grab Black's king, toss him into the cold darkness, and slam shut the dungeon door; unfortunately, this is a complete illusion) ''} 30. Be4 $19 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins for Black. Yes, sometimes Santa can be a complete bugger, bringing us only coal. The queen isn't the absentee spouse everyone believes her to be. A geometric gremlin peers forth, completely messing up Fischer's intention. Unbelievably, it seems Fischer hallucinated and missed this trick, when any hopes White may have entertained about a glorious mate go up in a puff of smoke. White finds himself a full rook down and his constantly devolving attack reminds us of one of those left to right displays in a natural history museum, of apes gradually morphing into the Homo Sapiens we are today – but in reverse!''} {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: White's best is (the admittedly depressing) line 'c' with ''} dxc2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} 31. hxg6 fxg6 32. Bxg6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: ', when ''} hxg6 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '='}) 30. Qh6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The queen, believing herself a great seductress, prances about in the attitude of Salome dancing before Black's King Herod. The h6-square is merely a rest stop on the way to greater glory. Or so she dreams. The reality of the white queen's ambitions is closer to tossing a handful of seeds into a sealed, airless glass jar and then hoping they sprout. ''} Qf8 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Contrary to popular belief, Larsen didn't always lose to Fischer.''} (30... c1=Q+ 31. Rxc1 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Fischer finds himself in seriously straitened financial circumstances, with a bank balance of $7.33, whereas Larsen's bankroll is in the millions – now the intended mating combination falls slightly short of brilliant)''} Rxc1+ {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Played with an air of finality, but is it really so? "I stand on the threshold of unending greatness," declares the queen, who was a little off on her prediction. This isn't a mystery novel, so I will let the reader know beforehand: The queen is not the murderer she hoped to be!)''} 32. Kh2) {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Here the game came to an abrupt and unscheduled conclusion, since''} 31. Qxh7+ {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} 0-1

