{I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example. ''} 1. e4 {Sokolov,Vladimir Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)' Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Moskalenko,Viktor: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Sokolov,Vladimir "A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)''} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: What is this? White is sacrificing a pawn? ANSWER: Well, yes and no. In fact, as we will see, White can regain the pawn by force. What he is doing is trying to bring about a more open game than usually occurs after 4 e5. He forces the exchange of bishop for knight, thereby gaining his traditional Winawer birthright of the bishop-pair and unopposed dark-square bishop, but without closing the centre. He thereby hopes that he will be able to make more effective use of the bishops. QUESTION: Does this get played much nowadays? ANSWER: No. In recent years, it has lost popularity, but in the past, it was played regularly by Alekhine, Smyslov and Fischer, so it must be respected. It is one of the sharpest replies to the Winawer, and the black player needs to be well armed against it.  Fischer had won with this move against Uhlmann two rounds earlier, so it is  probable that IM Kovacevic made special preparations for just this variation.' White spends a tempo to force the exchange x. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'White spends a tempo to force the exchange x.''} Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion.' Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Accepting the consequences is the right way. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Accepting the consequences is the right way.''} 6. Bc4 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'This is the point of White's fourth move., With the aid of this typical anti-Winawer queen raid, White regains his sacrificed pawn.' Steve Giddins: 'This is the point of White's fourth move., With the aid of this typical anti-Winawer queen raid, White regains his sacrificed pawn. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' This manoeuvre is generally considered to be the most dangerous resource against the Winawer Variation. Black has to face it in many lines. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This manoeuvre is generally considered to be the most dangerous resource against the Winawer Variation. Black has to face it in many lines.''} e6 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' The best defence versus g4 in all open lines.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'The best defence versus g4 in all open lines. ''} 7. Bb3 Be7 8. Be3 {Steve Giddins: 'This is the tabiya for the variation. QUESTION: So what is going on? ANSWER: White has regained material equality, and has his two bishops, with the dark-squared bishop potentially being quite strong. However, he has poor development, and it is also not entirely clear where his king will go. It is unlikely to be safe on the queenside, whilst kingside castling takes a little time, in view of the need to keep the g2-pawn defended. Black has three main moves here, all of which are equally respectable. ' A key position. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'A key position.''} a6 $5 {Steve Giddins: 'This is probably the most solid option. Black plans to develop with ...b6, ...Bb7, ...Qe7 and ...0-0-0.' Steve Giddins: 'This is probably the most solid option. Black plans to develop with ...b6, ...Bb7, ...Qe7 and ...0-0-0. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?'' This has always been considered a good reply.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This has always been considered a good reply. ''} 9. f4 {The most popular (natural) move for White and at least it is not worse than the alternatives. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'The most popular (natural) move for White and at least it is not worse than the alternatives.''} Qc7 $1 {Steve Giddins: ''!' Recommended by Vukovic in "Sahovski Glasnik" in 1935.' Steve Giddins: ''!' Recommended by Vukovic in "Sahovski Glasnik" in 1935. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Originally, recommended by Vukovic in Sahovski Glasnik in 1935. The idea of the fianchetto with ...b7 is a key concept for Black! (see also line-C 8...b6) Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Originally, recommended by Vukovic in Sahovski Glasnik in 1935. The idea of the fianchetto with ...b7 is a key concept for Black! (see also line-C 8...b6)''} 10. O-O $6 {Steve Giddins: ''?!'' Steve Giddins: ''?!' Moskalenko,Viktor: '?!'' This pseudo-active move leads to disaster for the 11th World Champion. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This pseudo-active move leads to disaster for the 11th World Champion.''} Na5 $1 {[%cal Gf6g4] Sokolov,Vladimir: '[ Ng4!]' Steve Giddins: 'This move conceals several points. As well as preparing ...Bb7 and ...0-0-0, it also prevents White from castling long, and sets a tactical trap.  '!' "The key move! White must meet the threat of 11...Ng4, forcing 12 Be7 Nh6 13 Bh4 Bb6 14 Bg3 0-0-0 with better play for Black." (Kovacevic 75) Black prevents 0-0-0 and threatens Ng4, casting White's 10th in doubt.' Steve Giddins: 'This move conceals several points. As well as preparing ...Bb7 a