he pawn? ? (Sokolov 70, Wade/O'Connell 72, Kovacevic 75, Maric 76, Wicker 79) Moskalenko,Viktor: '? (Sokolov 70, Wade/O'Connell 72, Kovacevic 75, Maric 76, Wicker 79) Sokolov,Vladimir: '?' ? (Sokolov 70, Wade/O'Connell 72, Kovacevic 75, Maric 76, Wicker 79)''} Nxb3 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: How can we assess this position? ANSWER: Black has a fine game. He has completed his development, his pawn on e4 is securely defended, and the white queen is somewhat vulnerable on h4. Objectively, the game is no more than equal, as White has certain trumps too (the dark-squared bishop, most notably), but in practice, White's position is probably the more difficult to play. ' Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: How can we assess this position? ANSWER: Black has a fine game. He has completed his development, his pawn on e4 is securely defended, and the white queen is somewhat vulnerable on h4. Objectively, the game is no more than equal, as White has certain trumps too (the dark-squared bishop, most notably), but in practice, White's position is probably the more difficult to play.  Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' This strong and logical move exposes the disadvantages of White's position: a delay in development, a bad pawn structure and a lack of coordination between the pieces. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This strong and logical move exposes the disadvantages of White's position: a delay in development, a bad pawn structure and a lack of coordination between the pieces.''} 14. axb3 {Steve Giddins: 'is probably best play' Here Kovacevic introduced his famous knight tango: Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Here Kovacevic introduced his famous knight tango:''} b5 $5 {Sokolov,Vladimir: '!' Steve Giddins: '!' '!' Steve Giddins: 'Underlining the slight vulnerability of the white queen.  '!' (Maric 76)' Steve Giddins: 'Underlining the slight vulnerability of the white queen.  '!' (Maric 76) Moskalenko,Viktor: '!' Moskalenko,Viktor: '(Maric 76) Sokolov,Vladimir: '!' '!' (Maric 76)'' I like this manoeuvre, which serves to improve Black's 'worst' piece. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'I like this manoeuvre, which serves to improve Black's 'worst' piece.''} 15. g4 $6 {Steve Giddins: '!?' '!?' Steve Giddins: ''?!' "This is simply suicidal." (Kovacevic 75)' Steve Giddins: ''?!' "This is simply suicidal." (Kovacevic 75) Moskalenko,Viktor: '?!'' After this 'natural' move White faces serious difficulties. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'After this 'natural' move White faces serious difficulties.''} b4 $1 {Steve Giddins: ''!' (Mednis 74, Suetin 88, Gurevich 90)' Steve Giddins: ''!' (Mednis 74, Suetin 88, Gurevich 90) Moskalenko,Viktor: '!' Moskalenko,Viktor: '(Mednis 74, Suetin 88, Gurevich 90) '!' (Mednis 74, Suetin 88, Gurevich 90)''} 16. g5 bxc3 17. gxf6 Bxf6 $1 $40 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Reaching the desired position – the 'French' army is ready to attack.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Reaching the desired position – the 'French' army is ready to attack. ''} 18. bxc3 $5 {Sokolov,Vladimir: '?' Steve Giddins: '!' '!' Steve Giddins: 'Setting a nice trap.  '!?' ? (Sokolov 70)' Steve Giddins: 'Setting a nice trap.  '!?' ? (Sokolov 70) Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?' Moskalenko,Viktor: '? (Sokolov 70) Sokolov,Vladimir: '?' ? (Sokolov 70)'' A tactical trick in a difficult situation. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'A tactical trick in a difficult situation.''} Bb7 $3 {Sokolov,Vladimir: '!' Steve Giddins: 'A brilliant riposte.' Steve Giddins: 'A brilliant riposte. Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Sokolov,Vladimir: '!''' Rona Petrosian! According to an anecdote told by Viktor Korchnoi in his book Memuary Shakhmatista, 2006 in the Russian language, it was Petrosian's wife who helped Kovacevic to find this strong reply. I don't think it was – Vlatko Kovacevic simply played a brilliant game! Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Rona Petrosian! According to an anecdote told by Viktor Korchnoi in his book Memuary Shakhmatista, 2006 in the Russian language, it was Petrosian's wife who helped Kovacevic to find this strong reply. I don't think it was – Vlatko Kovacevic simply played a brilliant game!''} 19. c4 d5 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' In accordance with the 'tango' steps, after five moves the knight heads back to f8. This is stronger than the obvious: Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'In accordance with the 'tango' steps, after five moves the knight heads back to f8. This is stronger than the obvious:''} 20. exd5 e4 $1 {[%cal Ge7h4] Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Now the game is over in view of ...h4  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Now the game is over in view of ...h4 ''} 21. Qg3 {Steve Giddins: '''' Steve Giddins: ''' Moskalenko,Viktor: '''} (21. Qxe4 Rfe8) 21... Qxg3+ {Steve Giddins: ''!'' Steve Giddins: ''!' Moskalenko,Viktor: '!''} 22. Nxg3 Bxa1 {Steve Giddins: 'The attack is overwhelming. '} 23. Rxa1 f6 24. Kf2 Rfe8 25. Rd1 a5 26. c5 Red8 27. c4 a4 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'The Yugoslav master continues to conduct the attack with great verve. ' Another tango step – and one more piece joins in the attack.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Another tango step – and one more piece joins in the attack. ''} 28. b4 a3 29. b5 a2 30. c6 Bc8 {Sokolov,Vladimir: '!!' Sokolov,Vladimir: '[ Ng2]' Steve Giddins: 'Precise to the very end. There is no defence to the threat of 31...Nxe2. '!'' Steve Giddins: 'Precise to the very end. There is no defence to the threat of 31...Nxe2. '!' Moskalenko,Viktor: '!' Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Sokolov,Vladimir: '!!' Sokolov,Vladimir: '[ g2]' '!''' ''A superb game, particularly when you consider who was playing the White side'' - Psakhis Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '''A superb game, particularly when you consider who was playing the White side'' - Psakhis''} 31. Bb6 0-1

