{[%evp 0,57,19,11,11,11,5,6,6,19,19,-5,-14,-14,-10,-46,12,-80,-54,-54,-51,-41,-20,-31,2,10,10,-47,-11,-61,-11,18,16,-15,16,26,28,0,3,-4,1,-8,30,-30,10,-33,-15,8,54,33,33,-10,-10,-54,226,214,334,334,836,989] Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 45 Ghosts. Steinitz, nicknamed "the Austrian Morphy" (although two styles could hardly be more dissimilar), apparently exercises a great influence on Fischer, who has restored several of his pet lines to prominence. One of these is the bizarre 9.Nh3!?, found wanting at the turn of the century, and perhaps best left there. Bisguier appears unimpressed, regaining his gambit Pawn with a strong initiative. But he misses several opportunities to gain an advantage and is gradually outplayed. Just at the critical moment, when the chances are roughly equal, he commits the same kind of gross oversight that had doomed Fischer against Spassky (game 18). He suffers the same fate. '} 1. e4 {Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: ', Best by test. '} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Bobby Fischer: ', The last time I played this move in a tournament was when I was 12, at the 1955 US Junior Championship. '} a6 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '; Steinitz considered this to be an unsound sacrificial continuation! '} 4. Bxc6 {Bobby Fischer: ', Tarrasch branded this a "duffer's move" and Panov called it "primitive." But there is no other way for White to try for an advantage.'} dxc6 5. O-O f6 6. d4 Bg4 (6... exd4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'escaping the perpetual and should win.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 7. Nxd4 Ne7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'After 26 ...Re8! therefore, the best White has is'} 8. Nc3 { Bobby Fischer: '(to prevent ...Qh5)'} Ng6) 7. c3 (7. dxe5 Qxd1 8. Rxd1 Bxf3 9. gxf3 fxe5 10. f4 Nf6) 7... exd4 8. cxd4 Qd7 (8... c5 9. d5 Bd6 10. Nbd2 Ne7) 9. h3 $5 {Steinitz,W Bobby Fischer: ', To my knowledge, this is the first time that this move had been employed in Grandmaster chess for over seventy years. It is one of Steinitz's many unique opening contributions. The famous cable match game in 1891 between Steinitz and Tchigorin, which ended in a victory for Black, apparently caused the chess world to shy away from this variation. '} Bh5 10. Ne5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Played by Steinitz in the 6th game of his second match with Tchigorin in 1892.'} Bxd1 11. Nxd7 Kxd7 {} (11... Be2 $1 12. Re1 Kxd7) 12. Rxd1 Re8 13. f3 {Bobby Fischer: ', A difficult choice.'} Ne7 {Bobby Fischer: '; So Black regains the Pawn, but I have faith in my two Bishops. '} 14. Nc3 Kc8 15. Be3 f5 {Bobby Fischer: '; The Queen is forced off the h-file.'} 16. Rac1 fxe4 17. fxe4 g6 18. Bf4 {Bobby Fischer: ', I was worried about the maneuver ...Nd5-f4.'} Bg7 19. d5 Rd8 20. Na4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Serves the double purpose of preventing ...c4 and of enforcing f4. For all the good it does, Black's Bishop on b6 might just as well be a Pawn for the rest of the game. '} Rhf8 21. g3 g5 22. Bxg5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', To prepare c3, driving the Knight away from d4.'} Rf7 { h5; f5 Bobby Fischer: '; Intending ...Qh5 followed by ...Nf5.'} 23. Kg2 cxd5 24. exd5 Kb8 25. Re1 Bf8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Black is playing for an advantage.'} 26. Rf1 {Bobby Fischer: ', The critical position. '} Rg7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; A pity that just when the game was getting interesting, Black has to make this terrible mistake.'} 27. Bf6 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: ', Bisguier slumped and his chest collapsed, as he saw that Black cannot avert the loss of a piece. '} Rg8 28. Rce1 Re8 29. d6 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} cxd6 $1 {;  8xe4 Bobby Fischer: '(threatening ...R8xe4). Bad would be'} 30. Bxe7 Bxe7 31. Rf7 1-0

