{Grigoriants,Sergey: 'I have chosen this Fischer game because it is not as well-known compared to his others. It was played in 1970 when he was already close to becoming the world champion. He put on a great performance in this game and his opening preparation was superb. '} 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Bb5+ Bd7 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Fischer once said that he doesn't like to play against the French Winaver. Weird pawn structures are typical for this opening. Still in this game the great Bobby showed one of model plans for White, and many hungreds games were won by White in such a style in the future. '} 4. Bc4 Bg4 5. f3 Bf5 (5... Bc8) 6. g4 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Black gave up the very important dark-squared bishop in order to destroy White's queenside pawn structure. This is a very risky strategy and probably the reason the whole system is not very populiar at the top level nowadays.'} Bc8 $2 7. Nc3 Nbd7 8. g5 Nb6 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Black is trying to avoid weakening the kingside dark squares.'} 9. Bb5+ $1 Nfd7 10. f4 $1 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'A logical and straightforward approach.'} Nxd5 11. Nxd5 {[ h6] [ h6] [ h6]} c6 12. Bc4 $1 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'This idea has become standard practice. White has found a weak point in Black's position.'} cxd5 13. Bxd5 Ne5 {Grigoriants,Sergey: '?!' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'It looks logical not lose a tempo and develop the knight, but actually this is not the best decition. This knight could be more useful on d8 where it defends the f7-pawn. And the other knight might be well placed on e7, where it could go to f5 at a suitable time.'} (13... e5 14. Nf3 Qa5 15. Bb3 $18 {would be hopeless.} e4 16. Qe2 $5 Nc5) 14. Be4 $1 {'' (eco 74/81, bco 82) ''} Bg4 {Grigoriants,Sergey: '?' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'The critical moment of the game. After the text move White's initiative becomes very strong and Fischer conducts it with great power.'} 15. Nf3 Nc6 16. d4 $1 {[ g4-g5] [ g4-g5] [ g4-g5] Grigoriants,Sergey: 'From this moment Fischer shows his best and even the stubborn Stockfish takes a while before evaluating the position as better for White.'} e6 17. c3 Qc7 {[ e7] [ Ke7] [ e7]} 18. Qa4 f6 (18... Bd6) 19. Rg1 Bxf3 20. Bxf3 O-O-O 21. Qc4 {[ 22.e6, 22.g6] [ 22.Ne6, 22.Ng6] [ 22.e6, 22.g6] Grigoriants,Sergey: '+-' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Now the black position collapses completely.'} Kb8 22. Be3 Bd6 23. gxf6 $1 gxf6 24. Bxc6 bxc6 25. Rf1 $1 ({The endgame after} 25. O-O-O) 25... e5 (25... Rhg8 {#}) 26. fxe5 fxe5 27. O-O-O Rhf8 28. Rxf8 {'!' Fischer's Chess Games p312#589 (Wade/O'Connell) I10/252 (Trifunovic) Moles p244 (Moles) '!'} Rxf8 29. dxe5 Bxe5 {#} 30. Qb4+ 1-0

