{Demuth,Adrien: 'A few months after starting to play chess, I received a book for my 8th birthday. It was "My 60 best games" by Bobby Fischer. I read it a couple of times, with my father. Of course none of us were able to understand all the subtleties, but we were already sensible to the beauty of most of the games. I am still a huge fan of his play, and I am glad to comment one of his games now! This game of Bobby Fischer, was played during his first Candidates tournament, in 1959. At only 16, Fischer managed to get very nice wins in this super strong tournament. This game against Pal Benko, shows his natural tactical skills. '} {Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 11 Unheard melodies.  Paul Morphy is the idol of all the romantics who pine for the swashbuckling chess of yesteryear; but it is rarely possible to succeed with that kind of flamboyance any longer since players now are more evenly matched. A rise in the standard of defense has necessitated a corresponding adjustment in the character of attack. In modern chess most of the beauty resides in the annotations. Brilliancies often exist only as grace notes - because the opposition anticipates and thwarts them with appropriate rejoinders. To the uninitiated, some of the most hard-fought struggles seem devoid of all bravura. That is the situation in this game. Confj-onted with a dozen beautiful outlandish losing variations, Benko chooses what appears to be a prosaic one. Is this "ugliness" not a by-product of skill? Though the reader may feel cheated, and the winner fj-ustrated, does it not argue for the perspicacity of the loser who sidestepped those seductive invitations?'} 1. e4 {Demuth,Adrien Bobby Fischer} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ (4... Ba5 5. e5 (5. b4) (5. exd5) 5... c5) 5. bxc3 dxe4 (5... Ne7 6. Qg4 (6. Bd3 c5 7. exd5 exd5 8. dxc5) (6. exd5 Qxd5 7. Nf3 Bd7 8. c4) 6... O-O 7. e5 (7. Bd3 e5 8. Qh4 exd4) 7... f6 (7... f5) (7... c5 8. Bd3) 8. Nf3 c5 9. exf6 Rxf6 10. Bg5 e5 (10... Qa5 11. Bxf6 Qxc3+ 12. Ke2 Ng6 13. Rc1 gxf6 14. h4) 11. Qg3 Re6 12. Nxe5 Nbc6 13. Bd3 Qc7 14. O-O Nxe5 15. dxe5 c4 16. Bf6 Ng6 17. Bxg6 hxg6 18. Qxg6 Kh8 19. Rae1 gxf6 20. exf6) 6. Qg4 {Demuth,Adrien: 'Fischer is facing a Classical Sicilian. He always has been fond of the Sozin Attack, that is caracterised by 6.c4 (and can be played against several types of Sicilian. It's not a surprise to see that it was already his favourite here.'} (6. f3 c5 (6... e5) (6... Nd7) 7. Be3 (7. Be2 Nc6 (7... Nf6 8. fxe4 Nxe4 9. Qd3 Qa5) 8. fxe4 cxd4 (8... Nf6) 9. Nf3 dxc3 10. Bd3 e5 (10... Qb6) 11. O-O Nge7 12. Qe1 O-O 13. Qxc3 Ng6 14. Bc4 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. Be3 Rc8) 7... Qa5 8. dxc5 (8. fxe4 Qxc3+ 9. Bd2 Qxd4 10. Nf3 Qxe4+) 8... Nc6 (8... Nf6) 9. Kf2 Nf6 10. Qd6 Nd5 (10... Ne7) 11. Re1 Nxe3 12. Rxe3 e5 13. Bc4 Be6 14. Bxe6 Rd8) 6... Nf6 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?'} 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 {Demuth,Adrien: 'In the Sozin Attack, White decides to castle to the kingside, and he will try to advance with f2-f4 later.'} Nbd7 (8... Rg6 9. Qe3 (9. Qd2 b6 10. Bb2 Bb7 11. O-O-O Qe7 12. c4 Nbd7 13. Ne2 O-O-O) 9... Nc6 10. Bb2 Qd6 (10... b6) 11. f3 exf3 12. Nxf3 Bd7 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. c4 Ng4 15. Qd2 f5 16. d5 Nb8 17. h3 Nf6 18. Ne5 Ne4 19. Qd4 Rg3 20. Nf7 Qf4+ 21. Kb1 c5 22. Qe5 Qxe5 23. Bxe5 Rdg8 24. Bd3 Rxg2 25. Bxe4 fxe4 26. Nd6+ Kc7 27. Nxe4+ Kb6 28. Nf6 Ba4 29. Nxg8 Bxc2+ 30. Kc1 Nd7 31. Rdg1) (8... c5 9. Ne2 Bd7 (9... Nbd7) 10. Bg5 Rg6 11. Qh4 Bc6 12. Nf4 (12. d5 exd5 13. Nf4 Rxg5 (13... Qa5 14. Bxf6) 14. Qxg5 Nbd7 15. Be2 Qa5) 12... Qa5 13. O-O-O Qxa3+ 14. Kb1 Nd5 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 16. c4 e5) 9. Ne2 (9. a4 c5 (9... Nb6 10. a5 Nbd5 11. Ne2 Qd6) (9... b6 10. a5 Bb7) 10. a5 Qc7 11. Qd2 b6 (11... Rb8 12. Ne2) 12. Ne2 (12. axb6 Qxb6 13. Ne2 Ba6 14. g3 Bb7) 12... Bb7 13. Qf4 Qc8) (9. Nh3 b6 (9... c5 10. Be2 Qa5 11. Bd2 Rxg2 (11... cxd4 12. cxd4 Qf5) 12. Ng5 cxd4 13. Qg7 Rxg5 14. Qxg5 Qxg5 15. Bxg5 dxc3 16. O-O-O) (9... Z0) 10. Ng5 (10. Be2 Bb7 11. O-O Qe7 12. a4 O-O-O 13. Ba3 c5 14. Qh4 Qd6 15. Rfd1 e3 16. f3 Rxg2+ 17. Kxg2 Rg8+ 18. Ng5 (18. Kh1 Ng4) 18... h6 19. Rg1 (19. dxc5 hxg5 20. cxd6 gxh4+ 21. Kf1 Ne5) 19... hxg5 20. Qg3 Qd5 21. Kf1 Nh5 22. Qg2 Nf4 23. Qg3 Nxe2 24. Kxe2 Qc4+) 10... Rg6 11. Qh4 Bb7 12. Nxh7 Nxh7 13. Qxh7 Qf6 14. Qh3 (14. h4 O-O-O 15. Bg5 Rh8) 14... O-O-O 15. Qe3 Qe7 16. Bb2 f5 17. O-O-O Nf6 18. c4 Rf8 (18... Ng4) 19. f3 f4) (9. f3 b6 (9... c5)) 9... b6 (9... c5 10. Z0 (10. a4 Qc7 (10... b6 11. a5 Bb7 12. dxc5 bxc5 13. Ng3 Ng4 14. Qf4 Qf6) 11. dxc5 Qxc5 12. Qd2 Nb6 13. a5 (13. Qd4 Qxd4 14. cxd4 Bd7) 13... Nbd5) (10. h3 Qa5 11. Bd2 b6 (11... Qa4 12. Ng3 b6 13. dxc5 bxc5 (13... Rg6 14. Qh4 Nxc5) 14. Be2 Ba6 15. O-O Rg6 16. Qh4 Bxe2 17. Nxe2 Qxc2) 12. c4 Qa4 13. Nc3 Qxc2 14. Nb5 cxd4 15. Bb4 a5 16. Nd6+ Ke7 17. Nxc8+ Kd8) (10. g3 b6 11. Bg2 Bb7 12. O-O Qe7 13. a4 Ng4 14. Qf4 f5) (10. Ng3 Qc7 11. Qe3 Qc6 12. a4 a6 13. dxc5 Qxc5 14. Qxc5 Nxc5)) 10. Bg5 {Demuth,Adrien: 'White can't really do without this move.'} (10. a4 Ba6 11. Ba3 c5 $18 {White has too many threats, Black can't handle it.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'A finesse aimed at provoking'} 10. Ng3 Rg6 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'after which White's initiative is blunted.'} (10... Bb7 $1 {Demuth,Adrien} 11. Be2 (11. Bb2 $6 Qe7 12. O-O-O) 11... Qe7 12. O-O $8 O-O-O 13. f3 Rg6 $16) {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 11. Qd2 Bb7 12. Bb2 {Demuth,Adrien: '?!'} Qe7 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: '!' Demuth,Adrien: ''!'' Demuth,Adrien: 'and White more or less has to give up his bishop.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'For 7 Nb3 see game 58, note to Black's sixth move. No good is'} 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. c4 Ng4 15. h3 Nxf2 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'}) (10. Bd2 $1 $16) (10. Bb2 {Demuth,Adrien This is now considered to be the best.} {Bobby Fischer: 'And..'} Bb7 $5 11. O-O-O {Bobby Fischer: 'only helps Black strengthen his center, but after'} Ng4 $13 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has promising tactical chances.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 12. Qf4 {Fischer,R-Gligoric,S Bled ct 1959 Bobby Fischer: 'see game 13 (Fischer,R-Gligoric,S; Bled ct, 1959). By putting immediate pressure on the center, Black forces the Knight to a passive post.'} (12. Qxh7 Qg5+) 12... Qg5 13. Rd2 Nxf2 14. Qxf2 e3) 10... Qe7 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?' Demuth,Adrien: 'Suppressing this annoying bishop is a very common plan, even if it takes a couple of tempi.'} 11. Qh4 (11. Ng3 Ng4 12. Bxe7 Nxh6 $1 13. Bh4 (13. Bb4 a5) 13... Rg4) (11. Nf4 Ng4 (11... Bb7 12. Bb5 Ng4 (12... c6 $2 {Demuth,Adrien As often, this is too slow.} 13. Be2 O-O-O 14. f3) 13. Bxe7 Nxh6 14. Bh4 Nf5 15. Bg3 O-O-O $11 {Forcing a perpetual check, but nothin more.}) 12. Bxe7 Nxh6 $132 {The battle is very tensed and approximately equal.} 13. Bh4 Nf5) 11... Bb7 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: ''!''} 12. Ng3 (12. Nf4 h6 { g8+! +- Bobby Fischer: '(threatening Qg8+! +-'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Gligorich suggests simply'} 12... O-O-O {Gligoric,S} {Bobby Fischer: 'Also satisfactory is'} 13. Nh5 $1 Rg6 14. Nf4 Rxg5 15. Qxg5 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'The text exposes Black to a vicious attack.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Or..'} Rg8 16. Qb5 {Demuth,Adrien In these kinds of Sicilian, it's very usual to see White taking with the c-pawn (while the common principles tell us to use the a-pawn). The point is to have some play on the c-file.} e5) 13. Qxh6 (13. Bxh6 Rh8 $40) 13... O-O-O {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 14. Qh4 Rh8 15. Nh5 Qf8 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'threatening to bring the Queen to g3)'} 16. Bxf6 $18 {Demuth,Adrien} Nxf6 $5 {Demuth,Adrien This was also a possible move order.} 17. Qxf6 Rxh5) 12... h6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Another virtue of Black's last Inove was that it freed this Pawn.'} 13. Bd2 {Demuth,Adrien: 'This is the first really important moment of the game. Black should have gotten rid of the light-squares bishop.'} (13. Qxh6 Ng4 14. Bxe7 Nxh6 $18 {And Black is defenceless against the coming mate.} 15. Bh4 (15. Bb4 $4 a5) 15... Rg4 $18) (13. Bxh6 Rg4 14. Qh3 $5 {Demuth,Adrien} O-O-O 15. Be2 (15. Bd2 Rdg8 16. Be2 $18 {, Judovic,A Bobby Fischer: 'with a decisive attack . Judovic,A'} R4g7) 15... Rgg8 (15... Rg6) 16. Qh4 Rh8 $1 $15 {This may look very dangerous, but White is actually unable to make use of the long dark squared diagonal.} {Bobby Fischer: 'A.) It's too late now for'} 17. Qf4 {Demuth,Adrien: '??'} Nd5 18. Qd2 Rdg8 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'} {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 19. c4 e3 {Bobby Fischer: 'B.)'} 20. Bxe3 {Demuth,Adrien: '?'} Nxe3 21. Qxe3 Bxg2 22. Rg1 Rxh2 $1) (13. Bc1) 13... O-O-O $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' Demuth,Adrien: '?!' Demuth,Adrien: ''?''} (13... Rg4 $40 { xh6 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening Nxh6 with a strong attack.'} 14. Qxh6 $1 {Demuth,Adrien} (14. Qh3) 14... O-O-O 15. c4 Ng8 16. Qe3 f5 17. Nh5 $3 e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. O-O-O Nd3+ 20. cxd3 Qxa3+ 21. Kc2 $13 {And this is still a really complicated position, with at least a draw for White.} exd3+ {Demuth,Adrien} 22. Bxd3 $1 Qa2+ 23. Kc3 $1 Rxd3+ $1 24. Qxd3 $1 {This little move decides the game.} Qa3+ 25. Kc2 $1 Be4) 14. Be2 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?' Demuth,Adrien: ''!''} Nf8 (14... e3 15. fxe3 Bxg2 16. Rg1 Be4 (16... Bb7 17. e4)) (14... Qd6) 15. O-O {Demuth,Adrien: 'Once again, Black has a vast choice. It wasn't easy, and he couldn't find the best move.'} (15. Nh5 $18 Rxg2 (15... N8d7 $16) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds after..'} 15... N8h7 16. Bf4 Rxg2 17. Be5 Nd5 (17... Rg5 18. Nxf6 Nxf6 { h5 +- Bobby Fischer: 'threatening Qh5 +- Wins.' Demuth,Adrien: '+-'} {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 19. Qxh6 Rg6 { g4, Gligoric,S Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Qg4, Gligoric,S'} {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 20. Qh4 {Demuth,Adrien: '?' Demuth,Adrien: 'This is losing by force, and the finish is really elegant.'} Rdg8) 18. Qxe7 Nxe7 19. Bg3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'A.) On..'} e3 20. Rf1 Ng5 21. fxe3 $1 Nf5 22. Bf4 Ne4) (15... Nd5 16. Qxe7 Nxe7 17. Bxh6 {Demuth,Adrien: '!'} (17. Ng3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Mate.'}) 17... Rxg2 {Demuth,Adrien: '?'} 18. Ng3 e3 19. Bxe3 {Demuth,Adrien: '!'} Rxg3) 16. Qxf6 Qxf6 17. Nxf6 $1 $18 Rg6 $1 {Demuth,Adrien The last way to hold, but the position remains bad.} 18. Ng4 $1 e3) ({Bobby Fischer: 'B.) The best chance is'} 15. Bc1 $1) 15... Ng6 {Demuth,Adrien: '?' Demuth,Adrien: 'After this move, Fischer won't give another chance.'} 16. Qxh6 {Demuth,Adrien: '!'} (16. Qh3 Nd5 17. Qxh6 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} Nh4 18. c4 Rh8 19. Qg5 f6 20. Qg4 Rdg8 21. Qxe4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins the exchange.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Not..'} Nc3 22. Qe3 {Demuth,Adrien: '+-'} Nxe2+ 23. Qxe2 Bxg2) 16... Rh8 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' Demuth,Adrien: '?!'} 17. Qg5 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Already Black is without a satisfactory defense.' Demuth,Adrien: '!' Demuth,Adrien: ''+-!'' Demuth,Adrien: 'Black is defenceless, but he will try to make Fischer's task as hard as possible.'} (17. Qe3 $1 Nd5 18. Qg5 Nxc3 19. Bh5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins the Queen.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Not..'} Qxg5 20. Bxg5 Rdg8) 17... Rdg8 18. f3 $1 (18. c4 Nh4 $1 19. Qe3 (19. Qf4 Nxg2 20. Kxg2 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins material.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} e3+) 19... Nf3+ (19... Ng4 20. Bxg4 Rxg4) 20. gxf3 Nd5 21. Qxe4 (21. cxd5 Qh4) 21... Nc3) (18. Qb5 $1 { f2+ Bobby Fischer: 'Threatens Qf2+'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 18. f4) 18... e3 (18... Nh4 19. fxe4 $1 Rxg5 20. Bxg5 $1) (18... exf3 19. Bxf3 {Demuth,Adrien: '+-' Demuth,Adrien: 'Black can't take on f6, so it's completely lost.'}) 19. Bxe3 (19. Qxe3 {Demuth,Adrien: 'The point is that Black is unable to properly protect h7.'} Nd5 20. Qg5 (20. Qe4 Qf6) (20. Qf2 Qh4 $1 $18) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Benko gave this move a lot of thought. On the alternative'} 20. Qd3 Qh4 $1) 20... f6 21. Qg4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins. (This key line wouldn't work had Black interpolated 13 ... Nxb3! earlier!)'} f5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Otherwise'} (21... Rh4) 22. Qg5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'C.)'} Qh7 {Lombardy,W}) 19... Nf8 (19... Nh4 20. Rf2 Rxg5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'B.)'} 21. Bxg5 Nf5 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: 'A very nice blow.'} 22. Ne4 {Demuth,Adrien: 'I think I had never seen quadruple pawns before.'}) 20. Qb5 {Demuth,Adrien: 'The last finesse, White shouldn't leave his opponent have a free lunch on a1.'} Nd5 21. Kf2 {Demuth,Adrien: 'Now, this is over.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'A.) On..'} 21. Bd2 c6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 21... a6 22. Qd3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins' Demuth,Adrien: ''+-''} (22. Qa4 b5 23. Qa5 Qh4) 22... Qh4 {Demuth,Adrien}) 22. Qd3 Qh4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'}) 21... a6 22. Qd3 Rxh2 23. Rh1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now it's skin and bones.'} (23. c4 Rxg3 24. Kxg3 Qh4# $18 {If the queen moves, 23.h5 will mate.}) 23... Qh4 24. Rxh2 Qxh2 (24... Qxg3+ 25. Kg1) 25. Nf1 $1 (25. Ne4 $14 {White obviously keeps the better chances, but things are complicated!} {Bobby Fischer: 'On..'} Rxg2+ 26. Ke1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is devastating: e.g.,'} f5 27. Nf2 Nxe3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Any.'} 28. Qxe3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'forces mate.'} f4 $1 {Demuth,Adrien}) 25... Rxg2+ 26. Ke1 Qh4+ 27. Kd2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black resigns' Demuth,Adrien: 'A really well organised attack, that made his opponent's position completely fall apart.'} Ng6 {Demuth,Adrien It was still possible to misplay, and to lose most of White's advantage.} 28. Re1 Ngf4 29. Bxf4 Nxf4 30. Qe3 Rf2 1-0

