{Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Matanovic,A: 'Chess Informant''} 1. e4 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Matanovic,A'} c5 {Le soviétique avait ajourné le matin contre Larsen pendant 03h30 après s'être couché à 04 h du matin pour analyser. Après une courte pause de 30 mns, il devait affronté le n1 mondial!} 2. Nf3 e6 3. d3 Nc6 4. g3 d5 5. Nbd2 (5. Bg2 dxe4 6. dxe4 Qxd1+ 7. Kxd1) 5... Bd6 6. Bg2 Nge7 7. O-O O-O {Neil McDonald: '!?' '!?' Neil McDonald: 'They always tell you in books not to grab poisoned pawns, but Fischer, Geller, Kasparov, Anand and Topalov are just some of the idiots who don't heed this advice! '} 8. Nh4 (8. Re1 Bc7 9. e5 Ng6) (8. Qe2) 8... b6 {Neil McDonald: 'Black is obviously taking risks, but removing the b2-pawn prevents White from achieving a smooth attacking development with 9 0-0-0. It is therefore the best way for Geller to disrupt the logical flow of the game. '} (8... Be5 9. f4 Bf6 10. Nhf3 g6 11. c3 Bg7) (8... dxe4) 9. f4 dxe4 10. dxe4 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?!'} Ba6 11. Re1 c4 (11... e5 {A l'époque, c'était la ligne principale mais qui a été remplacée par f5 quelques années plus tard.}) 12. c3 Na5 13. e5 Bc5+ {Neil McDonald: '?!' '?!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Neil McDonald: 'This is consistent with the Ukrainian Grandmaster's plan to choose the most risky moves;'} 14. Kh1 Nd5 (14... Rc8 $16 15. b4) 15. Ne4 {Neil McDonald: '?!' '?!'} Bb7 16. Qh5 ({This is consistent with the Ukrainian Grandmaster's plan to choose the most risky moves, but} 16. Nxc5 {was objectively safer and better, as later played by Kasparov and Anand. Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?!'}) 16... Ne7 {Neil McDonald: '?' '?' Neil McDonald: 'It is ironic that Geller played this wild opening line to upset Fischer, but he himself is the first to lose his nerve. He has made a lot of 'crazy' moves and feels that it is time for a couple of 'sane' developing moves: namely bringing out the bishop with check, and then putting the rook on f8. But normal moves don't work in crazy positions.'} (16... Qe8 {is the modern approach to the position, which was used by the young Magnus Carlsen to score a crushing victory in 2004.}) 17. g4 Bxe4 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!'} 18. Bxe4 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'Not only attacking d5, but also opening up the c2-square to the white queen. '} g6 19. Qh6 Nd5 ({An in-depth investigation of the Poisoned Pawn is beyond the scope of this book. Suffice to say that} 19... Kh8 $5 20. Nf3) 20. f5 {Neil McDonald: '?' '?' Neil McDonald: 'Despite thinking for more than twenty minutes, Fischer misses the win and as a consequence loses the game. Not that the winning line was at all easy to find – after the game there was an hour long post mortem in the tournament hall involving Geller, Fischer and other intrigued competitors without the decisive continuation being discovered. The American genius had exclaimed angrily that "it was a crushing position!" and he was right. But whereas Fischer would win almost every time with a tiny advantage and a clear plan to improve his position, here he falters as he has a huge advantage but no strategic signposts to guide him. And it was a lack of inspiration in such situations that was Fischer's Achilles' heel. It should be added that only a handful of players were equipped with the necessary qualities to take advantage of it, and none of them managed to successfully compete with Fischer during his peak years. They were almost always defeated by Fischer's tactical and strategical mastery without ever being able to confuse the position.' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?' Neil McDonald: 'Despite thinking for more than twenty minutes, Fischer misses the win and as a consequence loses the game. Not that the winning line was at all easy to find - after the game there was an hour long post mortem in the tournament hall involving Geller, Fischer and other intrigued competitors without the decisive continuation being discovered. --- The American genius had exclaimed angrily that "it was a crushing position!" and he was right. But whereas Fischer would win almost every time with a tiny advantage and a clear plan to improve his position, here he falters as he has a huge advantage but no strategic signposts to guide him. And it was a lack of inspiration in such situations that was Fischer's Achilles' heel. It should be added that only a handful of players were equipped with the necessary qualities to take advantage of it, and none of them managed to successfully compete with Fischer during his peak years. They were almost always defeated by Fischer's tactical and strategical mastery without ever being able to confuse the position.'} Re8 21. fxg6 {Neil McDonald: 'At first glance it looks as if White's attack is about to succeed, as mate on d7 is threatened. '} fxg6 $17 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'However, Geller is able to block the d-file and threaten a mate of his own on f1. As we discussed above, this counterattack by the black queen would have been impossible if White had taken the chance to play 21 Qc2. '} 22. Nxg6 Qd7 {Neil McDonald: '!!' '!!' Neil McDonald: 'A tremendous move. The bishop shuts out the white queen's attack on e5 and clears the way for 23...Rb8, followed either by 24...Rb1 with a winning simplification, or 24...Rb2 with a lethal counterattack against g2. ' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!!' Neil McDonald: 'A tremendous move. The bishop shuts out the white queen's attack on e5 and clears the way for 23...Rb8, followed either by 24... Rb1 with a winning simplification, or 24...Rb2 with a lethal counterattack against g2.'} (22... hxg6 23. Qxg6+ Kf8 {And so he should play the 'odd' but strong} 24. Rf1+ $1 {, which keeps the c5-square free for the black queen to return to the defence. The tempo she might have gained by checking the white king from c5 is the difference between life and death in such situations.}) 23. Nf4 Rad8 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '-+'} (23... Nxf4 $1 {is suddenly decisive. The difference with 20 Bg4? one move earlier is that Black can't use his queen in the centre to hinder the white attack as ...Qd3 would allow White the killer check Qa4+!. Fischer missed this subtlety and so ends up losing in 25 moves with White. Neil McDonald: 'is suddenly decisive. The difference with 20 Bg4? one move earlier is that Black can't use his queen in the centre to hinder the white attack as ...Qd3 would allow White the killer check Qa4+!. --- Fischer missed this subtlety and so ends up losing in 25 moves with White.'} 24. Bxf4 $142 Rad8 25. Rad1 Qg7 26. Rxd8 Rxd8 27. Bxh7+ $13 {Geller} Qxh7 $142 $5 {Averbakh} 28. Qg5+ $6) 24. Nh5 {Neil McDonald: 'White's last hope is a bishop check on a4. Geller scotches this with a fine king manoeuvre. '} Kh8 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'It turns out that the safest place for the black king is on the third rank. '} 25. Nf6 {Neil McDonald: 'After'} Nxf6 (25... Qg7 26. Nxe8 Rxe8 27. Qxg7+ $17 {Neil McDonald: 'The correct move was'} Kxg7 $142 {Fischer Fischer Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: ', as played by Tal a couple of months after the present game. Upon' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Ü 4/581 Matanovic,A: 'Ue 4/581''} 28. Bg5 {1-0 Tal,M-Bogdanovic,R YUG-URS Budva 1967 (6) Neil McDonald: ', to meet the threat of invasion by the white queen on h7, the move'}) 26. exf6 Rg8 27. Bf4 Rxg4 {the black king is delighted on d5, and the white king is in despair on h1. 0-1 (27) Fischer,R-Geller,E Monte Carlo 1967 EXT 2008 [ChessBase II]} 28. Rad1 Rdg8 (28... Qxd1 29. Qxh7# $19) 29. f7 (29. f7 Qxf7 30. Be5+ R4g7 31. Qxh7#) 0-1

