{Alexander Volzhin: 'This line was known not to be very good but it has received a certain amount of attention recently and from time to time it occurs even at the very highest level, mainly in Michael Adams' games.'} 1. e4 {ChessBase Alexander Volzhin} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The initial position of the variation. Now Black has two different plans. One possibility is the fianchetto of his dark-squared Bishop, and another is to play the light-squared Bishop to f5 and the arising position looks like a typical Caro Kann.'} d6 $5 { g8-h6-f5; × d4} 5. O-O $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Although this game was played almost 40 years ago Fischer's plan is considered as the best nowadays. The idea of this move (followed by 6 .Qd2) is to prevent Black's Knight manoeuvre to f5-square via h6.'} (5. Bxc6+) (5. c3) 5... Bg4 (5... Bd7) 6. h3 $1 Bh5 (6... h5 7. d4 (7. c4 b5 (7... Qf6 8. Qb3 O-O-O 9. Bxc6 bxc6 10. hxg4 hxg4 11. Nh2 Qh6 (11... d5) 12. Qg3) (7... Bd7 8. d4) 8. cxb5 Nd4 9. bxa6+ c6 10. Nxd4 Bxd1 11. Bxc6+ Ke7 12. Nf5+ Kf6 13. Bxa8 Qxa8 14. Rxd1 Qxe4 15. Nc3 Qa8 16. Ne3 Qxa6 17. d4 Kg6 18. b4) 7... b5 8. Bb3 Nxd4 {Kaminski-Kiedrowicz, Bielsko-Biala 1991.} (8... Qf6) 9. hxg4 {Alexander Volzhin} hxg4 10. Ng5 (10. Bxf7+ Kxf7 11. Ng5+ Kg6 (11... Ke8 12. c3 Be7 13. cxd4 Bxg5 14. Qxg4 Bf6 (14... Bxc1) 15. Nc3 c6 16. Ne2 Rh4 17. Qg3 exd4 18. Nxd4) 12. c3 Be7 13. cxd4 $1 Bxg5 14. Qxg4 Nh6 15. Qg3 Nf7 16. f4 exf4 17. Bxf4 Qf6 18. e5 Qf5 19. Nd2 Rh5 20. Bxg5 $18 {Fischer,R-Bergraser,C Monte Carlo 1967} Rxg5 21. Qb3 Qd7 22. e6 Qc6 23. Qd3+ Kh6 24. Ne4) 10... Nh6 11. Be3 Be7 12. Qd2 (12. Nxf7 Nxf7 {Fischer,R-Seidman,H ch-USA 1958} 13. Bxd4 {} exd4 14. Bd5 Ne5 15. Bxa8 Bg5 16. Bc6+ Ke7 17. Qxd4 Bf4 18. Rd1 $40 Rh1+ $2 19. Kxh1 Qh8+ 20. Kg1 Nf3+) 12... Nxb3 13. axb3 {Arakhamia-Petrovic, Tuzla 1987, with a tiny edge in both cases.} Qd7 14. Nc3 f6 15. Nxb5 fxg5 (15... Qxb5 16. Ne6) 16. Qa5 $5 Bd8 17. Na7 Qf7 18. Qd5 Qh5 19. Qe6+ Be7 $2 20. f3 gxf3 21. Rxf3 g4 22. Nc6 Ng8 23. Rf5 Qh1+ 24. Kf2 Qxa1 25. Rf7) (6... Bd7) 7. c3 Qf6 (7... Nf6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'the immediate'} 8. d4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is not good in view of'} Nd7 $1 (8... b5 9. Bb3 {Alexander Volzhin: ', weakening Black's K-side.'} Be7 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nbd2 $14 Qc8 12. Qb1 $1 {Sokolsky,A} {Alexander Volzhin: 'Another possible plan is'} Re8 13. a4 Qb7 {Alexander Volzhin: 'aiming for a small but steady advantage, but recent practice proves Black has good chances of equalising:'} 14. Nh4 Na5 $5 {Alexander Volzhin} 15. Bc2 Nc4 16. Nxc4 bxc4 17. Nf5 Rab8 18. Qa2 Be2 19. Rfe1 Bd3 20. Bxd3 cxd3 21. f3 {and Black has little to worry about, Tiviakov-Galliamova, Elista 1998.} {The alternatives are:} Bf8 {Alexander Volzhin} 22. b4 g6 23. Ng3)) 8. g4 Bg6 $2 {Alexander Volzhin: 'This natural looking move is indeed the decisive mistake!'} 9. d4 {Alexander Volzhin: '!' Alexander Volzhin: 'Exploiting Black's mistake White starts a K-side attack without delay.'} Bxe4 10. Nbd2 $1 Bg6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The only move.'} (10... Bxf3 11. Nxf3 {Alexander Volzhin: 'It's not good for Black to take the d4-pawn:'} e4 12. Re1 (12. Ng5 d5 13. f3 Qd6 14. Qe1) 12... d5 13. Bg5 Qd6 $8 (13... Qe6 14. c4) ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Tempting is'} 13... Qg6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'as was played in Bronstein-Kholmov, Tbilisi 1959 is harmless:'} 14. Qb3 { c7-c6; Bronstein,D-Kholmov,R URS 1959} b5 {Alexander Volzhin} 15. Qxd5 bxa4 16. Ne5 Qe6 17. Qxe6+ fxe6 18. Nxc6) 14. c4 dxc4 (14... f6 $2 15. cxd5 Qxd5 $2 16. Bb3) 15. d5 $16 { Alexander Volzhin: 'and the endgame clearly favors White'} b5 $2 16. dxc6 bxa4 17. Rxe4+ Ne7 18. Bxe7 $1 Bxe7 19. Qe2 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and Black can't avoid gross material losses.'}) (10... Bd3 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Re1 O-O-O {and in this complicated position Black's chances are by no means bad.} 13. Re3) 11. Bxc6+ {Alexander Volzhin: '!'} bxc6 12. dxe5 (12. Qa4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'or'} Ne7 (12... Kd7 13. dxe5 dxe5 $16 14. Nc4 $5 Bd6) 13. dxe5 $1 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: '!'} dxe5 14. Re1 $1 e4 15. Nxe4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'winning the Queen.'} Qxf3 {} 16. Qxc6+ Nxc6 { 0-0-0 Alexander Volzhin: 'intending to castle Q-side should be played, although in my opinion White is clearly better here.'} 17. Nf6+ {} Kd8 18. Re8#) 12... dxe5 (12... Qd8) 13. Nxe5 $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The decisive breakthrough. Now White's attack becomes unstoppable.'} Bd6 (13... O-O-O 14. Qe2 Kb7 15. Nb3) 14. Nxg6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Now Black is helpless against threats on the g-file.'} (14. Nxc6 h5 $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and White wins by force:'}) 14... Qxg6 (14... hxg6 15. Ne4 Qe5 (15... Qh4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is also insufficient:'} 16. Nxd6+ cxd6 17. Qxd6 Qxh3 18. Re1+) 16. Nxd6+ Qxd6 17. Qf3 $18 {[%emt 0:00:15]  g5 Alexander Volzhin: 'and Black can't parry the threat of Ng5'}) 15. Re1+ Kf8 ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Black can't accept the sacrifice in view of'} 15... Ne7 16. Nc4 $1 $18 { f2-f3; xg4 Alexander Volzhin: '!' Alexander Volzhin: 'with decisive threats.'} {Alexander Volzhin: 'It would be very good for Black to transfer his light-squared Bishop to the g6-square but White's last move made this manoeuvre impossible:'} O-O-O $2 ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Black can't allow the opening of the h-file:'} 16... Rd8) (16... h5) 17. Qa4) 16. Nc4 h5 17. Nxd6 cxd6 (17... Qxd6 18. Qxd6+ $1 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: '!'} {Alexander Volzhin: 'Or'} cxd6 19. Bf4 Rd8 20. Rad1 d5 21. Rd4 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and White wins.'}) 18. Bf4 d5 (18... Rd8 19. Qe2 hxg4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is also bad:'} 20. hxg4 Qh7 21. Bxd6+ { g1 Alexander Volzhin: ', Rg1 to follow.'}) 19. Qb3 hxg4 {Alexander Volzhin: '?' Alexander Volzhin: 'A blunder but Black's position is lost anyway.'} (19... Ne7 20. Rxe7 $18 Kxe7 21. Qb7+) (19... Nf6 20. Qb7 Re8 21. Rxe8+ Nxe8 22. Re1 Qf6 23. Qc8) 20. Qb7 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Bobby Fischer at his best!'} (20. Qb4+ Ne7 21. Qxe7+ Kg8 22. h4 $1) 20... gxh3+ 21. Bg3 $1 Rd8 22. Qb4+ $1 (22. Qb4+ Ne7 23. Qxe7+ Kg8 24. Qxd8+) 1-0

