{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller. ''GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller.  ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller. '''} 1. d4 {Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer'' Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer''} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 6. Nc3 {Bobby Fischer: 'A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge.''A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge.'''} Nbd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More flexible than''More flexible than ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'More flexible than'''} 6... d5 {Bobby Fischer: 'which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued''which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued'''} 7. Ne5 c6 8. e4 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now Black could have equalized easily with''and now Black could have equalized easily with ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'and now Black could have equalized easily with'''} dxe4 {Bobby Fischer: 'instead of..''instead of.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'instead of..'''} (8... Be6 $2 {Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966''Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 ChessBase: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966'''}) 9. Nxe4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Bh3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not..''Not.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Not..'''} 10... c5 $2 11. Qf3 $1) 11. Re1 Nd7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' '=' '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..''Et'Etc.. ChessBase: '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..''' c.. ChessBase: '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'''}) 7. b3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter.''A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter.'''} 7. e4 e5 {Bobby Fischer: 'also presents Black with no opening problems.''also presents Black with no opening problems. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'also presents Black with no opening problems.'''}) 7... e5 8. dxe5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after''Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after'''} 8. e4 exd4 9. Nxd4 Re8) 8... dxe5 9. e4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' '=' '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).''"T'"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH). ChessBase: '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).''' he turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH). ChessBase: '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).'''} Re8 10. Ba3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is''Better is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Better is'''} 10. a4 { Bobby Fischer: '>=''>= ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: '>='''} a5 11. Ba3 {Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto.''whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto.'''}) (10. Bb2 { Bobby Fischer: 'is correct''is correct ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: 'is correct'''}) 10... c6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position."''Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position." ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position."'''} 11. Bd6 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!"''Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!" ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!"'''} 11. Qe2 { Bobby Fischer: 'was indicated''was indicated ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: 'was indicated'''}) 11... Qa5 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after''This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after'''} 12. Qd3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal).''Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal). ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal).'''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'What else?''What else? ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'What else?'''} 12. b4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent.''Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent.'''} Qa6) 12... Re6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again.''Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again.'''} 13. b4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After''No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After'''} 13. Ne2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black has the pleasant choice of either''Black has the pleasant choice of either ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black has the pleasant choice of either'''} Nxe4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Or..''Or.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Or..'''} (13... Ne8 {Bobby Fischer: '- both of which win a Pawn.''- both of which win a Pawn. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '- both of which win a Pawn.'''})) ({Bobby Fischer: 'After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended''After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended'''} 13. Ng5 Rxd6 14. Qxd6 Qxc3 (14... h6 $1 $19 {Kholmov,R}) 15. Qe7 $36 {Bobby Fischer: 'with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions.''with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions.'''}) 13... Qa3 $1 { xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6''Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6 ChessBase: ' xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6'''} 14. Bc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn.''The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn.'''} Qxb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White was hoping for''Perhaps White was hoping for ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White was hoping for'''} 14... Ne8 15. Ba5 b6 16. Rab1 $1 bxa5 17. Rb3 Nc5 $1 18. bxc5 Qxc5 19. Rfb1 $132 {Bobby Fischer: 'where Black's technical difficulties are great.''where Black's technical difficulties are great. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'where Black's technical difficulties are great.'''}) 15. Rab1 Qe7 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A cute tactical point.''A cute tactical point. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A cute tactical point.'''} 15... Qf8 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses the exchange after''loses the exchange after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'loses the exchange after'''} 16. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: '(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6).''(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6). ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6).'''} Re8 17. Bd6) 16. Rfd1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Now..''Now.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Now..'''} 16. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by''is refuted by ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by'''} Nc5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'For all practical purposes the game is over.''For all practical purposes the game is over. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'For all practical purposes the game is over.'''}) 16... Ne8 17. Ba5 {Bobby Fischer: 'No rest for the weary.''No rest for the weary. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'No rest for the weary.'''} Rd6 18. Qe2 Rxd1+ 19. Qxd1 Bf8 20. Nd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bad - as is everything else.''Bad - as is everything else. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Bad - as is everything else.'''} 20. Bb4 Qf6 21. Bxf8 Nxf8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing.''leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing.'''}) 20... Qa3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Winning even more material.''Winning even more material. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Winning even more material.'''} 21. Nc4 Qc5 22. Bf1 b5 23. Nd2 (23. Bb4 {Bobby Fischer: 'drops a piece to''drops a piece to ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'drops a piece to'''} Qd4 $19) 23... Qa3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing.''This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing.