{ChessCalisthenics: 'A45: Trompowsky Attack'} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Bg5 c5 3. c3 {Contrary to the other line of this survey with e3, White should take on f6 first if he intends to support his d4 pawn with the c-pawn.} Qb6 {And here Black should play 3...Ne4 transposing into a sub-line of the 2...Ne4 Trompowsky after 2.Bf4 (Bh4) c5 where 3.f3 is normal and 3.c3 considered rightly as dubious, again because of the b2 problem. A good illustration of this estimation is provided by the famous game Terentiev,  (possibly Denis and not Anatoli) against Joe Gallagher, analysed in this section.} (3... Ne4 4. Be3 cxd4 5. Bxd4 Nc6 6. f3 e5 7. Be3 Nc5 8. Bf2 d5 9. b4 Ne6 10. e4 d4 11. a3 Be7 12. cxd4 exd4 13. Nd2 O-O 14. Bc4 Nf4 15. g3 Ng2+ 16. Kf1 Ne3+ 17. Bxe3 dxe3 18. Nb3 {Leopoldi,N-Browne,W/Las Vegas 1975/EXT 2006/0-1 (36)}) 4. Qb3 $5 {A move that is condemned by theory. The reason why White should have taken on f6 and why Black should have accepted the favourable transposition afore-mentioned is that now, after ChessCalisthenics: 'N'} (4. Qc2 $1 Ne4 {is not so strong because of} 5. dxc5 Nxc5 (5... Qxc5 6. Be3 Qc6 7. Nd2) 6. Nd2 {Where White gets a "normal" d-pawn position with his dark squared bishop still present on the board. And if}) (4. Qc2 cxd4 5. Bxf6 $1 Qxf6 (5... gxf6 6. cxd4 {is our game Tseitlin-Naumann.}) (5... exf6 6. cxd4 Nc6 7. e3 d5 8. Nc3 Bf5 9. Bd3 Nb4 10. Qa4+ Bd7 11. Bb5 Bxb5 12. Nxb5 Nc6 13. Ne2 Bb4+ 14. Nbc3 Bxc3+ 15. bxc3 {like a variation in the same game is this time obviously in White's favour.}) (5... d3 $5 6. exd3 gxf6 7. Qb3) 6. cxd4 Nc6 7. e3 d5 8. Nc3 e6 9. a3 {is all-right for White because of the "locking-up" of the bishop on c8. See the comment about Black's 8th move, with reversed colours, in the game Tseitlin-Naumann.}) (4. b3 cxd4 5. Qxd4 Qxd4 6. cxd4 Ne4 7. Be3 e6 8. f3 Bb4+ 9. Kd1 Nf6 10. Bd2 Bxd2 11. Kxd2 Nc6 12. e3 d6 13. Nc3 Bd7 14. Rb1 Ke7 15. b4 b6 16. Ba6 Bc8 17. Bb5 Bd7 18. Nge2 a6 {Mariotti,S-Valenti,G/Tivoli 1973/EXT 2001/1-0 (39)}) 4... cxd4 (4... e6 5. Nd2 (5. Bxf6 gxf6 6. dxc5 (6. e3 $5) 6... Bxc5 7. Qxb6 Bxb6 8. Nd2) 5... d5 6. Bxf6 gxf6 7. Qxb6 axb6 8. Ngf3 (8. e4 $5) 8... Nc6 (8... c4 $1 9. a3 (9. e4 b5 10. a3 b4) 9... b5 10. Rb1 f5 11. e3 Nd7 12. Be2 Nb6 $15) 9. e3 Bd7 10. a3 cxd4 11. cxd4 Bd6 12. Be2 Ke7 13. O-O Rhc8 14. Rfc1 Rc7 15. Rc3 $14 {1/2-1/2 Boidman,Y-Koutsin,S Prievidza(9) 1996}) (4... Ne4 5. Be3) (4... Qxb3 $5 {is possibly better. It appears that the doubled pawns on the b-file which give the opponent a strong square in front of them is a handicapping weakness that the opening of the a-file is insufficient to compensate.} 5. axb3 cxd4 6. Bxf6 gxf6 7. cxd4 Nc6 {Quite surprisingly this position has apparently never been tested in practice!} 8. e3 Nb4 9. Kd2 {At least is it clearer in this case: Black can always play .. .a5 and White has very little to expect from the a-file. Indeed, it will be extremely difficult for him to unblock the b4 square because of its control by Black's dark-squared bishop which has no counterpart anymore.}) 5. Qxb6 axb6 6. Bxf6 gxf6 7. cxd4 {So, by inversion of moves, we have reached, if not the critical position of the 3.Bxf6 gxf6 4.c3 Trompowsky (after 4...Qb6 5.Qb3 cxd4 6.Qxb6 axb6 7.cxd4 ), then, at least, something interesting for White.} Nc6 8. Nf3 $2 {A slack move. It is clear that this knight sees his perspectives limited by the presence of the black pawn on f6.} (8. e3 $1 Nb4 9. Kd2 (9. Na3 $5 Rxa3 10. bxa3 Nc2+ 11. Kd2 Nxa1 12. Bd3 (12. Bb5) 12... e5 13. Ne2) 9... Rxa2 10. Rxa2 Nxa2 11. Bc4 Nb4 12. Nc3 e6 (12... Na6 13. Nd5) 13. d5 $1 Rg8 14. g3 Bc5 15. Nge2 Ke7 16. Ra1 d6 17. Ra8 Rd8 {is not so bad for White. Indeed, Black's extra pawn on the b-file does not look very useful in front of the b2 pawn. Apart from that, Black is also a bit restrained to pretend to be able to exploit his pair of bishops and, in this configuration, his pawn structure with his d-pawn not on d5 is worse than usual. Still, I would not call it a white advantage as Fritz does! Still, I would not mind testing it in practice.. .}) 8... Nb4 9. Kd2 (9. Na3 $5 Rxa3 10. bxa3 Nc2+ 11. Kd2 Nxa1 12. e3 {Will win the knight back in return of the a3 pawn with a similar comment as previously regarding its objective value as a pawn up, doubled, and this time on an open file.}) 9... Rxa2 10. Rxa2 Nxa2 11. Na3 {This is the problem. White cannot prevent Black from putting a pawn on d5 unless by playing the compromising 11.e4 which opens the position for the black bishops and gives the opponent the plan of undoubling the f-pawns by ...f6-f5. And if he plays 11.Nc3 with the idea of strengthening his center after 11...Nxc3 12.bxc3, Black will be happy with this plan giving him a goal for his b-pawns.} (11. e4 Bh6+ 12. Kd1 d6 13. Bc4 Nb4 14. Nc3) 11... d5 {And that gives a completely different forecast.} 12. e3 e6 13. Nc2 Nb4 14. Nxb4 (14. Bb5+ {is slightly better.} Kd8 (14... Bd7 15. Bxd7+ Kxd7 16. Nxb4 (16. Ra1 Nc6) 16... Bxb4+ 17. Kd3 Ra8 18. Rc1 Ra2 19. Kc2 {is bearable.}) 15. Rc1 Nxc2 16. Rxc2 Bd6 $15 {Followed by e6-e5 liberating the bishop on c8 with a small advantage for Black.}) 14... Bxb4+ 15. Kc2 Bd7 {Now it is difficult. Black's two bishops will demonstrate their power.} 16. Kb3 Bd6 17. Bd3 Ke7 18. Ra1 Rc8 19. Ra7 {Unlike the great Bobby himself in the very first game of his Homeric World Championship tussle against Spassky in Reykjavik 1972 when he took the "poisoned" pawn...} (19. Bxh7 $2 f5 {ChessCalisthenics: '-+'} 20. g4 Rh8) 19... Bc6 20. g3 (20. Bxh7 $2 f5 $19) 20... h6 21. Ra1 Kd7 22. Ra7 Kc7 23. Ra1 b5 24. Rc1 Kb6 25. Ra1 Bd7 26. Nh4 Rc6 27. Ra8 {By giving up the first rank, it seems to accelerate the defeat but Black had a strong plan to make progress which was hard to oppose:} (27. Rd1 Kc7 28. Ra1 Ra6 29. Rc1+ Kb6 {Followed by ...Ra8 and ...b4 with the idea ...Ba4+.}) 27... Rc1 28. Rh8 (28. Rd8 $5 Kc7 29. Rh8 $11) 28... b4 29. Rxh6 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: '??'} (29. Ra8 Rh1 30. Nf3 e5) (29. Nf3 $1 {was incredibly more stubborn, after which there are so many possibilities of attack and defence that it is easy to get confused for both camps. That is why, I will give here only the most salient lines hoping that I have not forgotten anything big! As far as my numerous hours of analysis of this position can tell, the winning plan has to develop in 5 stages. First, activate the center to take as many squares as possible from White's light pieces. ChessCalisthenics: ''} e5 $1 (29... Ra1 30. Kc2 Ba4+ (30... b3+ 31. Kc3 $1 e5 32. Nd2 $1 e4 33. Be2 Re1 34. Bh5 Ba4 35. Nxb3) (30... e5 31. Nd2) 31. b3 $3 (31. Kd2 Bd1 $1 32. Nh4 b3 {Threatening mate.} 33. Kc3 Bc7 34. e4 Kc6) 31... Ra2+ 32. Kc1 $1 Rxf2 (32... Bxb3 $2 33. Nd2 Ba4 34. Bb1 Ra3 (34... b3 35. Bxa2 bxa2 36. Kb2 Bb4 37. Ra8 Kb5 38. Nb3) 35. Rxh6 {With counterchances}) 33. Nd2 Bb5 34. Bxb5 Kxb5 35. Rxh6 f5 36. Kc2) 30. Nd2 $1 (30. dxe5 fxe5 31. Rxh6 Kc7 32. Nd2 (32. e4 Ra1 33. Kc2 b3+ $1 34. Kxb3 d4 35. Kc4 Ba4 $1 {Threatening two different mates with 36...Rc1+ 37.Kd5 Bc6+ or with 36.. .b7-b5+ 37.Kd5 Bb3+.} 36. Nxe5 Bxe5 37. Rh5 b5+ 38. Kd5 Bg7 39. Rh7 Bb3+ 40. Kc5 Rd1 41. Be2 Bf8+ 42. Kxb5 Rc1) 32... e4 33. Be2 Re1 $1 34. Bf1 (34. Bh5 Ra1 35. Kc2 Ba4+ 36. b3 Bb5 37. Bxf7 Bd3+ 38. Kb2 Be5#) 34... Ra1 35. Kc2 b3+ 36. Kxb3 (36. Kc3 Ba4 37. Rh7 Rc1+ 38. Kd4 Rd1 39. Rxf7+ Kc6 40. Kc3 b5) 36... Ba4+ 37. Kc3 Rc1+ 38. Kd4 Rd1 39. Kc3 d4+ $1 40. exd4 Rc1#) 30... e4 31. Be2 {Then, drive the bishop off the a6-f1 diagonal so that our bishop can use it.} Re1 32. Bh5 Bb5 $1 {Next, use the mating motives which start to appear to weaken the position of the enemy king.} (32... Ra1 33. Kc2 Bb5 34. b3) 33. Ka2 $1 (33. Bxf7 Ra1 34. Kc2 Bd3+ 35. Kb3 Be2 36. Bxd5 Bd1+ 37. Kc4 Rc1#) (33. Kc2 Bd3+ 34. Kb3 Rc1 35. Bxf7 (35. Ka2 Rc2 36. Nb3 Bc4 37. Rxh6 Rxf2) 35... Rc2 36. Bxd5 Rxd2 37. Rxh6 Rxf2) (33. Rxh6 Ra1 34. Kc2 Bd3+ 35. Kb3 Kb5 36. Bxf7 (36. Rxf6 Bc4+ 37. Nxc4 (37. Kc2 b3+ 38. Nxb3 Bd3+ 39. Kc3 Bb4#) 37... dxc4+ 38. Kc2 b3+ 39. Kd2 Bb4+) 36... Rd1 37. Rh5 Rxd2 38. Rxd5+ (38. Bxd5 Bc2+ 39. Ka2 Ka4) 38... Kc6 39. Rf5 b5 $1 40. Rxf6 Kc7 41. Ka2 (41. g4 Bb1 42. Rxd6 Kxd6 43. g5 Rxf2 44. g6 Ke7) 41... Bc4+ 42. Kb1 b3 43. Kc1 Rc2+ 44. Kb1 Re2 45. Kc1 Re1+ 46. Kd2 Bb4#) (33. Ra8 Rh1 34. Bxf7 Bc6 $1 35. h4 Rh2 36. Rd8 Kc7 37. Rh8 Rxf2 38. Kc2 Bxg3 39. Rxh6 Re2 40. Kd1 Re1+ 41. Kc2 Bf2 42. Rxf6 Bxe3) 33... Bc4+ 34. b3 {After, combining tactics against the king and the knight, win the f2 pawn or the knight but in that case be very careful that the opponent has not enough pawns for it: On white squares they are easy targets for his bishop.} Be2 $1 {The key move.} 35. Bxf7 Rd1 (35... Kc6 36. Rc8+ Kd7 (36... Bc7 37. Bxd5+) 37. Rc2 Bd3 38. Rb2) 36. Nb1 Kc6 $1 (36... Rf1 37. Nd2 Rxf2 38. Kb1 Rxh2 39. Bxd5 Bd3+ 40. Kc1 f5 $17) 37. Rxh6 (37. Rc8+ Bc7 38. Rh8 Rf1 $1 39. Be8+ Kb6 40. Rxh6 Rxf2 41. Ka1 Bd1 42. Ba4 Ka6 43. Rh7 b5 44. Rxc7 bxa4 45. bxa4 Bb3 46. Nc3 bxc3 47. Rxc3 Bc4) 37... Be7 $1 {The square h5 is guarded, so all the important pawns can be kept.} 38. Bh5 Rf1 $1 39. Bxe2 (39. Kb2 Rxf2 40. Kc1 Rf1+ 41. Kb2 Bd3 42. Nd2 Re1 43. Rh7 Kd6) 39... Rxf2 40. Kb2 Rxe2+ 41. Kc1 Rxe3 {5th and last phase. When f2 has fallen, the position collapses, and e3 follows revealing a deadly passed pawn.} 42. Nd2 Re1+ 43. Kc2 Kd6 (43... e3 44. Nf3 Rf1 45. Ne5+ Kb5 46. Nd3 Rf2+ 47. Kc1 Rd2 48. Ne1 Rxd4 {Wins too}) 44. g4 Rg1 (44... e3 45. Nf3 Rf1 46. Rh3) 45. Rh4 Ke6 {White does not have to be a pawn down, in a near state of zugzwang, to be lost, ...Bd6 is also coming.}) 29... Ka5 $1 $19 30. Ka2 b3+ 31. Kxb3 Ba4+ 32. Ka2 Kb4 33. b3 Bxb3+ 34. Kb2 Rd1 35. Bb1 (35. Bh7 Rd2+ 36. Ka1 Ka3 37. Nf3 Rd1+ 38. Bb1 Bb4) (35. Nf3 Rxd3 36. Rh8 $19) 35... Rd2+ 36. Ka1 Kc3 (36... Kc3 37. Rh8 (37. Nf3 Ba3 38. Nxd2 Bb2#) 37... Ba3 38. Rc8+ Kb4 39. Bh7 (39. Rc2 Bxc2 40. f3 Rd1 41. h3 Bxb1 42. Nf5 Bc2+ 43. Ka2 Bb3#) 39... f5) 0-1

