{The Legend of Henrique Mecking  We are still far away from normality here in South America and there are no "real" tournaments in sight yet, those where we actually move the pieces on a wooden chessboard and press the button of the clock. We are all now getting used to playing online tournaments and even I played a couple of them.   In one of these tournaments Brazil's legendary first Grandmaster, Henrique Mecking, took part. As it usually happens whenever he plays, his participation was met with great anticipation and finished with some controversy. Mecking is an "old timer", and his best years were during the Cold War period. So, sometimes he sees a few ghosts and his opponents are unjustly accused of cheating. But let us change the subject...  This article is a small tribute to an outstanding player that competed with the very best. A prodigy, winner of 2 Interzonal Tournaments and once the number 3 in the FIDE rating list (behind Karpov and Korchnoi). I hope that readers who are not familiar with his games will appreciate how strong he was and delve even more deeply in the analysis of his games.  Brazil is often associated with football and samba (and now is also a "reference" on how not to prevent COVID-19, unfortunately). But once, in those times of Pele, Rivellino, Carlos Alberto, Tostao and so many great football players, Brazilians also dreamed of having a World Champion in chess. It was not to be, but his legacy is still felt.  I will show some of Mecking's game, my personal favourites, in chronological order starting with his duel (as a teenager, 18 at the time) against someone who was getting ready to perform the most remarkable series of wins in chess history.  Fischer was clearly Mecking's idol. Both were lonely prodigies that studied chess mostly by themselves.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bg5 $5 {I have seen this game many times in my life, so it is not a coincidence that I have played this interesting move many times during my career.} Ne4 5. Bh4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 dxc4 $1 {Fischer was usually a step ahead and he goes for what is considered today the best move for Black.} 7. e3 Be6 8. Rb1 ({There are many other possible plans for White, and it is not clear what is the best one.} 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Nh3 $5 {was the fashion a few years ago.}) (8. Nf3) (8. Qb1) 8... b6 {One of many possible moves.} (8... Bg7) (8... Nd7) (8... c5) 9. Nf3 Bg7 10. Nd2 O-O 11. Nxc4 {Diagram [#]} Bd5 $1 {A strong move, posing White some concrete problems since his development is not so easy now.} 12. Qd2 (12. Qc2 {was more natural, with the idea of playing e4 next. A possible continuation would be} c5 $1 13. e4 Bxc4 14. Bxc4 cxd4 15. Bd5 d3 $1 16. Qxd3 (16. Qd1 Na6 17. Bxa8 Qxa8 18. Qxd3 Nc5 $44) 16... Nc6 17. O-O Qc7 $11 {with an equal position.}) 12... Qd7 {A multi-purpose move: the queen can go either to a4 or e6.} 13. Na3 $6 {Not an easy move to make. White could have sacrificed a pawn with} (13. f3 Qe6 14. Na3 Bxa2 15. Ra1 Bd5 {with a big fight ahead.}) 13... c5 (13... e5 $1 $36 {was more precise, with the initiative for Black.}) 14. f3 Qa4 $5 15. Nb5 {Diagram [#] We are arriving at the critical moment of the game. The following moves show how good these two players were - in such a complicated position the best compliment comes from having their moves being approved by the computer.} Nc6 $5 {Fischer was not one to shy away from calculation and he must have analysed very deeply beforehand to be able to play this move since White has many interesting options at his disposal.} (15... Rc8 16. Bg3 cxd4 17. exd4 $13) 16. Nc7 Qxa2 {Diagram [#] This is a very interesting position and Mecking's choice, although tempting, was probably not the best. But who could resist trapping Fischer's queen?} (16... Rad8 $4 17. Nxd5 Rxd5 18. Bb5 $18) 17. Qc1 $5 (17. Qxa2 {was not the best:} Bxa2 18. Ra1 Rac8 {and Black escapes.}) (17. Rb2 $1 {was probably the most dangerous:} Qa1+ 18. Kf2 Rad8 19. Nxd5 Rxd5 20. Ra2 Qb1 21. Bc4 $1 Qxh1 22. Bxd5 $14 {and this position looks very unpleasant for Black.}) 17... Rac8 18. Ra1 Qb3 19. Ra3 {The queen is trapped, but Black has very serious compensation.} Rxc7 20. Rxb3 Bxb3 $44 {I wonder whether Fischer had all of this prepared when he went for 15...Nc6, and concluded that he had good compensation.} 21. Qa3 Na5 22. Bg3 e5 $1 {A very important move to foresee, since without it White would have a big advantage.} 23. Bxe5 (23. dxe5 Rd7 24. Be2 Rfd8 25. Bh4 (25. Qc1 Nc4 $44) 25... Bxe5 $1 (25... Rd1+ 26. Kf2) 26. Bxd8 Bxc3+ 27. Kf2 Rxd8 $44 {- probably White is somewhat better, but Black would have quite good practical compensation.}) 23... Bxe5 24. dxe5 Rd8 25. Be2 Rcd7 26. Qc1 Bc4 27. Qc2 {I think that Black could still search for a winning attempt here, but Fischer decided not to tempt his fate and forced a draw.} Bb3 28. Qc1 Bc4 29. Qc2 Bb3 30. Qc1 {A really great fight!} 1/2-1/2

