1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Be7 8. Qf3 Qc7 9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. g4 ({The main alternative to} 10. Bd3 {, as we've already seen.}) 10... b5 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 12. g5 Nd7 13. a3 ({Yury Lapshun: 'This was in vogue in the 1960s, but has been superseded by '} 13. f5 $5 {Yury Lapshun: '.'}) 13... Rb8 ({The older move} 13... Bb7 {became unpopular after M.Tal-S.Gligoric, Moscow 1963, which saw Tal win a fine game with} 14. Bh3 $1 {.}) 14. h4 b4 15. axb4 Rxb4 16. Bh3 O-O $146 {Yury Lapshun: '!?' Yury Lapshun: 'A daring idea introduced in this game. '} (16... Qc5 {is considerably safer.}) {Yury Lapshun: 'In this position White would like to eliminate the pawn on e6 so as to play Nc3-d5. There are two ways to do this. '} 17. Nf5 {Yury Lapshun: '!?'} ({Yury Lapshun: 'The knight need not be accepted, so a better approach is the forcing '} 17. Nxe6 {Yury Lapshun: '!'} fxe6 18. Bxe6+ Kh8 19. Nd5 Qc4 $13 {Yury Lapshun: 'and now '} 20. Bf5 $1 {with the menace of Bxh7 is very strong.}) 17... Nc5 $1 {Yury Lapshun: 'Fischer continues to play thematically and press against the e-pawn whilst building up against the white king. '} 18. Nxe7+ Qxe7 19. h5 $6 ({Yury Lapshun: 'Gligoric prefers '} 19. Qe3 {Yury Lapshun: '.'} Bb7 20. Rd4 $11) 19... Bb7 20. h6 $6 Bxe4 21. Nxe4 Nxe4 22. hxg7 Rc8 $1 $17 {Yury Lapshun: '!'} (22... Kxg7 $2 23. Bf5 $1 exf5 24. Rxh7+ Kxh7 25. Qh5+ Kg7 26. Rh1 $18) 23. Rh2 ({Yury Lapshun: 'Gligoric explains that Minic's likely intention'} 23. Bf5 $2 exf5 24. Qh5 {Yury Lapshun: 'fails to'} Rxc2+ $1 25. Kxc2 Qc7+ 26. Kb1 Nc3+ $19 {Yury Lapshun: ', so he had to slow down.'}) 23... Ra4 24. Kb1 d5 {Yury Lapshun: 'And Fischer, whose counterattack was by now in full swing, won in 10 more moves.'} 25. c4 Raxc4 26. Bf1 Rb4 27. Qh3 Nc3+ 28. Kc1 Na4+ 29. Kb1 Rxb2+ 30. Rxb2 Nc3+ 31. Kc1 Qa3 $19 32. Bd3 Qa1+ 33. Kd2 Qxb2+ 34. Ke1 Ne4 $1 0-1

