{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 3 Bear Hug. In what appear to be a perfectly equal positions, Petrosian consistently finds seemingly innocuous moves which gradually overwhelm his opponent. He accomplishes his objective simply by exchanging pieces and maneuvering for victory without taking unnecessary risks. This essentially defensive technique has the virtue, when it doesn't utterly succeed, of producing a draw. Fischer, by contrast, generally chooses the sharpest course, however precipitous is may become. Occasionally he overreaches himself, but it makes for interesting chess. In this game, replete with errors on both sides, Petrosian succeeds in pinning his opponent for a time to a static endgame. But Fischer manages to burst his bonds, only to blunder on the very next move (51...Kd6). Petrosian, however by blundering in his turn, restores the balance. The ensuing Rook and Pawn ending produces a thrilling draw. '} 1. c4 {Bobby Fischer} Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nf3 d6 6. O-O Nc6 7. d3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 7. d4 {Bobby Fischer: 'I intended'} e5) {Bobby Fischer: 'Petrosian is striving for an English Opening formation, a slow system for which he is temperamentally suited. '} 7... Nh5 8. d4 {Bobby Fischer: ', Reckoning he can afford this loss of time in view of Black's misplaced KN.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 8. Rb1 f5 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 9. Qc2 a5 10. a3 f4 $40 {Petrosian,T-Vasiukov,E Moscow 1956 0-1 Petrosian,T-Vasiukov,E Moscow 1956 0-1 Bobby Fischer: '(Petrosian - Vasiukov, Moscow 1956) Black obtains an excellent aggressive setup. I was impressed by that game as Petrosian must have been, since he got crushed.'}) 8... e5 9. d5 (9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8 Rxd8 11. Nd5 Rd7 12. Bh3 f5 13. g4 Nf6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'If'} 14. gxf5 (14. Nxf6+ Bxf6 15. gxf5 gxf5 16. Bxf5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '??'} Rg7+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 14... Nxd5 15. cxd5 Rxd5) 9... Ne7 10. e4 {Bobby Fischer: 'This is the right time to get in'} f5 (10... c5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Petrosian-Boleslavsky, USSR Championship prelims 1957, continued:'} 11. Ne1 Kh8 12. Nd3 f5 13. Rb1 Nf6 $10 {Petrosian,T-Boleslavsky,I ch-URS 1957 Petrosian,T-Boleslavsky,I ch-URS 1957 Bobby Fischer: '='}) 11. exf5 gxf5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Tempting but unsound is'} 11... Nxf5 12. g4 Nd4 13. gxh5 Bg4 14. Nxd4 $1 Bxd1 15. Ne6 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'And on'} 11... Bxf5 12. Ng5 Qd7 13. Ne6 $1 Bxe6 14. dxe6 Qxe6 15. Bxb7 Rab8 16. Bg2 Qxc4 17. Nd5 $1 $16 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'White comes out on top.'}) 12. Nxe5 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} Nxg3 {Bobby Fischer: '; A "desperado" combination: this Knight (which is doomed anyway) sells its life as dearly as possible. '} 13. hxg3 {Bobby Fischer: ', Simple and good.'} ({I had expected} 13. fxg3 {(Bobby Fischer) , but Petrosian avoids the attack on the kingside and plays for control of the central squares. His judgment turns out to be correct.}) 13... Bxe5 $2 (13... dxe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'keeping a fluid pawn center, offers more play. I was unduly worried about White's passed Queen pawn after'} 14. c5 $13) 14. f4 $1 Bg7 {Neil McDonald: 'Exercise 4: -- After'} 15. Be3 {Neil McDonald: 'Sometimes we have to go back to go forwards.'} Bd7 ({Rather, Black should have prepared a retreat for the knight. After} 15... Kh8 $1 {(Hübner) the knight can be redeployed via g8 to f6, where it is far more influential than on g6. The idea of ...Kh8 and ...Ng8 has been used many times in the King's Indian Defence by Kasparov, Nunn and others, but evidently it wasn't an established procedure in 1958.}) 16. Bd4 {Bobby Fischer: ', Forcing the trade of Black's most active piece. White soon obtains a firm grip on the position. '} Ng6 {Neil McDonald: 'and Fischer struggled to draw in a game he later selected for inclusion in 'My 60 Memorable Games':' Neil McDonald: 'Black's knight ended up on a passive square. At move 15, can you suggest a better way to deploy the horse?'} 17. Re1 $2 {Bobby Fischer: ', A careless transposition.' Bobby Fischer: 'Now by'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct was'} 17. Bf3) 17... Rf7 $2 (17... Bxd4+ 18. Qxd4 h5 $1 $10 { h5-h4  h5-h4 Bobby Fischer: 'followed by h4, Black could exchange his isolated King's Rook Pawn for White's Night Pawn and the game would be dead equal.'}) 18. Bf3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black doesn't get a second chance. '} Qf8 19. Kf2 Re8 20. Rxe8 Qxe8 21. Bxg7 Rxg7 22. Qd4 b6 23. Rh1 {Bobby Fischer: ', White has effortlessly achieved a plus and now he wants to improve his position before embarking on a committal course.'} (23. b4 $1 { c4-c5  c4-c5 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening c5, is much sharper, and poses more immediate problems.'}) 23... a5 {Bobby Fischer: '; My first free breath! '} 24. Nd1 Qf8 25. Ne3 {Bobby Fischer: ', Petrosian keeps building without getting sidetracked - even by good moves.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I was more afraid of'} 25. Bh5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'tying me up completely. Then the Rook can't move because of Bxg6 followed by a check on h8.'} Re7 $2 26. Bxg6 hxg6 27. Rh8+ $18) 25... Rf7 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 26. b3 Qg7 {Bobby Fischer: '; The exchange of Queens eases the cramp. White can't afford to retreat and cede this important diagonal. '} 27. Qxg7+ Kxg7 28. a3 Rf8 29. Be2 {Bobby Fischer: ', White constantly finds ways to improve his position.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 29. b4 $2 axb4 30. axb4 Ra8 {Bobby Fischer: 'and Black seizes the open file.'}) 29... Ne7 30. Bd3 h6 31. Rh5 Be8 32. Rh2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Avoiding a little trap:'} 32. Nxf5+ Nxf5 33. Rxf5 Rh8 $1 $19 { Lg6} (33... Rg8 $1 {(Bobby Fischer) followed by ...Bg6, winning the exchange.})) (32. Rxf5 Rh8 $1 $19) 32... Bd7 33. Rh1 Rh8 34. Nc2 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Headed for an even stronger post on d4. I was amazed during the game. Each time Petrosian achieved a good position, he maneuvered into a better one. '} Kf6 35. Nd4 Kg7 36. Be2 {Bobby Fischer: ', Feigning an invasion with Bh5 and Rh1 and Ne6. White has two wings to operate on: Black must be flexed to react appropriately, and this requires alertness. '} Ng8 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Panicking and giving him the opportunity he's been waiting for to sneak b4 in at a moment when Black can't counter with ... axb4 and ...Ra8. Petrosian likes to play cat-and-mouse, hoping that his opponents will go wrong in the absence of a direct threat. The amazing thing is - they usually do! Witness a case in point.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I should just have ignored his "threat" with, say,'} 36... Ra8 37. Bh5 Rc8 38. Re1 Kf6 {Bobby Fischer: 'and if'} 39. Ne6 c6 $10) 37. b4 $1 Nf6 38. Bd3 $1 (38. bxa5 Ne4+ 39. Kg2 bxa5 40. Rb1 Nc5 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds.'}) 38... axb4 (38... Ne4+ {Bobby Fischer: '?'} 39. Bxe4 fxe4 40. bxa5 bxa5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 40... Ra8 41. axb6 cxb6 42. Rb1 $18) 41. Rb1 $18 { Tb7  Tb7 Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Rb7 wins easily.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'White also invades after'} 38... Kg6 39. bxa5 bxa5 40. Rb1 $18 { Tb7}) 39. axb4 Kg6 40. Ra1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', White has finally achieved his ideal setup, but Black's game is still tenable. '} Ng4+ 41. Ke2 Re8+ 42. Kd2 Nf6 43. Ra6 (43. Ra7 Rc8) 43... Rb8 44. Ra7 Rc8 {Bobby Fischer: 'transposes to the game.'} 45. c5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', This Pawn sac caught me completely by surprise. It's the only line that gives Black any trouble. '} bxc5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 45... Nxd5 $2 46. c6 $18) 46. bxc5 dxc5 47. Nf3 $1 Kf7 $1 (47... Nxd5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses to'} 48. Ne5+ $18) 48. Ne5+ Ke7 49. Nxd7 Nxd7 50. Bxf5 Rf8 $1 51. g4 (51. Bxd7 Kxd7 52. Ke3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 52. Ra6 Rg8) 52... Kd6 53. Ra6+ Kxd5 54. Rxh6 Re8+ 55. Kf3 c4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'should draw.'}) 51... Kd6 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Should be the losing move!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 51... Nf6 $1 52. Be6 Nxd5 $1 53. Bxd5 Rxf4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'Winning the last Pawn and forcing a draw.'} (53... Rd8 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'also draws.'})) 52. Bxd7 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} Kxd7 53. Ke3 Re8+ {Bobby Fischer: '; The idea is to force his King to the King side, away from the passed c pawn.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 53... c4 54. Ra6 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 54. Kf3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 54. Kd3 Rg8 $10 (54... Rb8 {Bobby Fischer})) 54... Kd6 55. Ra6+ Kxd5 56. Rxh6 c4 57. Rh1 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'As Petrosian points out in the Russian bulletins of the tournament, White can win with the following line:'} 57. Rh7 {Petrosian,T Petrosian,T Bobby Fischer: '!'} c6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'What if Black tries to improve? For example, after'} 57... c5 58. Rd7+ Ke6 59. Rd1 Rb8 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now there are two main lines:'} 60. g5 $2 (60. f5+ $1 Ke5 61. Re1+ Kd4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'ofter'} 61... Kf6 62. Kf4 c3 63. g5+ Kg7 64. g6 c2 65. Kg5 Rb1 66. f6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 62. g5 c3 63. f6 c2 64. Rc1 $1 (64. f7 $2 Rb1 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'draws.'}) 64... Ke5 65. Kg4 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Snuffs out Black's resistance.'}) 60... c3 61. Kg4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 61. Rc1 Kf5 62. Rxc3 c4 $1 63. Rxc4 Rb3+ $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a draw by blockade although two pawns down.'}) 61... Rb4 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 62. Re1+ Kf7 63. Kf5 c2 64. Rc1 Rc4 65. g6+ Kg7 66. Kg5 Rc3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws.'} 67. f5 {Bobby Fischer} Rg3+ 68. Kf4 Rg2 69. Ra1 Rd2 70. Kg5 Rg2+ 71. Kf4 Kf6 72. Ra6+ Ke7 73. Ra7+ Kf8 74. Ra8+ Ke7 75. Ra7+ Kf8) 58. Rd7+ Kc5 59. Rd1 c3 60. g5 Kc4 61. g6 c2 62. Rc1 Kc3 63. f5 Rg8 64. Kf4 Kd2 65. Rxc2+ Kxc2 66. Kg5 c5 67. f6 c4 68. f7 Rxg6+ (68... Rf8 {Bobby Fischer} 69. g7 Rxf7 70. g8=Q Rc7) 69. Kxg6 c3 70. f8=Q $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'White is a tempo ahead of the game, where Black's Pawn succeeds in reaching c7, instead of c6 (as here).'}) 57... c3 58. g5 c5 59. Rd1+ {Bobby Fischer: ', It's tough right down the line.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 59. g6 Rg8 60. f5 Ke5 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 61. Kg4 Kf6 62. Rc1 c4 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 63. Rxc3 Rc8 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'White can make no headway.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 59. Kg4 Re2 $1 60. g6 Ke4 $1 61. Kg5 Rg2+ 62. Kf6 Kxf4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc, as the russian bulletins also point out.'}) 59... Kc4 60. g6 c2 61. Rc1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 61. Rg1 Rd8 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'The threat of ...Rd1 forces Rc1 (not'} 62. Rc1 $8 (62. g7 $2 Rg8 $1 $19 { Txg7  Txg7 Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'followed by ...Rxg7 and wins.)'})) 61... Kd3 62. f5 Rg8 $1 63. Kf4 Kd2 64. Rxc2+ Kxc2 65. Kg5 c4 66. f6 c3 67. f7 {Bobby Fischer: ', Drawn. I offered the draw, not realizing it was bad etiquette.'} (67. f7 {Bobby Fischer: ' It was Petrosian's place to extend the offer after'} Rxg6+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'If Black wants to get melodramatic'} 67... Rc8 68. g7 Kb1 69. f8=Q Rxf8 70. gxf8=Q c2 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'reaches the same position.'}) 68. Kxg6 Kb1 (68... Kb2 {Bobby Fischer} 69. f8=Q c2 {with a book draw.}) 69. f8=Q c2 $10) 1/2-1/2

