{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 4 Tact and tactics. The presence in Argentina of Pilnik, Najdorf, and Eliskases, who chose to remain there after participating in the Buenos Aires chess Olympic of 1939, created a chess renaissance, as attested by the annual event at Mar del Plata which, though not lavish with prizes, offers an exotic vacation and attracts the world's best. Fischer tied for 3-4 with Ivkov, a mere half point behind Pachman and Najdorf in a strong field of fifteen. After a lackluster opening by both sides, and a middle game that, with the exception of 26. (come back here), can scarcely be described as more than routine, Fischer pilots the game into an even ending. Both he and Pilnok then prodceed to complicate; but the latter is drawn into making a false lead, which Fischr explots by obtaining a passed center Pawn. From this point on, although Pilnik does all that can be done to stave off the inevitable, Fischer is not gulled into making a single wrong step. ' Neil McDonald: 'Later in Jakovenko-Carlsen, Jakovenko's omission of 49 Kg1 reminds me of a similar incident in one of Fischer's games.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 6. h3 {Fischer,R-Bolbochan,J Stockholm izt 1962 Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Varna ol 1962 Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1962 Fischer,R-Bolbochan,J Stockholm izt 1962 Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Varna ol 1962 Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'see games 35, 40, 43.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 6. Bc4 {Fischer,R-Tal,M Beograd ct 1959 Fischer,R-Bednarski,J Habana ol 1966 Fischer,R-Geller,E Skopje 1967 Fischer,R-Tal,M Beograd ct 1959 Fischer,R-Bednarski,J Habana ol 1966 Fischer,R-Geller,E Skopje 1967 Bobby Fischer: 'see games 17, 55, 58.'}) 6... e5 7. Nb3 Be7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 7... Be6 {Unzicker,W-Fischer,R Varna ol 1962 Unzicker,W-Fischer,R Varna ol 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'see game 42.'}) 8. O-O ({Bobby Fischer: 'Another try is'} 8. Bg5 O-O $1 (8... Nbd7 {Bobby Fischer: '?'} 9. a4 $1 $16 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'gives a powerful bind.'}) 9. Nd2 Nxe4 $1 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 11. Bxd8 Nxd1 12. Be7 Re8 13. Nc4 Nxb2 $1 14. Nb6 Rxe7 15. Nxa8 Na4 16. O-O-O {Bobby Fischer: '(Fischer-Ghitescu, Leipzig, 1960)'} Rd7 $1 $14 {Fischer,R-Ghitescu,T Leipzig ol 1960 Fischer,R-Ghitescu,T Leipzig ol 1960 Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with the better game.'}) 8... O-O 9. Be3 Be6 10. f3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A sharper alternative is'} 10. a4 Qc7 11. a5 Nbd7 12. Nd5 Nxd5 13. exd5 Bf5 14. c4 Bg6 15. Kh1 Rac8 16. Qd2 Qd8 17. Rac1 h6 18. f4 exf4 19. Bxf4 Bg5 $10 {Smyslov,V-Gligoric,S Habana 1962 Smyslov,V-Gligoric,S Habana 1962 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '(Smyslov-Gligorich, Havana, 1962).'}) 10... Qc7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Premature is'} 10... d5 11. exd5 Nxd5 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 13. Qxd5 Bxd5 14. Rfd1 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'With a slight edge in the ending.'}) 11. Qe1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Once popular, this whole system is now known to give White nothing. It hinders neither Black's development his Queen's side expansion. '} Nbd7 12. Rd1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 12. a4 d5 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'is strong.'}) 12... b5 13. Rd2 (13. a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is met by'} Nb6 14. Bxb6 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} Qxb6+ $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='}) 13... Nb6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More direct is'} 13... b4 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 Bf5 16. Qf2 a5 $15 {× c2 × c2 Bobby Fischer: 'with good play against White's backward c pawn.'}) 14. Qf2 $2 {Bobby Fischer: ', Careless,'} (14. Bxb6 $8 {Bobby Fischer: 'is necessary.'}) 14... Rab8 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; I figured if he didn't take it off last move he wouldn't take it now; so I wanted to build a little more and keep the option of moving the Knight to a4 as well as c4.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'But Black should pounce on the chance to play'} 14... Nc4 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 15. Bxc4 bxc4 16. Bb6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 16. Na1 Rab8 17. Rb1 Rb7 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'is strong.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 16. Nc1 Rab8 17. Na4 c3 $1 18. bxc3 $2 Qc6 19. Nb6 Bd8 $19) 16... Qc8 17. Na5 Nd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White's in trouble. e.g.,'} 18. Nd5 (18. Be3 Bd8 19. Nd5 Bxd5 20. Rxd5 Nf6 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '... Wining at least the exchange.'}) 18... Bxd5 19. Rxd5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 19. exd5 Nxb6 20. Qxb6 Bd8 21. Qb4 Rb8 22. Qa3 Bxa5 23. Qxa5 Rxb2 $19) 19... Rb8 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'winning at least a Pawn.'}) 15. Bxb6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Pilnik hastens to make amends for his omissino.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 15. Na5 $2 d5 $1 $17 {Stahlberg,G Stahlberg,G Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins material. (Stahlberg).'}) 15... Rxb6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Bd7 18. f4 Bf6 (18... f5 19. c3 (19. c4 Bf6 20. fxe5 dxe5 ({if} 20... Bxe5 21. Nd4) 21. Nc5 $11) 19... Bf6 20. fxe5 dxe5 (20... Bxe5 21. Nd4) 21. Nc5 $10) 19. c3 Rbb8 20. fxe5 Bxe5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 20... dxe5 21. d6 $36 {Bobby Fischer: 'gives White active play.'}) {Bobby Fischer: ' Black's advantage of the Bishop pair is neutraliazed by the weakness of his c6, which White White can later occupy with his Knight. '} 21. Nd4 g6 22. a3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More to the point was'} 22. Bf3 {} b4 23. cxb4 Rxb4 24. Nc6 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='}) 22... a5 23. Kh1 (23. Bf3 {  Bobby Fischer: 'should again be played.'}) {Bobby Fischer: 'White starts drifting. '} 23... b4 24. cxb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 24. axb4 axb4 25. c4 b3 $1 $36 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'holds the initiative.'}) 24... axb4 25. Rc2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 25. Bf3 bxa3 26. bxa3 Ra8 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'presents its problems.'}) 25... Qb6 26. Nc6 {Bobby Fischer: ', This is the position White was playing for. A draw now looks secure. '} bxa3 $1 27. Qxb6 (27. bxa3 $15 {Bobby Fischer: 'is also adequate.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 27. Nxb8 $2 Qxf2 28. Rxf2 a2 29. Rf1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 29. Rc1 Bxb2) 29... Bf5 30. Rcc1 Rxb8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 27... Rxb6 28. bxa3 Ra8 29. Nxe5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: ', This gives Black a strong passed e Pawn.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 29. Ra2 {} Rb2 30. Rxb2 Bxb2 31. Bb5 $1 Rxa3 {Bobby Fischer: '(otherwise a4)'} 32. Ne7+ Kf8 33. Nxg6+ {Bobby Fischer: 'with a draw in view. On'} hxg6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or on'} 33... Ke8 34. Re1+ $1 (34. Bxd7+ Kxd7 35. Rxf7+ {Bobby Fischer: '?'} Kd8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins a piece.'}) 34... Kd8 35. Bxd7 fxg6 $10 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 35... Kxd7 36. Nf8+ { Sxh7} Kd8 {(or any)} 37. Nxh7 $11)) 34. Bxd7 $10 {  Bobby Fischer: 'produces opposite colored Bishops.'}) 29... dxe5 30. Rc3 (30. Rc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'gets nowhere after'} Rd6 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the sacrifice'} 31. Bb5 $6 {Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by'} Bxb5 32. Rfxf7 Rc8 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or on'} 30. Ra2 Ra5 31. Bc4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 31. Rd1 Ba4 $19) 31... Rc5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the d-Pawn falls.'}) (30. Ra1 {Bobby Fischer: '?'} Rxa3 $19) 30... Rb2 $1 {Neil McDonald: 'Pilnik played'} {Neil McDonald: 'Unlike Fischer, we can't exonerate Pilnik from putting too much faith in the in initiative, as in the game his sharp play quickly led to disaster after'} 31. Rc7 {Bobby Fischer: ', Desperately striving for counterplay.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Neil McDonald: 'In 'My 60 Memorable Games', Fischer gives the alternative' Neil McDonald: 'Commenting on this little piece of analysis in his monograph on Fischer, Hübner remarks that: "Fischer always strives for activity. Advancing the d-pawn only weakens it. White should do something [about] the main tactical drawback of his position, the vulnerable back rank, and [after' Neil McDonald: 'Incidentally, I'm not sure if the criticism implicit in the claim that 'Fischer always strives for activity' is justified. Fischer was Black in this game, he probably wasn't concerned whether White is objectively okay after 32 Kg1 - he would have carried on pressing for the win. What he did need to know, however, was whether White could upset the cart so to speak with a violent attack against the f7 point with Bc4 and d5-d6. He had to verify that Black can ward off the danger after' Neil McDonald: 'Having said that, Hübner's suggestion of'} 31. Bc4 {Bobby Fischer: '(to prevent ...Ra2)'} Kg7 {Neil McDonald: '] play'} 32. d6 (32. Kg1 {." Hübner says he isn't certain Black is winning after that. Neil McDonald: 'is an excellent one. He intends to answer'} Rc8 {with} 33. Rb3 {, escaping from the pin.}) 32... f5 33. Bd5 Ra6 $19 { xd6  xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'again wins the d-Pawn.' Neil McDonald: 'when Black picks up the d6-pawn and wins easily.' Neil McDonald: '- White has no time for'} 34. Rc7 {because of} Rxd6 {, winning the passed pawn and killing of White's initiative.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or' Neil McDonald: 'The fact that Fischer is focused on making sure White can't do anything dramatic is made clear in a further note by Fischer at this point: "[If]'} 31. Bf3 f5 32. Rc7 Bb5 33. Re1 e4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'squelches White's play.' Neil McDonald: 'squelches White's play." Yes, Fischer is concerned with keeping White in a bind. Whether or not Pilnik could survive with 31 Bc4 Kg7 32 Kg1 isn't of much relevance. Therefore we can take issue with the idea that Fischer is suggesting impulsive moves because he overestimated the value of the initiative - instead he is trying to keep things under control. You can bet that if Fischer had been White in this game he would have been looking at all possible saving moves, including quiet ones.'}) 31... Bf5 32. g4 {Bobby Fischer: ', A wild hope.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Hopeless is'} 32. Bc4 Rc2 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 33. d6 Rxc4 $1 34. Rxc4 Bd3 35. Rfc1 Bxc4 36. Rxc4 Rxa3 37. Kg1 Kg7 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Black soon picks off the d-Pawn and wins easily.'}) 32... Be4+ 33. Bf3 Bd3 34. d6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 34. Re1 e4 $1 35. Bg2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 35. Bxe4 Re8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 35... Rd8 36. Rc5 Kg7 $1 37. Kg1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 37. g5 h6 38. h4 hxg5 39. hxg5 Rh8+ 40. Kg1 Rh4 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins!.'} (40... Rh5 $1 $19)) 37... f5 38. gxf5 gxf5 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the two passed center Pawns should win. (if'} 39. d6 Kf6 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: ').'}) 34... Rd8 35. Re1 Rxd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Najdorf chided me after the game for "missing"'} 35... e4 $1 36. Bxe4 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} Rb1 $1 37. Rxb1 Bxe4+ 38. Kg1 Bxb1 $19 {Najdorf,M Najdorf,M Bobby Fischer: 'and wins.'}) 36. Rxe5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Falling into the trap.'} (36. Re7 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds out longer, but'} Rf6 37. Bd5 e4 38. Bxe4 Bxe4+ 39. R7xe4 Rff2 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 36... Rf6 $1 37. Re3 $8 {Bobby Fischer: ', Forced.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 37. Bg2 Rb1+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.'}) 37... Rxf3 $1 38. Rxf3 Be4 39. Rcxf7 Rf2 40. Rf8+ Kg7 {Bobby Fischer: '; White resigns.  After 41. Rf7, Kh6 wins. Or 41. Rf4...'} (40... Kg7 {Bobby Fischer: 'After' Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 41. R8f7+ (41. R8f4 Bd5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'cooks White's goose.'} (41... Bxf3+ 42. Kg1 Rg2+ 43. Kf1 Bc6 44. Rc4 Bb5 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'also wins.'})) 41... Kh6 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 0-1

