{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 28 A peccable draw. What proved to be the last game of this ill-starred match is a good example of how Reshevsky, by virtue of pluck, stamina, and alertness, salvages a draw from a lost position. It exemplifies, too, the demoralising effect that continuously strong resistance can have on even the most robust opponent. Fischer rapidly wrests the initiative and wins the exchange as the result of a pretty combination (28 .... Qxe4). However, he has difficulty gaining the offensive because Reshevsky throws obstacle after obstacle in his path. Nevertheless, Fischer's material advantage begins to make itself felt. He misses clear wins (on moves 38 and 42) whereupon his game deteriorates sufficiently to permit his stubborn opponent to set up an adequate defence. Still, there are several surprises in store just at the very end. '} 1. c4 {Bobby Fischer} Nf6 2. d4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. Be2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Inferior is'} 5. e5 {Letelier,R-Fischer,R Leipzig ol 1960 Bobby Fischer: 'See Game 21. Letelier, R-Fischer,R Leipzig ol 1960'}) 5... d6 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 7. d5 {Olafsson,F-Fischer,R Zürich 1959 Bobby Fischer: 'see Game 7. Olafsson,F-Fischer,R Zürich 1959'}) 7... Nc6 8. d5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Match game 9 (Reshevslcy as White) had continued:'} 8. Be3 Re8 9. dxe5 (9. d5 Nd4 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'equalises fully'}) 9... dxe5 10. Qxd8 Nxd8 11. Nb5 Ne6 12. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: ''full of sound and fury, signifying nothing' - Evans'} Re7 $10 {Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R (9) Los Angeles 1961 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'See Game 57 (note to Black's move 13). Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R (9) Los Angeles 1961'}) 8... Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 f5 11. exf5 (11. f3 f4 { g6-g5   Bobby Fischer: 'followed by ... g5, etc., gives Black a strong attack on the K-side.'}) 11... Nxf5 (11... gxf5 {Bobby Fischer: 'keeping Black's pawn front mobile, is very strong.'}) 12. f3 Nd4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 12... Nf6 {Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Bled 1961 Bobby Fischer: 'see Game 30. Gligoric, S-Fischer,R Bled 1961'}) 13. Ne4 b6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Hindering White's thematic break with c5.'} 14. Bg5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Apparently gaining a tempo, but only driving the queen to a better square.'} (14. Bd2 { Bobby Fischer: 'or'}) (14. Re1 { Bobby Fischer: 'appear to be more accurate.'}) 14... Qe8 15. Bd2 {Bobby Fischer: 'This bishop must retreat eventually after ... h6. The idea is to prepare b4 without having to fear the reply ... a5.'} a5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Gaining more time. White must now stop for b3 (to enforce b4).'} 16. Re1 ({The immediate} 16. a3 {(Bobby Fischer) allows} a4 $1 {fixing the Q-side.}) 16... Nxe2+ {Bobby Fischer: 'Otherwise the bishop retreats to f1.'} 17. Qxe2 h6 18. b3 (18. a3 { b2-b4} a4 $1) 18... g5 19. a3 Qg6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now it's obvious that it was a mistake to force Black's queen to e8 - its presence on g6 lends momentum to the K-side initiative.'} 20. b4 Nf6 21. bxa5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now Reshevsky is hoping to get some counterplay. But -'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'The solid'} 21. Ndf2 { Bobby Fischer: 'is correct, but after'} Nh5 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black stands better.'}) 21... g4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '- doesn't give him time to get it in.'} (21... bxa5 22. Ndf2 Nh5 23. c5 $132) 22. Ndf2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 22. axb6 $2 gxf3 23. Qxf3 Nxe4 24. Qxe4 Bf5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins a piece.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 22. Nxf6+ Bxf6 23. f4 Bf5 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'White's in trouble.'}) 22... gxf3 23. Qxf3 Nh5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Stepping up the pressure.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 23... Nxd5 24. Qg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds.'}) 24. Qe3 bxa5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Finally!'} 25. Rac1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Typically, Reshevsky wants to mobilise his queenside without making any concessions or creating any kingside weaknesses.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 25. g3 Nf4 26. Kh1 Nh3 $1 $40 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'it's just a matter of time before Black invades on the light squares.'}) 25... Bf5 26. c5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Loses material, but probably the best chance.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 26. g3 Rf7 { af8  Bobby Fischer: 'followed by ... Raf8 White is not long for this world.'}) 26... Nf4 27. Qg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Blunders the exchange.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'But it is no longer possible to play'} 27. g3 Nxd5 $19) 27... Bxe4 $1 28. Rxe4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Flustered, White makes the task somewhat easier.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is'} 28. Nxe4 {} Qxg3 29. hxg3 Nd3 30. cxd6 cxd6 31. Rc6 Nxe1 32. Bxe1 $36 {Bobby Fischer: 'with many more chances of holding the ending than in the actual game.'}) (28. Bxf4 $2 exf4 {Bobby Fischer: '-+'}) 28... Qxe4 $1 29. Nxe4 Ne2+ 30. Kh1 Nxg3+ 31. hxg3 Ra6 $1 32. cxd6 cxd6 33. a4 {-+ / ; Evans,L Bobby Fischer: '"Black has won the exchange, but the technical difficulties confronting him are enormous. His pawns are discombobulated, his bishop is hemmed in and his rooks are virtually immobilised. Still, one has the feeling Fischer should win this game." (Evans)'} Rf7 34. g4 Bf8 35. Kh2 Kh7 36. Rc8 Rb6 37. Ra8 Rb3 38. Bxa5 Rf4 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'The right concept is to destroy the blockade on e4 with'} 38... Re3 $1 39. Nc3 (39. Ng3 Rd3) 39... e4 40. Re8 Bg7 41. Nxe4 Be5+ $19) 39. Bc7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'With his usual tenacity, Reshevsky finds the only move to keep the game alive. White is still quite lost, however.'} Rxe4 40. Rxf8 Rd3 41. Rf6 Rxg4 42. Rxd6 Rg7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now the 'technical difficulties' become more real than apparent.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 42... Rd2 $1 43. Rd7+ Kg6 44. Bxe5 Rgxg2+ 45. Kh3 Rg5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'winning easily.'}) 43. Rc6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Forced.'} (43. Bb6 {Bobby Fischer: '10ses to'} Rd2 $19) ({Bobby Fischer: 'and'} 43. Ba5 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses to'} Rdg3 $19) 43... Rxd5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 43... Rd2 44. Bxe5 Rgxg2+ 45. Kh3 Rg5 46. Bf4 Rd3+ 47. Kh2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if White plays'} 47. Kh4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'the reply is'} Rd4 48. Rf6 Kg7) 47... Rh5+ 48. Kg2 Rdxd5 49. Rc7+ {Bobby Fischer: '!'} Kg6 50. Rc6+ $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws.'}) 44. Rc2 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Again I had overlooked White's reply.'} e4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Discouraged, I gave it one final try.'} 45. a5 Rd3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 45... e3 46. Bf4 Re7 47. Re2 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws.'}) 46. Bf4 Rf7 47. g3 e3 48. Rc1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Reshevsky, once more in severe time-pressure, overlooked that'} 48. Kg2 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws easily. For on'} Rxf4 49. Rc7+ $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'is the saving clause.'}) 48... Re7 49. Re1 Ra3 50. Re2 Kg6 51. Kg2 (51. Bd6 $2 Rd7 $1 52. Bxa3 Rd2 $19 53. Rg2 {Bobby Fischer} e2 54. Bb4) 51... Rxa5 52. Rxe3 (52. Bxe3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws easily. Black can't make anything out of the pin on the e-file.'}) 52... Ra2+ 53. Kf3 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'A comedy of errors.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 53. Kh3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'in order to keep Black's king out of g4 after the exchange of rooks, e.g.'} Rxe3 54. Bxe3 h5 55. Bf4 Ra1 56. Bc7 Kf5 57. Bf4 Rb1 58. Bc7 $1 Rh1+ 59. Kg2 Rc1 60. Bf4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '(gaining a vital tempo by hitting the rook)'} Rb1 61. Kh3 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'maintaining the blockade.'}) 53... Rb7 {Bobby Fischer: '?' Bobby Fischer: 'Returning the favour.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'As Evans pointed out in Chess Life, "The best winning chance is'} 53... Rxe3+ {Evans,L} 54. Bxe3 h5 {Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Kf5." Disgusted, I no longer thought there was a win. However, later I worked out a problem-like variation (after 54 ... h5):'} 55. g4 $2 (55. Ke4 Kf6 $1 56. Bd4+ Ke6 57. Kf4 Ra4 58. Ke3 Kf5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'leads to variations similar to 'c'.'}) (55. Kf4 Ra5 $1 56. Bd2 Rf5+ 57. Ke4 Kf6 {Bobby Fischer: 'and Black's king will eventually penetrate to g4. For example'} 58. Bf4 (58. Ke3 $2 Kg5) 58... Ra5 $19 { a4+, f5 Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Ra4+and Kf5'}) (55. Bf4 Kf5 56. Bd6 Rb2 57. Bf4 Rb3+ 58. Kg2 Kg4 59. Bd6 Rb2+ 60. Kg1 Kh3 61. Be5 Rb4 $1 62. Bc7 (62. Bf4 $2 h4) 62... Rg4 $1 63. Kf2 Kh2 64. Be5 Kh1 65. Kf3 Rg8 66. Bf4 Rf8 67. Kf2 (67. Ke3 Kg2) 67... h4 68. Kf3 h3 69. Kf2 h2 70. Kf1 Ra8 71. Kf2 Ra2+ 72. Kf1 Ra3 $1 73. Kf2 Rf3+ $3 74. Kxf3 Kg1 75. Be3+ Kf1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the pawn queens.'}) 55... h4 $19) 54. Re6+ Kf5 55. Re5+ Kf6 56. Rd5 Rb3+ 57. Kg4 1/2-1/2

