{ChessCalisthenics: 'A25: English Opening vs King's Indian with ...Nc6 but without early d3''A25: English Opening vs King's Indian with ...Nc6 but without early d3 Maric,Rudolf: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'A25: English Opening vs King's Indian with ...Nc6 but without early d3''' Stohl,Igor: 'As far as the MegaBase is concerned, this is the only game Bobby Fischerplayed in 1969. And probably even this claim should be corrected, as Karsten Mueller in his Fischer monography quotes GM Andy Soltis, who says the match in the Metropolitan Chess League between the Manhattan and Marshall chess clubs took place in November 1968 (Soltis played for Marshall). In any case this was the last game before Fischer took a break from chess, which lasted more than a year. In this sense it can be perceived as a prelude to his return in 1970, followed by two years of his dominance, culminating in Reykjavik 1972. The chess world has never experienced anything like this before or afterwards - just to illustrate the point, Fischer's convincing 12.5:8.5 win in the title match against Spassky cost him 5 rating points in the then newly introduced ELO system! '} 1. c4 e5 (1... c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Ndb5 Bb4 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. Nxc3 d5 9. e3 O-O 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Be2 Bf5 12. Nb5 Qb6 13. O-O a6 14. Nd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. exd4 Rac8 17. Bd1 Bc2 18. Be3 Bxd1 19. Rfxd1 Rc2 20. Rd2 Rfc8 21. Rxc2 Rxc2 22. Rc1 Rxc1+ 23. Bxc1 Nd7 $15 {Saidy,A-Fischer,R New York 1964 This is the arguably most famous Saidy-Fischer game. It brought Fischer the record 11/11 result in the US-championship and was completely different from ours - Bobby was in no mood for quiet technical play against a solid opponent, he avoids symmetry and seeks a complex fight from the very beginning.}) 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. g3 f5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Fischer has already indicated he is not interested in fighting/playing for equality and this is only underlined by the aggressive text move.'} ({The more usual and less committal choice here is} 3... g6 {, which leads to the Closed Sicilian with opposite colours. When our game was played, Spassky was very successful with this system with White. Later on antidotes for Black have been found and the Closed Sicilian became a rare guest on the elite level, nowadays it's considered a rather unambitious choice. So with White a certain setup even with an extra tempo is regarded "soft", while with Black it's described as ambitious - this seemingly paradoxical statement requires some explanation. The main reason is that White in general has higher ambitions in the opening, in the Sicilian they are connected mainly with a quick d4 in open systems. Black often can't afford analoguous set-ups (with d5), but lines with 1...e5 fully suit his plans to get active counterplay. However, choosing suitable lines is not simple: Adjectives such as active, ambitious or even aggressive have a positive connotation, but in chess the line between them and evaluations as double-edged, risky and dubious is often a very thin one. Play with opposite colours is an important issue in our game and we will touch on it in more concrete detail in the following notes.}) 4. Bg2 Nf6 5. d3 {Stohl,Igor: 'I have seen two published versions of this game, so I have opted for the one, which seems more logical from my point of view (it's not the one in MegaBase).'} ({But is} 5. e3 {objectively better than the text move? Might well be:} {However, White has to reckon also with the tactical} d5 $5 (5... Bc5 $6 {is really provocative and} 6. Nge2 {is both logical and tempting - a quick d4 push shall give White an advantage.} ({instead of the passive} 6. d3 {, transposing to our game})) ({After} 5... d6) ({or} 5... g6 {White can also profit from the fact that in the future he can push d2-d4 in one move.}) 6. Nxd5 (6. cxd5 Nb4 7. d3 Nfxd5 {leads to the same position}) 6... Nxd5 7. cxd5 (7. Bxd5 $143 $2 Nb4 $36) 7... Nb4 8. d3 ({Returning material is more sensible than} 8. Qb3 $6 e4 9. f3 Nd3+ $44 {}) 8... Nxd5 9. Nf3 (9. a3 Nf6 $5 10. Ne2 Bd6 11. O-O O-O $132) 9... Bd6 (9... Bb4+ 10. Bd2 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qd6 12. O-O O-O 13. e4 $5 $146 Ne7 (13... Nf6 14. Qc3 $36) 14. d4 fxe4 15. Nxe5 Bf5 (15... Nf5 $5) 16. Qc3 Rad8 17. Rac1 Nd5 18. Qb3 Qb6 19. Qxb6 axb6 20. Rfe1 c6 21. Bxe4 Bxe4 22. Rxe4 $16 {Kramnik,V (2799)-Bartel,M (2674) Dortmund 2012 For more details about this game see the notes in CBM 150 by Marin.}) 10. O-O Nf6 {Despite the warnings in the note to move 3 Black has opened the centre and it's not so simple to refute his direct approach. The most viable try is probably emulating Kramnik and changing the character of the position with} 11. Qb3 ({The immediate} 11. e4 $5 {is similar}) 11... Qe7 12. e4 $5 fxe4 13. dxe4 c6 (13... Qe6 $5 14. Qc2 O-O {seems more circumspect.}) 14. Nh4 Be6 15. Qc2 g6 (15... O-O {Pena Gomez,M (2456)-Bolado Saez,S (2328) Villava 2009} 16. Nf5 $36) 16. Bg5 O-O 17. Rad1 Qc7 18. Nf3 Kg7 19. b3 Rad8 20. Bc1 $5 $14 Bg4 $6 21. h3 Bxf3 22. Bxf3 Qe7 23. Be3 Ne8 24. Be2 a6 25. Qc1 Kh8 26. Bh6 Ng7 27. Bg5 $18 {Anton Guijarro,D (2686)-Tejedor Fuente,E (2457) Marbella 2019}) 5... Bc5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Active development, but it has its downsides as well - the  goes to an exposed square.'} ({Apart from the aforementioned schemes with} 5... d6) ({or} 5... g6) ({Black has also the sortie} 5... Bb4 $5 {This is also the move which occurs most often in practice and has quite a good reputation. Sicilian systems with b5 are very popular indeed and even here} 6. Bd2 O-O {leads to a fully playable Anglo-Dutch setup.}) {Stohl,Igor: 'instead of the passive'} 6. e3 {Stohl,Igor: 'Saidy plays the most natural and arguably the best move. White restricts the c5 and prepares ge2 - the  will support the d4 advance without limiting the g2.' Stohl,Igor: ', transposing to our game'} ({The refined} 6. a3 {is interesting, but Black doesn't have to play the mechanical} a5 ({Developing moves} 6... d6 {and}) (6... O-O {, or even}) (6... a6 $5 $13 {with unclear consequences all seem to be a better choice.}) 7. e3 {- later on we will see how White can profit from the insertion of a3 and a5.}) 6... f4 $5 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!?'' Stohl,Igor: 'Aggression in the spirit of moves 3 and 5, Black wants to prevent his opponent from consolidating, even if it means investing material. Altogether this is not so typical for Fischer, who is better known for increasing small advantages, excellent technique and consuming "poisoned" pawns (in the Najdorf on b2, in the Two Knights with White). However, the American didn't completely shy away from positional sacrifices, although he was not an avid gambiteer like e.g., Alekhine or Kasparov. Objectively the text move (Sicilan Grand Prix Attack a tempo down!) is probably not fully correct, but it's far easier to say so with hindsight, more practical material and computer support. Directly at the board the element of surprise must have played an important, if not pivotal role.'} ({A simpler solution is} 6... O-O {, here I will risk an example from my own practice:} 7. Nge2 Qe8 ({After} 7... f4 {White can not only accept the sacrifice, but has also} 8. d4 $1 $14 {/}) ({A possible alternative is} 7... d6 8. O-O a6 $5) 8. O-O d6 9. d4 (9. Nd5 Nxd5 10. cxd5 Nd8 11. d4 Bb6 $132 {seems rather harmless, but}) (9. Na4 $5 {deserves attention - White wants not so much to take the  immediately, but waits for the most suitable moment to get the . This position arose with opposite colours in the game Tiviakov-Kasparov, Wijk aan Zee 2001, where White had the extra move b3. However, this rather helped Black and after 10.e3 b6 11.f2?! b7 12.h4 xb3 13.axb3 d7 14.g3 f5 15.ae1 c6 16.exf5 gxf5! the active bishops and prospects on the g gave him a sizeable initiative.}) 9... Bb6 10. Na4 Bd7 11. b3 Nd8 12. Nxb6 axb6 13. Nc3 Bc6 14. d5 Bd7 15. f4 Nf7 16. a4 Qe7 17. Ra2 h6 18. Re2 $14 {Stohl,I (2550)-Hracek,Z (2485) Rimavska Sobota 1991}) 7. exf4 {Stohl,Igor: 'This is one of the sacrifices, which should be accepted.'} (7. Nge2) ({or} 7. Nf3 {is playable, but inconsistent and doesn't give White an advantage.}) ({Apart from the logical text move, also} 7. gxf4 {deserves attention. White's centre remains solid, but his kingside is weakened and} d6 (7... exf4 8. d4 Bb4 9. e4 $5 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]  suits White}) 8. Nge2 O-O 9. h3 Nh5 $5 {leads to an unclear position.}) 7... O-O {Stohl,Igor: '?!' Stohl,Igor: 'Black's compensation consists mainly of his pressure on the f-file and the a7-g1 diagonal and in this respect the text-move is natural.'} ({However, because of the possibility we'll mention in the following note, more accurate was} 7... d6 $5 {This was played in subsequent games, when the main reaction} 8. Nge2 {after}) 8. Nge2 {Stohl,Igor: 'Natural development.'} ({The greedy} 8. Bxc6 $2 dxc6 9. fxe5 Ng4 $19 {is out of question.}) ({However, White had two interesting and more concrete alternatives:} 8. Be3 Qe7 (8... d6 $6 9. Bxc5 dxc5 10. Bxc6 bxc6 11. fxe5 Ng4 12. f4 Bf5 13. Nf3 $16 {White willingly returns both pawns and has a large positional advantage.}) ({The direct} 8... Bxe3 9. fxe3 exf4 10. gxf4 Re8 11. Qd2 {is also unconvincing, here Black sorely misses a tempo for his development:} Ng4 12. Nd5 Qh4+ 13. Kd1 Nf2+ 14. Kc2 Nxh1 15. Nxc7 $18) 9. Bxc5 Qxc5 10. Nge2 $16 {The  swap has helped White; Black is pressed to show tangible compensation.}) (8. fxe5 $5 {seems to be even more critical:} {The complications after} Bxf2+ (8... Nxe5 $2 9. d4) (8... Qe8 9. Nf3 (9. Bxc6 $5 dxc6 10. d4 $16) 9... d6 10. d4 Nxd4 11. Nxd4 Qxe5+ 12. Be3 Ng4 13. Nc2 Bxe3 14. Nxe3 Nxe3 15. fxe3 Qxe3+ 16. Qe2 Qg5 {Andrade Ocana,J (2196)-Santos,A (2272) Odivelas 2000} 17. Ne4 Qg6 18. Qd3 $18) 9. Kxf2 Ng4+ 10. Ke1 Nf2 11. Qe2 (11. Bd5+ Kh8 12. Qh5 $5) 11... Nxe5 12. Nf3 $16 {give White very good chances to gradually consolidate his material advantage.}) 8... Qe8 {Stohl,Igor: 'The queen heads directly for the kingside.'} ({However, even here more accurate was} 8... d6 $5 {After} 9. h3 (9. O-O Qe8 {tranposes, while}) (9. Be3 Nd4 10. O-O Qe8 $44 {gives Black play.})) 9. O-O {Stohl,Igor: 'This has been White's main reaction, but even a natural move such as castling is not indisputable. The  goes to the flank, on which it could easily come under attack. This is even more clear in the analogous line with opposite colours, in which White has already played d3. Here 9...0-0? is downright dangerous due to 10.h4, but it's better to keep White guessing with 9...h6!?. The extra tempo here is not so crucial, as Black's  is not yet the target of a direct attack. The position is at the very least unclear, as the aggressive 10.exf5?! (10.g3!? retains more tension) 10...xf5 11.g4 gives Black a noticeable advantage after 11...xg4 12.xf7+ xf7 13.e5+ g8 14.xg4 d4! '} ({However, let's check our concrete position. Interesting is} 9. fxe5 $5 {} Ng4 10. f4 Nf2 11. Qd2 Nxh1 12. d4 $44 {/ , but this has yet to be tested.}) ({A playable try is} 9. h3 {, which after} d6 {leads to the position from the previous note.} 10. Ne4 ({More promising is the courageous} 10. fxe5 $142 $5 {} dxe5 11. Be3 Bxe3 12. fxe3 Qg6 13. Qd2 $14) (10. Be3 $5) 10... exf4 11. Bxf4 (11. Nxc5 fxg3 $1 $132 {is good for Black, as an attempt to keep the extra piece would be playing with fire.}) 11... Nxe4 12. dxe4 Qf7 (12... Be6 $1 {} 13. b3 g5 $44) 13. O-O Qxc4 14. e5 Kh8 15. exd6 cxd6 16. a3 a5 17. Qd2 Bf5 18. Rac1 Qe6 19. Kh2 Be4 20. Bxe4 Qxe4 21. Nc3 Qg6 $132 {Sibarevic,M-Kovacevic,V Yugoslavia 1970 both material and position were roughly equal.}) ({Even more unpleasant is the concrete} 9. Nb5 $1 {, hitting the c7 disrupts Black's atacking plans. Sacrificial orgies like} exf4 (9... Bb6 10. c5 $1 $16) 10. Nxc7 Bxf2+ (10... Qh5 11. Bxf4 $1 $18) 11. Kxf2 Qe5 {leave the engines unimpressed and very sceptical:} 12. Nxa8 $5 (12. Bxf4 Ng4+ 13. Ke1 Rxf4 14. gxf4 Qxc7 15. Qb3 $16) 12... Ng4+ 13. Kf1 fxg3+ 14. Nf4 $1 Rxf4+ 15. Bxf4 Qxf4+ 16. Qf3 $18) 9... d6 {Stohl,Igor: 'tranposes, while'} 10. Na4 {Stohl,Igor: 'Fighting the c5 is a good idea, but the realization is not quite ideal.'} ({Stohl,Igor: 'Older annotators condemned'} 10. Be3 Bxe3 11. fxe3 exf4 12. exf4 Qe3+ {Stohl,Igor: ', but it's far from clear after'} 13. Kh1 Ng4 (13... Bg4 $5 14. h3 Bxe2 15. Nxe2 Rae8 16. Bf3 Nb4 17. Nc1 {Black's pressure hasn't brought him anything concrete, but he still retains reasonable compensation after} Nc6 $44) 14. Bd5+ (14. Ne4 $1 Bf5 15. N2c3 $16 {and White gradually untangles}) 14... Kh8 15. Kg2 Qe8 $1 $19) ({If we imagine the position with the inserted moves a3 and a5, the sortie 11.b5 would give White an advantage. However, here} 10. Nb5 Bg4 $1 (10... Qh5 11. d4 $1 Bg4 12. f3 exd4 {Steindorsson,S (2213)-Simonsen,O (2214) Reykjavik 2001} 13. fxg4 d3+ 14. Kh1 $16) 11. Kh1 (11. h3 $2 {runs into} Qh5 $1 $40) 11... Qd7 $132 {/ allows Black to chase the b5 away and retain some pressure.}) ({If White doesn't fancy 10.e3, a better version of this line is} 10. fxe5 $5 dxe5 11. Be3 Bxe3 12. fxe3 Bg4 13. Qd2 Qh5 14. Rae1 $14 {/, Chernyshov,K (2496)-Matras,O (2264) Prague 2017}) ({Another promising attempt to refute Fischer's idea is} 10. Ne4 Nxe4 11. dxe4 Qh5 (11... Qf7 12. b3 Bg4 13. h3 $16) 12. Kh1 ({Returning the } 12. f5 $5 g6 13. Be3 $16 {also deserves attention.}) 12... Rf6 13. Nc3 (13. f5 g6 14. f4 $5 (14. fxg6 $2 {Miezis,N (2523)-Simonsen,O (2265) Gausdal 2003} Bg4 $1 15. Qd5+ Kh8 16. Ng1 Bxf2 $36) 14... gxf5 15. Bf3 $16) 13... Bg4 $6 (13... Qxd1 $142 14. Nxd1 Be6 15. b3 $14 {/ and even here Black doesn't have full compensation.}) 14. f3 Rh6 15. h4 Be6 16. f5 Bxc4 17. Bxh6 Bxf1 {Oepen,L-Hermann,T Germany 1987} 18. Bxf1 gxh6 19. Nd5 $16) 10... Bd4 {Stohl,Igor: '!' Stohl,Igor: 'Black will have to part with his , but he wants to get to best possible deal and, contrary to the a4, centralizes his minor piece.'} 11. Nxd4 {Stohl,Igor: 'Consistency, which doesn't deserve to be condemned too harshly.'} ({After} 11. fxe5 {Black has the unpleasant} Bg4 $1 12. Kh1 $142 $1 $14 {[%cal Gf2f3]} ({We already know the motif} 12. h3 $2 Qh5 $1 $17) ({The tactical reaction} 12. exf6 Bxe2 13. Bd5+ Kh8 14. f7 Qe5 $142 $1 (14... Qe7 15. Bg5 Bxd1 (15... Qe5 $1 $13) 16. Bxe7 Bxa4 17. Bxf8 Rxf8 18. b3 $16) (14... Bxd1 15. fxe8=Q Raxe8 16. Nc3 $1 $14) 15. Bf4 (15. Qb3 Bxf1 16. Bf4 Qh5 (16... Qe7 $5 17. Rxf1 Nd8 $15) 17. Rxf1 Ne7 $15) 15... Qh5 $5 (15... Bxd1 16. Bxe5 Bxa4 17. Bxc6 dxe5 18. Bxa4 Rxf7 $11) 16. Qd2 Ne5 $44 {allows Black to retain the initiative.})) 11... exd4 {Stohl,Igor: '!' Stohl,Igor: 'Doubling his pawns, but it's more important to keep the a4 offside.'} (11... Nxd4 $143 12. Nc3 $14 {doesn't give Black sufficient compensation.}) 12. h3 (12. Re1 Qg6 {only chases Black's  where it wants to go. However, after} 13. Qb3 $5 $13 {the position remains at the very least unclear.}) 12... h5 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!'' Stohl,Igor: 'Saidy wanted to play g4, Fischer naturally doesn't allow this without a fight. The insertion of h3 and h5 in my opinion slightly favours Black - White must now reckon with h4 at a suitable moment, much later on we will see the g3 becoming more vulnerable. '} 13. a3 {Stohl,Igor: 'White tries it on the other flank.'} (13. b3 {also deserves attention, later on White can e.g., place his forces as in the game, but try to avoid the potentially weakening a3.}) ({Also} 13. f3 $5 {is interesting, White insists on the g4 advance and Fischer would still have to prove he has enough for the pawn.}) 13... a5 $44 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: ''' Stohl,Igor: 'Natural symmetrical prophylaxis, Black prevents b4.'} 14. b3 {Stohl,Igor: '?!'} ({White doesn't proceed with his plan, after} 14. Rb1 Bf5 {the impatient} 15. b4 $6 {allows} axb4 16. axb4 Qg6 {, advantageously regaining the .}) ({The rather passive text move is not an engine favourite. However, even the supposedly better} 14. Re1 $5 Qg6 15. Qb3 {is not quite clear. Black can play} Rb8 $5 $132 {, preparing f5 and setting a trap} 16. Bxc6 $2 bxc6 17. Qxb8 Qxd3 $40 {[%cal Rc8h3,Rd3f3] with a raging attack on the light squares.}) 14... Qg6 15. Nb2 (15. Ra2 $5 Bf5 16. Rd2 {also deserves consideration. We'll see the point in a short while - although the a4 is not ideally placed, it does have an important role, namely covering the c5-square.}) 15... Bf5 16. Qc2 Nd7 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!'' Stohl,Igor: 'Fischer targets White's weaknesses. An important component of his mastery was that he often achieved positions, in which seemingly simple play was also strong and effective. '} 17. Re1 ({Occupies an open file, but} 17. Rd1 $5 {makes a better impression. Overprotecting the d3 allows the g2 to remain on the long diagonal, after} Nc5 {White can try} 18. Kh2 $13 {[%cal Yf2f3,Yg3g4]  f3,g4} ({Even the attempt to free himself by returning the  with} 18. Bd2 $5 {deserves attention. In both cases the position remains quite murky.})) 17... Nc5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Black wouldn't have had this manoeuvre, if the knight had stayed on a4 - see the note to move 15. '} 18. Bf1 ({Here after} 18. Bd2 {White must reckon with} h4 $5 19. g4 Bxd3 20. Nxd3 Qxd3 21. Qxd3 Nxd3 $15) 18... Ra6 $1 $15 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!'' Stohl,Igor: '!' Stohl,Igor: 'Another straightforward and easily understandable manoeuvre, which activates the a8 far more effectively, than stereotyped occupation of the open e-file.'} 19. Bd2 {Stohl,Igor: '?' Stohl,Igor: 'Although until now we have criticized Saidy more than once, only this lax move is a more serious mistake.'} ({He should have met the b6 threat with} 19. Qd1 $1 $13 {and although White's forces are very passive, there is still no obvious way for Black to break into the defensive fortress. Here} Rb6 (19... Rb8 {[%cal Yb7b5]} 20. Be2 {[%csl Rh5]}) 20. Na4 {is nothing to worry about.}) 19... Rb6 {Stohl,Igor: 'Increasing the pressure, now b3 falls.'} 20. Bxa5 (20. Na4 $2 {now runs into} Nxd3 $19 {[%cal Rd3f2,Rd3e1] , which both attacks the e1 and threatens xf2.}) 20... Rxb3 (20... Nxa5 21. b4 Nab3 {is also advantageous for Black, but the simplification after} 22. Rad1 $15 {slightly eases White's task.}) 21. Bd2 (21. Bxc7 $2 Rc8 {drops material:} 22. Bxd6 (22. Na4 Rxc7 23. Nxc5 Rc3 $19) 22... Rc3 $19 ({Also after} 22... Qxd6 23. Na4 Rxd3 $1 $19 {White's position collapses.})) 21... Ra8 22. a4 (22. Bb4 Nxb4 23. axb4 Rxa1 24. Rxa1 Rxb4 $15 {is not too attractive and indeed, White could have improved even later on.}) 22... Ra6 $1 $17 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!'' Stohl,Igor: '!' Stohl,Igor: 'A deja vu to move 18.'} 23. a5 Kh7 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' 'N''Maric,Rudolf: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N''' Stohl,Igor: '!'} ({Stohl,Igor: 'The impatient'} 23... Nb4 24. Bxb4 Rxb4 25. Ra3 (25. Re7 b6 $5 $44) 25... b6 26. Rea1 Qe6 {Stohl,Igor: '?!'} (26... bxa5 $44) (26... Bd7 $5) 27. axb6 Raxb6 28. Ra8+ Kh7 29. Qd1 {Stohl,Igor: '!'} g6 30. Na4 Nxa4 31. R8xa4 Rxa4 32. Rxa4 Bxh3 33. Ra7 Bxf1 34. Rxc7+ Kh6 35. Qxf1 {Stohl,Igor: '' Stohl,Igor: 'Karpov,A-Bellon Lopez,J Madrid 1973' Stohl,Igor: 'had already Black fighting for his life, in the end he managed to salvage a draw. The move orders in Fischer's and Karpov's games were slightly different, but both reached the same position after White's 23rd move. Bellon, who was only an IM then, possibly forgot the predecessor - thus Fischer's daring idea almost claimed an even more illustriuous victim, than Anthony Saidy.'} h4 36. Kg2 Rb2 37. Kf3 d5 38. gxh4 {Karpov, A (2660)-Bellon Lopez,J (2400)/Madrid 1973/MCL/1/2-1/2 (49)}) ({The text move is sensible prophylaxis, anticipating} 23... b6 24. axb6 Raxb6 25. Ra8+ Kh7 26. Bc1 $132 {[%cal Re1e8] and moving the  to a safer square in advance.}) 24. Red1 {Stohl,Igor: '?' Stohl,Igor: 'The tension is high, Black's pressure is very unpleasant and White cracks up.'} ({K. Mueller points out} 24. Ra2 $142 $5 Nb4 $5 (24... b6 25. Na4 Bxd3 (25... Rxd3 26. Qd1 $1) 26. Qd1 Bxf1 27. Rxf1 Rd3 28. axb6 (28. Nb2 Rc3 $1 $17) {} 28... cxb6 29. Nxc5 Rxg3+ $11 (29... Rxa2 $2 30. Nxd3 Qxd3 31. Qxh5+ $18)) 25. Bxb4 Rxb4 $15 {is nice for Black, but still far from decisive.}) ({Even} 24. Bg2 {is stronger, than the text-move.}) 24... b6 {Stohl,Igor: '!' Stohl,Igor: 'The postponed break is even more effective, as White now completely lacks counterplay and his g1 will quickly become the main target.'} 25. Be1 (25. axb6 Raxb6 {Stohl,Igor: 'doesn't help either, Black mostly even has a choice how to wrap up the game:'} 26. Ra2 (26. Rdb1 Nxd3 (26... Nb4 27. Bxb4 R6xb4 {} 28. Nd1 Rxd3 $1 $19) 27. Nxd3 Rxd3 $1 28. Rxb6 Rxg3+ 29. fxg3 Bxc2 30. Rxc6 Qxg3+ 31. Bg2 Be4 $19) (26. Na4 Nxa4 (26... Bxd3 27. Bxd3 Rxd3 $19) (26... Rxd3 $5 $19) 27. Rxa4 Rb2 28. Qc1 Rb1 29. Qxb1 Rxb1 30. Rxb1 Bxd3 $19 {} 31. Bxd3 Qxd3 32. Rb2 h4 $1) 26... Nxd3 $19 (26... h4 $5 27. g4 Bxg4 28. hxg4 Qxg4+ 29. Kh2 Rxb2 $19) 27. Nxd3 Bxd3 28. Bxd3 Rxd3 $19 {[%csl Rg3][%cal Rh5h4,Rb6b3,Rd3g3]}) ({Hopeless is} 25. Na4 Bxd3 $19 ({or the more energetic} 25... Rxd3 $1 26. Bxd3 Nxd3 $19)) 25... bxa5 $19 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '-+'' Stohl,Igor: 'Material is again equal, but the difference in piece coordination is enormous - the game is practically over. '} 26. Na4 Rxd3 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Maric,Rudolf: '!'' Stohl,Igor: 'The most energetic solution, other moves would give White chances to resist.'} 27. Bxd3 (27. Nxc5 $142 Rxd1 (27... Rxg3+ $2 28. fxg3 Bxc2 29. Nxa6 $132) 28. Qxd1 dxc5 $19 {could only prolong the game without changing the final result.}) 27... Bxd3 {Stohl,Igor: 'Natural enough, but according to the engine even stronger was'} (27... Nxd3 $142 $1 28. Qd2 Ncb4 $19 {}) 28. Qa2 Nb4 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' Stohl,Igor: 'For the time being Fischer spurns material.'} (28... Nxa4 29. Rxd3 (29. Qxa4 Bc2 30. Qb5 Rb6 $19) 29... Qxd3 30. Qxa4 g6 $5 $17 {/-+ also gives Black a large and probably winning advantage.}) 29. Qa3 {ChessCalisthenics: '?' '?''Maric,Rudolf: 'ChessCalisthenics: '?'''} ({Stohl,Igor: 'Somewhat more resilient was'} 29. Qb2 $142 $5 {Stohl,Igor: '!?' Stohl,Igor: ', but the endgame after'} Nxa4 (29... Ne6 $6) 30. Rxa4 $17 Bc2 31. Rxa5 Rxa5 32. Bxb4 Bxd1 33. Bxa5 c5 $19 {[%csl Rc4,Rg1] is untenable in the long run. White's  is vulnerable and after winning the c4 Black can combine an attack on the light squares with the advance of the passed pawns.}) 29... Nc2 30. Qb2 (30. Qa2 $142 $5 $19) ({There is no hope after} 30. Nxc5 Nxa3 31. Nxa6 Nc2 32. Rxa5 Qe4 $19) 30... Nxa1 31. Rxa1 (31. Nxc5 dxc5 32. Rxa1 Bxc4 $18) 31... Nxa4 {Stohl,Igor: 'The simple swap is good enough, but'} (31... Qf5 $19 {or}) (31... Qe4 $5 $19 {are roughly equivalent.}) 32. Rxa4 Qe4 33. Bxa5 $2 {Stohl,Igor: '?!'} ({Stohl,Igor: 'Saidy shortens his suffering, which would have been equally unbearable after'} 33. Qd2 {ChessCalisthenics: '-+' '-+''Maric,Rudolf: 'ChessCalisthenics: '-+'''} Rb6 34. Ra1 Bxc4 $19) 33... Rxa5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Simple tactics net Black a piece. '} 34. Rxa5 Qe1+ 35. Kh2 Qxa5 {Stohl,Igor: 'Of course, the evaluation of the position is unequivocal:'} 36. Qxd4 {Stohl,Igor: 'As I have already mentioned, there are two versions of our game and it's not clear, if Saidy resigned immediately, or only after taking on d4.'} Qf5 $19 {Stohl,Igor: '-+!?'} (36... Qd2 $19) (36... Bg6 $19 {An impressive game by Fischer. He used an enterprising, albeit risky pawn-sacrifice to put his opponent under pressure. Mainly due to Saidy's passive play he tightened the screws with a skillful blend of active (10...d4!, 11...exd4!, 16...d7!, 18...a6!, 22...a6!) and prophylactic measures (12...h5, 13...a5, 23...h7!), culminating in the decisive breakthrough 24...b6 and 26...xd3!. And make no mistake, the pressure was strong indeed: Saidy managed to enter Black's half of the board only 4 times and it was always only via the otherwise insignificant a5-square.}) 0-1

