1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Ndb5 Bb4 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. Nxc3 d5 9. e3 O-O 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Be2 Bf5 12. Nb5 Qb6 13. O-O a6 14. Nd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. exd4 Rac8 17. Bd1 Bc2 18. Be3 {Saidy must have calculated that he could trade off all the rooks to reach the bishop versus knight ending, assuming it to be a draw (it probably is too). But if he had wanted to 'hang tough' keeping the two bishops then he could have tried:} (18. Be2 $5 Bb3 19. Re1 $5 (19. Be3 Bc4 $1 $15 {with a similar ending to the game.} (19... Rc2 20. Bd3 (20. Bd1 Rxb2 21. Bc1 Ra2) 20... Rxb2 21. Rab1 Rxb1 22. Rxb1 Bc4 23. Bxc4 dxc4 24. Rxb7 $11)) 19... Rfe8 20. Be3 Rc2 21. Bd3 Rxb2 22. Rab1 Rxb1 23. Rxb1 Bc4 24. Bxc4 dxc4 25. Rxb7 Nd5 $15) 18... Bxd1 19. Rfxd1 Rc2 20. Rd2 Rfc8 21. Rxc2 Rxc2 22. Rc1 Rxc1+ 23. Bxc1 {Okay, this is the position we are really interested in. First Fischer brings his knight to e6, attacking the d-pawn and at the same time extending some influence over the kingside.  White wanders the king over to the middle to protect the d-pawn, but I wonder if it wasn't better to prevent Black's pawn advance on the kingside...?} Nd7 24. Kf1 (24. g4 $5 {Compare with the next game Karpov-Kasparov. White covers potential entry points for Black's king on the kingside. It seems logical to place the pawns on light squares, complementing the dark-squared bishop.} {Instead, let's first tie one of White's pieces to protecting the d-pawn:} Nf8 (24... f5 $5 25. f3 (25. gxf5 $6 Kf7 {and ...Kf6xf5.}) 25... Kf7 (25... fxg4 {A premature attempt to create avenues for Black's king.} 26. fxg4 Nf6 27. h3 (27. g5 $2 Ne4 {followed by Black king in on the light squares.}) 27... h5 28. gxh5 Nxh5 29. Kg2 Kf7 30. Kf3 {White should hold the position without too much difficulty. Black's king cannot make progress on the kingside.}) 26. Kf2 Ke6 27. Kg3 {I don't see Black making progress here. There is no route for the king into the position.}) 25. f3 Ne6 26. Be3 g6 27. Kg2 f5 28. Kg3 Kf7 {and now, should White capture on f5, or allow Black to play ...g5? I suspect both are good enough to draw.} 29. gxf5 (29. Bf2) (29. Kg2 {and in both cases just wait.}) 29... gxf5 {Compare with the game. With that pawn stoutly sitting on f3 covering entry squares, I don't see how Black makes progress if White just waits. For example:} 30. Kh4) 24... Nf8 25. Ke2 Ne6 26. Kd3 h5 $1 {Taking some squares on the kingside.} 27. Be3 Kh7 28. f3 Kg6 29. a4 Kf5 30. Ke2 g5 31. Kf2 Nd8 32. Bd2 Kg6 33. Ke3 Ne6 34. Kd3 Kf5 35. Be3 f6 36. Ke2 Kg6 37. Kd3 f5 38. Ke2 f4 39. Bf2 Ng7 40. h3 Nf5 ({If the analysis later on from 'American Chess Quarterly' is correct (I don't know who originally gave this, I am quoting from 'The Games of Robert J. Fischer' , Batsford 1972) then it is possible that it is better to play:} 40... Ne6 41. Bg1 Kf5 42. Bf2 g4 43. hxg4+ hxg4 44. Kd3 Kg5 {followed by ...Ng7-f5, with similar play to the game continuation.}) 41. Kd3 g4 42. hxg4 hxg4 43. fxg4 Nh6 44. Be1 ({Black has made great progress, but even at this late stage, according to American Chess Quarterly White could still have drawn with} 44. Ke2 Nxg4 45. Bg1 Kf5 46. Kf3 Nf6 ({Messing about with the pawns on the other side doesn't appear to help Black:} 46... a5 47. g3 fxg3 48. Kxg3 $11) (46... b5 47. axb5 axb5 48. g3 fxg3 49. Kxg3) 47. Bh2 Nh5 48. a5 Kg5 49. g4 fxg3 50. Bxg3 $11) 44... Nxg4 {Now it is over as Black's king enters.} 45. Bd2 Kf5 46. Be1 Nf6 47. Bh4 Ne4 48. Be1 Kg4 49. Ke2 Ng3+ 50. Kd3 Nf5 51. Bf2 Nh4 52. a5 Nxg2 53. Kc3 Kf3 54. Bg1 Ke2 55. Bh2 f3 56. Bg3 Ne3 {Conclusion: White's decision to enter the bishop versus knight ending was correct. Preventing Black creating space on the kingside, and so blocking out the king, would have held the game.} 0-1

