1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 ({Black can meet} 2. e4 {with} c5 {, which reaches a Gurgenidze System against the Maroczy Bind via} 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nf6 6. Nc3 d6 {.}) ({Meanwhile there's a transposition into a Grünfeld after} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 d5 {.}) 2... Bg7 {Nigel Davies: 'QUESTION: How would Black have reached a Grünfeld Defence had White now played 3 d4 as after 3...Nf6 White has 4 e4? --- ANSWER: That is an excellent question and the answer is that he can't.'} ({Fischer played 2...Bg7 because he was happy to enter a King's Indian Defence with 3 d4 Nf6 4 e4 d6, Grünfeld players should play} 2... c5 $1 {preventing this.}) 3. g3 (3. d4 Nf6 4. e4 {would have taken us into a King's Indian Defence, which Fischer specialized in throughout much of his career.}) 3... c5 4. Bg2 Nc6 {Nigel Davies: 'We have now reached a Symmetrical English, an opening known to give rise to complex middlegames but not pose Black any particular opening problems. Smyslov's next move is also known to be one of White's least dangerous options, but it does throw the players on their own resources, for what that's worth against Fischer.'} 5. b3 e6 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: 'It is Black who is now playing for the centre, aiming for ...Nge7 and ...d7-d5.'} 6. Bb2 Nge7 7. Na4 (7. Nf3 {Nigel Davies: 'would have transposed into an earlier game between the same too players, V.Smyslov-R.Fischer, Buenos Aires 1970. After'} O-O 8. Na4 e5 $1 9. O-O (9. Nxc5 $2 e4 $1 10. Bxg7 exf3 {wins material}) 9... d6 10. e3 f5 11. d3 h6 12. Ne1 f4 {Black had excellent play, though on that occasion the game ended in a draw.}) 7... Bxb2 8. Nxb2 O-O 9. e3 {Nigel Davies: 'Still being rather leisurely with his development.'} (9. h4) ({White should probably play} 9. Nf3 {, but then} e5 {starts to put pawns on dark squares to complement his light-squared bishop.}) 9... d5 10. cxd5 (10. Ne2 d4 {would take space and leave White in a kind of reversed Benoni.}) 10... Nxd5 11. Ne2 b6 $1 12. d4 ({White sees that after} 12. O-O Ba6 {he would not be able to fee his game with d2-d4, but this is playable for him if he then plays} 13. d3 {.}) 12... Ba6 {Nigel Davies: '!'} 13. dxc5 ({It's understandable that White plays this way because} 13. Nc4 {is well met by} b5 {. But now Fischer seizes the initiative and never lets it go.}) 13... Qf6 $1 14. Nc4 Nc3 $1 $17 {Nigel Davies: '!'} 15. Nxc3 (15. Qc1 Nxe2 16. Kxe2 Rac8 17. cxb6 Ne5 $19) ({White also loses castling rights after} 15. Qc2 Nxe2 16. Kxe2 {and then} Nb4 $1 {is very strong.}) 15... Qxc3+ 16. Kf1 Rfd8 17. Qc1 Bxc4+ 18. bxc4 Qd3+ 19. Kg1 Rac8 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: 'Losing no time to accentuate his lead in development. The sacrificed pawn will soon be recovered.'} 20. cxb6 axb6 21. Qb2 Na5 22. h4 $4 (22. Bf1 Nxc4 23. Qf6 $15) 22... Nxc4 23. Qf6 {Nigel Davies: 'White is hoping to get counterplay on the kingside with h4-h5, but Fischer's next move snuffs that danger out and leaves Black with an advantageous endgame.'} Qf5 $1 24. Qxf5 gxf5 25. h5 (25. Kh2 {is strongly met by} Rd2 {, so White tries to activate his rook along the fourth rank.}) 25... Rd2 26. Rc1 ({White should have kept to his original plan with} 26. Rh4 {. The pin on the c-file proves to be unimportant.}) 26... Rc5 27. Rh4 Ne5 28. Rxc5 {Nigel Davies: '?!' Nigel Davies: 'Making matters worse.'} ({White should have played} 28. Rf1 {though} Rxa2 {leaves him a pawn down.}) 28... bxc5 29. Ra4 c4 30. h6 Kf8 31. Ra8+ Ke7 32. Rc8 {Nigel Davies: 'Placing the rook behind Black's passed pawn, which is the recommended course of action in the endgame. However, Black is just a pawn up here with very active pieces.'} Rxa2 $19 33. Bf1 Rc2 34. Kg2 $2 Ng4 35. Kg1 (35. Bxc4 Nxe3+ {wins the bishop.}) 35... Rxf2 36. Bxc4 Rf3 (36... Kf6 $1) 37. Kg2 Rxe3 38. Rh8 Nxh6 39. Rxh7 Ng4 40. Bb5 Rb3 41. Bc6 Rb2+ 42. Kg1 Ne5 43. Ba8 Rb8 44. Bh1 0-1

