k wins easily after the immediate} 38. Rf4 {, by replying} Rh7 {, as Black's king can then again safely run to the queenside, via e8, d7 and c6. Suddenly, after the text-move, Black can no longer easily run his king away from the vulnerable kingside. --- Black can now rush his a-pawn towards the queening square, but White will be able to cover a1 adequately with his bishop, by clearing the long dark-square diagonal with the desperado pawn sacrifice c4.}) 38... a3 39. Rf4 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?''} ({Even now} 39. Bxh8 Rxh8 40. Rfe1 {is correct.}) 39... a2 40. c4 {[%cal Rf6a1] ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Purdy' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): Should Black capture on c4 with bishop or pawn? --- ANSWER: Only capturing with the bishop holds on to the win.''} (40. Ra1) 40... Bxc4 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The correct capture.''} ({After} 40... bxc4 $2 41. d7 c3 42. Bxc3 Rhd8 43. Rh4 Ke7 {(forced)} 44. Bf6+ Kxd7 45. c4 Re8 46. cxd5 e5 47. Ra1 Kd6 48. Rc4 Ra3 49. Rc2 Rea8 50. Re2 e4 51. h4 {Stockfish calls it dead even.}) 41. d7 Bd5 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Putting the bishop back on d5 means White has effectively gained a tempo (d6-d7) at the meagre cost of a doubled c-pawn.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Putting the bishop back on d5 means White has effectively gained a tempo (d6-d7) at the meagre cost of a doubled c-pawn. Craig Pritchett: '?!' Craig Pritchett: 'Fischer apparently played this move fairly quickly to force White to make a (difficult) sealed move.'''} ({Instead, Black can win a crucial tempo himself by playing Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Instead, Black can win a crucial tempo himself by playing Craig Pritchett: 'However, Kasparov indicates that he probably let the win slip (in strictly objective terms!) by not playing the remarkable pawn sacrifice''} 41... e5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: ', and if''} 42. Bxe5 ({or} 42. Bxh8 Rxh8 43. Rh4 Rd8 44. Rh7+ Ke6 45.