49. Ke1 Rg1+ 50. Kd2 Rf2+ 51. Ke3 Rf3+ 52. Ke2 Rg2+ 53. Ke1 $10 {Purdy Reeh: '='}) 44. Rh4 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threatening perpetual check.''} e5 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's king is given necessary luft.''} (44... a1=Q $2 {allows the perpetual with} 45. Rh7+ Kg8 46. Rh8+ {.}) 45. Rh7+ Ke6 46. Re7+ Kd6 47. Rxe5 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black has only one path toward winning chances. Our choices: a) Promote immediately with 47...a1Q. --- b) Pick off the d7-pawn with 47 ...Kxd7. --- c) Play 47...Rxc3+, creating three connected queenside passers. --- EXERCISE (critical decision): Only one of the moves gives Black a prayer to win. Which one? --- ANSWER: Only line c) offers Black winning chances.''} Rxc3+ {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} ({Instead: a)} 47... a1=Q $4 {loses to} 48. Rexd5+ Kc6 49. Rd6+ Kb7 50. Rxa1 Rxa1 51. d8=Q {.}) ({b)} 47... Kxd7 $2 48. Rdxd5+ Kc7 49. Rxc5+ Kb6 50. Rxb5+ Ka6 {doesn't succeed either because} 51. Rb2 $1 a1=Q 52. Re6+ Ka5 53. Re5+ Ka6 (53... Ka4 $4 {is helpmate after} 54. Rb4#) 54. Re6+ {is perpetual check.}) 48. Kf2 ({Not} 48. Kf4 $4 Rf3# {.}) 48... Rc2+ 49. Ke1 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky defends perfectly for the moment, finding only moves.''} ({Not} 49. Ke3 $4 Ra3+ 50. Rd3 f4+ $1 51. Kxf4 Rf2+ 52. Ke3 Rxd3+ 53. Kxd3 Kxd7 54. Rxd5+ Kc6 55. Rd8 Rxf6 $1 {(removal of the guard) and Black wins.}) 49... Kxd7 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 50. Rexd5+ Kc6 51. Rd6+ Kb7 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '''} 52. Rd7+ {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: 'So far, we must still be in both players' (certainly Fischer's) overnight analysis.'''} ({Reeh: 'Note that White loses after'} 52. Re6 {Reeh: '?'} Rc8 (52... Ra3 53. Rd7+ Kc8 54. Rd8+ Kc7 $1 {(Kasparov).})) 52... Ka6 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Now there is no perpetual check and Black's three pawns are more valuable than White's extra bishop.''} 53. R7d2 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's best chance.''} Rxd2 54. Kxd2 b4 {Damit endet die mit dem 43. schwarzen Zug eingeleitete Abwicklung. Trotz seiner Mehrfigur steht der Anziehende mit dem Rücken an der Wand. Verhält er sich jetzt passiv, wird er von den drei verbundenen Bauern am Damenflügel langsam aber sicher erdrückt. Spassky sucht daher seine einzige Gegenchance und verschafft sich seinerseits einen Freibauern.} 55. h4 $1 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky reminds his opponent that Black isn't the only one threatening to promote. I allowed Stockfish to run to a luxurious depth 59 and it concluded that the position is a dead even assessment of 0.00, which means that Spassky's position came back from the dead.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky reminds his opponent that Black isn't the only one threatening to promote. I allowed Stockfish to run to a luxurious depth 59 and it concluded that the position is a dead even assessment of 0.00, which means that Spassky's position came back from the dead. Craig Pritchett: 'Black's avalanche of queenside pawns is distinctly menacing, but White has his own last trump on the kingside. By playing h4-h5, he creates a dangerous passed pawn and obtains good drawing chances.'''} (55. Kc2 $2 Kb5 56. Kb2 c4 57. Ka1 c3 $19) 55... Kb5 56. h5 c4 {[%cal Rc4c3] ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Gligoric,Svetozar: '[ c3]'' Droht 37...c3+.} 57. Ra1 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} ({Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not, of course,''} 57. h6 $2 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??''} c3+ 58. Kd3 a1=Q {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: ', and Black wins.''} 59. Rxa1 Rxa1 60. h7 Rd1+ $1 61. Kc2 Rh1 62. h8=Q Rxh8 63. Bxh8 Kc4 $19 {Reeh: 'Gligoric'}) ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Evading the trap'} 57. hxg6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??'} c3+ 58. Bxc3 (58. Kc2 a1=Q 59. Rxa1 Rxa1 60. g7 Ra8 {and Black wins, since neither g-pawn is going anywhere.}) 58... Rd8+ $19 {Matanovic}) 57... gxh5 {[%cal Rg5g8]} 58. g6 h4 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It's Black's armada of passed pawns versus White's bishop and g-pawn. The game is still even.''} (58... Ra3 59. g7 Rg3 60. Rxa2 c3+ 61. Ke2 b3 62. Ra8 $11 {Reeh: 'Gligoric'}) (58... c3+ 59. Kd3 $11 {Matanovic}) 59. g7 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: '!' Craig Pritchett: 'This is again correct.''' Auf 59. Lxh4 folgt Tg8 nebst 60...Txg6.} ({Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not Craig Pritchett: 'Black's three pawns should eventually triumph over the bishop after'''} 59. Bxh4 {ChessBase Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: '?'''} b3 (59... Rg8 60. Rxa2 ({or} 60. Rg1 c3+ 61. Kd3 Rxg6 $1) 60... Rxg6 {and Black's pawns are too much for White to deal with.}) 60. Kc3 Rg8 $19 {Purdy}) 59... h3 60. Be7 {[%cal Re7f8] Reeh: '!' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Bf8, followed by g7-g8Q.''} Rg8 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: '!' Craig Pritchett: 'We don't know whether this remarkable, self-stalemating rook move, was also part of Fischer's overnight analysis. No matter its precise provenance, however, it remains a brilliant idea. Black's rook will be completely tied down, but it will also, crucially, take White's bishop and pawn out of play, setting up a tense and highly unusual battle between White's rook and Black's five, far-advanced passed pawns. --- According to Botvinnik, Fischer's "paradoxical solution" to his technical problems at this point was his "highest creative achievement ... Nothing similar had previously occurred in chess. Spassky was astounded and soon lost ... Smyslov found a draw for White, but would he have found it at the board, sitting opposite Fischer?"'''} 61. Bf8 {[%cal Rf8a3] Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If you don't have the money to buy, then rent. White can't win Black's rook outright, but he can place it in a state eternal paralysis.'' Damit hat Weiß den gegnerischen Turm vollständig vom Geschehen ausgeschlossen, doch müssen sich sein König und Turm einer Lawine von mittlerweile fünf (!) schwarzen Freibauern erwehren. Normalerweise eine hoffnungslose Aufgabe, hier aber beherrscht der Läufer die wichtige Diagonale a3-f8, und dieser Umstand sollte einen schwarzen Sieg unmöglich machen.} (61. Rxa2 $4 {ChessBase} Rxg7 $19) (61. Bf6 h2 62. Kc2 Re8 $1 63. Rh1 ({or} 63. Kd2 f4) 63... Re2+ 64. Kd1 Rg2 65. Rxh2 Rg1+ 66. Kc2 c3 {.}) 61... h2 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If Fischer's pawns are an athlete's body, then his rook represents the love handles. I am no longer capable of distinguishing between good and evil. How on earth is a human supposed to assess such a position? We note that deprivation is both sides' inheritance: 1. Black is playing a rook down, since White's bishop and g-pawn are its jailers. --- 2. Black still has five passed pawns and king versus the white rook and king. --- The assessment remains at dead even.''} ({White can defend after} 61... c3+ 62. Kd3 h2 63. Rf1 f4 64. Rd1 $1 f3 (64... Ka4 65. Kc2 $1) 65. Kd4 f2 66. Kd3 Kc6 67. Kc2 $1 {, as his king and rook have been able to position themselves so that Black's king can't cross the d-file, via d5 and e4, or force through his queenside pawns. Here Black can try the sacrificial} a1=Q 68. Rxa1 Kd5 69. Kd3 c2 70. Kxc2 Ke4 {(so far analysis by endgame composer Robert Burger), but after the continuation} 71. Rf1 $1 Kf3 72. Bc5 Rxg7 73. Rxf2+ Kg3 74. Rxh2 {, White holds (Soltis).}) 62. Kc2 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky transfers his king to b2 to activate his own rook.''} Kc6 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer's near perfect intuition seizes upon his only chance to extract a full point: Transfer his king to the centre and kingside.'' Der schwarze König muß seine Getreuen am Königsflügel unterstützen; die drei Verbundenen am Damenflügel sind sowieso unantastbar.} 63. Rd1 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: '!'} ({The more accurate path to the draw lies in the following line:} 63. Rh1 $1 Kd5 64. Kb2 b3 65. Rxh2 c3+ 66. Ka1 Ke4 67. Rh3 c2 68. Rh1 Ke3 69. Kb2 f4 70. Kxb3 f3 71. Kxc2 f2 72. Kb2 Ke2 73. Kxa2 f1=Q 74. Rxf1 Kxf1 {and draws.}) 63... b3+ 64. Kc3 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '?'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: 'This isn't a good sign.''' Besser war 64.Kb2, um dem Turm das Verlassen der ersten Reihe zu ermöglichen.} ({Reeh: 'Spassky could have immediately secured the half-point by playing'} 64. Kb2 {Reeh: '!' Reeh: ', and if'} f4 ({Reeh: 'or'} 64... h1=Q 65. Rxh1 Kd5 66. Rd1+ Ke4 67. Re1+ (67. Rc1 Kd3) 67... Kd3 68. Rd1+ Ke2 69. Rc1 $11 f4) 65. Rd6+ Kc7 66. Rd1 $1 f3 67. Kc3 f2 68. Kb2 $1 $11 {Reeh: ', reaching a position where Black's king can never reach the d-file or the b4/a3 squares, and White defends, as pointed out by Gligoric. --- Although White hasn't yet jeopardized the draw, Spassky's move allows Black to activate his king by sacrificing his h-pawn, so that he can still press hard for the full point.'}) 64... h1=Q $1 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Decoy. This is Black's best practical chance. Fischer gives up a pawn to allow his king to cross over to the kingside.'' Ohne dieses Opfer kann der schwarze König die d-Linie nicht überschreiten.} (64... f4 {ChessBase} 65. Rd6+ Kc7 66. Rd1 {Reeh: ''Flohr''} f3 67. Kb2 $10 {Purdy Reeh: '='}) 65. Rxh1 Kd5 66. Kb2 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' '!?''} ({Simpler is} 66. Re1 $1 f4 67. Rd1+ Ke4 68. Re1+ Kf3 69. Ra1 $1 {and Black can't make progress. If} Ke2 {White can play} 70. Kxc4 $1 f3 71. Kxb3 f2 72. Rxa2+ {and draws.}) 66... f4 67. Rd1+ {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: '!'''} (67. Kc3 Ke4 $19 {Matanovic}) ({White still holds, but must avoid} 67. Rh8 $4 c3+ 68. Ka1 f3 69. Rxg8 f2 {, and Black mates.}) 67... Ke4 68. Rc1 Kd3 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (critical decision): Spassky's two candidate moves are 69 Rd1+ or 69 Rc3+. One draws, the other loses. Which is your pick? --- ANSWER: Only the check on c3 draws.''} 69. Rd1+ $2 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '??'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I'm sorry to inform you that this is not a teachable moment. This blunder, which waves bye bye to the draw, comes from a mix of the unimaginable strain of playing for the world championship, mixed with fatigue.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I'm sorry to inform you that this is not a teachable moment. This blunder, which waves bye bye to the draw, comes from a mix of the unimaginable strain of playing for the world championship, mixed with fatigue. Craig Pritchett: '?' Craig Pritchett: 'The final mistake - White loses a critical tempo. Kasparov called Spassky's move "a tragic oversight."'''} ({Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The lateral rook check saves the game: Craig Pritchett: 'Smyslov's draw could have been secured with''' In Zeitnot begeht Spassky den entscheidenden Fehler. Remis halten ließ sich mit} 69. Rc3+ {ChessBase: '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} Kd4 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'repeats''} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or Craig Pritchett: 'or if''} 69... Ke2 70. Rxc4 f3 71. Rc1 $11 {Matanovic} f2 72. Kxb3 f1=Q 73. Rxf1 Kxf1 74. Kxa2) 70. Rf3 c3+ (70... Ke4 {ChessBase} 71. Rc3 f3 72. Rxc4+ Ke3 73. Rc1 { 74.b3 = Purdy}) 71. Ka1 ({not} 71. Rxc3 $4 a1=Q+ 72. Kxa1 Kxc3 {and wins}) 71... c2 72. Rxf4+ Kc3 (72... Kd3 {ChessBase} 73. Rf1 { 74.b2 +- Purdy}) {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Craig Pritchett: 'White can also draw in this line after the alternative'''} 73. Rf3+ (73. Bb4+ Kd3 74. Rf1 Rxg7 75. Kb2 $11 {Smyslov Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 73... Kd2 74. Ba3 $1 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '=!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Gligoric) Craig Pritchett: ', and if now'''} Rxg7 {ChessBase} 75. Rxb3 Rc7 (75... Ra7 76. Rh3 $5 ({or just} 76. Bb2) 76... Rxa3 77. Rh2+ Kd3 78. Rxc2 $1 Kxc2 {with stalemate.}) 76. Bb2 $10 {Reeh: '=' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '- Gligoric and Timman''} (76. Kxa2 $4 Ra7 $1 $19)) 69... Ke2 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '-+!'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 70. Rc1 f3 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 71. Bc5 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '"Who cares about this useless appendage?" says the French nobleman, as he begins his walk to the guillotine. It must have been incredibly painful for Spassky to let his once powerhouse g-pawn go.'' Nun kommt das Eingreifen dieses Läufers zu spät.} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Taking the c-pawn loses as well:'} 71. Rxc4 f2 $19 72. Re4+ Kf3 73. Re7 ({or} 73. Rb4 a1=Q+ $1 74. Kxa1 f1=Q+) 73... f1=Q 74. Rf7+ Kg2 75. Rxf1 Kxf1 {, when the kingside pieces cancel each other out so the game is in reality a king and pawn ending, with Black two pawns up) Cyrus Lakdawala: ', when the kingside pieces cancel each other out so the game is in reality a king and pawn ending, with Black two pawns up) Craig Pritchett: ', and Black wins.''} 76. Ka1 Ke2 77. Kb2 Kd3 78. Ka1 Kd2 ({here} 78... Kc2 $1 {with zugzwang is faster}) 79. Kb2 a1=Q+ 80. Kxa1 Kc3 81. Kb1 b2 82. Ka2 Kc2 {and wins.}) 71... Rxg7 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Who let him in here?''} 72. Rxc4 Rd7 {ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Purdy' Reeh: ''!' 'Purdy' Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} ({The simple} 72... f2 $1 {also wins:} 73. Bxf2 Kxf2 74. Rb4 Rg1 75. Ra4 Rb1+ {gets the job done.}) 73. Re4+ ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Or if'} 73. Rc1 Rd1 $19 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) ({If White passes with} 73. Bb6 {then} Rd2+ 74. Ka1 Rd1+ {ends the game.}) 73... Kf1 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!''} 74. Bd4 (74. Rf4 Rd2+ 75. Ka1 Rd1+ 76. Kb2 a1=Q+) {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Spassky understands that playing on would be a profitless exercise:''} 74... f2 {ChessBase: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '[ d4]'' Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' '!'' und Spassky gab auf. Es droht einfach 74... Txd4 75. Txd4 Ke2. Weiß muß seinen Turm gegen den f-Bauern geben, und wie Schwarz anschließend mit den Bauern a2+b3 gewinnt, dürfte ja bekannt sein, nicht wahr?!  Diese bittere Niederlage nach heroischer Verteidigung brach den Widerstand des Titelverteidigers endgültig. Bobby Fischer führte nun mit drei Punkten Vorsprung, ließ in den Folgebegegnungen nichts mehr anbrennen und wurde nach dem Sieg in der 21. Partie mit einem 12,5 : 8,5 Erfolg über Boris Spassky elfter Weltmeister der Schachgeschichte.} 75. Rf4 {ChessBase} ({or} 75. Bxf2 Rd1 $1 76. Ra4 Rb1+ 77. Kc3 Kxf2) ({or if} 75. Be5 Rd1 76. Kxb3 Re1) 75... Rxd4 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} 76. Rxd4 Ke2 77. Re4+ (77. Rf4 f1=Q) 77... Kf3 78. Re8 f1=Q 79. Rf8+ Ke2 80. Rxf1 Kxf1 {Reeh: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins in the way we we've already seen above.''} 81. Ka1 Ke2 82. Kb2 Kd3 83. Ka1 Kd2 84. Kb2 a1=Q+ 85. Kxa1 Kc3 86. Kb1 b2 87. Ka2 Kc2 $19 {Purdy 0-1 (87) Spassky,B (2660)-Fischer,R (2785) Reykjavik 1972 100 Jahre Schach [Reeh,Oliver]} 0-1

