{Marin,Mihail: 'In the early '60s, Fischer had publicly challenged the World Champion Botvinnik to a World title match in which he, Fischer, would give him the odds of two points. Of course, nothing came out of it and Fischer needed another decade before working his way up to the supreme match, but in Reykjavik the 0-2 start effectively took place. And I cannot believe it was a coincidence, as in the first game Fischer more or less committed suicide in a dead drawn ending while he did not show up for the second one. The third game, however, was an entirely different business and Fischer started playing at full strength. '} 1. d4 {Marin,Mihail Marin,Mihail: 'Marin Spassky opened with this move in four out of his first five games with White (meaning until game nine). This policy must have been influenced by the fact that prior to the match he had twice defeated Fischer in the Grünfeld, but the challenger never repeated this opening during the match. After his first and only "genuine" win in Reykjavik against the Najdorf poisoned pawn in game 11, Spassky never returned to 1.d4 until the end of the match.'} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 ({Deviating from game one in which he played} 3. Nc3 {Marin,Mihail}) 3... c5 {Marin,Mihail: '!?' Marin,Mihail: 'There is a small paradox regarding Fischer's repertoire before Reykjavik. He was known for his stable but narrow opening choice, almost exclusively 1.e4 with White and the Najdorf Sicilian against 1.e4. But against 1.d4 he used several fianchetto openings, the King's Indian, the Grüfeld and the Benoni and starting with the late '60s the Nimzo-Indian. The Benoni is by no means more solid than the Grünfeld, but Fischer must have known well that Spassky would not try something agressive from the early phase.'} 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nd2 { c4 Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Marin,Mihail: ' c4'} (7. e4) 7... Nbd7 8. e4 (8. Nc4 Nb6 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '=!'}) 8... Bg7 9. Be2 (9. Bd3 $143 {Schwarz droht gelegentlich: c4, e5 Marin,Mihail: 'And from time to time Black threatens: c4, e5' Marin,Mihail: 'Schwarz droht gelegentlich: c4, e5'}) (9. Bc4 $143 {Schwarz droht gelegentlich: b5 Marin,Mihail: 'And from time to time Black threatens: b5' Marin,Mihail: 'Schwarz droht gelegentlich: b5'}) 9... O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 {Gligoric,Svetozar: 'N!?' Marin,Mihail: 'In those years, this used to be the main line which does make some sense as it is a developing move preparing b2-b3 and b2. But with the passing of the years White started feeling that weakening the control along the d1-h5 diagonal may be premature.'} ({Marin,Mihail: 'This is why'} 11. a4 {Marin,Mihail: 'eventually became the main line, with the typical continuation'} Ne5 {Marin,Mihail} 12. Ra3 g5) (11. f3 {Petrosjan Marin,Mihail: 'Petrosian Petrosjan'}) {Marin,Mihail: 'Since the program does not allow inserting two game quotes on the same move, here is the link to the game mentioned by Larsen:'} 11... Nh5 $5 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' ?! Marin,Mihail: 'A provocative but entirely sound novelty, taking advantage of the weakened pressure along the light diagonal. Larsen does not agree that this move is new, mentioning a game Timman-Ljubojevic (see the link below). But actually that game only featured a similar idea, while the position was different. And if we talk about similarities, there is an even older example:'} ({Two years later, Mecking, who never accepted that he was weaker than Fischer, experimented with what later became Black's main plan.} 11... Ne5 {Marin,Mihail} 12. b3 {Developing the bishop to b2 offers Black free hands on the kingside.} (12. a4 $5) 12... g5 13. Bb2 g4 14. Rfe1 Nh5 15. Nd1 Nf4 16. Bb5 Rf8 17. Ne3 Qg5 18. Nf5 Bxf5 19. exf5 {Kortschnoj,V-Mecking,H Augusta 1974 (1-0, 42)} Nf3+ $1 20. Nxf3 gxf3 21. g3 Bxb2 22. Qxb2 Qxf5 $15) ({If I am not wrong, the moment when 11.c2 ceased to be the main line was when Mecking played the following game:} 11... Nb6 $5 {Marin,Mihail An interesting idea by Bilek who had played it three times in the '60s. But of course, Mecking's game's impact was stronger.} 12. Bb5 (12. a4 Nfxd5 $1 13. exd5 Bxc3 14. Bb5 Bxd2 15. Bxe8 Bxc1 16. Bxf7+ Kxf7 17. Qxc1 {Kapu,J-Bilek,I Budapest 1964 (1-0, 31)} Qf6) (12. Re1 {allows} Ng4 {taking full advantage of c2 and ...b6. A much later game revealed Black's idea:} 13. h3 $6 Nxf2 14. Kxf2 Qh4+ 15. Kf1 Bd4 16. Nd1 Qxh3 $1 {0-1 Gurgenidze,B-Tal,M URS-ch24 Final 1957 (18) Coelho,L (2041)-Rocha,W (2361) Brasilia 1999 (0-1, 27)  I am not sure whether Black started the whole attack spontaneously, or was inspired by}) 12... Bd7 13. a4 Bxb5 14. Nxb5 a6 15. Nc3 Nfd7 16. a5 Nc8 17. b3 Na7 18. Bb2 Nb5 19. Nxb5 Bxb2 20. Qxb2 axb5 21. Qc3 b4 22. Qg3 Nf6 $15 {Polugaevsky,L (2620)-Mecking,H (2635) Luzern 1977 (½-½, 42)}) 12. Bxh5 {Marin,Mihail: 'Of course the only reasonable answer. Otherwise the knight would display annoying activity.'} (12. g3 Ne5 13. f4 Ng4 14. Nc4 {×  Marin,Mihail: '× '} Bxc3 { b5 Byrne Marin,Mihail: ' b5 Byrne  b5 Byrne'}) (12. f4 Bd4+ 13. Kh1 Ndf6 { 14... g4 Purdy Marin,Mihail: ' 14... g4 Purdy  14... g4 Purdy'}) (12. Rd1 Nf4 13. Bf1 Ne5 14. Nc4 g5 $1 {Smyslov}) 12... gxh5 13. Nc4 {Marin,Mihail: 'Also principled.'} (13. a4 Ne5 14. Nd1 $1 {Marin,Mihail: 'is a bit too slow.'} Qh4 (14... f5 $1 {(Marin,Mihail) Marin} 15. exf5 Rf8 16. Ne3 Ng4 17. Ra3 (17. Ndc4 {Kverndal,L-Wibe,T Norway 1990 (0-1, 36)} Qf6) 17... Qh4 18. h3 (18. Nf3 Qf6) 18... Nxe3 19. Rxe3 (19. fxe3 $6 Bxf5 $1 20. Rxf5 $2 Qe1+ 21. Nf1 Rxf5 22. Qxf5 Rf8 $19) 19... Bxf5 {with comfortable play.}) 15. Ne3 Ng4 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Nc4 Qf6 $2 {Nunn} (17... g3 18. fxg3 Qxe4 19. Qxe4 Rxe4 20. Nxd6 Re5 21. Nxf7 $16 {Nunn}) 18. Bd2 Qg6 19. Bc3 Bxc3 20. bxc3 b6 (20... f5 $142 21. f3 Bd7 $13 {Byrne}) 21. Rfe1 Ba6 22. Nd2 Re5 23. f4 $1 $18 {Gligoric-Kavalek/Skopje ol/1972/}) (13. Nd1 Ne5) (13. b3 Ne5 14. Bb2 Bd7 15. Rae1 $14) 13... Ne5 { 14.e5 e5 Purdy Marin,Mihail: ' 14.e5 e5 Purdy  14.e5 e5 Purdy'} 14. Ne3 ({Marin,Mihail: 'In principle, allowing Black to keep his central knight does not look right. Instinctively I felt that'} 14. Nxe5 {Marin,Mihail: 'was the principled continuation, but actually Black's bishops become very active.'} Bxe5 15. Be3 {Byrne Marin,Mihail: '(Byrne)'} (15. f4 {Marin,Mihail} Bd4+ 16. Kh1 f5 (16... Bd7 $13) 17. e5 dxe5 18. fxe5 Bxe5 19. Bf4 {''} Bxf4 20. Rxf4 Qd6 21. Rf3 Bd7 22. Raf1 Re5 {ae8 with normal play for Black.}) 15... Qh4 $5 {Marin,Mihail Making full use of the bishop's strength before exchanging it on c3.} (15... f5 {Marin,Mihail may still be okay for Black but it is a bit irritating that after} 16. f4 Bxc3 17. Qxc3 Rxe4 {White has} 18. Rf3) 16. f4 (16. h3 Bxh3 $1 17. f4 (17. gxh3 Qxh3 18. f3 Qxf3 $19) 17... Bxc3 18. Qxc3 (18. bxc3 Bf5 19. Bf2 Bxe4 20. Qxe4) 18... Qg3 19. Qd2 Qg7 $11) (16. g3 {Deprives White of a later f3-g3.} Qe7 17. Rfe1 Bd7 18. a4 a6 19. a5 f5 $11) 16... Bxc3 17. Qxc3 (17. bxc3 Bf5 $1 18. Bf2 Bxe4 $1 $15) 17... Rxe4 18. Rf3 Bg4 19. Rg3 f5 20. Bf2 Qe7 21. Re1 (21. h3 h4 $15) 21... Rxe1+ 22. Bxe1 Re8 23. Bf2 Qe2 24. h3 h4 25. Rd3 Bh5 $11) 14... Qh4 {Gligoric,Svetozar: '!' Marin,Mihail: 'This move eventually turned out well for Black, but only because of White's lack of resolution on the next move.'} (14... Ng4 $1 {(Marin,Mihail) Marin !? Boleslavsky  Black should make use of this square while still available.} 15. f3 (15. h3 Nxe3 16. Bxe3 Bd7 17. a4 Qh4 $11) (15. Nxg4 hxg4 16. f3 f5 $5 17. fxg4 fxg4 18. Bf4 b6 $1 {Preparing to neutralise b5 with ...a6.} 19. Nb5 Rf8 $1 20. Bg3 Rxf1+ 21. Rxf1 Ba6 $11) 15... Nxe3 16. Bxe3 Bd7 {[%cal Gc3b5]} 17. a4 f5 18. Rae1 Qf6 $132) (14... a6 $5 {Marin,Mihail} 15. a4 Ng4 {may be even simpler.}) 15. Bd2 {?!} (15. f3 $1 {Byrne !? Boleslavsky} Bd7 {Marin,Mihail} 16. Ne2 $1 Ng6 17. Ng3 {Black is in strategic trouble and concrete play does not help him.} Bd4 (17... Be5 18. Nc4 Bd4+ 19. Be3 $16) 18. Kh1 Be5 19. f4 Bxf4 20. Nef5 Bxf5 21. Bxf4 Bg4 (21... Bxe4 22. Nxe4 Nxf4 23. g3 $18) 22. Bxd6 $16 {Marin}) (15. Nb5 Qe7 { 