ping after having made some queenside progress, too. The pawn on e4 is doomed. '} 28. Re3 (28. Bg5 $4 Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Qxg5 $18) (28. Ra1 {!?} Rxe4 (28... Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Rxe4 30. Rxe4 Rxe4 31. Ra6 $130) 29. Nxe4 Bxa1 30. Nxd6 Rxe2 31. Qxe2 $130 {Krogius}) 28... h5 {Marin,Mihail: '!' Marin,Mihail: 'Fischer does not hurry on his way to win his first game against Spassky ever.'} (28... Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Rxe4 30. Rxe4 Rxe4 31. Rxe4 Qxe4 32. Qf6 $132 {Byrne Marin,Mihail: 'Byrne We can continue the line with'} Bf5 {Marin,Mihail The only chance to maintain a (symbolic) advantage} 33. Qg5+ Kf8 34. Bxd6+ Ke8 {Checks have almost come to an end and Black threatens to start his own decisive attack with ...e1+ and ...e4+.} 35. Qe3 $1 Qxe3 36. fxe3 c3 37. e4 $1 Bxe4 38. Bf4 Bxd5 39. Kf2 Kd7 40. Ke3 c2 41. Kd4 Ke6 42. Bc1 $11 {Black cannot break White's fortress.}) 29. R3e2 Kh7 (29... Bxc3 $142 {Byrne}) 30. Re3 Kg8 (30... Bxc3 31. Qxc3 Rxe4 32. Rxe4 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Qxe4 34. Qf6 $132) 31. R3e2 Bxc3 $142 {Marin,Mihail: '' ''' Byrne'} 32. Qxc3 Rxe4 {Marin,Mihail: 'After checking everything Fischer goes for the decisive operation. '} 33. Rxe4 Rxe4 34. Rxe4 {Marin,Mihail: 'In principle the fewer major pieces on the board, the better saving chances. But now White's back rank will be weak, so keeping a rook on the board deserves examining.'} (34. Ra1 {Marin,Mihail: 'Marin'} Re8 {Marin,Mihail: '!'} 35. Qd4 {Marin,Mihail} (35. Ra6 {Marin,Mihail} Qb1+ 36. Bc1 Bf5 37. Rxd6 Bc2 38. Qd2 c3 39. Qg5+ Bg6 40. Kg2 Qd3 41. Be3 c2 $19 42. Rc6 Rxe3 $19) 35... Bf5 36. Bd2 Be4 37. Bc3 f6 {Black has neutralised White's counterplay and has an extra pawn and the better structure. The threat ...f5-f3 needs also to be permanently reckoned with.} 38. Qxf6 Qxf6 39. Bxf6 Bxd5 {With rooks on the board this should not cause big technical problems.} 40. f4 {The only way to free the king.} (40. Rd1 Bf3) 40... gxf3 41. Rd1 Bb7 42. Rxd6 Re1+ 43. Kf2 Re2+ 44. Kf1 Be4 $19 {[%cal Ge4d3]}) 34... Qxe4 $19 {Smith} 35. Bh6 (35.