{GAME N. 22 Bad judgement. Once a contender for the title, Szabo's performances nowadays are spotty and unpredictable. Here, with breathtaking bluntness, he attempts to wipe Fischer from the board. In the process he leaves himself wide open on the dark squares. Fischer promptly invades on the queenside, by means of a curious queen manoeuvre, while Szabo, preoccupied with his own kingside attack, fails to realise the danger in time. 21... Re3! is the blow that ends all effective resistance. Rather than fight on against hopeless odds, Szabo resigns three moves later.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O 5. Bg5 ({For} 5. e5 {see Game 21. Letelier,R-Fischer,R Leipzig ol 1960}) ({For} 5. Nf3 {see Games 7. Olafsson,F-Fischer,R Zürich 1959 28. Reshevsky, S-Fischer,R (11) Los Angeles 1961 30. Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Bled 1961}) 5... d6 ({After} 5... h6 6. Be3 $1 {allows White to set up a Siimisch formation (6 ... d6 7 f3) where the inevitable Qd2 will be more effective than usual.} d6 7. f3) 6. Qd2 ({Better is simply} 6. Be2 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Nf3 h6 9. Bh4 exd5 10. cxd5 g5 11. Bg3 Nh5 $13 {leading to a double-edged struggle. Larsen,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966} ({not} 11... b5 $2 12. Nd2 $1)) 6... c5 $1 7. d5 ({Instead} 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 $11 {is equal,}) ({whilst} 7. dxc5 dxc5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. e5 Ng4 10. f4 f6 $17 {is better for Black.}) 7... e6 8. Bd3 (8. dxe6 Bxe6 9. Nf3 Nc6 10. Be2 Bg4 $1 11. O-O Re8 12. Qf4 Bxf3 13. Bxf3 Nd4 14. Rad1 Re5 $11 {leads to equality, as in Evans,L-Gligoric,S Dallas 1957}) 8... exd5 {Diagram [#] White has no advantageous way to recapture.} 9. Nxd5 {In this opening variation Black must play sharply. White has a space advantage, but he temporarily lags in development.} (9. exd5 Nbd7 10. f4 {(to stop ... Ne5)} h6 11. Bh4 Re8+ 12. Nge2 Ne4 $1 13. Bxd8 Nxd2 14. Bc7 Bxc3 15. bxc3 Ne4 16. Bxe4 Rxe4 17. Bxd6 Nb6 18. Kf2 Bg4 $1 $17 {with a promising ending.}) (9. cxd5 a6 $1 10. f4 (10. a4 {is met by} Qa5 {threatening ... b5}) 10... h6 11. Bh4 Nxe4 $1 12. Bxd8 Nxd2 13. Bc7 Bxc3 14. bxc3 Ne4 $1 15. Bxe4 Re8 $17) 9... Be6 10. Ne2 Bxd5 {Releasing the tension and, by forcing White to recapture with a pawn, eliminating the backward d-pawn on an open file.} 11. exd5 ({Or} 11. cxd5 c4 $1 12. Bc2 Nbd7 13. O-O Nc5 14. Nc3 b5 $1) 11... Nbd7 12. O-O ({Not} 12. f4 $2 Qe8 $1 13. Qc2 $2 {(to stop ... Ne4)} Ng4 $1 {invading on the weak dark squares (notably e3)}) 12... Ne5 13. f4 $2 {This lemon, weakening e3 and e4, came as a pleasant surprise. Szabo misjudges White's attacking prospects.} ({After} 13. Nc3 $11 {the game is even.}) 13... Nxd3 14. Qxd3 h6 15. Bh4 Re8 16. Rae1 (16. Nc3 {is the best of a bad choice, though} Qb6 $1 {threatening ... Ng4 creates problems for White.} 17. h3 {allows} Nh5 $1 $17 {increasing the pressure on White's game.}) 16... Qb6 $1 17. Bxf6 (17. b3 Ne4 $1 $17 {gives Black a bind.}) 17... Bxf6 18. f5 g5 19. b3 Qa5 $1 {Diagram [#] A nettlesome manoeuvre!} 20. Rc1 $2 (20. a4 {loses to} Bd4+ 21. Kh1 ({not} 21. Nxd4 $2 Rxe1 $1) 21... Re3 22. Qd1 Rae8 $19 {and the e-file pin is decisive.}) ({The best defence is} 20. Qb1 $1) 20... Qxa2 21. Rc2 Re3 $1 22. Qxe3 Qxc2 23. Kh1 a5 24. h4 a4 {White's pawns fall like ripe apples. I'll never forget the disgusted look on Szabo's face as he took his king and just sort of shoved it gently to the centre of the board, indicating his intention to resign.} 0-1

