1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 {Adam Hunt: 'Unquestionably the most aggressive move against the Najdorf. White looks to double the black pawns on f6.'} e6 7. f4 Qb6 {Adam Hunt: 'And this is the most principled reply, challenging the weakened white queenside at an early stage. Warning: playing this variation requires a lot of knowledge!'} 8. Qd2 {Adam Hunt: 'White takes up the gauntlet, sacrificing the b2-pawn for a lead in development.'} (8. Nb3 {takes the knight off its most dangerous square and leads to quieter play: for instance,} Nbd7 9. Qf3 Qc7 10. O-O-O b5 11. a3 Bb7 {, as in V.Akopian-L.Van Wely, Cap d'Agde (rapid) 1996.}) 8... Qxb2 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. e5 {Adam Hunt: 'The most direct move, which has undergone a resurgence of interest in recent years.'} ({The sharpest alternative is} 10. f5 {and here after} Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 $5 {is a very interesting move. Many variations of the Poisoned Pawn have now been worked out to a forced draw. Here are the moves of one such example:} dxe5 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ne4 Qxa2 16. Rd1 Be7 17. Be2 O-O 18. O-O Ra7 19. Rf3 Kh8 20. Rg3 Rd7 21. Qh6 Rf7 22. Qh5 Rxd1+ 23. Bxd1 Qa5 24. Kf1 Qd8 25. Qxf7 Qxd1+ 26. Kf2 Qxc2+ 27. Kf3 Qd1+ 28. Kf2 Qc2+ 29. Ke3 Bc5+ 30. Nxc5 Qxc5+ 31. Kd2 Qf2+ 32. Kc3 Qd4+ 33. Kc2 Qf2+ 34. Kc3 {½-½ F.Vallejo Pons-G.Kasparov, Moscow 2004.}) 10... dxe5 11. fxe5 Nfd7 12. Bc4 {Adam Hunt: '?!' '?!' Adam Hunt: 'EXERCISE: White has a big lead in development, so Black must act with caution. What do you make of 12...Nxe5?'} (12. Ne4 {is the most testing move here, first introduced into practise by Paul Keres in the late 1950's. After} h6 $1 13. Bh4 Qxa2 14. Rd1 Qd5 {a heavily discussed theoretical position is reached, which we will leave for another day!}) 12... Bb4 {Adam Hunt: '!' '!' Adam Hunt: 'Gaining a tempo with development.'} ({ANSWER:} 12... Nxe5 $6 {is highly risky! Amazingly the only game according to Mega Database 2012 with this move was played by Tringov himself a year prior to this game. That game went that game went} 13. Nxe6 Qa5 14. Bd8 {when Black has to be on his toes to survive, but} (14. Bb5+ $5 {might be the way forward:} Bd7 15. Bxd7+ Nbxd7 16. Nxf8 Rxf8 17. O-O {with lasting compensation based upon the weakness of the black king}) 14... Nf3+ $1 15. gxf3 Qe5+ 16. Ne4 fxe6 {was eventually drawn in J.Bednarski-G.Tringov, Kecsemet Zonal 1964.}) 13. Rb3 (13. Bxe6 $2 O-O $1 (13... Bxc3 14. Bxf7+ Kxf7 15. O-O+ Kg8 16. Qf4 Bxd4+ 17. Kh1 Nxe5 18. Qxd4 h6 19. Qxe5 hxg5 20. Qe8+ Kh7 21. Qh5+ Kg8 22. Qf7+ Kh7 {White does not have more than a perpetual check.}) 14. Rb3 Qa5 15. Bxf7+ Rxf7 16. e6 Rf8 17. exd7 (17. Rxb4 Qxb4 18. e7 Re8 19. Nf5 Nf6 20. Bxf6 Bxf5 21. Qd5+ Kh8 22. Qf7 Rxe7+ 23. Qxe7 Qxe7+ 24. Bxe7 Nc6 25. Bc5 Bxc2 $17 {0-1 Kurajica,B-Tukmakov,V/Soviet Union 1965/MCD (80)}) 17... Bxd7 18. a3 Bxc3 19. Rxc3 Nc6 20. Nf3 Rae8+ 21. Kf2 Ne5 $17 {0-1 Opl,K (2270)-Stohl,I (2550)/Austria 1991/EXT 1997 (39)}) 13... Qa5 14. O-O O-O {Adam Hunt: 'Again Fischer refuses the second pawn. There is a line that even he dare not cross!'} (14... Nxe5 $6 {Adam Hunt: 'allows the disruptive sacrifice'} 15. Rxb4 $1 Qxb4 16. Nxe6 $1 {[%cal Gd2d8] Adam Hunt: 'which looks crushing for White, but the ice cold defence'} Qb6+ 17. Be3 Nxc4 {Adam Hunt: '!' '!' Adam Hunt: 'seems to just about hold: for example,'} 18. Nxg7+ Kf8 19. Rxf7+ $1 Kxf7 (19... Kg8 20. Rf8+ $1 Kxf8 21. Qf2+ Kxg7 22. Bxb6 Nxb6 23. Qd4+ {- see 19...xf7.}) 20. Qd5+ Kxg7 21. Bxb6 Nxb6 22. Qd4+ {Postny,E: ' - see 19...xf7.'} Kg8 23. Qxb6 Nc6 {Adam Hunt: 'with a bizarre material imbalance.'} 24. Ne4 $16 {White's queen and knight are worth more than Black's uncoordinated forces.}) 15. Nxe6 $2 {Adam Hunt: 'Very scary, but the art of good defence is to stay cool and not panic, even if something happens on the board that you have not seen. I'm guessing, though, that Fischer had, of course, seen this particular sacrifice and knew it to be unsound.' This sacrifice does not work in this case.} (15. Bf6 $5 {This one is sufficient only for equality. This position was a matter of discussion in the mid 60's.} Nxf6 $1 (15... gxf6 $2 16. Qh6 Qxe5 (16... Qc5 17. Ncb5 $1 Nxe5 18. Rg3+ Ng6 19. Rh3 Rd8 20. Rxf6 $18) 17. Nf5 $3 exf5 18. Ne4 $1 Bd2 (18... Re8 19. Rh3 Nf8 20. Nxf6+ Qxf6 21. Qxf6 Be6 22. Bxe6 fxe6 23. Rff3 $2 (23. Rg3+ Ng6 24. Rxg6+ hxg6 25. Qxg6+ Kf8 26. Qf6+ Kg8 27. Rf3 Bd6 28. Rh3 $18) 23... Bd6 $16 {1-0 Mazzoni,G-Bobotsov,M/Le Havre 1966/EXT 1998 (80)}) 19. Nxd2 Qd4+ 20. Kh1 Ne5 21. Rg3+ Ng4 22. h3 Qe5 23. Rf4 Qe1+ 24. Nf1 Qxg3 25. Rxg4+ Qxg4 26. hxg4 $18 {1-0 Byrne,R-Evans,L/New York 1965/MCL (36)}) 16. exf6 Rd8 17. Rxb4 Qxb4 18. Qg5 g6 19. Rf4 Qf8 (19... Rxd4 20. Qh6 Qf8 21. Qxf8+ Kxf8 22. Rxd4 Nc6 23. Rd6 Ke8 {0-1 Byrne,R-Zuckerman,B/New York 1966/MCD (54)}) 20. Rh4 b5 21. Bd3 b4 22. Bxg6 hxg6 23. Rh6 Rxd4 24. Rxg6+ {½-½ Hartston,W-Mecking,H/Hastings 1966/MCL}) 15... fxe6 16. Bxe6+ Kh8 17. Rxf8+ Bxf8 18. Qf4 Nc6 (18... Bc5+ $2 19. Kh1 Nf8 20. Bxc8 Nc6 21. Bxb7 $18 {½-½ Pujol Sans,M-Natalucci,F/Groningen 1971/EXT 2001 (69)}) (18... Qxe5 {would give the piece back and reach an approximately level ending after} 19. Qxe5 Nxe5 20. Bxc8 b5 {.}) 19. Qf7 {Adam Hunt: 'EXERCISE: Find a way to defend against the threat of Qg8 mate. Bear in mind that Black is currently a knight for a pawn up... --- ANSWER:'} Qc5+ {Without this check Black would simply resign. The queen now protects f8.} 20. Kh1 Nf6 $1 {Adam Hunt: 'Beautifully done. Black generates counterplay based on the weakness of the white back rank and the loose bishop on g5.' The only defence, but sufficient. White's attack evaporates.} 21. Bxc8 (21. exf6 Bxe6 22. Qxe6 Qxg5 {would also be winning for Black.}) 21... Nxe5 (21... Rxc8 22. Bxf6 Qe3 $1 {would do the job too.}) 22. Qe6 Neg4 {Adam Hunt: '. The white attack has been stopped in its tracks and Black has the decisive attack.'} (22... Z0 {Summary: The first principle of successful defence is to remain calm under pressure. Here Fischer calculated accurately and saw through the white aggression. At the right moment he could return some of his extra material in order to defuse the attack. We will revisit this 'money in the bank' strategy later in the chapter.}) 0-1

