[BlackTeamCountry "USA"]

{[%evp 0,46,17,16,21,-7,37,46,37,45,55,47,28,28,28,19,31,31,37,18,18,-64,-79,-83,-83,-75,-33,-88,-62,-111,-68,-145,-128,-128,-115,-145,-144,-229,-250,-437,-359,-592,-517,-743,-737,-780,-740,-1197,-1301] I have chosen this game because Fischer was still very young at the time (17 years), but he already played at a high level. My favourite idea in this game is the final queen sacrifice.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 {Fischer is playing his favourite opening, the King's Indian.} 4. e4 O-O $5 {Black wants to provoke e5 and then undermine the center with ..d6. This is an interesting plan but nowadays ...d6 is almost always played.} (4... d6) 5. e5 (5. Nf3 c6 $5 {Maybe this was Fischer's intention for the game} (5... d6 {transposes a normal King's Indian.}) 6. Be2 d5 $132) 5... Ne8 6. f4 d6 $1 {This is the essence of this line, with similar ideas the Alekhine Defence where Black is provokes e5 and then undermines the centre with ...d6.} 7. Be3 (7. Nf3 {This is the main move when} c5 8. dxc5 Nc6 {would be similar to the game.} 9. cxd6 exd6 10. Be2 Be6 $132) 7... c5 $1 {Attacking the centre is natural for this line.} 8. dxc5 Nc6 $1 {A very important move. Now Black attacks the e5-pawn with a developing move and is ready to sacrifice a few pawns in order to open up the position to get at the white king.} 9. cxd6 exd6 10. Ne4 $6 (10. exd6 $2 {would open up the position while lagging in development.} Nxd6 $17) (10. Nf3 $1 {This is the main move. White should try to finish kingside development.} Be6 11. Be2 Qa5 $132) 10... Bf5 $1 {Fisher is trying to provoke White into taking on d6.} 11. Ng3 $6 (11. Nxd6 {This is best, but Black remains better.} Nxd6 12. Qxd6 Qe8 $1 $15 {Surely Black should not exchange the queen. Now the threat is ...Rd8 and ...f6, and Black has a pleasant position.}) 11... Be6 12. Nf3 Qc7 $5 {Fischer does not want to exchange queens, and still gains an advantage, but 12...dxe5 is objectively best.} (12... dxe5 $1 13. Qxd8 Rxd8 14. Bc5 (14. fxe5 Nxe5 $17) 14... exf4 $1 15. Ne2 Nd6 $17) 13. Qb1 $6 {Preparing f5 is too slow.} (13. f5 $1 {This gives White some counterplay.} Bxf5 14. Nxf5 gxf5 15. Bd3 f4 16. Bxf4 dxe5 17. Bg3 f5 18. O-O Rd8 $15) 13... dxe5 14. f5 e4 $5 {A very nice stylish move. Fisher is trying to open up the position at any cost.} (14... gxf5 $1 {is also strong, but is not as nice as the game continuation.} 15. Nxf5 Bxf5 16. Qxf5 Nd6 17. Qg4 e4 18. Nd2 f5 19. Qg3 Nb4 $1 $40 {White's position is collapsing.}) 15. fxe6 $2 (15. Qxe4 $1 {would be the best reply, but White was probably scared of the open e-file.} gxf5 16. Qh4 (16. Nxf5 $4 Qa5+ 17. Bd2 Qxf5 $19) 16... Ne7 $1 $17 {[%cal Ge7g6]}) 15... exf3 16. gxf3 f5 $1 {Not recapturing the pawn is very nice. Now Black is threatening ...f4 winning a piece, so the next White moves are forced.} 17. f4 {Preventing ...f4, but now the e4 and g4 squares are very weak.} Nf6 $1 18. Be2 Rfe8 $1 19. Kf2 Rxe6 20. Re1 Rae8 {Material is equal, but the position is completely lost for White. His king is very weak, and the rooks on the open file are very strong. All Black's pieces are participating in the attack.} 21. Bf3 Rxe3 $1 {A very nice way to finish he game.} 22. Rxe3 Rxe3 23. Kxe3 {[#]} Qxf4+ $3 {The icing on the cake. I love this move very much. Finishing the game with a queen sacrifice is always a phenomenal feeling.} (23... Qxf4+ 24. Kxf4 (24. Kf2 Ng4+ 25. Kg2 Ne3+ 26. Kf2 Nd4 $19) 24... Bh6# $1 {Checkmate in the centre of the board. Fischer punished his opponent, who forgot the general principle: Get your king to safety in the opening as quicky as possible.}) 0-1

