{ChessCalisthenics: 'B70: Sicilian Dragon: 6 g3 and 6 Be2 (without a later Be3)''B70: Sicilian Dragon: 6 g3 and 6 Be2 (without a later Be3) Chris Ward: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B70: Sicilian Dragon: 6 g3 and 6 Be2 (without a later Be3)'''} 1. d4 {Although many might remember Fischer's thoughts on the Dragon as being simply a case of 'sac, sac mate!', lesser known are the outings in which he found himself on the Black side of the opening. Admittedly he wasn't having to face the sharpest Yugoslav Attack variations but as this game demonstrates, he did have an aptitude for handling this set-up! ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Although many might remember Fischer's thoughts on the Dragon as being simply a case of 'sac, sac mate!', lesser known are the outings in which he found himself on the Black side of the opening. Admittedly he wasn't having to face the sharpest Yugoslav Attack variations but as this game demonstrates, he did have an aptitude for handling this set-up!''} ({Of course he was a Najdorf enthusiast but after} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. g3 {still today many a strong player, in the same way way that 6 f4 can transpose to a Levenfish, allow a swift to a 'Fianchetto' system against the Dragon and in fact Bobby had the odd encounter with} g6 7. Bg2 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. Nde2) 1... Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 {And yes, we have ourselves a King's Indian Defence with White deploying the Fianchetto variation. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'And yes, we have ourselves a King's Indian Defence with White deploying the Fianchetto variation.''} 6. Nc3 ({But rather than continuing with} 6. c4 {leading into the expected Queen's pawn type of game, White unleashes the text suggesting that we might transpose into either a 'Pirc' or... yep you've guessed it!}) 6... c5 7. e4 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. Nde2 {And yes we've transposed to a Fianchetto system against the Dragon where Black has already castled making ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'And yes we've transposed to a Fianchetto system against the Dragon where Black has already castled making''} (9. Nxc6 bxc6 10. e5 dxe5 11. Bxc6 {less effective. Indeed then both} Rb8 {and 11...Bh3 have scored quite well for Black in practice who has plenty of action to compensate for White's structural advantage.}) 9... Bd7 ({Returning to my Dragon Vs Najdorf debate and given the choice, even a die hard Najdorf player like Fischer doesn't choose to prioritise} 9... a6 {now, the move that was already included in his set-up via the earlier detailed move order.} 10. h3 Bd7 {Typically White retreats his knight to e2 in response to ...Nc6 and highlighting why the Dragon is obviously superior to the Najdorf, ...a6 wasn't a priority. Nevertheless...} 11. Nf4 (11. b3 b5 12. Be3 Qc8 13. Kh2 Qc7 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 Nd8 {was again a nibble for White but} 16. Bd4 Rc8 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Qd4+ Kg8 19. c3 e5 $1 20. dxe6 fxe6 21. Rad1 Nf7 22. f4 {with an imbalanced pawn structure tendered chances for both sides and this game wasn't destined to end tamely either!:} Qc5 23. Qxc5 Rxc5 24. Rd3 Rfc8 25. Rfd1 a5 26. g4 Kf8 27. Re3 Ke7 28. Bd5 Kf6 29. g5+ Ke7 30. c4 Nd8 31. Nd4 bxc4 32. bxc4 Kf7 33. Rde1 exd5 34. Re7+ Kf8 35. Rxd7 Rxc4 36. Ree7 Rxd4 37. Rxh7 Kg8 38. Rhg7+ Kf8 39. Rh7 {This time there is no avoiding the repetition! 1/2-1/2 Balinas, R-Fischer,R Manila 1967}) 11... Rc8 12. Ncd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 Ne5 (13... e6 $1 14. Ne3 Qc7 {looks satisfactory to me as the weakened d-pawn is counter-balanced with action elsewhere.}) 14. Bg5 Re8 15. b3 h6 16. Be3 Be6 17. Nf4 ({Erecting the standard bind through} 17. c4 {to tender a small plus looked logical but instead we get some puzzling play that demonstrates Fischer's mentality.}) 17... Bd7 18. Nd5 Be6 19. Nf4 Bd7 20. Nd5 Bc6 {(Yes 20...Be6 would have repeated for the draw but despite objectively being very slightly worse, it is Black who plays for the win.)} 21. Nb6 Rb8 22. Rc1 Nd7 23. Nxd7 Qxd7 24. Qd2 Kh7 {Now chances are equal but the game was played out:} 25. c4 b6 (25... b5 26. c5 Qb7 $5 27. cxd6 Rbd8 {was a bit more dynamic but Black bides his time.}) 26. Rfe1 Qb7 27. Bd4 Bxd4 28. Qxd4 a5 29. h4 {White retains his advantage with} h5 30. Bh3 Qc7 31. a3 Bb7 32. b4 axb4 33. axb4 Ra8 34. c5 bxc5 35. bxc5 Ra6 36. cxd6 Qxd6 37. Qxd6 Rxd6 38. Rc7 Rb6 39. Bg2 Rb4 40. e5 Bxg2 41. Kxg2 Kg7 42. Kf3 Kf8 43. Rec1 {only now seeing things grind to a halt i.e. 1/2-1/2 Matanovic, A-Fischer,R Monte Carlo 1967}) 10. b3 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' 'N''Chris Ward: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N'''} ({With plenty to be found on it in the archives, the main theory here revolves around the} 10. h3 {that intercepts Black ensuing plan.}) (10. Nd5 Rc8 (10... Nxd5 11. exd5 Ne5 12. h3 b5 13. c3 Nc4 14. b3 Nb6 15. Be3 Rc8 16. Rc1 Rc7 17. Bd4 Bxd4 18. Nxd4 Qc8 19. Qd2 Rc5 20. Qh6 e5 21. dxe6 fxe6 22. h4 Rf6 23. Nf3 Qf8 24. Qxf8+ Rxf8 25. Ng5 {Kulhanek,T (2382) -Pribyl,J (2393)/Prague 2003/EXT 2006/1-0 (48)}) (10... Ne5 11. h3 Rc8 12. a4 Bc6 13. Ra2 Ned7 14. Bg5 h6 15. Be3 Kh7 16. Qd2 e6 17. Nxf6+ Nxf6 18. f3 Qc7 19. b4 b6 20. b5 Bb7 21. a5 Nd7 22. axb6 axb6 23. c4 Ne5 24. Rc1 Nxc4 25. Qb4 {Afek,Y (2329)-L'Ami,E (2438)/Amsterdam 2002/CBM 089 ext/0-1 (50)}) 11. Bd2 Ne5 12. Bc3 Re8 13. Nxf6+ Bxf6 14. f4 Ng4 15. e5 dxe5 16. fxe5 Nxe5 17. Nd4 Qb6 18. Kh1 Bg4 19. Qe1 Nc4 20. Bd5 Nxb2 21. Rb1 Qc5 22. Bxf7+ Kxf7 23. Bxb2 Qd5+ 24. Kg1 Kg8 {Maryasin,B (2380)-Averbakh,Y (2445)/Netanya 1993/EXT 1998/0-1 (39)}) (10. a4 Nb4 11. Nd5 Nfxd5 12. exd5 Bf5 13. c3 Nd3 14. Nd4 Bxd4 15. cxd4 Nxc1 16. Rxc1 Qb6 17. Qd2 Rac8 18. Rfe1 Rxc1 19. Rxc1 Rc8 20. Rxc8+ Bxc8 21. Qc3 Bf5 22. a5 Qb5 23. h4 Kg7 24. Qc7 Qxb2 {Rivera Kuzawka,D (2440)-Ivkov,B (2505)/Oviedo 1993/CBM 038 ext/1/2-1/2 (36)}) 10... Qc8 {A common Black theme. Although...Bh3 might not bring with it mate now that ...h5-h4 wouldn't have a rook behind it, it is still a handy idea. Furthermore White is prohibited from playing h2-h3 thus leaving the g4-square available to a black minor piece. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'A common Black theme. Although...Bh3 might not bring with it mate now that ...h5-h4 wouldn't have a rook behind it, it is still a handy idea. Furthermore White is prohibited from playing h2-h3 thus leaving the g4-square available to a black minor piece.''} 11. Bb2 {The first purpose of b2-b3 was to control the c4-square and the second was to facilitate this. Fair enough but regular subscribers will know personally I'm not a great fan of White's system. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'The first purpose of b2-b3 was to control the c4-square and the second was to facilitate this. Fair enough but regular subscribers will know personally I'm not a great fan of White's system.''} Bh3 $1 {The natural follow up. White's light-squared bishop isn't bad just because there is temporarily a pawn obstructing it on e4 and the trading off of it frees up some room for Black to manoeuvre in whilst weakening the diagonal towards h1. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!' Chris Ward: 'The natural follow up. White's light-squared bishop isn't bad just because there is temporarily a pawn obstructing it on e4 and the trading off of it frees up some room for Black to manoeuvre in whilst weakening the diagonal towards h1.''} 12. f3 Bxg2 13. Kxg2 d5 $5 {Fischer was a super talented 13 year old at the time and although he could have prepared the text first through ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'Fischer was a super talented 13 year old at the time and although he could have prepared the text first through''} (13... Rd8 {, I like this aggressive approach.}) 14. exd5 {Obviously . ..dxe4 was threatened whilst e4-e5 wasn't available. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Obviously . ..dxe4 was threatened whilst e4-e5 wasn't available.''} Rd8 {Of course Black could now follow up with ...Nb4 but with the c3-knight pinned to the bishop on b2, Black was ready for ...Nxd5 anyway. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Of course Black could now follow up with ...Nb4 but with the c3-knight pinned to the bishop on b2, Black was ready for ...Nxd5 anyway.''} 15. Qc1 Nb4 $5 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!?''} ({More ambitious than} 15... Nxd5 16. Nxd5 Rxd5 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Qb2+) 16. a3 Nbxd5 {Definitely Black is for preference and already looking like a Dragon player in the making! ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Definitely Black is for preference and already looking like a Dragon player in the making!''} 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 ({It certainly looked natural to get on the case of trying to exploit the weak squares in White's position such as c3 and e3 but actually here} 17... Rxd5 $5 {was maybe more testing as amongst other things it brought with it the threat of ...Rh5 and then ...Qh3+.}) 18. Bxg7 Kxg7 19. c4 Qe6 {I think it's more like equal now but in hitting e2, Black naturally continues to mobilise his forces. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'I think it's more like equal now but in hitting e2, Black naturally continues to mobilise his forces.''} 20. Qb2+ Nf6 ({Upon reflection} 20... Qf6 21. Qxf6+ Nxf6 22. Rfd1 {and a likely draw was more prudent but even back then, eager to keep more pieces on, Fischer wasn't afraid to play with fire.}) 21. Nf4 Qf5 $6 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '?!''} ({Perhaps} 21... Qd6 {was rejected because of} 22. Nd5 {but then not only would} Kg8 23. Nxf6+ exf6 {not have been too bad because of the central control but in fact 22...b5 might have thrown the cat amongst the pigeons.}) {Clearly Black wanted to get in ...e5. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Clearly Black wanted to get in ...e5.''} 22. Nd5 (22. Rae1 $1 {putting a stop to Black's plan whilst hitting e7 looked rather attractive but we're still approaching a pivotal moment in Black's career!?}) 22... e6 $4 ({Instead ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'Instead''} 22... e5 {and we might still have made a Dragon player out of him! ChessCalisthenics: '' '''Chris Ward: 'and we might still have made a Dragon player out of him! ChessCalisthenics: ''''} 23. g4 Qg5 $14) 23. g4 $1 {Oops! The rather big issue here is that the black queen can be driven away from anywhere that will keep the f6-knight protected. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!' Chris Ward: 'Oops! The rather big issue here is that the black queen can be driven away from anywhere that will keep the f6-knight protected.''} Qg5 24. h4 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!''} Qxh4 25. Rh1 Qg5 26. Rxh7+ $3 {An early scarring, perhaps contributing to the great Bobby Fischer's bias against the Dragon! ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: '!!' Chris Ward: 'An early scarring, perhaps contributing to the great Bobby Fischer's bias against the Dragon!''} ({Rather than} 26. Nxf6 $4 Rd2+) 26... Kf8 ({Alas it's painful viewing now but} 26... Kxh7 27. Nxf6+ Kg7 28. Ne4+ {simply dropped the queen.}) 27. Qxf6 {And Black is a piece down, unable to preserve the queens in view of immediate mate threats. ChessCalisthenics: 'Chris Ward: 'And Black is a piece down, unable to preserve the queens in view of immediate mate threats.''} Qxf6 28. Nxf6 $18 {1-0 (28) Sobel,R-Fischer,R Montreal 1956 [Chris Ward]} 1-0

