{Just as at the time of Fischer's comeback in 1992 we wondered how strong his play was then, it is also just as unclear today at what point Fischer became the strongest player of his day - certainly before1971/72. Over the New Year period of 1963/64 Fischer again won the championship of the USA - with a score of 100%.   Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This game was played in the third round of the U.S. championship 1963. Many argue that Fischer was at the peak of his strength during this time. This game and the tournament as a whole are definitely an argument for that statement. Fischer won the U.S. championship with a stunning 11/11, not conceding a single draw. This has never happened before and never been repeated since. Robert Eugene Byrne, playing with the white pieces, was one of the strongest American players at the time and also a participant in the candidates cycle later on. It adds to the incredibility that a player of such calibre loses to Fischer with White in just 21 moves. In the comments I have included Fischer's own account as written in his book "My 60 Memorable Games". Let's begin! ' So wie man bei Fischers Comeback 1992 rätselte, wie stark er da spielte, so unklar ist auch heute noch, ab wann Fischer der stärkste Spieler der damaligen Zeit war - gewiss nicht erst 1971/72. Zum Jahreswechsel 1963/64 gewann Fischer wieder einmal die USA-Meisterschaft - mit 100%.} 1. d4 Nf6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Opening = D71 - Grünfeld Verteidigung-3.g3 /Gruenfeld Defence-3.g3'} 2. c4 g6 3. g3 c6 4. Bg2 (4. d5 b5 $1 5. dxc6 bxc4 6. cxd7+ Nbxd7 7. Bg2 Rb8 8. Nf3 Bg7 9. O-O O-O $10 {Byrne,R-Fischer,R ch-USA 1962}) 4... d5 5. cxd5 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Byrne takes a dry approach, aiming for a symmetrical pawn structure at the first opportunity. Fischer points out that 5.b3 would have kept more tension.'} (5. Qb3) 5... cxd5 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. e3 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '?!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is an unusual, and frankly, not great setup for White. By placing the pawn on e3, the dark-squared bishop cannot be developed on the c1-h6 diagonal and the light squares are weakened. The latter will play an important role in the game.'} (7. Nf3 O-O 8. Ne5 (8. O-O {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'is the absolute main line when White hopes for a little edge due to his one-move-advantage.'} Ne4 $1 $10) 8... Bf5 9. O-O Ne4 10. Qb3 Nc6 11. Qxd5 Nxc3 12. bxc3 Qxd5 13. Bxd5 Nxe5 14. dxe5 Bxe5 $10 {Benko,P-Fischer,R ch-USA 1962}) (7. Bg5 Nc6 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. e3 e6 10. Nf3 O-O 11. O-O Bd7 12. Qe2 Rc8 13. Rfd1 Bg7 14. Rac1 Qe7 15. e4 dxe4 16. Qxe4 Rfd8 17. Qe3 Qb4 18. d5 exd5 19. Nxd5 {½-½ Prang,E-Schlemermeyer,W/BL9596 1996/BL 90}) 7... O-O 8. Nge2 Nc6 9. O-O b6 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'N'} (9... e6 10. b3 Qd7 11. Qd2 b6 12. Bb2 Ba6 13. Rfd1 Rac8 14. Bf1 Rfd8 {½-½ Hertneck,G-Nunn,J/BL 9495 Duisburg-München 36 1995}) 10. b3 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'N' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' D71: Fianchetto Grünfeld: 3 g3 d5 sidelines and 5 cxd5 (without Nf3)' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer comments: "It's hard for either side to introduce an imbalance into this essentially symmetrical variation. Deadeye equality also ensues afer 10. f4 e6 11.b3 a6 12.e1 c8 13.a3 e8 14.c1 (Stahlberg-Flohr, Kemeri 1937)". Indeed, the position is equal. The main difference in the placement of the e-pawn is now emphasised by Fischer with'} ({Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'Predecessor:'} 10. Nf4 e6 11. b3 Ba6 12. Re1 Rc8 13. Ba3 Re8 14. Rc1 $10 {Stahlberg,G-Flohr,S Kemeri 1937} Bf8 15. Bxf8 Kxf8 16. Qd2 Qd6 17. h4 Red8 18. Qb2 Kg8 19. Red1 Ne7 20. a3 Rc6 21. Na2 Rdc8 22. Nb4 Rxc1 23. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 24. Qxc1 {½-½ (24) Marini,L-Eliskases,E Mar del Plata 1960}) 10... Ba6 11. Ba3 Re8 {The position is now so completely level that White becomes careless.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Stepping out of the a3-f8 diagonal and introducing the idea ...e7-e5 in the position. ' Die Stellung ist so völlig ausgeglichen, dass Weiß nun leichtsinnig wird.} 12. Qd2 $6 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'A logical move, connecting the rooks. Yet a mistake. White ignores the black idea and misses the chance to move the rooks out of diagonals of the threatening bishops.'} ({After Nach} 12. Rc1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'makes sense to stop ...e7-e5.'} Rc8 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now, once again, ...e7-e5 is a threat and White is advised to play the prophylactic'} (12... e5 $6 13. Nxd5 $1 Nxd5 14. Rxc6 $16 {leaves White a pawn up.}) 13. Re1 {the white rooks would be clear of the diagonals of the opposing bishops, White would be ready for f4 and things would be ok. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'was seen in some games, but becomes quite uncomfortable for White after' wären die weißen Türme aus den gegnerischen Läuferdiagonalen raus, wäre Weiß zu f4 bereit und alles in Ordnung. wären die weißen Türme aus den gegnerischen Läuferdiagonalen raus, wäre Weiß zu Sf4 bereit und alles in Ordnung.} (13. Rc2 $1 g5 $5 {to stop a future e2-f4 when the black position is still to be preferred, but it is close to equal.} (13... e5 {is no good because of} 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Rd2 $1 $14 {Here we see the idea behind 13.c1-c2.})) 13... e5 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Nd3 17. Rxc8 Qxc8 {when Black has good compensation for the sacrificed pawn.}) (12. f4 $5 e6 $17 { f8, a8-c8-c7, ec8}) 12... e5 $5 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '!' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): 'LiveBook: 3 Games' Of course Fischer does not miss the chance to bring some tension into the game.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Suddenly Fischer is able to introduce the imbalance he was looking for. Fischer says about this moment: "I was a bit worried about weakening my queen's pawn, but felt that the tremendous activity obtained by my minor pieces would permit White no time to exploit it. 12...e6 would probably lead to a draw." Fischer's assessment is spot-on. The black piece activity more than compensates the weakening of the pawn structure. ' Die Gelegenheit Spannung in die Partie zu bringen lässt sich Fischer natürlich nicht entgehen.} (12... e6 $10) 13. dxe5 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Increases the pressure on d5'} (13. Rac1 exd4 (13... Rc8 14. Rfd1 e4 15. f3 $1) 14. exd4 Rc8 15. f3 $17) 13... Nxe5 14. Rfd1 $2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'The pressure on the isolated pawn grows' The wrong rook. The fact that White lost this apparantly totally normal and level position after only 7 more moves is what has made this game famous. Huschenbeth,Niclas: '"Add another to those melancholy case histories entitled: The Wrong Rook." is Fischer's comment. It is understandable that Byrne wanted to break the pin of the e2-knight on the f1-a6 diagonal, but this rook is dearly needed to defend the king side, particularly the point f2. Therefore, the other rook (the right one) should have moved to d1.' Der falsche Turm. Dass Weiß diese scheinbar völlig normale und ausgeglichene Stellung in nur 7 Zügen verliert, hat die Partie berühmt gemacht.} (14. Rad1 $1 { Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '='} {After the natural Auf das natürliche} Qd7 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'is best, connecting the rooks and planning ventures to the king side via f5 or g4. White has two options:' soll laut Fischer} (14... Qc8 $5 {should be the best move according to Fischer. which may well be possible but not all his variations are correct: Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'was the best move according to Fischer, but modern-day engines think otherwise. As opposed to d8-d7 it allows the defence' Das soll laut Fischer der beste Zug sein, was gut möglich ist, aber nicht alle seine Varianten sind korrekt:} 15. Nxd5 (15. Bb2 {} Qf5 $36 {(Fischer) (Fischer)}) (15. Rc1 Qd7 $1 16. Rcd1 Rad8) (15. Qc1 Ne4 16. Nxd5 Bxe2 17. Bxe4 Kh8 $1 18. Qxc8 Raxc8 19. Ne7 Rc7 20. Rc1 Rd7 21. Rfe1 Bf3 $1 $19) (15. Bd6 $1 {provoking} Nd3 {. After} 16. Nxd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Rd8 18. Bxa8 Qxa8 19. Ba3 {White's position is still holding up. Black has compensation for the sacrificed material, but not more.}) 15... Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Rd8 17. f4 Rxd5 $1 (17... Ng4 $5 18. e4 Nf6 $1 19. f5 $1 (19. Be7 Nxe4 20. Qe3 Rxd5 21. Rxd5 Qe6 $17) 19... Nxe4 20. Qe3 Rxd5 21. Rxd5 Bxe2 22. Qxe2 Nc3 23. Qf3 Nxd5 24. Qxd5 $16) 18. Qxd5 Bb7 $1 19. Qd2 (19. Qd8+ Qxd8 20. Rxd8+ Rxd8 21. fxe5 Bxe5 $17) 19... Qc6 $1 (19... Qh3 $1 20. Nd4 Ng4 {according to Fischer, but after soweit Fischer, aber nach} 21. Nf3 $1 {I can see no compensation for Black. ist keine Kompensation für Schwarz zu sehen.} (21. Rfe1 Nxe3 $1 $19) (21. Nc2 h5 $40)) 20. Qd5 Qc8 21. Qd2 Qc6 $11) (14... Ne4 $2 15. Nxe4 dxe4 16. Bxe4 Qxd2 17. Rxd2 Nc4 18. Bxa8 Nxd2 19. Rd1 Nc4 20. Bc6 $1 {Averbakh,Y Averbakh,Y} (20. bxc4 Rxa8 $17 {× c4,  (Fischer) × c4, }) 20... Nxa3 21. Bxe8 Bxe2 22. Rd7 $18) (14... Rc8 15. Nxd5 Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Bd3 17. Bg2 Rc2 18. Qxc2 $18) (14... Nd3 15. Qc2) (14... Qc7 15. Qc1 $1 Ne4 $5 16. Nxd5 $1 Qxc1 17. Nxc1 Bxf1 18. Bxe4 Ba6 19. Ne7+ Kh8 20. Bxa8 Rxa8 21. f4 $16) 15. Qc2 $16 { d2, fd1 Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now, White is threatening to take on d5.'} ({or the positional exchange sacrifice} 15. Nf4 {. After} Bxf1 16. Kxf1 {White certainly has compensation, but Black should still have the upper-hand after} Rad8 17. Nfxd5 Nxd5 18. Nxd5 Kh8 $15) (15. Nxd5 $2 {is unsurprisingly no good:} Nxd5 16. Bxd5 Rad8 17. Qc2 Qb5 18. Nc3 Qa5 {and too many white pieces are hanging.}) 15... Rac8 16. Qb1 $1 {follow. But Fritz likes Black's position: folgen. Aber Fritz gefällt die schwarze Stellung:} (16. Bb2 Qg4 {Black once again goes after the weakened light squares in the white camp.} 17. h3 Nf3+ 18. Kh1 Qe4 $1 $15) 16... Qg4 $1 (16... Qf5 $1 {With this paradoxical move, Black forces a favourable endgame.} 17. Qxf5 gxf5 18. Rd2 Ne4 $1 19. Nxe4 dxe4 20. Rfd1 {White has survived the worst and can hope to equalise in this endgame, even though Black is the one pushing.}) 17. h3 (17. f3 Qh5 18. Rf2 (18. Bc1) 18... Qh6) 17... Qh5 18. Nf4 Qh6 19. Ncxd5 Bxf1 20. Rxf1 Nxd5 21. Nxd5 Kh8 $13) 14... Nd3 $1 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now, the weakening of the light squares with the early e2-e3 becomes an obvious problem. The knight is deeply placed within the white position, controlling many important squares. White tries to get rid of it, but only makes matters worse. '} 15. Qc2 $6 {[%mdl 512] Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' Huschenbeth,Niclas: '?' Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''?!'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]' This move loses. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer says: "There is hardly any other defense to the threat of . .. e4." It is true that f6-e4 cannot be stopped, yet the game move loses right away. White stays afloat with e2-d4 or e2-f4.' Der Zug verliert,} ({But the alternatives die Alternativen} 15. Nd4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ''} Ne4 16. Nxe4 dxe4 17. Bb2 Rc8 $36 18. a4 {is certainly not a pleasant position for White, but Black does not break through immediately. Expansion on the queenside with} h5 $5 $17 {looks attractive now.}) ({and und} 15. Nf4 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is relatively best.'} Ne4 16. Nxe4 dxe4 {are hardly any better. können auch nicht gefallen.} (16... Bxa1 $2 17. Nd6) 17. Rab1 Rc8 18. Nxd3 (18. Bb4 {Stopping g7-c3 and preparing the trade of knights. White is clearly worse, but still fully in the game.}) 18... Bc3 $1 19. Qe2 Bxd3 20. Qg4 f5 21. Qh3 Bxb1 $1 22. Rxd8 Rexd8 23. Bf1 Rd1 24. Kg2 Bd3 $1 25. Bxd3 exd3 $19) (15. f3 Bh6 16. f4 (16. Nf4 $2 d4 $1) 16... Bg7 $1 { e4}) (15. Nc1 $5 Ne4 16. Nxe4 dxe4 17. Rb1 $15) 15... Nxf2 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: '!!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''!'' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '-+!' This move may be suggested immediately by chess engines, but their reasoning is wrong.  Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Now it becomes evident why the rook was still needed on f1. The white king is exposed and comes under a furious attack. Interestingly, Byrne and the commentators thought that this move is played out of desperation and White is clearly better! Fischer says: "The complete justification for this sac does not become apparent until White resigns!" ' Zwar wird dieser Zug a tempo von Schachprogrammen angezeigt, doch die Begründung ist falsch.} 16. Kxf2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' And now Bf3 would win.'} Ng4+ 17. Kg1 (17. Ke1 Nxe3 $19 {The fork of queen and bishop decides the game right away.}) 17... Nxe3 18. Qd2 $8 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'After the first intrusion with the knight on d3, now the other one has landed on e3! Later Byrne wrote about this position: "As I sat pondering why Fischer would choose such a line, because it was so obviously lost for Black, there suddenly comes..."'} Nxg2 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer is not interested in regaining material by taking the rook on d1. He is going for the throat (of the king)! Once again Byrne: "This dazzling move came as the shocker... the culminating combination is of such depth that, even at the very moment at which I resigned, both grandmasters who were commenting on the play for the spectators in a separate room believed I had a won game!" '} (18... Nxd1 19. Rxd1 $10) ({Not} 18... Bxe2 19. Nxe2 Bxa1 20. Rxa1 $11) 19. Kxg2 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} d4 $1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '' The key to the sacrificial line which started with 15...xf2!. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'This is the punchline of the combination initiated with 15...d3xf2 and likely the move missed by Byrne and commentators alike. Black sacrifices the central pawn to open up the h1-a8 diagonal with devastating effect.' Das ist der Clou der mit 15...xf2! eingeleiteten Opfervariante. Das ist der Clou der mit 15...Sxf2! eingeleiteten Opfervariante.} ({On the other hand, White could be completely satisfied after Dagegen könnte Weiß nach} 19... Bxe2 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '!?'} 20. Nxe2 Bxa1 21. Rxa1 {though the engines are counting in an advantage in material for Black. Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'would give Black nothing; the position is roughly equal.' völlig zufrieden sein, während Programme schwarzen Materialvorteil errechnen.} Qd7 $15) 20. Nxd4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '[#]'} Bb7+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '!' Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Fischer: "The king is at Black's mercy."'} 21. Kf1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): '=' Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): '?'} ({After Auf} 21. Kg1 {things go as follows: Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'allows a strong deflection:' geht's so:} Bxd4+ 22. Qxd4 Re1+ $1 23. Kf2 ({or} 23. Rxe1 Qxd4+ $19) 23... Qxd4+ {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s): ' Overworked Piece, Deflection'} 24. Rxd4 Rxa1 $19 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Black is up a pawn and exchange and will have no difficulties to convert.'} 25. Rd7 Rc8 26. Rxb7 (26. Bb2 Rh1) 26... Rxc3 27. Rb8+ Kg7 28. Bb2 Rxa2 $19) (21. Kf2 Qd7 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'is similar to the game.'} 22. Rac1 (22. Rab1 $11) (22. Nf3 {does not save White:} Qxd2+ 23. Nxd2 Bxc3 24. Rac1 Bd4+ 25. Kf1 Bc8 $1 $19 {and the mate threat Bc8-h3 means White needs to give material.}) 22... Qh3 23. Nf3 Bh6 24. Qd3 Be3+ 25. Qxe3 Rxe3 26. Kxe3 Re8+ 27. Kf2 Qf5 $1 $19) 21... Qd7 $1 {Fritz 5.00 (5s): 'Threatening mate: Qh3' Source: Fischer "Meine 60 denkwürdigen Partien" (German edition of "My 60 memorable games") Huschenbeth,Niclas: 'Simple, yet powerful. The strongest piece moves for the first time and the game is over - White resigned as the invasion of the queen via h3 is deadly. Fischer writes: "A bitter disappointment. I'd hoped for 22.f2 h3+ 23.g1 e1+!! 24.xe1 xd4 with mate to follow shortly." Let us take a look at this beautiful variation:' Quelle: Fischer "Meine 60 denkwürdigen Partien" (dt. Fassung)} (21... Qd7 $1 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''!''} 22. Qf2 (22. Ndb5 Qh3+ 23. Kg1 Bh6 $19) 22... Qh3+ (22... Bxd4 23. Rxd4 Qc6 $11) 23. Kg1 Re1+ $3 24. Rxe1 (24. Qxe1 Qg2#) 24... Bxd4 $19 {Huschenbeth,Niclas: ''-+''} 25. Qxd4 Qg2# {This indeed would have been a fitting conclusion to this brilliant game by Fischer.}) 0-1

