{Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'It's unlikely that anyone will want to repeat Fischer's 12.d5 as Black gets good counterplay. Fischer's idea was to prevent ...f7-f5 by going 13.g4, but this runs into 13...h5!.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Because of Keres' illness the game was played on 15.06 onstead of 13.06.  ' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Die Partie wurde wegen Krankheit von Keres erst am 15.06. statt am 13.06. gespielt. '} 1. e4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Breutigam,Martin Breutigam,Martin Die Partie wurde wegen Krankheit von Keres erst am 15.06. statt am 13.06. gespielt.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Nd7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'This choice by Keres was certainly not a surprise for Fischer since he had already faced this line a month ago in the same tournament! Although in the first game Keres was completely crushed by Fischer he persisted with his pet line that later got his name. Black's main idea is to face d5 with b6 and f5 creating counterplay on the kingside. If White keeps his centre flexible, Black wants to take on d4 twice and place his bishop on f6.'} 12. d5 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'This looks premature as Black is well placed to launch a counterattack with ... f7-f5. Fischer prevents this but in doing so weakens his kingside.'' Breutigam,Martin: 'This looks totally logical. White secures an advantage in space and in addition the knight on a5 is out of the play. Nevertheless, after this game the advance was considered premature for decades. ' Breutigam,Martin: 'Das sieht durchaus logisch aus. Weiß sichert sich Raumvorteil, außerdem steht der Springer a5 abseits. Dennoch galt der Vorstoß nach dieser Partie jahrzehntelang als verfrüht. '} ({Interestingly, more than five weeks previously in the seventh round of the candidates tournament against the same opponent the 19 year old Bobby Fischer had played Interessanterweise hatte der 19-jährige Bobby Fischer über fünf Wochen zuvor, in der siebten Runde des Kandidatenturniers, gegen denselben Gegner} 12. dxc5 {Breutigam,Martin - and won! Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'This was Fischer's reaction the first time he faced the Keres variation(!) 1 month before the current game.' gespielt - und gewonnen!} dxc5 13. Nbd2 Qc7 $6 ({for zu} 13... Bb7 $1 {see Volokitin-Ragger in this database. siehe Volokitin-Ragger in dieser Datenbank.}) 14. Nf1 Nb6 15. Ne3 Rd8 16. Qe2 Be6 17. Nd5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer-Keres. Candidates Curacao.[7] 1962'} Nxd5 18. exd5 Bxd5 19. Nxe5 Ra7 20. Bf4 Qb6 21. Rad1 g6 22. Ng4 Nc4 23. Bh6 Be6 24. Bb3 Qb8 25. Rxd8+ Bxd8 26. Bxc4 bxc4 27. Qxc4 Qd6 28. Qa4 Qe7 29. Nf6+ Kh8 30. Nd5 Qd7 31. Qe4 Qd6 32. Nf4 Re7 33. Bg5 Re8 34. Bxd8 Rxd8 35. Nxe6 Qxe6 36. Qxe6 fxe6 37. Rxe6 Rd1+ 38. Kh2 Rd2 39. Rb6 Rxf2 40. Rb7 Rf6 41. Kg3 {1-0 Fischer,R-Keres,P, Curacao 1962.}) (12. Nbd2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} cxd4 (12... exd4 {Is the modern way of playing the Keres variation although it backfired badly for Svidler in his game against Inarkiev.} 13. cxd4 Nc6 14. d5 Nce5 15. Nxe5 Nxe5 16. a4 $1 Rb8 17. axb5 axb5 18. f4 Ng6 19. Nf3 Bh4 20. Rf1 Bg3 21. f5 Ne5 22. Ng5 Bh4 $2 23. f6 $1 $18 {1-0 (26) Inarkiev,E (2732)-Svidler,P (2745) Germany 2016}) 13. cxd4 Nc6 14. a3 (14. Nf1 exd4 $5 (14... Nb6 $6 15. b3 Bf6 16. Bb2 Bd7 17. Ne3 $16 {1/2 (48) Tal,M-Keres,P Moscow 1963}) 15. Nxd4 Nxd4 16. Qxd4 Ne5 17. Qd1 Bf6 18. Ne3 Be6 $13 {0-1 (52) Ivkov,B-Keres,P Beverwijk 1964}) 14... exd4 15. Nb3 Nde5 (15... d3 $5) 16. Nfxd4 Bf6 {0-1 (41) Tal,M-Keres,P Curacao 1962 Candidates [ChessBase/Keres/Tal]}) (12. a4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Rb8 13. axb5 axb5 14. dxc5 dxc5 15. Nbd2 Bb7 16. Nf1 Nc4 $1 17. Ne3 (17. b3 $5 Nd6 18. Ne3 $13) 17... Nxe3 18. Bxe3 Qc7 19. Qe2 c4 $13 {0-1 (32) Borisek,J (2541)-Karjakin,S (2763) Porto Carras 2011}) 12... Nb6 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Opens up the path for the bishop on c8 and at the same time takes aim at the d5-pawn, which is to be undermined by ...f5. ' Breutigam,Martin: 'Macht dem Läufer c8 die Sicht frei und nimmt zugleich Kontakt zum Bauern d5 auf, der mit ...f5 unterminiert werden soll. '} 13. g4 {Breutigam,Martin: '?' '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Breutigam,Martin: 'This does for the moment prevent ...f5, but it weakens the kingside too much; and in addition Black now gets rid of his bad bishop.' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer stops f5 in a rather radical way but Keres will be able to generate immediate counterplay. This must have been Fischer's preparation but it seems Black gets a clear edge.' Breutigam,Martin: 'Dies verhindert zwar bis auf Weiteres ...f5, schwächt aber zu sehr den Königsflügel; außerdem wird Schwarz nun seinen schlechten Läufer los.'} ({Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'Keres pointed out that after''  Besser ist} 13. Nbd2 {Breutigam,Martin is better.} f5 {Breutigam,Martin} (13... Bd7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 14. Nf1 Nb7 15. b3 a5 $14 {0-1 (58) Hou,Y (2680)-Caruana,F (2811) Wijk aan Zee 2018 CBM 183 [Roiz,M] The game was excellently annotated by GM Roiz in CBM 183.} (15... c4 $5)) 14. exf5 Bxf5 15. Bxf5 Rxf5 16. Ne4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '1/2 (31) Ponomariov,R (2705)-Mamedyarov,S (2752) Kemer 2007' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'is better for white'} Qd7 {White's d-pawn is in trouble because of the threat of 17...Qb7.}) 13... h5 {Breutigam,Martin: '!' '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} (13... Bd7 $6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon If Black doesn't act immediately White is able to control Black's counterplay on the kingside.} 14. Kh2 h5 $6 15. g5 $1 $16 {[%cal Ye1g1,Yf3h4,Yf2f4]}) 14. Nh2 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'Protecting the g-pawn but weakening the dark squares. Keres wastes no time in taking advantage of this.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A rather awkward retreat but there were no real choices.'} (14. gxh5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Bxh3 15. Nh2 Bg5 $17) 14... hxg4 15. hxg4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Bg5 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: '!'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres exchanges his dark-squared bishop highlighting White's weakness on the dark squares. '} 16. Nd2 g6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres starts the procedure of getting access to the very important h file.'} (16... Bf4 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 17. Ndf1 (17. Ndf3 $6 g5 $1 $17 {[%cal Yg8g7,Yf8h8]}) 17... g6 $17) 17. Ndf3 Bxc1 ({Black could also consider} 17... Bf4 {(Breutigam,Martin) . The text White to forestall Black's attack on the kingside by offering the exchange of queens, though admittedly the endgame is also rather poor.}) 18. Qxc1 Kg7 19. Qg5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer probably felt that he was losing the battle for the h-file so he made sure that Black will not have enough fire power to organise an attack against his king. Unfortunately the exchange of queens doesn't solve White's structural problems.'} Nb7 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'This allows White to get some counterplay on the queenside.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Although Black's position remains at least pleasant I don't fully understand his decision to retreat the knight on b7 instead of placing it more aggressively on c4. My guess would be that 21.b3 a3! was probably bothering him although the knight on a3 is out of danger and actually helps Black's queenside play.'} (19... Qxg5 {Breutigam,Martin} 20. Nxg5 Bd7 (20... Nac4 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 21. Rab1 (21. b3 Na3 $1 22. Bd1 (22. Bd3 c4 $19) 22... Rh8 $19 {[%cal Yf7f5]}) 21... Rh8 22. Bd3 Bd7 23. Kg2 Rh6 {[%cal Ya8h8,Yf7f5]}) 21. Kg2 c4 22. Nh3 Nb7 23. f4 f6 24. Kg3 Nc5 25. Nf3 Nba4 26. Rab1 a5 27. Nd2 Rh8 28. Nf2 Nb6 29. Rh1 b4 30. Rxh8 Rxh8 31. b3 cxb3 32. Nxb3 Nxb3 33. Bxb3 Rc8 34. cxb4 Rc3+ 35. Kg2 a4 36. Bd1 exf4 37. b5 a3 38. Bb3 Bxb5 39. Bd1 Rc5 40. Nd3 Bxd3 {0-1 Blatny,F-Filip,M, Prague 1963.}) ({In his notes to the game Keres preferred} 19... Bd7 {(Breutigam,Martin) , maintaining the initiative on both sides of the board.}) ({It was also worth thinking about keeping the queens on the board with} 19... f6 {Breutigam,Martin}) 20. Qxd8 Rxd8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: ''} 21. a4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer hurries to play a4 before Black plays d7 and makes it impossible or very difficult to achieve this advance.'} bxa4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres correctly chooses to spoil his pawn structure in order to create a target (b2) and get White's bishop. In the resulting position Black will be able to create more pressure against the white pawns because the white bishop would already be exchanged.'} (21... Bd7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 22. axb5 (22. a5 Nc4 23. b4 Rh8 $17) 22... axb5 23. b3 $1 $15) 22. Bxa4 Nxa4 23. Rxa4 Bd7 24. Ra2 {Breutigam,Martin: 'Nigel Davies: 'White now has some counterplay on the queenside, which makes it quite difficult for Black. He's still slightly better but only manages to score the full point after some ups and downs.'' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keeping the option of playing ea1 doesn't make much sense since Black will put his bishop on b5.'} (24. Raa1 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 24... c4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} 25. Nd2 Bb5 26. Nhf1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?'} (26. Kg2 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Rh8 (26... Nc5 27. Rh1 $1 Nd3 28. Nhf1 Nf4+ 29. Kg1 {White's setup seems rather stupid but it is not easy for Black to make progress.}) 27. Re3 $1 {[%cal Ye3h3] Exchanging a pair of rooks would make Black's initiative on the kingside a lot less lethal.} Nc5 28. Rh3 Rxh3 29. Kxh3 Rh8+ 30. Kg2 f6 $15) 26... Rh8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?'} (26... Nc5 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Bringing the knight to d3 and f4 would give black a serious attack against the white king.} 27. Ne3 Nd3 28. Rb1 Rh8) 27. Ne3 (27. f3 $1 {[%cal Ye1e2,Ye2h2] Kapnisis,Spyridon would give White good chances of surviving.}) 27... Rh4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} 28. Kg2 Rah8 29. Nf3 Rh3 30. Nf1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer transfers his knight to g3 to protect the pawn on e4 and help White exchange a pair of rooks.'} (30. Raa1 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Exchanging rooks with the help of the other rook is unadvisable since the pawn on b2 will be lost:} Nc5 31. Rh1 (31. Nd2 Nd3 $19) 31... Rxh1 32. Rxh1 Rxh1 33. Kxh1 Nxe4 $19 (33... Nd3 $19 {[%cal Yd3b2]})) 30... Nc5 31. Ng3 Bd7 (31... Nd3 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon would prevent White from exchanging rooks with 32.Rh1:} 32. Rh1 Nxf2 $1 $19) 32. g5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} f6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A serious mistake which allows Fischer to get back in the game.'} (32... Bg4 $19 {Kapnisis,Spyridon It is very difficult to suggest a plan for White. d3-f4 will soon lead to a decisive advantage for Black.}) 33. gxf6+ Kxf6 34. Re3 (34. Rh1 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Rxh1 35. Nxh1 Nxe4 36. Rxa6 Bh3+ 37. Kg1 Rb8 38. Ng3 Nxg3 39. fxg3 Rxb2 40. Rxd6+ Kf5 $17) 34... Ke7 35. Nd2 Rh2+ (35... Nd3 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 36. Rxd3 (36. Nxc4 Rh2+ 37. Kg1 Nf4 $19 {[%cal Yh2g2]}) 36... cxd3 37. Rxa6 Rf8 {[%cal Yh3h2]} 38. Ngf1 Rhh8 $19 {[%cal Yf8a8]}) 36. Kg1 Bb5 37. Ra1 R2h4 38. Kg2 Rf8 39. Rf3 (39. Rh1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Rxh1 40. Nxh1 Nd3 $17) 39... Rb8 40. Kg1 Ra8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres creates the threat of a5-a4 fixing the b2 pawn as an even bigger weakness.'} (40... Nd3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 41. b3 $1 (41. Rb1 Bd7 42. Nxc4 Nf4 $19 {[%cal Yb8h8,Yd7g4,Yh4h2]}) 41... cxb3 42. c4 b2 43. Rb1 Ba4 44. Rxd3 Bc2 45. Ra3 Bxb1 46. Nxb1 Rb4 47. Kg2 $15) 41. Ra5 Rc8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'c8 is the best square for the rook in order to put the knight on d3.'} 42. Ra3 (42. Ra1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nd3 $1 43. Rb1 Nf4 $19) 42... Ra8 (42... Nd3 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 43. Ra2 Ra8 $5 $19 {[%cal Ya6a5,Ya5a4]}) 43. Ra5 Ra7 44. Kg2 Nb7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres retreats his knight to achieve a5-a4 but this means that e4 will not be under pressure for a couple of moves. '} 45. Ra1 a5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} (45... Nc5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 46. Ngf1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer immediately takes advantage of the less pressure against e4 to regroup his knight to e3 and create some counterplay against the weak c4 pawn.'} Nc5 47. Ne3 a4 (47... Nxe4 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 48. Nxe4 Rxe4 49. Rh1 $1 Rd7 50. Kg3 $16 {[%csl Re4][%cal Yh1h6]}) (47... Nb3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 48. Nxb3 cxb3 49. Rg3 $13) 48. Rh3 (48. Rg3 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Kf6 49. Rf3+ $11 (49. Rg4 $5)) 48... Rxh3 49. Kxh3 Nd3 50. Ra2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer prefers to give up f2 in order to get c4, a questionable decision.'} (50. Kg3 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nxb2 51. Nc2 {The passed pawn on a4 is firmly blocked and it is not easy for Black to make progress.}) 50... Nxf2+ 51. Kg3 Nd3 52. Nexc4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Ra8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres tries to bring his rook to the kingside but he doesn't manage to do it in the best possible way.'} (52... Rc7 $1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 53. Na5 (53. Nb6 $2 Nc5 54. Kf3 Rb7 55. Nbc4 Rb8 $19 {[%csl Re4][%cal Yb8f8,Yf8f4]}) (53. Ne3 Rc8 54. Nd1 $5 Bd7 $19 {[%cal Yc8h8,Yg6g5,Yd3f4] it seems rather easy for Black to put his g6 passed pawn in motion which, in cooperation with the black pieces, will create big problems for White on the kingside.}) 53... Rc8 54. c4 Bd7 $17) 53. Nb6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer puts immediate pressure on a4 so that Keres doesn't have enough time to bring his rook to the kingside.'} Ra6 (53... Rh8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 54. c4 $1 Be8 55. Nxa4 Nf4 $44) 54. Nbc4 Nc5 55. Kf3 Ra8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} 56. b4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer takes advantage of the fact that Keres has kept his rook on the a-file.'} Nb3 57. Na3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} Bd7 (57... Be2+ {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 58. Ke3 Bd1 59. Nac4 $13 {[%cal Yc4b2]}) 58. Kg2 (58. Ndc4 $1 $13 {[%cal Ya2h2] Kapnisis,Spyridon Activating the rook through the second rank provides White with enough counterplay to secure equality.}) 58... Bg4 (58... g5 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 59. Nac4 (59. Rb2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nxd2 60. Rxd2 Rf8 61. Rd3 Kf6 {[%cal Yf6g5,Yg5f4]} 62. c4 Kg5 63. Nb5 $13) 59... Rc8 60. Ne3 (60. Rxa4 $5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Nxd2 61. Nxd2 Rxc3 62. Ra7+ Kf6 63. Kf2 $11) 60... Bd7 61. c4 Rb8 62. b5 Nc5 63. Nd1 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} Kd8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!?' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Keres transfers his king to the queenside in order to control White's passed pawn. If he is given the chance, the black king could penetrate White's position through the weak dark squares. '} 64. Nc3 Ra8 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '[#]'} 65. Nf3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!' Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'Fischer correctly activates the d2 which is not needed for the defence.'} (65. Kg3 {Kapnisis,Spyridon Activating the king may have been interesting but activating the knight looks more logical with the white king staying safe on g2.} Kc7 66. Kh4 Rh8+ 67. Kg5 Rh2 $15) 65... Kc7 66. Ng5 Kb6 67. Nf7 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} Rf8 {[#]} 68. Rf2 $2 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: 'A serious mistake which gives Black a winning advantage. Fischer should have sacrificed his knight to get a couple of Black's pawns and decent drawing chances.'} (68. Nxd6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon: '!'} Kc7 (68... Rf6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 69. Nxa4+ Nxa4 70. Rxa4 Rxd6 71. Ra6+ Kc5 72. Ra7 $1 $11) 69. Nxa4 Kxd6 (69... Ra8 70. Nf7 $11 Rxa4 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 71. Rxa4 Nxa4 72. Nxe5 {Black should be able to hold but he is the one who should be careful.}) 70. Nxc5 Kxc5 71. Ra7 Bg4 72. Kg3 Bf3 73. Rc7+ Kb6 74. Rc6+ Ka5 75. Rxg6 Bxe4 76. Re6 $11 Rf5 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} 77. d6 {White will soon get the e5-pawn and the game will end up in a draw.}) 68... a3 $19 69. Rf3 (69. Nxd6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon leads to some very entertaining variations:} Bh3+ $1 {wins an important tempo, giving Black a winning advantage.} (69... Rxf2+ $2 70. Kxf2 Kc7 71. Nf7 Nxe4+ 72. Nxe4 a2 {[#] Black will have a new queen in the next move but it will not suffice to get the point!} 73. Nc5 $1 {[%cal Yc5b3]} a1=Q 74. b6+ $1 Kc8 (74... Kxb6 75. Nxd7+ Ka5 76. Ndxe5 $11) 75. b7+ Kc7 76. b8=Q+ Kxb8 77. Nxd7+ Kb7 78. Ndxe5 {[#] White's threat of promoting the d5-pawn is very serious and Black has nothing better than to force a draw by perpetual.} Qd4+ 79. Kf3 g5 80. d6 Qf4+ $11) 70. Kg1 Rxf2 71. Kxf2 Kc7 72. Ne8+ (72. Nf7 Nxe4+ 73. Nxe4 a2 $19) 72... Kd8 73. Nd6 Ke7 $19) 69... Bg4 70. Rf2 a2 (70... Ka5 $5 $19 {Kapnisis,Spyridon}) 71. Nxa2 Nxe4 72. Rf1 Bf5 73. c5+ (73. Nh6 {Kapnisis,Spyridon} Bh3+ $19) 73... dxc5 0-1

