{Tal,M: 'Chess Informant'} 1. e4 {Kuzmin,Alexey Tal,M} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Qc7 5. Nc3 (5. Nb5) (5. c4) 5... e6 6. g3 a6 7. Bg2 Nf6 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bc5 10. Bf4 d6 (10... Bxd4 11. Bxc7 $16) 11. Qd2 h6 (11... Nd7) 12. Rad1 e5 13. Be3 Bg4 (13... Bxe3 14. fxe3 $1 Ke7 $2 15. Rxf6) 14. Bxc5 dxc5 (14... Qxc5 15. Qxd6 $16) 15. f3 Be6 16. f4 Rd8 ({Kuzmin,Alexey: 'Later Black started to prefer'} 16... O-O $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey: 'In a very recent game Kobalia - Geller, Sochi 2019, White continued'} 17. f5 (17. Nd5 {Kuzmin,Alexey} Bxd5 18. exd5 Qd6 19. fxe5 Qxe5 20. d6 {but after} Qxb2 {Black kept equality in a complicated deeply analysed variation.}) 17... Rad8 18. Qe3 Bc8 19. Nd5 Nxd5 20. exd5 f6 $14) (16... O-O $1 17. f5 Bc4 18. Rfe1 b5 19. b3 b4 $1 20. bxc4 bxc3 21. Qxc3 Rfd8 $44) 17. Nd5 Bxd5 18. exd5 {[ d6] Tal,M: '[#C5 d6]'} e4 (18... exf4 $2 19. Rde1+ $18) 19. Rfe1 $1 (19. d6 Qc6) ({Fischer could play} 19. c4 $16 {Kuzmin,Alexey and then start to lay siege to the е4-pawn. However, at that very moment he started a transposition to an advantageous ending.}) 19... Rxd5 20. Rxe4+ Kd8 ({Another retreat was weaker:} 20... Kf8 $2 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 21. Re8+ Kxe8 22. Bxd5 $16 {Keeping the queens is obviously to White’s advantage.}) 21. Qe2 (21. Qxd5+ Nxd5 22. Rxd5+ Kc8 23. Ree5 Kb8 $1 24. Rxc5 Qb6 $13) 21... Rxd1+ 22. Qxd1+ Qd7 (22... Kc8 23. Re5 Rd8 24. Qf3 $14) 23. Qxd7+ Kxd7 24. Re5 $16 b6 ({An unexpected} 24... Kd6 $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey was the correct decision.} 25. Bxb7 ({In the event of} 25. Bf1 b5 {White did not manage to activate the bishop.}) (25. b4 $5 {is interesting but after} b6 26. bxc5+ bxc5 27. Bf1 Rb8 $1 {Black holds the position.}) 25... Rb8 26. Bxa6 Rxb2 27. Bc4 Rxc2 28. Bxf7 c4 {Black has sufficient counterplay for maintaining equality.}) 25. Bf1 a5 26. Bc4 Rf8 27. Kg2 Kd6 28. Kf3 Nd7 29. Re3 Nb8 30. Rd3+ Kc7 31. c3 Nc6 32. Re3 Kd6 33. a4 Ne7 34. h3 Nc6 35. h4 (35. g4 g5) 35... h5 ({If Black continued to stay put -} 35... Ne7 {Kuzmin,Alexey White would break through the defence by the pawn storm} 36. g4 Nc6 37. h5 Ne7 38. g5 Nc6 39. g6 $1) 36. Rd3+ (36. g4 {Kuzmin,Alexey} hxg4+ 37. Kxg4 {with the idea to further exchange the f-pawn for the g-pawn was another strong plan. Black would be forced to build a passive defence on the 6th rank:} Ne7 38. Kg5 Ng8 $16) 36... Kc7 37. Rd5 (37. Re3 {Kuzmin,Alexey}) 37... f5 38. Rd2 Rf6 39. Re2 Kd7 40. Re3 g6 ({Taimanov should play} 40... Na7 $5 {Kuzmin,Alexey trying to transfer the knight on to d6.}) 41. Bb5 Rd6 42. Ke2 Kd8 ({The move} 42... Rf6 $1 {Kuzmin,Alexey was the only way to try and avoid the exchange of rooks.}) 43. Rd3 Kc7 44. Rxd6 $18 Kxd6 45. Kd3 Ne7 46. Be8 Kd5 47. Bf7+ Kd6 48. Kc4 Kc6 49. Be8+ Kb7 50. Kb5 Nc8 51. Bc6+ Kc7 52. Bd5 Ne7 ({The counterplay attempt} 52... Nd6+ {Kuzmin,Alexey} 53. Ka6 Ne4 {cannot save him:}) 53. Bf7 Kb7 54. Bb3 Ka7 55. Bd1 Kb7 56. Bf3+ Kc7 ({After} 56... Ka7 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 57. c4 {Black immediately falls into zugzwang and is forced to let the opponent’s king invade -} Ng8 58. Kc6 Nf6 59. Kd6 Ne4+ 60. Ke6 Nxg3 61. Kf6 {with an easy win.}) 57. Ka6 Ng8 (57... Nc8 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 58. Bd5 {1-0 (71) Fischer,R-Taimanov,M Vancouver 1971 CBM 196 [Kuzmin,Alexey]} Nd6 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 59. Bg8 Ne4 60. Bf7 Nxg3 61. Bxg6 {[#]} {Now White's aim consists in forcing the exchange of the h5-pawn vs the f4-pawn. After doing this, the passed pawn on the h-file will easily decide the issue.} Kc6 62. Be8+ Kc7 63. Ka7 (63. Kb5 $18 {also wins.}) {Zugzwang! The knight has to retreat.} 63... Ne2 ({Neither} 63... Kd8 64. Bc6 Kc7 65. Bf3 $18) ({nor} 63... c4 64. Ka6 {can help - in both cases White wins easily.} Ne2 65. Bxh5 Nxf4 66. Bf3 $18) 64. Bxh5 Nxf4 65. Bf3 $18 {and White wins.}) 58. Bd5 Ne7 59. Bc4 Nc6 (59... Kc6 {Kuzmin,Alexey} 60. Bf7) 60. Bf7 Ne7 61. Be8 Kd8 62. Bxg6 ({Another version of the sacrifice -} 62. Kxb6 $2 {Kuzmin,Alexey} Kxe8 63. Kxa5 {- would be a blunder!:} Kd7 64. Kb5 Kd6 65. a5 Nd5 66. a6 Nc7+ 67. Kb6 c4 68. a7 Na8+ $1 69. Kb7 Kd7 {with a draw.}) 62... Nxg6 63. Kxb6 Kd7 64. Kxc5 Ne7 65. b4 axb4 66. cxb4 Nc8 67. a5 Nd6 68. b5 Ne4+ 69. Kb6 Kc8 70. Kc6 Kb8 71. b6 1-0

