1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'In Santa Monica 1966 Spassky played'} 7... O-O 8. c3 d5 {Fischer-Spassky/Santa Monica/1966/½/35/ Nagesh,Havanur: 'the Marshall Attack leading to draw. He had used the same system with Tal in the Candidates' Match 1965 drawing all his games.'}) 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 {Breyer Nagesh,Havanur: 'The Breyer Variation, a favourite of Spassky.'} ({In the Havana Olympiad he had played} 9... h6 {(Smyslov Variation) and the game was a hard-fought draw. However, the variation had received some hard knocks in the games, Fischer-Gligoric, Rovinj/ Zagreb 1970 and Fischer-Ivkov, 1970 Interzonal.}) 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Fischer decided not to play his old line'} 11. Nh4 {Simagin Nagesh,Havanur: 'on account of'} exd4 (11... Nxe4 12. Nf5 Ndf6 13. Qf3 Bxf5 $44 (13... Bb7 $44)) 12. cxd4 Nb6 13. Nd2 c5 {Nagesh,Havanur: '=' '=' '=' Nagesh,Havanur: 'that led to an easy draw in Robert Byrne-Boris Spassky, Moscow 1971.'} 14. Bc2 cxd4 15. Nhf3 Re8 16. Nxd4 Bf8 17. b3 Bb7 $10 {Byrne-Spassky/Moskau/1971/}) (11. c4 c6 12. c5 Qc7 13. cxd6 Bxd6 14. Bg5 exd4 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Qxd4 Ne5 17. Nbd2 Rd8 18. Qe3 Nd3 $1 19. Qh6 Bf4 20. Qxf6 Rd6 {Fischer-Portisch/Santa Monica/1966/½/71/}) 11... Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. b4 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'This plan preventing the freeing advance...c5 was popular in those days.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'In his own games with White Spassky had preferred'} 13. Nf1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'and currently it is in vogue.'} Bf8 14. Ng3 g6 15. Bg5 h6 16. Bd2 Bg7 $11) 13... Bf8 (13... a5 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!?' '!?'} 14. Nb3 axb4 15. cxb4 Rb8 16. Na5 c6 (16... Ba8 17. d5) 17. Nxb7 Rxb7 18. Bb3 $16 {Suetin-Tringov/Titove Uzice/1966/}) (13... exd4 $5) (13... d5 $5) 14. a4 (14. Bb2 { a3}) 14... Nb6 (14... a5 15. bxa5 (15. axb5 axb4 16. Bb2 bxc3 17. Bxc3 c6 $10 {Kavalek-Portisch/Wijk aan Zee/1969/}) 15... Rxa5 16. Rb1 $16 Ba6 (16... Qa8 17. axb5 exd4 18. cxd4 Nxe4 19. Nxe4 Bxe4 20. Rxe4 $1 Rxe4 21. Ng5 Rh4 22. g3 $1 $16 (22. Nxf7 $6 Kxf7 23. Bg5 Re4 24. Qf3+ Nf6 25. Bxf6 Re1+ $10 {Geller-Portisch/1973/})) 17. d5 Qa8 18. Ba3 c5 19. dxc6 Qxc6 20. Bb4 {Beljavsky-Petrosjan/1973/}) (14... c5 15. bxc5 exd4 16. cxd4 dxc5 $14 {Balashov-Podgaez}) (14... d5 15. Nxe5 (15. dxe5 Nxe4 16. Nxe4 dxe4 17. Bg5 $1 f6 18. Bxe4 Bxe4 19. Rxe4 Nxe5 20. Rd4 Nxf3+ 21. Qxf3 Qc8 $10 {Wasjukov-Zuidema/Wijk aan Zee/1973/}) 15... dxe4 16. f4 exf3 17. Ndxf3 Nxe5 18. Nxe5 Bd6 19. Bf4 $14 {Wasjukov-Cholmov/Dubna/1973/}) 15. a5 (15. axb5 axb5 $11) 15... Nbd7 16. Bb2 (16. Rb1 d5 $1 $10 {Savon-Voigt/Skopje ol/1972/}) 16... Qb8 { 17... c5 Nagesh,Havanur: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!?'' Nagesh,Havanur: 'A novelty in this position. Otherwise the idea is known. The move indirectly defends e5 and targets White's b-pawn. However, its drawback is that it shuts out the QR.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Later'} 16... Rb8 {= Nagesh,Havanur: 'followed by...Ba8 came to be seen as the antidote to White's play. Consequently the line with 13. b4 was given up.'} 17. Rb1 Ba8 18. Ba1 g6 19. c4 bxc4 (19... exd4 20. cxb5 axb5 21. Nxd4 d5 22. N4f3 dxe4 23. Ng5 e3 $1 24. Bb3 exf2+ 25. Kxf2 Bd5 $10 {Keene}) 20. dxe5 Nxe5 21. Nxe5 dxe5 22. Bc3 Bc6 23. Qe2 Bb5 24. Nxc4 c5 25. bxc5 Rc8 $1 {Browne-Karpov/Amsterdam/1976/}) (16... g6 {=}) 17. Rb1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'Gligoric,Svetozar: '!'' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Indirectly defending the b-pawn.'} ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'Here'} 17. c4 $1 {!?} bxc4 18. Ba4 {'' Nagesh,Havanur: 'recommended by Timman and Kasparov is met by'} (18. Nxc4 $5 {deserves attention. If} exd4 19. Qxd4 $14 {White has more space and better development.}) 18... c6 (18... Rd8 $142 $10 {Anand(1989)}) (18... exd4 19. Nxd4 (19. Bxd4 Re7 $11) 19... Ne5 20. Bxe8 Qxe8 $44) 19. Nxc4 Qc7 (19... exd4 20. Qxd4 d5 21. exd5 Rxe1+ 22. Nxe1 Nxd5 23. Nd3 $16 {Kavalek-Reshevsky/Chicago/1973/}) 20. dxe5 dxe5 21. Qb3 $16 {Savon-Muschin/USSR/1973/}) 17... c5 18. bxc5 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'The battle is for the control of e5 and this move strikes at d6, t