{49: Personal Style: 'FISCHER (Born 1943) Usually the style of a chessmaster has to grow; it may take years before one may speak of any style at all. As a rule the young player begins with pure combination, and then in the course of time finds that he must add water to win. He learns the methods of positional play, and develops endgame technique. It is quite possible to begin as a combinative player - a tactician - and yet in maturing to acquire an out-and-out positional style of play. The exceptional Grandmaster Fischer, however, is another and almost unique story in modern chess. At the age of twenty he already possessed a fully rounded style. He is both tactician and strategist, an openings expert without peer, and a virtuoso of the endgame. The following game against Reshevsky is a good example of his super-class, technical style '} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3 g6 (6... b5 {Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Varna ol 1962}) 7. g4 Bg7 8. g5 $5 (8. Be3 O-O 9. g5 Ne8 $1) (8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 $10) 8... Nh5 $1 (8... Nfd7 9. Be3 Nc6 10. Qd2 $16) 9. Be2 e5 (9... Nc6 $5 10. Nb3 (10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Bxh5 gxh5 12. Qxh5 Rb8 $44)) 10. Nb3 (10. Nf5 gxf5 11. Bxh5 f4) 10... Nf4 11. Nd5 $10 (11. Bc4 Nc6 12. Nd5) 11... Nxd5 (11... O-O {Tal,M 49: Personal Style: ''} 12. h4 (12. Nxf4 exf4 13. Bxf4 Bxb2 14. Bxd6) 12... f5 (12... Nd7 $5 13. Nxf4 exf4 14. Qxd6 Be5)) (11... Nxe2 12. Qxe2 Be6 $10) (11... Nxh3 $2 12. Be3 { b6}) 12. Qxd5 Nc6 (12... Be6 {Tal,M} 13. Qxb7 Nd7 14. Be3 $16) 13. Bg4 Bxg4 (13... f5 {Tal,M} 14. gxf6 (14. exf5 Ne7 $1 15. Qd3 gxf5 16. Bh5+ Ng6) 14... Qxf6 15. Bxc8 Rxc8 16. c3 Rc7 17. Be3 $16 { 0-0-0}) (13... Qe7 {[%cal Yc8e6]} 14. Z0 Be6 $140) 14. hxg4 Qc8 $1 { xg4; b4} 15. Qd1 (15. Qxd6 Qxg4 16. Qd3 Rd8 17. Qe2 Qg2 18. Rf1 h6 $36) 15... Nd4 $2 (15... Qe6 {Tal,M} 16. Be3 O-O-O $10) (15... d5 $5 16. exd5 (16. Qe2 d4) (16. c3 d4) 16... Nb4 17. c3 (17. O-O Nxc2 18. Rb1 O-O) 17... Qc4 $1 18. cxb4 $2 (18. Rh3 Nxd5 (18... Qe4+ 19. Kf1 Nc2 $2 20. Nd2 $18) 19. Qe2 Qc7 $10) 18... Qe4+ 19. Kd2 Rd8 20. Kc3 Bf8 $1 21. a3 (21. Nc5 Bxc5 22. bxc5 Rxd5 $19) 21... Rc8+ 22. Kd2 (22. Nc5 $2 Bxc5 23. bxc5 Rxc5+ 24. Kb3 Qc4#) 22... Be7 $1 $40) 16. c3 Nxb3 17. axb3 Qe6 18. Ra5 $1 f6 $2 (18... O-O {} 19. Rd5 Rad8 { f7-f5}) (18... b5 {} 19. Qd5 Qxd5 20. exd5 Kd7) 19. Qd5 $1 Qxd5 (19... Qxg4 $2 20. Qxb7 O-O 21. gxf6 {49: Personal Style: '!'}) 20. Rxd5 Kd7 21. gxf6 Bxf6 22. g5 {49: Personal Style: '!'} Be7 (22... Bg7 23. Rd3 {[%cal Gd3h3]} Z0 24. Rdh3 $140 {[%csl Rh7] winning the h-pawn}) 23. Ke2 $16 {× d6, h7} Raf8 24. Be3 Rc8 25. b4 {49: Personal Style: 'Intending in due course to dissolve his doubled pawn by b5, and saddle Black with a weak pawn on the Queenside '} (25. c4 Kc7 26. b4 { c4-c5; b4-b5; f2-f4}) 25... b5 $5 {49: Personal Style: 'This should be avoided if possible. Black now has three vulnerbla points - a6, d6 and h7. How to profit from a situation like this was the subject of "Technique of Maneuvering". The present game prettily supplements the examples there given '} 26. Rdd1 {49: Personal Style: '!'} Ke6 27. Ra1 Rc6 28. Rh3 {49: Personal Style: 'threatening to win the h-pawn '} (28. Rh4 $2 h5 $1) 28... Bf8 (28... d5 $5 29. exd5+ Kxd5 30. Rah1 Kc4 31. Rxh7 Rxh7 32. Rxh7 Re6 33. Kd2 (33. Kf3 $2 e4+ 34. Kf4 Kb3) 33... Kb3 34. Kd3 Kxb2 35. Ke4 $1 Bf8 (35... Kxc3 36. Kd5 Rd6+ 37. Kxe5 Bf8 38. Rc7+ $1 Kb3 39. Rc8 Be7 40. Re8 Rd7 41. Ke6 $18) 36. Rh8 Bg7 37. Rg8 Re7 38. Rc8 $1 $18 { c6}) (28... Z0 29. Rah1 {[%csl Gh7]}) 29. Rah1 Rc7 (29... d5 $2 30. exd5+ Kxd5 31. Rxh7 Rxh7 32. Rxh7 Kc4 33. Rf7 Rc8 34. Rf6 Kb3 35. Rxa6 Kxb2 36. Bd2) 30. Rh4 $1 {49: Personal Style: 'Putting Black in a state of zugzwang '} d5 (30... Rc4 31. f3 Rc7 32. Kf2 $1 (32. R4h3 $1) 32... d5 33. Ra1 Rc6 34. exd5+ Kxd5 35. Rd1+ Ke6 36. Rd8 $18) (30... Rf7 31. Ra1) 31. Ra1 $1 (31. exd5+ Kxd5 32. Rd1+ Ke6 33. Rd8 Bg7 $1) 31... Rc6 (31... dxe4 32. Rxa6+ Kd5 (32... Kf5 33. Rf6#) 33. Rb6 $18 (33. Rxg6 {[%cal Gh4h8] loses a pawn})) 32. exd5+ Kxd5 33. Rd1+ Ke6 34. Rd8 Kf5 (34... Rc7 35. Ra8 $18) (34... Z0 35. Re8+ $140 Kf5 36. Rxe5+ Kxe5 37. Bd4+ Kf5 38. Bxh8 Kxg5 39. Rxh7) 35. Ra8 Re6 36. Rh3 $1 Bg7 (36... Kg4 37. Rg3+ Kh5 (37... Kh4 38. Rf3 Bg7 39. Ra7 Rg8 40. Rd7 $1 {[%cal Gd7d1,Gd1h1] Black is in a mating net}) 38. Rf3 Bg7 39. Rxh8 Bxh8 40. Rf8 Bg7 41. Rf7 $18) (36... Ke4 37. Rf3 Bg7 38. Rxh8 Bxh8 39. Rf8 Bg7 40. Rf7 Bh8 41. f3+ Kd5 42. Rxh7 Re8 43. Kd3 $18) (36... Z0 37. Rf3+ $140) 37. Rxh8 Bxh8 38. Rxh7 Re8 39. Rf7+ Kg4 (39... Ke4 {49: Personal Style: ''} 40. f3+ Kd5 41. Kd3 $18) (39... Ke6 40. Ra7 $18) 40. f3+ Kg3 41. Kd3 $2 {49: Personal Style: 'An aethestic flaw '} (41. Kf1 $1 $18 { f2+ 49: Personal Style: ''} Z0 (41... Kh4 42. Kg2) 42. Bf2+ Kh3 43. Rh7#) 41... e4+ (41... Rc8 42. Bc5 $18) 42. fxe4 Rd8+ 43. Bd4 Kg4 44. Rf1 Be5 (44... Kxg5 45. Rg1+ Kf4 46. Rxg6) 45. Ke3 Bc7 (45... Bxd4+ 46. cxd4 Kxg5 47. e5) 46. Rg1+ Kh4 (46... Kh5 47. Kf3 Rd7 48. e5 Rf7+ 49. Ke4 Rf5 50. e6 Bd8 51. Bf6 Bxf6 52. gxf6 Rxf6 53. Ke5 Rf2 54. Re1 {1-0 (54) Fischer-Reshevsky New York 1962 The MiddleGame - Two [49: Personal Style]}) 47. Kf3 Rd7 (47... Rf8+ 48. Bf6 Kh3 49. Rh1+ Bh2 50. e5 Re8 51. Ke4) 48. e5 Rf7+ 49. Ke4 Rf5 50. e6 Bd8 51. Bf6 $1 Bxf6 52. gxf6 Rxf6 53. Kd5 Rf2 54. Re1 1-0

