{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 38 Detective story. Occasionally one comes across a miraculous victory in which, despite intensive post-mortems, there seems to be no losing move or pattern, no blunder on the part of the vanquished. But how can that be possible? A loser must make a mistake somewhere, however infinitesimal, however it may evade detection. Is it Keres' opening novelty which leads him to disaster? Could his defense have been improved afterward? If so: where? Whatever the answer, the reader is invited to share the magnifying glass with Fischer and hunt for that elusive error. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'I think everyone has one game that first comes in mind at the simplest mention of a player's name (try it thinking about your favourite players and you will understand what I mean). I like many of Fischer's victories, but this is "that game" for me. '} 1. e4 {Quintiliano,Renato Bobby Fischer} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 ({Some years later, Fischer also gave new life to} 4. Bxc6 {Quintiliano,Renato} dxc6 5. O-O $1 {with nice victories against Gligoric and Portisch.}) 4... Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The good old days. No computers, no Berlin, no deeply-studied Marshall Gambit... '} 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 ({For} 9. d4 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 36. Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V Stockholm izt 1962}) 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Nd7 {Bobby Fischer: '; "Now it is not easy to fmd a satisfactory continuation for White." (KERES.) Keres' novelty, introduced on this occasion, has since become quite fashionable. I was - and still am - unimpressed. Black loses time redeveloping his knight to b6, but the K-side is weakened by its absence and it's questionable whether the Knight is not better where it stands originally. ' Quintiliano,Renato: '!?' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move rightly receives the name of Keres Variation. It failed to impress Fischer in this game, as for his notes he thought the manoeuvre f6-d7-b6 was simply a waste of time and left Black's kingside unprotected. Althought he succesfully proved his points in the present game, we can tell from the future that Keres "won" the theoretical discussion, as his idea survived the test of the time and it is actually a very interesting line, in which we can find many recent games in the database. '} 12. dxc5 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: ''!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer gives an exclamation mark to this capture, probably influenced by how well it worked in the game.'} (12. Nbd2 {Bobby Fischer: 'was all the rage, but' Quintiliano,Renato: 'is the most played, and Black has two paths to follow: the classic'} cxd4 ({Or the modern line} 12... exd4 $5 {Quintiliano,Renato} 13. cxd4 Nc6 14. d5 Nce5 {which leads to complicated and unbalanced positions, this has been the most played by Black in the last years.}) 13. cxd4 Nc6 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'may equalize.' Quintiliano,Renato: '=' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with nice chances of equalizing, as Black' position remains solid as usual.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'But not'} 12. dxe5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'gives Black the option of'} Nxe5 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with complete freedom.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with favourable simplifications.'}) ({"In spite of having won this game, it is probable that Fischer is not very convinced of the correctness of this continuation, because in a later game he closed the center with Quintiliano,Renato: 'In a later round in the same tournament, Fischer tried'} 12. d5 {(Bobby Fischer) ." (KERES.) According to that logic, Keres must not be convinced of the correctness of 11...Nd7 since he later varied with the old 11...Qc7 (against Gligorich at Hastings, 1965). Quintiliano,Renato: 'which is also playable, but he didn't play well and soon got an inferior position, allowing Keres to strike back in the line.'}) 12... dxc5 13. Nbd2 Qc7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; This is supposed to lead to trouble. If such a natural developing move is bad, then what kind of a position is this for Black?' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The start of a wrong plan, which aims to face the manoeuvre f1-e3-d5 by defending that square with as many pieces as possible. Fischer poses the following funny question here: "If such a natural developing move is bad, what should we think about this position for Black?". Although it makes sense, we should not take such evaluation as definitive. The only possible conclusion is that Keres didn't find the best way to deal with White's plan of manoeuvring the knight to d5.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'At the time Boleslavsky in his notes gave "' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In his notes, Boleslavsky recommends'} 13... f6 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '" and this cryptic evaluation apparently cowed the chess world into abandoning 12.dxc5 - until very recently. After' Quintiliano,Renato: 'as the equalising path'} 14. Nh4 Nb6 15. Nf5 Rf7 {Bobby Fischer: '(Fischer-Ivkov, Havana, 1965)'} 16. Qg4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer gives this as an improvement'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'instead of my' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Over'} 16. Nxe7+ $2 {Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Habana 1965 Quintiliano,Renato: 'that just helped Black in'}) 16... Kh8 17. h4 $1 { h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening h5 followed by Nf3-h4 is in White's favor: e.g.,' Quintiliano,Renato: 'planning to increase the pressure on the kingside by means of h5-f3-h4-g6, when Black does not have a clear way to prevent this plan, for example:'} g6 18. Nh6 Rg7 19. Qf3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: '''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and White keeps the initiative.'}) ({Later, Black found the right plan:} 13... Bb7 {Quintiliano,Renato} 14. Nf1 Nc4 $1 15. b3 Nd6 {[%csl Ye4];0-1 Velimirovic,D (2484)-Blagojevic,D (2486) YUG-chT Herceg Novi 2001 (5) and by attacking e4, Black prevents the knight from making it to d5.}) 14. Nf1 Nb6 15. Ne3 Rd8 16. Qe2 Be6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black has apparently managed to establish firm control over d5. '} 17. Nd5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', The idea is to open lines and take advantage of Black's weakened K-side. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'But Fischer doesn't care!'} Nxd5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In the event of'} 17... Bxd5 18. exd5 f6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer indicates the strong idea'} 19. h4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is strong. Now if'} Nxd5 (19... Nac4 20. h5 Nd6 21. Qd3 $1 e4 22. Rxe4 $1 Nxe4 23. Qxe4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'the attack seems winning for White.'}) 20. h5 $40 { e4  e4  e4  e4 Bobby Fischer: '(threatening 21.Qe4) is powerful.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with the queen coming to e4, Black is under serious pressure.'}) 18. exd5 Bxd5 19. Nxe5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Since his early attacking days, Keres has switched to positional-defensive chess. But this type of position is too much even for him. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'At first sight, we could think that White didn't accomplish too much with the last operation, as the central pawns were just exchanged and nothing terrible seems to be happening for Black here. But if we examine the position more closely, it will be clear that Black is the side who needs to be careful in his next moves: the c2 became very active, and his king looks dangerously abandoned on g8.'} Ra7 {Bobby Fischer: '; To defend the second rank. What else can Black do? White threatens to build up with Bf4 and Rad1, and already sacrificial themes are in the aIr.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move seemed awkward to me, and I can still remember how perplexed I was when seeing Fischer's analysis and being forced to agree with him that there is nothing better for Black. White is threatening to build annoying pressure by simple moves like f4-ad1, and some sacrificial attacking ideas are already in the air. I really like this position, especially because Fischer insists on supporting his evaluation with concrete lines. Let's check them:'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 19... Bd6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'loses due to'} 20. Qd3 $1 Bxe5 21. Qxh7+ Kf8 22. f4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'winning a piece.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 19... Be6 20. Nxf7 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'wins a healthy pawn.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 19... Bf8 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'looks a solid retreat, but after'} 20. Qh5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is forced to compromise his kingside structure.'} g6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 20... h6 21. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with more than one dangerous threat.'}) 21. Qh4 Bg7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 21... Be7 22. Qg3 { xg6  xg6  xg6  xg6 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening 23.Nxg6.'}) 22. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White has a strong attack.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally'} 19... f6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'allows a beautiful attack:'} 20. Qh5 $1 fxe5 21. Bxh7+ Kf8 22. Rxe5 Bf7 23. Rf5 Bf6 24. Rxf6 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'White invests more material to force the black king to go back to the centre, where it will not survive too long.'} gxf6 25. Bh6+ Ke7 26. Re1+ Be6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 26... Kd6 27. Bf4+ Kc6 28. Be4+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 27. Bf5 Rd6 28. Bf4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) (19... Re8 $2 20. Qd3 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 20. Bf4 Qb6 {Bobby Fischer: '; The threat was 21.Ng6.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Not much is changed by'} 20... Qc8 21. Rad1 {Bobby Fischer: 'quietly continues the build-up.' Quintiliano,Renato: ''}) 21. Rad1 $1 { xd5  xd5  xd5  xd5 Bobby Fischer: 'Threatening' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Simple and strong, creating the threat of xd5 followed by e4. Instead of looking for forced sequences, Fischer just improves his position.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Instead of seeking a "violent solution," my instinct told me to strengthen the position. '} g6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Eliminating stock combinations against h7, but creating new weaknesses on the dark squares.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'There is no simple solution for Black. Keres prevents ideas on h7, but on the other hand weakens the dark squares around his king.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'What's better? On'} 21... Bxa2 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Sometimes, if you are going to suffer, it is better to do it by grabbing a pawn. So, had Fischer just ignored or forgot the a2-pawn? Of course not! In that case, Black would fall into a forced sequence, which I have no doubt that Fischer had calculated until the very end:'} 22. Rxd8+ Qxd8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 22... Bxd8 23. Nc4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'revealing problems on the back rank'} Qe6 24. Qd1 Rd7 25. Nd2 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 23. b4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'a strong blow'} cxb4 24. cxb4 Bxb4 {Bobby Fischer: '(the Knight can't move because of Nc6)'} (24... Nc4 25. Nc6 $18) 25. Qe4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'White sacrifices the rook, but in return manages to break through Black's defences'} Bxe1 26. Qxh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8+ Ke7 28. Bg5+ f6 29. Ng6+ Kd7 30. Bf5+ Kc7 31. Bf4+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins the Queen.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and Black loses the queen.'}) (21... Z0 {Bobby Fischer} 22. Rxd5 Rxd5 23. Qe4) 22. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer immediately turns his attention to the new weakened squares on the kingside.'} Nc4 {Bobby Fischer: '; An attempt to bring this Knight toward the embattled sector.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres struggles to bring his pieces on the queenside to more useful positions.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 22... Bxa2 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'is still bad'} 23. Rxd8+ Qxd8 24. Bh6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has just too many threats. For example,' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and White has too many threats. For instance:'} f5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 24... f6 25. b3 $1) ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 24... Bf8 25. Qe8 Ra8 26. Nf6+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Kh8 27. Bxf8 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 25. Qe5 $1 Bd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 25... Bf8 26. Qe8 Qxe8 27. Rxe8 Rf7 28. Ne5 Rf6 29. Nd7 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 26. Rd1 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'a very precise move, Black is simply defenceless against so many threats'} Nc4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 26... fxg4 27. Rxd6 Rd7 28. Qg7+ $3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Quintiliano,Renato: '+-!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''+-!!''}) 27. Qe6+ Kh8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 27... Rf7 28. b3 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} fxg4 29. bxc4 Qh4 30. Rxd6 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Qxh6 ({after} 30... Qxf2+ {Quintiliano,Renato} 31. Kh1 $1 $18 {there is no perpetual for Black, but there is mate for White.}) 31. Rd8+ Kg7 32. Qe5+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 28. b3 $1 fxg4 29. bxc4 Rd7 30. Bg5 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 23. Bh6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Another simple, yet strong move.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Some recommended the more direct' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer mentions that someone suggested the variation'} 23. Nh6+ Kg7 24. Rxd5 Rxd5 25. Nxf7 {Bobby Fischer: 'I thought this might win at the time, but it looked speculative - and' Quintiliano,Renato: 'but after'} Qf6 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'refutes.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is the side with a winning position.'}) {Bobby Fischer: 'Since Black is tied up in knots, I felt sure of a patient strategical victory. '} 23... Be6 (23... Nxb2 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses to' Quintiliano,Renato: 'now loses due to'} 24. Rxd5 $1 Rxd5 25. Be4 Rd8 26. Qxb2 f5 27. c4 $1 $18 { g7#  g7#  g7#  g7# Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '(threatening Qg7 mate).' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and White wins.'}) 24. Bb3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Pinning the Knight and piling on the pressure. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'It is very nice how Fischer manages to increase the pressure on Black's camp by means of natural moves.'} Qb8 {× e5 × e5 × e5 × e5 Bobby Fischer: '; To prevent White's Queen, in some variations, from penetrating to e5. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is basically forced to keep an eye on e5, as after a possible exchange on c4, an appearance of the White queen on e5 would be devastating.'} 25. Rxd8+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Bxd8 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The lesser evil, but this provides other tactical motifs for White.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 25... Qxd8 $2 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'would be even worse:'} 26. Bxc4 Bxc4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 26... bxc4 27. Qe5 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 27. Nf6+ $1 Kh8 28. Qe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and Black can't avoid mate in the next moves.'}) 26. Bxc4 bxc4 27. Qxc4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Cashing in! "Converting a spatial advantage into a material one." (See Evans' New Ideas in Chess.) ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Converting the positional advantage into a material one. The back rank becomes the main source of problems for Black now, as Fischer skilfully demonstrates from now.'} Qd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 27... Qxb2 28. Rxe6 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!''}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 27... Bxc4 $4 28. Re8#) 28. Qa4 Qe7 29. Nf6+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Kh8 30. Nd5 Qd7 31. Qe4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Back to the old stand, angling for e5 again. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Back to the old juicy e5-square.'} Qd6 (31... Bxd5 $2 32. Qe8+ {Bobby Fischer: 'mates.'} Qxe8 33. Rxe8#) {Bobby Fischer: 'The weakness of Black's first rank has consistently proved to be his undoing throughout the mid-game. '} 32. Nf4 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer grabs a second pawn, which is enough to win the game.'} (32. c4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is also good.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'was a slower way, also enough to win.'}) 32... Re7 {Bobby Fischer: '; Token resistance. '} 33. Bg5 ({Quintiliano,Renato: 'It is a little mysterious why Fischer didn't play'} 33. Bf8 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins outright.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'here, that basically would force Black to resign.'}) 33... Re8 34. Bxd8 Rxd8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White also wins after'} 34... Qxd8 35. Qe5+ f6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 35... Kg8 36. Nd5 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!''}) 36. Qxc5 Bxa2 37. Rxe8+ Qxe8 38. c4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is the easiest path to victory.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with an easy endgame.'}) 35. Nxe6 Qxe6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 35... Re8 36. Qe5+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.'}) 36. Qxe6 fxe6 37. Rxe6 Rd1+ 38. Kh2 Rd2 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres probably put his hopes on this rook ending in view of his active rook, but Fischer finds a precise idea to clarify things: '} 39. Rb6 Rxf2 40. Rb7 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is a pawn and a king down now, which means he is technically lost.'} Rf6 41. Kg3 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns. Not only is Black a Pawn behind, but his King on the first rank is cut off as well.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'You can argue this is not the most beautiful game of Fischer and I would agree, of course. We didn't see either spectacular sacrifices or unexpected original ideas like his most famous games, but I really like the way he developed a strong initiative right from the opening and kept it until the very end, supporting even the most natural moves by concrete calculation. It's remarkable that on move 19 Black is almost running out of moves, and White's position is so superior that against any move there is a tactical refutation. I believe there is something poetic about such games as well. After all, it is a true masterpiece.'} ({Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres saw no reason to offer further resistance, as any Soviet master would know this endgame is beyond any chance of salvation. But let's see a possible line to make things clear:'} 41. Kg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'A likely winning line is'} Kg8 42. b4 cxb4 43. cxb4 Rd6 44. a4 Rd3+ 45. Kh2 Ra3 46. a5 Ra4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if' Quintiliano,Renato: 'If'} 46... Kh8 47. Kg1 Ra2 48. Kf1 Kg8 49. Ke1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White just brings the king to the queenside and wins, as if'} Rxg2 50. b5 axb5 51. a6 Ra2 52. a7 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 47. Kg3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the King invades!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and again the king marches to the queenside, with an easy win.'}) 1-0

