{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 6 A small oversight. Fischer's openini repertoire has been less extensive than most practicing Grandmasters', yet his contributions to theory have been numerous. 20 g5!? is one of his innovations. However. it is not responsible for Shocron's defeat. Neither Is Shocron's response; In retrospect. his system of defense seems sutprlslllfly adequate. Neventheless. after defending sensibly. Shocron outfoxes himself. Thinking he has seen one move further than his adversary; he provokes a combination. but his vision is one move short. In consequence, though otherwise it had withstood all of Fischer's assaults, his game crumbles. '} 1. e4 {Shipov,Sergei Shipov,Sergei Bobby Fischer Shipov,Sergei: 'Shipov'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O (7... d6 {Bobby Fischer} 8. c3 {1-0 (40) Fischer,R-Shocron,R Mar del Plata 1959 My 60 Memorable Games [Bobby Fischer]}) 8. c3 d6 9. h3 ({F or an immediate} 9. d4 $5 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 36.}) 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 ({For Keres'} 11... Nd7 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 38.}) 12. Nbd2 Bd7 ({Some alternatives are:} 12... Kh8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Re8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Rd8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Nc6 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Bb7 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Nd7 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... cxd4 {(Bobby Fischer) . I don't think there's any easy way for black to achieve equality- but who knows?}) 13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3 g6 15. dxe5 (15. Bd2 Bf8 {(Bobby Fischer) and now: The quiet} 16. Rc1 ({The sharp} 16. b4 cxb4 17. cxb4 Nc4 18. Nxc4 bxc4 19. Rc1 exd4 20. Nxd4 d5 ({not} 20... c3 21. Bb3 Nxe4 22. Rxe4 $1 Rxe4 23. Qf3 Bc6 24. Qxc3 {wins.}) 21. exd5 Rxe1+ $2 ({better is} 21... Qd6) 22. Bxe1 Qd6 {(Fischer - Matanovich, Bled, 1961) And now as Keres pointed out, white can retain the pawn with} 23. Nf3 $1 {Not} Qxd5 $2 ({or} 23... Nxd5 24. Be4) 24. Bc3 {wins a piece.}) 16... Bg7 $2 ({after} 16... Nc6 17. d5 $1 Na5 ({on} 17... Nd8 18. c4 $1 {robs black of the normal counterplay with c4. Olaffson - Ivkov Buenos Aires 1960, and no white can leisurely build a strong attack with Kh2, g4, Rg1, and later the stock sac Nf5}) 18. b4 Nb7 19. a4 {with a slight advantage.}) 17. b4 Nc6 18. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 19. exd5 Ne7 20. dxc5 Nxd5 ({if} 20... dxc5 21. Be3 Qd6 22. Bxc5 Qxd5 23. Bb3 $1) 21. Bb3 Nf6 ({if} 21... Bc6 22. Bg5 $1) 22. cxd6 Qxd6 23. Ng5 $1 Rf8 24. Be3 Qxd1 25. Rexd1 h6 26. Ne4 $1 Nxe4 27. Rxd7 Rac8 28. c4 Ng5 29. Bxg5 hxg5 30. c5 {Black Resigns, (Fischer - Rinaldo, US Open 1957.)}) 15... dxe5 16. Nh2 Rad8 17. Qf3 Be6 ({On} 17... h5 $2 {Bobby Fischer} 18. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 19. exd5 {Black's K-side is weakened.}) 18. Nhg4 Nxg4 19. hxg4 (19. Nxg4 Bxg4 $1 20. hxg4 c4 $10) 19... Qc6 $6 (19... Nc4 $1 20. Nd5 Bxd5 21. exd5 Nb6 22. Rd1 Rd6 23. a4 Red8 24. axb5 axb5 25. Be3 c4 $10 {Keres,P-Matanovic,A/Bled/1961/0,5/83/}) 20. g5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'The old line'} 20. Qg3 $142 $1 f6 (20... Qc7 21. Nf5 $1 $40) (20... Bf6 {Bobby Fischer: 'is better.'} 21. g5 Bg7 22. Qh4 $40) 21. g5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'is good for white. (Boleslavsky - Tal, USSR 1957).  I'd always thought my line was an improvement (the idea is to clear g4 for a knight before black can force an exchange with Nc4.) but a closer look at this game shows black may have resources.'} Kh8 22. b3 Rf8 23. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 24. exd5 Qxd5 25. gxf6 Bxf6 26. Bh6 $16 {Boleslavsky,I-Tal,M/Tbilisi/1957/1:0/93/}) 20... Nc4 (20... Bxg5 {Bobby Fischer: 'puts white's idea to the crucial test. After'} 21. Nd5 $1 Bxc1 (21... Bxd5 $2 22. Bxg5 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins the exchange.'}) 22. Nf6+ Kh8 $1 ({if} 22... Kf8 {Bobby Fischer} 23. Raxc1 Re7 24. Nxh7+ Kg8 25. Rcd1 $1 Ra8 (25... Rxd1 26. Nf6+ Kg7 27. Rxd1 Nb7 28. g4 $1 {followed by g5 with a strong attack.}) 26. Nf6+ Kg7 27. Nd5 Bxd5 28. Rxd5 {white's better.}) 23. Raxc1 (23. Nxe8 Bg5 24. Nf6 Nc4 $44 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'black has good play for the exchange.'}) 23... Rf8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 23... Re7 24. Qg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'threatening either Qxe5 or Qh4)'} Qc7 25. Qh4 $36) 24. Qg3 Qc7 $13 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 25. Qg5 Kg7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'And now white can force a draw with Nh5+ etc. Or try for more with f4 or Re3.'}) 21. Ng4 Bxg4 $8 22. Qxg4 Nb6 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For Unzicker's'} 22... f6 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'see game 10.'} 23. gxf6 Bxf6 24. a4 Nb6 25. axb5 axb5 26. Be3 $16) 23. g3 c4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Wrong for example is'} 23... Nd7 24. a4 c4 $1 (24... b4 $2 25. cxb4 cxb4 26. Bb3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'And this bishop comes crashing back into the game via the open diagonal.'}) 25. axb5 axb5 $10) 24. Kg2 Nd7 $10 25. Rh1 Nf8 26. b4 $1 (26. b3 {Bobby Fischer: 'leads to trouble after'} b4 $1 27. cxb4 c3 $1 $44 28. a3 $2 {Bobby Fischer} Ne6 29. Qh3 h5 30. gxh6 {e.p.} Nd4 $1 {and wins.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 26. a4 b4 $1 27. cxb4 Bxb4 28. a5 {Bobby Fischer: '(threatening Ba4)'} Qc7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'is satisfactory.'}) 26... Qe6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bad, however, is'} 26... cxb3 $6 {Bobby Fischer: '?' Bobby Fischer: 'e.p.'} 27. Bxb3 Qxc3 28. Be3 $36 {× c7/} (28. Bb2 $5 {Bobby Fischer} Qxb2 29. Qf3 Ne6 30. Bxe6 Rf8 $1 {holds.} ({But not} 30... fxe6 $2 31. Rxh7 $1 Rf8 32. Rah1 $1 Qxf2+ 33. Qxf2 Rxf2+ 34. Kxf2 Rd2+ 35. Kf3 Bxg5 36. Rh8+ {mates.})) 28... Rd7 {Bobby Fischer} ({not} 28... Qc8 $2 {Bobby Fischer} 29. Qf3 Ne6 30. Rxh7 $1 {wins.}) 29. Rxh7 $1 {should win.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Sharper is'} 26... a5 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 27. a3 ({if} 27. bxa5 {Bobby Fischer} Qa6 28. a4 Qxa5) 27... Ra8 $10) 27. Qe2 a5 $1 28. bxa5 Qa6 29. Be3 Qxa5 30. a4 Ra8 (30... Qxc3 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 31. axb5 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'leads to equal play.'}) 31. axb5 Qxb5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'But not'} 31... Qxa1 $2 32. Rxa1 Rxa1 33. Qxc4 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black's resigns.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'More active is'} 31... Qxc3 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '(if'} 32. Rac1 {Bobby Fischer} Ba3 $1 {).}) 32. Rhb1 Qc6 33. Rb6 $1 Qc7 34. Rba6 Rxa6 35. Rxa6 $14 {× c4/} Rc8 36. Qg4 Ne6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'wrong would be an attempt to simplify with'} 36... Bc5 $6 37. Bxc5 Qxc5 38. Ba4 $1 $16 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'and black has trouble finding a move. If'} Ne6 {Bobby Fischer} ({or} 38... Rd8 {Bobby Fischer} 39. Rc6 Qa7 40. Rxc4 Rd2 41. Qf3 {staying a pawn ahead.}) 39. Bd7) 37. Ba4 Rb8 38. Rc6 Qd8 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 38... Qd7 $3 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'and it is not clear how white can improve his position any further. If'} 39. Kh2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 39. Rxc4 $2 Qd3 $1 40. Rc6 Rb1 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a strong attack.'}) 39... Rb1 $1 40. Rb6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 40. Rxc4 Qd3 41. Rc8+ Kg7 42. Qh4 Bxg5 $1 43. Bxg5 Rh1+ $1 44. Kxh1 Qf1+ {Bobby Fischer: 'with a perpetual.'} 45. Kh2 Qxf2+ $10) 40... Qd3 41. Rxb1 ({not} 41. Rxe6 $2 {Bobby Fischer} Qf1 $1) 41... Qxb1 42. Bd7 Nc7 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} ({on} 42... Nc5 {Bobby Fischer} 43. Bc6 {followed by Bd5 should win.}) 43. Bc6 Qd3 $14 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with drawing chances.'}) 39. Rxe6 $1 Qc8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'having seen that'} 39... fxe6 {Bobby Fischer: '?'} 40. Qxe6+ Kf8 41. Qxe5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing.'}) 40. Bd7 $1 $18 (40. Bd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Qxd7 41. Rxg6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins his queen.'}) 1-0

