[Event "Short & Sweet: The King's Indian Attack"]
[Site "Main Level"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Ibrahimoglu, Ismet"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A07"]
[PlyCount "77"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]
[SourceTitle "Main Level"]
[Source "Chessable.com"]

{As you can see, Fischer used the King's Indian Attack not only against the French and Sicilian but against the Caro-Kann too.} 1. e4 c6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 g6 4. Ngf3 Bg7 5. g3 Nf6 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 Bxf3 9. Qxf3 {In my opinion, White is already better. We were presented with a pair of Bishops for free, so I would be happy to get this position.} Nbd7 10. Qe2 {Preparing e4-e5 or f2-f4. Our Queen is perfectly placed on e2.} dxe4 $6 {As a rule of thumb, you shouldn't open up the position when your opponent has a pair of Bishops.} 11. dxe4 Qc7 12. a4 {Grabbing space on the queenside and preparing a4-a5 in some cases.} Rad8 13. Nb3 b6 14. Be3 {Black's queenside is already under serious pressure.} c5 15. a5 e5 $6 {Weakening light squares everywhere. White is much better now. It is very educational to see how Fischer uses all of his positional trumps to destroy Black's position step by step.} 16. Nd2 Ne8 17. axb6 axb6 18. Nb1 $1 {[%csl Gb5,Gd5][%cal Bb1c3] This Knight has done its job. Now, it will be relocated to d5 or b5.} Qb7 19. Nc3 Nc7 20. Nb5 Qc6 21. Nxc7 Qxc7 22. Qb5 {All of White's plans are connected with the light squares. Our Bishop on g2 may look dumb but it will join the game via f1.} Ra8 23. c3 {[%csl Gb4,Gd4] Fischer doesn't rush. He knows that Black lacks counterplay, and he can slowly build up his plans.} Rxa1 24. Rxa1 Rb8 25. Ra6 Bf8 26. Bf1 $1 Kg7 27. Qa4 {White will put the Bishop on b5 followed by Ra7.} Rb7 28. Bb5 Nb8 29. Ra8 {Black's pieces are paralysed. White is positionally winning.} Bd6 30. Qd1 {Fischer decided to change targets and switches his attention to the h6-square.} Nc6 31. Qd2 ({The computer-like} 31. Bh6+ Kxh6 32. Rg8 $3 {would end the game faster.}) 31... h5 32. Bh6+ Kh7 33. Bg5 {Threatening both Bf6 and Qd5.} Rb8 34. Rxb8 Nxb8 35. Bf6 Nc6 36. Qd5 Na7 37. Be8 {A complete triumph of White's strategy. Fischer's Bishops are monsters in this game. These types of monsters that were hiding under our beds when we were young...} Kg8 38. Bxf7+ Qxf7 39. Qxd6 {There was no point in continuing. Black's position completely collapsed. A superb performance by the legendary American.} 1-0

