{In this game Fischer obtains the Bishop pair early in the opening and proceeds to use them in conjunction with other positional pluses to secure the victory.} 1. e4 c6 2. d3 {(Avoiding standard lines of the Caro-Kann after 2.d4 d5. White instead plays the King's Indian Reversed.)} d5 3. Nd2 g6 4. Ngf3 Bg7 5. g3 Nf6 ({A more popular plan here is} 5... e5 {followed by} 6. Z0 Ne7) 6. Bg2 O-O 7. O-O Bg4 8. h3 {(Putting "the question" to the Bishop.)} Bxf3 {(Black hopes to hold his own by simplification, but at the price of giving White the two Bishops.)} 9. Qxf3 Nbd7 10. Qe2 {[%cal Re4e5] (Threatening the aggressive pawn march e5-e6.)} dxe4 11. dxe4 Qc7 12. a4 {(A probing move typical of such positions. White hopes to induce a weakening of Black's Queenside pawns.)} Rad8 13. Nb3 b6 {[%csl Ra6,Ga7,Rb5,Gb6,Rb7,Yc5,Rc6,Yd4,Ye3][%cal Rc1e3,Re3a7] (Concerned about Be3, which puts pressure on the a-pawn, Black feels compelled to play the text to block the e3-a7 diagonal. Nonetheless, this permanently weakens the light squares there. This weakness is further aggravated by the absence of the light-squared Bishop.)} 14. Be3 c5 {[%csl Ra6,Rb5,Rb7,Rc4,Rc6,Rd5][%cal Yb3a5,Yb3c5,Yb3d4,Ye3d4,Gc5d4,Gb6a5] (Trying to limit the scope of the Nb3 and Be3, but the light squares are further weakened.)} 15. a5 $1 {(Preparing to use the a-file.)} e5 {[%csl Rd5][%cal Yf2f4,Ye4e5] (Now d5 is permanently weak, yet without this move White could expand with f4 and e5.)} 16. Nd2 {[%csl Yb3,Yc4,Ye4,Yf3] (The Knight is maneuvered so as to press on the light squares.)} Ne8 17. axb6 axb6 18. Nb1 Qb7 {(Trying to trade off the heavy pieces to defuse White's initiative.)} 19. Nc3 Nc7 20. Nb5 $1 {[%csl Gb5] (In order to secure control of b5.)} Qc6 21. Nxc7 Qxc7 22. Qb5 Ra8 23. c3 Rxa1 {(Hoping to draw by trading down.)} 24. Rxa1 Rb8 25. Ra6 Bf8 {[%csl Ge3,Gg2] (Now after considerable maneuvers which have left Black's Queenside blockaded on the light squares, the time has come to bring the Bishops into concerted play.)} 26. Bf1 $1 Kg7 27. Qa4 {[%csl Ga1,Ga2,Ga3,Ga4,Ga5,Ga6,Ga7,Ga8] (Nailing down control of the a-file.)} Rb7 28. Bb5 (28. Bb5 {Already White threatens} Z0 29. Bxd7 Qxd7 30. Qxd7 Rxd7 31. Rxb6) 28... Nb8 29. Ra8 Bd6 30. Qd1 $1 {(Shifting back to create threats against Black's Kingside.)} ({Note} 30. Qd1 Ra7 31. Rxa7 Qxa7 32. Qxd6) 30... Nc6 31. Qd2 {(Now Bh6+ is the threat.)} h5 32. Bh6+ {(Still strong.)} Kh7 33. Bg5 $1 {(Threatening Bf6.)} Rb8 34. Rxb8 Nxb8 35. Bf6 $1 {(White's two Bishops hamstring Black's position.)} (35. Bf6 {For example,} Nd7 {loses to} 36. Bxd7 Qxd7 37. Bxe5) 35... Nc6 36. Qd5 Na7 37. Be8 $1 {(Winning material.)} Kg8 38. Bxf7+ Qxf7 39. Qxd6 {Black resigns.} 1-0

