{Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'When I started to play chess in the mid 70s Bobby Fischer was a living legend. He had won the World Championship in 72 against Boris Spasskyj in such a convincing way that put him in a class of his own. But then he had stopped playing! He hadn´t played a single game of chess since he became the champ. He even refrained from defending his title in 75. Adding colour he seemed to have vanished. Nobody knew where Bobby was or what he was doing. This is how you create a myth.  I immediately fell in love with this magic guy who played chess almost like a god. When I heard stories about his rather bizarre behaviour during his career I was even more attracted. Clearly Bobby was a man who knew what he wanted. And he wanted to play chess! And so did I.  Maybe the excentric American had disappeared, but his games were still there. A lot of books appeared around the time of the 72-match, so you could easily get started. A few years later I got my copy of a book with all the games of Robert James Fischer as his full name was. To me and to everybody else he was just Bobby.  Looking at the games it was clear that from 1970 until 1972 he had reached a completely new level and showed a playing strength that none of his rivals could match. Winning 21 games in a row on the way to the World Championship speaks for itself.  In my view it all started in the famous Soviet vs. Rest of the World match in Belgrade in 1970. Fischer hadn´t played a tournament for 18 months previously, but he had trained daily, it is fair to assume, knowing the American's total dedication to chess. Already in the first game against the former World Champion Tigran Petrosian we could see a well prepared Fischer displaying his trademark style, which is crystal clear aggressive positional chess. '} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin} c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The Exchange Variation against the Caro-Kann was new in Fischers repertoire. As a young player he mostly went for the Two Knights Variation 1.e4 c6 2.c3 d5 3.f3, but this is the mature Fischer! The Exchange Variation suits his style very well. It is active and direct and very clear cut.'} Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 ({I recommended} 5... Qc7 {in my book Grandmaster Repertoire: The Caro-Kann. Petrosian's move is the old one, where Black primarely tries to get his light-squared bishop out before he plays ...e6. Please notice how determined Bobby is to prevent this plan.}) 6. Bf4 (6. h3 e5 {Fischer=: F Bulletin: 'Fischer=:F'}) (6. Bg5 Ne4 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. d5 Ne5 9. Qa4+ Qd7 10. Qxe4 Qf5 $15 {F}) 6... Bg4 (6... g6 {Petrosian=:P}) 7. Qb3 Na5 (7... Qc8 $142 {P h5, g6 Hort Bulletin: ' h5, g6 Hort'}) (7... b6 {is too weakening and}) (7... Qb6 {leads to double pawns.}) 8. Qa4+ Bd7 9. Qc2 e6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Petro accepts that the bishop won´t get out on g4, but he hopes to activate it on the other flank instead. This plan is also thwarted by White. '} (9... a6 {P}) 10. Nf3 Qb6 {Bulletin: ' b5'} (10... b5 $5 {[%csl Gc4] Petrossian}) 11. a4 $146 {[%csl Gb5,Gd3,Rd7] Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3. Bulletin: 'N!' 'N!' Bulletin: 'Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3.' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''N' Fischer's new move, which prevents 11.. d7-b5 exchanging the bad d7 with the very good d3.' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The new move.'} (11. O-O Bb5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'was known from an old Capablance game.'} 12. Nbd2 Bxd3 13. Qxd3 Rc8 14. Rab1 Be7 15. h3 O-O 16. Rfe1 Nc4 17. Nxc4 Rxc4 18. Ne5 Rcc8 19. Bg5 Qd8 20. Bxf6 gxf6 21. Ng4 Kh8 22. f4 f5 23. Ne5 Bd6 24. Qf3 Bxe5 25. Rxe5 Rg8 26. Re2 Qh4 27. Kh2 Rg6 28. g3 Qf6 29. Rg1 Kg7 30. Qd3 a6 31. Rc1 h5 32. h4 Kh6 33. c4 dxc4 34. Rxc4 Rxg3 35. Qxg3 Rxc4 36. Rd2 Qg6 37. Qg5+ Qxg5 38. hxg5+ Kg6 39. Kg3 Rc6 40. Kf3 Rd6 {Maroczy,G-Capablanca,J Lake Hopatcong 1926 0-1}) 11... Rc8 ({Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Black can´t exploit the hole on b3:'} 11... Nb3 $142 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''''} 12. Ra2 Rc8 13. O-O Be7 {F}) (11... Qb3 12. Qe2 $1 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!''} Bxa4 $2 (12... Nc4 13. Bc1) 13. Rxa4 Qxa4 14. Bb5+ $18 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''+-' F'}) 12. Nbd2 Nc6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Threatening b4.'} 13. Qb1 Nh5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Pushing the white bishop away from the dominant f4-square. '} (13... g6 $142 {F}) 14. Be3 h6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '?' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'This is bad. After'} (14... f5 15. g4 $1 fxg4 16. Ng5 Bd6 17. Bxh7 {F}) (14... g6 {P}) (14... Qc7 $1 15. O-O (15. Bxh7 Nf4 16. Bxf4 Qxf4 $44) 15... Nf4 {the game is still pretty open although White is better developed.}) 15. Ne5 Nf6 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'A depressing retreat.'} ({Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Petro had planned'} 15... Nxe5 16. dxe5 Bc5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'but then saw that'} 17. a5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!'} Qc7 18. Nf3 {F} (18. g4 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'was winning a piece for White - and panicked. It isn´t completely clear though, but after'} Bxe3 19. fxe3 Qxe5 20. gxh5 Qxe3+ 21. Be2 (21. Kd1 $16 {White will hide his king on c2.}) 21... Bb5 22. Qd1 {P f1-g3 P Bulletin: ' f1-g3 P'})) 16. h3 Bd6 17. O-O Kf8 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '?' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Another strange decision.'} (17... O-O $142 {F} 18. f4 {was also unpleasant for Black, but not completely clear after something like} Be8 {followed by ...Ne7} ({Not} 18... Nxe5 $2 19. fxe5 Bxe5 {that fails once again to the intermediate move} 20. a5 $18)) 18. f4 Be8 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Now with the king on f8 this is pretty hopeless. Black´s pieces can´t coordinate in a harmonious way and he is forced to play without a real plan. Petrosian was by nature a great defender and he finds some resources later on. '} (18... Nxe5 19. fxe5 Bxe5 20. a5 $18 {F}) 19. Bf2 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'A nice regrouping'} Qc7 (19... g6 20. f5 gxf5 21. Bxf5 exf5 22. Qxf5 Qd8 23. Bh4 $18 {F}) 20. Bh4 Ng8 21. f5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Fischer opens lines'} Nxe5 22. dxe5 Bxe5 23. fxe6 Bf6 24. exf7 Bxf7 25. Nf3 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'After the opening of the e- and f-files the Black king is feeling rather exposed on f8. Objectively White is winning, but Petro finds a good try'} Bxh4 (25... g5 26. Bf2 Kg7 27. Bd4 {F}) 26. Nxh4 Nf6 27. Ng6+ {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Typical Fischer who likes to force the play. There was also the strong though quiet'} (27. Qc2 {followed by ae1. Black will never solve the problem with his king.}) 27... Bxg6 28. Bxg6 Ke7 $3 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''!!' F' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The black king hopes to escape to the queenside on the dark squares.'} 29. Qf5 Kd8 (29... Rhd8 $5 {followed by ...Kf8}) 30. Rae1 Qc5+ (30... Qd6 31. Re6 Qc5+ 32. Kh1 Rc6 33. Rfe1 {and White keeps the pressure.}) 31. Kh1 Rf8 $19 {F} (31... Rc6 $142 {F/P Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''' F/P' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'was suggested by the players, but'} 32. b4 {keeps the attack going and just wins.}) ({Unfortunately} 31... Kc7 {doesn´t work due to} 32. Qf4+ Qd6 33. Re7+ Nd7 34. Bf5 Rhd8 35. Rxg7 $18) 32. Qe5 $1 {F/P Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'The king won´t get to b8'} Rc7 (32... Qc7 33. Qxd5+ $18 {F} (33. Rxf6 $1 $18)) 33. b4 $1 {F} Qc6 (33... Qxc3 34. Qd6+) 34. c4 $1 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Opens more lines while the king is still stuck in the centre'} dxc4 35. Bf5 {Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: '!' Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: 'Prevents ...c8. Now a check on the d-file will be lethal.'} Rff7 36. Rd1+ Rfd7 37. Bxd7 Rxd7 (37... Nxd7 38. Rfe1 $18 {F/P}) 38. Qb8+ Ke7 (38... Qc8 39. Rxd7+ Nxd7 40. Qd6 $18 {F Fischer/Petrosian Schandorff,Lars: ''+-' F'}) 39. Rde1+ (39. Rde1+ Kf7 40. Qe8#) 1-0

