{GAME N. 26 Time will tell. The opening has always been regarded as the old warrior's weak point, and were it not for this handicap who knows how far Reshevsky might have gone toward the summit? Whatever the case, being familiar with the latest wrinkles does have the merit of saving time on the clock and, hopefully, of catching an opponent off guard. Although Reshevsky is bested in the theoretical duel (after 13 Bf3) his practical cunning enables him to extricate himself-at a great cost of time. In the end it is the clock, as much as Fischer's persistence, that causes his downfall.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 {Allowing White the chance to get a Maroczy bind (with 5 c4). Apparently Reshevsky had booked up on this for the match. Black's idea is to dispense with an early ... d6 and possibly strive for a later ... d5 (thereby saving a tempo).} 5. Nc3 ({In match game 11 I got an edge with the more traditional} 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (11) Los Angeles 1961}) 5... Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Be2 ({In the 4th and 6th games of the match I continued with} 7. Bc4 O-O 8. Bb3 Ng4 (8... Na5 $2 9. e5 Ne8 10. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 11. Ne6 $3 $18 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1958}) 9. Qxg4 Nxd4 {and White got a clear advantage both with Qh4 and Qd1 respectively.} 10. Qd1 $16 {Fischer, R-Reshevsky,S (4) New York 1961} (10. Qh4 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (6) Los Angeles 1961})) 7... O-O ({On} 7... d5 $2 8. Bb5 $18 {wins a pawn.}) 8. f4 {Despite his familiarity with the Dragon Variation, I felt Reshevsky really didn't know the latest wrinkles in Alekhine's Attack.} ({The point of Black's 'accelerated fianchetto' becomes apparent after the indifferent} 8. O-O $2 d5 $1 9. exd5 Nb4 $11) 8... d6 ({Now on} 8... d5 $2 9. e5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. Bc4 $18 {gives White a winning ending; Olafsson,F-Larsen,B Wageningen 1957}) 9. Nb3 {Diagram [#]} Be6 {I was right. This is the old (and second-rate) move.} ({Correct is} 9... a5 $1 10. a4 Be6 11. Nd4 $2 ({after} 11. g4 Nb4 {Black's knight can no longer be dislodged by a3}) ({the best White has is} 11. O-O Rc8 {with an equal position}) 11... Qb6 $1 12. Nxe6 Qxe3 13. Nxf8 Ng4 {and Black has a strong attack; Makievsky,N-Veressov,G URS 1954}) 10. g4 d5 11. f5 Bc8 ({Lipnitsky recommends} 11... gxf5 $5 {It's interesting.}) 12. exd5 Nb4 {Diagram [#]} 13. Bf3 $1 {The modern way-White maintains his centre pawn and sacs two pawns on the kings ide where Black must expose his king to get them.} (13. d6 {has been known to be only a draw since the famous Alekhine - Botvinnik encounter, Nottingham 1936, which continued:} Qxd6 $1 (13... exd6 $2 14. g5) 14. Bc5 Qf4 15. Rf1 Qxh2 16. Bxb4 Nxg4 $1 17. Bxg4 Qg3+ 18. Rf2 Qg1+ $11 {with a perpetual.}) ({Another weak line is} 13. fxg6 hxg6 14. Bf3 Bxg4 $1 15. Bxg4 Nxg4 16. Qxg4 Nxc2+ 17. Kf2 Nxa1 18. Rxa1 Rc8 $1 {with a good game. If} 19. Bd4 $2 Rc4 {(Panov)}) 13... gxf5 14. a3 fxg4 15. Bg2 $1 ({On} 15. axb4 gxf3 16. Qxf3 Bg4 {followed by ... Bh5-g6. White's whole idea is to keep Black's lightsquared bishop restricted to the queenside.}) 15... Na6 16. Qd3 $1 ({Nei's improvement over} 16. Qe2 Bf5 $1 {and the bishop retreats to g6, when necessary, defending the kingside.}) 16... e6 {The best choice in a difficult position. Up to here we had both played briskly, but now Reshevsky began to consume time on the clock.} ({After} 16... Nd7 17. O-O-O Ne5 18. Qe2 {Black's game is lifeless. White has h3 and Bd4 in the offing.}) 17. O-O-O ({Black gets the initiative after} 17. d6 $2 Nd5 $1 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. Qxd5 Re8 {etc.}) 17... Nxd5 {The lesser evil.} ({On} 17... exd5 18. h3 g3 19. Bd4 {is strong.}) 18. h3 $1 g3 19. Rhg1 Qd6 $1 {Reshevsky is putting up a firstrate defence.} 20. Bxd5 exd5 {Diagram [#] Despite his material deficit, it is obvious White has a strong attack. His problem is how to land a haymaker.} 21. Nxd5 $2 {This gives Black a little breathing space.} ({Nowadays I would have played} 21. Bd4 $1 {without giving it a second thought. After} Bxd4 22. Rxg3+ Bg7 (22... Kh8 23. Qxd4+ f6 24. Rf3 {leads to a bind}) 23. Rdg1 Qh6+ 24. Kb1 Be6 25. Rxg7+ Qxg7 26. Rxg7+ Kxg7 27. Qg3+ Kh8 ({White wins after} 27... Kf6 28. Qd6 Kg7 29. Nxd5 $18) 28. Qe5+ Kg8 29. Qg5+ Kh8 30. Qf6+ Kg8 31. Ne2 $18 {with a winning bind.}) 21... Kh8 22. Bf4 (22. Bd4 {is less convincing now after} Bxd4 (22... f6 $2 23. Rxg3 Qxd5 24. Bxf6 $1) 23. Qxd4+ f6 $16 {and White has no forced win.}) 22... Qg6 23. Qd2 {Overlooking his reply.} ({Vukovic suggests} 23. Qf3 {but} Bf5 $1 24. Rxg3 Qc6 {holds.}) 23... Bxh3 $1 {Reshevsky chopped it off fasthe doesn't wait to be asked twice.} 24. Rxg3 Bg4 {Black has succeeded in transferring this bishop to the kings ide and in sealing the g-file.} 25. Rh1 {So White begins operations on the h-file!} Rfe8 26. Ne3 {Diagram [#]} Qe4 $2 {Anxious to simplify and ease the tension (in time-pressure) Reshevsky finally goes astray.} ({The tempting} 26... Qf6 {is refuted by} 27. Nxg4 Qxb2+ 28. Kd1 Rad8 29. Rd3 $18) ({But simply} 26... f5 $1 {holds, e.g.} 27. Qh2 Kg8 $16) 27. Qh2 $1 {Now the roof caves in on Black's kingside.} Be6 ({The late Abe Turner suggested} 27... Bf5 {but then} 28. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 29. Nxf5+ Qxf5 30. Nd4 $18) 28. Rxg7 $1 {That does it! Once this bishop is gone, White has a field day. "Why didn't White play 28 Nd2 ... ? So far as I can see Black can then resign. Or am I missing something?" (A. R. B. Thomas in a letter to Chess). Right, Mr. Thomas!} (28. Nd2 $18) 28... Kxg7 29. Qh6+ Kg8 ({On} 29... Kh8 {mates in two.} 30. Be5+ $18) 30. Rg1+ Qg6 31. Rxg6+ fxg6 32. Nd4 Rad8 33. Be5 Rd7 34. Nxe6 Rxe6 35. Ng4 Rf7 36. Qg5 Rf1+ 37. Kd2 h5 38. Qd8+ (38. Qd8+ {After} Rf8 39. Nh6+ $18 {taxes even Reshevsky's defensive ability.}) 1-0

