{GAME N. 20 Theoretical scuffle. Former world champion, Dr. Max Euwe had for decades been considered one of the wor/d's leading authorities on opening theory. His Chess Archives ranks with Modern Chess Openings as an indispensable source of reference. It is no small wonder, then, when he selects a risky but playable variation. Fischer, however, just a little better versed in its intricacies, introduces a nuance on move 15 which ruffles his opponent no end. Fischer's method of dispatching his veteran adversary - on home grounds, as it were - is deceptively simple. After a mere eighteen moves the opening has become an ending and the duel is over. Euwe fights on, but to no avail.} 1. e4 {Petronijevic,Zoran Petronijevic,Zoran Donev Petronijevic,Zoran} c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 {At that time I was convinced the Panov-Botvinnik attack was the sharpest} Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 ({In our game at Buenos Aires 1960, Ivkov played Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer often played The Panov's Attack. For instance, in his game against Ivkov (Buenos Aires 1960) played was: ' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer spielte oft den Panov-Angriff. Z. B. wurde in seiner Partie gegen Ivkov (Buenos Aires 1960) gespielt: '} 5... e6 6. Nf3 Be7 7. c5 O-O 8. Bd3 b6 9. b4 bxc5 ({better is} 9... a5 {>=} 10. Na4 Nfd7 $1) 10. bxc5 Nc6 11. O-O Bd7 12. h3 Ne8 13. Bf4 $16 {with a bind. Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Buenos Aires 1960} Bf6 {Petronijevic,Zoran} 14. Bb5 Nc7 $16) 6. Nf3 ({On Botvinnik's old} 6. Bg5 {Botvinnik,M} e6 $1 (6... dxc4 $2 7. d5 Ne5 8. Qd4 $16 {is strong}) 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bxf6 Qxf6 9. Nxd5 Qd8 10. Nc3 ({if} 10. Bc4 Be6 11. Qe2 $2 b5 $1) 10... Qxd4 11. Qxd4 Nxd4 12. O-O-O Bc5 13. Na4 Ne6 $11) 6... Bg4 $5 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'In his comments, Fischer considered the move 6...e6 to be better. According to current theory, both moves are of aproximate equal value.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'In seinem Kommentar schätzte Fischer den Zug 6...e6 als besser ein. Der Theorie nach sind beide Züge etwas gleichwertig. '} ({Risky but playable. Safer is} 6... e6) 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Qb3 Bxf3 9. gxf3 e6 ({on} 9... Ndb4 10. Be3 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Qxd4 12. Bb5+ Nc6 13. O-O $40 {White gets a strong attack. (Evans,L-Henin,n Las Vegas op 1965}) 10. Qxb7 Nxd4 11. Bb5+ Nxb5 12. Qc6+ {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'This move was reccomended by Nimzowich in his book: "The praxis of my system".' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Dieser Zug wurde von Nimzowich in seinem Buch: "Die Praxis meines Systems" empfohlen.'} Ke7 13. Qxb5 Nxc3 ({An alternative is Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Nowadays the move' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Heute gilt der Zug'} 13... Qd7 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'is considered to be better.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'als besser.'} 14. Nxd5+ {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'for instance,' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'z. B.'} exd5 (14... Qxd5 15. Qxd5 (15. Bg5+ {Petronijevic,Zoran} f6 16. Qxd5 exd5 17. Be3 Ke6 18. O-O-O Bb4 19. Rd3 Rhd8 20. a3 Rac8+ 21. Kb1 Bc5 22. Re1 Kd6 $11 {½-½ Karpov,A-Kramnik,V/Linares 1993/CBM 34 (99)}) 15... exd5 16. O-O $16 {gives White good play against Black's isolated d-Pawn and a-Pawn)}) 15. Qb4+ (15. Qe2+ Kf6 16. h4 $18 {wins, according to Evans) Evans,L}) 15... Ke8 16. Qd4 $16 {with a clear advantage}) 14. bxc3 {Position after} ({The move Der Zug} 14. Qc5+ {Petronijevic,Zoran leads to an equal play. führt zu gleichem Spiel.} Qd6 15. Qxc3 Ke8 (15... f6 $5 {[%cal Ye7f7]}) 16. O-O Qc5 17. Qb3 Bd6 $11 {1-0 Thomas,I-Soueid,M/GER 1996/EXT 2001 (34). (33)}) 14... Qd7 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Diagram '} ({after} 14... Qd5 15. Qxd5 exd5 16. Rb1 $14 {gives White a slight edge.}) 15. Rb1 $1 $16 {The innovation. Months before the game I had shown this line to Benko and he suggested this innocent-looking move. Upon looking deeper I found that, horrible as White's Pawn structure may be, Black can't exploit it because he'11 be unable to develop his K -side normally. It's the little quirks like this that could nlake life difficult for a chess machine. Benko,P Petronijevic,Zoran: 'White wins the open b-file.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Weiß gewinnt den Kampf um die offene b-Linie.'} Rd8 $2 ({also difficult is} 15... Qxb5 16. Rxb5 Kd6 $1 17. Rb7 f6 18. Ke2 Kc6 19. Rf7 a5 20. Be3 $16 {with an enduring pull}) (15... Rc8 $5 {Petronijevic,Zoran}) 16. Be3 Qxb5 17. Rxb5 Rd7 18. Ke2 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer does not hurry: he can take a7 later.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer hat keine Eile: den a7 kann er später schlagen.'} (18. Ra5 $6 {is unnecessary. White can win the a-Pawn at his leisure}) 18... f6 19. Rd1 $1 {To swap Black's only active piece. To swap Black's only active piece. To swap Black's only active piece. Donev: 'Der Abtausch des passiven Turms h1 gegen den aktiven auf d7 ist die stellungsgemäße Methode, den Vorteil zu bewahren.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer follows a logical positional plan: he exchanges the only active black piece.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer folgt einem logischen positionellen Plan: er tauscht die einzige aktive Figur von Schwarz. '} (19. Ra5 $5 $16 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Kf7 {Donev} 20. Bxa7 (20. Rxa7 $2 Rxa7 21. Bxa7 Bd6 $13) 20... Rb7 $5 $16) 19... Rxd1 20. Kxd1 Kd7 {Position after} 21. Rb8 {Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer pins the black bishop to his rook.' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer fesselt den schwarzen Läufer mit seinem Turm.'} (21. Bxa7 Bd6 22. Rb7+ Kc6 23. Rxg7 Bxh2 $16 {[%emt 0:00:22] would be hard to win. Now the threat is c5}) 21... Kc6 22. Bxa7 g5 {Striving to untangle the K -side.} 23. a4 Bg7 24. Rb6+ Kd5 25. Rb7 Bf8 26. Rb8 {Still trying to decide how to squeeze the most out of it.} Bg7 27. Rb5+ Kc6 28. Rb6+ Kd5 29. a5 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!' Donev: 'Der a-Bauer strebt unaufhaltsam vorwärts.'} f5 (29... Ra8 $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} 30. Rb7 $18) 30. Bb8 $1 Rc8 (30... Bxc3 $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} 31. a6 $18) 31. a6 Rxc3 {Position after} 32. Rb5+ {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!'} (32. a7 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Ra3 33. Rd6+ Kc4 34. Rxe6 Bd4 $13 {The black pieces are very active. Die schwarzen Figuren stehen sehr aktiv.}) 32... Kc4 ({After the game Euwe showed me a cute trap he might have played for - and almost fainted when I fell into it! The line arises after} 32... Kc6 33. Ra5 Bd4 {and he asked, "What do you do now?" I looked a few seconds and played} 34. Be5 $2 {whereupon he uncorked} (34. Ke2 $18 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '+-!' Donev: '+-!' Donev: ' 35.a7' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer'}) (34. a7 $2 {Petronijevic,Zoran} Bxa7 $11 {Donev: '=' Donev: ' 35...xf3'}) 34... Rc5 $1 $11 {which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. which leads to a draw. Upon reconsideration, however, simply 34 Ke2 wins. It's these tidbits that you remember best. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '=!!'}) 33. Rb7 {Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!' Donev: 'Weiß strebt den Turmtausch an.'} Bd4 34. Rc7+ Kd3 35. Rxc3+ Kxc3 36. Be5 {Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Black resigns. He can't stop the a-Pawn. Petronijevic,Zoran: '!' Donev: '!'} 1-0

