{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 47 The Indian sign? Bisguier is the one Grandmaster who consistently obtains decent positions against Fischer, only to throw them away for no apparent reason. Out of something like a dozen encounters, he has squeezed but a single draw. Here is the only game in which Bisguier is outplayed from the start. Still, he does catch Fischer napping and nearly escapes. Describing his sensations before the game, Bisguier wrote: "For the first time I was really in doubt as to what Bobby would play against me as White... I was hoping to play the Black side of the King's Gambit or the Two Knights' Defense, but he 'surprised' me with the Ruy Lopez... taken by surprise I was not so prepared or comfortable as I would like to have been. Now that Bobby has added psychology to his arsenal of weapons he is a much more dangerous opponent than ever before." '} 1. e4 {Bobby Fischer} e5 2. Nf3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'In a previous round, against Evans, I had hazarded a King's Gambit:'} 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 {Fischer,R-Evans,L ch-USA 1963 Bobby Fischer: 'and White won only after some uneasy moments.'}) 2... Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bernstein tried the Marshall Attack against me in the 1959-60 US Championship, and an interesting struggle developed:'} 8... d5 $5 {Marshall,F} 9. exd5 e4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'in place of the usual'} 9... Nxd5) 10. dxc6 exf3 11. Qxf3 Bg4 12. Qg3 Bd6 13. Qh4 Re8 14. f3 Bf5 15. d4 Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Ng4+ 17. Kg3 Qxh4+ 18. Kxh4 Rxe1 19. fxg4 Rxc1 20. gxf5 Rd8 21. a4 $18 {Fischer,R-Bernstein,S ch-USA 1959 Bobby Fischer: 'White won shortly.'}) 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 11... Nd7 {Fischer,R-Keres,P Curacao ct 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'see game 38: Fischer,R-Keres,P; Curacao ct, 1962.'}) 12. Nbd2 Nc6 13. dxc5 {Rauser,V Bobby Fischer: ', The Rauzer Attack. White gives up the center in order to exploit Black's weakened squares on d5 and f5. '} dxc5 14. Nf1 Rd8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Risky.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is the usual'} 14... Be6 15. Ne3 Rad8 16. Qe2 g6 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 15. Qe2 Nh5 {Bobby Fischer: '; An old line rehabilitated by Reshevsky against Bronstein at Zurich 1953.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'If now'} 15... Be6 16. Ne3 g6 17. Ng5 Bc8 18. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 19. exd5 Bxg5 (19... Rxd5 20. Qf3 $1 Be6 21. Nxe6 fxe6 22. Qg4 $1) 20. Bxg5 Rxd5 21. Rad1 $16 {Lipnitzsky,n Bobby Fischer: 'with a plus (LIPNITZKY).'}) 16. g3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Bronstein's move. - after first interpolating 16.a4 Rb8.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'This idea bankrupts Black's strategy. The slight weakening of the K-side is inconsequential, but Black's loss of time with his KN is. Actually the sharpest continuation is'} 16. a4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'as I played against Eliskases at Mar del Plata, 1960 (I simply forgot to interpolate it here)'} Rb8 17. axb5 axb5 18. g3 $1 g6 19. h4 $1 Be6 20. Ne3 c4 21. Ng5 Bxg5 22. hxg5 Na5 23. Ng4 Bxg4 24. Qxg4 Nb3 25. Bxb3 cxb3 26. Be3 $16 {Fischer,R-Eliskases,E Mar del Plata 1960 Bobby Fischer: 'with a great advantage.'}) 16... g6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Simply'} 16... Nf6 { Bobby Fischer: 'may be best. Then on'} 17. h4 h6 18. Ne3 Be6) (16... Bxh3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is bad owing to'} 17. Ng5 Bxg5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 17... Bxf1 18. Qxh5 Bxg5 19. Bxg5 f6 20. Bxf6 $1 gxf6 21. Kxf1 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'White's better.'}) 18. Bxg5 Nf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 18... Bxf1 $2 19. Bxd8) 19. Bxf6 gxf6 20. Ne3 $146 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has more than enough for the Pawn.'}) 17. h4 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bronstein's'} 17. Kh2 {Bronstein,D-Reshevsky,S Zürich ct 1953}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'and Weinstein's'} 17. Kg2 {Weinstein,R Bobby Fischer: 'are time-consuming and hence weaker. When I told Bronstein (at Mar del Plata 1960) that the text was a tremendous improvement over his game with Reshevsky, he replied: "Of course. After seven years one must find an improvement."'}) 17... Be6 18. Ne3 f6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Probably best.'} (18... c4 19. Ng5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is similar to the quoted game with Eliskases.'}) 19. Nd5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Of course! Chess is a matter of timing. Given another move or two Black would be able not only to defend hilnself against this invasion, but even try for the initiative. '} Qb7 {Bobby Fischer: '; Prudent.'} ({Bobby Fischer: '"Winning" the Pawn would allow White's Bishop-pair to enter the game with powerful effect: e.g.,'} 19... Bxd5 20. exd5 Rxd5 21. c4 $1 Nd4 22. Nxd4 Rxd4 23. cxb5 axb5 24. Qxb5 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now Eliskases recommends'} c4 {Eliskases,E Bobby Fischer: 'but I fail to see how this improves matters since'} 25. Be3 Rb8 26. Qa4 Rb4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'is answered by'} 27. Qe8+) 20. Nxe7+ Qxe7 $16 { Bobby Fischer: '; White has the two Bishops - or a "half point" advantage. '} 21. Nh2 { h2-...-d5 Bobby Fischer: ', This Knight is also bound for d5. '} Ng7 22. Ng4 c4 23. Qf3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Winning the second "minor exchange."'} Bxg4 (23... Rf8 24. Ne3 {× d5 Bobby Fischer: 'the Knight is ready to pounce on d5, especially after Black's Rook has been deflected from the d-file.'}) 24. Qxg4 Ne6 25. h5 {Bobby Fischer: '?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'More accurate is'} 25. Be3 { Bobby Fischer: 'if'} Nc5 $2 26. Bxc5 Qxc5 27. Qe6+ Kg7 28. Rad1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'penetrates decisively.'}) 25... Kh8 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '; Alert.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I had expected'} 25... g5 {Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon'} 26. Be3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is even more devastating than before.'}) 26. Kg2 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 26. hxg6 Rg8 $132 {Bobby Fischer: 'White is in trouble!'}) 26... g5 {Bobby Fischer: '; Forced - eventually.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 26... Rg8 27. Rh1 gxh5 $2 (27... g5 { Bobby Fischer: 'is better.'}) 28. Qxh5 Nf4+ 29. Bxf4 exf4 30. e5 $1 Rg7 31. exf6 Qxf6 32. Bxh7 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 27. Be3 Nf4+ $1 28. Kh2 $1 (28. gxf4 $2 gxf4 $17 { g8; fxe3 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening 29...Rg8 as well as 29...fxe3 regains the piece advantageously.'}) 28... Nd3 29. Bxd3 cxd3 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Now the advanced soldier must fall.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Exchanging a pair of Rooks would make it more difficult, but White still maintains his grip after'} 29... Rxd3 {} 30. Red1 Rad8 31. Rxd3 cxd3 (31... Rxd3 $2 32. Qc8+ Nd8 33. Qxa6) 32. Rd1 Rd7 33. Rd2 $36 { c5 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening a winning bind with 34.Bc5.'}) 30. Red1 Rd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 30... b4 31. Rd2 bxc3 32. bxc3 Qa3 33. Rad1 Qxc3 34. Qe6 Kg7 35. h6+ $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 31. Rd2 Na5 {Bobby Fischer: '; A useless excursion, but there was no good defense. Strangely enough, Black's difficulty stems from his third move and its consequent weakening on b6. If the pawn were still on a 7 (preventing a later Bb6) he might well hold.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 31... Rad8 32. Rad1 Qf7 33. Bb6 Rb8 34. Bc5 Rbd8 35. Qf3 $18 {× d3 Bobby Fischer: 'picks up the d-pawn at leisure.'}) 32. b3 Qd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 32... Rc8 $2 33. Rxd3 $1) 33. Rad1 Re8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 33... Rad8 34. Rxd3 Qxd3 35. Rxd3 Rxd3 36. Bb6 $1) 34. Rxd3 Qxd3 {Bobby Fischer: '; Black is braced to give up his Queen for two Rooks and keep control of the crucial d-file. '} 35. Qxd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns. A devastating X-ray.'} (35. Qxd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'After'} Qxd7 36. Rxd7 {Bobby Fischer: 'it's just a matter of time. E.g.,'} Re6 37. Kh3 $18 { h3-g4-f5 Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Kg4-f5, etc.'}) 1-0

