{Game 31: 'The next game covers the main line of the ...Nc6-variation.'} 1. e4 {Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Ftacnik,L'} c5 2. Nc3 (2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. f3 e6 7. Be3 Nc6 8. Qd2 {1-0 (35) Fischer,R-Spassky,B Belgrade (25) 1992 [Game 31]}) 2... Nc6 3. Nge2 d6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 e6 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Qd2 Be7 (7... a6 8. O-O-O Qc7 9. f3 Bd7) (7... Ng4 8. Bg5) 8. f3 a6 9. O-O-O O-O 10. g4 $1 ({I was tempted to recommend} 10. Kb1 {as a more flexible approach, with the idea} Nxd4 ({but I failed to find anything special for White after} 10... d5 11. Be2 Qc7 $1 12. exd5 Nxd5 13. Nxd5 exd5 {as in Giri - Van Wely, Amstelveen 2017.}) 11. Qxd4 Nd7 12. h4 b5 13. Bg5 $1 {with serious pressure on the dark squares}) 10... Nxd4 (10... Nd7 11. h4 Nxd4 ({After} 11... Nde5 12. Nxc6 $1 bxc6 13. Be2 Rb8 14. f4 Nd7 15. h5 Qa5 16. Qe1 Qb4 17. b3 $16 {White's prospects on the kingside are far superior to those of Black on the queenside, Da Riva Alonso - Kratochvil, email 2011.}) 12. Bxd4 b5 {1-0 (35) Fischer,R-Spassky,B Belgrade (25) 1992 [Game 31]}) 11. Bxd4 b5 12. g5 (12. Bxf6 $6 Bxf6 13. Qxd6 Qa5 $40) 12... Nd7 13. h4 {Game 31: 'transposes to our main game.'} b4 (13... Bb7 14. Kb1 {Game 31: 'is playable but after'} (14. a3 $1 Rc8 15. Kb1 Ne5 16. Qe3 Nc4 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. f4 $1 {This is much more incisive than 18.Rhg1 the move which was mentioned by Emms in Play the Najdorf: Scheveningen Style. White will soon build a deadly initiative in the centre and on the kingside.} b4 19. axb4 Rxb4 20. f5 $1 exf5 21. Rhe1 $1 fxe4 22. Nxe4 Bd5 ({In the event of} 22... Bxe4 23. Qxe4 Re8 24. Re2 $18 {the pin decides.}) 23. Nf6+ $5 {The computer confirms that this is the most direct route to victory.} ({However, the simpler} 23. Ng3 Be6 24. Nh5 Bg4 25. Nxg7 Bxd1 26. Rxd1 {should also do the trick.}) 23... gxf6 24. Bc3 Rb5 25. Rg1 fxg5 26. Qd4 {White went on to win convincingly in Ustimenko - Susla, corr. 2014.}) 14... Rc8 (14... b4 {Black avoids the Na4-b6 idea from the main game.}) 15. Bd3 b4 16. Ne2 a5 17. Ng3 Qc7 18. h5 Ne5 $13 {Salazar-Panno/Buenos Aires/1991/}) (13... Qc7 14. Kb1 b4 15. Na4 a5 ({Gelfand suggested a pawn sacrifice, but it does not quite work:} 15... Bb7 16. Qxb4 Bc6 17. Nc3 Rfb8 18. Qc4 Ne5 19. Qe2 Bb5 20. Qf2 Bxf1 21. Rhxf1 Nc4 22. b3 Rb4 23. Rd3 Rc8 {Aagaard - P.H. Nielsen, Malmo/Copenhagen 2004. This gave compensation for Black, according to Gelfand 15 years ago. But after} 24. f4 $1 $146 Na3+ 25. Kb2 Nc4+ 26. Ka1 Na3 27. Be3 $1 $18 {with Bc1-b2 coming, White has a winning position. The extra pawn is only a small part of it.}) 16. h5 Nc5 17. g6 $3 h6 (17... Nxa4 $2 18. h6 $1 {leads to a mating attack instantly.}) 18. Nxc5 dxc5 19. Be3 Rd8 20. Qc1 Rxd1 21. Qxd1 c4 22. gxf7+ Kxf7 {Nechepurenko - Seeman, Moscow 2006. This is awfully concrete, but also totally desperate for Black.} 23. Qd4 $1 $146 Ba6 (23... c3 24. e5 Bb7 25. Bc4 Rd8 26. Qf4+ Ke8 27. Bb5+ Bc6 28. Qc4 Rc8 29. Qxe6 Bxb5 30. Qg6+ Kf8 31. Bxh6 Qxe5 32. Bxg7+ Qxg7 33. Qf5+ Qf7 34. Qxc8+ $16) 24. Bh3 Qd6 (24... Rd8 25. Bxe6+ $1 Kxe6 26. Qxg7 $16 {and White has excellent winning chances with the three pawns for the bishop.}) 25. Rg1 Qxd4 26. Bxd4 Bf6 27. e5 Be7 28. Rg4 $1 Bc8 29. Rf4+ Ke8 30. Bf2 c3 31. Bf1 Ba6 32. Bxa6 Rxa6 33. bxc3 bxc3 34. Rc4 $16 {White picks up a pawn and has a good chance of winning the endgame.}) 14. Na4 (14. Ne2 {Game 31: '!?' Game 31: 'was successful in Anand - Andreikin, Moscow 2013. But the best way to play is like Fischer, of course.'} a5 15. Be3 Ne5 16. Nd4 Bb7 17. h5 b3 $1 18. axb3 a4 19. b4 a3 $132 {Ilincic-Cvetkovic/Jugoslavia-ch/1990/}) 14... Bb7 $6 (14... Qa5 15. b3 Nc5 (15... Bb7 $5 16. a3 (16. Kb1 {is also interesting. Emms mentions} d5 {but} 17. g6 $1 $146 {is a killer response.}) 16... d5 {Emms reaches this position in a note and gives Black's last move an exclam, but modern engines show a refutation:} 17. g6 $1 fxg6 ({or} 17... e5 {Kurgansky - Polishchuk, email 2007} 18. Be3 $146 d4 19. gxh7+ Kh8 20. Bh6 $1 Bf6 21. axb4 $16) 18. Bh3 e5 19. Be6+ Kh8 20. h5 g5 21. h6 g6 22. Ba1 d4 23. Bxd7 {1-0 Potrata - Wagner, corr. 2012. This is the critical line. But here too White is simply better if he knows what he is doing.}) 16. Bxc5 (16. Nxc5 $1 dxc5 17. Bb2 $14 Rd8 (17... Qxa2 $2 {is surprisingly just lost after} 18. Qf4 Qa5 19. Bc4 $18 {when Black has no defence against the attack on the kingside.}) 18. Qh2 Rxd1+ 19. Kxd1 Qd8+ ({If} 19... Qxa2 20. Kc1 Qa5 {White has:} 21. g6 $1 h6 ({or} 21... fxg6 22. h5 Qd8 23. hxg6 h6 24. Bxg7 $1 {with a winning attack}) 22. gxf7+ Kxf7 23. Rg1 Bf6 24. Qf4 {Black is defenceless.}) 20. Kc1 a5 21. Bc4 Ba6 22. Rd1 Qf8 23. Bxa6 Rxa6 24. Qc7 $1 Rd6 25. Rg1 Qd8 (25... Bd8 26. Qb7 Qe7 27. Qb5 Qd7 28. Qc4 $1 (28. Qxc5 $2 Bb6) 28... Qc6 29. a4 $16) 26. Qxc5 $16 {Moll - Punzon Moraleda, email 2009.}) 16... dxc5 17. Qf4 Ra7 18. Kb1 Rd7 19. Bc4 Rd4 $5 20. Rxd4 cxd4 21. e5 Bd7 22. Nb2 Bb5 23. Qxd4 Rd8 24. Qe4 Qb6 $44 {Inkiov-Vera/Varna/1986/}) (14... a5 15. Rg1 Ba6 {is a reasonable strategy, in similar style to the French Defence. After} 16. Bxa6 Rxa6 {as in A. Horvath - Efimenko, Zalakaros 2015, White should play:} 17. Qh2 $5 $146 Rc6 (17... Qb8 18. Kb1) 18. Kb1 Qc7 19. b3 $1 {White has the better prospects. The following fantasy variation is worth inspecting.} e5 20. Be3 Rc8 21. Rd2 Nf8 22. h5 Ne6 23. Nb2 Rc3 24. Qe2 Nc5 25. g6 fxg6 26. hxg6 h6 27. Bxc5 $5 Qxc5 28. Rgd1 $14 {White has a positional advantage.}) 15. Nb6 $1 (15. Qxb4 $6 Bc6 $17) 15... Rb8 ({Game 31: 'Of course the simple tactical point is:'} 15... Nxb6 16. Qxb4 {Game 31: ''} Bxe4 $2 17. Bxb6 $18) 16. Nxd7 (16. Qxb4 $2 Bc6 $1 $19) 16... Qxd7 17. Kb1 (17. Qxb4 $2 Bxe4) 17... Qc7 (17... f5 18. gxf6 Bxf6 19. Bxf6 Rxf6 20. Qxb4 $14) (17... a5 $5) 18. Bd3 Bc8 $6 (18... f5 19. gxf6 Bxf6 20. Bxf6 Rxf6 21. Rh3 d5 22. Rg1 $14) (18... e5 19. Be3 Rfd8 20. Ba7 $1 Ra8 21. Qf2 Rdc8 22. Bb6 Qc6 23. Rdg1 $16) (18... Rfd8 $5) 19. h5 e5 20. Be3 Be6 (20... f5 21. exf5 Bxf5 22. g6 $16) 21. Rdg1 a5 (21... d5 22. exd5 Bxd5 23. Bxh7+ Kxh7 24. Qxd5 $18) 22. g6 $1 Bf6 (22... fxg6 23. hxg6 hxg6 (23... h6 24. Bxh6 gxh6 25. Qxh6 Bf6 26. g7 $18) 24. Rxg6 Bf7 25. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 26. Bh6+ $1 $18 (26. Bb6 $2 Bxa2+ $1 27. Kxa2 Qxb6 28. Qh6+ Kf7 29. Qh5+ Ke6 $13)) 23. gxh7+ Kh8 (23... Kxh7 24. Bg5 Qe7 25. f4 $1) 24. Bg5 $1 Qe7 (24... Bxg5 25. Qxg5 f6 26. Qg6 Qf7 27. h6 Qxg6 28. Rxg6 gxh6 29. Rhxh6 $16) 25. Rg3 Bxg5 26. Rxg5 Qf6 27. Rhg1 Qxf3 (27... Kxh7 28. Qg2 Rg8 29. Rg6 $1 $18) (27... Qh6 28. Qg2 $18) 28. Rxg7 Qf6 (28... Qf4 29. Qg2 Qh6 30. b3 $1 Rfd8 31. Bc4 Bxc4 32. bxc4 Rf8 33. Qg4 a4 34. Rg8+ Kxh7 35. Qf5+ $18) 29. h6 a4 30. b3 axb3 31. axb3 Rfd8 32. Qg2 Rf8 33. Rg8+ $1 Kxh7 34. Rg7+ Kh8 35. h7 (35. h7 Rfd8 36. Rg8+ Kxh7 37. Rh1+) 1-0

