{Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If you asked me to pick the most instructive game ever played in the theme of demonstrating a territorial advantage, this one would be number one on my list. '} 1. e4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer reaffirms his loyalty to his “best by test”, favourite first move. '} e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'En garde! The problem with being the best is that everyone else is happy to hold you to a draw. In this game, Gheorghiu made his intention to draw the game clear from the start. '} 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. d4 Be7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'There are people who (probably correctly) believe that those who play Petroff's Defence are tired of life itself. I dare you to look at this position and be entertained. This is the passive/super-solid, “I refuse to weaken at any cost, will build a fortress and draw you” version of the Petroff.'} (5... d5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is Black's main move.'}) 6. Bd3 Nf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Let's back off and cede White territory.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It isn't too late for'} 6... d5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'}) 7. h3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer violates one principle – Don't make non-developing moves in the opening without good reason – to give precedence to another: Prevent swaps when you are in possession of a territorial advantage. In this case White's last move disallows ...Bg4, which means that Black's c8-bishop will be denied natural posts on both g4 and f5. '} O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More space gained. '} Nc6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A novelty at the time and not a great one.'} ({I'm surprised Gheorghiu didn't continue with the ultra-solid manoeuvre} 9... Nbd7 10. Nc3 Nf8 {, as in O.Bernstein-H.Süchting, Coburg 1904, where Black did manage to draw.}) ({Alternatively,} 9... d5 {is better, but of course this is in direct violation of Black's zero tolerance policy for any central pawn move which could potentially weaken his position.}) 10. Nc3 h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This seems a little inconsistent with his no weaknesses plan. Black wants to tuck his bishop away with on f8 without fear of Bg5. The problem with pushing the h-pawn is that it creates a future juicy sacrificial target for White on h6. '} 11. Re1 Bf8 12. Rxe8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer deliberately violates the principle about not swapping pieces when we own more space for two reasons: 1. White wants his bishop developed on the aggressive f4-square, rather than passively on e3. 2. After the swap on e8, Black's queen will lose time when White places his other rook on e1.'} ({I like Fischer's move more than} 12. Be3 d5 {.}) 12... Qxe8 (12... Nxe8 {is also a loss of time since Black's knight would need to be moved back to f6.}) 13. Bf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A superior square over e3, since Fischer intends Qd2, Re1 and d4-d5, when the f4-bishop prevents Black from responding with the freeing move ...Ne5. '} Bd7 14. Qd2 $16 Qc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Intending another swap, via ...Bf5. '} 15. d5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer clears the way for Nd4, which in turn controls f5, denying Black ...Bf5. '} Nb4 ({He was slightly better off with} 15... Ne7 {, intending} 16. Re1 ({or} 16. Nd4 Nf5) 16... Bf5 17. Bf1 Qd7 {and to unravel with ...Re8 at some point, though White still holds a nagging space edge.}) 16. Ne4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The same no-swap-when-you-own-more-space principle is again violated!'} ({Another option was simply to secure the d3-bishop first with either} 16. Bb1) ({or} 16. Bf1 {.}) 16... Nxe4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu gets another swap, yet is not outplaying Fischer, since Black loses a little ground each time.'} (16... Nxd3 17. Nxf6+) (16... Nh5 {is an option, but Black should be wary of} 17. Bb1 Nxf4 ({the careful} 17... Na6 {looks better, intending ...Nxf4 next move, or if} 18. Be3 {then} Nc5) 18. Qxf4 Qd8 19. a3 Na6 20. Neg5 $1 Qf6 ({not} 20... hxg5 $4 21. Nxg5 Qf6 22. Bh7+ Kh8 23. Qh4 {and wins}) ({while} 20... Qe7 $2 21. Bh7+ Kh8 22. Bc2 $1 Kg8 23. Re1 hxg5 24. Rxe7 gxf4 25. Rxd7 {is a wretched ending for Black}) 21. Qxf6 gxf6 22. Ne4 Bg7 23. Ng3 $1 {(seizing control over f5)} f5 24. Bxf5 Bxb2 25. Ra2 Bxf5 26. Nxf5 Bg7 27. Re2 Nc5 28. Re7 Rc8 29. g4 {with advantage to White.}) 17. Bxe4 Na6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu's create-no-weakness strategy continues.'} ({I would play} 17... a5 18. a3 Na6 {, when White should avoid grabbing the a5-pawn and continue with} 19. b4 $1 {, denying Black's knight use of c5.}) 18. Nd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Preventing ...Bf5. '} Nc5 19. Bc2 a5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu finally summons the nerve to push a pawn past his own third rank, preventing b2-b4. '} 20. Re1 Qd8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): Black threatens the unravelling plans ...Qf6, followed by ...Re8, as well as ...Be7 followed by ...Bg5. How can White prevent Black's dream of freedom? ANSWER: Go after the black king with a direct attack by lifting the rook to the third rank. '} 21. Re3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Steinitz pointed out the principle: The side with more territory must take action and attack, otherwise the advantage is in danger of dissipating via apathy. This simple yet powerful move pushes Black closer and closer to the edge of tolerance levels. '} b6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} (21... Qf6 22. Rg3 Kh8 23. Ra3 b6 24. b4 Nb7 25. Be3 $16) ({Instead: a)} 21... Be7 $4 {(intending ...Bg5) is crushed by} 22. Bxh6 $1 {, a sacrifice Black is unable to accept.}) ({b)} 21... Qh4 22. Rg3 g6 23. Nf3 Qh5 $1 {is uncomfortable, yet still better than what Black got in the game.}) ({c) The engine suggests the radical} 21... g5 $5 22. Rg3 Bg7 23. Be3 Qe7 {, when Black has created a huge hole on f5 and slightly weakened his king, yet his pieces are far more active here than in the game.}) 22. Rg3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Threat: Bxh6. '} Kh8 23. Nf3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer unearths a hidden fallibility in Black's seemingly solid position. Threat: Qd4, followed by Bxh6. '} Qe7 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Not'} 23... Qf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} 24. Be3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '(threat: Bd4, followed by Qxh6+, when Black's queen moves away)'} Re8 (24... Qe7 25. Bd4 f6 26. Nh4 {and White wins.}) ({or} 24... Qxb2 $2 25. Bd4 Qb4 26. Qxh6+ Kg8 27. Qh7#) 25. Bd4 $18) 24. Qd4 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The threat is stronger than the execution.'} ({My student Jason suggested} 24. Bxh6 {, which is sound yet messier than Fischer's line. After} gxh6 ({however, Black can refuse the piece in favour of} 24... Ne4 $1 {, forcing} 25. Bxe4 Qxe4 {, when White has “only” won a pawn}) 25. Qd4+ f6 26. Nh4 $1 Be8 27. Qg4 {the engine has White up by almost +5 (although when I played Black, it just kept shuffling for White, refusing to take action).}) 24... Qf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The things we humans do, just to survive. Up until now, Gheorghiu tenderly nourished his pristine formation with the morality level of the Dalai Lama, Mother Teresa and Mr. Rogers, combined. Here he is forced to do that which he feared most: to sustain heavy damage to his own structure.'} (24... Re8 25. Bxh6) ({There wasn't much choice as} 24... f6 $2 {punctures the kingside light squares beyond redemption. For example:} 25. Nh4 {(threatening a fork on g6)} Be8 26. Nf5 Qf7 27. Bd2 $1 Nd7 28. Bc3 Qh5 29. Re3 Ne5 30. f4 Nd7 31. Ne7 Nc5 32. Bg6 $1 {and wins since} Bxg6 33. g4 {leaves Black's queen trapped.}) 25. Qxf6 gxf6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Our offended eyes hurt. How's that “I refuse to weaken my structure” strategy working out for you? '} 26. Nd4 $1 Re8 27. Re3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The ominously creeping reverse motion of White's rook bears watching. This move displays a remarkable depth of understanding of the position's requirements. Black's rook is his most active piece. So Fischer either eliminates it (after which Black's h-pawn falls, as shown in the notes below) or forces Black to back off and cede the e-file. '} Rb8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Gheorghiu looks for play on the queenside with a potential ...b6-b5 break.'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'If instead'} 27... Rxe3 28. Bxe3 { f5+-  Nf5+-} Kg7 {, White has an incredible winning plan:} 29. Kh2 $3 {with the simple yet brilliant idea to walk the king up to h5 and then pick off the h6-pawn. A human is better at spotting such ideas than the engines; for both Fritz and Stockfish, 29 Kh2 is their fourth (!) choice.}) 28. b3 b5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Otherwise it's just passive defence for Black. '} 29. cxb5 Bxb5 30. Nf5 ({The engines prefer to take the b-pawn:} 30. Nxb5 Rxb5 31. Re8 Kg7 {, when simply} 32. Bxh6+ Kxh6 33. Rxf8 Kg7 34. Rc8 Rb4 ({or} 34... Rb7 35. Ra8) 35. Rxc7 Rd4 36. Ra7 Rd2 37. Bf5 Rxa2 38. b4 {is lost for Black.}) 30... Bd7 (30... h5 31. Ng3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'is no fun for Black either.'} h4 32. Nf5 $18) 31. Nxh6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Rb4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Did Fischer blunder? After all, his bishop on f4 hangs and, if eliminated, then his knight on h6 is loose as well. EXERCISE (combination alert): White to play and not lose material. ANSWER #1: Shift the rook over to g3, threatening mate on g8. '} 32. Rg3 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It's not easy to refuse a “request” this definite.'} ({ANSWER #2: The engine's alien “mind” found the more mathematical solution} 32. Nxf7+ $3 {. This geometric anomaly really lords it over Black, who appears to be winning a piece, but isn't:} Kg7 ({or} 32... Kg8 33. Nh6+ {, when} Kg7 $4 34. Rg3+ {forces mate in two}) 33. Nd8 $3 {and if} Rxf4 34. Rg3+ {, Black must block with ...Bg4, handing back the extra piece, since} Kh6 $2 {gets mated by} 35. Nf7+ Kh5 36. Rg8 $1 {(threats: Rh8+ and Rxf8)} Bh6 37. Rh8 {and Rxh6.}) 32... Bxh6 (32... Rxf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'allows both'} {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and'} 33. Rg8# {# # Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'} (33. Nxf7#)) 33. Bxh6 Ne4 34. Bg7+ Kh7 ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A mistake in a lost position, as'} 34... Kg8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'doesn't save the game either: after'} 35. Bxe4 Rxe4 36. Bxf6+ $18 Kf8 37. Rc3 {White's win is trivially easy from this stage, and Capa, Fischer or Magnus-like technique isn't required.}) 35. f3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. The pinned knight is lost.'} 1-0

