1. b3 {Bobby used Larsen's opening to defeat Tukmakov at Buenos Aires and Mecking at Palma de Mallorca in 1970.} e5 2. Bb2 d6 3. c4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'One inherent flaw with the b3/c4 mix is that, in normal 1 c4 English formations, White often plays for an immediate b2-b4. In this case, even if we play the move later, it comes with tempo loss. '} Nf6 4. g3 g6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black's early...Nf6 hinders ...f7-f5 but in this case, he doesn't play for ...f7-f5 at all. QUESTION: What other plan is there for Black? ANSWER: In this formation Black plays for a central build-up with ...c7-c6 and ...d6-d5. '} 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. e3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Probably White's best formation in the b2-b3 English versus KID. QUESTION: What do you like about White's set-up? ANSWER: I like its inherent flexibility. White can play for d2-d4, or else d2-d3 and later challenging Black's centre with f2-f4. '} Nbd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Hindering his own ...d6-d5 pawn break. It may be better to delay his b8-knight's development to d7, since it weakens his grip over the key d5-square.'} ({QUESTION: How would Black go about playing for a ...d6-d5 break? ANSWER: As mentioned earlier, the main plan for Black when he plays an early ...Nf6 is to play for ...c7-c6 and ...d6-d5, like this:} 7... c6 {(the most accurate move)} 8. Nge2 d5 {(Black achieves his thematic central break)} 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. d4 e4 11. h3 {(to guard against a future ...Bg4)} Nc6 12. O-O Re8 {, reaching a reversed King's Indian Attack position where chances are approximately balanced, M.Ujtelky-T.V.Petrosian, Leipzig Olympiad 1960. White plays on the queenside and down the c-file, while Black may try and build for a kingside attack.}) 8. Nge2 Re8 9. d3 Nf8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Where is he going with the knight? ANSWER: Black's last move is dual purpose: 1. He removes his knight from d7, possibly to reinstate the ...c6-c6, ...d6-d5 plan. 2. Black may play for kingside light squares and a kingside attack with a future ...h7-h5 and ...Nh7-g5 plan.'} ({Black must move the knight at some point or another anyway. For instance, K.Spraggett-J.Nogueiras Santiago, Clermont Ferrand 1989, saw} 9... c6 10. Qd2 Nf8 11. d4 {(hindering ...d6-d5)} e4 12. h3 h5 13. d5 c5 14. O-O-O N8h7 15. g4 $1 {with opposite wing attacks.}) 10. O-O c6 11. b4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White expands on the queenside but, as mentioned earlier, it took him two moves to achieve b2-b3-b4 – an inherent flaw in our b3/c4 systems. QUESTION: Is there any way to fix this issue? ANSWER: I don't see one, other than beginning with the 1 c4 move order, but then the game is no longer a Nimzo-Larsen Attack. Perhaps we should open the game with the Orang-utan – maybe it should be renamed the Accelerated Nimzo-Larsen Attack! – 1 b4!? to eliminate this problem! '} Qc7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This looks passive and unnecessary.'} ({Black should go for the thematic} 11... d5 12. cxd5 cxd5 13. Rc1 {, but he may have feared a coming Nb5 trick.}) 12. a4 Be6 13. Rc1 Qd7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Getting off the c-file and clearly demonstrating that his earlier ...Qc7?! was a pure waste of energy. '} 14. Re1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: What is the purpose of White's last move? ANSWER: He wants to avoid an exchange of light-squared bishops and prepares to sidestep the coming ...Bh3 with Bh1. ' Bobby often went out of his way to save his Bishops (..Re8 and ...Bh8 as Black and Re1 and Bh1 here).} Bh3 15. Bh1 d5 16. cxd5 cxd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'EXERCISE (planning): By means of what expedient can White win material or greatly improve his position?    ANSWER: Step 1: Threaten a fork on c7. '} 17. Nb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Ne6 $2 {Black overeacts to the threat of Nc7/Rc7. Instead of the text which drops a pawn, Black should have played 17...Re7 maintaining the balance.} ({QUESTION: Rather than his all-important e-pawn, shouldn't Black hand over a7 with} 17... Rad8 18. Nxa7 Ng4 19. Qc2 Bh6 20. Nc3 d4 {, when at least he has some fishing chances? ANSWER: I completely agree. Black's decision to hand over e5 is suicidal, but I suppose some of us show a marked inclination for non-existence over a miserable, prolonged existence.}) ({Black can also try} 17... Re7 $5 {, but then his centre comes under heavy assault after} 18. f4 $1 {.}) 18. Bxe5 Rec8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black begins to unravel, dropping another pawn.'} ({He had to try} 18... Ng4 19. Bxg7 Kxg7 {.}) 19. Rxc8+ {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Rxc8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Resigning himself to the loss of pawn number two;'} ({since} 19... Qxc8 20. Qb3 Qd7 21. Nec3 {wins the one on d5.}) 20. Nxa7 Re8 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'It isn't too late to retrace his steps with'} 21. Nb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '.' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Time for Black to resign, right? He is two pawns down with an inferior position – and against Bobby Fischer! The only factor on which Black places some hope is that it's a simultaneous game, where bone-headed decisions from great players are commonplace occurrences. '} Ng5 22. Bf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Why deviate a single inch if the path you follow is the true one?'} (22. Bxf6 Bxf6 23. Qb3 Be6 24. Nf4 $18 {is another good continuation for White.}) ({Fischer was perhaps worried about his kingside light squares, and missed a method of refracting the opponent's aggression until it transformed into a hypnotic semblance of its original intent:} 22. Qa1 $1 Qf5 {(after formal salutations, the parties begin negotiations for rights to f3)} 23. Bc3 $1 {(allowing the dreaded check)} Nf3+ 24. Bxf3 Qxf3 25. Nf4 $1 {– a triple purpose move which effectively ends Black's resistance, since the knight: 1. Covers the mate threat on g2. 2. Attacks the attacker on h3. 3. Cuts off the black queen's coverage of f6.}) 22... Ne6 23. Nbc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!'} d4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black seizes his chance to disrupt. At last Peinado abandons his deference to his famous opponent and, for the first time in the game, engages in a hostile gesture. '} {.} 24. Nxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} Nxd4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?'} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Black generates serious counterplay after' Black can try to complicate matters with} 24... Nxf4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' but after} 25. gxf4 Nh5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', threatening tricks on both f4 and d4.'} 26. Nce2 Bxd4 27. Nxd4 Nxf4 28. Bf3 {White is still winning.}) 25. exd4 Rxe1+ 26. Qxe1 Qxd4 27. Nb5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer plays the latter part of the game in remarkably un-Fischer-like fashion. Now Black is allowed a miraculous save, reminiscent of one of those commercials with a guy dying of thirst on a desert island, suddenly rescued by a bikini-clad beauty, carrying a frosty mug of beer.'} ({White can still consolidate with} 27. b5 $1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '.'} Ng4 28. Bd2 Qxd3 29. Ne2 $1 {, intending Nf4.}) 27... Qxd3 28. Bxb7 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'After a vexing mistake, what often destroys our position isn't the mistake itself but, instead, our loss of composure from having made the mistake in the first place. The d3-pawn may have been expendable but the loss of initiative clearly isn't. Fischer grossly underestimates Black's potential in the position.'} ({He should challenge the dark squares in an attempt to rectify previous indiscretions with} 28. Be5 $1 {.}) 28... Nd5 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} 29. Bd6 $2 {The right square, but the wrong piece! Correct is} (29. Nd6 Bd7 30. Qd2 Qb1+ 31. Kg2 Nxb4 32. Nxf7 $18 {Black's ...Nd5 is tricky (....Qf3 ideas) and Bobby overlooks threats to his King.}) 29... h6 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Covering against back rank tricks. Suddenly, Black may be winning! He threatens ...Qf3!. '} 30. Nc7 $2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A rash gesture which leaves Fischer no room to retreat from what he just set in motion.' White had to relieve the pressure on the backrank with 30.f4, but Black is already doing well. For example:} ({Cyrus Lakdawala: 'White's last chance was'} 30. f4 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!'} Qf3 31. Bxd5 Qxd5 32. Kf2 {Cyrus Lakdawala: ', but even here his survival chances look rough with his dilapidated kingside light squares in a sad state of disrepair.'} Bd4+ 33. Nxd4 Qxd4+ 34. Qe3 Qxd6) 30... Bc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A move clearly demonstrating hostile sentiments toward White's queen, who by now is almost out of wiggle room on the first rank. Houdini's evaluation: -9.34 – the equivalent of an extra queen for Black! '} (30... Nc3 31. Bg2 Bxg2 32. Qe8+ Kh7 33. Kxg2 Qxd6 34. Qxf7 {offers White chances to resist.}) {Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: Fischer would have been slaughtered had Black found'} 31. Qc1 Bb2 $6 (31... Bd2 $1 {is crushing.} 32. Qa1 Nc3 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(denial gets harder and harder when rudely confronted with an inescapable truth: Black's attackers, supple as eels navigating seaweed, weave their way past all obstacles, and White is completely busted)'} 33. Bc5 (33. Bg2 Ne2+ 34. Kh1 Bc1 $3 {(the bishop inserts itself, an unwanted parenthesis in an otherwise flowing sentence, effectively choking off the white queen's passageway along the first rank, all the while calmly leaving the h3-bishop en prise – now White's queen simmers in resentment from her new downgraded status)} 35. Bxh3 Qf3+ $1 36. Bg2 Qxf2 {with deadly threats on both g1 and e1. White's penitent king looks upon his punishment as reward. Defeat is the great eradicator of vanity, but not if the defeat merely hides within the analysis! Fischer's best games appear as close to the absolute as the human mind is capable – but most certainly not in this game, even if it was simul! SUMMARY: When Black plays an early ...Nf6 against our b3/c4/g3 English set-up, he plans ...c7-c6 and an eventual ...d6-d5 central challenge.}) 33... Ne2+ 34. Kh1 Bc3 {wins the Queen which cannot abandon the backrank.}) 32. Qe1 Bc3 33. Qc1 {Cyrus Lakdawala: '. EXERCISE (combination alert): Black just agreed to a draw in a completely winning position. What did he miss? ' Again 33...Bd2! was a winner.} 1/2-1/2

