{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 1 Too little, too late. Although Sherwin makes no serious errors in the opening, he misses several equalizing opportunities. Demonstrating the technical virtuosity that is to become his hallmark, Fischer, with astonishing maturity, gradually strengthens his grip by accumulating small advantages: the better center and the two Bishops. Sherwin, meanwhile, attempts to consolidate his position - only to see his 14-year-old opponent shatter it with a thunderbolt (18 xh7). It brings to mind Alekhine's combinations, which also seemed to spring from nowhere. Sherwin, lashing back, refuses to fall. However, his defense finally disintegrates under a series of acute blows to his wobbly King. ''GAME N. 1 Too little, too late. Although Sherwin makes no serious errors in the opening, he misses several equalizing opportunities. Demonstrating the technical virtuosity that is to become his hallmark, Fischer, with astonishing maturity, gradually strengthens his grip by accumulating small advantages: the better center and the two Bishops. Sherwin, meanwhile, attempts to consolidate his position - only to see his 14-year-old opponent shatter it with a thunderbolt (18 xh7). It brings to mind Alekhine's combinations, which also seemed to spring from nowhere. Sherwin, lashing back, refuses to fall. However, his defense finally disintegrates under a series of acute blows to his wobbly King.  ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 1 Too little, too late. Although Sherwin makes no serious errors in the opening, he misses several equalizing opportunities. Demonstrating the technical virtuosity that is to become his hallmark, Fischer, with astonishing maturity, gradually strengthens his grip by accumulating small advantages: the better center and the two Bishops. Sherwin, meanwhile, attempts to consolidate his position - only to see his 14-year-old opponent shatter it with a thunderbolt (18 xh7). It brings to mind Alekhine's combinations, which also seemed to spring from nowhere. Sherwin, lashing back, refuses to fall. However, his defense finally disintegrates under a series of acute blows to his wobbly King. ''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This is the first game of Fischer's book, "My 60 Memorable Games", and I think it is a perfect prelude for a great work. I remember when I first studied this game it made a strong impression on me, because even as young as age 14, we can already see many qualities that would bring great successes for Fischer later: excellent opening knowledge, accurate calculation and very strong logical reasoning behind any move, always supported by energetic, concrete play. '} 1. e4 {Quintiliano,Renato Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer''} c5 2. Nf3 e6 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'This is the move Fischer was waiting for.'' ' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Dies ist der Zug, auf den Fischer wartete.'' '} 3. d3 {Bobby Fischer: ', This used to be my favorite. I thought it led to a favorable variation of the King's Indian reversed, particularly after Black has committed himself with ...e6. ', This used to be my favorite. I thought it led to a favorable variation of the King's Indian reversed, particularly after Black has committed himself with ...e6. 'Bobby Fischer: ', This used to be my favorite. I thought it led to a favorable variation of the King's Indian reversed, particularly after Black has committed himself with ...e6. ' Maddox, ChessBase: 'KIA.'' ' ' Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The KIA was one of Fischer's weapons for many years against 2...e6 in the Sicilian. In his own words, he was convinced that White gets a good KID (also one of Fischer's main lines as Black) with reversed colours, since Black has already "committed" his position with 2...e6.'} Nc6 4. g3 Nf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer-Ivkov, Santa Monica, 1966, continued''Fischer-Ivkov, Santa Monica, 1966, continued ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer-Ivkov, Santa Monica, 1966, continued''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'The immediate'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'A different set-up was tried by Ivkov:' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Das sofortige'' '} 4... d5 5. Nbd2 ({A little bit of modern finesse:} 5. Qe2 $5 {Quintiliano,Renato is an interesting option, as after} Nf6 6. Bg2 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. e5 Nd7 {White has now} 9. c4 $1 {[%csl Gb1] when delaying the development of the knight is useful in this position, as White can increase the tension in the centre with c3, or moving the d-pawn would be a serious concession for Black.}) 5... Bd6 6. Bg2 Nge7 7. O-O O-O {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black managed to avoid losing space in the centre with e4-e5, but'} 8. Nh4 $1 $36 {Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: '!' '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with chances for initiative.''with chances for initiative. ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with chances for initiative.''' Quintiliano,Renato: '!?' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!' Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'is Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966.'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'preparing the march of the f-pawn, still allowed him to play for the initiative in' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'geschah in Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966.'' '} ({Also here White has tried other ideas nowadays, for instance:} 8. Re1 {Quintiliano,Renato} Qc7 9. Qe2 f6 10. c3 Bd7 11. a3 $5 {[%cal Gb2b4,Yd3d4];1-0 Kovalev,V (2655)-Kanmazalp,O (2481) TUR-chT Ankara 2018 (12.6)})) 5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O ({Bobby Fischer: 'More usual is''More usual is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'More usual is''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The most played is'} 6... d5 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'leads to standard KIA lines.'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'but Black refrains from fighting for the centre in this game. As we will see, not a wise strategy against the young Fischer.' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'führt zu normalen KIA-Abspielen.'' '}) {Bobby Fischer: 'but Black has purposely delayed placing his center Pawns. Has he a new idea in mind? ''but Black has purposely delayed placing his center Pawns. Has he a new idea in mind?  ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'but Black has purposely delayed placing his center Pawns. Has he a new idea in mind? '''} 7. Nbd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After''After ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'After''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'White also has an edge after'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In his notes, Fischer is little impressed by' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Ein Plus hat Weiß auch nach'' '} 7. e5 {Quintiliano,Renato: ', although he thinks White has a small advantage after'} Nd5 ({We can find a recent model game on} 7... Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato} 8. Re1 f6 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. c4 $1 {probably aiming c3 and d3-d4, with strong control in centre.} Qb6 11. Nc3 d6 12. a3 $1 {a clever move and the start of a strong plan} e5 {Black had not an easy way to arrange his central pawns, but now he has to be permanently worried about the light squares} 13. Be3 Bg4 14. h3 Bh5 {the next moves by White were really strong:} 15. b4 $1 Qd8 16. g4 $1 Bg6 17. Nh4 Bf7 18. Nf5 $16 {[%csl Gf5,Yg2];1-0 Svidler,P (2745)-Van Foreest,J (2615) Bundesliga 1617 2016 (8.1) White's creative play is crowned with his dominance over the light squares. His position seems full of potential, the Bg2 and the Nf5 in special are very strong and put a lot of pressure on Black's camp. White won convincingly in}) 8. Nbd2 ({White also achieved an advantage after} 8. c4 {Quintiliano,Renato} Nc7 9. Nc3 Rb8 $2 10. Ne4 $1 b6 11. Bf4 {[%csl Gd6]} Bb7 12. h4 $14 {[%cal Yd3d4];1-0 Delchev,A (2648)-Gislason,G (2318) London Classic op 5th 2013 (3) White has successfully prevented Black from getting some air with ...d7-d6 and can continue with d3-d4 next, with a pleasant space advantage.}) 8... f6 (8... d6 {Quintiliano,Renato is probably a better choice.}) 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. Re1 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'gives whites an edge.''gives whites an edge. ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'gives whites an edge.''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In this position Black's main problem is that there is not an easy way to develop the central pawns, as they could become targets or leave Black with weak squares in his camp, so White's play should be easier.'}) ({It should be noted that} 7. Re1 {Quintiliano,Renato is probably more precise, as White keeps more flexibility in his next choices.}) 7... Rb8 {Bobby Fischer: '; Sherwin slid the Rook here with his pinky, as if to emphasize the cunning of this mysterious move.''; Sherwin slid the Rook here with his pinky, as if to emphasize the cunning of this mysterious move. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '; Sherwin slid the Rook here with his pinky, as if to emphasize the cunning of this mysterious move.''' Quintiliano,Renato: '?!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This seems too odd for me, but Black can justify that after either ...b7-b6 or ...b7-b5 the rook is better placed out of the diagonal, and he has a point.'} ({Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'We see'' ' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Die Fortsetzung'' '} 7... d5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'would still transpose to the main line:'} 8. Re1 b5 9. e5 Nd7 10. Nf1 b4 ({If I could give advice to someone who is afraid of getting mated as Black in such positions, it would be study Leinier Dominguez' games in this line! Like in this nice example:} 10... a5 {Quintiliano,Renato} 11. h4) 11. h4 a5 12. Bf4 (12. h5 {Quintiliano,Renato} h6 $1 {First: it is very important stop this pawn, as h5-h6 would leave the dark squares very weak on the kingside.} 13. N1h2 Re8 14. Ng4 Bf8 $1 {[%cal Yf8e8,Ye7f8] Second: the regroupment with e8-f8 prevents sacrifices on h6, securing the kingside.} 15. Bf4 a4 {only now, Black can continue his actions on queenside} 16. c4 bxc3 17. bxc3 Ba6 18. Bf1 a3 $132 {0-1 Grischuk,A (2783)-Dominguez Perez,L (2754) Beijing Sportaccord Basque rapid 2013 (1.1)}) 12... a4 13. a3 $1 $132 {Fischer,R-Miagmasuren,L Sousse izt 1967 Bobby Fischer: '!' '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer-Mjagmasuren, Sousse Interzonal 1967, leads to double-edged play where Black's chances on the Q-side countervail White's K-side attack - but White usually comes first.''Fischer-Mjagmasuren, Sousse Interzonal 1967, leads to double-edged play where Black's chances on the Q-side countervail White's K-side attack - but White usually comes first. ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Fischer,R-Miagmasuren,L Sousse izt 1967 Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer-Mjagmasuren, Sousse Interzonal 1967, leads to double-edged play where Black's chances on the Q-side countervail White's K-side attack - but White usually comes first.''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'in Fischer,R-Miagmasuren,L Sousse izt 1967.'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'An important move for White, as similar to the march of the h-pawn on the kingside, Black was about to weaken many squares on the queenside now with a4-a3. Fischer assessed this position as double-edged in view of Black's counterplay on the queenside, but points out that White is usually faster going for the king. Actually, we can say he was the first player to prove this evaluation in practice, by playing the following beautiful game:' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'folgte in Fischer,R-Miagmasuren,L Sousse izt 1967.'' '} bxa3 {Quintiliano,Renato} 14. bxa3 Na5 $2 {Black just tried a bad plan and got no counterplay, while White's attack comes undisturbed.} 15. Ne3 Ba6 16. Bh3 d4 17. Nf1 Nb6 18. Ng5 Nd5 19. Bd2 Bxg5 20. Bxg5 Qd7 21. Qh5 Rfc8 22. Nd2 Nc3 23. Bf6 $1 Qe8 24. Ne4 g6 25. Qg5 Nxe4 26. Rxe4 c4 27. h5 $1 cxd3 28. Rh4 Ra7 29. Bg2 $1 dxc2 30. Qh6 Qf8 31. Qxh7+ $3 {1-0 Fischer,R-Myagmarsuren,L Interzonal-07+ 1967 (3)}) 8. Re1 d6 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'By refraining from ...d6-d5, black hopes to neutralize white's e4-e5.'' ' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Indem er von ...d6-d5 Abstand nimmt, hofft Schwarz, das weiße e4-e5 zu neutralisieren.'' '} 9. c3 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Supporting d2-d4. If black gives away space in the center, white shouldn't be afraid to take it.'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'As Black is not intending to fight for the centre, Fischer proceeds to place another pawn there.' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Unterstützt d2-d4. Wenn Schwarz im Zentrum Raum abtritt, sollte Weiß nicht davor scheuen, diesen zu besetzen.'' '} b6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Not bad.'; Not bad.'Bobby Fischer: '; Not bad.' Maddox, ChessBase: '?!'' ' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Not a bad move, according to Fischer.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'But I had expected''But I had expected ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'But I had expected''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Queenside expansion is better:'' ' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Besser ist eine Expansion am Damenflügel:'' '} 9... b5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'was expected'} 10. d4 cxd4 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if''if ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'if'''} 10... b4 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: '?!'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'fails tactically due to'} 11. e5 $1 bxc3 $2 ({Quintiliano,Renato: 'Better is'} 11... dxe5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'but after'} 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. c4 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds the advantage''holds the advantage ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'holds the advantage''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is deprived of counterplay, while White keeps his positional advantages.'}) 12. exf6 cxd2 13. Nxd2 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins a piece''wins a piece ChessBase: '+-!' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins a piece'''}) 11. cxd4 d5 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'with equality''with equality ChessBase: '=''Bobby Fischer: 'with equality' Maddox, ChessBase: '.'' '} 12. e5 {Quintiliano,Renato} Nd7 {this position resembles the main lines, but I still prefer White after} 13. Nb3 $14 {[%csl Gc5][%cal Yc1e3,Ya1c1] as besides having more space, he can play for advantage exploiting exposed squares in Black's camp, like c5.}) 10. d4 Qc7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; This leads to trouble.''; This leads to trouble. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '; This leads to trouble.''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This mistake is the start of Black's problems.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black should strive for counterplay by opening the c-file:''Black should strive for counterplay by opening the c-file: ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black should strive for counterplay by opening the c-file:''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Black has counterplay after'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black should had taken the last chance to fight for the centre with' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Gegenspiel hat Schwarz nach'' '} 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 d5 12. e5 Nd7 $132 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.''etc. ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'etc.''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and he is solid, although White's play is more pleasant.'}) 11. e5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Fischer gets e4-e5 in anyway. Now black has no time for ...d6-d5.'' ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer finds an energetic way to exploit Black's imprecise play.' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Fischer kommt trotzdem zu e4-e5. Nun hat Schwarz keine Zeit für ...d6-d5.'' '} Nd5 {Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: '?!'' '} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Worse is''Worse is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Worse is''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Not'' ' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Nicht'' '} 11... Nd7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase: '?'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'was bad in view of the nice tactical idea:'} 12. exd6 Bxd6 13. Ne4 cxd4 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Bf4 e5 16. Nxe5 $1 Ndxe5 17. cxd4 {Bobby Fischer: 'winning a pawn.'winning a pawn.'Bobby Fischer: 'winning a pawn.' Maddox, ChessBase: '+-'' ' ' Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: 'winning a pawn.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'The best try is''The best try is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'The best try is''' Bobby Fischer: ''Maddox, ChessBase: 'Best was'' ' Quintiliano,Rena