{B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack Garry Kasparov: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack''} 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 {'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation.  ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation. '''} 4. f4 (4. Nf3 {– Game Nos.73 and 77}) 4... Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O ({The alternative is} 5... c5 6. dxc5 (6. Bb5+ Bd7 {Savon-Korchnoi, 41st USSR Championship, Moscow 1973}) 6... Qa5 7. Bd3 Qxc5 8. Qe2 Bg4 $1 9. Be3 Qa5 10. O-O O-O 11. h3 (11. Rad1 Nc6 12. Bc4 Nh5 $5 {Spassky-Fischer, 17th matchgame, Reykjavik 1972}) 11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nc6 13. Ne2 $6 Nd7 14. c3 $2 Nde5 $1 15. fxe5 Nxe5 16. Qg3 Nxd3 {. 0-1 (Hübner-Korchnoi, Skelleftea 1989).}) 6. Be2 {''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi). ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: ''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi).'''} ({After this game everyone, including Fischer, switched to} 6. Bd3 {(Volume 4, Game No. 72).}) 6... c5 7. dxc5 Qa5 8. O-O Qxc5+ 9. Kh1 Nc6 10. Nd2 {'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation.'''} (10. Bd3 {followed by Qe1 and Be3 is better (Boleslavsky-Ufimtsev, Sverdlovsk 1951),}) ({or first} 10. Qe1 {. 'Prior to the tournament,' Korchnoi remembers, 'in a holiday home near Moscow, I had studied this very position with my helper, grandmaster Vasyukov. Generally speaking, at that time I did not play the Pirc Defence, and its various branches were totally unfamiliar to me, but Vasyukov persuaded me to take a risk: Fischer was bound to play this way! And Fischer did not let me down.'}) 10... a5 {Garry Kasparov: '!''A strong novelty. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'A strong novelty.'''} ({Garry Kasparov: 'or''or ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'or'''} 10... Be6 11. Nb3 Qb6 12. Bf3 (12. g4 $1 {was judged to favour White.}) 12... a5 13. Re1 a4 14. Be3 Qa6 15. Nd4 a3 16. b3 Bg4 17. Nxc6 Qxc6 18. Nd5 Bxf3 19. Nxe7+ Kh8 20. gxf3 Qd7 21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. Qxd5 Bxa1 23. Rxa1 Qh3 24. Qd1 b5 25. c3 {Arkhipov,S (2532)-Safin,S (2518)/Abu Dhabi 2000/CBM 077 ext/ 0-1 (39)}) ({At that time} 10... Nd4 11. Nb3 Nxb3 12. axb3 {(Vasyukov-Bastrikov, Kiev 1957)}) 11. a4 (11. Nb3 $6 {'This soon creates serious difficulties for White.} Qb6 12. a4 {Defending against the threat of ...a5-a4-a3. 0-1 (32) Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V 9. Candidates Tournament, Curaçao 1962 My Great Predecessors V [Garry Kasparov]} ({The alternatives were unfavourable:} 12. Nd5 Nxd5 13. exd5 a4 $1 14. dxc6 $6 (14. Nd2 Nb4 15. Nc4 Qc5) 14... axb3 15. cxb7 Rxa2 $1 {(Korchnoi)} 16. bxc8=Q Rxc8 17. Bd2 Bxb2 {and wins;}) ({or} 12. a3 a4 13. Nd2 Qc5 ({or} 13... Qd4 {.}))) ({Non-routine moves demand non-routine replies to refute them,' writes Korchnoi, who recommends} 11. Nc4 {. Here are some sample variations:} Bg4 $1 (11... Ng4 12. Nd5 $1 (12. Bxg4 Bxg4 13. Qxg4 Qxc4 {is insipid}) 12... Nf2+ 13. Rxf2 Qxf2 14. Be3 Qh4 15. Ncb6 $1 ({not} 15. g3 Qh3 {Westerinen-Donner, Bamberg 1968}) 15... Rb8 16. Nxc8 Rfxc8 (16... Rbxc8) 17. g3 Qh6 18. f5 g5 19. Qd2 $1 ({not} 19. h4 Bxb2 {Filipowicz-Plater, Warsaw 1964}) {or} 19... Bxb2 (19... Bf6 20. h4 {.}) 20. Bxg5 Qg7 21. Rf1 {, and 'Black has a difficult position'}) 12. Be3 Qh5 13. Bxg4 Nxg4 14. h3 ({or} 14. Bg1 b5 $1 15. Nb6 Bd4 $1 16. h3 Bxg1 17. Nxa8 Nf2+ 18. Rxf2 Qxd1 19. Rxd1 Bxf2 {with equ