{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 13 Something new.  During the mid-fifties, Gligorich, Reshevsky, and Najdorf were considered the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters. Within a few years Fischer managed to surpass them. However, in so doing, he succeeded in beating Gligorich only once - up to 1966 (see game 56). On the occasion of this first win, Fischer employs a novel attacking system (13 Qe2) against the Dragon Variation. Gligorich fails to react vigorously enough and makes the mistake of castling too soon, thereby exposing himself to the same type of sacrificial combination that demolished Larsen in game 2. '} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bobby Fischer Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 ({So for a game appearing on this Dragons site this game does feature a pretty weird move order but it is sort of plausible that the same middlegame position could be reached via slightly normal means e.g.} 2... d6 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. f3 Nc6 7. Bc4 Bd7 8. Bb3 {and now} Na5 {rather than 8...Bg7. Okay so I could try to justify prioritising some of those moves over others but I'm certainly not going to try to pull the wool over your eyes! What is true is that this strictly speaking 'B57' encounter struggles to fit into a serious opening classification but lacking in slow play games in this current World situation, I thought we'd get retro and maybe occasionally feature the odd Bobby Fischer or other all time greats game.}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 {Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation.''} 6. Bc4 {Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square.''} ({Most popular here is} 6. Bg5 {which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as'} g6 7. Bxf6 exf6 {isn't great for the pawn structure!}) 6... Bd7 ({Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap Bobby Fischer: 'Recently in a skittles game someone tried' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap''} 6... g6 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '!?' Bobby Fischer: 'against me. The game continued:' Bulletin: '!?' '!?' '!?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '?''} 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. e5 $1 {, especially bad in the case of Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!' Chris Ward: ', especially bad in the case of''} dxe5 $4 (8... Nh5 $2 {(Bobby Fischer) (correct is} 9. Qf3 $1 e6 (9... d5 10. Nxd5 $1) 10. g4 Ng7 11. Ne4 Qa5+ (11... d5 12. Nf6+ Ke7 13. Qa3+) 12. Bd2 Qxe5 13. Bc3 {Black resigns. 1-0; Fischer,R-Amateur}) (8... Ng4 $8 {(Bobby Fischer) NOT Bulletin: '' '' ''}) 9. Bxf7+ $1 {Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'winning the Queen - that was another skittles game!)' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''}) ({Of course} 6... e6 {blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now! Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now!'}) (6... Qb6 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 11. Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959'}) 7. Bb3 ({A useful retreat waiting for Black to commit himself. Although} 7. f3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} e6 {is a plausible sequence, certainly} 8. Ndb5 {is well met by} Qb8 $1 {and frequently against such set ups Fisher favoured challenging Black's structure through f4-f5.}) (7. Be3 {(Bobby Fischer) is met by} Ng4 $1 {On}) (7. Bg5 {Bobby Fischer} e6 8. Bxf6 $2 Qxf6 9. Ndb5 O-O-O 10. Nxd6+ Kb8 $19 {with a winning attack Also strong is Gligoric,S (GLIGORICH) Bulletin: '; Gligoric,S'}) (7. O-O {Bobby Fischer} g6 8. Nxc6 $1 Bxc6 (8... bxc6 9. f4) 9. Bg5 Bg7 10. Nd5 $1 $16) 7... g6 {As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack! Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack!''} 8. f3 ({Upon the immediate Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon the immediate''} 8. Be3 {Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in''} Ng4 $5 {Instead White puts paid to that option. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'Instead White puts paid to that option.''} 9. Nxc6 {Bobby Fischer} bxc6 (9... Nxe3 $2 10. Bxf7+ $1) 10. Qf3 (10. Bxa7 $2 c5) 10... Ne5 11. Qg3 $16) 8... Na5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Releasing the central tension this way is wrong. Correct is'} 8... Nxd4 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '>=' Bulletin: ' Chris Ward: '!?''} 9. Qxd4 Bg7 {is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is''} 10. Bg5 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: 'White still keeps control.' Bulletin: '' '' ''} O-O {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 11. Qd2 b5 12. Bh6 a5 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. a4 b4 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 Rc8 {with chances for both sides in Amonatov,F (2636)-Li,C  (2732) Riadh 2017.}) ({The obvious 8...Bg7 sees us most likely headed for a 9 Bc4 Yugoslav Attack but intriguing is that Gligoric (back then considered one of the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters) reached this position three years later and on that occasion preferred} 8... Rc8 {when Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) when'} 9. Nxc6 {(justified as Black usually wants to recapture with the b-pawn but this might render ...Rc8 a waste of time.)} bxc6 10. Be3 Bg7 11. Qd2 O-O 12. O-O-O Qa5 13. Bh6 c5 {(trying to put the c8-rook to good use through the threat of ...c4)} 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Qe2 {was in Ciocaltea,V-Gligoric,S Belgrade 1962 objectively a little better for White given the idea of e4-e5.}) 9. Bg5 {The downside of Black's last move is that it released the pressure on White's centrally posted steed and thus allowed White's dark-squared bishop to venture further afield. On White's part, Fischer seems determined to eek out the ...h6 that creates the odd weakness in the defensive black pawn shield. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'The downside of Black's last move is that it released the pressure on White's centrally posted steed and thus allowed White's dark-squared bishop to venture further afield. On White's part, Fischer seems determined to eek out the ...h6 that creates the odd weakness in the defensive black pawn shield.''} Bg7 10. Qd2 h6 {Essentially a fork in the road for Black. The decision made effectively takes Bh6 away from White's options whereas Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Essentially a fork in the road for Black. The decision made effectively takes Bh6 away from White's options whereas''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'A concession. But on'} 10... O-O {wouldn't have incurred the same weaknesses. However regards for example Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'wouldn't have incurred the same weaknesses. However regards for example''} 11. Bh6 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: 'followed by h4-h5 produces a strong and almost mechanical attack.' Bulletin: '' '' '' Bulletin: ' h2-h4-h5'} Bxh6 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 12. Qxh6 {I would add that although an exchange sac on c3 wouldn't be as effective with the knight on a5 rather than e5 as it obstructs the a-pawn (e.g. following 12...Rc8 13 h4 Rxc3 14 bxc3 Qc7) an alternative solution presents itself in the form of} e5 $5 13. Nde2 b5 {when Black definitely has reasonable play to compensate for his backward d6-pawn.}) 11. Be3 Rc8 {Obviously useful although a quick reminder that castling is no longer possible whilst the h6-pawn hangs. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Obviously useful although a quick reminder that castling is no longer possible whilst the h6-pawn hangs.''} 12. O-O-O Nc4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} 13. Qe2 {Given my not exactly secretive affiliation to the Dragon bishop (and hence its opposite number too), it won't come as a big surprise that I'm not a fan of this move! Though not yet of course the player he was to become, Fischer was pretty nifty at the age of 16. Apparently Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he awarded the move with a '!!' but I'm simply going to offer the respect that one of the greatest ever players is due by not awarding it with a '?!'! Bobby Fischer: '!?' Bulletin: '!?' '!?' '!?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Given my not exactly secretive affiliation to the Dragon bishop (and hence its opposite number too), it won't come as a big surprise that I'm not a fan of this move! Though not yet of course the player he was to become, Fischer was pretty nifty at the age of 16. Apparently Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he awarded the move with a '!!' but I'm simply going to offer the respect that one of the greatest ever players is due by not awarding it with a '?!'!''} ({Keeping things simple is Bobby Fischer: 'A totally new idea at the time.' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Keeping things simple is''} 13. Bxc4 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} Rxc4 {and then either Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'and then either''} 14. Kb1 {or 14 g4 with the sort of advantage that we've been used to seeing White get in the 'Anti-Soltis' variations. Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) or 14 g4 with the sort of advantage that we've been used to seeing White get in the 'Anti-Soltis' variations.'} (14. g4 {(Bobby Fischer) was the usual, and good, procedure. The text pennits Black to capture what was considered, then, to be the more important of the White Bishops.})) 13... Nxe3 ({NOT} 13... Qc7 $2 {Bobby Fischer} 14. Ndb5) 14. Qxe3 O-O ({This isn't unreasonable but given Black has delayed castling up to this point, he could hang back further with Bobby Fischer: 'Reminiscent of game 12, it is now Gligorich who castles into it! At Mar del Plata 1960, Marini played against Ine the stronger' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'This isn't unreasonable but given Black has delayed castling up to this point, he could hang back further with''} 14... Qb6 $5 {This is a very useful move when White's dark-squared bishop has been removed and specifically now ... e5 is a piece winning threat. As it happens that actually occurred the following year with Bobby Fischer: '(threatening -e5' Bulletin: ';  e7-e5 Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'This is a very useful move when White's dark-squared bishop has been removed and specifically now ... e5 is a piece winning threat. As it happens that actually occurred the following year with''} 15. Qd2 Qc5 {very sensibly seeing Black look to edge forward. However after Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'very sensibly seeing Black look to edge forward. However after''} 16. f4 {Black should have either finally castled or else continued with some queenside expansion via 16...b5 but instead lost his way through Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black should have either finally castled or else continued with some queenside expansion via 16...b5 but instead lost his way through''} h5 $6 {when Bobby Fischer: '?' Bobby Fischer: '(better is' Bulletin: '?' '?' '?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '?!' Chris Ward: 'when''} (16... b5 {(Bobby Fischer) or Bulletin: ''}) (16... O-O {(Bobby Fischer) >= Bulletin: ''}) 17. Nf3 Bh6 18. e5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with a powerful attack. Fischer, R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960'} Ng4 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} ({Note} 18... dxe5 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 19. Nxe5 Qxe5 20. fxe5 Bxd2+ 21. Rxd2 Ng4 22. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 23. Rxd7 {is simply a bad ending.}) 19. Rhe1 O-O 20. Ne4 {was all one way traffic in Fischer,R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960}) ({Kotov recommends} 14... Qa5 {Bobby Fischer Bulletin: 'Kotov,A'} 15. Kb1 {(he gives only} (15. g4 $2 Qg5 $1) (15. f4 {(after} O-O 16. h3 e6 $13 {but Black's two Bishops may offset the weakness of the d-Pawn. Bulletin: '× d6; '}) 15... Qc5 16. Qd3 a6 $11 {and Black's all right. So best is probably Bulletin: '=' '=' '='}) 15. g4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Timing is important. ON'} (15. h4 Qb6 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} (15... h5 {(Bobby Fischer) locks it up.}) 16. h5 g5 {seems to offer both sides chances although the Soltis like 15...h5!? stopping White's h-pawn in its tracks looks quite appealing too.}) 15... Qa5 {Unless Black is serious about sacing the exchange on c3, this isn't the right move here. Instead again Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Unless Black is serious about sacing the exchange on c3, this isn't the right move here. Instead again''} (15... Qb6 $5 {looks best and after Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) looks best and after'} 16. Qd2 ({Remember} 16. h4 e5 $1) 16... a5 $5 {brings the concept of ...a4 into play. It still seems fine to me.}) 16. h4 e6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 16... h5 17. g5 Ne8 18. f4 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: '-f5 gives White a strong game.' Bulletin: '' '' '' Bulletin: ' f4-f5'} e5 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 19. fxe5 Qxe5 {at first may seem promising because the Dragon bishop has some life. Unfortunately} 20. Nd5 Nc7 21. Nf6+ $1 Bxf6 22. gxf6 Qxf6 23. Rhf1 $1 Qe5 ({Or} 23... Qxh4 24. Qh6 $1 {bringing into play both Qxg6+ and Nf5 i.e. a massive attack!}) 24. Nf3 {is suddenly a mega initiative for White with the threat to d6 and} Qe7 25. Qh6 {highlighting the danger to Black.}) 17. Nde2 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds out after'} 17. g5 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} hxg5 18. hxg5 Nh5 19. f4 {is surely most direct. White aims for f4-f5 and if Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is surely most direct. White aims for f4-f5 and if''} e5 {then Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) then'} (19... Qc5 $132 {(Bobby Fischer) (threatening e5 Bulletin: ' e6-e5'}) 20. Nd5 $1 exd4 21. Ne7+ Kh7 22. Qf3 {is cute. Bad things are going to happen on the h-file!}) 17... Rc6 (17... Ne8 $1 {was the best defence both protecting d6 and anticipating g4-g5. Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) was the best defence both protecting d6 and anticipating g4-g5.'}) 18. g5 hxg5 ({On} 18... Nh5 {Bobby Fischer} 19. gxh6 Bf6 20. f4 $40 {continues the Pawn stampede.}) 19. hxg5 Nh5 20. f4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Rfc8 21. Kb1 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} ({With the idea of f4-f5 still on White's mind, this is calm and strong although actually} 21. e5 $5 {blocking out the Dragon bishop, taking advantage of the d-file pin and vacating the e4-square for the knight held a certain amount of appeal too! Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) blocking out the Dragon bishop, taking advantage of the d-file pin and vacating the e4-square for the knight held a certain amount of appeal too!'}) ({An important preparatory move. On the immediate} 21. f5 $5 {Bobby Fischer} exf5 22. Nd5 Qxa2 $1 $132 {gives Black good play.}) 21... Qb6 (21... Bxc3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 22. Nxc3 Rxc3 23. bxc3 Rxc3 24. Qd4 {doesn't remotely cut it and so instead Black tries another approach.}) 22. Qf3 {However White of course isn't interested in a queen swap. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'However White of course isn't interested in a queen swap.''} Rc5 23. Qd3 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} ({We all know it's not always easy to deliver mate when Black has a Dragon bishop and White no equivalent dark-squared bishop to challenge it. Nevertheless I was very surprised not to see the tempting} 23. Rxh5 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} gxh5 24. Qxh5 {given how strong the threat of f4-f5 is.}) ({Several Yugoslav chess journalists scurried forward toward the analysis room, where Matanovich was explaining the game on a demonstration board. Apparently the feeling was that I had just blundered. The more obvious} 23. f5 {(Bobby Fischer) looks good, but Black still has defensive resources with} exf5 24. Rxh5 ({(if} 24. Nd5 Qd8 25. exf5 Bxf5 26. Rxh5 $2 Rxc2 $1 $19 {wins for Black)}) 24... gxh5 25. Nf4 Rxc3 26. bxc3 Rxc3 27. Qxh5 Rxb3+ 28. cxb3 Qe3 $132 {, etc.}) 23... Bxc3 {Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn.''} ({Indeed Bobby Fischer: 'The threat against the d-Pawn is awkward to meet. On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Indeed''} 23... R5c6 24. f5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} exf5 25. Rxh5 {might see the neat line Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'might see the neat line''} fxe4 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} (25... gxh5 26. exf5 {is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim. Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing. Or if' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim.''}) 26. Qxe4 gxh5 27. Bxf7+ Kxf7 28. g6+ Kg8 29. Qd5+ {and essentially Black is getting mated.}) (23... R8c6 {Bobby Fischer} 24. Na4 $18 {wins the exchange. Finally on}) (23... Bf8 {Bobby Fischer} 24. f5 $1 exf5 25. Nd5 Qd8 (25... fxe4 26. Qxe4 Bf5 27. Qxf5 $18 {wins a piece)}) 26. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 (26... Rxd5 27. Bxd5 gxh5 28. exf5 $18) 27. Nf6+ Kg7 28. Qh3 $18 {releases an avalanche.}) 24. Nxc3 ({Not} 24. bxc3 {Bobby Fischer} Bb5) 24... Nxf4 {Bobby Fischer: 'This is what the crowd thought I had overlooked.'} 25. Qf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Nh5 ({Upon Bobby Fischer: 'On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon''} 25... e5 {simplest is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'simplest is''} 26. Ne2 {The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn. Bobby Fischer: '+-!' Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.' Bulletin: '+-!' '+-!' '+-!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn.''}) 26. Rxh5 $1 {Just as strong as ever here. Bobby Fischer: 'I've made this sacrifice so often, I feel like applying for a patent!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Just as strong as ever here.''} gxh5 27. Qxh5 Be8 ({There can be no running as Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'There can be no running as''} 27... Kf8 28. Qh8+ Ke7 29. Qf6+ Ke8 30. Rh1 {demonstrates. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'demonstrates.''} Bb5 {Bobby Fischer} 31. Bxe6 $1 fxe6 32. Qxe6+ Kd8 ({OR} 32... Kf8 33. Rh8+ Kg7 34. Qf6# {mate)}) 33. Rh8+ Kc7 34. Rxc8# {mate)}) 28. Qh6 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!'} ({Keeping the king trapped is logical although actually} 28. Rh1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} Kf8 29. Bxe6 $1 {would have done the business too i. e.} fxe6 ({Or} 29... Rxc3 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Qf6+ Kf8 32. Rh8#) 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Rh7+ Bf7 ({Or} 31... Kd8 32. Qf6#) 32. Qf6+ Kd7 33. Qxf7+ Kc6 34. Qd7#) 28... Rxc3 29. bxc3 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 29. Rh1 {would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through''} Qd4 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds out for a while.'}) 29... Rxc3 ({White still retains a winning attack after} 29... Qe3 {Bobby Fischer} 30. Rh1 Qxc3 31. g6 Qg7 32. Qh2 $1 $18 {Bronstein,D}) 30. g6 $1 fxg6 ({The threat was Qh7+ followed by g7+ whilst} 30... Rg3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 31. Qh7+ Kf8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qh4+ {simply picks up the rook.}) 31. Rh1 {Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7.''} Qd4 32. Qh7+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'Again a mistake!'} 32. Bxe6+ {Bobby Fischer: 'mates more quickly. Black resigns' Bulletin: ''} Bf7 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 33. Qh7+ Kf8 34. Qxf7# {equally does the job although Black resigns now anyway as Rf1+ will soon be mating.}) 1-0

[Event "Short & Sweet: The King's Indian Attack"]
[Site "Main Level"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Myagmarsuren, Lhamsuren"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C00"]
[PlyCount "61"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]
[SourceTitle "Main Level"]
[Source "Chessable.com"]

{Crushing victories like this have convinced me to start employing the King's Indian Attack myself.} 1. e4 e6 2. d3 d5 3. Nd2 Nf6 4. g3 c5 5. Bg2 Nc6 6. Ngf3 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. e5 ({Nowadays, it is considered best to start with} 8. Re1 {.}) 8... Nd7 9. Re1 b5 10. Nf1 b4 11. h4 a5 12. Bf4 a4 13. a3 $1 {It was the first game that featured this move. White wants to avoid *...a4-a3* from Black. It would let Black weaken our queenside structure because *b2-b3 *would be forced. Black can then proceed with ideas like ...Nd4 to claim more dark squares on the queenside.} bxa3 {It looks correct to open up the queenside.} 14. bxa3 Na5 15. Ne3 {Fischer decided that he can reach the g4-square through the more active e3.} Ba6 16. Bh3 $6 ({White sees that Black reduced control over the e6-square by removing his Bishop from c8 but} 16. Ng5 {followed by Qh5 was simple and strong. White's attack would be almost impossible to parry without material losses.}) 16... d4 17. Nf1 (17. Ng4 {seems more thematic.}) 17... Nb6 18. Ng5 Nd5 19. Bd2 (19. Qh5 {right away was possible and probably stronger.}) 19... Bxg5 20. Bxg5 Qd7 21. Qh5 {White slowly brings his pieces to Black's King.} Rfc8 22. Nd2 (22. Nh2 $1 {with the familiar idea of Ng4 was simple and strong.}) 22... Nc3 23. Bf6 $1 {[%csl Rg7] Setting up a deadly net which will strangle Black's King. Qg5 is an immediate threat.} Qe8 (23... gxf6 24. exf6 Kh8 25. Nf3 {25.Bf5! exf5 26.Re7! may be even stronger. It is a line that the one and only Kingscrusher suggested to me!} Nd5 26. Ne5 Nxf6 27. Qh6 Qe7 28. Bxe6 {I am sure Fischer saw that when he decided on 23. Bf6!.}) 24. Ne4 $1 {[%cal Ge4f6,Ge4g5,Ge4d6] Bringing more pieces into play. If Black takes on e4 we recapture with the Rook and can consider Rg4 ideas.} g6 ({Black's last chance was} 24... Bb7 {but after} 25. Bxg7 Kxg7 26. Qg5+ {followed by Nd6 White's attack would be very strong.}) 25. Qg5 {[%cal Bh4h5]} Nxe4 26. Rxe4 c4 27. h5 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Be4h4,Gh4h8] Very instructive. White uses his h-pawn to force more weaknesses while preparing Rh4. This is what we call a multipurpose move!} cxd3 28. Rh4 Ra7 29. Bg2 $3 {What an amazing move! White decides that he needs the light-squared Bishop to finish Black.} dxc2 30. Qh6 $1 {[%csl Rg7]} Qf8 31. Qxh7+ {[%csl Rg8][%cal Bh5g6,Bg2e4]} ({Black resigned. It is a shame because he didn't let Fischer demonstrate a beautiful idea} 31. Qxh7+ Kxh7 32. hxg6+ Kxg6 33. Be4# {.}) 1-0

