{I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example. ''} 1. e4 {Sokolov,Vladimir Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)' Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Moskalenko,Viktor: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Sokolov,Vladimir "A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)''} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: What is this? White is sacrificing a pawn? ANSWER: Well, yes and no. In fact, as we will see, White can regain the pawn by force. What he is doing is trying to bring about a more open game than usually occurs after 4 e5. He forces the exchange of bishop for knight, thereby gaining his traditional Winawer birthright of the bishop-pair and unopposed dark-square bishop, but without closing the centre. He thereby hopes that he will be able to make more effective use of the bishops. QUESTION: Does this get played much nowadays? ANSWER: No. In recent years, it has lost popularity, but in the past, it was played regularly by Alekhine, Smyslov and Fischer, so it must be respected. It is one of the sharpest replies to the Winawer, and the black player needs to be well armed against it.  Fischer had won with this move against Uhlmann two rounds earlier, so it is  probable that IM Kovacevic made special preparations for just this variation.' White spends a tempo to force the exchange x. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'White spends a tempo to force the exchange x.''} Bxc3+ ({Deviations: the retreat} 4... Ba5 {must be weaker. White has a pleasant choice between} 5. e5 ({also} 5. b4) ({or, the exchange} 5. exd5 {which is more favourable in this line.}) 5... c5 {the Armenian Variation.}) 5. bxc3 dxe4 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion.' Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Accepting the consequences is the right way. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Accepting the consequences is the right way.''} ({Deviations: after Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Deviations: after''} 5... Ne7 {Steve Giddins: ', for example, hopes to transpose into the main line, if White plays a rapid e4-e5, but he has no reason to do so. After' White can play Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'White can play''} 6. Qg4 $5 ({or, the solid Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'or, the solid''} 6. Bd3 $5 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?''} c5 7. exd5 exd5 8. dxc5 $1 $14 {Steve Giddins: ', Psakhis claims that Black is not really equalizing, and I agree. The two bishops are likely to be strong in such an open position.' Steve Giddins: ', Psakhis claims that Black is not really equalizing, and I agree. The two bishops are likely to be strong in such an open position. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' and Black faces a tough struggle for the draw, since Kuzmin-Chernin, USSR Cup 1984. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'and Black faces a tough struggle for the draw, since Kuzmin-Chernin, USSR Cup 1984.''}) ({or, even} 6. exd5 Qxd5 $6 7. Nf3 Bd7 8. c4 $16 {1-0 Miladinovic,I (2559)-Jovic,A (2386)/Kragujevac 2010 (34)}) 6... O-O 7. e5 {similar to the Warsaw Variation.} ({If here} 7. Bd3 $6 {suggested by Shirov, but} e5 $1 8. Qh4 exd4 $132 {seems good for Black.}) 7... f6 $6 ({I think the best try is} 7... f5 $142 $5 {only a few games}) (7... c5 8. Bd3 {the main Warsaw Variation.}) 8. Nf3 c5 9. exf6 Rxf6 10. Bg5 e5 (10... Qa5 11. Bxf6 Qxc3+ 12. Ke2 Ng6 13. Rc1 $1 gxf6 14. h4 $40 {[%cal Gh4h5]}) 11. Qg3 Re6 12. Nxe5 Nbc6 13. Bd3 $1 Qc7 14. O-O Nxe5 15. dxe5 c4 16. Bf6 Ng6 17. Bxg6 hxg6 18. Qxg6 Kh8 19. Rae1 gxf6 20. exf6 {1-0 Vallejo Pons,F (2697)-Dizdarevic,E (2475)/Plovdiv 2010}) 6. Qg4 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'This is the point of White's fourth move., With the aid of this typical anti-Winawer queen raid, White regains his sacrificed pawn.' Steve Giddins: 'This is the point of White's fourth move., With the aid of this typical anti-Winawer queen raid, White regains his sacrificed pawn. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' This manoeuvre is generally considered to be the most dangerous resource against the Winawer Variation. Black has to face it in many lines. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This manoeuvre is generally considered to be the most dangerous resource against the Winawer Variation. Black has to face it in many lines.''} ({Steve Giddins: 'In recent years, especially at amateur level, the move' The Winckelmann-Reimer Gambit} 6. f3 {Steve Giddins: 'has achieved a degree of popularity. White makes it a real gambit, along the lines of the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit (1 d4 d5 2 e4 dxe4 3 Nc3 Nf6 4 f3). QUESTION: What do you think of the line? ANSWER: To be brutally honest, I think it is what is sometimes euphemistically called "a consignment of geriatric shoemakers"! Unlike the Blackmar-Diemer, here White is not even threatening to capture on e4 (because of the reply ...Qh4+), so Black has a wide choice.' can be met with the straightforward} c5 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'looks the most logical,'} (6... e5 $5 {is probably a good alternative}) ({but John Watson's} 6... Nd7 {is also good. White has nothing for his pawn, in my opinion.}) 7. Be3 (7. Be2 {was played by GM Hector:} Nc6 (7... Nf6 $1 8. fxe4 Nxe4 9. Qd3 Qa5 $16) 8. fxe4 cxd4 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Nf3 dxc3 10. Bd3 e5 (10... Qb6 $5) 11. O-O Nge7 12. Qe1 O-O 13. Qxc3 Ng6 14. Bc4 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. Be3 Rc8 {and Black is still better, in ½-½ Hector,J (2500)-Brinck Claussen,B (2380)/Copenhagen 1997/ (34)}) 7... Qa5 $1 8. dxc5 ({In the game Simon-De Francesco, Balatonbereny 1995, White tried:} 8. fxe4 Qxc3+ 9. Bd2 Qxd4 10. Nf3 Qxe4+ $17 {, but with the three extra pawns you never lose!}) 8... Nc6 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Kf2 Nf6 10. Qd6 {As in Velicka-Babula, CZE-chT 1995. And here, instead of 10...exf3, I would recommend:} Nd5 $1 {playing for the initiative} (10... Ne7 $5) 11. Re1 Nxe3 12. Rxe3 e5 $1 $17 {Black intends simply ...e6 and ...d8. For instance,} 13. Bc4 Be6 $1 14. Bxe6 Rd8 $1 {winning the queen.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' The best defence versus g4 in all open lines.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'The best defence versus g4 in all open lines. ''} 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 {Steve Giddins: 'This is the tabiya for the variation. QUESTION: So what is going on? ANSWER: White has regained material equality, and has his two bishops, with the dark-squared bishop potentially being quite strong. However, he has poor development, and it is also not entirely clear where his king will go. It is unlikely to be safe on the queenside, whilst kingside castling takes a little time, in view of the need to keep the g2-pawn defended. Black has three main moves here, all of which are equally respectable. ' A key position. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'A key position.''} Nbd7 $5 {Steve Giddins: 'This is probably the most solid option. Black plans to develop with ...b6, ...Bb7, ...Qe7 and ...0-0-0.' Steve Giddins: 'This is probably the most solid option. Black plans to develop with ...b6, ...Bb7, ...Qe7 and ...0-0-0. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?'' This has always been considered a good reply.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This has always been considered a good reply. ''} (8... Rg6 {immediately forces the white queen to determine her position, and prevents the pin on the knight after Bg5. However, in view of the fact that the queen often finds herself somewhat stranded on h6, it is arguably not so logical to force her back to a central post so early.} 9. Qe3 (9. Qd2 {is a major alternative: for example,} b6 10. Bb2 Bb7 11. O-O-O Qe7 12. c4 Nbd7 13. Ne2 O-O-O {, J.Dukhin-M.Ustinov, Rybinsk 2001, as quoted by Watson; Black is fully developed and stands well}) 9... Nc6 10. Bb2 Qd6 $6 (10... b6) 11. f3 exf3 12. Nxf3 {was another Fischer game, played just two rounds before the main game, in the same tournament. It is worth seeing, as it shows how dangerous White's dark-squared play can be in this line, if he gets going:} Bd7 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. c4 Ng4 $6 15. Qd2 f5 $2 {. This weakens the dark squares even further. R.Fischer-W.Uhlmann, Zagreb 1970, concluded:} 16. d5 $1 Nb8 17. h3 Nf6 18. Ne5 Ne4 19. Qd4 Rg3 20. Nf7 Qf4+ 21. Kb1 c5 22. Qe5 Qxe5 23. Bxe5 Rdg8 24. Bd3 {(Fischer begrudges giving his wonderful dark-squared bishop for the rook!)} Rxg2 25. Bxe4 fxe4 26. Nd6+ Kc7 27. Nxe4+ Kb6 28. Nf6 Ba4 29. Nxg8 Bxc2+ 30. Kc1 Nd7 31. Rdg1 {1-0. A powerful display by Fischer, and an example of what Black must be wary of in this variation.}) ({Instead,} 8... c5 9. Ne2 Bd7 (9... Nbd7 {transposes to the note to Black's 9th, below}) {is an interesting line, recommended by John Watson in his latest French volume. Funnily enough, I saw it recommended by Andrew Martin in a small pamphlet on the Winawer some 25 years ago (!), and once used it to good effect myself:} 10. Bg5 Rg6 11. Qh4 Bc6 12. Nf4 $2 ({naive, to say the least; critical is} 12. d5 $1 exd5 13. Nf4 Rxg5 (13... Qa5 $2 14. Bxf6 {defends c3, which is the point of White's 12th}) 14. Qxg5 Nbd7 15. Be2 Qa5 {when Black has a pawn for the exchange, and a powerful pawn centre, with chances about equal}) 12... Qa5 $1 {(refuting White's last; the game continued in amusing fashion)} 13. O-O-O $2 Qxa3+ 14. Kb1 Nd5 $1 {(the move White had missed)} 15. Nxd5 Bxd5 16. c4 e5 $1 {and White did not last very long in J.Hickman-S.Giddins, Bradford 1988.}) 9. Ne2 {The most popular (natural) move for White and at least it is not worse than the alternatives. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'The most popular (natural) move for White and at least it is not worse than the alternatives.''} ({A) The Serbian WEAPON:} 9. a4 $5 {is a modern and dynamic idea, played in many lines (with 4.a3) by Ivanisevic and Miladinovic. White opens up the game to the maximum, harassing the black king which has castled queenside. The queen's bishop may be activated along the diagonal a3-f8:} c5 (9... Nb6 $5 {can be an antidote for Black} 10. a5 Nbd5 11. Ne2 Qd6 $5 $132 {preparing ...d7 and 0-0-0}) ({Or} 9... b6 10. a5 Bb7 $13 {no games with this line}) 10. a5 Qc7 11. Qd2 {It looks as if White is wasting lots of tempi, but the situation is highly unclear. The problem is the unsafe position of the black king.} b6 $5 (11... Rb8 12. Ne2 $14 {Ivanisevic-Psakhis, playchess.com 2006}) 12. Ne2 (12. axb6 Qxb6 13. Ne2 Ba6 $132 {} 14. g3 Bb7 $1) 12... Bb7 13. Qf4 {Miladinovic-Franic, Zadar 2007. Here after:} Qc8 $1 {Black's position is strong enough}) ({Steve Giddins: 'Ljubojevic once surprised Korchnoi with' B) Ljubojevic's WEAPON: Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'B) Ljubojevic's WEAPON:''} 9. Nh3 $5 {Steve Giddins: 'and scored a striking victory. QUESTION: What is the point? ANSWER: The move has a couple of points. Firstly, it defends g5, so the bishop will be defended additionally if it comes to that square. Secondly, f2 is defended, which may be important if Black later plays ...Ng4 (a recurring theme in this line, as we will see). Korchnoi's rather bad defeat resulted in the move becoming quite popular for a while, but it seems that objectively, Black is fine:' Steve Giddins: 'and scored a striking victory. QUESTION: What is the point? ANSWER: The move has a couple of points. Firstly, it defends g5, so the bishop will be defended additionally if it comes to that square. Secondly, f2 is defended, which may be important if Black later plays ...Ng4 (a recurring theme in this line, as we will see). Korchnoi's rather bad defeat resulted in the move becoming quite popular for a while, but it seems that objectively, Black is fine: Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?'' was a success for White in its time. White prepares g5 or g5 while leaving the bishop on f1, with free development. However, after Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'was a success for White in its time. White prepares g5 or g5 while leaving the bishop on f1, with free development. However, after''} b6 $1 {Steve Giddins: '(sticking to the standard plan)' Steve Giddins: '(sticking to the standard plan) Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Black can exploit the line 8...b6, see also next game. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Black can exploit the line 8...b6, see also next game.''} ({Steve Giddins: 'Korchnoi chose' As usual in this variation Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'As usual in this variation''} 9... c5 $143 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: ''' is very unclear: Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'is very unclear:''} 10. Be2 Qa5 11. Bd2 {Steve Giddins: '!' '!'} Rxg2 $6 {Steve Giddins: '?' '?' Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '?!''} ({Ftacnik's} 11... cxd4 12. cxd4 Qf5 {is probably still fine for Black, who follows up with ...b6 and ...Bb7}) {Steve Giddins: ', but after'} 12. Ng5 {Steve Giddins: '!' '!' Steve Giddins: '(revealing another point of 9 Nh3 – sometimes, the knight can jump to this square, rather than the bishop; the rook on g2 is now in trouble)'} cxd4 13. Qg7 {Steve Giddins: '!' '!'} Rxg5 $8 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '''} 14. Qxg5 Qxg5 15. Bxg5 {Steve Giddins: 'he was just worse and went on to lose in L.Ljubojevic-V.Korchnoi, Tilburg 1986.'} dxc3 16. O-O-O $14 {and White's chances are better in the resulting ending, Ljubojevic-Kortschnoj, Tilburg 1986.}) (9... Z0) 10. Ng5 {Steve Giddins: 'is more active: for example,' This possibility is one of the differences in comparison with e2, but it doesn't matter! Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'This possibility is one of the differences in comparison with e2, but it doesn't matter!''} (10. Be2 Bb7 11. O-O Qe7 {led to an impressive black win in G.Lane-J.Tisdall, Gausdal 1987:} 12. a4 O-O-O 13. Ba3 c5 14. Qh4 Qd6 15. Rfd1 e3 16. f3 Rxg2+ $1 17. Kxg2 Rg8+ 18. Ng5 (18. Kh1 Ng4 {wins}) 18... h6 19. Rg1 $2 (19. dxc5 {was essential, when} hxg5 20. cxd6 gxh4+ 21. Kf1 Ne5 {is unclear, according to the computer}) 19... hxg5 20. Qg3 Qd5 21. Kf1 Nh5 22. Qg2 Nf4 23. Qg3 Nxe2 24. Kxe2 Qc4+ {0-1.}) 10... Rg6 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!''} 11. Qh4 Bb7 12. Nxh7 Nxh7 13. Qxh7 Qf6 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'with an unclear position; White has won a pawn, but still lags in development, and will have to lose further time with his queen' Steve Giddins: 'with an unclear position; White has won a pawn, but still lags in development, and will have to lose further time with his queen Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' and Black has obtained fantastic compensation for a pawn – apart from a certain psychological advantage! Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'and Black has obtained fantastic compensation for a pawn – apart from a certain psychological advantage!''} 14. Qh3 ({After} 14. h4 O-O-O $1 15. Bg5 Rh8 $1 {Black is OK}) 14... O-O-O 15. Qe3 Qe7 16. Bb2 $6 f5 17. O-O-O Nf6 $132 {With an excellent positional game for Black:} 18. c4 Rf8 (18... Ng4 $5) 19. f3 f4 $1 $36 {in Rombaldoni-Rahal, Verona 2007}) ({C) An amateur WEAPON:} 9. f3 {Black is solid after} b6 $5 (9... c5 $13)) 9... b6 $1 {Steve Giddins: ''!' Recommended by Vukovic in "Sahovski Glasnik" in 1935.' Steve Giddins: ''!' Recommended by Vukovic in "Sahovski Glasnik" in 1935. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Originally, recommended by Vukovic in Sahovski Glasnik in 1935. The idea of the fianchetto with ...b7 is a key concept for Black! (see also line-C 8...b6) Ste