{[%evp 0,57,19,11,11,11,5,6,6,19,19,-5,-14,-14,-10,-46,12,-80,-54,-54,-51,-41,-20,-31,2,10,10,-47,-11,-61,-11,18,16,-15,16,26,28,0,3,-4,1,-8,30,-30,10,-33,-15,8,54,33,33,-10,-10,-54,226,214,334,334,836,989] Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 45 Ghosts. Steinitz, nicknamed "the Austrian Morphy" (although two styles could hardly be more dissimilar), apparently exercises a great influence on Fischer, who has restored several of his pet lines to prominence. One of these is the bizarre 9.Nh3!?, found wanting at the turn of the century, and perhaps best left there. Bisguier appears unimpressed, regaining his gambit Pawn with a strong initiative. But he misses several opportunities to gain an advantage and is gradually outplayed. Just at the critical moment, when the chances are roughly equal, he commits the same kind of gross oversight that had doomed Fischer against Spassky (game 18). He suffers the same fate. '} 1. e4 {Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: ', Best by test. '} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 {Bobby Fischer: ', The last time I played this move in a tournament was when I was 12, at the 1955 US Junior Championship. '} Nf6 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '; Steinitz considered this to be an unsound sacrificial continuation! '} 4. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Tarrasch branded this a "duffer's move" and Panov called it "primitive." But there is no other way for White to try for an advantage.'} (4. d3 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'is tame.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'And after'} 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Qe7 $1 $17 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'White has no compensation for the Pawn.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally,'} 4. d4 {Lange,M Bobby Fischer: 'leads to the Max Lange attack.'}) 4... d5 5. exd5 Na5 (5... Nd4 $5 {Fritz,A}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'and'} 5... b5 $5 {Ulvestad,O Bobby Fischer: 'Ulvestad, are both interesting but unsound.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 5... Nxd5 6. d4 $1 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'is so strong that 5...Nxd5 is practically extinct.'} (6. Nxf7 $5 {Bobby Fischer: 'is the "Fried Liver Attack".'})) 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 h6 9. Nh3 $5 {Steinitz,W Bobby Fischer: ', To my knowledge, this is the first time that this move had been employed in Grandmaster chess for over seventy years. It is one of Steinitz's many unique opening contributions. The famous cable match game in 1891 between Steinitz and Tchigorin, which ended in a victory for Black, apparently caused the chess world to shy away from this variation. '} Bc5 (9... Bd6 {Steinitz,W Bobby Fischer: '(STEINITZ) might be worth investigating. If'} 10. d4 {Chigorin,M Bobby Fischer: '(TCHIGORIN) then'} e4 {Fischer,R Bobby Fischer: '(FISCHER).'}) (9... Bf5 {Bobby Fischer: 'is too crude:'} 10. O-O Qd7 11. Re1 Bxh3 12. gxh3 Qxh3 13. Bf1 {Bobby Fischer: 'and Black is busted, e.g.,'} Qg4+ $2 14. Qxg4 Nxg4 15. h3 $18 {Steinitz,W Bobby Fischer: 'wins a piece (STEINITZ).'}) (9... g5 10. d3 g4 11. Ng1 Bc5 12. Nc3 {Bobby Fischer: 'effectively wards off the threats ('} Qb6 {Bobby Fischer: 'is answered by'} 13. Na4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ').'}) 10. O-O {Bobby Fischer: ', Played by Steinitz in the 6th game of his second match with Tchigorin in 1892.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is'} 10. d3 $1 {} O-O 11. Nc3 Re8 12. O-O Bxh3 13. gxh3 Qd7 14. Bg4 Nxg4 15. hxg4 {Fischer,R-Radoicic,M Poughkeepsie 1963 Bobby Fischer: 'etc., as I played vs. Radoicich here in a later round.'}) 10... O-O ({Bobby Fischer: 'Dr. Gottschall, in the 1892 Deutsche Schachzeitung, suggests'} 10... g5 {Bobby Fischer: 'remarking it strange that a player as aggressive as Tchigorin did not chance it. Gottschall gives'} 11. Kh1 g4 12. Ng1 Ne4 13. Bxg4 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'let us suppose White tries to avert material loss with'} 13. b4 Nxf2+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'or Gottschall's'} 13... Bxf2 14. d3 Qh4 15. dxe4 Bg3 16. Nh3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 16. h3 gxh3 17. gxh3 Rg8) 16... Bxh2 $1 17. Kxh2 g3+ 18. Kg1 Bxh3 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 14. Rxf2 Bxf2 15. bxa5 Qh4 $1 16. Qf1 Bg3 17. h3 Rg8 $19 { Bobby Fischer: 'with a crushing attack.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'of no avail is'} 13. Qe1 Qd4 14. Bd1 Nxf2+ 15. Rxf2 Qxf2 16. Qxe5+ $2 Be6) 13... Nxf2+ 14. Rxf2 Bxf2 $14 {Gottschall,H Bobby Fischer: 'and, although Black has won the exchange, prefers White's practical chances.'}) 11. d3 Bxh3 {} ({Bobby Fischer: 'This certainly seems an improvement over the aforementionec Steinitz-Tchigorin match game which continued:'} 11... Nd5 12. c4 Ne7 13. Kh1 Bxh3 14. gxh3 Nf5 15. f4 exf4 16. Bxf4 Ne3 17. Bxe3 Bxe3 18. Nc3 $18 {Steinitz,W-Chigorin,M (6) Habana WCH 1892 Bobby Fischer: 'and White won easily with his Q-side majority.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Gottschall's'} 11... Nh7 {Gottschall,H Bobby Fischer: 'also merits attention.'}) 12. gxh3 Qd7 13. Bf3 {Bobby Fischer: ', A difficult choice.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 13. Bg4 Nxg4 {Bobby Fischer: 'followed by ...f5 gives fair attacking chances.'} 14. hxg4 f5 $40) ({Bobby Fischer: 'I rejected'} 13. Kg2 {Bobby Fischer: 'since this was the square I had reserved for my Bishop.'}) 13... Qxh3 {Bobby Fischer: '; So Black regains the Pawn, but I have faith in my two Bishops. '} 14. Nd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'It would be a mistake to play for the win of a Pawn by'} 14. Bg2 Qh4 $1 15. Qe1 Rfe8 $1 16. Qxa5 Ng4 17. h3 Bxf2+ 18. Rxf2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 18. Kh1 Qg3) 18... Qxf2+ 19. Kh1 e4 $1 20. hxg4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 20. dxe4 Rxe4) 20... exd3 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a winning attack.'}) 14... Rad8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 14... e4 $2 15. Nxe4 Nxe4 16. Bxe4 Bd6 17. f4 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 15. Bg2 Qf5 {Bobby Fischer: '; The Queen is forced off the h-file.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 15... Qh4 $2 16. Nf3 Qh5 17. Qe1 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins a Pawn.'}) 16. Qe1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps better is'} 16. Qf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'with possibilities of a slightly favorable ending.'}) 16... Rfe8 17. Ne4 Bb6 18. Nxf6+ {Bobby Fischer: ', I was worried about the maneuver ...Nd5-f4.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'But sharper is'} 18. b4 Nb7 19. b5) 18... Qxf6 19. Kh1 c5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Stronger is'} 19... g5 {;  a5-...-h4  Bobby Fischer: 'preventing White's break on f4 once and for all. Then by bringing his Knight to h4(!) Black could get a good game.'}) 20. Qc3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Serves the double purpose of preventing ...c4 and of enforcing f4. For all the good it does, Black's Bishop on b6 might just as well be a Pawn for the rest of the game. '} (20. Qe2 {Gegen c4 gerichtet. c3 verhindert c6-d4, e3, g1, e4 g-Linie} c4 21. dxc4 e4 $1 22. Rb1 (22. Bxe4 $4 Qe5 23. f3 f5 $19) 22... Bc7 $36 {Agur}) (20. Bd2 Nc6 21. Qe4 Nd4 22. c3 Nf5 23. Qc4 Nd6 (23... Nh4 24. Be4 g5 $5 $14) 24. Qg4 c4 $1 (24... e4 25. dxe4 Nxe4 26. Be3) 25. dxc4 e4 26. Be3 Nxc4 27. Bxb6 (27. Rab1 Bxe3 28. fxe3 Qg5 $1 $36) 27... axb6 $36) 20... Nc6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Too late now is'} 20... g5 $2 21. f4 $1) 21. f4 Nd4 22. Qc4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', To prepare c3, driving the Knight away from d4.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I didn't like the looks of'} 22. fxe5 Qxe5 23. Bf4 Qe2 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 22... Qg6 { h5; f5 Bobby Fischer: '; Intending ...Qh5 followed by ...Nf5.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 22... Qe6 23. Qa4 Qd7 $2 24. Qxd7 Rxd7 25. c3 Nc2 26. Bc6 $1) 23. c3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After the game a kibitzer suggested'} 23. Be4 Qh5 24. f5 {Bobby Fischer: 'but this allows Black to turn the tables by'} Qe2 25. Rg1 Nf3 $1) 23... Nf5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 23... Ne2 24. f5 Qf6 (24... Qh5 $2 25. Bf3 $1) 25. Be3 Nf4 26. Be4 {Bobby Fischer: 'is tremendous.'}) 24. fxe5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 24. Be4 Qh5 $13 {Bobby Fischer: 'just who has got the attack is not quite clear!'}) 24... Rxe5 25. Bf4 (25. Qf4 {Bobby Fischer: 'turns out badly after'} Bc7 $1 26. Be4 Qh5 $1) 25... Re2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Black is playing for an advantage.'} (25... Ne3 26. Bxe3 Rxe3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'is absolutely equal.'}) 26. Be4 {Bobby Fischer: ', The critical position. '} Rxb2 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; A pity that just when the game was getting interesting, Black has to make this terrible mistake.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 26... Re8 $1 {;  8xe4 Bobby Fischer: '(threatening ...R8xe4). Bad would be'} 27. Rg1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After 26 ...Re8! therefore, the best White has is'} 27. Bf3 { Bobby Fischer: '(to prevent ...Qh5)'} Rxb2 28. Rae1 $10 { Bobby Fischer: 'with even chances, owing to the Bishop pair.'}) 27... Qh5 28. Raf1 Ne3 $1 29. Qb5 Rxe4 30. dxe4 Nxf1 31. Qe8+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 31. Rxf1 Qg4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 31... Kh7 32. Rxg7+ Kxg7 33. Be5+ Qxe5 34. Qxe5+ f6 35. Qe7+ Kg6 36. Qe8+ Kg5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'escaping the perpetual and should win.'}) 27. Be5 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: ', Bisguier slumped and his chest collapsed, as he saw that Black cannot avert the loss of a piece. '} Re8 28. Rxf5 Rxe5 29. Rxe5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns.'} 1-0

