{Grigoriants,Sergey: 'I have chosen this Fischer game because it is not as well-known compared to his others. It was played in 1970 when he was already close to becoming the world champion. He put on a great performance in this game and his opening preparation was superb. '} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Fischer once said that he doesn't like to play against the French Winaver. Weird pawn structures are typical for this opening. Still in this game the great Bobby showed one of model plans for White, and many hungreds games were won by White in such a style in the future. '} 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Black gave up the very important dark-squared bishop in order to destroy White's queenside pawn structure. This is a very risky strategy and probably the reason the whole system is not very populiar at the top level nowadays.'} Qa5 $2 (6... Ne7 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'is the most popular option.'}) (6... Qc7) 7. Bd2 Qa4 (7... Ne7 8. Qg4 $16 {(eco 74)}) 8. Qg4 ({The alternatives are} 8. Qb1 {A slightly strange move that provokes Black to play} c4 {releasing the pressure on the d4-pawn}) ({or the direct} 8. h4) 8... Kf8 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Black is trying to avoid weakening the kingside dark squares.'} (8... Ne7 $5 9. Qxg7 Rg8 10. Qxh7 cxd4 11. Nf3 Nbc6 $13 {Mayer-Solomon USA 1976}) (8... g6 {is also quite playable} 9. Nf3) 9. Qd1 $1 (9. Bd3 $2 c4 10. Be2 Qxc2 $19) ({Strong modern engines are quite happy with} 9. h4 $5 Nc6 10. h5 Nxe5 11. Qg3) 9... b6 10. h4 $1 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'A logical and straightforward approach.'} ({A completely different picture of battle arises after} 10. dxc5 $5 bxc5 11. c4 Ba6 12. cxd5 Bxf1 13. Kxf1 exd5 14. f4) ({or} 10. c4 Ne7) 10... Ne7 (10... cxd4 11. cxd4 Qxd4 12. Nf3 {(Levy)}) ({A computer recommendation which looks scary from a human point of view is} 10... h5 11. dxc5 bxc5 12. c4 Ne7) (10... Ba6 $5) 11. h5 {[ h6] [ h6] [ h6]} h6 12. Rh4 $1 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'This idea has become standard practice. White has found a weak point in Black's position.'} Ba6 13. Bxa6 ({Also possible is} 13. Nf3 $5 Bxf1 14. Kxf1 cxd4 15. Nxd4 Kg8 16. Kg1 Nbc6 17. Nf5 $5 {with a complicated position.}) 13... Nxa6 {Grigoriants,Sergey: '?!' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'It looks logical not lose a tempo and develop the knight, but actually this is not the best decition. This knight could be more useful on d8 where it defends the f7-pawn. And the other knight might be well placed on e7, where it could go to f5 at a suitable time.'} (13... Qxa6 $1 14. Rf4 Ke8 ({Also playable is} 14... Nbc6 15. Qf3 Nd8 16. Ne2 Rc8 {but here White has a very interesting pawn break:} 17. c4 $5 Qxc4 18. dxc5 Qxc5 19. Bb4 Qc7 20. Bd6 Qd7 21. c4 $1 Kg8 (21... Rxc4 22. Rxc4 dxc4 23. Rc1 $16) 22. Rd1 $5 Rxc4 23. Rxc4 dxc4 24. Nc3 $44 {with good compensation for the material.}) 15. Qf3 Rf8 16. Rg4 Rg8 {and Black's position on the kingside is surprisingly hard to break, while there is counterplay on the queenside.}) 14. Rf4 $1 {'' (eco 74/81, bco 82) ''} Qd7 {Grigoriants,Sergey: '?' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'The critical moment of the game. After the text move White's initiative becomes very strong and Fischer conducts it with great power.'} ({The only way to keep the balance was a very computerish move} 14... g5 $3 15. hxg6 Nxg6 16. Rf3 Nb8 $5 $132 {Hard to believe, but according to engines Black's chances are not worse here! This line perfectly illustrates the complicated character of the French Winawer.}) (14... Ke8 15. dxc5 $1 $16 {People quite often forget about such 'antipositional' moves, but a pawn is a pawn after all.}) 15. Qf3 Nc6 16. Nh3 $1 {[ g4-g5] [ g4-g5] [ g4-g5] Grigoriants,Sergey: 'From this moment Fischer shows his best and even the stubborn Stockfish takes a while before evaluating the position as better for White.'} Rc8 17. g4 Qe8 {[ e7] [ Ke7] [ e7]} (17... Qe7 $2 18. g5 $1 hxg5 19. Nxg5 Qxg5 20. Rxf7+ { g5  Bg5  g5}) ({A better try probably would be} 17... Nd8 18. g5 hxg5 19. Nxg5 cxd4 20. cxd4 Rxc2 21. Qg4 $5 {with initiative for White.}) 18. g5 Ne7 (18... Ke7 19. gxh6 gxh6 20. Ng1 $5 $16) 19. gxh6 gxh6 ({Grigoriants,Sergey: 'After'} 19... Rxh6 {Grigoriants,Sergey: 'White is pushing in style:'} 20. Rh4 (20. Rf6 $5 $16)) 20. Rf6 Nf5 (20... Ng8 21. O-O-O $5 Ke7 22. Rf4 cxd4 23. Rg1 $18) 21. Nf4 {[ 22.e6, 22.g6] [ 22.Ne6, 22.Ng6] [ 22.e6, 22.g6] Grigoriants,Sergey: '+-' Grigoriants,Sergey: 'Now the black position collapses completely.'} Ke7 22. Nxd5+ Kd8 23. Ne3 $1 Nxe3 24. Bxe3 Rc7 25. dxc5 $1 Nxc5 ({The endgame after} 25... Qc6 26. O-O-O+ Kc8 27. Qxc6 Rxc6 28. Rd6 $18 {would be hopeless.}) 26. Rd1+ Ke7 (26... Kc8 27. Qa8# {#}) 27. Bxc5+ bxc5 28. Rxe6+ {'!' Fischer's Chess Games p312#589 (Wade/O'Connell) I10/252 (Trifunovic) Moles p244 (Moles) '!'} (28. Rxe6+ Kxe6 29. Qf6# {#}) 1-0

