{Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Matanovic,A: 'Chess Informant''} 1. e4 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Matanovic,A'} c5 {Le soviétique avait ajourné le matin contre Larsen pendant 03h30 après s'être couché à 04 h du matin pour analyser. Après une courte pause de 30 mns, il devait affronté le n1 mondial!} 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 {Neil McDonald: '!?' '!?' Neil McDonald: 'They always tell you in books not to grab poisoned pawns, but Fischer, Geller, Kasparov, Anand and Topalov are just some of the idiots who don't heed this advice! '} 8. Qd2 Qxb2 {Neil McDonald: 'Black is obviously taking risks, but removing the b2-pawn prevents White from achieving a smooth attacking development with 9 0-0-0. It is therefore the best way for Geller to disrupt the logical flow of the game. '} 9. Rb1 Qa3 10. f5 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?!'} (10. e5 {A l'époque, c'était la ligne principale mais qui a été remplacée par f5 quelques années plus tard.}) 10... Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 (13. Be2) 13... Nd5 {Neil McDonald: '?!' '?!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Neil McDonald: 'This is consistent with the Ukrainian Grandmaster's plan to choose the most risky moves;'} ({This is consistent with the Ukrainian Grandmaster's plan to choose the most risky moves, but} 13... dxe5 {was objectively safer and better, as later played by Kasparov and Anand. Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?!'} 14. Bxf6 $16) 14. Nxd5 cxd5 15. Be2 {Neil McDonald: '?!' '?!'} ({An in-depth investigation of the Poisoned Pawn is beyond the scope of this book. Suffice to say that} 15. Bd3 $5 dxe5 16. O-O {is the modern approach to the position, which was used by the young Magnus Carlsen to score a crushing victory in 2004.}) 15... dxe5 16. O-O Bc5+ {Neil McDonald: '?' '?' Neil McDonald: 'It is ironic that Geller played this wild opening line to upset Fischer, but he himself is the first to lose his nerve. He has made a lot of 'crazy' moves and feels that it is time for a couple of 'sane' developing moves: namely bringing out the bishop with check, and then putting the rook on f8. But normal moves don't work in crazy positions.'} ({And so he should play the 'odd' but strong} 16... Ra7 $1 {, which keeps the c5-square free for the black queen to return to the defence. The tempo she might have gained by checking the white king from c5 is the difference between life and death in such situations.}) 17. Kh1 Rf8 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!'} 18. c4 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'Not only attacking d5, but also opening up the c2-square to the white queen. '} Rxf1+ 19. Rxf1 (19. Bxf1 $6 h6 20. Bh4 Qe3) 19... Bb7 20. Bg4 {Neil McDonald: '?' '?' Neil McDonald: 'Despite thinking for more than twenty minutes, Fischer misses the win and as a consequence loses the game. Not that the winning line was at all easy to find – after the game there was an hour long post mortem in the tournament hall involving Geller, Fischer and other intrigued competitors without the decisive continuation being discovered. The American genius had exclaimed angrily that "it was a crushing position!" and he was right. But whereas Fischer would win almost every time with a tiny advantage and a clear plan to improve his position, here he falters as he has a huge advantage but no strategic signposts to guide him. And it was a lack of inspiration in such situations that was Fischer's Achilles' heel. It should be added that only a handful of players were equipped with the necessary qualities to take advantage of it, and none of them managed to successfully compete with Fischer during his peak years. They were almost always defeated by Fischer's tactical and strategical mastery without ever being able to confuse the position.' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?' Neil McDonald: 'Despite thinking for more than twenty minutes, Fischer misses the win and as a consequence loses the game. Not that the winning line was at all easy to find - after the game there was an hour long post mortem in the tournament hall involving Geller, Fischer and other intrigued competitors without the decisive continuation being discovered. --- The American genius had exclaimed angrily that "it was a crushing position!" and he was right. But whereas Fischer would win almost every time with a tiny advantage and a clear plan to improve his position, here he falters as he has a huge advantage but no strategic signposts to guide him. And it was a lack of inspiration in such situations that was Fischer's Achilles' heel. It should be added that only a handful of players were equipped with the necessary qualities to take advantage of it, and none of them managed to successfully compete with Fischer during his peak years. They were almost always defeated by Fischer's tactical and strategical mastery without ever being able to confuse the position.'} ({Neil McDonald: 'The correct move was'} 20. Qc2 $142 {Fischer Fischer Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: ', as played by Tal a couple of months after the present game. Upon' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: 'Ü 4/581 Matanovic,A: 'Ue 4/581''} e4 {1-0 Tal,M-Bogdanovic,R YUG-URS Budva 1967 (6) Neil McDonald: ', to meet the threat of invasion by the white queen on h7, the move'} (20... Be7 $142 21. Bxe7 Kxe7 22. Qxh7 Rb8 23. Qxg7+ Kd6 $13 {Geller}) 21. Bg4 $1 {is suddenly decisive. The difference with 20 Bg4? one move earlier is that Black can't use his queen in the centre to hinder the white attack as ...Qd3 would allow White the killer check Qa4+!. Fischer missed this subtlety and so ends up losing in 25 moves with White. Neil McDonald: 'is suddenly decisive. The difference with 20 Bg4? one move earlier is that Black can't use his queen in the centre to hinder the white attack as ...Qd3 would allow White the killer check Qa4+!. --- Fischer missed this subtlety and so ends up losing in 25 moves with White.'}) (20. Bd1 $142 $5 {Averbakh}) 20... dxc4 21. Bxe6 {Neil McDonald: 'At first glance it looks as if White's attack is about to succeed, as mate on d7 is threatened. '} Qd3 $17 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'However, Geller is able to block the d-file and threaten a mate of his own on f1. As we discussed above, this counterattack by the black queen would have been impossible if White had taken the chance to play 21 Qc2. '} 22. Qe1 (22. Bf7+ Kd7 23. Bxc4 Qxd2 $17) 22... Be4 {Neil McDonald: '!!' '!!' Neil McDonald: 'A tremendous move. The bishop shuts out the white queen's attack on e5 and clears the way for 23...Rb8, followed either by 24...Rb1 with a winning simplification, or 24...Rb2 with a lethal counterattack against g2. ' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!!' Neil McDonald: 'A tremendous move. The bishop shuts out the white queen's attack on e5 and clears the way for 23...Rb8, followed either by 24... Rb1 with a winning simplification, or 24...Rb2 with a lethal counterattack against g2.'} 23. Bg4 (23. Bf5 Bxf5 24. Qxe5+ Kd7 $19) 23... Rb8 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '-+'} 24. Bd1 {Neil McDonald: 'White's last hope is a bishop check on a4. Geller scotches this with a fine king manoeuvre. '} Kd7 {Neil McDonald: '!' '!' Neil McDonald: 'It turns out that the safest place for the black king is on the third rank. '} 25. Rf7+ {Neil McDonald: 'After'} Ke6 26. Rf1 (26. Rxg7 Bxg2+ 27. Kxg2 Rb2+ 28. Kh1 Qd5+) 26... Rb2 27. Bg4+ Kd5 {the black king is delighted on d5, and the white king is in despair on h1. 0-1 (27) Fischer,R-Geller,E Monte Carlo 1967 EXT 2008 [ChessBase II]} 0-1

