{FROM THE CHESS INFORMANT ARCHIVES Borislav Ivkov 1933-2022 On 14th February of this year Borislav Ivkov – the former World Championship Candidate and a giant of Yugoslav and Serbian chess – passed away in his native city of Beograd at the age of 88. There follows a short tribute to the outstanding grandmaster. Borislav Ivkov was born in Beograd on 12th November 1933. Before he was 12 years old, Ivkov gained an exceptional result for "Pancevo Dinamo" at the Team Championship of the Vojvodina, and it was clear that a very bright future lay ahead of him. By 1947 he was the youth champion of his native city, and then he won the first-ever junior championship of the Federal Republic of Yugoslavia. He gained the Master title at the age of sixteen by finishing in 4th-7th place in the Yugoslav Championship in Zagreb. On his international debut in the tournament at Bled the following year, he took 5th-6th place, with wins against the likes of Milan Vidmar, Vasja Pirc and Herman Pilnik. In 1951 he came to the attention of the chess world with his victory in the inaugural FIDE World Junior (Under-20) Championship, which was held in Birmingham in England. Regarding this success, Ivkov later said: "I don't remember whether it was Botvinnik or Petrosian who once told me that the most important thing is to be a world champion, because later you remain a former world champion. The title is current, but the "former" remains forever. And as if they predicted that it would be my most significant personal success in my career." Ivkov would go on to have many more successes in a career that lasted into the second decade of the 21st century. Among them one may mention his three Yugoslav championship titles (Sarajevo 1958; Zenica 1963 & 1972), his six silver and three bronze Olympiad medals as part of the Yugoslav national team, and his numerous victories in international tournaments such as Mar del Plata 1955, Buenos Aires 1955 (these victories gaining him the title of Grandmaster), Beverwijk 1961, Zagreb 1965, Sarajevo 1967, Malaga 1968 & 1969, Beograd 1969 and Stockholm 1971. Looking at his tournament record, one is struck by his remarkable consistency and the near absence of tournament "failures". He won individual tournament games against World Champions Smyslov, Tal, Petrosian, Fischer and Karpov, many of them in very fine style, and his status as a player of the highest class was confirmed by his inclusion in the "Rest of the World" team that faced the USSR. in the "Match of the Century" in Beograd in 1970. As late as 2006 Ivkov was part of the Serbian team that won the gold medal in the European Seniors team championship in Davos. He was a long-time member of the famous "Crvena Zvezda (Red Star) club", although he also represented the clubs "Partizan" and "Radnicki" for a time. His outgoing personality and elegant appearance made him a popular figure wherever he went in his long career, which lasted from the 1940s until the 2010s. "Bora" Ivkov passed away on 14th February 2022 as a result of complications from COVID-19. Following his funeral, a commemorative ceremony was held in the ceremonial hall of the Assembly of the City of Beograd, in the presence of the family, the leadership of the Chess Federation of Serbia and numerous friends and admirers. Grandmaster Aleksandar Matanovic reminded the audience of the most important moments in the career of his long-term teammate, and then a letter from grandmaster Ljubomir Ljubojevic was read out. Ivkov was buried in the Beograd's "Alley of Deserving Citizens" – a location reserved for the country's most significant political, social, cultural and sporting figures.  A few extracts from Ivkov's games follow. Diagram [#] This position was reached after White's 33rd move, which was 33.Kf1. The chances are approximately level. However, Ivkov got the better of his famous opponent as follows:} 33... g6 34. g4 $6 {This move would have been better avoided.} f5 $1 35. gxf5 $2 {The engines consider this capture to be a serious mistake.} ({They indicate instead} 35. exf5 gxf5 36. Kg2 {, though Black stands better.}) 35... gxf5 36. exf5 $6 {While after this incautious capture Black is clearly winning.} ({A better defence is} 36. f3 $5) 36... e4 37. Ke1 Ne5 38. Bc5 Nf3+ 39. Kf1 Kf7 {With the deadly threat of 40...Rg8.} 40. Kg2 {Avoiding mate, but there all the same follows...} Rg8+ 41. Kh1 {Diagram [#]} Rg1+ $1 {Liquidating to a technically-won endgame. After} 42. Rxg1 Nxg1 43. Be3 Nf3 44. Kg2 d2 45. Bxd2 Nxd2 46. Kg3 Kf6 47. Kf4 h5 48. Ke3 Nf3 49. Kxe4 Ng5+ 50. Kf4 Nxh3+ 51. Kg3 Ng5 52. Kh4 Kxf5 53. Kxh5 Ne4 {Fischer resigned.} 0-1

