{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 49 A complex trap. While generally thought of as "one movers," some opening traps are deeper and more beautiful than others because falling into them requires a certain degree of skill. They might not attract and, if they did, might work for an amateur! Steinmeyer's concept beginning with 13...Qf4+ is both subtle and novel. The only trouble is that it meets with a smashing refutation. Instead of simplifying, as Steinmeyer hopes, his variation enmeshes him in complications. The nail in the coffin is 16.Ne5, after which Black's Queen can no longer be extricated without fatal loss of material. '} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 {Fischer,R-Petrosian,T Zagreb ct 1959 Bobby Fischer: 'see game 16. Fischer,R-Petrosian,T; Zagreb ct, 1959.'}) 2... d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On tour (1964) I experimented with the weird'} 5. Nc5 $5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Most of my opponents countered with'} e5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Some replied with'} 5... b6 6. Na6 Nxa6 7. Bxa6 Qd5 $1) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Still others played'} 5... Qc7 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Nxd3 e6 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has more space, but only experience can tell whether he has the edge; however the Knight on d3 discourages the normal freeing maneuver ...c5 and/or ...e5. At least it's something to break the monotony.'}) 6. Nxb7 Qxd4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 6... Qb6 7. Nc5 Bxc5 8. dxc5 Qxc5 9. c3 $16 {Fischer,R-Petrosian,T Bled blitz 1961 Bobby Fischer: 'White's better. Fischer,R-Petrosian,T; Bled blitz, 1961.'}) 7. Qxd4 exd4 8. Bd3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'with the better ending.'}) 5... Bg6 6. Nf3 Nf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More usual is the immediate'} 6... Nd7 {Bobby Fischer: 'to prevent Ne5.'}) 7. h4 (7. Bd3 e6 8. O-O Be7 9. c4 O-O 10. Bxg6 hxg6 $10 {Evans,L-Benko,P ch-USA 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'leads to equality. Evans,L-Benko,P; ch-USA, 1962.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 7. Ne5 Nbd7 8. Nxg6 hxg6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black is solid.'}) 7... h6 8. Bd3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'White can try to exploit the order of Black's moves by'} 8. Ne5 {Bobby Fischer: 'but'} Bh7 9. Bc4 e6 10. Qe2 Nd5 $1 $10 { d7 Bobby Fischer: '=!' Bobby Fischer: 'followed by ...Nd7 equalizes.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 10... Qxd4 $2 11. Nxf7 $1)) (8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Nbd7 12. O-O-O Qc7 13. Ne4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Spassky-Petrosian, 13th match game, 1966, continued'} 13. Qe2 O-O-O 14. Ne5 Nxe5 15. dxe5 Nd7 16. f4 $16 {Spassky,B-Petrosian,T (13) Moscow WCH 1966 Bobby Fischer: 'with an edge.'}) 13... O-O-O 14. g3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '(Geller,E-Petrosian,T; Moscow, 1967.), and now'} Nxe4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'instead of'} 14... Ng4 $2 {Geller,E-Petrosian,T Moscow 1967}) 15. Qxe4 Bd6 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds White to a minimal pull.'}) 8... Bxd3 9. Qxd3 e6 10. Bd2 Nbd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 10... Qc7 11. c4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 11. O-O-O Bd6 12. Ne4 Bf4 $1 13. Nxf6+ gxf6 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'is satisfactory.'}) 11... Nbd7 12. Bc3 {Bobby Fischer: '(the whole idea is to prevent Black from swapping Bishops)'} a5 $1 13. O-O $5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 13. O-O-O Bb4 $1) 13... Bd6 14. Ne4 ({Bobby Fischer: '(Tal suggested'} 14. d5 $5 {Tal,M Bobby Fischer: 'mixing it up, but'} Bxg3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'mixing it up, but'} 15. dxe6 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 15. fxg3 cxd5 16. cxd5 Nxd5 17. Bxg7 Rg8 18. Qh7 N7f6 19. Bxf6 Nxf6 20. Qxh6 Qxg3 21. Qd2 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='}) 15... Ne5) 14... Nxe4 15. Qxe4 O-O $10 {Fischer,R-Donner,J Varna ol 1962 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer,R-Donner,J; Varna ol, 1962.'}) 11. O-O-O Qc7 12. c4 O-O-O (12... Bd6 $1 13. Ne4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 13. Ne2 O-O-O 14. Kb1 e5 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='}) 13... Bf4 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'leads to immediate simplifications.'}) 13. Bc3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Now Black no longer can force the exchange of Bishops. '} Qf4+ $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; The start of a faulty concept.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 13... Bd6 14. Ne4 Bf4+ 15. Kb1 Ne5 $1 16. Nxe5 Bxe5 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'produces equality.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 13... c5 14. d5 $1) 14. Kb1 Nc5 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'He still has time to back out with'} 14... Qc7 {}) 15. Qc2 Nce4 {Bobby Fischer: '; Now there is no turning back.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 15... Ncd7 16. Ne5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is very strong: e.g.,'} Nxe5 17. dxe5 Nd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 17... Ng4 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Rd1+ Kc8 20. Rd4) 18. Rd4 Qxe5 19. Rxd7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 16. Ne5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: ', A clear refutation. The Queen's retreat is cut off and the ancient weakness on Black's f7 is etched more sharply than ever.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Shamkovich, L-Goldberg, N; USSR, 1961, continued with'} 16. Ba5 $2 {Shamkovich,L-Goldberg,n URS 1961 Bobby Fischer: 'which won only against inferior defense.'}) 16... Nxf2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'What else?'} 16... Nxg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses to'} 17. fxg3 Qxg3 18. Rd3 Qf4 19. Rf3 Qe4 20. Nxf7 $18) ({Bobby Fischer: 'And'} 16... Nxc3+ {Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by'} 17. bxc3 $1 Rg8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 17... Ng4 18. Nh5 $1 Qf5 19. Qxf5 exf5 20. Nxf7) 18. Rd3 h5 19. Rf3 Qh6 20. Nxf7 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'}) 17. Rdf1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Probably what Steinmeyer overlooked when he went into this whole mess. Black resigns.'} (17. Rdf1 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'On'} Qxg3 18. Rxf2 { f3} Qe3 {Bobby Fischer: '(otherwise Rf3)'} 19. Re2 Qf4 20. Nxf7 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins at least the exchange.   Motivated by my lopsided result (11-0!), Dr Kmoch congratulated Evans (the runner up) on "winning" the tournament ... and then he congratulated me on "winning the exhibition."'}) 1-0

