{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer started the 1957-58 US Championship with a modest 2-1 score then went on a 81/2-11/2 spree that won the tournament. Some of his games were marred by shocking tactical lapses, including double-question mark moves by both players. But his slips were offset by this game, which won the second brilliancy prize.'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis Anand,V Andrew Soltis: 'PS'} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4 e6 7. O-O (7. Bb3 {Anand,V} Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. f4 Nbd7 10. f5 e5 11. Nde2 b5 12. Nd5 Bb7 13. Nec3 Nc5 14. Nxf6+ Bxf6 15. Bd5 Bxd5 16. Nxd5 h6 17. Qg4 Kh7 18. Rf3 Rc8 19. Rh3 Nd7 20. Qh5 Rc4 21. Bxh6 g6 22. fxg6+ fxg6 23. Qg4 Rf7 24. Rf1 Rxc2 25. Bf8+ {1-0 (25) Mikhalchishin-Klimakov}) 7... b5 8. Bb3 b4 9. Nb1 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'PS: '?''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'For a brief time in the 1950s this retreat, with Nd2-c4 in mind was a major alternative to'} 9. Na4 {Anand,V Andrew Soltis: ', which can expose the knight to attack from ...Bd7, ...Qa5'} Bd7 10. f4 (10. c3 $1 $146 {*} Nc6 11. Re1 Rb8 12. f3 Be7 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. cxb4 Rxb4 15. Be3 O-O 16. Rc1 Bd7 17. Re2 $14 {Rogic,D (2445)-Pavasovic,D (2505) Dresden (zt) 75/(228) 1998}) (10. Be3 {Ue 74/(265)}) 10... Nc6 11. f5 e5 12. Nf3 $5 $146 (12. Ne6 {Ue 45/282}) 12... h6 (12... Be7 13. Bg5 $1 Nxe4 $140 14. Qd5 Nxg5 15. Nxg5) 13. Qe1 (13. c3) 13... Be7 14. Qg3 $6 Kf8 $1 15. Qe1 Rb8 16. Bd2 (16. a3 Na5 (16... a5 17. axb4 axb4 18. Bc4 {#C5 b3}) 17. axb4 Nxb3 18. cxb3 Bc6 19. Nc3 $132) 16... Qe8 $1 17. a3 a5 {[#C5 #C8g8-h7]} 18. Kh1 Kg8 19. Rg1 $2 (19. axb4 axb4 20. Bc4 Na5 $1) (19. h3) 19... bxa3 $1 (19... Kh7 $5 20. g4 Nd4 21. Nxd4 exd4 22. g5 hxg5 23. Rxg5 $1 Kg8 $1 (23... Bc6 24. Qh4+ Kg8 25. Rxg7+ $18) 24. Rxg7+ $140 Kxg7 25. Qg3+ Kf8 26. Rg1 Ng4 27. Qxg4 Bf6) 20. Rxa3 Nd4 $1 $17 {[#5Ee4, #CDa4]} (20... d5 21. exd5 Bxa3 22. dxc6 Bxc6 23. Nxe5) 21. Nxd4 exd4 22. Ra1 Bc6 23. Bxa5 Nxe4 24. Rf1 h5 $1 $40 {[#C5 h4]} 25. Nb6 h4 26. Bd5 (26. Nd5 Bg5) 26... Bg5 {[#C5 #CDg3]} 27. Rf3 h3 $1 {[#5E#5Ca8-h1]} (27... Rxb6 28. Qxe4 $1) 28. Bxc6 (28. g3 Rxb6 $1) 28... Qxc6 29. Qe2 Bf4 $1 $19 30. Kg1 (30. Rxf4 Ng3+ 31. hxg3 hxg2+ 32. Kg1 Rh1+ 33. Kf2 Rxa1) 30... Be3+ 31. Kh1 Re8 $1 32. Qc4 Nf2+ 33. Rxf2 Qxc4 (33... Qe4 34. Rf3 hxg2+ 35. Kxg2 Rxh2+ 36. Kxh2 Qxf3) 34. Nxc4 Bxf2 35. Nxd6 Re2 36. b4 Bg3 $1 37. gxh3 Bxd6 {0-1 (37) Reinderman,D (2541)-Anand,V (2781) Wijk aan Zee 74/265 1999 [Anand,V]}) 9... Bd7 ({In his first book, Fischer said he didn't believe Soviet analysis that claimed White had enough compensation after} 9... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis} 10. Qf3 Bb7 {and he gave PS: '!'} (10... d5 {PS} 11. c4 bxc3 12. Nxc3 Nxc3 13. Qxc3 $16 {Chenkin-Yudovich/1954}) 11. Ba4+ Nd7 12. Bc6 (12. Nc6 {PS} Nec5 $1) 12... Bxc6 13. Nxc6 Qb6 14. Qxe4 d5 {as evidence. But the Russians suggested 12.Nd2 was better, but there haven't been enough practical tests to tell. PS: '' PS: 'Euwe'}) 10. Be3 Nc6 ({Taking the pawn now is too dangerous} 10... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis} 11. Qf3 d5 12. c4 bxc3 {PS} 13. Nxc3 Nxc3 14. bxc3 Be7 $13 {Euwe}) 11. f3 Be7 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Black misses his best equalizing chance} 11... Na5 {Andrew Soltis}) 12. c3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'White now obtains the upper hand on the queenside 12. ...a5 13.Nb5! Qb8 13.a4'} bxc3 (12... a5 {(Andrew Soltis) PS} 13. Nb5 $1 Qb8 14. a4 $14 {Euwe}) 13. Nxc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'PS: '!''} (13. Nxc3 {(Andrew Soltis) PS} Na5 $1 {Euwe}) 13... Bxc6 (13... cxb2 {(Andrew Soltis) Black loses two queens in theree moves after} 14. Nxd8 bxa1=Q 15. Bd4 $1 {PS: '+-' PS: 'Euwe'}) 14. Nxc3 O-O 15. Rc1 Qb8 ({Overlooking, or underestimating, White's combination. Better was} 15... Bb7 {(Andrew Soltis) after which} 16. Qd3 Rc8 17. Rfd1 {and Na4-b6, or the immediate 16.Na4, which offers White a slight pull}) (15... Qd7 {(Andrew Soltis) PS}) (15... d5 $5 {(Andrew Soltis) PS}) 16. Nd5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} exd5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Black might have tried to hold the extra pawn after} 16... Bxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 17. exd5 Nxd5 ({However after the text Black has long-term problems on the light squares as he would also have after} 17... e5 18. Rc6 $1 Qb5 (18... a5 19. Qe2 Qb4 20. Rfc1) 19. Rb6 Qa5 20. Rb7 {PS: ''}) 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. b3 Qb5 {. But apparently didn't trust positions such as} 20. Qd2 Rfe8 (20... Rfc8 {PS} 21. Rcd1 $16 {Fischer}) 21. Rfd1 Bf6) ({The same goes for} 16... Nxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 17. exd5 Bb5 $2 {which invites a terrific exchange offer} 18. dxe6 Bxf1 19. exf7+ Kh8 20. Qxf1 {followed by ... Bd5, Rc6 and Qc4 PS: '' PS: 'Fischer'}) 17. Rxc6 dxe4 18. fxe4 Qb5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'If Black had counted on'} 18... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'when he chose his 15th or 16th move, he would have been disappointed now to see'} 19. Rxf7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (19. Bd5 $5) (19. Rb6 {(Andrew Soltis) PS} Qa7 20. Bd5 Rab8 21. Rb3 Nc5 $13 {Kmoch}) 19... Rxf7 20. Bxf7+ {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (20. Qd5 $2 {(Andrew Soltis) is refuted by PS: '+-' PS: 'Euwe'} Ng5 21. Bxg5 Qa7+ 22. Kh1 Raf8 $1 {PS: ''}) 20... Kxf7 21. Qd5+ {Andrew Soltis: 'with a big edge'} Kf8 {Andrew Soltis: 'PS'} 22. Qxe4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'PS'}) 19. Rb6 Qe5 20. Bd4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qg5 (20... Qxe4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} 21. Re1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'PS: '+-''}) 21. Qf3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Fischer has two flaws, Larry Evans wrote in 1963 : Overconfidence "which causes him sometimes to forget that his opponents are also capable of finding good moves" and "impetuosity in winning positions." Here he allows an excellent maneuver that nearly equalizes. With} 21. Rb7 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) Black's straits are dire because} Nxe4 22. Re1 {costs material} d5 (22... Rac8 23. Rxe4 Rc1 24. Rbxe7 Rxd1+ 25. Bxd1) 23. Rxe7 $1 Qxe7 24. Bxd5) 21... Nd7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 22. Rb7 (22. Bxf7+ {Andrew Soltis} Kh8 23. Rb7 Ne5 {is fine for Black PS: '!' PS: 'Euwe'}) 22... Ne5 23. Qe2 Bf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black threatens 24. ...Nf3+ 25.Qxf3 Bxd4+ with good drawing chances due to the bishops of opposite color'} 24. Kh1 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} a5 25. Bd5 Rac8 {Andrew Soltis: 'White had threatened 26.Bxe5 dxe5 27.Rxf7 or 26. ...Qxe5 27.Rb5'} 26. Bc3 a4 ({Black would love to drum up kingside play with} 26... Ng4 {(Andrew Soltis) . But he has nothing to show for the pawn after} 27. Bd2 Qh4 28. h3 Ne5 29. Bxa5) 27. Ra7 Ng4 28. Rxa4 {Andrew Soltis: 'PS: '!''} ({Fischer took barely a minute to reject} 28. Bxf6 {Andrew Soltis} Nxf6 29. Rxa4 Nxd5 30. exd5 Qxd5 31. Ra7 Rce8) ({But} 28. Bd2 {(Andrew Soltis) was a worth alternative especially since} Rc2 {allows} 29. Rxf7 $1 Rxf7 30. Qb5 {and wins}) 28... Bxc3 29. bxc3 Rxc3 $2 (29... Ne3 $142 30. Qd2 h6 31. Rc1 Rfe8 $132) 30. Rxf7 $1 $18 {[%mdl 64] Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: 'Why two exclamation points? Isn't 30. ...Rxf7 31.Ra8+ obvious? Yes, but White's combination runs much deeper. He say that 30. ...Qxd5, which ends discovered checks and threatend ...Rc1+ is refuted by 31.Rxf8+ Kxf8 32.Qf1+! Qd7 33.Ra8 Ke7 34.Ra7+'} Rc1+ {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'PS: '??''} ({But White's main point is revealed after} 30... h5 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) which seems to win} 31. Rf1+ {PS: '?'} (31. Qb2 $2 Kh7 $1 32. Qxc3 Rxf7 33. Bxf7 Qf4 34. Bg8+ Kh8 $1) (31. Qf1 Kh7 $1 32. Rxf8 Rc1 33. Bg8+ Kh6 34. Bc4 (34. Be6 {PS} Rxf1+ 35. Rxf1 Qb5 {PS}) 34... Ne3 $1) (31. Rf3+ {PS: '?'} Kh7 32. Rxc3 Nf2+ (32... Qe5) (32... Qd2 {PS: '-+!'}) 33. Kg1 Nh3+ 34. Kh1 Nf2+ $11) ({Nevertheless, there is a White win - even though he must play a queen and pawn endgame after} 31. Rxf8+ $1 Kxf8 32. Qf1+ $1 Nf6 (32... Qf6 {PS} 33. Qxf6+ (33. Ra8+ Ke7 34. Ra7+ Kd8 35. Qb1 Nf2+ 36. Kg1 Nh3+ $11 37. gxh3 $2 Qg5+ 38. Kf2 (38. Kf1 Rf3+ 39. Ke1 Qg1+ 40. Ke2 Qg2+ 41. Kd1 Rf1#) 38... Qe3+ 39. Kg2 Qxh3+ 40. Kg1 Qe3+ 41. Kg2 Qxa7 $19 {PS}) 33... gxf6 34. Rc4 Re3 35. Rc1 Nf2+ 36. Kg1 Nxe4 $14 {PS}) 33. Rc4 Rxc4 34. Qxc4 {PS: ''} Nxd5 35. exd5 {PS: '+-' PS: 'Euwe'}) 31... Kh7 32. Rxf8 Rc1+ 33. Rf1 $2 Qf4 $1 {PS: '-+!!' PS: 'Euwe'}) (30... Rxf7 {(Andrew Soltis) PS} 31. Ra8+ $18) (30... Qxd5 {(Andrew Soltis) PS} 31. Rxf8+ Kx