{GAME N. 16 Four Queens. Fischer extracted a slight minus score from the seven games in which he was called upon to face the Caro-Kann, prompting Botvinnik to note: "Fischer's both strong and weak point lies in that he is always true to himself and plays the same way regardless of his partners or any external factor. " This variation is not only complex and critical - but perilous. After a single slip the edge passes to Black. But Petrosian overestimates his position and, somewhat recklessly, dissipates his advantage. In timepressure he misses a forced draw and Fischer regains the upper hand. From this point on, with four marauding Queens roaming the board, the play becomes "rich and strange" - resulting in a tortuous draw.  Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B11: Caro-Kann: Two Knights Variation'' Andrew Soltis: 'In his book Grandmasters in Profile, Dimitrije Bjelica included sample of handwriting and signatures that he had collected from top players. Some of their written comments were trivial, others tried to be profound. Botwinnik, for example, wrote "What is chess! That nobody knows." Fischer, however, write (in block letters) : "MY VARIATION AGAINST THE CARO-KANN IS GOOD BUT I HAVE BEEN A LITTLE UNLUCKY WITH IT." Nothng illustrated Fischer's stubbornness more than his insistence on playing the Two Knights (3.Nf3 Bg4) variation of the Caro. In his first 10 games he used it, Fischer managed a single win, three losses and six draws. The following game was typical. He continuously believed he was the one with winning chances, while Petrosian was the opposite.'} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics Fischer, Ragozin'} c6 ({Tal teased Fischer about his track record against the Caro by pushing his pawn to c6 at the start of one of their games at Curacao - then holding it there for several seconds before continuing to} 1... c5 {, to begin the Sicilian he intended all along.}) 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 ({The purpose of this line is to exclude the possibility of ...Bf5. For example,} 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 $2 5. Ng3 Bg6 ({if} 5... Bg4 6. h3) 6. h4 h6 7. Ne5 Bh7 8. Qh5 g6 9. Bc4 $1 e6 10. Qe2 $16 {(threatening Nxf7) and Black has a terrible game. Andrew Soltis: 'Alekhine-Bruce/Plymouth/1938'}) (3... Nf6 4. e5 Ne4 5. Ne2 $1 Qb6 6. d4 c5 7. dxc5 Qxc5 8. Ned4 Nc6 9. Bb5 a6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. O-O Qb6 12. e6 $1 fxe6 13. Bf4 $16 {4 is good for White. Fischer,R-Olafsson,F Beograd ct 1959 1-0 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Olafsson,F/Belgrade ct/1959/1-0/' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R-Olafsson,F Beograd ct 1959 1-0'}) 4. h3 Bxf3 ({In our first round game here, Smyslov played} 4... Bh5 5. exd5 cxd5 6. Bb5+ Nc6 7. g4 Bg6 8. Ne5 Rc8 9. d4 e6 10. h4 {to prevent ...} ({(correct is} 10. Qe2 $1 {>= Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'}) 10... f6 11. Nxg6 hxg6 12. Qd3 Kf7 13. h5 gxh5 14. gxh5 Nge7 $11 {Fischer,R-Smyslov,V Bled ct 1959 Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '=' Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Smyslov,V/Bled ct/1959/' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R-Smyslov,V Bled ct 1959'}) 5. Qxf3 {Position after} Nf6 ({the old} 5... e6 6. d4 dxe4 (6... Qb6 7. Qe3 Ne7 8. Bd3 dxe4 9. Qxe4 Nd7 10. O-O Nf6 11. Qh4 Rd8 12. Ne2 Qa5 13. b4 $5 Qxb4 14. Bg5 Neg8 15. Rab1 Qe7 16. c4 g6 17. d5 Bg7 18. dxc6 bxc6 19. Be4 Qc7 20. Qf4 e5 $10 {Hickl,J-Csom,I/Tel-Aviv/1988/½-½/27}) 7. Nxe4 (7. Qe3 Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. bxc3 Nf6 10. g4 h6 11. Rb1 Qc7 12. g5 $44 {Timmermann,G-Scheeren,P/NLD-TCH/1987/1-0/40}) (7. Qxe4 Nf6 8. Qd3 Nbd7 (8... Na6 9. a3 Nc7 10. g3 a5 11. Bg2 Ncd5 12. O-O Be7 13. b3 Nxc3 14. Qxc3 O-O 15. Qd3 Qd7 16. Bb2 b5 17. c4 $14 {Yermolinsky,A-Dautov,R/Frunze/1988/½-½/63}) 9. Be2 Qc7 10. O-O Bd6 11. Ne4 Bh2+ $5 12. Kh1 Bf4 $10 {Smyslov,V-Chandler,M/Hastings/1981/½-½/38}) 7... Qxd4 (7... Nf6 8. Bd3 Nbd7 9. c3 (9. O-O Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Nf6 11. Qh4 Qc7 12. Bf4 Bd6 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. c3 h6 15. Rae1 O-O-O 16. Bc2 Nd5 17. Qe4 Qf4 $10 {Balashov,Y-Sigfusson,S/Reykjavik/1989/½-½/32}) 9... Nxe4 10. Qxe4 Nf6 11. Qe2 Qd5 12. O-O Qh5 13. Qxh5 Nxh5 14. Be3 $14 {Boleslavsky,I-Bronstein,D/Budapest ct-plof/1950/½-½/49}) 8. Bd3 $146 {gives White a good attack for the Pawn. And on} Nf6 (8... Nd7 9. Be3 Qd5 10. O-O-O Ngf6 11. Kb1 $13 {Trifunovic}) 9. c3 Qd8 10. O-O Be7 11. Rd1 Nbd7 12. Qg3 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 g6 14. Bf4 $44 {Kortschnoj,V-Spassky,B/URS-ch/1959}) (5... dxe4 6. Nxe4 Nd7 7. Ng5 $5 ({(better is simply} 7. d4 {>= Bulletin: '' '' '' '' '' Andrew Soltis: ''}) 7... Ngf6 8. Qb3 e6 9. Qxb7 Nd5 $1 $17 {Black gets good play. Fischer,R-Cardoso,R Portoroz izt 1958 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Cardoso,R Portoroz izt 1958'}) 6. d3 ({Inferior is} 6. d4 dxe4 7. Nxe4 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (7. Qe3 Nbd7 8. Nxe4 Nxe4 9. Qxe4 Nf6 10. Qd3 Qd5 $1 $11 {is equal. Fischer,R-Keres,P Bled 1961 Bulletin: '=!' '=!' '=!' '=!' '=!' Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Keres,P Bled 1961'}) 7... Qxd4 8. Bd3 Nbd7 $17 {threatening Ne5}) ({on} 6. e5 Nfd7 7. e6 $2 ({(playable is} 7. Qg3 e6 8. Be2 {Spassky,B-Reshko,n Leningrad 1961 Bulletin: 'Spassky,B-Reshko/Leningrad/1961/' Andrew Soltis: 'Spassky,B-Reshko,N Leningrad 1961'}) 7... fxe6 8. d4 e5 $1 $17) ({finally} 6. g3 dxe4 7. Nxe4 Nxe4 8. Qxe4 Qd5 $1 9. Qxd5 cxd5 10. Bg2 e6 $11 {Suetin,A Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '='} 11. c4 Nc6 12. cxd5 Nb4 $1 {gives Black an even ending}) 6... e6 7. g3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({A recent try is} 7. Bd2 {followed by 0-0-0. Against Larsen, at Zurich 1959, I tried}) (7. a3 Bc5 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O Nbd7 $11 {and black got a satisfactory game. Fischer,R-Larsen,B Zürich 1959 Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '=' Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Larsen,B/Zuerich/1959/' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R-Larsen,B Zürich 1959'}) 7... Bb4 (7... Nbd7 8. Bg2 Bc5 9. O-O O-O 10. Qe2 Bd4 11. Kh2 Bxc3 12. bxc3 dxe4 13. dxe4 Qa5 $10 {Smyslov,V-Flohr,S/URS-ch/1950}) 8. Bd2 ({not} 8. Bg2 $2 d4 9. a3 Qa5 $19) 8... d4 ({Inferior is} 8... Qb6 9. O-O-O (9. a3 $6 Bxc3 10. Bxc3 d4 11. Bd2 Qxb2 12. Qd1 Qb6 13. Bg2 e5 14. O-O c5 15. f4 Nc6 16. Rb1 Qc7 $15 {Gunawan,Ro-Rogers,I/Jakarta zt/1993/0-1/43}) 9... d4 10. Ne2 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Bxd2+ 11. Rxd2 Nbd7 $11 {Simagin}) 9. Nb1 Bxd2+ ({in this tournament Keres and Benko both tried Andrew Soltis: 'Trading the dark-squared bishops and putting Black's center pawns on dark squares is dyed-in-wool Tarrasch dogma. But it also solves White's main development problem. Eight rounds earlier Keres played'} 9... Qb6 {forcing White to weaken the Q-side with} 10. b3 {But Black's Queen is slightly misplaced after Andrew Soltis: '?'} (10. Bg2 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics} O-O 11. O-O Nbd7 12. Bc1 a5 13. Qe2 a4 14. a3 Be7 15. f4 e5 16. Nd2 Qc7 17. Nf3 c5 18. f5 Rfc8 19. g4 h6 20. g5 hxg5 21. Bxg5 c4 22. Rf2 Qb6 23. Bc1 c3 24. bxc3 Rxc3 {Paragua,M (2529)-Bologan,V (2665)/Tripolis 2004/CBM 102/0-1 (37)}) (10. c3 $5 $14 {Mednis} Bc5 $1 11. Bc1 Nbd7 12. Nd2 Ne5 13. Qe2 dxc3 14. bxc3 O-O-O 15. d4 Bxd4 16. cxd4 Rxd4 17. Qe3 Rhd8 18. Be2 Qa5 $13 {Daniliuk,S-Bezgodov,A/Cheliabinsk/1987}) 10... Nbd7 (10... a5 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics Andrew Soltis: 'and then'} 11. a3 Bxd2+ (11... Be7 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'left Fischer's queenside in a mess that resulted in his ugliest defeat ever.'} 12. Bg2 a4 13. b4 Nbd7 14. O-O c5 15. Ra2 O-O 16. bxc5 Bxc5 17. Qe2 e5 18. f4 Rfc8 19. h4 Rc6 20. Bh3 Qc7 21. fxe5 Nxe5 22. Bf4 Bd6 23. h5 Ra5 24. h6 Ng6 25. Qf3 Rh5 26. Bg4 {Fischer,R-Keres,P/Bled/ Zagreb/Belgrade 1959/Candidates/0-1}) 12. Nxd2 Qc5 13. Qd1 g5 14. Bg2 Rg8 15. O-O Nbd7 16. Nc4 Ke7 17. Qe1 Nb6 18. f4 Nxc4 19. bxc4 gxf4 20. Rxf4 Nd7 21. Qf2 Raf8 22. Rb1 b6 23. Rf1 f6 24. Rh4 {Morovic Fernandez,I (2590)-Granda Zuniga,J (2610)/Yerevan 1996/EXT 1997/1-0 (43)}) 11. Bg2 a5 12. a3 Bxd2+ ({(the retreat} 12... Be7 $6 {seems illogical- even though Keres beat me with it) Fischer, R-Keres,P Bled ct 1959 0-1 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Keres,P Bled ct 1959 0-1'}) 13. Nxd2 Qc5 14. Qd1 h5 15. h4 $1 $16 {with an edge. (Fischer-Benko. ) Petrosian apparently didn't want to get involved with this line despite the fact that his countryman, Tal. accused me of "bad judgment" for preferring White here. Fischer, R-Benko,P Beograd ct 1959 1-0 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Benko,P/Belgrade ct/1959/1-0' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R-Benko,P Beograd ct 1959 1-0'}) 10. Nxd2 e5 ({An improvement over} 10... Qa5 11. a3 Nbd7 12. Qe2 $1 {with e4-e5 in mind. e.g.} h5 13. h4 Ng4 14. Bh3 Ndf6 15. O-O O-O-O 16. Nc4 {and 17.e5 with advantage}) 11. Bg2 c5 {Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'N''} (11... O-O {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics} 12. O-O Nbd7 13. Qe2 Qe7 14. Rae1 c5 15. f4 Rac8 16. Bf3 Qe6 17. Bg4 Nxg4 18. hxg4 b5 19. b3 f6 20. Kg2 Nb6 21. fxe5 fxe5 22. Rxf8+ Rxf8 23. g5 Rc8 24. Rb1 a6 25. Qh5 c4 26. bxc4 {Bolbochan,J-Van Scheltinga,T/Dubrovnik 1950/MCD/1/2-1/2}) 12. O-O Nc6 {Position after} 13. Qe2 Qe7 ({The critical juncture. In our earlier game (round two) Petros ian continued with Andrew Soltis: 'Petrosian had beaten Fischer in this line 14 rounds before with'} 13... g5 14. Nf3 $2 ({(Simagin gives} 14. f4 gxf4 15. gxf4 Qe7 16. Nc4 Nd7 17. Qg4 {+/-; ·'with advantage" but after Smagin,V Bulletin: '; Smagin,V'} O-O-O 18. fxe5 Kb8 $15 {Black succeeds in planting his Knight on e5 where it cannot be dislodged) Bulletin: '× e5'}) ({Fearing a prepared line, Petrosian deviates. On 13 ... g5 I had intended Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer indicated he was ready to improve with'} 14. c3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'and if Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} Qe7 15. Nf3 h6 16. cxd4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} exd4 (16... Nxd4 17. Nxd4 cxd4 18. Rac1) (16... cxd4 17. h4 Rg8 18. hxg5 hxg5 19. Rfc1 O-O-O 20. b4 $1 Kb8 21. b5 Na5 22. Qd2 $16 {wins a pawn.}) 17. e5 $1 O-O-O 18. Rfe1 $14 {Now the Bishop diagonal is unblocked and Black can't set up a blockade on his e5, as in our first game.}) 14... h6 15. h4 Rg8 16. a3 Qe7 17. hxg5 hxg5 18. Qd2 Nd7 19. c3 O-O-O 20. cxd4 exd4 $17 {with advantage for black. Fischer, R-Petrosian,T Bled ct 1959 0-1 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Petrosian,T Bled ct 1959 0-1'}) ({After} 13... O-O 14. f4 Kh8 15. f5 Ng8 16. g4 $36 f6 {White has a tough nut to crack, but his initiative is permanent. The text indicates Petrosian's intention to castle long without trying to prevent f4.}) 14. f4 O-O-O 15. a3 Ne8 ({Also playable is} 15... Nd7 16. b4 f6 {and if} 17. Nc4 b5) 16. b4 {Position after Andrew Soltis: '!'} cxb4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Wide open! Safer is Andrew Soltis: 'Very risky, particularl when'} 16... f6 {Andrew Soltis: 'would have given Black at least equality Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''} 17. b5 (17. bxc5 Qxc5 18. fxe5 Nxe5) 17... Na5 18. Nb3 Nxb3 19. cxb3 Kb8 20. a4 $11 {Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '='}) 17. Nc4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '! Ragozin''} ({Now Black has time to consolidate. Correct is Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer pointed out that'} 17. fxe5 $1 {with advantage in all variations: Andrew Soltis: 'favors White after Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} {Andrew Soltis: 'But he overstated White's chances in the alternatives'} Qxe5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'and'} 17... bxa3 18. Nc4 Rf8 19. Rxa3 $16 b5 20. Qg4+ $1) (17... Nxe5 18. axb4 Kb8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Rxa7 was threatened'} 19. Nf3 f6 20. Qf2 $1 Nxf3+ {penetrates)} (20... Qxb4 21. Nxe5 fxe5 22. Qf7) 21. Qxf3 Nd6 (21... Qxb4 22. e5 $1) 22. Ra5 a6 23. Qf4 Rhe8 24. Rd5 $16) (17... Kb8 18. axb4 Nxb4 (18... Nxe5 19. Nf3 {transposes to (c)}) 19. Nc4 Nc6 (19... Nc7 20. Nd6 $1 Rhf8 (20... f6 21. Rab1 a5 (21... fxe5 22. Nf7) 22. Ra1 b6 23. Nc4 $16 {PS}) 21. Rfb1 Nca6 22. Qd2 Qxe5 23. Nxb7 $1 Kxb7 24. Rxa6 $1 $18 {wins}) 20. Qf3 Rf8 21. e6 $1 Qxe6 22. e5 $1 $18 {followed by Rfb1 and White has a winning attack. Bulletin: ' fb1 '}) 18. Rxf7 Qxg3 19. e5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} Qe3+ (19... bxa3) 20. Qxe3 dxe3 21. Nc4 $16) 17... f6 $1 ({I had expected} 17... bxa3 18. fxe5 {transposing to "b" above.} Kb8 19. Rxa3 Nxe5 20. Nxe5 Qxe5 21. Rxf7 Nd6 {both of which are fine for Black}) 18. fxe5 fxe5 19. axb4 Nc7 {Black wants to secure a Q-side blockade. The b-Pawn won't run away. Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The knight will have an excellent outpost at b5 and a better one at c3. Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''} (19... Qxb4 $5 {(Bulletin) Ragozin}) 20. Na5 Nb5 $1 $17 {I already knew I'd been outplayed. Petros ian didn't even consider 20 Nxb4, opening up the lines. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} (20... Nxb4 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 21. Rfb1 Nca6 22. Qd2 b6 23. Nc4 $40) 21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. Rf2 g6 ({on Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer said he realized his game was going downhill when Petrosian quickly played solid moves rather than 20. ...Nxb4. Here Black rejected'} 22... Qxb4 23. Qg4+ Rd7 24. Rf7 Rhd8 25. Qxg7 {regains the pawn}) (22... Rhf8 $142 {(Bulletin) Ragozin}) 23. h4 Kb7 ({Ordinarily one would expect Petrosian to simplify and simplify in order to reach a winning ending. 23 ... Rhf8! is strong.} 23... Rhf8 $1) 24. h5 Qxb4 ({Really risky! I was amazed he was allowing so much counterplay. 24 ...Rhf8 is still right. On Andrew Soltis: 'Black missed a good chance for'} 24... Rhf8 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'on the last two moves. Now his king's insecurity becomes a factor'}) (24... gxh5 25. Qxh5 Rhf8 26. Rf5 $1) 25. Rf7+ Kb6 ({on} 25... Kb8 26. Qf2 Rhf8 27. c4 $1 Nc7 (27... dxc3 $2 28. Raxa7 Rxf7 29. Qb6+ $18) 28. Qf6 $16 {with good play}) 26. Qf2 $1 a5 ({not Andrew Soltis: 'It was too late for'} 26... Rhf8 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 27. c4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} Nc3 (27... Rxf7 28. Qxf7 Qc3 29. Rb1 $1 Qxd3 30. c5+ $1 Kxc5 31. Qe7+ {and wins}) 28. Raxa7 $18 {wins. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '+-''}) (26... Qc5 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 27. c4 dxc3 $2 28. Ra6+ $1 {Andrew Soltis: '+-!'}) (26... Qc3 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 27. Ra4) 27. c4 {Position after} Nc3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Continuing to underestimate the danger. Safer is} 27... Nd6 {>= Bulletin: '' '' '' '' '' Andrew Soltis: ''}) 28. Rf1 $2 ({Why didn't I play Andrew Soltis: 'Both players missed'} 28. Qf6 $1 {immediately? If then Andrew Soltis: 'threatening, among other thigns, Qg7 and Rb7+ e.g.'} Rdf8 (28... Rhf8 29. Rf1 $1 Rxf7 30. Qxd8+ $1 Rc7 31. Rf7 $18 {wins.}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer gave Black's best line as'} 28... Qc5 29. Qg7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} Ka6 {Andrew Soltis: 'but White would have a clear edge after'} (29... a4 30. Ra7 $1) (29... Ra8 30. Rb7+ Ka6 31. Qc7 Rhc8 32. Rb5 $1 $18) (29... Rdg8 30. Rb7+ Ka6 31. Qc7 Rc8 32. Rxa5+ $1 $18) 30. Ra7+ $1 Qxa7 31. Rxa5+ Kxa5 32. Qxa7+ Kb4 33. Qb6+ Ka3 34. c5 $36 {and White's c-Pawn is dangerous. Andrew Soltis: 'The power of the queen against two rooks was an important lesson for Fischer, and one he used for the next decade. But even in that line Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''} gxh5 35. Qxc6 Kb2 $1 {and ...Kc2 would be hard to win.}) (28... Qd6 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 29. Qg7 Rdg8 $2 (29... a4 30. Rb7+ Kc5 31. Re7) 30. Rb7+ Kc5 31. Rxa5+ $18) 29. Qxe5 Rxf7 30. Qxh8 Qc5 31. e5 $16 {- it's difficult for Black in view of his exposed King and White's passed e-Pawn.}) 28... a4 {Still playing with reckless abandon! Safer is 28 ..... Qd6 to prevent Qf6.} (28... Qd6 {/\ Bulletin: '' '' '' '' '' Andrew Soltis: '' Andrew Soltis: '!f6'}) 29. Qf6 Qc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '?''} (29... Qd6 $142 $1 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 30. Qg7 Rdg8 $1 31. Rb7+ Kc5 32. Qf7 Rf8 33. Qg7 Rhg8 34. Qxh7 {Andrew Soltis: '??'} Rh8 35. Qxg6 Qxg6 36. hxg6 Ne2#) 30. Rxh7 $1 ({Now I decided to start playing for the win. On Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer rejected'} 30. Qg7 Rdg8 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} 31. Rb7+ Ka6 32. Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: 'because his queen can't escape rook attacks after'} Rc8 33. Qg7 Rcg8 $11 {draws by a "perpetual check" on the Queen. Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '='}) (30. hxg6 $143 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics} hxg6 31. Qg7 Rdg8 32. Rb7+ Ka6 $15) 30... Rdf8 $1 {Forcing what looks like a favorable ending} 31. Qxg6 Rxf1+ (31... Rxh7 $1 {(Bulletin) Ragozin} 32. Qxh7 a3 $1 (32... Rxf1+ {game Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Dies war nach dem Turnierbach die Partiefortsetzung.'}) 33. Qg7 Ne2+ 34. Kh2 Rxf1 35. Bxf1 a2 36. Qg8 {Andrew Soltis: '=' 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '=''} a1=Q 37. Qb8+ $10 {Andrew Soltis: '=' Andrew Soltis: 'Ragozin'}) (31... a3 32. Qg7 Ne2+ 33. Kh2 Rxf1 34. Bxf1 a2 (34... Rxh7 35. Qxh7 a2 36. Qg8 $11) 35. Qa7# {PS}) 32. Bxf1 Rxh7 33. Qxh7 a3 {Petrosian had been banking on the speed of this Pawn.} 34. h6 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The only defense against two Black queens is two White queens.'} a2 35. Qg8 a1=Q 36. h7 {Position after Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} Qd6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer, who still thought he was the one with winning chances, felt Black should have forced him to sue to peace after Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '?''} ({In time-pressure, Petrosian overlooks} 36... Ne2+ {Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!'} 37. Kf2 Nxg3 $1 {and White has nothing better than to take a perpetual with Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} 38. Qb8+ $11 {Bulletin: '=' '=' '=' '=' '='}) 37. h8=Q Qa7 (37... Qe1 $5 $11 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics}) 38. g4 Kc5 $1 {A good last-ditch try. Curiously, the King will be safer in White's territory where it obtains shelter from the cluster of Pawns. Andrew Soltis: 'Black realizes his king is safer here - or on b4 - than on b6. Both players remembered their earlier game when Petrosian marched his king to a3, despite White's queen and rook, just before Fischer resigned.'} 39. Qf8 $2 {wins the e-Pawn. Finally on Forced, to defend against the threat of' Qxe5+. Not} ({right is} 39. Qh2 $1 {immediately, preventing Black's King from reaching safety behind the lines. If then Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!'} {Andrew Soltis: 'On other mvoes'} Qf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'White can push his passed pawn'} (39... Qa1 40. Qg7) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Or profit from a trade of queens'} 39... Qa2 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '?''} 40. Qxa2 Nxa2 41. Qa8 $1 $18 (41. Qg7 $1 {followed by g4-g5, Qf6})) (39... Qae7 40. Qa8 $40 Qdc7 $2 ({Another natural defensive idea, fails utterly to} 40... Kb6 $4 41. Qb2+ Qb4 42. Qxb4+ Qxb4 43. Qb8+ Ka5 (43... Kc5 44. Qf8+) 44. Qa7#) 41. Qa3+ Kb6 42. Qhb2+ $1) 40. g5 {or}) 39... Qae7 $8 {Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''} (39... Qxf8 40. Qxf8+ Kb6 41. Qb4+ Ka6 (41... Kc7 42. Qe7+) 42. Qa3+ Kb7 43. Qxa7+ Kxa7 44. g5 $18 {and queens}) 40. Qa8 {I thought this was it! The two Queens are closing in for the kill.} Kb4 $1 41. Qh2 {Position after} Kb3 $1 {Slippery as an eel! Andrew Soltis: 'The king is remarkably safe. Fischer/Ragozin/PS: 'Abbruchstellung''} 42. Qa1 {After the game a kibitzer asked Petrosian if he thought 42 c5 would have won for White. Petrosian, who must have analyzed it for many hours (not knowing, of course, what my sealed move was), simply replied: "I don't know."} ({Now White sealed. It's fantastically complicated! The tournament bulletins suggest} 42. c5 {Andrew Soltis: '!?'} Qxc5 (42... Qg6 43. Be2 $1 Qgg5 44. Bd1+ $1) 43. Qg8+ Ka3 44. Qc2 Qb4 45. Qa8+ Qa4 ({not} 45... Na4 $2 46. Qc1+ Ka2 47. Qg8+ Qb3 48. Qc2+ $1 $19) 46. Qcxa4+ Nxa4 47. Qxc6 {"with good winning chances, " but after Bulletin: '' '' '' '' '' Bulletin: 'Ragozin'} Nc3 $14 {it's likely Black can draw. Bulletin: '=!' '=!' '=!' '=!' '=!' Bulletin: 'Fischer'}) ({With} 42. Qd2 {Whtie would threaten Be2-d1+ - and win after} Qa3 ({But Black's queen becomes too active after} 42... Qh4 {to entertain serious winning chances.}) 43. Qxc6) 42... Qa3 {The only move to stop mate on b2. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''} 43. Qxa3+ Kxa3 44. Qh6 {Now White has to try to make do with the g-Pawn. Bulletin: 'g4-g5-g6-g7-g8'} Qf7 $1 45. Kg2 ({on} 45. Qxc6 Nd1 $1) (45. Qd6+ Kb3 46. Qxe5 Qf3 $1 {White's next move stops Qf3 and enables him to meet ...Nd1 with 46.Qc1+}) 45... Kb3 {Bulletin: 'd1'} ({not} 45... Nd1 46. Qc1+ Nb2 {and the Knight is stranded out-of-play. The text renews the threat of ..Nd1. Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' '+-' '+-'}) 46. Qd2 Qh7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} 47. Kg3 {A gross oversight, but probably best anyway! White can't wIn anymore. If 47 g5 Qh4, etc. Position after Bulletin: '"An gross oversight, but probably best anyway." Fischer' Andrew Soltis: '?'} (47. g5 Qh4 $44) (47. Be2 $142 $5 $14 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics}) 47... Qxe4 $1 {Bulletin: 'ChessCalisthenics: ''' Andrew Soltis: 'White overlooked this move (48.dx4 Nxe4+ 49.Kh4 Nxd2 50.g5 Nxf1 51.g6 d3! and wins. Now he should play 48.g5'} 48. Qf2 $2 ({Having overlooked Petrosian's last move, I was somewhat shaken! Not} 48. dxe4 $2 Nxe4+ 49. Kh4 (49. Kh3 {(Bulletin) ChessCalisthenics} Nxd2 $19) 49... Nxd2 50. g5 Nxf1 51. g6 d3 $19 {wins. Also on}) (48. Qd1+ $2 Nxd1 49. dxe4 Ne3 50. Be2 Nxc4 51. g5 Nd6 52. g6 Ne8 $19 {and Black again wins. The right retort, however, is 48 g5! and it's still a hard fight.}) (48. Qxc3+ $2 dxc3 49. dxe4 c2 $19 {wins.}) (48. g5 $1 $13 {Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' '!' '!'}) 48... Qh1 $1 $17 {Bulletin: 'Petrosian accepted the offered draw. "After having fought so hard for the draw, however, Petrosian was obviously unprepared to readjust his frame of mind and start playsing for a win." Fischer' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black has good winning chances after'} 48... Qh1 $1 {I offered a draw, afraid that he wouldn't accept. Black certainly has the edge now. If} 49. g5 {e4! OR IF} ({Andrew Soltis: 'or'} 49. Bg2 Qh6 $17 {After having fought so hard for the draw, however, Petrosian was obviously unprepared to readjust his frame of mind and start playing for a win. So ... Andrew Soltis: 'threatening ...Nd1 or ...Ne2-f4+. Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '''}) 49... e4 $1 $17 {DRAW Andrew Soltis: '. But, according to Fischer, Petrosian couldn't start thinking about a win after playing defense for so long. Fischer only managed to win three games - and lost four - in the final 12 round of the Candidates Tournament. But after virtually guaranteeing Tal first prize, by losing a near-winning game to him in the penultimate round, he ended on a high note. Bjelica recalled going with him to a movie theater on the day before the final round to see "Lust for Life", the Kirk Douglas film about Vincent Van Gogh. At one point "Bobby whispered to me : 'If I don't beat Smyslov I'll cut off my own ear.'" The next day he outplayed the former world champion by move 20 and won in 54 moves. Fischer/Ragozin/PS: '!''}) 1/2-1/2

