{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 6 A small oversight. Fischer's openini repertoire has been less extensive than most practicing Grandmasters', yet his contributions to theory have been numerous. 20 g5!? is one of his innovations. However. it is not responsible for Shocron's defeat. Neither Is Shocron's response; In retrospect. his system of defense seems sutprlslllfly adequate. Neventheless. after defending sensibly. Shocron outfoxes himself. Thinking he has seen one move further than his adversary; he provokes a combination. but his vision is one move short. In consequence, though otherwise it had withstood all of Fischer's assaults, his game crumbles. '} 1. e4 {Fischer/PS Shipov,Sergei Shipov,Sergei Bobby Fischer Shipov,Sergei: 'Shipov'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O (7... d6 {Bobby Fischer} 8. c3 {1-0 (40) Fischer,R-Shocron,R Mar del Plata 1959 My 60 Memorable Games [Bobby Fischer]}) 8. c3 d6 9. h3 ({F or an immediate} 9. d4 $5 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 36. Fischer/PS: 'Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V Stockholm izt 1962'}) 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 ({For Keres'} 11... Nd7 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 38. Fischer/PS: 'Fischer,R-Keres,P Curacao ct 1962'}) 12. Nbd2 Bd7 ({Some alternatives are:} 12... Kh8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Re8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Rd8 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Nc6 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Bb7 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... Nd7 {Bobby Fischer}) (12... cxd4 {(Bobby Fischer) . I don't think there's any easy way for black to achieve equality- but who knows?}) 13. Nf1 Rfe8 14. Ne3 g6 {Fischer/PS: ' e7-f8(-g7)'} 15. dxe5 (15. Bd2 Bf8 {(Bobby Fischer) and now: The quiet} 16. Rc1 ({The sharp} 16. b4 cxb4 17. cxb4 Nc4 18. Nxc4 bxc4 19. Rc1 exd4 20. Nxd4 d5 ({not} 20... c3 21. Bb3 Nxe4 22. Rxe4 $1 Rxe4 23. Qf3 Bc6 24. Qxc3 {wins. Fischer/PS: '+-'}) 21. exd5 Rxe1+ $2 ({better is} 21... Qd6 {Fischer/PS: ''}) 22. Bxe1 Qd6 {(Fischer - Matanovich, Bled, 1961) And now as Keres pointed out, white can retain the pawn with Fischer/PS: 'Fischer,R-M