ank, and [after' Neil McDonald: 'Incidentally, I'm not sure if the criticism implicit in the claim that 'Fischer always strives for activity' is justified. Fischer was Black in this game, he probably wasn't concerned whether White is objectively okay after 32 Kg1 - he would have carried on pressing for the win. What he did need to know, however, was whether White could upset the cart so to speak with a violent attack against the f7 point with Bc4 and d5-d6. He had to verify that Black can ward off the danger after' Neil McDonald: 'Having said that, Hübner's suggestion of' Neil McDonald: 'Incidentally, I'm not sure if the criticism implicit in the claim that 'Fischer always strives for activity' is justified. Fischer was Black in this game, he probably wasn't concerned whether White is objectively okay after 32 Kg1 – he would have carried on pressing for the win. What he did need to know, however, was whether White could upset the cart so to speak with a violent attack against the f7 point with Bc4 and d5-d6. He had to verify that Black can ward off the danger after'} 31. Bc4 {Bobby Fischer: '(to prevent ...Ra2)' Fischer/PS: '!a2'} Kg7 {Neil McDonald: '] play'} 32. d6 (32. Kg1 {." Hübner says he isn't certain Black is winning after that. Neil McDonald: 'is an excellent one. He intends to answer'} Rc8 {with} 33. Rb3 {, escaping from the pin.}) 32... f5 33. Bd5 Ra6 $19 { xd6  xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'again wins the d-Pawn.' Neil McDonald: 'when Black picks up the d6-pawn and wins easily.' Neil McDonald: '- White has no time for' Neil McDonald: '– White has no time for'} 34. Rc7 {because of Neil McDonald: 'because of '} Rxd6 {, winning the passed pawn and killing of White's initiative.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or' Neil McDonald: 'The fact that Fischer is focused on making sure White can't do anything dramatic is made clear in a further note by Fischer at this point: "[If]'} 31. Bf3 f5 32. Rc7 Bb5 33. Re1 e4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'squelches White's play.' Neil McDonald: 'squelches White's play." Yes, Fischer is concerned with keeping White in a bind. Whether or not Pilnik could survive with 31 Bc4 Kg7 32 Kg1 isn't of much relevance. Therefore we can take issue with the idea that Fischer is suggesting impulsive moves because he overestimated the value of the initiative - instead he is trying to keep things under control. You can bet that if Fischer had been White in this game he would have been looking at all possible saving moves, including quiet ones.' Neil McDonald: 'squelches White's play." Yes, Fischer is concerned with keeping White in a bind. Whether or not Pilnik could survive with 31 Bc4 Kg7 32 Kg1 isn't of much relevance. Therefore we can take issue with the idea that Fischer is suggesting impulsive moves because he overestimated the value of the initiative – instead he is trying to keep things under control. You can bet that if Fischer had been White in this game he would have been looking at all possible saving moves, including quiet ones.'}) {Neil McDonald: 'Unlike Fischer, we can't exonerate Pilnik from putting too much faith in the in initiative, as in the game his sharp play quickly led to disaster after 31 Rc7:'} 31... Bf5 32. g4 {Bobby Fischer: ', A wild hope.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Hopeless is'} 32. Bc4 Rc2 {Bobby Fischer: '!'} 33. d6 Rxc4 $1 34. Rxc4 Bd3 35. Rfc1 Bxc4 36. Rxc4 Rxa3 37. Kg1 Kg7 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Black soon picks off the d-Pawn and wins easily.'}) 32... Be4+ 33. Bf3 Bd3 34. d6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 34. Re1 e4 $1 35. Bg2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 35. Bxe4 Re8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 35... Rd8 36. Rc5 Kg7 $1 37. Kg1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 37. g5 h6 38. h4 hxg5 39. hxg5 Rh8+ 40. Kg1 Rh4 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins!.'} (40... Rh5 $1 $19)) 37... f5 38. gxf5 gxf5 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the two passed center Pawns should win. (if' Fischer/PS: '-+ e,f'} 39. d6 Kf6 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: ').'}) 34... Rd8 35. Re1 Rxd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Najdorf chided 