{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer had two favorite countries to play in : Argentina and Yugoslavia. Of the 15 international tournaments, not counting interzonals and olympiads, in which he competed, four were held in Argentina, including his first, mar del playa 1959 and his last, Buenos Aires 1970 - and also his worst, Buenos Aires 1960. This was his favorite game from Mar del Plata.'} 1. e4 {MIKHALCHISHIN Mikhalchishin,A Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. c4 {Andrew Soltis: 'This had been considered a virtual refutation of 4. ... a6 since the 1920s but Black's system made a comeback in the 1950s.'} Qc7 (5... Nf6 {(Fischer/PS) } 6. Nc3 Bb4 $11) 6. Nc3 Nf6 7. Bd3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '?''} ({Fischer expressed the conventional wisdom of the 1960s when he said the restricting} 7. a3 {gave White a major edge. But today Fischer/PS: '!'} Be7 (7... Nc6 {Fischer/PS} 8. Be3 Ne5 (8... b6 9. Rc1 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Bd6 11. Na4 Rb8 12. Bd3 $2 (12. c5 $1 bxc5 13. e5 Qb7 14. Rxc5 $16) 12... Bf4 13. Rc2 e5 $15 {Beliavsky,A-Vasiukov,E/URS-ch 1974}) (8... Be7 9. Rc1 Ne5 10. Be2 Ng6 11. O-O b6 12. f4 O-O 13. b4 Bb7 14. Bd3 Rac8 15. Nb3 d6 16. Qe2 Rfe8 17. h3 Nd7 18. Qf2 Ba8 19. Rc2 Qb8 20. Rfc1 Bh4 21. g3 $6 {Torre,E-Karpov,A/Bad Lauterberg 1977} (21. Qd2 $5 $14)) 9. Be2 $1 Nxc4 $2 10. Bxc4 Qxc4 11. Rc1 $18) (7... b6 $1 {(Fischer/PS) Taimanov} 8. Be3 Bb7 9. f3 Nc6 10. Rc1 Ne5 11. Be2 Ng6 12. Qd2 Rb8 $1 13. O-O Bd6 $13) 8. Be3 (8. Be2 {Fischer/PS} O-O 9. O-O d6 10. Be3 Nbd7 11. Rc1 b6 12. b4 $1 Bb7 13. f3 Rac8 14. Qe1 Qb8 15. Qf2 $16 {Spielmann,R-Tartakover,S}) 8... O-O 9. Rc1 Nc6 {and ...Nxd4 is one of a few lines recognized as equal.}) 7... Nc6 8. Be3 (8. Nde2 {Fischer/PS} Bc5 9. Bf4 d6 10. O-O Ne5 11. h3 Nfd7 12. Rc1 O-O 13. Bb1 Ng6 14. Bg3 Nde5 15. b3 Bd7 16. Kh1 $14 {Sokolov-Suetin, Moscow 1982}) 8... Nxd4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '?''} ({Fischer recommended} 8... Ne5 $1 {so that Black can meet} 9. O-O {with} ({and} 9. Rc1 {with} Nfg4 {Fischer/PS: '!'}) (9. Be2 {Fischer/PS} Nxc4 10. Bxc4 Qxc4 11. Rc1 Qb4 12. O-O d6 13. Nb3 $2 Nxe4 14. a3 Nxc3 $19) 9... Neg4 $1) 9. Bxd4 Bc5 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Blacks' idea in such situations,as it was mentioned before, is to fight over the control of Black squares.It is very instructive to follow, how Fischer is destroying the opponents plan.' Blacks' idea in such situations,as it was mentioned before, is to fight over the control of Black squares.It is very instructive to follow, how Fischer is destroying the opponents plan.} 10. Bc2 $1 d6 11. O-O Bd7 12. Na4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Another interesting option was' Another interesting option was} 12. Bxc5 Qxc5 (12... dxc5 13. f4 e5 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 exf4 16. Re1+ Kf8 17. d6 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'led to very unpleasant position with the King in the center.' led to very unpleasant position with the King in the center.}) 13. Qd3 Ke7 14. Rad1 Rhd8 15. Qg3 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'which lead to very unclear position,as King in the center is placed not bad at all.' which lead to very unclear position,as King in the center is placed not bad at all.}) 12... Bxd4 ({Black seems to lose this game because he believes in the absolute defensibility of his position even after White controls b6. Here} 12... Bxa4 13. Bxa4+ Ke7 {is more promising.}) 13. Qxd4 Rd8 ({MIKHALCHISHIN: 'stronger was' stronger was} 13... e5 {Andrew Soltis: 'followed by ...Bc6'} 14. Qd2 O-O 15. Nc3 Be6 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'with sufficient control over d5 and Whites efforts to attack pawn d6 are not real.' with sufficient control over d5 and Whites efforts to attack pawn d6 are not real.}) 14. Rfd1 O-O {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'There was no reason to fear 15.Qxd6 Qxd6 16.Rxd6 because of 16. ...Bxa4 and ...Nxe4'} 15. Rac1 ({MIKHALCHISHIN: 'nothig would give' nothig would give} 15. Nb6 Bc6 16. f3 e5 17. Qe3 Nd7 18. Nxd7 Bxd7 19. b3 Be6 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'preparing f7-f5 later.' preparing f7-f5 later.}) (15. Qxd6 {Fischer/PS} Qxd6 16. Rxd6 Bxa4 17. Rxd8 Rxd8 18. Bxa4 Nxe4 $11) 15... Qa5 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram ' Diagram [#]} ({Black must delay the usual liberating advances (...d5 or ...b5)} 15... b5 {Fischer/PS: '?'} 16. cxb5 axb5 17. Nc3 {after which b5 is a lingering target. Fischer/PS: '' Fischer/PS: '× b5'} (17. e5 $2 {Fischer/PS} dxe5 18. Bxh7+ Nxh7 $19)) (15... Bc6 $5 $11 {Fischer/PS}) 16. Qb6 $1 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'it is Bobbys plan - to try to exploit his own better technique in the endgame.,' Andrew Soltis: 'A poweful move - used to similar effect by Viswanathan Anand in a 1995 Candidates match with Gata Kamsky - that trades off the only active enemy piece. If Black tries 16. ... Qe5 17.Rxd6 Ng4 his attack dies in its tracks after 18.g3 Qh5 19.h4 Ne5 20.Bd1 Qg6 21.Nc5' it is Bobbys plan - to try to exploit his own better technique in the endgame.,} Qxb6 (16... Qg5 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'to very sharp play led the piece sacrifice' to very sharp play led the piece sacrifice} 17. Qxb7 Rb8 18. Qxa6 Ra8 19. Qxd6 Bxa4 20. b3 Be8 21. a4 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'and three pawns will become dangerous sooner or later.' and three pawns will become dangerous sooner or later.}) (16... Qe5 {Fischer/PS} 17. Rxd6 Ng4 18. g3 Qh5 19. h4 $16) 17. Nxb6 Bc6 18. f3 {Andrew Soltis: 'White is making slow progress - a good policy since Black may be tempted into 18. ...d5? 19.cxd5 exd5, which leads to a textbook disadvantage after 20.e5 Nd7 21.Nxd7 Rxd7 22.Rd4'} Nd7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 19. Nd5 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Possible was' Andrew Soltis: 'And can defend his only weakness, d6, after' Possible was} 19. Nxd7 Rxd7 20. b4 (20. Rd2 Rfd8 {and ...Kf8-e7} 21. Rcd1 {Fischer/PS} Kf8 $11) 20... Rc8 21. Bb3 Rdd8 22. Kf2 Kf8 23. Ke3 Ke7 24. f4 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'but the advantage is minimal.' but the advantage is minimal.}) ({Black will have good counterplay after} 19. Na4 Ne5 20. Bb3 g5 $1 {Fischer/PS: '=!'}) 19... Bxd5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Black shifts from watchful wating to a strategy of making White's bishop "bad" and setting his own pawns on dark squares. That might work with the queens on the board. But here he underestimates the force of White's b4 and c4 in an endging. The other way of transforming the position} 19... exd5 20. exd5 Ne5 {was adequate according to Fischer} 21. dxc6 bxc6 22. b3 c5 23. Be4 Rfe8 {Fischer/PS: '='}) ({But he felt that} 19... Ne5 {was best Fischer/PS: '!'} 20. Ne3 (20. Ne7+ Kh8 21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. Ba4 g5 {Fischer/PS: '=!' Fischer/PS: ' h8-g7-f6-e7'}) 20... g5 $1 {stopping f4}) 20. exd5 e5 (20... Ne5 {Fischer/PS} 21. Be4 b6 $11 (21... f5 22. dxe6 $1)) 21. b4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: ' c4-c5''} g6 ({A recurring theme now is the threat of bishop-takes knight, since then c4-c5 would decide the game positionally. Black's last move avoids, for example} 21... a5 22. a3 axb4 23. axb4 Ra8 24. Bf5 $1 {and it prepares ...f5 and ... Kg7-f6 Fischer/PS: '!'}) 22. Ba4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} b6 (22... a5 $1 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'was the only chance to create own counterplay.' was the only chance to create own counterplay.}) ({"A knight is always badly placed on b6" proves true after} 22... Nb6 23. Bb3 {and c5 Fischer/PS: ' c4-c5'}) 23. Rd3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black must play 23. ... a5 (and if 24.a3 then 24. ...f5)'} f5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (23... a5 $142 {Fischer/PS} 24. a3 (24. Bxd7 Rxd7 25. bxa5 bxa5 26. c5 dxc5 27. Rxc5 a4 $11) 24... f5 $14) 24. Ra3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'White threatens Bxd7 and Rxa6, and he can meet 24. ...Nf6 with 25. Bc6!'} Nb8 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Diagram ' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: ''' Diagram [#]} ({White wins after} 24... a5 25. bxa5 bxa5 26. Bc6 {Fischer/PS: '!'} Nb6 (26... Nc5 {Fischer/PS} 27. Rxa5 $18) (26... Nb8 {Fischer/PS} 27. Bb5 $1) 27. Rb1 $1 Nxc4 $2 28. Rc3 Nd2 29. Rb2 {Fischer/PS: '+-'}) (24... Nf6 {Fischer/PS} 25. Bc6 $18) 25. c5 $1 {MIKHALCHISHIN: 'Destroying the opponents structure in the center and opening the position,while Blacks Knight is out of action.' Destroying the opponents structure in the center and opening the position,while Blacks Knight 