{Andrew Soltis: 'As he prepared for the fourth FIDE Candidates tournament, Paul Keres went on a fishing trip with Vladas Mikenas, the veteran Estonian master. While they were relaxing they talked chess and discovered a dangerous sacrificial line in the Najdorf Sicilian. "To celebrate our success," Mikenas recalled, they decided Keres should save the idea for a game with Fischer, who was becoming the world's most devoted Najdorf-ile. As luck would have it, Fischer was due for Black against Keres in the first round when the Candidates began in September 1q959. The timing was perfect and "the 'fish' went for the bait" Mikenas wrote.'} 1. e4 {Ftacnik,Lubomir Andrew Soltis Ftacnik,Lubomir Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov'} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'The Najdorf Variation was always Fischer's main and very successful weapon for Black in reply to 1 e4. Knowing this, for the present game Keres had prepared an original novelty.''} 6. Bg5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'The fashion of that time.''} (6. Bc4 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) – Game No.51.}) (6. Be2 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) – Game No.70.}) 6... e6 7. f4 Be7 ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Later Bobby also employed'} 7... Qb6 $5 $13 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Ftacnik,Lubomir: '(Garry Kasparov) (Game No.67).'}) ({In the 1970s Black began saving a tempo on ...Be7, by playing the immediate} 7... Nbd7 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Garry Kasparov} 8. Qf3 Qc7) ({or} 7... Qc7 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) (Volume 3, Game Nos.25, 88 and 89).}) 8. Qf3 Qc7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Needing a draw against Gligoric in the final round at Portoroz to qualify for this Candidates Tournament, Fischer made the stunning choice of the "Argentine Variation"' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'In the game Gligoric-Fischer (Portoroz Interzonal 1958) the following sharp variation occurred:''} 8... h6 9. Bh4 g5 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!?''} 10. fxg5 Nfd7 {Andrew Soltis: 'despite the complications of'} 11. Nxe6 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!?''} (11. O-O-O {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Garry Kasparov} Ne5 12. Qe2 hxg5 13. Bg3 Nbd7 14. Qe1 $6 b5 15. a3 Bb7 {... 0-1 Cardoso-Fischer, 1st matchgame, New York 1957}) (11. Qh5 $1 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) is stronger}) 11... fxe6 12. Qh5+ Kf8 13. Bb5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'after' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: ', which had been discarded after the famous 'Argentine tragedy'. In the 14th round of the Interzonal tournament in Gothenburg (1955) it was tried simultaneously in three games between Soviet and Argentine grandmasters - and in all three White won: Keres against Najdorf, Geller against Panno, and Spassky against Pilnik. But Fischer made a new move –''} Rh7 $1 $10 {Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Portoroz izt 1959 Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'he had the better of it when a draw was agreed at move 32. Fischer/PS: '=!' Fischer/PS: 'Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Portoroz izt 1959'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Portoroz izt 1959 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and after''} 14. Qg6 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Garry Kasparov} ({later} 14. O-O+ {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Garry Kasparov} Kg8 15. g6 Rg7 16. Rf7 Bxh4 17. Qxh6 {was played, but here too Black parried the attack by} Rxf7 18. gxf7+ Kxf7 19. Rf1+ Bf6) 14... Rf7 15. Qxh6+ Kg8 16. Qg6+ Rg7 17. Qxe6+ Kh8 18. Bxd7 Nxd7 19. O-O-O Ne5 {he gained a draw.}) 9. O-O-O Nbd7 10. Be2 $5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'A novelty, aimed at surprising and confusing his young opponent.''} ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'and Walther played'} 10. Bd3 {Walther,E-Fischer,R Zürich 1959 Andrew Soltis: '(Fischer/PS) Walther,E-Fischer,R Zürich 1959' Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!?' Ftacnik,Lubomir: '(Garry Kasparov) (this move was later revived by Spassky in the 15th game of the 1972 match)'}) (10. g4 {CT 1959: Gligoric,S-Fischer,R; Smyslov,V-Fischer,R Andrew Soltis: '(Fischer/PS) Gligoric,S-Fischer,R Beograd ct 1959 Smyslov,V-Fischer,R Beograd ct 1959'} b5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir} 11. Bxf6 {, after which Bobby tried both the usual} Nxf6 ({and the audacious} 11... gxf6 $5) 12. g5 Nd7 13. a3 Bb7 ({and in the end he arrived at the most logical way of creating counterplay –} 13... Rb8 $1 {.})) ({In Zürich Tal played} 10. Qg3 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) against Fischer}) 10... b5 (10... h6 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) Garry Kasparov} 11. Bh4 b5 {is also interesting; for example:} 12. e5 Bb7 13. exf6 Bxf3 14. Bxf3 Bxf6 15. Bxf6 Rc8 $1 16. Bxg7 Rh7 {with sharp play (Kliavins-Tal, Riga 1959).}) 11. Bxf6 Nxf6 ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'and''} 11... Bxf6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'on account of''} 12. Bxb5 $1 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '(although after''} O-O $1 {(Ftacnik,Lubomir) (Garry Kasparov) the open b-file may compensate Black for his material deficit).}) ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer rejected both''} 11... gxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '?!''} 12. Qh5 Nb6 (12... O-O $2 13. Rd3) 13. a3 $16 { f4-f5 Ftacnik,Lubomir: ' f4-f5 Garry Kasparov: 'followed by f4-f5''}) 12. e5 $5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Here is the surprise.''} ({Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '"On''} 12. a3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} Rb8 $1 $17 { b5-b4 Ftacnik,Lubomir: ' b5-b4 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'followed by ...b5-b4 gives good counterplay." (Fischer)''}) 12... Bb7 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'What else?''} 13. exf6 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!?'' Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: '!''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'The soul of the "fishing trip variation". White is worse after'} 13. Qg3 dxe5 14. fxe5 Nd7 15. Qxg7 (15. Bf3 {Andrew Soltis} O-O) 15... Qxe5 $17 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '''}) 13... Bxf3 14. Bxf3 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: 'Garry Kasparov: 'This is the point of the cunning queen sacrifice: Black has two pieces attacked and it is not at all easy for him to make the correc