{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 13 Something new.  During the mid-fifties, Gligorich, Reshevsky, and Najdorf were considered the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters. Within a few years Fischer managed to surpass them. However, in so doing, he succeeded in beating Gligorich only once - up to 1966 (see game 56). On the occasion of this first win, Fischer employs a novel attacking system (13 Qe2) against the Dragon Variation. Gligorich fails to react vigorously enough and makes the mistake of castling too soon, thereby exposing himself to the same type of sacrificial combination that demolished Larsen in game 2. ' Andrew Soltis: 'By 1959 Fischer had become good friends with Svetozar Gligoric, the dean of Yugoslav players, who was 20 years his senior. But he wasn't entirely impressed with his skill. In that 1964 list of the 10 greatest players of all time he included only three contemporary players, Boris Spassky, Mikhail Tal and Sammy Reshevsky. But in 1970, when he was asked on a Yugoslav TV show if he had changed his list, Fischer revised it to include Gligoric, as well as Tigran Petrosian and Mikhail Botwinnik.'} 1. e4 {Bulletin Bulletin Bulletin Bobby Fischer Bulletin: 'Chris Ward' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 ({So for a game appearing on this Dragons site this game does feature a pretty weird move order but it is sort of plausible that the same middlegame position could be reached via slightly normal means e.g.} 2... d6 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. f3 Nc6 7. Bc4 Bd7 8. Bb3 {and now} Na5 {rather than 8...Bg7. Okay so I could try to justify prioritising some of those moves over others but I'm certainly not going to try to pull the wool over your eyes! What is true is that this strictly speaking 'B57' encounter struggles to fit into a serious opening classification but lacking in slow play games in this current World situation, I thought we'd get retro and maybe occasionally feature the odd Bobby Fischer or other all time greats game.}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 {Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation.''} 6. Bc4 {Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square.'' Garry Kasparov: '"Fischer's universal weapon against nearly all forms of the Sicilian Defence." (Gligoric)'} ({Most popular here is} 6. Bg5 {which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as'} g6 7. Bxf6 exf6 {isn't great for the pawn structure!}) 6... Bd7 ({Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap Bobby Fischer: 'Recently in a skittles game someone tried' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap'' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's last is a finesse to reach a Dragon position : After 7.Be3 Fischer would be barred by 7. ...Ng4! from playing the system he used against Larsen. Black can't play' Garry Kasparov: 'The immediate'} 6... g6 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '!?' Bobby Fischer: 'against me. The game continued:' Bulletin: '!?' '!?' '!?' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '?'' Andrew Soltis: 'without risking Fischer/PS: '!?'' Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'is not good on account of'} 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. e5 $1 {, especially bad in the case of Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!' Chris Ward: ', especially bad in the case of'' Andrew Soltis: 'which Reuben Fine overlooked in his book on the 1972 Fischer-Spassky match.' Garry Kasparov: '(Gligoric)'} dxe5 $4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Garry Kasparov: '?'} (8... Nh5 $2 {(Bobby Fischer) (correct is Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} 9. Qf3 $1 e6 (9... d5 10. Nxd5 $1) 10. g4 Ng7 11. Ne4 Qa5+ (11... d5 12. Nf6+ Ke7 13. Qa3+) 12. Bd2 Qxe5 13. Bc3 {Black resigns. 1-0; Fischer,R-Amateur}) (8... Ng4 $8 {(Bobby Fischer) NOT Bulletin: '' '' '' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'}) (8... Nd7 9. exd6 exd6 10. O-O Ne5 11. Be2 Be6 12. Re1 {with the initiative for White.}) 9. Bxf7+ $1 {Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'winning the Queen - that was another skittles game!)' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!'' Andrew Soltis: '+-'}) ({Of course} 6... e6 {blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now! Bulletin: '(Chris Ward) blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now!' Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.51)'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 6... Qb6 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 11. Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959 Bulletin: 'Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959' Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.57) is better, but Black wants to fianchetto his king's bishop and transpose into one of the varieties of the Dragon Variation.'} 7. Nb3 (7. Nde2 e6 8. O-O Be7 9. Bb3 O-O 10. Kh1 Na5 11. Bg5 Qc5 $1 12. f4 b5 13. Ng3 Nxb3 $1 (13... b4 $2 {Fischer,R-Benko,P Bled ct 1959}) 14. axb3 Bb7 (14... b4) 15. Nh5 Kh8 $11) 7... e6 8. Bf4 Ne5 9. Be2 Bd7 (9... Be7 10. Be3 Qc7 11. f4 Nc4 12. Nb5 Qc6 13. Bxc4 Qxc4 14. Qd3 Qc6 15. N3d4 Qd7 16. O-O-O a6 17. Nc3 b5 18. a3 Bb7 $10 {Lastin,A-Lukin,A RUS-ch Elista (06) 1995 1-0 52 - CBM 50}) 10. O-O (10. a4 Be7 11. a5 Qc7 12. a6 O-O 13. O-O Rfb8 $15 {½ Kasparov,G-Kramnik,V Horgen CS (09) 1995 - CBM 50}) 10... Be7 11. a4 O-O 12. a5 Qc7 13. Qd4 Bc6 14. a6 b6 15. Qe3 Ng6 16. Bg3 e5 17. Rad1 Rad8 18. f4 exf4 19. Bxf4 Nxf4 20. Qxf4 $10 {Rublevsky,S-Svidler,P Novosibirsk (07) 1995 ½ 25 - CBM 50}) 7. Bb3 {Garry Kasparov: 'Bobby is aiming to take play into 'his' set-up,'} ({A useful retreat waiting for Black to commit himself. Although} 7. f3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} e6 {is a plausible sequence, certainly} 8. Ndb5 {is well met by} Qb8 $1 {and frequently against such set ups Fisher favoured challenging Black's structure through f4-f5.}) (7. Be3 {(Bobby Fischer) is met by Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} Ng4 $1 {On Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} 8. Nxc6 bxc6 9. O-O Nxe3 10. fxe3 e6 11. Qf3 Qf6 12. Qxf6 gxf6 13. Rxf6 Bg7 14. Rf2 Rb8 15. Nd1 Ke7 $44 {Landenbergue,C-Kortschnoj,V Bern Training (1) 1992 0-1 27}) (7. Bg5 {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} e6 8. Bxf6 $2 Qxf6 9. Ndb5 O-O-O 10. Nxd6+ Kb8 $19 {with a winning attack Also strong is Gligoric,S (GLIGORICH) Bulletin: '; Gligoric,S'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'although since the time of the old game Vidmar-Bernstein (San Sebastian 1911)'} 7. O-O {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} g6 8. Nxc6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'was considered more unpleasant for Black; for example:'} Bxc6 (8... bxc6 9. f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'A.Sokolov-Sax, Brussels 1988'}) 9. Bg5 Bg7 10. Nd5 $1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} Bxd5 11. exd5 O-O 12. Re1 Rc8 13. Bb3 Qd7 14. c3 Rc7 15. h3 Rfc8 16. Qd3 Kf8 17. Rad1 Ng8 18. Re2 Bf6 {, gradually neutralizing the opponent's pressure (Razuvaev-Makarychev, Tbilisi 1973).}) (7. Nxc6 {Fischer/PS} bxc6 8. O-O e6 9. Qe2 Be7 10. b3 d5 11. Bd3 d4 12. Na4 c5 13. Nb2 Bc6 14. Nc4 Qc7 15. f4 Nd7 16. Bd2 Rc8 17. a4 O-O $10 {Hernandez,Gi-Kortschnoj,V Moscow olm (09) MEX-SUI 1994 0-1 72 - CBM 45}) 7... g6 {As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack! Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack!''} 8. f3 ({Upon the immediate Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon the immediate''} 8. Be3 {Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in'' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} Ng4 $5 {Instead White puts paid to that option. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!?' Chris Ward: 'Instead White puts paid to that option.''} 9. Nxc6 {Bobby Fischer} bxc6 (9... Nxe3 $2 10. Bxf7+ $1) 10. Qf3 (10. Bxa7 $2 c5) 10... Ne5 11. Qg3 $16 {Andrew Soltis: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Bg7 12. O-O-O O-O 13. h4 c5 14. h5 c4 15. Ba4 Bxa4 16. Nxa4 Qa5 17. Nc3 Nd3+ 18. Rxd3 cxd3 19. Bd2 Rfc8 20. hxg6 hxg6 {½-½ Staniszewski,P-Bielczyk,J/Bielsko Biala 1988/EXT 88b}) (8. h3 $146 {Fischer/PS} Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bg7 10. O-O O-O 11. Qd3 b5 $1 12. a3 (12. Nxb5 $6 Nxe4 13. Qxe4 Bxb5 14. Re1 e6 $15 {[%cal Gg7b2]}) 12... a5 13. Bg5 Rb8 14. Rfe1 a4 (14... b4 15. axb4 axb4 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Re8 18. Ra2 $14) 15. Ba2 b4 16. axb4 Rxb4 $11 {Short,N-Kortschnoj,V/Horgen 1995}) 8... Na5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Another attempt to confuse the young grandmaster. "Releasing the central tension this way is wrong," Fischer rightly thinks.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Releasing the central tension this way is wrong. Correct is' Garry Kasparov: '"Correct is'} 8... Nxd4 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '>=' Bulletin: ' Chris Ward: '!?'' Andrew Soltis: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} 9. Qxd4 Bg7 {is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is'' Garry Kasparov: ', but after'} 10. Bg5 {Bobby Fischer: '' Bobby Fischer: 'White still keeps control.' Bulletin: '' '' '' Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'White still keeps control."'} {Garry Kasparov: '. Indeed, after'} O-O {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} ({Garry Kasparov: '. Therefore Black must play more energetically –'} 10... Qa5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 11. Qd2 Rc8 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({but not} 11... O-O 12. O-O-O Be6 13. Nd5 $1 Qxd2+ 14. Rxd2 Bxd5 15. exd5 {... 1-0 Korchnoi-Panno, Havana 1969}) {Garry Kasparov: 'nothing is achieved with the timid'} 12. O-O (12. O-O-O $1 Rxc3 $1 13. bxc3 Nxe4 $5 ({or} 13... O-O 14. Kb1 Re8 15. Bh6 Bxh6 16. Qxh6 Qxc3 17. Qd2 Qc7 {, De Firmian-Makarychev, Oslo 1984}) 14. fxe4 Bxc3 15. Bxf7+ Kxf7 16. Qd5+ Qxd5 17. exd5 Bb5 {and Black has reasonable compensation for the exchange.}) 12... Be6 13. Bh6 Bxh6 14. Qxh6 Qh5 {Garry Kasparov: 'with equality, Renet-Korchnoi, Swiss Team Ch. 1995'} 15. Qxh5 Nxh5 $10 {Renet,O-Kortschnoj,V SUI-chT (03) 1995 ½ 26 - EXP 49}) (10... Z0) 11. Qd2 (11. O-O-O h6 12. Bh4 Bc6 13. Qe3 $1 {he has an unpleasant initiative:} Nd7 14. Rxd6 $5 g5 15. Rg6 $1 e6 16. Rxg7+ Kxg7 17. Bf2 a5 18. a4 Qf6 19. Kb1 b6 20. h4 Nc5 {(Tal-Stein, 37th USSR Championship, Moscow 1969) and after a highly interesting battle this game ended in a draw.}) 11... b5 12. Bh6 a5 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. a4 b4 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 Rc8 {with chances for both sides in Amonatov,F (2636)-Li,C  (2732) Riadh 2017.}) ({The obvious 8...Bg7 sees us most likely headed for a 9 Bc4 Yugoslav Attack but intriguing is that Gligoric (back then consid