d. Apparently the feeling was that I had just blundered. The more obvious} 23. f5 {(Bobby Fischer) looks good, but Black still has defensive resources with Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} exf5 24. Rxh5 ({(if} 24. Nd5 Qd8 25. exf5 Bxf5 26. Rxh5 $2 Rxc2 $1 $19 {wins for Black) Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 24... gxh5 25. Nf4 (25. Z0) ({. Also not} 25. g6 $6 Be6 $1 26. Na4 Qb4 27. Nxc5 Rxc5 {.}) ({. However, as Hübner points out, White has the much stronger} 25. Qxh5 $1 Be6 26. Bxe6 fxe6 27. Qg6 $1 Z0 (27... Qc7 28. Rh1 $1 Qe7 (28... Qf7 $2 {loses to} 29. Rh8+ $1) 29. Nf4 Qe8 30. Qh7+ Kf8 31. Rh6 $1 Bxh6 32. Qxh6+ Ke7 (32... Kg8 33. Nh5) 33. Qxe6+ {, when the queen and knight make short work of the black king (I would mention that after} Kf8 {White wins most quickly by} 34. Qh6+ {). "Fischer's treatment of this position is characteristic," thinks Hübner. "His feeling for the initiative did not betray him, when he was pondering upon the move 23 f5. But because even his calculating skills failed to bring clarity to the incalculable mess of variations, which this sacrifice would have resulted in, he preferred a safe move, as he did not like giving material on a speculative basis. However, he underestimates the defensive resources of the second player after the text-move. As will be shown in the following note, Black can obtain sufficient counterplay... Therefore, the move 23 Qd3 does not deserve an exclamation but a question mark."}) ({. And yet Fischer's intuition did not betray him! The b3-bishop has been removed from the board, but the bishop at g7 has remained, helping Black to set up a defence. Instead of 27...Qc7? he should play} 27... Re5 $1 28. Nf4 d5 $1 {; for example:} 29. Z0 (29. Nh5 Qc7 30. exd5 Qf7 $1) (29. Nd3 Kf8 30. exd5 (30. Nxd5 Qc6 {with equality}) 30... Re3 31. Na4 Qd6 32. dxe6 Qxe6) (29. Rh1 Kf8 $1 30. exf5 (30. Nh5 Qc7 31. exf5 Qf7 $1) 30... exf5 31. Qxb6 ({or} 31. Qh7 Rce8 32. Ng6+ Kf7 33. Nxe5+ Rxe5 34. Rf1 Qe6 {etc}) 31... axb6 32. Ng6+ Kf7 33. Nxe5+ Bxe5 34. Nxd5 Rg8 {with a draw in the ending}) (29. Ka1 Kf8 30. exd5 exd5 31. Qh7 Rce8 32. Ng6+ (32. a3 $5) 32... Kf7 33. Nxe5+ Rxe5 34. Qh5+ Kf8 35. Qh2 d4 $1 36. Nb1 Qc5 {. In all these sharp variations, forcing play does not bring White any obvious benefits, and so Fischer justifiably chose a safer move.}))) 25... Rxc3 26. bxc3 Rxc3 27. Qxh5 Rxb3+ 28. cxb3 Qe3 $132 {, etc. Garry Kasparov: 'etc."'} ({In my opinion,} 28... Bc6 $1 29. Nd5 Bxd5 30. Rxd5 Qf2 {is even better.})) 23... Bxc3 {Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn.'' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'A fatal error – exchanging the main defender of the king, which is all the more strange, in that Gligoric realized perfectly well just how ruinous it could be (cf. the note to Black's 10th move). But what should Black play?'} ({Indeed Bobby Fischer: 'The threat against the d-Pawn is awkward to meet. On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Indeed'' Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 23... R5c6 {Garry Kasparov: 'there could have followed'} 24. f5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Andrew Soltis: 'is an improved version of (b) e.g. Fischer/PS: '!'' Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({or} 24. e5 d5 25. f5 $1 {Gligoric}) 24... exf5 25. Rxh5 {might see the neat line Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'might see the neat line'' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!'' Garry Kasparov: '!'} fxe4 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} (25... gxh5 26. exf5 {is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim. Bobby Fischer: '+-' Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing. Or if' Bulletin: '+-' '+-' '+-' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim.'' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} Rxc3 27. Nxc3 Bxc3 28. Qd5 $1 Rf8 29. bxc3 Qc6 30. Qd3 {and wins.}) 26. Qxe4 gxh5 27. Bxf7+ Kxf7 28. g6+ Kg8 29. Qd5+ {and essentially Black is getting mated.}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black burns his bridges because he had no good way to defend d6. For example'} 23... R8c6 {Bobby Fischer Garry Kasparov: '?'} 24. Na4 $18 {wins the exchange. Finally on Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-''}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'And after'} 23... Bf8 {Bobby Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'White decides matters with'} 24. f5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!''} exf5 25. Nd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and then Rxh5! and Nf6+ must win'} Qd8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'if'} 25... fxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Garry Kasparov: ', then'} 26. Qxe4 Bf5 27. Qxf5 $18 {wins a piece) Garry Kasparov: ', winning a piece'}) 26. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 26... Rxd5 27. Bxd5 gxh5 28. exf5 $18) 27. Nf6+ Kg7 28. Qh3 $18 {releases an avalanche. Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer).'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'It only remains to seek counterplay by sacrificing the d6-pawn. This plan, but in an inaccurate formulation, was hit upon by Gligoric, who suggested'} 23... Be8 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '(?), with the idea of'} 24. Qxd6 {Garry Kasparov: '(?)'} ({but this is bad because of} 24. f5 $1 {, when White wins}) 24... Qxd6 25. Rxd6 Bxc3 {with chances of endgame salvation. Garry Kasparov: 'with equality:'} 26. Nxc3 ({or} 26. bxc3 $6 Bc6) 26... Nxf4 {.}) ({Therefore the only continuation was} 23... Bb5 $1 {, to break up the linkage of the knights at c3 and e2, which cements together White's position, and to exchange queens. For example:} 24. Nxb5 ({or} 24. Qxd6 Qxd6 25. Rxd6 Bxc3 26. Nxc3 Nxf4) 24... Qxb5 25. Qxb5 (25. c4 $2 Rxc4 26. f5 Qe5 27. f6 Rxe4 28. Nc3 Re3) 25... Rxb5 26. Z0 (26. Rxd6 Rb4 $1 27. c4 b5 $1 28. cxb5 Rxe4 29. Nc3 Rb4 {and Black is close to equality (pointed out by Hübner).}) ({. In my view, instead of 26 Rxd6, White has the stronger} 26. c3 $1 Rd8 27. Kc2 Rc5 28. Rdf1 {, and although there is still all to play for, he retains some initiative thanks to the greater potential of his bishop. But with the disappearance of its opponent – the bishop at g7, Black's position quickly collapses, the decisive role in this being played precisely by the bishop at b3.} (28. Z0))) 24. Nxc3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Not Garry Kasparov: 'Of course not'} 24. bxc3 {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Garry Kasparov: '?'} Bb5 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 24... Nxf4 {Bobby Fischer: 'This is what the crowd thought I had overlooked.'} 25. Qf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' Garry Kasparov: '!'} Nh5 ({Upon Bobby Fischer: 'On' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Upon'' Andrew Soltis: 'Another defensive "sac"' Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 25... e5 {simplest is Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'simplest is'' Garry Kasparov: ', both'} {Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 26. Ne2 {The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn. Bobby Fischer: '+-!' Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.' Bulletin: '+-!' '+-!' '+-!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn.'' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'since there is no defense to 27.Nxf4 exf4 28. Qxf4 and Qh4 Fischer/PS: '+-!'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer) are sufficient.'} (26. Nd5 {Garry Kasparov: '(Gligoric)'} Rxd5 {would misfire because of})) ({Fischer's notes made no mention of} 25... Rxc3 26. bxc3 (26. Qxf4 $1 {wins outrighrt.}) 26... e5 {probably because}) 26. Rxh5 $1 {Just as strong as ever here. Bobby Fischer: 'I've made this sacrifice so often, I feel like applying for a patent!' Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Just as strong as ever here.'' Garry Kasparov: 'The same combination as in the game with Larsen (Game No.52). "I've made this sacrifice so often, I feel like applying for a patent!" (Fischer)'} gxh5 27. Qxh5 ({The computer's direct pressure on f7 would have been even more quickly decisive –} 27. Rf1 $1 {, and if} Be8 {, then} 28. Bxe6 $1 R8c7 29. g6 $1 {. But for a human it is natural to try and create threats on the h-file.}) 27... Be8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({There can be no running as Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'There can be no running as'' Andrew Soltis: 'Black loses faster after' Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 27... Kf8 28. Qh8+ Ke7 29. Qf6+ Ke8 (29... Kf8 30. Rf1 Be8 31. Bxe6 {and wins.}) 30. Rh1 {demonstrates. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'demonstrates.''} Bb5 {Bobby Fischer} 31. Bxe6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer'} fxe6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} 32. Qxe6+ Kd8 ({OR} 32... Kf8 33. Rh8+ Kg7 34. Qf6# {mate)}) 33. Rh8+ Kc7 34. Rxc8# {mate)}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'It would also not have helped to play'} 27... Rxc3 {Fischer/PS} 28. g6 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Kf8 (28... Qf2 29. bxc3) 29. Rf1 $1 Rf3 30. Qxf3 f5 31. exf5 exf5 32. Qd5 $18 {PS}) ({"If} 27... Ra5 {, with the idea of ...Qd4 or ...Qe3, the intermediate move} 28. e5 $1 {would have won most quickly." (Gligoric).}) 28. Qh6 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bulletin: '!' '!' '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Cutting off king escapes so that 29.g6 or 29.Rh1 must win'} ({Keeping the king trapped is logical although actually} 28. Rh1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} Kf8 29. Bxe6 $1 {would have done the business too i. e.} fxe6 ({Or} 29... Rxc3 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Qf6+ Kf8 32. Rh8#) (29... Ke7 30. Bxc8 Rxc3 31. g6 $1 Rxc8 32. Qg5+ {etc would also have won.}) 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Rh7+ Bf7 ({Or} 31... Kd8 32. Qf6#) 32. Qf6+ Kd7 33. Qxf7+ Kc6 34. Qd7#) 28... Rxc3 29. bxc3 $1 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: '!''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Andrew Soltis: 'More exact than' Garry Kasparov: 'Not'} 29. Rh1 {would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through'' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '?!'' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Qd4 {Bobby Fischer: 'holds out for a while.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'}) ({But Garry Kasparov: 'but there was another possibility:'} 29. g6 $1 {was an alternative win Fischer/PS: 'h7, g7'} Rg3 (29... Rxb3 30. Qh7+ Kf8 31. axb3) 30. Qh7+ Kf8 31. Qh8+ Ke7 32. Qh4+ {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} Kd7 (32... f6 {Fischer/PS} 33. Qxg3 Qc7 (33... Rd8 34. Rh1 Rd7 35. Ba4 $18) (33... Bd7 34. Qh2 {.}) 34. Rh1 $18 {PS}) 33. Qxg3 fxg6 34. Bxe6+ $1 Kxe6 {Fischer/PS} 35. Qg4+ {Garry Kasparov: 'and Qxc8 – Gligoric'} Ke7 36. Qxc8 $18 {Ragozin}) 29... Rxc3 ({White still retains a winning attack after Andrew Soltis: 'Or' Garry Kasparov: 'After the more tenacious'} 29... Qe3 {Bobby Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'White would have own most quickly by'} 30. Rh1 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({if} 30. Bxe6 Rc5) 30... Qxc3 31. g6 Qg7 32. Qh2 $1 $18 {Bronstein,D Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and gxf7+ wins as pointed out by David Bronstein who was much taken by this game. Fischer/PS: '+-!' Fischer/PS: 'Bronstein,D'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(pointed out by both Bronstein and Gligoric)'} Qe5 ({or} 32... Qf6 33. g7 Qxg7 34. Rg1) 33. Qh7+ Kf8 34. Rg1 $1 Qg7 35. Bxe6 $1 {.}) 30. g6 $1 fxg6 ({The threat was Qh7+ followed by g7+ whilst} 30... Rg3 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward'} 31. Qh7+ Kf8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qh4+ {simply picks up the rook.}) 31. Rh1 {Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7. Bulletin: 'Chris Ward: 'Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7.''} Qd4 32. Qh7+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'Again a mistake!' Andrew Soltis: 'As in the Larsen game, White missed a faster mate.'} 32. Bxe6+ {Bobby Fischer: 'mates more quickly. Black resigns' Bulletin: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: ''' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer) would have mated two moves more quickly – this bishop deserved the right to land the decisive blow!'} Bf7 {Bulletin: 'Chris Ward' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS'} 33. Qh7+ Kf8 34. Qxf7# {equally does the job although Black resigns now anyway as Rf1+ will soon be mating.}) 32... Kf8 {Fischer/PS} 33. Rf1+ Qf2 34. Rxf2+ Rf3 35. Rxf3+ Bf7 36. Qxf7# {1-0 (36) Fischer,R-Gligoric,S Bled/Zagreb/Belgrade 1959 [Andrew Soltis]} 1-0

