{Andrew Soltis: 'By 1959 Fischer's games had already made an impressionon opening theory - and not in ancient lines such as the Bishop's Gambit and Two Knights Defense 9.Nh3 which he would revive in the 1960s - but in cutting edge variations. The folllowing was only the first grandmaster game featuring Pal Benko's antidote to 6.Bc4. Yet Black's crushing defeat threatened to push 6. ...Qb6 into retirement, a year after it had been introduced.' Demuth,Adrien: 'A few months after starting to play chess, I received a book for my 8th birthday. It was "My 60 best games" by Bobby Fischer. I read it a couple of times, with my father. Of course none of us were able to understand all the subtleties, but we were already sensible to the beauty of most of the games. I am still a huge fan of his play, and I am glad to comment one of his games now! This game of Bobby Fischer, was played during his first Candidates tournament, in 1959. At only 16, Fischer managed to get very nice wins in this super strong tournament. This game against Pal Benko, shows his natural tactical skills. '} {Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 11 Unheard melodies.  Paul Morphy is the idol of all the romantics who pine for the swashbuckling chess of yesteryear; but it is rarely possible to succeed with that kind of flamboyance any longer since players now are more evenly matched. A rise in the standard of defense has necessitated a corresponding adjustment in the character of attack. In modern chess most of the beauty resides in the annotations. Brilliancies often exist only as grace notes - because the opposition anticipates and thwarts them with appropriate rejoinders. To the uninitiated, some of the most hard-fought struggles seem devoid of all bravura. That is the situation in this game. Confj-onted with a dozen beautiful outlandish losing variations, Benko chooses what appears to be a prosaic one. Is this "ugliness" not a by-product of skill? Though the reader may feel cheated, and the winner fj-ustrated, does it not argue for the perspicacity of the loser who sidestepped those seductive invitations?'} 1. e4 {Demuth,Adrien Bobby Fischer} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bc4 {Demuth,Adrien: 'Fischer is facing a Classical Sicilian. He always has been fond of the Sozin Attack, that is caracterised by 6.c4 (and can be played against several types of Sicilian. It's not a surprise to see that it was already his favourite here.'} Qb6 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'Benko first used this move at the 1957 Hungarian Championship and used it one other time, at Portoroz, before this game.' Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'For' Garry Kasparov: 'than'} 6... Bd7 {Fischer,R-Gligoric,S Bled ct 1959 Bobby Fischer: 'see game 13 (Fischer,R-Gligoric,S; Bled ct, 1959). By putting immediate pressure on the center, Black forces the Knight to a passive post.' Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.56). "In each game some surprise awaited Fischer. The young grandmaster did not always successfully parry them, but he always remained true to himself." (Ragozin) A rapid ascent in chess undoubtedly generates a number of problems, one of which is the amount and quality of knowledge that is required for a young talent to be able to compete successfully in super-tournaments. An opening repertoire, which has recently served him faithfully in middle-ranking events, proves completely inadequate for play against highly-experienced grandmasters. In my time I was greatly helped by intensive sessions with trainers – Alexander Nikitin and Alexander Shakarov, and also consultations with Botvinnik himself. But Fischer always worked practically alone. At that time his repertoire was still very restricted, and each of the participants in the Candidates tournament in Yugoslavia tried to exploit this (Bobby's problems playing White against the Caro-Kann Defence have already been mentioned). Thus for his inexperienced opponent Benko had saved up this queen thrust, unusual for those times, which was later regularly employed by Stein, and in the 1990s taken up by Kramnik.'}) ({A new, more interesting way of avoiding the usual variations with} 6... e6 {(Game No. 51)}) 7. Nde2 {Garry Kasparov: 'As subsequent experience has shown, this is not the best move, although Fischer associates it with an original plan of an attack on the king.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'For 7 Nb3 see game 58, note to Black's sixth move. No good is'} 7. Ndb5 a6 ({or the less hackneyed} 7... Bg4 $5 {, Topalov-Kramnik, Linares 1998}) 8. Be3 Qa5 9. Nd4 Nxe4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'} (9... e6 {Demuth,Adrien This is now considered to be the best.} 10. O-O Be7 11. Bb3 O-O 12. f4 Bd7 (12... Qc7 13. Qf3 {leads to a position from the Sozin Attack}) 13. f5 Nxd4 14. Bxd4 exf5 15. exf5 Bc6 16. Qd3 Rae8 17. Rad1 Nd7 ({not} 17... Bd8 $6 18. Kh1 Nd7 19. Qg3 Bf6 20. Bd5 $1 {De Firmian-Kramnik, Erevan Olympiad 1996}) 18. Qg3 Bf6 19. Qxd6 Bxd4+ 20. Qxd4 Nf6 21. Nd5 Bxd5 22. Bxd5 Rd8 23. c4 {1/2-1/2 (Short-Kramnik, Novgorod 1996).}) (9... Ng4 $5 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. Bd2 g6 {is also played}) ({, but} 9... Ne5 {is questionable:} 10. Nb3 Qc7 11. Be2 {Ivanchuk-Kramnik, Paris rapidplay 1995}) 10. Qf3 $1 {Demuth,Adrien} Nf6 (10... f5 $6 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. O-O-O $1 $16) 11. Nxc6 Qc7 $8 12. Nxe7 Bxe7 13. Bb3 $16) ({Bobby Fischer: 'And..'} 7. Nxc6 $5 bxc6 {Bobby Fischer: 'only helps Black strengthen his center, but after'} 8. O-O $13 {Bobby Fischer: 'White has promising tactical chances.'} Z0 (8... g6 9. e5 (9. Qe2 Ng4 $5) 9... dxe5 10. Qe2 Qd4 11. Be3 Qd6 12. Rad1 Qc7 13. f4 Bg4 14. Qf2 e4 15. Rde1 Bf5 16. h3 h5 {with equality  (Topalov-Kramnik, Novgorod 1997).}) (8... e6 9. Z0 (9. b3 Be7 10. Bb2 O-O 11. Qe2 e5 12. Kh1 Qc7 13. Rae1 Nd7 14. Na4 Bb7 15. Bd3 Rfe8 16. c4 Bg5 17. Qc2 h6 18. b4 {with a minimal plus for White, which he masterfully converted into a win (Karpov-Stein, 39th USSR Championship, Leningrad 1971).}) ({. Incidentally, Fischer played} 9. Bf4 {, which demonstrates the difference in the styles of the two great players:} Qxb2 10. Qd3 Qb4 11. Rab1 Qc5 12. Rfd1 e5 13. Bg5 Be7 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Ne2 Be6 16. Bxe6 fxe6 17. Rb7 Kf7 18. Rdb1 d5 19. Ng3 h5 20. h4 a5 21. Qf3 Kg6 $2 (21... Qxc2 22. Nxh5 f5 {is correct}) 22. R1b3 a4 23. Rc3 Qd6 24. Nxh5 f5 25. exf5+ exf5 26. Rxe7 {and wins (Fischer-R.Byrne, New York blitz 1971).}))) (7. Nb3 e6 8. O-O (8. Z0) ({. When the methods of defence against such attacks had been refined, White began playing} 8. Bf4 Ne5 9. Be2 {; for example:} Z0 (9... Be7 10. Be3 Qc7 11. f4 Nc6 12. Bf3 a6 13. O-O O-O 14. a4 b6 15. g4 Rb8 16. g5 Nd7 17. Bg2 Re8 18. Rf3 {(Kasparov-Anand, Linares 1994) and the minor differences from the usual Scheveningen (Karpov-Kasparov, 24th matchgame, Moscow 1985) – white king at g1 (instead of h1) and queen at d1 (instead of d2) – turn out to favour White.}) (9... Bd7 10. Be3 (10. a4 Be7 11. a5 Qc7 12. a6 O-O 13. O-O Rfb8 {1/2-1/2 Kasparov-Kramnik, Horgen 1995}) 10... Qc7 11. Nb5 Bxb5 12. Bxb5+ Nc6 13. Qf3 a6 14. Bd3 d5 $1 {with equality (J.Polgar-Kramnik, Dortmund 1996). For the moment the overall statistics of this variation favour Black: for every four wins by him, there are only three for White. True, by no means all the results of the games stem from the outcome of the opening, and so one can talk only about a tendency, which confirms Fischer's old words: "There is no direct refutation of Benko's audacious move 6...Qb6."})) 8... Be7 9. Be3 Qc7 10. f4 O-O 11. Bd3 $13 {Fischer,R-Saidy,A/ch-USA 1966} (11. g4 $2 d5 $1) 11... a6 12. g4 {(a standard attack)} b5 13. g5 Ne8 (13... Nd7 {is safer}) 14. Qh5 g6 $6 ({it is also possible to avoid this weakening:} 14... Nb4 15. Rac1 Bb7) 15. Qh6 f5 $6 16. exf5 gxf5 17. Nd4 {... 1-0 (Fischer-Saidy, New York 1966/67).}) 7... e6 8. O-O {Demuth,Adrien: 'In the Sozin Attack, White decides to castle to the kingside, and he will try to advance with f2-f4 later.'} Be7 9. Bb3 O-O ({Black can also delay the evacuation of his king, to retain the possibility of hiding it on the other wing:} 9... a6 $5 10. Bg5 Qc7 11. Ng3 $6 b5 12. Kh1 $6 h5 $1 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. Nxh5 Bb7 15. Ng3 O-O-O {with good play for the pawn  (Kasparov-Timman, Manila Olympiad 1992).}) 10. Kh1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Later other moves were also tried, but without particular success.' Demuth,Adrien: 'White can't really do without this move.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 10. Be3 Qc7 11. f4 {Demuth,Adrien: '?!'} (11. Ng3 Rd8 12. f4 d5 13. e5 Nd7 14. Nb5 Qa5 15. Nd4 Nc5 16. c3 Nxb3 17. axb3 Qc7 18. Qg4 Kh8 19. Nh5 Rg8 20. Rf3 f5 21. exf6 Bxf6 22. Nxc6 bxc6 23. Rg3 Qf7 24. Nxf6 gxf6 25. Qxg8+ Qxg8 26. Rxg8+ Kxg8 27. Rxa7 Rxa7 {½-½ Nilsson,N-Tischbierek,R/Copenhagen op 1991}) 11... Ng4 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: '!' Demuth,Adrien: ''!'' Demuth,Adrien: 'and White more or less has to give up his bishop.'}) (10. Bg5 Rd8 11. Kh1 Ne5 $6 (11... Na5 $14) 12. f4 Neg4 13. Qe1 $16 Qc5 14. Nc1 $1 h6 15. Nd3 Qc7 (15... Qe3 16. Bh4 Qxe1 17. Raxe1 $16) 16. Bh4 Ne8 $8 17. Bg3 $1 a6 18. Qe2 Ngf6 19. f5 exf5 (19... e5 20. Nb4 $16) 20. e5 $1 dxe5 21. Nxe5 $18 {Wahls,M-Miralles,G/Luzern 1989} Be6 22. Nxf7 $1 $18) 10... Na5 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'The desire to eliminate the powerful bishop is logical,' Demuth,Adrien: 'Suppressing this annoying bishop is a very common plan, even if it takes a couple of tempi.'} ({although} 10... Ng4 11. Qe1 Bh4 {(Nunn)}) ({or} 10... Rd8 {with the idea of ...d6-d5 also has its point.}) 11. Bg5 {Andrew Soltis: 'This points out a tactical flaw in ...Qb6, ...Na5. Black can't play 11. ...Bd7? because 12.e5! dxe5? 13.Bxf6 hangs the bishop'} {Garry Kasparov: '"A finesse aimed at provoking'} Qc5 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: ''!'' Andrew Soltis: 'And this solves the problem while preparing ...b5-b4'} 12. f4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A finesse aimed at provoking'} 12. Be3 {Andrew Soltis: '?!'} Qc7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'after which White's initiative is blunted.' Andrew Soltis: '=' Garry Kasparov: 'after which White's initiative is blunted." (Fischer)'}) 12... b5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Another virtue of Black's last Inove was that it freed this Pawn.'} 13. Ng3 {Demuth,Adrien: 'This is the first really important moment of the game. Black should have gotten rid of the light-squares bishop.'} b4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' Demuth,Adrien: '?!' Demuth,Adrien: ''?'' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer, and after him Nunn in the Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings, and Nikitin and I in our monograph on the Sicilian Defence – we all attached a question mark to this move!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Gligorich suggests simply' Andrew Soltis: 'Also good is'} 13... Bb7 {Gligoric,S Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'Gligoric,S''} 14. Nh5 b4 ({or} 14... Kh8 {he has a perfectly sound position.})) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Also satisfactory is' Andrew Soltis: 'Since this opening was virtually being invented at the time, one can appreciate Fischer's common sense : He doesn't get fancy but just coordinates and protects his pieces and pawns (Bb3, Bg5/f2-f4) and directs then at the kingside (Ne2-g3 rather than 7.Nb3). Nevertheless, he later concluded that' Garry Kasparov: 'But the immediate'} 13... Nxb3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!'' Garry Kasparov: ', restricting White's choice, is even better; for example:'} 14. axb3 (14. cxb3 {Demuth,Adrien In these kinds of Sicilian, it's very usual to see White taking with the c-pawn (while the common principles tell us to use the a-pawn). The point is to have some play on the c-file.} b4 15. e5 (15. Bxf6 Bxf6 16. e5 bxc3 17. Ne4 Qb4 18. Nxf6+ gxf6 19. Qg4+ Kh8 20. Qh4 $11 {Forcing a perpetual check, but nothin more.}) 15... dxe5 16. Bxf6 bxc3 $1 17. Ne4 Qb4 18. bxc3 Qxe4 19. Bxe7 Re8 20. Bd6 Bb7 $132 {The battle is very tensed and approximately equal.}) 14... Bb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'would equalize'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or..' Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 14... b4) 15. Nh5 Kh8 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'The text exposes Black to a vicious attack.' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '='' Garry Kasparov: '(assessed as equal by Fischer)'} 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Nxf6 gxf6 18. Qd2 Rg8 19. Rf2 Rg4 {with a perfectly good game. In Fischer's opinion, "The text exposes Black to a vicious attack", but, as we will see, even after it his fate was still altogether unclear.}) (13... h6 $2 {Demuth,Adrien As often, this is too slow.} 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. e5 dxe5 16. Nce4 Qe7 17. Nh5 $18 {White has too many threats, Black can't handle it.}) ({and even} 13... Kh8) 14. e5 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: '!?' Demuth,Adrien: ''!'' Andrew Soltis: 'This opens e4 up to White's knight and invites Black into 14. ...bxc3 15.exf6 Bxf6 16.Bxf6 gxf6 17.Ne4 Qf5 18.Nxd6 with a crushing initiaitive e.g. 18. ...Qg6 19. Rf3 Kh8 20.Rg3 Qh6 21.Qg4!, threatening 22. Qg8+!. No better is 15. ...gxf6 16.Bh6 f5 17.Nh5!'} (14. Bxf6 $5 {Demuth,Adrien This was also a possible move order.} gxf6 15. Na4 Qc7 16. Qh5 $40) 14... dxe5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A.) It's too late now for'} 14... Nxb3 {Demuth,Adrien: '??' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is now too late in view of'} 15. exf6 gxf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 15... Bxf6 16. Nce4) (15... Nxa1 16. fxe7 Re8 17. Nce4 {and wins.}) 16. Bh6 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'B.)'} 14... bxc3 {Demuth,Adrien: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is also bad:'} 15. exf6 Bxf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 15... gxf6 16. Bh6 f5 17. Nh5 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'threatening to bring the Queen to g3)' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} (17. Rf3 $18 {Demuth,Adrien}) 17... cxb2 18. Rb1 Bh4 {(otherwise the manoeuvre of the queen to g3 is decisive)} 19. Qd3 Nxb3 {(too late!)} 20. Qh3 $1) (15... Z0) 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Ne4 $1 Qf5 18. Nxd6 Qg6 19. Rf3 $18 {, Judovic,A Bobby Fischer: 'with a decisive attack . Judovic,A' Garry Kasparov: '(Yudovich)'} ({or simply} 19. bxc3 {.}) 19... Kh8 20. Rg3 Qh6 21. Qg4 { g8+! +- Bobby Fischer: '(threatening Qg8+! +-'} Qg6 22. Qh4) 15. Bxf6 {Garry Kasparov: 'The critical moment of the game: what to take and with what?' Demuth,Adrien: 'Once again, Black has a vast choice. It wasn't easy, and he couldn't find the best move.'} ({Here} 15. Nce4 {fails to} Qd4) 15... gxf6 {Demuth,Adrien: '?' Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'The first, but already decisive mistake – to lose in a sharp Sicilian, in contrast to the 'Spanish torture', Black does not have to make many mistakes! However, Fischer does not attach any mark to this move, regarding Black's position as already bad.' Demuth,Adrien: 'After this move, Fischer won't give another chance.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'A.) On..' Andrew Soltis: 'Step two in the combination :' Garry Kasparov: 'Indeed, it was also poor to play'} 15... Bxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'is refuted by'} 16. Nce4 Qe7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 16... Qd4 {Demuth,Adrien: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?' Demuth,Adrien: 'This is losing by force, and the finish is really elegant.'} 17. Nxf6+ gxf6 18. Qg4+ Kh8 19. Rad1 {Demuth,Adrien: '!'} Qxb2 {Demuth,Adrien: '?'} 20. Nh5 Rg8 21. Qxg8+ {Demuth,Adrien: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!'} Kxg8 22. Rd8# {Bobby Fischer: 'Mate.'}) (16... Qc6 17. Nxf6+ gxf6 18. Qg4+ Kh8 19. Qh4) 17. Nh5 $1 Kh8 {Garry Kasparov: '(?)'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..' Garry Kasparov: '. Black has a far more tenacious defence:'} 17... Bh4 18. fxe5 { g4, Gligoric,S Bobby Fischer: 'followed by Qg4, Gligoric,S' Garry Kasparov: '(and, as pointed out by Gligoric, 19 Qg4, however...)'} Kh8 $1 (18... Nxb3 $2 19. Nhf6+ $1) {; for example:} 19. Z0 (19. Qg4 f5 $1 20. exf6 Bxf6 21. Nexf6 gxf6 22. Qh4 Nxb3 23. axb3 Bb7 $1 24. c3 (24. Nxf6 $6 Rg8 $1) 24... Rad8 {.}) (19. Nhf6 $5 Bb7 20. Qh5 Bxe4 21. Nxe4 Nxb3 22. axb3 f5 23. Nd6 {and although with such a powerful knight White's chances are better, for the moment there is no question of him winning.})) (17... Z0) 18. Nexf6 (18. Nhxf6 gxf6 19. fxe5 fxe5) 18... gxf6 19. fxe5 fxe5 20. Nf6 { h5 +- Bobby Fischer: 'threatening Qh5 +- Wins.' Demuth,Adrien: '+-' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} Rg8 {(otherwise 21 Qh5)} 21. Nxg8 Kxg8 22. Qf3 Bb7 23. Qg3+ Kf8 24. Qxe5 {and White wins.}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'B.) The best chance is' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer said Black could put up stiffer resistance with' Garry Kasparov: 'I would add that in the event of'} 15... bxc3 $1 16. Ne4 (16. Bxe5 $5 {Demuth,Adrien} cxb2 17. Bxb2 Bb7 18. Qg4 g6 $1 $15 {This may look very dangerous, but White is actually unable to make use of the long dark squared diagonal.}) 16... Qb4 {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of Fischer's'} 17. Qg4 (17. bxc3 Qxe4 18. Bxe7 Bb7 ({or} 18... Re8 {Black maintains the balance without difficulty. Thus Benko's mistake in this game was not 13... b4, but only the incautious 15...gxf6.})) 17... Bxf6 18. Nxf6+ Kh8 {Andrew Soltis: 'then'} 19. Qh4 h6 20. Ng4 $40 { xh6 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening Nxh6 with a strong attack.' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '' Fischer/PS: ' xh6''} (20. Z0) ({. Hübner also examines} 20. bxc3 {, coming to the conclusion that this was no better:} Qxc3 21. Ng4 (21. fxe5 Ba6 $1) 21... exf4 22. Nxh6 gxh6 23. Qxh6+ Kg8 24. Qg5+ Qg7 25. Qxa5 Bb7 26. Qd2 f3 ({it seems to me that Black can even play} 26... Rfd8 $5 27. Qe2 e5 {with good counter-chances}) 27. gxf3 Bxf3+ 28. Rxf3 Qxa1+ 29. Kg2 Qe5 $1 {and White has only perpetual check –} 30. Rg3+ Kh7 31. Rh3+ {etc.}) 20... Nxb3 $1 {Demuth,Adrien} (20... cxb2 {Demuth,Adrien Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'would prompt White to take the perpetual check'} {Andrew Soltis: 'or'} 21. Rad1 $1 (21. Nxh6 bxa1=Q 22. Ng4+ Kg8 23. Nf6+ $1) ({because White would get the worst of it} 21. Rab1 Kh7 (21... Nxb3 $1 22. Nxh6 f6 23. Rxb2 Qc4 24. Ng4+ Kg8 25. Ne3 Qd4 26. Rxb3 Ba6 {and Black's position is even preferable.}) ({not} 21... Qe4 $2 22. Nxh6 $1 gxh6 23. Qxh6+ Kg8 24. f5 $1 exf5 ({or} 24... Qg4 25. Rf3 $1) 25. Bd5 $1 Qxd5 26. Qg5+ Kh7 27. Rf3 f4 28. Rxf4 exf4 29. Qxd5)) (21. Z0) (21. Rae1 Nxb3 22. Nxh6 f6 $1 23. axb3 Bb7 24. Qh5 (24. Ng4+ Kg8 25. fxe5 Qd2 {favours Black}) 24... Bxg2+ $1 25. Kxg2 Qb7+ 26. Rf3 gxh6 27. Qxh6+ Qh7 28. Qxh7+ Kxh7 29. fxe5 {with a roughly equal endgame.}) 21... Nxb3 (21... e4) 22. Nxh6 $1 (22. cxb3 Ba6 $1 {etc}) 22... Nd2 $1 (22... f6 $1 23. axb3 Qe4 24. Nf5+ Kg8 25. Ne7+ Kf7 26. Nxc8 Raxc8 27. Rd7+ Kg8 28. Qg4 {(Hübner), and after} Qh7 29. fxe5 Kh8 $1 {the b2-pawn causes White a mass of problems.}) 23. a3 $1 {This little move decides the game.} (23. Ng4+ Kg8 24. Nf6+ {leads to a draw}) 23... Qb7 (23... Qc5 24. Rxd2 $18) (23... Qc3 $4 24. Nf5+ Kg8 25. Ne7#) 24. fxe5 $1 Nxf1 25. Ng4+ Kg8 26. Nf6+ gxf6 27. exf6 $18 {And Black is defenceless against the coming mate.}) 21. Nxh6 Nxa1 22. a3 Qb7 23. fxe5 f5 $3 24. exf6 Rxf6 25. Ng4+ Kg8 26. Nxf6+ gxf6 27. Qxf6 Qg7 $13 {And this is still a really complicated position, with at least a draw for White.}) 16. Nce4 {Demuth,Adrien: '!'} Qd4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' Demuth,Adrien: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Played after a lengthy, but already belated think.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Benko gave this move a lot of thought. On the alternative' Andrew Soltis: 'Black poses more problems with' Garry Kasparov: 'According to Fischer, after'} 16... Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: '. There is a win but it requires extraordinary calculation' Garry Kasparov: 'White would have won with the cavalry charge'} 17. Nh5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds after..'} 17. Qg4+ Kh8 18. Qh4 Rg8 19. Nxf6 Rg7 20. Qh6 Bb7 $1) 17... f5 (17... Kh8 $1 {Demuth,Adrien The last way to hold, but the position remains bad.} 18. Nexf6 $1 Qd8 19. fxe5 $16) 18. Nhf6+ $1 Kg7 19. Qh5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!''} Bxf6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not..' Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 19... h6 20. Rf3 {Demuth,Adrien: '+-'} Rh8 ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 20... exf4 21. Rh3 Rh8 22. Ne8+ $1 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 21. Rg3+ Kf8 22. Qxh6+ $18 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 20. Nxf6 {Demuth,Adrien: '+-' Demuth,Adrien: 'Black can't take on f6, so it's completely lost.'} h6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 20... Rh8 21. Qg5+ Kf8 22. Qh6+ Ke7 23. Qh4 Kf8 24. Nxh7+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins the exchange.' Garry Kasparov: 'etc'}) 21. Rf3 $1 Rh8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not..'} 21... Kxf6 22. Qh4+ {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Kg7 23. Rg3+ Kh7 (23... Kh8 24. Rh3) 24. Rh3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins.' Garry Kasparov: 'with mate'}) (21... exf4 22. Rh3 Rh8 23. Ne8+) 22. Ne8+ {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Rxe8 23. Rg3+ Kf8 24. Qxh6+ Ke7 25. Qh4+ {Andrew Soltis: 'White pockets the queen after'} {Andrew Soltis: 'The only way to test White further is'} Kd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 25... Kf8 26. Rh3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!''} Qd8 27. Qh6+ Ke7 28. Qg5+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins the Queen.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: ', winning the queen'}) 26. Rd3+ Kc6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 26... Kc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'in the last line when he must fine'} 27. Ba4 $1 { f2+ Bobby Fischer: 'Threatens Qf2+' Andrew Soltis: 'threatening Qf2+ Fischer/PS: ' f2+'' Garry Kasparov: 'with the threat of Qf2+'}) 27. Ba4+ Kb7 28. Bxe8 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins material.' Garry Kasparov: 'with crushing threats.'}) 17. Qh5 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Already Black is without a satisfactory defense.' Demuth,Adrien: '!' Demuth,Adrien: ''+-!'' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-!'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'It only required the opponent to spoil prematurely his pawn structure, and White promptly creates an efficient attacking mechanism, involving Qh6 and Nh5, with his knight at e4 also playing a far from minor role.' Demuth,Adrien: 'Black is defenceless, but he will try to make Fischer's task as hard as possible.'} Nxb3 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black has no defence.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'A.) On..' Andrew Soltis: 'A form of resignation. Also lost was' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer gives'} 17... Kh8 18. Qh6 Rg8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'If..'} 18... exf4 19. Nh5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins' Demuth,Adrien: ''+-''} Rg8 {Demuth,Adrien} 20. Nexf6 Bxf6 21. Nxf6 Rg7 22. Rad1 Qb6 23. Nh5 $18) 19. Nxf6 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-''}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'B.)'} 17... exf4 18. Nf5 $1 {Demuth,Adrien: 'A very nice blow.'} exf5 {Garry Kasparov: '(it transpires that quadrupled pawns occur not only in studies and problems)' Demuth,Adrien: 'I think I had never seen quadruple pawns before.'} 19. Rxf4 {Demuth,Adrien: 'The point is that Black is unable to properly protect h7.'} Qxe4 (19... fxe4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Otherwise'} 20. Rh4 $18) 20. Rxe4 fxe4 21. Qxa5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'Wins. (This key line wouldn't work had Black interpolated 13 ... Nxb3! earlier!)' Garry Kasparov: '(this variation would not have worked if Black had played 13...Nxb3!)'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'C.)' Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 17... Kg7 {Lombardy,W Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'Lombardy,W'' Garry Kasparov: '(Lombardy)'} 18. Rad1 Qxb2 19. Qh4 Bb7 20. Nxf6 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '+-!'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 18. Qh6 $1 ({Not} 18. axb3 {because then} exf4 $1 {staves off immediate collapse.}) 18... exf4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On..' Andrew Soltis: 'Black needs both ...exf4 and' Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 18... f5 {Andrew Soltis: 'to save himself. But he can only play one move at a time. Here 18. ...f5 would allow White to drive the queen away with' Garry Kasparov: 'Bobby was planning'} 19. c3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is devastating: e.g.,' Andrew Soltis: 'after which Nh5 wins'} (19. Ng5 $1 {Demuth,Adrien} Bxg5 20. Qxg5+ Kh8 21. Qf6+ Kg8 22. Rad1 $18 {If the queen moves, 23.h5 will mate.}) 19... bxc3 20. bxc3 Qb6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Any.'} 21. Nh5 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'forces mate.' Garry Kasparov: 'with inevitable mate.'}) 19. Nh5 f5 20. Rad1 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Demuth,Adrien: 'The last finesse, White shouldn't leave his opponent have a free lunch on a1.'} (20. Nef6+ {Demuth,Adrien It was still possible to misplay, and to lose most of White's advantage.} Bxf6 21. Nxf6+ Qxf6 22. Qxf6 Nxa1 23. Rxa1 Bb7 $14 {White obviously keeps the better chances, but things are complicated!}) ({A bit of technique would have been required to win} 20. Nhf6+ Bxf6) 20... Qe5 ({Black is mated after} 20... Qxb2 21. c3 {!. He played out.}) 21. Nef6+ {Demuth,Adrien: 'Now, this is over.'} Bxf6 22. Nxf6+ Qxf6 23. Qxf6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Now it's skin and bones.'} Nc5 24. Qg5+ Kh8 25. Qe7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: '!''} Ba6 26. Qxc5 Bxf1 27. Rxf1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black resigns' Demuth,Adrien: 'A really well organised attack, that made his opponent's position completely fall apart.'} 1-0

