{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 17 A very near miss. This is one of the four games that Fischer lost to Tal who, in winning this tournament, earned the right to meet and trounce Botvinnik for the world championship. In jest the whimsical Tal signed Fischer's name, in addition to his own, when asked for an autograph. "Why not?" he quipped; "I've beaten Bobby so often ... that gives me the right to sign for him!" A careful reading of Fischer's notes will reveal a clear echo of the strong emotions that engulfed him during this tense encounter. He misses a win in the opening and several draws along the way, demonstrating dramatically how a continuously advantageous position can abruptly be turned into defeat by seemingly insignificant miscalculations. ' Pachman: The 1959 Candidates Tournament was a four-leg marathon over 28 rounds, played alternately in three Yugoslav towns--Veldes, Agram, and Belgrade. After the event FIDE discussed the possibility of changing the system [because many of the participants found that it was too exhausting]. ///  Myers: Tal won the tournament by the fantastic score of 20/28, a full 1½ points ahead of Keres, despite the fact that Keres was able to beat Tal 3 out of 4 in their individual encounters. Tal made up for this by beating Fischer in all four of their games, including this 27th round victory that put Tal beyond reach.} 1. e4 {Myers Polugaevsky Fischer Purdy Mednis Pachman Khalifman} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 {Pachman: Tal really only needed two draws from the last two rounds, for it was unlikely that Keres would win against both Gligoric and Olafsson. Many players in Tal's position would have proceeded cautiously and aimed at attaining a safe position in the opening. For two reasons Tal adopted different tactics. First a quiet game does not suit his style, and second he apparently wanted to be instrumental in deciding the outcome of the tournament. He therefore chose one of the sharpest systems of the Sicilian defense. His daring was crowned with success, but only after some nerve-racking moments.} 6. Bc4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer's favourite move, which brought him many wins. At that time the theory of this sharp variation was still in the 'stone age', and the players began improvising from literally the 8th-9th move (which suited Tal perfectly well). The real development of the variation occurred only in the early 1990s. I myself had played 6 Bc4 several times, and then Short played it against me in the 1993 world championship match, which marked a colossal leap forward for the variation! Black had to solve numerous problems...'} e6 {Garry Kasparov: 'To avoid'} 7. Bb3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'I had no better luck against Blackstone, in an exhibition game at Davis, California, 1964, with'} 7. O-O Be7 8. Bb3 Qc7 (8... O-O 9. f4 b5 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Nfd7 12. Qh5 {(Anand-Kasparov Moscow rapidplay 1996)} ({or} 12. Be3 {(Short-Kasparov, Novgorod 1997), although in both cases Black has sufficient counter-chances.})) 9. f4 b5 10. f5 b4 11. fxe6 $5 (11. Nce2 e5 12. Nf3 Bb7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'is bad for White)'}) 11... bxc3 12. exf7+ Kf8 13. Bg5 Ng4 $1 $19 {Fischer,R-Blackstone,J Davis sim 1964 Bobby Fischer: '! and Black should win. Fischer,R-Blackstone,J Davis sim 1964'}) 7... b5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Certainly the most critical reply.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'This reaction must be prompt! In our first lap game here Tal played the weaker' Garry Kasparov: ''In our first lap game here Tal played the weaker'} 7... Be7 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'In fact 7...Be7 is dangerous, not on account of 8 f4, but in view of'} 8. f4 (8. g4 $1 {– a kind of modification of the Keres Attack.}) 8... O-O {Bobby Fischer: '(for 8 ... b5 see the note to Black's 8th move)'} 9. Qf3 Qc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now'} 10. f5 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'instead of'} 10. O-O $2 b5 11. f5 b4 $1 12. Na4 e5 13. Ne2 Bb7 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'and Black stands better)' Garry Kasparov: 'and Black stands better'}) 10... e5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not' Garry Kasparov: 'But it is better to play'} 10... Nc6 11. Be3 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a bind'} Nxd4 12. Bxd4 b5 {, or even earlier, 8...Nc6.}) 11. Nde2 b5 12. a3 Bb7 13. g4 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'with a strong attack.' Garry Kasparov: 'with a strong attack.' (Fischer)'} (13. Z0)) ({In my match with Short I tried} 7... Nbd7 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} 8. f4 Nc5 {;}) ({and then} 7... Nc6 8. Be3 Be7 9. f4 O-O 10. O-O (10. Qf3 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $1) 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 12. e5 $1 (12. a3 Bb7 {is equal, Fischer-Spassky, Reykjavik 4th matchgame 1972}) 12... dxe5 13. fxe5 Nd7 14. Ne4 Bb7 15. Nd6 Bxd6 16. exd6 Qg5 {with a struggle for equality, but in the end I switched to 7...b5 .}) 8. f4 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Premature aggression.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'R.Byme-Evans, US Championship 1967 went'} 8. Qf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'but White got nothing after'} Bb7 9. Bg5 b4 10. Na4 Nbd7 11. O-O Qa5 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 13. Rfe1 Be7 $10 {Byrne,R-Evans,L ch-USA 1967 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Byrne, R-Evans,L ch-USA 1967'}) ({Against Olafsson, at Buenos Aires 1960, I continued Garry Kasparov: 'As it later transpired,'} 8. O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'is more accurate, for example:'} Be7 ({if} 8... b4 9. Na4 Nxe4 (9... Bd7 10. Re1 Be7 11. a3 (11. f4) 11... a5 12. axb4 axb4 13. Bd2 Na6 14. c3 $146 (14. Qe2) 14... bxc3 15. Bxc3 (15. Nxc3 $5 $11) 15... O-O 16. Qe2 Qb8 $1 17. Bc4 Nb4 $1 {#5E#CDa4} 18. Bb5 (18. b3 d5 $1 $36) 18... Bxb5 19. Nxb5 Nc2 $1 20. Qxc2 (20. e5 Nxa1 $5 (20... dxe5 21. Bxe5 Qb7 22. Qxc2 (22. Bxf6 $2 Nxe1 $1 $19) 22... Qxb5 $11) 21. exf6 Rxa4 22. fxe7 Re8 $17) 20... Qxb5 21. Bd2 Rab8 22. Bc1 Rfc8 23. Nc3 Qb7 $15 {Ardeleanu,A (2420)-Rodriguez,A (2495) Ubeda 71/247 1998 [Rodriguez,Am]}) 10. Re1 Nf6 11. Bg5 $40 {with attack}) ({if} 8... Bb7 {, then} 9. Re1 Nbd7 10. Bg5 {Fischer-Rubinetti, Palma de Mallorca 1970}) 9. Qf3 $5 Qc7 ({NOT} 9... Bb7 $2 10. Bxe6 $1) ({or} 9... Qb6 $5 10. Be3 Qb7 11. Qg3 b4 12. Na4 Nbd7 13. f3 O-O {Short-Kasparov, London rapidplay 1993}) 10. Qg3 b4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (10... O-O 11. Bh6 Ne8 12. Rad1 Bd7 {and then} 13. f4 {(A.Sokolov)} (13. a3 {(Short)}) ({or} 13. Nf3 $5 {(which I had already tried out in Kasparov-Gelfand, Linares 1993)} a5 $1 {with double-edged play  (Short-Kasparov, London 18th matchgame 1993).})) 11. Nce2 {Garry Kasparov: 'is inferior, Fischer-Olafsson, Buenos Aires 1960'} g6 12. a3 $2 (12. Bh6 $1 {is very strong)}) 12... Nxe4 13. Qe3 Nf6 14. axb4 O-O $13 {with a double-edged position. Fischer,R-Olafsson, F Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer,R-Olafsson,F Buenos Aires 1960}) (8. Bg5 {- 71/(247)}) 8... b4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer attached a '!' to this move, but it should have been '?!';'} ({it was demonstrated long ago that after} 8... Bb7 $1 {White has not even a hint of an advantage:} 9. f5 ({or} 9. O-O Be7 10. e5 dxe5 11. fxe5 Bc5 $1) 9... e5 10. Nde2 Nbd7 11. Bg5 Be7 {.}) 9. Na4 Nxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'is also playable'} 9... Bb7) ({or} 9... Be7 {is again more solid, although today experts on this variation prefer altogether not to hurry with ...b5-b4. On the morning of the day this game was played, Tal analysed this variation with Averbakh, Koblents and Petrosian, who had called in. 9...Nxe4 appealed to Tigran: 'Such a nice little central pawn... hmmm, hmmm... I think I would take it!' But Mikhail had his doubts, and it was only after sitting down at the board and glancing at Fischer, that he mentally waved his hands: 'Ah, what the devil - I'll take it!' Such preparation is not so much an indication of Tal's flippancy, as of the level of opening theory at that time...}) 10. O-O g6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'An audacious challenge to the opponent!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Correct is'} 10... Bb7 $142 {Bobby Fischer: '>=' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 11. f5 e5 12. Qh5 (12. Ne6 $1 fxe6 13. Qh5+ $40 {Vukovic} Kd7 ({or} 13... g6 14. fxg6 Kd7 15. Be3) 14. fxe6+ Kc8 {(6)} 15. Be3 {with an attack;}) 12... Qe7 13. f6 Nxf6 14. Qh4 exd4 15. Bg5 Nbd7 16. Rae1 Ne5 17. Nb6 h6 18. Bxf6 gxf6 19. Ba4+ Kd8 20. Qxd4 Rg8 21. Nd5 Bxd5 22. Qxd5 Ra7 23. Re4 Rg4 24. Re2 Qe6 25. Qa5+ Rc7 26. Bb3 Qd7 27. Ba4 Qe6 28. Bb3 d5 29. Bxd5 Bc5+ 30. Kh1 Qxd5 31. h3 Rh4 {0-1 Mitrovic,P-Maric,D/Nis YUG 1995/}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'It was dangerous to play either'} 10... Nf6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 11. Qf3 (11. f5 e5 12. Bg5 Ra7 13. Ne6 fxe6 14. Bxf6 gxf6 15. Qh5+ Ke7 16. fxe6 Bxe6 17. Rxf6 Bxb3 18. cxb3 Kd7 19. Qg4+ Kc7 20. Qxb4 Nc6 21. Qb6+ Kd7 22. Nc5+ Ke7 23. Re6+ Kf7 24. Qxc6 dxc5 25. Rf1+ Kg7 26. Re8 Qd6 27. Qf3 Rg8 28. Qh5 Kh8 29. Rxe5 Rag7 30. Re2 Qd4+ 31. Kh1 Bd6 32. Ref2 Qe4 33. Qh3 Be5 34. Rd1 Bd4 35. Rfd2 {0-1 Kosiorek,M-Jakubiec,A/Polanica Zdroj POL 1996/}) 11... d5 12. f5 e5 (12... Bd6 13. Re1 O-O 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. Nxe6 Bxe6 16. Rxe6 $16) 13. Re1 e4 14. Qg3 Bb7 (14... Bd6 $2 15. Qxg7 Bxh2+ 16. Kxh2 Rg8 17. Rxe4+ $18 {Keres}) 15. Kh1 Nbd7 16. Be3 g6 17. Qh3 Rg8 18. fxg6 hxg6 19. c3 bxc3 20. Nxc3 Bg7 21. Qg3 Rc8 22. Rad1 Rh8 23. Nf5 gxf5 24. Qxg7 Rg8 25. Qh6 Ng4 26. Qh7 Ndf6 27. Qxf5 Qc7 28. Bg1 Rh8 29. h3 Rh5 30. Qf1 Qe5 31. Qe2 d4 32. Rxd4 Rxc3 33. bxc3 e3 34. Bxf7+ Kxf7 35. Qc4+ Bd5 36. Rxd5 Qxd5 37. Qc7+ Kg6 38. Qg3 Ne4 39. Qxg4+ Rg5 40. Qf3 Rg3 {0-1 Burkett,M-Berclaz,P/corr IECG 1999/}) (10... d5 $2 11. f5 Be7 (11... Ra7 12. fxe6 $16 {[%cal Rh5e5,Yd1h5] Khalifman}) 12. fxe6 fxe6 13. Nxe6 Bxe6 14. Qh5+ g6 15. Qe5 Qd6 16. Qxh8+ Kd7 17. Bf4 Qc6 18. Bxb8 Qb5 19. Qe5 Qxb8 20. Qxb8 Rxb8 21. Rad1 Kc6 22. Rfe1 Bg8 23. c4 dxc4 24. Bxc4 Bc5+ 25. Nxc5 Nxc5 26. Bxg8 Rxg8 27. Re7 h5 28. Re5 Na4 29. Re6+ Kc5 30. Rd2 a5 31. Re5+ Kb6 32. Rd6+ Kc7 33. Ra6 Nxb2 34. Re7+ Kb8 35. Rxa5 Rc8 36. h3 Rc1+ 37. Kh2 Nd1 38. Ra6 g5 39. Rg6 Rc8 40. Rxg5 Nc3 41. Rxh5 Nxa2 42. Rb5+ {1-0 Delanoy,A-Touzane,O/Montpellier FRA 1997/}) (10... Be7 11. Qf3 d5 12. Be3 O-O 13. c4 bxc3 14. Nxc3 Nf6 15. g4 Bb7 16. Bc2 Nc6 17. Nxc6 Bxc6 18. Rad1 Qc8 19. g5 Ne8 20. Bxh7+ Kxh7 21. Qh5+ Kg8 22. Rf3 g6 23. Qh6 Ng7 24. Rh3 Nh5 25. Rxh5 gxh5 26. g6 fxg6 27. Qxg6+ Kh8 28. Rd2 Bf6 29. Qxh5+ Kg8 30. Rg2+ Bg7 31. Rxg7+ Kxg7 32. Bd4+ Rf6 33. Qg5+ Kh7 34. Qh4+ Kg8 35. Qxf6 {1-0 Plessier,F-Viret,J/corr FRA 1982/}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'but'} 10... Nc5 {Garry Kasparov: 'came into consideration.'} 11. f5 e5 12. Bxf7+ Kxf7 13. Ne6 Nxe6 14. fxe6+ Ke8 15. Qf3 Ra7 16. Be3 Re7 17. Bb6 Bb7 18. Bxd8 Bxf3 19. Bxe7 Kxe7 20. Rxf3 Kxe6 21. Raf1 Be7 22. Rf7 Bf6 23. Rb7 a5 24. Rb6 Rc8 25. Rd1 Nc6 26. c4 bxc3 27. Nxc3 e4 28. Kf1 Bxc3 29. bxc3 d5 30. c4 dxc4 31. Rd4 Ke5 32. Rxc4 Rf8+ 33. Ke1 Nd4 34. Ra6 Kd5 35. Rc3 Rb8 36. Rxa5+ Ke6 37. Rc4 Rd8 38. Raa4 Nb5 39. Rxe4+ Kf6 40. Rf4+ Ke5 41. Rae4+ Kd6 42. a4 Nc7 43. Rf7 g5 44. Rf2 Ra8 45. Rd2+ Kc5 46. Re5+ {1-0 Rositsan,B-Livshits,R/Toronto CAN 1992/}) (10... Qc7 11. f5 d5 12. fxe6 fxe6 13. Qh5+ g6 14. Qh3 Bd6 15. Nxe6 Bxh2+ 16. Qxh2 Qxh2+ 17. Kxh2 Bxe6 18. Nb6 Ra7 19. Nxd5 Bxd5 20. Bxd5 Re7 21. a3 Re5 22. Bc4 Rh5+ 23. Kg1 Ng3 24. Re1+ Kd7 25. Bf4 Rh1+ 26. Kf2 Ne4+ 27. Kf3 Rxe1 28. Rxe1 bxa3 29. Rd1+ Kc6 30. Bd5+ Kb5 31. Bxe4 Rd8 32. Rxd8 {1-0 Bangiev,A-Kaplan,L/Biel SUI 1993/}) 11. f5 $1 {Fischer: This riposte caught Tal completely unaware. Black's king, trapped in the center, will soon be subject to mayhem. Garry Kasparov: 'This breakthrough suggests itself.'} gxf5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 11... exf5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 12. Bd5 Ra7 13. Nxf5 $1 gxf5 14. Qd4 $18 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'is inferior.'}) (11... d5 12. fxe6 fxe6 13. Qf3 Qc7 (13... Ra7 14. Be3 Rb7 15. Nb6 Qxb6 16. Ba4+ Kd8 17. Nc6+ Nxc6 18. Bxb6+ Rxb6 19. g3 Bg7 20. Rad1 Bd7 21. Qe3 Kc7 22. Rf7 Bf8 23. Qxe4 Bc5+ 24. Kg2 dxe4 25. Rdxd7+ Kb8 26. Bxc6 Rxc6 27. Rb7+ Kc8 28. a3 bxa3 29. b4 Bd4 30. Rbd7 Rxc2+ 31. Kh3 a2 {0-1 Liebold,T-Haensel,T/Schoeneck U20 GER 1996/}) 14. Bf4 Qb7 15. Be5 Bg7 16. Bxg7 Qxg7 17. Qe3 Qa7 18. Rad1 Nc6 19. c4 bxc3 20. Nxc3 Nxd4 21. Rxd4 Nxc3 22. bxc3 Qe7 23. Qe5 Rg8 24. Rf6 Ra7 25. Ba4+ Kd8 26. c4 Rf8 27. Rxf8+ Qxf8 28. cxd5 exd5 29. Rxd5+ Rd7 30. Bxd7 Bxd7 31. Qb8+ Ke7 32. Rxd7+ {1-0 Wei,M-Hoffmann,A/Canberra AUS 2003/}) 12. Nxf5 $1 Rg8 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'A serious error.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Woozy, Tal stumbles into a dubious defense. Better is' Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 12... d5 $142 $6 13. Nh6 $1 (13. Be3 exf5 14. Bxd5 Bd6 15. g3 Rg8 16. Qh5 Qe7 17. Rxf5 Bxf5 18. Qxf5 Nxg3 19. Qc8+ Qd8 20. Qxd8+ Kxd8 21. Bxa8 Nf5+ 22. Kf2 Nxe3 23. Kxe3 Kc7 24. Rf1 f6 25. Be4 Nd7 26. b3 Be5 27. Rd1 Rg5 28. Bd3 Rg2 29. Bxh7 Rxh2 30. Bf5 Nb6 31. Nc5 Kc6 32. Nd3 Bc3 33. Be4+ Kb5 34. Nf4 a5 35. Rd8 a4 36. Rd1 a3 37. Ne6 Be5 38. Nf4 Rh4 39. Nd3 Bc3 {½-½ Carrasco Sagredo,M-Pallardo Lozoya,J/Mislata ESP 2004/}) 13... Bxh6 14. Bxh6 {Garry Kasparov: ', White would have had no more than decent compensation for the pawn.'} a5 15. Qh5 $18 {Myers}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'not' Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} 12... exf5 $2 13. Qd5 Ra7 (13... Be6 14. Qxa8 Bg7 15. Be3 Bxb3 16. cxb3 O-O 17. Rxf5 Re8 18. Raf1 Qd7 19. Qd5 Qe6 20. Qxe6 fxe6 21. Rf7 Rf8 22. Rxf8+ Bxf8 23. Rc1 Nd7 24. Rc4 d5 25. Rc6 Kf7 26. Rxa6 e5 27. Ra7 Ke6 28. Kf1 d4 29. Bc1 d3 30. Ke1 h5 31. Be3 Nef6 32. h3 Nd5 33. Kd2 e4 34. Bd4 Bh6+ 35. Kd1 Ne3+ 36. Bxe3 Bxe3 37. Rb7 Nc5 38. Nxc5+ Bxc5 39. Kd2 Bf2 40. a4 Bg3 41. a5 Bf4+ 42. Kd1 e3 43. Rg7 Kf6 44. g3 e2+ 45. Ke1 Be5 46. Rg8 Bxb2 47. Re8 Bc3+ 48. Kf2 d2 49. Kxe2 Be5 50. Rxe5 Kxe5 51. a6 Ke4 52. a7 d1=Q+ 53. Kxd1 Kf3 54. a8=Q+ Kxg3 55. Ke2 h4 56. Qg8+ Kxh3 57. Kf2 {1-0 Bates,T-Falkowski,K/East Detroit USA 1985/}) (13... Qc7 $5) 14. Qd4 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'spearing a rook' Garry Kasparov: '.'} Qf6 $2 15. Qxa7 Nc6 16. Qe3 Qg6 17. c3 Rg8 18. Qe2 a5 19. Bc4 Ne5 20. Bb5+ Ke7 21. Bf4 Bg7 22. Rad1 Bf6 23. cxb4 axb4 24. Nb6 Bb7 25. Nd5+ Kf8 26. Ne3 Be7 27. g3 h5 28. Nxf5 Ng5 29. Bxg5 Qxg5 30. Nxe7 Nf3+ 31. Rxf3 Bxf3 32. Qxf3 Qxe7 33. Rf1 Rg4 34. Bc6 Rg5 35. Bd5 Rg7 36. Qxf7+ Rxf7 37. Rxf7+ Qxf7 38. Bxf7 Kxf7 39. Kf2 Ke6 40. h3 Kf5 41. Kf3 d5 42. g4+ hxg4+ 43. hxg4+ Ke5 44. Ke3 {1-0 Pflug,M-Knechtel,R/Bayern GER 1996/}) (12... Bb7 $5 13. Qh5 $2 (13. Qd4 Rg8 14. Qxb4 Bc6 15. Nd4 Qh4 16. Nxc6 Nxc6 17. Qb6 Rxg2+ 18. Kxg2 Qg4+ 19. Kh1 Ng3+ 20. hxg3 Qh3+ 21. Kg1 Qxg3+ 22. Kh1 Qh3+ $11 {Myers}) (13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. Qh5+ Kd7 15. Qf7+ Kc8 16. Qxe6+ Nd7 17. Be3 Kc7 (17... Kb8 18. a3 $44) 18. Bd4 Qg5 19. Bxh8 Nec5 20. Qe2 Nxa4 21. Rad1 Re8 22. Qc4+ Nac5 23. Rf2 a5 24. Bd4 {½-½ Petrosian,A-Mikhalchishin,A/Ptuj SLO 1998/}) (13. Nh6 Bxh6 14. Qh5 Qe7 15. Bxh6 Rg8 16. g3 Nd7 17. Rae1 Ne5 (17... Rg6 $142 $5 {[%cal Yf7f5] Khalifman}) 18. Bf4 $44 (18. Nb6 $5 Rb8 19. Ba4+ $1 Kd8 20. Nd7 Rg5 21. Qh4 Rg6 22. Qxe7+ Kxe7 23. Nxb8 Rxh6 24. Bc6 Bxc6 25. Nxc6+ Nxc6 26. Rxe4 $16 {Myers}) 18... Ng4 19. Bxd6 Rg5 20. Qxh7 Nxd6 21. Qh8+ Kd7 22. Nb6+ Kc6 23. Nxa8 Nb5 24. Re4 Qc5+ 25. Kh1 Nd6 26. Ba4+ Kd5 27. Rxg4 Rxg4 28. h3 Qe3 29. hxg4 Kc5+ 30. Kh2 Qe2+ {0-1 Szeles,K-Sax,G/Magyarorszag HUN 1972/}) (13. Qg4 $1 Nf6 14. Qxb4 Qc7 15. Nb6 Ra7 16. Nd4 Be7 17. Be3 Rg8 18. g3 Rg4 19. Ba4+ Bc6 20. Qa5 Bxa4 21. Qxa4+ Nbd7 22. Nxd7 Qxd7 23. Qa3 h5 24. Rf3 d5 25. b4 h4 26. Raf1 a5 27. Rxf6 axb4 28. Qb2 Bxf6 29. Rxf6 hxg3 30. hxg3 Qc7 31. Ne2 Rxg3+ 32. Kf2 Rxe3 33. Kxe3 Ra3+ 34. Kd2 Qa5 35. Rf1 {½-½ Agulnick,M-Turko,S/corr IECC 2002/}) 13... Rg8 14. Ng3 (14. g3 Nf6 15. Qe2 $44 d5 16. Bf4 Ne4 17. Nd4 Nd7 18. Nxe6 fxe6 19. Qh5+ Rg6 20. Qxh7 Rg7 21. Qh5+ Rf7 22. c4 Ndf6 23. Qg6 dxc4 24. Rae1 Qc8 25. Be3 Qc6 26. Nb6 Bc5 27. Ba4 Bxe3+ 28. Rf2 Bxf2+ 29. Kf1 Bxb6 30. Bxc6+ Bxc6 31. Ke2 c3 32. Rc1 Bb5+ {0-1 Gorlin,A-Thiel,P/Saint Paul USA 2000/}) 14... Nf6 15. Qh4 Nbd7 16. Qxb4 (16. Bg5 Be7 17. Rae1 Rg6 18. Qxb4 Qc7 19. Qd2 Qc6 20. Re2 O-O-O 21. Rf3 Kb8 22. Rc3 Qb5 23. Bf4 Nd5 24. Bxd5 Qxd5 25. Rd3 Qc6 26. b3 e5 27. Nf5 Rdg8 28. Bg3 Bf8 29. c4 Nc5 30. Nxc5 Qxc5+ 31. Qe3 Qxe3+ 32. Nxe3 Be4 33. Rd1 f5 34. Nc2 h5 35. Ne1 Be7 36. Kf1 Kb7 37. c5 d5 38. Bxe5 Bxc5 39. g3 h4 40. Rc1 Bb6 {0-1 Geanta,H-Cornea,R/Tusnad ROM 2000/}) 16... Qc7 17. Qc3 Qxc3 18. Nxc3 h5 19. Ba4 Be7 20. Rf4 Nd5 21. Nxd5 Bxd5 22. c4 Bb7 23. Rf2 h4 24. Ne2 Rc8 25. b3 d5 26. Bxd7+ Kxd7 27. Rxf7 Ke8 28. Rxe7+ Kxe7 29. Ba3+ Kd7 30. Rf1 Rcd8 31. Rf7+ Kc8 32. cxd5 Rxd5 33. Kf2 Rd7 34. Rxd7 Rxg2+ 35. Ke3 Kxd7 36. h3 Rh2 37. Ng1 Rxa2 38. Bc5 a5 39. Kf4 Bd5 40. Kg5 Rc2 41. Bd4 Bxb3 42. Nf3 a4 43. Kxh4 a3 44. Kg5 a2 45. h4 Rc1 46. h5 a1=Q {0-1 Kudriashov,A-Anetbaev,B/Tashkent URS 1972/}) 13. Bd5 $1 (13. Qh5 $5 {Gligoric} Nf6 14. Qf3 Qc7 (14... Ra7 15. Ng3 Ng4 16. h3 Ne5 17. Qf2 Rc7 18. Bf4 $15) 15. Ng3 Ng4 16. Qxa8 Bb7 17. Qa7 Nc6 18. Qb6 Qe7 19. Bf4 Nd8 20. Nc5 Bc6 21. Nce4 Qh4 22. h3 d5 23. hxg4 dxe4 24. Rad1 Be7 25. Rxd8+ {1-0 Piscopo,P-Schiappoli,D/Gallipoli Terra del Salento ITA 2000/}) 13... Ra7 ({Garry Kasparov: '''} 13... exd5 $2 14. Qxd5 Bxf5 (14... Ra7 15. Qxe4+ $18 {Khalifman}) 15. Rxf5 Ra7 $8 16. Qxe4+ Re7 17. Qxb4 Re2 {Garry Kasparov: 'is not possible in view of'} 18. Bg5 $1 (18. Rf2 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '– G.K.'} Rxf2 19. Qe1+ Be7 20. Qxf2 Qd7 21. Nb6 Qc6 22. Be3 $18 {Myers}) 18... Rxg5 ({after} 18... Rxg2+ 19. Kxg2 Rxg5+ 20. Rxg5 Qxg5+ 21. Kf2 Qf5+ 22. Kg3 Qg5+ 23. Qg4 Qe3+ 24. Kg2 Qd2+ 25. Kg1 {the king escapes from the checks – G.K.}) 19. Rxg5 Qxg5 20. Qxb8+ $18 {Panov,V Bobby Fischer: 'wins. Panov,V' Garry Kasparov: '. Almost all the game Fischer played in the style of Tal, but the whole problem for him was that Tal did not defend in the style of Fischer, but found the only saving counter-chances!'  (Panov) 'After making a couple of mistakes, I felt myself to be on the very edge of the abyss, especially since Fischer was playing keenly and with great self-control. At the same time, sensing the "smell of my blood", Keres began pressing Gligoric with all his might. It was all pointing to the fact that the question of first place would be decided in the last round or even after it. But Bobby, not wishing to remain a pawn down for long, hurried to restore material equality, thereby losing part of his advantage.' (Tal)'}) 14. Bxe4 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'correct is' Garry Kasparov: 'According to Fischer, 'correct is'} 14. Be3 $142 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '>=' Kevitz Garry Kasparov: '!'} Nc5 ({or} 14... exd5 15. Bxa7 Bxf5 16. Rxf5 Qd7 17. Qxd5 Qxa7+ 18. Kf1 Rg4 19. Re1 Qe7 20. Qa8 {– G.K.}) 15. Qh5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Also in White's favour is'} Rg6 (15... Nxa4 $2 16. Bxa7 exd5 $4 17. Rae1+ $18) (15... Rc7 $5 16. Qxh7 Rg4 (16... Rg6 17. Nxc5 dxc5 18. Bc4 exf5 19. Bxf7+ $1 Rxf7 20. Qxg6) 17. Qh5 Rg6 (17... exf5 $2 18. Bxc5 dxc5 19. Rae1+) (17... Rg8 18. Nb6 exd5 19. Bxc5 Be6 20. Rae1 {etc}) 18. Nh4 Rg8 19. Nxc5 dxc5 20. Bc4 {, although this is hardly much stronger than the continuation in the game.}) (15... exd5 16. Bxc5 dxc5 17. Rae1+) 16. Rae1 $1 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White's every piece is bearing down on Black's King (KEVITZ).' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and White's every piece is bearing down on Black's king.''} (16. Z0) 16... Rc7 17. Nxc5 dxc5 18. Bb3 exf5 19. Bf4+ $16) 14... exf5 15. Bxf5 {Mednis: This is clearly off the mark. White exchanges off a second piece, this time against Black's undeveloped c8. White's light-square Bishop has been moved five times to do this, and it just can't be right. In addition, White's g-pawn will experience some weakness with Black's rook pointed against it.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Probably it's better to avoid exchanges with'} 15. Bd5 $142 $5 $16 {Fischer Mednis Polugaevsky Garry Kasparov: '!?'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 15. Bf3 $142 $5 $16 {Fischer}) (15. Be3 $143 $2 Re7 16. Bb6 Qd7 17. Bxf5 Qxa4 18. Bxc8 Qc6 $1 $19 {Khalifman}) 15... Re7 $1 16. Bxc8 (16. c4 $5) 16... Qxc8 17. Bf4 $2 {Polugaevsky: This is an oversight. Fischer fails to see his opponent's shrewd plan and falls into a clever trap. Garry Kasparov: 'This essentially blunders a piece.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'The right move is simply' Garry Kasparov: 'Of course, White should have played'} 17. c3 $142 $1 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and if'} Qc6 $2 (17... Qg4 18. g3 Qxd1 19. Rxd1 bxc3 20. Nxc3 $14 {Myers}) ({or} 17... Qb7) {Garry Kasparov: 'then'} 18. Rf2 $16 {Garry Kasparov: ', after which the offside knight at a4 gradually comes back into play and Black has simply a 'threadbare' position with a guaranteed inferior endgame.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'not' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 17. Qxd6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'because of'} Rxg2+ 18. Kxg2 Re2+ 19. Kf3 Bxd6 20. Kxe2 Qxc2+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins. if 17 ...Qc6 18 Rf2.'}) (17. Rf2 $5 $14 {[%cal Gc2c3,Yc1f4] Myers}) 17... Qc6 $1 18. Qf3 $6 (18. Qd2 $142 $5 {Myers}) 18... Qxa4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ''Such a surprise that I didn't dare believe my eyes! Tal finds an inspired defence!' (Fischer) A staggering revelation: the capture of the piece had not even been considered by White – a vivid illustration of how non-concretely they thought in those times. Naturally, Tal took the knight: concrete, calculating play was his main trump!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Such a surprise that I didn't dare believe my eyes! 1 had expected' Garry Kasparov: 'It is curious how he himself explained his decision: 'I was faced with a choice: should I go into a slightly inferior endgame'} 18... Qxf3 $2 19. Rxf3 Re2 20. Rf2 Rxf2 21. Kxf2 $14 {Bobby Fischer: 'l and White has a slight edge after a3 because of Black's disconnected Pawns.' Garry Kasparov: 'or, after accepting the piece sacrifice, subject myself to a very strong attack? I could not see a forced mate, it is true, but, perhaps, only because I wasn't looking for one. If I had been playing White, I would have considered the attack to be decisive. Nevertheless, I chose the second path...''}) (18... Rg6 $2 19. c4 bxc3 20. Nxc3 Qxf3 21. Rxf3 $14) 19. Bxd6 Qc6 $1 20. Bxb8 $8 (20. Bxe7 $2 Qxf3 21. Rxf3 Bxe7 $15 {Khalifman}) 20... Qb6+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'White remains a clear Pawn ahead after 20 ..' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 20... Qxf3 $2 21. Rxf3 Bg7 (21... Re2 22. Bg3 (22. g3 $2 Rg6 $1 $132) 22... Bc5+ 23. Bf2 Rxc2 24. Bxc5 $14 {Khalifman}) 22. c3 $16 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'with an extra pawn.'}) 21. Kh1 Qxb8 {Mednis: Unbalanced, wild, crazy attacking positions are very difficult to judge correctly during the progress of the game. Wisdom and clarity often appear only after exhaustive post-mortems. Fischer, incorrectly, thought that White had a winning attack here; and Tal, incorrectly, thought that Black was lost. Why then did not Tal exchange Queens on move 18? Well, reason number 1 is that Tal is a very brave person, and reason number 2 is that he felt that Fischer could very well misplay the attack. Fischer: The crowd was shouting and whistling with each move. Later I was informed that many sports fans were in the audience. Maybe some soccer match had been canceled. As a consequence, chess was the main attraction that day in Belgrade. Garry Kasparov: ''The crowd was shouting and whistling with each move. Later I was informed that many sports fans were in the audience. Maybe some soccer match had been cancelled. As a consequence chess was the main attraction that day in Belgrade.' (Fischer) I should point out that the hall accommodated about 2000 spectators... 'A famous position: it was here that a widely-known psychological duel took place between us,' related Tal. 'Fischer first wrote down the move 22 Rae1!, without doubt the strongest, and wrote it not in his usual English notation, but in European, almost Russian! And not very deftly he pushed the score-sheet towards me. "He's testing me out," I thought: would I frown or display my nervousness. I could force myself to smile, but Bobby was no longer a child, he would understand. What then was I to do? I very much wanted him to change his decision. And so I calmly stood up and began walking alongside the board. I shared a joke with someone, glanced for appearance's sake at the demonstration board and with a contended expression returned to my position. I am sure that all this time Fischer was looking at me, and not calculating variations. He cast one more glance at me (I endured it), crossed out the move on his score-sheet and gave the queen check!''} 22. Qc6+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: ''When after the game I asked Fischer why he didn't play 22 Rae1, he replied: "Well, you laughed when I wrote it down!" (Tal)'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Many annotators believed that' Garry Kasparov: 'Well, at that time it was customary to turn any annotated game into a poem, whereas now the impassive machine instantaneously parries'} 22. Rae1 $142 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'was the winning move. Tal himself confessed he thought Black was lost after that. But 22 .' Purdy Garry Kasparov: 'with'} Kd8 $1 {Fischer: I've studied this position for ages, it seems, and this is the best I can find.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'holds all lines' Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} 22... Rg6 $2 23. Qxf7+ Kd7 24. Rd1+ $1 Rd6 (24... Kc6 25. Qxf8 Qxf8 26. Rxf8 $18) 25. Rxd6+ Kxd6 26. Rf6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins. I've studied this position for ages, it seems, and the best I can find is' Garry Kasparov: '!'} Ke5 27. Qxf8 Qxf8 28. Rxf8 Rd7 $16 {Myers}) (22... Kd7 $6 23. Rd1+ (23. Qd5+ $5 Qd6 24. Qb7+ Ke8 25. Qc8+ Qd8 26. Qxa6 $14 {Myers})) 23. Rd1+ $2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'also after'} 23. Rxe7 $5 Bxe7 24. Qxf7 (24. Qd5+ {White can only dream of a draw}) 24... Re8 (24... Rg6 25. Rd1+ Bd6 26. Qxh7 $14) 25. Rd1+ Bd6 26. Qxh7 $11 {Khalifman}) 23... Kc7 $1 {Fischer} (23... Kc8 $2 24. Qc6+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins'}) 24. Qf4+ (24. Rd4 Qb7 $1) 24... Kb7 25. Rd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'stronger than Fischer's variation'} Qc7 (25... Qe8 $1 $17 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 26. Qf3+ (26. Qxb4+ Qb5 27. Qxb5+ axb5 {, and White faces a struggle for a draw.}) 26... Re4 27. Rf6 Kb8 28. a3 Rg6 29. Rxf7 Bd6 30. Qf2 Qc6 31. axb4 Rh6 $17 {Myers}) (25... Qc8 $2 26. Qxb4+ Ka8 27. Qa5 $1 $18 {Haygarth}) 26. Qxb4+ Kc8 27. Rxa6 Qb7 $1 28. Qxb7+ Kxb7 29. Raf6 Rg7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw'}) 22... Rd7 23. Rae1+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'Black holds after' Garry Kasparov: 'White also misses the mark with'} 23. Rad1 $2 Bd6 (23... Qb7 $1 24. Rfe1+ Be7 25. Rxd7 Qxd7 26. Qa8+ Qd8 27. Qxa6 Kf8 28. Qb7 Rg6 $1 $19 {Myers}) (23... Qc7 24. Rde1+ Be7 25. Qf6 Kd8 $15) 24. Rxf7 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 24. Rf6 $2 Rg6 $19 25. Rdxd6 $2 Qxd6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) (24. Rfe1+ $1 Kd8 25. Qxa6 Rg5 $15) 24... Qc7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Garry Kasparov: '!'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'and on'} 23. Rxf7 $4 Qd6 $1 $19 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 23... Be7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally Tal "develops" his Bishop. Not' Garry Kasparov: 'Of course, not'} 23... Kd8 $4 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 24. Rxf7 $1 Be7 25. Rfxe7 Rxe7 26. Rd1+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 24. Rxf7 $2 (24. Qf6 $142 $1 $11 {[%csl Ra6,Rf7] Myers}) 24... Kxf7 25. Qe6+ Kf8 $1 {This move caught Fischer by surprise.} (25... Kg7 $2 26. Qxd7 $18 {Fischer}) 26. Qxd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 26. Rf1+ {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is bad due to'} Kg7 27. Rf7+ Kh8 28. Qxd7 Rd8 29. Qg4 $2 (29. Rxh7+ Kxh7 30. Qxe7+ Kg6 31. Qe6+ Kg5 32. h4+ Kxh4 $19 {Khalifman}) (29. Qxe7 $4 Rd1+ $19) 29... Qe5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Garry Kasparov: ', when Black wins.'}) 26... Qd6 27. Qb7 Rg6 28. c3 $1 {Mednis: White's attack is gone, and he is left with a material disadvantage of only two pawns for Black's Bishop. Thus the dream of a win is gone. Well, how about a draw? The chances for this are excellent, mainly becasue of the inadvertent situation on the kingside where Black has the wrong Bishop for his h-pawn. Thus an endgame with the queenside pawns off the board would be drawn. Because of the exposed nature of Black's King, it is also very questionable if Black can mount a decisive attack in the middle game. With his 28th move White starts on the right plan for exchanging queenside pawns.} a5 $6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on' Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 28... bxc3 29. Qc8+ Bd8 (29... Kg7 30. bxc3 Bd8 $19 {Myers}) 30. Qxc3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw.'}) 29. Qc8+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On the wrong track. Right is' Garry Kasparov: ''Right is'} 29. cxb4 $1 Qxb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 29... axb4 30. a3 $1 bxa3 31. bxa3 Qxa3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'draws'}) 30. Qf3+ Kg7 31. Qe2 $146 {Bobby Fischer: 'draws, since Black can't possibly build up a winning K-side attack and his own King is too exposed.' Garry Kasparov: 'drawing, since Black can't possibly build up a winning kingside attack and his own king is too exposed.' (Fischer)'} Bd6 $19) (29. Qa8+ $5) 29... Kg7 30. Qc4 Bd8 31. cxb4 axb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on'} 31... Qxb4 32. Qe2 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'White should draw with best play.' Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw.'} Bc7 $17) 32. g3 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'Overlooking Black's 34th move.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Creating losing chances. I don't see how Black can make any progress after'} 32. Qe4 $142 Bc7 (32... Qd2 $1 33. Rb1 Bf6 34. a4 Bxb2 35. Rf1 h5 36. Qf3 Rf6 37. Qg3+ Kh6 $19 {Myers}) 33. Qe7+ Kg8 34. Qe8+ Qf8 35. Qe4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'etc' Garry Kasparov: 'was more tenacious.'} Bd6 $19) (32. a3 Qd2 $1 33. Qe4 b3 34. a4 Bf6 $19 {Myers}) (32. Qe2 $5) 32... Qc6+ 33. Re4 Qxc4 34. Rxc4 Rb6 $1 {Fischer: I overlooked this.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'I overlooked this. Now Black has winning chances. I had planned on a draw after' Garry Kasparov: 'But not'} 34... Be7 $2 35. a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'dissolving Black's b-Pawn' Garry Kasparov: '!'} b3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is answered by'} (35... bxa3 36. Rc7 Kf7 37. bxa3 Ra6 $19 {Myers}) 36. Rc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'followed by' Garry Kasparov: 'and 37 Rb7.'} Re6 37. Rb7) 35. Kg2 Kf6 (35... b3 $5) 36. Kf3 Ke5 37. Ke3 (37. a3 {Bobby Fischer: 'is met, as always, by ..'} b3 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'Once White can eliminate Black's b-Pawn it's a theoretical draw.' Garry Kasparov: '!'} (37... bxa3 $2 38. bxa3 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '='})) 37... Bg5+ 38. Ke2 Kd5 39. Kd3 Bf6 40. Rc2 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Too passive. I wanted to avoid ilnmobilizing my Q-side Pawns with' Garry Kasparov: 'Drawing chances would have been retained by'} 40. b3 $142 {Bobby Fischer: 'but it's the best hope now. On' Garry Kasparov: ', and if'} Be7 {Garry Kasparov: ', then'} (40... Be5 41. Ke3 Rb8 $19 {Myers}) 41. Rd4+ $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'preserves drawing chances.'}) 40... Be5 (40... b3 $1 41. axb3 Rxb3+ 42. Ke2 Ke4 $19 {Myers}) 41. Re2 Rf6 42. Rc2 Rf3+ 43. Ke2 Rf7 44. Kd3 Bd4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ''To watch all this tightrope-walking was too much for me, and I appeared in the hall only after three hours' play,' recalls grandmaster Averbakh, one of the Riga player's seconds. "Tal's position was now absolutely won, but Koblents and Mikenas, Keres's second, were reclining in their seats, clutching their hearts and sucking tranquillisers..." ''} 45. a3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'on' Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 45. b3 Rf3+ 46. Ke2 Rf2+ 47. Kd3 Rxc2 48. Kxc2 Ke4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Garry Kasparov: 'and wins.'}) 45... b3 46. Rc8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Equally hopeless is' Garry Kasparov: 'nor'} 46. Re2 Rf3+ 47. Kd2 Bxb2 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc' Garry Kasparov: 'would have helped.'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Neither'} 46. Rd2 Rf3+ 47. Ke2 Rf2+ $19 {Fischer}) 46... Bxb2 (46... Rf3+ 47. Ke2 Rf2+ 48. Kd3 Be5 49. Rd8+ Ke6 50. Re8+ Kd6 51. Rd8+ Ke7 52. Rd5 Ke6 53. Rb5 Rxb2 $19 {Myers}) 47. Rd8+ Kc6 48. Rb8 Rf3+ 49. Kc4 Rc3+ 50. Kb4 Kc7 (50... Ba1 51. a4 b2 $1 {0-1 (51) Fischer,R-Tal,M Bled/Zagreb/Belgrade 1959 My 60 Memorable Games [Bobby Fischer]} ({Garry Kasparov: 'In view of'} 51... b2 $1 52. Kxc3 b1=Q+ $1 {[%emt 0:00:31] If 53 Kxc3 b1=Q+! The discovered-check theme is strangely reminiscent of the finale of game Garry Kasparov: '.'})) 51. Rb5 Ba1 52. a4 b2 $1 (52... b2 $1 53. Kxc3 b1=Q+ $1) 0-1

