{GAME N. 20 Theoretical scuffle. Former world champion, Dr. Max Euwe had for decades been considered one of the wor/d's leading authorities on opening theory. His Chess Archives ranks with Modern Chess Openings as an indispensable source of reference. It is no small wonder, then, when he selects a risky but playable variation. Fischer, however, just a little better versed in its intricacies, introduces a nuance on move 15 which ruffles his opponent no end. Fischer's method of dispatching his veteran adversary - on home grounds, as it were - is deceptively simple. After a mere eighteen moves the opening has become an ending and the duel is over. Euwe fights on, but to no avail.} 1. e4 {Garry Kasparov Fischer Petronijevic,Zoran Petronijevic,Zoran Donev Petronijevic,Zoran} c6 {Garry Kasparov: 'and later'} 2. d4 ({In earlier games he obstinately but unsuccessfully played} 2. Nc3 {Garry Kasparov} d5 3. Nf3 {(cf. Volume 2, Game No.72)}) 2... d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. c4 {At that time I was convinced the Panov-Botvinnik attack was the sharpest Garry Kasparov: '"At that time I was convinced the Panov-Botvinnik attack was the sharpest," writes Fischer.'} (4. Bd3 {(Garry Kasparov) (Game No.76).}) 4... Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 ({In our game at Buenos Aires 1960, Ivkov played Garry Kasparov: 'In the variation' Garry Kasparov: '"Later, by the efforts of many grandmasters, but mainly Petrosian (but later also Karpov – G.K.), it was shown that' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer often played The Panov's Attack. For instance, in his game against Ivkov (Buenos Aires 1960) played was: ' Petronijevic,Zoran: 'Fischer spielte oft den Panov-Angriff. Z. B. wurde in seiner Partie gegen Ivkov (Buenos Aires 1960) gespielt: '} 5... e6 6. Nf3 Be7 {Garry Kasparov: ', Fischer gained an advantage against Ivkov (Buenos Aires 1960) by'} (6... Bb4 {(Garry Kasparov) is the simplest, in analogy with the Nimzo-Indian Defence, and Black has a comfortable game." (Botvinnik)}) 7. c5 (7. cxd5 {Garry Kasparov} Nxd5 8. Bd3 Z0 ({, 