{GAME N. 22 Bad judgement. Once a contender for the title, Szabo's performances nowadays are spotty and unpredictable. Here, with breathtaking bluntness, he attempts to wipe Fischer from the board. In the process he leaves himself wide open on the dark squares. Fischer promptly invades on the queenside, by means of a curious queen manoeuvre, while Szabo, preoccupied with his own kingside attack, fails to realise the danger in time. 21... Re3! is the blow that ends all effective resistance. Rather than fight on against hopeless odds, Szabo resigns three moves later.} {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer briefly played postal chess but virtually none of his games survived. One intriguing specimen is a miniature he apparently lost in Chess Review's annual tournament, the Golden Knights, in 1955. The game A.W. Conger began'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis Andrew Soltis Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS Fischer/PS Fischer/PS: 'Andrew Soltis''} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 O-O ({Szabo points out a drawback to Black's fourth move. After the normal Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'Szabo points out a drawback to Black's fourth move. After the normal''} 4... d6 {(Andrew Soltis) Black can meet Andrew Soltis: 'Black can meet Fischer/PS: 'Andrew Soltis'' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'Black can meet''} 5. Bg5 {with Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'with''} h6 6. Bh4 c5 (6... O-O {Andrew Soltis Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'Andrew Soltis''} 7. f4 c5 8. d5 Qa5 9. Qd2 {and now instead of Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'and now instead of''} e6 $1 ({He played Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'He played''} 9... Qc7 $2 10. Bd3 e6 {and resigned after Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'and resigned after''} 11. Nb5 Qb6 {although} {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'although''} 12. Nxd6 $1 Qxd6 13. e5 {is not that clear. Fischer only played one known game over-the-board against Bg5 that early. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer/PS: 'is not that clear. Fischer only played one known game over-the-board against Bg5 that early.''})) 