{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 19 A long voyage home. Illustrating, rather subtly, how a weaker player may be lured to his own destruction, Fischer entices his opponent to abandon his passive though solid attempts to settle for a draw. Wrongly convinced that he holds an advantage, Gudmundsson. with 16 e4, gives Fischer the opportunity to launch a long. unclear sacrifidal combination. Gudmundsson makes matters unexpectedly easy with 24 Rb I, but the analysis accompanying the text shows the sacrifice to be sound in all variations. Fischer's performance from here on is typical in its clarity and forcefulness. ' Andrew Soltis: 'Yefim Geller once complained that his fault was he tried to play perfect chess. "I look at a mass of variations and spend time and strength on them, " he told Koblentz. Geller said the ideal was the "golden mean" of Fischer, Tal and Botwinnik, who calculated neither too much nor too little. How much Fischer calculated is not certain. Or, to be more exact, how far. In the following game, he begins a combination at move 15. He may have decided to play it when he visualized how strong 19. ...Bxc3 would be. Or he may have looked further and stopped when he appreciated Black's winning chances after 21. ...Re1+. or he may have kept calculating until he saw the favorable heavy piece endgame that could arise six moves later after 24.fxg6. Whatever the case, it's an impressive example of analysis.'} 1. d4 {Bobby Fischer} Nf6 2. Nf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Solid but passive.'} d5 ({In My 60 Memorable Games, Fischer called 2.Nf3 "solid but passive". However, he went further in Chess Life in October 1963 calling it a "common error". He explained that it gave up the option of meeting the King's Indian with the Sämisch Variation ("undoubtedly his most effective weapon"). And, in a note that looks quaint today, he added that the Exchange Variation of the Grünfeld Defense is "no longer strong" because the center can be pinned -} 2... g6 3. c4 Bg7 4. Nc3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 Nxc3 7. bxc3 c5 8. Bc4 O-O 9. O-O Bg4 {"and White's center must collapse"}) 3. e3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Voluntarily locking in the Bishop lacks energy and reduces White's options.'} g6 4. c4 (4. c3 {Colle,E Bobby Fischer: '4 c3 would lead to the Colle System. Colle,E' Andrew Soltis: '(Fischer) Colle,E'}) 4... Bg7 5. Nc3 O-O 6. Qb3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'AFTER'} 6. Be2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} c5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'it's difficult for White to equalize: A.)'} 7. O-O $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'B.)'} 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. Qb3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Qc7 10. O-O b6 11. a4 Nc6 $17 {Goglidze,n-Botvinnik,M Moscow 1935 Bobby Fischer: 'Black's better. Goglidze,n-Botvinnik,M Moscow 1935'}) (7. dxc5 Qa5 8. cxd5 (8. O-O dxc4 9. Bxc4 Qxc5) 8... Nxd5 9. Qxd5 Bxc3+ 10. Bd2 (10. Kf1 Bg7 11. Bd2 Qc7 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black regains his Pawn at will, with a strong attack)'}) 10... Rd8 $1 11. Bxc3 Qxc3+ 12. bxc3 Rxd5 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'with the superior ending IF'} 13. Rd1 Rxc5 14. Rd8+ Kg7 15. O-O Nc6 16. Re8 b6) 7... cxd4 8. Nxd4 Nc6 9. cxd5 Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Qxd5 11. Bf3 Qc4 12. Nxc6 bxc6 $17 {Aaron,M-Gligoric,S Stockholm izt 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'Aaron,M-Gligoric,S Stockholm izt 1962'}) 6... e6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Another good build-up is 6 ... c6 followed by ... e6, ... b6, ...Bb7, Nbd7, .. . c5, etc.'} (6... c6 { b7-b6, b7, bd7, c6-c5 Bobby Fischer: '/\ b7-b6, Bb7, Nbd7, c6-c5'} 7. Z0 b6 $140 8. Z0 Bb7 9. Z0 Nbd7 10. Z0 c5) 7. Be2 (7. Bd2 {Fischer} dxc4 8. Bxc4 Nc6 9. Be2 b6 10. O-O Bb7 11. Rfd1 Qe7 12. a3 $1 (12. Be1 e5 13. d5 Na5 14. Qc2 e4 15. Nd4 c5 16. dxc6 Nxc6 17. Nxc6 Bxc6 $10) 12... e5 13. d5 (13. dxe5 Nxe5 14. Nxe5 Qxe5) 13... Na5 14. Qa2 e4 15. Nd4 (15. b4 $5 exf3 16. Bxf3 c6 17. bxa5 cxd5 (17... Nxd5 18. Nxd5 cxd5 19. Bb4 Qe6 20. Rac1 $16) 18. axb6 axb6 19. Nb5 $14) 15... c5 (15... Rfd8 $2 16. b4 Nxd5 17. bxa5 Bxd4 18. exd4 e3 19. Bf3 $1 $18 (19. fxe3 $2 Nxc3 20. Bxc3 Qxe3+ 21. Kh1 Qxc3 22. Rac1 Qh3 $17) (19. Be1 $2 exf2+ 20. Kxf2 Qe3+ 21. Kf1 Nxc3 $17)) 16. dxc6 Nxc6 $11 {Cifuentes Parada,R-Kouatly,B/Wijk aan Zee II 1988}) 7... Nc6 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Threatening to win the two bishops with ...Na5. White can't support 8. c5 for long (8. ...b6 9.Bb5 Bd7). Probably the best strategy against Fischer was 8.cxd5 exd5 9.Bd2 and later Na4/Bb4'} 8. Qc2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Probably best (Black threatened ...Na5 winning the two Bishops). As Evans pointed out in Trophy Chess (in an analogous position): "8 cxd5 exd5 pennits Black to solve the problem of his QB. He has the semiopen e~file and good squares for his pieces. It is now White who must fight for equality!"'} (8. cxd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} exd5 $17 {Evans,L}) 8... dxc4 9. Bxc4 e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 10. dxe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'ALSO GOOD IS' Andrew Soltis: 'or'} 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. dxe5 Ng4 12. e6 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'NOT'} 12. f4 $2 Nxe5 $1 13. fxe5 Qh4+ 14. g3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Qxc4 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'ETC'}) 12... Bxe6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. O-O $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Andrew Soltis: '='}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'IF' Andrew Soltis: 'White gets the worst of it after'} 10. d5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '?''} Na5) 10... Ng4 11. O-O ({Bobby Fischer: 'STRONGER IS' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer indicated the text was weaker than'} 11. e6 $1 { Bobby Fischer: '>=' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '!' Fischer: '''} Bxe6 12. Bxe6 fxe6 13. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'when WHite has nothing better than a draw by perpetual check'} (13. Qe4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Nce5 $1 14. Nxe5 $2 Nxf2 $1 15. Qc4 Bxe5 16. Qxe6+ Rf7 17. Qxe5 Nd3+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins), and Black seems to have nothing better than a draw by perpetual after'}) 13... Rxf3 14. gxf3 Qh4 15. fxg4 Qxg4+ 16. Kh1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Qf3+ $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'ETC' Andrew Soltis: '='}) 11... Ncxe5 12. Nxe5 Nxe5 13. Be2 c6 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'The chances are now equal.'} 14. f4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Apparently stronger is' Andrew Soltis: 'Even against the best line'} 14. e4 { Bobby Fischer: 'BUT AFTER' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '''} Qh4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black stands well Fischer: '!''} 15. h3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'IF'} 15. f4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} Ng4 {Andrew Soltis: '. White should be equal with 15. Be3 but...'}) 15... g5 $1 16. f4 (16. Nd1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} f5 17. f4 Ng6 18. fxg5 f4 $1) 16... gxf4 17. Bxf4 (17. Rxf4 Qg3) 17... Kh8 $40 {Bobby Fischer: 'gives Black good prospects along the ventilated g-file.' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '' Fischer: 'g''}) 14... Ng4 $1 15. h3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Bf5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'White was doubtlessly expecting 15 ... Nf6' Andrew Soltis: 'Black is better after this shot'} 16. e4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'with an ideal center.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Provoking a powerful combination. White should abandon his hopes in the center and settle for'} 16. Qb3 { Bobby Fischer: '>=' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '''} Nf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'leaves White with a slightly backward e-pawn and a slightly bad bishop at c1. Fischer gave the key variation as'} 17. Qxb7 Ne4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '!''} 18. Qxc6 ({More resistant is} 18. Nxe4 Bxe4 19. Bf3 {when Black has a significant initiative - but White has a pawn.}) 18... Rc8 19. Qa6 Nxc3 20. bxc3 Bxc3 21. Ba3 Bxa1 22. Bxf8 Bd4 $1 23. exd4 Qxd4+ 24. Kh1 Kxf8 $15 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black is better, but White has excellent drawing chances.' Andrew Soltis: 'with advantage. Fischer: '''}) 16... Qd4+ 17. Kh1 Nf2+ 18. Rxf2 $8 {Bobby Fischer: ''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'All forced. NOT'} 18. Kh2 Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'and Black wins quickly after'} 19. Bd3 $2 Ng3 $1) 18... Qxf2 19. exf5 Bxc3 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'This clean-cut line reduces White's options. Inexact would be' Andrew Soltis: 'The knight defends better than the bishop attacks after'} 19... Rfe8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '''} 20. Ne4 $1 Qe1+ 21. Kh2 gxf5 (21... Bd4 22. Be3 $1 Qxa1 23. Bxd4) 22. Ng3 {Bobby Fischer: 'and if'} Bd4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} 23. Be3 $1) 20. bxc3 Rae8 21. Bd3 ({Forced} 21. Bd1 Re1+ 22. Kh2 Qg1+ 23. Kg3 Rxd1) 21... Re1+ 22. Kh2 Qg1+ 23. Kg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Rfe8 {Bobby Fischer: 'Just as complicated is 23...gxf5 24 Bxf5 Rfe8 25 Bxh7+ Kg7 26 Qf5 etc'} (23... gxf5 {Fischer} 24. Bxf5 Rfe8 25. Bxh7+ Kg7 26. Qf5) 24. Rb1 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Also bad is'} 24. Qf2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} R8e3+ $1 25. Bxe3 Rxe3+ 26. Qxe3 Qxe3+ $19) ({Bobby Fischer: 'The toughest defense is' Andrew Soltis: 'The losing move. White has to insert an exchange of pawns on g6 in order to deny Black another attacking line (...gxf5! followed by ... Kh8/ ...Rg8+ or ...R8e6-g6+). The pertinent variation runs'} 24. fxg6 $1 hxg6 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now a.)'} (24... R8e3+ {Fischer} 25. Bxe3 Rxe3+ 26. Kg4 Qxa1 27. gxf7+ Kxf7 (27... Kg7 28. Bxh7) 28. Bc4+ Kg7 29. Qd2 $1 $18 {Schach Echo} Qxc3 30. Qd7+ Kh6 31. Qd6+ Kg7 $11 {PS}) 25. Bxg6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'b.)'} 25. Rb1 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} R8e3+ $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'if' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '!''} 26. Bxe3 {Bobby Fischer: 'leads to the same)'} (26. Kg4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Qh2 27. Bxe3 Rxe3) 26... Rxe3+ 27. Kg4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 27. Kh4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Qh2 28. Qf2 Rxh3+ $1) 27... Qh2 28. Qf2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 28. Bxg6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Qg3+ 29. Kh5 Qxf4 $1 30. Bf5 Rg3 $1 31. Bg4 Kg7 32. Qc1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 32. Kh4 Rxg4+ 33. hxg4 Qh2+ $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'mates'}) 32... Re3 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 28... Rxd3 (28... Rxh3 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} 29. Bxg6 $1 fxg6 30. Rh1 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=!' Bobby Fischer: 'holds' Andrew Soltis: '=!'}) 29. Rb2 $1 (29. Rxb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Rxh3 30. Rb8+ Kh7 31. f5 Rh6 $1 $19) 29... Rxc3 30. Rd2 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White has drawing chances even though a Pawn behind.' Andrew Soltis: 'and Fischer believed he had drawing chanes. Fischer: '''}) 25... R1e2 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 25... R8e2 26. Bxf7+ Kh8 (26... Kxf7 27. Qf5+ $11 {PS}) 27. Qf5 Rxg2+ (27... Qxg2+ 28. Kh4 Qf2+ 29. Kg5 Rg1+ (29... Qg3+ 30. Qg4 (30. Kh5 Re5) (30. Kh6 Qh4+ 31. Qh5 Re6+ 32. Bg6 Qe7 33. Bb2 R1e3 34. c4+ Kg8 $19 {[%cal Re3h3] h3}) 30... Rg2 31. Qxg3 Rxg3+ 32. Kf6 Rgg1 $13 {PS}) 30. Kh5 (30. Kh6 Qh4+ 31. Bh5 Rg7 {[%cal Re2e7] Te2-e7-+} 32. Qf8+ Rg8 33. Qf5 $11) 30... Qf3+ (30... Kg7 $11 {PS}) 31. Kh6 {Schach Echo}) 28. Kh4 Qf2+ 29. Kh5 Qf3+ {Andrew Soltis: '? PS'} (29... Kg7 30. Bc4 Qf3+ 31. Kh4 Qg3+ $11 {PS}) 30. Kh6 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White wins.'}) (25... fxg6 26. Qxg6+ $11 {PS}) 26. Bxf7+ Kh8 (26... Kxf7 27. Qf5+ $11 {PS}) 27. Qf5 Rxg2+ 28. Kh4 Qe1+ 29. Kh5 Kg7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '(threatening 30 .. Rh8+)'} 30. Bxe8 Qxe8+ 31. Kh4 Qd8+ 32. Qg5+ Rxg5 33. fxg5 Qd1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'with an easy win'}) 24... gxf5 { h8; 8e6  Bobby Fischer: 'Threatening either Kh8; R8e6 with a devastating check to follow on the g-file.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: ' h8; 8e6 ''} 25. Bd2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '?''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'no better is' Andrew Soltis: 'Now'} 25. Bxf5 {Andrew Soltis: 'loses to'} R1e2 26. Bxh7+ Kh8 27. Qf5 Rxg2+ 28. Kh4 Rg7 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '(among others) wins' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer: '-+''}) (25. c4 $142 {(Fischer) PS}) 25... Rxb1 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Now it's easy because the bishops run out of squares'} 26. Qxb1 Qxb1 27. Bxb1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Final Position after'} Re2 {Bobby Fischer: 'This is what Gudmundsson overlooked. Ifnow 28 i.el l:el picks off one of the Bishops. So ... White resigns'} (27... Re2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Resigns in view of 28.Bc1 Re1'} 28. Bc1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'} Re1 $19) 0-1

