{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 23 No holds barred. Their first encounter after Tal became world champion proves to be an old-fashioned slugfest. Typically aggressive, Fischer rapidly achieves a winning bind, but unwisely permits Tal to touch off 'a dazzling array of fireworks' with 14 ... Nxe5. The struggle see-saws for seven moves before settling in a perpetual check. The quality of this confrontation left little doubt that, in time, Fischer would yet take Tal's measure. He did just that less than a year later, after Tal had lost his return match with Botvinnik (Game 32). Reading Tal's palm, Fischer predicted: "The next world champion will be ... Bobby Fischer!" ' Mikhail Tal: 'Any contest between these two giants arouses interest, and this could have gone either way. After a seesaw struggle it settles in a draw by perpetual check -- a valid if frustrating result.' Obwohl nur 21 Züge lang und dann auch noch mit Remis endend ist die Begegnung zwischen dem frischgebackenen Weltmeister Tal und dem 17jährigen Fischer die zentrale Partie der Schacholympiade in Leipzig gewesen.} 1. e4 {Fischer/Tal/Huebner Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Huebner,R Bobby Fischer Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Knaak'} e6 {Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Prepared in advance by Tal and Koblenz for this encounter despite Tal's indifferent success with the French Defense. They reasoned -- correctly -- that Fischer was not totally at ease with the Winawer.' Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'What is this, immodesty? Even the most subjective of chess players has never given an exclamation mark to this move, which has been known for a long time. But should this move deserve approval (for reasons not appertaining to chess), then the immodesty of the annotator is not so great. He really only deserves half an exclamation mark, the other half being due to A. Koblents, since it was during the process of preparing for this game that the idea of playing the French Defence occurred to us, although I have adopted this opening very, very rarely, and without great success. Even so, the choice of such a variation must have been an unpleasant surprise for Fischer, since positions of this type have occurred in his games very rarely, and a study of his games showed that the American Champion feels much less confident in unfamiliar positions. To be honest I should admit that, after deciding prior to the game to play the French Defence, at the board it took me about 10 minutes to decide on the advance of my e-pawn.' Tal soll 12 Minuten für diesen Zug gebraucht haben.} 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Ba5 {Mikhail Tal: 'Unpopular retreat -- but never refuted. A variation which has only very recently risen from the grave. Back in 1954 the 9th game of the Smyslov-Botvinnik match, and the Unzicker-Botvinnik encounter at the Amsterdam Olympiad, created the firm opinion that the system with 5...Ba5 was unfavourable for Black. Five years passed, and in one of the 1960 chess bulletins a note by international master Konstantinopolsky appeared, in which new plans found for Black in this variation were described. I remember how, before the match with Botvinnik, Koblents and I spent some considerable time playing through these variations, though at the time we were unable to test them, since in the 1960 match Botvinnik did not adopt this system. A short time later chess magazines all over the world, including our (Latvian) magazine, published the interesting game Matanovic-Mititelu, played in the Budapest Zonal Tournament, in which Mititelu won as Black. In my game with Fischer the following moves were made very quickly.'} ({A dubious alternative to the usual} 5... Bxc3+ {(Bobby Fischer) see Game 24. Fischer, R-Darga,K Berlin (W) t-ch FRG-USA Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Fischer,R-Darga,K Berlin (W) t-ch FRG-USA'}) 6. b4 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Alekhine's recommendation.' Fischer/Tal/Huebner: '!' Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Alekhine,A'} cxd4 {Mikhail Tal: 'Also playable is 6...cxb4 7. Nb5 Nc6 8. Nf3 a6 9. Nd6+ Kf8 10. Bd3 b3+ 11. Kf1 Bc7.'} (6... cxb4 {Bobby Fischer} 7. Nb5 $40 {yields a potent attack.}) 7. Qg4 {Bis heute ist die Güte dieses Zuges ungeklärt. Mit 7.b5 kann es Weiß ruhiger angehen. Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Knaak: 'Bis heute ist die Güte dieses Zuges ungeklärt. Mit 7.b5 kann es Weiß ruhiger angehen.''} Ne7 ({After} 7... Kf8 {Bobby Fischer} 8. bxa5 dxc3 9. a4 $1 {followed by 10 Ba3+ is strong (Lilienthal and Zagoriansky) Fischer/Tal/Huebner: ' a3+; Lilienthal-Zagoryansky'}) 8. bxa5 ({Also good is} 8. Nb5 {Bobby Fischer}) 8... dxc3 9. Qxg7 Rg8 10. Qxh7 Nbc6 {Mikhail Tal: '!' Mikhail Tal: 'Theory says White stands better on 10...Nd7 11. Nf3 Nf8 12. Qd3 Qxa5 13. h4! (Smyslov-Botvinnik, 9th match game 1954.) Improvement number one.'} ({On Mikhail Tal: 'Against Smyslov, Botvinnik played more passively:'} 10... Nd7 {Bobby Fischer Mikhail Tal: 'and after'} 11. Nf3 Qc7 (11... Nf8 12. Qd3 Qxa5 13. Bg5 $1 {fairly quickly found himself in a difficult position.}) 12. Bb5 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. O-O d4 $5 {(Archives)} 15. Nxd4 (15. Bg5 {Huebner,R} Bc6 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Qh4+ Ke8 18. Ng5 Qxe5 19. f4 $14 {Euwe ECO 2.Auflage 16/17 Fußnote 80} Qf5 $17) 15... Qxe5 16. Qd3 $16 {is better for White. Fischer/Tal/Huebner: ' Fischer / Hübner'} Qg7 {Huebner,R} (16... O-O-O {Huebner,R} 17. Qc4+ (17. Qxc3+ $2 Bc6 $19) (17. g3 Nd5 (17... Bb5 $2 18. Qxc3+ Nc6 19. Nxb5 $18) 18. Rb1 Qc7 $15) 17... Kb8 18. Bf4 Rxg2+ 19. Kh1 (19. Kxg2 Qxf4 $40 {-+  20... e5}) 19... Rxh2+ 20. Bxh2 Rh8 21. f4 (21. Nf3 Bc6 22. Qxc6 Rxh2+ $17) 21... Qe4+ 22. Rf3 Nf5 23. Qd3 (23. Qe2 $2 Ng3+ $19) 23... Qxd4 $19) (16... Rd8 {Huebner,R} 17. Qe3 Qg7 (17... Qd5 18. f3 Ba4 19. Ne2 Nf5 20. Qxc3 (20. Qf2 Qc4 $17 21. Bf4 Bxc2 22. Rac1 Rd2) 20... Bb5 21. Nf4 Qd4+ 22. Qxd4 Nxd4 23. Rf2 Nb3 $19) 18. g3 e5 (18... Nd5 19. Qe4 $10) (18... Ba4 19. Ne2 Nd5 (19... Bxc2 20. Qxc3) 20. Qe4 Bb5 21. Re1 $10 (21. a4 $10)) 19. Nf3 $14) 17. g3 e5 18. Ne2 Bb5 19. Qf3 Qh7 20. Re1 Bc6 21. Qe3 O-O-O $17 22. Qxe5 $2 (22. Nxc3 $2 Nf5) 22... Qxh2+) 11. Nf3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Diagram [#]'} ({Mikhail Tal: 'The analysis in Konstantinopolsky's article was devoted to the continuation'} 11. f4 {(Bobby Fischer) bolsters the centre but shuts in the queen's bishop and weakens the dark squares. Mikhail Tal: '. We can refer directly to this article anyone wishing to have a wander through a maze of innumerable complications, but in our game there was a completely different story.'}) 11... Qc7 {Mikhail Tal: 'There's nothing wrong with 11...Qxa5 12. Rb1 Qc7 13. Bf4 Bd7 14. Bg3 O-O-O (Damjanovich-Udovcich, Yugoslavia 1963).'} ({On} 11... Qxa5 {Bobby Fischer} 12. Ng5 $1 (12. Rb1 {Fischer/Tal/Huebner} Qc7 13. Bf4 Bd7 14. Bg3 O-O-O {Damjanovic-Yudovich Yugoslavia 1963}) 12... Rf8 13. f4 $16 {(followed by the advance of the h-pawn) ties Black up. Fischer/Tal/Huebner: ' h2-h4-h5-...'} Bd7 {Fischer/Tal/Huebner} 14. Be2 {(Levy)}) {Mikhail Tal: 'In playing'} 12. Bb5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'Harmoniously pursuing development without losing time.' Fischer/Tal/Huebner: '!' Fischer/Tal/Huebner: '"Sharper and boosted by Fischer's advocacy but still not necessarily better than 12 B-KB4." (Wicker 79)' Mikhail Tal: 'After this move White probably cannot count on obtaining an opening advantage. Fischer reckoned that it would be unfavourable for Black to play' Aus heutiger Sicht kann höchstens 12.f4 Chancen auf vorteilhaftes Spiel geben Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Knaak: 'Aus heutiger Sicht kann höchstens 12.f4 Chancen auf vorteilhaftes Spiel geben''} ({Also playable is Mikhail Tal: 'Black is set more difficult problems after'} 12. Bf4 {Bobby Fischer Fischer/Tal/Huebner: '! This move is recommended by Tal, although Fischer prefers 12 Bb5.' Mikhail Tal: 'as occurred in the game Unzicker-Dückstein (Zurich 1959).'} Bd7 13. Be2 O-O-O 14. Qd3 Qxa5 15. O-O Rg4 16. Bg3 {Unzicker, W-Dueckstein, A Zürich 1959 Fischer/Tal/Huebner: 'Unzicker,W-Dueckstein,A Zürich 1959'}) 12... Bd7 {Fischer/Tal/Huebner: '"Now both White's KNP, which Black seriously