{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 24 Asking for trouble. The Winawer Variation has given Fischer consistent trouble. He has had the utmost difficulty cracking Black's tortoise-like shell; even his successes are unconvincing. Maintaining the same line of attack year after year has provided his opponents with ample opportunity to sharpen their defences. Darga's 12 ... f6 obliges Fischer, in order to get something out of the opening, to speculate on a pawn sacrifice (13 Ba3!?). Though Darga's reaction may not be ideal, he proceeds sensibly and equalises. Underestimating Fischer's chances, however, he falls prey to a scintillating middlegame attack. And so, once again, by virtue of his native ability, Fischer avoids the retribution that is the usual price for failing to secure an advantage in the opening. '} 1. e4 {Fischer u.a. Bobby Fischer} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ ({Bobby Fischer: 'For'} 5... Ba5 {Fischer,R-Tal,M Leipzig ol 1960 Fischer,R-Tal,M Leipzig ol 1960 Fischer,R-Tal,M Leipzig ol 1960 Fischer,R-Tal,M Leipzig ol 1960 Bobby Fischer: 'see Game 23. Fischer,R-Tal,M Leipzig ol 1960'}) 6. bxc3 Ne7 7. a4 {Smyslov,V Smyslov,V Smyslov,V Smyslov,V Bobby Fischer: 'Diagram [#] Smyslov's favourite, largely responsible for Botvinnik's giving up the Winawer Variation.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Sharper is'} 7. Qg4 {Bobby Fischer: '. I felt that Black's carapace could be cracked only by positional means, but my results have been somewhat disheartening.'}) 7... Qc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'I may yet be forced to admit that the Winawer is sound. But I doubt it! The defence is anti-positional and weakens the kingside.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'More usual is'} 7... Nbc6 8. Nf3 Qa5 9. Qd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 9. Bd2 Bd7 10. Be2 c4 11. h4 $5 f6 12. h5 fxe5 13. h6 gxh6 14. Nxe5 Nxe5 15. dxe5 O-O-O 16. Rxh6 Ng6 $17 {Fischer,R-Padevsky,N Varna ol 1962 Fischer,R-Padevsky,N Varna ol 1962 Fischer,R-Padevsky,N Varna ol 1962 Fischer,R-Padevsky,N Varna ol 1962 Bobby Fischer: 'Black has secured an advantage; Fischer, R-Padevsky,N Varna ol 1962'}) 9... Bd7 10. Bd3 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now Black has two main continuations:'} c4 (10... f6 $1 11. O-O fxe5 12. Nxe5 (12. dxe5 $10 {Smyslov,V-Uhlmann,W Habana 1964 Smyslov,V-Uhlmann,W Habana 1964 Smyslov,V-Uhlmann,W Habana 1964 Smyslov,V-Uhlmann,W Habana 1964 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'as Smyslov tried against Uhlmann at Havana, 1964' Fischer u.a.: '='}) 12... Nxe5 13. dxe5 O-O 14. c4 Qxd2 15. Bxd2 Bc6 $10 {Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Stockholm izt 1962 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Stockholm izt 1962 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Stockholm izt 1962 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Stockholm izt 1962 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Stockholm izt 1962' Fischer u.a.: '='}) 11. Be2 f6 12. Ba3 O-O-O (12... Ng6 13. O-O O-O-O 14. Bd6 $16 {Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Buenos Aires 1960 Bobby Fischer: 'White keeps the edge; Fischer, R-Uhlmann,W Buenos Aires 1960'}) 13. O-O Nf5 14. Rfe1 Be8 15. g4 $5 Nfe7 16. Bf1 Bd7 $10 {Fischer,R-Weinstein,R ch-USA 1960 Fischer,R-Weinstein,R ch-USA 1960 Fischer,R-Weinstein,R ch-USA 1960 Fischer,R-Weinstein,R ch-USA 1960 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Fischer,R-Weinstein,R ch-USA 1960' Fischer u.a.: '='}) 8. Nf3 b6 { a6  a6  a6  a6 Bobby Fischer: 'The idea is to eliminate the bad bishop with ... Ba6.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'An alternative is'} 8... Bd7 9. Bd3 Nbc6) 9. Bb5+ $1 Bd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More radical is'} 9... Kf8 {Bobby Fischer: '!?'} 10. Bd3 Ba6) ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 9... Nec6 {Bobby Fischer: '(after 9 ... Nbc6 Black can no longer enforce ... Ba6)'} 10. O-O Ba6 11. Ng5 h6 12. Nh3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'is in White's favour.'}) 10. Bd3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'After the continuation'} 10. O-O Bxb5 11. axb5 a5 12. Ng5 h6 13. Nh3 Nd7 14. Nf4 O-O $10 {Ivkov,B-Byrne,R Sousse izt 1967 Ivkov,B-Byrne,R Sousse izt 1967 Ivkov,B-Byrne,R Sousse izt 1967 Ivkov,B-Byrne,R Sousse izt 1967 Bobby Fischer