{GAME N. 26 Time will tell. The opening has always been regarded as the old warrior's weak point, and were it not for this handicap who knows how far Reshevsky might have gone toward the summit? Whatever the case, being familiar with the latest wrinkles does have the merit of saving time on the clock and, hopefully, of catching an opponent off guard. Although Reshevsky is bested in the theoretical duel (after 13 Bf3) his practical cunning enables him to extricate himself-at a great cost of time. In the end it is the clock, as much as Fischer's persistence, that causes his downfall.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 ({Throughout the match Reshevsky played the 'Accelerated Dragon', not allowing Fischer his favourite set-up after} 2... d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 {followed by 0-0-0.}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 {Allowing White the chance to get a Maroczy bind (with 5 c4). Apparently Reshevsky had booked up on this for the match. Black's idea is to dispense with an early ... d6 and possibly strive for a later ... d5 (thereby saving a tempo).} 5. Nc3 ({In match game 11 I got an edge with the more traditional Garry Kasparov: 'In the 8th and 10th games there was a solid positional dispute in the Maróczy set-up –'} 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Be3 O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'etc. Subsequently, on his way to the top, Bobby was happy to play this variation as Black (Game No.96).'} 10. Qd2 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (11) Los Angeles 1961}) 5... Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Be2 ({In the 4th and 6th games of the match I continued with Garry Kasparov: 'The two players also tried'} 7. Bc4 O-O 8. Bb3 {Garry Kasparov: '. In the match there occurred'} Ng4 ({Garry Kasparov: ', bringing to mind the opening catastrophe'} 8... Na5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 9. e5 Ne8 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 10. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 11. Ne6 $3 $18 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1958 Garry Kasparov: '!!' Garry Kasparov: 'and wins (Fischer-Reshevsky, New York 1958/59). Bobby – a genuine professional from his youth – picked up this idea from the magazine Shakhmaty v SSSR, where the twin game Bastrikov-Shamkovich (Sochi 1958) was published.'}) (8... Z0) 9. Qxg4 Nxd4 {and White got a clear advantage both with Qh4 and Qd1 respectively.} 10. Qd1 $16 {Fischer, R-Reshevsky,S (4) New York 1961 Garry Kasparov: '(4th game)'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 10. Qh4 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (6) Los Angeles 1961 Garry Kasparov: '(6th) with some advantage to White.'})) 7... O-O ({On} 7... d5 $2 8. Bb5 $18 {wins a pawn.}) 8. f4 {Despite his familiarity with the Dragon Variation, I felt Reshevsky really didn't know the latest wrinkles in Alekhine's Attack.} ({The point of Black's 'accelerated fianchetto' becomes apparent after the indifferent Garry Kasparov: 'A subtlety of the accelerated Dragon:'} 8. O-O $2 d5 $1 9. exd5 Nb4 $11 {Garry Kasparov: 'with equality.'}) 8... d6 ({Now on Garry Kasparov: 'Now if'} 8... d5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'there is'} 9. e5 Ne4 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is inferior:'} (9... Ne8 10. Bf3 Nc7 11. Qd2 {and Black has a cramped position.}) 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. Bc4 $18 {gives White a winning ending; Olafsson,F-Larsen,B Wageningen 1957 Garry Kasparov: 'Olafsson-Larsen, Wageningen 1957'}) 9. Nb3 {Diagram [#]} ({Another well-known trap: if} 9. O-O {, then} Qb6 $1 {(Romanishin-Sosonko, Tilburg 1979) .}) 9... Be6 {I was right. This is the old (and second-rate) move. Garry Kasparov: '"I was right. This is the old (and second-rate) move." (Fischer).'} ({Correct is Garry Kasparov: 'As is well known, the inclusion of'} 9... a5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} 10. a4 Be6 {Garry Kasparov: '(Villard-Keres, Parnu 1937)'} 11. Nd4 $2 ({after Garry Kasparov: ', completely neutralizes the'} 11. g4 {Garry Kasparov: 'thrust, since after'} Nb4 {Black's knight can no longer be dislodged by a3} (11... d5 $5 {) the knight can no longer be driven away by a2-a3.})) ({the best White has is Garry Kasparov: ', and so all that remains is the modest'} 11. O-O Rc8 {with an equal position}) (11. Z0) ({, or} 11. Bf3 {(Bronstein-Korchnoi, Leningrad 1959). Nevertheless, modern theory also considers 9...Be6 to be acceptable.}) 11... Qb6 $1 12. Nxe6 Qxe3 13. Nxf8 Ng4 {and Black has a strong attack; Makievsky,N-Veressov,G URS 1954 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'is also unfavourable for White (Euwe)'}) 10. g4 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} d5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Reshevsky is following the famous Alekhine-Botvinnik game (Nottingham 1936), which – just think about it! – was played before his very eyes.'} ({However, towards the end of the century} 10... Na5) ({or} 10... Rc8 {became more popular.}) 11. f5 (11. e5 d4 $1 {Levenfish-Botvinnik, Moscow 1936.}) 11... Bc8 ({Lipnitsky recommends Garry Kasparov: '"Lipnitsky recommends'} 11... gxf5 $5 {It's interesting. Garry Kasparov: 'It's interesting," writes Fischer, but for the moment no one has been found who is willing to try this: after'} 12. gxf5 Bc8 13. Rg1 {White's initiative is more dangerous than in the game.}) 12. exd5 Nb4 {Diagram [#]} 13. Bf3 $1 {The modern way-White maintains his centre pawn and sacs two pawns on the kings ide where Black must expose his king to get them. Garry Kasparov: '"The modern way – White maintains his centre pawn and sacs two pawns on the kingside where Black must expose his king to get them." (Fischer)'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Alekhine played'} 13. d6 {has been known to be only a draw since the famous Alekhine - Botvinnik encounter, Nottingham 1936, which continued: Garry Kasparov: ', and after'} Qxd6 $1 (13... exd6 $2 14. g5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 14. Bc5 Qf4 15. Rf1 Qxh2 16. Bxb4 Nxg4 $1 17. Bxg4 Qg3+ 18. Rf2 Qg1+ $11 {with a perpetual. Garry Kasparov: 'Botvinnik gained a draw by perpetual check.'}) ({Another weak line is} 13. fxg6 hxg6 14. Bf3 Bxg4 $1 15. Bxg4 Nxg4 16. Qxg4 Nxc2+ 17. Kf2 Nxa1 18. Rxa1 Rc8 $1 {with a good game. If} 19. Bd4 $2 Rc4 {(Panov)}) 13... gxf5 14. a3 ({The wild play after} 14. g5 Ng4 15. Bc5 Na6 16. Bd4 e5 $1 17. dxe6 $1 {(Bondarevsky-Alatortsev, Tbilisi 1937) was not to Fischer's taste. His attention had been attracted by another, inconspicuous game, played in one of the Baltic tournaments.}) 14... fxg4 15. Bg2 $1 ({On Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 15. axb4 {Garry Kasparov: 'White did not like'} gxf3 16. Qxf3 Bg4 {followed by ... Bh5-g6. White's whole idea is to keep Black's lightsquared bishop restricted to the queenside.} 17. Qg2 Bh5 {and ...Bg6: "White's whole idea is to keep Black's queen's bishop restricted to the queenside."}) 15... Na6 (15... Nbxd5 {(or Nfxd5)} 16. Nxd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 e6 18. Bg2 $1 {is inferior.}) 16. Qd3 $1 ({Nei's improvement over Garry Kasparov: '"Nei's improvement over'} 16. Qe2 Bf5 $1 {and the bishop retreats to g6, when necessary, defending the kingside. Garry Kasparov: 'and the bishop retreats to g6, when necessary, defending the kingside."  (Fischer)'}) 16... e6 {The best choice in a difficult position. Up to here we had both played briskly, but now Reshevsky began to consume time on the clock. Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'Played after a long think: Sammy was trying to find his way in an unfamiliar situation. In Fischer's opinion, this is "the best choice in a difficult position", but in my view it is a positional mistake: the opening of the centre can only be to White's advantage.'} ({After Garry Kasparov: 'After the slow'} 16... Nd7 {Garry Kasparov: '(Yakovich-Lerner, Kuybyshev 1986)'} 17. O-O-O (17. h3 $1 Ne5 18. Qe2 g3 19. Bf4 Ng6 20. Bxg3) 17... Ne5 18. Qe2 {Black's game is lifeless. White has h3 and Bd4 in the offing.}) ({or} 16... Nc7 17. O-O-O Nce8 18. h3 $1 g3 19. Ne2 Nd6 20. Nxg3 Bd7 {(Oll-Pigusov, Beijing 1997)} 21. Nd4 $1 {Black also fails to solve all his problems.}) ({Apparently the best way out was} 16... Qd6 $1 17. O-O-O {, and now} Nh5 $1 ({but not} 17... Bd7 {– the source game: Nei-Pitskaar, Tallinn 1951 – after which Fischer was probably planning} 18. h3 $1) 18. h3 Nf4 {, exchanging one of the powerful white bishops (Yakovich-Sadler, Koge 1997).}) 17. O-O-O ({Black gets the initiative after} 17. d6 $2 Nd5 $1 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. Qxd5 Re8 {etc.}) 17... Nxd5 {The lesser evil.} ({On Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 17... exd5 {Garry Kasparov: 'there would have followed'} 18. h3 g3 19. Bd4 {is strong.}) 18. h3 $1 g3 19. Rhg1 Qd6 $1 {Reshevsky is putting up a firstrate defence. Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '"Reshevsky is putting up a first-rate defence." (Fischer)'} ({And indeed, after} 19... Nac7 $2 20. Bxd5 $1 {and Rxg3}) ({or} 19... Bxc3 $2 20. bxc3 Qh4 21. Bxd5 exd5 22. Qxd5 {the black king is on the verge of disaster.}) ({But Hübner recommends instead} 19... Qh4 20. Bxd5 exd5 21. Bd4 Bxd4 {. However, here too after} 22. Nxd4 $1 ({rather than} 22. Rxg3+ Kh8 23. Nxd4 Nc5 24. Qe3 Ne4 {with chances of a defence – Hübner}) {, White has very unpleasant pressure:} 22... Z0 (22... Nc5 23. Qe3 Kh8 24. Nf3 $1 {etc}) (22... Kh8 23. Nxd5 Rg8 ({not} 23... Qg5+ 24. Kb1 Qxd5 $2 25. Qxg3 {and wins}) 24. Ne2 $1) (22... Qf4+ 23. Kb1 Nc5 24. Qb5 b6 (24... Ne4 25. Nxe4 Qxe4 26. Rxg3+ Kh8 27. Qc5 $1) 25. Nde2 Qh4 26. Rd4 {, picking up the g3- and d5-pawns.})) 20. Bxd5 exd5 {Diagram [#] Despite his material deficit, it is obvious White has a strong attack. His problem is how to land a haymaker. Garry Kasparov: '"Despite his material deficit, it is obvious White has a strong attack. His problem is how to land a haymaker." (Fischer)'} 21. Nxd5 $2 {This gives Black a little breathing space. Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Over-hasty: this pawn will never run away from White.'} ({Nowadays I would have played Garry Kasparov: '"Nowadays I would have played'} 21. Bd4 $1 {without giving it a second thought. After} Bxd4 22. Rxg3+ Bg7 (22... Kh8 23. Qxd4+ f6 24. Rf3 {leads to a bind}) 23. Rdg1 Qh6+ 24. Kb1 Be6 25. Rxg7+ Qxg7 26. Rxg7+ Kxg7 27. Qg3+ Kh8 ({White wins after Garry Kasparov: 'if'} 27... Kf6 28. Qd6 Kg7 29. Nxd5 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'wins'}) 28. Qe5+ Kg8 29. Qg5+ ({it would appear that} 29. Nd4 {is even simpler – G.K.}) 29... Kh8 30. Qf6+ Kg8 31. Ne2 $18 {with a winning bind. Garry Kasparov: 'with a winning bind." (Fischer); for example:'} Rfd8 32. Nf4 Nc7 33. Na5 {etc.}) 21... Kh8 22. Bf4 ({Garry Kasparov: '"'} 22. Bd4 {is less convincing now after} Bxd4 (22... f6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'is more tenacious:'} 23. Rxg3 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer condemned this line on account of'} Qxd5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (23... Bf5 $1 24. Qf3 Be4 25. Qg4 Bh6+ (25... Rg8 26. Ne3 $1) {, but even here after} 26. Be3 $1 (26. Ne3 Qc6 {and ...Rg8 is not so clear}) 26... f5 27. Qh4 Bxe3+ 28. Rxe3 {White has an obvious advantage.}) 24. Bxf6 $1) (22... Z0) 23. Qxd4+ f6 $16 {and White has no forced win.} 24. Qh4 $1 {Black cannot escape:} Bf5 ({or} 24... Qe5 25. Rxg3 Bd7 26. Nf4 $1 Bf5 27. Nd4) 25. Nf4 Qe5 (25... Qc7 26. Nd4) 26. Rde1 {.}) 22... Qg6 23. Qd2 {Overlooking his reply. Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '"Overlooking his reply.'} ({Vukovic suggests Garry Kasparov: '"Vukovich suggests'} 23. Qf3 {but} Bf5 $1 24. Rxg3 (24. Ne7 $1 Qf6 25. Nxf5 (25. Nd4 $2 Bxc2 $1) 25... Z0 ({, not fearing} 25... Qxb2+ 26. Kd2 Rad8+ 27. Ke1 Rfe8+ (27... Qxc2 28. Bd6 $1) 28. Kf1) ({, and registering an advantage for White after} 25... Qxf5 26. Rxg3 ({or} 26. Nd4 {.}))) 24... Qc6 {holds. Garry Kasparov: 'holds." (Fischer)'}) 23... Bxh3 $1 {Reshevsky chopped it off fasthe doesn't wait to be asked twice. Garry Kasparov: '"Reshevsky chopped it off fast – he doesn't wait to be asked twice. Black has succeeded in transferring this bishop to the kingside and in sealing the g-file." (Fischer)'} 24. Rxg3 Bg4 {Black has succeeded in transferring this bishop to the kings ide and in sealing the g-file.} 25. Rh1 {So White begins operations on the h-file! Garry Kasparov: 'Henceforth White's hopes in this game are associated with play along the h-file.'} Rfe8 ({If} 25... Rad8 $6 {, then the reply} 26. Qg2 $1 f5 27. c3 {is strong.}) 26. Ne3 {Diagram [#]} Qe4 $2 {Anxious to simplify and ease the tension (in time-pressure) Reshevsky finally goes astray. Garry Kasparov: 'A time-trouble blunder, leading to a rapid defeat.'} ({The tempting Garry Kasparov: '"The tempting'} 26... Qf6 {is refuted by} 27. Nxg4 Qxb2+ 28. Kd1 Rad8 29. Rd3 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'etc.'}) ({But simply} 26... f5 $1 {holds, e.g.} {Garry Kasparov: '."(if'} 27. Qh2 (27. Z0) (27. Qg2 $1 {is stronger, with a dangerous initiative, although after} Rad8 $1 ({or} 27... Rac8 28. c3 Qf7) 28. c3 Rd7 29. Nxg4 fxg4 30. Rxg4 Qd3 31. Rg3 Qe4 {Black's resources are still far from exhausted.}) 27... Kg8 $16 {Garry Kasparov: ')." (Fischer)'}) 27. Qh2 $1 {Now the roof caves in on Black's kingside.} Be6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black's defence has been broken, and the game concludes with a series of simple, but pretty blows.'} ({The late Abe Turner suggested Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 27... Bf5 {but then Garry Kasparov: 'Bobby gives'} 28. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 29. Nxf5+ Qxf5 30. Nd4 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'and wins.'}) 28. Rxg7 $1 {That does it! Once this bishop is gone, White has a field day. "Why didn't White play 28 Nd2 ... ? So far as I can see Black can then resign. Or am I missing something?" (A. R. B. Thomas in a letter to Chess). Right, Mr. Thomas! Garry Kasparov: '!?'} (28. Nd2 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 28... Kxg7 29. Qh6+ ({Or} 29. Rg1+ Kf8 30. Qh4 {.}) 29... Kg8 ({On} 29... Kh8 {mates in two.} 30. Be5+ $18) 30. Rg1+ Qg6 31. Rxg6+ fxg6 32. Nd4 Rad8 33. Be5 Rd7 34. Nxe6 Rxe6 35. Ng4 Rf7 36. Qg5 Rf1+ 37. Kd2 h5 38. Qd8+ (38. Qd8+ {After} Rf8 39. Nh6+ $18 {taxes even Reshevsky's defensive ability.}) 1-0

