{Andrew Soltis: 'In the 1950s, Samuel Reshevsky compiled a remarkable match record, defeating Najdorf, Gligoric, Lombardy, Bisguier and Donald Byrne. After he overpowered Benko, in a ten game match in September 1960, veteran columnist Hermann Helms wrote in the New York World, Telegram & Sun : "Reshevsky looms as the powerhouse on the North American chess scene despite the ascendancy of teenage Bobby Fischer. A match between Reshevsky and Fischer would certainly be welcomed." Ten months later, a best of 16 game match between the two began. But the tension between them had been palpable for years. When they competed at Buenos Aires 1960, Reshevsky was quoted by Taimanov as saying "I would settle for 19th - if Fischer placed 20th." The ill will helped poison, and ultimately shorten their match, which became the first Fischer fiasco to gain widespread media attention.'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 g6 5. Nc3 (5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be2 Bg7 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (8) Los Angeles 1961}) 5... Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Be2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Announcing that he had something special. Two years before at a U.S. Championship game, Reshevsky fell into a book trap'} (7. Bc4 O-O 8. Bb3 Ng4 (8... Na5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'and Black could have resigned after'} 9. e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Ne8 10. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 11. Ne6 $3 $18 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1958 Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-!!' Fischer u.a.: 'Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1958''} dxe6 (11... Kxe6 12. Qd5+ Kf5 13. g4+ Kxg4 14. Rg1+ Kh3 15. Qg2+ Kh4 16. Qg4#) 12. Qxd8 {. Typically for a Fischer-Reshevsky game, he played on until the 42nd move.}) 9. Qxg4 Nxd4 10. Qd1 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (4) New York 1961} (10. Qh4 $16 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S (6) Los Angeles 1961})) 7... O-O (7... d5 $2 8. Bb5 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 8. f4 (8. O-O $2 d5 $1 9. exd5 Nb4 $10 {Andrew Soltis: '=' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 8... d6 (8... d5 $2 9. e5 Ne4 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Nxc6 bxc6 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. Bc4 $18 {Olafsson,F-Larsen,B Wageningen 1957}) 9. Nb3 Be6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer was well ahead of the curve in arguing that'} 9... a5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} 10. a4 Be6 {Andrew Soltis: 'was superior. THen'} 11. Nd4 $2 (11. g4 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'was bad since White never has the a2-a3 move that proves essential in this game.'} Nb4) (11. O-O {} Rc8 $10 {Andrew Soltis: '='}) 11... Qb6 $1 12. Nxe6 Qxe3 13. Nxf8 Ng4 $40 {Makievsky,n-Veressov,G URS 1954}) 10. g4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer showed a fondness for this move is Sicilians and Pirc/Moderns. For example, a game with Korchnoi at Curacao began 1.e4 d6 2.d4 Nf6 3.Nc3 g6 4.f4 Bg7 5.Nf3 O-O 6.Be2 c5 7.dxc5 Qa5 8.O-O Qxc5+ 9.Kh1 Nc6 10.Nd2 a5 11.Nb3 Qb6 12.a4 Nb4 and in this Dragon-like position he allowed 13.g4? Bxg4! with advantage to Black after 14.Bxg4 Nxg4 15. Qxg4 Nxc2.'} d5 ({Endorsed by theory since 1936 but after this game the debate resumed over whether} 10... Na5 {was better.}) 11. f5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bc8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (11... gxf5 $5 {Lipnitzky,n}) 12. exd5 Nb4 13. Bf3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Alekhine had tried'} 13. d6 Qxd6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} (13... exd6 $2 14. g5) 14. Bc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'against Botwinnik at Nottingham 1936 but the game petered out to perpetual check after'} Qf4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 15. Rf1 Qxh2 16. Bxb4 Nxg4 $1 17. Bxg4 Qg3+ 18. Rf2 Qg1+ $10 {Alekhine,A-Botvinnik,M Zürich 1934 Andrew Soltis: '='}) (13. fxg6 hxg6 14. Bf3 Bxg4 $1 15. Bxg4 Nxg4 16. Qxg4 Nxc2+ 17. Kf2 Nxa1 18. Rxa1 Rc8 $1 $36 19. Bd4 $2 Rc4 {Panov,V}) 13... gxf5 {Andrew Soltis: '"I thought he was a little bit wild in the openings," Reshevsky said, many years later, of his pre-match evaluation of Fischer. "I knew he was original in the openings but I thought he took too many chances."'} 14. a3 fxg4 (14... Nxg4 15. Bxg4 Bxc3+ 16. bxc3 Nxd5 17. Bh6 $16 {Spassky-Listengarten,USSR,1953}) 15. Bg2 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'This had been recommended by Ludek Pachman who analyzed'} 15. axb4 gxf3 16. Qxf3 Bg4 { g4-h5-g6 Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: ' g4-h5-g6 Fischer''} 17. Qg2 Bh5 18. Bd4 Bg6 19. O-O-O {as favorable to White}) 15... Na6 16. Qd3 $1 {Nej,n Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: 'Nej''} (16. Qe2 Bf5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 16... e6 (16... Nd7 17. O-O-O Ne5 18. Qe2 { h2-h3; d4} Qc7 19. Bd4 ({White would probably do better with} 19. h3 {after which Fischer u.a.: '!?'} g3 20. Rd4 {to stop ...Nc4 and prepare Qh5/Rh4 or Bf4) can be answered by} Ng6 21. Rc4 Qd6 {with level chances.}) 19... Ng6 $1 {is fine for Black because his knight plugs up the g-file. A trade of dark-squared bishops will open c5 and e5 for Black's pieces.} 20. Bxg7 Qf4+ $6 (20... Kxg7 21. h3) 21. Kb1 Kxg7 22. h3 h5 23. Rd4 Qe5 24. Qd2 gxh3 25. Bxh3 Bxh3 26. Rxh3 {Jakovich-Lerner/USSR/1986}) ({This stops the bishop from bolstering the center by ...Bf5-g6 and enables White to force open a file with h3 e.g.} 16... Nc7 17. O-O-O Nce8 18. h3 g3 (18... Nd6 19. hxg4 Bxg4 20. Rdf1 Re8 (20... h5 21. Bh3 Bxh3 22. Rxh3 Qd7 23. Bd4 Rfc8 24. Bxf6 Bxf6 25. Rg3+ Bg7 26. Rfg1 Ne8 27. Qd4 f6 28. Nc5 $16) 21. Bh3 Bh5 22. Rfg1 Bg6 23. Rxg6 hxg6 24. Nd4 Nd7 25. Be6 Ne5 26. Qf1 Ndc4 (26... Nd7 {[%cal Yd7f8]} 27. Qg1 (27. Bxf7+ Nxf7 28. Ne6 Qb8 29. Qh3 Nf8 $19) 27... Nf8 $17 {PS}) 27. Bxf7+ Nxf7 28. Ne6 $18 {Soloviev,S}) 19. Ne2 Nd6 20. Nxg3 {and Bd4}) ({A similar defense is} 16... Qd6 {with the idea of ...Qg6 as well as ...Nh5-f4. For instance} 17. O-O-O Nh5 (17... Bd7 18. Rhf1 Rac8 19. Bd4 $16 {Nei-Pitksaar/USSR/1951}) 18. h3 Nf4 $1 19. Qe4 Nxg2 20. Qxg2 Qg6 $1 {is fine for Black}) 17. O-O-O (17. d6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '?''} Nd5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'and Black would suddenly have plenty of play'} 18. Bxd5 exd5 19. Qxd5 Re8 $36 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '''}) (17. h3 $5 g3 18. Rg1 Kh8 19. O-O-O exd5 20. Bd4 Nc7 21. Bxd5 {Kurajica-Mihalchishin/Banja Luka/1976}) 17... Nxd5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Better chances are offered by'} 17... exd5 18. h3 g3 19. Bd4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '''} Nh5 $1) 18. h3 $1 g3 {Andrew Soltis: 'With no knight to defend h7, Black must keep the h-file closed'} 19. Rhg1 Qd6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} 20. Bxd5 exd5 21. Nxd5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '?''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'An older Fischer said a wiser Fischer would've played'} 21. Bd4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'because White is winning after'} Bxd4 22. Rxg3+ {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bg7 (22... Kh8 23. Qxd4+ f6 24. Rf3 { d4-h8}) 23. Rdg1 Qh6+ 24. Kb1 Be6 25. Rxg7+ Qxg7 26. Rxg7+ Kxg7 27. Qg3+ Kh8 (27... Kf6 28. Qd6 Kg7 29. Nxd5 $18) 28. Qe5+ {Andrew Soltis: 'followed by check on g5 and f6 and Ne2-f4-h5 and Nbd4'} Kg8 29. Qg5+ Kh8 30. Qf6+ Kg8 31. Ne2 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 21... Kh8 {Andrew Soltis: 'now 22.Bd4 Bxd4 23.Qxd4+ has much less bite'} 22. Bf4 (22. Bd4 Bxd4 (22... f6 $2 23. Rxg3 Qxd5 24. Bxf6 $1) 23. Qxd4+ f6 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 22... Qg6 23. Qd2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Some annotators claimed'} 23. Qf3 {Vucovic,V Andrew Soltis: 'would have won more quickly Fischer u.a.: 'Vucovic,V''} {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer shot back that'} Bf5 $1 (23... Bh6 24. Rxg3 Bxf4+ 25. Qxf4 Qe6 26. Qd4+) (23... Be6 24. Rxg3 Bxd5 25. Rxd5 Qf6 26. Rxg7 $1 Qxg7 27. Rg5) 24. Rxg3 Qc6 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'saves the day Fischer u.a.: '''}) 23... Bxh3 $1 24. Rxg3 Bg4 25. Rh1 Rfe8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black parries the threat of 26.Nc7 Qe6 27.Rxg4+ Qxg4 28.Rxh7+! Kxh7 29. Qh2+. He is a move or two (...f5, ...Re4 or ...Rad8) away from weathering the storm.'} 26. Ne3 Qe4 $2 (26... Qf6 27. Nxg4 Qxb2+ 28. Kd1 Rad8 29. Rd3 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'But in time pressure, Reshevsky overlooks a combination. Necessary was'} 26... f5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'so that Fischer u.a.: '!''} 27. Qh2 (27. Nxg4 fxg4 28. Qh2 Rac8 (28... Kg8 $142 {PS}) 29. Rxg4 {would be met by} Re1+ $1 {Fischer u.a.: 'Soloviev'}) 27... Kg8 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 27. Qh2 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Murderous, since 27. ...Bf5 fails to 28.Rxg7 Kxg7 29.Nxf5+ Qxf5 30.Nd4 e.g. 30. ...Qe4 31.Qh6+ Kg8 (31. ...Kh8? 32.Be5+) 32.Rg1+ Fischer u.a.: '+-!''} Be6 (27... Bf5 {Turner,A} 28. Rxg7 $1 Kxg7 29. Nxf5+ Qxf5 30. Nd4 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 28. Rxg7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'No one resigned or offered draws in Fischer-Reshevsky games until the board was nearly barren. But here'} 28. Nd2 $18 {Thomas,A Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'might have ended matters immediately. Fischer u.a.: '+-' Fischer u.a.: 'Thomas,A''}) 28... Kxg7 29. Qh6+ Kg8 (29... Kh8 30. Be5+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 30. Rg1+ Qg6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The rest was'} 31. Rxg6+ fxg6 32. Nd4 Rad8 33. Be5 Rd7 34. Nxe6 Rxe6 35. Ng4 Rf7 36. Qg5 Rf1+ 37. Kd2 h5 38. Qd8+ (38. Qd8+ {Andrew Soltis: 'The match ended, with the score tied 51/2-51/2, because of a scheduling dispute that escalated into accusation, recrimination and eventually a forfeit in Reshevsky's favor.'} Rf8 39. Nh6+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) 1-0

