{Andrew Soltis: '"Chess players, as a rule, are very revengeful people," Tal wrote in his game collection, meaning that they are always looking for a chance to retaliate against an opponent who inflicted a particularly memorable defeat on them. When Tal met Fischer in the second round at Bled, it was three months since he had lost a World Championship rematch (literally "revenge match" in Russian) to Botwinnik. Now it was Fischer's turn for payback, to avenge the 0-4 humiliation he suffered at Tal's hands in the 1959 Candidates Tournament.'} 1. e4 {Fischer,R} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nc3 ({Garry Kasparov: '"No doubt Tal expected'} 5. Nb5 {Fischer,R-Najdorf,M Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Garry Kasparov: 'which I had played exclusively at Buenos Aires 1960. I still think that might be best," wrote Fischer, and later he confirmed this (Game Nos.91 and 98) .'}) 5... Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: 'The ...Nc6, ...e6 system was very new and only given half a page in Rolf Schwarz's 587-page book on the SIcilian (1961).'} {Play usually continues} 6. g3 {This fianchetto line is one of the main replies to the Sicilian Taimanov.} Nf6 $2 {"Probably the losing move!" – Fischer. Interestingly, against 6 Be3 or 6 Be2 there is nothing wrong with playing 6...Nf6 before ...a7-a6, but here it proves to be a serious inaccuracy, as we will see next. Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'A purely 'manual' mistake.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal plays a natural move - and finds himself lost by move 10. In fact, he gives 6. ...Nf6 two question marks in the tournament book. He explained that he planned to play' Garry Kasparov: 'Tal intended the usual'} 6... a6 { Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: 'and 7. ...Nf6 but "once a year or so" he reversed the move order in his calculations and paid the price. Essential was 6. ...a6 - yet several strong GMs have fallen into the same trap since 1961 - including Benko, Judith Polgar and Yevgeny Vasiukov. Fischer,R: '''} 7. Bg2 Nf6 {Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.93) and had already written down 6...a6 on his score-sheet, but, by his own admission, "roughly once a year it would happen that I would write down the first move of a variation, but make the second." "Probably the losing move!" states Fischer. "Tal looked worried immediately after having made it, but I'm not sure he was convinced he had been really careless." Well, let's see...'} 8. O-O {, when Black has several choices such as} d6 (8... Bc5) ({, and} 8... Nxd4 9. Qxd4 Bc5 {. Instead, with his next move Tal "modifies" the move order and is severely punished for the rest of the game.})) 7. Ndb5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'And among the players who failed to punish 6. . ..Nf6? by playing other seventh moves are Keres and Vlastimil Hort.'} Qb8 {From now on Fischer will never let his opponent rest, creating new threats on every move, with a particular focus on the d6-square. Andrew Soltis: 'Black hopes to recoup with ...a6'} (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 Qd8 9. Bf4 e5 10. Bg5 $16 {Garry Kasparov: 'is bad for Black – Fischer.'}) 8. Bf4 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Ne5 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: '"Tal took a long time on this risky reply.'} ({Later it was discovered that the ugly Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer considered' Garry Kasparov: 'Moreover, in the variation'} 8... e5 {offers more resistance. There can follow} 9. Bg5 a6 10. Bxf6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 10. Na3 b5 11. Bxf6 b4 $1) 10... axb5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 10... gxf6 11. Na3 {Garry Kasparov: ', Black should go in for'} {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of'} b5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (11... Bxa3 {Fischer,R Garry Kasparov: '!'} 12. bxa3 Ne7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer was wrong this line is better for Black than what happens to him in the game.' Garry Kasparov: '!'} 13. Qf3 {'!?' '!?' Johan Hellsten: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '!?'} (13. Bc4 Qc7 14. Qd3 d6 15. O-O Be6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. Bxd5 Ke7 $11) (13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. Qxd5 (14. exd5 Qc7 15. c4 d6) 14... b5 ({or} 14... d6 {, with a somewhat inferior, but by no means lost position.})) 13... Qc7 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 14. Bg2 d6 15. O-O {, intending Nd1-e3, with a more comfortable game for White in Alexa. Ivanov-I.V.Ivanov, US Championship, Long Beach 1989. But if Black is aiming at such kind of play, I guess he should rather go for the Sveshnikov (1 e4 c5 2 Nf3 Nc6 3 d4 cxd4 4 Nxd4 Nf6 5 Nc3 e5) straight away!} Rg8 16. Nd1 $14 {Al.Ivanov-I.Ivanov,USA,1989}) 12. Nd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'to be clearly in White's favor and indicated it was no better than the knight move. Yet two decades later in a similar position in the Sveshnikov Sicilian was regarded as quite playable.'} f5 13. Nb1 $1) 11. Bg5 $16 Bb4 12. Bxb5 Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 d6 {."} ({Or first} 13... Qc7 {– indeed, the weakness of White's queenside greatly hinders the conversion of his extra pawn.})) 9. Be2 $1 {From here the bishop covers the f3-square, thus creating the threat of 10 Qd4. Johan Hellsten: 'From here the bishop covers the f3-square, thus creating the threat of 10 Qd4. ' Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: 'Botwinnik, who annotated this game for Chess Life, thought this was part of a trap set by White's sixth move (!). By watching h5 and f3, WHite prepares both Qd4 and BXe5/f4 Fischer,R: '!'' Garry Kasparov: '"Perhaps Tal underestimated this simple move. It prepares Qd4 and keeps an eye on the b5-square." (Fischer)'} ({The immediate} 9. Bxe5 Qxe5 10. f4 Qb8 11. e5 a6 12. exf6 axb5 {is better for Black than the game}) 9... Bc5 {On most other moves 10 Qd4 is troublesome for Black; Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'But this definitely leads to defeat,'} ({e.g. Andrew Soltis: 'For instance' Garry Kasparov: 'as do the lines indicated by Fischer:'} 9... a6 {'?' '?' Johan Hellsten: '?'} 10. Qd4 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} d6 11. Rd1 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} axb5 12. Bxe5 $18 {with a huge edge, now that the bishop is untouchable due to mate. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} dxe5 13. Qd8#) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 9... d6 10. Qd4 Nc6 11. Nxd6+ (11. Qxd