ediate} 14. Nbd6+ {.})) 13. exf6 axb5 14. fxg7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Also excellent is'} 14. Ne4 {; Keres,P Fischer,R: ''} Bf8 15. Qd4 {Fischer,R: '' Fischer,R: 'Keres' was another good option – Keres.} g6 {but Fischer's way is surer.} (15... Qa7 16. Qe5 g6 17. Bxb5 Qe3+ 18. Kf1 Qf3+ 19. Kg1 b6 20. Be2 $1 Qe3+ (20... Qxe2 21. Nd6+) 21. Kf1 Bc5 22. b4 $1 {and wins.})) 14... Rg8 15. Ne4 Be7 16. Qd4 {'!' A move with several virtues: it protects the important g7-pawn, speeds up development and enables a future check on f6. The b5-pawn can of course wait. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Ra4 {This inventive move won't solve Black's problems, but he was already short of a good defence. Garry Kasparov: 'A loss of a tempo,'} ({On Garry Kasparov: 'but the slightly more tenacious'} 16... Qc7 {; Botvinnik,M Fischer,R: '' White could consider Andrew Soltis: '' Garry Kasparov: '(Botvinnik) would also have lost in view of'} 17. O-O-O $5 (17. Bd3 b6 18. Nf6+ Bxf6 19. Qxf6 Qc5 20. O-O-O Qh5 21. h3 {etc.}) 17... Rxa2 18. Kb1 {with similar play to later on in the game.}) (16... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 18. Qxf6 Qe3 19. Kf1 $18 {Tal}) 17. Nf6+ Bxf6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 17... Kd8 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'White wins easily with Fischer,R: '?''} 18. Qb6+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} ({of the flashy} 18. Nxg8 Rxd4 19. Nxe7)) 18. Qxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threats include 19.Bh5, 19.Bd3 and 19.Qh6'} Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (18... Qa7 {Fischer,R} 19. Bh5 $18) ({If} 18... Qd6 {White could reply} 19. Bd3 {.}) 19. O-O-O {'!' Fischer has observed that his king will be safe on b1, irrespective of the capture on a2. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'An accurate move, which essentially concludes the game.'} ({while both Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 19. Bh5 {Garry Kasparov: ', then'} d5 {Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: '' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer).'} (19... Re4+ {and 20. ...d5!})) ({In contrast, Andrew Soltis: 'IN each of their two previous games, Fischer found enough sparkling moves to win most games. But he failed to follow them up and had to settle for a loss and a draw. This time his play remains at a consistantly high level - with only one slip to come. Here he avoids'} 19. Bxb5 $2 {failed to} Qa5+ {Fischer,R: '-+' Andrew Soltis: 'which hangs a bishop Fischer,R: '-+''}) ({and} 19. Bd3 Qc5 {give Black more hope of survival. Andrew Soltis: '!'} 20. Bxh7 Qe3+ 21. Kf1 Qf3+ 22. Kg1 Qe3+ 23. Kg2 Qe2+ 24. Kh3 Rxg7 $1 25. Qxg7 Qh5+ {with perpetual check}) 19... Rxa2 ({Or} 19... Qd8 20. Qh6 Rxa2 21. Qxh7 Ke7 22. h4 {and wins.}) 20. Kb1 Ra6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'or' Garry Kasparov: 'while if'} 20... Ra5 {Garry Kasparov: '– the immediate'} 21. Bh5 d5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 21... d6 22. Rxd6 $1) 22. Rxd5 $1 exd5 23. Re1+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: '.'}) ({Or Andrew Soltis: 'Black had no defense to Bh5, e.g.' Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 20... Qa5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer gives'} 21. b3 $1 { h5 with the strong threat of 22 Bh5. Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '!' Fischer,R: ' h5'' Garry Kasparov: 'and Bh5'}) 21. Bxb5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Another good option was Garry Kasparov: '"I was so intent on snatching material and not botching this one that I missed'} 21. Bh5 {Fischer,R: '!'} d6 ({or} 21... d5 22. Rxd5 $1 Rxg7 23. Rd3 Rg8 $2 24. Rd8+) 22. Rhe1 ({or} 22. f5 $1 {– G.K.}) 22... Qe7 23. Qh6 Kd7 24. Qxh7 $18 {with a quick win – Fischer. Garry Kasparov: 'with a quick win in view." (Fischer).'} Qe8 25. f5 e5 (25... Kc7 26. fxe6 Bxe6 27. Rxe6 $1) 26. Rxe5) 21... Rb6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal pointed out'} 21... Ra5 {Fischer,R} {Andrew Soltis: 'But White can sidestep the trap with'} 22. c4 $1 $18 {Tal Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-!' Fischer,R: 'Tal''} (22. Qh6 $2 Rxb5 23. Qxh7 {Andrew Soltis: 'allows'} Rxb2+ 24. Kxb2 Qb6+ 25. Kc1 Qe3+ 26. Rd2 Qa3+ 27. Kd1 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} Qf3+ $1 {Tal Andrew Soltis: 'with perpetual check Fischer,R: '!' Fischer,R: 'Tal''})) 22. Bd3 {Tal tries to reach an inferior endgame after} e5 {Fischer,R: '!?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Also lost is' Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 22... Qd8 23. Qh6 f5 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 24. Qh5+ Ke7 {Garry Kasparov: ', both'} 25. g4 $40 {Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer)'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 25. Be2 {with the idea of} Rxg7 26. Qh4+ Ke8 27. Bh5+ {. The point of 22. ...e5, besides attacking the queen is that 23.Qxe5+ Qxe5 takes Black from a hopeless middlegame to a merely wretched endgame. Garry Kasparov: 'etc are possible.'})) 23. fxe5 $1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'This is stronger than'} 23. Qxe5+ Qxe5 24. fxe5 Rxg7 $16 {, but in vain... Garry Kasparov: ', although even here after'} 25. Rhe1 Kd8 26. Bf5 {it is doubtful whether Black can save the game. "In top-flight chess, you have to drive your advantage home unmercifully." (Fischer)}) 23... Rxf6 24. exf6 { xh7 Now the threat of 25 Bxh7 proves irresistible. Andrew Soltis: 'A rare case where it was Fischer who gave up his queen for two rooks. Fischer,R: ' xh7'' Garry Kasparov: 'Threatening Bxh7.'} Qc5 $8 {Fischer,R: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Black manages to trade off the pawns for a roo know Fischer,R: '''} (24... Qb6 {, then} 25. Rhf1 $18 {'!' – Fischer. '!' Johan Hellsten: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and 26. Bxh7 is kaput Fischer,R: '+-''}) 25. Bxh7 Qg5 26. Bxg8 Qxf6 27. Rhf1 Qxg7 28. Bxf7+ {With such a material advantage, the rest is easy.} Kd8 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '+-''} 29. Be6 ({White can choose between several winning plans. It may seem that} 29. Rd3 {followed by the advance of the h-pawn is fastest but Black get counterplay from} b6 30. h4 Ba6 31. c4 Qh7 32. Kc2 b5) ({Perhaps} 29. Bd5 {is a cleaner win but it's hard to resist doubling rooks on the seventh rank.}) 29... Qh6 (29... Kc7 30. Bf5 $36 {Garry Kasparov: 'was no better (Fischer).'}) 30. Bxd7 {'!' '!' Johan Hellsten: '!'} Bxd7 31. Rf7 Qxh2 32. Rdxd7+ {. White soon won.} Ke8 33. Rde7+ Kd8 34. Rd7+ ({Andrew Soltis: 'Tal gave an alternative win'} 34. Rxb7 $5 {Fischer,R Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer,R: '!?''} Qh1+ 35. Ka2 Qd5+ 36. b3 Qa5+ 37. Kb2 Qe5+ 38. c3 Qe2+ 39. Ka3 Qa6+ 40. Kb4 Qd6+ 41. Kb5 Qd3+ 42. Kb6 Qe3+ (42... Qd6+ 43. Ka7 {Andrew Soltis: 'and wins. That's fine for the armchair analyst - but the practical player would follow Fischer's example, which leaves White a much larger margin of error'} Qa3+ 44. Kb8 Qd6+ 45. Rfc7 Qxg3 46. b4 $18 {[%cal Yb4b6,Yc3c6] PS}) 43. Ka6 Qe6+ 44. Ka7 $18 {Tal}) 34... Kc8 35. Rc7+ Kd8 36. Rfd7+ Ke8 37. Rd1 b5 38. Rb7 Qh5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 38... Qxg3 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'White wins by both the crude'} 39. Rxb5 $18 (39. Rh1 Qe5 40. Rh8+ $1)) 39. g4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qh3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Again Black can't take the pawn because 40.Rh1 and the mate threat.'} (39... Qxg4 40. Rh1 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer'} Qd4 41. Rh8+ $1 Qxh8 42. Rb8+ $18 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 40. g5 Qf3 41. Re1+ Kf8 42. Rxb5 Kg7 43. Rb6 Qg3 44. Rd1 Qc7 45. Rdd6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qc8 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat was Rg6+ Kh7 47.Rh6+ Kg7 48. Rbg6+ Kf8 49.Rh8+ and 50.Rh7+'} (45... Qe7 46. Rg6+ Kh7 47. Rh6+ Kg7 48. Rbg6+ Kf8 49. Rh8+ Kf7 50. Rh7+ $18) (45... Qc5 46. Rb7+ Kg8 47. Rd8+ $18) 46. b3 Kh7 47. Ra6 (47. Ra6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat of 48.Ra7 Kg8 49. Rdd7 has no answer'} Kg7 48. Ra7+ Kg8 49. Rdd7 $18) 1-0

