{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's career record with certain strong grandmasters improved markedly as he matured. Following his disastrous start against Tal, he scored 3½-1½ in their last five games. And after scoring 4-7 with only one win, in his first 11 meetings with Gligoric, he was 5-0 in their last five games.  But there was one major exception : Yefim Geller. In between their first game, below, and their last, which Fischer won, Geller amassed the best record of any Fischer rival - five wins, two draws and one loss.'} 1. e4 {Fischer u.a.} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 d6 {Garry Kasparov: 'The trademark move of the Steinitz Defence Deferred.'} 5. O-O ({Garry Kasparov: 'Usually White played'} 5. Bxc6+ {Andrew Soltis: 'were endorsed by theory. Today, Black usually replied with a neutral moe such as 5. ...Nf6, but at the time 5.O-O was controversial because of'} bxc6 6. d4) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or' Andrew Soltis: 'A fairly ununsual move in 1961, when'} 5. c3 {Garry Kasparov: '(Volume 2, Game No.88). "At that time this was considered inferior because it allows the pin which Black can initiate with his next move. The text is more non-committal. White can deploy his forces to greater effect after he gets a look at Black's reply." (Fischer)' Andrew Soltis: 'or'}) 5... Bg4 $6 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '"This aggressive sally weakens Black's queenside." (Fischer). However, this is not so terrible.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'The classical move is'} 5... Bd7 $5 {Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.7).'}) ({I should also mention the attempt} 5... Be7 {–} 6. Bxc6+ $5 bxc6 7. d4 exd4 8. Nxd4 c5 9. Nc6 Qd7 10. Na5 $1 Bf6 11. Qd3 Ne7 12. Nc3 Rb8 13. Rb1 O-O 14. Bd2 {with a slight but enduring advantage for White (Karpov-Portisch, 2nd matchgame, Milan 1975).}) 6. h3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} Bh5 ({Garry Kasparov: '"As a result of this game' Andrew Soltis: 'To save this variation - which seemed to be on life-support after this game - masters soon began adopting'} 6... h5 {Garry Kasparov: 'became fashionable. I had intended' Andrew Soltis: 'since'} {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer said he would have replied'} 7. d4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'and if'} (7. c4 $5 b5 (7... Qf6 8. Qb3 $1 O-O-O 9. Bxc6 bxc6 10. hxg4 hxg4 11. Nh2 Qh6 (11... d5 $1 $40 {Zhuravlev}) 12. Qg3) (7... Bd7 8. d4 $16) 8. cxb5 Nd4 9. bxa6+ c6 10. Nxd4 $1 Bxd1 11. Bxc6+ Ke7 12. Nf5+ Kf6 13. Bxa8 Qxa8 14. Rxd1 Qxe4 15. Nc3 Qa8 16. Ne3 Qxa6 17. d4 Kg6 18. b4 $18 {Grabczerski-Brzuska/Warsaw/1961/ Fischer u.a.: 'Grabczerski-Brzuska Warsaw 1961'}) (7. hxg4 $2 hxg4 8. Ne1 $4 Qh4 {is suicide for WHite.}) 7... b5 8. Bb3 Nxd4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '?'' Andrew Soltis: 'then'} (8... Qf6 { Fischer u.a.: '' Andrew Soltis: ''}) 9. hxg4 hxg4 10. Ng5 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '." (Fischer). Forcing' Andrew Soltis: 'a line whose merits are still in doubt'} (10. Bxf7+ $5 {Fischer u.a.} Kxf7 11. Ng5+ Kg6 (11... Ke8 12. c3 Be7 13. cxd4 Bxg5 14. Qxg4 Bf6 (14... Bxc1) 15. Nc3 c6 16. Ne2 Rh4 $6 17. Qg3 exd4 18. Nxd4 $16 {Skuja-Poznyak, corr. 1975/76}) 12. c3 Be7 13. cxd4 Bxg5 14. Qxg4 Nh6 15. Qg3 Nf7 16. f4 exf4 17. Bxf4 Qf6 18. e5 Qf5 19. Nd2 Rh5 20. Bxg5 Rxg5 21. Qb3 Qd7 22. e6 Qc6 23. Qd3+ Kh6 24. Ne4 {1-0 Poletayev-Ginburg, corr. 1976}) 10... Nh6 {Fischer u.a. Garry Kasparov: '– the evaluation of this mind-boggling position, which has now been tested on numerous occasions (in particular it has been upheld for Black by Yusupov and Timman), remains not altogether clear even at the start of the 21st century, although White's chances are most probably somewhat better.'} 11. Be3 $5 (11. Bd5 Be7) (11. g3 Be7 12. f4 c6) 11... Be7 12. Qd2 (12. Nxf7 Nxf7 13. Bxd4 exd4 14. Bd5 $2 Ne5 $1 15. Bxa8 Bg5 16. Bc6+ Ke7 17. Qxd4 Bf4 18. Rd1 Rh1+ $1 19. Kxh1 Qh8+ 20. Kg1 Nf3+ $1 $19 {Belyavsky-Rapoport, corr. 1976}) 12... Nxb3 13. axb3 Qd7 14. Nc3 f6 {} 15. Nxb5 $1 fxg5 (15... Qxb5 16. Ne6) 16. Qa5 Bd8 17. Na7 $16 Qf7 18. Qd5 Qh5 19. Qe6+ Be7 20. f3 gxf3 21. Rxf3 g4 22. Nc6 Ng8 23. Rf5 $1 {[} Qh1+ 24. Kf2 Qxa1 25. Rf7 $18 {] Poleshchuk. 1-0 Voronovsky,D-Ginburg,V/12 St.Petersburg Ch. 1992 (25)}) (6... Bd7 $1) 7. c3 Qf6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '"Geller looked quite happy after his novelty,' Andrew Soltis: 'Geller had no poker face and his expressions during the game were revealing. Fischer recalled how he looked very pleased with himself after coming up with this new move.'} (7... Nf6 { Fischer u.a.: '' Andrew Soltis: ''} 8. d4 Nd7 (8... b5 {Fischer u.a.} 9. Bb3 Be7 10. Be3 O-O 11. Nbd2 Qc8 12. Qb1 Re8 13. a4 Qb7 14. Nh4 Na5 15. Bc2 Nc4 16. Nxc4 bxc4 17. Nf5 Rab8 18. Qa2 Be2 19. Rfe1 Bd3 20. Bxd3 cxd3 21. f3 Bf8 22. b4 g6 23. Ng3 {½-½ Kavalek,L-Hort,V/Prague 1990 (23)}) 9. Be3 Be7 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. Re1 (11. a3 $5 {Vasyukov}) 11... b5 12. Bc2 exd4 13. cxd4 Nb4 14. Bb1 c5 15. d5 Bf6 16. Qc1 c4 17. a3 Nd3 18. Bxd3 cxd3 19. Nd4 Bxd4 20. Bxd4 Rc8 {(Matulovic-Spassky, Leningrad 1964). Evans called 7...Qf6? a "patently prepared variation", but it is hard for me to say why Geller played this: bluff was never his element! And this move was perhaps the most unfortunate opening experiment in his career.}) 8. g4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"I realized the danger inherent in weakening my kingside, but felt that I could capitalize on Black's lack of development (the traffic jam on his kingside) before he could get to my king." (Fischer)'} Bg6 9. d4 $1 {10.g5 Garry Kasparov: 'A pawn sacrifice for the sake of opening the position.' Andrew Soltis: 'This offers a sacrifice Black felt he should accept in view of 10.Bg5 and d4-d5. '} Bxe4 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Garry Kasparov: '"What else? The threat was 10 Bg5 followed by d4-d5 winning a piece. He still looked happy." (Fischer).'} ({He could have reduced the damage by} 9... b5 10. Bc2 Qd8 {but that would admit he'd wasted two moves.}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'If' better is} 9... h6 10. Re1 b5 11. Bc2 Qd8 {Garry Kasparov: ', then' but White commands a comfortable edge after} 12. a4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Soltis).'}) 10. Nbd2 Bg6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'After' Andrew Soltis: 'Black can temporarily keep the center closed with'} 10... Bxf3 11. Nxf3 e4 {Garry Kasparov: '. The following recommendation by Fischer is far stronger:'} 12. Re1 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '!'} (12. Ng5 {Keres Fischer u.a.: ' Keres'} d5 13. f3 Qd6 {Garry Kasparov: '(?)'} (13... Z0) ({. But on the other hand, after} 13... e3 $1 14. f4 {(saving the knight)} h5 $5 ({or} 14... h6 {) White loses his advantage.})) 14. Qe1 $1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '(?)'} ({it is better to play} 14. fxe4 Qg3+ 15. Kh1 {, winning}) {, but this is unclear in view of} 14... e3 $1 {.}) (12. Z0) 12... d5 {Andrew Soltis: 'But Fischer noted how strong'} 13. Bg5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'was'} Qd6 (13... Qe6 14. c4 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-!'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'with a powerful liquidation of the center'}) ({Garry Kasparov: ', or'} 13... Qg6 14. Qb3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} b5 15. Qxd5 {Garry Kasparov: 'etc'} bxa4 16. Ne5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Besides, Geller still seemed happy with this position.'} Qe6 17. Qxe6+ fxe6 18. Nxc6 $18) 14. c4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '(typically Fischer-like play!)'} dxc4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 14... f6 15. cxd5 Qxd5 16. Bb3 {Garry Kasparov: 'and Rxe4+'}) ({; I would add} 14... b5 $5 15. cxb5 f6 16. bxc6 fxg5 17. Ne5 $1 {followed by f2-f3}) 15. d5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'and wins, and'} b5 16. dxc6 bxa4 17. Rxe4+ Ne7 18. Bxe7 Bxe7 19. Qe2 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) (10... Bd3 11. Bxc6+ bxc6 12. Re1 O-O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'is also insufficient in view of'} 13. Re3 $1 $16 {Smyslov,V-Medina/Tel Aviv/1964/1-0/ Fischer u.a.: 'Smyslov,V-Medina,n Tel Aviv 1964' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Smyslov-Medina, Tel Aviv Olympiad 1964).'}) 11. Bxc6+ {Garry Kasparov: 'Breaking up Black's queenside, so that he is unable to hide his king there.'} bxc6 12. dxe5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Later Fischer thought up'} 12. Qa4 {Fischer u.a.: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'with a very pretty idea:'} Ne7 ({Garry Kasparov: ', but then he noticed'} 12... Kd7 $1 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Nc4 Bd6 $16 {Garry Kasparov: 'with an unclear game. Indeed, after'} 15. Rd1 Re8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Black would have gained counter-chances – the white king is exposed as well!'} (15... Z0) 16. Nxd6 cxd6 17. Qb4 $1 {and then} Kc7 18. Qa5+ Kd7 (18... Kb7 19. Be3 c5 20. Bxc5 $1) 19. Qc5) (12... Z0) 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Re1 e4 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 15. Nxe4 Qxf3 16. Qxc6+ $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!!'} Nxc6 17. Nf6+ Kd8 18. Re8#) 12... dxe5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Perhaps' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer made no mention of'} 12... Qd8 $5 {Keres Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!?' Fischer u.a.: 'Keres'' Garry Kasparov: '(Keres) was the lesser evil, although after' Andrew Soltis: 'perhaps because'} 13. Qa4 (13. Re1 {it would also not have been easy for Black to complete his development without loss of material.}) 13... Qd7 14. Re1 d5 15. e6 $1 fxe6 16. Ne5 {so clearly favors White}) 13. Nxe5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Harold Lommer, the endgame composer, reported on Bled 1961 for the brief-lived American Chess Quarterly. He recalled how around this move Geller "seemed very sure of his position and moved rather quickly, the suddenly stopped and gasped. Something must have gone very, very wrong." What Geller overlooked is unclear but he surely saw 13. ...Qxe5?? 14.Re1.'} Bd6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'If' Andrew Soltis: 'There is no safe harbor for the king anymore'} 13... O-O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'there would have followed'} 14. Qe2 Kb7 15. Nb3 $18 { a5+ Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-' Fischer u.a.: ' a5+'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(with the threat of Na5+)' Andrew Soltis: 'and Na5+. But it was unclear, even to the grandmaster spectators, how bad Black's position was. On 14.Nxc6 h5!?, for example, White's king can become a serious target.'} Rd5 16. f4 {etc.}) 14. Nxg6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"A little surprise, permitting him to open his h-file.' Andrew Soltis: 'One of those Fischer moves that looks ridiculous at first : White allows the opening of the h-file and trades a strong knight for a nothing bishop - and only slowly reveals it power.'} (14. Nxc6 h5 (14... Z0) ({. Perhaps Black's most tenacious defence is} 14... Ne7 $1 15. Nxe7 Bxe7 16. Nf3 O-O ({completing his development;} 16... Rd8 17. Qa4+ $1) ({, while if} 16... Be4 {, then} 17. Nd4 c5 18. Qe2 $1) 17. Bg5 Qd6 {, but after} 18. Bxe7 Qxe7 19. Re1 Qf6 20. Ne5 Rfd8 21. Qf3 {White sets about converting his extra pawn.}) 15. Nc4 $1 hxg4 16. Nxd6+ cxd6 17. Re1+ Kf8 18. hxg4 Re8 19. Rxe8+ Kxe8 {White retains his extra pawn and every chance of winning, by playing} 20. Qe2+ {,} (20. f3) ({, or even} 20. Nb8 $5 {– in the event of} Qh4 $2 {he is threatened with seemingly inevitable mate, but after} 21. Qa4+ $1 Kf8 22. Nd7+ Ke7 {he mates the black king in study-like fashion by sacrificing virtually all his pieces:} 23. Bg5+ $1 {(both diversion and blocking)} Qxg5 24. Re1+ Kd8 25. Re8+ $3 {(decoy)} Kxe8 26. Nf6+ Ke7 27. Qd7+ Kxf6 28. Qxd6# {.})) 14... Qxg6 {Garry Kasparov: '"Geller took a half hour on this recapture and stopped looking happy.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"He rejected' Andrew Soltis: 'White doesn't fear'} 14... hxg6 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 15. Ne4 Qe5 (15... Qh4 16. Nxd6+ cxd6 17. Qxd6 Qxh3 $2 {Andrew Soltis: '??' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '?''} 18. Re1+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: 'and mates." (Fischer)' Andrew Soltis: '. Nevertheless Geller spent about half an hour on his reply.'}) (15... Z0) 16. Nxd6+ Qxd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'is more resilient, but after'} 17. Qf3 $16 (17. Qe2+) ({, or} 17. Re1+ Kf8 18. Qf3 {is also unpleasant for Black}) 17... O-O-O (17... Ne7 18. Re1 $1) 18. Bf4 Qd5 19. Qe3 $1 Kb7 (19... Qb5 20. g5 $1) 20. Rad1 Qxd1 21. Rxd1 Rxd1+ 22. Kg2 Nf6 23. c4 $1 Rd7 24. Qb3+ Kc8 25. Qa4 Kb7 26. Qb4+ Kc8 27. Qc5 Kb7 28. Be3 {White wins all the same.}) 15. Re1+ {Andrew Soltis: 'Geller's "clock kept ticking away valuable minutes while he scratched his head pondering his position." Lommer wrote, "He was smoking so many cigarettes that he was soon enveloped in a while halo of smoke."'} Kf8 ({Garry Kasparov: '"Another difficult decision. On' Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 15... Ne7 16. Nc4 O-O-O (16... Rd8 $5 $16 {(Fischer u.a.) Soloviev}) ({Garry Kasparov: '. And after the more tenacious'} 16... h5 $5 {(Fischer u.a.) Soloviev Garry Kasparov: 'he had a pleasant choice between'} 17. Qe2 ({and} 17. g5 Kf8 18. Qe2 Re8 19. Nxd6 cxd6 20. Qxa6 {with an extra pawn}) 17... Ra7 18. Ne5 Qe6 19. Bg5 $1 Bxe5 20. Qxe5 Qxe5 21. Rxe5 f6 22. Re4 $1 {and Rae1 with a technically won ending.}) (16... Z0) 17. Qa4 $40 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: ''' Garry Kasparov: 'White's attack comes first." (Fischer)' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's king jumps from the frying pawn into the fire. Instead he pins his hopes for king safety on f8 and his counterplay in the opening of the h-file'}) 16. Nc4 (16. Nf3 {was also very strong, to exploit the unfortunate position of the black queen, but Bobby could not deny himself the pleasure of exchanging knight for bishop.}) 16... h5 17. Nxd6 cxd6 ({Garry Kasparov: '"The best chance is' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer believed'} 17... Qxd6 $142 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: ''' Garry Kasparov: '," writes Fischer, but I think that he would have 'automatically' gained a win in the endgame after' Andrew Soltis: 'was best but didn't say how he would proceed. Keres in the tournament book, recommended'} 18. Qxd6+ ({but more in keeping with the tempo of the game is} 18. Qa4 $1 {and after} hxg4 19. Bf4 {and now} Qc5 (19... Qf6 20. Qb4+ Ne7 21. Bxc7 Re8 22. Rxe7 $1 {are winning for White}) 20. Re5) (18. g5 $5) 18... cxd6 19. Bf4 Rd8 20. Rad1 d5 21. Rd4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: ''' Garry Kasparov: 'etc.'} ({although there is something to be said for} 21. Bc7 Rd7 22. Bb6 Nh6 23. f3)) 18. Bf4 {Garry Kasparov: 'The last interesting moment.'} d5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '"Loses outright.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'After this move Black had 45 minutes left for 22 moves. Tal, an interested bystander since Fischer was his chief rival for first prize, suggested'} 18... Rd8 {Tal Fischer u.a.: 'Tal,M' Andrew Soltis: 'after the game. But'} 19. Qe2 (19. Qb3 Z0 ({, and now not} 19... hxg4 $2 {on account of the thunderous blow} 20. Bxd6+ $3 Qxd6 21. Rad1 Qc7 22. Qb4+ {(a highly unusual instance of a back-rank weakness)} Ne7 (22... c5 23. Qxc5+ $1) 23. Qb7 $3 Rxd1 24. Rxd1 Nd5 25. Qa8+ Ke7 26. Qxh8 {and wins}) ({, but the more tenacious} 19... Qf6 $1 20. Qb6 hxg4 21. Rad1 $1 Nh6 22. h4 $1 Qxh4 23. Rxd6 Rxd6 24. Bxd6+ Kg8 25. Re8+ Kh7 26. Rxh8+ Kxh8 27. Qxa6 {and, although the passed a-pawn is very strong, Black retains drawing chances.})) (19. g5 Z0 (19... Ne7 20. Qe2 $1 Nd5 (20... Rd7 21. Rad1) 21. Bd2 {and there is no good defence against the threats of c3-c4 and Qxa6.}) ({, or} 19... Nf6 20. Qe2 $1 Kg8 21. Qxa6 Ne4 22. Qd3 $5 {(in the style of Fischer: transposing into an endgame with bishop against knight, also supported by rooks)} Nxg5 23. Qxg6 Nf3+ 24. Kf1 fxg6 25. Re3 $1 (25. Re6 Kf7 26. Rxd6 Rxd6 27. Bxd6 Nd2+ 28. Ke2 Nc4 {and ...Nxb2 is not so clear}) 25... Nd2+ 26. Kg2 Nc4 27. Re2 Kf7 (27... Rf8 28. Bh2 $1) 28. b3 Na3 (28... Nb6 29. c4) 29. Rae1 {and White should win.})) 19... hxg4 20. hxg4 {Andrew Soltis: 'leaves Black without a good continuation'} {Garry Kasparov: '. "If'} Qh7 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '?'' Andrew Soltis: 'allows'} (20... Z0) ({. But after} 20... f5 $1 21. Rad1 d5 $1 {the win for White is problematic:} ({but not} 21... Rh4 22. f3 d5 ({or} 22... fxg4 23. Bxd6+) 23. Qf2 Qh7 24. Qc5+) 22. g5 ({or} 22. Rd4 c5 23. Ra4 c4 24. g5 Rh4 $1 25. Qe5 Qh5 $1 26. Qxf5+ Nf6 27. Kg2 Rg4+ 28. Bg3 Rxg5 29. Qf3 Rb8) 22... Rh4 $1 23. Qe5 Re8 24. Qd6+ Qxd6 25. Bxd6+ Ne7 26. Rd4 Rxd4 27. cxd4 Kf7 28. Rxe7+ $5 {(transposing into the pawn endgame looks like the best chance)} Rxe7 29. Bxe7 Kxe7 30. Kg2 Kd6 $1 31. b4 Kc7 32. Kf3 Kb6 33. Kf4 $1 Kb5 34. Kxf5 Kxb4 35. Ke5 c5 36. f4 c4 37. f5 c3 38. f6 gxf6+ 39. gxf6 c2 40. f7 c1=Q 41. f8=Q+ {and White picks up the d5-pawn, but the question of whether he can win this queen ending requires a separate investigation.}) 21. Bxd6+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-'' Garry Kasparov: 'wins." (Fischer)'}) 19. Qb3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'With an inevitable invasion at b7.'} hxg4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Geller thought for about forty minutes on this move! Alas, it was too late...'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Geller spent 40 of those 45 minutes glumly calculating variations such as'} 19... Ne7 20. Rxe7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '!''} Kxe7 21. Qb7+ $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer u.a.: '+-''}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'or'} 19... Nf6 20. Qb7 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'as in the game'} Re8 21. Rxe8+ Nxe8 22. Re1 Qf6 23. Qc8 $18 (23. Qb8 {and wins})) 20. Qb7 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Much better than'} 20. Qb4+ Ne7 21. Qxe7+ Kg8 22. h4) 20... gxh3+ 21. Bg3 Rd8 22. Qb4+ {Garry Kasparov: '. A terrible rout, quite uncharacteristic of Geller. Well, you shouldn't employ such opening variations...'} (22. Qb4+ {Andrew Soltis: '"The roof almost caved in at the tumultuous burst of applause." Lommer wrote. "It was almost as though the spectators were greeting in applause the next world's champion."'} Ne7 23. Qxe7+ Kg8 24. Qxd8+ $18) 1-0

