{[%mdl 4096] ChessCalisthenics: 'B22: Sicilian: 2 c3' Andrew Soltis: 'Perhaps the least known of Fischer's greatest combinations was also the longest. Fischer's opponent, Arthur Bisguier, has achieved some excellent positions against him since they met five years before and was in fine form in Bled, where he defeated Geller, Keres and Najdorf. But at move 14 Fischer grabs a White pawn in what appears to be a highly double-edged position. Over the course of the next 10 moves, Bisguier rejects one drawish opportunity after another because his winning prospects appear too good to waste. Only at move 30 does it become clear Fischer has seen much further than hm.'} 1. d4 Nf6 2. Nf3 d5 3. c4 e6 4. e3 c5 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. cxd5 ({Fischer's opening repetoire reflected an odd philosophy : every position, even one of pure symmetry, contained within it the possibilities for an advantage - for Black as well as White. The key was finding the right moment to break symmetry. For example on} 6. a3 a6 ({But in the eighth game of his Candidates Match with Petrosian, Fischer broke the pattern earlier with} 6... Ne4 $5 7. Qc2 Nxc3 8. bxc3 Be7 {. The position soon turned in his favor after} 9. Bb2 O-O 10. Bd3 h6 11. O-O Na5) 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. b4 {with advantage.}) 6... Nxd5 7. Bd3 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. a3 cxd4 10. exd4 Nf6 ({Fischer never showed much interest in playing against hanging pawns} 10... Nxc3 11. bxc3) 11. Be3 b6 12. Rc1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' 'N'} (12. Qe2 Bb7 13. Rfd1 (13. Rad1 Rc8 14. Rfe1 Nd5 15. Nxd5 Qxd5 16. Bc4 Qh5 17. d5 Na5 18. Ba2 Bxd5 19. Bxd5 exd5 20. Bxb6 Nc6 21. Bxa7 Bxa3 22. bxa3 Nxa7 23. Qa2 Nb5 24. Rxd5 Nc3 25. Rxh5 Nxa2 26. Rhe5 g6 27. h4 Rc3 {Vogt,L (2466)-Grund,H (2390)/Germany 1999/GER-chT/1/2-1/2 (48)}) 13... a6 14. Ne5 b5 15. Rac1 Qd6 16. Bf4 Nxd4 17. Qe3 Qb6 18. Qh3 Rfd8 19. Be3 b4 20. Qh4 bxc3 21. Bxh7+ Kf8 22. Bxd4 Rxd4 23. Rxd4 cxb2 24. Rcd1 Qc7 25. Bb1 Bc5 26. Rd7 {1-0 Peev,P (2335) -Milovanovic,R (2420)/Ulcinj 1997/EXT 1999}) 12... Bb7 13. Qe2 ({This gives Black more of a chance to sharpen the position than} 13. Bb1 Rc8 14. Qd3) 13... Ng4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black decides to punish the apparently inexact Be3. Bisguier loses only because he, in turn, decides to punish Black's dogmatism.'} 14. Bf4 ({White's position is not worse after} 14. Rfd1 Nxe3 15. Qxe3 Bf6 16. Be4) ({or} 14. Bb1 Nxe3 15. fxe3 Qd6 16. Qd3 {. But why not preserve the bishop?}) 14... Nxd4 {Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: '"This is unbelievable, " Euwe wrote, "considering the many obvious resources which White has at his disposal which will soon become clear.'} 15. Nxd4 Qxd4 16. Qxg4 ({Now} 16. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 17. Qxg4 Qf6 {is only a slight edge for Black. However, White appears to get an edge with his next two moves.}) 16... Qxd3 17. Rfd1 Qg6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Black has seen through} 17... Qa6 18. Be5 g6 19. Rd7 Bc5 ({True Black may weather the storm after} 19... Rae8 20. Qa4 {. But he opts instead to ruin his pawns in order to keep an extra one}) 20. Qh4 $1) 18. Qxg6 fxg6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 19. Rd7 Bc5 ({Not} 19... Rxf4 20. Rxe7 Bd5 $2 21. Nxd5 exd5 22. Rcc7) 20. Be3 {Andrew Soltis: 'White invites ...Bxe3 - which remains an error for four moves.'} Bc6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({White would have the upper hand after} 20... Bxe3 $2 21. fxe3 Bc6 ({It's not clear who has the edge after} 21... Rf7 22. Rxf7 Kxf7 23. Nb5 Kf6 24. Rc7 Be4 25. Rxa7 Rd8) 22. Rd6) 21. Rc7 Rfc8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({If Black had inverted his move order, with} 21... Bxe3 22. fxe3 Rfc8 23. Rxc8+ ({In fact Whtie can do better with because then} 23. Nb5 $1 Rxc7 (23... Bd7 24. Nxa7 $1) (23... Bb7 24. Nd6 Rxc7 25. Rxc7 Bd5 26. e4 Bb3 27. Nc8 $1 {Favors White}) 24. Nxc7 {wins outright}) 23... Rxc8 {White's king would be a tempo ahead of the game after} 24. Nb5 Bd7 25. Rxc8+ Bxc8 26. Nxa7 Bd7 27. Kf2 {. The knight is freed after, for example} Kf7 28. Ke2 Ke7 29. Kd3 Kd6 30. Kc4) 22. Rxc8+ {Andrew Soltis: '?!'} ({Again White plays for a win, passing up a likely draw in} 22. Nb5 Bxb5 23. Rxc8+ Rxc8 24. b4) 22... Rxc8 23. Nb5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Black can transpose into the note to White's 22nd move with 22. ...Bxb5 23.b4 but he has better :'} (23. Bxc5 bxc5 24. f3 Kf7 {offers Black good winning chances.}) 23... Bd7 24. b4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({the outlines of a winning edge appear after} 24. Bxc5 bxc5 $1 25. Nd6 Rb8 26. Rc2 Rb3) ({White again rejects drawing chances, this time in the form of} 24. Nxa7 Ra8 25. Rxc5 $1 bxc5 26. Bxc5 Kf7 27. b4 Rb8 28. f3) 24... Bxe3 ({Bisguier may have been misled by thinking Fischer would answer 24.b4 with} 24... Bxb5 25. bxc5 bxc5 26. Rxc5 Rxc5 27. Bxc5 {and try to win with the extra, doubled pawn}) 25. Rxc8+ Bxc8 26. fxe3 ({There are no more tricks for White since} 26. Nxa7 Bxf2+ 27. Kxf2 Bd7 {wins easily}) 26... Bd7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({or perhaps White had assumed Black would choose} 26... a6 27. Nd6 Bd7 28. Nc4 b5 29. Ne5 {when his chances in the good knight v. bad bishop ending are slim.}) 27. Nd6 (27. Nxa7 $142 $5 Kf7 (27... b5 {trapped the knight. In contrast with the note to move 21, the White king is denied c4 in this variation and will run out of moves following} 28. Kf2 Kf7 29. Ke2 ({Then Black's king can pick off the knight. But the winning task would be hard after} 29. Kf3 e5 {or} (29... Ke7)) 29... Ke7 30. Kd3 Kd6 31. Kd4 e5+ 32. Ke4 g5) 28. Kf2 $15) 27... Kf8 28. e4 e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Although the defender here usually benefits from a trade of pawns, here Black needed to stop 29.e5 and Kf2-e3'} 29. Kf2 Bc6 30. Nc4 ({After the pawn swap, the bishop will dominate the knight, making zugzwang possible. But} 30. b5 {is met by} Ke7 $1 31. Nc8+ Kd8 32. Nxb6 (32. Nxa7 Bxe4) 32... Bxb5 33. Nd5 Kd7 {. Black's chances are increasing}) 30... Bxe4 31. Nxe5 Ke7 32. Nc4 Ke6 33. Ne3 ({the pawn endings such as} 33. g3 Kd5 34. Nd2 Kd4 35. Nxe4 Kxe4 36. h3 h5 {are lost.}) 33... Ke5 34. Ke2 Kd4 35. Kd2 Bc6 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Zugzwang looms (32.Ke2? Kc3)'} 36. g3 Bd7 37. Nd1 (37. Ng2 g5 38. Ne3 Be6 39. Nc2+ Ke4 40. Ke2 Bc4+ 41. Kf2 Kd3 42. Ne3 (42. Ne1+ Kc3 43. Nf3 h6 44. Ne5 Bd5 $19) 42... Be6 $19 {Matanovic}) (37. Nc2+ Kc4 38. Na1 Bf5) (37. h4 h5 38. Ng2 Kc4 39. Nf4 Bf5 {and wins in either case.}) 37... Ke4 {Andrew Soltis: 'since 38.Ke2 Bg4+ and Bxd1 is hopeless, White must allow the king to reach g2.'} 38. Ne3 Kf3 39. Kd3 g5 40. Kd4 Be6 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'ChessBase: '!''} 41. Ke5 (41. Kd3 {Andrew Soltis: 'the h-pawn was lost after'} h5 {[%cal Gh5h4] h4-+ Matanovic} (41... Bh3 {and ...Kf2. The rest of the game goes})) 41... Kxe3 42. Kxe6 Kf3 43. Kf5 Kg2 44. Kxg5 Kxh2 45. g4 b5 46. Kf5 Kg3 47. g5 g6+ $1 {ChessCalisthenics: 'ChessBase: '!'' Andrew Soltis: 'A curious incident occurred after Bled ended while Fischer toured Yugoslavia and was striken with what the New York Times called apparent appendicitis. He was in Zagreb for a simul when he suddenly felt abdominal pains. Bjelica recalled : "We postponed the exhibition and raced off to Banja Luka in an ambulance. During that time I could see what great love he had for chess. Bobby gripped his stomach in one hand, and in the other he held Keres' book, My Greatest Games. Fischer refused to have an appendectomy and the pain slowly receded. He spent three days in the hospital, playing offhand games and thumbing through Keres."'} 48. Ke5 Kf3 $19 0-1

