{Andrew Soltis: 'The Interzonal organizers scheduled a three-day break after the fourth round so that most of the players would enjoy a rest day. Even though Fischer had to play off the Portisch adjournment, he got a virtual day off in his next game, thanks to the astonishing depth of his opening preparation. He took less than 10 minutes to achieve a winning position.'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis Postny,E Garry Kasparov} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bg5 e6 7. f4 Qb6 {Andrew Soltis: 'This move was given a question mark by Vladimir Simagin, the Russian openings authority, in 1963. That reflected the volatile nature of this opening : In international games played in 1962, Black was winning nearly twice as many games as White in this variation. By 1963 the ratio was reversed.' Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({Before the early 1960s Bobby played only} 7... Be7 {(Garry Kasparov) – Game No.55.}) 8. Qd2 Qxb2 9. Rb1 ({Ten years later Spassky brought} 9. Nb3 {(Garry Kasparov) into fashion (Volume 3, Game No.41).}) 9... Qa3 10. e5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Following the source game (Tolush-Furman, Leningrad 1954).'} ({At that time the theory of this sharp variation was only just being developed and White used to choose between the move in the game and the modest} 10. Bxf6 {Garry Kasparov} gxf6 11. Be2 {(Parma-Fischer, Bled 1961; Havana 1965; Rovinj/Zagreb 1970).}) ({Later the main continuation became} 10. f5 $1 {Garry Kasparov} Nc6 11. fxe6 fxe6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. e5 {(Fischer-Geller, Monaco 1967; Kavalek-Fischer, Sousse Interzonal 1967).}) 10... dxe5 11. fxe5 Nfd7 12. Bc4 ({Tal's move} 12. Ne4 $6 {(Garry Kasparov) (Volume 2, Game No.121) lost its attraction on account of} h6 $1 {(Korchnoi-Tolush, 25th USSR Championship, Riga 1958).}) 12... Be7 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (12... h6 $2 13. Bxe6 $1 $40) ({The reliable continuations are: The reliable continuations are:} 12... Bb4 13. Nxe6 {Andrew Soltis} (13. Rb3 {Andrew Soltis Garry Kasparov} Qa5 14. a3 (14. Bxe6 O-O 15. Bxf7+ Rxf7 16. e6 Rf8 17. Rxb4 $1 Qxb4 18. e7 Re8 19. Nf5 Nf6 20. Bxf6 Bxf5 21. Qd5+ Kh8 22. Qf7 Rxe7+ 23. Qxe7 Qxe7+ 24. Bxe7 Nc6 25. Bc5 Bxc2 26. Rf1 Kg8 27. Rf2 Bg6 28. Nd5 Bf7 29. Ne3 Ne5 30. Rd2 Re8 31. Ke2 b5 32. Rd6 a5 33. a3 Nc4 34. Rc6 b4 35. axb4 axb4 36. Kf2 Ne5 {Kurajica,B-Tukmakov,V/Jugoslavija-URS 1965 (36)}) (14. O-O O-O (14... Bc5 15. Kh1 Bxd4 16. Qxd4 Nc6 17. Qf4 Ndxe5 18. Ne4 O-O 19. Rg3 Ng6 $13 {Nunn}) (14... Nxe5 $5) 15. Bf6 $1 {Byrne} (15. a3 Bc5 16. Kh1 Bxd4 17. Qxd4 Nc6 18. Qf4 Ncxe5 $17 {Boleslavski}) (15. Nxe6 $2 fxe6 16. Bxe6+ Kh8 17. Rxf8+ Bxf8 18. Qf4 Nc6 $1 19. Qf7 Qc5+ 20. Kh1 Nf6 $1 21. Bxc8 Nxe5 22. Qe6 Neg4 {0-1  (Tringov-Fischer, Havana 1965).}) 15... Nc6 {} (15... Nxf6 $1 16. exf6 Rd8 $1 17. Rxb4 $1 Qxb4 18. Qg5 (18. Qd3 $5 {Geller}) 18... g6 {is correct, with an unclear game} 19. Rf4 $5 (19. Nxe6 Bxe6 20. Bxe6 Qxc3 21. Qh6 Qc5+ 22. Kh1 Qf8 $19) (19. Ne4 $1 Qf8 20. Nf3 (20. c3 Nc6 21. Nxc6 bxc6 22. h4 Rd5 $1 23. Bxd5 exd5 24. Ng3 h6 25. Qe3 Be6 26. h5 g5 $17 27. Nf5 Bxf5 28. Rxf5 Re8 29. Re5 Rxe5 30. Qxe5 Qc8 31. Qe7 c5 32. Qd6 d4 33. cxd4 c4 34. Qe5 c3 35. Qe3 c2 36. Qc1 Qc4 37. Kh2 Qe2 38. Qb2 Qxh5+ {0-1 Ortega-Bobotsov Socchi 66}) 20... Nc6 21. Qh4 $13 { Geller/Nunn}) 19... b6 $5 (19... Qf8 20. Rh4 b5 21. Bd3 b4 22. Bxg6 hxg6 (22... fxg6 23. f7+ $1 $18) 23. Rh6 Rxd4 24. Rxg6+ {½-½ Hartston-Mecking/Hastings 1967 (24)}) 20. Rh4 (20. Nxe6 Bxe6 21. Bxe6 Qc5+ $19) 20... Qf8 21. Kf1 (21. Nce2 $5 Bb7 $2 (21... Ra7 $142 $13 {/}) 22. Nxe6 $1 Rd1+ 23. Kf2 fxe6 24. Bxe6+ Kh8 25. Qxg6 (25. Rxh7+ $1 Kxh7 26. Qh4+ Qh6 27. Bg8+ $18) 25... Qc5+ 26. Nd4 $1 {1-0 P.Szekely-B.Toth Budapest 67}) (21. Bxe6 Rxd4 $1 $19 (21... fxe6 $2 22. f7+ Kxf7 23. Rxh7+ $18)) (21. Qe3 Bb7 (21... Ra7 $2 22. Ne4 Rad7 $2 23. Rxh7 $1 {1-0 Tringov-Palmasson Havanna ol 66}) 22. Qh3 (22. Nxe6 fxe6 23. Bxe6+ Kh8 24. Qh3 Qc5+ 25. Kh1 h5 $19) 22... Qc5 23. Nce2 Rxd4 $1 {[%cal Yb8d7]  24...Sd7/}) 21... Ra7 $2 (21... Rd6 22. Nf3 Nd7 23. Ne4 Bb7 24. Nxd6 (24. Rxh7 K