{Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 36 Gaston and Alphonse. "I like to coax my opponents into attacking, to let them taste the joy of the initiative, so that they may get carried away, become careless, and sacrifice material," wrote Korchnoi, whose comments are interwoven in the notes. Fischer needs no coaxing. He improves on a well-known Capablanca line (with 15.d5). Still, the advantage he derives, if any, is microscopic. Korchnoi seems to labor under the delusion that he has the worst of it, though Fischer keeps asserting that White has nothing. Nevertheless, he overreaches himself, giving Korchnoi a chance to assume the initiative. But Black falters and then cracks under the pressure of the clock. ''} 1. e4 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. d4 {Yates,F; Alekhine,A Yates,F; Alekhine,A Ernst: 'Yates,F; Alekhine,A Bobby Fischer: ', An old try, championed by Yates and Alekhine, but discarded in the course of progress. It is still theoretically crucial - on its success (or failure) hinges the necessity of White's losing a tempo here with the customary 9 h3. ''} (9. h3) 9... Bg4 10. Be3 exd4 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Gligorich's''} 10... d5 $142 {Gligoric,S Gligoric,S Ernst: ' Gligoric,S Bobby Fischer: 'is probably best.'' ; Gligoric,S}) ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'On the old''} 10... Nxe4 $5 11. Bd5 Qd7 12. Bxe4 d5 13. Bxh7+ Kxh7 14. dxe5 $16 {Byrne,R Byrne,R Ernst: 'Byrne,R Bobby Fischer: 'White, according to Robert Byrne, can keep his extra Pawn and weather the attack.''}) 11. cxd4 Na5 (11... d5 12. e5 Ne4 13. Nbd2 Nxd2 14. Qxd2 Bxf3 15. gxf3 Bb4 16. Qc2 Bxe1 17. Qxc6 Bb4 18. Bxd5 $16 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'is known to favor White.''}) 12. Bc2 Nc4 (12... c5 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'may be better;''} 13. Nbd2 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Hence''} 13. dxc5 dxc5 14. Nbd2 $14 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'seems the only try for an advantage.''}) 13... cxd4 14. Bxd4 Nc6 15. Be3 d5 16. exd5 Nb4 $10 {Yates,F-Bogoljubow,E New York 1924 Yates,F-Bogoljubow,E New York 1924 Ernst: 'Yates,F-Bogoljubow,E New York 1924 Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '(Yates,F-Bogoljubow,E; New York, 1924)'' '='}) 13. Bc1 c5 14. b3 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Interesting is''} 14. Nbd2 Nxd2 15. Qxd2 Bxf3 16. gxf3 {Geller,E-Panno,O Amsterdam 1956 Geller,E-Panno,O Amsterdam 1956 Ernst: 'Geller,E-Panno,O Amsterdam 1956 Bobby Fischer: '(Geller,E-Panno,O; Amsterdam, 1956)''}) 14... Na5 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Korchnoi is of a mind that the retreat''} 14... Nb6 {= Kortschnoj = Kortschnoj Ernst: '= Kortschnoj Bobby Fischer: 'completely equalizes. But White can keep a pull after'' Korchnoi,V: =} 15. Nbd2 cxd4 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'maybe better is''} 15... Nfd7 $142 { ?} 16. h3 {Bobby Fischer} Bh5 17. g4 Bg6 18. d5 Bf6 19. Rb1 h5 $1 {Pietzsch-Szabo, 1962.}) 16. h3 Bh5 17. g4 $1 Bg6 18. Nxd4 $36 {Pietzsch,W-Matanovic,A Habana 1962 Pietzsch,W-Matanovic,A Habana 1962 Ernst: 'Pietzsch,W-Matanovic,A Habana 1962 Bobby Fischer: '(Pietzsch,W-Matanovic,A; Habana, 1962)''}) 15. d5 $1 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: '"A strong continuation which improves on''} 15. Bb2 Nc6 $1 16. d5 Nb4 $15 {Capablanca,J-Bogoljubow,E London 1922 Capablanca,J-Bogoljubow,E London 1922 Ernst: 'Capablanca,J-Bogoljubow,E London 1922 Bobby Fischer: '(Capablanca-Bogolyubow, London 1922) when Black gains the advantage of the pair of Bishops." (KORCHNOr.)''}) 15... Nd7 ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'On''} 15... Nxe4 16. Rxe4 Bxf3 17. Qxf3 Bf6 18. Nc3 b4 19. Bb2 bxc3 20. Bxc3 Bxc3 21. Qxc3 $16 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'White has a big advantage.''}) 16. Nbd2 Bf6 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: '; Aiming to strike on the dark squares before White can mobilize a K-side initiative. ''} 17. Rb1 c4 {Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: '; Korchnoi considers this overambitious, believing that it creates too many Pawn weaknesses.''} ({Ernst: 'Bobby Fischer: 'He thinks Black ought to play''} 17... Ne5 $142 {Kortschnoj Korchnoi,V: } 18. h3 Nxf3+ 19. Nxf3 Bxf3 20. Qxf3 b4 {Ernst: '