{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 34 Hanging pawns unhung. Classical theory expounds the danger of "hanging Pawns," but Fischer demonstrates here, in a revolutionary manner, that they are just as often an asset as a liability. Bertok's errors seem insignificant, yet he drifts into a passive position. On the verge of exploiting Black's loose center, he always lacks just the one tempo needed to do so. Meanwhile, using the open b-file as a base of operations, Fischer manages to force White into a defensive posture. In the midst of this Q-side tension, the winning move (21...g5) comes unexpectedly on the opposite wing. '} {Andrew Soltis: 'Stockholm was a 23-round marathon but by the 15th round Fischer had a commanding lead. Draws in the remaining seven games would have easily assured his qualification for the next Candidates Tournament. But Fischer's main opponent was history. He was intent on surpassing Alexander Kotov's record in winning the 1952 Interzonal. This game, from the penultimate round, put him within reach. But he fell just short of Kotov's winning percentage and three-point margin of victory' White lost because he cannot exert pressure on the Hanging Pawns.} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Konikowski Bobby Fischer} d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Be7 {Charousek,R; Petrosian,T Bobby Fischer: '; A refinement attributed to Petrosian, but actually played by Charousek in the 'nineties - and probably dating back even farther. '} 4. Nf3 {Bobby Fischer: ', White, having no other good waiting move, is obliged to develop, thus restricting his option of playing this Knight to e2.'} (4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 c6 6. e3 Bf5 7. g4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'R.Byrne's'} 7. Nge2 $1 {Byrne,R Bobby Fischer: 'is best)' Andrew Soltis: '; Byrne,R'}) 7... Be6 (7... Bg6 $5 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'is better.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: ''}) 8. h3 {Botvinnik,M-Petrosian,T Moscow WCH 1963 Bobby Fischer: 'was played frequently in the 1963 title match between Botvinnik and Petrosian. White is slightly better.'}) 4... Nf6 5. Bg5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Back to the main line. The shadow boxing is over. '} O-O 6. e3 h6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Petrosian usually omits this move (see note to Black's 8th). '} 7. Bh4 b6 {Tartakover,S Bobby Fischer: '; Tartakover's Defense. '} 8. cxd5 {Bobby Fischer: ', The best procedure, opening the c-file and preparing Rc1 with pressure on Black's c-pawn.' Andrew Soltis: ''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'An alternative is' Neil McDonald: 'and'} 8. Bd3 $5 {Neil McDonald: '. These will be examined in the games that follow.'} Bb7 9. O-O Nbd7 10. Rc1 c5 11. Qe2 dxc4 12. Bxc4 Ne4 $10 {Petrosian,T-Fischer,R/Belgrade ct1959/ Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '(Petrosian-Fischer, Candidates' 1959.)' Andrew Soltis: '=' Andrew Soltis: 'Petrosian,T-Fischer,R Beograd ct 1959'}) ({White has also tried a variety of moves here that delay the setting up of the hanging pawns, such as} 8. Be2) 8... Nxd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'A basic premise of the Tarakower Variation is that by trading two sets of minor pieces Black can defend the hanging or isolated pawn more easily.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Inferior here is' Neil McDonald: 'Actually there is nothing particularly wrong with'} 8... exd5 {Neil McDonald: ', when Black can activate his game in the style of Kramnik-Yusupov below after'} 9. Bd3 {Bobby Fischer: 'and'} Bb7 (9... Z0 {Bobby Fischer} 10. Ne5 $1 {followed by f4 with a Pillsbury attacking formation: White has g4-g5 in the air - this line is playable for Black only with his pawn on h7 (instead of h6).}) 10. Ne5 $1 { f2-f4, g2-g4-g5 } (10. Rc1 Nbd7 11. O-O Ne4 12. Bg3 c5 {etc with a complex game. Theory might claim a slight advantage for White, but who cares? If Black is happy with the space and counterchances offered by the hanging pawns, he won't be concerned by their slight vulnerability that theory converts into the sign . Of course, things are different if the verdict is a clear advantage to White, as that means that the theorists think they have detected a drawback to the black position that goes beyond mere suspicion about the hanging pawns. You have to check carefully to see if they are right. Nonetheless, I must confess I like clarity and would always play 8...Nxd5 to get some pieces off the board. But that's no more than saying I prefer the games of Capablanca (or Fischer!) to those of Alekhine.})) 9. Bxe7 Qxe7 10. Nxd5 {Neil McDonald: 'White captures before Black has the chance to play ...Bb7, thus denying him the option of recapturing on d5 with the bishop,'} ({as occurred after} 10. Rc1 Bb7 11. Nxd5 Bxd5 {in the Najdorf-Spassky game.}) 10... exd5 {Bobby Fischer: '; The text is drawish, but I had already clinched first prize. '} 11. Be2 {Neil McDonald: 'The sort of respectful move you play against Fischer when he has been crushing everyone in sight. At the Stockholm Interzonal the US legend had notched up 12 wins and 8 draws at this point.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Sharper is' Andrew Soltis: 'In My 60 Memorable Games, Fischer analyzed'} 11. Rc1 Be6 $1 12. Qa4 c5 13. Qa3 Rc8 {Andrew Soltis: 'and gave one continuation which was'} 14. Be2 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now' Andrew Soltis: 'which he said was equal after'} ({But by the 1972 World Championship match he had found} 14. Bb5 $1 {Andrew Soltis , with the idea that} Nd7 ({Spassky, one of the acknowledged Tartakower experts during the 1960s, had never lost with it - until Fischer beat him in the sixth match game after} 14... a6 15. dxc5 bxc5 16. O-O Ra7 17. Be2 Nd7 18. Nd4 $1) 15. O-O {leaves Black vulnerable to Bxd7 and dxc5.}) 14... Kf8 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'levels;' Andrew Soltis: '='} ({Bobby Fischer: 'while' Andrew Soltis: 'and double edged after'} 14... Qb7 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is the prescription for maintaining tension. If'} 15. dxc5 bxc5 16. O-O (16. Rxc5 $2 Rxc5 17. Qxc5 Qxb2 $17 {Bobby Fischer: 'is bad for White.'}) 16... Qb6 $13 {Bobby Fischer: 'is double-edged.'})) 11... Be6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '; The right post. At b7 this Bishop would block the b-file and obstruct later operations there. ' Neil McDonald: 'The best square for the bishop.' Andrew Soltis: 'Another page of the Tarakower plan calls for developing the bishop here, not b7, so that Black can occupy the b-file with heavy pieces'} ({The alternative} 11... Bb7 {would equally defend the d5-pawn and so facilitate ...c7-c5, but then the bishop would get in the way of any future counterplay against b2 based on ...Rab8 etc and also be vulnerable to attack in some lines. TIP: If you have the choice between going to e6 or b7 in the Tartakower, and you can't decide either way, ...Be6 is almost certainly the right move.}) 12. O-O (12. Ne5 $142) 12... c5 13. dxc5 $2 {Bobby Fischer: ', Producing hanging center Pawns which, in this case, exert a tremendously cramping influence on White's future development.' White's timing is bad. He cannot put pressures on the Hanging Pawns.} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer called this a mistake and said'} 13. Ne5 $142 {Andrew Soltis: ''} Nd7 $10 {Andrew Soltis: '=' Andrew Soltis: 'was better - although only equal. In fact, Black would stand excellently after'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 13... c4 $2 14. b3 b5 15. a4) {with equality.} 14. Z0 {Bobby Fischer} (14. Nd3 {Andrew Soltis} c4 15. Nf4 b5 {. In the years since 1962 White has obtained a small edge with 13.Qd2 or 13.Qa4 - and it was realized that Bertok's real mistake was his next move.})) 13... bxc5 {Neil McDonald: 'The by now familiar pawn structure has emerged.'} 14. Qa4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({A better plan is} 14. Ne5 {Andrew Soltis} Nd7 15. Nd3 $1 {and Nf4 since ...c4 leaves the d-pawn more vulnerable than inthe last note (that is without dxc5 bxc5}) 14... Qb7 $1 15. Qa3 (15. b3) 15... Nd7 16. Ne1 {Bobby Fischer: ', What else is there? Black's center is well-prutected, and he is ready to assume the Q-side initiative with ... a5 and ...Qb4. ' Bobby Fischer: ', What else is there? Black's center is well-prutected, and he is ready to assume the Q-side initiative with ... a5 and ...Qb4. ' Bobby Fischer: ', What else is there? Black's center is well-prutected, and he is ready to assume the Q-side initiative with ... a5 and ...Qb4. ' Konikowski: '?!' Andrew Soltis: 'The knight heads to f4 but the pressure on d5 isn't quite enough to balance Black's queenside initiative as we'll see at move 19. WHite might have done better with'} (16. Rac1 $5 {Konikowski} Rfb8 17. Rc2 Bf5 18. Rd2 Nf6 $15) (16. Ba6 {Andrew Soltis} Qc7 17. b3) 16... a5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Neil McDonald: '!' Neil McDonald: 'An excellent move. The white queen is further boxed in and has to reckon with ...Qb4! when the exchange Qxb4 ...a5xb4 greatly invigorates the black pawns, while letting Black take the queens off on a3 will double White's pawns and make c5 into a passed pawn.' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's powerful plan of ...Qb4 had been made famous in Rubinstein-Nimzovich, Carlsbad 1907'} 17. Nd3 c4 {Neil McDonald: '!' Neil McDonald: 'Normally we would frown upon this advance, as the hanging pawns lose their flexibility and a whopping hole appears on d4. However, Fischer has seen that he can gain decisive pressure against the b2-pawn by disrupting its defence. This strategy depends on the fact that the white minor pieces are on very poor squares, especially the knight. Try a little experiment: swap the white knight and bishop around, so that the bishop is on d3 and the knight on e2. Then things are far healthier for White, as the bishop can retreat to c2, stopping Black playing ...Bf5, and the knight can go to d4. As things stand – put the knight back on d3, bishop on e2 please! – the pawn advance drives the knight away from its defence of b2, and also makes possible ...Bf5, which prevents the pawn being guarded by Rab1.'} 18. Nf4 Rfb8 {Neil McDonald: '!'} ({Not} 18... Qb4 $4 19. Qxb4 axb4 20. e4 $1 {when it's all gone wrong for Black, in view of} dxe4 21. Nxe6 fxe6 22. Bxc4 {.}) 19. Rab1 $2 {Neil McDonald: 'Just losing time.' Andrew Soltis: 'White's knight maneuver made sense but he fails to follow it through. The alternatives are'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'White's game is already difficult, e.g.,' Andrew Soltis: 'An assault on d5 with'} 19. Bf3 Nf6 20. Rfd1 {Andrew Soltis: '. But Black's c-pawn proves too srtrong after'} Qxb2 21. Qxb2 Rxb2 {'-+'} 22. Nxd5 Nxd5 23. Bxd5 Bxd5 24. Rxd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and now'} c3 $1 25. Rdd1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 25. Rc5 c2 26. Rc1 Rd8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) (25. Rad1 {Andrew Soltis} c2 26. Rc1 Rab8 {and ...Rb1}) 25... c2 26. Rdc1 Rab8 27. Kf1 Rb1 {Konikowski: '-+'} 28. Ke2 Rxa1 29. Rxa1 Rb1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Best is' Neil McDonald: 'Fischer suggests' Andrew Soltis: 'An attack on e6 (which Fischer used successfully in the Spassky game in a slightly different setup) Here'} 19. Nxe6 $142 {Andrew Soltis: ''} fxe6 20. Bg4 {Andrew Soltis: 'offers White slight chances of saving a half point after'} Ra6 $1 21. b3 $1 {Neil McDonald: ', sacrificing a pawn, as the best drawing chance. Yes, it's already that bad for White, and his only crime has been to play too passively.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 21. Qe7 $2 Nf8) ({Bobby Fischer: 'or'} 21. Rab1 Qb4 22. Qc3 Qxc3 23. bxc3 Rab6) 21... cxb3 22. axb3 Qxb3 23. Qe7 Nf8 24. Ra3 {Bobby Fischer: 'with good drawing chances.'} {Bobby Fischer: '(if 24'} Qb4 25. Qxb4 Rxb4 26. Be2 Ra7 27. Rfa1 {Konikowski: 'Fischer'} a4 28. Bd1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.)' Andrew Soltis: '='}) ({Liquidating everything with} 19. e4 $1 {Andrew Soltis , as recommended by Keres and not mentioned by Fischer. After} dxe4 (19... Qxb2 20. Qxb2 Rxb2 21. exd5 Bxd5 22. Rfe1 {White should have no fear}) 20. Nxe6 fxe6 21. Bxc4) 19... Bf5 $1 20. Rbd1 Nf6 21. Rd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'The following variation gives some insight into the nature of White's problem:' Andrew Soltis: 'This loses in the middlegame, whereas'} 21. Bf3 Qxb2 (21... Be4 22. Bxe4 Nxe4 $15) 22. Qxb2 Rxb2 23. Nxd5 Nxd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'is a lost ending. For example'} 24. Bxd5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 24. Rxd5 Be6 25. Rc5 Rc8 $1 {''} 26. Rxa5 c3 27. Rc1 (27. Ra8 {Andrew Soltis} Rxa8 28. Bxa8 f5 $1 {and the c-pawn wins.}) 27... c2 28. Be4 Rb1 $1 29. Rxb1 cxb1=Q+ 30. Bxb1 Rc1#) 24... Rc8 25. e4 Be6 $1 26. Bxe6 fxe6 27. a4 c3 {'-+'} 28. Rc1 c2 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'and White, completely tied up, must lose material.'} 29. f3 {Konikowski} Rd8 $19) 21... g5 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '; Practically forcing the win of a piece. ' Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '; Practically forcing the win of a piece. ' Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '; Practically forcing the win of a piece. ' Konikowski: '!' Neil McDonald: '!' Neil McDonald: 'Another "anti-positional" pawn attack on the knight! The first (17...c4) "weakened" the black centre, the second "weakens" the kingside. Of course, everything has to go in inverted commas, as when Black has such a strong initiative it is irrelevant to talk of structural weaknesses. NOTE: You can't judge a position purely on the strength or otherwise of a pawn structure. Far more important is how well the pieces are coordinated. ' Andrew Soltis: 'Now 22.Nh5 NE4 23.Rc2 Qb4! wins a la Nimzovich, and is much cleaner than 23. ...Ng3 24.Nf6+'} 22. Nxd5 {Bobby Fischer: ', To break the hammer-lock.' Andrew Soltis: '?!' Neil McDonald: 'A desperate sacrifice;'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Neil McDonald: 'but if'} 22. Nh5 Ne4 23. Rc2 Qb4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing.' Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing.' Bobby Fischer: 'is crushing.' Konikowski: '' Neil McDonald: '!' Neil McDonald: '(Fischer). Then after'} 24. Qxb4 axb4 {the hanging pawns have become a terrible trio that smash through the queenside:} 25. Ra1 b3 26. Rcc1 c3 $1 {and White can't escape losing a rook to a fork on b2.}) 22... Nxd5 23. Bxc4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not' Andrew Soltis: 'White has even less compensation after'} 23. Bf3 $2 Bd3) 23... Be6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Black has some temporary discomfort but it's only a matter of time before he consolidates and wins with his extra piece. '} 24. Rfd1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Blundering a Pawn.' Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'The lesser evil is' Andrew Soltis: 'White preserves his dignity a bit longer with'} 24. Bxd5 $142 {Andrew Soltis: ''} Bxd5 25. f3 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'but White is still lost if Black exercises a modicum of caution.'} ({Euwe suggested} 25. Qd6 {Andrew Soltis on the grounds that} Bxg2 26. Qxh6 {was "very risky". In fact} Ra6 $1 {would end the game speedily.})) 24... Nxe3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: '; Threatening mate. ' Neil McDonald: 'Deadly simplification with the threat of mate on g2.'} 25. Qxe3 (25. Bxe6 Qxg2#) 25... Bxc4 {Konikowski: '-+' Andrew Soltis: 'The rest warrants no comment'} 26. h4 Re8 27. Qg3 Qe7 28. b3 Be6 29. f4 g4 {Bobby Fischer: '; Sealing the g-file and neutralizing all threats. '} 30. h5 Qc5+ 31. Rf2 Bf5 {Bobby Fischer: '; White resigns.'} 0-1

