{B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack Garry Kasparov: 'ChessCalisthenics: 'B09: Pirc Defence: Austrian Attack''} 1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 {'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation.  ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation. ''' Garry Kasparov: 'Korchnoi has played not more than a dozen games in his entire career with this defence, considering it 'not altogether correct'. On this occasion, taking account of the narrowness of his opponent's opening repertoire, he was trying to catch him in a specific variation.'} 4. f4 (4. Nf3 {– Game Nos.73 and 77 Garry Kasparov: '– Game Nos.73 and 77'}) 4... Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O ({The alternative is} 5... c5 6. dxc5 (6. Bb5+ Bd7 {Savon-Korchnoi, 41st USSR Championship, Moscow 1973}) 6... Qa5 7. Bd3 Qxc5 8. Qe2 Bg4 $1 9. Be3 Qa5 10. O-O O-O 11. h3 (11. Rad1 Nc6 12. Bc4 Nh5 $5 {Spassky-Fischer, 17th matchgame, Reykjavik 1972}) 11... Bxf3 12. Qxf3 Nc6 13. Ne2 $6 Nd7 14. c3 $2 Nde5 $1 15. fxe5 Nxe5 16. Qg3 Nxd3 {. 0-1 (Hübner-Korchnoi, Skelleftea 1989). Garry Kasparov: '. 0-1 (Hübner-Korchnoi, Skelleftea 1989).'}) 6. Be2 {''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi). ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: ''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.' (Korchnoi).''' Garry Kasparov: ''According to the state of theory in the summer of 1962, 6 Be2 was the best move in this position.'  (Korchnoi).'} ({After this game everyone, including Fischer, switched to} 6. Bd3 {(Volume 4, Game No. 72).}) 6... c5 7. dxc5 Qa5 8. O-O Qxc5+ 9. Kh1 Nc6 10. Nd2 {'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'An idea of Evgeny Vasyukov, who himself thought up its refutation.''' Schachmaty 1962/Kortschnoj: '?!'} (10. Bd3 {followed by Qe1 and Be3 is better (Boleslavsky-Ufimtsev, Sverdlovsk 1951), Schachmaty 1962/Kortschnoj: ''}) ({or first} 10. Qe1 {. 'Prior to the tournament,' Korchnoi remembers, 'in a holiday home near Moscow, I had studied this very position with my helper, grandmaster Vasyukov. Generally speaking, at that time I did not play the Pirc Defence, and its various branches were totally unfamiliar to me, but Vasyukov persuaded me to take a risk: Fischer was bound to play this way! And Fischer did not let me down.' Schachmaty 1962/Kortschnoj: '' Garry Kasparov: '. 'Prior to the tournament,' Korchnoi remembers, 'in a holiday home near Moscow, I had studied this very position with my helper, grandmaster Vasyukov. Generally speaking, at that time I did not play the Pirc Defence, and its various branches were totally unfamiliar to me, but Vasyukov persuaded me to take a risk: Fischer was bound to play this way! And Fischer did not let me down.''}) 10... a5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' '!''A strong novelty. ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'A strong novelty.''' Schachmaty 1962/Kortschnoj: '!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'or''or ChessCalisthenics: 'Garry Kasparov: 'or'''} 10... Be6 11. Nb3 Qb6 12. Bf3 (12. g4 $1 {was judged to favour White.}) 12... a5 13. Re1 a4 14. Be3 Qa6 15. Nd4 a3 16. b3 Bg4 17. Nxc6 Qxc6 18. Nd5 Bxf3 19. Nxe7+ Kh8 20. gxf3 Qd7 21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. Qxd5 Bxa1 23. Rxa1 Qh3 24. Qd1 b5 25. c3 {Arkhipov,S (2532)-Safin,S (2518)/Abu Dhabi 2000/CBM 077 ext/ 0-1 (39)}) ({At that time} 10... Nd4 11. Nb3 Nxb3 12. axb3 {(Vasyukov-Bastrikov, Kiev 1957)} Bd7 (12... b5) 13. e5 $1 Ne8 14. Be3 $1 $36 {Wasyukov-Bastrikov/USSR/1962}) 11. a4 (11. Nb3 $6 {'T