{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 38 Detective story. Occasionally one comes across a miraculous victory in which, despite intensive post-mortems, there seems to be no losing move or pattern, no blunder on the part of the vanquished. But how can that be possible? A loser must make a mistake somewhere, however infinitesimal, however it may evade detection. Is it Keres' opening novelty which leads him to disaster? Could his defense have been improved afterward? If so: where? Whatever the answer, the reader is invited to share the magnifying glass with Fischer and hunt for that elusive error. ' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's terrific Interzonal result raised expectations extraordinarily high. In retrospect, he wasn't as strong as he seemed. Fischer hadn't played particularly well against the stronger player at Stockholm and his weaknesses were exposed at Curacao when he lost his first two games, to Benko and Tal. After missing an easy win in the third round (but eventually winning), he was close to tears after losing to Korchnoi. When he faced Keres at the end of the first cycle at Curacao, Fischer appeared to be playing the role of spoiler more than contender.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'I think everyone has one game that first comes in mind at the simplest mention of a player's name (try it thinking about your favourite players and you will understand what I mean). I like many of Fischer's victories, but this is "that game" for me. '} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis Quintiliano,Renato Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 ({Some years later, Fischer also gave new life to} 4. Bxc6 {Quintiliano,Renato} dxc6 5. O-O $1 {with nice victories against Gligoric and Portisch.}) 4... Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The good old days. No computers, no Berlin, no deeply-studied Marshall Gambit... '} 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 ({For} 9. d4 {(Bobby Fischer) see game 36. Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V Stockholm izt 1962}) 9... Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Nd7 {Bobby Fischer: '; "Now it is not easy to fmd a satisfactory continuation for White." (KERES.) Keres' novelty, introduced on this occasion, has since become quite fashionable. I was - and still am - unimpressed. Black loses time redeveloping his knight to b6, but the K-side is weakened by its absence and it's questionable whether the Knight is not better where it stands originally. ' Quintiliano,Renato: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'This became known as the "Keres variation" after this game (although it had been tried before). Black prepares to exchange twice on d4 and apply pressure with ...Bf6 and ... Nc6.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move rightly receives the name of Keres Variation. It failed to impress Fischer in this game, as for his notes he thought the manoeuvre f6-d7-b6 was simply a waste of time and left Black's kingside unprotected. Althought he succesfully proved his points in the present game, we can tell from the future that Keres "won" the theoretical discussion, as his idea survived the test of the time and it is actually a very interesting line, in which we can find many recent games in the database. '} 12. dxc5 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: ''!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer gives an exclamation mark to this capture, probably influenced by how well it worked in the game.'} (12. Nbd2 {Bobby Fischer: 'was all the rage, but' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Quintiliano,Renato: 'is the most played, and Black has two paths to follow: the classic'} cxd4 ({Or the modern line} 12... exd4 $5 {Quintiliano,Renato} 13. cxd4 Nc6 14. d5 Nce5 {which leads to complicated and unbalanced positions, this has been the most played by Black in the last years.}) 13. cxd4 Nc6 $10 {Bobby Fischer: 'may equalize.' Quintiliano,Renato: '=' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with nice chances of equalizing, as Black' position remains solid as usual.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'But not'} 12. dxe5 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Quintiliano,Renato: 'gives Black the option of'} Nxe5 $1 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with complete freedom.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with favourable simplifications.'}) ({"In spite of having won this game, it is probable that Fischer is not very convinced of the correctness of this continuation, because in a later game he closed the center with Quintiliano,Renato: 'In a later round in the same tournament, Fischer tried'} 12. d5 {(Bobby Fischer) ." (KERES.) According to that logic, Keres must not be convinced of the correctness of 11...Nd7 since he later varied with the old 11...Qc7 (against Gligorich at Hastings, 1965). Quintiliano,Renato: 'which is also playable, but he didn't play well and soon got an inferior position, allowing Keres to strike back in the line.'}) 12... dxc5 13. Nbd2 Qc7 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; This is supposed to lead to trouble. If such a natural developing move is bad, then what kind of a position is this for Black?' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '?'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'The start of a wrong plan, which aims to face the manoeuvre f1-e3-d5 by defending that square with as many pieces as possible. Fischer poses the following funny question here: "If such a natural developing move is bad, what should we think about this position for Black?". Although it makes sense, we should not take such evaluation as definitive. The only possible conclusion is that Keres didn't find the best way to deal with White's plan of manoeuvring the knight to d5.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'At the time Boleslavsky in his notes gave "' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In his notes, Boleslavsky recommends'} 13... f6 {Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '" and this cryptic evaluation apparently cowed the chess world into abandoning 12.dxc5 - until very recently. After' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Quintiliano,Renato: 'as the equalising path'} 14. Nh4 Nb6 15. Nf5 Rf7 {Bobby Fischer: '(Fischer-Ivkov, Havana, 1965)'} 16. Qg4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer gives this as an improvement'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'instead of my' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Over'} 16. Nxe7+ $2 {Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Habana 1965 Quintiliano,Renato: 'that just helped Black in'}) 16... Kh8 17. h4 $1 { h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening h5 followed by Nf3-h4 is in White's favor: e.g.,' Quintiliano,Renato: 'planning to increase the pressure on the kingside by means of h5-f3-h4-g6, when Black does not have a clear way to prevent this plan, for example:'} g6 18. Nh6 Rg7 19. Qf3 $16 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: '''' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and White keeps the initiative.'}) ({Later, Black found the right plan:} 13... Bb7 {Quintiliano,Renato} 14. Nf1 Nc4 $1 15. b3 Nd6 {[%csl Ye4];0-1 Velimirovic,D (2484)-Blagojevic,D (2486) YUG-chT Herceg Novi 2001 (5) and by attacking e4, Black prevents the knight from making it to d5.}) 14. Nf1 Nb6 15. Ne3 Rd8 16. Qe2 Be6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black has apparently managed to establish firm control over d5. '} ({White has a strong Nd5@ coming up. He stands better after} 16... f6 {Andrew Soltis} 17. Nh4 (17. Nd5 Nxd5 18. exd5 Nb7 19. a4 Bd7 20. Nh4 {as recommended by Fischer}) 17... Bf8 18. Nd5 $1 {as later played by Gligoric or}) 17. Nd5 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', The idea is to open lines and take advantage of Black's weakened K-side. ' Andrew Soltis: 'By indirectly trading e-pawns, WHite opens the center for his pieces.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'But Fischer doesn't care!'} Nxd5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'In the event of'} 17... Bxd5 18. exd5 f6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer indicates the strong idea'} 19. h4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'is strong. Now if' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!''} Nxd5 (19... Nac4 20. h5 Nd6 21. Qd3 $1 e4 22. Rxe4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'favoring White ChessBase: '!''} Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 23. Qxe4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'the attack seems winning for White.'}) 20. h5 $40 { e4  e4  e4  e4 Bobby Fischer: '(threatening 21.Qe4) is powerful.' Andrew Soltis: 'and Qe4 ChessBase: '' ChessBase: ' e4'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with the queen coming to e4, Black is under serious pressure.'}) 18. exd5 Bxd5 19. Nxe5 {Bobby Fischer: ', Since his early attacking days, Keres has switched to positional-defensive chess. But this type of position is too much even for him. ' Andrew Soltis: 'White's tactical resources include a discovered attack on the queen after Bf4, and a combination of Rad1xd4 and Qe4. Among the futile defenses are' Quintiliano,Renato: 'At first sight, we could think that White didn't accomplish too much with the last operation, as the central pawns were just exchanged and nothing terrible seems to be happening for Black here. But if we examine the position more closely, it will be clear that Black is the side who needs to be careful in his next moves: the c2 became very active, and his king looks dangerously abandoned on g8.'} Ra7 {Bobby Fischer: '; To defend the second rank. What else can Black do? White threatens to build up with Bf4 and Rad1, and already sacrificial themes are in the aIr.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'This move seemed awkward to me, and I can still remember how perplexed I was when seeing Fischer's analysis and being forced to agree with him that there is nothing better for Black. White is threatening to build annoying pressure by simple moves like f4-ad1, and some sacrificial attacking ideas are already in the air. I really like this position, especially because Fischer insists on supporting his evaluation with concrete lines. Let's check them:'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 19... Bd6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'loses due to'} 20. Qd3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'ditto'} Bxe5 21. Qxh7+ Kf8 22. f4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '+-'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'winning a piece.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 19... Be6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} 20. Nxf7 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!'' Andrew Soltis: 'with Qxe6 or Qxe7 to come ChessBase: '!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'wins a healthy pawn.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 19... Bf8 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'looks a solid retreat, but after'} 20. Qh5 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is forced to compromise his kingside structure.'} g6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 20... h6 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 21. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with more than one dangerous threat.'}) 21. Qh4 Bg7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 21... Be7 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 22. Qg3 { xg6  xg6  xg6  xg6 Bobby Fischer: 'threatening 23.Nxg6.'}) 22. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '' Andrew Soltis: 'followed by Bg5, Nf6+' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White has a strong attack.'}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally'} 19... f6 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'allows a beautiful attack:'} 20. Qh5 $1 fxe5 (20... g6 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Nxg6) 21. Bxh7+ Kf8 22. Rxe5 Bf7 23. Rf5 Bf6 24. Rxf6 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'White invests more material to force the black king to go back to the centre, where it will not survive too long.'} gxf6 25. Bh6+ Ke7 26. Re1+ Be6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 26... Kd6 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 27. Bf4+ Kc6 28. Be4+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.'}) 27. Bf5 Rd6 28. Bf4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins.' Andrew Soltis: 'as pointed out by Fischer ChessBase: '+-''}) (19... Re8 $2 20. Qd3 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-' Andrew Soltis: 'attacking the bishop and h7'}) 20. Bf4 Qb6 {Bobby Fischer: '; The threat was 21.Ng6.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Not much is changed by'} 20... Qc8 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 21. Rad1 {Bobby Fischer: 'quietly continues the build-up.' Quintiliano,Renato: ''}) 21. Rad1 $1 { xd5  xd5  xd5  xd5 Bobby Fischer: 'Threatening' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: ' xd5'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Simple and strong, creating the threat of xd5 followed by e4. Instead of looking for forced sequences, Fischer just improves his position.'} {Bobby Fischer: 'Instead of seeking a "violent solution," my instinct told me to strengthen the position. '} g6 {Bobby Fischer: '; Eliminating stock combinations against h7, but creating new weaknesses on the dark squares.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'There is no simple solution for Black. Keres prevents ideas on h7, but on the other hand weakens the dark squares around his king.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'What's better? On' Andrew Soltis: 'This anticipates the threats of 22.Bxh7+ or 22.Rxd5. The only alternative FIscher gave in his notes was'} 21... Bxa2 {Andrew Soltis: 'and it's not surprising that this is punished by' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Sometimes, if you are going to suffer, it is better to do it by grabbing a pawn. So, had Fischer just ignored or forgot the a2-pawn? Of course not! In that case, Black would fall into a forced sequence, which I have no doubt that Fischer had calculated until the very end:'} 22. Rxd8+ Qxd8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 22... Bxd8 23. Nc4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'revealing problems on the back rank'} Qe6 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 24. Qd1 Rd7 25. Nd2 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 23. b4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'a strong blow'} cxb4 24. cxb4 Bxb4 {Bobby Fischer: '(the Knight can't move because of Nc6)'} (24... Nc4 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 25. Nc6 $18) 25. Qe4 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'White sacrifices the rook, but in return manages to break through Black's defences'} Bxe1 26. Qxh7+ Kf8 27. Qh8+ Ke7 28. Bg5+ f6 29. Ng6+ Kd7 30. Bf5+ Kc7 31. Bf4+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'wins the Queen.' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '+-'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and Black loses the queen.'}) (21... Z0 {Bobby Fischer} 22. Rxd5 Rxd5 23. Qe4) ({Black's difficulties are also underlined by} 21... Qf6 {(Andrew Soltis) and then} 22. Rxd5 Rxd5 23. Qe4 Qh4 24. g3 Rxe5 25. Qxe5 {and Qb8+ wins}) 22. Ng4 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer immediately turns his attention to the new weakened squares on the kingside.'} Nc4 {Bobby Fischer: '; An attempt to bring this Knight toward the embattled sector.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres struggles to bring his pieces on the queenside to more useful positions.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'After'} 22... Bxa2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Quintiliano,Renato: 'is still b