lack is tied up in knots, I felt sure of a patient strategical victory. '} 23... Be6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'This plugs the e-file but only temporarily. After'} 23... Nxb2 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses to' Andrew Soltis: 'White has' Quintiliano,Renato: 'now loses due to'} 24. Rxd5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!''} Rxd5 25. Be4 {Andrew Soltis: 'wining material'} Rd8 26. Qxb2 f5 27. c4 $1 $18 { g7#  g7#  g7#  g7# Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: '(threatening Qg7 mate).' Andrew Soltis: '(ChessBase)  g7#' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and White wins.'}) 24. Bb3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Pinning the Knight and piling on the pressure. ' Quintiliano,Renato: 'It is very nice how Fischer manages to increase the pressure on Black's camp by means of natural moves.'} Qb8 {× e5 × e5 × e5 × e5 Bobby Fischer: '; To prevent White's Queen, in some variations, from penetrating to e5. ' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '× e5'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is basically forced to keep an eye on e5, as after a possible exchange on c4, an appearance of the White queen on e5 would be devastating.'} ({To avert Qe5. On} 24... Bxg4 {Andrew Soltis} 25. Rxd8+ Qxd8 26. hxg4 Qd5 (26... Nd6 27. Qe5 $1 Bf6 28. Qxd6) 27. Rd1 Qe6 {White can slowly build up his position} 28. Bxc4 ({or enter into a favorable endgame} 28. Qxe6 fxe6 29. Bxc4) 28... bxc4 29. Qd2 Ra8 30. Re1 Qd6 31. Qe2) 25. Rxd8+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Bxd8 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'The lesser evil, but this provides other tactical motifs for White.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not' Andrew Soltis: 'Black loses quickly after'} 25... Qxd8 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '?'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'would be even worse:'} 26. Bxc4 Bxc4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if'} 26... bxc4 27. Qe5 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-' Andrew Soltis: '!'}) 27. Nf6+ $1 Kh8 28. Qe5 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and Black can't avoid mate in the next moves.'}) 26. Bxc4 bxc4 27. Qxc4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Cashing in! "Converting a spatial advantage into a material one." (See Evans' New Ideas in Chess.) ' Andrew Soltis: 'not just winning a pawn but threatening 28.Rxe6 fxe6 29.Qxe6+ and mates with Qe8. Of course 27. ...Bxc4 28.Re8#' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Converting the positional advantage into a material one. The back rank becomes the main source of problems for Black now, as Fischer skilfully demonstrates from now.'} Qd6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not'} 27... Qxb2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 28. Rxe6 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!''}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or'} 27... Bxc4 $4 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 28. Re8#) 28. Qa4 Qe7 (28... Qd7 {(Andrew Soltis) No improvement is} 29. Rd1 $1) 29. Nf6+ {Quintiliano,Renato: '!'} Kh8 30. Nd5 Qd7 31. Qe4 $1 {Bobby Fischer: ', Back to the old stand, angling for e5 again. ' Andrew Soltis: 'The pressure, based on 31. ... Bxd5 32. Qe8+ mounts' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Back to the old juicy e5-square.'} Qd6 (31... Bxd5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 32. Qe8+ {Bobby Fischer: 'mates.'} Qxe8 33. Rxe8#) {Bobby Fischer: 'The weakness of Black's first rank has consistently proved to be his undoing throughout the mid-game. '} 32. Nf4 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Fischer grabs a second pawn, which is enough to win the game.'} (32. c4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is also good.' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Quintiliano,Renato: 'was a slower way, also enough to win.'}) 32... Re7 {Bobby Fischer: '; Token resistance. '} 33. Bg5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Once again, Fischer missed a knockout' Quintiliano,Renato: 'It is a little mysterious why Fischer didn't play'} 33. Bf8 $1 $18 {Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'wins outright.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '+-!'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'here, that basically would force Black to resign.'}) 33... Re8 34. Bxd8 Rxd8 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White also wins after'} 34... Qxd8 35. Qe5+ f6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'not'} 35... Kg8 36. Nd5 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-!' Quintiliano,Renato: ''!''}) 36. Qxc5 Bxa2 37. Rxe8+ Qxe8 38. c4 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is the easiest path to victory.' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '+-'' Quintiliano,Renato: 'with an easy endgame.'} ({better than} 38. Qb6 {Andrew Soltis} Qe1+ 39. Kh2 Qe5)) 35. Nxe6 Qxe6 ({Bobby Fischer: 'On'} 35... Re8 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 36. Qe5+ $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'is decisive.'}) 36. Qxe6 fxe6 37. Rxe6 Rd1+ 38. Kh2 Rd2 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres probably put his hopes on this rook ending in view of his active rook, but Fischer finds a precise idea to clarify things: '} 39. Rb6 Rxf2 40. Rb7 $1 {Quintiliano,Renato: 'Black is a pawn and a king down now, which means he is technically lost.'} Rf6 41. Kg3 {Bobby Fischer: ', Black resigns. Not only is Black a Pawn behind, but his King on the first rank is cut off as well.' Quintiliano,Renato: 'You can argue this is not the most beautiful game of Fischer and I would agree, of course. We didn't see either spectacular sacrifices or unexpected original ideas like his most famous games, but I really like the way he developed a strong initiative right from the opening and kept it until the very end, supporting even the most natural moves by concrete calculation. It's remarkable that on move 19 Black is almost running out of moves, and White's position is so superior that against any move there is a tactical refutation. I believe there is something poetic about such games as well. After all, it is a true masterpiece.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Keres didn't resume the adjourned position,whose outcome is transparent after' Quintiliano,Renato: 'Keres saw no reason to offer further resistance, as any Soviet master would know this endgame is beyond any chance of salvation. But let's see a possible line to make things clear:'} 41. Kg3 {Bobby Fischer: 'A likely winning line is'} Kg8 42. b4 cxb4 43. cxb4 Rd6 44. a4 Rd3+ 45. Kh2 {Andrew Soltis: 'etc.'} Ra3 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 46. a5 Ra4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'if' Quintiliano,Renato: 'If'} 46... Kh8 47. Kg1 Ra2 48. Kf1 Kg8 49. Ke1 {Quintiliano,Renato: '!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'White just brings the king to the queenside and wins, as if'} Rxg2 50. b5 axb5 51. a6 Ra2 52. a7 {Quintiliano,Renato: '+-'}) 47. Kg3 $18 {Bobby Fischer: 'and the King invades!' Quintiliano,Renato: 'and again the king marches to the queenside, with an easy win.'}) 1-0

