{Alexander Volzhin: 'This line was known not to be very good but it has received a certain amount of attention recently and from time to time it occurs even at the very highest level, mainly in Michael Adams' games.' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's form varied considerably in 1962, from Stockholm to Curacao to Varna, and he finished the year with his closest brush with failing to win the U.S. Championship. But in several of his victories, such as the following, he seemed more dominant than he did in any other time in his career.'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis ChessBase Alexander Volzhin} d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 (2... Nf6 3. c4 (3. Bb5+ Bd7 4. Bc4 Bg4 5. f3 Bf5 (5... Bc8) 6. g4 $1 Bc8 7. Nc3 Nbd7 8. g5 Nb6 9. Bb5+ Nfd7 10. f4 Nxd5 11. Nxd5 c6 12. Bc4 cxd5 13. Bxd5 $18 {Fischer,R-Bergraser,C Monte Carlo 1967}) (3. d4)) 3. Nc3 Qd8 {Andrew Soltis: '?!' Andrew Soltis: 'A 19th Century idea with a 20th Century twist. Black hopes to disrupt White's development with a fianchetto bearing down on d4.'} (3... Qa5 {Lorin D'Costa: 'is the main variation of the Scandinavian, in which Black accepts a slight loss of time with the queen sortie in order to develop freely; e.g.'} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 Nc6 (5... c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Bd2 e6 {.}) 6. d5 $5 (6. Bb5) 6... Nb4 7. Bb5+ c6 (7... Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Nxd7 9. a3 Nf6 10. axb4 Qxa1 11. O-O Qa6 12. Re1 $40 O-O-O $2 13. Ne5) 8. dxc6 bxc6 9. Ba4 Ba6 $2 (9... Bd7 { Andrew Soltis: ''}) 10. a3 Rd8 11. Bd2 Qf5 12. axb4 Rd6 13. Bb3 Ne4 14. Rxa6 Rxd2 15. Qc1 Nxc3 16. bxc3 Rd6 17. O-O {Fischer,R-Seidman,H ch-USA 1958}) (3... Qd6 {is also becoming popular, as the queen is more active here than on d8.}) 4. d4 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The initial position of the variation. Now Black has two different plans. One possibility is the fianchetto of his dark-squared Bishop, and another is to play the light-squared Bishop to f5 and the arising position looks like a typical Caro Kann.'} g6 $5 { g8-h6-f5; × d4 Andrew Soltis: ''!?'  g8-h6-f5; × d4'} ({Fischer-Addison, Palma de Mallorca went} 4... Nf6 {Andrew Soltis} 5. Bc4 Bf5 $6 6. Qf3 Qc8 7. Bg5 $1 Bxc2 8. Rc1 Bg6 9. Nge2 {with a strong initiative.}) 5. Bf4 $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Although this game was played almost 40 years ago Fischer's plan is considered as the best nowadays. The idea of this move (followed by 6 .Qd2) is to prevent Black's Knight manoeuvre to f5-square via h6.'} (5. Nf3 Bg7 6. h3 Nf6 $14 (6... Nh6 7. g4 $1 {Sokolsky,A})) ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Another possible plan is'} 5. Bc4 Bg7 6. Nf3 {Alexander Volzhin: 'aiming for a small but steady advantage, but recent practice proves Black has good chances of equalising:'} Nh6 7. Bxh6 $5 {Alexander Volzhin} ({The alternatives are:} 7. Ne4 {Alexander Volzhin} Nf5 8. c3 O-O 9. O-O Nd7 10. Bf4 Nd6 11. Bd3 {Kaminski-Kiedrowicz, Bielsko-Biala 1991.}) (7. Bg5 {Alexander Volzhin} Nf5 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 h6 10. Bf4 c6 11. Be5 {Arakhamia-Petrovic, Tuzla 1987, with a tiny edge in both cases.}) 7... Bxh6 8. Ne5 O-O 9. O-O Nd7 10. Qe2 Nb6 11. Rad1 Nxc4 12. Qxc4 Bf5 13. Qb3 Rb8 {and Black has little to worry about, Tiviakov-Galliamova, Elista 1998.}) 5... Bg7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black would have a comfortable game after 6.Nf3 Nh6 and ...Nf5 or 6.Nb5 Na6 and ....c6'} ({Alexander Volzhin: 'the immediate'} 5... Nh6 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is not good in view of'} 6. Be5 $1 f6 7. Bf4 {Alexander Volzhin: ', weakening Black's K-side.'}) 6. Qd2 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's move, an innovation, allows him to carry out a strong setup reminiscent of "sac, sac, mate". But it depends on the soundness of the pawn sacrifice.'} ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Tempting is'} 6. Nb5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'as was played in Bronstein-Kholmov, Tbilisi 1959 is harmless:'} Na6 { c7-c6; Bronstein,D-Kholmov,R URS 1959} 7. Nf3 {Alexander Volzhin} Nf6 8. c4 c6 9. Nc3 Bg4 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O Nd7 12. Be3 e5 13. d5 c5 14. Ne1 Bxe2 15. Qxe2 f5 {and in this complicated position Black's chances are by no means bad.}) (6. Nf3 Nh6) 6... Nf6 {Lorin D'Costa: 'The pawn is taboo:'} ({Alexander Volzhin: 'It's not good for Black to take the d4-pawn:'} 6... Qxd4 7. Qxd4 Bxd4 8. Nb5 (8. Nd5 Bb6 9. Bxc7 Nf6 10. Nxb6 axb6 11. Bxb6 {leaves White a clear pawn ahead.}) 8... Bb6 $8 {Andrew Soltis: ''} 9. Nxc7+ Bxc7 10. Bxc7 $16 { Alexander Volzhin: 'and the endgame clearly favors White'}) (6... Bxd4 $2 7. O-O-O Nc6 8. Bb5 Bd7 9. Nd5 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'much better than'} 9. Bxc6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: ''?''} Bxc6 10. Qxd4 $2 Qxd4 11. Rxd4 Bxg2) (9. Nf3 Bg7 10. Ne4 {with massive compensation for the pawn, as Black can't really unravel and get castled here.}) 9... e5 10. Nf3 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and Black can't avoid gross material losses.' Andrew Soltis: 'and wins '+-''}) 7. O-O-O c6 {Lorin D'Costa: 'Black tries not to commit his king yet, but will be forced to make a decision on the next move anyway.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer felt'} 7... Nd5 { Andrew Soltis: ''''} 8. Be5 (8. Bh6 $5 Bxh6 9. Qxh6 Nxc3) 8... O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'was better'} 9. h4 h5 10. Nge2 $16) (7... h6 {prevents Bh6 but it doesn't really help Black because he can't castle due to Bxh6. In some positions ...h6 can be a useful defensive resource, but in most cases Black will be hard pressed to defend the h6-pawn and then castle kingside.}) 8. Bh6 O-O $2 {Alexander Volzhin: 'This natural looking move is indeed the decisive mistake!'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer felt the king belonged on the queenside after'} 8... Bxh6 { Andrew Soltis: 'and ...Bf5'} 9. Qxh6 Bf5 { 0-0-0 Alexander Volzhin: 'intending to castle Q-side should be played, although in my opinion White is clearly better here.'} 10. Nf3 {Andrew Soltis} Nbd7 11. Ne5 Nb6 12. d5 Nbxd5 13. Nxd5 Nxd5 14. Qg7 Rf8 15. Rxd5 Qxd5 16. Bc4 Qc5 17. Rd1 {1-0 Borkowski,F-Zoltek,T/Lubniewice 1981/MCD (17)}) 9. h4 {Alexander Volzhin: '!' Alexander Volzhin: 'Exploiting Black's mistake White starts a K-side attack without delay.' Andrew Soltis: '!' Lorin D'Costa: 'The standard attack on the fianchetto, as we saw in Chapter Three. White can play either f3, g4 and h5, or just sacrifice the h-pawn with h5 to open up the h-file. '} Qa5 10. h5 $1 ({Timing matters : on} 10. Be2 {Andrew Soltis} Be6 $1 11. h5 Nbd7 {and Black's king may escape} 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. hxg6 fxg6 14. Qh6+ Kf7 15. Nf3 Ke8) ({Should} 10. Kb1 {be played here? It does defend the a2-pawn but it also loses what could be a crucial tempo in the upcoming king hunt. Fischer decides not to bother with this defensive king move and prefers to go out with all guns blazing to mate the black king!}) 10... gxh5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The only move.' Lorin D'Costa: 'This looks horrible but it still deserves attention. If Black can play, for example, ...Bf5 and ...Bg6 then he may be able to shore up the kingside; hence Fischer's next move.'} ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Black can't allow the opening of the h-file:'} 10... Rd8 11. hxg6 fxg6 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Qh6+ Kg8 14. Nf3 $18 {[%emt 0:00:15]  g5 Alexander Volzhin: 'and Black can't parry the threat of Ng5'}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'the key alternative is'} 10... Nxh5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is also insufficient:'} 11. Be2 (11. Bxg7 Kxg7 12. Re1 {(threatening the e7-pawn and Re5)} Be6 13. Re5 Qc7 14. Rexh5 $1 gxh5 15. Rxh5 f6 {and White has a raging attack:} 16. Z0 (16. Qh6+ Kf7 17. Qxh7+ Ke8 {and the king escapes, so White should develop his minor pieces instead. Just because Qh6 is playable, it doesn't mean you should play it and neglect development (unless it leads to forced mate of course!).}) (16. Bd3 Bg8 17. Nge2 {and White still enjoys huge compensation for the exchange. The h7-pawn will go and the black king will find he will have to make a long journey over the centre very soon.} Kf7 18. Bxh7 Ke8 19. Nf4 {is very good for White.} (19. Z0))) 11... Nf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'after which'} 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Qh6+ Kg8 14. g4 $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and White wins by force:' Andrew Soltis: 'and 19.g5 must win'} Rd8 15. g5 Nh5 16. Bxh5 gxh5 17. Rxh5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'or'} Bf5 (17... Qf5 18. g6 $1 Qxg6 19. Rg5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'winning the Queen.'}) 18. g6 $1 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: '!'}) (10... Bxh6 11. Qxh6 Ng4 12. Qd2 g5 {was tried in M.Velcheva-I.Khurtsilava, Zanka 1995. Then came} 13. h6 $1 {preventing Black from playing ...h6 and setting up future threats on g7. After} Kh8 14. Rh5 $1 {, threatening Rxg5, White still had a big attack; if} f6 {then} 15. f3 {wins the knight on g4.}) 11. Bd3 {Alexander Volzhin: '!'} Nbd7 ({Alexander Volzhin: 'It would be very good for Black to transfer his light-squared Bishop to the g6-square but White's last move made this manoeuvre impossible:' Andrew Soltis: 'Whtie is in no hurrty since' Lorin D'Costa: 'Preventing '} 11... Bf5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: '??' Andrew Soltis: ''?'' Lorin D'Costa: 'as'} 12. Qg5 {Andrew Soltis: 'loses immediately' Lorin D'Costa: 'would pin this to the queen on a5.'}) 12. Nge2 Rd8 {Lorin D'Costa: 'To vacate the f8-square for the king or knight. '} 13. g4 $1 {Alexander Volzhin: 'The decisive breakthrough. Now White's attack becomes unstoppable.'} (13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. Ng3 {to attack h5 and threaten Nf5+ looks very good for White as well. That pawn on h5 isn't running anywhere and Black hasn't even started any counterplay on the queenside against the white king.}) 13... Nf8 ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Black can't accept the sacrifice in view of' Andrew Soltis: 'the game is over soon after'} 13... Nxg4 14. Rdg1 $1 $18 { f2-f3; xg4 Alexander Volzhin: '!' Alexander Volzhin: 'with decisive threats.' Andrew Soltis: ''+-!'  f2-f3; xg4' Lorin D'Costa: 'and the open g-file is deadly for Black.'} (14. Bxg7 {Andrew Soltis} Kxg7 15. Rdg1 Kh8 16. f3 Ngf6 17. Qh6 Rg8 18. Rxg8+ Kxg8 19. Rg1+ Ng4 20. Qxh7+ Kf8 21. Qh8# {PS})) ({and immediately after Lorin D'Costa: 'Or: '} 13... hxg4 {Andrew Soltis} 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Qh6+ Kh8 16. Bxh7 $18 {PS Lorin D'Costa: 'will be mate in a few moves.'}) 14. gxh5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Now Black is helpless against threats on the g-file.'} Ne6 (14... Ng4 15. Rdg1 Bxh6 16. Qxh6 Kh8 17. Qf4 Nf6 {is maybe Black's best defence, but his long-term prospects are not good. After} 18. Kb1 $1 {, how can Black develop?} Z0 (18... Be6 {allows} 19. Qg3 {and Black cant stop Qg7 mate as ...Ne6 isn't possible anymore.} (19. Z0)) ({. Or} 18... b5 19. Ne4 {and once that defensive knight on f6 goes, so will Black's position; e.g.} Nxe4 20. Qxf7 {.})) 15. Rdg1 Kh8 ({Alexander Volzhin: 'Or'} 15... Kf8 16. Bxg7+ Nxg7 17. Qh6 Ng4 18. Qxh7 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: 'and White wins.' Andrew Soltis: 'and 19.Qh8+ or 19.f3 wins '+-''}) 16. Bxg7+ Nxg7 17. Qh6 Rg8 ({Andrew Soltis: 'also lost was'} 17... Ne6 18. Nf4 $1 $18 {Alexander Volzhin: '!' Andrew Soltis: ''+-!''}) ({If} 17... Bg4 18. Rxg4 {the rook is untouchable due to mate on h7.}) 18. Rg5 Qd8 (18... Nf5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'is also bad:' Lorin D'Costa: 'loses to '} 19. Rxg8+ {Lorin D'Costa: 'or'} Nxg8 (19... Kxg8 20. Rg1+ Kh8 21. Bxf5 {.}) 20. Qf8 { g1 Alexander Volzhin: ', Rg1 to follow.' Lorin D'Costa: 'and the black pieces are paralysed; Rg1 will force mate on g8 or g7'}) 19. Rhg1 {Lorin D'Costa: 'Bringing in a second major piece down the only open file. Black is already completely lost yet White has done nothing spectacular to achieve this position! '} Nf5 {Alexander Volzhin: '?' Alexander Volzhin: 'A blunder but Black's position is lost anyway.' Andrew Soltis: 'Equivalent to resignation'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer would have liked to finish off with'} 19... Qf8 {Lorin D'Costa: 'is met by '} 20. d5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'which points out how fragile the defense is'} {Andrew Soltis: 'After'} Bd7 {Andrew Soltis: 'White has'} ({Lorin D'Costa: 'or'} 20... cxd5 21. Nxd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and 22.Nxf6.' Lorin D'Costa: '; again, once the f6-knight is lured away Black cannot protect h7'} Nxd5 22. Qxh7#) 21. d6 $1 {Lorin D'Costa: 'and the e7-, f6- and g7-squares cannot all be defended.'} Nf5 22. Qxf8 Raxf8 (22... Rgxf8 23. Bxf5 h6 24. dxe7 Rfb8 25. Rg7 Bxf5 26. Rxf7) 23. Bxf5 Rxg5 24. Rxg5 h6 25. dxe7 Rb8 26. Rg3 $1 Bxf5 27. Rf3 $18 {Andrew Soltis: ''+-''}) 20. Bxf5 {Alexander Volzhin: 'Bobby Fischer at his best!'} 1-0

