1. e4 {Garry Kasparov ChessBase ChessBase ChessBase} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Later it was established that'} 7... Be7 {Pilnik,H-Fischer,R Mar del Plata 1959 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'is more accurate. This, in particular, is what Fischer played against Geller in Stockholm and on Curaçao  (Volume 2, Game No.98).'}) 8. O-O ({The slightly premature bishop sortie to e6 can be called into question by the energetic} 8. f4 {Garry Kasparov} Qc7 9. g4 $1 {.}) 8... Nbd7 9. f4 Qc7 10. f5 {Garry Kasparov: 'The main plan at that time.'} ({By the mid-1970s White had switched to} 10. a4 {Garry Kasparov} Be7 11. Kh1 $5 {(one of Geller's many ideas) – this is a tabiya of the 1974 Candidates match Karpov-Polugayevsky (for more details see Volume 5).}) 10... Bc4 11. a4 Be7 (11... Rc8 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '(Geller-Najdorf, Zürich Candidates 1953; Schmid-Evans, Varna Olympiad 1962) is not good on account of'} 12. a5 (12. Bxc4 {Garry Kasparov} Qxc4 13. a5 $1 {(and if} Nxe4 $2 14. Ra4 $1 {). Black should keep his rook on a8, so that after the typical ...b7-b5 and the en passant capture a5xb6 the a6-pawn is not hanging.}) 12... Be7 13. Bxc4 Qxc4 14. Ra4 $1 Qc7 15. Be3 h6 16. Rf2 $16 {Schmid,L-Evans,L Varna ol 1962}) 12. Be3 ({In the following decade} 12. Bg5 {Garry Kasparov} O-O {appeared (Karpov-Gheorghiu, Moscow 1971). In the 1990s White again sought an advantage in this direction, but without particular success; for example:} 13. Kh1 Rfc8 14. a5 h6 15. Bxf6 $5 Nxf6 16. Bxc4 Qxc4 17. Qf3 Rc7 18. Nd2 Qb4 19. Rfb1 Rac8 20. Ra4 Qc5 21. Rba1 Bd8 22. Nf1 ({after} 22. Nc4 Qxc4 $1 23. Rxc4 Rxc4 {Black has excellent compensation for the queen}) (22. g3 $5) 22... b5 23. axb6 Qxb6 {with sufficient counterplay (Oll-Kasparov, Moscow Olympiad 1994).}) 12... O-O 13. a5 ({Garry Kasparov: '"A critical alternative is'} 13. g4 d5 $1 14. exd5 (14. g5 d4 $1) (14. Nxd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 Nf6 16. d6 $2 Bxd6 17. Bxc4 Qxc4 18. Qxd6 Qxg4+ 19. Kh1 Qe4+) 14... Bb4 15. g5 Bxc3 16. gxf6 Bxb2 17. fxg7 Rfd8 18. Rb1 Bc3 $17 {Garry Kasparov: '. White's pawns are overextended and his king is exposed." (Fischer)'}) 13... b5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Escaping the bind.'} (13... h6 14. g4 Nh7 15. Bf2 $36 { h2-h4}) 14. axb6 Nxb6 {Garry Kasparov: 'A tabiya of 1962 – and the entire decade! Bobby upheld it against both Tal and Geller on Curaçao.'} 15. Bxb6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'This is what Tal played, but it is a premature exchange.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Geller acted more subtly:'} 15. Kh1 $1 Rfc8 16. Bxb6 Qxb6 17. Bxc4 ({the present generation has also made its contribution to the development of this variation:} 17. Ra4 $5 {Garry Kasparov} Bb5 $1 18. Nxb5 axb5 19. Rxa8 Rxa8 20. Qd3 Ra4 21. Nd2 {1/2-1/2 Anand-Gelfand, Moscow 1989}) 17... Rxc4 18. Qe2 Rb4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (18... Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) ({. However, the last word in this variation nevertheless remained with Black: instead of 18...Rb4?! he has the stronger} 18... Rac8 $1 {Garry Kasparov} 19. Ra2 Bd8 $1 20. Rfa1 Qb7 {with equality, as in the games Karpov-Stoika (Graz 1972) and Karpov-Byrne (Leningrad Interzonal 1973).}) 19. Ra2 $1 {Geller,E-Fischer,R Curacao ct 1962} h6 $1 (19... Qb7 {Garry Kasparov} 20. Na5 Qc7 21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. exd5 {and White gained enduring pressure on the queenside.}) 20. Rfa1 Bf8 21. Rxa6 (21. Ra4 $1 {Garry Kasparov} Rc8 $6 (21... Rxa4 {is more tenacious}) 22. Rxb4 $1 ({avoiding the unclear} 22. Rxa6 Qb7 $1 23. Ra7 Qc6) 22... Qxb4 23. Qxa6 Rxc3 {(otherwise Black is simply a pawn down)} 24. bxc3 Qxe4 25. Qd3 $1 {and White gradually converted his exchange advantage (Karpov-Bronstein, Moscow 1971).}) 21... Rxa6 22. Rxa6 Qb7 23. Na5 Qc7 24. Nb3 Qb7 $10 {Zuckerman,B} 25. Ra5 {Garry Kasparov} Qc6 $1 ({but not immediately} 25... Nxe4 $2 26. Nd5 $1) {, and} 26. Nd5 $2 {fails to} Rxb3 $1 {.}) 15... Qxb6+ 16. Kh1 Bb5 $1 { c6, a6-a5 Garry Kasparov: 'A very strong move with the idea of ...Bc6 and ...a6-a5.'} 17. Bxb5 {Garry Kasparov: 'In Fischer's opinion, "White has already dissipated his theoretical advantage.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"He should settle for'} 17. Nxb5 {} axb5 18. Qd3 { Garry Kasparov: 'with opposite colored bishops." But Unzicker is blindly copying Tal's play...'}) 17... axb5 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Ra4 $1 {a , × e4 Garry Kasparov: 'Another strong move – and now White must be extremely cautious.'} 20. c3 Qa6 21. h3 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: '"It's hard for White to hit upon a constructive plan," writes Fischer.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"At Curaçao 1962, Tal played against me'} 21. Rad1 Rc8 22. Nc1 ({or} 22. Rfe1 {Garry Kasparov} b4 $1 {– G.K.}) 22... b4 23. Nd3 $5 {Garry Kasparov: '(White's in a bad way anyhow)'} bxc3 24. bxc3 {Garry Kasparov: 'and now'} {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of my'} Ra5 {Tal,M-Fischer,R Curacao ct 1962 Garry Kasparov: 'lemon'} (24... Rxc3 $19 {Garry Kasparov: 'wins outright'} {Garry Kasparov: '. If'} 25. Nxe5 (25. f6 {Garry Kasparov} gxf6 $1 {– G.K.}) 25... dxe5 26. Qxe5 (26. Qd8+ Bf8 $1) 26... Bb4 $1 27. Qxc3 Qxf1+ $1 {Kmoch,H Garry Kasparov: '(Kmoch)."'})) ({Of course, White had to give up dreams of an advantage long ago, but also his problems should not be exaggerated. Forty years later Hübner nevertheless found a constructive plan –} 21. g3 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) , leaving the h3-square for the king and intending to meet . ..Bg5 with h2-h4, while if} Qa8 22. Qxa8 Rfxa8 {, then} 23. Kg2 {and Kf3 with a probable draw.}) 21... Rc8 22. Rfe1 h6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"A handy luft, as becomes apparent later." (Fischer)'} 23. Kh2 Bg5 24. g3 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'Now this is a fatal weakening!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'According to Fischer, 'Better is'} 24. Rad1 { Garry Kasparov: '', although after'} b4 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) Black retains the initiative.}) ({As he does after} 24. Qd3 {Garry Kasparov} Qc6) ({or} 24. Red1 {Garry Kasparov} Qb6 $1 {with the idea of} 25. Qxd6 $6 Bf4+ 26. Kh1 Qxd6 27. Rxd6 Rxe4 28. Rd5 Re2 29. Rb1 b4 {etc.}) 24... Qa7 $1 25. Kg2 ({If} 25. Rg1 {(Garry Kasparov) Black would have won by} Rxa1 26. Nxa1 b4 $1 {; for example:} 27. h4 ({or} 27. Nc2 bxc3 28. bxc3 Rxc3 29. Rg2 Qa4) 27... bxc3 28. bxc3 Bxh4 $1 {etc.}) 25... Ra2 26. Kf1 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'A final blunder –- however, in an already hopeless position:'} (26. Rxa2 Qxa2 27. Re2 (27. Qxb5 {Garry Kasparov} Qxb2+ 28. Re2 Qxc3 29. h4 Be3 30. Ra2 Kh7) 27... Rxc3 $1) ({or} 26. Qd3 {Garry Kasparov} Rxb2+ 27. Re2 Rxe2+ 28. Qxe2 Qc7 $1 {and ...Qxc3, winning.}) 26... Rxc3 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer gives'} 26... Rxc3 27. Rxa2 (27. bxc3 $2 Qf2#) 27... Rf3+ 28. Ke2 Rf2+ 29. Kd3 Qxa2 {Garry Kasparov: 'etc'} ({whereas the 'blood-thirsty' machine demands} 29... Qc7 $5 {(Garry Kasparov) with the threat of ...Qc2 mate.}) 30. Ra1 Qxb2 $19) 0-1

