{Nigel Davies: 'White's kingside gets broken up with 11...Bxh3, but he has the two bishops and he is a pawn up.' Andrew Soltis: 'By late 1963 Bisguier had already lost eight straight times to Fischer and was developing a Bobby complex. After agreeing to play in a Labor Day weekend tournament, the New York State Open, he had second thoughts when he discovered Fischer was committed to playing. Bisguier feared he wouldn't even finish second if, as usual, he lost to Fischer. But he agreed to compete when he was assured of an amount equal to the second prize regardless of his result.'} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 $5 4. Ng5 (4. d3 $14) (4. O-O Nxe4 5. Nc3 Nxc3 6. dxc3 Qe7 $1 $17) (4. d4 {Lange,M}) 4... d5 ({Fischer hadn't played 3.Bc4 since a U.S. Junior Championship game in which his opponent shocked him with the Wilked-Barre Variation} 4... Bc5 $5 {, which he had never seen. In Chess Life, Fischer recalled how he almost burst out laughing at seeing opponent overlook} 5. Nxf7 {but after} Bxf2+ 6. Kxf2 Nxe4+ 7. Ke3 $2 Qh4 {he was lucky to avoid losing}) 5. exd5 Na5 (5... Nd4 $5 {Fritz,A}) (5... b5 $5 {Ulvestad,O}) (5... Nxd5 6. d4 $1 $40 (6. Nxf7 $5)) 6. Bb5+ ({Bronstein "actually blundered away a piece" in the 1958 Olympiad with} 6. d3 h6 7. Nf3 e4 8. dxe4 Nxc4 {, Fischer wrote in Chess Life. In truth, Bronstein game was played in 1956, Bronstein planned the sack in advance, he got permission from his team to do it, and he won}) 6... c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 8. Be2 h6 9. Nh3 $5 {Steinitz,W Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: 'Steinitz,W'' Andrew Soltis: 'A move superbly suited for Fischer, whose love of the two bishops trumped the risks (...Bxh3). He blamed "vigorous Russian propaganda" for discrediting 9.Nh3 after Steinitz used it unsuccessfully against Tchigorin in the 1890s - the last time 9.Nh3 was used in grandmaster chess until this game.'} Bc5 (9... Bd6 {Steinitz,W Andrew Soltis: '. But White stood well after d3, O-O, Kh1 and Ng1'} 10. d4 {Chigorin,M} e4 {Fischer,R}) (9... Bf5 10. O-O Qd7 11. Re1 Bxh3 12. gxh3 Qxh3 13. Bf1 Qg4+ $2 14. Qxg4 Nxg4 15. h3 $18 {Steinitz,W}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'When 9.Nh3 was later adopted by Nigel Short and Gata Kamsky, opponents preferred'} 9... g5 {Andrew Soltis: 'or'} 10. d3 g4 11. Ng1 Bc5 12. Nc3 Qb6 13. Na4 $1) 10. O-O (10. d3 $1 { Nigel Davies: 'as in Steinitz - Chigorin, is probably better. ChessBase: '''} O-O 11. Nc3 Re8 12. O-O Bxh3 13. gxh3 Qd7 14. Bg4 Nxg4 15. hxg4 {Fischer,R-Radoicic,M Poughkeepsie 1963}) 10... O-O ({Nigel Davies: 'Black has an aggressive and very interesting alternative here in'} 10... g5 {Nigel Davies: '!?' '!?' Nigel Davies: 'after which White's best, according to Gottschall, is'} 11. Kh1 g4 12. Ng1 Ne4 13. Bxg4 $1 (13. b4 {Nigel Davies: 'is met by'} Nxf2+ (13... Bxf2 14. d3 Qh4 15. dxe4 Bg3 16. Nh3 (16. h3 gxh3 17. gxh3 Rg8) 16... Bxh2 $1 17. Kxh2 g3+ 18. Kg1 Bxh3) 14. Rxf2 Bxf2 15. bxa5 Qh4 $1 16. Qf1 Bg3 17. h3 Rg8 $19 { Nigel Davies: 'with a winning attack. ChessBase: '-+' ChessBase: '''}) (13. Qe1 Qd4 14. Bd1 Nxf2+ 15. Rxf2 Qxf2 16. Qxe5+ $2 Be6) 13... Nxf2+ 14. Rxf2 {Nigel Davies: 'with compensation for the exchange after, for example,'} Bxf2 $14 {Gottschall,H Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Gottschall,H''} 15. Nh3) 11. d3 Bxh3 { Nigel Davies: 'A controversial move, breaking up White's kingside but giving him the two bishops. ChessBase: '''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Bisguier, unaware of the antique theory, nevertheless improves over a Steinitz-Tchigorin game that went'} 11... Nd5 12. c4 Ne7 13. Kh1 Bxh3 14. gxh3 Nf5 15. f4 {Andrew Soltis: 'with advantage to White. Boleslavsky, among others, believed Black was better off avoiding ...Bxh3 altogether, and should use the time White spends on Kh1 and Ng1 to bring his own knight back into the game ...Nb7, ...Bb6 and ...Nc5'} exf4 16. Bxf4 Ne3 17. Bxe3 Bxe3 18. Nc3 $18 {Steinitz,W-Chigorin,M (6) Habana WCH 1892}) (11... Nh7 {Gottschall,H}) (11... Nb7 12. Nc3 {transposes into the note to Black's 11th move in Steinitz - Chigorin.}) 12. gxh3 Qd7 13. Bf3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Wholly wrongheaded is'} 13. Bg4 Nxg4 14. hxg4 f5 $40 {Nigel Davies: 'gives Black a dangerous attack, ChessBase: '''}) ({Nigel Davies: 'but White had an alternative in'} 13. Kg2) ({Fischer added that} 13. h4 {"is too materialistic even for me}) 13... Qxh3 14. Nd2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'so was'} 14. Bg2 Qh4 $1 15. Qe1 Rfe8 $1 16. Qxa5 Ng4 17. h3 Bxf2+ 18. Rxf2 (18. Kh1 Qg3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and wins'}) 18... Qxf2+ 19. Kh1 e4 $1 20. hxg4 (20. dxe4 Rxe4) (20. Qd2 exd3 21. Qxf2 Nxf2+ {and dxc2}) 20... exd3 $19 {Andrew Soltis: 'and Black wins'}) 14... Rad8 ({Nigel Davies: 'After'} 14... e4 $2 15. Nxe4 Nxe4 16. Bxe4 Bd6 {Nigel Davies: 'White defends himself with'} 17. f4) 15. Bg2 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qf5 ({Nigel Davies: 'And here' Andrew Soltis: 'Black would like to play'} 15... Qh4 $2 {Nigel Davies: 'is met by' Andrew Soltis: 'but White safely wins at least a pawn with'} 16. Nf3 Qh5 17. Qe1 {Nigel Davies: 'hitting both a5 and e5.' Andrew Soltis: '!'}) 16. Qe1 (16. Qf3) 16... Rfe8 17. Ne4 Bb6 18. Nxf6+ ({Nigel Davies: 'Another strong line was' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's instinct told him'} 18. b4 Nb7 19. b5 {Nigel Davies: 'after which' Andrew Soltis: 'was "more like it" and the idea is justified by'} cxb5 ({but on} 19... Nxe4 20. Qxe4 ({and he can try for even more with} 20. Bxe4 Qg4+ 21. Kh1 Ba5 22. Qd1) 20... Qxe4 21. Bxe4 {White's superiority is obvious}) 20. Nxf6+ {wins the knight on b7.} Qxf6 21. Bxb7) 18... Qxf6 19. Kh1 c5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Bisguier said he expected to get excellent play from 20 ...c4 but underestimated Fischer's reply. They agreed that'} 19... g5 {;  a5-...-h4  Nigel Davies: 'was better, preventing f2-f4 and preparing to transfer the knight on a5 to h4 via b7, d6 and f5. ChessBase: ';  a5-...-h4 '' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'was best, to stop f2-f4.'}) 20. Qc3 $1 {Nigel Davies: 'Preventing c5-c4 and preparing f2-f4.'} (20. Qe2 {Gegen c4 gerichtet. c3 verhindert c6-d4, e3, g1, e4 g-Linie} c4 21. dxc4 e4 $1 22. Rb1 (22. Bxe4 $4 Qe5 23. f3 f5 $19) 22... Bc7 $36 {Agur}) (20. Bd2 Nc6 21. Qe4 Nd4 22. c3 Nf5 23. Qc4 Nd6 (23... Nh4 24. Be4 g5 $5 $14) 24. Qg4 c4 $1 (24... e4 25. dxe4 Nxe4 26. Be3) 25. dxc4 e4 26. Be3 Nxc4 27. Bxb6 (27. Rab1 Bxe3 28. fxe3 Qg5 $1 $36) 27... axb6 $36) 20... Nc6 ({Nigel Davies: 'After' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's knight gets to d4 now but it's only temporary. He is a move late with'} 20... g5 $2 {Nigel Davies: 'White can play'} 21. f4 $1 {Nigel Davies: 'anyway'} gxf4 22. Bxf4) 21. f4 Nd4 22. Qc4 $1 ({Nigel Davies: 'Stronger than' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer rejected'} 22. fxe5 Qxe5 23. Bf4 {Nigel Davies: 'when Black has'} Qe2 {Andrew Soltis: 'in favor of dislodging the knight with c3'}) 22... Qg6 { h5; f5 Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: ' h5; f5''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black still has a menacing attack if he can play ...Qh5 and ...Nf5. Fischer dismissed'} 22... Qe6 {Andrew Soltis: 'with the comment that'} 23. Qa4 Qd7 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'loses material to'} ({However Black has better in} 23... Re7 24. c3 Nf5 {threatening Rxd3. White must avoid} 25. Be4 $2 ({but his edge is minimal after} 25. Qe4 exf4 26. Bxf4 Qd7 27. Be5 Nh4) 25... Ng3+ $1 26. hxg3 Qh3+ 27. Kg1 c4+) 24. Qxd7 Rxd7 25. c3 Nc2 (25... Ne2 26. Bc6) 26. Bc6 $1) 23. c3 (23. Be4 Qh5 24. f5 Qe2 25. Rg1 Nf3 $1) 23... Nf5 ({Nigel Davies: 'After'} 23... Ne2 {Nigel Davies: 'White consolidates his position with'} 24. f5 Qf6 (24... Qh5 $2 25. Bf3 $1) 25. Be3 Nf4 26. Be4 {Nigel Davies: 'which leaves him with a bind.'}) 24. fxe5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'again'} 24. Be4 Qh5 $13 {Andrew Soltis: 'is dubious'}) 24... Rxe5 25. Bf4 (25. Qf4 Bc7 $1 26. Be4 Qh5 $1) 25... Re2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black plays to win, rather than equalize with'} (25... Ne3 26. Bxe3 Rxe3 $10 {Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: '=''} 27. Be4) 26. Be4 Rxb2 $2 {Nigel Davies: 'Losing on the spot.'} ({Nigel Davies: 'Black should play' Andrew Soltis: 'A blunder that sharply curtails the game. Following the correct'} 26... Re8 $1 {;  8xe4 Nigel Davies: 'which threaten s to capture the bishop on e4 and then mate on g2. White's best reply is ChessBase: ';  8xe4'' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's threat of ...27.R8xe4 would force'} 27. Rg1 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (27. Bf3 { Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: ''' Andrew Soltis: 'then'} Rxb2 {Andrew Soltis: 'is perfectly sage and the position is in balance e.g.'} 28. Rae1 $10 { Nigel Davies: 'with approximate equality. ChessBase: '=' ChessBase: '''} Rxe1 29. Rxe1 Qf6 30. Qd5 Ne7) 27... Qh5 28. Raf1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black wins with'} Ne3 $1 29. Qb5 Rxe4 30. dxe4 Nxf1 31. Qe8+ (31. Rxf1 Qg4 $19) 31... Kh7 32. Rxg7+ Kxg7 33. Be5+ Qxe5 34. Qxe5+ f6 {Andrew Soltis: 'and the king eludes checks'} 35. Qe7+ Kg6 36. Qe8+ Kg5 $19) 27. Be5 $1 $18 {Nigel Davies: '!' '!' Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: '+-!'' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'This wins the pinned knight. Fischer games are often decides by a centralized bishop. e.g. games 2, 4, 25, 76 and Game 93. Two centralized bishops dominate here.'} Re8 28. Rxf5 Rxe5 29. Rxe5 1-0

