{Nigel Davies: 'In the early 1960s Bobby Fischer experimented with the Black side of the Ruy Lopez. He played a few games with the Classical defence 3...Bc5 and in the 1963 US championship adopted the so-called 'Norwegian Variation' with 3...a6 4 Ba4 b5 5 Bb3 Na5. Fischer was probably attracted to this line by the fact that it gains the two bishops. But meanwhile he loses time and White gains the ascendancy in the center...' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer overhauled his 1.e4 e5 repetoire in the early-to-mid 1960s and there are several theories of why. One is that he was influenced by the games of Steinitz. Another is that he was trying to avoid prepared Ruy Lopez defenses. But perhaps the most influential factor was his claim that he'd found a surefire way for Black to equalize against the Lopez - and presumably didn't want it to be played against him when he was White. Fischer never said what he had in mind but he apparently meant 4. ...b5 and 5. ..Na5. Yet he never repeated this experiment :'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 b5 5. Bb3 Na5 6. d4 ({Nigel Davies: 'The attempt to refute Black's play with'} 6. Bxf7+ $6 {Nigel Davies: 'is refuted by' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} Kxf7 7. Nxe5+ Ke7 $1) ({White's best move is probably Andrew Soltis: 'More accurate is'} 6. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'since after Black defends the e-pawn d2-d4 has more oomph. As White against Sven JOhannessen at Havana 1966 he secured a positional advantage after'} d6 7. d4 Nxb3 8. axb3 f6 9. c4 Bb7 10. Nc3) 6... exd4 7. Qxd4 { 8.f7 f7 9.d5 +-  8.f7 f7 9.d5 +- Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: ' 8.f7 f7 9.d5 +-''} Ne7 $1 {Nigel Davies: 'White was threatening 8.Bxf7+ followed by 9.Qd5+, picking up the rook on a8.' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!''} 8. c3 $2 {Nigel Davies: 'After this Black gets ...d7-d5 in when his two bishops become quite dangerous.'} (8. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'are more promising. For example'} Nxb3 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} (8... d6 {threatening the Noah's ark like ... c5-c4} 9. Qc3 $1 Nxb3 10. axb3 Bb7 11. Re1 {after which Black may have to castle queenside.}) 9. axb3 Bb7 { d5=}) ({White should prefer simply Andrew Soltis: 'Since Black is developing slowly,'} 8. Nc3 {Andrew Soltis: 'or'} Bb7 9. O-O (9. Bg5 Nxb3 10. axb3 Nc6 11. Qe3 f6 12. Bf4 Bb4 13. O-O Bxc3 14. Qxc3 O-O 15. Rad1 d6 {was fine for Black in Emms - Yuneev, Leningrad 1990}) 9... d6 10. Qd3 Nxb3 {(Lind - Ernst, Karlskrona 1983) and now} 11. axb3 {looks fairly equal.}) 8... Nxb3 9. axb3 Bb7 10. Bf4 ({Nigel Davies: 'Simply'} 10. O-O $142 {Nigel Davies: 'would also have been an improvement on Addison's choice as' Nigel Davies: 'would also have been an improvement on Addison's choice as ChessBase: '''} d5 11. exd5 Nxd5 $2 {Nigel Davies: 'is answered by'} 12. Re1+ $16 {Euwe Euwe Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Euwe''}) ({Nigel Davies: 'Euwe has suggested' Andrew Soltis: 'More consistent was'} 10. b4 {Nigel Davies: 'so as to meet' Andrew Soltis: 'so that'} d5 {Nigel Davies: 'with'} 11. e5 $1 $16 {Euwe Euwe Nigel Davies: '!' '!' Nigel Davies: 'when Black is in a bind because of the difficulties he will have contesting the dark squares (notably c5).' Nigel Davies: 'when Black is in a bind because of the difficulties he will have contesting the dark squares (notably c5). ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Euwe'' Andrew Soltis: 'leaves Black with dark-square holes that can be exploited by Nbd2-b3-a5. ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Euwe''}) 10... d5 $1 11. e5 ({Nigel Davies: 'In this position'} 11. exd5 {Nigel Davies: 'can be answered by'} Nxd5 {Nigel Davies: 'as White is not ready to play Re1+. But now Black takes the initiative on the queenside.'} 12. Qe5+ Qe7 $15 {  ×b2b3 Andrew Soltis: 'favors Black's bishops ChessBase: '' ChessBase: '  ×b2b3''}) 11... c5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'This shot, based on 12.Qxc5?? Nf5!, greatly improves the future of the b7-bishop. It also ensures that both of White's most positionally desirable moves - b3-b4 and e5-e6 - will have to be sacrifices, and probably unsound.'} 12. Qd3 (12. Qxc5 {Nigel Davies: '??' '??' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} Nf5 $19 {Nigel Davies: 'wins the queen.' Nigel Davies: 'wins the queen. ChessBase: '-+''}) 12... Ng6 13. Bg3 {Nigel Davies: 'The bishop looks badly placed on this square because its activity is inhibited by the pawn on e5.'} ({Nigel Davies: 'But after' Andrew Soltis: 'White would love to trade this piece, for example'} 13. Bg5 Be7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'But the more accurate'} 13... Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: 'and 14. ...h6 he keeps the upper hand. For example'} 14. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} (14. b4 $5 cxb4 15. O-O {and} Nxe5 16. Re1 f6 17. Bf4 Bd6 18. Nxe5 fxe5 19. Bxe5 O-O {is a solid advantage.}) 14... h6 $15 {Kmoch}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black can't dislodge the exchange by'} 13... Qd7 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '?''} 14. O-O h6 {Andrew Soltis: 'because og'} 15. e6 $1 $40 {Kmoch Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Kmoch''}) 14. Bxe7 Qxe7 15. Qe2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} ({Nigel Davies: 'After'} 15. O-O {Nigel Davies: 'Black can just play' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} Nxe5 $1) (15. Qe3 {is only slightly in Black's favor.}) 15... O-O $17 {Kmoch} (15... Nf4 $145 {Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: 'RR''} 16. Qf1 f6 {Nigel Davies: 'White's game falls apart.'} 17. g3 Ng6 $19)) 13... Be7 14. Nbd2 Nf8 $1 ({He may have toyed with the idea of} 14... O-O 15. h4 {in order to swap bishops with 16.h5 and 17.Bh4. He is worse after} f6 16. exf6 Bxf6 17. h5 Re8+ 18. Kf1 Ne5 {but there are chances for him after a trade of minor pieces and b3-b4/Nf3}) 15. O-O Ne6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black's delay in castling means he can harass WHite's with ...g5-g4. On the other wing,'} 16. Rad1 (16. b4 cxb4 17. Nb3 bxc3 {isn't worth a pawn}) 16... g5 $1 17. h3 (17. Rfe1 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} g4 $19) 17... h5 18. Rfe1 Qb6 19. Nf1 d4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'This bars White's only good maneuver Ne3-f5, and is based on 20.cxd4 cxd4 21.Nxd4? Rd8, which costs material.'} 20. N3d2 g4 { 21... h4 22.h2 g3 23.fg c4 nebst 24... d3+ Euwe  21... h4 22.h2 g3 23.fg c4 nebst 24... d3+ Euwe Nigel Davies: 'Threatening 21... h4 22.Bh2 g3 23.fg c4 followed by 24... d3+.' Nigel Davies: 'Threatening 21... h4 22.Bh2 g3 23.fg c4 followed by 24... d3+. ChessBase: ' 21... h4 22.h2 g3 23.fg c4 nebst 24... d3+ Euwe''} 21. h4 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat was 21. ...h4 22.Bh2 g3! 23.fxg3 c4! and 24. ...dxc3+'} (21. Ra1 $4 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} gxh3 (21... h4 22. Bh2 g3 23. fxg3 c4 24. bxc4 bxc4 25. Qxc4 dxc3+ $18) 22. gxh3 h4 23. Bh2 $19) 21... Qc6 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black is careful not to allow c4 e.g.'} 21... O-O-O 22. c4 { 23.de4, 24.fd2 Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'followed by Ne4 and Nfd2 with chances to make a stand ChessBase: ' 23.de4, 24.fd2''}) 22. Qe4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Not'} 22. Ne4 {Nigel Davies: 'is met by'} c4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: '!''} 23. bxc4 Nc5 $1 {winnng a piece.}) ({But it was time for WHite to become desperate, and a worthwhile try was} 22. f3 $5 {and Qf5/Ne4. For example} O-O-O 23. Qf5 Rd7 24. c4 $1 {and Ne4.}) 22... O-O-O 23. Qxc6+ Bxc6 24. c4 ({Nigel Davies: 'After'} 24. Ra1 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} dxc3 25. bxc3 Kb7 {Mit einem gewonnenem Endspiel. Euwe Mit einem gewonnenem Endspiel. Euwe Nigel Davies: 'Black has a winning endgame according to Euwe' Nigel Davies: 'Black has a winning endgame according to Euwe ChessBase: 'Mit einem gewonnenem Endspiel. Euwe''}) 24... Kd7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'With the d-file sealed, Black's fastest winning idea is to put rooks on a8 and b8 followed by ...a4 or ...bxc4'} 25. Ra1 Ra8 26. Ne4 { 27.fd2  27.fd2 Nigel Davies: 'ChessBase: ' 27.fd2''} Bxe4 $1 {Nigel Davies: 'Before White has time to play 27.Nfd2'} ({This rules out reinforcing the knight with Nfd2 or getting counterplay from} 26... Rhb8 27. Nf6+ Bxf6 28. exf6 Rb7 29. Nd2) 27. Rxe4 Ng7 $1 { f5  f5 Nigel Davies: 'The winning move. Black is going to play ... Nf5, win