{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N: 46 Romp. Chess Life, January 1964, reported: "In the later stages of the tournament some of Fischer's opponents did almost as much to guarantee his 11-0 score as Bobby did. The building tension worked to Fischer's advantage ... On Monday, December 30, Fischer won his last game of 1963 - defeating Pal Benko with a neat little combination, after Benko had shown some suicidal tendencies in the management of his defense." And so, in twenty-one moves, another Grandmaster is demolished. Benko misses a chance to simplify (on move 15) in order to reach an inferior but possibly tenable ending. This is fortunate for the reader, who otherwise would be cheated of White's scintillating 19th move. That alone is worth the price of admission! ''GAME N: 46 Romp. Chess Life, January 1964, reported: "In the later stages of the tournament some of Fischer's opponents did almost as much to guarantee his 11-0 score as Bobby did. The building tension worked to Fischer's advantage ... On Monday, December 30, Fischer won his last game of 1963 - defeating Pal Benko with a neat little combination, after Benko had shown some suicidal tendencies in the management of his defense." And so, in twenty-one moves, another Grandmaster is demolished. Benko misses a chance to simplify (on move 15) in order to reach an inferior but possibly tenable ending. This is fortunate for the reader, who otherwise would be cheated of White's scintillating 19th move. That alone is worth the price of admission!  ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N: 46 Romp. Chess Life, January 1964, reported: "In the later stages of the tournament some of Fischer's opponents did almost as much to guarantee his 11-0 score as Bobby did. The building tension worked to Fischer's advantage ... On Monday, December 30, Fischer won his last game of 1963 - defeating Pal Benko with a neat little combination, after Benko had shown some suicidal tendencies in the management of his defense." And so, in twenty-one moves, another Grandmaster is demolished. Benko misses a chance to simplify (on move 15) in order to reach an inferior but possibly tenable ending. This is fortunate for the reader, who otherwise would be cheated of White's scintillating 19th move. That alone is worth the price of admission! '''} {Andrew Soltis: 'The following was the most dramatic game Fischer won from Benko in a series that hadn't been as one-sided as might be supposed. Benko had crushed Fischer positionally in their first game, at Portoroz and won in 27 moves at Buenos Aires 1960. Their four previous U.S. Championship games resulted in one Benko loss and three draws. Fischer's reputation for Benko-bashing was based on the four wins, three draws and one loss in their two Candidates Tournaments (although that included one Fischer victory that easily could have been a loss).  In Chess Life, Fischer explained that he passed up a promising queen sacrifice 13.Qxe5 Ng4! 14.Qxg7+!. Among the reasons was that he had already assured himself of the brilliancy prize when he won Game 44 seven rounds previous.'} 1. e4 {Tactical Analysis 2.10 (10s) Szabo,Krisztian Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer''} g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 (3. c4 d6 4. Nc3 {Bobby Fischer: 'transposes to a King's Indian.''transposes to a King's Indian. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'transposes to a King's Indian.'''}) ({Bobby Fischer: 'An unorthodox try''An unorthodox try ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'An unorthodox try'''} 3. h4 $5) 3... d6 4. f4 {Bobby Fischer: ', Sharpest.'', Sharpest. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: ', Sharpest.'''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Another build-up is''Another build-up is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Another build-up is'''} 4. Be3 Nf6 5. f3 {Bobby Fischer: 'etc.''etc. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'etc.'''}) 4... Nf6 5. Nf3 O-O (5... c5 $5 {Szabo,Krisztian Garry Kasparov: 'Spassky-Fischer, 17th matchgame, Reykjavik 1972.' is the other main line, seen in a game between Spassky-Fischer, Reykjavik (m/17) 1972.}) 6. Bd3 {Garry Kasparov: 'The most energetic move.' Szabo,Krisztian: 'The most dynamic move.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'An improvement over''An improvement over ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'An improvement over''' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer abandoned' Szabo,Krisztian: 'Fischer abandoned' Andrew Soltis: 'A rare move at the time, this soon replaced the more popular'} 6. Be2 {Bobby Fischer: 'which I played against Korchnoi - Curacao, 1962. That game continued:''which I played against Korchnoi - Curacao, 1962. That game continued: ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'which I played against Korchnoi - Curacao, 1962. That game continued:''' Garry Kasparov: 'after his defeat in a well-known game with Korchnoi  (Curaçao Candidates 1962), which we will analyse in Volume 5.' Szabo,Krisztian: 'after his defeat in a well-known game with Korchnoi,'} c5 7. dxc5 Qa5 8. O-O Qxc5+ 9. Kh1 Nc6 10. Nd2 a5 $1 {Szabo,Krisztian: ''!''} 11. Nb3 (11. a4 {Szabo,Krisztian} Nb4 12. Nb3) 11... Qb6 12. a4 Nb4 13. g4 Bxg4 $1 $17 {Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V Curacao ct 1962 Bobby Fischer: '!' '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with big advantage.''with big advantage. ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Fischer,R-Korchnoi,V Curacao ct 1962 Bobby Fischer: '!' Bobby Fischer: 'with big advantage.'''} 14. Bxg4 {Szabo,Krisztian} Nxg4 15. Qxg4 Nxc2 $19 {and Black is winning, Fischer-Korchnoi, Curacao 1962.}) ({and} 6. e5 dxe5) 6... Bg4 $2 {Bobby Fischer: '; Preparing to sac the "minor exchange."''; Preparing to sac the "minor exchange." ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '; Preparing to sac the "minor exchange."''' Szabo,Krisztian: '?!' Szabo,Krisztian: ''?'' Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'An extremely dubious reply. At that time the theory of this variation (and of many others, however) was only just being developed.' Szabo,Krisztian: 'A dubious reply. At that time the theory of this variation was only just being developed. Nowadays it is clear that the light-squared bishop is important for Black.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Interesting is Valvo's''Interest