1. c4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 e6 6. Ndb5 Bb4 7. a3 Bxc3+ 8. Nxc3 d5 9. e3 O-O 10. cxd5 exd5 11. Be2 Bf5 12. Nb5 Qb6 13. O-O a6 14. Nd4 Nxd4 15. Qxd4 Qxd4 16. exd4 Rac8 17. Bd1 Bc2 18. Be3 Bxd1 19. Rfxd1 Rc2 20. Rd2 Rfc8 21. Rxc2 Rxc2 22. Rc1 Rxc1+ 23. Bxc1 Nd7 24. Kf1 ({Perhaps the safest way of holding this ending was} 24. g4 $5 Nf8 25. Kg2 Ne6 26. Be3 {.}) 24... Nf8 25. Ke2 Ne6 {Compared to the previous examples, White's situation seems less dangerous because his Kingside has no weakness. However, we shall soon be surprised.} 26. Kd3 h5 27. Be3 Kh7 28. f3 {From a theoretical point of view, the correct arrangement of Pawns.} Kg6 29. a4 ({avoiding the sharp} 29. g4 {, in order not to have to find a narrow path to salvation:} Z0 (29... f5 30. h3 $1 Ng5 ({if} 30... fxg4 {, then} 31. hxg4 $1 ({but not} 31. fxg4 $2 Ng5 $1) 31... h4 32. Ke2 {with a draw}) 31. gxh5+ (31. Bxg5 $2 Kxg5 32. gxh5 f4 {and wins}) 31... Kxh5 32. Bxg5 Kxg5 33. Ke3 g6 $1 (33... f4+ 34. Kf2 {with a draw}) 34. b4 $1 ({a far from obvious resource;} 34. Kf2 $2 Kh4 35. Kg2 g5 36. b3 b6 37. a4 a5 38. Kh2 g4 {was bad for White}) 34... Kh4 ({or} 34... b5 35. h4+ $1 Kxh4 36. Kf4 Kh3 37. Ke5 Kg3 38. Kxd5 Kxf3 39. Kc6 $1) 35. Kf4 Kxh3 36. Ke5 Kg3 37. Kxd5 Kxf3 38. Ke6 $1 f4 39. d5 Kg4 40. d6 f3 41. d7 f2 42. d8=Q f1=Q 43. Qd4+ Qf4 44. Qg1+ Kh5 45. Qh1+ Kg5 46. Qxb7 {with a draw.}) (29... hxg4 30. fxg4 f5 31. h3 Ng5 $1 32. h4 $1 Nf7 $1 33. h5+ Kf6 34. gxf5 Kxf5 {, and here} 35. a4 $3 ({but not} 35. Kc3 $2 Ke4 $1 36. Bf2 Nd8 37. Kb4 Ne6 38. Ka5 Nxd4 39. Kb6 Nb3 40. Kxb7 a5 41. Ka6 d4 42. Be1 d3 43. Kb5 Kd5 44. a4 d2 45. Bxd2 Nxd2 46. Kxa5 Nc4+ {and ...Nxb2, winning}) 35... Kg4 (35... Nh6 36. Bg1 Ng4 37. Kc3 Nf6 38. Kb4 Ke4 39. Kc5 Nxh5 40. Kb6 {and Kxb7}) 36. Kc3 Kxh5 (36... Nh6 {is less good:} 37. Kb4 Nf5 38. Bg1 Kf3 39. Kc5 Kg2 40. Kxd5 $1 Kxg1 41. Ke5 {etc}) 37. Kb4 Kg4 38. Kc5 Kf3 39. Bg1 Nh6 ({or} 39... Ke4 40. Kb6 $1 Nd8 41. Kc7 Ne6+ 42. Kxb7 Nxd4 43. Kxa6 Ne6 44. Kb5 d4 45. Kc4) 40. a5 $1 ({after} 40. Kxd5 $2 {the black knight succeeds both in participating in the trapping of the bishop, and in stopping the d-pawn:} Ng4 41. Ke6 Kg2 42. d5 Kxg1 43. d6 Nf6 {– but it does not have sufficient agility for the battle with the a-pawn!}) 40... Nf5 41. Kb6 Kg2 42. Kxb7 Kxg1 43. Kxa6 Kf2 44. Kb6 g5 45. a6 Nd6 46. Kc6 {with a draw.})) 29... Kf5 30. Ke2 g5 31. Kf2 Nd8 32. Bd2 Kg6 33. Ke3 Ne6 34. Kd3 Kf5 35. Be3 f6 36. Ke2 Kg6 37. Kd3 f5 {During the last moves, Black has systematically improved his position. Saidy limited himself to waiting moves, relying on the lack of weaknesses. Little by little will become clear that the huge space advantage is at least as dangerous for White as the disposition of the h and g pawns on dark squares.} 38. Ke2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Although, even after'} 38. g3 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} g4 39. f4 (39. fxg4 fxg4 40. b3 Kf5 41. Bd2 Nd8 42. Be3 Nf7 43. Bd2 Nd6 44. Bf4 Ne4 45. Bb8) 39... h4 40. Bd2 (40. Bf2 h3 41. Be1 Nf8 42. Bb4 Nd7 43. b3 Nf6 44. Be1 Ne4 45. b4 Kf6 46. Kc2 {it is not apparent how Black can breach this fortress.}) 40... h3 41. Be3 Kf7 42. Bd2 Ke7 43. Be3 Kd7 44. Bd2 Kc6 45. b4 (45. Be1 b5 46. a5 b4 47. Bxb4 Kb5 48. Bd2 Ka4 49. Kc3 Kxa5) 45... Ng7 46. Bc3 Nh5 47. Be1 Nf6 48. Kc2 Ne4 49. Kb3) 38... f4 {***! As can be seen from the following comment, this is a favorable moment for Black to declare his intentions because the necessity of defending the d-pawn restricts the possibilities of the white bishop.} 39. Bf2 {***Diagrama [#]} Ng7 {[#]} ({***} 39... g4 $1 40. Kd3 (40. fxg4 hxg4 $19) (40. g3 Kf5 $19) (40. Kd2 Kg5) 40... Kg5 {h4, / \Ng7-f5, g3} 41. h4+ (41. Be1 Ng7 42. Ba5 (42. Bf2 Nf5 43. Kc3 g3 44. hxg3 fxg3 45. Be1 h4) 42... gxf3 43. gxf3 Kh4) 41... Kg6 (41... Kf5 42. Be1 Nf8 43. Ba5 Ng6 44. Bd8) 42. Be1 Ng7 43. Bd2 gxf3 (43... Nf5 44. fxg4 hxg4 45. Bxf4) 44. gxf3 Nf5 45. Be1 (45. Bxf4 Nxh4 46. Ke2 Nf5)) 40. h3 ({Hard to believe, but this is the decisive mistake; or, if we put it another way, this was the last moment when I managed to find a draw for White. Saidy should have continued his waiting tactic:} 40. Kd3 Nf5 41. Kc3 (41. Ke2 g4) 41... b6 42. Kb4) ({*** In their "Winning endgame technique", Beliavsky and Mikhalchishin recommend} 40. g4 fxg3 41. Bxg3 ({Remarkably,} 41. hxg3 $19 {would transpose to the game Levenfish-Kotov}) 41... Ne6 {42.h3 (??) "building a fortress." The fortress would break down after the rather uncomplicated 42...Nxd4+ and this might be the reason why the comment was not taken seriously so far. The authors must have had in mind something like} (41... g4 {doesn't offer more chances because after} 42. fxg4 hxg4 43. Ke3 {there is no way to make further progress.}) 42. Ke3 Kf5 {(threatening g4)} 43. h3 {when it is hard to see how Black can make any progress. White is coordinated well enough to control the f4-square, while the general exchanges generated by an eventual ...g4 would not offer winning chances because, unlike in the game Karpov-Kasparov, the queenside is not blocked and the White king can generate adequate counterplay if pushed away little by little from the d4-pawn.}) 40... Nf5 {(This is a real zugzwang!)} 41. Kd3 g4 {Once the white King stepped to the left, this move became possible.} 42. hxg4 ({You will probably be not surprised that after} 42. Ke2 g3 43. Bg1 {the total (and ridiculous, I would say) passivity of the white Bishop will lead White to catastrophe:} Kf7 44. Ke1 Ke6 45. Ke2 Kd6 46. Ke1 Kc6 47. Ke2 Kb6 48. b4 Nh4 49. Kf1 Kc6 {again zugzwang: White has to advance one of his Queen side PAwns, opening the door to the black King.}) 42... hxg4 43. fxg4 Nh6 44. Be1 $2 (44. Ke2 Nxg4 45. Bg1 (45. Be1 Kf5 46. Kf3 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Nh2+ {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'and ...Ke4'}) ({not} 45. Kf3 $2 Nxf2 $1 46. Kxf2 Kf5 47. Kf3 a5 48. b3 b6) 45... Kf5 46. Kf3 Nf6 (46... b6 47. g3 fxg3 48. Kxg3 Nf6 49. Kf3) 47. Bh2 (47. b4 Ne4 48. Bh2 Ng5+ 49. Ke2 Ke4 50. Bg1 Ne6) 47... Nh5 48. a5 $5 ({not} 48. b3 $2 a5 49. Bg1 Ng7 50. Bf2 Ne6 51. Bg1 Ng5+) 48... Kg5 49. g4 fxg3 50. Bxg3 $11 Nf6 51. Be5 Ne4 52. Ke3 Kg4 53. Kd3 Kf3 54. Kc2 Ke3 55. Kb3 Ng5 56. Kb4 {(forcing the knight to guard c5)} Ne6 57. Z0 ({, and after the incautious} 57. Bf6 $2 Kd2 $1 58. Be5 Kd3 {White ends up in zugzwang:} 59. Bf6 ({or} 59. b3 Nxd4 60. Kc5 Nxb3+) 59... Kc2 $1 60. Ka3 Nf4 {and ...Nd3}) ({, but after} 57. Kc3 $1 {his position is invulnerable.})) 44... Nxg4 {The ending has reached familiar contours: the position was opened on the Kingside. The Queen side configuration favors White: a counterplay is possible there. This is why Black has to win the g2 pawn, not just to exchange it!} 45. Bd2 ({while after} 45. a5 Kf5 46. b3 {Black wins by} Ne3 $1 47. g3 Ng2 48. Bf2 Kg4 $1 49. gxf4 Kf3 50. Bg1 Nxf4+ 51. Kd2 Ke4 {.}) 45... Kf5 46. Be1 {Garry Kasparov: 'comes to the same thing;'} (46. a5 {would not have saved White in view of} Nf2+ $1 ({but not} 46... Nh6 $2 47. g3) 47. Ke2 Ne4 48. Be1 Kg4 49. Kd3 (49. b3 Ng3+) 49... Nd6 50. b3) 46... Nf6 ({He would have been denied this chance by the immediate manoeuvre of the knight to f5 –} 46... Nh6 $1 {; for example:} 47. b3 ({or} 47. a5 Kg4 48. Bf2 Nf5 {and wins – cf. the note to White's 49th move}) 47... Kg4 48. Ke2 Nf5) 47. Bh4 Ne4 (47... Nh5 {The last critical moment.} 48. Be1 $2 ({After} 48. Bf2 {Black had the interesting resource} b6 $1 {with the possible sequel} 49. b3 Kg4 50. Be1 Ng3 51. Bf2 Ne4 52. Be1 Ng3 53. Bf2 Nf5 54. Ke2 b5 $1 55. axb5 axb5 56. b4 Ng3+ 57. Kd3 Nf1 58. Ke2 Ne3 59. g3 Nc2 60. gxf4 Kxf4 61. Kd2 Kf3 {and wins.}) ({Therefore it was essential to play} 48. a5 Kg4 49. Bf2 Ng3 50. b3 $1 {(depriving the knight of the important c4-square)} Nf5 51. Ke2 {. Having succeeded in playing a4-a5 and b2-b3, White gains a draw after all:} Nh4 52. Bxh4 Kxh4 53. Kf3 Kg5 54. g3 fxg3 55. Kxg3 {. But after the move in the game he loses material.}) 48... Kg4 49. Ke2 {0-1 (56) Saidy,A-Fischer,R New York 1964 My Great Predecessors 4 [Garry Kasparov]} (49. a5 {is too late, since White is no longer helped by the inclusion of b2-b3:} Ng3 $1 50. Bf2 (50. b3 Nf5 $1) 50... Ne4 51. Ke2 Nd6 $1 52. Z0 (52. b3 Ne4 53. b4 (53. Be1 Ng3+ 54. Kd3 Nf5) 53... Nxf2 $1 54. Kxf2 Kg5 $1 55. Ke2 Kf6 $1 56. Kf2 Kg6 $1 57. Ke2 (57. g4 fxg3+ 58. Kxg3 Kg5 $1) 57... Kg5 58. Kf2 Kg4) ({, or} 52. Be1 Nc4 $1 53. b3 Ne3 54. Kf2 Nf5 55. Bc3 Nd6 56. Ke2 Nb5 57. Bb2 Kg3 58. Kf1 f3 {and wins.}))) 48. Be1 Kg4 49. Ke2 (49. b3 Ng3 50. Bf2 Nf5 (50... Ne4 51. Ke2 Nxf2 52. Kxf2 a5) 51. Ke2 Nd6 (51... Ng3+) 52. Be1 Ne4 53. Kd3 Kg5 (53... b6 54. Ke2 (54. Kc2 Ng3 55. Kd3 Kf5) ({, or} 54. b4 b5 55. a5 f3 $1 56. gxf3+ Kxf3 57. Bh4 Nf2+ 58. Kd2 Nh3 59. Be7 Ke4 60. Bc5 Ng5 61. Bb6 Ne6 62. Kc3 Ke3 63. Bc5 Nf4 64. Kc2 Ne2 65. Ba7 Ke4 {is no better}) 54... Ng3+ 55. Kd3 Nf5 56. b4 Nh4 57. b5 axb5 58. axb5 Nxg2 59. Ba5 f3 $1 {and the f-pawn cannot be stopped.}) 54. Ke2 (54. Kc2 Kf5 55. Kd1 Kg4 56. Ke2) 54... Kf5 55. Kd3 (55. Bh4 Kg4 56. Be1 Ng3+) 55... Kg4 56. Ke2 Ng3+ 57. Kd3 (57. Bxg3 Kxg3 58. Kf1 f3) 57... Nf5 58. Bf2 Nh4 59. Bxh4 Kxh4 60. Ke2 Kg3 61. Kf1 f3) 49... Ng3+ 50. Kd3 ({If} 50. Kf2 {there would have followed} Nf5 51. Bc3 Ne3 $1 {; for example:} 52. a5 Nd1+ 53. Ke2 Nxc3+ 54. bxc3 Kg3 55. Kf1 f3 $1 {.}) 50... Nf5 {(This is the difference! Because the Bishop doesn't defend Pd4, the King cannot return to the help of Pg2)} 51. Bf2 Nh4 52. a5 (52. Bxh4 Kxh4 53. Ke2 Kg3 54. Kf1 f3 $19 {is easily winning}) 52... Nxg2 53. Kc3 Kf3 54. Bg1 Ke2 55. Bh2 f3 56. Bg3 Ne3 {and facing the threat Nf5 White decided to call it a day. There is a paradox in this article. We have analyzed 3 drawish endings, won by the side with a Knight every time. Were these games instructive at all? I believe so. All the games were played very high, meaning mistakes were not casual. I am sure that the reader will find more than 1-2 mistakes in my annotations as well. Those acquainted with Botvinnik's writing, a monument of clarity and concision, will have the sensation that the whole exposure could have occupied only one-third of the space I used. The patriarch might have said with a brief smile: "Not bad, kid, for your age". My main hope, however, is that I have passed the reader the pleasure of examining this kind of position, which is much more complex than they seem. As a last and decisive argument, I have also included the game played by Botvinnik himself.} 0-1

