{Bulletin: 'Inf.2/114' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer popularized, if not invented, several of the positional ideas taken for granted in openings today. This goes beyond mere theoretical novelties in one variation of another. For example, in the following game Black pushes his c-pawn, the bedrock of the Benoni to c4. This had been considered highly dubious in similar positions - until this game.'} {Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer popularized, if not invented, several of the positional ideas taken for granted in openings today. This goes beyond mere theoretical novelties in one variation of another. For example, in the following game Black pushes his c-pawn, the bedrock of the Benoni to c4. This had been considered highly dubious in similar positions - until this game.''} 1. d4 {Bulletin Andrew Soltis Bulletin} Nf6 2. c4 c5 {Andrew Soltis: 'A new weapon for Fischer' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'A new weapon for Fischer''} 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 g6 6. e4 d6 7. Be2 Bg7 8. f4 O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 (9... Nbd7 {Andrew Soltis} 10. O-O Re8 11. Nd2) 10. Nd2 c4 $1 ({previously Bulletin: 'previously'} 10... Ng4 {Andrew Soltis} 11. Bxg4 Qh4+ {had been analyzed and found wanting, and Bulletin: 'had been analyzed and found wanting, and'}) (10... a6 {Andrew Soltis} 11. a4 Ng4 {was only adequate. Bulletin: 'was only adequate.'}) ({Quieter alternatives such as Bulletin: 'Quieter alternatives such as'} 10... b6 {(Andrew Soltis) and 11. ...BA6, or ...Na6-c7 followed by ...b6/...Ba6 only came into fashion in the 1980s. But Fischer's move declares Black's intention to batter e4 with ...Nbd7-c5. This theme later appeared in different move orders, such as 8.Nf3 O-O 9.O-O Re8 10.Nd2 Nbd7 and now 11.f4 is answered by 11. ...c4 12.Bf4? b5 or 12.Bxc4 Nc5. Bulletin: 'and 11. ...BA6, or ...Na6-c7 followed by ...b6/...Ba6 only came into fashion in the 1980s. But Fischer's move declares Black's intention to batter e4 with ...Nbd7-c5. This theme later appeared in different move orders, such as 8.Nf3 O-O 9.O-O Re8 10.Nd2 Nbd7 and now 11.f4 is answered by 11. ...c4 12.Bf4? b5 or 12.Bxc4 Nc5.'}) 11. Bf3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black has the edge now as he would after' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Black has the edge now as he would after''} 11. Bxc4 Nxe4 $17 12. Ncxe4 {Andrew Soltis} (12. Ndxe4 {Andrew Soltis} Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 Rxe4+ 14. Be2 Bg4 $19) 12... f5) ({White's problem is that he has no time to exploit the d4 hole with Nf3-d4 because Black's Nbd7-c58e4 is faster, and Bulletin: 'White's problem is that he has no time to exploit the d4 hole with Nf3-d4 because Black's Nbd7-c58e4 is faster, and'} 11. O-O {Andrew Soltis} b5 ({Today book is Bulletin: 'Today book is'} 11... Nbd7 12. Kh1 (12. Bxc4 {Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis'} Nc5 13. e5 (13. Re1 Ng4 14. Nf3 (14. e5 Nd3 $1 $19 15. Nf1 Nxe1 16. Qxe1 dxe5 17. f5 Bxf5 18. h3 Nf6 19. g4 Bd7 20. Ng3 Rc8 21. Bb3 h6 22. Qf2 e4 23. g5 hxg5 24. Bxg5 Ng4 25. Bxd8 Nxf2 26. Kxf2 Bxc3 27. bxc3 e3+ 28. Kg2 Rcxd8 29. Re1 Kg7 30. Nf1 e2 31. Ng3 Re3 32. c4 Rde8 33. Kf2 Bxh3 34. Ba4 R8e5 35. Rxe2 Rxe2+ 36. Nxe2 Kf6 37. d6 Re4 38. Nc3 Rxc4 39. Nd5+ Ke5 40. Bb3 Rd4 41. Kg3 Rxd5 42. Bxd5 Kxd5 43. Kxh3 Kxd6 {0-1 Pihlajasalo,A-Zaitsev,I/Jyvaskyla 1994/EXT/[jhebert] (43)}) 14... Qb6 $19) (13. Qf3 Ng4 14. Ne2 f5 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Nb3 Qb6 17. Kh1 Ne4 $17 {Foisor}) 13... Ng4 $5 {[%csl Re3,Re5][%cal Rg4e3] Not mentionned in ECO. Wird nicht in der Enzyklopädie erwähnt.} (13... dxe5 14. fxe5 Rxe5 15. Nf3 Re8 16. Kh1 Nfe4 $1 17. Nxe4 Rxe4 18. Bg5 (18. Qc2 Bf5 19. Bg5 Qb6 $36) (18. Be2 Bf5 19. Bg5 Qb6 20. Nd2 Rd4 $17) 18... f6 19. Rc1 fxg5 20. d6+ Kh8 21. Bd5 Rf4 22. Rxc5 g4 23. Nd2 Qxd6 $17 {Foisor}) 14. Nde4 (14. Nb3 Qb6 15. Qd4 Rxe5 $3 16. Na4 Nxa4 17. fxe5 Bxe5 18. Qxb6 Nxb6 19. Be2 Bxh2+ 20. Kh1 Be5 $17) 14... Nxe4 15. Nxe4 dxe5 16. d6 exf4 $17 17. Bxf7+ Kxf7 18. Qd5+ Kf8 19. Rxf4+ Bf5 20. Bd2 Qb6+ 21. Kh1 Qd4 {0-1 Nikitin,A-Nevostrujev,V/Kemerovo 1991/TD/[jhebert] (21)}) 12... Nc5 13. e5 dxe5 14. Nxc4 (14. fxe5 $1 Rxe5 15. Nxc4 Rf5 {can quickly turn in his favor. Bulletin: 'can quickly turn in his favor.'}) 14... exf4 {offers White little but Bulletin: 'offers White little but'}) 12. Nxb5 $4 {loses to Bulletin: 'loses to'} Qb6+) 11... Nbd7 12. O-O b5 $17 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Again 13.Nxb5? falls into 13. ...Qb6+ ''' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: '!'' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Again 13.Nxb5? falls into 13. ...Qb6+''} 13. Kh1 a6 ({Don't change a favorable pawn structure without a compelling reason. This is a recurring theme in Fischer's games and here it helps explain why Black rejects adventures such as Bulletin: 'Don't change a favorable pawn structure without a compelling reason. This is a recurring theme in Fischer's games and here it helps explain why Black rejects adventures such as'} 13... b4 {Andrew Soltis} 14. Nb5 c3 15. bxc3 bxc3 16. Nxc3 (16. Nxd6 cxd2 17. Nxe8) 16... Nxd5) 14. a4 Rb8 ({and here Bulletin: 'and here'} 14... b4 {Andrew Soltis} 15. Na2 c3 ({also tempting is Bulletin: 'also tempting is'} 15... Nc5 16. e5 dxe5 17. fxe5 Nxd5 18. Nxc4 Be6 19. Nd6 Bxe5 $1 {and ...Qh4. But one gets the impression that after 12. ...b5, Fischer felt he would win through routine means and wanted to minimize risk from here on. Bulletin: 'and ...Qh4. But one gets the impression that after 12. ...b5, Fischer felt he would win through routine means and wanted to minimize risk from here on.'}) 16. bxc3 bxc3 17. Nxc3 Nxd5 {is promising and Black has good chances of consolidating his material edge after Bulletin: 'is promising and Black has good chances of consolidating his material edge after'} 18. Nxd5 Bxa1 19. Ba3 Bb7 20. Bxd6 Ra7) 15. axb5 axb5 16. e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'Early desperation but understandable in view of ... Nc5 and ...b4.' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Early desperation but understandable in view of ...Nc5 and ...b4.''} dxe5 17. Nde4 Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Nf6 ({Years later, when Pomar was asked which of his games gave him the greatest satisfaction, he cited his "study-like draw" with Fischer at Stockholm 1962. Fischer takes pains not to let him get away this time, and doesn't try for the quicker win with Bulletin: 'Years later, when Pomar was asked which of his games gave him the greatest satisfaction, he cited his "study-like draw" with Fischer at Stockholm 1962. Fischer takes pains not to let him get away this time, and doesn't try for the quicker win with'} 18... Nb6 {Andrew Soltis} 19. d6 f5) 19. d6 Be6 ({Both Bulletin: 'Both'} 19... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis} 20. Bxe4 exf4) ({and Bulletin: 'and'} 19... Bf5 {Andrew Soltis} 20. Ng3 e4 {were promising alternatives. Black's move is based on 20.fxe5 Nxe4 21. Bxe4 Bxe5 Bulletin: 'were promising alternatives. Black's move is based on 20.fxe5 Nxe4 21.Bxe4 Bxe5'}) 20. Nc5 e4 $1 ({White figures he has nothing better than to plunge into Bulletin: 'White figures he has nothing better than to plunge into'} 20... exf4 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Bxf4 Nd7 22. Nxe6 Rxe6 (22... fxe6 23. Ra7 Rc8) 23. Bd5 Bxb2) 21. Nxe4 Nxe4 22. Bxe4 Qb6 {Andrew Soltis: 'The d-pawn, White's only real asset, appears doomed by ...Rbd8' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'The d-pawn, White's only real asset, appears doomed by ...Rbd8''} 23. f5 {Andrew Soltis: '!?'} gxf5 24. Bc2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'First rank mates deter' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'First rank mates deter''} 24. Bxf5 Bxf5 25. Rxf5 Qxd6 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'}) (24. d7 {Andrew Soltis} Red8 25. Bxf5 Rxd7 26. Qh5 Bxf5 27. Qxf5 Qf6 $1) 24... Qd4 25. Qh5 (25. Qxd4 {Andrew Soltis} Bxd4 26. Rd1 Bd7 27. h3 Bf6 28. Ra7 Rbd8 $15) 25... Qg4 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({White is still alive and kicking after Bulletin: 'White is still alive and kicking after'} 25... Qxd6 {Andrew Soltis} 26. Bf4 Be5 27. Bxe5 Qxe5 28. Bxf5) 26. Qxg4 fxg4 27. Bg5 Bxb2 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The exchange sacrifice is the best reply to the threat of Rad1/d6-d7' Bulletin: 'Andrew Soltis: 'The exchange sacrifice is the best reply to the threat of Rad1/d6-d7''} 28. Rad1 b4 ({There are many many wins here and Black can be excused for wanting to avoid the counterplay of Bulletin: 'There are many many wins here and Black can be excused for wanting to avoid the counterplay of'} 28... Bd7 {Andrew Soltis} 29. Be7 {and Rd5 Bulletin: 'and Rd5'}) 29. d7 Red8 30. Ba4 b3 31. Rfe1 Kg7 ({Black takes his time Bulletin: 'Black takes his time'} 31... c3 {Andrew Soltis} 32. Bxd8 Rxd8 (32... c2 33. Bc7 $1 {and White wins Bulletin: 'and White wins'}) 33. Rxe6 $1) 32. Bxd8 Rxd8 33. Rd6 Bf6 34. Red1 Bg5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Black controls the queening square at c1 and threatens ...Rb8 and ...b2. The win could still slip after Bulletin: 'Black controls the queening square at c1 and threatens ...Rb8 and ...b2. The win could still slip after'} 34... c3 {Andrew Soltis} 35. Rxe6 $1 fxe6 36. Bxb3) 35. Rb6 h6 36. Rc6 ({As ugly as it looks, White should create luft with Bulletin: 'As ugly as it looks, White should create luft with'} 36. g3 {Andrew Soltis}) 36... Ra8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 37. Bb5 Bxd7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 38. h4 Bxc6 39. Bxc6 c3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 40. hxg5 c2 41. gxh6+ Kh8 0-1

