{Bulletin: 'Inf.2/296' Bulletin: 'This eagerly awaited clash between the USA and USSR, who were in a tight race for the world team championship, was delayed over a week when the Soviets refused to postpone the start of the round from 4pm to 6pm on Friday to accommodate Fischer's religious convictions -- despite assurances before going to Cuba that his request for a change in schedule would be honored. America protested by forfeiting on all four boards. Finally Moscow relented and cabled instructions to play the round on a free day. World champion Petrosian was replaced on first board by Spassky, who fought back heroically after being pinned to the ropes all game. This eagerly awaited clash between the USA and USSR, who were in a tight race for the world team championship, was delayed over a week when the Soviets refused to postpone the start of the round from 4pm to 6pm on Friday to accommodate Fischer's religious convictions -- despite assurances before going to Cuba that his request for a change in schedule would be honored. America protested by forfeiting on all four boards. Finally Moscow relented and cabled instructions to play the round on a free day. World champion Petrosian was replaced on first board by Spassky, who fought back heroically after being pinned to the ropes all game. Inf.2/296'} 1. e4 {Bulletin} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 {Bulletin: 'Ending speculation that Spassky would risk his pet Marshall Attack with 8...d5.'} (8... d5 $5 {Fischer-Spassky/Santa Monica/1966}) 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Ng3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 15. b3 $1 (15. d5 Nc4 $1 $11) 15... cxd4 $6 {Bulletin: 'Opening the c-file to get some breathing space. Spassky-Portisch, Luagno 1968, continued 15...Nc6 16. d5 Ne7 17. c4 closing the center.'} (15... Nc6 16. d5 $1 $14 (16. Be3 $2 exd4 (16... Rc8 17. d5 Ne7 18. a4 (18. Qd2 Ng6 19. a4 bxa4 20. b4 cxb4 21. cxb4 Qc7 22. Bxa4 Bxa4 23. Rxa4 Qc4 $11) 18... bxa4 $1 19. bxa4 Qa5 20. c4 Ng6 21. Qd2 Qc7 22. Reb1 Rb8 23. Nf5 Nh7 24. g4 $1) 17. cxd4 cxd4 18. Nxd4 d5 $1 $11) 16... Na5 (16... Ne7 17. c4 (17. Be3 Qc8 18. Qd2 Kh8 19. a4 Nfg8 20. axb5 $1 axb5 21. Rxa8 Qxa8 22. Bxc5 $1 $16) 17... Ng6 18. Nf5 Nf4 19. Bxf4 exf4 20. Nxd6 Bxd6 21. e5 Bf8 22. Qd3 g6 23. e6 Bg7 24. exd7 Qxd7 $11) 17. Nh2 { f4} Nh7 18. f4 Qh4 19. Qf3 exf4 20. Bxf4 Ng5 21. Qf2 $16) 16. cxd4 Nc6 17. Bb2 g6 {Bulletin: 'An alternative is 17...Rc8 18. a3 Qb6 19. b4 a5 as in Janovsevic-Geller, Belgrade 1969.'} ({Abstractly speaking,} 17... Rc8 {makes more sense at this point in the game, because it develops the last piece, with the exception of the queen.}) 18. Qd2 $18 {Bulletin: '' '' Bulletin: 'Marin,Mihail: 'Commentators of that time were so impressed by the further course of the game that their evaluation after this move varied from  to +- (!) In fact, the truth is somewhere between = and , as usual.''} Bg7 19. Rad1 Qb6 20. Nf1 $1 {Bulletin: 'Aiming to redeploy this knight on d5, a typical Ruy Lopez theme to exploit Black's cramp.'} (20. Bb1 {Matanovic,A-Liberzon,V/Yerevan 1965/MCL (1/2, 40)} Rac8) 20... Rad8 {Bulletin: 'Marin,Mihail: 'This is a first sign of failing to understand the position properly.''} (20... Rac8 $5 21. Ne3 (21. Bb1 Qb7 $5 22. Ne3 exd4 23. Nxd4 Nxe4) 21... exd4 22. Nxd4 Qc5 23. Nxc6 (23. Nf3 Ne5 $5 $11) (23. f3 Nh5 $1 $36) 23... Bxc6 24. f3 Qg5 $5) 21. Ne3 Qb8 {Bulletin: 'Marin,Mihail: 'Starting with this move, Spassky simply moves his pieces around. He probably was convinced that his talented, but still very young, opponent, would commit suicide by himself at some point. The score (2-0) from their previous encounters may have caused him to underestimate the player who was to dethrone him, only 6 years later...''} 22. Bb1 Qb7 {Bulletin: 'Marking time and regrouping, since there is no way to improve his position. If 22... exd4 23. Nd5 regains the pawn, so Black tries to maintain his strong point on e5.'} 23. Rc1 Kh7 ({With the black rook on d8 and the white rook on c1,} 23... exd4 {Spassky: '?'} 24. Nxd4 $14 Nxe4 $2 {loses to} 25. Bxe4 Rxe4 26. Nxc6 Bxc6 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Qc3+ $18) 24. a3 Bc8 {Bulletin: 'In effect, Spassky is saying "come and get me!" White gains too much space whenever Black relinquishes the center. Too dangerous is 24...exd4 25. Nxd4 Nxe4 26. Bxe4 Bxb2 27. Qxb2 Rxe4 28. Nxc6 Bxc6 29. Ng4 (or Qf6).'} 25. Bc3 Bd7 26. Qb2 Qb8 27. b4 {Bulletin: 'Spassky: '!''} Kg8 28. Rcd1 Nh7 29. Ba2 Ng5 {Bulletin: 'In cramped positions, exchanges ease pressure.'} 30. Nxg5 hxg5 31. dxe5 dxe5 (31... Nxe5 32. Nd5 $16) 32. Nd5 $16 Ne7 (32... Nd4 33. Qd2 Ne6 34. g3 {Kg2, Th1, h4}) 33. Nxe7+ {Bulletin: 'Spassky: '''} Rxe7 34. Qd2 {Bulletin: 'White clearly maintains the initiative.'} Bf6 35. Qd6 $1 Kg7 {Bulletin: 'Marin,Mihail: 'So far, this is the closest Fischer ever got to beating Spassky prior to the Reykjavik match, but now he goes astray. ''} 36. Qxa6 $4 {Bulletin: 'Allows counterplay. Much stronger is 36. Re3! Spassky: '?''} (36. Bxe5 $1 Rxe5 37. Qxb8 Rxb8 38. Rxd7 $16) (36. Re3 $16) 36... Rc8 $1 37. Rd6 $1 $11 (37. Ba1 Be6 38. Bxe6 Rxe6 39. Qa5 Bd8 $19) 37... Rxc3 38. Rxf6 Be6 {Bulletin: 'Turning the tables. If 39. Bxe6 Kxf6! Now White must sacrifice the Exchange and fight for a draw. Spassky: '!''} 39. Rxe6 fxe6 40. Rd1 Qb7 {Bulletin: 'An error on the last move of the time control. Spassky fails to find 40...Qe8! White sealed his reply and the game was adjourned. Spassky: '?''} (40... Qf8 $1 {f2, -+ Petrosian}) 41. Qxb7 Rxb7 42. Bxe6 $44 Rxa3 43. Kh2 Ra4 44. Rb1 Rc7 45. f3 Ra6 {Bulletin: 'If 45...Rc6 46. Bd7 holds.'} 46. Bb3 Ra3 47. Rb2 Ra1 48. Kg3 Kf6 (48... Rc6 49. Kg4 Rc3 50. Bd5 Raa3 51. h4 gxh4 52. Kxh4 Ra1 53. Rd2 Raa3 54. Kg4 Rd3 55. Rc2 Rac3 56. Ra2 Ra3 57. Rb2 {½-½ (57) Fischer,R-Spassky,B Havana 1966 Fischer [Spassky]}) 49. Kg4 Rc3 50. Bd5 Raa3 51. h4 gxh4 52. Kxh4 Ra1 53. Rd2 Raa3 54. Kg4 Rd3 55. Rc2 {Bulletin: 'Marin,Mihail: 'Parma,B''} (55. Re2 {Bulletin} Rac3 {½-½ (57) Fischer,R-Spassky,B Havana La 1966 EXT 99 [Bulletin] Bulletin: '½-½ (57) Fischer,R-Spassky,B Havana 1966 EXT 1999 [Bulletin]'}) 55... Rac3 56. Ra2 Ra3 57. Rc2 (57. Rb2 {Bulletin Bulletin: 'A draw, as neither side can make progress. Three years later Spassky wrested the crown from Petrosian in 1969 and then faced Fischer for the last time before their famous title bout at Reykjavik in 1972 (see Spassky-Fischer, Siegen 1970).'}) 1/2-1/2

