{Andrew Soltis: 'Most international players are forced to overhaul their opening repetoire as they age because their favorite variations have become too familiar or neutralized by new ideas. Fischer's problem with his favorite Black lines was different : By the mid-1960s he was too strong for some of them. Solid variations that he used to equalize when he was a teenager were no longer appropriate when grandmasters were playing to draw with him from move one. For example, the Fianchetto Variation of the King's Indian with 6. ...Nc6'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis Ftacnik,L} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nc3 d6 6. Nf3 Nbd7 ({Fischer helped popularize} 6... Nc6 {Andrew Soltis} 7. O-O e5 {which offers sharp middlegames after} 8. d5 ({or an ending after} 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Nxe5 dxe5 10. Bg5 Qxd1 11. Raxd1 c6) 8... Ne7 {. He had even beaten Donner in the 9.dxe5 line at Zürich 1959 - but that required many little mistakes by Whtie. In fact, Darga-Fischer, Bled 1961 was drawn soon after 11. ...c6 and not long afterwards Fischer swore off grandmaster draws and adopted 6. ...Nbd7}) 7. O-O e5 8. e4 c6 9. Rb1 a6 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'A fine move, which prepares ...b5 as in the Old Indian, as well as ...exd4, ... c5 followed by ...Ne5 and ...Be6.'} ({Donner was impressed when Portisch played 9.Rb1 against Najdorf in the first round of the Piatigorsky Tournament and got an excellent game after} 9... Re8 {Andrew Soltis} 10. h3 a5 11. Re1 exd4 12. Nxd4 Nc5 13. Bf4) 10. b4 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'This concedes c4 without much in return'} exd4 11. Nxd4 Re8 12. h3 (12. Qc2 {Andrew Soltis} Ne5 {allows Black fine play} 13. Na4 (13. Nce2 b5) 13... b5) 12... Ne5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: ' c4' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: ' c4''} 13. Qe2 b5 $1 14. cxb5 cxb5 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '!?' Ftacnik,L: '!?' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!?''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Capturing towards the center is also quite good since'} 14... axb5 15. f4 {Andrew Soltis: 'could be answered by'} Nc4 (15... Qb6 {Andrew Soltis} 16. fxe5 (16. Be3 Nc4 17. Bf2 Qa6) 16... dxe5) ({Or even} 15... Nh5 $6 {(Andrew Soltis) . But Black's idea is to get pressure on e4 and the c-file with ...Bb7 and ...Rc8}) 16. Rd1 $13 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} (16. Nxc6 $2 {Andrew Soltis} Qb6+)) 15. Rd1 {Andrew Soltis: 'An instincitve move - but this rook will be needed to defend the e-pawn while the other rook ends up looking silly on b3.'} Bb7 16. f4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'There was no ideal square for the dark squared bishop, but the text is grossly weakening. Rudolf Maric likened White's position to a soap bubble : "It expands until it explodes."'} Nc4 $17 {Andrew Soltis: 'threatening 17. ...Nxe4 18.Nxe4 f5'} 17. Qd3 (17. a4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Nxe4 18. Nxe4 Bxe4 19. Bxe4 d5 $17) 17... Rc8 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '' Ftacnik,L: '' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '''} 18. Kh2 Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Black will batter the e-pawn with ...Qb8-a8 and ...Re7/... Rce8 - and displays grat care by choosing this move over} 18... Qb6 {(Andrew Soltis) . One difference is 18. ...Qc7 stops} 19. Bb2 Nxb2 20. Rxb2 {20. ...Qxc3. Another idea if that 19.Nb3, with the idea of Nd2xc4, can be met by 19. ...Na3! 20. Bxa3 Qxc3 since 21.Qxc3 Rxc3 22.e5 fails to 22. ...Bxg2 23.exf6 Be4}) 19. Rb3 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '×e4' Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '×e4''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'a cheerless move. Donner later said he should have tried'} 19. Nd5 $142 {Donner Andrew Soltis: 'and dream of Nc6 after Ftacnik: '' Ftacnik: 'Donner''} Nxd5 20. exd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'But Black obtains a commanding position from'} Bxd4 21. Qxd4 Re2 $13 {Andrew Soltis: 'e.g. Ftacnik: '''} 22. f5 {Andrew Soltis} (22. Bb2 {Andrew Soltis} Nxb2 23. Rxb2 Rxb2 24. Qxb2 Qc2 25. Rd2 Qxb2 26. Rxb2 Rc3) 22... Ne5) 19... Re7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 20. Re1 Rce8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now 21.Re2, preparing Nd5, is answered by 21. ...Qb8, e.g. 22.Nd5 Nxd5 23.exd5 Rxe2 24. Nxe2 Qa7! with a punishing threat of ...Qf2 (25.Qf3 Bxd5 26.Qxd5 Rxe2 or 25. Nc3 Qf2 26.Ne4 Qxa2).'} 21. Nc2 Qc8 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Virtually winning a pawn. White should at least try 22.Na3 - as ugly as that looks (22. ...Nxa3 23.Rxa3 Rc7 and ... Rc4-d4 or 22. ...Qa8 23.Nxc4 bxc4 24.Qxc4 d5). Note the subtle difference in queen moves. This one is better than 21. ...Qb8 because ...'} 22. Ne3 Nxe3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (22... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 23. Ned5 $1 $36) 23. Rxe3 Nxe4 $1 $19 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '+-!' Ftacnik,L: '+-!' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '+-!''} 24. Bxe4 ({now} 24. Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis} Bxe4 25. Rxe4 Rxe4 26. Bxe4 {allows} Qxc1 {That would win despite bishops of oppostie color} 27. Rb1 (27. Bd5 Re1) 27... Qc4) 24... Bxe4 25. Qxd6 {Ftacnik,Lubomir: '?' Ftacnik,L: '?' Andrew Soltis: '?'} (25. Rxe4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Rxe4 26. Nxe4 Qxc1 27. Nxd6 Re1 28. Ne4 Rh1+ 29. Kg2 Qg1+ 30. Kf3 Bd4 $19) 25... Rd7 26. Qc5 ({white can resign} 26. Qxd7 {Andrew Soltis} Qxd7 27. Rxe4 Rxe4 28. Nxe4 Qd1 {etc.}) 26... Rc7 27. Nxe4 Rxc5 28. Nxc5 Bd4 {0-1 (28) Donner,J-Fischer,R Santa Monica 1966 MCL [Ftacnik,Lubomir]} 0-1

