1. e4 {Larsen u.a. Ftacnik,L Larsen u.a. Ivkov,Borislav} e5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'A minor surprise, coming from Larsen. The King of the Hypermoderns meets the premiere Classicist on his own turf.''} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer, having played the White side of the Lopez all his life, understood the terrain like his own backyard.''} a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Nxe4 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'I'm not sure if this was a surprise for Fischer, since Larsen had just written a pamphlet on the Open Ruy Lopez at the time this game was played. Larsen writes: "I thought that ordinary chessplayers ought to play such openings instead of following the latest fashion by playing, for instance, the slow positional manoeuvres of the Closed Variation." I completely agree with Larsen's sentiment. I involuntarily wince when some of my students frantically attempt to memorize some Komodo/Houdini-checked incomprehensible yet super-fashionable opening variation played by Carlsen and Nakamura (which only they understand!); I'm reminded of zoo chimps who imitate human behaviour (I say this out of love, at the grave risk of facing the reader's wrath: the truth will set us free!), without understanding what it means to be human. I just wonder why the Open Lopez isn't played more often today, since it isn't so easy for White to extract an edge against it, and it feels every bit as sound as the Closed lines and offshoots such as the Marshall Gambit. ''} 6. d4 b5 ({QUESTION: Does Black get slaughtered if he opens the e-file with} 6... exd4 {(Larsen u.a.) - ?   ANSWER: Not slaughtered, but perhaps led into a superior (for White) ending, after the forcing sequence} 7. Re1 d5 8. Nxd4 Bd6 9. Nxc6 Bxh2+ 10. Kh1 $1 Qh4 11. Rxe4+ $1 dxe4 12. Qd8+ Qxd8 13. Nxd8+ Kxd8 14. Kxh2 {, as in J.R.Capablanca-Ed. Lasker, New York 1915. White's two pieces are worth more than Black's rook and two pawns for the following reasons: 1. White leads in development. 2. Black's e-pawn sits on e4, which is critical since it will be exceedingly difficult for Black to roll his majority forward, given White's grip on the dark squares. The position may yet be defensible, but Black's 37% score in practice isn't very encouraging.}) 7. Bb3 d5 8. dxe5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why not take on e5 with the knight, which frees White's queen to swoop into the kingside later on?''} ({ANSWER: The problem with} 8. Nxe5 {(Larsen u.a.) is that it activates Black's queenside pawn majority and allows his c-pawn to support d5, normally a source of worry; i.e. after} Nxe5 9. dxe5 c6 10. c3 Bc5 11. Nd2 Nxd2 12. Bxd2 Bf5 {with a comfortable game for Black, and soon drawn in V.Hort-A.Karpov, Bugojno 1980.}) 8... Be6 9. c3 (9. Qe2 Be7 10. Rd1 O-O 11. c4 bxc4 12. Bxc4 Qd7 $5 {Ekström}) 9... Bc5 ({The more conservative option is to post the bishop on e7, reserving c5 for the knight, which Unzicker adopted against Fischer a couple of rounds later:} 9... Be7 {Larsen u.a.} 10. Bc2 O-O 11. Nbd2 f5 12. Nb3 Qd7 13. Nbd4 Nxd4 14. Nxd4 c5 15. Ne2 $5 Rad8 16. Nf4 Qc6 17. a4 Bc8 18. axb5 axb5 19. Qh5 g6 20. Qh6 Rf7 21. f3 Bf8 22. Qh3 Ng5 23. Qg3 Ne6 24. h4 {, and now Black looks fine after the thematic central counter} d4 {.}) 10. Nbd2 O-O 11. Bc2 Bf5 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '?!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Larsen plays the move he advocates in his book.''} (11... f5 12. Nb3 {Larsen u.a.} Bb6 13. Nfd4 Nxd4 14. Nxd4 Bxd4 15. cxd4 c5 (15... f4 16. f3 Ng3 $1 {is regarded as critical}) 16. f3 cxd4 17. Qxd4 Rc8 18. Bd3 Nc5 {and White stood a shade better, due to Black's weakness on the dark squares and d4 in particular, C.Lakdawala-E.Sevillano, Calexico (blitz playoff) 2006.}) (11... Nxf2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(the Dilworth Variation) ''} 12. Rxf2 {Larsen u.a.} f6 13. exf6 Bxf2+ 14. Kxf2 Qxf6 {, when Black gets rook, pawn and a lead in development for the two minor pieces. White has struggled to show any advantage here and, in fact, only manages a 44% score in my databases.}) (11... Nxd2) 12. Nb3 Bg4 ({Current theory regards this move as dubious (on account of 13 h3! – see the next note) and everyone plays} 12... Bg6 {(Larsen u.a.) instead. But that is now and this was then.}) 13. Nxc5 (13. Qe1 $1 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ng5 (14... Nxe5 15. Kg2 $1) 15. Qe2 $40 {Mednis}) ({The critical line runs} 13. h3 $1 {Larsen u.a.} Bh5 14. g4 $1 Bg6 15. Bxe4 dxe4 16. Nxc5 exf3 17. Bf4 {with advantage to White, A.Karpov-V.Korchnoi, Baguio City (14th matchgame) 1978.}) 13... Nxc5 14. Re1 (14. Be3 Nd7 (14... Ne6 {Larsen u.a.} 15. Qd3 g6 {game}) 15. Qd3 g6 16. Qxd5 Ndxe5 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '=' '='}) 14... Re8 (14... Bh5 $2 15. Bg5 (15. h3 {Larsen u.a.} Re8 16. Bf4 Ne6 17. Bd2 Nc5 18. Bf4 Ne6 19. Bd2 {½-½ Karpov,A-Kortschnoj,V/Baguio City-Wch 1978 (19)}) 15... Bxf3 16. Qxf3 Qxg5 17. Qxd5 $16) (14... Qd7 $2 15. Be3 Ne6 16. Bxh7+) (14... d4 {Larsen u.a.} 15. h3 Bh5 16. cxd4 Bxf3 17. Qxf3 Nxd4 18. Qc3 Qd5 19. Be3 Nxc2 20. Qxc2 Nd3 21. Red1 Rfd8 22. Qxc7 Qxe5 23. Qxe5 Nxe5 24. b3 f6 25. Bb6 Rxd1+ 26. Rxd1 Rc8 27. Rd2 h5 28. Be3 Kf7 29. f4 {½-½ Karpov,A-Kortschnoj,V/Baguio City-Wch 1978 (29)}) 15. Be3 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!'} (15. Bf4 d4 $5) 15... Ne6 ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Larsen claimed equality after ''} 15... Nd7 16. Qxd5 (16. Qd3 Nf8 (16... g6 17. Bg5 Ne7 18. Nd4 $14)) 16... Ndxe5 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '=' '=' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: ', but I don't see why. After ''} 17. Qxd8 {Larsen u.a.} Raxd8 18. Nxe5 Nxe5 19. Bf4 f6 20. a4 {, White looks slightly better in the ending, due to his bishop pair.}) 16. Qd3 g6 $5 {? Mednis ? Mednis Larsen u.a.: '?Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Sometimes a move need not be strong (or what a computer considers strong), if it contains the property of agitating the opponent's mind with false dreams.   QUESTION: This looks suicidal. Why did Larsen puncture all the dark squares around his king?   ANSWER: It does indeed appear as if the leaking dark squares will prove to be the genesis of Black's future misery. Larsen's move is made even more radical by the fact that he doesn't own a dark-squared bishop! I think he played 16...g6 for two reasons: 1. The alternative, 16...Nf8, entails a retreat, which isn't in Larsen's never-back-down chess character. 2. Larsen wasn't yet terrified of Fischer (remember, this game was played well before the 0-6 drubbing) and decided to provoke him into an attack. ''} (16... Nf8) 17. Bh6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '"I don't place faith in a post-death paradise," muses the bishop, who decides to retool and dedicate his life to selfish, temporal happiness. ''} Ne7 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More weirdness.''} ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Most players would have gone for the simplifying sequence ''} 17... Bf5 18. Qd2 Bxc2 19. Qxc2 Ne7 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '.''} 20. Nd4 $14) (17... d4 $2 18. Qe4 $1) 18. Nd4 { d2} Bf5 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The only move. ''} ({Larsen avoids the strategically sour line} 18... Nxd4 $2 {Larsen u.a.} 19. Qxd4 Nf5 ({not} 19... Bf5 $4 {as} 20. e6 $1 {forces mate}) 20. Bxf5 Bxf5 21. e6 f6 22. e7 $1 Qd6 23. Rad1 Kf7 ({or} 23... c6 24. Bf4) 24. Bf8 {, when White dominates.}) 19. Nxf5 {Ivkov,Borislav: ''} (19. Qd2 Nxd4 20. cxd4 Bxc2 21. Qxc2 Nf5 22. Be3 Re6 $14) 19... Nxf5 20. Bd2 $5 (20. Qh3 $1 Nxh6 21. Qxh6 $16 {Mednis}) 20... Qh4 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?''} ({I would have activated the queenside pawn majority with} 20... c5 $1 {(Larsen u.a.) , after which Black remains only slightly worse.}) 21. Qf1 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'QUESTION: Why back up to f1?   ANSWER: It's a strange move. Fischer wants to play g2-g3 without allowing ...Qh3. ''} ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'But not ''} 21. Qxd5 $2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'due to ''} Rad8 22. Qc6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(White's clever point: d2 doesn't hang) ''} Ne7 $19 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Oops! My mistake. Yes it does!).''}) ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'More natural is ''} 21. Qf3 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or''} Ng5 $5 (21... Nfg7 {Larsen u.a.} 22. a4 c6 23. b4 {and White has a pleasant edge, due to his two bishops and the fact that Black must stay on the lookout for an untimely a4xb5.}) 22. Qxd5 (22. Qf4 $1 Qxf4 23. Bxf4 Ne6 24. Bd2 c5 $13) (22. Qe2 {Larsen u.a.}) 22... Rad8 23. Bxg5 Qxg5 24. Qc6 Re6 25. Qxc7 Rd2 $40 {Larsen}) 21... Nc5 22. g3 ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer prefers to hang on to both bishops since ''} 22. Bxf5 $2 gxf5 {(Larsen u.a.) looks like merely abstract damage. Despite Houdini's rosy assessment after} 23. Be3 Ne6 24. f4 c6 25. a4 {, the position seems balanced to me, since I don't see any way for White to exploit the doubled pawns.}) 22... Qc4 23. Qg2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer may be overestimating his chances. We sense counterfeit enthusiasm and by this point I'm not so certain Fischer retained 100% faith in his attack.''} (23. Qxc4 dxc4 $15 { d3}) ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'He was better off heading for a slightly favourable ending after''} 23. b3 $142 Qxf1+ 24. Kxf1 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '=' '='} Ng7 {(Larsen u.a.) , when White's bishops still offer him an edge, whereas now he loses one of them.}) 23... Nd3 24. Bxd3 Qxd3 25. Bg5 (25. Rad1 c6 26. Bg5 Rxe5 $1 {Mednis Larsen u.a.: 'Mednis'}) 25... c6 26. g4 (26. Rad1 Rxe5 $1 {Mednis}) 26... Ng7 27. Re3 {Ivkov,Borislav: '?' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'This looks really ominous. Three white attackers encircle Black's king. The reality, according to the computers (and Larsen!) is that Black has adequate resources to defend. ''} (27. Rad1 $2 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!'} Rxe5 $3 {Larsen u.a.: 'Mednis'}) 27... Qd2 {Ivkov,Borislav: '!!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Very annoying. The queen pins the e3-rook and attacks b2.''} 28. b3 (28. Bf6 Qxb2 29. Rd1 (29. Rf1 d4 $1) 29... Qxa2 30. Qh3 Qc2 31. Rf1 g5) ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fischer isn't interested in a dynamically balanced ending after''} 28. f3 Qxg2+ 29. Kxg2 $10 {Larsen Larsen Larsen u.a.: '=' '='} Ne6 {Larsen u.a.} 30. Bf6 {.}) ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'However, he might have abandoned the queenside in favour of the attack with ''} 28. Qh3 $1 { 29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2  29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2 Larsen u.a.: '  29.h7+ h7 30.h3, 31.d2 Cyrus Lakdawala: '!?''} Qxb2 29. Rae1 $1 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and if ''} Qxa2 (29... d4 30. Rf3 (30. cxd4 {Larsen u.a.} Qxd4 31. Bf6) 30... Ne6 31. Bf6 g5 32. Qh6 Qd2 33. Kf1 dxc3 34. Rf5 { 35.g5+-}) 30. Qh6 $1 Ne6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '?' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'then ''} 31. Bf6 g5 ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'or''} 31... Qd2 32. g5 $18 {33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# 33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# Larsen u.a.: '33.h7 h7 34.h3 g8 35.h8# Cyrus Lakdawala: ', followed by''} Z0 {Larsen u.a.} 33. Qxh7+ $1 Kxh7 34. Rh3+ {etc}) 32. Rf3 $18 { 33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis  33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis Larsen u.a.: ' 33.f5, 34.g5 Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: ', intending Rf5, Rxg5+ and wins (as suggested by GM Mednis), although Black's defence can certainly be improved: for example, 30...Qc4 or 29...d4 both seem to end in a draw with best play.''}) 28... b4 $5 {Ivkov,Borislav: '-+!' Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Attack and counterattack mutually develop along distinctly separate evolutionary tracks. Larsen plays like a White House press secretary, declaring a public position while all the time realizing that secret measures are in the works, hidden from the public's view.   EXERCISE (critical decision): How should White meet Black's last move?  We must condense, into a single choice, the plots and schemes of the last dozen moves. Pick carefully from the following options: a) 29 Qh3, going for the king, intending Qh6, Bf6 and (after forcing ...Ne6) mate by Qxh7+, Rh3+ and Rh8. b) 29 cxb4, simply liquidating the problem. c) 29 f3, agreeing to an ending, having decided that lines 'a' and 'b' both fail. ''} (28... a5 29. Qh3 (29. f3 $6) 29... a4 30. b4 d4 31. cxd4 Qxd4 32. Rae1 Qxb4 33. Bf6 Qf4 $14 {Larsen}) (28... c5) 29. Qh3 $4 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Fiery élan fails to make up for a move's fundamental unsoundness. Sometimes we place faith in things whose nature is unstable and ultimately unreliable. If White's attack is a banquet, then Fischer's last move is the dish of steamed kale. Externally, it appears as if Black's king is barely alive, as his watchful hospital nurses continue to ply him with oxygen, morphine and lime Jello, but the injection of 28 b3 b4! has reinvigorated him completely. Fischer normally possessed a deadly accurate pattern recognition algorithm in complex positions, but not this time! When the devil very cleverly disguised as a serpent in the Garden of Eden, encouraged the not-terribly-bright Eve to defy God and eat an apple – I would be tempted by a mango or a slice of watermelon. But an apple? Big deal! – a realization should have struck her: "Hey! This could be a con job: Snakes don't talk!" Here we see a rare example of Fischer, in Eve-like fashion, falling for the devil's tricks by failing to read the fine print in the contract.''} ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'ANSWER: White's best is (the admittedly depressing) line 'c' with ''} 29. f3 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} bxc3 (29... Qxg2+ 30. Kxg2 a5 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '=' '='}) 30. Qxd2 cxd2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: ', when ''} 31. Rd1 $10 {Larsen u.a.: '=' '='} (31. Bf6 {Larsen u.a.} a5 32. Re2 Reb8 33. Rxd2 a4 34. Rc1 axb3 35. axb3 Rxb3 36. Rxc6 Rxf3 37. Rxd5 {is a probable draw.})) ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Line 'b' is no better: ''} 29. cxb4 $2 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '??''} d4 $1 $19 {Mednis Mednis Larsen u.a.: 'Mednis Cyrus Lakdawala: 'and White drops a piece due to the pin on the rook.''}) 29... bxc3 30. Qh6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'The queen, believing herself a great seductress, prances about in the attitude of Salome dancing before Black's King Herod. The h6-square is merely a rest stop on the way to greater glory. Or so she dreams. The reality of the white queen's ambitions is closer to tossing a handful of seeds into a sealed, airless glass jar and then hoping they sprout. ''} Ne6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!' Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Contrary to popular belief, Larsen didn't always lose to Fischer.''} ({Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'Here the game came to an abrupt and unscheduled conclusion, since''} 30... Ne6 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '!''} 31. Bf6 d4 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Fischer finds himself in seriously straitened financial circumstances, with a bank balance of $7.33, whereas Larsen's bankroll is in the millions – now the intended mating combination falls slightly short of brilliant)''} 32. Qxh7+ {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(Played with an air of finality, but is it really so? "I stand on the threshold of unending greatness," declares the queen, who was a little off on her prediction. This isn't a mystery novel, so I will let the reader know beforehand: The queen is not the murderer she hoped to be!)''} Kxh7 33. Rh3+ {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: '(it appears as if powerful hands grab Black's king, toss him into the cold darkness, and slam shut the dungeon door; unfortunately, this is a complete illusion) ''} Qh6 $19 {Larsen u.a.: 'Cyrus Lakdawala: 'wins for Black. Yes, sometimes Santa can be a complete bugger, bringing us only coal. The queen isn't the absentee spouse everyone believes her to be. A geometric gremlin peers forth, completely messing up Fischer's intention. Unbelievably, it seems Fischer hallucinated and missed this trick, when any hopes White may have entertained about a glorious mate go up in a puff of smoke. White finds himself a full rook down and his constantly devolving attack reminds us of one of those left to right displays in a natural history museum, of apes gradually morphing into the Homo Sapiens we are today – but in reverse!''}) 0-1

