{Andrew Soltis: 'Jacqueline Piatigorsky made it clear there wouldn't be a second Piatigorsky Cup in 1966 if Fischer refused his invitation as he had in 1963. This time Fischer said yes - but he was clearly rusty. After the first half of the double-round tournament, he was alone in next-to-last place, with a 31/2-51/2 score, and appeared headed for his worst result since 1959. Fischer's turnaround began in the 10th round.'} 1. e4 {Ftacnik,L Ftacnik,L} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O (7... d6 8. c3 {1-0 (38) Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S Santa Monica 1966 [Andrew Soltis]}) 8. c3 {Andrew Soltis: '1-0 (38) Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S Santa Monica 1966 Fischer [Ftacnik/PS]'} d6 9. h3 Nd7 10. d4 Nb6 11. Nbd2 exd4 12. cxd4 d5 13. Bc2 ({Black solves his QB problem after} 13. e5 Bf5) 13... Be6 ({But} 13... dxe4 14. Bxe4 {favors White solidly} Nd5 15. Qc2) ({As does} 13... Nb4 14. Bb1 dxe4 15. Bxe4 N4d5 16. Nb3 {because of the stellar outpost for the knight at c5.}) 14. e5 Qd7 ({After} 14... Nb4 15. Bb1 c5 {White secures a different outpost, d4, with} 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Nb3 Be7 18. Nfd4 {. Then he can operate with threat of pushing his f-pawn or capturing on e6 followed by Qg4}) 15. Nb3 (15. Nf1 $1 {N} Nb4 16. Bb1 Bf5 17. Bg5 $1 Bxg5 (17... f6 18. Bh4 $14) 18. Nxg5 Nc4 $2 (18... h6 $14 {}) 19. Ng3 $1 $16 Bxb1 (19... Bg6 20. e6 $1 fxe6 21. Bxg6 hxg6 22. Qg4 $18) 20. Qxb1 f5 (20... g6 21. Nh5 $1) 21. exf6 gxf6 22. Ne6 Rfe8 23. Qf5 Nd6 24. Qxf6 $18 Ne4 25. Rxe4 dxe4 26. Nf5 {1-0 Byrne,R-Janetschek,K/Baden 1980 (26)}) 15... Bf5 (15... Na4 16. Bg5 Nb4 17. Bxe7 Qxe7 18. Qb1 g6 19. a3 Nxc2 20. Qxc2 Rfc8 21. Rac1 $14 {Matulovic-Leban,Sarajevo 1968}) 16. Bg5 ({But here is White occupies c5 with a pawn} 16. Nc5 Bxc5 17. Bxf5 Qxf5 18. dxc5 {he has no more than equality after} Nd7 $1 19. Qxd5 Nb4 20. Qe4 Qxe4 21. Rxe4 Nc2 22. Rb1 Nxc5) 16... Rfe8 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'This is a poor attempt to improve on a 1959 Spassky-Tal game that went'} 16... Bb4 17. Re2 Rfe8 18. Rc1 Nc4 19. Na1 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'with advantage to White. Reshevsky called his move a "tactical error...from which I was unable to recover," hinting that he overlooked White's 20th move.'} Bxc2 20. Qxc2 Re6 {Spassky-Tal/Moscow/1959/} 21. b3 $1 Na3 22. Qf5 $14 {Pachmann}) ({so that if} 16... Bxg5 17. Nxg5 h6 {is met by} 18. e6 $1 fxe6 19. Nc5 {with a big edge}) ({best may be} 16... f6 17. exf6 Bxf6 {although 18.Nc5 and Rc1 assure White a positional pull}) 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. Rc1 $1 Nb4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'White's superiority would be obvious after'} 18... Bxc2 19. Qxc2 Nc4 (19... Nb4 20. Qd2 a5 21. a3 Nc6 22. Qg5 $16) 20. Nc5 Qe8 21. Ng5 $16 {Hazai}) (18... Nc4 19. Nc5 Qc8 (19... Nxb2 $2 20. Qe2 $18) 20. b3 Nb6 21. Re2 { ec2 Reshevsky}) 19. Nc5 $1 ({But with only a pair of knights left, Black can live with} 19. Bxf5 Qxf5 20. a3 Nd3 21. Qc2 Qg6 22. Nh4 Qg5 23. Qxd3 Qxh4) 19... Bxc2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'The addition of Nc5 makes itself felt by'} 19... Qc8 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik/PS: '?''} 20. Bxf5 Qxf5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and now'} 21. Qb3 Nc6 22. Nxa6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik/PS: '+-' Ftacnik/PS: 'Reshevsky''}) 20. Qd2 $1 $16 ({Andrew Soltis: 'White would retain some edge with'} 20. Rxc2 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik/PS: '?''} Qf5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik/PS: 'Reshevsky''} 21. Rce2 {since} Nxa2 $2 22. Qb3 {traps the knight. But the text ensures a huge disparity in minor pieces.}) ({Clearly better than} 20. Nxd7 Bxd1 21. Nxb6 cxb6 22. Rexd1 Nxa2) 20... Qe8 ({By protecting the rook, Black avoids} 20... Qc8 21. Qxb4 Bf5 22. Nxa6) 21. Qxb4 a5 (21... Bg6 22. Nh4 {[%cal Yf2f4]}) 22. Qc3 Bg6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Reshevsky avoided'} 22... Bf5 {Andrew Soltis: 'because of'} 23. Nh4 Bc8 24. f4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'There would be scant hope of salvation after Ftacnik/PS: '' Ftacnik/PS: 'Reshevsky''} Qd8 25. f5 Re8 26. Qg3) 23. Nh4 Na4 (23... b4 24. Qg3 Nd7 25. f4 $14) 24. Qb3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'in the tournament book Reshevsky said he took "desperate measures" apparently hoping for'} 24. Nxa4 bxa4 25. f4 (25. Qa3 Rb8 26. Rc5 Rb4 $1 27. Rxd5 Qa8) 25... Be4 $132) 24... Nxc5 25. Rxc5 c6 26. Rec1 Re6 27. f4 f5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A supremely ugly move is'} 27... Be4 $2 28. f5 Rh6 29. Qg3 {Andrew Soltis: 'and 30.f6 was lost'} Kh8 30. f6 gxf6 31. Rxc6 {Reshevsky} (31. Qf4)) 28. a4 ({White wants to inflict maximum damage on the queenside before harvest time. But} 28. Qc2 Ra6 29. g4 {was safe enough to win.}) 28... bxa4 (28... b4 29. Qc2 Ra6 30. g4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Reshevsky'}) 29. Qxa4 Rb8 30. Qa3 Qd8 31. Nxg6 (31. Rxa5 Qb6 32. Rac5 Qxb2 33. Qxb2 Rxb2 34. Rxc6 Rxc6 35. Rxc6 Rd2 $14 {Andrew Soltis: 'Reshevsky'}) 31... hxg6 32. Rxc6 (32. Rxa5 Rxb2) 32... Rxc6 33. Rxc6 Qh4 34. Rxg6 Kh7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'By swapping off the e6 rook, White can win with mate/promotion threats. For example'} 34... Qxf4 {Andrew Soltis: 'loses to'} 35. Qe7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qc1+ 36. Kh2 Qf4+ {Andrew Soltis: 'he wins with'} 37. Rg3 Qh6 38. Qd7 (38. e6 Rf8 39. Qd7 {and e6-e7}) 38... Rxb2 39. Qxd5+ $18) 35. Rg5 Rb4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'again'} 35... Qxf4 {Andrew Soltis: 'falls to'} 36. Qe7 Qe3+ (36... Qc1+ 37. Kh2) 37. Kh2 Qf4+ 38. Rg3 $18 Qh6 {fails to} 39. e6 Rxb2 40. Qf7 {since there is no perpetual after} Rxg2+ 41. Kxg2 Qd2+ 42. Kf3) 36. Qf3 Kh6 37. g3 $1 Qxh3 38. Qxd5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Resigns in view of'} 38. Qxd5 Rxb2 (38... Rb6 39. Qg8) 39. Qe6+ Kh7 40. Qg6+ Kg8 41. Qxg7# {Andrew Soltis: 'Reshevsky'}) 1-0

