{Andrew Soltis: 'In an interview in New In Chess in 1990, Lajos Portisch recalled how he and Fischer once compared notes about their methods of study : "Lajos, is it true that you work eight hours a day?" the American asked. "And I said, 'Why are you asking? People also think you work eight hours a day.' Then Bobby said, "Oh, yes, but they think I am crazy." Ever since they first met at Bled 1961 Fischer had been giving the Hungarian lessons in the opening and ending. But at Santa Monica, it was a difference in their understanding about piece values that stood out.'} 1. d4 {Garry Kasparov Andrew Soltis Wells Ftacnik,L Ftacnik,L} Nf6 2. c4 e6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!'' Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '"To throw White off balance. I felt Portisch was just too well-versed in the King's Indian." (Fischer)'} 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 (4. Bg5 {Spassky,B Andrew Soltis: '(Ftacnik) Spassky,B'} h6 5. Bh4 c5 6. d5 d6 7. e3 Bxc3+ $1 8. bxc3 e5 $10 {Fischer}) 4... b6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!''} (4... O-O {(Garry Kasparov) – Game No.14.}) 5. Nge2 (5. Bd3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '– Games Nos.17 and 39.'} Bb7 6. Nf3 O-O (6... Ne4 $1 7. O-O f5 (7... Nxc3 $1 8. bxc3 Bxc3 9. Rb1 Nc6 $1 $17) 8. Bxe4 fxe4 9. Nd2 Bxc3 10. bxc3 O-O 11. Qg4 Rf5 $1 $10 {Gligoric,S-Larsen,B/Habana/1967/} 12. Nxe4 $2 h5) 7. O-O Bxc3 (7... d5) 8. bxc3 Be4 9. Qc2 Bxf3 $1 (9... Bxd3 {Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R/USA-ch/1965}) 10. gxf3 c5 $13) 5... Ba6 {Bronstein Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: 'Bronstein'' Garry Kasparov: 'Bronstein's move,'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'which, like'} 5... Ne4 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '(Korchnoi-Botvinnik, Moscow 1960),'} 6. Qc2 Bb7 7. f3 (7. a3 $142) 7... Bxc3+ {Taimanov,M-Levin,N/URS-ch/1967} (7... Nxc3 $1 {} 8. Nxc3 (8. bxc3 Bd6 $1 9. e4 Nc6 $15 {× c3, c4}) 8... Qh4+ 9. Qf2 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Qxf2+ 11. Kxf2 Ba6 $1 $10 { b8-c6-a5})) ({enables Black to avoid the variation} 5... Bb7 {Garry Kasparov} 6. a3 Be7 7. d5 $1 {; for example:} O-O 8. Ng3 ({Botvinnik's move; Rubinstein played} 8. g3) ({, and Euwe –} 8. e4 {immediately}) 8... Re8 9. Be2 Bf8 10. e4 d6 11. O-O Nbd7 12. Be3 c6 13. Qd2 {with advantage to White (Kasparov-Kramnik, 6th matchgame, Moscow rapidplay 2001).}) 6. Ng3 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Since the main point of RUbinstein's 4.e3 was to break the pin with 5. Ne2 and 6.a3, Fischer made a valid point when he criticized this as inconsistent. But' Garry Kasparov: 'In his matches with Bronstein and Smyslov, Botvinnik played'} 6. a3 {Garry Kasparov: '('à la Rubinstein'), after which Black can continue'} {Garry Kasparov: ', or'} Bxc3+ ({Garry Kasparov: ', either'} 6... Be7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 7. Nf4 d5 8. cxd5 Bxf1 9. Kxf1 {Garry Kasparov: ', and now'} {Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} exd5 (9... Nxd5 $5 {(Garry Kasparov) (Timman-Hübner, Montreal 1979)}) 10. g4 $1 $14 {Botvinnik,M-Smyslov,V/Wch-Moscow/1954 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '– Volume 2, Game No.70'}) (6... Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) 7. Nxc3 d5 {Andrew Soltis: 'was known to be inoffensive after'} 8. b3 O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'with the idea of ...Nc6 and ...d5xc4 – despite the fact that after the battles between Botvinnik and Smyslov (1957) it was considered that White has a minimal advantage here, Fischer successfully upheld this position as Black against Addison and Evans  (New York 1965/66).'} 9. a4 Nc6 {Andrew Soltis: '. When Evans tried to create a bind against him inthe 1965-66 U.S. Championship with'} 10. Bb2 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} (10. Be2 dxc4 11. Ba3 $1 Re8 12. b4 {Andrew Soltis: ', Fischer broke out in memorable fashion. First he played'} Ne7 13. O-O (13. b5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Bb7 14. O-O $14) 13... Ned5 14. Rc1 c6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!''} 15. Bf3 b5 16. a5 Qc7 17. Qc2 Rad8 18. Rfd1 Bb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'and then confounded his opponent with'} 19. Rd2 Nxc3 20. Qxc3 c5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: 'The threats of ...Bxf3 and ... cxd4 gave him a decisive edge. Ftacnik: '!''} 21. dxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Bxf3 22. gxf3 Rxd2 23. Qxd2 Rd8 24. Qe1 Rd3 25. Bb2 Nd5 $17 {Evans,L-Fischer,R/USA-ch/1965}) 10... dxc4 11. bxc4 Na5 12. Nb5 c6 13. Na3 Qe7 $1 14. Qc2 c5 15. Be2 cxd4 16. exd4 Rfc8 17. O-O Rc6 $1 18. Bf3 Nd5 $17 {Addison,W-Fischer,R/USA-ch/1965}) 6... Bxc3+ $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'A principled decision in the spirit of Nimzowitsch.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 6... O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'but no mention of this.' Garry Kasparov: ', then'} 7. e4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '.'} Nc6 {Garry Kasparov: 'is also insufficient:'} ({Garry Kasparov: '. The game Portisch-Reshevsky (13th round) continued'} 7... c5 8. d5 d6 9. Be2 exd5 10. exd5 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Nbd7 12. O-O Re8 13. Qa4 $36 {Portisch,L-Reshevsky,S/Santa Monica Piatigorsky /1966/ Garry Kasparov: 'with some advantage to White.'}) (7... Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) 8. Bd3 $1 d5 (8... Nxd4 $2 9. Qa4 $18) ({or} 8... e5 {Garry Kasparov} 9. d5 Bxc3+ 10. bxc3 Na5 11. Qe2 c6 12. Nf5 $1 {with an attack, Spassky-Hübner, Munich 1979}) 9. cxd5 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 exd5 11. e5 Ne4 12. a3 $16 {Portisch,L-Spassky,B/ Moscow/1967 Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Portisch-Spassky, Moscow 1967).'}) (6... d5 $4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 7. Qa4+) ({The 1965 edition of Modern Chess Openings gave three alternatives that favored White} 6... h5 {Andrew Soltis}) (6... Nc6 {Andrew Soltis}) ({and} 6... c5 {(Andrew Soltis) . And one that allegedly equalized}) 7. bxc3 d5 8. Qf3 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Later Portisch set his opponent far more difficult problems with'} 8. Ba3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '– Volume 3, Game No.36.'} dxc4 $1 9. Qf3 Qd5 10. e4 Qc6 $17) (8. cxd5 $142 {F Andrew Soltis: '(Ftacnik) F'} Bxf1 9. Kxf1 Qxd5 10. Qd3 {Gligoric-Portisch/Malaga/1961/}) 8... O-O (8... Qd7 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 9. cxd5 exd5 10. Bxa6 $16 {Bronstein-Portisch/Budapest/1961}) 9. e4 $5 {? Portisch Wells: '?!' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!?' Ftacnik: '? Portisch'' Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'White overestimates his attacking chances.' Wells: 'An extremely commital decision. It does not require great explanation to make the point that with the exchange on e4, white's c-pawn weakness becomes 'permanant' in a way that it was not before. White hopes that his piece play will compensate, and that it will be hard for black to organise further pressure on c4 to justify the placing of the bishop on a6.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Nimzo-Indian players have had to master positions like' Garry Kasparov: 'The correct continuation was'} 9. cxd5 $142 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '''} exd5 (9... Bxf1 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 10. dxe6 $1) 10. Bxa6 Nxa6 11. Qe2 Qc8 12. O-O c5 {Andrew Soltis: 'ever since Botwinnik won the game of his life from Capablanca at AVRO 1938. Fischer felt this version was equal and later experience with'} 13. dxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} (13. Bb2 {Andrew Soltis}) ({and} 13. f3 {(Andrew Soltis) supported him}) (13. a4 $5 {Garry Kasparov} cxd4 14. cxd4 Qc4 15. Ba3 {with a slight positional plus.}) 13... Nxc5 14. c4 $10 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'with equality – Fischer'}) 9... dxe4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!'' Garry Kasparov: 'An unpleasant surprise: now the weakness of the c4-pawn becomes a constant headache for White.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Portisch was expecting'} 9... dxc4 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 10. Bg5 h6 {Garry Kasparov: '(Saidy-Fischer, New York 1965/66) and had evidently prepared'} 11. Bd2 $2 (11. h4 $142 $1 $40 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Bb7 $1 12. Bxf6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(pointed out by Fischer)'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Spassky's pretty idea'} 12. Nh5 $5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Nbd7 $1 (12... hxg5 $2 13. hxg5 {Garry Kasparov: 'is incorrect in view of'} {Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} Nxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (13... Nh7 $1 {Garry Kasparov} 14. Nf6+ gxf6 15. Rxh7 fxg5 16. Qh5 Qf6) 14. Nf6+ $3 $18 {Spassky,B Garry Kasparov: '!!'})) 12... Qxf6 13. Qxf6 gxf6 14. Bxc4 {Garry Kasparov: 'with an obvious advantage.'}) 11... Nbd7 12. e5 Nd5 13. Nf5 (13. Nh5 Qh4 $1) 13... exf5 14. Qxd5 Re8 $1 15. Bxc4 (15. O-O-O c5) 15... Nxe5 $1 16. Qxd8 Nxc4+ 17. Qxe8+ Rxe8+ 18. Kd1 Nxd2 19. Kxd2 Re2+ $19 {Saidy,A-Fischer,R/USA-ch/1965}) 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Qxe4 Qd7 $1 { b8-c6-a5: × c4 Andrew Soltis: '!!' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!' Ftacnik: ' b8-c6-a5: × c4'' Garry Kasparov: 'A fresh surprise.' Wells: 'Excellent judgement indeed. It is true that the alternative was none too satisfactory. Still the appreciation that the minor pieces will combine so well with the Black queen, while White's minor pieces will miss the queen very seriously, and moreover that the rook pair will be unable either to defend the c-pawns or to drum up serious counterplay, represents top quality understanding, even more so in its own time.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'A stunningly nonchalant move - and, Portisch confessed, "a very unpleasant surprise". He had only considered' Garry Kasparov: 'Instead of the routine'} 11... Nd7 12. Bd3 Nf6 13. Qh4 $16 {, Andrew Soltis: '. But Fischer's move offers a temporary rook sacrifice (12.Qxa8 Nc6) knowing that the queen will be trapped and have to give herself up for the other rook. Primers teach beginners that a queen and a pawn is equal to two rooks. Despite this, many masters prefer having the rooks. Yet Fischer readily went into endings with the queen and an extra pawn - and won, e.g. versus Bisguier, Stockholm Interzonal 1962, Donald Byrne, Western Open 1963, and versus Bilek, Havana 1965. Ftacnik: '' Ftacnik: ','' Wells: 'would have afforded white just the kind of king-side counterplay which he was seeking.'} c5 {Garry Kasparov} 14. Bg5 h6 {with approximate equality, Black intends the far more unpleasant manoeuvre ... Nc6-a5, and does not begrudge giving up two rooks for the queen: White's queenside pawns are very weak!}) 12. Ba3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'A sign of confusion: the inclusion of the moves Ba3 and ...Re8 is in Black's favour, since in a number of variations he gains an important tempo (the bishop comes under attack after ...Qa4).'} (12. Qxa8 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Nc6 13. Qxf8+ {Garry Kasparov} Kxf8 14. Ba3+ Kg8 {and ...Na5! was also unfavourable for White.}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'White is positionally worse after'} 12. Bd3 f5 13. Qe2 Nc6 14. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} (14. Bf4 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) ; for example:} Ne7 ({or} 14... Rfe8 15. O-O Na5 16. Be5 Qc6 17. c5 Bxd3 18. Qxd3 bxc5 19. Rab1 {and "Black's extra pawn is not important" – Hübner}) 15. O-O Ng6 16. Be5 c5 17. a4 Rfd8 18. a5 {with equality.}) 14... Rfe8 $1 15. f4 (15. Bf4) 15... Na5 $17 {Portisch}) 12... Re8 13. Bd3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer recommended' Garry Kasparov: '"'} 13. O-O-O $142 {Wells: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'as more "consistent" Ftacnik: ''' Garry Kasparov: 'seems more consistent, making a real fight of it," writes Fischer. "Such double-edged lines, however, are not to Portisch's taste." For example,'} Nc6 {Garry Kasparov} (13... Qa4 {(Garry Kasparov) is parried by} 14. Qxa8 Qxa3+ 15. Kb1 Qxc3 16. Qf3 $1) 14. Bd3 f5 15. Qe2 Z0 ({, and now} 15... e5 {(Hübner) is unclear in view of} 16. dxe5 Rxe5 ({or} 16... Nxe5 17. Be4 $1) 17. Qc2 Qe6 18. f4 Qh6 19. g3) ({, but after} 15... Na5 $5 16. Bb4 Nb7 {Black's chances are clearly better.})) ({Hübner's recommendation} 13. c5 {(Garry Kasparov) is also dubious (it weakens the light squares) in view of} Bxf1 14. Qxa8 {(otherwise .. .Nc6-a5)} Nc6 15. Qxe8+ Qxe8 16. Rxf1 (16. Kxf1 $6 Qc8 $1) 16... Qd8 $1 {followed by the moves ...Qh4 or ...Qd5.}) 13... f5 14. Qxa8 $2 {Wells: '?!' Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '?'' Garry Kasparov: 'Portisch is seduced by the traditional evaluation "two rooks are stronger than a queen". But in the given instance it is just the opposite: the more numerous white army is unable to achieve coordination!'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'And he called this "very bad judgement". White's pawn at c4, will be condemned once there is no White queen to defend it. Then the rooks-plus-bishop are no match for the queen-plus-knight. Portisch's mea culpa was that he realized how bad his position was and thought this was his best drawing chances. For example'} 14. Qe2 $17 { Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '' Ftacnik: ''' Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer) was undoubtedly necessary, although after'} Nc6 {Andrew Soltis} ({or} 14... Qa4 {Garry Kasparov} 15. Bc1 Nc6 {White would have had to think primarily about defence.}) 15. O-O e5 16. d5 e4 {is horrible for White}) 14... Nc6 $19 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '-+''} 15. Qxe8+ Qxe8 16. O-O Na5 17. Rae1 Bxc4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black is aiming to convert his advantage by purely technical means, avoiding any activation of the opponent's pieces.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer believed he should have improved his queen position before cashing in. He gave'} 17... Qa4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!''} 18. Bb4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'if'} 18. Bc1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Bxc4 19. Bxc4 Qxc4 $19 {Garry Kasparov: 'should easily win'}) 18... Bxc4 19. Bxc4 Nxc4 20. Rxe6 a5 21. Be7 Nd2 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!''} 22. Rfe1 Ne4 23. f3 ({However, White can fight a bit harder with} 23. Re5 {Andrew Soltis} Qxa2 24. Rxf5 h6 $1 {Garry Kasparov} 25. Rf8+ (25. h4 Nxc3) 25... Kh7 26. Bd8 Qd2 $1 27. Rf1 Nxc3 28. Bxc7 Qxd4 29. Re1 Qc4 {and wins.}) ({Garry Kasparov: '. More tenacious defences would not have helped:'} 23. Bh4 {Andrew Soltis} g5 (23... h6 $1 {Garry Kasparov} 24. Re7 Nxc3 25. Rxc7 Nd5 26. Rc8+ Kh7 27. f3 Qxa2) 24. f3) (23. Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) 23... Qxa2 $1 $19 {Fischer Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and wins Ftacnik: '-+!' Ftacnik: 'Fischer'' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Curtains." (Fischer).' Wells: '-Fischer. In any case, I do not want to re-write notes to this rightly acclaimed classic. The point here is how the presence of structural weaknesses in white's game enabled the combination of queen and knight to be particularly effective.  v. is not an unchanging equation - different structures will lead to markedly varying assessments.'}) 18. Bxc4 (18. Bxf5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Qa4 $19 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 18... Nxc4 19. Bc1 c5 {Wells: '' Garry Kasparov: '!'} 20. dxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'White needs files for his rooks and targets for his bishop. His chances of drawing woul dbe close to zero after'} (20. d5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '?''} e5 21. Rd1 {Garry Kasparov} Qd7 22. Rfe1 (22. d6 $6 Nxd6 23. Rd5 Qe6 $1) 22... Qd6 {Black has a clear advantage:} 23. Re2 Kf7 24. f3 (24. f4 e4) 24... Ke7 25. h4 Kd7 {etc.}) ({And in the event of} 20. Re2 {Garry Kasparov} cxd4 21. cxd4 Qd7 22. Rfe1 Kf7 $1 23. g3 (23. a4 Qxd4 $1 24. Rxe6 Qd7 {and wins}) {, the advance of the queenside pawns is decisive:} 23... b5 24. Bf4 a5 25. h4 b4 26. h5 a4 {etc.}) 20... bxc5 21. Bf4 h6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'It is crucial for Black to control the dark squares, such as by driving the bishop into submission with ...g5 and ...f4. Now 22.h4 is met by 22. ...e5! 23.Bxe5 Nxe5 24.f4 Nf3+ 25.gxf3 Qa4 and the win, after picking up two pawns it should be easy. Ftacnik: '!''} 22. Re2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 22. h4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} e5 $1 23. Bxe5 (23. Be3 {Garry Kasparov} Qb5) 23... Nxe5 24. f4 Nf3+ $1 25. gxf3 Qa4 $19 {× a2, c3, f3, f4, h4 Fischer Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer) the white pawns become easy prey for the queen. But now Black begins a decisive offensive on the kingside.'}) 22... g5 23. Be5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'A rare instance, when a 'powerful' bishop on e5 is transformed into a target of attack. Portisch rightly attached a question mark to this move,'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'This puts the game beyond redemption. Better was' Garry Kasparov: 'and suggested the more resilient'} 23. Be3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'which Portisch claimed would have drawn after Ftacnik: '!''} Qb5 (23... Qc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 24. f4 g4 25. Bf2) 24. f3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '!''} (24. f4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Nd6 { 25...e4 Garry Kasparov: '!'}) {Garry Kasparov: 'rather than'} 24... e5 ({, although after} 24... Ne5 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) and ...Qc4}) 25. Bf2 { e1= Portisch Andrew Soltis: '. But that forgets that in queen-versus-pieces endings it is usually the king that makes the difference 25. ...Kf7 and ...Ke6 Ftacnik: ' e1= Portisch''} Kf7 {Garry Kasparov} 26. Rfe1 {) White would hardly have been able to hold out.}) 23... Qd8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 24. Rfe1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Futile was'} 24. f4 Nd2 $1 25. Rfe1 Ne4 $36 {Fischer Andrew Soltis: 'followed by ...Qd5 or ...Qd3 Ftacnik: '' Ftacnik: 'Fischer''}) 24... Kf7 25. h3 f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Beginning the pursuit of the bishop.'} 26. Kh2 a6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '"Taking care of details,'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'so that the pawn will not be within the bishop's reach after'} 26... Qd5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} 27. Bb8 {Garry Kasparov: '." (Fischer)'}) 27. Re4 Qd5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'On the previous move, this would have given White a chance to threaten the a-pawn with Bb8. But here Black threatens 28. ...Ne3 as well as 28. ...f3 29.gxf3 Nd2.'} 28. h4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Black's threats are irresistible: if'} 28. R4e2 $142 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik' Garry Kasparov: 'he would have replied'} f3 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 29. gxf3 (29. Re4 fxg2 $19 { d2 Garry Kasparov: 'and ...Nd2.'}) 29... Nd2 $19 {Fischer}) 28... Ne3 $1 {Wells: '-+'} 29. R1xe3 (29. f3 Qd2 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 30. Rg1 Qf2 $19 {Fischer Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik: '-+' Ftacnik: 'Fischer''}) 29... fxe3 30. Rxe3 Qxa2 31. Rf3+ (31. f3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Qf2 32. Re4 gxh4 $19) 31... Ke8 32. Bg7 Qc4 33. hxg5 (33. Bxh6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ftacnik'} Qxh4+ 34. Rh3 Qxf2 35. Bxg5 a5 $19) 33... hxg5 34. Rf8+ Kd7 35. Ra8 Kc6 {Garry Kasparov: '. It is staggering to what extent Fischer surpassed one of the world's leading grandmasters in depth of evaluation of a non-standard position.'} 0-1

