{Inf.2/51 Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's final score at Havana was 15-2, or 88.2 percent. Yet once again he failed to win the prize for best score on top board - because Tigran Petrosian scored 111/2-11/2, or 88.4 percent. Fischer lost his chance to catch him in his penultimate game. Florin Gheorghiu offered a draw on the 15th move but Fischer refused and was ground down in 50 moves. Perhaps his defensive heroics in this game from the seventh round of the finals had made him feel indestructable.'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky'} Nf6 2. Nf3 c5 3. d5 ({Another new position from Fischer. On} 3. c4 {(Andrew Soltis) he would continue} cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6) 3... b5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '!?''} 4. c4 Bb7 $5 ({Better than} 4... e6 {(Andrew Soltis) which transposes into the Blumenfeld gambit. Now} 5. a4 bxc4 6. Nc3 {and 7.e4 was considered best at the time}) 5. g3 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '?!''} (5. Nbd2 $142 {(Andrew Soltis) Boleslavsky} bxc4 6. e4 e6 7. dxe6 fxe6 8. e5 $1 {9.Sc4}) 5... g6 6. Bg2 bxc4 7. Nc3 Bg7 8. O-O O-O 9. Ne5 d6 10. Nxc4 Nbd7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '' Boleslavsky: '''} ({White's choice of development is modest but not without sting. A 1984 Soviet game went} 10... Ba6 {Andrew Soltis} 11. Ne3 Nbd7 12. Qa4 {with a small edge for White}) 11. Re1 Ba6 12. Qa4 Qc8 ({Black stands well after} 12... Bxc4 {Andrew Soltis} 13. Qxc4 Rb8 {e.g. Boleslavsky: ''} 14. Rb1 Ne5 15. Qa4 Qc7 16. Bf4 Rb4 17. Qc2 Rfb8 {. But Fischer likes his bishops - and doesn't seem to mind Na5-c6}) 13. Na5 Nb6 $13 ({Black wants to know which piece is going to c6. The square turns out to be very useful to White after} 13... Re8 {Andrew Soltis} 14. Bg5 Rb8 15. Rab1 Rb4 16. Qc6 {and the} Ne5 17. Qxc8 Rxc8 18. Nc6) 14. Qh4 ({but on} 14. Qc6 {Andrew Soltis} Qb8 $1 {White has to save his queen from ...Rc8. and has nothing but a repetition after} 15. Bh3 Bc8 16. Bg2 Ba6 ({and the worst of} 16... Bd7 17. Qb7 Qxb7 18. Nxb7 a5)) 14... Re8 15. Bg5 Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Since the knight is vulnerable to ...Nbd7 and ...e6xd5'} 16. Nc6 Bb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'But on c6 the knight is vulnerable to ...Nbd7 and ... e6xd5'} 17. e4 {Andrew Soltis: 'However, f2-f4 and e4-e5 appear faster'} Nbd7 ({bad was} 17... Bxc6 {Andrew Soltis} 18. dxc6 Qxc6 19. e5 Nfd5 20. Nxd5 Nxd5 {because of} 21. e6 $1 {which threatens Rad1}) 18. f4 $16 Kh8 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Calculated insouciance : Black seems to have taken the slightest of precautions. Yet he threatens the bishop with ...Ng8! and 20. ...f6 and keeps the idea of ...exd5 and ...Nxd5 alive. Boleslavsky: '19... Sg8''} 19. e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '!''} (19. Rad1 $5) 19... dxe5 20. fxe5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '??''} ({In an east german book on the tournament, Boleslavsky took White to task for missing a golden opportunity to upset the American champion. He argued that} 20. Nxe5 {(Andrew Soltis) was much stronger, citing for instance Boleslavsky: '!'} Nxe5 ({after} 20... Kg8 {with ...e6 in mind, there are two critical continuations and neither bears out Boleslavsky's optimism :} 21. Nc6 {and now Boleslavsky: '!'} (21. Nc4 {and now Agur gives} Nb6 22. d6 Qc8 ({However, Black has a better reply in} 22... Qb8 23. Nxb6 Bxg2 24. Nxa8 Bxa8 25. Re2 Qb7 26. Rf1 exd6) 23. Nxb6 ({but this is refuted by} 23. Rxe7 Rxe7 24. Nxb6 axb6 25. dxe7 Ng4 $2 (25... Bxg2 26. Bxf6 $1 Bf3 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Rf1 Bb7 29. f5 $1) 26. e8=Q+) 23... axb6 24. Bxb7 Qxb7 25. dxe7 Ng4 $1 {and ...Bd4}) 21... c4 $1 {opens up the b6-g1 diagonal so that Black can meet} 22. Nxe7+ {with} ({the main line is} 22. Kh1 Qb6 23. Rab1 e6 $1 {and White's knight is in trouble. It is lost after} 24. b3 ({Best is} 24. f5 $1 gxf5 25. Qxc4 {and the game is just beginning.}) 24... exd5 25. Ne7+ Rxe7 $1 26. Rxe7 d4 27. bxc4 ({and worse following} 27. Qh3 dxc3 28. Bxf6 Nxf6 29. Rxb7 Qe3 {because of ...c2.}) 27... Bxg2+ 28. Kxg2 Qc6+) 22... Rxe7 23. Rxe7 Qc5+) 21. fxe5 Nd7 22. e6 fxe6 23. Rxe6 Bf6 {Boleslavsky} 24. Rae1) 20... Nxd5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 21. Nxd5 (21. Bxd5 {(Andrew Soltis) Boleslavsky} Bxc6 22. Bxf7 Nxe5 23. Rxe5 Qxe5 24. Bxg6 Qd4+ $19) 21... Qxc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'If the king still stood of g8, the White would win with Nf6+ or Ne7+'} 22. e6 $6 Ne5 $1 ({Not} 22... fxe6 $4 {Andrew Soltis} 23. Nf4 $1 {and White wins Boleslavsky: '24.Sg6'}) 23. Rxe5 $6 ({the attack falls apart after} 23. exf7 {Andrew Soltis} Rf8 24. Nf4 (24. Bxe7 {Boleslavsky} Rxf7 $40 {-+}) 24... Nf3+ $1 25. Bxf3 Bd4+) 23... Bxe5 24. exf7 Rf8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Boleslavsky: '25... Ld4 26.Kh1 Dd5!''} 25. h3 $2 (25. Nf4 Qb6 (25... Bd4+ {Andrew Soltis} 26. Kh1 Qb6 ({Black can also put in a bid for the brilliancy prize with} 26... Rxf7 $1 27. Bxc6 Bxc6+ 28. Ng2 Rf2) 27. Bxb7 Qxb7+ {wins}) 26. Bxb7 c4+ $1 $17) 25... Rxf7 26. Nf4 Rxf4 $1 27. Bxc6 {Andrew Soltis} Bd4+ (27... Rxh4 {Boleslavsky} 28. Bxb7 Rxh3 29. Bxa8 Rxg3+ $19) 28. Kh2 Rf2+ 29. Kg1 Rxb2+ {. Fischer took only 77 minutes, an hour less than his opponent.} 0-1

