{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 57 Change of pace. Larsen, uncharacteristically, forces an early exchange of Queens so that he can spring a surprise in the resulting endgame. Fischer beats him to it (13 ... b6) and proceeds to defend with meticulous care. It looks as if a draw must ensue, but Larsen presses. He is rebuffed, and again a draw seems imminent. Larsen senses no danger and, as if by inertia, continues to play for a win. As the game simplifies, the self-inflicted dark square weaknesses in the Dane's position gradually reveal themselves. By move thirty it becomes Fischer's turn to assume the initiative, and he probes these flaws judiciously. Thrown on the defensive, Larsen makes one or two reckless moves out of which Fischer constructs elegant combinations. Thus, what begins as a barren endgame is transformed into an exhibition of chess sensibility and virtuosity. ' Andrew Soltis: 'This game, like the last two, illustrates Fischer's ability to blend the tactical and technical in the endgame. Botwinnik later compared him to his most famous student. "From a combinational point of view, I suggest Kasparov outstripped Fischer," he wrote. "But in the endgame, Fischer was stronger."'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis ChessBase ChessBase ChessBase Bobby Fischer ChessBase: 'Matanovic,Aleksandar'} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 ({Larsen had won some good games with} 5. Nf3 {Bobby Fischer} O-O 6. Be3 {but after} e5 $1 {(which no one seems to have played) White gets no advantage.} 7. Be2 ({And} 7. d5 Ng4 8. Bg5 f6 9. Bh4 Qe8 $36 {gives Black dynamic play. Now} 10. h3 Nh6 11. g4 $5 f5 {is too risky for White.}) ({Finally on} 7. dxe5 dxe5 8. Qxd8 Rxd8 9. Nd5 $2 Na6 $1 $17 {gives Black the better ending.}) 7... Nc6 {transposes into well-known modem lines.}) 5... O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} Nc6 8. Be3 ({A bit of a surprise. I had expected Andrew Soltis: 'Larsen had injected several ideas into the Classical King's Indian. For example, in Benko's line'} 8. d5 {Bobby Fischer} Ne7 9. Ne1 Nd7 10. Nd3 (10. f3 {Andrew Soltis} f5 11. g4 {, he showed that what was considered the book refutation} h5 12. g5 h4 13. Nd3 f4 {followed by winning the g-pawn, was actually a strong gambit after} 14. Kh1 Kf7 15. c5 Rh8 16. Qb3 $1) 10... f5 11. Bd2 {Now I had in mind} c5 $1 ({Larsen-Najdorf, Santa Monica 1966, had continued} 11... Nf6 $2 12. f3 f4 13. c5 $1 g5 14. Rc1 Ng6 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. Nb5 Rf7 17. Qc2 $1 Ne8 18. a4 $16 {and White came first on the Q-side since Black's attack never got off the ground. Larsen, B-Najdorf,M Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 ChessBase: 'Larsen,B-Najdorf,M Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966'}) 12. f3 f4 $1 $36 ({But not} 12... Nf6 $2 13. g4 $1 f4 14. h4 $1 {and Black's K -side counterplay is completely stymied) with active chances.})) 8... Re8 {Bobby Fischer: '!' ChessBase: '!' '!' '!'} ({The cleanest way to equalize. Najdorf found this move after some painful experiences with 8 ... l} 8... Ng4 {Bobby Fischer}) 9. dxe5 ({in his match vs. Reshevsky. The main point if that Andrew Soltis: 'Black is at least equal after'} 9. d5 {Bobby Fischer} Nd4 $1 $11 {levels ChessBase: '=!' '=!' '=!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} 10. Nxd4 {Andrew Soltis} exd4 11. Bxd4 Nxe4) 9... dxe5 10. Qxd8 Nxd8 ({A dubious improvement over} 10... Rxd8 $1 {(Bobby Fischer) as played by Reshevsky in his match with Benko. Reshevsky,S after} 11. Bg5 {Black must not play} Rd7 $2 {(after which Benko's} (11... Rf8 $1 $11 {ChessBase: '=!' '=!' '=!'}) 12. Bd1 $3 $16 {followed by Ba4 was very strong) but 11 ... Rf8! solves all his problems.Benko,P ChessBase: ' a4; Benko,P'}) 11. Nb5 Ne6 12. Ng5 Re7 $11 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after' ChessBase: 'Matanovic,Aleksandar: '=''} ({White's pressure is strong after} 12... Nxg5 {Andrew Soltis} 13. Bxg5 Re7 14. Rad1 Be6 15. f4 $1) 13. Rfd1 ({Larsen is attempting to in1prove on Reshevsky-Fischer, Santa Monica 1966, which continued:} 13. Nxe6 {Bobby Fischer} Bxe6 14. f3 c6 15. Nc3 Rd7 16. Rfd1 Bf8 17. Kf2 b6 18. b3 Rb7 19. Na4 Nd7 20. Nb2 b5 $11 {with an eventual draw. ..Larsen, B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 ChessBase: '=' '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Larsen,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966'}) ({Najdorf, who had popularized 8. ...Re8, had pronounced} 13. Nxa7 {Andrew Soltis} Nf4 $1 {good for Black}) 13... b6 $1 ({In my 9th match game with Reshevsky, 1961, I tried Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer is the first to vary from book. HIs ninth match game with Reshevsky, which went'} 13... c6 $5 {(Bobby Fischer) Instead of Andrew Soltis: ''!?''} 14. Nxe6 ({Larsen told me he had intcnded Andrew Soltis: 'Larsen had been waiting to test a new idea'} 14. Nxa7 $5 {ChessBase: 'Larsen,B' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: ''!?' Larsen,B'} Bd7 15. Nxe6 Bxe6 16. f3 {But after} Rd7 $1 $44 {But after (threatening ...Rd4) Black has fair play for the Pawn, considering that the Knight is stranded on a7. The book text was an improvement that 1 had hatched some tinle ago ChessBase: ' d4'}) 14... Bxe6 15. Nc3 Rd7 $11 {Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R (9) Los Angeles 1961 ChessBase: '=' '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R (9) Los Angeles 1961' Andrew Soltis: ''=' Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R (9) Los Angeles 1961'}) 14. c5 $6 {Bobby Fischer: '!?' ChessBase: '!?' '!?' '!?' ChessBase: 'Matanovic,Aleksandar: '?!'' Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Typically, Larsen adopts an enterprising continuation. He should settle for} 14. Nxe6 {(Bobby Fischer) >= ChessBase: ''} Bxe6 15. f3 $11 {with a draw in view. By overestinlating his chances, he gradually drifts into a losing position. ChessBase: '=' '=' '='}) 14... Nxc5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Naturally not Andrew Soltis: 'One point of the pawn sacrifice is'} 14... bxc5 $2 {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: ''?''} 15. Nxe6 Bxe6 16. Bxc5 Rd7 17. f3 $18 {wins. Andrew Soltis: 'and Black's pawns are too weak '+-''} c6 {Andrew Soltis} 18. Rxd7 Nxd7 19. Nc7) 15. Rd8+ Bf8 ({No better is Andrew Soltis: 'White also has a big edge after'} 15... Re8 {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: '?'} 16. Rxe8+ Nxe8 17. Bxc5 bxc5 18. Bc4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: ''!''} Nd6 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Nxd6 cxd6 20. Nxf7 Kf8 21. Nxd6) ({Or} 15... Ne8 $2 {Bobby Fischer} 16. Bxc5 bxc5 17. Nxc7 Rxc7 18. Rxe8+ Bf8 19. Nxh7 $1) 16. Nxa7 Rxa7 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'} ({On} 16... Bb7 {Bobby Fischer} 17. Rxa8 Bxa8 18. f3 $36 {White has a slight pull despite his misplaced Knight. After} c6 19. Nc8 Rb7 20. Rd1 {maintains some pressure.}) 17. Rxc8 {Bobby Fischer: 'White recovers his pawn with even chances.'} Kg7 ({Black wisely resists the temptation of Andrew Soltis: 'Black anticipates Bh6'} 17... Ncxe4 $4 {Bobby Fischer} 18. Nxe4 Nxe4 19. Bh6 {Andrew Soltis: 'and wins'}) ({But even more accurate than the text is Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer later said'} 17... h6 $1 {Bobby Fischer Andrew Soltis: ''!''} 18. Nf3 Kg7 19. Bxc5 bxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'was a straighter road to equality but even then Whtie is a bit better'} 20. Bd3 $11 {completely neutralizing any initiative for either side. ChessBase: '=' '=' '='} c4 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Bxc4 Nxe4 22. Rd8) (17... Nb3 {Andrew Soltis} 18. Rd1 Rxa2 19. Rdd8 Nd7 20. h4 $1 {threatening Bc4 or Bb5}) 18. f3 Ne8 19. a3 $2 {Andrew Soltis: ''?''} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Larsen's reluctance to siInplify will soon backfire. Correct is' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer thought'} 19. Bxc5 $11 {Bobby Fischer: '!' ChessBase: '!' '!' '!' ChessBase: 'Matanovic,Aleksandar: '=''} bxc5 {Bobby Fischer} 20. Rb8 $14 {with theoretical winning chances because of the passed a-Pawn. But it would be difficult to make headway because of the opposite colored Bishops. Andrew Soltis: 'was best but there's little if anything after '''} f6 {(Andrew Soltis) followed by ...Nd6/...Re8}) 19... Nd6 $15 {ChessBase: 'Matanovic,Aleksandar: ''' Andrew Soltis: 'Now 20.Rb8 Nd7! 21.Rd8 Nb7 leads to a repertition of moves'} 20. Rd8 ({Optimistic as ever!} 20. Rb8 {Bobby Fischer} Nd7 21. Rd8 Nb7 22. Rc8 Nd6 $11 {would lead to a draw by repetition. ChessBase: '=' '=' '='}) 20... h6 21. Nh3 Ne6 22