{Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller. ''GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller.  ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'GAME N. 59 The erring Bishop. In order to restrain Black from creating complications, Kholmov employs an unpretentious system. But he posts his Bishop prematurely on a3. then penetrates ambitiously with 11.Bd6. Fischer. with the routine 11...Qa5. promptly refutes. It is instructive to observe how. from now on. he creates an unhealthy climate wherever the Bishop seeks lodging. His Queen returns to a3 on no less than three separate occasions. prompting one annotator to inquire, dryly. whether he was perhaps inventing perpetual motion. As early as move twelve it became apparent to both players that White was lost. However. Kholmov did his best to avert the inevitable for another twenty moves. Fischer (with White) had lost to Kholmov in their only previous encounter. played via telephone to Havana in '65. Here. Fischer's victory brought him first place, a half point ahead of Geller. '''} {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer won Skopje 1967 thanks to this penultimate game. But an ominous sign had appeared six rounds earlier when he insisted on playing in a separate area, without spectators, and using a set in which the distinction between the queen and king was more pronounced. The organizers refuses and Fischer forfeited the game. Cooler heads prevailed and Fischer agreed to finish out the tournament and reschedule the forfeit. But for the rest of Fischer's career he became increasingly more inflexible in his demands.'} 1. d4 {Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer'' Bobby Fischer: 'ChessBase ChessBase ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer''} Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O d6 6. Nc3 {Bobby Fischer: 'A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge.''A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A straightforward but essentially passive development. The idea is to avoid creating a weakness with c4; however, a drawback is White can no longer dominate the center with a Pawn-wedge.'''} Nbd7 ({Bobby Fischer: 'More flexible than''More flexible than ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'More flexible than''' Andrew Soltis: 'At the Piatgorsky Cup Fischer tried'} 6... d5 {Bobby Fischer: 'which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued''which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'which I adopted against Ivkov at the Piatigorsky Cup, 1966. That game continued''' Andrew Soltis: ', to penalize Ivkov for blocking White's c-pawn. That was good enough for equality but no more after'} 7. Ne5 c6 8. e4 {Bobby Fischer: 'and now Black could have equalized easily with''and now Black could have equalized easily with ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'and now Black could have equalized easily with'''} dxe4 {Bobby Fischer: 'instead of..''instead of.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'instead of..'''} (8... Be6 $2 {Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966''Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 ChessBase: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 Bobby Fischer: 'Ivkov,B-Fischer,R Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966'''}) 9. Nxe4 Nxe4 10. Bxe4 Bh3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Not..''Not.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Not..'''} 10... c5 $2 11. Qf3 $1) 11. Re1 Nd7 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' '=' '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..''Et'Etc.. ChessBase: '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..''' c.. ChessBase: '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: 'Etc..'''}) 7. b3 {Andrew Soltis: 'A unique position already. More common is the Pirc-like'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter.''A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A normal continuation, although it is dubious that White's Bishop is effective once posted on b2 or a3. This systelTI is characterized by Pawn sYlrunetry and quiet play with drawing tendencies. But a draw is precisely what I could not afford in this crucial encounter.'''} 7. e4 e5 {Bobby Fischer: 'also presents Black with no opening problems.''also presents Black with no opening problems. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'also presents Black with no opening problems.'''}) 7... e5 8. dxe5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after''Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Dissipating the central tension. Black has no trouble getting play after''' Andrew Soltis: 'White's dilemma is that if he plays'} 8. e4 {Andrew Soltis: ', he creates a target'} exd4 9. Nxd4 Re8 10. Bb2 Nc5 11. Re1 Bg4 {. But if he doesn't, Black cramps his game with ...e4!}) 8... dxe5 9. e4 $10 {Bobby Fischer: '=' '=' '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).''"T'"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH). ChessBase: '=' '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).''' he turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH). ChessBase: '=' ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '=' Bobby Fischer: '"The turbulent complications of the normal K's Indian can hardly arise, and the position can already be evaluated as even." (TRIFUNOVICH).'''} Re8 10. Ba3 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Better is''Better is ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Better is'''} 10. a4 { Bobby Fischer: '>=''>= ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: '>='''} a5 11. Ba3 {Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto.''whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'whereupon the Bishop will be immune from eventual attack after ....Qa5. "Even so early, White is on the wrong track. The Bishop has nothing to seek on the a3-f8 diagonal. Yet, while it is easy now to condemn this move, till now it has often been adopted by White with never a harsh word. The punishment which ensues, however ... is more severe than any this commentator has observed hitherto.'''}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 10. Bb2 { Bobby Fischer: 'is correct''is correct ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: 'is correct'''} c6 {and ...Qe7, Black has little to fear. BUt the bishop gets into trouble on a3.}) 10... c6 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position."''Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position." ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black invites the Bishop to seize a strong position."'''} 11. Bd6 $2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!"''Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!" ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Kholmov consulned over half an hour on this mistake. The idea is to keep Black bottled up while exerting pressure on the e-Pawn. The only trouble with the move, however, is that it loses. As Tarrasch wrote: "When you don't know what to do, wait for your opponent to get an idea - it's sure to be wrong!"'''} 11. Qe2 { Bobby Fischer: 'was indicated''was indicated ChessBase: ' Bobby Fischer: 'was indicated'''}) 11... Qa5 $1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after''This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'This normal freeing maneuver is now devastating. Position after''' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer believed this punished 11.Bd6, but'} 12. Qd3 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal).''Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal). ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black now has a shot which wins two pieces for a Rook; or, as it turns out, a lowly Pawn (which proves fatal).''' Andrew Soltis: '??' Andrew Soltis: 'This was the real error.'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'What else?''What else? ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'What else?'''} 12. b4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent.''Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Qa3 leaves White in the same predicmnent.'''} Qa6) ({White can allow} 12. a3 $1 Qxc3 {because both players must accept a draw by repetition of moves} 13. Bb4 Qb2 14. Rb1 ({Note that if White insists on trapping the queen with} 14. Qe2 a5 15. Rfb1 {the cost is too great} Qxa1 16. Rxa1 axb4) 14... Qa2 15. Ra1 Qb2 {. This would have been the shortest game Fischer ever played.}) 12... Re6 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again.''Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Springing the trap! White cannot avoid Inaterialloss. Geller, who was tied with lne for the lead, had displayed great interest in lny game - up to now. When he saw this position, he smiled wryly. I never noticed him looking at the game again.''' Andrew Soltis: 'Astonishgly strong. The threats are 13. ..Ne8 trapping the bishop, and 13. ...Rxd6 14.Qxd6 Qxc3.  Kholmov had counted on 13.Ng5 Rxd6 14.Qxd6 Qxc3? 15.Qe7. But now he saw 14. ...h6! 15.b4 Qa3 costs him material.'} 13. b4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After''No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'No matter how White wriggles and squimls, he cannot escape the fate in store for him. After'''} 13. Ne2 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black has the pleasant choice of either''Black has the pleasant choice of either ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black has the pleasant choice of either'''} Nxe4 {Bobby Fischer: 'Or..''Or.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Or..'''} (13... Ne8 {Bobby Fischer: '- both of which win a Pawn.''- both of which win a Pawn. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '- both of which win a Pawn.'''})) ({Bobby Fischer: 'After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended''After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'After the gmne Kholmov told me he had originally intended'''} 13. Ng5 Rxd6 14. Qxd6 Qxc3 (14... h6 $1 $19 {Kholmov,R}) 15. Qe7 $36 {Bobby Fischer: 'with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions.''with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'with active play. However, he saw (too late) that simply 14 ... h6! squelches all such illusions.'''}) 13... Qa3 $1 { xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6''Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6 ChessBase: ' xd6 Bobby Fischer: 'Renewing the ancient threat of Rxd6'''} 14. Bc7 {Bobby Fischer: 'The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn.''The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'The wandering Bishop hopes to receive succor. But, in so doing, it must abandon protection of the b-pawn.'''} Qxb4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White was hoping for''Perhaps White was hoping for ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White was hoping for''' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer indicated this was much better than'} 14... Ne8 15. Ba5 b6 16. Rab1 $1 bxa5 ({.  But that shortchanges Black, who could win with} 16... Rd6 17. Qc4 ({Same for} 17. Qe3 Bh6 18. Qe1 (18. Qxh6 Qxc3) 18... Bd2 $1) 17... Ba6 18. Qxa6 Qxc3 {since the bishop remains trapped}) 17. Rb3 {Andrew Soltis: 'after which Black's best winning try is unclear'} Nc5 $1 18. bxc5 Qxc5 19. Rfb1 $132 {Bobby Fischer: 'where Black's technical difficulties are great.''where Black's technical difficulties are great. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'where Black's technical difficulties are great.'''}) 15. Rab1 Qe7 $1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'A cute tactical point.''A cute tactical point. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'A cute tactical point.'''} 15... Qf8 $2 {Bobby Fischer: 'loses the exchange after''loses the exchange after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'loses the exchange after'''} 16. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: '(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6).''(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6). ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: '(if the Rook retreats, then White's Bishop returns to d6).'''} Re8 17. Bd6) ({Black should keep c5 free since White would have serious comepnsation after} 15... Qc5 $2 16. Ng5 Re8 17. Na4 Qd4 18. Qb3) 16. Rfd1 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Now..''Now.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Now..''' Andrew Soltis: 'Here Black can meet'} 16. Ng5 {Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by''is refuted by ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'is refuted by''' Andrew Soltis: 'with'} Nc5 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'For all practical purposes the game is over.''For all practical purposes the game is over. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'For all practical purposes the game is over.'''} 17. Qc4 Qxc7) 16... Ne8 17. Ba5 {Bobby Fischer: 'No rest for the weary.''No rest for the weary. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'No rest for the weary.'''} Rd6 18. Qe2 Rxd1+ 19. Qxd1 Bf8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black threatens to win again with ...Qa3 and 20.Bb4 should lose to 20. ...Qf6 (not 20. ...c5? 21.Nd5) 21. Bxf8 Nxf8'} 20. Nd2 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Bad - as is everything else.''Bad - as is everything else. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Bad - as is everything else.'''} 20. Bb4 Qf6 21. Bxf8 Nxf8 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing.''leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'leaves White a Pawn behind with his weak squares still showing.'''}) 20... Qa3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Winning even more material.''Winning even more material. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Winning even more material.'''} 21. Nc4 Qc5 22. Bf1 b5 23. Nd2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'the dust settles after'} 23. Bb4 {Bobby Fischer: 'drops a piece to''drops a piece to ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'drops a piece to'''} Qd4 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'when White is dead lost'}) 23... Qa3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing.''This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'This curious shuttle has proved White's undoing.''' Andrew Soltis: 'powereful for the third time in the game. Black wins material'} 24. Nb3 Nc5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 25. Bxb5 {Andrew Soltis: '!?'} ({Bobby Fischer: 'Desperation. After''Desperation. After ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Desperation. After''' Andrew Soltis: 'Black tuns out of squares'} 25. Bd8 Ne6 $1 26. Ba5 Nd6 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'Black wins as he pleases.''Black wins as he pleases. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Black wins as he pleases.'''}) (25. Qd8 Bb7) 25... cxb5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Safer, despite appearances, since} 25... Nxb3 26. Rxb3 Qxa5 27. Bxc6 Be6 28. Rb5 {. Black is still winning in that line} Qxc3 29. Bxa8 Nc7 30. Rb8 Bxa2 {but it is needlessly complex.}) 26. Nxb5 {Bobby Fischer: 'Position after''Position after ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Position after'''} Qa4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Forced, but more than sufficient'} 27. Nxc5 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White had intended''Perhaps White had intended ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Perhaps White had intended'''} 27. Qd5 {Bobby Fischer: 'But..''But.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'But..'''} Qxe4 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'spells finished''spells finished ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'spells finished'''}) 27... Qxa5 28. Qd5 Rb8 29. a4 Bh3 $1 {Bobby Fischer: 'Quickest.''Quickest. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Quickest.'''} 30. Qxe5 Rc8 31. Nd3 Qxa4 32. Ne1 ({Black wins after} 32. Nf4 Qxc2 33. Re1 Bb4 34. Nd4 Qc5 $1) 32... a6 {Bobby Fischer: 'White resigns''White resigns ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'White resigns''' Andrew Soltis: '!'} (32... a6 {Bobby Fischer: 'The Knight has no good square. If''The Knight has no good square. If ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'The Knight has no good square. If''' Andrew Soltis: 'The handwriting on the wall reads'} 33. Nd4 ({Bobby Fischer: 'Or..''Or.. ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Or..'''} 33. Nc3 Qc4) ({Bobby Fischer: 'Finally''Finally ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'Finally'''} 33. Na7 Rc7 34. Ra1 Qd7 (34... Qc4) 35. Rxa6 Rxa7 36. Rxa7 Qxa7 37. Qxe8 Qa1 $19 {Bobby Fischer: 'delivers Mate. ........Afterwards, Geller tried to offer my opponent some sympathy. I overheard a dismayed Kholmov telling him that 1 had "seen everything!" This game was particularly sweet because it was my first win against a Russian in almost a dozen tries (since game 52) - and my frrst with Black since 1962 (Korchnoi at Curacao''delivers Mate. ........Afterwards, Geller tried to offer my opponent some sympathy. I overheard a dismayed Kholmov telling him that 1 had "seen everything!" This game was particularly sweet because it was my first win against a Russian in almost a dozen tries (since game 52) - and my frrst with Black since 1962 (Korchnoi at Curacao ChessBase: 'Bobby Fischer: 'delivers Mate. ........Afterwards, Geller tried to offer my opponent some sympathy. I overheard a dismayed Kholmov telling him that 1 had "seen everything!" This game was particularly sweet because it was my first win against a Russian in almost a dozen tries (since game 52) - and my frrst with Black since 1962 (Korchnoi at Curacao''' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and wins'}) 33... Bg7 $19) 0-1

