{Andrew Soltis: 'One round after the Miagmasuren game, Fischer showed signs of the strain of playing in his first Interzonal since Stockholm : During his game with the Colombian Miguel Cuellar, he complained about "weak lighting." But the tournament judge refused to do anything, so Fischer and Cuellar ended up shifting the tournament. The tournament organizers are "against me", Fischer later said. "They're waiting for me to lose a game."'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis} Nf6 2. c4 c5 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. Bg5 h6 9. Bf4 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '!?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'An attempt to avoid the sharp'} 9. Bh4 g5 {Andrew Soltis}) 9... g5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: '"I don't believe in psychology", Fischer famously said, "I believe in good moves." The text has elements of both : White either has to lose time with his bishop or admit he was wrong last move and transpose into the previous note with'} 10. Bc1 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Overestimates the weakness of Black's kingside pawns'} (10. Bg3 $5 Nh5 {Andrew Soltis} 11. Bb5+ Kf8) (10. Bb5+ {Andrew Soltis} Kf8 11. Be3 Ng4 12. Bd2 a6 13. Be2 {which offers him little}) (10. Be3 {Andrew Soltis} O-O {followed by ... Ng4 and ...f5}) 10... O-O 11. Nd2 ({On} 11. Be2 {(Andrew Soltis) Black might have continued with} b5 $1 12. Bxb5 Nxe4 {, a common benoni theme.}) 11... Nbd7 12. Be2 Ne5 $11 {Andrew Soltis: 'Only in the 1970s was it appreciated that ...g5 - even without a gain of tmepo - was worthwhile to secure e5. For example, in the standard Bebnoni line that runs 8.Be2 O-O 9.O-O Re8 10.Nd2 White was found to have good play after 10. ... Nbd7 11.h3 g5!'} 13. Nf1 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Since Black stands excellently after} 13. O-O {Andrew Soltis} a6 14. a4 Rb8 {, White aims to exploit the f5 and h5 holes with Ng3 or Ne3}) 13... b5 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'This diverts him. Black would hold the initiative after 14.Ng3 b4 15. Nb1 c4'} 14. Bxb5 Qa5 {Andrew Soltis: 'the threat is ...Nxe4 (and if 15.Bd3 Nxd3+ 16.Qxd3 Nxe4!)'} 15. Ng3 c4 16. O-O Rb8 17. Qa4 ({the bishop looks well placed after} 17. Bc6 {(Andrew Soltis) and White gets the play he needs after} Nxc6 ({but Black should ignore it with} 17... Nd3 $1 {and Ng4}) 18. dxc6 Qc5 19. Qa4 $1) 17... Qxa4 18. Bxa4 Nd3 $44 19. Bb5 $1 ({a less artificial defense of b2 was} 19. Rb1 {Andrew Soltis} Nd7 20. Bc2 Nb4 21. Bd1 Nc5 22. Be3) 19... Ng4 $1 20. Nge2 (20. Bxc4 {Andrew Soltis} Nxb2 21. Bxb2 Rxb2 22. Rac1 Bd4 23. Nge2 Bc5 24. Na4 $1 {White is clearly better but 21. ...Bd7 leaves matters uncertain.}) 20... Nxc1 21. Raxc1 Ne5 22. b3 ({Black has ample play for a pawn, e.g.} 22. Rc2 {Andrew Soltis} f5 23. Ng3 (23. exf5 Bxf5 24. Rd2 Bd3) 23... f4 24. Nge2 f3 25. gxf3 Bh3) 22... cxb3 23. axb3 a6 24. Ba4 ({the bishops begin to dominate after} 24. Bc4 {Andrew Soltis} Nxc4 25. bxc4 Rb4 26. Nd1 f5) 24... Nd3 25. Rc2 f5 26. Ng3 $6 (26. f3 $5) ({no better was} 26. exf5 {Andrew Soltis} Bxf5 27. Ra2 (27. Rd2 Nc5 28. Nd4 $2 Bd3) 27... Nc5) 26... f4 27. Nge2 (27. Nf5 $5 $13) (27. Nh5 {Andrew Soltis} Be5 {White's knight is a new worry (...Bg4, ...Kh7-g6}) 27... f3 $17 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now 28.gxf3 Rxf3 and ...Bh3 is very strong.'} 28. Ng3 fxg2 29. Kxg2 Bg4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 30. Nf5 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'A blunder that drops a piece.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'amongst the nice finishes are'} 30. f3 $2 Bxf3+ $1 $19 31. Rxf3 {Andrew Soltis} (31. Kh3 {Andrew Soltis} Nf4#) 31... Ne1+) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Best is'} 30. Nce2 $17 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bf3+ {Andrew Soltis} 31. Kg1 Nc5) (30. Nd1 {Andrew Soltis} Bf3+ 31. Kg1 Nf4 $1 {Black threatens 32. ...Rh3#}) 30... Nf4+ $1 31. Kg3 (31. Kh1 $2 Bf3+ 32. Kg1 Nh3#) (31. Kg1 Bxf5 32. exf5 Bxc3 $19) 31... Bxf5 32. exf5 Bxc3 $19 33. Kf3 ({Since} 33. Rxc3 {Andrew Soltis} Ne2+ {leaves Black a rook up, White decides to play a piece down until adjournment. The finish was efficient :}) 33... Be5 34. Ke4 Rb4+ 35. Rc4 Rfb8 36. f6 Kf7 37. Kf5 Rxc4 38. bxc4 Ne2 39. Re1 (39. c5 {Andrew Soltis} Rb4 $1) 39... Nd4+ 40. Kg4 h5+ {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 41. Kh3 (41. Kxh5 {Andrew Soltis} Rh8+ 42. Kg4 Rh4+ 43. Kxg5 Nf3+ 44. Kf5 Rf4#) 41... Kxf6 0-1

