{Andrew Soltis: 'Max Euwe was among the admirers who said the endings in which Fischer excelled was rook and bishop versus rook and knight. But there is another candidate - endings with bishops of opposite color and one pair of rooks. Fischer won some examples of this not-so-rare ending that other grandmasters might have concluded were drawn. His games with Pachman at Havana 1966, Maric at Skopje 1967, Hort at Palma 1970, and Parma at Rovinj-Zagreb 1970 are memorable. BUt the following is the most impressive of the lot.'} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nh4 exd4 ({This gambit} 11... Nxe4 12. Nf5 {was popular from 1961 to 1967, then disappeared for no compelling reason}) 12. cxd4 Nb6 13. Nd2 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!?'} (13. Bc2 Nfd5 14. Nf3 $5 $13 (14. Nf5 $6 Bxf5 15. exf5 Bf6 $15 {Unzicker,W-Antoshin/Sochi/1965/})) ({Andrew Soltis: 'The offer still holds (13. ...Nxe4 14.Nxe4 Bxh4 15.Qh5). But Fischer later switched to'} 13. Nf3 {Fischer,R-Barczay,L/Sousee izt/1965/1-0/24/ Andrew Soltis: 'and obtained a big positional edge against Barczay at Sousse following'} d5 (13... c5 $1) 14. e5 Ne4 15. Nbd2 Nxd2 16. Bxd2 Bf5 17. Bc3) (13. Nc3 {Fischer,R-Robatsch,K/Vinkovci/1968/½-½/57/}) 13... Nfd5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (13... Nxe4 $6 {Anand: '?'} 14. Nxe4 Bxh4 15. Qh5 $44 d5 16. Ng5 Bxg5 17. Bxg5 $16) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Superior was'} 13... c5 {Andrew Soltis: 'with pressure on e4 after'} 14. Bc2 cxd4 15. Nhf3 Re8 {Andrew Soltis: 'and ...Bb7'} 16. Nxd4 Bf8 $13 {Byrne,R-Spassky,B/Moscow/1971/}) 14. Nhf3 Nb4 15. d5 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The position has transposes into a variation usually reached by 9. ...Nd7 10.d4 Nb6 10.Nbd2 cxd4 12.cxd4 Nb4 13.d5. White's move lays claims to c6 for a knight e.g. 13. . ..Bf6 14.Nf1 a5 15.a3 Na6 16.Bc2 and 17. Nd5'} (15. Nf1 c5 16. a3 Nc6 17. Be3 {½-½ Jansa,V (2495)-Matanovic,A (2495) Kostic Memorial 1979 (15)}) 15... c5 ({Also good is} 15... Nd3 16. Re2 Bf6 {and ...Nxc1}) 16. dxc6 Nxc6 17. Nf1 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: ''} Bf6 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Gf6b2] Anand: '?' Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'White should drive the bishop off b3 first'} 17... Na5 $1 $10) 18. Be3 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} (18. Rb1 {[%csl Rb2]} Be6 19. Ne3 Rc8 20. Ng4 Bxg4 21. hxg4 a5 22. Qe2 {1-0 Tal,M-Kuzmin,G Sochi schev 1970} (22. g5 Be7 23. a3 $14 {[%csl Gb3,Gc1]})) 18... Na5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'White needn'y spend a tempo on 18.Rb1 because he wins material with'} 18... Bxb2 $2 19. Qc2 {Anand: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bxa1 (19... Nc4 20. Bxc4 Bxa1 21. Bb3 Nb4 22. Qd2) 20. Qxc6 $18) 19. Bd4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (19. Bc2 Nac4) 19... Bb7 ({The difference between the knights on f1 and b6 is illustrated by} 19... Nxb3 20. axb3 $1 Bb7 21. Ng3 Re8 22. Nh5 Rxe4 $2 23. Rxe4 Bxe4 24. Bxf6 gxf6 25. Qd4) 20. Ng3 Nbc4 21. Bxc4 Nxc4 22. Nh5 $36 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Anand: ''} Ne5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'This accepts an inferior endgame - but one with bishops of opposite color - and that explains its appeal compared with the difficult endgame of'} 22... Bxd4 23. Qxd4 f6 24. Rad1 $32 (24. Nf4)) 23. Nxe5 dxe5 (23... Bxe5 24. f4) 24. Bc5 Qxd1 ({Worse was} 24... Re8 25. Qg4 {and 26.Rad1 - and}) (24... Be7 25. Qxd8 {is a blunder}) 25. Raxd1 Rfd8 26. Nxf6+ gxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Once he became a grandmaster Fischer played remarkably few pure bishops-of-opposite color endings. He won a pawn-up ending with the infamous bishops against Angelo Sandrin in 1957. He drew three equal-material endings of various lengths with Reshevsky early in his career. And he saved one dead-lost ending, two pawns down, against Walther at Zürich 1959. But after that he avoided the ending or drew quickly as soon as only bishops and pawns were left. Yet with heavy pieces on the board, the winning chances increased significantly. A pair of rooks or queends add, for example, the wild card factor of a mating threat, as in Game 95. Also, a rook can be used to break the blockade of pawns or even with an Exchange sacrifice to create a passed pawn.'} 27. Rxd8+ (27. f3) 27... Rxd8 28. Be7 $14 Rd4 (28... Rd2 $2 29. Re3 $1 {the mate threat Rf3+ and Bxf6 wins a pawn e.g.} h5 30. Bxf6 Rxb2 31. a3) 29. Re3 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bxe4 30. Bxf6 Kf8 (30... Bd5 31. Rxe5 Rd1+ 32. Kh2) 31. a3 Bc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black had nothing better in view of 32.f3'} 32. Rxe5 Rd5 33. Re3 ({Of course,} 33. Rxd5 $2 {gives away almost all of White's chances. Now he prepares to bring his king to g5, shielded by pawns at f5 and g4. Note that Black has no good way of getting his rook to a good square such as e6 that would allow his king to flee to the first rank. And 33. ...Rd2?? loses to 34.Be7+}) 33... Rf5 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'The rook is clumsy here. BEtter is'} (33... h5 34. f3 Kg8 {and ...Kh7-g6}) 34. Be5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} h5 35. f3 a5 36. Kf2 a4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black takes the normal precautions on both wings.'} 37. g4 hxg4 38. hxg4 Rg5 39. Bf6 $1 {[%csl Gf6,Rf8] Andrew Soltis: 'A powerful idea that revives the idea of mate.'} Rd5 (39... Rg6 $2 40. g5 {and Black must play the rest of the game without king or rook}) 40. f4 {Matanovic,Aleksandar: '+-'} Rd2+ 41. Kg3 Rg2+ 42. Kh4 $1 {[%cal Gh4g5]} Rd2 43. f5 Bd5 44. Kg5 Rd1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Despite a bit of his slopiness, Black's position looks solid. His pawns are easily protected and 45.Rh3 means nothing after ...Ke8'} 45. Rc3 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Re1 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Black also perishes quickly with} 45... Bc4 $2 46. Rh3 Ke8 47. Rh8+ Kd7 48. Rd8+) (45... Ke8 {Black loses a pawn on} 46. Rc5 {. Then the win isn't easy} Kd7 47. Rxb5 Kc6 48. Rb4 Bb3 49. Kh6 {followed by Kg7 and g4-g5-g6 or an exchange sacrifice on f7 - will suffice}) 46. Rh3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (46. Rd3 Bc4 47. Rd8+ Re8) 46... Ke8 47. Rd3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'the bishop can'tmove because of 48.Rd8#'} 1-0

