{Maric,R: 'Inf 4/336' Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer was justly proud of his 6 hour,12 minute marathon with Leonid Stein. His opponent was both the reigning Soviet Champion and the winner of the recent Alekhine Memorial Tournament at Moscow - to which Fischer, like other Westerners, was not invited. When Fischer was about to bolt the Interzonal, Bjelica asked him who would be left to face Petrosian in 1969. Fischer replied as if there was no one worthy : "I beat Stein here and Stein was the winner of the Moscow tournament. I've proved I'm the best."' Andrew Soltis: 'In this violent clash of wills between the American and Soviet champions, Fischer's prosecution of the attack is crowned by a brilliant bishop sacrifice. Stein declines, carrying the struggle into the endgame. Voted best game of 1967 by the jury of the prestigious CHESS INFORMANT. Fischer was justly proud of his 6 hour,12 minute marathon with Leonid Stein. His opponent was both the reigning Soviet Champion and the winner of the recent Alekhine Memorial Tournament at Moscow - to which Fischer, like other Westerners, was not invited. When Fischer was about to bolt the Interzonal, Bjelica asked him who would be left to face Petrosian in 1969. Fischer replied as if there was no one worthy : "I beat Stein here and Stein was the winner of the Moscow tournament. I've proved I'm the best."' Es ist vielleicht ungerecht, dass Leonid Stein in unserer Partiensammlung nur mit einer Verlustpartie vertreten ist, ausgerechnet auch noch in dem Jahr, in welchem er seinen vielleicht größten Triumph feiern konnte (Sieg beim Superturnier in Moskau) Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: 'Es ist vielleicht ungerecht, dass Leonid Stein in unserer Partiensammlung nur mit einer Verlustpartie vertreten ist, ausgerechnet auch noch in dem Jahr, in welchem er seinen vielleicht größten Triumph feiern konnte (Sieg beim Superturnier in Moskau)''} {'ChessBase: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer was justly proud of his 6 hour,12 minute marathon with Leonid Stein. His opponent was both the reigning Soviet Champion and the winner of the recent Alekhine Memorial Tournament at Moscow - to which Fischer, like other Westerners, was not invited. When Fischer was about to bolt the Interzonal, Bjelica asked him who would be left to face Petrosian in 1969. Fischer replied as if there was no one worthy : "I beat Stein here and Stein was the winner of the Moscow tournament. I've proved I'm the best."'''} 1. e4 {Maric,Rudolf: 'Ftacnik,L' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase ChessBase ChessBase: 'Andrew Soltis''} e5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Garry Kasparov: 'In my view, Stein's avoidance of his usual Sicilian Defence, of his favourite Accelerated Dragon, is a first, moral concession to the opponent.''} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Stein, a devout Sicilophile, defended the Lopez rarely. Here he uses a new four-move maneuver of the problem knight to reinforce e5 and prepare ...d5 or ...c5.' Andrew Soltis: 'The attempt to dispense with this prophylactic move (which prepares d4 and prevents the pin on g4) has not fared too well. Black achieved easy equality in Fischer-Korchnoi, Stockholm 1962 after 9. d4 Bg4 10. Be3 exd4 11. cxd4 Na5 12. Bc2 Nc4 13. Bc1 c5 14. b3 Na5 15. d5 Nd7 16. Nbd2 Bf6. Stein, a devout Sicilophile, defended the Lopez rarely. Here he uses a new four-move maneuver of the problem knight to reinforce e5 and prepare ...d5 or ...c5.' 'ChessBase: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Stein, a devout Sicilophile, defended the Lopez rarely. Here he uses a new four-move maneuver of the problem knight to reinforce e5 and prepare ...d5 or ...c5.'''} Bb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Garry Kasparov: 'Rare for that time.''} ({Earlier Stein had employed} 9... Nd7) ({but} 9... Na5) (9... h6) ({or} 9... Nb8 {were the main continuations.}) 10. d4 Na5 (10... exd4 11. cxd4 d5 12. e5 Ne4 13. Nc3 Na5 14. Bc2 f5 15. exf6 Bxf6 $1 $13 16. Nxe4 {Ftacnik,L} dxe4 17. Bxe4 Bxe4 18. Rxe4 c5 19. d5 $16) ({The time of the Zaitsev Variation –} 10... Re8 $1 {– had not yet arrived.}) 11. Bc2 Nc4 {Maric,R: 'N' Maric,Rudolf: 'N' Andrew Soltis: 'Garry Kasparov: 'A very intricate plan: Black wants to take his opponent away from the well-trodden paths. "Believe it or not, this knight is headed for d7!"  (Fischer)'' Die mit 9...b7 eingeleitete Variante wird nicht oft gespielt, vielleicht wirkte diese Partie zu abschreckend. Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: 'Die mit 9...b7 eingeleitete Variante wird nicht oft gespielt, vielleicht wirkte diese Partie zu abschreckend.''} (11... exd4 {Ftacnik,L} 12. cxd4 d5 (12... c5 13. Nbd2 $16) 13. e5 Ne4) ({Andrew Soltis: 'This manoeuvre is perhaps more appropriate with the centre closed:'} 11... c5 {Ftacnik,L Andrew Soltis: '!?'} 12. Nbd2 (12. d5 Nc4 {. In the 1960s this was played by Antoshin, and in the late 1980s by Romanishin. After} 13. a4 Nb6 14. b3 (14. Qe2 Nxa4 15. Bxa4 bxa4 {is unclear, Shirov-Grischuk, 3rd matchgame, New Delhi 2000}) 14... Bc8 15. Qe2 $1 (15. Be3 bxa4 $1 16. bxa4 Nc4 {with equality, Anand-Romanishin, 2nd and 4th matchgames, New York 1994}) 15... Bd7 16. c4 b4 17. Nbd2 g6 18. Nf1 Nh5 19. g4 Nf4 $6 ({but} 19... Ng7 $5 {is better, with the idea of ...f7-f6, ...Rf7, ...Bf8, ...Kh8 and ...Nc8-e7-g8 – a regrouping in the spirit of Chigorin and Rubinstein}) 20. Bxf4 exf4 21. Qd2 Bf6 22. e5 $1 dxe5 23. Nxe5 {with equality, Anand-Romanishin, 2nd and 4th matchgames, New York 1994.}) 12... cxd4 $1 13. cxd4 exd4 14. Nxd4 Re8) 12. b3 Nb6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black's pawn is protected because of ... Rd1+ (13.dxe5 dxe5 14.Qxd8 Raxd8 15.Nxe5 Nxe4!)' 'ChessBase: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Black's pawn is protected because of ...Rd1+ (13.dxe5 dxe5 14.Qxd8 Raxd8 15.Nxe5 Nxe4!)'''} 13. Nbd2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'If'} 13. dxe5 {Andrew Soltis: '?!'} dxe5 14. Qxd8 Raxd8 15. Nxe5 {Andrew Soltis: ', then'} Nxe4 {Maric,Rudolf: 'Ftacnik,L: '=!' '=!'' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: '.'} 16. Bxe4 Bxe4 $11) 13... Nbd7 {Andrew Soltis: 'More active is 13...exd4 14. cxd4 c5.' Andrew Soltis: 'More active is 13...exd4 14. cxd4 c5. Garry Kasparov: 'An inferior version of the Breyer Variation has arisen: White has already advanced his b-pawn, but Black has not yet played ...Re8.''} (13... Re8 {Ftacnik,L: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase'} 14. Bb2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Garry Kasparov: ', since Fischer's recommendation'' Fischer gab} 14. dxe5 dxe5 15. Nxe5 {an, aber Schwarz spielt} Bc5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Garry Kasparov: '!?'' (statt 15...d6 - Fischer) und hat keine Probleme.} (15... Bd6 16. Nef3 Bxe4 (16... Nxe4 17. Nxe4 Bxe4 18. Bg5 $1 $16) 17. Nxe4 Nxe4 18. Qd3 $1 $36 {Andrew Soltis: '"with initiative" is dubious in view of'} (18. Bxe4 $2 Rxe4 19. Rxe4 $4 Bh2+ $19) 18... f5 {with equality.}) 16. Nd3 Nxe4 $1 {Unzicker-Gligoric, Siegen Olympiad 1970}) ({Andrew Soltis: '. The typical'} 14. Nf1 $2 $10 {Stein,L-Lutikov,A Moscow 1966 Andrew Soltis: 'is probably best – Fischer writes that in Stein-Lutikov (Moscow 1966) "Black equalized easily", but after'} h6 $6 ({it is better to play} 14... c5 {, and if} 15. d5 {, then} c4 $1 {with a complicated struggle, Balashov-Klovans, Odessa 1974}) 15. Ng3 Bf8 16. a4 $1 bxa4 17. bxa4 a5 18. Bd3 g6 19. Qc2 Nfd7 20. Be3 Ba6 21. Bxa6 Rxa6 {Stein could have retained an advantage with} 22. d5 $1 {. Stein's play between the 9th and 13th moves is typical of the level of opening theory at that time, when there existed a rather light-hearted approach to the choice of variation for a forthcoming game: the main thing was to avoid routine, and then let's see what happens!}) (14. Z0) 14... Bf8 15. c4 $14) (13... Nfd7 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase' ist eine Spezialität von Ibragimov, der damit eine ausgezeichnete Bilanz hat.}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'The "more active"'} 13... exd4 {Ftacnik,L} 14. cxd4 c5 {Andrew Soltis: '(Fischer) would also have failed to equalize after'} 15. Bb2 {.}) 14. b4 $1 {Maric,Rudolf: 'Ftacnik,L: ' b2, c3-c4'' Andrew Soltis: 'In an extensive study of Fischer's openings, prepared by an elite Soviet team in 1971, this game was cited as a case of how "Fischer metes out punishment for fanciful opening play designed merely to avoid the beaten path." White's move eyes 15 (14. ...a5 15.Nb3!) and stops 14. ...c5 15.bxc5 dxc5 16.dxe5' Andrew Soltis: 'Thwarts c5 and prepares a dominating buildup by 15. Bb2 followed by c4. In an extensive study of Fischer's openings, prepared by an elite Soviet team in 1971, this game was cited as a case of how "Fischer metes out punishment for fanciful opening play designed merely to avoid the beaten path." White's move eyes 15 (14. ...a5 15.Nb3!) and stops 14. ...c5 15.bxc5 dxc5 16.dxe5' Andrew Soltis: 'Thwarts c5 and prepares a dominating buildup by 15. Bb2 followed by c4. In an extensive study of Fischer's openings, prepared by an elite Soviet team in 1971, this game was cited as a case of how "Fischer metes out punishment for fanciful opening play designed merely to avoid the beaten path." White's move eyes 15 (14. ...a5 15.Nb3!) and stops 14. ...c5 15.bxc5 dxc5 16.dxe5 Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'A novelty!'' 'ChessBase: 'Andrew Soltis: 'In an extensive study of Fischer's openings, prepared by an elite Soviet team in 1971, this game was cited as a case of how "Fischer metes out punishment for fanciful opening play designed merely to avoid the beaten path." White's move eyes 15 (14. ...a5 15.Nb3!) and stops 14. ...c5 15.bxc5 dxc5 16.dxe5''' Fischers Neuerung, die sich gegen c7-c5 stellt. Andrew Soltis: 'ChessBase: 'Fischers Neuerung, die sich gegen c7-c5 stellt.''} (14. Bb2 $10 {(Ftacnik,L) Keres,P-Gligoric,S Zürich 1959} c5 15. Nf1 Re8 16. a4 Bf8 17. Ng3 Qc7 18. Qd3 $6 (18. d5 $1) 18... c4 (18... d5 {with equality}) 19. bxc4 bxc4 20. Qd2 {, and here} d5 $1 {would have given Black excellent play. This game would certainly have been known to Fischer and, not having any great liking for set-ups with d4-d5, he found a way to engage in an open battle – roughly as he did five years later in the 10th game of his match with Spassky (G