{Andrew Soltis: 'This completes a trilogy begun with Games 68 and 69. In each case, Fischer secured an early advantage in space and nursed it into the middlegame while his opponent seemed preoccupied. Here the space edge is converted to a winning initiatve by move 20.'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis Bangiev Bangiev} e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 Ne7 $6 $146 {Andrew Soltis: 'This idea of Steinitz's allows Black to decide later between ...d5 and ...Ng6'} (3... Nf6 4. Nc3 {Andrew Soltis} c6 {and 5. ...d5, e.g.} 5. Bb3 d5 6. exd5 cxd5 7. d4 Bd6 8. Nge2 O-O 9. O-O ({but} 9. Bxf4 Bxf4 10. Nxf4 Re8+ 11. Nfe2 {is the acid test. How this came to light was typically bizarre : In 1964 Fischer was given carte blanche to write articles for Chess Life. He decided to use the opportunity to analyze the forgotten - deservedly so - games of an 1862 match between Steinitz and the obscure Italian master Serafino Dubois. IN one of the games featurng a Bishop's Gambit, Fischer continued with the analysis from} Ng4 12. Nxd5 Be6 13. h3 Bxd5 14. hxg4 Bxg2 15. Rh2 Bf3 16. Qd3 {- working out to mate in one line - and concluded White was much better. Computers disagree - and grandmasters have virtually ignored 9.Bxf4 so Fischer's analysis remains almost untested after 40 years.}) 9... g5 10. Nxd5 Nc6) (3... Qh4+) (3... Nc6) (3... d5) (3... f5) 4. Nc3 c6 5. Nf3 d5 6. Bb3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'The drawback to 3. ...Ne7 is the lack of pressure on e4. On 6. ...d4 7.Ne2 c5 8.Ng5 and Black is lost.'} dxe4 7. Nxe4 $16 Nd5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black could justify his third move with'} 7... Ng6 $2 {Andrew Soltis: '- and be crushed by'} 8. Neg5 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qe7+ {Andrew Soltis} 9. Kf2) 8. Qe2 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (8. c4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'more subtle than'} Nf6 9. Nxf6+ Qxf6 10. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'after which 10 ...Bd6 or 10. ...c5 is unclear'} Be7 11. d4 Nd7 12. Qe2 $1 g5 $2 {Andrew Soltis: 'allows'} 13. Nxg5 $1 Qxg5 14. Bxf4 $18 {Andrew Soltis: 'with a powerful attack'}) 8... Be7 9. c4 Nc7 ({putting the knight offsides} 9... Nb4 {(Andrew Soltis) , is playing with fire. Particularly since} 10. d4 Bg4 11. Bxf4 Bxf3 12. gxf3 Qxd4 {loses to the pretty} 13. Rd1 Qb6 14. Nd6+ Kf8 15. Nc8 $1) 10. d4 O-O $6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'One of the best things about the King's Gambit is psychological : Defenders look at White's short-term tactical threats and Black's extra pawn - but underestimate White's space edge. Here Black should fight for operating room with'} 10... Bg4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'andthen'} 11. c5 $1 O-O 12. Bxf4 Ne6 13. Bxe6 fxe6 $1 14. Bd6 $11 Bxd6 {Andrew Soltis} 15. Nxd6 Bxf3 16. gxf3 Qh4+ 17. Qf2 Qf4 {In this, and other unplayed variations, Minic fails to appreciate how safe Black is after ...fxe6}) 11. Bxf4 Ne6 12. Be3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black can minimize the damage with 12. ...Nd7 and 13. ...Nf6 but White's advantage is distinct after 13.O-O Nf6 14.Nxf6+ (or 14.Ng3 and Nf5) Bxf6 15.d5!'} Bb4+ $6 (12... Nd7) 13. Kf2 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'White wants to embarass the bishop with c4-c5 and Qc4.'} (13. Nc3) 13... Nd7 14. c5 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (14. Rhf1 $1 $16) 14... Nf6 15. Nxf6+ Qxf6 16. Rhf1 Nf4 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Much better than'} 16... Re8 $2 17. Qc4 $1 Ba5 18. Kg1 $1 $16) 17. Bxf4 Qxf4 18. g3 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'The only bid for advantage. For example'} 18. Kg1 Bg4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'intending ...Bxf3 or ...Rae8('} 19. Qe5 Qxe5 20. Nxe5 Be6 $11 {Andrew Soltis: 'is roughly equal.'}) 18... Qh6 ({White carried out his idea after} 18... Qf6 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Kg2 Bg4 20. Qc4 $1 {threatening Ne5 as well as Qxb4. Black can avoid material loss by} Qe6 21. Qxb4 Qe2+ 22. Rf2 Bxf3+ 23. Kg1 Qe4 24. Re1) 19. Kg1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'The idea of an Exchange sacrifice mus thave occurred to White when he examined'} 19. Qe7 $5 Qf6 $1 $14 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (19... Bh3 20. Kg1 $1 Bxf1 21. Rxf1 $16 Qf6 {Andrew Soltis} 22. Qxb7 {gives him great compensation. But on furhter reflection, 19.Qe7 disapoints since WHite's edge is relatively minor after}) 20. Qxf6 {Andrew Soltis} gxf6 21. a3 Ba5 22. d5 $1) 19... Bh3 $4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (19... Be6 $1 $11 20. Bxe6 {Andrew Soltis} Qxe6 (20... fxe6 21. Ne5 Qd2 {is only a slight edge for White}) 21. Qxe6 fxe6 22. a3 Ba5 23. Ng5 Rfe8) ({Missing the point of White's move, just as} 19... Bg4 {Andrew Soltis} 20. Qe7 Rae8 21. Bxf7+ {would}) 20. Ne5 $1 $18 {Bangiev: '!' '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Now it's too late to clog the b3-f7 diagonal. 20. ...Be6 21.Nxf7! Bxf7 22.Rxf7 Rxf7 23.Rf1 or 20. ...Bd2 21.Nxf7 Qe3+ 22.Rf2! and wins'} Bxf1 (20... Be6 21. Nxf7 $1 Bxf7 22. Rxf7 Rxf7 23. Rf1 Raf8 $2 24. Rxf7 Rxf7 25. Qe8# {#}) 21. Rxf1 {Bangiev: '' ''} Bd2 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Bangiev: 'e3'} (21... g6 22. Nxf7 Qg7 23. Nd8+ $1 Kh8 24. Rxf8+ {Andrew Soltis: 'followed by mating checks at'} Qxf8 25. Qe5+ Qg7 26. Qe8+ $18 Qg8 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Qxg8#) (21... Qd2 {Bangiev} 22. Qe4 $18 Kh8 {Andrew Soltis} 23. Nxf7+ Rxf7 24. Rxf7 Rd8 25. Qe5 Rg8 (25... Qe1+ 26. Qxe1 Bxe1 27. Rxb7 Bd2 28. Kf2 g6 29. d5 $1) 26. Qxg7+ Rxg7 27. Rf8+ Rg8 28. Rxg8#) 22. Rf3 $1 ({Black weathers the worst of the storm after} 22. Nxf7 {Andrew Soltis} Qe3+ 23. Rf2 Qxe2 24. Rxe2 Bc1 {although White can still play for a win with} 25. Ne5+ Kh8 26. d5) 22... Rad8 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A superior defense is'} 22... Kh8 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'But White is close to a win after'} 23. Nxf7+ (23. Rxf7 {Andrew Soltis} Qe3+) (23. Bxf7 {Andrew Soltis} Bc1 {don't convince.}) 23... Rxf7 24. Bxf7 {Bangiev: '' '' Andrew Soltis: 'threatening 25. Be8! and 24. ...Bg5 25.d5!'} Bg5 25. d5) ({again Black is mated after} 22... g6 {Andrew Soltis} 23. Nxf7 Qg7 24. Nd8+ $1) 23. Nxf7 Rxf7 24. Qe7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'It's hopeless after 24. ...Rf8 25.Bxf7+ or 25.Rxf7 Qe3+ 26.Rf2+'} 1-0

