{Andrew Soltis: 'Of all the gaps in the colletions of Fischer "complete" games, the most glaring is the three final games from his 1958 match with Milan Matulovic. Fischer, preparing for the Interzonal at Portoroz, had arranged matches with Dragoljub Janosevic (two games, both draws) and Matulovic. But only one of the Matulovic games, a 41-move Fischer loss with Black, has surfaced. The scores of Fischer's two wins and the drawn fourth game have disappeared. None of their other games in memorable - except this.'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. g3 e5 7. Nde2 Be7 8. Bg5 $5 $146 {Andrew Soltis: 'A logical move designed to reinforce d5. The idea fails in similar positions because of the Nxe4 desperado. For example 6.Be2 e5 7.Nb3 Be7 8.Bg5 O-O and now 9.Nd2 Nxe4! 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 was equal in Fischer-Ghitescu Leipzig 1960'} Nbd7 (8... Be6) ({Black can still play} 8... Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis} 9. Nxe4 Bxg5 10. Nxd6+ Ke7 11. Nxc8+ {offers slim winning chances.}) 9. Bh3 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Matulovic had some success exploiting the good knight versus bad bishop after 9. ...Nb6 10.Bxc8 Rxc8 11.Bxf6 Bxf6 12.a4!'} b5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'A novelty. Now 10.Bf6 Nxf6 11.Bxc8 Rxc8 gives Black quick queenside pressure that more than makes up for his Bishop.'} (9... O-O $5 10. a4 $1 $14) 10. a4 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (10. Bxd7+ Qxd7 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. Nd5 Bd8 $13) ({White falls into a psychological trap of his own making. He went into the game thinking that 9. Bh3 was good, then saw that passive moves such as} 10. a3 {(Andrew Soltis) would give Black a comfortable game. Therefore, the position seemed to call for a vigorous response to 9. ...b5}) 10... b4 11. Nd5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'the positional recipe of'} 11. Bxd7+ $6 {Andrew Soltis: 'followed by 12.Bxf6 and 13. Nd5 turns out badly after'} Qxd7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 12. Bxf6 bxc3 $1 13. Bxg7 $2 Rg8 14. Bh6 Qh3 $1 15. Be3 (15. Bc1 {Andrew Soltis} Qg2) 15... cxb2 16. Rb1 Qg2 17. Rg1 Qxe4 18. Rxb2 Be6 $17) 11... Nxd5 12. Qxd5 Rb8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Black's edge after} 12... Nb6 {Andrew Soltis} 13. Qc6+ Bd7 14. Bxd7+ Nxd7 15. Bxe7 Kxe7 {is not nearly as great s in the game.}) 13. Bxe7 Kxe7 $1 {[ f6] Andrew Soltis: '!!'} ({By providing extra protection to d6, Black is ready to start punching back with 14. ...Nf6. In contrast} 13... Qxe7 {Andrew Soltis} 14. Rd1 Rb6 15. a5 Rb5 16. Qxd6 Qxd6 17. Rxd6 {can only favor White}) 14. Qd2 Nf6 15. Bg2 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'White should swap bishops, although Black has a slight edge due to his dominance of dark squares after'} 15. Bxc8 Qxc8 16. f3 Qc5 $1 $15) 15... Bb7 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Better was'} 15... Qb6 $1 $17 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'seizing the board's best diagonal'}) 16. Qd3 $2 (16. Qe3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Which is why White should have played'} Qc7 17. O-O) 16... Qb6 $17 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 17. O-O ({Had White anticipated this with} 17. a5 {Andrew Soltis} Qc5 18. O-O {Black would still hold an edge ,but not as great, following 18. .. .Rhc8 and ...Bc6}) 17... a5 $1 18. Rfd1 Ba6 19. Qd2 Rhc8 20. h3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'A grandmaster's instinct tells him 20. ...Rc4, followed by overwhelming the c-file, should be strong 21.b3 Nxe4!'} h5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'But it was more important to stop 21.g4 and Ng3. For example'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'The point is that White's position is so bad that he doesn't deserve chances like that, or'} 20... Qc6 $2 21. Nd4 $3 $11 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'which Fischer claimed was equal, presumably because of complications like'} exd4 {Andrew Soltis} 22. e5 Nd5 23. exd6+ Kf8 24. Qf4 Bb7 25. Rxd4 Qxc2 26. d7) (20... Rc4 $6 21. g4 $1 Nxe4 $6 22. Bxe4 Rxe4 23. Ng3 $11 {Andrew Soltis: 'with compensation.'}) 21. b3 $2 (21. Rac1) ({White should insist on} 21. g4 {(Andrew Soltis) and then} hxg4 22. Ng3 gxh3 23. Bxh3 Rc4 24. Qg5 {with such ideas as} Kf8 25. Rxd6 $1 (25. Nf5 Qxf2+ 26. Kxf2 Nxe4+) 25... Qxd6 26. Nf5 {. Black may still be winning, but he can retain his huge positional edge without risk by means of 21. ...h4!}) 21... Bxe2 $1 22. Qxe2 Rc3 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'White can't save all the children with 23.Kh2 Rbc8 24.Rd2 Qc5 25.Rc1? Rxb3 or 25.Ra2 g6 as in the game and 25.Rad1 Rxc2 26.f4 h4 leads nowhere'} 23. Rd3 Rbc8 24. Rxc3 ({Another desperate lunge was} 24. Rad1 {Andrew Soltis} Rxc2 25. Qf3 R8c3 26. g4 {. Then Black can win through caution} hxg4 ({or by hunting down more pawns} 26... Rb2) (26... Rxd3 27. Rxd3 Rc1+ 28. Kh2 Re1) 27. hxg4 g6) 24... Rxc3 {Andrew Soltis: '"White can almost resign on the spot", wrote Gligoric. The threat of 25. ...Rxg3 denies him time to activate his rook.'} 25. Kh2 Qc5 $1 26. Ra2 (26. Rc1 $2 Rxb3 $1) 26... g6 $1 $19 {[ d7-b8-c6-d4] Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Another high-class safety move, that grants Black time for ...Nd7-b8-c6-d4, among other winning ideas.'} 27. Bf1 Qd4 28. f3 $2 ({Feeble surrender of e3. But after} 28. Bg2 {Andrew Soltis} h4 {Black wins by exploiting f4} 29. gxh4 (29. g4 Nh7 {and . ..Ng5-e6-f4.}) 29... Nh5) 28... Re3 $1 29. Qg2 Qd1 $1 30. Bc4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'On'} 30. Bd3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black should insert'} g5 $1 (30... h4 $1 31. gxh4 {Andrew Soltis} Qxf3 32. Qxf3 Rxf3 {because then the e-pawn is doomed})) 30... Qxf3 31. Qxf3 Rxf3 {Andrew Soltis: 'White played to adjournment'} 32. Kg2 Re3 33. Bd3 Nxe4 34. Bxe4 Rxe4 35. Kf2 d5 36. Ra1 d4 37. Rd1 Re3 38. h4 Rc3 39. Rd2 Ke6 (39... Kd6 {Andrew Soltis} 40. Kg2 f5 {then waited a few days before resigning. 0-1 (40) Matulovic,M-Fischer,R Vinkovci 1968 [Andrew Soltis]}) 40. Kg2 f5 0-1

