{Andrew Soltis: 'Three months after Netanya, Fischer won another, considerably stronger, tournament in eastern Croatia. The race for first prize was virtually over after a little more than a week, since Fischer began 81/2-1/2. His best game was this gem against a little known master in the ninth round.'} 1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. g3 e5 4. Bg2 d6 5. e3 $6 (5. Nf3 $1 f5 6. d3 $14) ({Black can play his usual King's Indian setup after 5.e3 or meet} 5. e4 {with} f5 {and then} 6. exf5 gxf5 7. Qh5+ Kf8 {as Fischer once tried v. Donald Byrne}) 5... Nf6 6. Nge2 O-O 7. O-O c6 8. d4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Here'} 8. d3 {Andrew Soltis: 'would transpose into a position Fischer knew well (from the King's Indian Reversed). But instead of'} Re8 (8... d5 {, which creates the mirror image of games 56 and 84, Fischer recommended}) 9. e4 a6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'If White then stops 10. ...b5 with'} 10. a4 {Andrew Soltis: ', Black equalizes by securing control of b4 and c5 with'} a5 $1 $11 {Andrew Soltis: '!'}) 8... Re8 $1 9. Rb1 $2 (9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8 Rxd8 $15 {Andrew Soltis: 'is drawish, although Fischer thought it favored Black '=''}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer claimed in the Informant that'} 9. Qc2 Qe7 (9... a5 $5 $146 10. a3 (10. Rd1 Qc7 11. c5 dxc5 12. dxc5 Na6 13. Na4 Nb4 $13) 10... Na6 11. Rd1 Qc7 12. Bd2 (12. d5 $5) 12... Bg4 13. f3 Bd7 $13) 10. e4 {Andrew Soltis: 'can be met by'} exd4 11. Nxd4 Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 12. Nxe4 Bxd4 13. Rd1 Be5 $1 14. f4 Bg7 15. Nxd6 Bg4 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'with a slight edge. But sometimes the theoretically best moves simplify so much that they leave few chances to win against a good defender. THat explains why when Karl Burger played 10. e4 in the 1965 U.S. Championship, Fischer played 10. ...Nbd7 11.d5 a5 and eventually won by invading along the h-file.'} 16. Nxe8 Bxd1 $15 {Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer'}) (9. b4 e4 10. b5 Bf5 11. a4 Nbd7 12. Ba3 c5 13. Qb3 h5 14. Rad1 {Spiridonov-Matulovic/Athen/1969}) 9... e4 $1 $17 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 10. b4 (10. f3 $5) 10... Bf5 $1 11. h3 h5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'A familiar plan. Bisguier-Fischer, Western Open 1963 - a game best known for Fischer's falling asleep at the board and being woken by his opponent - went 1. d4 Nf6 2.c4 g6 3.Nc3 Bg7 4.Nf3 O-O 5.e3 d6 6.Be2 Nbd7 7.O-O e5 8.b4 Re8 9.Bb2 e4 10.Nd2 Nf8 11.Qc2 Bf5 12.d5 h5 13.Nb5 h4 with a promising attack.'} 12. Nf4 Nbd7 13. a4 Nf8 14. c5 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'This is a serious midreading of the situation. White may have wanted to avoid'} 14. b5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: ''''} c5 {but lockign the c-file and center fatally slows his counterplay.}) 14... d5 {Andrew Soltis: ''''} 15. b5 N8h7 16. Bd2 Ng5 {Andrew Soltis: 'White can open a file at will and can try for a second with a4-a5-a6. Black, on the other hand, must rely on sacrifices to open kingside lines. But his chances are far superior for the simple fact that mate ends the game. His options include : (a) a sacrifice on f3, e.g. 17.a5 Nf3+! 18.Bxf3 exf3 etc. (b) a sacrifice on d4, that is moving the knight from f6 and playing for ...Bxd4, . ..e3, or (c) a sacrifice on g4.'} 17. Rb2 (17. a5 $5 Nf3+ $1 18. Bxf3 (18. Kh1 g5 (18... Nxd2 19. Qxd2 Qxa5 $17 {Soloviev}) 19. Nxh5 Nxh5 20. g4 Bg6 21. bxc6 bxc6 22. gxh5 Bxh5 $17 {PS}) 18... exf3 19. Ra1 g5 $17 {Soloviev}) 17... Qd7 18. Kh2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer gave'} 18. h4 $6 {Andrew Soltis: ''!''} Bg4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'as winning after'} 19. hxg5 {Andrew Soltis: ''!?''} (19. bxc6 bxc6 20. Qc2 Nf3+ $17 {PS}) 19... Bxd1 20. gxf6 Bf3 {Andrew Soltis: ''!''} 21. fxg7 {Andrew Soltis: 'and now'} g5 $1 22. Nfe2 (22. Nh3 Qg4 23. Rfb1 h4) 22... Qg4 23. Re1 h4 $19 {Andrew Soltis: ''-+' Soloviev'}) 18... Bh6 19. a5 Bg4 $3 ({In Boy's Life, Fischer said he spent a good deal of time weighing the merits of three candidate moves} 19... h4) (19... Nf3+ {and 19. ...Bg4. But the pawn move is not forcing enough. A bit better is} 20. Bxf3 exf3 21. Qxf3 Bxf4 22. gxf4 cxb5 {although it is not clear how Black makes progress after} 23. Qg3 a6) 20. hxg4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'White will almost be defenseless on the kingside after'} 20. Qb3 Nf3+ 21. Bxf3 (21. Kh1 Bxf4 22. exf4 (22. hxg4 Bg5 23. a6 hxg4 24. axb7 Qxb7 25. Qa4 Kg7 $19 {PS}) 22... Bxh3 23. bxc6 bxc6 24. Qb7 Qg4 25. Qxc6 Bxg2+ 26. Kxg2 Nh4+ 27. Kh2 Qf3 28. Rg1 Ng4+ 29. Kh3 Nxf2+ 30. Kh2 Qg2+ $1 31. Rxg2 Nf3#) 21... Bxf3 {Andrew Soltis: '. For example'} 22. bxc6 bxc6 23. Qb7 Qf5 24. Qxc6 Bxf4 {Andrew Soltis: '. Fischer gave the remarkable finish of'} 25. gxf4 (25. exf4 Ng4+ 26. Kg1 Nxf2 $1 (26... Ne5 $19 {Soloviev} 27. g4 $1 Bxg4 (27... Nxg4 28. Nxd5 Rac8 29. Qb7 e3 30. Ne7+ Rxe7 31. Qxf3 Nxf2 32. Bxe3 Nxh3+ 33. Kg2 Rce8 34. Bc1 Re1 $13) (27... hxg4 28. fxe5 Qh5 29. e6 fxe6 30. Qd6 Qxh3 31. Qh2) (27... Nxc6 28. gxf5 gxf5 29. Nxd5 Kh8 30. Ne3 Rg8+ 31. Kh2 Rg6 32. d5 Rag8 33. h4 Rg2+ 34. Nxg2 Rxg2+ 35. Kh3 Rg7 36. Bc3 Bg2+ 37. Kh2 $18) 28. dxe5 (28. hxg4 Qxg4+ 29. Kh2 Nf3+ 30. Kh1 Qh3#) 28... Bxh3 (28... Bf3 29. Kh2) 29. f3 Bxf1 (29... g5 30. Nxd5 exf3 31. Ne7+ Rxe7 32. Qxa8+ Kh7 33. Qxf3 g4 34. Qa8 $18) 30. Nxd5 (30. Kxf1 Qh3+ 31. Ke1 Qh1+ 32. Kf2 Qxf3+) 30... Qh3 31. Nf6+ Kh8 (31... Kg7 32. Nxe8+ Rxe8 33. Be1 (33. Qf6+ Kg8 34. Be1 Bd3 $17) 33... exf3 {[%cal Rf1e2]} 34. Rh2 Qg4+ 35. Kxf1 Qxf4 36. Qf6+ Qxf6 37. exf6+ Kxf6 $13) 32. Be1 Rec8 (32... Reb8 33. Nd7 $13) 33. Qd5 Kg7 (33... e3 34. Qxf7 e2 35. Qh7#) 34. Nxe4 Ba6 $36 {PS}) 27. Kxf2 Qxh3 {[%cal Re4e3]} 28. Rfb1 Qg2+ 29. Ke3 Bg4 30. Be1 Qf3+ 31. Kd2 e3+ 32. Kc2 Bf5+ 33. Kc1 Bxb1 (33... e2 34. Nxd5 Kg7 35. Rb3 Qh1 36. Kd2 $16) 34. Kxb1 Rad8 $13 (34... Qf1 35. Re2)) 25... Ng4+ 26. Kg3 h4+ 27. Kxh4 Kg7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 28. Qxd5 Rh8+ 29. Kg3 Rxh3+ 30. Kxh3 Ne5+ $19) 20... hxg4 $19 {Andrew Soltis: ''-+''} 21. Rh1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'it should be no surprise that the convoluted defense'} 21. Bh1 $5 Nf3+ 22. Kg2 {Andrew Soltis: 'fails'} Bxf4 23. gxf4 (23. exf4 Qf5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and ...Qh5. e.g.'} 24. Rg1 Qh5 25. Kf1 Qh2 26. Bxf3 exf3 27. Be3 Rxe3 $1 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 28. fxe3 Qxb2 $19 {Soloviev}) 23... Kg7 24. Rg1 Rh8 25. Kf1 (25. Ne2 g3 26. fxg3 Qh3+ 27. Kf2 Ng4# {PS}) 25... Nxg1 26. Kxg1 Rxh1+ 27. Kxh1 g3 28. Qf1 Rh8+ 29. Kg1 Ng4 $1 30. fxg3 Nh2 $19 31. Qg2 Nf3+ 32. Kf1 Rh2 $19) (21. a6 Nf3+ 22. Bxf3 gxf3 23. axb7 Rab8 (23... Qxb7 24. Rh1 Bxf4 25. exf4 Kg7 26. Kg1 Rh8 27. Rxh8 Rxh8 28. Qf1 Ng4 29. f5 Rh2 30. Nd1 Rg2+ 31. Qxg2 fxg2 $17) 24. bxc6 Qg4 25. Rh1 (25. Nh3 Kg7 26. c7 Bxe3 27. c8=Q Rexc8 28. bxc8=Q Rxc8 $19) 25... Bxf4 26. exf4 Kg7 27. Kg1 Rh8 $19 {PS}) 21... Nf3+ 22. Bxf3 gxf3 23. Kg1 (23. Nh3 $5 Kg7 24. Kg1 Rh8 25. Qf1 (25. a6 Bxe3 $1 $19 {Soloviev} 26. Bxe3 Rxh3 27. Rxh3 Qxh3 28. Qf1 Qh5 29. Bg5 Qxg5 30. axb7 Rh8 31. b8=Q Qh6 32. Qxh8+ Qxh8 33. bxc6 Ng4 $19 {PS}) 25... a6 $1 (25... Qg4 26. a6 $132) 26. bxc6 bxc6 27. Nf4 (27. Na4 Qg4 28. Rb6 Rh7 29. Rxc6 Rah8 30. Rxf6 Kxf6 31. Nb6 Bg5 32. Nxd5+ Kg7 33. Nf6 Bxf6 34. c6 Rxh3 35. Rxh3 Rxh3 $19 {PS}) 27... Bxf4 28. Rxh8 Rxh8 29. exf4 Qc8 {and Black wins as in the game.}) 23... Bxf4 $1 ({not} 23... Kg7 {because} 24. Rxh6 $1 Kxh6 25. a6 {gives White the chances he doesnt deserve}) 24. exf4 Kg7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now 25.a6 is too slow, 25. ...Kh8 26.Rxh8 Rxh8 27.Qf1 Qg4 and ...Qh5 wins'} 25. f5 Rh8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 26. Bh6+ {Andrew Soltis: 'Desperation. Black also wins after'} (26. Rxh8 Rxh8 27. Bg5 Qxf5 28. Bh4 g5 (28... Rxh4 29. gxh4 Qh3 30. Qf1 Qg4+ 31. Kh2 Qxh4+ 32. Kg1 Qg5+ 33. Kh1 Ng4 34. a6 Qh4+ $19 {PS})) 26... Rxh6 27. Rxh6 Kxh6 28. Qd2+ ({Andrew Soltis: 'the queenside operation finally succeeds with'} 28. a6 Qxf5 29. axb7 Rh8 30. bxc6 Kg7 31. b8=Q {Andrew Soltis: 'but the patient dies after'} Rh1+ $3 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 32. Kxh1 Qh3+ 33. Kg1 Qg2#) 28... g5 29. bxc6 Qxf5 $1 30. Nd1 Qh3 31. Ne3 Kg6 {Andrew Soltis: 'White has no defense to ... Rh8'} (31... Kg6 32. cxb7 Rh8 $19) 0-1

