{Andrew Soltis: 'Miguel Quinteros, a close friend of Fischer's, liked to grab material and hold on to it with both hand. But in the following game, the Argentine GM is forced to attack and responds by offering the most audacious sacrifice ever played against Fischer. Even though the battle is lost by move 27, it has more spectacular might-have-beens than any other Fischer game.'} 1. c4 {Andrew Soltis} g6 2. Nc3 Bg7 3. Nf3 c5 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black attacks d4 before White can get his Maroczy house in order. For example, 6.Nb3 d6 7.e4 b6 8.Be2 Bxc3+ 9.bxc3 Nf6 and ...Ba6'} 6. Nc2 (6. e3 Nf6 $11) 6... Bxc3+ $1 7. bxc3 Nf6 8. f3 {Andrew Soltis: 'In the long run, Black should prevail on the queenside, after ...Qa5, ...BA6, ...Rc8 and ...Nd7-c5. But in the short run he may get mated. WHite wil play 9.e4, the knight to d5 via e3 and watch for the right time to push his f-pawn.'} d6 9. e4 Be6 10. Be2 Rc8 11. Ne3 Qa5 12. Bd2 Ne5 13. Qb3 Nfd7 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'This dooms the c-pawn (after ... Nc5 and ...Qa4). Note that 14.Qxb7 ? if refuted by Rb8'} 14. f4 (14. Qxb7 Rb8 $19) ({After} 14. O-O {(Andrew Soltis) White can meet} Nc5 {with} ({But Black does better with} 14... Qc5 $1 {threatening ...BXc4 as well as ...Nb6 e.g.} 15. Qxb7 Bxc4 $1) 15. Qb4) 14... Nc5 15. Qc2 (15. Qb5+ Nc6 $1 $19 (15... Qxb5 {Andrew Soltis} 16. cxb5 Ned3+ {drops material})) 15... Nc6 16. O-O Qa4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'White pawns fall in any endgame. 17.Qxa4 Nxa4 18.Rab1 Na5 19.Rb4 Nb6 or 19.Rb5 b6 20.f5 Bd7 21.Rd5 Nc5'} 17. Qb1 (17. Qxa4 $2 Nxa4 18. Rab1 Na5 19. Rb4 Nb6 $19) 17... Na5 {Andrew Soltis: 'If there were no kings, Black should win e.g. 18.Bd1 Qa3 19.Bb3 Naxb3 20.axb3 Qxb3 21.Rxa7 Qxb1 22.Rxb1 Nxe4'} 18. e5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({The position demands open lines by} 18. f5 {Andrew Soltis} Bxc4 19. Nxc4 Nxc4 20. Bg5 {promises little after} f6) 18... dxe5 19. fxe5 O-O ({Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 19... Nxc4 20. Nxc4 Bxc4 21. Rf4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} b5 22. Bxc4 bxc4 23. Qf1 $1 O-O (23... Nd7 {Andrew Soltis} 24. Rxf7 Nxe5 25. Rf4) 24. Rxc4 Qd7 25. Bh6) 20. Rf4 Nd7 $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Better was'} 20... Qd7 $1 { c7-+ Andrew Soltis: 'with threats of ...Qxd2 and ...Qc7xe5 but there is no Black win, as annotators claimed, after'} 21. Rd4 $1 {Andrew Soltis} Qc7 22. Qb4 b6 23. Nd5 Bxd5 24. cxd5 Qxe5 25. Bg4) 21. Nd5 $1 Rfe8 22. Qe4 b5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({In his introduction to the Russian edition of My 60 Memorable Games, Alexey Suetin recommended} 22... Nf8 {(Andrew Soltis) and gave} 23. Rh4 b5 24. Bg5 Bxd5 25. cxd5 Qxe4 26. Rxe4 a6 {as proof that Black is holding. Holding, perhaps, but 22. ...b5 is going for the win. White would be lost after, say, 23.Be3 Nxc4 24.Bd4 Nc5}) 23. Rxf7 $3 Kxf7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A terrific shot based on variations such as'} 23... Bxf7 24. e6 Nf6 ({The key to many of the lines that follow is ...Bg4. For example,} 24... bxc4 {(Andrew Soltis) allows} 25. Bg4 $1 Rcd8 26. exf7+ Kxf7 27. Rf1+ Nf6 28. Qe6+ Kg7 29. Nxf6 Rxd2 30. Nh5+ $1 {and mates}) 25. Nxf6+ exf6 26. exf7+ Kxf7 27. Qd5+ Kg7 28. Qd7+ Kh8 29. Bh6 Rg8 {Andrew Soltis: 'is faster than the'} 30. Bg7+ $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'endgame.'} (30. Qf7 {Andrew Soltis} Rc6 31. Bf3 $3)) ({Black can try to upset White's calculations by inserting} 23... Nc5 {(Andrew Soltis) . Suetin gave} 24. Qh4 Bxf7 25. Nxe7+ Rxe7 26. Qxe7 {as the crucial position. There are convincing wins after} Ne6 ({Adrian Mikhalchishin, in Shakmaty v. SSSR, offered a third idea} 26... bxc4 {. He analyzed} 27. Bg4 $1 Qe8 28. Qxa7 Ra8 29. Qxc5 Nb3 30. Qd6 {and wins.} Rd8 (30... Nxa1 31. e6 $1 Rd8 32. exf7+ Kxf7 33. Be6+) 31. Qf6 {and 32.e6}) (26... Bxc4 27. Bh6 Bf7 28. Rf1 Qxa2 29. Qf6) ({There is a fifth option.} 26... Nxc4 $1 {Black is winning after} 27. Bg4 (27. Bh6 Ne6 28. Bg4 Re8) ({White can improve with} 27. Rf1 Nxe5 28. Qxe5 {After which} Qe4 $1 29. Qf6 {should only favor him slightly}) 27... Re8 28. Qxc5 Nxd2) 27. Rf1 Rf8 ({Dvoretsky claimed} 27... Rc7 $1 {was equal}) 28. Bh6 Nc6 29. Qxe6 $1) 24. Qh4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Kg7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({It should be no surprise that Black cannot allow a capture on h7} 24... Bxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 25. Qxh7+ Ke6 26. Qxg6+ Kxe5 27. Bf4+ $1 {and mates}) ({But what about} 24... Nf8 {(Andrew Soltis) ? There are some pretty lines such as} 25. Rf1+ Bf5 26. Rxf5+ ({to justify his sacrifice, White may have to plunge into} 26. g4 Nxc4 27. gxf5 $1 (27. Bf4 Qa3 $1 {when Qc5+ wins for Black}) 27... Nxd2 {White has} 28. fxg6+ Ke6 29. Nf4+ Kd7 30. g7 $1 {and now} Ne6 (30... Ng6 31. Nxg6 {favors White}) 31. Qh3 $1 {with at least a draw}) 26... gxf5 27. Bh5+ Ke6 (27... Ng6 {puts a cloud nunder this line.}) 28. Qd4 $1 Ng6 29. Nc7+ {and mates}) 25. Nxe7 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'White's threats include 26.Qh6+ Kh8 27.Bg5 and Bf6+ or 27.Nxg6+ Kh8 28.Bd3!'} Qc2 {Andrew Soltis: '!!'} ({After} 25... Nc6 {(Andrew Soltis) he can finish off neatly with} 26. Bh6+ Kh8 27. Nxc8 Rxc8 28. Rd1 $1 g5 29. Qf2 Ncxe5 30. Rxd7 $1 Nxd7 31. Qd4+ {and mates}) ({One of the many difficult variations, cited by Mikhalchishin, runs} 25... Rxc4 {Andrew Soltis} 26. Qh6+ $1 Kh8 27. Nxg6+ Kg8 28. Bd3 Rc7 29. Rf1 $1 {with the idea of} Nc4 ({The prettiest line runs} 29... Nc6 30. Nf4 Nf8 31. Nxe6 Nxe6 32. Bxh7+ $1 Rxh7 33. Qg6+ Ng7 34. Qf7+ Kh8 35. Qf8+ $1 {and mates}) ({LEading to fireworks is} 29... Bc4 {and then} 30. Rf7 $5 ({However, White wins with} 30. e6 $1 Bxd3 31. exd7 {mates}) (30. Ne7+ Rxe7 31. Qg5+ {as pointe out by Dvoretsky.}) 30... Kxf7 $2 (30... Qd1+ 31. Rf1 Bxd3 $1 32. Rxd1 Bxg6) 31. Qxh7+ Ke6 32. Qh3+ Kf7 $1 {with a perpetual ceck - but sputters out with}) 30. Ne7+ Rxe7 31. Bxh7+ Rxh7 32. Qxe6+ Kh8 33. Qe8+) 26. Qh6+ $2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'The Informant claimed Black missed a simple win with'} 26. Nxc8 $1 Rxc8 (26... Qxd2 27. Nd6 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '. But then'} Rf8 {Andrew Soltis} 28. Qe7+ Kh8 29. Qxe6 Qe3+ 30. Kh1 Qxe2 {can only favor Black}) 27. Qe7+ Kh8 28. Bh6 Rg8 29. Qxe6 Qxe2 30. Qxd7 $18) ({Yet White does have btter than 26.Qh6+. The most promising try is} 26. Bh6+ {Andrew Soltis} Kh8 27. Nxc8 $1 {as suggested by Herman Pilnik in 64.} Qxe2 28. Nd6 g5 29. Bxg5 ({and White can improve with} 29. Bg7+ $1 {.}) 29... Rg8 30. Bf6+ Nxf6 31. Qxf6+ Rg7 32. g3 Bg8 $2 ({but Black can improve with} 32... Nxc4 {or earlier with 28. ...Rb8.}) 33. Nf5 {. But the final verdict of 23. Rxf7 seems clear. With best play White has at least a draw.}) 26... Kh8 27. Rc1 Qxc1+ $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Simplest. Two rooks and a knight for a queen.'} 28. Bxc1 Rxe7 $19 29. cxb5 Nc4 30. Qh4 ({White has no way of impeding Black's progress after} 30. Bg5 {Andrew Soltis} Rf7 {and Ncxe5. He tries to create a passed b-pawn}) 30... Rf7 31. Qd4 Kg8 $1 32. Bf4 Rc5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The main threat was 33. ...Rd5! (33.Qxc4 Rd1+. ) The pieces swarmed during the remainder'} 33. Bf3 Rxb5 34. h3 Ncxe5 35. Ba8 Rf8 36. Bxe5 Nxe5 37. Qxa7 Bd5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 38. Bxd5+ Rxd5 39. Qe3 Ra5 40. Qe2 Rfa8 41. a4 Nf7 42. h4 Rxa4 0-1

