{Andrew Soltis: 'When Miguel Najdorf escaped from a lost endgame at the Leipzig Olympiad in 1960, Bobby angrily knocked over the pieces according to the Argentine. "You'll never play again in South America," Najdorf told him. But in July 1970 Fischer's exile ended when he - and Don Miguel - played at a Buenos Aires international. Najdorf recalled how much Fischer had changed in a decade. At Buenos Aires 1960 Fischer habitually appeared at the board in a sweater and slacks. He noticed how Najdorf, a wealthy insurance company executive, changed suits each day and asked how many he owned. Not taking the question seriously, Najdorf replied 150. At Buenos Aires 1970 Fischer approached Najdorf and said, "I have broke your record. I have 187 suits." "Bravissimo. I congratulate you," said Najdorf. "But I have to confess, I never had more than 30." Suits or not, the 27-year-old Fischer was a much more mature player in 1970. He allowed only four draws at Buenos Aires, pointedly refusing draws in positions he might have accepted ten years before :'} 1. c4 {Andrew Soltis} g6 2. g3 Bg7 3. Bg2 c5 4. Nc3 Nc6 5. e3 (5. Nf3 Nf6 6. d4 $14) 5... Nf6 6. d4 O-O 7. Nge2 (7. d5 Na5 $1) 7... d6 8. O-O Bf5 9. d5 (9. b3 $11) ({This provocative move is designed to draw White's pawns forward. FOr example} 9. e4 {Andrew Soltis} Bd7 10. f3 cxd4 11. Nxd4 Qc8 $1 {threatening 12. ...Nxd4 13.Qxd4 Be6 winning a pawn}) 9... Na5 10. e4 Bd7 11. b3 a6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 12. Rb1 ({This shows Black has won the battle of the opening since after the natural} 12. Bb2 {Andrew Soltis} b5 13. Qd3 Rb8 14. Rab1 {he wins a pawn with} bxc4 15. bxc4 Rb4 16. Nd1 Rxc4 17. Ne3 Ra4 18. Qxa6 $4 (18. Nc3 Bb5) 18... Nc4 {. Szabo prepares to dissolve the queenside.}) 12... b5 $15 13. cxb5 axb5 14. b4 cxb4 15. Rxb4 Qc7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({The b-pawn is as weak as the a-pawn and White will stand well after} 15... Rb8 {Andrew Soltis} 16. Nd4) 16. Nxb5 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black would have good winning prospects after'} 16. Be3 Nc4 17. Bd4 (17. Bc5 {Andrew Soltis} Ra5 {. Szabo is looking for complications.}) 17... Ra5 $15) 16... Qc5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({and would have found them after} 16... Bxb5 {Andrew Soltis} 17. Rxb5 Qc4 18. a4 Nxe4 19. Qe1 $1 {threatening Rb4 or Rxa5} Nc3 (19... Bc3 20. Nxc3 Nxc3 21. Rb2 Nb3 22. Bg5 {offers chances to both sides}) 20. Nxc3 Bxc3 21. Qe4 $1) 17. Qd4 Nxd5 $1 ({This was not very difficult to calculate. What was hard was deciding whether to pull the tactical trigger here or try to improve Black's chances with preliminary moves. After} 17... Rfb8 {(Andrew Soltis) White has nothing better than} 18. a4 {, and Black can't improve on} Nxd5 19. Qxc5 dxc5 20. Rb1) ({similar is} 17... Rab8 {Andrew Soltis} 18. a4 Nxd5 19. Qxc5 dxc5 20. Rb1 {. There is a slight difference between thisposition and the previous because of} Nb6 {, rather than Nb4. THe difference favors Black after} 21. Nec3 (21. Bf4 e5 22. Be3 Nxa4) ({But White has a better defense in} 21. Rd1 $1 {and then} Bc6 22. Nec3 Nxa4 23. Nxa4 Bxb5 24. Nxc5) 21... Nxa4 $1) 18. Qxc5 dxc5 {Andrew Soltis: '=' Andrew Soltis: 'Around here Szabo offered a draw, explaining later that Fischer had rejected "many complicated continuations" and seemed "agreeable to a draw". "But when I offered peace he shook his head indignantly."'} 19. Rb1 Nb4 20. Nc7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'The shots continue. On'} 20. Nbc3 Nc4 $17 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black keeps improving his position'} 21. Ra1 {Andrew Soltis} Nc2 22. Rb1 Nd4) ({Worse is} 20. Nec3 {Andrew Soltis} Nc4 21. a3 {because of} Nxa3 22. Bxa3 Bxc3) ({White can transpose to the note on move 17 with} 20. a4 {(Andrew Soltis) but instead of} Rfb8 ({Black has} 20... Nc4 21. Rd1 Bc6 22. Nec3 Bxc3) 21. Be3 $1 {Andrew Soltis} Bxb5 22. axb5 Rxb5 23. Bxc5) 20... Ra7 21. a3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'White's rook is embarassed after'} (21. Nd5 {Andrew Soltis} Nxd5 22. exd5 Bf5 23. Rb6 Nc4) 21... Rxc7 22. axb4 cxb4 {Andrew Soltis: '!?'} ({This shows astonishing faith in his ability to win an endgame in which there will only be kingside pawns. The alternative} 22... Rb7 {Andrew Soltis} 23. Bg5 Rxb4 {appears to favor Black more} 24. Bxe7 Rfb8 25. Rbd1 Bb5 26. Rfe1 Rb2 27. Bf3 Nb3 {. But it also contains chances for a nasty surprise.}) 23. Rxb4 Rc2 24. Nd4 $2 (24. Nf4 $1 $15 Rfc8 {Andrew Soltis} 25. Rb1 Bd4 26. Ba3 Kf8 (26... Nc6 27. Rb7) 27. Nd5) 24... Bxd4 $1 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 25. Rxd4 Bb5 26. Re1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Annotators were quick to claim that'} 26. Rfd1 {Andrew Soltis: 'loses to'} Be2 $19 {Andrew Soltis: 'Actually the win is not certain after'} ({also muddy is} 26... Nb3 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Rb4 Nxc1 28. Rxb5 Ne2+ 29. Kh1 $1) 27. Bh6 $1 {Andrew Soltis} Nc6 28. Rd5 Bxd1 29. Bxf8 Rc1 30. Bh6 Rb1 31. Rc5 $1 Bc2+ 32. Bf1 Bxe4) 26... Nb3 27. Rb4 Nxc1 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 28. Rxb5 Ne2+ 29. Kf1 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({This allows the f-pawn to be captured with check in key lines. Correct was} 29. Kh1 $1 {Andrew Soltis} Nc3 30. Rc5 Rd8 31. Bf3 $1 {which leaves Black with good winning chances but nothing convincing after} Rdd2 (31... Rd3 32. Kg2) 32. Rf1) 29... Nc3 $1 30. Rc5 ({Soviet analysis vaguely sai} 30. Rb7 {(Andrew Soltis) "held out some chances" and that "some" is right} Rd8 31. Kg1 $1 Rdd2 32. Rf1) 30... Rd8 {Andrew Soltis: 'Whtie is on the cusp of losing now.'} 31. Bh3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (31. Re3 Rd1+ 32. Re1 Rdd2 $19) ({White is on the cusp of losing} 31. Bf3 {Andrew Soltis} Rdd2 32. Be2 Nxe2 33. Rxc2 Nxg3+) (31. f4 {Andrew Soltis} Rdd2 32. Rc8+ Kg7 33. Re3 Rxg2 34. Rexc3 Rcf2+ 35. Ke1 Rxh2 {. But at least the rook endgames require some technique. Szabo's move shortens the game considerably.}) 31... Rdd2 32. Rc8+ Kg7 33. Re3 Nd1 $1 34. Rf3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'also losing was'} 34. Rxc2 Nxe3+) (34. Re1 {Andrew Soltis} Rb2) (34. Rd3 $1 {Andrew Soltis} Nxf2 $1) 34... Rxf2+ 35. Rxf2 Rxf2+ 36. Kg1 (36. Ke1 Rxh2) 36... Re2 37. Bg4 (37. Bg2 {Andrew Soltis} Re1+ 38. Bf1 Rxe4) 37... Rxe4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'The rook endgame 38.Bxd1 Re1+ isn't difficult.'} (37... Rxe4 38. Bxd1 Re1+) 0-1

