{ChessCalisthenics: 'C19: French: 3 Nc3 Bb4: Main line: 7 Nf3 and 7 a4' Andrew Soltis: 'Two of Fischer's games at Buenos Aires exasperated annotators. In his tactical brawl with Quinteros, they went to extraordinary efforts to prove hewas lost at some point or another. In the following game, it was a single move 18.Rg3, that provoked them. It was variously described as an unworthy trap, a profound waiting move, a wasted tempo, and a clever case of prophylaxis. Perhaps Boris Gulko, who reanalyzed the game in 2002, came closest when he praised it - and yet called it "one of the bizarre and inexplicably 'stupid' mvoes of all time."'} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Qc7 7. Nf3 $1 Nc6 8. Be2 ({Fischer was virtually alone in preferring this order to} 8. a4 {Andrew Soltis} Bd7 ({Perhaps he was afraid of} 8... cxd4 9. cxd4 Nb4 {when White has to try} 10. Rb1 ({or gambit the pawn} 10. Bb5+ Bd7 11. Bxd7+ Kxd7 12. O-O Qxc2 13. Qe1) 10... Nxc2+ 11. Kd2) 9. Bd2) 8... Bd7 9. O-O Nge7 10. a4 Na5 $6 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N' ChessCalisthenics: ''?!'' Andrew Soltis: '?'} (10... b6 $1 11. Ba3 Na5 12. Ng5 h6 13. Nh3 O-O 14. Re1 Bxa4 15. Nf4 Qc6 16. Nh5 Ng6 17. Bd3 Bb5 18. dxc5 Bxd3 19. cxd3 Rfc8 20. Re3 d4 21. Rh3 dxc3 22. cxb6 c2 23. Nf6+ gxf6 24. Qc1 Nb3 25. Qxh6 {Rohde, M (2425)-Lein,A (2525)/Lone Pine 1978/MCL/0-1}) (10... cxd4 11. cxd4 Na5 12. Qd2 h6 13. h4 Nc4 14. Qf4 f5 15. exf6 Qxf4 16. Bxf4 gxf6 17. Nd2 Nxd2 18. Bh5+ Kf8 19. Bxd2 Nf5 20. g3 Kg7 21. Rfb1 b6 22. c3 Nd6 23. Be2 Nc4 24. Bxc4 dxc4 25. f3 {Walek,M (2374)-Kubala,M (2390)/ Czechia 2005/EXT 2008/0-1 (47)}) (10... Rc8 11. Re1 cxd4 12. cxd4 a5 13. c3 Na7 14. Ba3 Qxc3 15. Qb1 Qc2 16. Qxb7 Qc7 17. Reb1 Nac6 18. Bd6 Qxb7 19. Rxb7 Nd8 20. Rb6 Rc2 21. Bb5 Nc8 22. Rb8 Nxd6 23. exd6 f6 24. Kf1 Bxb5+ 25. axb5 {Kavalek,L-Geller,U/Netanya 1971/EXT 1998/1-0}) ({This is premature before Whtie has provided a target for the knight with Ba3. Standard mvoes are} 10... f6 {Andrew Soltis}) 11. Re1 cxd4 12. cxd4 Nc4 13. Bd3 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!''} h6 (13... O-O $2 14. Bxh7+ Kxh7 15. Ng5+ Kg6 16. Qg4 {(Trifunovic 70)} f5 {Andrew Soltis} 17. exf6 e5 18. Ne6+ Kxf6 19. dxe5+ Nxe5 (19... Kf7 20. Qxg7+ Kxe6 21. Qh6+ Rf6 (21... Ng6 22. Qxg6+ Rf6 23. Qxf6#) 22. Qxf6#) 20. Nxc7) 14. Nd2 $1 $16 {(eco 81) '!' (eco 81) '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'White prepares Qg4 or Qe2, as well as Bxc4 followed by invasion of d6 via Ne4'} Nxd2 ({Black doesn't stand badly after} 14... O-O-O {Andrew Soltis} 15. Bxc4 $2 (15. Qg4 g6 16. Qe2 {leaves him seriously weak on both wings}) 15... dxc4 16. Ba3 Nf5) 15. Bxd2 Nc6 16. Qg4 g6 17. Re3 ({Botwinnik, in his preparation for the unplayed 1970 match, pointed out how much Fischer liked to shift his rooks along the third rank. There's a tactical explanation here.} 17. Bxg6 {(Andrew Soltis) is handled by} Rg8 (17... fxg6 18. Qxg6+ Ke7 (18... Kf8 19. Bxh6+) 19. Qg7+ {. THe text threatens 18.Bxg6 Rh9 19. Bxf7+ Kxf7 20.Rf3+})) 17... O-O-O 18. Rg3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (18. Rf3 $1 f5 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Qxg6 ({to avoid getting the worst of it, White would have to calculate} 19. exf6 e5 20. Qxg6 e4 (20... Nxd4 21. Rg3 e4 22. Bf1) 21. Bxe4) 19... Nxd4 {. Indeed, 18.Rg3 - which Euwe said "does not accomplish anything" - prepares Qf4-f6 while stopping ...f5 with 19.Qxg6 Rdg8 20.Qxg8+. Another point is that on 18. ...Ne7 19.Qf3 Nf5 White's rook penetrates via 20.Bxf5 gxf5 21.Rg7.}) ({White would love to paralyze the kingside by planting a heavy piece on f6, but} 18. Qf4 {(Andrew Soltis) allows} f5 $1 {with a fine game.}) 18... Kb8 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: ''?''} ({This clears c8 for a rook or a bishop and avoids} 18... Na5 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Qf4 $1 Rdf8 20. Bb4 {. But by evacuating his king from a potentially hot file Black put it on a closed - yet ultimately hotter - diagonal}) 19. Rf3 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!''} f5 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: ''?''} ({Andrew Soltis: 'White would rule the kingside after'} 19... Be8 $18 20. Rf6 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) , but Black's combination has a dramatic flaw.}) 20. exf6 e5 {With this move Black seems to take the initiative. The white queen is under attack, Black threatens an eventual e5-e4, and White's d-pawn is endangered. On the contrary, Fischer has prepared a crushing counterstroke.} 21. Qg3 $1 {'!' '!'} ({Black's wildest dreams are realized by} 21. Qxg6 {Andrew Soltis} Rdg8 22. Qh5 (22. Qf7 Rxg2+ $1 23. Kxg2 Bh3+) (22. Qxg8+ Rxg8 23. f7 Rf8 24. Bxh6 Nxd4 {the outcome is unclear - but not unwelcome}) 22... Bg4) 21... Nxd4 (21... e4 {Andrew Soltis: '??'} 22. Bf4 $18) 22. Re3 e4 (22... Nf5 {Andrew Soltis} 23. Bxf5 Bxf5 24. Qxe5) 23. Rxe4 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!'' '!!'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black has excellent survival chances after'} 23. Qxc7+ $2 {This is the variation Black was expecting when he played his 21st move.} Kxc7 24. Bf1 {(Florian 83)} Bc6 {Andrew Soltis} (24... Rdf8 {(Andrew Soltis) . But now the threat of Bf4 drags him into a lost endgame via what Gulko called a "very brilliant point".})) 23... Qxg3 24. Rxd4 $3 {"A quiet intermediate move, after which it becomes clear that in spite of her apparent freedom, the queen is doomed." (Florian 83) "A quiet intermediate move, after which it becomes clear that in spite of her apparent freedom, the queen is doomed." (Florian 83) ChessCalisthenics: ''!!' "A quiet intermediate move, after which it becomes clear that in spite of her apparent freedom, the queen is doomed." (Florian 83)' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Black's queen is trapped (24. ...Qc7 25.Bf4)'} Qg4 $6 {'?!' '?!'} (24... Qc7 25. Bf4 $18) (24... Qxd3 $5 {}) 25. Rxg4 Bxg4 {Andrew Soltis: 'White must regain the exchange, leaving him a pawn ahead.'} 26. Bxg6 {"White has radically changed the structure of the position with the temporary queen sacrifice and the subsequent sacrifice of the exchange. Black's menacing centre pawns have disappeared, and the f6 pawn has become a threat." (Florian)} Rhg8 ({The rooks prove extremely awkward. After} 26... Rdf8 {Andrew Soltis}) 27. Bh7 $1 {'!' '!'} ({simpler than} 27. Bd3 {Andrew Soltis} Rdf8 28. Bc3 Bf3 29. Be5+ {which should also win}) 27... Rh8 (27... Rgf8 {Andrew Soltis} 28. Bxh6 Rxf6 29. Bg5 {. The remainder was a mop-up}) 28. Bd3 Rde8 $2 {'?' '?'} (28... Rhf8 29. Bxh6 Rxf6 30. Bg5 $18 {(Trifunovic 70) '+-' (Trifunovic 70) ChessCalisthenics: '+-' '+-'}) (28... Bh5 $142) 29. f7 Re7 (29... Ref8 30. Bg6) 30. f8=Q+ $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!''} Rxf8 31. Bb4 Rff7 32. Bxe7 Rxe7 33. f3 Bd7 34. a5 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!''} Kc7 35. Kf2 Rf7 36. Ke3 Kd6 37. g3 Kc5 38. f4 Bg4 39. Rb1 Re7+ 40. Kd2 b6 41. axb6 axb6 42. h3 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: ''!''} Bd7 (42... Bxh3 43. Rh1 Bd7 44. Rxh6 Rg7 45. Rg6 $18 {(Trifunovic 70)}) 43. g4 d4 44. f5 Re3 45. f6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Rf3 {ChessCalisthenics: '?'} (45... Re8 $142 $18) 46. Rf1 Rxf1 47. Bxf1 Be6 {Andrew Soltis: 'and Black resigned'} 1-0

