1. d4 {Garry Kasparov} Nf6 2. c4 c5 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'Back in 1966 Bobby had expanded his opening repertoire, adding to his King's Indian, Grünfeld and Nimzo-Indian the no less active Modern Benoni Defence.'} 3. d5 e6 4. Nc3 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. e4 g6 7. Bf4 (7. Be2 {Garry Kasparov} Bg7 8. f4 O-O 9. Nf3 Re8 10. Nd2 c4 $1 {was Pomar-Fischer, Havana Olympiad 1966.}) (7. Nf3 {(Garry Kasparov) – Game No.89.}) 7... a6 (7... Bg7 8. Qa4+ Bd7 9. Qb3 $1 $16) 8. a4 Bg7 9. Nf3 O-O 10. Be2 Bg4 11. O-O Re8 12. h3 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'Overlooking a classical tactical stroke.'} ({The correct continuation is} 12. Nd2 {Garry Kasparov} Bxe2 13. Qxe2 Nh5 14. Be3 Nd7 15. a5 {(Korchnoi-Torre, Leningrad Interzonal 1973; Beliavsky-Kasparov, 47th USSR Championship, Minsk 1979)} ({or} 15. g4 {(Portisch-Kasparov, Moscow 1981) with slightly the better game for White, but double-edged.})) 12... Nxe4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'A famous trick! In my youth I began playing the Modern Benoni under the influence of my schoolmate, the future grandmaster Elmar Magerramov (who, like all of us, learned his opening subtleties from Fischer), and it was our secret dream to catch an opponent in this trap!'} 13. Nxe4 (13. hxg4 Bxc3 14. bxc3 (14. Qb3 $1 {Garry Kasparov} Bb4 $1 15. Bd3 Nd7 16. g5 (16. Bxe4 $6 Rxe4 17. Bxd6 Nf6) ({, or} 16. Rfc1 Ndf6 17. g5 Ng4 18. Bxe4 Rxe4 19. Rc4 Rxc4 20. Qxc4 Qd7 {is worse for White}) 16... f5 17. Nd4 $5 cxd4 $1 18. Qxb4 Ndc5 19. Rad1 Nxg5 {etc.}) 14... Nxc3 $19) 13... Rxe4 14. Bg5 Qe8 15. Bd3 Bxf3 16. Qxf3 Rb4 $19 17. Rae1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Desperation,'} ({since no consolation is offered by both} 17. Rfe1 {Garry Kasparov} Be5 18. a5 Rxb2 19. Rab1 Rxb1 20. Rxb1 Qc8 {followed by ...Nd7 and ...Rb8}) ({and} 17. Qg3 {Garry Kasparov} Be5 18. f4 ({Janosevic-Zuckerman, Vrnjacka Banja 1971; or} 18. Rfe1 Rxa4) 18... Bxb2 19. Rfe1 Qc8 {.}) 17... Be5 ({Of course not} 17... Qxa4 $4 {Garry Kasparov} 18. Re7 {.}) 18. Qd1 ({After} 18. Bh6 {(Garry Kasparov) there can follow} Rxb2 {(and if} 19. Qf6 {, then} Nc6 $1 {)}) ({while if} 18. a5 {(Garry Kasparov) –} Rxb2 ({or} 18... Nd7 {.})) 18... Qxa4 19. Qxa4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Equivalent to capitulation.'} ({However,} 19. Bc2 {Garry Kasparov} Qa2 20. Re2 Nd7 21. b3 Qa3 $1 22. Rfe1 Rd4 23. Qb1 Nb6 24. Be3 Nxd5 $1 25. Bxd4 Bxd4 {was equally hopeless.}) 19... Rxa4 20. f4 Bd4+ (20... Bxb2 $2 21. Re8+ Kg7 22. Be7 $1) 21. Kh1 ({If} 21. Kh2 {Garry Kasparov} Nd7 22. Re7 {Black would have won by} Nb6 $1 23. Rxb7 Rb4 $1 {.}) 21... Nd7 22. Re7 Nf6 (22... Nb6 $5 {Garry Kasparov}) 23. Rxb7 ({Or} 23. Bxf6 {Garry Kasparov} Bxf6 24. Rxb7 Rd4 {and wins.}) 23... Nh5 $1 (23... Nxd5 {Garry Kasparov: 'suggests itself, but Fischer has his own way.'} 24. f5 $5) 24. Kh2 (24. Re1 {Garry Kasparov} Bf2 $1) 24... Be3 $1 25. Be2 Bxf4+ 26. Bxf4 Rxf4 27. Rb6 Rxf1 28. Bxf1 Rd8 $1 29. Bxa6 (29. Rxa6 {Garry Kasparov} Kg7 30. Bb5 Kf6 31. Bc6 Ke5 32. Ra7 Rf8 {0-1 (34) Uhlmann,W-Fischer,R Palma de Mallorca 1970 My Great Predecessors 4 [Garry Kasparov]}) 29... Kg7 30. Bb5 Kf6 31. Bc6 Ke5 32. Rb7 Rf8 33. Re7+ Kd4 34. Rd7 Nf6 $1 (34... Nf6 35. Rxd6 $2 Ke5 $1) 0-1

