1. d4 {Garry Kasparov PS} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nf3 (6. e4 {Garry Kasparov} g6 7. Bf4 {– Game No.86.}) 6... g6 7. e4 Bg7 8. Be2 O-O 9. O-O {Garry Kasparov: 'Gligoric's favourite variation.'} Re8 10. Nd2 Nbd7 11. a4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({The advantages of this move over} 11. Qc2 {(Garry Kasparov) have been described in the notes to Volume 3, Game No.33.}) 11... Ne5 (11... a6 {Garry Kasparov} 12. Qc2 Ne5 13. h3 g5 $5 {Bakos-Berliner, Montreal 1956.}) 12. Qc2 $1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Petrosian began with'} 12. Ra3 {PS Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '.'} g5 {Garry Kasparov: 'and Petrosian and Portisch –'} 13. Qc2 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) (12. h3 {PS} g5 13. Nf3 Nxf3+ 14. Bxf3) (12. f4 {PS} Neg4 13. Re1 g5 14. Nf1) {Garry Kasparov: 'In the early 1980s I tried'} 12... g5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'A fresh idea for those times,' Garry Kasparov: 'in a training match with Magerramov and came to the conclusion that White can retain some advantage. But at the start of the 1970s the play on the dark squares appeared rather promising for Black, which attracted Fischer with his very concrete approach to the solving of opening (and also other) problems. This was a step forward: in the era of Botvinnik, Smyslov and Petrosian the ...g6-g5 advance, creating a chronic weaknesses on the light squares, could not even have been conceived.'} ({and more solid than the '1972 hit'} 12... Nh5 {(Garry Kasparov) , after which Gligoric played} 13. Bxh5 gxh5 14. Nd1 $1 {(cf. Volume 3, Game No.33).}) 13. Nf3 {Garry Kasparov: 'A sound move (exchanging the dangerous knight), but not the most active.'} (13. Nc4 $11 Nxc4 {PS} 14. Bxc4 Ng4 15. Ne2 (15. Be2 $6 Ne5 16. f4 gxf4 17. Bxf4 Kh8 18. Bh5 Ng6 19. Nb5 Nxf4 20. Rxf4 a6 21. Na3 Qg5 22. Bxf7 Qxf4 23. Bxe8 Bg4 {0-1 Regulski,Z-Zieba,Z/POL corr 1989/CR97 (23)} (23... Bg4 24. h3 Bd4+ 25. Kh1 Be5 26. Kg1 Qh2+ $19)) 15... Qf6 (15... a6 16. Ra3 Qe7 17. Rg3 h6 18. f4 Qxe4 19. Bd3 Qb4 20. fxg5 hxg5 $15 {Najdorf M.-Ree H.,Wijk aan Zee 1971}) (15... Ne5 $5 16. Bb5 (16. Ng3 a6 $1 17. a5 Bd7 18. Be2 Ng6 19. Nh5 Be5 $132) 16... Rf8 17. Ng3 a6 $5 (17... g4 18. Bf4 Ng6 19. Bd2 a6 20. Be2 Re8 21. f4 gxf3 22. Bxf3 Bd4+ 23. Kh1 Qh4 $132 {Hering - Holm, FRG 1980}) 18. Be2 Qe7 19. Ra3 Re8 $5 20. Re1 Ng6 21. Bd2 Be5 22. Nf5 Qf6 23. Rb3 $13) 16. Ng3 $5 {Nikoloff-Hulak, New York 1989} (16. f3 Ne5 17. Bb5 {Garcia Gonzales-Andres, Habana 1985}) 16... Qg6 17. Be2 Be5 (17... Bd4 $142 $5) 18. Bxg4 Bxg4 19. f4 gxf4 20. Bxf4 Bd4+ 21. Kh1 Re7 22. Ra3 $16 { Nickoloff B.-Hulak K.,New York 1989}) (13. Nd1 $5 {PS} g4 $5) ({Subsequently Gligoric successfully employed} 13. b3 {(Garry Kasparov) , Bb2 and Rae1}) 13... Nxf3+ 14. Bxf3 h6 $2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'In Gligoric's opinion,'} 14... Nd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'was better, when Black equalizes after'} 15. Bg4 ({but} 15. a5 $5 {Garry Kasparov} Ne5 16. Be2 {promises White more}) 15... Ne5 16. Bxc8 Rxc8 $11 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 15. Bd2 a6 (15... Qe7 {Garry Kasparov} 16. Rae1 $1) 16. Be2 Qe7 (16... Bd7 {(Garry Kasparov) was more normal, but Bobby conceives a risky – and provocative! – raid into the opponent's den  (he used a similar idea in the 2nd game of his match with Larsen – Game No.96).}) 17. Rae1 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Qe5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({It was not yet too late for Black to 'change his mind', by playing} 17... b6 {(Garry Kasparov) . But then after} 18. Kh1 {White would have retained a solid and quite favourable position. Therefore Fischer, a subtle psychologist, goes in for a bluff – and amazingly quickly achieves his aim: the audacious queen sortie upsets Gligoric's mental equilibrium!}) 18. Kh1 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'A useful prophylactic move, intensifying the threat of f2-f4.'} Qd4 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'There is no longer anything better;'} ({for example:} 18... Bd7 {Garry Kasparov} 19. f4 $1 Qd4 20. e5 $1 Nxd5 ({or} 20... dxe5 21. fxg5 hxg5 22. Bxg5) 21. Nxd5 Qxd5 22. Bc4 Qc6 (22... Bf5 23. Qxf5 Qxc4 24. exd6) 23. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 24. fxg5+ Kg8 25. gxh6 dxe5 26. hxg7 {and wins.}) 19. f3 $16 (19. f4 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) was more energetic, and after} g4 {White gains the advantage, Garry Kasparov: ', transposing to the position after 19 f4 g4 above.'} 20. Z0 ({, with both the manoeuvring} 20. Bd1 Bd7 21. Be3 Qb4 22. Bf2 $1 Nh5 (22... Nh7 23. Bg3 {and e4-e5}) 23. Bh4 {with the idea of 24 h3 (and if} Nf6 {, then} 24. e5 {)}) ({, and the sharp} 20. e5 Nxd5 21. Rd1 $5 {with the threat of an invasion at d6;} ({but not Klovans's recommendation} 21. Nxd5 Qxd5 22. Bc4 {on account of} Bf5 $1 23. Qxf5 Qxc4 24. exd6 Re6 {with equality.}))) ({Black would also have been set complicated problems by} 19. Be3 {Garry Kasparov} Qb4 20. f4 $1 {.} Z0 ({. Since} 20... Nxe4 $6 21. Nxe4 Rxe4 (21... Qxe4 22. Bd3 $1 {and fxg5}) 22. Bd3 Bf5 23. fxg5 hxg5 24. Qf2 Bg6 25. Bxe4 Qxe4 26. Bxg5 {and Bf6!}) ({, or} 20... Bf5 $6 21. fxg5 Nxe4 22. Rxf5 Nxc3 23. gxh6 $1 Nxe2 (23... Rxe3 24. Rg5 $1 {allows White a dangerous attack}) 24. Qxe2 Bxh6 25. Qf2) ({, all that remains for Black is} 20... g4 21. Bd2 Qd4 {, transposing to the position after 19 f4 g4 above.})) 19... Nh5 {PS: '' '' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Black succeeds in gaining control of the f4-square.'} 20. Nb5 $4 {PS: '?' '?' Garry Kasparov: 'White decides to punish his opponent – by trapping the queen. But this is an illusion!'} (20. g3 $1 { d1, f4} Bh3 {Garry Kasparov} 21. Rg1 Bd7 22. a5 Rab8 ({but not Kapengut's recommendation} 22... g4 $6 {in view of} 23. Qc1 Kh7 24. Rgf1 $1) 23. f4 (23. Ra1 f5 $5) 23... g4 {with a complicated game.}) (20. Nd1 {PS} Bd7 21. a5 Qf6) ({In my view, White could still have retained the better prospects with} 20. a5 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) followed by Nd1 and Bc3, exploiting the unfortunate position of the black queen and the holes in the enemy position.}) 20... axb5 21. Bxb5 Qe5 $1 $19 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 22. Bc3 Qe7 23. Bxe8 Qxe8 24. Bxg7 Kxg7 {Garry Kasparov: 'The outcome is that Black has two pieces for a rook and pawn and every chance of winning. The battle has already effectively ended, without hardly having begun...'} 25. b4 {PS: '!' '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Creating at least some counterplay.'} cxb4 26. Qb2+ Qe5 27. Qxb4 Nf4 28. Rd1 b6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (28... g4 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) was more accurate, expanding the scope of the bishop:} 29. fxg4 (29. g3 Ne2 $1) (29. a5 g3 $1 30. h3 Bxh3 $1) ({, or} 29. Rd2 gxf3 30. gxf3 Kh7 31. Qb3 Bh3 32. Rg1 Rg8 $1 33. Rxg8 Kxg8 34. Qb2 Bg4 $3) 29... Bxg4 30. Rb1 (30. Rde1 Nd3 31. Qxb7 Rf8 32. Re3 Qd4 {etc}) 30... Bh5 $1 31. Qd2 Ne2 32. Rb4 Qc3 $1 33. Qe1 Bg6 $1 34. Qb1 (34. Qa1 Ra5) 34... Qe5 {and ...Nc3, winning.}) 29. Rf2 $2 {PS: '??' '??' Garry Kasparov: 'Missing a one-move tactic.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Gligoric recommended'} 29. Rd2 {PS: '' ''}) ({and} 29. Qxb6 {(Garry Kasparov) was also much more tenacious:} Ba6 $1 ({not} 29... Rxa4 30. g3 $1) 30. Rfe1 ({after} 30. Rg1 g4 $1 {Black is threatening ...g4-g3}) 30... Be2 $1 31. Ra1 g4 32. Qe3 gxf3 33. gxf3 Kh7 {etc.}) 29... Nd3 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Here White could already have resigned.'} 30. Qxb6 Nxf2+ 31. Qxf2 Rxa4 32. Kg1 Ra1 33. Qe1 Ra2 34. Qg3 Qb2 35. h4 Ra1 0-1

