{Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'If we speak of playing until the exhaustion of all resources we cannot think of a better example than Fischer. The Soviets frequently considered his approach as childish and absurd, but many of their players had to pay dearly for not withstanding Fischer’s prolonged pressure in simple positions.  Fischer had a good start at the Palma de Mallorca 1970 Interzonal, being in the sole lead with 5½ points after 6 rounds. But a less fortunate sequence followed, with a loss against Larsen, two games saved against Matulovic and Naranja, and a couple of fighting draws with Portisch and Polugaevsky. As a result, he trailed half a point behind the new leader, Geller, who had 8 points after 11 rounds.  Bobby was obviously nervous, even though his prospects of gaining one of the six qualifying places was beyond any doubt. But for him it was first place that really counted, together with high quality performances in his games. The turning point came in Round 12 when he met Geller, one of the very few grandmasters against whom he had a lifetime negative score.' Nigel Davies: 'Prior to this game Efim Geller had an excellent personal score against Bobby Fischer. As he also had White he was probably expecting his draw offer to be accepted without a second thought. When Fischer refused, Geller was clearly shaken... '} 1. Nf3 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'As reported in the tournament book by Wade and Blackstock: “Gufeld, Geller’s second, when asked why not 1.e4 ?, replied at length, mentioning that it was contrary to the theory of probability that Geller’s phenomenal run of success with it against Fischer could continue. ” Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele.' Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Trifunovic,Petar As reported in the tournament book by Wade and Blackstock: “Gufeld, Geller’s second, when asked why not 1.e4 ?, replied at length, mentioning that it was contrary to the theory of probability that Geller’s phenomenal run of success with it against Fischer could continue. ” Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele.'} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. O-O c6 $1 $11 6. d4 d5 7. cxd5 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '“Geller offered a draw. Fischer sat back and quietly laughed, with Geller also participating, then Fischer said something, Geller went a bit red and the game went on.” (Wade/Blackstock) This small psychological defeat would eventually result in an ending in which White was a pawn down.' Nigel Davies: 'With this move Geller offered a draw – which was greeted by laughter from Fischer. As Geller was leading 5-2 in wins (with two draws) before this game, this showed remarkable self confidence on Fischer's part. ' PS: 'Remisangebot von Weiß'} cxd5 8. Ne5 $146 (8. Nc3 $1) 8... Bf5 9. Nc3 (9. Qb3 Nc6 $1 10. Qxb7 Nxd4 11. Nc3 Qd6 12. Bf4 Rfb8 $19) 9... Ne4 10. Be3 (10. Nxe4 {Nigel Davies: 'is better, though this does not look great for White either; for example,'} Bxe4 11. f3 (11. Bf4 Bxg2 12. Kxg2 Qb6 13. Qd2 Bxe5 14. Bxe5 Nc6 {is fine for Black.}) 11... Bf5 12. e4 dxe4 13. fxe4 Bxe5 14. dxe5 Qxd1 15. Rxd1 Be6 $19) 10... Nxc3 11. bxc3 Nc6 12. Nxc6 bxc6 13. Qa4 (13. Bf4 $1 $11) 13... Qb6 $1 $15 14. Rac1 Rab8 15. c4 $2 (15. Rfd1) 15... Bxd4 $15 16. Bxd4 Qxd4 17. e3 {Nigel Davies: '?' '?' Nigel Davies: 'After this Black has the advantage.' PS: '?'} (17. Qxc6 $11 {(Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani) Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele}) ({White should have played} 17. Rfd1 {, when} Qb2 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Bxd5 Qxe2 20. Qxa7 {would probably be a draw.}) 17... Qe5 {PS: ''} 18. cxd5 cxd5 19. Rfd1 e6 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Diagram [#]'} 20. Qxa7 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '?!' '?!'} ({Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Geller, who was mentally focused on achieving a draw, was not prepared to embark on such a sharp line as'} 20. g4 $6 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Trifunovic,Petar: '?!''} Be4 21. f3 Bxf3 $1 22. Bxf3 Qxe3+ 23. Kg2 Rb2+ 24. Rc2 Rfb8 $17 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '' '' Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: ' Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele' Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: ' Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele Trifunovic,Petar: '''}) 20... Ra8 21. Qd4 Qxd4 22. Rxd4 Rxa2 23. e4 $1 dxe4 24. Bxe4 Bxe4 25. Rxe4 $15 {Nigel Davies: 'With best play this endgame should still be a draw, but with an extra pawn Black clearly has whatever chances are going. '} Rb8 26. Re3 g5 {Nigel Davies: 'A typical and thematic move. Black gains space while making it difficult for White to advance his kingside pawns (f2-f4 or h2-h4) without getting them split. '} 27. h3 Kg7 28. Rc7 Kg6 29. Rf3 f6 30. Re7 Re2 31. g4 Rb1+ 32. Kg2 Ree1 {[ 33...g1 34.h2 h1 35.g2 bg1#] [ 33...g1 34.h2 h1 35.g2 bg1#] Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '[ 33...Rg1 34.Kh2 Rh1 35.Kg2 Rbg1#]'} 33. Ra3 h5 {PS: '!'} 34. Raa7 {[ 35.g7 h6 36.h7 g6 37.ag7#] [ 35.g7 h6 36.h7 g6 37.ag7#] Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Diagram [#] [ 35.Rg7 Kh6 36.Rh7 Kg6 37.Rag7#]'} Rg1+ 35. Kf3 hxg4+ 36. hxg4 Rb3+ 37. Ke2 Rxg4 38. Rxe6 {Nigel Davies: 'This further exchange of pawns brings the draw that little bit closer. But White still has to play accurately. '} Rb1 39. Raa6 Rf4 40. Ra2 Rh1 {[ 41...f2 42.f2 h2] [ 41...f2 42.f2 h2] Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '[ 41...Rf2 42.Kf2 Rh2]'} 41. Rea6 Rb4 42. R6a4 Rbb1 43. Ra8 Rhg1 44. Kf3 Rb5 45. R8a5 Rb3+ 46. Ke2 Rbb1 47. Ra8 Kf5 48. R2a5+ Kg4 49. Ra4+ Kh5 50. Rh8+ Kg6 51. Rg8+ Kf7 52. Rd8 Rbe1+ 53. Kf3 Re5 54. Rd2 Rf5+ 55. Ke2 Re5+ 56. Kf3 Kg6 {Nigel Davies: 'This position was reached after the second time control. White should be able to draw, but in practice he still needs to be careful. '} 57. Re4 {PS: '!'} Rf5+ 58. Ke2 Ra5 59. Re3 {PS: '!'} Kh5 60. Red3 Raa1 61. Rd8 f5 62. Kf3 Ra3+ 63. R2d3 g4+ 64. Kf4 Rxd3 65. Rxd3 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'The game enters its final phase, with only one rook on each side. The unfavourable position of his king causes White some problems.'} Rf1 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Diagram [#]'} 66. Rd2 $4 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '[Too harsh Marin,Mihail]' Nigel Davies: '?' '?' Nigel Davies: 'It seems that the Soviet contingent were horrified when they saw this move.' PS: '?'} ({Nigel Davies: 'They had analysed'} 66. Kg3 $1 $11 {Nigel Davies: ', which should lead to a draw.'}) 66... Kh4 $1 $19 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '[Not quite so Marin,Mihail]'} 67. Kxf5 (67. Ke3 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani} g3 68. Kf3 Kh3 $19 {Marin, Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele}) 67... g3 {Nigel Davies: 'Geller had thought this move impossible, and was intending to answer it by taking on g3 with check, followed by taking the rook on f1 with his king. After seven hours of torture, the mind can play tricks. '} 68. f4 Kh3 69. Rd3 {PS: '!'} ({Geller’s later confusion may be explained by the fact that in many lines the f-pawn does not guarantee a draw against the rook:} 69. Kg5 $2 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani} g2 70. Rxg2 Kxg2 71. f5 Kf3 72. f6 Ke4 73. Kg6 Ke5 74. f7 Ke6 $19 {Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele}) 69... Kh4 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Fischer was known for going straight for the winning line if he knew there was one. But the position is still drawn so he repeats the moves, in Botvinnik-style, to reduce his opponent’s alertness.'} (69... Kh2 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani} 70. Kg4 g2 71. Rh3+ Kg1 72. f5 {Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele} Kf2 73. Rh2 $1 $11 {Matanovic}) 70. Rd2 (70. Rd8 $1 $11) 70... Ra1 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Diagram [#]'} 71. Ke5 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: '?' '?' Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'Rightly identified by Wade and Blackstock as the decisive mistake, even though the continuation they analyzed is flawed. White should not have conceded the g4-square to the enemy king, even though the last move looks like a nice centralization.' PS: '?'} ({White should have improved the placement of his rook:} 71. Rd8 $1 {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani} g2 72. Rh8+ Kg3 73. Rg8+ Kf3 74. Rxg2 $1 (74. Ke5 $2 {!! = Wade, Robert; Blackstock,Lesly} g1=Q 75. Rxg1 Rxg1 76. f5 Kg4 77. f6 Rf1 78. Ke6 Kg5 79. f7 Kg6 $19 {Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele}) (74. Ke6 $1 $11) 74... Kxg2 75. Kg6 $3 (75. Ke6 $2 Re1+ 76. Kd6 Rf1 77. Ke5 Kg3 $19) 75... Kf3 {. Unlike the previous line, Black’s own king prevents him from delivering a decisive first rank check.} 76. f5 $11 {Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Daniele}) (71. Kg6 $2 Ra6+ 72. Kf5 Ra8 $1 $19) 71... Kg4 $1 72. f5 Ra5+ {Trifunovic,Petar; Marin,Mihail; Vocaturo,Dani: 'This win helped Fischer recover his form and confidence and he went on to win the tournament with a fantastic 18½ out of 23, 3½ points ahead of the rest of the field. Anatoly Karpov was also famous for “torturing” opponents in virtually equal positions. Even though I do not remember any famous example when he made use of this particular skill in a crucial tournament or match game, I cannot end this article without including one of his games.'} 73. Kd4 Rxf5 {0-1 (73) Geller,E-Fischer,R Palma de Mallorca Interzonal 1970 10 Great Ways [Nigel Davies]} 0-1

