{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer began 1970 predicting that Korchnoi or Larsen had the best chance of becoming Spassky's challenger in the 1972 World Championship match. That scenario changed after Pal Benko agreed to give up the third U.S. spot in the 1970 interzonal. Benko was paid $2,000 for "stepping down and making room" for Fischer, USCF executive director Ed Edmondson told Bobby in a letter just before Palma. Fischer nevertheless was undecided. He was making last minute demands and threatening not to play until just before he left for the Balearic island. After a first round hiccup, when he blundered a piece but managed to draw against Hübner, he settled down with the following win.'} 1. c4 {Andrew Soltis} g6 2. Nc3 ({Black can meet} 2. e4 {with} c5 {, which reaches a Gurgenidze System against the Maroczy Bind via} 3. Nf3 Nc6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nf6 6. Nc3 d6 {.}) ({Meanwhile there's a transposition into a Grünfeld after} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 d5 {.}) 2... Bg7 {Nigel Davies: 'QUESTION: How would Black have reached a Grünfeld Defence had White now played 3 d4 as after 3...Nf6 White has 4 e4? --- ANSWER: That is an excellent question and the answer is that he can't.'} ({Fischer played 2...Bg7 because he was happy to enter a King's Indian Defence with 3 d4 Nf6 4 e4 d6, Grünfeld players should play} 2... c5 $1 {preventing this.}) 3. g3 (3. d4 Nf6 4. e4 {would have taken us into a King's Indian Defence, which Fischer specialized in throughout much of his career.}) 3... c5 4. Bg2 Nc6 {Nigel Davies: 'We have now reached a Symmetrical English, an opening known to give rise to complex middlegames but not pose Black any particular opening problems. Smyslov's next move is also known to be one of White's least dangerous options, but it does throw the players on their own resources, for what that's worth against Fischer.'} 5. b3 ({At Belgrade, Petrosian played} 5. Nf3 {(Andrew Soltis) but ended up with the worst of it after Fischer seized the center with} e6 6. O-O Nge7 7. d3 O-O 8. Bd2 d5 {like Game 79, but with the colors reversed} 9. a3 b6 10. Rb1 Bb7 11. b4 cxb4 12. axb4 dxc4 13. dxc4 Rc8) 5... e6 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: 'It is Black who is now playing for the centre, aiming for ...Nge7 and ...d7-d5.'} 6. Bb2 Nge7 7. Na4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'This improves on'} 7. Nf3 {Nigel Davies: 'would have transposed into an earlier game between the same too players, V.Smyslov-R.Fischer, Buenos Aires 1970. After'} O-O 8. Na4 e5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: ', which would transpose into Smyslov-Fischer from Buenos Aires four months before. Since'} 9. O-O (9. Nxc5 $2 e4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'is dubious, White had to accept a passive setup. It was in that game that Fischer rejected a draw by saying, "I don't take draws in under 40 moves."'} 10. Bxg7 exf3 {wins material}) 9... d6 10. e3 f5 11. d3 h6 12. Ne1 f4 {Black had excellent play, though on that occasion the game ended in a draw.}) 7... Bxb2 8. Nxb2 O-O 9. e3 {Nigel Davies: 'Still being rather leisurely with his development.'} (9. h4) ({White should probably play} 9. Nf3 {, but then} e5 {starts to put pawns on dark squares to complement his light-squared bishop.}) 9... d5 10. cxd5 (10. Ne2 d4 {would take space and leave White in a kind of reversed Benoni. Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Black has too much of the center'}) ({Smyslov is also trying to improve over his celebrated loss to Tal six years before which went} 10. Nf3 {Andrew Soltis} Nf5) 10... Nxd5 11. Ne2 b6 $1 12. d4 ({White sees that after Andrew Soltis: 'Soviet annotators said'} 12. O-O Ba6 {he would not be able to fee his game with d2-d4, but this is playable for him if he then plays} 13. d3 {. Andrew Soltis: 'was necessary, although Black can set up a good version of the Maroczy-Bind center with'} e5 {Andrew Soltis}) 12... Ba6 {Nigel Davies: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: '"A brilliant positional sacrifice in the style of the young...Smyslov!" wrote Vasily Panov. White has king problems now 13.Nc4 b5'} ({Black has a fine game after} 12... cxd4 {Andrew Soltis} 13. exd4 (13. Nxd4 Nxd4 14. Qxd4 Ba6 15. Nc4 Rc8 {. But the text is better}) 13... Ba6 14. Nc4 Qf6) 13. dxc5 ({It's understandable that White plays this way because} 13. Nc4 {is well met by} b5 {. But now Fischer seizes the initiative and never lets it go.}) 13... Qf6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now 14.Nd4 bxc5 15. Nxc6 Qxb2 16.Rb1! (not 16.Rc1 Nxe3) allows White to escape but 14. ...Rad8! increases pressure'} 14. Nc4 Nc3 $1 $17 {Nigel Davies: '!' Andrew Soltis: '!'} 15. Nxc3 (15. Qc1 Nxe2 16. Kxe2 Rac8 17. cxb6 Ne5 $19 18. bxa7 {Andrew Soltis} Nxc4 19. a8=Q Rxa8 20. bxc4 (20. Bxa8 Nb2+) 20... Rac8) ({White also loses castling rights after} 15. Qc2 Nxe2 16. Kxe2 {and then} Nb4 $1 {is very strong.}) 15... Qxc3+ 16. Kf1 Rfd8 17. Qc1 ({White has less of a chance of relied in} 17. Qe1 {Andrew Soltis} Bxc4+ 18. bxc4 Qxc4+ 19. Kg1 Rac8 20. Rc1 Qa4) 17... Bxc4+ 18. bxc4 Qd3+ 19. Kg1 Rac8 {Nigel Davies: '!' Nigel Davies: 'Losing no time to accentuate his lead in development. The sacrificed pawn will soon be recovered.' Andrew Soltis: 'The c-pawn is doomed after ...Ne5 or ..Na5'} 20. cxb6 axb6 21. Qb2 Na5 ({also good is} 21... Qxc4 {Andrew Soltis} 22. Qxb6 Rd2 {and ...Ne5}) 22. h4 $4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (22. Bf1 Nxc4 23. Qf6 $15 Qd5 {Andrew Soltis} ({However} 23... Qd2 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) is more dangerous e.g.} 24. h4 (24. Bxc4 Rxc4 25. Kg2 Rd5) 24... Rd5 {and ...Rd5 or}) 24. Bxc4 Rxc4 25. h4 $1 {would threaten h5-h6. If Black stops that with} h5 {White plays} 26. Kh2 {and puts up some middlegame resistance.}) ({White's king problems become serious after} 22. Qxb6 {Andrew Soltis} Nxc4 23. Qb3 (23. Qb5 Rb8 24. Qc5 (24. Bb7)) 23... Qd2 24. a4 Nb2 {and Nd3}) 22... Nxc4 23. Qf6 {Nigel Davies: 'White is hoping to get counterplay on the kingside with h4-h5, but Fischer's next move snuffs that danger out and leaves Black with an advantageous endgame.'} Qf5 $1 ({White's only active piece is exchanged. Unsound was} 23... Nxe3 {Andrew Soltis} 24. fxe3 Rc2 25. Kh2 (25. Qf3)) 24. Qxf5 gxf5 25. h5 (25. Kh2 {is strongly met by} Rd2 {, so White tries to activate his rook along the fourth rank.} 26. Rhf1 {Andrew Soltis} Ne5 27. Kg1 Rcc2 28. a4 Ng4 {and wins}) 25... Rd2 26. Rc1 ({White should have kept to his original plan with Andrew Soltis: 'The win is a matter of time after'} 26. Rh4 {. The pin on the c-file proves to be unimportant.} Ne5 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Rd4 Rb2 28. a4 Rcc2 29. Rf1 Ng4) 26... Rc5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Black has a passed c-pawn in mind. This is faster than} 26... Rxa2 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Rh4 b5 28. Bf1 Ra4 29. Rd4) 27. Rh4 Ne5 (27... b5 {Andrew Soltis} 28. a4 $1 bxa4 29. Bf1 {winning a piece}) 28. Rxc5 {Nigel Davies: '?!' Nigel Davies: 'Making matters worse.'} ({White should have played} 28. Rf1 {though} Rxa2 {leaves him a pawn down.}) 28... bxc5 29. Ra4 c4 30. h6 Kf8 31. Ra8+ (31. f4 {Andrew Soltis} Ng4 32. Rxc4 $2 Rxg2+ 33. Kxg2 Nxe3+) 31... Ke7 {Andrew Soltis: 'The threat of ...c3-c2 wins a pawn'} 32. Rc8 {Nigel Davies: 'Placing the rook behind Black's passed pawn, which is the recommended course of action in the endgame. However, Black is just a pawn up here with very active pieces.'} Rxa2 $19 33. Bf1 Rc2 34. Kg2 $2 ({White can prolong matters with} 34. Rc7+ {Andrew Soltis}) (34. Rh8 $2 {Andrew Soltis} c3 35. Rxh7 Rxf2) 34... Ng4 35. Kg1 (35. Bxc4 Nxe3+ {wins the bishop.}) 35... Rxf2 36. Bxc4 Rf3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'also winning is'} 36... Kf6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'and ...Kg6 e.g.'} 37. Rh8 {Andrew Soltis} Rd2 38. Bb5 Kg6 {. The technical phase is crowned by a cute finish}) 37. Kg2 Rxe3 38. Rh8 Nxh6 39. Rxh7 Ng4 40. Bb5 Rb3 41. Bc6 Rb2+ 42. Kg1 Ne5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 43. Ba8 Rb8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} {Andrew Soltis: 'After'} 44. Bh1 ({The bishop has no good squares} 44. Bg2 {Andrew Soltis} Rb3 45. Kh2 f4 $1 46. gxf4 Ng4+ 47. Kg1 Rb1+ 48. Bf1 Ne3) 44... Rb1+ {Andrew Soltis} 45. Kg2 Ng4 {the e-pawn marches to victory} 0-1

