{Chess Informant Vasiukov,Evgeni: 'Vasiukov,E: 'Chess Informant''} 1. e4 {Vasiukov,Evgeni Vasiukov,Evgeni Vasiukov,Evgeni Vasiukov,Evgeni: 'Vasiukov,E'} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nb5 d6 6. c4 (6. Bf4 {– Games Nos. 91 and 98.}) 6... a6 7. N5c3 Nf6 8. Be2 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Na3 b6 11. Be3 Bd7 12. Rc1 Qb8 (12... Qc7 13. Nd5 $1) 13. f3 Ra7 14. Nc2 Rd8 15. Qe1 Be8 16. Qf2 Rb7 17. a4 a5 $11 18. Nd4 Nxd4 19. Bxd4 Nd7 20. Qg3 Bf6 21. Bxf6 Nxf6 22. Rfd1 e5 $1 23. Qh4 h6 24. Rd2 Nd7 25. Bd1 Nc5 26. f4 exf4 27. Qxf4 Ne6 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'On the threshold of time-trouble Black deviates from the correct path –'} 27... Re7 28. Bc2 Re5 ({"in the spirit of the blockade", as Taimanov writes; or} 28... Bc6) 29. Rcd1 ({or} 29. Rf1) 29... f6 {with a sound and at least equal position. The removal of the knight from the ideal c5-square ultimately leads to a breakthrough by White at this initially well-fortified point!}) 28. Qg3 Qc7 29. Nd5 Qc5+ 30. Kh1 Bc6 (30... Ng5 $5) 31. Rc3 $1 Ng5 32. Bc2 Bxd5 {Garry Kasparov: 'This exchange leaves Fischer with his favourite bishop against a knight.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'However, after'} 32... Re8 {Garry Kasparov: 'Taimanov was concerned about'} 33. h4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ', as after'} {Garry Kasparov: 'not'} Nxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (33... Nh7 {, Black's defence, especially in time-trouble, is not easy:} 34. Z0 (34. b3 Re5 $6 (34... Re6 $1 {is correct}) 35. Ne3 $1 Nf6 $2 ({or} 35... Bd7 36. Rd5 Qc7 37. c5 $1 bxc5 38. Rcxc5 $1 {with an extra pawn}) 36. Nf5 g6 37. Rxd6 Nh5 38. Qg4 Kh7 39. Qxh5 $3 Rxf5 (39... gxh5 40. Rxh6+ Kg8 41. Rg3+ Kf8 42. Rh8#) 40. exf5 Qxd6 (40... gxh5 41. f6+ {and mate}) 41. Qg4 Re7 42. fxg6+ fxg6 43. Rg3) (34. Ne3 $5 Re6 $1 ({but not} 34... Bxe4 $2 35. Bxe4 Rxe4 36. Rd5 Qc7 37. Nf5 g6 38. h5 {and wins}) 35. Nf5 Rg6 36. Qe3 $1 Re6 $1 37. Qf4 ({or} 37. Qd4 Nf6 38. Rg3 g6 39. Nxd6 Ra7 40. Qxc5 bxc5 {with drawing chances}) 37... Nf6 $1 38. Rxd6 Rxd6 39. Nxd6 Rd7 40. e5 Re7 41. Re3 Ng4 42. Re2 Nxe5 (42... h5 $5) 43. Nf5 Re6 44. Nd4 Re7 {, maintaining the balance, albeit with difficulty.})) 34. Bxe4 Rxe4 35. Nf6+ $18) 33. Rxd5 Qc7 34. e5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer immediately expands the scope of his bishop.'} dxe5 (34... Qc6 $5) 35. Qxe5 Rdb8 {Garry Kasparov: 'Another small step towards ruin.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'The cool-headed'} 35... Ne6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '"would have allowed the safe consolidation of the position." (Taimanov). For example:'} 36. Qe4 g6 37. Rxd8+ Nxd8 38. h4 h5 39. Re3 Ne6 {etc.}) 36. Bf5 $1 Qxe5 ({Or} 36... g6 37. h4 $1 {.}) 37. Rxe5 g6 38. h4 $1 Nh7 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Another time-trouble error.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'The simple'} 38... f6 {Garry Kasparov: 'would have forced a rook endgame, which, though somewhat inferior for Black, is in principle tenable:'} 39. Be6+ ({or} 39. Rb5 gxf5 40. hxg5 hxg5 41. Rxf5 Kg7 {(Taimanov)} 42. Rd3 Kg6 43. Rfd5 Re8 44. Rd6 g4) 39... Kg7 40. Ree3 Nxe6 41. Rxe6 Kf7 42. Rc6 Re8 {etc.}) 39. Bg4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'The bishop occupies the long diagonal and begins to dominate the knight. It is strange that Taimanov did not take this bitter lesson into account and six months later, in his match with Fischer, he again allowed the same balance of forces (Games Nos.91 and 93).'} Nf6 40. Bf3 Rd7 $2 {Garry Kasparov: '"The last move before the time control and already the decisive mistake.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"'} 40... Rc7 {Garry Kasparov: 'was essential, leaving the d7-square free for the knight." (Taimanov)'}) 41. Rb5 Rd4 {Garry Kasparov: 'The sealed move.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'In Taimanov's opinion, the modest'} 41... Rdd8 {Garry Kasparov: 'was more tenacious, but even then after'} 42. b3 $1 {the threat of c4-c5 is still impending.}) 42. c5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'An energetic start to the adjournment session!'} ({"In our analysis," writes Taimanov, "Vasyukov and I were still hoping to save the game in the event of the tempting Garry Kasparov: 'But, in turn, earlier he could have won by'} 42. h5 g5 43. c5 Re8 (43... g4 $1 44. Bc6 Nxh5 45. cxb6 (45. Rxb6 Rbd8) ({, or} 45. Kg1 Rxa4 46. Rxb6 Ra1+ 47. Kf2 Rd8 {is also unclear}) 45... g3 $1 46. Bf3 Rxa4 47. Rc1 Nf4 {and ... Ne6.}) 44. cxb6 g4 {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of'} 45. b7 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: '.'} (45. Bc6 $1) 45... gxf3 46. b8=Q f2 $1 47. Qg3+ Ng4 48. Rc1 Re3 49. Z0 (49. Qh4 Rde4 {with a draw after} 50. Rg5+ hxg5 51. Qxg5+ {. But Fischer also saw this..." However, at the end of this variation Black gains not a draw, but a win –} Kh8 52. Rc8+ Re8 {.}) ({. Therefore White should make a draw by} 49. Rb3 {(instead of 49 Qh4?)} Rxg3 50. Rxg3 Re4 51. Rf1 {.} (51. Z0))) 42... Rxh4+ 43. Kg1 {Garry Kasparov: 'The outcome is decided by the total domination of the bishop over the knight.'} Rb4 ({Not} 43... Rxa4 44. Rxb6 {, and White's passed pawn will cost a piece.}) {Garry Kasparov: '"The clearest way, which was certainly decided on during adjournment analysis.'} 44. Rxb4 $1 ({"In the event of} 44. Rb3 Rxb3 45. Rxb3 Nd7 46. c6 Ne5 47. Bd5 Rc8 $1 48. Rxb6 Kf8 {things would have been much more difficult for White." (Taimanov). For example:} 49. Ra6 Ke7 50. Rxa5 Kd6 51. b4 f5 52. Bb3 Nxc6 53. Rd5+ Ke7 54. Rc5 Kd6 {with drawing chances.}) 44... axb4 45. Rc4 bxc5 (45... Nd7 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 46. c6 {Garry Kasparov: 'etc'} Nc5 47. a5 $1 $16) 46. Rxc5 Kg7 ({Or} 46... g5 47. a5 g4 48. Bc6 Rc8 49. Rc4 {and a5-a6.}) 47. a5 Re8 48. Rc1 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '"A text-book manoeuvre!" (Taimanov). The rook is placed behind the passed pawn.'} Re5 ({If} 48... Re7 {, then} 49. a6 Ra7 50. Bb7 {etc.}) 49. Ra1 Re7 50. Kf2 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'The start of a victorious raid by the king.'} Ne8 51. a6 Ra7 52. Ke3 Nc7 53. Bb7 Ne6 54. Ra5 $1 Kf6 55. Kd3 Ke7 56. Kc4 Kd6 57. Rd5+ Kc7 58. Kb5 {Garry Kasparov: '. Fischer conducted this ending in his patent style.'} 1-0

