1. c4 {Garry Kasparov Garry Kasparov: 'Just as he did in the 'Match of the Century', and then in the 4th and 6th games of the match in Buenos Aires (1971), Petrosian avoids 1 d4!, which could have set his opponent opening problems. Possibly he was afraid of not being able to maintain the tension, which such concrete play demands.'} c5 ({In the 2nd game of the 'Match of the Century' Fischer replied} 1... g6 {Garry Kasparov} 2. Z0 ({, and after} 2. Nc3 c5 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 Nc6 5. Nf3 (5. e3 e6 {is equal}) 5... e6 6. O-O Nge7 7. d3 (7. e3 O-O 8. d4 {is more active}) 7... O-O 8. Bd2 d5 9. a3 b6 10. Rb1 Bb7 11. b4 $2 (11. cxd5 Nxd5 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 {is equal}) 11... cxb4 12. axb4 dxc4 13. dxc4 Rc8 {he seized the initiative.}) ({. In the 4th game Petrosian 'corrected himself' –} 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 {, but again was unable to engage in a serious battle:} d5 4. Nf3 Bg7 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Bd2 c5 7. Rc1 Nxc3 8. Bxc3 cxd4 9. Nxd4 O-O 10. e3 Qd5 $1 11. Nb5 Qxd1+ 12. Rxd1 Nc6 {with equality.})) 2. Nf3 g6 3. e4 (3. d4 {(Garry Kasparov) – Game No.96.}) 3... Bg7 4. d4 Nc6 {Garry Kasparov: 'In my opinion, it is better to develop the knight on the 3rd move,'} (4... cxd4 5. Nxd4 Nc6 $5) ({whereas at this moment the variation with} 4... Qa5+ $5 {(Garry Kasparov) is more interesting (the source game: Gipslis-Stein, 34th USSR Championship, Tbilisi 1966).}) 5. dxc5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (5. d5 {Garry Kasparov} Nd4 {is unclear – Pachman-Smyslov, Havana 1962.}) 5... Qa5+ 6. Nfd2 $1 (6. Bd2 {Garry Kasparov} Qxc5 7. Nc3 d6 8. Be2 Nf6 9. O-O O-O 10. h3 {is also good. In both cases White has a favourable version of the Maróczy set-up.}) 6... Qxc5 7. Nb3 Qb6 8. Be2 d6 9. O-O Nf6 ({Black also fails to equalize with} 9... Be6 {Garry Kasparov} 10. Be3 Qc7 11. Qd2 Nf6 12. f3 O-O 13. Nc3 {(Olafsson-Larsen, Buenos Aires 1980). Black has lost time on his queen sortie, whereas White has made the useful move Nb3.}) 10. Nc3 O-O 11. Be3 $14 Qd8 12. Rc1 Be6 13. Nd4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'What for?'} ({After} 13. f3 $1 {(Garry Kasparov) Black has a difficult position: he does not have the usual relieving knight exchange on d4 and his queen cannot move out from d8. For example:} Rc8 ({or} 13... Ne5 14. Nd5) 14. Nd5 Nd7 15. Qd2 b6 16. Nd4 {with persistent pressure. But, apparently, Petrosian was thinking only of a draw...}) 13... Nxd4 14. Bxd4 Qa5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Black breathes more freely.'} 15. f4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Not a bad move, but one that demands very concrete, energetic play: it nevertheless weakens the pawn structure (one recalls a similar attempt by Larsen – Game No.96).'} (15. Nd5 $6 {Garry Kasparov} Nxe4 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. Qd4+ Nf6 {did not work}) ({but} 15. Qd3 $5 {(Garry Kasparov) and then b2-b3 came into consideration.}) 15... a6 16. f5 ({Petrosian probably did not even consider the variation} 16. b4 $5 {Garry Kasparov} Qxb4 17. Rb1 Qa5 18. Rxb7 {: after} Rfe8 {the weakness of the c4-pawn promises Black sufficient counterplay.}) 16... Bd7 17. c5 $1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'White has nevertheless pushed forward...'} Bc6 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 18. cxd6 exd6 19. fxg6 $6 (19. Bc4 $1 Z0 {Garry Kasparov} ({. He had to reckon with} 19... Nxe4 {(Garry Kasparov) – here after} 20. fxg6 hxg6 21. Bxg7 Kxg7 22. Nxe4 Bxe4 23. Qxd6 Bf5 24. Bd5 $1 {White has a dangerous initiative.}) (19... Kh8 {(Garry Kasparov) is more cautious, but in this case too after} 20. Rf4 $5 ({or} 20. Bd5) 20... Rae8 21. Qd3 {White retains somewhat the better chances.})) 19... fxg6 20. Bc4+ Kh8 21. Bd5 $6 (21. Nd5 $5) ({Or} 21. Qd3 {Garry Kasparov} Rae8 22. Rce1 Ng4 23. Nd5 Ne5 {with equality.}) 21... Rae8 $1 22. h3 $6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Exaggerated peaceableness.'} Bxd5 23. Nxd5 Nxd5 24. Rxf8+ Rxf8 25. exd5 Qb4 $1 26. Bxg7+ Kxg7 {Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw on the 44th move...'} 27. b3 Qf4 $1 $15 28. Rc3 Qf2+ 29. Kh2 Rf7 30. a4 h5 31. Rc4 Re7 32. Qa1+ Qf6 33. Qc3 Qxc3 34. Rxc3 Kf6 35. Rf3+ Kg5 36. Rf8 Re3 37. g3 Re2+ 38. Kg1 Rb2 39. h4+ $1 Kg4 40. Rf6 Kxg3 41. Rxg6+ Kxh4 42. Rxd6 Rxb3 43. Rg6 Rb4 44. Rg7 1/2-1/2

