{ChessCalisthenics: 'C15: French: 3 Nc3 Bb4: Unusual White 4th moves' Andrew Soltis: 'At Rovinj-Zagreb Fischer was in trouble as White against Korchnoi and needed miraculous escapes against Borislav Ivkov and Walter Browne. Nevertheless, he managed to exact some revenge. Ten years before Wolfgang Uhlmann had defeated him impressively in a Winawer French. They met again two years later, and drew in a difficult Winawer. But Fischer won easily in a third Winawer at Herceg-Novi, and this was their fourth.'} 1. e4 e6 2. Nc3 ({Andrew Soltis: 'The chief virtue of this transpositional trick is to avoid'} 2. d4 d5 {1-0 (31) Fischer,R-Uhlmann,W Zagreb 1970 HOW C15 DM [Myers]} (2... c5 {, a favorite move order of Bent Larsen.})) 2... d5 3. d4 Bb4 4. a3 Bxc3+ 5. bxc3 dxe4 6. Qg4 {Andrew Soltis: 'Another suspect line, like the Two Knights Variation of the Caro-Kann, that Fischer seemed attracted to on general principles (B! v. N) despite his below expectation results with it.'} Nf6 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 Rg6 (8... Nbd7 $5) 9. Qe3 Nc6 $6 (9... b6 $5 10. Ne2 Bb7 11. Nf4 Rg8 12. Bb2 Nbd7 $132) 10. Bb2 (10. Ne2 Ne7 11. Nf4 Ned5 $15 {(Keres 69, eco 74/81)}) 10... Qd6 $146 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A new move which disappeared after this game. Black prepares quick queenside castling but allows White'snext move, compared with'} 10... Ne7 11. c4 $5 (11. Ne2) (11. O-O-O b5 (11... Qd6 12. f3 Nf5 13. Qe1 Bd7 14. fxe4 Qf4+ 15. Kb1 Qxe4 16. Nf3 O-O-O 17. Ne5 Rg7 18. Rg1 Qxe1 19. Rxe1 Ng4 20. Nxd7 Kxd7 21. d5 Nxh2 22. c4 Rg6 23. Rh1 Nxf1 24. dxe6+ fxe6 25. Rxh7+ Kc8 26. Rxf1 {Wade,R (2375)-Padevsky,N (2430)/Munich 1958/MCD/1/2-1/2}) 12. Nh3 a6 13. c4 bxc4 14. Bxc4 Rb8 15. d5 exd5 16. Nf4 Rxb2 17. Nxg6 Rb6 18. Nxe7 Qxe7 19. Bxd5 Qe5 20. Bb3 Qa1+ 21. Kd2 Qe5 22. Kc1 Qa1+ 23. Kd2 Qe5 24. Ke1 Bg4 25. f3 {Ligterink,G (2470)-Van der Wiel,J (2590)/Ter Apel 1987/CBM 002/0-1 (38)}) (11. f3 Ned5 $1 {and 13. ...e3}) 11... b6 12. Ne2 Bb7 13. Nf4 $132 {(Gligoric 75)}) ({Andrew Soltis: 'At Herceg-Novi, Uhlmann tried'} 10... b6 11. f3 (11. Nh3 Bb7 12. c4 Qe7 13. Nf4 Rg8 14. d5 Nb8 15. Qc3 Nbd7 16. dxe6 fxe6 17. c5 bxc5 18. Qh3 Rg4 19. Nh5 O-O-O 20. O-O-O Rdg8 21. Nxf6 Nxf6 22. Qb3 Nd5 23. g3 R4g5 24. Bb5 Rf5 25. Rhe1 Rxf2 {Grabarczyk,B (2437)-Schmidt,W (2415)/Police 2007/CBM 119 ext/1-0 (41)}) (11. O-O-O Bb7 {but Fischer expanded effortlessly and won after} 12. h3 h5 13. c4 Qd6 14. Ne2 O-O-O 15. Nf4 Rgg8 16. Be2 Qe7 17. d5 $1) 11... exf3 12. Nxf3 Bb7 13. c4 Ne7 14. d5 Nf5 15. Qc3 Qe7 16. Bd3 exd5+ 17. Qe5 Ne4 18. cxd5 Bxd5 19. Qxd5 Nc3+ 20. Qe5 Re6 21. Bxc3 Rxe5+ 22. Bxe5 Nd4 23. O-O Nxf3+ 24. gxf3 O-O-O 25. Bg3 {Kazhgaleyev,M (2490)-Anapolsky,S (2455)/Orel 1994/EXT 1997/1-0 (36)}) (10... e5 11. O-O-O Qe7 12. h3 a6 13. d5 $6 Nb8 14. Ne2 Nbd7 15. Ng3 Nc5 $15 {(Euwe 81) Fichtl-Bartosek Vimperk 1951}) 11. f3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (11. c4 $5 e5 12. d5 {(Vukovic 35)}) 11... exf3 $6 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Informant recommended'} 11... e5 $5 {Andrew Soltis: 'and gave'} 12. fxe4 ({but} 12. O-O-O {threatening fxe4 is more in keeping with the spirit of the opening e.g.} Nd5 (12... exf3 13. Nxf3 e4 14. d5) 13. Qe1 exd4 14. cxd4) 12... exd4 13. cxd4 Nxe4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 14. O-O-O f5 $13 {Myers: '(Vukovic, Sokolov 70, Zeuthen/Jarlnaes 71, eco 74/81, Gligoric 75, Levy 75, Euwe 81)' Andrew Soltis: 'as equalizing'} (14... Re6 15. d5) (14... Bf5 15. g4 Rxg4 16. Bh3)) 12. Nxf3 Bd7 13. O-O-O O-O-O ({a tempting alternative was} 13... b5 {to secure control of c4. After} 14. Bxb5 Rxg2 (14... Rb8 15. c4 {White's edge is evident. But}) (14... Na5 {may offer enough compensation}) 15. Ne5) 14. c4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Now it becomes easy to see now why Fischer was fond of this variation : White's bishops come alive, and he can change the pawn structure favorably, with a timely d4-d5, h3-h3 and g2-g4. Most of the tactics favor him. For example, if Black tries to get off the hot file with 14. ...Qf8 and 15. ...Qh6 he invites 15.d5! (15. ...exd5 16.cxd5 Re8 17.Qg1 Bg4? 18.dxc6 Bxf3 19.Qxa7 and wins)'} Ng4 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Because of the vulnerability of a7, Black should try} 14... Kb8 {and if} 15. h3 Be8) 15. Qd2 f5 ({unpalatable but safer was} 15... Kb8 16. h3 Nf6 17. g4) 16. d5 $1 Nb8 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black is losing after'} 16... Nce5 17. Nxe5 Nxe5 18. Qd4 {Myers: '(Sokolov 70)'} Nf7 19. Qxa7) (16... exd5 $2 17. cxd5 Ne7 18. h3 (18. Qd4 Kb8 19. h3 Nf6 (19... Nh6 20. Ne5 Rgg8 {holds out hope}) 20. Ne5 Rg7 21. Nc4) 18... Nf6 19. Ne5 $1 {(Gligoric 75)}) (16... Ne7 17. h3 Nf6 18. Nh4 Ne4 19. Qd4 {(Levy 75)}) 17. h3 Nf6 18. Ne5 (18. Nh4 Ne4 19. Qe3 Qg3 {(Levy 76)}) 18... Ne4 ({Now on} 18... Rg7 {White should sidestep the dramatic} 19. Nc6 ({instead} 19. Qh6 {wins smoothly} Ne8 20. c5 $1 Qxc5 (20... Qe7 21. c6) 21. dxe6) 19... Bxc6 20. Bxf6 Qxa3+ 21. Bb2 Qg3) 19. Qd4 ({Also good was} 19. Qa5 {since} Nf2 {is crushed by} 20. c5 Qf8 21. Nxg6 hxg6 22. Be5) 19... Rg3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'The rook lands in trouble here. Afte'} 19... Rgg8 $142 20. Qxa7 (20. Nf7 $6 Qf4+ 21. Kb1 Rdf8 22. dxe6 Bxe6 {(Gligoric 75)}) 20... b6 {(Gligoric 75)} (20... Qe7 {Whtie is close to a win but not quite there yet}) (20... Ng3 $2 21. c5 Qf8 22. c6)) 20. Nf7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Qf4+ 21. Kb1 c5 (21... Rf8 $2 22. dxe6 $18) ({White's primary idea is a last rank mate} 21... Re8 22. dxe6 Rxe6 23. Qh8+ Re8 24. Qxe8+ $1) 22. Qe5 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Most all of Black's army will be hanging now.'} Qxe5 23. Bxe5 Rdg8 (23... Rf8 24. Nd6+ Nxd6 25. Bxd6 $18 {(Levy 75)}) 24. Bd3 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The win willbe clear after Bxe4 and Nd6+. e.g. 24. . ..Nf2 25.Nd6+ Kd8 26.Nxb7+ Ke7 27.dxe6 Bxe6 28.Bxg3 Rxg3 29.Rde1'} ({Hoping for} 24. Bxg3 Rxg3 {with survival chances}) 24... Rxg2 25. Bxe4 fxe4 26. Nd6+ Kc7 (26... Kd8 27. Nxb7+ Ke7 28. d6+ Ke8 29. Nxc5 {the win is straightforward.}) 27. Nxe4+ Kb6 28. Nf6 Ba4 (28... Rf8 29. dxe6 Bxe6 (29... Ba4 30. Rd6+) 30. Rd6+) 29. Nxg8 Bxc2+ 30. Kc1 Nd7 {ChessCalisthenics: '??'} (30... Bxd1 31. Nf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'wins a piece.'} Ba4 32. Bxb8 Rc2+ 33. Kb1 exd5 34. Nd7+ Ka5 35. cxd5 Rc4 36. Bc7+ b6 $18) 31. Rdg1 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'White wals away with a full rook'} Rxg1+ 32. Rxg1 $18 1-0

