{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer's skill with heavy pieces became more pronounced in his later years. His powerful use of rooks against Geller at Palma 1970 and queen and rooks against Matulovic at Skopje 1967 and against Petrosian in the eighth game of the 1971 match are testimony to that. IN the following game he converts routine pressure against an isolated d-pawn into a fine demonstration of winning technique.'} {Sokolov,V: 'Chess Informant''Chess Informant Sokolov,Vladimir: 'Sokolov,V: 'Chess Informant'''} 1. e4 {Andrew Soltis Sokolov,V Sokolov,V Sokolov,V: 'Sokolov,Vladimir Sokolov,Vladimir: 'Sokolov,V''} e6 2. d4 c5 3. Nf3 cxd4 4. Nxd4 a6 5. Nc3 Qc7 6. g3 Nf6 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. O-O d6 (8... Be7 9. Re1 O-O 10. Nxc6 dxc6 11. e5 $16) 9. Re1 Bd7 {Andrew Soltis: 'But without tactics to support it, Nxc6 was considered by Sicilian canon of the 1960s to be a positional error that strengthened Black's center (...bxc6) or helped his development (...Bxc6).'} ({Both} 9... Be7 {(Andrew Soltis) and}) (9... Rb8 {(Andrew Soltis) allow White to isolate the enemy pawns by tactical means with} 10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. e5 dxe5 12. Rxe5 $1) 10. Nxc6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} bxc6 11. b3 (11. Na4 Rb8 $1 12. c4 c5 $11) 11... Be7 ({In the only previous international game with this line, Black stopped White's next move with} 11... e5 {(Andrew Soltis) . But then} 12. Na4 Be7 13. c4 c5 14. Nc3 Bc6 15. Bg5) 12. e5 $1 $146 {Sokolov,V: '!' '!' Sokolov,V: 'N''N Sokolov,Vladimir: 'N!' Sokolov,Vladimir: 'Sokolov,V: '!' Sokolov,V: 'N''' Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({A temporary pawn sacrifice - temporary because Black's ninth move denies him ...Nd7. White would have little after} 12. Na4 {Andrew Soltis} Rb8 13. c4 c5 14. Nc3 Bc6) 12... dxe5 13. Bb2 O-O 14. Qe2 Nd5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({This threatens 15. ...f6 and seems to solve Black's opening problems. But it actually trades an isolated c-pawn for an isolated d-pawn. The extra pawn couldn't be held} 14... Bd6 {Andrew Soltis} 15. Na4) (14... Bc5 {Andrew Soltis} 15. Na4 Bd4 16. Rad1 Bxb2 17. Nxb2 e4 18. Nc4) ({But Black would stand well with} 14... Rfd8 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) and then} 15. Na4 (15. Qxe5 Qxe5 16. Rxe5 Be8) 15... e4 16. Nc3 Bb4) 15. Nxd5 (15. Qxe5 $2 {Andrew Soltis} Qxe5 16. Rxe5 Bf6) 15... exd5 ({no better was} 15... cxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 16. Bxe5 Bd6 (16... Qa5 17. c4) 17. Bxd6 Qxd6 18. c4) 16. Bxe5 Bd6 ({Now this was forced} 16... Qa5 $2 {Andrew Soltis} 17. Bxg7) 17. Bxd6 Qxd6 18. c4 Be6 ({Black is still fairly well off after} 18... Rae8 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Qd2 d4 $1 20. b4 c5) 19. Rad1 a5 (19... Qc5 20. cxd5 cxd5 21. Qe5 $16) ({It was too late for} 19... d4 {Andrew Soltis} 20. Qe5 $1 Rad8 21. Qxd6 Rxd6 22. c5 Rdd8 23. Re4 $1 {. So Black tries to liquidate the queenside.}) 20. cxd5 cxd5 ({Ridding himself of the bishop actually makes it harder to defend d5, e.g.} 20... Bxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Qh5 Rfd8 22. Bxd5 cxd5 23. Re5 $1) 21. Rd4 ({Black holds hidden trumps such as} 21. Qh5 {Andrew Soltis} Rfd8 22. Re5 $2 Bg4 $1) 21... a4 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Tactically sound (22.bxa4 Ra5 and ...Rfa8) but positionally awful. White now gets an easily protected pased h-pawn and a second weak pawn to attack. Better was'} (21... Rfc8 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and ...Rc5. For example'} 22. Red1 (22. Qd3 {Andrew Soltis} Rc5 23. Rd1 Rd8 {and ...Qe5}) 22... Rc5 23. Qd2 Rd8 $16) 22. b4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} a3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Otherwise 23.a3 and White just piles up on d5 and watches for the right time to push the b-pawn.'} 23. Qd2 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Rab8 (23... Rfb8 {Andrew Soltis} 24. Bxd5 Bxd5 25. Rxd5 Qxb4 $2 26. Rd8+) (23... Ra4 {Andrew Soltis} 24. Rb1 Rd8 25. Rb3 {and 26.b5}) 24. Re3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Rfc8 ({A counterattack on b4} 24... Rb5 {(Andrew Soltis) is repulsed by} 25. Rxa3 Rfb8 26. Rb3) 25. Rxa3 $18 Qe5 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({better was} 25... Qb6 {Andrew Soltis} 26. Rb3 Rc4 (26... Rc1+ 27. Qxc1 Qxd4)) 26. Rad3 h6 ({Luft is late.} 26... Rb5 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Bxd5 Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Rxd5 29. Rxd5 Rc1+ $2 30. Qxc1 Qxd5 31. Qc8+) ({Black's 25th move may have been based on} 26... Bf5 {Andrew Soltis} 27. Rxd5 Qa1+ {but} 28. Qd1 Rc1 29. Rd8+) 27. Bxd5 Bxd5 28. Rxd5 Qa1+ 29. Kg2 Kh7 ({After} 29... Rc1 {(Andrew Soltis) White can approximate the game with} 30. Rd8+ Rxd8 31. Rxd8+ Kh7 32. Qd3+ g6 {and now} 33. Qd4 $1 Qxd4 34. Rxd4 Ra1 35. b5 $1 ({rather than} 35. Rd2 Rb1 36. a3 Rb3) 35... Rxa2 36. Rb4 Kg7 37. b6 Ra8 38. Kf3 Kf6 39. Ke4 Ke6 40. b7 Rb8 41. Rb6+ Kd7 42. Kd5) 30. R3d4 Rc1 31. Qd3+ {Andrew Soltis: 'This was not designed to stop ...Rg1+ and ...Qf1+ because White's king would be safe on h4. His idea was rather to probe the kingside pawns before resorting to pushing his passed pawns.'} g6 32. Rd7 (32. a3 $2 {Andrew Soltis} Rc3 {or}) (32. Qe2 {Andrew Soltis} Re1 {is negative progress}) 32... Rg1+ 33. Kh3 Qxa2 ({Annotators said White wins slowly after} 33... Kg7 {Andrew Soltis} 34. Rf4 ({but mate is preferable} 34. Rxf7+ Kxf7 35. Qc4+ Kf8 (35... Kg7 36. Qc7+ Kf6 37. Rd6+) 36. Qc5+ Kg7 37. Qd5 Kh8 38. Qe5+ Kh7 39. Rd7+) 34... Rf8 35. b5) 34. R4d6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Resigns. The threat is Qxg6+. and 34. ...Rg8 35.Rf6 Rg7 36.Re7 followed by 37.Qd7 is convincing. For example 36. ...h5 37. Qd7 Qa8 38.Rfxf7 and Black's counterattack dies after 38. ...Qg2+ 39.Kh4 g5+ 40.Kxh5'} 1-0

