{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer believed he suffered from lack of practice at Belgrade and decided to play himself into shape in 1970. He stayed in Yugoslavia for a "Tournament of Peace" that began on Katerina Island, reportedly setting 41 conditions for his participation. All were met but he didn't decide to play until an hour and a half before the first round.'} 1. d4 {Andrew Soltis} Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. f3 O-O 6. Be3 b6 $5 7. Bd3 $1 Bb7 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A trap, well-known at this time, goes'} 7... c5 $2 8. e5 $1 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and wins'}) 8. Nge2 c5 9. d5 ({Now} 9. Qd2 {Andrew Soltis} Nc6 $1 {so that} 10. O-O (10. d5 Ne5 11. Nc1 e6 12. Be2 Ba6) 10... Ng4 $1 11. fxg4 cxd4 {is fine for Black}) 9... e6 10. O-O (10. Bg5 $5) 10... exd5 11. exd5 ({an unassuming alternative is} 11. cxd5 {Andrew Soltis} Ba6 {White hopes to get an improved version of Szabo's setu in Game 20 - improved because ...b6, ...Bb7 is marginally useful.}) 11... Nbd7 $1 12. Bg5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Black is not allowed to set up the cohesive formation of ...Ne8, ...f5, ...Ne5, ...Qe7, ... Nf6 and ...Rae8.'} h6 13. Bh4 Ne5 14. f4 Nxd3 15. Qxd3 Qd7 ({here} 15... Bc8 {(Andrew Soltis) is natural, but Fischer may not have trusted} 16. Ne4 g5 (16... Bf5 17. N2g3 Bxe4 18. Nxe4 g5 {to get out of the pin})) 16. Bxf6 {Andrew Soltis: 'White eliminates the most annoying enemy piece - at the expense of turning the dark-squared bishop into Black's best piece.'} ({Now} 16. f5 {Andrew Soltis} g5 17. Bf2 (17. Bg3 Nh5 18. Bf2 Be5 {is fairly balanced despite Black's holes.}) 17... Ng4) 16... Bxf6 17. f5 g5 ({White appears to have a promising game} 17... gxf5 {Andrew Soltis} 18. Rxf5 Bg7 19. Raf1) (17... Kg7 $2 {Andrew Soltis} 18. fxg6 fxg6 19. Nf4 $1 {and wins}) 18. Qh3 {Andrew Soltis: 'Ghitescu picks door number three, after seeing 18. ...Kh7? 19.Ne4! Qe7 20.N2c3'} ({OF the three candidate moves,} 18. Ne4 {(Andrew Soltis) promises good chances after} Bxb2 (18... Be5 19. f6 Bxb2 20. Nxg5 $1 hxg5 21. Rf5) 19. N2c3 (19. Rab1)) ({Better is} 18. Ng3 {(Andrew Soltis) e.g.} Bd4+ 19. Kh1 Rae8 20. f6 {followed by Nf5 or Nce4} Qg4 $2 (20... Re3 21. Qd1 {and Qh5}) 21. Nf5) 18... Be5 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (18... Kh7 19. Ng3 { h5}) 19. Qxh6 f6 20. Rf3 ({trying for a middlegame kill with} 20. h4 {Andrew Soltis} Rf7 21. hxg5 {turns in Black's favor after} Rh7 22. Qg6+ Rg7 23. Qh5 Rxg5 24. Qf3 Qh7 25. g3 (25. Ne4 Bh2+ 26. Kf2 Rxf5 27. Nxf6+ Rxf6 28. Qxf6 Rf8) 25... Bc8 {and ...Rxf5}) 20... Qh7 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'White's task is easier after two sets of heavy pieces are traded'} (20... Rf7 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Rh3 Rh7) 21. Qxh7+ Kxh7 $44 22. h4 {Andrew Soltis: '?' Andrew Soltis: 'Suddenly WHite must choose between active and passive defense. The energetic text is based on 22. ...gxh4 23.Rh3 but 22. ...g4 leaves White with two very weak knigside pawns. Better was 22.h3 and Kf1'} g4 23. Rd3 ({White's rooks are a tacitcal problem which he should begin solving with} 23. Re3 {(Andrew Soltis) and Re4. On} Rae8 24. Re4 (24. Kf2 Bc8) 24... Bc8 25. Rf1 $1 (25. Rxg4 Bxf5 {trapping the rook}) 25... Rg8 {he has fair chances.}) 23... Rae8 ({Black's pressure is so strong he should wait on the ...b5 idea, since} 23... Ba6 {Andrew Soltis} 24. b3 b5 25. cxb5 Bxb5 26. Nxb5 $1 {will favor White after} Bxa1 27. Nxd6 Be5 28. Nc4 {and d5-d6}) 24. Ng3 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Necessary was} 24. Rf1 {(Andrew Soltis) with the idea of Nf4-e6}) 24... Ba6 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now 25.Nb5 Bxb2 26.Rb1 Bxb5 27.cxb5 Bd4+ and ...Re5 must win'} 25. b3 b5 26. cxb5 Bxb5 27. Nxb5 ({Forced.} 27. Re3 $4 {Andrew Soltis} Bd4) 27... Bxa1 28. Nxd6 Bd4+ {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'The rook is too much for the king after 26.Rxd4? cxd4 27.Nxe8 Rxe8 28. Kf2 d3.'} 29. Kf1 Re5 30. Nc4 Rxd5 31. Ne3 $44 Rd7 32. Nxg4 Rg7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Material is relatively equal but Black prevails if he can win a pawn and/or break the coordination of the knights.'} 33. Nf2 Rfg8 34. Nfe4 (34. Nge4 $2 {Andrew Soltis} Rxg2 35. Ng5+ R8xg5 36. hxg5 Rxf2+) ({the rule of thumb - that a player who is down the exchange does not want to trade rooks is supported by} 34. Nh5 {Andrew Soltis} Rxg2 35. Rxd4 cxd4 36. Nxf6+ Kh6 37. Nxg8+ Rxg8 38. Ke2 (38. Ne4 Rg4) 38... Rg3) 34... Kh6 $13 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'This takes the check out of Nxf6 and creates the possibility of a winning plan, a last-rank mate'} 35. h5 $2 (35. Ne2 $5 $11 Rxg2 {Andrew Soltis} 36. Nxd4 cxd4 37. Nxf6 R8g3 $1 38. Rxd4 Rxa2 {and ...Rxb3-b1+}) 35... Rg4 36. Ke2 ({White has no counterplay} 36. b4 $2 {Andrew Soltis} Be5 $1 37. Kf2 cxb4) 36... Be5 37. Kf2 R4g7 38. Rf3 Rg4 39. Rd3 a6 {Andrew Soltis: 'Black seems to be taking his time sohe can adjourn. But by the time White sealed his 44th move, Black had broken through.'} 40. Rf3 Bd4+ 41. Kf1 Be5 42. Kf2 a5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 43. a4 ({Black wins with 43. ...a4. For example} 43. Rd3 {Andrew Soltis} a4 $1 44. bxa4 (44. Rf3 axb3 45. axb3 Ra8 {the rook decides} 46. Rd3 Ra2+ 47. Kf3 (47. Kf1 Rf4+ 48. Kg1 Bd4+ 49. Kh2 Rh4#) 47... Rf4+ 48. Ke3 Rxg2) 44... c4 45. Ra3 c3) 43... Rd8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({More exact than} 43... Rb8 {Andrew Soltis} 44. Rd3 $1 c4 45. bxc4 Rb2+ 46. Kf3 Rf4+ 47. Ke3 Rxg2 {because White has play with} 48. Rd8) 44. Ke3 Rb8 45. Kf2 c4 $1 $19 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 46. bxc4 Rb2+ 47. Kf1 (47. Ke3 {Andrew Soltis} Ra2 {does the trick} 48. c5 Ra3+ 49. Ke2 Rxa4) 47... Rb4 {Andrew Soltis: '. The a-pawn promotes.'} 0-1

