{ChessCalisthenics: 'E45: Nimzo-Indian: Rubinstein: 4...b6 5 Ne2 Ba6'} 1. d4 {ChessCalisthenics} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 b6 5. Nge2 Ba6 6. Ng3 Bxc3+ 7. bxc3 d5 8. Ba3 $146 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'An excellent novelty, prepared by Portisch after the failure in his game with Fischer in Santa Monica;'} ({which went} 8. Qf3 O-O 9. e4 dxe4 10. Nxe4 Nxe4 11. Qxe4 Qd7 $3 {(cf. Volume 4).}) 8... dxc4 ({It is safer to go into a slightly inferior endgame by} 8... Bxc4 9. Bxc4 dxc4 10. Qa4+ Qd7 11. Qxc4 Qc6 {, but – and this could have been anticipated! – Fischer took the pawn.}) 9. e4 (9. Qf3 $6 Qd5 10. e4 Qc6 {is inferior}) 9... Qd7 10. Be2 Nc6 11. Qc2 O-O-O 12. O-O h5 13. Rfd1 h4 {ChessCalisthenics: 'N'} (13... Ne7 {ChessCalisthenics} 14. Bc1 Rdg8 15. Nf1 g5 16. Ne3 Bb7 17. f3 g4 18. Nxc4 Qe8 19. Rf1 h4 20. Bd3 gxf3 21. Rxf3 Nh5 22. Ba3 f5 23. Re1 Nf6 24. Nd2 Rg7 25. Re2 Kb8 26. exf5 Bxf3 27. Nxf3 Ned5 28. Rxe6 {Graf,A (2623)-Cvek,R (2430)/Leipzig 2002/CBM 089/1-0 (39)}) (13... Kb8 {ChessCalisthenics} 14. Nf1 Bb7 15. Bxc4 h4 16. Ne3 h3 17. g3 Na5 18. Bd3 Nh7 19. d5 exd5 20. exd5 Ng5 21. Be2 g6 22. c4 Rhe8 23. Bb4 Rxe3 24. fxe3 Qe8 25. Kf2 Ne4+ 26. Ke1 Nxg3 27. hxg3 Qxe3 28. Qd3 {Estremera Panos,S (2340)-Eslon,J (2395)/Barcelona 1988/EXT 1997/ 1-0 (33)}) 14. Nf1 $44 {Garry Kasparov: 'A very sharp situation has arisen, where White has excellent compensation for the pawn.'} Nh5 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'And Black immediately makes a bad mistake! It seems to me that Fischer's handling of such complicated positions, where the opponent had the initiative, was not very successful. This was not his sort of play – too ragged and difficult for the development of a clear plan.'} (14... Bb5 $142 $15 {ChessCalisthenics}) 15. d5 $1 {ChessCalisthenics: '' Garry Kasparov: 'This breakthrough is effectively already decisive.'} Ne5 (15... exd5 $2 16. Rxd5 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'and wins'}) 16. dxe6 Qe8 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Black would have lost a piece after'} 16... Qxe6 $2 17. Rxd8+ $18 (17. Bxh5 {as} Rxh5 $2 {is not possible on account of} 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Qd1+ {and Qxh5.})) 17. Rxd8+ Qxd8 18. Bxh5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'As Portisch pointed out, if'} 18. Qa4 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'then'} Nf4 $1 19. Qxa6+ Kb8 { g5  Qg5© Garry Kasparov: 'and ...Qg5! is strong.'}) 18... Rxh5 19. f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Again'} 19. Qa4 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'is unfavourable, this time in view of'} Bb5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 20. Qxa7 (20. Qxb5 $2 Nf3+ {and ...Rxb5}) 20... fxe6 {with approximate equality.}) 19... Nd3 {ChessCalisthenics: '?'} (19... Qe8 $142 $5 {ChessCalisthenics} 20. Qd1 g6 $16) ({after} 19... fxe6 20. Qe2 $1 {and 21 fxe5 White should be able to convert his extra knight.}) 20. exf7 {ChessCalisthenics: '+-'} c5 {Garry Kasparov: 'A very resourceful defence, retaining a semblance of counterplay: for the moment the bishop at a3 is offside, while the knight at d3 is very strong.'} 21. Qe2 {Garry Kasparov: 'This move does not spoil anything;'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'but Fischer thought that'} 21. e5 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'was stronger. And indeed, after'} Rh6 (21... Rf5 22. Qa4 $1) ({or} 21... Rh8 22. Qa4 Kb7 23. Ne3 {(with the threat of Nxc4)} h3 24. Rd1 b5 25. Rb1 $1) 22. Qe2 {followed by Qg4+ and e5-e6 White would have won;} (22. Qa4 b5 23. Qd1 $1 {is also good.})) ({An even more forceful alternative is the computer's recommendation} 21. Qa4 $1 Kb7 22. Rd1 h3 $5 (22... b5 23. Rb1 $1 {,}) ({while if} 22... Rh8 {, then} 23. Bxc5 $1 {is immediately decisive}) 23. g3 Rh8 ({it is also bad to play} 23... g6 24. Ne3 {,}) ({or} 23... b5 24. Rb1) 24. Bxc5 $1 bxc5 25. Rb1+ Nb4 26. a3 $1 ({but not} 26. cxb4 $4 Qd4+) 26... Qd3 27. axb4 Qxb1 (27... Qxe4 28. Rb2) 28. Qd7+ Kb6 29. Qe6+ Kb7 30. Qe7+ Kb6 31. Qxc5+ Kb7 32. f8=Q Rxf8 33. Qxf8 Qxe4 34. Qxg7+ Kc6 35. Qh6+ {and Qxh3 – Black can resign.}) 21... Rh8 22. e5 Kb8 $1 23. e6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'The most accurate, since it brings the pawn closer to the queening square and restricts the opponent's counterplay.'} (23. Qg4 $6 Bc8 $1 (23... h3 {is slightly more tenacious, but here too Black is lost after} 24. Ne3 hxg2 25. e6 Bb7 ({or} 25... Qf8 26. Rd1) 26. Nxc4 Qf6 27. f5 {.}) 24. Qxg7 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (24. e6 Qf6 25. f5 g6 {'with an unclear game'. But in my opinion, the white pawns are so strong that almost any move wins – for example,} 26. Bc1 gxf5 ({or} 26... Bxe6 27. Qxg6 $1) 27. Qg8 $1 {.}) 24... Qf8 $1) 23... Qf6 24. Re1 $1 ({It was simpler to play} 24. Qe4 $1 h3 (24... Bb7 25. Qxc4 Nxf4 26. e7) 25. Ne3 $1 (25. g4 Bb7 ({or} 25... Nxf4 26. Re1) 26. Qf5 $1 Qe7 27. Ne3 g6 28. Nd5 $1 {is also possible}) 25... hxg2 (25... Bb7 26. Qf5) 26. Qf5 Qxc3 27. Rd1 $1 Qd4 (27... Bc8 28. f8=Q) 28. Rxd3 cxd3 29. Qe5+ Qxe5 30. fxe5 {and wins. Such powerful pawns should win automatically, although it is much harder to demonstrate this at the board, than in the quiet of one's home with the help of a computer. The psychological subtext of Portisch's decision is understandable to me: after employing a successful novelty in the opening and quickly gaining a winning position, although one not yet without its sharpness, he became nervous (it's no joke – to beat the great Fischer!) and as a result he chose 'the safest way', which was the developing move 24 Re1.}) 24... Bb5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Again the best chance.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'and'} 24... Nxe1 $2 25. Qe5+ $1 $18 {ChessCalisthenics: 'Decoy Double attack' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '(Portisch).'}) ({After} 24... Qe7 25. Rd1 h3 {White wins by both} 26. g3 (26. Qg4 $1 hxg2 27. Ne3 {.}) 26... Bb7 27. f5) 25. Bxc5 $3 {Garry Kasparov: 'A brilliant stroke, radically solving the problem of the a3 bishop.'} bxc5 {ChessCalisthenics: '?' Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (25... Nxc5 {ChessCalisthenics: ''} 26. Qe5+ Qxe5 27. Rxe5 Nxe6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (27... Rf8 {ChessCalisthenics: '+-'} 28. f5 {'with the idea of Nd2-f3-h4, winning'  (Portisch). This requires some refinement:} Kc7 $1 (28... Bc6 29. Nd2 Nd3 ({no better is} 29... Kc7 30. Nxc4 {,}) ({or} 29... Nb7 30. Ne4 $1 {with the threat of Ng5}) 30. Re3 b5 31. Ne4 Ne5 32. Ng5 {and wins;}) {but only} 29. Rd5 $1 ({and now not} 29. Nd2 $6 Kd6 $1 30. Nxc4+ Ke7 31. Rxc5 (31. Ne3 Bc6 32. c4 Rc8) 31... bxc5 32. Ne5 Kf6 33. Ng6 Ra8 34. g4 hxg3 35. hxg3 Bc4) ({or} 29. Ne3 $6 Kd6 $1 30. Rd5+ Ke7 31. Rxc5 bxc5 32. Nd5+ Kd6 33. Nf4 Be8 $1 34. Ng6 Bxf7 35. Nxf8 Be8 $1 36. Nd7 (36. Kf2 Ke5) 36... Bh5 {with good drawing chances}) 29... Bc6 (29... h3 30. Ne3) 30. Rd4 {(threatening Ne3-d5+)} g6 (30... Nb7 31. Rg4 $1 {;}) (30... h3 31. Ne3 hxg2 32. Nxc4) 31. fxg6 Nxe6 32. Rxc4 Kd6 33. Ne3 Bb7 34. Rxh4 {and wins. I think that after the exchange of queens, despite the possible pitfalls, Portisch would most probably have found a sure way to win.}) 28. f5 { d2-f3-h4+-  Nd2-f3-h4+-} (28. Rxe6 {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} Rf8 (28... Bd7 29. Rg6 Rf8 30. Rxg7 Be6 31. Rg6 Bxf7 32. Rh6 b5 33. a3 {and wins}) 29. Re7)) 26. Rb1 {ChessCalisthenics: '+-'} a6 27. a4 {Garry Kasparov: 'For a computer White's task is simpler here than after 25... Nxc5 26 Qe5+ Qxe5 27 Rxe5 Rf8, but for a human it is more difficult, since with the queens on he imagines that there are all sorts of threats.'} Nxf4 28. Qf3 $2 {ChessCalisthenics: '??' Garry Kasparov: 'Portisch nevertheless misses the win, by making the worst of the possible queen moves.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Later he pointed out'} 28. Qe4 $1 Qg5 (28... Nxe6 29. axb5) 29. Kh1 Nd3 (29... h3 30. g3 Nd3 31. e7 $1 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: ', winning.'}) 30. Qe3 $18 (30. Qf3 $1)) (28. Qxc4 $142 $1 {ChessCalisthenics} Qg5 29. Qa2 $18 c4 $5 30. axb5 Qc5+ 31. Kh1 h3 $1 32. bxa6+ Ka8 33. Rb2 $1 ({but not} 33. Rb7 $2 hxg2+ 34. Qxg2 Nxg2 35. e7 Qc8 $1 36. Rc7 $1 Qxc7 37. e8=Q+ Qc8 {with equality}) 33... Rd8 (33... Nxe6 34. Qa4 $1) 34. Qb1 $1 Qc6 (34... hxg2+ 35. Rxg2 Qc6 36. Qb7+ $1) 35. Ne3 hxg2+ 36. Nxg2 Nxe6 37. a7 {;}) ({and even by} 28. Qd2 $1 {with the amusing variation} Rd8 29. e7 Rxd2 30. f8=Q+ Ka7 31. e8=Q Nh3+ 32. gxh3 Qg5+ 33. Ng3 Bxe8 34. Qxe8 {, forcing} Qd8 35. Qxd8 {and Ne4.}) 28... Nxe6 {ChessCalisthenics: '' Garry Kasparov: '!'} 29. Qxf6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'It transpires that if'} 29. Qc6 {Garry Kasparov: 'there is the defence'} Nc7 $1 30. axb5 axb5 {Garry Kasparov: 'with equality (Portisch).'}) ({The machine also examines} 29. Qe4 Nc7 30. axb5 axb5 31. Qxc4 Qe5 $1 ({it is dangerous to play} 31... Qf5 $6 32. Rxb5+ $1 Nxb5 33. Qxb5+ Kc7 34. Qe8 Qc8 35. Qe7+ Kc6 36. Ne3) 32. Qxh4 (32. h3 Rf8) 32... Rxh4 33. f8=Q+ Kb7 34. Qf3+ Qe4 {with equality.}) 29... gxf6 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rxb5+ Kc7 32. Ne3 Rf8 33. Ra5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Now White only has slightly the better endgame.'} Kb7 $1 34. Nxc4 ({Or} 34. Nf5 Rxf7 35. Ra4 (35. Nxh4 Rd7) 35... Rh7 36. Rxc4 h3 {with equality.}) 34... Rxf7 35. Rxc5 Nxc5 36. Nd6+ Kc6 37. Nxf7 Ne4 38. Nh6 ({Tired and upset, Portisch does not even consider} 38. h3 $5 Nxc3 39. Nh8 Kd5 40. Ng6 {and Nxh4 with an extra pawn (however, here too a draw was the most probable outcome).}) 38... Kd5 (38... Nxc3 39. h3) 39. Nf5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Draw, although it was still possible to try'} h3 40. Ne3+ $1 Ke5 41. Nd1 hxg2 42. Kxg2 {– after all, White is a pawn up (for some reason no one has pointed out this chance).} 1/2-1/2

