{Andrew Soltis: 'Perhaps Fischer's most important legacy was a comment he made in typical Bobby-speak : "To get squares, ya gotta give squares." Translated, this means that in order to make progress in the middlegame, you often have to concede control of certain key points on the board. IN Game 92, we will see how ...f4, a heretical move in tehKing's Indian because it gives up e4, was a strategic killer. In the following game, he employs a similar version with 11.f5!'} 1. e4 {Garry Kasparov Andrew Soltis} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 dxc6 5. O-O {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({In Fischer's games one can sometimes see the influence of Morphy and Steinitz, but in the given instance he is the successor of Lasker, who played this variation just as masterfully  (although using the old} 5. Nc3 {Garry Kasparov}) ({or} 5. d4 {(Garry Kasparov) ).}) 5... f6 (5... Bg4 $5) 6. d4 {Garry Kasparov: 'One of the main tabiyas of the Ruy Lopez Exchange Variation.'} exd4 {Garry Kasparov: 'A popular reply,'} (6... Bg4 7. dxe5 $14 (7. Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) (7. c3 $5 {Garry Kasparov} exd4 (7... Bd6 8. Be3 Qe7 9. Nbd2 O-O-O {is more solid, Smyslov-Geller, 41st USSR Championship, Moscow 1973}) 8. cxd4 Qd7 (8... Bxf3 9. Qxf3 Qxd4 10. Rd1) ({, or} 8... c5 9. h3 $5 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 cxd4 11. e5 {is questionable}) 9. h3 Be6 (9... Bh5 10. Ne5 $1 Bxd1 11. Nxd7 Kxd7 12. Rxd1 {with slightly the better endgame, Fischer-Jimenez, Havana Olympiad 1966}) 10. Nc3 O-O-O 11. Bf4 {(Fischer-Gligoric, Havana Olympiad 1966)} g5 $1 {, not fearing Fischer's variation} 12. Bg3 h5 13. d5 cxd5 14. Rc1 Bd6 15. Na4 Kb8 16. Nc5 {in view of} Bxc5 $1 17. Rxc5 dxe4 {.}) 7... Qxd1 {Garry Kasparov} 8. Rxd1 fxe5 (8... Bxf3 9. gxf3 fxe5 {is met by the strong} 10. Be3 $1 Bd6 11. Nd2 Ne7 12. Nc4 O-O-O 13. Rd3 b5 14. Na5 {... 1-0 Fischer-Rubinetti, Buenos Aires 1970}) 9. Rd3 Bd6 $5 (9... Bxf3 10. Rxf3 Nf6 11. Nc3 Bb4 12. Bg5 Bxc3 13. bxc3 Rf8 14. Bxf6 Rxf6 15. Rxf6 gxf6 16. Rd1 {... 1/2-1/2 Fischer-Smyslov, Monte Carlo 1967}) 10. Nbd2 Nf6 (10... b5 $5 {Timman-Kasparov, 5th matchgame, Hilversum 1985}) 11. Nc4 Nxe4 (11... O-O $5 {Kasparov-Tal, 46th USSR Championship, Tbilisi 1978}) 12. Ncxe5 (12. Nfxe5 $5 {Bronstein-Lengyel, Moscow 1971}) 12... Bxf3 13. Nxf3 O-O {with equality (Fischer-Spassky, 16th matchgame, Reykjavik 1972).}) 7. Nxd4 Ne7 {Andrew Soltis: 'The flexible move neutralized White's piece play after 8.Bf4 Ng6 9.Bg3 Bc5 10.Nb3 Bd6!' Garry Kasparov: 'An idea of Portisch,'} ({who earlier played} 7... Bd6 {(Garry Kasparov) (this move was also employed by Gligoric).}) ({Modern theory recommends} 7... c5 {(Garry Kasparov) , exchanging the queens:} 8. Nb3 ({or} 8. Ne2 Qxd1 9. Rxd1 Bd7 10. Nbc3 Ne7 11. Bf4 O-O-O {, Fischer-Spassky, 27th matchgame, Belgrade 1992}) 8... Qxd1 9. Rxd1 Bg4 ({not} 9... Bd6 $6 10. Na5 $1 b5 $6 11. c4 $1 Ne7 12. Be3 f5 13. Nc3 f4 14. e5 {... 1-0 Fischer-Portisch, Havana Olympiad 1966}) 10. f3 Be6 11. Nc3 Bd6 12. Be3 b6 13. a4 a5 $5 14. Nb5 O-O-O) 8. Be3 Ng6 9. Nd2 $1 (9. f4 $6 {(Garry Kasparov) is premature:} c5 10. Nf3 Qxd1 11. Rxd1 Bg4 {with equality (Hort-Portisch, Skopje 1968)}) ({while after} 9. Nc3 {(Garry Kasparov) Unzicker would have replied} Bd6 {and ...0-0 as in the game.}) (9. Qh5 $5 {Garry Kasparov} Bd6 10. Nf5 O-O 11. f4 {is interesting (Karpov-Klovans, Daugavpils 1971).}) 9... Bd6 10. Nc4 ({Later both} 10. c3 {Garry Kasparov}) ({and} 10. Nf5 {(Garry Kasparov) occurred.}) 10... O-O 11. Qd3 $1 $146 { Andrew Soltis: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'Bobby's counter-preparation.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black's defensive setup received high marks after a Portisch game that continued' Garry Kasparov: 'The passive'} 11. Nxd6 {Ü 4/284} cxd6 {Andrew Soltis Garry Kasparov: '(Barczay-Portisch, Sousse Interzonal 1967)'} 12. Qd2 Re8 13. f3 Ne5 14. Bf2 d5 {with an edge}) ({or} 11. c3 {Garry Kasparov} Re8 (11... Qe7 $5) 12. f3 {(Damjanovic-Portisch, Palma de Mallorca 1967) leads to equality.}) 11... Ne5 ({But White has spotted a fundamental problem : Black lacks space