{I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example.} {I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example.  Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example. ''} 1. e4 {Sokolov,Vladimir Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)' Steve Giddins: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Moskalenko,Viktor: '"A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74) Sokolov,Vladimir "A rare circumstance where Fischer is surprised in the opening by a better prepared opponent. Being on unfamiliar ground, Bobby reacts inexactly and is simply swept off his feet." (Mednis 74)''} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: What is this? White is sacrificing a pawn? ANSWER: Well, yes and no. In fact, as we will see, White can regain the pawn by force. What he is doing is trying to bring about a more open game than usually occurs after 4 e5. He forces the exchange of bishop for knight, thereby gaining his traditional Winawer birthright of the bishop-pair and unopposed dark-square bishop, but without closing the centre. He thereby hopes that he will be able to make more effective use of the bishops. QUESTION: Does this get played much nowadays? ANSWER: No. In recent years, it has lost popularity, but in the past, it was played regularly by Alekhine, Smyslov and Fischer, so it must be respected. It is one of the sharpest replies to the Winawer, and the black player needs to be well armed against it.  Fischer had won with this move against Uhlmann two rounds earlier, so it is  probable that IM Kovacevic made special preparations for just this variation.' White spends a tempo to force the exchange x. White spends a tempo to force the exchange x. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'White spends a tempo to force the exchange x.''} Bxc3+ ({Deviations: the retreat Deviations: the retreat} 4... Ba5 {must be weaker. White has a pleasant choice between must be weaker. White has a pleasant choice between} 5. e5 ({also also} 5. b4) ({or, the exchange or, the exchange} 5. exd5 {which is more favourable in this line. which is more favourable in this line.}) 5... c5 {the Armenian Variation. the Armenian Variation.}) 5. bxc3 dxe4 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion.' Steve Giddins: 'QUESTION: Is this forced? ANSWER: Not forced exactly, but no other move really makes much sense, in my opinion. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' Accepting the consequences is the right way. Accepting the consequences is the right way. Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Accepting the consequences is the right way.''} ({Deviations: after Deviations: after Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'Deviations: after''} 5... Ne7 {Steve Giddins: ', for example, hopes to transpose into the main line, if White plays a rapid e4-e5, but he has no reason to do so. After' White can play White can play Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'White can play''} 6. Qg4 $5 ({or, the solid or, the solid Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: 'or, the solid''} 6. Bd3 $5 {Steve Giddins: 'Moskalenko,Viktor: '!?''} c5 7. exd5 exd5 8. dxc5 $1 $14 {Steve Giddins: ', Psakhis claims that Black is not really equalizing, and I agree. The two bishops are likely to be strong in such an open position.' Steve Giddins: ', Psakhis claims that Black is not really equalizing, and I agree. The two bishops are likely to be strong in such an open position. Moskalenko,Viktor: '!'' and Black faces a t