nn-Reimer Gambit The Winckelmann-Reimer Gambit} 6. f3 {Steve Giddins: 'has achieved a degree of popularity. White makes it a real gambit, along the lines of the Blackmar-Diemer Gambit (1 d4 d5 2 e4 dxe4 3 Nc3 Nf6 4 f3). QUESTION: What do you think of the line? ANSWER: To be brutally honest, I think it is what is sometimes euphemistically called "a consignment of geriatric shoemakers"! Unlike the Blackmar-Diemer, here White is not even threatening to capture on e4 (because of the reply ...Qh4+), so Black has a wide choice.' can be met with the straightforward can be met with the straightforward} c5 $1 {Steve Giddins: 'looks the most logical,'} (6... e5 $5 {is probably a good alternative is probably a good alternative}) ({but John Watson's} 6... Nd7 {is also good. White has nothing for his pawn, in my opinion.}) 7. Be3 (7. Be2 {was played by GM Hector: was played by GM Hector:} Nc6 (7... Nf6 $1 8. fxe4 Nxe4 9. Qd3 Qa5 $16) 8. fxe4 cxd4 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Nf3 dxc3 10. Bd3 e5 (10... Qb6 $5) 11. O-O Nge7 12. Qe1 O-O 13. Qxc3 Ng6 14. Bc4 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. Be3 Rc8 {and Black is still better, in ½-½ Hector,J (2500)-Brinck Claussen,B (2380)/Copenhagen 1997/ (34) and Black is still better, in ½-½ Hector,J (2500)-Brinck Claussen,B (2380)/Copenhagen 1997/ (34)}) 7... Qa5 $1 8. dxc5 ({In the game Simon-De Francesco, Balatonbereny 1995, White tried: In the game Simon-De Francesco, Balatonbereny 1995, White tried:} 8. fxe4 Qxc3+ 9. Bd2 Qxd4 10. Nf3 Qxe4+ $17 {, but with the three extra pawns you never lose! , but with the three extra pawns you never lose!}) 8... Nc6 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Kf2 Nf6 10. Qd6 {As in Velicka-Babula, CZE-chT 1995. And here, instead of 10...exf3, I would recommend: As in Velicka-Babula, CZE-chT 1995. And here, instead of 10...exf3, I would recommend:} Nd5 $1 {playing for the initiative playing for the initiative} (10... Ne7 $5) 11. Re1 Nxe3 12. Rxe3 e5 $1 $17 {Black intends simply ...e6 and ...d8. For instance, Black intends simply ...e6 and ...d8. For in