1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({Petrosian reverts to his favourite defence, but in contrast to Larsen he does not risk playing} 3... Bb4 {(Game No.95).}) ({In the 9th game he chose} 3... Nc6 4. Nf3 Nf6 {, and here Fischer satisfied himself with the minimal advantage of the first move, by playing} 5. exd5 (5. e5 {is more critical}) 5... exd5 6. Bb5 {. The demoralized Petrosian defended poorly:} Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Be7 9. Bg5 a6 $6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfe1 h6 13. Bh4 Qd7 14. Re2 $1 {, and White, after gaining a clear advantage, won on the 46th move.}) 4. Bg5 dxe4 ({In the Candidates tournament on Curaçao (1962) Petrosian defeated Fischer with the help of} 4... Bb4 5. e5 h6 6. Bd2 Bxc3 7. Bxc3 $6 ({but this time there would almost certainly have followed} 7. bxc3 Ne4 8. Qg4 $1) 7... Ne4 8. Ba5 $6 O-O $1 9. Bd3 Nc6 10. Bc3 Nxc3 11. bxc3 f6 $1 {.}) 5. Nxe4 Be7 ({The game Fischer-Petrosian (Stockholm Interzonal 1962) went} 5... Nbd7 6. Nf3 ({but later Bobby successfully employed} 6. Nxf6+ Nxf6 7. Nf3 {against Sarapu, Sousse Interzonal 1967}) 6... Be7 7. Nxf6+ Bxf6 8. Qd2 ({and} 8. h4 h6 $6 9. Bxf6 Qxf6 10. Qd2 O-O 11. O-O-O {against Bilek, Havana 1965}) 8... Bxg5 9. Nxg5 Nf6 10. Be2 O-O 11. Rd1 Qd6 12. O-O Bd7 13. Nf3 Rfd8 {with equality.}) 6. Bxf6 gxf6 $6 (6... Bxf6 7. Nf3 Nd7 {Fischer confidently defeated Pachman (Leipzig Olympiad) and Benko (Curaçao Candidates 1962), in the second of these games demonstrating a model plan of development:} 8. Qd2 O-O 9. O-O-O Be7 10. Bd3 Nf6 ({experts on this variation prefer} 10... b6 11. h4 Bb7 12. Neg5 Nf6 {, Anand-Bareev, Wijk aan Zee 2004}) 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. Qf4 c5 13. dxc5 Qa5 14. Qc4 $1 {with the initiative.}) 7. g3 (7. Bc4 $1 $14) ({although} 7. Nf3 {has always been considered the main continuation; for example:} Z0 (7... f5 8. Z0 (8. Ng3 c5 9. Bb5+ Bd7 $1 ({but not} 9... Nc6 $6 10. c3 O-O 11. Bxc6 bxc6 12. O-O cxd4 13. Nxd4 Qc7 14. Qh5 Kh8 15. Rfe1 f4 $2 16. Ne4 {... 1-0 Chigorin-Rubinstein, Lodz 1906})) (8. Nc3 Bf6 $1 ({not} 8... c6 9. g3 Nd7 10. Bg2 Qc7 11. Qe2 {, Bogoljubow-Alekhine, 18th matchgame, The Hague 1929}) 9. Qd2 c5 {with double-edged play  (Liberzon-Botvinnik, Moscow 1966).})) (7... b6 8. Bd3 ({or} 8. Bc4) 8... Bb7 9. Qe2 c6 10. Z0 (10. O-O-O Nd7 11. Kb1 Qc7 {and ...0-0-0, as Petrosian defended against Korchnoi (Moscow 1961) and Klovans (43rd USSR Championship, Erevan 1975).}) ({. With the bishop on d3 it is also possible to play} 10. O-O Nd7 11. c4 $1 (11. Rfe1 $6 {is tame:} Qc7 12. a4 a5 13. c3 Nf8 $1 {, Anand-Dreev, 2nd matchgame, Madras 1991}) 11... Qc7 12. Rac1 O-O-O $6 13. c5 $1 {(J. Polgar-Andersson, Malmö 2000).})) (7... a6 {(Morozevich's favourite move, but there is nothing new under the sun: it occurred back in the game E. Cohn-Swiderski, Vienna 1908)} 8. Z0 (8. g3 b5 9. Bg2 Bb7 10. Qe2 Bd5 (10... Nd7 11. O-O O-O 12. Rfd1 $1 Bd5 13. c3 f5 14. Ned2 c5 $6 15. dxc5 Nxc5 16. Nf1 Qc7 17. Rxd5 $1 exd5 18. Ne3 {with good play for the exchange, Kasparov-M.Gurevich, Sarajevo 2000}) 11. c3 (11. Nc3) ({, or} 11. Ned2 {has also been played}) 11... Nd7 12. b3 O-O 13. O-O f5 14. Ned2 c5 15. c4 {with a slight advantage for White (Ponomariov-Morozevich, Moscow 2001).}) ({, or the sharp} 8. c4 $5 f5 9. Nc3 Bf6 10. Qd2 c5 11. d5 $1 O-O 12. O-O-O {with attacking chances, as Shirov played against Topalov (Sarajevo 2000) and Morozevich (Astana 2001).}))) 7... f5 $1 (7... Bd7 8. Nf3 Bc6 9. Qe2 {favours White; for example:} f5 ({or} 9... Qd5 10. Ned2 Na6 $6 11. a3 O-O-O 12. Bg2 Nb8 13. O-O Qd7 14. Rfe1 Bf8 {and b2-b4, Smyslov-Boleslavsky, Leningrad/Moscow 1941}) 10. Ned2 Bf6 11. c3 Qe7 12. Bg2 Nd7 13. O-O O-O 14. Rfe1 Rfe8 $6 15. b4 $1 a6 16. a4 b6 17. Nc4 Be4 18. Rad1 Red8 19. g4 $1 {(Fischer-Minev, Havana Olympiad 1966).}) 8. Nc3 Bf6 ({After the passive} 8... c6 {White has the good reply} 9. Bg2 ({or} 9. Qd2 Nd7 10. O-O-O {, transposing into a position from Euwe-Flohr, 10th and 14th matchgames, Amsterdam 1932}) 9... b6 10. Nf3 (10. d5 $5 {is also unpleasant for Black, Fischer-Bills, Houston simultaneous 1964.})) 9. Nge2 (9. Nf3 c5 ({or} 9... Nc6 10. Ne2 (10. Bb5 Bd7) 10... Qd6 {with equality}) 10. dxc5 Qa5 $44 11. Qd2 {, and now} Bxc3 $1 ({but not} 11... Bd7 $2 {, Psakhis-King, Dortmund 1989, on account of} 12. Nb5 $1) 12. bxc3 (12. Qxc3 Qxc3+ 13. bxc3 Nd7 14. c6 bxc6 15. Nd4 Bb7 16. Bg2 Rc8 {with equality}) 12... Nd7 13. Nd4 (13. c6 bxc6 14. Nd4 Qe5+) 13... Qxc5 {, when he has no difficulties.}) 9... Nc6 $1 $146 10. d5 exd5 $1 ({Previously Black tried to solve his problems with} 10... Nb4 11. Bg2 (11. dxe6 $6 Qxd1+ ({or immediately} 11... Bxe6) 12. Rxd1 Bxe6) 11... Nxd5 12. Bxd5 (12. Nxd5 exd5 {is inferior}) 12... exd5 13. Qxd5 (13. Nxd5 $6 Bxb2) {; for example:} 13... Z0 (13... Bxc3+ 14. Nxc3 Qe7+ 15. Kd2 (15. Ne2 O-O {with equality, Steiner-Lilienthal, Budapest 1935}) 15... Be6 $5 (15... c6 {is quieter}) 16. Qxb7 O-O {with a dangerous attack for the pawn (Rauzer-Mazel, 9th USSR Championship, Leningrad 1934).}) (13... Qxd5 {(the simplest)} 14. Nxd5 Bxb2 15. Nxc7+ Kd8 16. Rb1 ({or} 16. Nxa8 Bxa1 17. c3 Bb2) 16... Kxc7 17. Rxb2 Re8 {with a drawn endgame (Pavey-Shainswit, New York 1956)} (17... Be6) ({, or} 17... b6 {is also good.}))) 11. Nxd5 ({The cautious} 11. Qxd5 Z0 ({. would have led to a draw after} 11... Qxd5 12. Nxd5 Bxb2 13. Nxc7+ Kd8) ({, but in addition the energetic} 11... Be6 {was possible, and if} 12. Qb5 {, then} O-O $1 {; for example:} 13. Bg2 ({or} 13. Qxb7 Qd6 14. Bg2 Nd4 15. Nxd4 Rab8 16. Qc6 Qxd4) 13... Nd4 14. Nxd4 Qxd4 15. Rg1 Qb6 16. Qxb6 axb6 17. Bxb7 Rab8 18. Ba6 b5 19. a4 b4 20. Nd1 Rfd8 {with active counterplay.})) 11... Bxb2 12. Bg2 O-O ({White has a very dangerous initiative after} 12... Bxa1 $2 13. Qxa1 f6 (13... O-O 14. Nf6+) 14. Nxf6+ Kf7 15. Nh5 Rg8 16. O-O {.}) 13. O-O ({After} 13. Rb1 Bg7 14. O-O Ne5 $1 {and ...c7-c6 it is not to easy for White to demonstrate that he has sufficient compensation for the pawn. Fischer acts with more cunning – not wasting a tempo on moving his rook, he forces his opponent to spend precious time seeking a reply to the eternal question: 'to take or not to take?'}) 13... Bh8 $6 (13... Bxa1 14. Qxa1 f6 15. Nef4 (15. Z0) (15. Rd1 Z0 (15... Rb8 16. Nef4 Qd6 17. Nh5 {with equality} ({or} 17. Re1)) (15... Ne5 $1 {(giving up the queen)} 16. Nb6 (16. f4 Ng4 17. h3 c6 $1) 16... axb6 17. Rxd8 Rxd8 {"and Black is better" (Korchnoi, Furman) –} 18. Nf4 ({or} 18. f4 Nf7) 18... c6) (15... Bd7 $6 {is less good:} 16. Nef4 Ne5 17. Nh5 c6 18. Ndxf6+ Rxf6 19. Qxe5 Rf7 20. h4 $1 Qe8 ({or} 20... Kf8 21. Bh3 $1) 21. Nf6+ Rxf6 22. Qxf6 Qe6 23. Qg5+ Kh8 24. Qf4)) 15... Ne5 16. Rd1 (16. Nh5 c6 17. Ndxf6+ Rxf6 18. Qxe5 Rf7 19. Re1 Qg5 ({if} 19... Bd7 {, then} 20. Rd1 Qg5 21. Rxd7 Qxh5 22. Qe6 Qg6 ({or} 22... Rb8 23. Bf1) 23. Qb3 Re8 24. Rxb7 Re6 25. Bf1 {with equality}) 20. Nf4 Bd7 21. Bf3 $1 Qf6 22. Bh5 Re8 (22... Rff8 23. Qc7 $1) 23. Bxf7+ Qxf7 24. Qc3 Rxe1+ 25. Qxe1 Qxa2 26. Qe7 {with a probable draw.}) 16... c6 $14) (13... Be5 $15) ({One can agree with the conclusion of Korchnoi and Furman: "There is no point in taking on a1 – even without this Black's position is good, and it is a pity to give up the powerful bishop, which is fulfilling important defensive functions. However, the bishop's retreat square was not altogether well chosen. Without wasting a tempo, Black should have played} 13... Bg7 $1 {immediately, and in the event of} 14. Nef4 Ne5 $1 15. Nh5 {(was it this manoeuvre that Petrosian feared?)} Z0 ({"} 15... c6 16. Nxg7 Kxg7 17. Qe2 {(what can White do without the queens?)} Re8 ({or} 17... Ng6 {he would have stood well."})) ({. In my opinion,} 15... Bh8 $1 {is even better, when the knight at h5 is occupying the place for the queen.})) 14. Nef4 Ne5 ({After} 14... Ne7 15. Nxe7+ Qxe7 16. Re1 {White has an easy game.}) 15. Qh5 Ng6 (15... c6 16. Ne3 Ng6) 16. Rad1 c6 17. Ne3 Qf6 ({After} 17... Nxf4 18. gxf4 Qf6 {White would have been rescued by} 19. Kh1 $1 Qg6 20. Qf3 Bg7 21. Rg1 Qf6 22. Bh3 Kh8 23. Rg5 $5 {with an equalizing initiative.}) 18. Kh1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Of course not'} 18. Bh3 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Nxf4 19. gxf4 Qg6+ $15 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 18... Bg7 ({Black is not tempted by} 18... Nxf4 {– cf. the note to 17...Qf6.}) 19. Bh3 Ne7 (19... Re8 {came into consideration, since after} 20. Bxf5 (20. Nxf5 $2 Re5) 20... Bxf5 ({only not} 20... Re5 $2 21. Ng4 $1 Qxf5 22. Rd8+ Nf8 23. Nh6+ Bxh6 24. Qxh6 Qe4+ 25. Kg1 Qb4 26. Nh5 {and wins}) 21. Qxf5 Nxf4 22. gxf4 Rad8 {Black would have retained somewhat the better prospects.}) 20. Rd3 Be6 21. Rfd1 Bh6 (21... Bxa2 22. c4 $1) ({or} 21... Rfe8 22. c4 {followed by Nxe6 and Rd7.}) 22. Rd4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'After his badly played opening White has nevertheless created real counterplay for the pawn, and now after'} 22. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 {Garry Kasparov: 'he would have had a choice between'} {Garry Kasparov: ', and'} 23. Rd7 (23. Nxf5 Nxf5 24. Bxf5 Qxf5 (24... exf5 25. Rd6) 25. Qxh6 {with a simple draw. But for the moment Fischer decided to maintain the tension, recognizing a certain lack of decisiveness on the part of his opponent and his approaching time-trouble.}) ({or} 23. Bxf5) 23... Bxe3 24. fxe3 Rab8 25. e4 Rf7 $13 26. Rf1 $1 {with good compensation for the insignificant material deficit}) 22... Bxf4 23. Rxf4 Rad8 24. Rxd8 $2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 24. Rb1 $6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'the two sides' chances are roughly equal:'} Kh8 (24... Qg6 25. Qf3 b6 (25... Rd7 26. c4 {, capturing on f5 in a favourable situation}) 26. Kg1 Rd2 27. Nxf5 Nxf5 28. Bxf5 Bxf5 29. Rxf5 Re8 (29... Rxc2 30. Rf6) 30. h4 $1 {etc.}) (24... b6 25. Nxf5 Rd5 26. c4 Rxf5 27. Bxf5 Nxf5 28. g4 Qe5 29. Qg5+ Qg7 30. Rxf5) ({, or} 24... Ng6 25. Bxf5 Bxa2 26. Bxg6 Qxg6 27. Rg4) 25. Rxb7 Nd5 $15) 24... Rxd8 25. Bxf5 $2 ({Garry Kasparov: '"Not'} 25. Nxf5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Qa1+ 26. Kg2 Bd5+ 27. f3 Ng6 {, when the white king is in a desperate position: for example, if} 28. Rh4 Bxf3+ {with a mating attack." (Korchnoi, Furman)}) 25... Nxf5 26. Nxf5 Rd5 $17 ({Not} 26... Bd5+ $2 27. f3 Kh8 28. Nh6 {with equality.}) 27. g4 (27. Nh6+ {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Kg7 $1 $17) 27... Bxf5 (27... Kh8 $1) 28. gxf5 h6 29. h3 Kh7 ({Garry Kasparov: '"Consideration should have been given to the energetic'} 29... Rd2 $1 30. Qf3 ({or} 30. Rg4+ Kh7 31. Rg2 b5 $1 32. Qg4 Rd8 {– G.K.}) 30... Rxc2 {Garry Kasparov: '; for example:'} {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 31. Qe4 (31. Qe3 Kh7 $1 32. Qxa7 (32. Rg4 Qxf5 33. Qe8 $2 Qf3+) 32... Qg5 33. Qe3 Rxa2 {and Black wins." (Korchnoi, Furman)}) 31... Rd2 32. Qe8+ Kh7 33. Rg4 Rd8 $17 {Garry Kasparov: ', parrying the threats'}) 30. Qe2 Qe5 31. Qh5 Qf6 32. Qe2 Re5 $2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'A clear advantage would have been retained by both'} 32... b5 $1 $17 {Garry Kasparov: '(Petrosian)'}) ({and} 32... Qg5 33. Qf3 Rd2 34. Rg4 (34. f6 Qd5 $1) 34... Qf6 35. Qe3 Rd1+ 36. Kg2 Qxf5 37. Qe8 Qd5+ $1 {, "transposing into a won queen ending" (Korchnoi, Furman):} 38. f3 Qd2+ 39. Kg3 Rg1+ 40. Kh4 Rxg4+ 41. fxg4 Qf2+ 42. Kh5 Qf3 43. Kh4 Kg7 {.}) 33. Qd3 (33. Qh5 {The culmination of the game, and perhaps also of the entire match.} {Of course,} b5 $1 {should have been played, when it is not apparent how White could have countered the advance of the queenside pawns. Again a comparison with Fischer's young opponent in the non-existent world championship match of 1975 suggests itself: Karpov would not have overlooked the repetition of the position!}) 33... Rd5 $2 (33... b5 $1 $17) 1/2-1/2

