{Rabar,B: 'Chess Informant'} 1. c4 {Garry Kasparov ChessBase} c5 2. Nf3 g6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nc6 5. e4 Nf6 6. Nc3 d6 $5 {ChessBase: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer liked to fianchetto his dark-squared bishop and was not afraid of the Maróczy bind, believing in the solidity of Black's rather passive position. This was in the nature of a challenge to Botvinnik, who considered that with the pawn wedge on e4 and c4 White had a stable positional advantage. Under the influence of Fischer, there was a rapid growth in the employment of this set-up for Black in the 1970s, then it again abated, before appearing in the arsenals of Anand and Ivanchuk in the 1990s. They still play this, as though demanding that White demonstrate the evidence of his advantage.'} (6... Nxd4 7. Qxd4 d6 8. Be3 (8. c5 {ChessBase}) 8... Bg7 9. f3 $14 O-O {Garry Kasparov} 10. Qd2 Qa5 11. Rc1 Be6 12. b3 (12. Nd5) ({, or} 12. Bd3 Rfc8 13. b3 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} a6 14. Ne2 $5 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Nd7 16. Nf4 Nc5 17. Be2 a5 18. h4 $1 {is more active, Kasparov-Kasimdzhanov, Batumi rapidplay 2001}) 12... Rfc8 13. Be2 (13. Bd3 $5) (13. Nb5 $5 Qxd2+ 14. Kxd2 Nd7 15. Nd4 Nc5 16. h4 {, Korchnoi-Anand, London rapidplay 1994}) 13... a6 14. Nd5 (14. Na4 $5) 14... Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Nxd5 16. cxd5 Bd7 {a draw was soon agreed.}) 7. Be2 (7. Be3 {ChessBase} Ng4) (7. f3 {ChessBase} Qb6 (7... Nxd4 {Garry Kasparov} 8. Qxd4 Bg7)) (7. Nc2 {ChessBase} Bg7 8. Be2 Nd7 9. Bd2 Nc5 10. O-O O-O 11. b3 a5 $15 {Korchnoi-Matulovic, Sarajewo 1969}) 7... Nxd4 8. Qxd4 Bg7 9. Bg5 (9. O-O O-O 10. Qe3 {Ü 8/333 Rabar,B: 'Ue 8/333'} (10. Bg5 {Ü 6/457 Rabar,B: 'Ue 6/457'})) (9. Bd2 {Ü 9/311 Rabar,B: 'Ue 9/311'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Consideration should also be given to the plan with'} 9. Be3 O-O 10. Qd2 {Ü 8/313 Rabar,B: 'Ue 8/313'} Be6 {Garry Kasparov} 11. O-O Qa5 12. Rab1 Rfc8 13. b3 {, which was successfully employed by Ivanchuk against Anand (Buenos Aires 1994), Khalifman (Yalta rapidplay 1995) and Kovacevic (Belgrade 1997).}) 9... h6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Generally speaking it is not usual to weaken the position 'just like that', but it would appear that Fischer is provoking the opponent into aggressive action with the aim of punishing Black for his opening liberty.'} (9... O-O {(ChessBase) Vgl.Suetin-Gurgenidze, USSR ch, 1960} 10. Qd2 {Garry Kasparov} (10. Qe3 $5 {Garry Kasparov}) 10... Be6 11. Rc1 Qa5 {is more cautious} 12. Z0 ({, with the hope after} 12. b3 Rfc8 13. f3 a6 14. Na4 Qxd2+ 15. Kxd2 Rc6 {(Karpov-Kavalek, Nice Olympiad 1974)} ({or} 15... Nd7 {(Kramnik-Ivanchuk, London rapidplay 1994) of holding a slightly inferior endgame})) ({, or after} 12. O-O {of defending by the cunning} a6 ({or} 12... Rfc8 13. b3 a6 14. f4 $1 b5 $1 15. f5 gxf5 16. exf5 Bd7 17. Kh1 {(this move was recommended by Shirov)} bxc4 18. Bxc4 Bb5 $1) {, with the idea of} 13. b3 $6 b5 $1 14. Nd5 (14. cxb5 axb5 15. Bxb5 Rfc8 $1 {and ...Rxc3!}) 14... Qxd2 15. Nxe7+ Kh8 {and ...Nxe4 with equality (Shirov-Ivanchuk, Monaco rapidplay 1997).})) 10. Be3 O-O 11. Qd2 Kh7 12. O-O (12. f3 {(Garry Kasparov) has also been played, but this is not the best way of exploiting the weakening ...h7-h6.}) 12... Be6 13. f4 ({Timman's move} 13. Bd4 $5 {(Garry Kasparov) was possibly more unpleasant for Black – after the inevitable exchange of the dark-squared bishops the weakness of the enemy kingside will begin to tell.}) 13... Rc8 14. b3 Qa5 15. a3 $5 {ChessBase: ' 16.f5 d7 17.b4 nebst d4' Garry Kasparov: 'At the sight of the opponent's weakened kingside Larsen wants to take the initiative, and he begins advancing his pawns – such was his style of play!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Therefore I prefer'} 15. Rac1 {ChessBase: '' ChessBase: 'Kholmov' Garry Kasparov: '(defending the knight) with some advantage, although the absence of any serious weaknesses allows Black to hope for gradual equality; for example:'} b5 (15... a6 {Garry Kasparov} 16. f5 Bd7 17. h3 Bc6 18. Bd3 Nd7 19. Nd5 Qxd2 20. Bxd2 Rfe8 {(Gulko-Petrosian, Biel Interzonal 1976).}) 16. Nxb5 Qxd2 17. Bxd2 Nxe4 18. Be3 a6 19. Nd4 Bd7 20. Bf3 Nc5 21. Rfd1) (15. f5 {ChessBase} Bd7 16. Bd4 Nxe4 $1) ({To} 15. Bf3 {(Garry Kasparov) had the satisfactory reply} a6 ({or} 15... Bg4 {, and if} 16. Rad1 {, then} Bxf3 17. Rxf3 b5 $1 {with equality.})) 15... a6 {ChessBase: ' 16. ... b5'} 16. f5 (16. b4 Qd8 $15) 16... Bd7 (16... gxf5 $2 {ChessBase} 17. exf5 Bxf5 18. Rxf5 Qxf5 19. Bd3 $18 {Shmit}) 17. b4 (17. Bd4 $2 {ChessBase} Nxe4 $1 {Shmit}) 17... Qe5 $5 {ChessBase: '!' Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'An accurately calculated reply!'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 17... Qd8 18. Rad1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: 'White could have attacked with every comfort. I think that the move of the queen to e5 (where it is exposed to various attacks!) was underestimated by Larsen and it unsettled him.'}) ({In the event of} 17... Qc7 {Garry Kasparov} 18. Rac1) 18. Rae1 $6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'For the moment White is equal to the occasion.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'It was tempting to try and exploit the uneasy position of the black queen by'} 18. Rad1 $5 {Garry Kasparov: ', but Black is rescued by'} Bc6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (18... Nxe4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 19. Nxe4 Qxe4 20. Rf4 (20. Bd3 {Garry Kasparov} Qe5 $1) 20... Qc6 21. Bf3 ({or} 21. Rh4 {Garry Kasparov} Bxf5 22. Bxh6 Rh8 $1 {and ...Kg8}) 21... Qc7 22. Rh4 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '(recommended by Rabar)'} Bxf5 {Garry Kasparov} 23. Bxh6 Rh8 $1 24. Bf4+ Kg8 25. Rxh8+ Bxh8 26. c5 Rd8 {with equality.}) ({, and} 18... b5 $2 {(Garry Kasparov) are both bad in view of} 19. Bd4 $1 Nxe4 20. Bxe5 Nxd2 21. Bxg7 Nxf1 22. Bxf8 {etc}) 19. Bd4 Nxe4 20. Qe3 Nxc3 21. Bxe5 Nxd1 22. Rxd1 $14 Bxe5) (18. Bd4 {ChessBase: '?'} Nxe4 {ChessBase: '!' ChessBase: 'Suetin'}) (18. Bf4 $2 {ChessBase} Nxe4 $1 {Suetin}) 18... Bc6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'Continuing the provocative course of trying to tempt the opponent.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Now in the event of'} 18... Nxe4 $6 {ChessBase Garry Kasparov: '?'} 19. Nxe4 Qxe4 20. Bd3 