{Andrew Soltis: 'Soviet critics were able to explain Fischer's quarterfinals match as a case of a big age difference (Fischer 28, Taimanov 45), of Taimanov's over-optimism and his carelessness when he fell behind. But when Fischer began to beat Larsen by the same 6-0 score, there were no easy answers. Botwinnik, who for years had expressed doubts about him, wrote : "It's a marvel. One can explain how Fischer won each game individually. But how can you explain the result in total?"'} 1. c4 {Andrew Soltis} g6 2. Nf3 Bg7 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 O-O {Andrew Soltis: '?'} 5. e4 d6 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O {Andrew Soltis: '?'} Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Nd2 (9. Bd2 {Ü 6/816}) (9. b4) ({Andrew Soltis: 'Before 1970 this was considered inferior to'} 9. Ne1 {Andrew Soltis: 'because Black can trade his "bad" KB with 9. ...Bh6. But about the time this game was played, it was realiezd that White can break favorably on the queen side with 10.b4 and 11.c5, when the knight has a much better outpost at c4 than it would at d3.'}) 9... c5 $1 {Andrew Soltis: '!?' Andrew Soltis: 'This "logical move" as Fischer put it, had been only played a few times before he used it against Korchnoi at Herceg-Novi. King's Indian dogma held that in a closed center, Black must direct his attention to the kingside and make as few pawn moves as possible.'} (9... Bh6 10. b4 $14) (9... Nd7 10. b4 f5 11. f3 $14 {Ü 10/832}) (9... Ne8 10. b4 f5 11. c5 $14 {Ü 9/614}) 10. Rb1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Korchnoi played'} 10. a3 {Ü 10/831 Andrew Soltis: ', again following conventional wisdom that said once White plays b2-b4, he must be able to answer ...cxb4 with axb4, to preserve pawn control of c5. BUt later experience showed that c5 was overrated. Afte'} Ne8 {Andrew Soltis} 11. b4 b6 12. Rb1 f5 13. f3 f4 14. a4 {Korchnoi was behind a tempo compared with the Larsen game and losing soon after} g5 15. a5 Rf6 $1 16. bxc5 $2 bxc5 17. Nb3 Rg6 18. Bd2 Nf6 19. Kh1 g4) 10... Ne8 (10... b6 11. b4 cxb4 12. Rxb4 Nd7 $13) 11. b4 b6 ({Every defensive step Black takes opens new windows for White. This allows White to open two files by way of a4-a5. THe alternative strategy was} 11... cxb4 {Andrew Soltis} 12. Rxb4 b6 {but after} 13. a4 Nc7 14. Nb5 Na6 15. Rb1 Nc5 16. Ba3 {Black must decide whether to create yet another weakness and stop a4-a5 with ...a5}) 12. a4 ({White's access to the b-file is balanced by Black's kingside attack after} 12. bxc5 {Andrew Soltis} bxc5 13. Nb3 f5 14. f3 ({But a two wing policy, such as} 14. Bg5 {and exf5/f2-f4 may be best.}) 14... Kh8 {and ...Ng8-f6-h5.}) 12... f5 ({Here} 12... a5 $2 {(Andrew Soltis) is one precaution too many :} 13. bxc5 bxc5 14. Nb3 {followed by Bf2 and Nb5 leaves Black with a weak a-pawn and no ...a6 to oust the knight}) 13. a5 Nf6 14. Qa4 (14. Bd3) ({A promising plan now is} 14. axb6 {Andrew Soltis} axb6 15. f3 Bh6 16. Nb3 Bxc1 17. Nxc1 $1 {followed by Nd3 and Qb3.}) 14... Bd7 15. Qa3 (15. b5 {Andrew Soltis} bxa5 16. Ra1 a6 $1 {liquidates the queenside into a likely draw.}) 15... Bh6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 16. Bd3 ({More in keeping with the nature of the position was} 16. bxc5 {Andrew Soltis} bxc5 17. a6 {afterwhich} Bxd2 {is very risky} ({Instead Black can challenge the file with} 17... Rb8 18. Rxb8 Qxb8 {after} 19. Nf3 Bxc1 20. Rxc1 Qc7 {and ...Rb8 White's advantage is microscopic.} (20... Qb4 $2 21. Qa1 $1 Nxe4 22. Nxe4 fxe4 23. Rb1 Qa4 24. Qxa4 Bxa4 25. Ng5 {with the advantage of Rb7})) ({But even with a 3-0 lead, Fischer might have played for a win with} 17... Nc8 $6) 18. Bxd2 Nxe4 19. Nxe4 fxe4 20. Bg5) 16... Qc7 17. bxc5 (17. Nf3 Bxc1 18. Rfxc1 h6 $13) 17... bxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'Now Black has time to demilitarize the file'} 18. exf5 (18. a6) ({Andrew Soltis: 'This exchange is useful in King's Indian positions when f5 is significantly weakened or White wins control of e4. Neither condition applied here and the capture is inferior to'} 18. Nf3) 18... gxf5 ({Not quite equal is} 18... Nxf5 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Nde4 Bxc1 20. Nxf6+ Rxf6 21. Rfxc1) 19. Bc2 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} (19. a6 Rab8 $15) ({White misses his chance for} 19. Nb5 {(Andrew Soltis) - perhaps believing Fischer's next move was too much of a concession. After} Bxb5 20. Rxb5 a6 21. Rb6 Kh8 {the chances are in rough balance} 22. Nb3 Bxc1 23. Nxc1 Rf7 {and ...Rg8}) ({But Larsen wants to set up 20. Nde4, e.g. 19. ...Rab8 20.Nde4 Rxb1 21.Nxf6+ with a slight advantage. This couldn't have been played immediately because of} 19. Nde4 {Andrew Soltis} fxe4 20. Bxh6 exd3 $1) 19... a6 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Black can give up b6 and even allow an invastion of c6, in order to control the more important b5'} 20. Nde4 $6 (20. Rb6 $11) 20... Bxc1 21. Nxf6+ Rxf6 22. Rfxc1 Raf8 $1 23. Rb6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Necessary was} 23. Qb3 {(Andrew Soltis) and Qb7. Black would probably do best to shift his attention from the kingside with} Qxa5 24. Ra1 Qc7 25. Rxa6 Rb8 26. Qa2 Nc8) 23... Bc8 24. Ne2 $2 (24. Rcb1 $15 Ng6 {Andrew Soltis} 25. Rc6 Qg7 26. Rb8 $2 Nh4 {and wins} 27. g3 Nf3+ 28. Kg2 (28. Kf1 Bb7 29. Rb6 Nd2+ 30. Ke1 Nxc4 31. R8xb7 Qxb7 32. Rxb7 Nxa3) 28... Ne1+) ({Better but not equal was} 24. f4 {Andrew Soltis} e4 25. Ne2 Ng6 {Black's impending ...Nh4 always packs a punch. e.g.}) 24... f4 $1 {Andrew Soltis: 'Giving up another choice square to keep the knight from the kingside. Swallowing his pride now with 25.Nc3 Nf5 26.Ne4 wouldn't save White 26. ...Rg6 27.Rcb1 Nd4 28.Bd3 Qg7 and the attack rolls on.'} 25. Be4 Nf5 26. Rc6 {Andrew Soltis: 'This offers slim hope of slowing the kingside attack with Rxc8'} Qg7 $1 27. Rb1 (27. Bxf5 Bxf5 28. Rxa6 Rg6 $19) ({Still overly optimistic. After} 27. Kh1 {Andrew Soltis} Nh4 (27... Rh6 $2 28. Rxc8) 28. Rg1 Rh6 {White loses immediately} 29. g3 ({but a neutral move such as} 29. Qb3 {keeps the issue in doubt}) 29... Nf3 30. Bxf3 Rxh2+ 31. Kxh2 Qh6+ 32. Bh5 Qxh5+ 33. Kg2 Bh3+ 34. Kh1 Bf1#) 27... Nh4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 28. Qd3 ({White may have counted on} 28. Rxc8 {Andrew Soltis} Rxc8 29. Qh3 {but the double attack falls to} Rcf8 30. Qxh4 Rh6 {trapping the queen. Failing that there is no satisfactory way of meeting 28. ...Bf5}) (28. Rbb6 {Andrew Soltis} Bf5 $1 29. Qd3 Qxg2+ (29... Nxg2)) 28... Bf5 $1 29. Kh1 f3 $1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'much quicker than'} 29... Qxg2+ 30. Bxg2 Bxd3 31. Rg1) 30. Ng3 ({another wipe out is} 30. g3 {Andrew Soltis} fxe2 31. gxh4 Bxe4+ 32. Qxe4 Rxf2) 30... fxg2+ 31. Kg1 Bxe4 32. Qxe4 Nf3+ 33. Kxg2 Nd2 0-1

