{Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer never changed his attitude about 6.Bc4 in the Sicilian Defense, as Hans Ree's encounter with him at Netyana 1968 will attest. Ree asked Fischer about the infamous story of how Steinitz believed he could give odds to God. Fischer, whose interest in Steinitziana was well known, replied that no one could give odds to the Almighty. He added : "But with White I should be able to draw against Him. I play 1.e4 and if we have a Ruy, the position would be balanced. I could never lose." Then Fischer became concerned - what if God played 1. ...c5? But he soon brightened up, "No, no, then I play Bc4 and I'm better," he said. "So what can He do?"'} {Rabar,B: 'Chess Informant'} 1. e4 {Garry Kasparov Andrew Soltis Rabar,B} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 Nc6 6. Bc4 e6 7. Bb3 Be7 8. Be3 O-O 9. O-O {Andrew Soltis: 'This position may seem routine yet Fischer had only played it once since Curacao'} Bd7 (9... a5) (9... Na5) (9... Nxd4 10. Bxd4 b5 11. Nxb5 Ba6 12. c4 $14 {Ü 9/356 Rabar,B: 'Ue 9/356'}) 10. f4 Qc8 $5 $146 {Rabar,B: '!?' Rabar,B: 'N'} (10... a6 11. f5 $14 Qc8 $2 {(Andrew Soltis) and found himself with a horrible game after} 12. fxe6 Bxe6 (12... fxe6 13. Nf5 Qc7 14. Bf4 $1) 13. Nxe6 fxe6 14. Na4 $1) (10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bc6) 11. f5 $5 (11. Ndb5 Ne8 $5 (11... Qb8 12. f5)) ({This tries for more than} 11. Qf3 {(Andrew Soltis) but should offer no more than equality}) 11... Nxd4 ({To win a pawn Black must allow White's bishop a commanding square on d4. The chief alternative is} 11... Na5 {Andrew Soltis} 12. fxe6 Bxe6 {favors White without risk to him} (12... fxe6 13. Nf5 $1)) 12. Bxd4 exf5 13. Qd3 $1 (13. Qf3 {Garry Kasparov} fxe4 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 {1-0 (46) Fischer,R-Larsen,B Denver 1971 My Great Predecessors 4 [Garry Kasparov]}) 13... fxe4 ({There was no reason to incur a kingside weakness with} 13... g6 $2 {Andrew Soltis} 14. Rae1 Re8 (14... Bd8 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Qg3) 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. Rxf5 $1 Qxf5 17. Qxf5 gxf5 18. Rxe7) 14. Nxe4 Nxe4 (14... Bf5 {Andrew Soltis} 15. Rxf5 Qxf5 16. Nxf6+) 15. Qxe4 Be6 16. Rf3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({he other move that suggests itself is} 16. Rae1 {(Andrew Soltis) . Then} Qc6 $2 ({However, Black has no major worries after} 16... Bxb3 17. cxb3 Bg5) 17. Qf4 $1 d5 (17... Bxb3 18. Rxe7 Bxc2 19. Rxf7) 18. Qe5 Bf6 19. Rxf6 {and wins}) 16... Qc6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({A major aim of White'slast move was to discourage} 16... Bxb3 {(Andrew Soltis) which now would be met by} 17. Rg3 $1 g6 18. Qxe7 {with sparkling variations to follow such as} Qe6 19. Qh4 Bxc2 (19... Bd5 20. Re3 $1 Qf5 21. Rf1 Qxc2 22. Qxh7+ $1) 20. Re3 Qf5 21. Qh6 $1 f6 22. Re7) ({But Larsen later pointed out} 16... d5 $1 {(Andrew Soltis) Then} 17. Rg3 ({He should play} 17. Bxd5 {instead. Then} Bc5 {leaves Black no more than a draw - but trailing 0-4, he plays for a win that isn't there.}) 17... g6 {leaves White scrambling for compensation} 18. Qe3 (18. Qe5 f6 19. Qh5 Bc5 $1 20. Rxg6+ Kh8 21. c3 Bf7 $1) 18... Re8) 17. Re1 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({Now} 17. Rg3 $4 {(Andrew Soltis) because after} Qxe4 {the queen attacks his bishop}) (17. Qe3 {Andrew Soltis} Bxb3 18. Qxe7 Bxc2 19. Rg3 {is refuted by} Bg6) 17... Qxe4 ({There was still no time for} 17... Bxb3 {(Andrew Soltis) because of} 18. Qg4 g6 19. Rxe7 Be6 (19... Bd5 20. Qh5 $1) 20. Rxe6 $1 Qc8 21. Re4 f5 22. Re7) 18. Rxe4 d5 (18... Bxb3 {Andrew Soltis} 19. Rxb3 {leads to a bad ending}) 19. Rg3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} g6 (19... dxe4 $2 {Andrew Soltis: '??' Andrew Soltis: 'leads to an instant death after'} 20. Rxg7+ $18 Kh8 {Andrew Soltis} 21. Rxf7+ Bf6 22. Bxf6+ Kg8 23. Rg7+ Kh8 24. Bc3) 20. Bxd5 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Bd6 $6 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'Black rejects an easy draw'} 20... Bxd5 21. Rxe7 a6 $11 (21... Rfe8 {(Andrew Soltis) in an effort to save the match.} 22. Rge3 {Garry Kasparov} Rxe7 23. Rxe7 a6 ({or} 23... Kf8 {suggested itself, avoiding a whitewash in the match.}))) 21. Rxe6 $1 (21. Rg5 Bf5 $5 $15 (21... Bxd5 22. Rxd5 Rfd8 $11)) 21... Bxg3 (21... fxe6 {Andrew Soltis} 22. Bxe6+ Rf7 23. Rf3 Raf8 24. Bxa7 {wins}) 22. Re7 $16 Bd6 23. Rxb7 {Andrew Soltis: 'Since one Black piece is tied to the defense of f7, White is free to push his c-pawn.'} Rac8 {Andrew Soltis: '?'} ({A final - and this time fatal - error. Correct was} 23... a5 {Andrew Soltis} 24. Rd7 {when White has a good chance to shepherd the pawns} (24. Z0 {Garry Kasparov}) (24. c4 {Garry Kasparov} Rae8 25. Rd7 (25. c5 Bxh2+ $1 {and ...Rd8}) 25... Be5 26. Bc5 (26. Bxe5 Rxe5 {is also unclear}) 26... Rd8 27. Re7 (27. Rb7 Rxd5 $1 28. cxd5 Rd8) 27... Bxb2 28. Rb7 Rxd5 29. cxd5 Rc8 30. Be7 Rc1+ 31. Kf2 Rc2+ 32. Kf3 f5 {, retaining hopes of a draw.}) 24... Rfd8 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 25. Rxf7 Bxh2+ 26. Kxh2 Rxd5 27. Rg7+ Kf8 28. Rxh7 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '(Soltis)'} Rh5+ (28... Ra6 {Garry Kasparov}) 29. Rxh5 gxh5 30. Kg3) 24. c4 a5 25. Ra7 (25. a3 $5 {Garry Kasparov}) 25... Bc7 ({Andrew Soltis: 'When he chose his 22nd move, Black may have assumed he could put up a good fight with'} 25... Bc5 26. Bxc5 Rxc5 {Andrew Soltis: 'but now saw that he can't stop the king from reaching d4 after'} 27. Kf2 { e3-d4 Rabar,B: '#C5 #C8e3-d4' Garry Kasparov: 'was hardly much better.'}) ({also lost is} 25... Bb4 {Andrew Soltis} 26. a3 Bd2 27. Kf2) 26. g3 Rfe8 27. Kf1 Re7 28. Bf6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({A fine move that is too-easily passed over. White avoids} 28. Bc3 {Andrew Soltis} Rce8 $1 {and forces the e7-rook to make a choice.}) 28... Re3 ({White can win the a-pawn with} 28... Rd7 {Andrew Soltis} 29. Bc3 {or bring the king in as reinforcement.}) 29. Bc3 h5 (29... Rxc3 {Garry Kasparov} 30. bxc3 Kf8 31. c5 Ke7 32. Bc4 {etc would also not have saved Black.}) 30. Ra6 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'Threatening Rxg6+ and reducing Blacl's counterplay to a smidgen'} (30. Bxa5 Bxa5 31. Rxa5 Rce8 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 32. Ra7 {Andrew Soltis} Re1+ 33. Kg2 R8e2+ 34. Kh3 Rd2) 30... Be5 31. Bd2 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} ({It's still a fight after} 31. Rxg6+ {Andrew Soltis} Kf8 32. Rh6 Bxc3 33. bxc3 Rce8) 31... Rd3 32. Ke2 Rd4 ({The rook had no good squares after} 32... Rxd2+ {Andrew Soltis} 33. Kxd2 Bxb2 34. Rxg6+ Kf8 35. Ra6) 33. Bc3 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Rcxc4 ({Virtual concession. Black also loses after} 33... Rg4 {Andrew Soltis} 34. Bxe5 Re8 35. Ra8) (33... Rxd5 {Andrew Soltis} 34. cxd5 Bxc3 35. bxc3 Rxc3 36. Rc6 $1) 34. Bxc4 (34. Bxd4 Rxd4 35. Rxa5 $16) 34... Rxc4 35. Kd3 $18 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} (35. Bxe5 $2 Re4+ $14) 35... Rc5 36. Rxa5 Rxa5 37. Bxa5 Bxb2 38. a4 Kf8 39. Bc3 ({White still had chances to go wrong :} 39. Bb6 {Andrew Soltis} Ke7 40. a5 Kd7 41. a6 Kc6 42. a7 Kb7 {. The text wins control of a7.39. ...Ba3 40.a5 Bc5 41.a6 and Bd4. The rest went :}) 39... Bxc3 (39... Ba3 40. a5 $18) 40. Kxc3 Ke7 41. Kd4 Kd6 42. a5 f6 43. a6 Kc6 44. a7 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Kb7 45. Kd5 h4 46. Ke6 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} 1-0

