1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Bd2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'In the fifties and early sixties Taimanov played'} 9. Ne1 {Garry Kasparov: '(Games Nos.29 and 64)'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 9. b4) ({Garry Kasparov: 'but from 1967 – only'} 9. Nd2 {Garry Kasparov: '.'}) 9... Ne8 (9... c5 10. dxc6 $1 bxc6 11. Bg5 Be6 $6 12. c5 $1 Ne8 13. cxd6 Nxd6 14. Qa4 $16 {Korchnoi,V-Byrne,R/Leningrad(izt)/1973/1:0 (28)/}) (9... Nh5 $5 10. g3 (10. Rc1 f5 (10... h6 $5 11. g3 $5 (11. Re1 c5 12. g3 b6 13. Nh4 $5 Nf6 14. a3 g5 15. Ng2 $14) 11... f5 (11... c5 12. dxc6 bxc6 13. b4 $14) 12. Nh4 Nf6 13. exf5 g5 14. Ng6 Nxg6 15. fxg6 Bf5 16. Bf3 Bxg6 17. c5 $5 $14) 11. Ng5 Nf4 12. Bxf4 exf4 13. Bf3 fxe4 14. Bxe4 Nf5 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 c6 17. Re1 Qe7 $10 {Korchnoi,V-Spragett,K/Montpelier(ct)/1985/}) 10... f5 11. exf5 (11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 c6 13. Qb3 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Qc8 $13) 11... Nxf5 12. Ne4 Nf6 13. Bd3 (13. Bg5 h6 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Bd3 Bg7 16. Kg2 c6 $5 17. Qb3 Kh8 18. h4 Qd7 $1 $10 {Frias,V-Nijboer,F/Wijk aan Zee/1991}) 13... Bh6 $5 14. Bc3 Ng7 $13 {Dorfman,J-Kupreichik,V/URS/1980}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Later Black began playing'} 9... Nd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'and ...f7-f5, which put the plan with 9 Bd2 out of fashion.'} 10. b4 (10. Rc1 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. c5 h6 13. cxd6 cxd6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Qb3 Qb6 17. Nb5 Rfc8 18. Be3 $14 {Pekarek,A-Tischer,G/Biel/1989/}) 10... f5 (10... h6 11. Qb3 Kh8 12. Rac1 f5 13. Ne1 fxe4 $5 14. Nxe4 Nf5 $13) 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 Nh5 13. g3 Bf6 14. exf5 gxf5 15. f4 Ng7 16. Rc1 Ng6 17. Kh1 a5 18. a3 axb4 19. axb4 Bxg5 20. fxg5 f4 21. Ne4 Bf5 22. Nf6+ Rxf6 23. gxf6 Qxf6 $44 {Shamkovich,L-Shirazi,K/Lone Pine/1981/}) 10. Rc1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'At that same time in another Candidates match there occurred'} 10. b4 f5 11. Qb3 Nf6 12. exf5 gxf5 13. c5 Kh8 $10 {Korchnoi,V-Geller,E/Moscow(m/3)/1971} 14. cxd6 cxd6 15. Rac1 Bd7 16. a4 Ng6 17. Bb5 h6 {with equality  (Korchnoi-Geller, 3rd matchgame, Moscow 1971).}) 10... f5 (10... c5 11. dxc6 bxc6 12. b4 Nc7 13. b5 d5 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Nxd5 Ncxd5 16. exd5 Nxd5 17. Bg5 Qd7 $10 {Ribli,Z-Torre,E/Alicante(m/8)/1983}) 11. exf5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'In the 3rd game'} 11. Qb3 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'was played (Game No.92).'} b6 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Ng5 Nf6 14. f4 $13 {Taimanov,M-Fischer,R/Vankuver(m/3)/1971}) ({An attempt to improve on the variation} 11. Ng5 h6 12. Ne6 Bxe6 13. dxe6 Qc8 14. c5 Qxe6 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. Nb5 Qd7 17. Bb4 Nc6 $1 18. Bxd6 Nxd6 19. Qxd6 Qxd6 20. Nxd6 Nd4 $1 {with equality (Geller-Minic, Skopje 1968).}) 11... gxf5 12. Ng5 h6 13. Ne6 Bxe6 14. dxe6 Qc8 15. Qb3 c6 {[ c7-e6]} ({After} 15... b6 {(weakening the light squares!)} 16. Nd5 (16. c5 bxc5 {is equal}) 16... Qxe6 17. Nxe7+ (17. Qh3 $5 Kh7 18. Bf3 {is also good, since} e4 $2 {is not possible on account of} 19. Bxe4 $1 Qxe4 20. Rfe1) 17... Qxe7 18. c5+ {White has full compensation for the pawn (Taimanov-Mark Tseitlin, Leningrad 1973).}) 16. Bh5 (16. Nd5 $2 cxd5 17. cxd5 Qb8 $19) 16... Qxe6 (16... Nc7 17. Bf7+ Kh7 $13) 17. Qxb7 Nf6 18. Be2 Rfb8 19. Qa6 (19. Qc7 $4 a5 $19 {[%cal Rf6e8]}) 19... Rxb2 20. Rfd1 e4 $5 (20... Nd7 {'!?' Garry Kasparov: 'was more cautious, but also more passive, after which there could have followed'} 21. Qa3 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '; for example:'} (21. Be3 f4 22. Qa3 Rxe2 23. Rxd6 Qf5 24. Nxe2 fxe3 25. Qxe3 Nb6 $13) 21... Rbb8 (21... Z0) (21... Rb7 22. Be3 c5 ({not} 22... Nc8 $2 23. c5 $1 {, when after} Nxc5 (23... dxc5 $2 24. Qa6 $1 Rb4 25. Nd5 $3) ({, or} 23... d5 $2 24. Qa6 $1 Rb2 25. Nxd5 $1 {is bad for Black}) 24. Bxc5 dxc5 25. Na4 {and Nxc5 White has a powerful initiative}) 23. Nb5 Nc8 24. Qd3 Nf6 25. Rb1 $1 {with enduring compensation for the pawn, thanks to the weakness of the light squares (in particular d5) in the opponent's position.}) 22. Be3 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({this is superior to} 22. Rb1 Rxb1 23. Rxb1 e4 $1 24. Rb7 Nc5 $1 {, when after} 25. Rc7 ({while} 25. Rxa7 $2 Rxa7 26. Qxa7 {fails to} e3 $1 27. fxe3 Bxc3 28. Bxc3 Qxe3+ 29. Kf1 Qf4+ 30. Bf3 Qxc4+ 31. Kf2 Nd3+ {etc}) 25... a6 26. Na4 Nxa4 27. Qxa4 Rb8 $1 {Black seizes the important file}) 22... Nc8 $15 23. Qa4 $1 f4 24. Bd2 Ne7 25. Ne4 {and the position is roughly equal.}) 21. Qa3 (21. Bf4 Rb6 22. Qa5) (21. Rb1 $5) 21... Rb7 22. Bf4 d5 23. cxd5 (23. Qa6 $5 Rb6 24. Qa3) 23... cxd5 (23... Nfxd5 24. Bc4 Kh7 25. Nxd5 ({not now} 25. Qa6 $6 Rb6 26. Qa5 Bxc3 $1) 25... cxd5 (25... Nxd5 26. Qa6) 26. Bb3 Rd8 (26... Qb6 27. Bxd5 $1 Nxd5 28. Rxd5 Bb2 29. Qh3 Bxc1 30. Qxf5+ Kg7 31. Rd6 $1) 27. Qa5 Rc8 (27... Qb6 28. Qxb6 axb6 29. Rc7 $1) 28. h3 Rxc1 29. Bxc1 {with pressure that fully compensates for the missing pawn.}) ({Otherwise White's task is immeasurably easier:} 23... Nexd5 $6 24. Bc4 Kh7 (24... Rd8) ({, or} 24... Re8 25. Qa6) 25. Qa6 $1 Rd7 (25... Rb6 $6 26. Qa5) 26. Nxd5 {with equality}) 24. Nb5 Ng6 $1 ({and this is much stronger than the insipid} 24... Ne8 25. Nd4 $1 {with good play for the pawn.}) 25. Nd4 (25. Nc7 {'!?'} Qf7 26. Nxa8 Nxf4 $44 27. Ba6 Rd7 $13 28. Qe3 N6h5 $1 ({not} 28... Ne6 $2 29. Bc8 $1 Ng4 30. Qb3 $1 Rd6 $1 31. Qb8 Bf8 32. Bxe6 Rxe6 33. Nc7 {and the knight is in play}) 29. Rc6 (29. g3 Nh3+ 30. Kg2 f4 $1) 29... Be5 30. Bc8 (30. Rdc1 d4 $1) ({, or} 30. Qc5 Nf6 31. Rc1 Qg7 32. g3 Ng4 $1 {with an excellent game}) 30... Re7 $1 ({not} 30... d4 $6 31. Qb3 $1 {), and apart from ...d5-d4 the threat of ...Nf6 and ...Qh5! is strong. At the board it was very difficult to judge the consequences of the exchange sacrifice: the probability of a mistake in the calculations was extremely great. In launching into this labyrinth, Black was most probably guided by intuition and... his belief in success; White, in rejecting 25 Nc7, was most probably guided by his sense of danger. And, as computer analysis demonstrates, the intuitive judgement of the grandmasters can be trusted.})) ({Garry Kasparov: 'If'} 25. Qg3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Black had prepared'} Nh5 $1 26. Bxh5 Nxf4 27. Qxf4 Rxb5 28. Rc7 Qe5 $1) 25... Qd7 ({but} 25... Qf7 {would have left the f5-pawn undefended, and after} 26. Qe3 ({or} 26. Qg3 {White quickly restores material equality and has no problems.})) 26. Qe3 ({Later the grandmaster reproached himself for the fact that he did not play} 26. Qg3 $5 {, and after} Kh7 {–} (26... Z0) (26... Ng4 27. h3 Be5 ({Fischer would almost certainly have rejected this variation; although he could have tried} 27... Nxf4 28. Qxf4 Ne5) ({, or} 27... N4e5) 28. Bxe5 N4xe5 29. f4 $1 exf3 30. Nxf3 f4 31. Nxe5 fxg3 32. Nxd7 Rxd7 33. Rc3 {and Rxg3 with a favourable endgame for White: the bishop is stronger than the knight.}) (26... Nxf4) 27. Rc6 $1 {"with great, possibly even insoluble difficulties for Black."} Z0 ({. I agree that after} 27... Ng4 $2 28. Rxg6 Kxg6 29. Nxf5 $1) (27... Rf8 28. Rdc1 $1 Nxf4 29. Qxf4 Ne8 30. Ba6 $1) ({, and} 27... Rc8 28. Rd6 Qa4 29. Nxf5 ({with the threat of Nxg7;} 29. Bxh6 $4 Qxd1+ $1) 29... Qxa2 (29... Rb2 $2 30. Bh5 $1) 30. Bf1 $1 Nxf4 ({or} 30... Qb3 31. Qxb3 Rxb3 32. Bxh6 $1) 31. Qxf4 Rf8 32. Nxg7 Kxg7 33. R1xd5 {his difficulties are obvious.}) ({. The clever} 27... Nxf4 28. Qxf4 Qe8 $5 {also fails to equalize in view of} 29. Rdc1 $1 (29. Qxf5+ Qg6) 29... Qg6 30. Rc7 {etc.})) 26... Kh7 ({Avoiding the simplifying} 26... Nxf4 27. Qxf4 Rf8 {(cf. the previous note), Fischer places his king under the threat of a dangerous pin along the seventh rank.} 28. Nxf5 ({or} 28. Qxf5 Qxf5 29. Nxf5 Rb2 $1) ({; true, the bold} 28. h3 $5 {is possible, with a double-edged game}) 28... Nh5 $1 29. Bxh5 (29. Nxh6+ Bxh6 30. Qxh6 Nf4 $1 {is dangerous}) 29... Rxf5 30. Qg4 Rf6 31. Qg3 Qd6 {with equality.}) 27. h3 (27. Rc6 Ng4 {[%cal Rg7d4]} 28. Bxg4 fxg4 29. Rd6 Qa4 $17) ({Had White played} 27. Bb5 $1 Z0 ({, Black would have had only a choice between the dubious} 27... Rxb5 $6 28. Rc7 $1 Qxc7 (28... Qe8 $2 29. Bxh6 $1) 29. Bxc7 Rb7 30. Bd6 f4 31. Bxf4 Nxf4 (31... Ng4 $2 32. Qg3 Nxf2 33. Qxf2 Bxd4 34. Qxd4 Nxf4 35. Qf6 $1 Ng6 36. Qc6) 32. Qxf4 Rab8 33. Kf1) ({, and the counter-stroke} 27... Ng4 $1 {, leading to a slightly inferior, but tenable ending:} 28. Bxd7 Nxe3 29. Bxe3 Rxd7 30. Nxf5 Rad8 31. Nxg7 Kxg7 32. Bd4+ Kf7 33. Rc6 Rd6 {etc.})) 27... Rf8 (27... a5) ({In the event of} 27... Ne8 {(covering the invasion square of the white rook)} 28. Bg3 Rb2 29. Bh5 Ne5 30. Rc5 $1 {Black would have had to think primarily about defence.}) 28. Ba6 $1 (28. Rc5 Ne8 (28... Z0) (28... Nxf4 29. Qxf4 Ne8 30. Bb5 Qd6 31. Qxd6 Nxd6 32. Bc6) 29. Bb5 $4 (29. Nb5 d4 30. Nxd4 Nxf4 31. Qxf4 Bxd4 32. Rc4 Bxf2+ 33. Qxf2 Qg7 {, retaining the extra pawn.} (33... Z0)) 29... Bxd4 $19 30. Rxd4 Rxb5) ({Here too the energetic} 28. Bb5 $1 {would have worked:} {with the rook on f8 this is now feasible; especially as if} Qf7 {, then} 29. Ba6 {is good}) 28... Rb6 ({The sharp} 28... Nh5 $5 {came into consideration:} 29. Bxb7 ({after} 29. Bd6 Qxd6 30. Bxb7 Ngf4 $1 {Black has excellent compensation for the exchange}) 29... Nhxf4 30. Ba6 Nxg2 31. Kxg2 f4 {. After this Taimanov recommended} 32. Qa3 $1 f3+ 33. Kf1 Z0 (33... Qxh3+ 34. Ke1 Nf4 35. Z0 (35. Bf1 Qh2 36. Qe3 Be5 (36... Ng2+ 37. Bxg2 fxg2 38. Ke2 Qh5+ 39. Kd2 Qe5 {is also good – G.K.}) 37. Nxf3 exf3 38. Qxf3 {, "and White wins spectacularly." But after} Bd6 $1 39. a3 Ng6 40. Qe2 (40. Qe3 $2 Rf7 $1) 40... Bxa3 41. Rb1 Rf5 {it is hard for White to find even a draw!}) ({. In fact, White wins by} 35. Kd2 Re8 36. Rc7 Qh2 37. Qe3 Nh3 38. Qc3 $1 Qxf2+ 39. Kc1) ({, or immediately} 35. Rc7 e3 (35... Re8 36. Bf1 $1) 36. Rxg7+ $1 Kxg7 37. fxe3 Kg8 38. Kd2 {etc.})) ({. However, Black too can play more strongly –} 33... Rb8 $3 {(clearing the way for ...Bf8-b4+) with a dangerous attack, giving at least equal chances:} 34. Z0 (34. Ke1 Bf8 35. Qe3 Nh4 $1 36. Bf1 Bb4+ 37. Rd2 Ng2+ 38. Bxg2 fxg2 39. Ne2 (39. f4 g1=Q+ 40. Qxg1 e3) 39... Rg8 40. Ng1 Qb5 {etc.}) (34. Qc5 Qxh3+ 35. Ke1 e3 36. Rc2 (36. Nxf3 Re8 $1 37. Qxd5 Nf4 38. Bf1 e2 $1 39. Bxh3 exd1=Q+ 40. Kxd1 Nxd5 {with equality}) 36... Nf4 37. fxe3 Qh4+ 38. Kd2 Qf2+ 39. Kc1 Qxe3+ 40. Rcd2 Rb6 41. Nb3 Qe8 $1 42. Qa5 Qe5 43. Rc2 Rc6 $1 {etc. True, such irrational and reckless play was not at all in Fischer's style...}))) 29. Rc7 ({It was not yet too late to cut off the queen from the a4-square by} 29. Bb5 $1 Rxb5 30. Rc7 Qe8 {(see 28.Bb5 above), but in this position, to judge by his comments, Taimanov did not even consider} 31. Rxg7+ $1 {, which he had to play in the game, but with far lesser effect. The riddle of this game is that over the course of three moves White could have played Bb5, but Black did not prevent this. Fischer was probably overeager to win, whereas Taimanov was labouring under an illusion.} (31. Z0) ({. Of the three possible continuations of the attack,} 31. Nxf5 $6 Nh5 32. Nxg7 Nxg7 33. Qxa7 {can immediately be rejected, since Black's defence is obvious:} Rg8 34. Bg3 Nf8 35. a4 Rb4 36. Rxd5 Qxa4 {.}) (31. Bxh6 {is more interesting, although after} f4 $1 {White faces a difficult choice, and only} 32. Qc3 $1 {maintains the uncertainty of the situation –} ({nothing good is promised by} 32. Rxg7+ Kxh6 33. Nf5+ Kg5 34. h4+ Kxf5 35. Qh3+ Ke5) ({, or} 32. Qe2 Kxh6 $1 33. Nf5+ Kh7 $1 34. Rxg7+ Kh8 35. Nd6 Qe5 36. Rxg6 Rb2 37. Rg5 $1 f3 $1 38. Rxe5 fxe2 39. Re1 d4 40. Nxe4 Nxe4 41. Rxe4 d3) 32... Kxh6 33. Nf5+ Kh7 34. Nxg7 Qb8 35. Ne6+ Kh6 36. Nxf8 Qxf8 37. Rc6 Rb6 38. Rxb6 axb6 39. Qd4 {.}) 31... Kxg7 32. Bxh6+ {, maintaining the balance after a tactical skirmish:} Kh7 $1 (32... Kh8 $6 33. Nxb5 Qxb5 34. Qg5 $1) (32... Kg8 33. Bxf8 Nxf8 $1 ({not} 33... Kxf8 $2 34. Nxf5 $1) 34. Qg5+ $1 ({not} 34. Nxf5 $2 Rb7 35. Nd6 Qh5 36. g4 Rg7) 34... Kf7 35. Nxf5 Qe5 36. Rc1 Ng6 37. Nh6+ Kg7 38. Nf5+ {with perpetual check}) 33. Bxf8 Nxf8 34. Nxb5 Qxb5 35. Qg5 $1 (35. Qxa7+ $6 Kg6) 35... Qb2 36. Qxf5+ Ng6 $1 ({or} 36... Kg7 37. g4 Qc2 38. Qg5+ Kf7 39. Rc1 Qxa2 40. Qf5 Ke7 41. g5) 37. h4 $1 Kg7 (37... Qe5 38. Qg5 $1 {and Rb1(c1) with the invasion of the rook}) 38. h5 Ne7 39. Qg5+ Kf7 40. h6 {etc.}) 29... Qa4 30. Rxg7+ $1 ({There was essentially no choice: after} 30. Be2 Ne8 $1 {there is nothing to hope for.}) 30... Kxg7 31. Bxh6+ Kf7 ({However, it was precisely} 31... Kh7 $1 {that would have underlined Black's obvious advantage –} 32. Be2 Rf7 $1 33. Nxf5 (33. a3 Nd7) 33... Ng8 $1 {, dispelling all the opponent's illusions: if} 34. g4 ({or} 34. Rxd5 {there follows} Qxa2 {, winning.})) 32. Be2 Rfb8 33. Nxf5 $16 Rb1 34. Rxb1 $6 (34. Kh2 $1 Qxa2 (34... Qd7 35. g4 Rxd1 36. Bxd1 Rb1 37. Bb3 ({Garry Kasparov: 'The attempt'} 37. Be2 Rb2 {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of'} 38. a3 (38. a4 $5 Qxa4 39. g5 (39. Bg5 Rb6 $1) {) is parried by an unexpected counter-sacrifice of the exchange –} 39... Rxe2 $3 ({rather than} 39... Nd7 $6 40. Bh5 {with a draw}) 40. Qxe2 (40. Nd6+ Kg8 $1 41. Qxe2 Qd7 42. Nb5 Nf4 43. Qf1 N6h5 44. Nd4 Qc8 {and wins}) 40... Qd7 41. Nd4 ({or} 41. Ne3 Nf4 42. Qf1 N6h5 43. Ng4 Qc7 {etc}) 41... Ng8 42. Qh5 N8e7 43. Qe2 Qc7+ 44. Kg1 Qc1+ 45. Qf1 Qb2 {.}) 38... Qc7+ 39. Kg2 Qb6 40. Nd4 {, "and each side still has his trumps." However, in my view, after} Nd7 $1 {Black has good winning chances:} 41. Z0 (41. Bg5 Nc5 42. Qc3 (42. h4 Ne6 $1 43. Nxe6 Kxe6) 42... Na4 43. Qe3 Qc5 44. Bd1 Nc3 (44... Nb6 $5) 45. Bb3 $1 {(with the threat of Qc1)} Ke8 $1 {etc.}) (41. Bd1 Nc5 $1 (41... Nde5 42. Bb3 $1 Qb7 43. Qc3 Nd3 44. Nf5 {, and if} Rxf2+ ({after} 44... Nh4+ $2 45. Nxh4 Rxb3 46. Qg7+ Ke6 47. Qg6+ Kd7 48. Nf5 $1 {White wins}) 45. Kh1 Ke6 46. Bxd5+ $1 Kxd5 47. Qd4+ Kc6 48. Qc3+ {with a draw}) 42. Qg5 $1 Ne6 $1 43. Qf5+ Ke7 44. Bg5+ Nxg5 45. Qxg5+ Qf6 46. Nc6+ Kf7 47. Qxd5+ Kg7 48. Qd7+ Kh6 $1 49. Qd4 Qxd4 50. Nxd4 Kg5 {and Black wins.})) 37... Ne5 38. Qc5 (38. Kg3 Rg1+ $1 39. Kf4 $2 Ng6+ 40. Kg5 Nh7+ 41. Kh5 Qxf5+ 42. gxf5 Nf6#)) (34... Rxd1) 35. g4 $16) 34... Rxb1+ 35. Kh2 Qd7 36. Nd4 ({Garry Kasparov: 'I am even more surprised about where these "many experts" were looking!...'} 36. Qg5 {'' Garry Kasparov: '?'} Nh7 (36... Qc7+ {Garry Kasparov: '?'} 37. g3 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} ({White has the far more tenacious} 37. Ng3 $1 Qd6 (37... Qe5 38. Qe3 $1) 38. Qd2 Qb4 39. Qc2) 37... Rb2 {, and if now Garry Kasparov: '?'} ({Black wins by} 37... Qb6 $1) 38. Bg7 {, then the crushing Garry Kasparov: '?'} ({instead, after} 38. Nh4 Rxe2 39. Qxg6+ Ke6 40. Qf5+ Kf7 (40... Ke7 $2 41. Ng6+) 41. Bg5 $1 Qb6 42. Kg2 {White has a decisive attack.}) 38... Qb6 $1 ({but not} 38... Qe5 {, which to my surprise was recommended by many experts, on account of} 39. Bxf6 Qxf6 40. Nh6+ {with a draw." – Taimanov})) ({the main thing is that the immediate} 36... Rb2 $1 37. Ng3 Qd6 {is very strong}) 37. Qh5 Nf6 $11) ({In my view, the only correct move was} 36. Ng3 $1 {– with the queens on the board and with his own king securely covered, White would always have had counterplay against the weakened black king:} Rb2 ({or} 36... Qc7 37. Bg5 $1 Rb6 (37... Qb6 38. Qc3) 38. Qd2) 37. Qc3 (37. Qa3 Rb6 38. Bg5 {is also possible}) 37... Rxa2 $6 (37... Rb7 {(or Rb6)} 38. Be3 $1 {and Qd2, maintaining the dynamic equilibrium}) 38. Nxe4 Nxe4 39. Qg7+ Ke8 40. Qxg6+ Qf7 41. Qc6+ Kd8 42. Qa8+ Kc7 43. Bb5 {with equal chances.}) 36... Qd8 (36... Qd6+ 37. g3 Qb4 $1 38. Nc6 {0-1 (42) Taimanov,M (2620)-Fischer,R (2740) Vancouver 1971} ({However,} 38. Nf5 {would merely have prolonged the resistance – play would have gone into a technical phase:} Qb6 $1 39. Qxb6 (39. a4 Rb2 $1) (39. a3 Re1 $1 40. Qxb6 axb6 41. Bb5 Ne5) 39... axb6 40. Be3 Rb2 41. Nd6+ Ke6 42. Nb5 Rxa2 43. Bxb6 Ne5 44. Be3 Nc6 {, preparing ...d5-d4.})) 37. g3 Qa5 $1 $19 {[ e1]} 38. Nc6 Qb6 39. Nxa7 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 39. Nd4 Ra1 $1 {.}) (39. Qxb6 axb6) 39... Qxe3 40. Bxe3 (40. fxe3 Re1 41. Bg4 (41. Bb5 Ne5 $1) 41... Nxg4+ 42. hxg4 Ne5 $19 {0-1 (42) Taimanov,M (2620)-Fischer,R (2740) Vancouver 1971} (42... Ne5 43. g5 Ng4+ 44. Kg2 Nxe3+ (44... Ra1 45. Kh3 Nf2+ 46. Kg2 Rxa2) 45. Kf2 Nc2 46. Nc6 e3+ 47. Kg2 Re2+ 48. Kh3 Rf2)) 40... Ra1 (40... Re1 $1 41. Bg4 {The sealed move. 0-1 (41) Taimanov,M-Fischer,R Vancouver 1971 My Great Predecessors 4 [Garry Kasparov]} ({Without resuming the game, White resigned:} 41. Bg4 Nxg4+ 42. hxg4 Ne5 {.})) 0-1

