{Tal,M: 'Chess Informant'} 1. e4 {Tal,M} c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e6 5. Nb5 ({The old line} 5. c4 Nf6 6. Nc3 {hardly ever occurs nowadays on account of} Bb4 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. Bd3 (8. e5 $6 Ne4) 8... e5 ({this is simpler than} 8... d5 9. exd5 exd5 10. cxd5 Nxd5 11. O-O O-O {, as played long ago by Tartakower against Spielmann and Rubinstein, Vienna 1908}) 9. O-O O-O 10. Be3 ({Kramnik-Kasparov, New York rapidplay 1994; or} 10. Bd2 Bc5 $1) 10... Bxc3 $1 11. bxc3 d6 {with equality.}) 5... d6 6. Bf4 {Garry Kasparov: 'This concrete attacking 'Morphy-style' move was a favourite weapon of Fischer, which brought him a number of wins.'} ({Six months before this, in the Interzonal in Mallorca, he chose the solid} 6. c4 {against Taimanov, but he was unable to gain even a hint of an advantage. Incidentally, by contrast, the next world champion, Karpov, used to prefer 6 c4, since here Black no longer has ...Bb4 and playing for a bind is more attractive than a move earlier.}) 6... e5 (6... Ne5 $2 {is not highly regarded on account of} 7. N1a3 Nf6 8. Bxe5 $1 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. Rd1+ Ke7 11. Nd6 {(Boleslavsky)} Ne8 ({or} 11... g6 12. h4 $1 {and Rh3-d3}) 12. Nxe8 Kxe8 13. Bb5+ Ke7 14. Nc4 {with a clear advantage.}) 7. Be3 Nf6 ({Clearly not} 7... f5 $2 8. N1c3 $1 {, as played long ago by Morphy against Anderssen (Volume 1, Game No.10).}) ({Nowadays} 7... a6 $5 8. N5c3 Nf6 {is more often played; for example:} 9. Z0 (9. Bc4 Be7 ({or even} 9... Be6 10. Nd5 Nxe4 $5 {– a sharp exchange sacrifice –} 11. Bb6 Qg5 12. Nc7+ Kd7 13. Bxe6+ fxe6 14. Nxa8 Qxg2 15. Rf1 Be7 16. Nd2 Rxa8 {with excellent counterplay, Macieja-Rublevsky, Rethymnon 2003}) 10. Nd5 Nxd5 11. Bxd5 O-O 12. Nc3 Kh8 13. O-O Be6 14. Bb3 Qd7 ({not} 14... Na5 $6 15. Nd5 Nxb3 16. axb3 Bxd5 17. Qxd5 Qc7 18. c3 {Fischer-Badilles, Philippines 1967}) 15. Nd5 Bd8 {with approximate equality.}) (9. Bg5 Be7 10. Bxf6 (10. Nd2 Bg4 $1 {with equality, Almasi-Ivanchuk, Polanica Zdroj 2000}) 10... Bxf6 11. Nd2 O-O $5 ({a sacrifice for the sake of activity;} 11... Be6 12. Nc4 Nd4 {is quieter}) 12. Nc4 b5 13. Qxd6 bxc4 14. Qxc6 Be6 15. Be2 Rb8 (15... Rc8 $5) 16. O-O (16. Rb1 $5) 16... Qd2 17. Rab1 Qxc2 18. Rfc1 Qd2 19. Rd1 Qc2 20. Qxa6 Rfc8 21. h3 Be7 {1/2-1/2  (Kasparov-Anand, Frankfurt rapidplay 1998).})) 8. Bg5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Bronstein's move.'} ({After} 8. Bc4 {Fischer's suggestion is good:} Be6 $1 9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. c4 $1 Be7 {with equality}) ({while if} 8. N1c3 a6 9. Na3 {the safest is} Rb8 10. Bg5 b5 11. Nd5 Qa5+ ({or} 11... Be7) 12. Bd2 Qd8 {(Aronin-Taimanov, 30th USSR Championship, Erevan 1962).}) 8... Qa5+ {Garry Kasparov: 'The start of another opening duel. In light of this game, the queen check was criticized and disappeared from serious play for a long time – once again what told was the magic of the names and the result. Although here, as we will see, White's advantage was largely of a psychological nature.'} (8... Be6 {is more popular, as in the 6th game of the present match and the 1st game of the Fischer-Petrosian match (Game No.98)}) ({or} 8... a6 9. Bxf6 gxf6 10. N5c3 Z0 ({, and here after} 10... f5 {White can choose between the sharp} 11. Qh5 $5 ({and the quiet} 11. Nd2 {, Ivanchuk-Sveshnikov, Tallinn rapidplay 2000}) 11... Nd4 12. Bc4 {(Bronstein-Polugayevsky, 31st USSR Championship, Leningrad 1963)}) ({, but there is the interesting alternative} 10... Be6 $5 11. Nd5 Rc8 12. c3 Bg7 13. Na3 f5 14. exf5 Bxf5 15. Nc4 O-O $1 {(Borisek-Sveshnikov, Nova Gorica 2001), and the position is a favourable version for Black of the Chelyabinsk Variation. However, in 1971 its time had not yet arrived...})) 9. Qd2 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (9. Bd2 Qd8 10. Bg5 Qa5+ 11. Bd2 Qd8) 9... Nxe4 10. Qxa5 Nxa5 11. Be3 (11. Nc7+ $2 Kd7 12. Nxa8 Nxg5 $17) {Garry Kasparov: 'His hopes are associated with'} 11... Kd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'Having taken the central pawn, Black is forced to delay his development and place his king awkwardly.'} 12. N1c3 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'A surprise – a strictly positional pawn sacrifice! Exchanging the opponent's only active piece, White pins his hopes on his lead in development. It turns out that Black has a rather tedious endgame, demanding precise defence. And of course, for Taimanov, with his leaning towards active piece play, such a turn of events was not to his taste. This novelty has an interesting history. The knight move was recommended by the Soviet master Alexander Nikitin (subsequently my trainer for many years) in his opening monograph, published in Moscow in 1969. "It is staggering that I, an expert on the Sicilian Defence, missed this theoretically important rejoinder of my compatriot, whereas Fischer 'spied' it in a book in a foreign language!" writes Taimanov. As a result he had to solve the problems at the board... Fischer was distinguished by such a sharp 'chess vision' from his youth. His work with sources of information was a big step forward in the preparation of professional players. Under the influence of his achievements, a special Central Chess Club opening index was created for Soviet grandmasters.'} (12. Nxa7 $11 d5 $11 {Garry Kasparov: 'with equality.'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 12. Bxa7 $11) ({Incidentally, I should also mention the later idea of} 12. f3 {(Wahls); for example:} Nf6 13. Bxa7 d5 (13... Nc6 $5) 14. f4 Nc6 (14... exf4 $5) 15. fxe5 Bb4+ 16. N1c3 {(Am.Rodriguez-Hellsten, Erevan Olympiad 1996)} Ne4 $5 {with sharp play.}) 12... Nxc3 ({Taimanov recommends the "clever"} 12... a6 {(? – G.K.)} 13. Z0 (13. Nxe4 axb5 14. Bxb5+ Kc7 15. O-O-O Be6 {"with equality" (Lukov-Kirov, Pamporovo 1982).}) ({. But after} 13. Nd5 $1 {Black loses the exchange:} Kc6 (13... axb5 $2 14. Bxb5+ Nc6 15. Nb6+ {and Nxa8}) ({, or} 13... Rb8 $2 14. Ba7 Nc6 15. Bxb8 Nxb8 16. Na7 $1 Ke6 17. Nc7+ Kd7 18. Na8 $1) 14. Nbc7 Nf6 15. Nxa8 Nxd5 16. Bd2 $1 Nb6 (16... b6 17. b4 $1) 17. Nxb6 Kxb6 18. Bxa5+ $5 Kxa5 19. Bc4 {etc.})) 13. Nxc3 Kd8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '"The choice of this move was not an easy one," writes Taimanov. "In subsequent years several other defensive plans have been considered, but, alas, not a single fully viable one has been found... It can be concluded that in general there is no real way for Black to equalize."'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'However, the modern computer does not share this pessimism. Even after the comparatively modest'} 13... b6 {Garry Kasparov: 'it is not at all easy (if in general possible) for White to demonstrate his advantage:'} 14. f4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: '(another move feared by Taimanov)'} (14. Z0) (14. Bb5+ Kc7 $1 15. Nd5+ Kb7 {and ...Be6.}) (14. O-O-O Bb7 15. f4 f6 $1 ({Taimanov gives only} 15... Nc6 16. Bb5) 16. fxe5 fxe5 17. Rd2 ({or} 17. Nd5 Rc8 18. b3) ({, but not} 17. Be2 $6 Bxg2 18. Rhg1 Bh3 $1 {Wedberg-Sjöberg, Stockholm 1985/86}) 17... Be7 18. Be2 h5 19. Nd5 {with pressure, only counterbalancing the missing pawn (Torre-Hug, Athens 1971).}) 14... Bb7 (14... f6 15. fxe5 ({or} 15. Bb5+ Kc7 16. Nd5+ Kb7) 15... fxe5 (15... dxe5 16. O-O-O+ Ke6 17. Nb5 Kf7 {is also acceptable}) 16. Be2 Bb7 17. O-O) 15. fxe5 dxe5 16. O-O-O+ Kc8 (16... Kc7 17. Nb5+ (17. Nd5+ Bxd5 18. Rxd5 Bd6 {and ...Nc6}) 17... Kc8 18. Bd3 (18. Nxa7+ $6 Rxa7 19. Bxb6 Nc6 $1) (18. Rd2 Be7) 18... Bc6 $1 19. Rhf1 f6 20. Rf2 Kb7 {with an extra pawn and a solid position.}) 17. Be2 $40 {Balashov Garry Kasparov: '!'}) (13... Kc7 14. Nd5+ Kb8 15. O-O-O Be6 16. f4 $14) (13... Be7 14. O-O-O Rd8 15. Nd5 Nc6 16. Bb5 Re8 17. Rhf1 $1 Bd8 18. f4 exf4 19. Bxf4 Re6 20. Nc3 Ke7 21. Bc4 {[%cal Rf4d6,Rf1f7]} Ne5 22. Bxe6 Bxe6 23. Rfe1 Ba5 24. Bxe5 dxe5 25. Nd5+ Kf8 26. Rxe5 Rd8 27. b3 {1-0 Adorjan,A-Bobotsov,M/Amsterdam 1971 (27)}) 14. Nb5 $1 $44 {Garry Kasparov: 'Immediately attacking the pawn.'} ({After} 14. O-O-O Be6 15. f4 {Black can play to retain his extra material –} f6 {,} ({but} 15... Nc6 16. Be2 Be7 {is simpler, practically forcing White to regain the pawn –} 17. Nb5 Kd7 18. Nxd6 Bxd6 19. fxe5 Nxe5 20. Bc5 Nc4 21. Bxc4 Bxc4 22. Rxd6+ Kc7 23. Rhd1 Be6 {with a probable draw.})) 14... Be6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Disturbed by the opponent's novelty, Taimanov hurries to adopt a defensive stance,'} (14... d5 15. Bxa7 { 16.b6 d7 17.c7 Tal,M: '#C5 16. #CCb6 #C8d7 17. #CDc7'} (15. Z0) (15. Nxa7 $2 d4 $1) (15. O-O-O d4 16. c3 a6 $1 17. Na3 Bf5 (17... Bxa3 18. bxa3 Bg4 {also comes into consideration}) 18. Nc2 Rc8 19. Bd3 Bxd3 20. Rxd3 Nc6 (20... Bd6 $5) 21. cxd4 Nb4 $1 22. Rd2 e4 $1 23. Kb1 Nd5 {etc}) (15. c3 Be6 16. Nxa7 Ke7 (16... Kd7) ({, or} 16... Nc4 17. Bxc4 dxc4 18. O-O-O+ Ke7 {is also good}) 17. b3 ({or} 17. b4 Nc4 18. Bxc4 dxc4) 17... f5 18. Bb6 Kf6 19. Be2 Be7 {, and in all these variations White can only count on a draw.}) 15... Kd7 $1 16. Be3 (16. O-O-O Kc6 $1 {and ...Be6}) 16... d4 17. Bd2 Kd8 $1 {(an indefatigable king!)} 18. f4 (18. c3 $6 Nb3 $1) ({, or} 18. Rc1 Be6 19. c3 d3 $1 20. Bxd3 Nc4) 18... f6 19. fxe5 fxe5 20. Bg5+ Be7 21. Bxe7+ Kxe7) 15. O-O-O b6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Taimanov is consistent in his own way,'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'but here too'} 15... d5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'was stronger; for example:'} 16. Bxa7 (16. f4 f6 $1 (16... Nc6 {with equality}) 17. Bxa7 Kd7 $1 ({if} 17... Rxa7 $6 18. Nxa7 Bc5 {there is now} 19. f5 $1 Bf7 20. Nb5) 18. Bb6 Bd6 $1 {, and Black successfully completes his development. In the event of} 19. fxe5 (19. f5 Bf7) 19... fxe5 20. c4 $6 Rhc8 $1 (20... Nxc4 {is equal}) 21. Nxd6 Kxd6 22. c5+ Rxc5+ $1 23. Bxc5+ Kxc5 {the compensation for the exchange is obvious even to the computer.}) 16... Rxa7 $5 17. Nxa7 Bc5 18. Nb5 Bxf2 {with excellent play for the exchange}) ({Balashov's recommendation} 15... Nc4 {is not so good, as White can play} 16. Bxc4 ({rather than} 16. Bxa7 Kd7 17. a4 Be7) 16... Bxc4 17. Nxd6 Bxd6 18. Bc5 $1 f6 19. Bxd6 Kc8 (19... Ke8 20. f4 $1) 20. Rhe1 {, retaining some initiative.}) 16. f4 $1 (16. Nxd6 {'?!'} Bxd6 17. Rxd6+ Ke7 {Tal} 18. Rd1 Bc4 $11) ({Of course not the greedy} 16. Nxa7 $2 Kc7 17. Nb5+ Kc6 $1 {. Instead of this Fischer opens the centre, builds up the pressure and gradually squeezes his opponent in a positional vice, forcing him to conduct a lengthy and gruelling defence.}) 16... exf4 (16... e4 17. Nxd6 (17. Nxa7 Kc7 18. Nb5+ Kc6 19. Nd4+ $16 {Balashov}) 17... Bxd6 18. Rxd6+ Ke7 19. Rd4 $16 (19. Rd1 $14)) ({Garry Kasparov: 'After'} 16... f6 17. fxe5 fxe5 18. Nxd6 $16 {Balashov Garry Kasparov: 'Black suffers – and now for free! – on account of his weak e5-pawn.'}) 17. Bxf4 Nb7 18. Be2 Bd7 (18... Be7 19. Bf3 $1) 19. Rd2 $1 Be7 ({If} 19... a6 {there could have followed} 20. Nc3 (20. Nxd6 {with equality}) 20... Bc6 (20... Be6 21. Bf3) 21. Bc4 $1 f6 {(Black is obliged to create new 'holes')} 22. Bd5 ({or} 22. Re1 {.})) 20. Rhd1 Bxb5 {Garry Kasparov: '"The only move," considers Taimanov: Black hurries to eliminate the annoying knight.'} (20... g5 21. Bxd6 $18) (20... g5 21. Nxd6 $18) (20... a6 21. Nc3 $16 {Balashov} (21. Bxd6 Bg5 $19) (21. Nxd6 Nxd6 22. Bxd6 (22. Kb1 Kc7 23. Bxd6+ Bxd6 24. Rxd6 Rhd8 {with equality}) 22... Bg5) 21... Bc6 22. Bc4 Kc7 23. Nd5+ (23. Re1 Bf6 $1 24. Bxf7 g5 25. Bg3 Bxc3 26. bxc3 Raf8 {with equality}) 23... Bxd5 24. Bxd5 Rae8 {he would have had every chance of parrying the onslaught; for example:} 25. Rd4 Rhf8 26. Rc4+ Kb8 27. Rc6 Bd8 {.}) 21. Bxb5 Kc7 ({In view of the subsequent play, Black should have guarded the e7-square against invasion by the white rooks:} 21... Rc8 $5 22. Ba6 Rc7 $1 23. c3 Kc8 24. Kc2 Rd8 25. Re2 h6 26. Rde1 Bf6 {, retaining a defensible position.}) 22. Re2 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Bf6 23. Rde1 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'After this subtle regrouping of his rooks, White's pressure increases.'} Rac8 (23... Nc5 24. Re7+ Bxe7 25. Rxe7+ Kd8 26. Bxd6 $16 {Balashov} (26. Rxf7 h6 (26... Rb8 27. Bc6 $1) 27. Bxd6 a6 28. Be7+ $1 Kc8 29. Be2 $1 Ra7 (29... h5 30. Bh4 $1) 30. Bg4+ Nd7 31. Rxg7 {and wins.}) 26... a6 27. Bc4 Rc8 (27... Rf8 28. b4 $16) 28. Rxf7 (28. Bxf7 Rc6 $17) 28... Ne4 29. Be6 (29. Be7+ Ke8 30. Bd5 Nc3 $1 31. bxc3 Rc7 32. Rxg7 Rxe7 33. Bc6+) 29... Nxd6 30. Rd7+ Ke8 31. Rxd6 $13 {PS}) 24. Bc4 Rhf8 25. b4 $1 $16 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} a5 26. Bd5 {[ e7] Tal,M: '[#C5 #CBe7]' Garry Kasparov: '!'} Kb8 27. a3 Rfd8 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (27... Rc3 28. bxa5 bxa5 29. Kd2 Rd8 30. Rb1 Rc7 31. Be3 $16 {PS}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'There is no choice – Black would have lost quickly after, for example,'} 27... axb4 28. axb4 Rc7 {Garry Kasparov: '(with a cunning trap)'} 29. Be3 $1 $18 {Balashov Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({but not} 29. Bxb7 Kxb7 30. Bxd6 Ra8 $1 31. Bxc7 $2 Bc3 $3 {and ...Ra1 mate!}) 29... Ka7 30. c4 Bc3 31. Ra2+ Kb8 32. Ree2 Re7 33. Bxb6 Rxe2 34. Rxe2 Bxb4 35. Re7 {etc.}) 28. Bxf7 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'White restores material equality and retains his active rooks – this is undoubtedly the best practical chance, the only real possibility of continuing to play for a win.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'But Taimanov is of a different opinion: "For perhaps the first time in the game Fischer deviates from his basic strategy of playing for a total bind.' Garry Kasparov: 'In addition, after'} 28. Re4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'it is also possible to play'} Bc3 (28... Rc7 29. Be3 $1 {was stronger and more consistent." However, after} Rc3 $1 {White would not have achieved anything:} 30. Bxb6 Rdc8 31. R1e2 Rxa3 {with equality.}) (28... Rd7 {, and if} 29. Be3 {, then} axb4 30. axb4 b5 {and ...Be5, approaching equality. This instance shows that the two players were thinking at the board in somewhat different categories: Taimanov – more abstractly, Fischer – more concretely (and in the end he was proved right!).}) 29. R1e2 $16 {Tal}) 28... Bc3 $1 $14 29. Bd2 d5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 30. Rd1 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (30. Re7 $6 {would have proved a blank shot:} Bxd2+ 31. Kxd2 Nd6 32. bxa5 Nc4+ 33. Ke2 Nxa5 34. Kd3 Nc6 35. R7e6 Kc7 36. Be8 Rd6 {.}) 30... d4 31. Bxc3 Rxc3 (31... dxc3 32. Bd5 {Balashov}) 32. Kb2 d3 $1 33. Kxc3 dxe2 34. Re1 Nd6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black has managed to hold out and even to activate his pieces, but it is still a thorny path to the desired draw. In the Fischer era there was a sharp rise in the standard of good and very good moves, which had to be made in order at least not to lose a game...'} 35. Bh5 Nb5+ $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'After expending much strength defending, in time-trouble "natural in view of the need to solve the problems, literally heaped up by Fischer's inexhaustible imagination", Taimanov fails to find a saving chance.'} (35... Rc8+ 36. Kb2 (36. Kd3) 36... Nc4+ (36... g6 $1 {(V.Belov), with the idea of exploiting the pin on the e-file, would have saved Black:} 37. Z0 (37. Bf3 $6 Nc4+ 38. Ka2 Nd2 $1) (37. Bg4 Rc4 38. Bxe2 Re4 $1 39. bxa5 bxa5 40. Kc3 (40. c3 Nc4+ 41. Kb3 Kc7 {with a draw}) 40... Nb5+ 41. Kd2 (41. Kd3 Rd4+ 42. Ke3 Nxa3 $1 43. Bd3 Rb4 {also leads to a draw}) 41... Nxa3 42. Bd3 (42. Ra1 Nxc2) 42... Rxe1 43. Kxe1 Nb1 $1 {and the knight is not inferior to the powerful bishop.}) (37. Bxe2 Re8 38. bxa5 $1 ({. nothing is achieved by} 38. Kc1) ({, or} 38. Kc3 Re3+ 39. Kd2 Rxa3 {, and if} 40. Bd3 (40. bxa5 $2 {is not possible:} Ne4+ $1) 40... axb4 41. Re6 Nc8 {with a draw}) 38... bxa5 39. Kc3 $1 (39. c3 a4 40. Kc2 Nb5 41. Kd2 {is parried by} Rd8+ 42. Bd3 Nxa3 43. Re7 Nb1+) 39... Nb5+ 40. Kd2 Nxa3 41. Rc1 $1 Rd8+ 42. Kc3 Rd5 43. Bf3 (43. Bd3 Nb5+) 43... Rc5+ $1 44. Kd3 Nc4 45. Rb1+ Kc8 46. Kd4 Rc7 {, gaining a draw.})) 37. Ka2 {Tal} (37. Kb3 Nd2+ 38. Ka4 Rxc2 (38... axb4 39. axb4 g6 (39... Rxc2 40. Rxe2 g6 41. Bf3) 40. Bg4 $1 ({it would appear that} 40. Bxe2 Rxc2 $6 ({but} 40... Re8 $1 {is more tenacious}) 41. Kb5 $1 {etc is also strong}) 40... Rxc2 41. Rxe2 Rc4 {in Taimanov's opinion Black would have had "good chances of saving this agonizing game." Alas, after} 42. h3 $1 {these chances would have evaporated like a morning mist:} Ne4 ({or} 42... Rd4 43. Re8+ Kc7 44. Re7+ Kc6 45. Be2) 43. Kb5 $1 Rd4 44. Rc2 Nf6 45. Bf3 Ne8 46. Re2 $1 Nc7+ 47. Kxb6 Rxb4+ 48. Kc5 Rb1 49. Re7 h5 50. Kd6 {with an overwhelming superiority.}) 39. Rxe2 $16 {Balashov}) 37... Ne3 $1) (35... Rc8+ {Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 36. Kb3 Nb5 {Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw'} 37. c4 Nd4+ 38. Kc3 Nb5+ $11) 36. Kb2 axb4 37. axb4 Rd4 {Garry Kasparov: 'A new period of suffering for Black begins.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"'} 37... g6 38. Bf3 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (38. Bxe2 $2 Re8 39. c3 Re3) 38... Nd4 39. Be4 Re8 40. Bd3 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'etc would not have helped." (Balashov)'}) 38. c3 Rh4 (38... Rd2+ 39. Kb3 Rd3 40. Kc4 {'+-' Tal}) 39. Bxe2 Nd6 40. Rd1 Kc7 41. h3 Rf4 ({The attempt} 41... b5 $2 {is not good on account of} 42. Rd5 Kc6 43. Bf3 $1 Nc4+ ({or} 43... Kd7 44. Rc5) 44. Kb3 Kb6 45. Be2 $1 {.}) 42. Rf1 Re4 43. Bd3 Re5 44. Rf2 h5 45. c4 {Garry Kasparov: 'The game was adjourned in this position, a dismal one for Taimanov. The next day there followed:'} Rg5 (45... Re3 46. Kc3 Ne4+ 47. Kd4 Rxd3+ 48. Kxe4 $18) 46. Kc3 Kd7 47. Ra2 Kc8 48. Kd4 Kc7 49. Ra7+ (49. h4 Rg4+ 50. Kd5 Rxh4 51. c5) (49. Re2 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} Kd7 50. c5 bxc5+ 51. bxc5 Nc8 52. Bb5+ Kc7 53. Ba6 {would also have left Black little chance of saving the game.}) (49. Rc2 $18 {Evans}) 49... Kd8 50. c5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '"A surprising error for a player of such high class.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"'} 50. Ra6 $1 Kc7 51. c5 bxc5+ (51... Nc8 52. Be4 {Garry Kasparov: 'with strong centralization'}) 52. bxc5 Nc8 ({or} 52... Nf5+ 53. Bxf5 Rxf5 54. Rg6 Rf4+ 55. Kd5 Rf7 56. Rg5 {and wins – G.K.}) 53. Rg6 $18 {Balashov Garry Kasparov: 'was better, and the minor piece endgame is hopeless for Black."  (Balashov).'}) (50. Ra2) 50... bxc5+ 51. bxc5 Ne8 52. Ra2 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'This strange retreat shows that Fischer had lost the thread of the game. Apparently before the resumption he reckoned that the position could be won 'as he pleased'.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'White could still have retained a big advantage by'} 52. Be4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Nc7 53. Kc4 Ne6 ({or} 53... Re5 54. Bf3 Re1 55. c6 h4 56. Ra5) 54. c6 Nc7 55. Ra3 $1 Re5 56. Rd3+ Kc8 57. Bf3 {.}) 52... Nc7 53. Bc4 Kd7 54. Rb2 ({Garry Kasparov: 'And the rook endgame after'} 54. Bb3 $1 Kc6 55. Ba4+ Nb5+ 56. Bxb5+ Kxb5 57. h4 {is drawn in view of} Rg3 $1 58. Kd5 Rd3+ 59. Ke6 Rg3 60. Rc2 Kc6 {.}) (54. Re2 Kc6 55. Re7 Rxg2 56. Bd3 Nb5+ 57. Kc4 Na3+ 58. Kb4 Nc2+ 59. Ka5 {would not have won, on account of the well-known operation} Kxc5 $1 60. Rc7+ Kd6 $1 61. Rxc2 Rxc2 62. Bxc2 g5 63. Kb6 g4 64. h4 Ke7 $1 {.}) 54... Kc6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'A splendid reply!'} 55. Bb3 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Perhaps some winning chances were offered by'} 55. Rb6+ Kd7 $11 56. Bb3 ({it transpires that the planned invasion} 56. Rd6+ Ke7 {leaves the g2-pawn undefended}) 56... Rxg2 57. Ba4+ Ke7 58. Rb7 Kd8 {, but Fischer simply gives up his passed pawn and begins playing practically anew... from a position in which many grandmasters would have agreed to a draw!}) 55... Nb5+ (55... Rxc5 56. Ba4+) 56. Ke3 (56. Ke4 Kxc5 {1-0 (87) Fischer,R (2740)-Taimanov,M (2620) Vancouver 1971}) 56... Kxc5 57. Kf4 Rg6 {Garry Kasparov: 'It is hard to imagine that a candidate for the title of world champion could lose such an endgame. But all the time Fischer creates various little problems, eating away at his opponent's strength and nerves.'} 58. Bd1 h4 59. Kf5 Rh6 (59... Rg3 60. Bg4 Nd6+ 61. Kg5 $16 {PS}) 60. Kg5 Nd6 61. Bc2 Nf7+ 62. Kg4 Ne5+ 63. Kf4 Kd4 64. Rb4+ Kc3 65. Rb5 Nf7 (65... Kxc2 66. Rxe5 Rg6 67. Rg5 $18 {Soloviev}) 66. Rc5+ Kd4 67. Rf5 g5+ 68. Kg4 Ne5+ ({Garry Kasparov: 'There was also another way to draw –'} 68... Rc6 {[%cal Rc6c2,Rf7h6]} {Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 69. Ba4 (69. Rxf7 Rxc2 70. Kxg5 Rxg2+ 71. Kxh4 Ke4 $11) (69. Rf2 $5 Ne5+ 70. Kxg5 Ke3 71. Rf5 Rxc2 72. Rxe5+ Kf2 ({or} 72... Kd4 {.})) 69... Nh6+ 70. Kxg5 Nxf5 71. Bxc6 $11 {Balashov} Ke5 72. Ba8 (72. Bf3 Ne3 73. Kxh4 Nxg2+ $1) 72... Ne3 73. Bf3 (73. Kxh4 Nxg2+ 74. Bxg2 Kf6 $11) 73... Nf5 $11 {PS}) 69. Kxg5 Rg6+ 70. Kxh4 Rxg2 71. Bd1 Rg8 ({Garry Kasparov: 'There is no need for such an extreme measure as'} 71... Ng6+ 72. Kh5 Ke4 (72... Ke3 73. Bg4 Nf4+ 74. Kh4 Nxh3 {, forcing the theoretically drawn endgame with rook and bishop against rook. Taimanov avoided this, fearing that his opponent would play it out right to the end. Fischer later confirmed that he was ready to make all the 50 moves provided for by the rules.}) 73. Rf7 Nf4+ 74. Kh6 Rg6+ (74... Rh2 75. h4 Rxh4+ 76. Kg5 Rh1 77. Rxf4+ Ke5 $11 {Balashov}) 75. Kh7 Rg1 $11 {Mikenas}) 72. Bg4 Ke4 73. Kg3 $11 {Garry Kasparov: 'Here the game was adjourned for the second time and, to Taimanov's misfortune, the following session took place only after the 3rd game, which he again "painfully" lost. By all the indicators the position is a dead draw. "I, and not only I," wrote Tal, "would not have played on in this position, whatever the tournament situation – it is a pure waste of time. But Fischer plays on to the bare kings!" I think that, from the viewpoint of match strategy, his decision to continue the struggle was fully justified. Fischer, with his well-developed 'killer instinct', realized very well that his opponent, who the previous day had lost an 'almost won' game, would be severely depressed and in no mood for playing. Indeed, Taimanov did not find the strength to dismiss the stormy events of the 3rd game: abandoning the analysis of the 'dead-drawn' adjourned position, together with one of his seconds, grandmaster Vasyukov, he spent the entire night looking for a win after the ill-starred 20 Qh3 (cf. Game No.92). No one could tear him away from this pointless occupation, either result of which would not have pacified him. It need hardly be said that after his sleepless night Taimanov arrived for the resumption with a heavy head and without a clear plan of how to make a draw. And the 'miracles' were not long in coming.'} Rg7 74. Rf4+ Kd5 75. Ra4 (75. Rf6 Nxg4 $4 76. hxg4 $18) ({"More practical chances were offered by} 75. Rf8 {, since after} Nxg4 $2 ({Black would have had to reply} 75... Ke4 76. Re8 Rf7 {G.K.}) 76. hxg4 {the endgame is won for White" (Balashov):} Ke6 77. Kh4 $1 {etc.}) 75... Ng6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'But here the transition into a rook ending –'} 75... Nxg4 $1 76. hxg4 Ke6 {'=' Garry Kasparov: 'would have led to a theoretical draw:'} 77. Kh4 (77. Rf4 Rf7 {Garry Kasparov: '!'}) 77... Rg8 $1 {Garry Kasparov: ', or'} 78. Rf4 (78. Kh5 Kf6 79. Ra6+ Kf7 80. g5 Rh8+ 81. Rh6 Ra8 {. However, for some reason Taimanov does not eliminate the 'Fischer bishop', which in the end strikes him a cruel blow...}) (78. g5 Kf5) 78... Ke5 $11 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 79. Rf5+ Ke6) (75... Rg8) 76. Ra6 (76. Ra5+ Kd6 77. Rg5 Ke7 $11) 76... Ne5 77. Kf4 Rf7+ 78. Kg5 Rg7+ 79. Kf5 Rf7+ 80. Rf6 Rxf6+ 81. Kxf6 Ke4 $4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Undoubtedly the most terrible nightmare in the chess biography of Mark Taimanov. Black had a simple choice of four moves, three of which led to a quick draw, and one to an equally quick loss. And this was the one that Taimanov made...'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'as well as the elegant'} 81... Nd3 {'!'} 82. h4 Nf4 83. Kf5 Kd6 $1 $11 {'=' Botvinnik (Nach 6 Sekunden) Garry Kasparov: '!'} 84. Kxf4 Ke7 {.}) (81... Nd3 82. Kf5 Kd6 $11) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 81... Kd6 82. Be2 Nd7+ 83. Kf7 (83. Kg7 Ke7 84. h4 Nf6 85. Bf3 Ne8+ 86. Kg6 Kf8 87. Bd5 Ng7 88. Bc4 Nh5) 83... Ke5 84. h4 Nf6 $11 {Matanovic}) ({The hard-earned draw would have been secured by} 81... Kd4 82. Bc8 Nf3 83. Bb7 Nh4 84. Kg5 Ke5 85. Kxh4 Kf6) 82. Bc8 $1 $18 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Oh horrors!'} Kf4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Such things happen only in co-operative problems! The king has placed itself such that it prevents its own knight from stopping the enemy pawn:'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'neither'} 82... Nf3 83. Bb7+ {'+-'}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'nor'} 82... Nd3 83. Bf5+ {'+-' Garry Kasparov: 'is possible.'}) 83. h4 Nf3 (83... Ng4+ 84. Kg7 $18) 84. h5 Ng5 85. Bf5 (85. Kg6 $22 Nf3 86. h6 Nh4+ 87. Kf6 {1-0 (87) Fischer,R (2740)-Taimanov,M (2620) Vancouver 1971} (87. Kf6 Nf3 88. h7)) 85... Nf3 86. h6 Ng5 87. Kg6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black is in zugzwang and the pawn slips through to the queening square.'} Nf3 88. h7 Nh4+ (88... Ne5+ 89. Kf6 {1-0 (89) Fischer,R-Taimanov,M Vancouver 1971 My Great Predecessors 4 [Garry Kasparov]}) 89. Kf6 1-0

