1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. Bd2 Ne8 (9... c5 10. dxc6 $1 bxc6 11. Bg5 Be6 $6 12. c5 $1 Ne8 13. cxd6 Nxd6 14. Qa4 $16 {Korchnoi,V-Byrne,R/Leningrad(izt)/1973/1:0 (28)/}) (9... Nh5 $5 10. g3 (10. Rc1 f5 (10... h6 $5 11. g3 $5 (11. Re1 c5 12. g3 b6 13. Nh4 $5 Nf6 14. a3 g5 15. Ng2 $14) 11... f5 (11... c5 12. dxc6 bxc6 13. b4 $14) 12. Nh4 Nf6 13. exf5 g5 14. Ng6 Nxg6 15. fxg6 Bf5 16. Bf3 Bxg6 17. c5 $5 $14) 11. Ng5 Nf4 12. Bxf4 exf4 13. Bf3 fxe4 14. Bxe4 Nf5 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 c6 17. Re1 Qe7 $10 {Korchnoi,V-Spragett,K/Montpelier(ct)/1985/}) 10... f5 11. exf5 $1 (11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 c6 13. Qb3 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Qc8 $13) 11... Nxf5 (11... Bxf5 12. Ng5 Nf6 13. g4 Bd7 14. Nge4 Nxe4 15. Nxe4 $16 {Taimanov}) 12. Ne4 Nf6 13. Bd3 (13. Bg5 h6 14. Bxf6 Bxf6 15. Bd3 Bg7 16. Kg2 c6 $5 17. Qb3 Kh8 18. h4 Qd7 $1 $10 {Frias,V-Nijboer,F/Wijk aan Zee/1991}) 13... Bh6 $5 14. Bc3 Ng7 $13 {Dorfman,J-Kupreichik,V/URS/1980}) (9... Nd7 10. b4 (10. Rc1 f5 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. c5 h6 13. cxd6 cxd6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 fxe4 16. Qb3 Qb6 17. Nb5 Rfc8 18. Be3 $14 {Pekarek,A-Tischer,G/Biel/1989/}) 10... f5 (10... h6 11. Qb3 Kh8 12. Rac1 f5 13. Ne1 fxe4 $5 14. Nxe4 Nf5 $13) 11. Ng5 Nf6 12. f3 Nh5 13. g3 Bf6 14. exf5 gxf5 15. f4 Ng7 16. Rc1 Ng6 17. Kh1 a5 18. a3 axb4 19. axb4 Bxg5 20. fxg5 f4 21. Ne4 Bf5 22. Nf6+ Rxf6 23. gxf6 Qxf6 $44 {Shamkovich,L-Shirazi,K/Lone Pine/1981/}) 10. Rc1 (10. b4 f5 11. Qb3 Nf6 12. exf5 gxf5 13. c5 Kh8 $10 {Korchnoi,V-Geller,E/Moscow(m/3)/1971}) 10... f5 (10... c5 11. dxc6 (11. a3 f5 12. b4 h6 13. Ne1 b6) 11... bxc6 12. b4 Nc7 (12... f5 13. c5 $1 $16) 13. b5 d5 (13... Ne6 14. bxc6) 14. cxd5 cxd5 15. Nxd5 Ncxd5 16. exd5 Nxd5 17. Bg5 Qd7 $10 {Ribli,Z-Torre,E/Alicante(m/8)/1983}) 11. Qb3 $14 {Garry Kasparov: '!?' Garry Kasparov: 'A new plan. The queen eyes the b7-pawn and increases the effectiveness of the threat to advance with c4-c5.'} (11. exf5 {Garry Kasparov: '– Game No.90.'} gxf5 12. Ng5 h6 13. Ne6 Bxe6 14. dxe6 Qc8 15. Qb3 c6 $132 {Taimanov,M-Fischer,R(1)/Vancouver cqf 1971}) 11... b6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Forestalling White's intentions, but weakening the c6-square, which will soon play an important role.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'There were some perfectly safe alternatives:'} 11... Kh8 {Tal} 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Ng5 h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 c6 $1 16. Qxb7 Nc7) (11... f4 12. c5 Kh8 13. cxd6 cxd6 14. Nb5 h6 15. Qc2 Bd7 16. Bb4 Rf6 $11 {Boleslavsky}) (11... h6 {Vitolinsh}) ({or} 11... Nf6 12. exf5 gxf5 13. Ng5 c6 14. f4 h6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Qc8 17. c5 Qc7 $1 18. Rcd1 e4 19. Be3 Nfd5 {.}) 12. exf5 gxf5 (12... Nxf5 $14 {[%csl Ge4]}) (12... Bxf5 $14 {[%csl Ge4]}) 13. Ng5 Nf6 (13... h6 14. Ne6 Bxe6 15. dxe6 Qc8 (15... c6 16. Bh5 $1) 16. c5 $1 (16. Nd5 $1 Qxe6 17. Nxe7+ Qxe7 18. c5+ Kh8 19. cxd6 Nxd6 (19... cxd6 20. Rfd1 {[%cal Rd2b4] b4}) 20. Rc6 $1 $16 {Taimanov}) 16... bxc5 (16... dxc5 17. Nd5 $16) 17. Nb5 $16 (17. Nd5 $1 $16 {Vitolinsh})) ({Garry Kasparov: '"If'} 13... Ng6 {Garry Kasparov: 'all the same'} 14. f4 {Garry Kasparov: 'is possible, and after'} Nxf4 15. Bxf4 exf4 16. Ne6 Bxe6 17. dxe6 $16 {Balashov Garry Kasparov: 'White's chances are preferable."  (Balashov)'}) 14. f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'This attack on the centre is a key link in White's plan.'} h6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Accepting the challenge,'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'since'} 14... e4 {'?!'} 15. Nd1 (15. Be3 h6 16. Nh3 {[%cal Yc3b5,Yb5d4] b5-d4} Ng4 $1 17. Bxg4 fxg4 18. Nf2 Nf5) (15. Kh1 $5 {[%cal Yd2e3] e3}) 15... h6 16. Nh3 {Garry Kasparov: 'followed by Ne3, Nf2 and Bc3 is not at all good for Black.'} Z0 17. Ne3 Z0 18. Nf2 Z0 19. Bc3 {Taimanov}) 15. fxe5 (15. Nh3 $5 {Hanging pawns Balashov}) 15... dxe5 (15... hxg5 16. exf6 Bxf6 17. Bh5 $18 {Taimanov} (17. Bd3 $5) 17... Kg7 18. Ne2 Rh8 19. g4 {Balashov}) 16. c5 $1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'The quiet'} 16. Nf3 $14 {Taimanov} Ng6 {would have left White with a slight positional plus, but he is aiming for a grand battle. "There now begins a hand-to-hand fight, demanding bold imagination, precise calculation and... strong nerves. Incidentally, in this game the nerves of both of us played tricks. At one point when, after making my move, by habit I was walking up and down the stage, Fischer unexpectedly interrupted his thinking and via his chargé d'affaires Edmondson complained to the chief arbiter Kazic that my walking was disturbing him. Kazic tactfully informed me of this, adding that he did not support Fischer's complaint, since I was within my rights to walk about on my half of the stage. But, not wanting a conflict, I suggested a gentlemen's compromise: if Bobby were to give up his habit of shaking his leg under the table, I would go behind the scenes. Fischer agreed and a consensus was found." (Taimanov) A highly characteristic episode. Firstly, Fischer's demands always increased at times when the match situation became aggravated (this also happened later in Reykjavik). Secondly, the behaviour of the arbiter looks strange – again what told was the magic of Fischer's name. It is doubtful whether Kazic would have risked speaking to him during the game (a scandal: disturbing his concentration!), whereas he spoke to Taimanov... And thirdly, if Bobby had been playing Karpov, the latter would have replied via his chargé d'affaires Baturinsky that everything was in accordance with the rules and that Fischer should refer to a psychologist!}) 16... Nfxd5 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'The time when Black had to find the only moves had not yet arrived:'} (16... hxg5 {'?'} 17. d6+ {'!'} Kh8 (17... Kh7 18. dxe7 Qxe7 {was condemned because of} 19. cxb6 Z0 (19... axb6 20. Bxg5 Be6 21. Qc2 {, when the black king risks coming under an attack (} Kg6 22. Bxf6 $1 Rxf6 23. g4 $1 {)}) ({, but} 19... Qc5+ 20. Kh1 Qxb6 $1 {was stronger, and if} 21. Qc2 {a pawn can be given up under more favourable circumstances –} Rb8 ({or even} 21... Kg6 $1 22. Na4 Qd6 23. Be3 Rh8) 22. Na4 Qd4 23. Bxg5 Ne4 $1 {.})) 18. dxe7 Qxe7 19. cxb6 axb6 20. Bxg5 $16 {Taimanov/Balashov} (20. Nb5 Be6 21. Qh3+ Kg8 22. Bxg5 $16 (22. Nxc7 $16))) (16... Kh8 17. d6 $1 $16 {Only a transposition. Tal}) 17. Nxd5 Nxd5 18. cxb6 (18. Rfd1 Kh8 19. Bb4 Qxg5 20. Qxd5 Rb8 21. cxb6 $4 Bb7 $19 {Balashov}) 18... axb6 (18... hxg5 $5 19. Bxg5 (19. bxc7 $1 Qd6 (19... Qd7 $2 20. Bc4 $18) 20. Bb4 Qxb4 (20... Qb6+ 21. Rf2 (21. Kh1 Be6 22. Bc4 Nxc7 23. Bxf8 Rxf8 $15) 21... Be6 $13 {PS}) 21. Qxd5+ Kh7 22. Qxa8 $18 {Evans}) 19... Qd6 $1 (19... Qxg5 20. Qxd5+ Kh7 21. Qxa8 $18 (21. Rc3 $5)) 20. b7 Rb8 $1 (20... Bxb7 21. Qxb7 $16 {Balashov/Taimanov}) 21. bxc8=Q Rxb3 22. Qxf8+ Bxf8 $13 {Cafferty}) 19. Rc6 $1 (19. Bc4 c6) 19... Kh8 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'Moving out of the diagonal pin.'} (19... hxg5 20. Bc4 Kh8 (20... Z0) ({, or} 20... Kh7 21. Bxd5 g4 22. Bb4 $1 Re8 (22... Rh8 23. Bf7 $1) 23. Bf7 Bd7 24. Qc2 $1 e4 25. Rd1 $1 Be5 26. Bg6+ $1 {. The culminating point of the game and of the entire match has been reached. Taimanov, as he put it, "experienced a genuine thrill of the struggle, judging my position to be highly promising," but when he got down to concrete calculation, disillusionment set in: "All my understanding of chess, all my experience and feeling convinced me that the position after 20 Qh3! should be winning – but no concrete ways to victory could be found! Disillusioned with the queen move, I began analysing other ideas – 20 Rd1 and 20 Bc4, but also in vain. And here I was overcome by a state of helplessness, of despair: 'Is this Fischer invulnerable, is he somehow bewitched?!' But time was passing and time-trouble was beginning to approach... I thought over this position for 72 minutes! During the half century that I have been playing I have never spent so much time on one move! And psychologically I simply collapsed. My energy dried up, apathy ensued, everything around lost sense, and I made the first move that came into my head, which turned out to be a losing one..."}) 21. Bxd5 (21. Bxg5 $1 Qxg5 22. Qh3+ Bh6 23. Rxh6+ Kg7 24. Rh7+ Kg6 25. Bxd5 f4 (25... Ra7 26. Rh8 $1) 26. Be4+ Bf5 27. Rxc7 Bxe4 28. Qh7+ Kf6 29. Qxe4) 21... f4 (21... g4) 22. Rc3 $1 { h3 Tal}) (19... Bb7 20. Ne6 Qd7 21. Rxc7 $1 Nxc7 22. Nxf8+ Qf7 (22... Kxf8 23. Bb4+ $18) 23. Qxf7+ Kxf7 24. Rxf5+ Kg8 25. Bc4+ (25. Nd7 $5) 25... Bd5 26. Bxd5+ Nxd5 27. Ne6 Bh8 28. Bxh6 Rxa2 $16) 20. Nf3 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer later stated that "Taimanov missed a win with 20 Qh3", but he did not give any concrete variations. That is how myths are born! Let us try to figure out what in fact Taimanov missed.'} (20. Qh3 $1 Nf6 (20... Rf6 21. Bc4 $1 {[%cal Rc6f6]} (21. Qh5 Bd7 $1 22. Rxf6 Qxf6 23. Nf7+ (23. Ne4 Qf8 24. Bg4 Nf4 25. Bxf4 exf4 26. Rxf4 Ra5 $17 {Balashov} (26... Qb4 $19 {PS})) 23... Kh7 24. Nxh6 Bxh6 25. Bxh6 Qg6 $11) (21. Nf7+ Rxf7 22. Bxh6 Bf6 $1 $19) 21... f4 (21... Rxc6 22. Nf7+ Kh7 23. Nxd8 Rxc4 {Klimov} 24. Nf7 {'='} Kg6 $1 25. Nxh6 (25. Nh8+ Kh7 26. Nf7 $11) 25... Nf4 26. Qf3 $1 (26. Bxf4 exf4 $36) 26... Rxa2 (26... Re4 27. Bxf4 exf4 28. Nxf5 Bxf5 29. g4 $1 $16) (26... Rb8 27. Bxf4 exf4 28. Qd5 Rc5 29. Qf7+ Kxh6 30. Rxf4 $16) (26... Ra7 27. Bxf4 exf4 28. Qd5 Rc5 (28... Rd4 29. Qc6+ Rd6 30. Qe8+ Kxh6 31. Qxc8) 29. Qg8 Ba6 30. Qe6+ Kh7 31. Nf7 $16 {Taimanov} (31. Nxf5 $18 {[%cal Re6f7] PS}) 31... Bd4+ 32. Kh1 Bxf1 33. Ng5+ Kg7 34. Qf7+ Kh6 35. h4 Bg7 36. Ne6 Bxg2+ 37. Kxg2 $18 {PS}) 27. Bxf4 exf4 (27... Rxf4 28. Qc6+ Kh7 29. Nf7 $16) 28. Qd5 Ba6 29. Qe6+ $18) (21... Bd7 22. Rxf6 (22. Bxd5 $18 {Taimanov} Bxc6 $17 {PS}) 22... Qxf6 23. Bxd5 $18 {PS}) (21... Bb7 22. Rxf6 Qxf6 23. Rxf5 $18) {Garry Kasparov: '. Taimanov judged'} 22. Rxf6 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} (22. Qh5 Bb7 (22... Qd7 $15 {Balashov} 23. Bxd5 $1 Qxd5 (23... Rxc6 24. Qf7 Qxf7 25. Nxf7+ Kg8 (25... Kh7 26. Bxc6 $16) 26. Nd8+ Be6 27. Nxe6 Rxe6 28. Bxe6+ $18 {PS}) 24. Rxf6 Bxf6 25. Qxh6+ Kg8 26. Qxf6 $18 {PS}) 23. Bxd5 (23. Rxf6 Qxf6) 23... Qxd5 $19) (22. Qh4 {Spassky/Bondarevsky} Bb7 23. Ne6 (23. Re6 {[%cal Re6e5] Kotov} Qd7 (23... Rxe6 $4 24. Nf7+ $18 {Kotov}) (23... e4 24. Bc3 (24. Bxd5 Bxd5 25. Rxf6 Qxf6 26. Bxf4 Kg8 $19) 24... Nxc3 25. bxc3 Qf8 $13 {PS}) 24. Rxe5 {Kotov}) 23... Qd7 $19) (22. Qd3 hxg5 23. Rxf6 Bxf6 $17) (22. Qf3 $5 Bb7 (22... hxg5 23. Bxd5 (23. Rxf6 Qxf6 24. Bxd5 (24. Qxd5 $18 {PS}) 24... g4 $17 {Balashov}) 23... Rxc6 24. Bxc6 Rb8 25. Bc3 $44 {Balashov}) 23. Rxf6 (23. Ne6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Qd7 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 24. Bxd5 Rxe6 $1 $15) 23... Nxf6 24. Nf7+ Kh7 {Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 25. Qxb7 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (25. Bd3+ Kg8 26. Qxb7 Qd4+ $1 27. Rf2 $1 Ng4 28. Qxa8+ Kxf7 29. Qc6 ({after} 29. Qc8 Qxf2+ 30. Kh1 Qd4 31. Qxc7+ Kf6 32. Qc6+ Ke7 {White has only perpetual check}) 29... Qxd3 30. Qxc7+ Kg6 31. Qxb6+ Kf5 {, regaining the material and easily maintaining the balance.}) 25... Qxd2 (25... Qd4+) 26. Qxa8 Qd4+ 27. Kh1 Qxc4 $17) (22. Z0) 22... Bxh3 (22... Qxf6 23. Qd3 Qxg5 (23... Bf5 24. Qxd5 $18 {PS}) 24. Qxd5 $18) (22... Nxf6 23. Nf7+ {Siehe Hauptvariante}) 23. Nf7+ Kh7 24. Nxd8 {Garry Kasparov: 'to be in his favour, but'} Nxf6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'and after'} 24... Bxf6 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 25. Nc6 Be6 26. Re1 b5 27. Bb3 Ra6 28. Nxe5 Ne3 29. Bxe6 Bxe5 30. Bb3 c5 {Black even has winning chances}) (24... Rxd8 25. Rc6 Bd7 26. Bd3+ Kh8 27. Rc2) 25. Nc6 Bf5 (25... Ne4 26. Bd3 Bf5 27. Bc3 $1 Re8 ({or} 27... Nd6 {gives Black at least an equal game.})) 26. Nxe5 $16 {/+- Taimanov}) (20... Z0) 21. Bc3 Bd7 $1 (21... e4 22. Bc4 $1) (21... Qe7 22. Nf3 $1 {[%cal Rf3h4]}) (21... Ng4 22. Rg6 $1 {Taimanov} (22. Nf3 Qd7 23. Nxe5 Nxe5 24. Rxh6+ Kg8 $1 25. Qg3 Re8 26. Rd1 Qe7 27. Bxe5 Qxe5 28. Bc4+ Be6 29. Qxe5 Bxe5 30. Rxe6 Rxe6 31. Bxe6+ $11) 22... Qe8 23. Qh5 $40 {Balashov}) (21... Ng8 22. Rg6 {[%cal Rg6g7]} (22. Nf3 $1 $40 Qe7 23. Nh4) 22... Qe7 {[%cal Yf8f6]} (22... Qe8 23. Bh5 Qb5 24. Nf3 $13) 23. Nf3 Kh7 24. Ng5+ (24. Rxg7+ Qxg7 25. Nxe5 $13) 24... Kh8 (24... Kxg6 $4 25. Qh5+ Kf6 26. Ne4+ Ke6 27. Bc4+ Kd7 28. Rd1+ Kc6 29. Bd5+ Kb5 30. Qe2+ Ka4 31. Bb3#) 25. Nf3 $11 {Sergei Soloviev}) (21... Nh7 $2 22. Rxh6 Bxh6 23. Bxe5+ $18) (21... f4 22. Qh4 Qd7 (22... Nh7 $4 23. Rxh6 (23. Nf7+ Rxf7 24. Qxd8+ $18) 23... Bxh6 24. Bxe5+ $18) (22... Bb7 {, and again it is not apparent how White can gain an advantage:} 23. Ne6 ({or} 23. Rd1 Qe7 24. Re6 Qc5+ 25. Kh1 Rae8 $1) 23... Qd7 $1 24. Rxc7 (24. Nxg7 Qxg7 25. Bf3 Bxc6 26. Bxc6 {, and then} Ra5 $5 ({if Black does not like} 26... Rxa2 27. Bxe5 Qg5) 27. Bxa5 bxa5 {is safe}) 24... Qxe6 25. Rxb7 Rxa2 $1 26. Bf3 Nd5 27. Bxd5 Qxd5 28. Rxb6 Kh7 {with equality. Thus, 20 Qh3! would not have won, but merely intensified the situation still further. Taimanov had obviously lost his objectivity, which is why he was so upset about not being able to find the ephemeral win. The tension Taimanov created that day (together with his opponent) exceeded his chess capabilities... Hence also the weak-willed knight retreat, which was a heartening sign for Fischer, who had been anxiously awaiting White's move for over an hour.}) 23. Bf3 $13) 22. Ne6 Bxe6 23. Rxe6 Qd7 (23... Ng4 24. Rd1 (24. Bxg4 fxg4 25. Rxh6+ Kg8 (25... Bxh6 $4 26. Qxh6+ Kg8 27. Qg6+ Kh8 28. Rxf8+ Qxf8 29. Bxe5+ Qf6 30. Bxf6#) 26. Rxf8+ Kxf8 27. Rh8+ Bxh8 28. Qxh8+ Kf7 29. Qh5+ $11) 24... Qc8 25. Bc4 Rf6 26. Re7 Rd6 $13 {PS}) 24. Rxe5 Ng4 25. Rd1 Qf7 26. Red5 $16) 20... Bb7 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'There is no longer any need to guard the e6-square.'} 21. Rg6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'In the event of'} 21. Rxh6+ $6 Bxh6 22. Bxh6 {Garry Kasparov: 'the same knight leap'} {Garry Kasparov: 'but not'} Re8 $17 {Yudovich Garry Kasparov: '?'} (22... Nf4 $1 {) consolidates Black's advantage:} 23. Bxf4 exf4 24. Qc3+ Kh7 25. Ne5 (25. Nd4 Qd6) 25... Qg5 26. Bf3 (26. Qxc7+ Qg7 {is also insufficient}) 26... Be4 $1 27. Rd1 Rfe8 28. Qxc7+ Re7 29. Rd7 Rae8 30. Nc6 (30. Kf1 Qg7 $1) 30... Bxc6 31. Qxc6 Qh4 32. Qe6 Kh8 {and wins.}) 23. Ng5 $1 {and Qh3 with an attack}) 21... Nf4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'A typical Fischer decision: for the sake of activity Bobby is prepared to spoil his pawns, by exchanging his knight for the enemy bishop, which was very favourable according to his scale of piece values (I should remind you: knight = 3 pawns, bishop = 3.25). In addition, the situation on the board is clarified: the black bishops find very good work, whereas White's play suddenly comes to a standstill.'} ({By contrast, the 'natural'} 21... e4 $2 {would have allowed him a powerful attack:} 22. Bxh6 $1 exf3 $1 (22... Kh7 $6 23. Rxg7+ Kxh6 24. Ng5 $1 Kxg7 25. Ne6+ {and Nxd8 is bad for Black}) ({, as is} 22... Bxh6 $6 23. Rxh6+ Kg7 24. Nd4 $1 Nf4 25. Ne6+ ({or} 25. Qg3+ Kxh6 26. Rxf4)) 23. Bxg7+ Kh7 24. Bxf8 Kxg6 25. Qxf3 ({it is also possible to play} 25. Bxf3 Qxf8 26. Be4 $1 fxe4 ({or} 26... Qc5+ 27. Kh1 Kf6 28. Qh3 $1) 27. Qg3+ Kh7 28. Rxf8 Rxf8 29. Qh4+ Kg7 30. Qxe4 {with equality}) 25... Qg5 ({not} 25... Qxf8 $2 26. Qh5+) 26. Ba3 {, and Black saves himself with perpetual check:} Nf4 ({or} 26... Nc3) 27. Qxb7 Nxe2+ 28. Kf2 (28. Kh1 Ng3+ $1) 28... Qh4+ 29. Kxe2 Qc4+ 30. Kf2 Qd4+ 31. Kf3 Qd3+ 32. Kf2 Qd4+ {.}) 22. Bxf4 exf4 23. Rd1 Qe7 24. Re6 (24. Bc4 $142 {Vitolins}) 24... Qc5+ 25. Kf1 Rfd8 $17 (25... Rad8 $36 {[%cal Rb7d5] d5} 26. Rxd8 $6 {0-1 (41) Taimanov,M (2620)-Fischer,R (2740) Vancouver 1971}) 26. Rxd8+ Rxd8 27. Qa4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({The best practical chance was} 27. Bc4 $1 {, blocking the c-file,} Z0 ({, and parrying} 27... Be4 $6 {with the unexpected} 28. Ng5 $1 {.}) ({. In this case Fischer would have had to make the only, 'problem-like' move} 27... Bc6 $1 {, in order to drive the bishop from c4 by ...b6-b5 (while if} 28. Re1 {he now wins by} Be4 $1 {, since} 29. Ng5 $2 {fails to} Rd2 $1 {.} (29... Z0))) 27... Qc1+ (27... Z0 28. Re8+ $140 Rxe8 29. Qxe8+ Kh7 30. Qf7 $132 {Soloviev}) 28. Kf2 Bf8 {[ c5] Garry Kasparov: '!'} 29. b4 (29. Re8 Bc6) 29... Be4 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '"Black's attack develops of its own accord, his doubled pawns providing important outposts for the attacking pieces." (Taimanov)'} ({But the move in the game makes things harder for Black. He could have won quickly and without any problems by} 29... Bd5 $1 30. Re8 (30. Re5 c6 $1 {with the murderous threats of ...Bxf3 and ...Bg7, and if} 31. Qd1 {, then} Qxd1 32. Bxd1 Be4 {and ...Bxb4})) 30. Re8 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'A blunder on the threshold of more time-trouble – an indication that White was feeling completely rattled and doomed. "Alas, by this point I was unable to control my nerves..." (Taimanov)'} (30. Qb5 {''} {Garry Kasparov: ', or'} Qe3+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '(this move suggests itself!)'} (30... Z0) ({, hoping for} 30... Kh7 $2 31. Rxe4 $1 {with equality}) ({. Black would have had to find the far from obvious} 30... Qc3 $1 {and then also several more accurate moves –} 31. Qe5+ Qxe5 32. Nxe5 Kg7 33. Rg6+ ({or} 33. a3 Rd2 34. Rg6+ Kh7 35. Rf6 Bg7 36. Rf7 Kg8 $1 37. Re7 Bxe5 $1) 33... Kh7 34. Rf6 Bg7 ({in my opinion, this is simpler than} 34... Bxb4 35. Rf7+ Kg8 36. Rxc7 Rd2 37. a4 Bxg2 38. Nc4 $1 Rc2 $1 39. Kxg2 Rxe2+) 35. Rf7 Kg8 $1 36. Re7 $1 Bxe5 $1 (36... Rd2 37. Ke1 Rb2 38. Bc4+ Kh7 39. Rxc7 Rxb4 40. Nf3 {is not so clear}) 37. Rxe5 Kf7 38. Bc4+ Kf6 39. Re6+ Kg5 {, winning. Even so, in an important event a player can't afford to miss chances such as 30 Qb5!}) 31. Ke1 (31. Kf1 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Rd1+ $1 32. Bxd1 (32. Ne1 {Taimanov Garry Kasparov: '!'} Bxg2+ (32... Bd3 33. Rxe3 Bxb5 34. Rb3) (32... Rd2 $6 33. Re8 c6 34. Qe5+ Kh7 35. Re7+ $1) 33. Kxg2 Qxe6 34. Bxd1 Qxe1 $17 {PS} 35. Qxf5 Qxb4 36. Qf6+ Bg7 37. Qd8+ Qf8 38. Qxc7 {, and thanks to the opposite-coloured bishops White can hope for a draw.}) 32... Bd3+ 33. Be2 Qc1+ $19 {Tal}) 31... Qc3+ 32. Kf2 Bxb4 33. Re8+ Rxe8 34. Qxe8+ Kh7 $19 {Tal}) (30. Qb3 Kh7 (30... Bd5 31. Qd3) (30... c6) 31. Z0 Bd5 32. Qd3 Bxe6 33. Qxd8 Bxb4 $19 {Soloviev}) ({or} 30. a3 Kh7 31. Qb3 (31. h4 Qc3 $1) 31... Ra8 32. a4 c6 {would also not have helped.}) 30... Bc6 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'The rest is not of any interest.'} 31. Qxc6 (31. Rxd8 Bxa4 (31... Qe3+ 32. Kf1 Bxa4 33. Rxf8+ Kg7 34. Rxf5 Bd1 $1 $19) 32. Rxf8+ Kg7 $19 {Balashov/Taimanov}) 31... Qxc6 32. Rxd8 Qf6 (32... Kg7 33. a3 Bd6 {.}) 33. Rc8 Qe7 34. Kf1 Kh7 $19 (34... Qxb4 $2 35. Nh4 {Balashov} f3 $1 36. Nxf3 Qd6 $19 {PS}) 35. Nd4 (35. a4 Bg7 (35... Qxb4 $2 36. Rxc7+ {[%csl Rh7]}) 36. a5 bxa5 37. bxa5 Qc5 38. a6 Bc3 $1 39. g3 fxg3 40. hxg3 Ba5 41. Kg2 Bb6 $40 {-+ Balashov}) (35. Bd3 Qd7 $19) 35... Bg7 36. Nb5 (36. Nxf5 {'?'} Qd7 {Vitolins} (36... Qe6 $4 37. Bd3 $1 $18 {Vitolins})) 36... Be5 37. a3 Qd7 38. Ra8 f3 $1 39. gxf3 Bxh2 40. Kg2 Qg7+ $1 41. Kxh2 Qe5+ 42. Kg1 0-1

