{Andrew Soltis: 'In Esthetics of Chess, the Russian chess historian Isaac Linder asked several leading players which game or combination made "a particularly strong impression on you?". Anatoly Karpov said he couldn't name one example but cited these : Legal's mate, Morphy v. Duke of Brunswick, the "little combinations" of Capablanca, the rook endgame in the last game of the Alekhine-Capablanca match - "and the original plan of attack in the tenth game of the Spassky-Fischer match".'} 1. e4 e5 {Andrew Soltis: '"In reply to 1.e4 practically any opening can be used against Fischer with the exception of 1. ...e5," Petrosian wrote to the Soviet Chess Federation in early 1972 when the authorities were canvassing its grandmaster elite for advice.'} 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 ({Nagesh,Havanur: 'In Santa Monica 1966 Spassky played'} 7... O-O 8. c3 d5 {Fischer-Spassky/Santa Monica/1966/½/35/ Nagesh,Havanur: 'the Marshall Attack leading to draw. He had used the same system with Tal in the Candidates' Match 1965 drawing all his games.'}) 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 {Breyer Andrew Soltis: 'The Breyer Variation was virtually unknown until Spassky and other members of the Soviet team used it in the 1955 Student Olympiad in Lyons' Garry Kasparov: 'ChessBase: 'Breyer'' Nagesh,Havanur: 'The Breyer Variation, a favourite of Spassky.'} ({In the Havana Olympiad he had played} 9... h6 {(Smyslov Variation) and the game was a hard-fought draw. However, the variation had received some hard knocks in the games, Fischer-Gligoric, Rovinj/ Zagreb 1970 and Fischer-Ivkov, 1970 Interzonal.}) 10. d4 Nbd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'The Breyer Variation, I should remind you, was Spassky's favourite weapon.'} 11. Nbd2 {Garry Kasparov: 'The main continuation and another match surprise by Fischer:'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'before this he had played only' Nagesh,Havanur: 'Fischer decided not to play his old line'} 11. Nh4 {Simagin Nagesh,Havanur: 'on account of'} exd4 (11... Nxe4 12. Nf5 Ndf6 13. Qf3 Bxf5 $44 (13... Bb7 $44)) 12. cxd4 Nb6 13. Nd2 c5 {Nagesh,Havanur: '=' '=' '=' Nagesh,Havanur: 'that led to an easy draw in Robert Byrne-Boris Spassky, Moscow 1971.'} 14. Bc2 cxd4 15. Nhf3 Re8 16. Nxd4 Bf8 17. b3 Bb7 $10 {Byrne-Spassky/Moskau/1971/ Andrew Soltis: '='}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'or'} 11. c4 {Garry Kasparov: '(Volume 2, Game No.96).'} c6 12. c5 Qc7 13. cxd6 Bxd6 14. Bg5 exd4 15. Bxf6 gxf6 16. Qxd4 Ne5 17. Nbd2 Rd8 18. Qe3 Nd3 $1 19. Qh6 Bf4 20. Qxf6 Rd6 {Fischer-Portisch/Santa Monica/1966/½/71/}) 11... Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. b4 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'This plan preventing the freeing advance...c5 was popular in those days.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Twenty years later Bobby was to prefer' Nagesh,Havanur: 'In his own games with White Spassky had preferred'} 13. Nf1 {Nagesh,Havanur: 'and currently it is in vogue.'} Bf8 14. Ng3 g6 15. Bg5 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} ({again avoiding both the main variation with} 15. a4) ({, and the currently popular} 15. b3 {– Volume 3, Game No.82}) 15... h6 16. Bd2 {Garry Kasparov: '(Game No.107).'} Bg7 $11) 13... Bf8 (13... a5 {Nagesh,Havanur: '!?' '!?'} 14. Nb3 axb4 15. cxb4 Rb8 16. Na5 c6 (16... Ba8 17. d5) 17. Nxb7 Rxb7 18. Bb3 $16 {Suetin-Tringov/Titove Uzice/1966/}) (13... exd4 $5) (13... d5 $5) 14. a4 (14. Bb2 { a3} Nb6 $1) 14... Nb6 {Garry Kasparov: 'A sensible idea: at the cost of two tempi Black removes the pawn tension on the queenside.'} ({Andrew Soltis: 'By 1972 a substantial amount of book was built around the Breyer, and this was the latest finesse. Black had gotten into trouble previously with'} 14... a5 15. bxa5 (15. axb5 axb4 16. Bb2 bxc3 17. Bxc3 c6 $10 {Kavalek-Portisch/Wijk aan Zee/1969/ Andrew Soltis: '='}) 15... Rxa5 16. Rb1 $16 Ba6 (16... Qa8 17. axb5 exd4 18. cxd4 Nxe4 19. Nxe4 Bxe4 20. Rxe4 $1 Rxe4 21. Ng5 Rh4 22. g3 $1 $16 (22. Nxf7 $6 Kxf7 23. Bg5 Re4 24. Qf3+ Nf6 25. Bxf6 Re1+ $10 {Geller-Portisch/1973/ Andrew Soltis: '='}) 22... Rh6 23. Nxf7 Rxh3 24. d5 $1 {(Geller), but} g6 $1 (24... Nf6 $2 25. Ng5 $1 R