{Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'The only published games Fischer played in the next 20 years were against an M.I.T. computer in 1977, all won by him. An article in 64, titled "a Match of Recluses", noted the appropriateness of the matchup of the two - the computer's programmer wouldn't let it play against other computers and Fischer refused to play against other humans. There were innumerable efforts to get Fischer to play serious chess. Viktor Korchnoi considered a match with him in 1980, under Fischer's 10-wins, draws-not-counting, with Fischer guaranteed $3 million. After the inevitable collapse, Emanuel Sztein, asked him what the likely outcome would have been. "I would lose approximately by the score of 10-3 or 10-4." Then why would you play such a match, he as asked. "In the match I would draw at least 25 games and who else could make so many draws with Fischer!" In fact, Fischer didn't play again until September 2, 1992, 20 years and a day after the first match ended. Fischer insisted the event be called a world championship rematch.''} 1. e4 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Chandler,M: 'Matanovic Anand''} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'This was considered a good-will gesture on Spassky's part, not testing Fischer on the 20 years of Sicilian theory he had missed.''} 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. Nf1 ({Andrew Soltis: 'Varying from'} 13. b4 {Fischer,R-Spassky,B/Reykjavik m-10/1972/ Andrew Soltis: 'as in Game 97.'}) 13... Bf8 14. Ng3 g6 15. Bg5 {Chandler,Murray G: '!?'} h6 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Black gets to use the vacant h7 square but his mix of ...h5 and ...f5 is a bad fit.''} (15... Bg7 16. Qc1 c5 17. d5 Nb6 18. Bh6 Nfd7 19. b3 a5 20. a4 bxa4 21. bxa4 Ba6 {Balashov,Y-Spassky,B/Tallin/1973/}) 16. Bd2 Bg7 ({Book at the time was} 16... c5 {which was supposed to achieve equality after} 17. dxe5 $2 ({But Fischer would likely have replied} 17. d5 {with the usual slight plus.}) 17... Nxe5 $1) ({Later in the match Spassky tried} 16... exd4 17. cxd4 c5 {but Fischer got a small edge with} 18. d5 Nb6 19. Ba5 $1 Nfd7 20. b3) 17. a4 {Chandler,Murray G: '!?'} (17. Qc1 {Ü 10/362 Chandler,Murray G: 'Matanovic'}) (17. Rc1 {Spassky,B-Unzicker,W/Santa Monika/1966/ Spassky,B-Unzicker,W/Santa Monika/1966/ Spassky,B-Unzicker,W/Santa Monika/1966/ Chandler,M: 'Spassky,B-Unzicker,W/Santa Monica/1966/'}) 17... c5 (17... Nb6 18. axb5 axb5 19. b3 Nfd7 20. Bd3 b4 21. Rxa8 Bxa8 22. dxe5 Nxe5 23. Nxe5 dxe5 24. Qc2 bxc3 25. Bxc3 {Karpov,An-Kortchnoi,V (1)/Lenigrad m/1971/}) (17... exd4 18. cxd4 c5 19. d5 Nb6 20. Qc1 Kh7 21. Ba5 Nfd7 $10 {Kuczynski,R-Kveinis,A/Warszawa op/1989/}) 18. d5 c4 19. b4 $1 $146 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!''} Nh7 {Chandler,Murray G: '?!' Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '?''} (19... cxb3 20. Bxb3 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Black's best may be''} Nc5 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'after which''} (20... Rc8 21. axb5 axb5 22. Qe2 {Andrew Soltis: 'and then'} Qb6 23. Reb1 Ra8 24. Ba4 {Andrew Soltis: '!'} Ba6 25. c4 $16 {Andrew Soltis: '!'}) 21. c4 $1 (21. Bc2 Nfd7) 21... bxc4 (21... bxa4 22. Bxa4 Re7 (22... Nxa4 23. Rxa4 Nd7 {and ...Qc7, ... Reb8}) 23. Bb4 {Andrew Soltis: 'favors White'} Rc8 (23... Qc7 24. Bxc5 Qxc5 25. Nd2 h5 26. Nb3 Qxc4 27. Na5 Qc8 28. Nxb7 Rxb7 29. Bc6 $16) 24. Qe2 $14) 22. Bxc4 {Chandler,Murray G: '' Chandler,Murray G: 'Chandler,M: 'Anand''} Rf8 23. Rb1 Nfd7 24. Qc2 $36 {}) ({This posts a difficult choice : Should Black abandon queenside play since} 19... a5 20. axb5 axb4 21. cxb4 {fails and devote his energy to the other wing? Or should he try}) 20. Be3 h5 21. Qd2 Rf8 (21... Ndf6 $5 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Anand Matanovic'}) 22. Ra3 $16 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!''} Ndf6 ({Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'A King's Indian player might prefer''} 22... h4 $6 23. Nf1 f5 24. exf5 (24. Bg5 Bf6 (24... Nxg5 25. Qxg5 Qxg5 26. Nxg5 $16) 25. Bh6 Bg7 26. Bxg7 Kxg7 27. exf5 gxf5 28. Ne3 f4 (28... Kg6 29. Nxf5 Rxf5 30. Bxf5+ Kxf5 31. Qc2+ Kf6 32. Qxh7 $18) 29. Nf5+ $16) 24... gxf5 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'for example''} 25. Ng5 Nxg5 26. Bxg5 Bf6 27. Bh6 Bg7 28. Bxg7 Kxg7 29. axb5 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Anand'} (29. f4 $16 e4 30. Ne3 Qf6 {followed by ...Kf7 and ... Rg8}) 29... axb5 30. Rxa8 Bxa8 31. Ra1 $16) 23. Rea1 Qd7 (23... h4 $2 24. Nxh4 Nxe4 25. Nxe4 Qxh4 26. Nxd6 $16) 24. R1a2 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!''} Rfc8 25. Qc1 Bf8 26. Qa1 Qe8 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '"This position, of course, had been evaluated by Spassky when he played his 19th move, " Balashov wrote in Shakhmatny Vestnik. "It appears completely defensible and solid."''} 27. Nf1 {Chandler,Murray G: '!'} ({Brilliant partly because of the idea (to attack b5 with a knight ona 3 after liquidating the a-file) and partly because th alternative route} 27. Nd2 {allows Black more counterplay with} h4 28. Ne2 (28. Ngf1 Nh5 29. Nb1 bxa4 $1 30. Rxa4 Nf4) 28... Kg7 29. Nb1 Nxe4 $1) 27... Be7 28. N1d2 ({Andrew Soltis: 'A bit safer is'} 28. N3d2 $5 {Andrew Soltis: '!' Andrew Soltis: 'and 29.f3 which rules out Nxe4'} Kg7 29. f3 $16) 28... Kg7 ({Duncan Suttles, making a rare return to chess, recommended} 28... Kh8 {but White's edge would be clear after} 29. Nb1 Ng8 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rxa8 Bxa8 32. Ra7 {. For example} f5 33. exf5 g5 34. Na3 Bxd5 35. Qd1 Bc6 36. f6 $1) 29. Nb1 {[ 30.ab5 ab5 31.a8 a8 32.a8 a8 33.a8 a8 34.a3] Chandler,Murray G: '!'} Nxe4 {Chandler,Murray G: '!' Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Perfectly timed, since White left no heavy pieces near the kingside. Black would have been lost soon if he had allowed 30.axb5 axb5 31.Rxa8 Rxa8 32. Rxa8 Qxa8 33.Qxa8 Bxa8 34.Na3''} (29... Nd7 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rxa8 Rxa8 32. Rxa8 Qxa8 33. Qxa8 Bxa8 34. Na3 $18) 30. Bxe4 f5 (30... Nf6 $5 $13 31. Nbd2 Nxe4 32. Nxe4 Bxd5 {favors White solidly after} 33. Ned2 ({or even} 33. Nfd2 f5 34. axb5 fxe4 35. bxa6)) 31. Bc2 Bxd5 32. axb5 axb5 33. Ra7 {Chandler,Murray G: '!'} Kf6 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!''} 34. Nbd2 Rxa7 (34... f4 35. Ne4+ Bxe4 36. Bxe4 Rxa7 37. Bxa7 $18) 35. Rxa7 Ra8 36. g4 $1 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!!''} (36. h4 $5) ({Thanks to Black's centralized king, the endings after} 36. Rxa8 Qxa8 37. Qxa8 Bxa8 38. Kf1 Ke6 (38... g5 39. g4) 39. g3 d5 {are not at all easy. WHite's winning task seems suddenly difficult. Yet he took only four minutes for his reply. "When I saw his next move I knew that he was good," Yasser Seirawan wrote in Inside Chess, "Bobby was back!"}) 36... hxg4 ({If Black concedes control of e4 the game is over. e.g.} 36... f4 37. Bb6) 37. hxg4 Rxa7 $2 (37... f4 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Anand'} 38. Rxa8 (38. Be4 fxe3 39. Bxd5 Rxa7 40. Ne4+ Kg7 41. Qxa7 {and wins}) (38. Bb6) 38... Qxa8 39. Qxa8 Bxa8 40. Bb6 $16) (37... fxg4 38. Nh2) 38. Qxa7 f4 ({Andrew Soltis: 'again'} 38... fxg4 39. Nh2 {Andrew Soltis: 'followed by Ne4+ and Nxd6! is bad. But there is also'} Qc8 (39... Be6 40. Ne4+ Kg7 41. Nxd6 $18) 40. Ne4+ Ke6 41. Nxd6 $1 Bxd6 (41... Kxd6 42. Bc5+) 42. Qxh7 $18) (38... Bc6 39. Nh4 Bd7 40. Bh6 $18 Bf8 {holds out some hope.}) (38... Qc6 39. Ng5 $1 Nxg5 40. Bxg5+ Kxg5 41. Qxe7+ {and wins}) (38... Qa8 39. Qxa8 Bxa8 40. gxf5 gxf5 41. Nh4 e4 {, which Evans claimed was still a fight. But it shouldn't last long after White puts his knights on g3 and f4 and a bishop on d4 , and}) (38... Be6 {and ...Qd7, which was unclear according to Balashov and Nikitin} 39. Qb7 Qd7 40. Qa8 Qc8) 39. Bxf4 $1 (39. Bb6 Qc8 $1 $16 {Andrew Soltis: 'with chances'}) 39... exf4 ({After} 39... Bxf3 {White's best appears to be} 40. Bxe5+ dxe5 41. Nxf3 {, for example} Ng5 42. Nxg5 Kxg5 43. Qe3+ Kf6 44. Qb6+ $1 {and Qe6}) 40. Nh4 $3 {Chandler,Murray G: '!'} Bf7 (40... Nf8 41. Qd4+ Ke6 42. Nxg6 Nxg6 43. Bf5+ $18 Kf7 44. Bxg6+ (44. Qxd5+ Kg7 45. Qe4) 44... Kxg6 45. Qxd5 $18) 41. Qd4+ $18 Ke6 ({Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Mates arise after''} 41... Kg5 42. Qg7 {Chandler,Murray G: ''} Qg8 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Anand'} (42... Kxh4 43. Qh6+ Kxg4 44. f3+ Kg3 45. Qh2#) (42... Nf6 43. Ndf3+ Kxg4 44. Bf5+ Kh5 45. Ng2 $1) 43. Nhf3+ Kxg4 44. Qh6 { h2#}) 42. Nf5 $1 {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '!''} Bf8 (42... Kd7 43. Qa7+) (42... Qc6 43. Qxf4 Kd7 44. Nxe7 Kxe7 45. Qe3+ $1 {and Qh6}) 43. Qxf4 Kd7 (43... gxf5 44. Qxf5+ Ke7 45. Qxh7 $18) 44. Nd4 Qe1+ {Chandler,Murray G: 'Andrew Soltis: '?''} ({This ends matters quickly but Black would be out of ammunition after} 44... Qe7 45. Nxb5 Ng5 46. Kf1) 45. Kg2 Bd5+ 46. Be4 Bxe4+ 47. Nxe4 Be7 48. Nxb5 Nf8 (48... d5 49. Qc7+ Ke6 50. Qc6+ Ke5 51. Ng3 Nf8 52. Nd4 $18) 49. Nbxd6 Ne6 50. Qe5 1-0

