{Petrosian, T - Fischer, R Bled, 1959.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 d5 5. a3 Bd6 $6 {A dubious alternative to the standard 5...Be7; and 5...Bxe3+.} 6. Nf3 O-O 7. c5 Be7 8. b4 Ne4 $6 (8... b6 9. Bb2 a5 {was a better way of aiming for counter-play.}) 9. Bb2 Nd7 10. Bd3 f5 11. Ne2 $1 {Preventing ...Nxc3 which Black should have played on the previous move.} Bf6 12. O-O Qe7 {[%cal Ye6e5] Trying to obtain some air by ...e5.} 13. Ne5 $1 {Petrosian has other ideas!} Nxe5 14. dxe5 Bg5 15. Bd4 $1 {[%csl Re4][%cal Rf2f3] Threatening to trap the Knight by 16.f3.} Bh6 16. f3 Ng5 {White has achieved complete domination in a remarkably short space of time.} 17. c6 $1 b6 $6 {Now Black's Bishop is completely immured.} (17... bxc6 {lost the exchange to} 18. Bc5 {[%cal Rc5f8]}) ({Comparatively best was the tortuous} 17... Rd8 {though White would still obtain excellent play by opening the c-file.}) 18. b5 a6 19. a4 axb5 {Black decides to exchange his Queen's Rook which would otherwise have been unable to enter the game. But the opening of the a-file only helps White. It must, however, be admitted that the alternatives were not attractive for Black.} 20. axb5 Rxa1 21. Qxa1 Nf7 22. Qc3 Qh4 23. Ra1 Ng5 24. Qe1 Qh5 25. Ra7 Qg6 ({Not} 25... Rf7 26. Nf4) ({or} 25... Qf7 26. h4) 26. Kh1 (26. Rxc7 $2 Nxf3+) 26... Qh5 27. f4 {White easily parries Black's only threat of the game, 27...Nxf3.} Ne4 28. Rxc7 {Tme to reap the harvest!} g5 29. Bxb6 Kh8 30. Bxe4 fxe4 31. Bc5 Rg8 {and Black resigned without waiting for White to play the crushing 32.Be7!} 1-0

