{Fischer played no more serious games in 1961. He passed up the U.S. Championship, the only time he failed to accept his invitation between 1957 and 1967, and didn't return to chess until his second Interzonal, at Stockholm. Again he started slowly, and it was only in the fourth round, against the new grandmaster Lajos Portisch, that he began to impress, when he played one of the most instructive rook-and-pawn endgames of the 20th Century.} 1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Bc4 Ngf6 6. Neg5 Nd5 ({Unlike the comparable position here with 3.d4, here} 6... e6 7. Qe2 Nb6 $2 {is refuted by} 8. Ne5 $1) 7. d4 h6 8. Ne4 N7b6 9. Bb3 Bf5 10. Ng3 Bh7 11. O-O e6 12. Ne5 Nd7 ({Black must try to exchange off at least one pair of minor pieces and avoid lines such as} 12... Be7 13. Nh5 O-O $2 14. Qg4 $1) 13. c4 N5f6 14. Bf4 Nxe5 ({He is more constricted after} 14... Be7 15. Qe2 O-O 16. Rad1 Re8 17. Rfe1) 15. Bxe5 Bd6 16. Qe2 O-O 17. Rad1 Qe7 ({White's bishop is his best piece but trading it off} 17... Bxe5 $2 18. dxe5 Nd7 19. Rd6 $1 {leaves Black in a bind}) 18. Bxd6 Qxd6 19. f4 {White enjoys a textbook edge in space - four occupied ranks versus three, with a no-man's land in between. The logical next step is to improve the scope of his rooks, such as with f4-f5.} c5 $2 ({Kmoch showed his Viennese-school caution when he recommended a waiting policy of} 19... Rfe8 $1 20. f5 $2 (20. Qf3 Rad8 21. f5 e5 $1) 20... Bxf5) 20. Qe5 Qxe5 21. dxe5 ({White can try to dominate the d-file with} 21. fxe5 Ng4 22. Rf4 Ne3 23. Rd2 cxd4 ({But Black complicated matters with} 23... Rad8 $1 24. Kf2 $2 (24. d5 b5 $5) 24... g5 $1) 24. Rfxd4) 21... Ne4 22. Rd7 $1 Nxg3 23. hxg3 Be4 {Black appears to be holding the balance 24.Rfd1 Bc6 25.R7d6 Rfc8! and ...Kf8 followed by ...Rc7} 24. Ba4 $1 {Taking away Bc6 while threatening 25.Re1} Rad8 25. Rfd1 Rxd7 26. Rxd7 g5 $1 27. Bd1 $1 {A new threat Bf3} Bc6 28. Rd6 Rc8 29. Kf2 Kf8 30. Bf3 Bxf3 31. gxf3 ({Afte} 31. Kxf3 Ke7 {, White has few prospects, since the king raid} 32. fxg5 ({He also achieves nothing from} 32. Ke3 f5) (32. f5 exf5 33. Rxh6 {as in the game, is at least equal for Black after} Rd8) 32... hxg5 33. Kg4 {allows} f5+ $1 34. Kxg5 Rg8+ 35. Kf4 Rg4+) 31... gxf4 $2 ({The trade opens a window of opportunity at move 33, compared with} 31... Ke7 32. f5 exf5 33. Rxh6 Rc6 $1) 32. gxf4 Ke7 33. f5 $1 {"When a young player if good at attacking or combinations, this is understandable" Alexander Kotov wrote in Shakhmaty v SSSR. "But a faultless endgame technique at the age of 19 is something rare. I can only recall one other chessplayer who at that age was equally skillful at endgames - Vasily Smyslov." Fischer's play here has a Smyslov-like quality. At each point when it appeared Black had equalized (at move 23, 26, or 32) White finds a new resource, such as the therat of 34.f6+ Ke8 35.Rd1+ and Rh1.} exf5 34. Rxh6 Rd8 ({Now the king and pawn endgame} 34... Rc6 35. Rxc6 bxc6 {will lose because the White king invades} 36. Kg3 Kd7 ({The instructive main line is} 36... f6 37. Kf4 Ke6 38. exf6 Kxf6 39. a3 a6 40. a4 a5 41. b3 $1 {the first mini zugzwang} Ke6 42. Kg5 Ke5 43. Kg6 Kf4 44. Kf6 Kxf3 45. Kxf5 Ke3 46. Ke6 Kd3 47. Kd7 $1 Kc3 (47... Kd4 48. Kd6 {explains Black's last move}) 48. Kxc6 Kxb3 49. Kb5 $1) 37. Kh4 $1) 35. Ke2 $2 Rg8 36. Kf2 Rd8 $2 ({Black could have taken advantage of White's inaccuracy with} 36... f4 $1 {threatening 37. ...Rg5. Then} 37. e6 {is a winning try} fxe6 ({But} 37... Rd8 {is safe} 38. Ke2 $2 Rd6 $1) 38. Rh7+) 37. Ke3 $1 Rd1 ({White's pawns are faster after} 37... f4+ 38. Kxf4 Rd4+ 39. Kf5 Rxc4 40. Rh7 {threatening e6}) 38. b3 Re1+ ({Black gains a tempo on the game after} 38... Ra1 39. Kf4 $2 ({But White does better with} 39. Rh2 Ke6 ({Or} 39... Rd1 40. Kf4 Rd3 41. Rh8) 40. Kf4 f6 41. Rh6 $1) 39... Rxa2) 39. Kf4 Re2 40. Kxf5 Rxa2 41. f4 Re2 42. Rh3 Re1 $1 {The rook is best placed on the e-file as we shall see} 43. Rd3 $3 ({The rook invasion} 43. Rh8 {fails to} Re3 {and then} 44. Rb8 (44. Rc8 Kd7) 44... Rxb3 {. Therefore, White tries to provoke ...b6 with his threat of Rc5. For example 43. ...Re2 44.Rd5 b6 and 45.Rd3! Re1 46.Rh3. This is the same position that occurred in the diagram but with the critical addition of .. b6 and White wins after 46. ...Re2 47.Rh8 Re3 48.Rb8! Rxb3 49.Rb7+ and Kg6}) 43... Rb1 {Black concedes the e-file. But now what? On 44.Ke4 Re1+ 45.Kd5 Rf1 he holds} 44. Re3 $1 {A new plan - 45.e6 will create a passed f-pawn.} Rb2 (44... a5 45. e6 fxe6+ 46. Rxe6+ Kd7 47. Re3 a4 {White wins with} 48. bxa4 Rb4 49. Re4 Rxa4 50. Ke5) ({but} 44... Rc1 {is a tougher nut to rack.}) ({He can also make more progress than Black after} 44... a6 45. e6 b5 46. exf7+ Kxf7 47. Ke5 bxc4 48. bxc4 Rd1 49. Rh3) 45. e6 a6 46. exf7+ Kxf7 47. Ke5 $1 Rd2 {Black cannot permit Kd5} 48. Rc3 $1 ({This is not as mysterious as it may seem : Black is denied counterplay from ...b5 compared with} 48. f5 b5 49. cxb5 axb5 50. Rc3 c4 $1 {which draws}) 48... b6 49. f5 Rd1 ({Active defense fails} 49... Re2+ 50. Kd5 Rf2 51. Kc6 Rxf5 52. Kxb6 Ke6 53. Kxa6 Ke5 54. Kb5 Kd4 55. Rc1 {and wins. For example} Rf3 (55... Rh5 56. Rd1+ Kc3 57. Rd5) 56. Rd1+ Kc3 57. Kxc5 Kxb3 58. Kb5) 50. Rh3 b5 51. Rh7+ Kg8 52. Rb7 bxc4 (52... b4 53. Ke6 $1 {as in the game.}) 53. bxc4 Rd4 54. Ke6 $1 (54. Ke6 {This is the first of three points in which Black's chances are crippled because of the bad placement of his rook :} Rxc4 55. Rb8+ Kh7 56. f6 {and White wins because of} Re4+ 57. Kf5 $1 {threatens both the unfortunate rook and 58.f7}) 54... Re4+ 55. Kd5 $1 ({White must force a trade of the f-pawn for the c-pawn. He has nothing after} 55. Kf6 Rxc4 56. Rb8+ Kh7 57. Kf7 Rb4) 55... Rf4 56. Kxc5 Rxf5+ 57. Kd6 $1 Rf6+ 58. Ke5 Rf7 ({Black's rook lacks the "checking disatnce". If the attacked rook goes to f1, White wins with the Lucena-like} 58... Rf1 59. c5 Rc1 60. Kd6 Rd1+ 61. Kc7 a5 62. c6 a4 63. Kb8 Rd8+ 64. Ka7) 59. Rb6 $1 Rc7 60. Kd5 Kf7 ({The a-pawn couldn't have been saved anyway} 60... Rd7+ 61. Kc6 Ra7 62. Rb8+ Kf7 63. c5 {and 64.Kb6 wins}) 61. Rxa6 Ke7 {For the third time Black is victimized by bad rook palcement because WHite has} 62. Re6+ $1 Kd8 63. Rd6+ Ke7 ({Since Black loses the pawn endgame} 63... Kc8 64. Rc6 {he must allow his king to be cut off from the queening square.}) 64. c5 Rc8 65. c6 Rc7 66. Rh6 Kd8 67. Rh8+ Ke7 68. Ra8 {White wins with Kd6 after Black's king moves.} 1-0

