{Fischer's opening range was deepening by 1962. He could answer 1.e4 with 1. . ..e5, or 1.d4 with 1. ...d5, for example, as he did in Curacao. And while he still relied on relatively few openings, they ranged from the cutting-edge Najdorf Sicilian to long-discarded ideas of Wilhelm Steinitz. Despite its success in this game, 5.Qe2 inexpicably failed to catch on.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. d4 ({An oddity of Fischer's repetoire is that he repeatedly avoided the main Petroff} 3. Nxe5 {lines. IN fact, against Bisguier in the 1959-60 U.S. Championship, he played the notorious drawing line} d6 4. Nf3 Nxe4 5. Qe2 {and only won when Black tried too hard to win.}) 3... exd4 4. e5 Ne4 5. Qe2 $5 {This makes an ugly impression - but if it succeeds it could be the refutation of 3. ...exd4.} Nc5 ({Black's knight is attacked and} 5... d5 {is met by} 6. exd6 {. This game was the first serious attempt to resuscitate 5.Qe2 since Steinitz-Pillsbury St. Petersburg 1896 which went}) (5... Bb4+ 6. Kd1 $5 (6. Nbd2 Nxd2 7. Bxd2 Bxd2+ (7... Qe7 8. O-O-O (8. Nxd4 $2 Bxd2+ $17) 8... Nc6 9. Bg5 Qe6 10. Kb1 Bc5 11. Qb5 b6 12. Bc4 $13 a6 (12... Qf5 13. e6 $5 fxe6 14. Bxe6) 13. Qb3 Qg6 14. h4 Na5 15. h5 Qf5 {Grechihin - Nikitin, USSR 1964 ECO}) 8. Qxd2 d6 $1 (8... d5 $1 9. Qxd4 O-O 10. O-O-O Be6 { Horton} 11. Be2 { Forintos, Haag} Nc6 12. Qf4 f6 $132) (8... Nc6 9. O-O-O O-O 10. Qf4 Qe7 11. Bb5 $16 {Forintos, Haag}) (8... c5 9. c3 dxc3 10. Qxc3 Qe7 11. Bc4 $16 {Serebritsky - Prodanovich, corr 1975}) 9. Qxd4 Nc6 10. Bb5 Bd7 11. Bxc6 Bxc6 12. O-O-O $16 {Boleslavsky ECO} Bxf3 13. gxf3 O-O 14. Rhg1 g6 15. exd6 cxd6 16. h4 Qa5 17. Kb1 Qc5 18. Qd2 Rac8 19. Rg5 Qc6 20. Rd5 Rfd8 21. h5 Qc4 22. f4 Rc5 23. Rd4 Qe6 24. Re1 Qf5 25. h6 Rc6 26. Rde4 Kf8 27. Qd4 f6 28. R1e2 d5 29. Qb4+ {1-0 Bender,H-Junge,W/DDR corr 1982/CR97-82 (29)}) (6. c3 $2 dxc3 7. bxc3 Nxc3 8. Qc4 Nd5+ 9. Kd1 c6 $17 {Sozin}) 6... d5 7. exd6 f5 8. Ng5 (8. dxc7 Qxc7 9. Nxd4 Nc6 10. c3 (10. Nxc6 bxc6 11. c3 (11. f3 $2 Ba6 12. Qxa6 Nf2+ 13. Ke2 Qe5+ (13... Nxh1 $17) 14. Be3 f4 {-+ ECO} 15. Qxc6+ Ke7 16. Qb7+ Kf6 17. Qc6+ $10 Kg5 $140 18. h4+ Kh5 19. g4+) (11. Be3 a5 12. c3 Ba6 13. Qe1 O-O-O+ 14. Kc2 Bxf1 15. Qxf1 Bc5 $40 16. Bxc5 Nxc5 17. Nd2 $2 Rxd2+ 18. Kxd2 Qf4+ 19. Kc2 Qe4+ $19 {Norway - Scott, 1935}) 11... Be7 $44 {Forintos, Haag} (11... Bc5 $2 12. f3 Ba6 13. Qxa6 Nf2+ 14. Kc2 Nxh1 15. Bf4 $16 {Gleeson - Rowley, corr 1971})) 10... Nxd4 11. cxd4 Bd7 $5 (11... Qd6 12. f3 Qxd4+ 13. Nd2 Bxd2 14. Bxd2 Qxd2+ 15. Qxd2 Nxd2 16. Kxd2 $10 {Steinitz - Pilsbury, St.Petersburg m(3) 1895}) 12. Be3 (12. f3 Rc8 $40 (12... O-O-O $40 {a4})) 12... Rc8 13. f3 O-O 14. Qc4+ Qxc4 15. Bxc4+ Rxc4 $15 {Morgan ECO}) 8... O-O 9. Qc4+ (9. dxc7 Qxc7 10. Qc4+ Qxc4 11. Bxc4+ Kh8 12. Rf1 h6 $15 {Bilguer}) 9... Kh8 10. Qxb4 {. Black ruined his initiative and lost after} (10. Nxe4 fxe4 11. dxc7 (11. Qxb4 $2 Bg4+ $1 (11... Nc6 12. Qe1 (12. dxc7 $2 Bg4+ 13. Be2 Bxe2+ 14. Kxe2 Rxf2+ 15. Ke1 Qh4 16. g3 Rxh2 $1 $19 {Mayer - Steiner, Temesvar 1896}) 12... Bg4+ 13. Be2 Bf5 $40 {Forintos, Haag}) 12. Be2 Bxe2+ 13. Kxe2 Nc6 14. Qe1 Qxd6 15. Kd1 Rae8 16. b3 e3 17. Ba3 Qf4 18. Bxf8 e2+ $17 {Lipschutz - Showalter, m 1896}) 11... Qf6 $2 (11... Bg4+ 12. Be2 (12. f3 $2 exf3 13. cxd8=Q fxg2+ 14. Qe2 Bxe2+ 15. Kxe2 gxh1=Q $19 {Forintos, Haag}) 12... Bxe2+ 13. Kxe2 Qh4 14. c8=Q (14. cxb8=Q $2 Qg4+ {Steinitz}) 14... Nc6 $5 (14... Qxf2+ 15. Kd1 {Minev, Kajkomdzozov  ECO}) 15. Qh3 (15. Qxa8 Qg4+ $19) (15. g3 Qh5+) 15... Qxf2+ 16. Kd1 d3 17. cxd3 Nd4 $40) (11... Qe7 $13 {Porreca ECO}) 12. cxb8=Q Qxf2 13. Qg3 (13. Be2 $2 Rxb8 14. Qxb4 d3 15. cxd3 exd3 16. Bxd3 Rd8 $17 {Kharus-Gurevich,D/URS/1975/}) 13... Bg4+ 14. Be2 Qf1+ 15. Qe1 $18 {Cvetkovic ECO}) (10. dxc7 $2 Nxf2+ 11. Ke2 Qe7+ 12. Kxf2 Qe1+ $19 {Steinitz}) (10. Nf7+ $2 Rxf7 11. Qxf7 Nxf2+ (11... Nxd6 $17) 12. Ke2 Nxh1 13. dxc7 Qd7 $1 14. cxb8=Q Qxf7 15. Qf4 Bd7 $19 {Steinitz}) 10... Nc6 11. Qa4 (11. Qa3 Nxf2+ 12. Ke1 Nxh1 13. dxc7 Qe8+ 14. Be2 f4 15. Kf1 Bd7 16. Nd2 Ne5 17. Ndf3 Ng4 18. Bd3 {Steinitz - Pilsbury, St.Petersburg m(1) 1895} h6 $17 {ECO}) (11. dxc7 $2 Nxf2+ $19) 11... Nxf2+ 12. Ke1 Nxh1 13. dxc7 {, and the verdict that remained on the opening books was that only a Steinitz could make 5.Qe2 work. Fischer never revealed what improvement he had in mind but 5. ...Bb4+ 6.Nbd2 ensures at least a small edge.}) 6. Nxd4 Nc6 (6... Be7 7. Nc3 O-O (7... Ne6 8. Nxe6 dxe6 9. Qg4 g6 10. Bh6 $16 {Yus}) 8. Bf4 d5 9. O-O-O $16 {Steinitz}) 7. Nxc6 bxc6 ({After} 7... dxc6 8. Nc3 {Black must anticipate problems on the d-file after 9.Be3 and 10.Rd1. But} Be7 (8... Bf5 9. Be3 h5 10. Rd1 Qe7 11. Rd2 Nd7 12. f4 O-O-O 13. Nd1 f6 14. exf6 Qxf6 15. Bxa7 Bb4 16. c3 Bc5 17. Bxc5 Nxc5 18. Rxd8+ Qxd8 19. Qd2 Re8+ 20. Be2 Nd3+ 21. Kf1 Qd6 22. Bxh5 Qxf4+ {0-1 De Vrieze-Groenendal/NLD-ch H corr 1989/CR97-89 (22)}) 9. Be3 Qd7 {is a likely solution}) 8. Nc3 (8. Be3 Qh4 (8... Rb8 $5) 9. Qc4 Qxc4 10. Bxc4 Na4 11. Bb3 Nb6 $14 {Spassky - Vistanetskis, Tallin 1959}) 8... Rb8 {'a6 10.g4 b4!'} ({This was commended at the time but} 8... Ba6 9. Qg4 (9. Qe3 Bxf1 10. Rxf1 Qh4 $15 {Boleslavsky ECO}) 9... Bxf1 10. Rxf1 d6 11. Be3 Qd7 {may be better} 12. Qxd7+ (12. Qg3 dxe5 $10 {Boleslavsky}) 12... Nxd7 13. exd6 cxd6 14. O-O-O $14 {Forintos, Haag}) 9. f4 $1 (9. a3 $5 Be7 (9... Ba6 10. Qg4 Bxf1 11. Rxf1 d5 (11... d6 $2 12. Be3 $1 Rxb2 13. O-O-O Qb8 14. Bxc5 dxc5 {Forintos, Haag} 15. Qd7#) 12. Bg5 $5 $14) (9... Ne6 10. f4 c5 $13 {Forintos, Haag}) 10. Be3 d5 (10... Rxb2 $2 11. Bxc5 Bxc5 12. Na4 Rb5 13. Qg4 $16) 11. exd6 cxd6 12. Bxc5 dxc5 13. Qe5 Qd6 14. Qxg7 {Keres - Bolbochan, Buenos Aires 1964} Bf6 $1 15. Ne4 Bxg7 16. Nxd6+ Ke7 $10 {Keres}) 9... Be7 (9... Ba6 $2 10. Qf2 Bxf1 11. Rxf1 Ne6 12. f5 Bc5 13. Qg3 Nd4 14. Qxg7 Rf8 15. Kd1 (15. Bg5 Be7 16. f6 {0-0-0 Ediciones Eseuve}) 15... Qh4 16. h3 $1) 10. Qf2 $1 {This gains control of key dark squares because 10. ...d6 11. Be3 Rxb2? loses a piece to 12.O-O-O! (12. ...Rb8 13.Bxc5 or 12. ...NA413.Nxa4 Rxa2 14.Nc3).} d5 (10... d6 11. Be3 Rxb2 $2 12. O-O-O $18) 11. Be3 Nd7 12. O-O-O O-O 13. g4 $1 ({Dr. Tarrasch's prescription for such positions was} 13. Na4 {followed by blockading with a minor piece on c5. But Fischer prefers a kingside pawn storm to the pitfalls of} Rb4 {'' Forintos, Haag} 14. b3 Rxa4 $5 15. bxa4 Nb6) 13... Bb4 14. Ne2 {''} ({Now however,} 14. Na4 {makes sense since ...Rb4 isn't available} Nb6 15. Nc5 (15. Nxb6 $1 axb6 16. f5 {followed by Bf4 and Qg3 was stronger. Black's queenside is healthy then but it offers no counterplay.}) 15... Bxg4 16. Na6 {is unclear}) 14... Nb6 ({Black must have appreciate, from reading Tarrasch if not Nimzovich, that} 14... c5 {was the thematic move. To play it, however, he has to evaluate some difficult unequal-material positions. On} 15. c3 {Black can play} Ba5 16. Rxd5 ({but} 16. Bxc5 Nxc5 17. Qxc5 Bb6 {is more hard to judge. Black has compensation for his pawn - but White can counter-sacrifice with} 18. Qxd5 $1 Qxd5 19. Rxd5 Bb7 ({Black's best bet may be to decline the offer and flee into a bishops of opposite color ending with} 19... Bxg4 20. Nd4 Be6) 20. Bg2 {with excellent chances.}) 16... Bb7 {skewers the rook.}) 15. Nd4 Qe8 16. c3 Be7 17. f5 $1 c5 $1 ({Black is properly alarmed. He realizes his counterplay is stillborn after} 17... Na4 18. Nb3 Ba3 19. bxa3 Nxc3 20. Re1 Qxe5 21. Bd4 {and on other moves White wins on the kingside with f6}) 18. Nb5 d4 $1 19. Bf4 $1 ({Black woul dhave excellent compensation after} 19. cxd4 cxd4 (19... c4 {with the idea of} 20. Nc3 Na4) 20. Nxd4 ({not} 20. Bxd4 Qc6+) 20... Na4 {and ...c5. Also promising is}) 19... dxc3 $2 ({Black's nerves fail - or perhaps he just missed White's 21st and 22nd moves. There were three candidates worth considering} 19... Bb7 20. Rg1 a6) (19... Na4 {and if} 20. Qc2 a6) (19... a6 20. Nxc7 Qa4 $2 (20... Qc6 21. f6 Qxc7 (21... Qxh1 $2 22. Bg2) 22. fxe7 Qxe7 {should suffice}) 21. f6 {favors White}) 20. Nxc3 Na4 21. Bb5 $3 Rxb5 22. Nxa4 {Black needed the knight for counterplay. LEft with the wrong minor pieces on the board, he has no answer to f6} Rb4 23. Nc3 Bb7 24. Rhe1 Kh8 ({There was no good defense to 25.f6 for example} 24... Qc8 25. f6 gxf6 ({no better is} 25... Bd8 26. Be3 $1 Rxg4 27. Bxc5 Re8 28. Rg1 g6 (28... Rxe5 29. Rxg4 Qxg4 30. Rg1 Qe6 31. Rxg7+ {and Qh4}) 29. Rxg4 Qxg4 30. Qd2 $1 {threatening 31.Qh6 or 31.Rxd8}) 26. Bh6 fxe5 {loses quickly to} 27. Rxe5 Re8 28. Rxe7 $1 Rxe7 29. Qf6) 25. f6 $1 Bd8 26. Bg5 {'!'} Rd4 {Desperation. The threat was 27.Rxd8 Qxd8 28.fxg7+. Black is losing after} (26... g6 27. Bh6 Rg8 (27... Rxg4 28. Bg7+ Kg8 29. Qxc5) 28. e6 $1) (26... Kg8 27. Qh4 $18 {Kramer/Postma} (27. Be3 $5 $16 {PS}) 27... h6 28. Bxh6 gxh6 29. Qxh6 Rxg4 30. Rg1 $18 {PS}) 27. fxg7+ {'+-'} Kxg7 28. Bf6+ Kg8 29. Qh4 $1 {There is no defense to Qg5#} Rxd1+ 30. Nxd1 ({Black avoids mate with} 30. Rxd1 Qxe5 $1) 1-0

