{Mikhail Tal said after the 1959 Candidates Tournament that most of Fischer's losses were due to his "repeating the same, and to a considerable extent, inferior opening systems." By 1952, he seemed most vulnerable in the 6.Be2 Najdorf Variation. He nearly lost to Geller in that line at Stockholm and did lose to him with it in the second round at Curacao. Tal was eager to pose another test.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be6 {This move had been discredited since Najdorf had lost with it in the 1953 Candidates Tournament - to Geller} ({Three days before, Fischer lost to Geller after} 7... Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Be3 Qc7 {this is unnecessary before White has played f2-f4} 10. a4 Be6 11. a5 Nbd7 {and now} 12. Nd5 $1 Nxd5 13. exd5 Bf5 14. c4 Bg6 $2 15. Rc1 Nc5 16. Nxc5 dxc5 17. b4 $1 {with a considerable edge}) 8. O-O ({This move helped revive 7. ...Be6 but in the 1970s it was realized that the bishop move has a drawback. After} 8. f4 {White has 9.f5 or 9.g4 coming up} Qc7 9. g4 $1 exf4 10. g5 Nfd7 11. Bxf4 {with an excellent game.}) 8... Nbd7 9. a4 Be7 10. f4 Qc7 11. Be3 O-O 12. a5 {In the Geller-Najdorf game, Black had inserted ...Rac8 but allowed White a strong Ra4!. Fischer's next move anticipates that at the cost of isolating his a-pawn.} b5 $1 13. axb6 Nxb6 14. f5 Bc4 15. Bxb6 $2 ({This logical move, to secure control of d5 was book - and also bad. Geller's second game with White against Fischer at Curacao saw the superior} 15. Kh1 {and then} Rfc8 16. Bxb6 Qxb6 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. Qe2 Rb4 ({Black can improve with} 18... Rac8 19. Ra2 Bd8 $1 {after which he has no major problems.}) 19. Ra2 $1 Qb7 20. Na5 Qc7 21. Nd5 Nxd5 22. exd5 {gave White a clear edge. Black later drew thanks to an Exchange sacrifice.}) 15... Qxb6+ 16. Kh1 Bb5 $1 {A difficult move to foresee. Black prepares 17. ...Bc6!, seizing the initiative.} 17. Bxb5 ({Despite the attention this position had drawn in its short history, few grandmasters realizes White was worse. But Fischer believed White should try to draw with the opposite colored bishops after} 17. Nxb5 axb5 18. Qd3) 17... axb5 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Ra4 $1 20. c3 ({In theory White has excellent chances because of d5 and the "good-knight-versus-bad-bishop". But in practice White lacks a realizable plan, whereas Black has a minority attack (..Rc8 and ...b4). A good illustration of the dangers was Fischer's game with Unzicker at the Varna Olympiad in 1962. White meandered about} 20. h3 Rc8 21. Rfe1 h6 22. Kh2 Bg5 {and suddenly found himself resigning after} 23. g3 $2 Qa7 24. Kg2 Ra2 25. Kf1 Rxc2 $1) 20... Qa6 $1 21. Rad1 Rc8 22. Nc1 {Freaming of reaching d5} b4 $1 23. Nd3 $1 (23. cxb4 Rxb4 24. Nd3 Rd4) ({or} 23. b3 Ra5 {are horrible}) 23... bxc3 24. bxc3 Ra5 $2 ({Fischer called this a "lemon" that denied him a quick win that would come from} 24... Rxc3 {Then PS: '!'} 25. Nxe5 ({Kmoch and Viktor Lyublinsky, in separate annotations, thought highly of} 25. f6 $1 Bxf6 ({But they underestimated} 25... gxf6 $1 {because} 26. Nf2 ({or} 26. Nc1 {loses to} Rd4 27. Rxd4 $2 Qxf1#) (26. Nxe5 dxe5 (26... fxe5 27. Qxf7+ Kh8 28. Qe8+ $18) 27. Qd7 Qa7 28. Qg4+ Kh8 (28... Kf8 29. Qh5 Qe3 (29... Rxe4 30. Qxh7 Rg4 31. Qh8+ Rg8 32. Qh6+ Ke8 33. Qh7 Rf8 $19) 30. Qxh7 Qg5 31. Rb1 Rc8) 29. Qh5 Rxe4 30. Qxf7 Rd4 31. Ra1 Ra3 32. Rac1 Rd8 $19)) 26. Nxe5 $1 Bxe5 $4 (26... dxe5 27. Qd8+ $1) (26... Rc7 27. Ng4 $15) 27. Qxf7+ $1) ({Best is the harmless} 25. Nf2 {after which} Ra2 {and ...Rcc2 lands him in serious trouble}) 25... dxe5 26. Qxe5 {is refuted by} (26. Qd8+ Bf8 27. f6 Rd4 28. Qb8 Rxd1 29. Rxd1 Qxf6 $19) 26... Bb4 $1 27. Qxc3 (27. f6 Rc8 28. fxg7 Bc3 $19) 27... Qxf1+ $1) 25. Qb3 Ra3 26. Qb1 Raxc3 27. Nb4 $1 Qa7 28. Nd5 R3c6 29. Qb3 ({White's position has improved markedly in four moves but he must avoid lines such as} 29. f6 $2 Bxf6 30. Nxf6+ gxf6 31. Rxf6 Qe3 $1) 29... Bf8 30. h3 Ra6 31. Rb1 Ra3 {Black's winning plans include : (a) doubling rooks on the seventh rank (b) trading queens followed by winning the e-pawn, and (c) creating a last-rank mate threat with ...h5-h4} 32. Qb5 Qd4 33. Rfe1 Rg3 ({After} 33... Ra2 {Black can't continue} 34. Qd7 Rcc2 {because} 35. Rb8 {threatening} Rxg2 36. Rxf8+ Kxf8 37. Qd8#) 34. Qe2 Qd3 35. Qh5 Qc2 ({And here} 35... Rc2 36. Rb7 $1 {gives White a winning attack} g6 37. Nf6+ Kg7 38. Qxh7+ Kxf6 39. Rxf7+ $1) 36. Qe2 Qxe2 37. Rxe2 h5 38. Ra2 Rd3 39. Ra7 h4 $1 ({After} 39... Rd4 40. f6 Rxd5 {White saves himself with} 41. Rbb7 Rd4 ({and he would win after} 41... Rd1+ 42. Kh2 h4 $2 43. Rxf7 Rcc1 44. Rxf8+ $1 Kxf8 45. fxg7+ Kg8) 42. fxg7 Rxe4 43. Rxf7 $1) 40. f6 g6 $2 ({This squanders Black's winning capital. Superior is} 40... gxf6 $1 {since} 41. Rbb7 {loses to} ({White can fight, however, with} 41. Rf1 $1 {then} f5 42. Rxf5 Rc1+ 43. Kh2 Rdd1 {fails to} 44. Rg5+ Kh7 45. Rh5+ {and} Kg6 46. Rxh4) 41... Rc1+ {and 42. ...Rdd1.}) 41. Kh2 $1 {Tal requires his opponent to make a critical choice with his next move - the sealed move.} Rxd5 $5 ({But Tal confessed that when the adjournment envelope was opened Black's move "came like a thunderbolt". It was easy to dispose of} 41... Rd4 $2 42. Rbb7 Rxe4 {by way of} 43. Rxf7 Rd4 44. Rg7+ $1 Bxg7 45. Rxg7+ Kf8 46. Ne7 {and wins.}) ({But no one in the Soviet delegation saw Fischer's move coming. THey must have expected} 41... Rd2 42. Rbb7 Rcc2 {after which} 43. Rb8 ({White can also try} 43. Ne3 Rc3 44. Rb8 Rxe3 45. Rxf8+ Kh7 (45... Kxf8 46. Ra8#) 46. Rfxf7+ Kh6 {but he has no win after} 47. Rh7+ Kg5 48. f7 Rg3) 43... Rxg2+ {is only a draw.}) 42. exd5 Bh6 43. Re7 ({White must watch the e-pawn} 43. Rd7 e4 $1 {as well as a potential mating attack}) (43. Rbb7 Bf4+ 44. Kh1 (44. Kg1 $1 Be3+ 45. Kh2 $1 Bxa7 46. Rxa7 Rc5 47. Ra8+ Kh7 48. Rf8 g5 $1) 44... Rc1#) 43... Bg5 44. Rf1 Bf4+ 45. Kg1 g5 46. Rb1 e4 $1 {On this square, the pawn denies White f3, so 47.Rbb7 Rc1+ is a perpetual check} ({Black faces another crisit. White is ready to threaten Rbb7. Trading rooks is almost always bad here} 46... Rc1+ 47. Rxc1 Bxc1 48. Kf2 Bf4 49. Kf3 {is lost} Kh7 50. Rxf7+ Kg6 51. Rf8 Kf5 52. Re8 Kxf6 53. Ke4 {etc.}) (46... Be3+ 47. Kf1 Rc2 {Black is still alive, but following} 48. Ke1 {he must be careful}) 47. Rxe4 Kh7 $1 48. Re7 Kg6 49. Rbb7 Kxf6 50. Rxf7+ Ke5 51. Rfc7 ({The powerfully centralized king and danger of rook checks ensure Black at least a draw e.g.} 51. Rb5 Rc1+ 52. Kf2 Rc2+ 53. Kf3 Rc3+ 54. Kg4 Rg3+ {and WHite is playing to lose. The conclusion was uneventful.}) 51... Ra8 52. Rb1 Kxd5 53. Rd1+ Ke6 54. Rc2 Ra3 55. Kf2 Rb3 56. Re2+ Kf5 57. Rd5+ Kf6 58. Re4 $11 1/2-1/2

