[EventType "team-tourn"]

{Into the early 1960s, Soviet players who had met him described Fischer as a chess-mad American who was willing to play five-minute chess at any hour. Tal recalled how he had already packed his bag to leave at the end of Zürich 1959 when he got a call from Fischer's room, elsewhere in the hotel. "I'm flying to New York in the hour," he said. "But if you agree to play some blitz I'll give up my ticket." But by the time of Curacao, when Tal was playing Fischer for the last times, the Latvian began to apprecaite him as a player of considerably greater depth, especially in the endgame. Game 34 was one example of the following, from the 11th round, was a second. The opening skirmishing was intriguing because both players seem to believe they had caught their opponent in a rare, virtually new sideline. Myers Marin Purdy Soltis: 'Myers: This game is given as #95 by GM Soltis in "The 100 Best Chess Games".'} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 {Neither Tal nor Fischer had ever Played this then trnedy opening before, and never would again} 5. Nb5 a6 6. Nd6+ Bxd6 7. Qxd6 Qf6 {An unusual concept : Instead of cobbling a pawn structure on dark squares (...d6) to shield his holes, Black bets on superior development and an explosive ...d5 sacrifice.} 8. Qd1 Qg6 9. Nc3 Nge7 10. h4 $1 ({When an opening is young, such as 5. ...a6 was in 1962, natural moves such as} 10. Be3 {are tried. But often they are rejected (here because of} d5 11. Nxd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 (12. exd5 Nb4) 12... Be6 {) in favor of eccentric but effective alternatives, such as 10.h4 and 11.h5}) 10... h5 11. Bg5 d5 $5 12. Bxe7 ({Today this opening has been more or less retire by} 12. exd5 $1 {Myers Marin Purdy Soltis: ''} Nb4 (12... Nd4 13. Bd3 Bf5 14. Bxf5 Nexf5 15. Qd3 $14) 13. Bxe7 $1 {which sentences Black to a poor ending after} Kxe7 14. Bd3 $1 (14. d6+ Kd8 15. Bd3 Nxd3+ 16. Qxd3 Qxd3 17. cxd3 Bf5 18. O-O-O Kd7 19. d4 e4 20. Rhe1 $14) 14... Nxd3+ (14... Qxg2 15. Be4 {and 16.a3}) 15. Qxd3 Qxd3 16. cxd3 Rh6 17. O-O-O Rg6 18. g3 Bg4 19. Rd2 f5 20. Re1 Kd6 21. d4 e4 22. b4 Bf3 23. b5 Rc8 24. Rc2 Kd7 25. bxa6 Rxa6 26. Kd2 Rc4 27. Rb1 b6 28. Ke3 Ra3 29. Rb3 Rxb3 30. axb3 Rb4 31. Kf4 Bg4 32. Ke5 Rxb3 33. d6 Kd8 34. Ne2 Rb5+ 35. d5 Rb1 36. Nf4 {1-0 Edwards,J-Rimlinger,P/corr USA 1987/}) 12... d4 $1 13. Bg5 $1 {Fischer is first to improve. Myers Marin Purdy Soltis: 'N'} (13. Ne2 Nxe7 (13... Kxe7 14. Ng3 Bg4 15. Be2 Rac8 16. Rc1 Rhd8 17. Qd2 Bxe2 18. Qxe2 Qg4 19. Nxh5 Qxg2 20. Ng3 d3 21. cxd3 Nd4 22. Qd1 Nf3+ 23. Ke2 Nd4+ 24. Ke1 Nf3+ {½-½ Clerides,P-Ustariz,C/Elista RUS 1998/}) 14. Ng3 (14. Qd3 Nc6 15. O-O-O O-O 16. Ng3 f5 17. exf5 Bxf5 18. Nxf5 Qxf5 19. Qxf5 Rxf5 20. Bc4+ Kf8 21. f3 Rd8 22. Bd3 Rf6 23. a3 b5 24. Rde1 Ke7 25. Rhf1 Kd6 26. Re2 Re8 27. Rfe1 Re7 28. Kb1 Rf4 29. Be4 Nd8 30. g3 Rf6 31. Ka2 Ne6 32. b4 Nf8 33. Rd2 Rc7 34. Rd3 Rxc2+ 35. Kb3 Rc4 36. Bb7 a5 37. bxa5 Nd7 38. Be4 Nc5+ 39. Kb2 Nxd3+ 40. Bxd3 Rxf3 41. Bxc4 bxc4 42. a6 Rf2+ 43. Kb1 Rf7 44. g4 hxg4 45. Rg1 Ra7 46. Rxg4 Kd5 47. Rg6 Ke4 48. Kc2 Kf5 49. h5 Ke4 50. a4 Kf4 51. a5 e4 52. h6 gxh6 53. Rxh6 e3 54. Rc6 Ke4 55. Rxc4 Rxa6 56. Rc5 d3+ 57. Kc1 Kd4 58. Rh5 Rg6 59. Kb2 Rg2+ 60. Kb3 e2 {0-1 Triplett,T-Golonka,S/Detroit USA 1992/}) 14... Bd7 (14... Bg4 15. Be2 Rc8 16. Qd2 Bxe2 17. Qxe2 Qe6 $15 {Myers}) 15. Be2 Bg4 16. Bxg4 Qxg4 17. Qxg4 hxg4 18. h5 Ng8 19. f3 Nf6 20. Kd2 Kd7 21. Raf1 gxf3 22. gxf3 Rh7 23. f4 Re8 24. fxe5 Rxe5 25. Rf5 Nxe4+ 26. Kd3 Nxg3 27. Rxe5 Nxh1 28. Kxd4 Ng3 29. Rd5+ Kc7 30. Re5 f6 31. Re7+ Kc6 32. Re8 Rxh5 33. c4 Nf5+ 34. Kc3 Nd6 35. Re7 Rg5 36. b4 Rg3+ 37. Kc2 Nxc4 38. a4 f5 39. b5+ axb5 40. axb5+ Kb6 41. Rf7 g6 42. Kd1 Rg2 43. Ke1 Nd6 44. Rf6 Kc5 45. Kf1 Rg4 46. b6 Kd5 47. Ke2 Ke5 48. Rf8 g5 49. Kf3 Rb4 50. Rg8 Rb3+ 51. Kf2 Kf4 52. Rd8 Ne4+ 53. Kg2 Rxb6 54. Rd1 Rb2+ 55. Kg1 b5 56. Rd3 b4 57. Rd1 b3 58. Re1 Rc2 {0-1 Pedersen,L-Nielsen,H/Esbjerg DEN 2007/}) (13. Bc5 $5 dxc3 14. bxc3 (14. f3 cxb2 (14... f5 15. exf5 Bxf5 16. bxc3 e4 17. fxe4 Qg3+ 18. Kd2 O-O-O+ 19. Bd3 Bxe4 20. Qe2 Rhe8 21. Be