{This little-known exhibition game, arranged in summer 1962 by Danish TV, is a good example of Fischer's sense of justive. Even in a game with nothing at stake but ego he wouldn't let Larsen get away with what he considered indulgences in the opening. Fischer plays for an edge with Black, grabs a pawn and ignites a sparkling battle of imaginations.} 1. f4 Nf6 2. Nf3 g6 3. d3 ({Fischer may hav ebeen influences by one of his early games when he crushed an amateur by exploiting a hole at e3} 3. b3 Bg7 4. Bb2 O-O 5. d3 $6 d5 6. Nbd2 Ng4 $1 7. Bxg7 Ne3 8. Qc1 Kxg7 {. Later in his career, Fischer preferred setups with ...d6, allowing White to tranpose into a Closed SIcilian, as in a 1971 Larsen-Fischer Candidates Match 1.f4 c5 2.Nf3 g6 and now 3.e4 Bg7 4.Be2 Nc6.}) 3... d5 4. Nbd2 {With colors reversed (1.d4 f5 2.c4 d6 3.Nc3 Nf6 and ... Nbd7, ...Qc7 followed by ...e5) this has been called Antoshin's variation.} d4 5. c3 ({now} 5. Nb3 c5 6. c3 {would challenge Black's strategy. White may have rejected this because of} (6. Nxc5 $4 Qa5+) 6... dxc3 7. bxc3 c4 {since} 8. dxc4 $2 Qxd1+ 9. Kxd1 {is no fun to play, even in an exhibition.}) 5... dxc3 6. bxc3 Nd5 $1 7. Qa4+ $1 Nc6 (7... Bd7 8. Qd4 {with an advantage}) 8. Ne5 Bg7 ({and here White wins after} 8... Nxc3 9. Qc4 $1) ({The real alternative was} 8... Nb6 {since} 9. Nxc6 (9. Qe4 Nxe5 10. fxe5) 9... Nxa4 10. Nxd8 Kxd8 {is nothing for Black to fear. White would probably stay in the middlegame - or is it still the opening with}) 9. Ne4 $1 ({Black has the better of} 9. Nxc6 {because of the vulnerability of c3 e.g.} Qd7 10. Nb1 Qxc6 11. Qxc6+ bxc6 12. Bd2 Rb8) 9... Nb6 $1 10. Qb3 $6 ({This breaks the pin favorably} 10. Nxc6 Nxa4 11. Nxd8 Kxd8 {and prompts White to offer a gambit "on spec."}) 10... Nxe5 11. fxe5 Bxe5 $1 ({Black has a positional edge after} 11... O-O 12. d4 ({or} 12. Bf4 Be6) 12... Be6 {. But allowing White to emerge from the opening relatively unscathed may have offended Fischer's sense of jurisprudence. He wants material.}) 12. Qb5+ Nd7 13. Bh6 $1 c6 14. Qb3 Nf6 15. Bg7 (15. Ng5 $2 Qd5 {and Black is on the road to victory.}) 15... Rg8 16. Bxf6 exf6 17. d4 f5 $1 ({White has better chances after} 17... Bc7 18. Nc5 {and 19.O-O-O!?}) 18. Nf2 ({but there's not a scintilla of compensation in} 18. dxe5 fxe4 19. Rd1 Qg5) ({or} 18. Nc5 Qh4+ 19. Kd2 Bxd4 $1) 18... Bf4 19. e4 $1 fxe4 20. Bc4 Rg7 ({Black may have been scared off} 20... Qe7 21. O-O b5 22. Bxf7+ Qxf7 23. Qxf7+ Kxf7 24. Nxe4 g5 25. g3 Bf5 26. Rae1 Rge8) 21. O-O b5 $6 ({simplest is} 21... Be3 $1 22. Rae1 Bxf2+ 23. Rxf2 f5 {since} 24. Rxe4+ {is refuted by} fxe4 25. Bf7+ Ke7 $1) 22. Nxe4 $1 bxc4 23. Qxc4 Be3+ 24. Kh1 Bf5 $1 (24... Bd7 25. Nf6+ Kf8 26. Qc5+ Qe7 27. Nxd7+) 25. Qxc6+ Kf8 26. Nd6 Rb8 (26... Be6 $2 27. Rae1 {and White is suddenly better}) 27. Qc5 ({It was too early for desperation} 27. Rxf5 Rb6 $2 (27... gxf5 28. Nxf5 Bg5) 28. Nxf7 {is refuted by} gxf5 29. Nxd8 Bg5) 27... Qb6 {Both first ranks become vulnerable now} 28. Qe5 (28. Qa3 Kg8 29. Rae1 (29. Rfe1 Bg5 30. Ne8 Bh4 $1) 29... Bd3 30. Rf3 Bd2) 28... Bg5 ({also good was} 28... f6 29. Qxf6+ Kg8 30. Qe5 Qc7) 29. Rae1 $1 {Threatening Qe8+} Qd8 ({Another trap was} 29... Bd7 30. Rxf7+ $1 Rxf7 31. Qh8#) 30. g4 Bf6 ({perhaps better as the immediate} 30... Be7 31. Nxf7 Kxf7 32. gxf5 Bf6 $1 33. Qe6+ Kf8 {is quite safe}) 31. Qd5 ({also lost is} 31. Qf4 Be7 32. Rxe7 Qxe7 33. Nxf5 (33. gxf5 gxf5 34. Nxf5 $2 Qb7+ 35. Rf3 Qxf3+ 36. Qxf3 Rb1+ 37. Qf1 Rxf1#) 33... Qb7+) 31... Be7 $1 32. gxf5 $2 (32. Rxe7 Qxe7 (32... Kxe7 $2 33. gxf5 Qxd6 34. f6+) 33. gxf5 Qe2 {is lost but better than the game.}) 32... Qxd6 33. Qxd6 Bxd6 34. f6 Rg8 35. c4 {With only a piece for a pawn, WHite manages to create play 35. ...Rb2? 36.c5!} g5 $1 36. c5 Bf4 37. h4 Rg6 $1 ({Last trap... on} 37... h6 38. h5 $1 {White's 39.Re7 or 39.d6 would cause more trouble than expected} Re8 39. c6 Rxe1 40. Rxe1 {and White wins}) 38. d5 Rxf6 39. hxg5 Bxg5 40. d6 Rc8 $1 {Simplest. Black gives back the piece and keeps the two extra pawns.} 41. Rxf6 Bxf6 42. d7 Rxc5 43. Re8+ Kg7 44. d8=Q Bxd8 45. Rxd8 Rc1+ 46. Kg2 Rc2+ 47. Kg3 Rxa2 0-1

