{Although his experience had ranged from weekend Swiss System opens to a Candidates Tournament, Fischer had never played much team chess until he succeeded Reshevsky as first board in the Olympiads. Reshevsky managed only a plus-one score at Munich 1958 and Tel Aviv 1964, and often drew in fewer than 30 moves. But Fischer fought in every game. At Leipzig 1960 he won 10, drew six and lost two. At Varna 1962, he began with a blistering 81/2-11/2 before slowing down to a final score of 11-6. His ability to win games with Black like the following kept the Americans in the medal race until the final round.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Qxd4 {This, with c2-c4, was regarded as a valid way of playing for a win while avoiding Najdorf/Dragon theory} Nc6 5. Bb5 Bd7 6. Bxc6 Bxc6 7. c4 Nf6 8. Nc3 g6 {A basic premise of 4.Qxd4 states : When White sets up a Maroczy bind pawn structure, Black should fianchetto. BUt when White adopts the more aggressive plan of 7.Nc3 and O-O-O Black plays ... e6,... Be7. Typical play here would be 9.O-O Bg7 10.Qd3 O-O 11.Nd4 Qb6.} 9. Bg5 Bg7 10. Nd5 $2 ({Blau tries to exploit the temporary pin on f6 at a prohibitive loss of tim. Better was} 10. O-O O-O 11. Qe3) 10... O-O $1 11. Qd3 (11. Nxf6+ exf6 {is nearly winning for Black} 12. Bh4 (12. Be3 f5) 12... Bxe4) (11. Bxf6 exf6 12. O-O f5 {is excellent for him}) 11... Bxd5 $1 ({good enough to equalize at least is} 11... e6 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13. Bxf6 Qxf6 14. Nd4 d5 15. Nxc6 bxc6 {. After the superior text move, Black has two pawns to harass before White manages to castle.}) 12. exd5 ({Black gets a nice game from} 12. cxd5 Qb6 ({but even better from} 12... Qa5+ $1 13. Bd2 (13. Qd2 Qa6 $1 14. Qb4 Rac8) 13... Qa4)) 12... Nd7 13. Qd2 ({Now} 13. Qe2 {is met by} Qa5+ 14. Bd2 Qa6 $1 15. Bg5 Nb6 16. Bxe7 $2 Rfe8) 13... Rc8 14. Rc1 b5 $1 15. b3 (15. cxb5 Rxc1+ 16. Qxc1 Qa5+ 17. Qd2 Qxb5 18. b3 Nb6) 15... bxc4 16. bxc4 Nc5 17. O-O {If White averts ...Ne4 he allows a nasty 17. ...Qa5+} Ne4 18. Qe3 Nxg5 19. Qxg5 $2 ({Here} 19. Nxg5 Qd7 20. Rfe1 {may hold Black's advanatage to a manageable level.}) 19... Rc5 $1 20. Qe3 ({Now} 20. Nd2 {invites} Qc7 {and a threat of ... e6. For example} 21. Rfe1 e6 22. Nb3 Rxc4 23. dxe6 fxe6 24. Rxe6 {keeps everything even, but after} Rxc1+ 25. Qxc1 (25. Nxc1 $2 Qc2) 25... Qxc1+ 26. Nxc1 Rc8 {Black has a very promising ending}) 20... Qc7 21. Nd4 ({And here} 21. Nd2 {is met strongly by} Ra5 22. Rc2 Rxd5) (21. Rc2 Rxc4 22. Rxc4 Qxc4 23. Qxa7 Bf6 24. Rd1 {defends better}) 21... Bxd4 $1 ({Black's heavy-piece pressure against c4 is much more effective thatn the immediate} 21... Rxc4 22. Nc6 $1 {e.g.} Rxc1 23. Nxe7+ Kh8 24. Rxc1) 22. Qxd4 Rc8 23. Rc2 ({White must lose material but can draw if he liquidates enough pawns. Best was probably} 23. Qe3 Rxc4 24. Rxc4 Qxc4 25. Qxe7 {although} Qxd5 26. Qxa7 Ra8 {and Black has the kind of advantage he can nurse for another 40 moves.}) 23... Rxc4 24. Rxc4 Qxc4 25. Qxa7 Qe4 $1 26. h3 ({Centralizing gives Black better chances with equal material than he would have had a pawn up in the last note. If White tries to break Black's hold with} 26. Qe3 Qxe3 27. fxe3 {he lands in a lost endgame} Ra8 28. Ra1 Ra3 29. Kf2 f5 {and ...Kf7-f6-e5}) ({and} 26. a4 Rc5 27. Qa8+ Kg7 28. Rd1 Qd4 $1 {just loses a pawn}) 26... Rc4 $1 {Black doesn't even allow a4. On 27.Qb8+ Kg7 28.Qb2+ f6 he has ..>Rc2 coming up (and 29.Qd2 Rd4 30.Re1 Rxd2 31.Rxe4 Kf7 is lost.)} 27. Rd1 Kg7 {Black threatens ...Ra4} 28. Qb7 Qe2 29. Rf1 Ra4 30. Qb3 Rxa2 31. Qc3+ f6 $1 ({The immediate} 31... Qe5 $2 32. Qxe5+ dxe5 {isn't convincing after} 33. Rd1 {with the idea of 34.d6 e.g.} Ra6 34. Rc1 Rd6 35. Rc5) 32. Qe1 Qe5 $1 (32... Qxe1 33. Rxe1 Kf7 34. Rd1 f5 35. g3 Kf6 36. Kg2 Ke5 (36... Ra5 37. f4) 37. Re1+) 33. f4 (33. Qxe5 fxe5 $1 34. Rd1 Kf6 35. g3 e4) 33... Qxd5 $1 ({Thanks to White's make-or-break last move, Black can also win the endgame with} 33... Qxe1 34. Rxe1 Kf7 35. Rd1 Ra4 $1 36. g3 Ra2 $1 {But Fischer tended to avoid rook endings whose outcome was less than 100 percent certain. Black can go for the kill now since his king will be magically safe on h6 - or even g3.}) 34. Qxe7+ Kh6 35. Qf8+ ({best here is probably} 35. Rf3 {But Black makes steady progress with} Qd1+ 36. Rf1 Qc2 $1 37. Qb7 Qe2 {threatening Rd2 and 39. ...d5!} 38. Qf3 Qxf3 39. Rxf3 Rd2) 35... Kh5 {Whtie missed his chance for Rf3 because now it loses outright to 36. ... Qd1+ 37.Kh2 Qxf3 or 37.Rf1 Qd2 38.g4+ Kh4 39.Qxf6+ Kg3!} 36. g4+ Kh4 37. Qxf6+ ({Black would have met} 37. Qh6+ {with} Kg3 {and mates. But here 37. ...Kg3 38.Qc3+ Kh4 39.Rf2 makes him start all over again.}) 37... Kxh3 38. Qc3+ Kxg4 39. Qc8+ Kh4 40. Qd8+ {The only defenses to 41. ...Qg2# are 41.Qg5+ Qxg5 and the faster losses of 41.Rf2 Qc5! and 41.Rf3 Qd1+ 42.Rf1 Qg4+.} Kh5 0-1

