{Fischer next made news by turning down his invitation to play in the first Piatgorsky Cup, which featured Petrosian, Keres, Gligoric, Reshevsky and Benko. Instead he entered a July 4th weekend tournament which began two days after the Piatgorsky. Fischer never explained publickly why he passed up the strgonest tournament of 1963 to play in a Swiss System open offering a $750 top prize. But his action was understood as a slight to Jacqueline Piatgorsky, who was sponsor of both the Cup and the ill-fated Reshevsky match two years before. Later Fischer wrote an over-the-top article in Chess Life, in which, among other things, he claimed that at least five of his Western Open games were of a higher quality than all but one of the Piatgorsky games. When passions cooled he didn't include any of the Western games in his 60 Memorable ones. But the following was a worthy candidate.} {Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Fischer next made news by turning down his invitation to play in the first Piatgorsky Cup, which featured Petrosian, Keres, Gligoric, Reshevsky and Benko. Instead he entered a July 4th weekend tournament which began two days after the Piatgorsky. Fischer never explained publickly why he passed up the strgonest tournament of 1963 to play in a Swiss System open offering a $750 top prize. But his action was understood as a slight to Jacqueline Piatgorsky, who was sponsor of both the Cup and the ill-fated Reshevsky match two years before. Later Fischer wrote an over-the-top article in Chess Life, in which, among other things, he claimed that at least five of his Western Open games were of a higher quality than all but one of the Piatgorsky games. When passions cooled he didn't include any of the Western games in his 60 Memorable ones.  But the following was a worthy candidate.''} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 {Berliner, who won the next world correspondence championship, was known for elaborately prepared openings and Fischer didn't want to find out what he had planned against a King's Indian or a Grünfeld Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Berliner, who won the next world correspondence championship, was known for elaborately prepared openings and Fischer didn't want to find out what he had planned against a King's Indian or a Grünfeld''} 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ ({After this line was temporarily discredited, by a Spassky win in the 1969 world championship match, Fischer switched to Agur: 'After this line was temporarily discredited, by a Spassky win in the 1969 world championship match, Fischer switched to'} 8... Nc6 9. Bc4 b5 {and made a 29-move draw with Spassky in the 1972 Championship match Agur: 'and made a 29-move draw with Spassky in the 1972 Championship match'}) 9. Bd2 Bxd2+ 10. Qxd2 O-O 11. Bd3 ({White's center looks impressive but if he can't find a way to capitalize on it in the middlegame it will be a liability in the endgame. For example, a queen trade after Agur: 'White's center looks impressive but if he can't find a way to capitalize on it in the middlegame it will be a liability in the endgame. For example, a queen trade after'} 11. Bc4 Nc6 12. O-O b6 13. Rfd1 Bb7 14. Qf4 Qf6 $1 15. Qxf6 (15. Qe3 Rfd8 16. d5 Qh6 $1 {as in Reshevsky-Fine, Hastings 1937-38 is excellent for Black. As Fischer put it, "The more pieces that are exchanged, the less valuable the pawn center is." Agur: 'as in Reshevsky-Fine, Hastings 1937-38 is excellent for Black. As Fischer put it, "The more pieces that are exchanged, the less valuable the pawn center is."'})) 11... b6 12. O-O Bb7 13. Rfd1 Nc6 14. Qb2 $2 {Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: '?''} ({Black can meet Agur: 'Black can meet'} 14. Qf4 {with Agur: 'with'} Qf6 {as in the Fine game. Agur: 'as in the Fine game.'}) ({White should settle for Agur: 'White should settle for'} 14. Qe3) 14... Qf6 {Now the d-pawn is frozen and becomes the topic of debate for example after 15.e5 Qf4 Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Now the d-pawn is frozen and becomes the topic of debate for example after 15.e5 Qf4''} 15. Rac1 Rfd8 16. Bb5 ({Black's pressure is also evident after Agur: 'Black's pressure is also evident after'} 16. Bb1 Rac8 17. Rc3 Qf4) 16... Rac8 17. Ne5 $6 {Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: '?!''} ({White cannot find a plan or even improve the position of his pieces because of trades Agur: 'White cannot find a plan or even improve the position of his pieces because of trades'} 17. Rc4 Ne7 {. Berliner's solution is bold : he trashes his textbook center in return for tactical chances on the c- and d-files. Agur: '. Berliner's solution is bold : he trashes his textbook center in return for tactical chances on the c- and d-files.'}) 17... Nxe5 18. dxe5 Qf4 ({after Agur: 'after'} 18... Rxc1 19. Qxc1 $1 {White stands better thanin the game Agur: 'White stands better thanin the game'}) 19. Rxc8 Rxc8 20. Qd4 {Threats of mate (21.Qd8+) and of 21.Qd7 Rb8 22.Qc7 save White's e-pawns for the moment Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Threats of mate (21.Qd8+) and of 21.Qd7 Rb8 22.Qc7 save White's e-pawns for the moment''} ({On Agur: 'On'} 20. f3 {Black has a strong plan of Agur: 'Black has a strong plan of'} h5 {followed by ...h4 and ...Rc5. Agur: 'followed by ...h4 and ...Rc5.'}) 20... g5 $1 {Agur: 'Schwarze Angriffsideen: c5, c1, c2 ×e5,×e4'} ({Black needed luft and there are two reasons why this way is best : The first is strategic. To defend e4 White must eventually play f2-f3 or Bf3, but that will invite ... g4 now. The other reason is tactical. Black rejects because of Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'Black needed luft and there are two reasons why this way is best :   The first is strategic. To defend e4 White must eventually play f2-f3 or Bf3, but that will invite ...g4 now.   The other reason is tactical. Black rejects because of''} 20... h6 21. f3 (21. Qd7 {with the idea of Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'with the idea of''} Qxe4 $4 {Agur: '?' '?' Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: '??''} ({But Black is closer to winning after Agur: 'Andrew Soltis: 'But Black is closer to winning after''} 21... Rc1 $1 {Agur: '