{When Mikhail Botwinnik, the paragon of preparation, was readying himself for a match with Fischer in 1970, he made an elaborate study of the American's games. Among his most telling insights was that Fischer liked "clear positions" so much that he was prepared for simplification whenever he held positional plusses. This ran counter to a common tenet of the Soviet School, that in favorable positions you should avoid exchanges unless they lead to a quickly realizable advantage. The following game shows a typical Fischer decision : At move 32 he trades queens even though there iis good reason to believe he has a faster win by other means. It may be a faster win - but it's not a clearer, more certain win.} 1. g3 c5 $1 ({Fischer wrestled with the problem of how to answer 1.g3 since Benko surprised him with it in the first round at Curacao. In that game Fischer replied the same way he would against 1.Nf3, with} 1... Nf6 2. Bg2 g6 {. But Benko used the difference in move order to play} 3. e4 $1 Bg7 4. d4 {. That left Fischer with a choice of playing into one of two systems new to him, a closed SIcilian in which he had already committed a knight to f6 or a Pirc defense. He chose the latter} d6 {and lost}) 2. Bg2 Nc6 3. d3 g6 4. e4 Bg7 5. f4 e6 {Black is playing a Sicilian with ...Nge7 while White avoids Nc3 in favor of Nbd2. WHite's knight turns out to be more of a problem than Black's.} 6. Nf3 Nge7 7. O-O Rb8 8. Nbd2 d6 9. a4 O-O 10. c3 $6 ({White faced three questions in the opening. He answered the first at move nine when he signaled he would open the a-file after ...b5. The second question was how to deal with the attack on b2, and this move answers it at the cost of creating a new target at c3. The third question was how to develop the queen knight. The Nh4/Ndf3 option is modest but so is} 10. Nc4 {and ...Na3 after ...a6, ...b5. Byrne delays the decsion.}) 10... a6 11. g4 b5 12. axb5 axb5 13. Nh4 b4 $1 ({There was no urgency for} 13... f5 14. gxf5 exf5 {since Black can stop f5 by other means}) 14. c4 ({White had to cede d4 because} 14. Qc2 {allows} bxc3 15. bxc3 Nd4 $1 16. Qd1 Nb5 $1) 14... Nd4 {The pawn structure favors Black and he can overwhelm the queenside with ...Qc7, ...Bd7 and eventually ...Ra8} 15. g5 ({If White had all day he might carry out an ambitious plan} 15. Nhf3 {, h2-h4-h5 and Nh2-g4. But taking precatuions with} Bd7 16. Nb3 {appears better}) 15... Bd7 16. Ndf3 Qc7 17. Nxd4 Bxd4+ 18. Kh1 Bg7 {The bishop retreat anticipated Nf3xd4 and clears the way for ... Nc6-d4 - if Black doesn't act in the center with ...Bc6 and ...d5 instead} 19. Rb1 Ra8 20. Be3 Ra2 21. Qd2 Re8 $1 {This discourages f4-f5 which would have been effective after 21. ...Nc6 22.f5!} 22. Qf2 Bc6 23. d4 $2 ({The benefit of Black's 21st move is evident after} 23. f5 $2 exf5 24. exf5 Nxf5 25. Nxf5 gxf5) ({But White should not change the center at all and show await events with} 23. Nf3 {. Now his White knight will remain out of play on h4}) 23... cxd4 24. Bxd4 Bxd4 25. Qxd4 Ba8 ({This is an overfinesse, since} 25... Bb7 {has the same plusses, such as clearing the square for ...Nc6 while avoiding the minus of a White counter-attack on the a-file. It would also render Black extra options ...b3 and ...BA6}) 26. Ra1 (26. Rfd1 Rd8 27. b3 {Black carries out the knight maneuver with} Nc6 28. Qe3 e5 29. f5 Nd4 {with advantage.}) 26... e5 $1 27. Qd2 ({After} 27. fxe5 dxe5 28. Qf2 {Black has} Qxc4 {protecting f7}) ({not} 27. Qf2 Rxa1 28. Rxa1 Qxc4) 27... Rxa1 28. Rxa1 exf4 ({White keeps his d-pawn rather than} 28... Qxc4 29. Qxd6 {because it will support the new outpost at e5.}) 29. b3 Nc6 30. Qxf4 $2 ({White should insert} 30. Nf3 {, not fearing} Ne5 ({Black can secure a small edge with} 30... Bb7 31. Qxf4 Ne5 {thanks to weak White pawns. But now Black has a better outpost at d4.}) 31. Nxe5 dxe5 32. Qxb4 Rb8 33. Qa5 $1) 30... Nd4 31. Qe3 $1 ({Black begins to pluck pawn after} 31. Rb1 Qe7) 31... Nc2 $1 ({Not} 31... Nf5 32. exf5 $1) 32. Qa7 {Whtie loses the desperado contest after} (32. Ra7 Qxc4) 32... Qxa7 ({Black's endgame is so easily won after this that it is hard to criticize Fischer for passing up a chance to win faster with} 32... Bb7 $1 {. That threatens Ra8 as well as 33. ...Nxa1. For exaempl} 33. Ra4 ({the key tactic is} 33. Rc1 Rxe4 $1 34. Qf2 (34. Bxe4 Bxe4+ 35. Nf3 Nd4) 34... Qe7) (33. Qa5 Qc8 34. Rf1 Rxe4 $1) 33... Ra8 34. Qf2 Rxa4 35. bxa4 b3) 33. Rxa7 Nd4 34. Rd7 ({after} 34. Ra4 {Black can win with} Nxb3 (34... Rb8 $1 35. e5 Bxg2+ 36. Kxg2 dxe5 37. Nf3 {is refuted by} Nxb3 38. Nxe5 Nd4 {and the b-pawn runs on}) 35. Rxb4 Nd2 {but much better is}) 34... Nxb3 35. Rxd6 Nc5 36. Rb6 b3 37. Kg1 Bxe4 $1 ({Again Black chooses the clearest. Of course} 37... Nxe4 38. Rxb3 Nxg5 {should win eventually, but now the b-pawn must win material.}) 38. Nf3 Ra8 $1 ({superior to} 38... Bc2 39. Rb5 Ne4) 39. Rb5 b2 40. Rxb2 Ra1+ 41. Kf2 Nd3+ {The game ended with} 42. Ke3 Nxb2 43. Kxe4 Nxc4 44. Kf4 Ra2 45. Kg3 Ne3 46. Bh3 0-1

