{Despite his 11-0 victory in the U.S. Championship, Fischer refused his invitation to the next Interzonal at Amsterdam, because he considered the top prize of $300 too low. Just before the tournament began in May 1964 Fischer was interviewed by a Moscow correspondent of the Russian news service APN. Their brief Q&A went : "But this deprives you of the chance to fight for the world championship title for at least three years." "I know." "What provoked your decision?" "I don't want to play." In fact, he didn't return to serious chess for 19 months, until the Capablanca Memorial in August 1965. It was Tal who captured the absurdity of the situation : For once, Bobby wanted to play chess - but someone else stopped him. He was barred by U. S. passport authorities from travelling to Fidel Castro's island. (It was not the last time he would come into conflict with teh State Department). But extraordinary arrangements were made for him to play by long distance. Fischer sat at what was known as the "Capablanca table", a favorite of the Cuban, in the sealed off back room on the Marshall Chess Club's second floor, with a window to his left so he could look out on the club's backyard. As usual following Fischer's absences, he returned with openings that were new to him - as well as ideas in heavily-analyzed lines very familiar.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4 e6 7. a3 ({By securing the bishop a sniper's nest at a2, he solves White's most pressing problem in the opening but at the cost of a tempo. Fischer, who understood the arcana of 6. Bc4 better than anyone, believed the tempo mattered. A year later at the Havana Olympiad, he challenged Stern to a skittles game and after} 7. Bb3 Be7 8. f4 $1 b5 9. f5 e5 10. Nde2 Bb7 11. O-O {he told Stein in Russian that Black's position was hopeless.}) 7... Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Ba2 b5 10. f4 Bb7 11. f5 ({Robatsch, then the leading advocate of 7.a3, used to like} 11. e5 {until it was found that} dxe5 12. fxe5 Nd5 13. Ne4 Nb4 $1 {equalizes}) 11... e5 12. Nde2 Nbd7 ({Black's weapons include pressure on the c-file and against e4, the blocking maneuver of ...Nbd7-b6-c4, and the wrecking power of the sacrifice on c3. But taking the e-pawn this early is toxic} 12... Nxe4 $2 13. Nxe4 Bxe4 14. Ng3 Bb7 15. f6 $1 Bxf6 16. Nh5 Be7 17. Qg4 g6 18. Bh6) 13. Ng3 Rc8 {The exchange "sac" is in the air now, e.g. 14.Nh5? Nxh5 15.Qxh3 Rxc3! 16.bxc3 Nf6 and ...Nxe4 leaves White with a position only a computer could love.} 14. Be3 ({White must give up a bishop for knight. But Where? On b6, as in the game, on c4 (after ...Nb6-c4) or on f6? For example,} 14. Bg5 Nb6 15. Bxf6 ({At the 1959 Candidates Tournament, Olafsson tried to preserve his bishops and played} 15. Nh5 {But then instead of Fischer's unsound} Rxc3 $2 ({Black should have tried to equalize with} 15... Nc4 $1)) 15... Bxf6 16. Rf3 Nc4 17. Bxc4 Rxc4 18. Rd3) 14... Nb6 {White's 14th move was a novelty, although hardly a surprise, and Fischer thought 17 moves over this.} 15. Bxb6 ({Black has an easy game after} 15. Kh1 Nc4 16. Bxc4 Rxc4 {and ...Qa7. By eliminating the knight this way Whtie can secure control of d6 with Nh5xf6!}) 15... Qxb6+ 16. Kh1 Qe3 $1 {The queen hinders virtually all White plans here, and if driven back to h6 or g5, it will stop Nh5 and threaten ...Ng4. For example 17.Rf3 Qh6 18.Qe2 Rc5 19.Rd1 Rfc8 and ...a5 or 17.Nh5 Nxe4 18.Qg4 Qg5! and 18.Nd5 Bxd5 19.Bxd5 Nf6!. Robatsch took 45 minutes trying to find a solution.} 17. Nd5 Bxd5 $1 {Black preserves his best kingside defensive piece} 18. Bxd5 Bd8 ({this is more promising than} 18... Nxd5 19. exd5 Bh4 20. Rf3 (20. Nh5 f6 $1) 20... Qh6 21. Ne4 {when it's not clear how Black can improve his position. For example} Rc4 22. Qe2 Rfc8 23. c3 Be7 $2 24. b3 R4c7 $2 {loses to} 25. Rh3) 19. a4 ({White needs a new target since Black has too free a hand after} 19. c3) ({or} 19. Bb3 {and because}) (19. Bb7 Rc7 20. Bxa6 $2 Qb6 {costs a piece}) 19... Bb6 20. axb5 ({there is no bishop trap now after} 20. Bb7 {but} Rc4 {threatening Rxa4 or Rd4 is strong}) 20... axb5 21. Ra6 {White finally appears ready for Rf3} b4 $1 {But it turns out that Rf3 is met by Ng4! and Nf2+} 22. Nh5 ({White can batten down the hatches with} 22. Bb3 Bc5 23. Qf3 {but Black retains an edge after} Qd4 {. Robatsch's move was based on a trap 22. ...Nxh5 23.Qxh5 Rxc2 and now 24.Qxf7+ wins.}) 22... Nxd5 23. Qg4 ({Superior to} 23. Qxd5 Rxc2 {and 24. ... Rc1 or 23.Qxd6?? Qe2 - but not as scary as it appears.}) ({the problem with} 23. exd5 {is tha tit junks the theme of exploiting d5 but doesn't get enough tactical compensation. Black is better after} f6 24. Qg4 (24. Rf3 Qd4 25. Rd3 Qxb2 26. Rxb6 Rxc2 27. Rc6 $1 Rxg2 28. Rh3 Ra8) 24... Rf7 25. Qxb4 Qe2) 23... g6 24. exd5 Rxc2 25. fxg6 ({Now} 25. Nf6+ Kg7 26. Rxb6 {fails to} ({In fact, Black's king is safe now. For example} 26. Ne4 h5 $1 27. f6+ (27. Qh4 Rc1 28. Qf6+ $2 Kh7 29. Ra1 Rxa1 30. Rxa1 Rc8) 27... Kh6 $1) 26... Rc1 $1) 25... hxg6 26. Nf6+ Kg7 27. Nh5+ ({White must acqueise to a bad ending} 27. Qh4 Rf2 $1 ({There are many land mines in this position. After 27.Qh4 Black would draw after} 27... Rh8 $2 28. Ne8+ Rxe8 (28... Kf8 $4 29. Qf6 Rh7 30. Ra8) 29. Qf6+ Kh6 30. Qh4+ Kg7) 28. Qxf2 Qxf2 29. Rxf2 Bxf2 30. Rxd6 Ra8) 27... Kh6 $1 {Once again this square is remarkably safe.} 28. Nf6 Rf2 {This stops Qh4+ Kg7 30.Qh7#} 29. Raa1 Ra8 $1 {Role reversal, instead of an attacker stretching the defender's pieces beyond their limits, ehre the defender does the stretchng.} 30. Qxb4 Kg7 ({Bl;ack avoids the final pair of traps} 30... Rxf6 $2 31. Qh4+) (30... Rxa1 31. Ng4+ Kg5 $4 32. h4+ $1) 31. Qxd6 ({On} 31. Ng4 {Black has a choice between a won endging} Qf4 (31... Rxf1+ 32. Rxf1 Qd3 {since} 33. Qe1 f5 34. Nxe5 dxe5 35. Qxe5+ Kh6 36. Qf4+ Kh5 $1 {fizzles}) 32. Qxf4 Rxf4 33. h3 Rxf1+ 34. Rxf1 f5 {and a won middlegame}) 31... Qe2 32. Ne8+ Rxe8 33. Rfe1 Qg4 {Members of the Najdorf cult know the Postscript : After the game Robatsch telephoned New York to ask where he went wron. Fischer replied, "The whole variation is worthless."} 0-1

