{Even numbers figured prominently in Fischer's performance at the Capablanca Memorial : In the field of 22, Fischer began with two victories, including an elegant endgame win from Smyslov in the second round. His shortest game was a 22-move win over Tringov. In the even-number rounds Fischer suffered two losses. This game was played in the 16th round and on September 16th. And in the end, he finished second, in a tie with two other grandmasters.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 9. exd5 Nxd5 ({Fischer demolished Seidman in the 1960-61 US Championship in the once-wild} 9... e4 10. dxc6 exf3 {line. Play went} 11. Qxf3 Bg4 12. Qg3 Bd6 13. f4 g5 $2 14. d4 Kh8 15. Re5 $1 gxf4 16. Bxf4 Nh5 17. Rxh5 $1 Bxh5 18. Nd2 {with a big positional advantage}) 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 c6 12. d4 Bd6 13. Re1 Qh4 14. g3 Qh3 15. Be3 Bg4 ({"An interesting, scarcely explored possibility" is} 15... h5 {wrote Leonard Barden in a 1963 book on the Ruy Lopez. He cited the game Boleslavsky-Saigin, Minsk 1961 in which Black obtained excellent play after} 16. Qf3 h4 17. Nd2 ({But this new idea disappeared after one test in interenational chess, and it came in the sixth round at Havana. Fischer improved against Wade with} 17. Bxd5 $1 cxd5 18. Nd2 Be6 19. Bf4 {. Although they drew} Bg4 20. Qg2 Qxg2+ 21. Kxg2 h3+ 22. Kg1 Bxf4 {there was no GM interest in 15. ...h5 after that}) 17... Bg4 18. Qg2 hxg3 19. fxg3 Qh5) 16. Qd3 Nxe3 $5 17. Rxe3 c5 $1 {This was Geller's new positional treatment of the Marshall, an attempt to improve over the hyper-analyzed 16. ...Rae8 and 17. ... Re6} 18. Bd5 ({The delayed fianchetto is attractive but inferior to} 18. Qf1 $1 Qh6 19. Nd2 {and Nf3, which gave Fischer a winning edge (which he squandered) against Donner a year later at the second Piatgorsky Cup.}) 18... Rad8 19. Nd2 Bb8 $2 (19... Bc7 {was better because}) 20. Bg2 Qh6 21. d5 $1 {...then this would have been refuted by 21. ...c4 22.Qd4? Bb6. Fischer's move improves over} (21. Rae1 {which Ciocaltea used to draw with Geller in this tournament after} cxd4 22. cxd4 Ba7 23. Nb3 Be6 {and ...Bxb3. Theory hasn't advanced much since 1965 and Geller's innovation remains unclear after 19. ...Bc7 20.Bg2 Qh6 21.Nf3 Bb6. Play could peter out with a repetition of moves 22.Ne5 Be6 23.Nf3 Bg4 (but White can keep matters alive with 23.NC6 cxd4 24.cxd4 Rd6 25.Qe4!)}) 21... c4 ({This was sharply criticized but if White is allowed to play 22.c4 the game is strategically decided. On} 21... f5 22. c4 f4 23. Ree1 fxg3 24. hxg3 {the try} Rxf2 {turns out to be a chimera after} 25. Kxf2 Qh2 26. Re3) 22. Qd4 Bf5 ({if now} 22... f5 {allows} 23. Re6 {since} Qxe6 24. dxe6 Rxd4 25. cxd4 f4 26. d5 $1 {is a hopeless ending. But}) (22... Bh3 {to pressure d5 after ... Bxg2 and ...Qh5 was a better try.}) 23. b3 $1 Rc8 $2 (23... cxb3 24. axb3 {and 25.c4 only helps White. But Black overlooked the possible blockade of} Rd7 25. c4 bxc4 26. bxc4 Rc8 {and ...Bc5 For example} 27. c5 Ba7 28. Qc4 Rdc7 $1 (28... Bxc5 $2 29. Qxc5) 29. d6 $2 ({Better for White is} 29. Rxa6 Rxc5 30. Rxh6 Rxc4 31. Nxc4 gxh6 32. Rf3 Rxc4 33. Rxf5 {the bishops of opposite colors pose annoying technical problems.}) 29... Bxc5 $2 (29... Rxc5 $1 {turns the game around}) 30. dxc7 $1 Bxe3 31. Qd5 $1 {wins For White but}) 24. bxc4 Bd6 {Black threatens 25. ...Bc5 e..g. 25.Rf3 Bc5 (not 25. ...Bg4? 26.Qxg4 Qxd2 27.Rxf7!) 26.Qf4 Qxf4 27.Rxf4 Bd3 with untidy complications.} 25. Qb6 $1 Bf4 {otherwise White pockets another pawn} 26. Qxh6 Bxh6 27. f4 g5 ({This only looks strong when compared to} 27... bxc4 28. Bf1) 28. Re5 Bd3 {Fischer spent 16 minutes to decide on} 29. c5 $3 ({Black is left with at least the will to resist after} 29. fxg5 Bg7) (29. cxb5 axb5 30. d6 Rxc3) (29. Bh3 Rce8) 29... Rxc5 30. d6 Rxc3 ({Black's rook is attacked and} 30... Rxe5 31. fxe5 g4 {is lost after} 32. Nb3 Be3+ 33. Kh1 Bc4 34. Rd1) 31. d7 gxf4 32. Rae1 (32. Re8 Bg5 33. Rae1 fxg3 34. hxg3 Rc2 35. Nf3 Bf6 36. Rd1 $1 {and wins}) 32... Bg7 33. Re8 Bd4+ 34. Kh1 Bf6 (34... Bb6 35. gxf4 {and Rg1 as in the game}) 35. gxf4 $1 {Opening the file decides 35. ...Bf5 36.Rg1 and Bh3+} 1-0

