{The year 1966 ended with Fischer's last U.S. Championship. He had repeatedly called for the national championship to be doubled in length, remembering how a loss to Mednis in the 11 round 1962-63 tournament nearly ended his streak of first place finishes at four. The following was the last of his 90 Championship games.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. c3 {Bisguier's losing streak against Fischer had reached 11 games and he had begun adopting rare sidelines as White but still got the worst of it.} Nf6 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. Bc2 Bg4 6. d3 ({More accurate is} 6. h3 {since} Bh5 {would deny Black the opportunity to fianchetto}) 6... g6 7. Nbd2 ({Book,as of 1967, was} 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Bg7 9. Qe2 O-O 10. O-O b5 {with equality.}) 7... Bg7 8. h3 Bd7 $1 {Exchanging on f3 when White can recapture with a knight allows White to play d4 quickly and solve the problem of what to do with his queen's knight.} 9. O-O O-O 10. Nh2 $2 ({Now} 10. d4 {grants Black enough counterplay to equalize with} cxd4 11. cxd4 Nb4 12. Bb1 Bb5 13. Re1 Nd3) ({The best solution to the problem-knight was} 10. Re1 {and Nf1. The f2-f4 plan has no bite when Black's e-pawn in unmoved.}) 10... b5 11. f4 b4 $1 {Fischer thrived on this attack on the center's gase.} 12. Nc4 (12. Qf3 bxc3 13. bxc3 Ne8 $1 {and the threat of ...Bxc3 proves annoying} 14. Bb2 Nc7 {and ...Nb5}) 12... d5 $1 13. Ne5 $6 ({White didn't like thewhole created at d4 after} 13. exd5 Nxd5 14. Qf3 e6) ({or} 13. Ne3 bxc3 14. bxc3 d4 {. But the text does worse pawn damage.}) 13... bxc3 14. bxc3 dxe4 15. dxe4 ({Now} 15. Nxd7 Nxd7) ({or} 15. Nxc6 Bxc6 16. dxe4 Bxe4 {costs White a pawn.}) 15... Nxe5 16. fxe5 Ne8 17. Nf3 ({White seizes the initiative with} 17. Qd5 {, threatening Rd1 - but it ends in Black's advantage after} Nc7 18. Qxc5 Ne6 19. Qd5 Qc7) 17... Nc7 18. Rf2 Bb5 19. Bg5 ({Black's queen is at least as useful as White's after} 19. Rd2 Qe8 {and ...Qc6, or}) (19. Qe1 Ne6 {and ... Qc7. So Bisguier allows a trade.}) 19... Qxd1+ 20. Rxd1 Rfe8 21. Bb3 c4 $1 {On one hand, this surrenders d4 and ham strings the bishop, but on the other hand, Black solves the problem of defending the c-pawn, allows the knight to take up e6 and denies d5 to White's bishop.} 22. Bc2 Ne6 23. Be3 Reb8 24. Rb1 $1 ({Since Rbf1 means nothing now because of ...Be8, it's White turn to stop a threatened invasion (...a6, ...Be8, ...Rb2). In the post-mortem} 24. Nd4 {was suggested} Bd7 $2 (24... Bxe5 25. Nxe6 (25. Nxb5 Rxb5 26. Ba4 Ra5 27. Bc6 {and Bd5 but again his chances are not as good as in this game.}) 25... fxe6 26. Bd4 Bxd4 ({however} 26... Bc7 $1 {and ...Rf8 retains solid winning chances.}) 27. Rxd4 Rd8 28. Rfd2) 25. Nf5) 24... a6 25. Rff1 Be8 26. Kf2 Nd8 27. Rxb8 $2 ({Trading off all rooks can only succeed if all invasion routes for the black king are sealed off. White is betting that he can always guard squares such as b2 and e5, a tall order. Better was} 27. Nd2 $1 {and if} Bxe5 28. Nxc4 Rxb1 29. Rxb1 Bxc3 30. Nb6 Rb8 31. Nd5 {. The main point is} Rxb1 32. Bxb1 Bf6 {allows White to sharply reduce his losing chances after} 33. Nxf6+ ({or} 33. Bc5 Nc6 34. Nc7) 33... exf6 34. Bd4) 27... Rxb8 28. Rb1 Rb5 $1 29. Rxb5 axb5 30. Ke2 h6 31. Kd2 g5 $1 {White should have prevented this expansion with 31. h4. Now 32.g4 Nc6 33.Bd4 leaves Black with a variety of winning ideas including 33. ...e6 and ...Nxd4/...Bf8} 32. h4 g4 33. Nd4 e6 34. Bf4 $5 ({White may have rejected} 34. h5 Bxe5 35. Bxh6 {because of} Bxd4 36. cxd4 f6 {which dooms the h-pawn. But} 37. Kc3 Bxh5 38. a4 {should draw}) 34... h5 $1 35. Bg5 Nb7 36. Bf6 $2 Bh6+ ({This would create an impregnable fortress after} 36... Bxf6 37. exf6 {and e4-e5, Ke3-f4. FOr example} Nc5 (37... e5 38. Nf5 Kh7 39. Ng7 $1 Bd7 40. Nxh5) 38. e5 Nd7 39. Nxb5 ({White can also try} 39. a4 {since} bxa4 $2 40. Bxa4 Nxf6 41. Bxe8 Nxe8 42. Ne2 {followed by Ke3-d4 or Nf4}) 39... Nxe5 40. Nd6 Bc6 41. g3 {and 42.a4}) 37. Bg5 ({White believes all four doubled pawns are safe if his king reaches d4. But a better practical try is} 37. Ke2 {, forcing Black to choose between ideas suck as} Nc5 {and ...Nd7 He also has tricks. For example,} (37... Bf4 {followed by ...Bg3, and a king raid via h7-g66.}) 38. g3 ({Also lost is} 38. Kf2 Bd2 39. Ne2 Bc6) 38... Nd7 39. Nxb5 Nxf6 40. Nc7 {seems to draw. But Black wins a pawn with} (40. Nd6 Bf8 $1) 40... Ba4 $1 41. Bxa4 Nxe4) 37... Bxg5+ 38. hxg5 Kg7 39. Ke3 Kg6 40. Kf4 Nc5 41. g3 ({White should pass} 41. Bb1 Na4 42. Ne2 Nb2 43. Nc1 Nd1 44. Ne2 {The dearth of tempi comes back to haunt him at move 43.}) 41... Bd7 ({Black might win after} 41... Nd3+ 42. Bxd3 cxd3 43. Nb3 Bc6 44. Nd2 Bb7 45. Nb3 h4 $5 {but the text is a higher percentage play.}) 42. a3 Be8 $1 {White's king is requried to defend g5 while his bishop must watch both a4 and d3. That leaves only the knight to move but 43.Ne3 Nd3+! 44.Bxd3? cxd3 loses immediately. Something has to give.} 43. Bb1 Na4 44. Ne2 Nb2 {again with the idea of Nd3+} 45. Nd4 Nd1 $1 46. Ne2 Nf2 $1 47. Ke3 Nh3 {A remarkable route for the knight that was on d7 at move 39.} 48. Nf4+ Kxg5 49. Ng2 ({Quite lost is} 49. Nxh3+ gxh3 50. Kf2 h4) 49... f6 50. exf6 Kxf6 51. Nh4 ({White's king is shut out by Black next move, but} 51. Kd4 e5+ 52. Kd5 {would lose to} Ng1 {and ...Ne2}) 51... e5 $1 52. Bc2 Bd7 53. Bb1 Ng5 {The knight begins another tour headed for g6} 54. Bc2 Nf7 55. Bb1 Nh8 $1 56. Bc2 Ng6 {White must allow the h-pawn to advance 57.Ng2 Kg5 and ...h4} 57. Nxg6 Kxg6 58. Kf2 Kg5 59. Kg2 h4 60. Kh2 h3 $1 {The g-pawn is hopelessly blockaded after} (60... hxg3+ $4 61. Kxg3) 61. Kg1 Kf6 62. Kh2 Ke7 {With the enemy king confined to the vicinity of h1, all Black needs to do is win king access to a4.} 63. Kg1 Kd6 64. Kf2 Kc5 65. Kg1 Kb6 66. Kh1 Ka5 67. Kg1 Bc6 68. Kh2 $1 Bb7 $1 ({on the immediate} 68... Bxe4 {White also queens} 69. Bxe4 Ka4 70. Bf5 Kb3 $1 71. Bxg4 e4 72. Bxh3 Kxc3 73. g4 Kd2 74. g5 c3 75. g6 {Note that 68.Kh1? would have lost to 68. ...Bxe4! when Black promotes with check.}) 69. Kg1 ({ot} 69. Bd1 Bxe4 70. Bxg4 Ka4 71. Bd7 $1 Bg2 $1 72. g4 e4 73. g5 e3 74. Bg4 Kxa3) 69... Bxe4 $1 70. Bxe4 Ka4 71. Bf5 (71. Bc6 Kxa3 72. Bxb5 Kb3 73. Bd7 Kxc3 74. Bxg4 Kd3 $1 75. Bxh3 (75. Bd1 e4 76. g4 Kd2 $1) 75... c3 76. Bf5+ e4 {and ...c2}) 71... Kb3 $3 {Black needs a tempo, not the a-pawn, to win} 72. Bxg4 e4 73. Bxh3 (73. Bf5 e3 74. Kf1 h2) 73... Kxc3 74. g4 Kd2 ({the point of Black's 68th move is repeated - he queens with check. ENdgame connoisseur Walter Korn compared Black's play to a study but he was was overstating the case when he claimed} 74... e3 75. Bf1 Kd2 ({Black still wins after} 75... Kb2 76. g5 c3 77. g6 e2 $1 {for example}) 76. g5 e2 77. Bxe2 Kxe2 78. g6 c3 {would draw}) 0-1

