{Fischer's next chess was in the Philippines, where an abortive international tournament was replaced by a series of clocked games, sponsored by the Manila electric company under the theme "Beat Bobby Fischer". The following game was virtually unknown until it appeared, with seven others from the series, in the British Chess Magazine in 1980. The real battle only lasts from move 7 to 14, but the exact nature of Fischer's moves, particularly his 10th and 11th, is impressive.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nge2 {Fischer began to test this move order in 1967 and later returned to it in his 1992 match with Spassky.} e5 {The principled reply, as the Russians would say. It was unfairly criticized at the time for surrendering d5.} 4. Nd5 Nf6 5. Nec3 {The point. White will develop his bishop on c4.} Be7 6. Bc4 O-O 7. d3 h6 $2 {Black wanted to avoid Nxe7+ and 9.Bg5 but this puts his king on the endangered species list} ({After} 7... d6 {we have a position similar to Anand-Ivanchuk, FIDE World Championship 2001 (1.e4 c5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Nc3 e5 4.Bc4 d6 5.d3 Be7 6.Nbd2 Nf6 7. Nf1 and Ne3/Nd5). Ivanchuk equalized by trading off the d5 knight and then swapping a pair of bishops with ..Bg5.}) ({Here Black should pursue the same policy with} 7... Nxd5 8. Nxd5 Bg5) 8. f4 $1 ({Geller gave} 8. O-O {in ECO but White shouldn't abandon the prospect of a kingside pawn storm so early.}) 8... d6 $2 {Black must exchange on f4.} 9. f5 $1 {This strategically decides the game. WHite stops ...Be6 and sets the stage for the advance of the g-pawn.} b6 ({It is too later for} 9... Nxd5 10. Nxd5 Bg5 {because} 11. Qh5 $1 Bxc1 12. Rxc1 {threatens f6 with a quick mate.} Qg5 {fals to} 13. Qxg5 hxg5 14. h4 $1 {since} gxh4 15. Rxh4 {leaves Black in a quandary over how to stop Kf2 and Rch1.}) ({But Black should at least eliminate the bishop with} 9... Na5) 10. h4 $1 {A striking move which prepares g4-g5, with or without Nxe7 and Qh5.} Bb7 ({The key variation is} 10... Nxd5 11. Bxd5 $1 Bxh4+ 12. g3 $1 Bxg3+ 13. Kf1 {a la a King's Gambit, gives an overwhelming attack. For example} Bd7 14. Qg4 Bf4 {and now a bit faster than} 15. Bxf4 (15. Rxh6 $1 {threatening 16.Bxf4 exf4 17. Qg6 and mates Black loses the queen after} Bxh6 16. Bxh6 Qf6 17. Bg5) 15... exf4 16. Rxh6 (16. Bxc6 Bxc6 17. Rxh6 Qd7) 16... Ne5) 11. a3 $1 ({Another exact move, preserving the bishop against 11. ...Na5 and stopping ...Nb4. The significance of this is shown by} 11. Nxf6+ Bxf6 12. Qh5 $2 {when Black has} Nb4 $1 {with good chances e.g.} 13. Bb3 d5 14. g4 c4 $1) ({Black would also have counterplay after} 11. Nxe7+ Nxe7 12. g4 d5 $1 13. exd5 (13. g5 $1 dxc4 14. gxf6 Nxf5 $1) 13... Nfxd5) 11... Rc8 12. Nxf6+ $1 Bxf6 13. Qh5 Ne7 {Black had to do something about 14.g4 and 15.g4, and this enables him to shoot back with 14.g4 d5! Play could continue 15.g5 g6! 16.fxg6 not 16.Qxg7? Bxg7 winning the queen - fxg6 17.Qg4 dxc4 18.Qe6+ Kh7 19.gxf6 Nc6 and Black is still alive. Whitecan also try 18.gxf6 Rxf6 19.Bxh6 and 20.O-O-O but the method Fischer chose was superior.} 14. Bg5 $1 {Another terrific move, threatening Bxf6} d5 (14... hxg5 15. hxg5 {is winning because of mate on the h-file.}) (14... Nc6 15. Bxf6 Qxf6 16. Nd5 Qd8 17. f6) 15. Bxf6 dxc4 ({Black cannot play} 15... gxf6 {because of mate after} 16. Qxh6 {and Rh3}) 16. Qg4 g6 17. dxc4 $1 ({There must have been a huge temptation to finish the game off in style} 17. Qg5 hxg5 (17... Nc6 18. Nd5 $1) (17... Kh7 18. h5 $3 {also wins}) (17... Nxf5 {bursts the bubble.} 18. Bxd8 (18. exf5 hxg5 19. hxg5 Qxf6) 18... hxg5 19. Bxg5 Nd4 {is a roughly equal ending}) 18. hxg5 {and Rh8 mate.}) 17... Qd6 ({This acknowledges defeat, but} 17... Kh7 {would have breathed life into} 18. Qg5 $3 Re8 (18... Rc6 19. h5 $1 Nxf5 20. hxg6+ Kg8 21. Nd5 $1) (18... Ng8 19. Bxd8 hxg5 20. hxg5+ Kg7 21. f6+ Nxf6 22. Bxf6+ Kg8 23. Rh8#) 19. h5 hxg5 20. hxg6+ Kg8 21. Rh8# {. Black's last move was designed to meet 18.Qg5 with 18. ...hxg5 19.hxg5 Qxf6!. So Fischer decides to cash in.}) 18. Bxe7 $1 Qxe7 19. fxg6 fxg6 20. Qxg6+ Qg7 21. Qxg7+ Kxg7 22. Rd1 Rcd8 23. Rxd8 Rxd8 24. Nd5 b5 25. cxb5 Bxd5 26. exd5 c4 27. a4 Rxd5 28. Ke2 Rd4 29. Rd1 $1 Re4+ 30. Kf3 Rf4+ 31. Ke3 c3 32. b3 1-0

