{By early 1968 Fischer had left New York for southern California and his post-Sousse retreat from chess extended to the summer. A proposed match with the teenage grandmaster Enrique MEcking collapsed when Mecking's father said it would take too much time away from his schooling - an excuse Fischer must have wondered at. Fischer agreed to play in a round-robin commemorating the 20th anniversary of the founding of Israel, another irony considering his evolving beliefs. His most impressive victory at the seaside resort of Netyana was a stunning illustration of the use of superior space.} 1. e4 c6 2. Nc3 d5 3. Nf3 Bg4 4. h3 Bxf3 5. Qxf3 Nf6 6. d3 e6 7. a3 Nbd7 ({Black's move was considered a significant improvement over} 7... Be7 8. g4 Nfd7 9. d4 {which had turned out badly for Botwinnik in a 1958 world championship match game. Much later it was realized that the culprit was Botwinnik's} Nf8 $2 ({and that Black would stand quite well after} 9... Qb6 $1 {e.g.} 10. Qd3 e5 $1 11. exd5 exd4)) 8. g4 $1 ({Much better than the book} 8. g3 g6 9. Bg2 Bg7 10. O-O O-O 11. Qe2 Ne8 {and 12. ...f5. White's move exploits the Black knight's lack of a good retreat, thanks to 7. ...Nbd7. For example, on 8.... g6 9.h4 h5 10.g5 Ng4 White assures himself an edge with 11.Bh3 or 11.Qg3}) 8... Bd6 9. g5 Ng8 ({Black may have been dissuaded from} 9... Ne5 {by} 10. Qe2 Nfd7 11. f4 {for example} Ng6 12. exd5 cxd5 13. f5 Nge5 14. fxe6 fxe6 15. d4) 10. h4 Ne7 11. h5 {White, it seems, is expanding on the kingside purely in the hopes that Black will castle there} Qb6 12. Bh3 $1 O-O-O 13. a4 $1 ({now what? On} 13. Qxf7 Rhf8 {Black wins}) 13... a5 ({Black's move stopped 14.a5 but ultimately allows the opening of the queenside by b2-b48a5 that costs Black the game. However, the real blame for his defeat lies elsewhere. So far, his shield of center pawns allows him to ignore the wings. But at some point the middlegame begins and Black must drop the shield to obtain squares for his pieces. This is difficult to time accurately because, for example} 13... d4 14. Ne2 Ne5 {will favor White after} 15. Qg2 Bb4+ 16. Kd1 $1 c5 17. f4) 14. O-O $1 Rhf8 15. Kh1 f5 ({again} 15... d4 {looks promising but} 16. Ne2 (16. Nb1 $1 {and Na3-c4 favors White}) 16... Qc5 17. c3 Ne5 18. Qg3) 16. Qg2 $1 g6 17. h6 $1 {Fischer's thinking becomes more transparent. By seizing control of two thirds of the kingside, he hopes to reduce Black's room for maneuver to a minimum, after which White can open the queenside at leisure.} Kb8 18. f4 Rfe8 $2 ({now was the time for} 18... dxe4 19. dxe4 e5 (19... Nc5) (19... Bb4 20. Na2 Bc5 {and ...fxe4, ...Nf5})) 19. e5 $1 Bc5 20. Qf3 {Even though his QB is "bad", White stops ...Be3 because Black's piece congestion is becoming suffocating.} Nc8 21. Bg2 Kc7 {Hoping to escape to the kingside and/or play ... Nb8-a6-b4.} 22. Ne2 Nb8 23. c3 Kd7 ({On} 23... Qb3 {White has} 24. Be3 $1 {and Nd4. For example} Bxe3 25. Qxe3 Qxb2 $4 26. Rfb1 Qc2 27. Nd4 Qxc3 28. Ra2 $1 {and Rb3 traps the queen}) 24. Bd2 $1 {White prepares a crushing b2-b4.} Na6 ({there is a rare mating pattern after} 24... Qxb2 $4 25. Rab1 Qxd2 26. Rxb7#) ({Flight to the kingside is grounded by} 24... Ke7 25. d4 Qxb2 26. Qd3 Bb6 27. Rfb1) 25. Rfb1 Bf8 ({The king can't go back} 25... Kc7 26. b4 axb4 27. d4 Bf8 28. cxb4 {and a5}) 26. b4 $1 axb4 27. cxb4 Bxb4 ({If Black spurns the b-pawn it becomes a battering ram} 27... Ke7 28. b5 Nc5 29. Be3 {followed by Rc1, Qf2 or a4-a5}) (27... Nxb4 {also loses} 28. a5 Qb5 29. Bxb4 Bxb4 30. Nd4 Qc5 31. Ra4 $1 {for example}) 28. a5 Qc5 29. d4 Qf8 30. Bxb4 Nxb4 31. Qc3 Na6 32. Rxb7+ Nc7 33. Nc1 {The knight heads to c5 or b4 for a knockout.} Re7 34. a6 1-0

