{Fischer often compared chess with basketball. In both games you have to maneuver around your main target. "Sometimes you have to spin near the basket in order to get a favorable position to shoot," he told Dragoljub Ciric, as Ciric recalled in his paper NIN. But this takes patience such as in the following. Clearly d4 is the basket but Black must spin a lot before he can shoot.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 b6 ({A remarkable choce considering Fischer never played the Normal} 2... e6 3. Nf3 b6 {Queen's Indian. What, one wonders, did he have in mind against 2. ...b6 3.f3?}) 3. Nc3 Bb7 4. f3 d5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. Nxd5 ({More natural is} 6. e4 Nxc3 7. bxc3 g6 {as in a Grünfeld.} ({Black can also treat the position more sharply with} 7... e5 8. dxe5 Qh4+)) 6... Qxd5 7. e4 Qd7 8. Bc4 g6 ({More enterprising than} 8... e6) 9. Qb3 $2 ({White doesn't have the freedom to pursue exotic ideas such as} 9. h4 {because Black quickly pounds d4 with ..Bg7, ...Nc6 and ...Rd8. But the text misplaces the queen and}) (9. Ne2 {and 10.Be3 was preferable.}) 9... e6 10. Ne2 Bg7 11. Be3 Nc6 12. Rd1 ({White threatens 13.d5 Na5? 14.dxe6 but} 12. Qd3 {would have stayed in the middlegame.}) 12... Na5 13. Qc2 Qc6 $5 ({A rare case where Fischer passed up the two bishops} 13... Nxc4 14. Qxc4 O-O 15. O-O Rad8 16. Nf4 Bc6 {and ...Bb5. Instead, he forces a queenless middlegame in which his knight becomes the most versatile piece on the board.}) 14. Bd3 Qxc2 15. Bxc2 O-O-O 16. Kf2 Rd6 $1 ({Even though this gives White tempo-gainers such as Bf4 and e4-e5 this is better} 16... Rd7 {because the rook may be needed for ...Rc6-c2}) 17. b3 ({necessary in view of ...Nc4, e.g.} 17. Rd3 Ba6 18. Rd2 Nc4) 17... Nc6 18. Rd2 Rhd8 19. Rhd1 Nb4 $1 ({Both sides have commited maximum force to d4 but Black, who can favorably change the pawn structure with ...f5, has much greater winning chances. He needs to spin a bit since} 19... f5 20. e5 R6d7 21. Nf4 {is not convincing.}) 20. Bb1 Ba6 21. a3 $2 ({White wants to trade his passive bishop for an active one but this weakening of b3 comes back to haunt him. True his options are limited} 21. Nc3 $2 c5 22. e5 cxd4 $1 23. exd6 dxc3 24. Rc1 Nd5) ({. But there was no immediate threat, since 21. ...Bxe2 22.Kxe2 Nc6 is ably handled by 23.e5 and 24.Be4. Moreover, White had a good pass in} 21. Bg5 {e.g.} R8d7 22. e5 Rd5 23. Nc3 Ra5 24. Ne4) 21... Nc6 22. Bd3 Bxd3 23. Rxd3 f5 {Now is the time for the break. Black doesn't threaten anything yet (24. ..fxe4 25. fxe4 Ne5 e.g. 26.Rc3 Ng4+ 27.Kf3 h5! 28.Rdc1 R6d7 29.Bf4) but he may in a move or two.} 24. R3d2 Na5 {On 25.Nc1 Black justifies his 16th move with 25. .. .Rc6, threatening 26. ...Rc3 with mounting pressure after 26.Rd3 Rc2+ 27.Kf1 fxe4 28.fxe4 Nc6.} 25. e5 $2 {Another serious concession that grants White only a few tactical chances, such as the pin after 25. ...Rc6?! 26.b4 Nc4 27. Rc1} R6d7 26. Nc1 (26. Rd3 Bxe5) (26. b4 Nc4 27. Rd3 Nb2) 26... Bf8 $1 27. b4 ({The center would also collapse after} 27. Ra2 c5 $1 28. Bg5 Rxd4) ({But White wrongly rejected} 27. a4 {apparently believing} Ba3 28. Ne2 Nxb3 {would iwin, but overlooking} 29. Rd3 {. Instead Black can increase his edge with 27. ... Be7 and 28. ..g5}) 27... Nc4 28. Ra2 ({Black can beat} 28. Rc2 {with} Nxa3 (28... Nxe5) 29. Rc6 f4) 28... Nxe5 29. Rc2 b5 $1 30. Ne2 Nc4 31. Rc3 $2 ({This allows a much quicker finish than} 31. Ra1) 31... e5 {This threatens, among other things, 32. ...c5!} 32. f4 exd4 33. Rxd4 (33. Bxd4 c5 (33... Bg7 34. Rcd3 Nxa3)) 33... Nxe3 34. Kxe3 (34. Rxd7 Ng4+ {costs a piece}) 34... Rxd4 35. Nxd4 Rxd4 {The pawn endgame (36.Kxd4 Bg7+) is hopeless.} 0-1

