{"There are no longer any surprises, any departures from book, and 19th century gambits," Fischer said in 1971. "Whatever position you've got, it's been played before." Whether he believed this was doubtful. In fact, Fischer's reuslts at Palma, good and bad, were influenced by surprise. He lost to Larsen with the Velimirovic Attack, he drew against Polugayevsky with 1.c4. As Black, Fischer caught Uhlmann in a known Benoni trap and scored 21/2-1/2 with the Alekhine defense. The following game was one of his seven straight wins to finish the tournament. It brought his record from four interzonals to 41 wins, 31 draws and only 3 losses - a 75.3 percent score} 1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. exd6 cxd6 6. Be3 g6 {Fischer had played 5. ..cxd6 and 6. ...g6 six rounds before against Minic. The night before this game Suttles worked out a new idea based on trading off bishops via d4.} 7. d5 $6 Bg7 8. Bd4 Bxd4 9. Qxd4 O-O 10. Nc3 e5 $1 11. Qd2 ({Black transforms his e-pawn from a liability to an asset. The tactical basis is} 11. dxe6 Nc6 $1 12. exf7+ (12. Qd2 Bxe6 {, with good play in either case.}) 12... Rxf7 {and 13. ...Re7+}) 11... f5 12. Nf3 ({Wade recommended} 12. h4 N8d7 13. h5 g5 14. h6 {in the tournament book perhaps with the idea of} Qf6 15. O-O-O Nc5 16. Nf3 g4 17. Qg5+ $1 {with an uncertain endgame. But White misjudges his middlegame chances.}) 12... N8d7 13. O-O-O ({White has little play after} 13. Be2 Qf6 14. O-O a5 {and ...Nc5. The adventurous alternative is}) ({the adventurous alternative is} 13. Qh6 Qe7 14. Ng5 {after which} e4 $1 {temps White to overextend himelf} 15. Ne6 Rf6 16. Nb5 Ne5 17. Nbc7 Rb8 18. c5 $1) 13... Qf6 14. Qh6 Qe7 15. Re1 e4 $1 {Both 16.Nd4 Ne5 17.f3 Nexc4 and 17.b3 a5 are unpleasant.} 16. Nd2 Ne5 17. h3 $2 ({Evans called this game typical "of how Fischer's opponents seem to fall into a hypnotic trance." Th best White could get was double-edged positions such as} 17. f3 Bd7 18. Qe3 $1 (18. fxe4 f4 $1) 18... Rac8 19. fxe4 f4 20. Qd4 Qg7 21. b3 Bg4 22. Nf3) 17... Nbd7 $1 ({SUttles had counted on} 17... Qc7 18. f3 $1 Nbxc4 19. Nxc4 Nxc4 20. Bxc4 Qxc4 21. g4 {leads to promising chances e.g.} b5 22. gxf5 Bxf5 23. fxe4 b4 24. exf5 bxc3 25. b3 {. After the text, White has to worry about ..>Nc5-d3 e.g. 18.g4 Nc5 19. Kb1 fxg4 and ...Bf5}) 18. Qe3 Qh4 {The threat is 19. ...Ng4 e.g. 19.f4 Ng4 20. gxh4 Qxh1 21.Nb5 Nf6!} 19. g3 Qf6 20. Kb1 Nc5 21. f4 exf3 22. Nxf3 f4 $1 {Well timed. Black gets control of the two open files and the b1-f5 diagonal.} 23. gxf4 Nxf3 24. Qxf3 Qh4 $1 {Fischer handled the next stage with precision. By attacking the rook he makes Rxf4 stronger (25.Bg2 Rxf4 26.Qe3 Bf5+ 27.Ka1 Rxc4)} 25. Be2 Bf5+ 26. Ka1 Rae8 27. Rc1 (27. Ref1 Ne4 $1 {Black has the upper hand.}) (27. Rd1 Be4 28. Nxe4 Nxe4 29. Rd4 {held out greater hope for White} Rxf4 30. Qe3 Qf2 31. Qxf2 Rxf2 32. Bg4) 27... Be4 $1 28. Nxe4 Rxe4 29. Rh2 Rfxf4 30. Qc3 Qe7 31. Bf1 Re3 32. Qd2 Ref3 $1 33. Re2 (33. Be2 Rf2 34. Rxf2 Rxf2 35. Re1 Qe5 {and White cannot last long.}) 33... Qf6 34. Bg2 Rf2 ({the prettier win begins with} 34... Rg3 {threatening ...Nd3. For example} 35. Rb1 (35. Qe1 Nd3 36. Qxg3 Nxc1 37. Re6 (37. Re4 Rf2 {loses quickly}) 37... Qd4 38. Qe3 Qxe3) ({The crucial line is} 35. Rce1 Nd3 36. Re8+ (36. Re6 Rxg2) 36... Kf7 37. R1e7+ Qxe7 38. Rxe7+ Kxe7 39. a3 Rf2 ({But Agar pointed out} 39... Re4 $1 {wins} 40. Bxe4 (40. Ka2 Re1 41. b4 Nc1+ 42. Kb1 Rge3 $1) 40... Rg1+ 41. Ka2 Nc1+) 40. Qc3 Re3 {and Black escapes perpetual check.}) 35... Ra3 $1 {and ... Nb3+ or}) 35. Rce1 Rxe2 36. Rxe2 (36. Qxe2 Rf2 37. Qe8+ Kg7 {and threates of mate and Rxg2 lead to} 38. Qe7+ Qxe7 39. Rxe7+ Kf6 {and wins}) 36... Rxc4 {Now there is no relief from 37.Re8+ Kf7 38.Re1 Rf4} 37. Qe3 Qe5 $1 {The rest is predictable} 38. Kb1 Qxe3 39. Rxe3 Rf4 40. Bf3 h5 41. Kc2 Kf7 42. Kd2 Rb4 43. Kc3 Rh4 44. b4 Nd7 45. Be2 Nf6 46. Rf3 Kg7 47. Rd3 g5 48. a3 g4 49. Bf1 Ne4+ 50. Kc2 Nf2 51. Re3 gxh3 52. Re7+ Kf8 0-1

