{[%mdl 4096]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 Nxd5 (4... exd5) 5. Nf3 (5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3 c5 7. Nf3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ $11 {Fischer}) 5... c5 {Garry Kasparov: 'The Semi-Tarrasch Defence would not have been a surprise for the opponent: Fischer had employed it before, and also against Reshevsky. Methodical play against the isolated d4-pawn was to his taste: here you don't have to agonize over which piece to place where.'} 6. e3 ({In his matches with Petrosian and Fischer, Spassky played} 6. e4 {– Volume 3, Game No.76.}) 6... Nc6 7. Bd3 (7. Bc4 {Botvinnik,M; Byrne,R Garry Kasparov: '– Game No.32.'} cxd4 8. exd4 Be7 9. O-O O-O 10. Re1 a6 $11 (10... b6 11. Nxd5 exd5 12. Bb5 $16 {Botvinnik,M-Alekhine,A Amsterdam AVRO 1938})) 7... Be7 (7... cxd4 8. exd4 g6 9. h4 $5 (9. O-O Bg7 10. Be4) 9... Bg7 (9... h6 {}) 10. h5 Ndb4 11. Bg5 Nxd3+ 12. Qxd3 Qa5 13. Kf1 h6 $2 14. hxg6 hxg5 15. Rxh8+ Bxh8 16. gxf7+ Kxf7 17. Qh7+ Bg7 18. d5 $18 {Balcerkovsky-Dunkelblum Varna ol 1962}) 8. O-O O-O ({After} 8... cxd4 9. exd4 O-O {White can play} 10. Re1 $1 {(Volume 2, Game Nos.117 and 120).}) 9. a3 ({Black need not fear} 9. Nxd5 Qxd5 10. e4 {in view of} Qh5 (10... Qd8 $6 {is less good:} 11. dxc5 Bxc5 12. e5 $1 {Kasparov-Begun, Minsk 1978}) 11. dxc5 Bxc5 12. Bf4 {(Keres-Tal, Zürich 1959)} Rd8 $1 {with rapid equality.}) 9... cxd4 10. exd4 $11 Nf6 (10... Bd7 11. Qc2 g6 12. Bh6 Re8 13. Ne4 Rc8 14. Qe2 f5 15. Nc3 Bf6 {Byrne,R-Bisguier,A ch-USA 1963}) (10... b6 $5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. Qc2 Bb7 $1 13. Bxh7+ Kh8 14. Be4 Nxd4 15. Bxd5 Nxc2 16. Bxb7 Nxa1 17. Bxa8 Rxa8 18. Bg5 f6 $11 {Fischer}) ({Years later, after numerous investigations, the main theoretical continuation became} 10... Bf6 {(Volume 2, Game No.119), and the position after 10...Nf6 was deemed rather dangerous for Black.}) 11. Bc2 (11. Be3 { e2, ad1 Fischer} b6 12. Rc1 ({Fischer claims} 12. Qe2 {and Rad1 is more flexible}) 12... Bb7 13. Qe2 Ng4 $5 {(chasing the bishop: Bisguier-Fischer, Bled 1961)}) ({or} 11. Bg5 $5 b6 12. Qe2 Bb7 13. Rad1 {(Korchnoi-Tal, 8th matchgame, Moscow 1968).}) 11... b6 12. Qd3 Bb7 13. Bg5 ({Or} 13. Bf4 Rc8 14. Rfe1 g6 {with equality (Averbakh-Kholmov, 29th USSR Championship, Baku 1961).}) (13. Re1 {is more cunning} Z0 ({, in order after} 13... g6 {to immediately play} 14. Bh6 (14. h4 $5 {Dvoretsky}) 14... Re8 15. Rad1 {with the initiative}) ({, while if} 13... Rc8 $2 {, as occurred in the well-known game Smyslov-Karpov, then} 14. d5 $1 {(cf. Volume 2, Game No.117).})) 13... g6 14. Rfe1 ({After} 14. Rad1 Nd5 15. Bh6 Re8 16. Rfe1 {both} Rc8 ({and} 16... Nxc3 {are good: Larsen-Najdorf, Palma de Mallorca 1969.})) 14... Re8 15. h4 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: '"Euwe criticized this 'aggressive pass' and Barden extolled it. I don't see how else White can make headway. He has to create some kingside threats before Black consolidates and piles up on his d-pawn."  (Fischer)'} Rc8 ({Black could have considered} 15... Qd6 {(however, before the 1970s this was not played in such positions)} 16. Nb5 ({or} 16. Rad1 Rad8) 16... Qb8 17. Rad1 a6 18. Nc3 Qc7 19. Bb3 Rad8 {, trying to neutralize the threat of the d4-d5 breakthrough.}) 16. Rac1 (16. Rad1 $5 {Kavalek-Larsen, 6th matchgame, Solingen 1970.}) 16... Nd5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'A standard relieving manoeuvre, but here it loses strength, since the g5-bishop is defended by the h4-pawn.'} (16... Qd6 {was better.}) 17. Ne4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({White is not satisfied with the tiny edge after} 17. Nxd5 Qxd5 ({if} 17... Bxg5 18. hxg5 Qxd5 {, then} 19. Qd2 {and Be4 is good}) 18. Bb3 Qd7 19. d5 Bf8 20. Red1 {(Dvoretsky)} ({or} 20. h5 $5 {.})) {Garry Kasparov: 'Bobby, by his own admission, realized that this was an "ugly positional blunder", but he was counting only on'} 17... f5 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'On encountering problems, Fischer begins sharpening the position: how else could he confuse an opponent who was twice his age and liked to think, as long as there was time?'} ({I should add that after the "more solid"} 17... Qc7 {(recommended by Hübner),} 18. Bb3 $1 Red8 19. Qd2 {would have been highly unpleasant, and therefore the sharpening of the play is the best practical chance.}) 18. Nc3 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Bxg5 19. Nxg5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (19. hxg5 {Garry Kasparov: '(cf. the following comment).'} Nxc3 (19... Z0) ({. Dvoretsky also examines the sharp move} 19... Nf4 {, which was tested in a training game Potkin-Motylev (Dagomys 2004):} 20. Qe3 e5 $1 ({not immediately} 20... Nxg2 $2 21. Kxg2 Nxd4 {on account of} 22. Be4 $1 Nxf3 23. Bxf3 f4 24. Qe2 Qxg5+ 25. Kf1) 21. dxe5 Nxg2 $1 22. Kxg2 Nxe5 23. Ba4 $1 (23. Red1 Bxf3+ 24. Qxf3 Nxf3 25. Rxd8 Nh4+ 26. Kg3 Rexd8 $1 {with sufficient counterplay}) 23... Rc4 $3 24. Bb3 ({if} 24. Rcd1 {, then} Bxf3+ 25. Qxf3 Qxg5+ 26. Qg3 Rg4 27. Bxe8 f4 {is equal}) 24... Kg7 25. Bxc4 Nxf3 26. Red1 Nd4+ ({it is simpler to play the line} 26... Nd2+ $1 27. Ne4 Rxe4 28. Qc3+ Rd4+ 29. Kh3 Bg2+ $1 30. Kh2 Nf3+ $1 31. Kxg2 Qxg5+ {with a perpetual check}) 27. Kf1 Rxe3 (27... Bg2+ $1 28. Kxg2 Rxe3 29. fxe3 Qxg5+ 30. Kf1 Qxe3 {with equality}) 28. fxe3 Qxg5 29. Rxd4 Qxe3 $6 (29... Qg2+ 30. Ke1 Qxb2 {with equality}) 30. Ne2 {, and the two rooks and knight proved stronger than the queen and three pawns.}) 20. bxc3 (20. Qxc3 $2 Ne5) 20... Na5 $1 $17 {[%cal Yb7f3,Rd8g5]  xf3, xg5 Fischer} 21. Ba4 Re7 (21... Bxf3 $6 22. Qxf3 Re7 23. Bb5 $1) 22. Ne5 {is parried by} Rec7 {(Dvoretsky)} 23. Qe3 Qd5 $1 {with an excellent game.}) 19... Nf4 20. Qe3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'An important moment.'} (20. Qg3 $2 Nh5 21. Qe3 (21. Z0) ({. White can also consider} 21. Qf3 {, when Black must play} Ba8 $1 ({since} 21... Qxd4 $6 22. Bb3 $1) ({, or} 21... Ng7 22. Rcd1 h5 23. d5 $1 Na5 24. Nxe6 Nxe6 25. Ba4 Qxh4 26. Bxe8 Rxe8 27. Qh3 $1 {is dangerous for him}) 22. Ba4 $1 Nxd4 23. Qe3 b5 {with equality.}) 21... Nxd4 {Fischer} 22. Ba4 $1 {White regains the pawn and easily maintains the balance:} Z0 (22... Bc6 $6 23. Rcd1) (22... f4 23. Qh3 Bc6 (23... b5 24. Nxe6 $1) 24. Nxe6 Nxe6 (24... Rxe6 $2 25. Rxe6 Bxa4 26. Rd6 $1) 25. Bb3) ({, or} 22... b5 23. Rcd1 $1 bxa4 24. Rxd4 (24. Qxd4 $5) 24... Qc7 25. Rxa4 a6 26. Nf3 {etc.})) ({But, as Dvoretsky has shown, the immediate} 20. Qf3 $1 {was stronger.} Z0 ({. The tactical justification of White's idea is} 20... Qxd4 21. Rcd1 Qc4 22. Rd7 Ba8 23. Nce4 $1) ({, or} 20... h6 21. Qxf4 hxg5 22. hxg5 Qxd4 23. Qg3 (23. Qh2 $5 Qg4 24. Rcd1 Qxg5 25. Bb3 $1 {) with a powerful initiative.})) ({. Defending the knight with the queen also does not give equality:} 20... Qc7 21. Qe3 $1 Na5 22. Be4 $1 fxe4 ({other moves are less good:} 22... Ba6 23. Nd5 $1 Ne2+ 24. Kh1 Qxc1 25. Rxc1 Rxc1+ 26. Kh2) ({, or} 22... Nc4 23. Nb5 $1 Nxe3 24. Rxc7) 23. Ncxe4 Qb8 24. Nf6+ Kg7 (24... Kh8 $2 25. Nf7+ $1 Kg7 26. Nd7) 25. Nxe8+ Rxe8 26. g3 Nd5 27. Nxe6+ Kg8 28. Qh6 Re7 29. Ng5 Qf8 (29... Rg7 30. Re6 {, intending Rce1}) 30. Qxf8+ Kxf8 31. Rxe7 Kxe7 32. Nxh7 {and "the rook and three pawns outweigh the pair of minor pieces." (Dvoretsky)}) (20... Qd6 21. Rcd1 $1 h6 ({or} 21... a6 22. g3 Nd5 23. Nxd5 exd5 24. Rxe8+ Rxe8 25. b4 {with a stable plus for White}) 22. Nh3 Nxh3+ 23. Qxh3 Rcd8 (23... Nxd4 $2 24. Nb5) ({, or} 23... a6 24. Qe3 $1 Kg7 25. Bb3 Na5 26. Bxe6 Nc4 27. Qe2 Nxb2 28. Qxb2 Rxe6 29. Rxe6 Qxe6 30. d5 Qf6 31. d6 {etc is bad for Black}) 24. h5 $1 Nxd4 (24... g5 25. d5 $1) 25. Ba4 $1 Rf8 26. Nb5 (26. Bb3 $5 {Dvoretsky}) 26... Qf4 27. Nxd4 Rxd4 {, and here White could have won by} 28. Rxe6 $1 ({instead of} 28. Rxd4 $2 Qxd4 29. hxg6 {, training game Inarkiev-Najer, Dagomys 2004,} Bd5 $1 30. Qxh6 Qg7 31. Qh5 Qh8 $1) 28... Rxd1+ 29. Bxd1 {(Inarkiev). Apparently, only with the far from obvious move 20 Qf3! was it possible to exploit the main defect of the "ugly positional blunder" 17...f5?! – the weakening of the black king. Now, however, Black can take on d4 and a double-edged struggle develops.})) 20... Qxd4 (20... Nxg2 $6 21. Kxg2 Nxd4+ {is dubious on account of} 22. Kf1 $1 {– Dvoretsky.}) 21. Nb5 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"Marvelously alert! After the practically forced trade of queens, White wins the exchange because of the imminent fork on d6." (Fischer). However, as we will now see, the exchange of queens was by no means obligatory.'} Qxe3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: '"Best." (Fischer)'} (21... Qd5 22. Qxf4 Qxb5 (22... Nd4 $2 23. Be4 $1 Rxc1 24. Qxc1 $1 fxe4 25. Qc7) (22... Ne5 23. f3 $18 {Soloviev}) 23. Nxe6 Qxb2 (23... Qd5 24. Nc7 Rxe1+ 25. Rxe1 Qf7 26. Ne6 $16) 24. Qh6 $1 $18 {[%cal Rc2b3] ; Reshevsky,S} ({suggested by Reshevsky;} 24. Bxf5 Qf6 $1) 24... Na5 (24... Rxe6 25. Rxe6 Nd4 26. Re7 $18) ({not} 24... Re7 25. h5 $1 Qf6 26. Nf4 $1 Nd4 27. Bb1) ({, and not Dvoretsky's move} 24... Ne7 {in view of} 25. Ba4 $1 Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Rb8 27. Rc7 Qa1+ 28. Kh2 Qe5+ 29. f4 Qxe6 30. Bb3 $1 Qxb3 31. Rxe7 {, winning}) 25. Bxf5 $1 gxf5 (25... Qf6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} {Garry Kasparov: 'but not Fischer's move'} 26. Ng5 $1 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'on account of'} (26. Rcd1 $1) 26... Rxc1 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 27. Qxh7+ (27. Rxc1 Re7 $1 $13 28. Be6+ Kh8 {Garry Kasparov: '– cf. below'} 29. Rd1 Nc6 30. Nf7+ (30. Bb3 Qg7 {with equality}) 30... Kg8 $1 (30... Rxf7 $2 31. Bxf7 Qxf7 32. Rd7 $1 {and wins}) 31. Bb3 Rxf7 32. Rd7 Ne5 $3 ({after} 32... Qxf2+ $2 33. Kh2 {Black cannot play} Ne5 {on account of} 34. Rd8#) 33. Bxf7+ Nxf7 {etc.}) 27... Kf8 28. Rxc1 (28. Qh6+ Qg7 $19) 28... gxf5 $15 {Soloviev}) (25... Rxc1 26. Rxc1 gxf5 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} (26... Qf6 27. Ng5 ({, the win is problematic:} 27. Z0) ({. True, by} 27. Nf4 {(leaving the g5-square for the queen)} Nc6 ({or} 27... Bc6) ({, but not} 27... Qxf5 $2 28. Rc7) 28. Bb1 $1 {with the idea of} Qb2 29. Rd1 Qb3 30. Rf1 $1 {White would nevertheless retain winning chances.}) 27... Re7 28. Be4 Qg7 $11 {Soloviev}) 27. Rc7 $18) 26. Rb1 Qh8 (26... Qc3 27. Re3) 27. Qg5+ Kf7 28. Qxf5+ Kg8 (28... Qf6 29. Qxh7+) 29. Re3 Rc3 30. Ng5 $1 Rf8 31. Re8 $1 $18 {Fischer}) ({In fact, the best continuation was} 21... Qxb2 $1 22. Nd6 Nxg2 $1 23. Kxg2 Nd4+ $1 24. Be4 $1 fxe4 $1 {(Dvoretsky) with a double-edged game:} 25. Z0 (25. Rxc8 Rxc8 26. Nxb7 Nc2 $1) (25. Nxb7 Nf5 26. Qf4 e3 $1 27. Rxc8 Rxc8 28. Rxe3 Qd2 $1 29. Ne4 Nxe3+ 30. Qxe3 Qxe3 31. fxe3 Rc4 $1 {and the rook is more agile than the two knights.}) (25. Rb1 Qa2 $1 26. Qxd4 (26. Nxb7 Nc2 $1) (26. Nxe8 Nf5 27. Nf6+ Kg7 28. Qf4 Kxf6 29. Nxe4+ Kf7 {with good compensation for the exchange}) 26... e3+ $1 27. Nde4 Qxf2+ 28. Kh3 Bxe4 29. Nxe4 Qf3+ 30. Ng3 Rc2 31. Qe4 Qf2 32. Rh1 e2 33. Rbc1 e1=Q 34. Rcxe1 Rc3 35. Rhg1 (35. Qg4 Qf5 $1) 35... Rxa3 36. Qc6 {and the game heads for a draw. Now, however, Black ends up by force in a difficult endgame.})) 22. fxe3 Nxg2 $1 23. Kxg2 Nd4+ {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({"} 23... Nb4+ 24. Be4 $1 Nd3 25. Bxb7 Rxc1 26. Rxc1 Nxc1 27. Nxe6 $1 {was no better." (Dvoretsky). But after} Re7 $1 28. Bd5 Kf7 {and ... Kf6 he would have more chances of saving himself than in the game (although here he has no practical chances of winning!).}) 24. Be4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"This game was played at the Beverly Hilton Hotel in Los Angeles, and I can still hear the audience gasping with each blow, thinking each of us had overlooked it in turn. 'Fischer is winning!' 'Reshevsky is winning!'. The true state of affairs will crystallize in a matter of moves." (Fischer)'} Bxe4+ ({"} 24... Rxc1 25. Rxc1 Bxe4+ 26. Nxe4 Nxb5 27. Nf6+ Kf7 28. Nxe8 Kxe8 29. Rc8+ Ke7 (29... Kd7 30. Rh8) 30. a4 Nd6 31. Rc7+ {is altogether hopeless for Black." (Dvoretsky)}) 25. Nxe4 Nxb5 26. Nf6+ Kf7 27. Nxe8 Rxe8 28. a4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: 'In the resulting ending White has to play very accurately: for the moment Black has two pawns for the exchange and a solid pawn structure.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Thus after'} 28. Red1 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Re7 $1 { b5-c7-d5 Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'and ...Nc7-d5  (Fischer) Black would not be in danger of losing.'}) 28... Nd6 29. Rc7+ Kf6 $1 (29... Re7 30. Rec1 $18 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'was hopeless. "The rook is needed to mobilize the kingside pawns." (Fischer)'}) 30. Rec1 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '"Keeping control of the open file," writes Fischer.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"On'} 30. Rxh7 {Garry Kasparov: '(or Rxa7)'} Rc8 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '." Hübner adds'} 31. Re2 Nf7 $1) (30. Rxa7 Rc8 {Fischer} 31. Re2 Rc4 $1 {etc}) ({and also} 30. Rd1 Ne4 31. Rdd7 a5 32. Rxh7 Rd8 $1 33. Rcd7 Rc8 $1 34. Rc7 Rd8 35. Rhd7 Rh8 {– and concludes: "Fischer's infallible positional feeling showed again."}) 30... h6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black begins advancing his pawns, which, however, should not have saved him.'} ({"} 30... Ne4 31. Z0 (31. Rxa7 Rd8 {came into consideration." (Dvoretsky). Indeed, after} 32. Rc2 Rd3 33. Kf3 h6 34. Rb7 ({or} 34. Rh7 Rd1 $1) 34... Rd6 35. b4 g5 {Black could still have resisted.}) ({. But in the light of the following methodologically valuable comment, White should play} 31. Rc8 $1 Rxc8 (31... Re7 32. R1c7 $1) 32. Rxc8 {, and after} Nc5 33. a5 Nd3 34. a6 $1 Nb4 {(the only move)} 35. Rc7 Nxa6 36. Rxh7 Nb4 37. Rxa7 {White retains his b-pawn, and with it every chance of winning.})) 31. Rxa7 ({"The interesting plan with} 31. b4 $5 {was suggested by Artur Yusupov.} Z0 ({. "Black loses after} 31... Ne4 32. Rc8 Re7 33. R1c7 {with the exchange of rooks, which, as usual, is advantageous to the side who is the exchange up.}) ({. "It is also bad to play} 31... Rd8 32. Rd1 Nb5 33. Rcd7) ({, and also} 31... Ra8 32. R1c6 Ne4 33. Rc8) ({, or} 31... a5 32. R7c6 Ne4 33. Rxb6 axb4 34. Rxb4 Rd8 35. Ra1 $1 {– G.K.}) ({. "If} 31... b5 {, there is} 32. axb5 $5 ({apart from} 32. Rxa7) 32... Nxb5 (32... Rb8 33. R1c6 $5 Nxb5 34. Rd7) 33. Rd7 {(domination!).}) ({. "Finally, if} 31... g5 {there follows} 32. h5 $1 g4 33. b5 Ra8 34. Rh7 a6 35. a5 $1 axb5 36. axb6 Nc4 37. b7 Rb8 38. Rb1 Nxe3+ (38... Nd6 39. Rxh6+) 39. Kf2 Nc4 (39... f4 40. Rxb5) 40. Rxh6+ Kg5 41. Rh7 Nd6 42. Rg7+ Kh6 43. Rd7 {and White wins."  (Dvoretsky)})) 31... Ne4 32. Ra6 (32. a5 bxa5 33. Rxa5 Rd8 34. Rc2 Rd3 35. Ra3 $16 {Soloviev}) 32... Rd8 $1 (32... Rb8 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'is too passive: White wins after'} 33. Rc6 $18 {Fischer} ({or first} 33. b4 {.})) 33. Rc2 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'A typically defensive move in time-trouble. "The only way to preserve winning chances,' thinks Fischer.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"After' Garry Kasparov: 'According to Hübner,' Garry Kasparov: 'Or' Garry Kasparov: 'Finally, the variation Fischer gives in brackets'} 33. Rxb6 Rd2+ 34. Kg1 g5 {Garry Kasparov: 'avoiding the complications after'} 35. hxg5+ ({Garry Kasparov: 'on'} 35. Rcc6 {Garry Kasparov: '!?'} gxh4 (35... Rd1+ 36. Kg2 {– cf. Hübner's analysis above}) 36. Rxe6+ Kg5 37. Rg6+ Kh5 {Garry Kasparov: 'is easily refuted by'} 38. Rxh6+ (38. a5 $1 {(Dvoretsky)}) ({or} 38. b4 {, winning). "I know no other example in which Fischer commits such a serious error of judgement in a rather clarified position," concludes Dr Hübner. In my view, both this mistake, and the oversight on the 36th move, are very typical of Fischer: he was very content with the course of the game, with the flights of imagination by the two players, and when he annotated the game he did not believe that he had reached a lost position, and did not seek confirmation of this. According to his inner conviction, the logical outcome should have been a draw! Although, it must be agreed, in an endgame with an exchange advantage and a passed pawn it is possible to find a win even without the help of a computer.}) 38... Kg4 $132 {Garry Kasparov: 'Black has enough play on the kingside to hold the draw'}) ({Garry Kasparov: ', but not'} 35. a5 $2 gxh4 36. a6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} h3 37. a7 h2+ 38. Kh1 Ng3#) {Garry Kasparov: 'this is more tenacious than'} 35... hxg5 (35... Nxg5 36. Rf1 Nh3+ (36... Ke5 37. a5 Ke4 38. Rb4+ $1 {Dvoretsky}) 37. Kh1 Nf2+ 38. Kg2 (38. Rxf2 $5 Rxf2 39. a5 {Dvoretsky}) 38... Z0 (38... Ng4+ 39. Kg3 Nxe3 40. Rh1 {"and White should win without much trouble"  (Hübner)} (40. Re1 f4+ $1 41. Kf3 Kf5 {Dvoretsky}) {, but Dvoretsky gives} 40... Ng4 41. a5 Kg5 $1 42. Rxe6 h5 {with sufficient counterplay for Black.}) ({. Even so, I was able to find a way to win:} 38... Ng4+ 39. Kg1 $1 Nxe3 40. Re1 f4 41. a5 Rg2+ 42. Kh1 Rg5 43. a6 Ra5 44. b4 Ra3 45. b5 f3 46. Kg1 Ng4 47. Rf1 f2+ 48. Rxf2+ Nxf2 49. Kxf2 {.})) 36. Rcc6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 36. a5 $2 g4 37. a6 {Garry Kasparov: '?'} Ng5 38. a7 Nf3+ 39. Kf1 (39. Kh1 g3 40. a8=Q g2#) 39... g3 40. a8=Q g2#) {Garry Kasparov: 'instead of'} 36... g4 (36... Rd1+ {Garry Kasparov: 'is also bad:'} 37. Kg2 Rd2+ 38. Kf1 Ng3+ 39. Ke1 Re2+ 40. Kd1 Rxe3 41. a5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} f4 42. a6 f3 43. Rxe6+ $1 Rxe6 44. Rxe6+ Kxe6 45. Ke1 $1 $18 {Soloviev Garry Kasparov: 'and the a-pawn cannot be stopped.'}) ({; if} 36... f4 $5 37. Rxe6+ Kf5 38. Re8 $1 {Inarkiev}) 37. Rxe6+ Kg5 38. Rh6 (38. Rb5 $18 {Soloviev Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: 'is stronger, and White wins – G.K.'}) 38... f4 $11 {Garry Kasparov: 'keeps the balance." However, Hübner shows that after'} 39. Rhg6+ {White also has a won position:} ({he perhaps also wins with} 39. exf4+ Kxf4 40. Kf1 Rd1+ 41. Ke2 Rd2+ 42. Ke1 Rd3 43. Rh2 $5 {Dvoretsky}) 39... Kf5 ({or} 39... Kh4 40. exf4 Kg3 (40... Rd3 41. Kg2 Rd2+ 42. Kf1 {etc}) 41. Rb3+ Kxf4 42. Rg8 {and Black's initiative peters out}) 40. exf4 Kxf4 41. a5 (41. Rg8 $5 Ng5 42. Rb4+ {Dvoretsky}) 41... Ng5 42. Rbf6+ $1 Ke5 43. a6 Nf3+ 44. Kf1 Nh2+ 45. Ke1 Rxb2 (45... Nf3+ 46. Rxf3) 46. a7 Ra2 47. Ra6 {.}) 33... Rd3 34. Rxb6 (34. Kf3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} Rb3 $15 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '(Fischer).'}) 34... Rxe3 35. a5 f4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Thanks to the opponent's slight delay, Black has activated his forces, creating potentially dangerous counterplay against the king.'} 36. Rf2 $2 {Garry Kasparov: 'A tragic oversight with the flag about to fall. "Reshevsky probably didn't see how Black's rook could get back in time to stop the a-pawn.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"White should settle for'} 36. a6 $142 f3+ 37. Kf1 ({Garry Kasparov: 'not'} 37. Kh2 $2 Re2+) 37... Rd3 38. Ke1 Re3+ 39. Kf1 Rd3 $11 {Garry Kasparov: 'with a draw. If'} 40. Kg1 Rd1+ 41. Kh2 f2 42. Rxf2+ Nxf2 43. Rb3 (43. a7 Ra1 $19 {Garry Kasparov: 'wins'}) 43... Rd7 44. Rf3+ Kg7 45. Rxf2 Ra7 $11 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: '('} 46. b4 Rxa6 47. b5 Rb6 48. Rb2 Kf6 49. Kg3 {– G.K.). But now it is doubtful that White can even draw!" (Fischer)}) ({Garry Kasparov: 'Amazing chess blindness! "The subtle move'} 36. Rb4 $1 {Garry Kasparov: '!!' Garry Kasparov: 'would have won," points out Dvoretsky. "White attacks the enemy knight, preparing at the appropriate moment to exchange it for his rook. For example:'} f3+ (36... g5 37. hxg5+ hxg5 38. a6 g4 39. a7 $18 {Garry Kasparov: 'and wins'}) 37. Kf1 Kf5 (37... f2 38. Rxf2+ Nxf2 39. Kxf2 {is also hopeless}) 38. a6 Rd3 39. Rc1 Rd2 40. Rxe4 Kxe4 41. a7 Rd8 42. b4 Ra8 43. Rc7 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} ({in the event of} 43. Ra1 $2 {the black king goes to the queenside}) 43... Rd8 44. b5 $18 {Soloviev Garry Kasparov: 'and White wins."'} Rd1+ (44... Ke3 45. Rc3+ Kf4 46. Rc1 $18) 45. Kf2 Rd2+ 46. Ke1 Re2+ 47. Kf1 Ra2 48. b6 Ra1+ (48... Ke3 49. Rc3+ Kd4 50. Rxf3 $18) 49. Kf2 Ra2+ 50. Kg3 f2 51. Rc1 $18 {PS}) 36... Nxf2 37. Kxf2 Re5 $1 38. b4 Re3 $1 39. a6 Ra3 {Garry Kasparov: 'Such a switching of the rook is easy to overlook in time-trouble! "Now White is stymied. In order to mobilize his queenside pawns, he must inch forward with b4-b5, Rb7, a6-a7, b5-b6 etc. But a half-dozen moves, in chess, can be a lifetime." (Fischer)'} 40. Rc6 $19 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: '"The last move of the time-control, and it definitely loses.'} ({Garry Kasparov: '"The best chance is'} 40. b5 $142 { b8, b5-b6-b7 Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'with the possibility of Rb8 and b5-b6 (giving up the a-pawn) followed by b6-b7, in some key variations." (Fischer)'} g5 (40... Kf5 41. Rc6) 41. hxg5+ hxg5 42. Rb8 (42. Rb7 g4 43. a7 Ra2+ 44. Kf1 g3 45. b6 f3 $19) 42... g4 (42... Ke5 $2 43. Rg8) 43. Rf8+ (43. b6 $1 g3+ 44. Kg2 {(Dvoretsky) he would easily have gained a draw:} Ra2+ 45. Kf1 ({or even} 45. Kf3 g2 46. Rg8 Rxa6 47. Rxg2) 45... Rxa6 46. b7 f3 47. Rf8+ {and Rxf3.}) 43... Ke5 (43... Kg5 44. Rg8+ Kh4 45. Rh8+ $11) 44. b6 g3+ 45. Kg2 Ra2+ 46. Kg1 $11 {Soloviev}) 40... g5 41. hxg5+ hxg5 42. b5 g4 {Abgabezug Garry Kasparov: 'The sealed move. "Black's pawns suddenly proliferate from nowhere!"  (Fischer)'} 43. Rc8 {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'The first move after the adjournment – and a final, fatal mistake by the tired veteran.'} (43. Rc1 { b1} g3+ 44. Kg1 (44. Kg2 Ra2+ 45. Kf3 Kf5 $19) 44... Ra2 $1 45. Rb1 {Garry Kasparov: '(? – G.K.)'} (45. Z0) ({. But} 45. b6 $1 {was far more tenacious:} Rxa6 ({nothing is achieved by} 45... f3 46. b7 Rg2+ 47. Kf1 Rh2 48. Ke1 Rb2 49. Kf1 $1 {with a draw}) 46. Rb1 Ra8 47. b7 Rb8 48. Kg2 e5 49. Kf3 Ke6 {– "with an easy win for Black" according to Dvoretsky's 'pre-computer' opinion, but after} 50. Rb6+ Kd5 51. Rb5+ Kd6 52. Rb6+ $1 ({not} 52. Ke4 $2 g2 53. Rb6+ Ke7 $1 54. Rg6 Rxb7 55. Rxg2 Kf6 {and wins}) 52... Kc7 53. Rb5 Rxb7 ({or} 53... Re8 54. b8=Q+ Rxb8 55. Rxe5 Rf8 56. Re6 $1) 54. Rxe5 Rb1 55. Re2 $1 Rb4 (55... Rf1+ 56. Kg2) 56. Re6 $1 Kd7 57. Rg6 {the position is a draw: the rook manoeuvres along the sixth rank, cutting off the king, and in the event of ...Rb1 it returns to the second rank. Again Bobby ignores the opponent's best chance: as one can see, not only in life, but also in chess he did not wish to accept unpleasant reality.}) 45... f3 46. b6 Rg2+ 47. Kf1 Rh2 $1 48. Ke1 Rh1+ 49. Kd2 Rxb1 50. a7 f2 51. a8=Q f1=Q $19 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'and Black wins, since White has no perpetual check."'}) 43... Kf5 44. b6 g3+ 45. Ke1 ({Garry Kasparov: '"He decides to let the pawns through rather than get mated after'} 45. Kg2 Ra2+ 46. Kg1 f3 $19 {Fischer Garry Kasparov: 'etc." (Fischer). But the move in the game also does not help.'}) 45... Ra1+ 46. Ke2 g2 47. Rf8+ ({Garry Kasparov: 'Or'} 47. Rg8 Rxa6 48. b7 (48. Rxg2 Rxb6 $19) 48... Rb6 $19 {Fischer Garry Kas