1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. Be3 (8. d5 {– Game Nos.29, 64, 90 and 92.}) 8... Ng4 9. Bg5 Bf6 $5 {Garry Kasparov: 'Nowadays this is deemed dubious. Fischer believed too implicitly that in such positions the exchange of the 'blunt' g7-bishop is useful for Black. But here it leads to a marked weakening of the dark-square complex around the king, and if White should succeed in opening the position while retaining the queens, he will gain good attacking chances.'} (9... f6 10. Bc1 f5 {is preferable} ({not} 10... Kh8 {– Game No.13}) 11. Z0 ({. Now double-edged play results from} 11. d5 Ne7 ({not} 11... Nd4 $6 12. Nxd4 exd4 13. Nb5 c5 14. dxc6 bxc6 15. Nxd4 Qb6 16. Nb3 {Reshevsky-Berliner, New York 1960}) 12. Ng5 Nf6 13. exf5 Nxf5 14. Bd3 Nd4 15. Ne2 Nf5 16. Ng3 c6 $1 {(Farago-J.Polgar, Hungary 1991)}) ({, while after} 11. Bg5 {Black has a choice of three continuations:} Z0 (11... Bf6 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 ({after} 12... Qxf6 13. h3 Nh6 14. d5 Nd8 15. c5 $1 Ndf7 16. cxd6 Nxd6 17. Nd2 Bd7 18. Rc1 Nhf7 {Black's pieces lack harmony, Ricardi-Kasparov, match simultaneous with the Argentine team, Buenos Aires 1992}) 13. exf5 $1 ({nothing is achieved by} 13. dxe5 dxe5 14. Qxd8 Rxd8 15. Nd5 Nxe4 16. Nxc7 Rb8 17. Rfd1 Bd7 {with equality, Portisch-Kasparov, Linares 1990}) 13... Bxf5 14. d5 Ne7 15. Qd2 Kg7 16. Nh4 Bd7 17. f4 exf4 18. Qxf4 Nf5 19. Nxf5+ Bxf5 20. g4 $1 Bd7 {(Kasparov-Anand, Geneva 1996)} 21. Qd4 $1 {, and White retains the initiative.}) (11... Qd7 {(along with 11...Qe8, this looks sounder: usually here a release of the central tension occurs and Black achieves an equal game without particular difficulty)} 12. dxe5 Ngxe5 13. Nxe5 dxe5 14. c5 Nd4 15. Bc4+ Kh8 {with equality (Portisch-Tringov, Antwerp 1955).}) (11... Qe8 12. dxe5 Ngxe5 ({or} 12... fxe4 13. Nxe4 Ngxe5 14. Rc1 Qf7) 13. exf5 Bxf5 14. Qd2 {(Simutowe-Sasikiran, New Delhi 2000)} Qf7 15. Nxe5 Nxe5 {with equality.}))) 10. Bxf6 Nxf6 (10... Qxf6 $6 11. h3) 11. d5 (11. dxe5 dxe5 12. Qxd8 Rxd8 13. Nd5 Ne8 $1 {[%csl Rd4] g4,×d4}) (11. h3 $1 exd4 12. Nxd4 Re8 13. Nxc6 bxc6 14. Qc2 Qe7 15. Rfe1 $1) 11... Ne7 12. Ne1 Nd7 {Garry Kasparov: 'Supporting the e5-pawn in the event of the thematic f2-f4.'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'Instead'} 12... Ne8 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} 13. f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} exf4 14. Rxf4 f5 15. exf5 Nxf5 16. Qd2 Qe7 17. Nc2 Bd7 18. Raf1 {Garry Kasparov: 'favours White (Reshevsky-Fischer, New York 1960/61).'} Neg7 19. Re4 Qd8 20. g4 Nh4 21. Rxf8+ Qxf8 22. Nd4 c5 23. dxc6 Bxc6 24. Nd5 Bxd5 {½-½ Reshevsky,S-Fischer,R/ch-USA 1960 (24)}) 13. Nd3 f5 (13... c5 $5) 14. exf5 $1 gxf5 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Too straightforward.'} (14... Nxf5 $5 15. Qd2 Nf6 16. Rae1 {(before playing f2-f4, White has to neutralize ...e5-e4)} c6 (16... Bd7 {is too slow:} 17. Bd1 $1 Qe7 ({or} 17... Re8 18. h3 $1) 18. f4 e4 19. g4 $1 {, with the same plan of a pawn storm}) 17. dxc6 ({or} 17. Bd1 cxd5 18. cxd5 Qb6 $1 19. Kh1 Qd4 $1) 17... bxc6 18. Bd1 Ba6 19. b3 Qb6 20. Kh1 Rae8 $1 {etc.}) 15. f4 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Ng6 {Garry Kasparov: 'Fischer goes in for the creation of a pawn island, hoping to retain at least some activity for his pieces, but this does not compensate for the defects in Black's position.'} ({White's strategic advantage is especially marked after} 15... e4 16. Ne1 Ng6 17. Nc2 {– here he can prepare unhindered the typical undermining move g2-g4 or else mount an offensive on the queenside.}) 16. Qd2 Re8 17. fxe5 Ndxe5 18. Nxe5 Rxe5 ({Garry Kasparov: 'In the event of'} 18... dxe5 19. c5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Nf4 20. Bc4 {Black has no way of opposing the opponent's threats in the centre –} Qg5 21. Kh1 {etc.}) 19. Nb5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} (19. Rae1 $142) ({The alternative offensive plan –} 19. Bd3 Bd7 20. Rae1 {(recommended by Mednis) would have led to a perfectly acceptable endgame for Black after} Qh4 $5 21. Rxe5 Nxe5 22. Bxf5 Nxc4 23. Qf4 $1 Qxf4 24. Rxf4 Bxf5 25. Rxc4 Rc8 {; for example:} 26. Nb5 a6 27. Nxc7 Bd7 28. Rc3 Kf7 29. Rf3+ Ke7 30. Ne6 Rc1+ $1 31. Rf1 Rc2 $1 {with equality.}) 19... Bd7 20. Bd3 {Garry Kasparov: '?!' Garry Kasparov: 'Reshevsky turns the play onto tactical lines, which contains both pluses (the win of a pawn) and minuses (now White's pawn structure is also not ideal).'} ({Garry Kasparov: 'It looks more promising to increase the pressure on the f5-pawn by'} 20. Nd4 $142 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} Qf6 21. Bd3 Rf8 22. Rf2 {etc.}) 20... Bxb5 {Garry Kasparov: '!'} 21. cxb5 Qd7 22. Qf2 ({The attempt to win the a7-pawn is interesting:} 22. Rac1 $5 b6 23. Rc3 Rf8 24. Qc2 Rf7 25. Ra3 Ne7 26. Rxa7 Nxd5 27. Ra8+ Kg7 28. Rf3 Qe6 29. Bc4 f4 {The attempt to win the a7-pawn is interesting:} 30. h3 Qg6 31. Qxg6+ hxg6 32. Rd3 Nb4 33. Rd2 {.}) 22... f4 (22... Ne7 23. Rae1) (22... Rf8 23. Qxa7) 23. Bxg6 hxg6 24. Qxf4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Relying on his great experience, Reshevsky decided to compete with the young talent in technique.'} Rae8 $1 (24... Rxd5 25. Qf6 $1 (25. Qe4 Rf5 (25... Rxb5 $2 26. Qxg6+ Qg7 27. Qe6+ Kh8 28. Rf7 Qd4+ 29. Kh1 Rh5 30. Raf1 Rg8 31. Rf8 Qg7 32. Rxg8+ Qxg8 33. Rf7 {is even worse for Black}) 26. Qxb7 Raf8 27. Rxf5 Qxf5 28. Qxa7 Qxb5 29. Qd4 {etc.}) 25... Kh7 $1 (25... Qe8 26. Rae1 Re5 27. Rxe5 Qxe5 (27... dxe5 28. Rf3 {[%cal Gf3h3,Rh3h8]}) 28. Qxg6+ Kh8 29. h3 $18) (25... Qg7 $4 26. Qe6+ $18) 26. a4 $16) 25. Rad1 Qg7 26. h3 R5e7 (26... Re2 27. Rd2 R8e4 28. Rxe2 Rxe2 (28... Rxf4 29. Rxf4 $18) 29. Qg4 $16) 27. Rd2 Re4 28. Qf2 b6 29. a3 R8e5 30. Qf6 {Garry Kasparov: '?!'} ({For the moment I would have preferred to retain the queens with} 30. Rd4 Re2 31. Qh4 {, although after the exchange of one pair of rooks –} Re1 ({but not} 31... Rxb2 $2 32. Qd8+ Kh7 33. Rc4 {and wins}) 32. Qg4 Rxf1+ 33. Kxf1 Qf7+ 34. Kg1 Kg7 {Black has a perfectly defensible position.}) 30... Qxf6 31. Rxf6 Kg7 32. Rff2 Kh6 ({After} 32... Rc4 $2 33. Rc2 $1 Rxc2 (33... Rc5 34. b4 $1) 34. Rxc2 Rxd5 35. Rxc7+ Kf6 36. a4 $1 {Black would have remained two pawns down:} Rd1+ 37. Kh2 Rb1 38. Rxa7 Rxb2 39. Ra6 {and wins.}) 33. Kh2 Rc4 34. Rc2 ({The attempt to retain all four rooks –} 34. Rf7 $5 {, in order to attack the g6-pawn, would also have set Black some problems, although they would also be quite resolvable:} Rc5 35. a4 Re4 36. b3 Rc3 $1 ({White's idea would be justified only after} 36... Re3 $2 37. Rd4 $1 Rxb3 38. Rh4+ Kg5 39. Rg4+ Kh6 40. Rf6 Rd3 41. Rfxg6+ Kh7 42. Rg7+ Kh8 43. R7g5 {and wins}) 37. Rf3 Rc1 $1 ({not} 37... Rxf3 $2 38. gxf3 Re3 39. Rc2 Rxf3 40. Rxc7 Rxb3 41. Rxa7 {and wins}) 38. Rf8 Kg7 39. Ra8 Re3 40. b4 Re4 41. Rb2 $5 (41. Rxa7 Rxb4 42. Re2 Rc5 43. Re7+ Kf6 44. Rexc7 Rxc7 45. Rxc7 Rxa4 46. Rc6 Ke5 {with a draw}) 41... Rd4 42. Rxa7 Rxd5 43. a5 Rxb5 44. Rb7 {. White has achieved a great deal: his passed pawn is threatening to queen, whereas one of the black rooks is awkwardly placed and the other is tied to the defence of the c7-pawn.} Z0 ({. Black must not delay –} 44... Kh6 $2 45. Ra2 $1 {, and the pawn cannot indeed be stopped. He must urgently activate his rooks, and he has two possibilities that in the end enable him to gain a draw with the help of the same mechanism that could have saved him in the game:}) (44... Rc4 45. a6 Rbxb4 46. Rxc7+ Rxc7 47. Rxb4 d5 48. Kg3 ({or} 48. Rxb6 Rc3 49. a7 Ra3 50. Rb7+ Kh6 51. h4 Kh5 52. g3 Kg4) 48... Rc3+ 49. Kf4 Ra3 50. Rxb6 d4 51. Ke4 d3 52. Ke3 Kh6 53. Kd2 Kg7 {with a draw.}) (44... Ra1 45. Rxc7+ Kh6 46. Rb7 Raxa5 47. bxa5 Rxb2 48. Rxb6 Ra2 49. a6 d5 50. Kg3 d4 51. Kf3 d3 52. Ke3 d2 53. Ke2 {with a draw.})) 34... Rxc2 35. Rxc2 Rxd5 36. a4 $1 Rd4 (36... Rc5 37. Rxc5 bxc5 38. Kg3 d5 (38... Kg5 {is lost:} 39. h4+ Kf5 40. Kf3 d5 ({or} 40... c4 41. g4+ Ke5 42. h5 gxh5 43. gxh5 d5 44. h6 Kf6 45. Ke3) 41. g4+ Ke5 42. b3 Kf6 43. Kf4 Ke6 44. h5 gxh5 45. gxh5 Kf6 46. a5 Ke6 47. Kg5 d4 48. Kf4 $1 Kf6 49. Ke4 Kg5 50. b4 cxb4 51. Kxd4 Kxh5 52. Kc4 {.}) 39. Kf4 d4 40. Ke4 (40. b3) 40... Kg5 41. g3 Kf6 42. b4 $1 cxb4 43. Kxd4 {[%cal Yd4c4,Rc4b4] xb4+- Evans}) 37. b3 Rd3 38. Rxc7 Rxb3 39. Rxa7 d5 40. Rd7 (40. Rb7 $1 Rb4 41. Rxb6 Rxa4 {Garry Kasparov: 'Nothing new is introduced into the character of the play by'} 42. Rd6 d4 43. b6 $18 (43. Kg3 Rb4 44. b6 d3 45. Kf3 d2 46. Ke2 Rb2 {with a draw.})) 40... Rd3 41. Rd6 Rd4 42. Rxb6 Rxa4 43. Kg3 Rb4 44. Rb8 {Abgabezug} d4 45. Kf3 (45. Kf4 {is more active, with a cunning trap. But Black could have avoided it by} d3+ $1 ({whereas after} 45... Rb3 $2 {the unexpected activation of the king proves decisive –} 46. Ke5 d3 47. Kf6 Kh7 48. Rb7+ {, and the attempt to retain the pawn with} Kh6 {is suppressed by a mating attack –} 49. g4 $1 g5 50. h4 gxh4 51. Rb8 Kh7 52. g5 {etc}) 46. Ke3 Rb3 $1 {and safely reached the haven of a draw:} 47. b6 g5 48. Kd2 Kg7 49. g3 Kh6 50. h4 gxh4 51. gxh4 Kg6 52. h5+ $1 Kg7 $1 53. b7 Kh7 54. h6 Rb1 55. Kxd3 Rb6 56. Kc4 Rb1 {.}) 45... Rb3+ 46. Ke4 d3 47. Ke3 g5 (47... Kg7 $142) 48. Rb6+ Kg7 $1 (48... Kh5 49. g4+ Kh4 50. Rh6+ Kg3 51. b6 Kg2 52. h4 gxh4 53. g5 h3 54. g6 d2+ 55. Kxd2 Rb2+ 56. Kc3 Rxb6 57. g7 Rxh6 58. g8=Q+ $18) 49. Kd2 Kf7 50. g3 Rb2+ {Garry Kasparov: '?' Garry Kasparov: 'Just one serious mistake, and the competition in technique proves lost – at a moment when, to gain a draw, Black only needed to solve one last little problem. Bobby goes into an elementary rook endgame, overlooking that White's only pawn will queen with the support of the king, whereas Black's passed pawn is deprived of such support and its prospects of becoming a queen are equal to zero.'} (50... Kg7 $142 51. h4 gxh4 52. gxh4 Kh7 53. h5 Kg7 54. Ke3 Kh7 55. h6 {, and now either of two rook moves onto the third rank –} Ra3 $1 ({or} 55... Rc3 $1) 56. Rd6 Rb3 57. b6 Kh8 $1 58. Kd4 d2 $1 59. Kc5 Rb2 60. Kc6 Rc2+ {(these checks save Black)} 61. Kd7 Rb2 62. Kc7 Rc2+ 63. Kb8 Rb2 64. b7 Kh7 {. It is surprising, but Fischer must have also found such a drawing mechanism when seeking a defence in the lengthy variations after 34 Rf7!?.}) 51. Kxd3 Rb3+ 52. Kc4 Rxg3 53. Rh6 Kg7 ({Running towards the white pawn is pointless:} 53... Ke7 54. Kc5 Kd7 55. Kb6 $1 {.}) 54. Rc6 $1 Rxh3 55. b6 Rh1 56. Kb5 Rb1+ (56... g4 57. Rc4 Rg1 58. b7 Rb1+ 59. Rb4 $18) 57. Ka6 Ra1+ 58. Kb7 g4 59. Kc8 Ra6 ({Garry Kasparov: 'Alas, if'} 59... g3 {Garry Kasparov: 'there follows the cruel'} 60. Rc3 {Garry Kasparov: '!' Garry Kasparov: ', and the black pawn is lost.'} Rg1 61. b7 $18) 60. Kc7 {Garry Kasparov: '. "A masterwork of technical accuracy by Reshevsky." (Mednis)'} 1-0

