1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Nb8 10. d4 Nbd7 11. Nbd2 (11. c4 {– Volume 2, Game No.96.}) 11... Bb7 12. Bc2 Re8 13. Nf1 ({During the twenty years that had passed since Reykjavik, Bobby had become more sedate, and rather than the sharp} 13. b4 {(Game No.104) he preferred an unhurried development of events. Chandler,M: 'Fischer,R-Spassky,B/Reykjavik m-10/1972/'}) 13... Bf8 14. Ng3 g6 15. Bg5 $5 ({It is interesting that Fischer never employed either the main continuation} 15. a4 c5 16. d5) ({or} 15. b3 {, which has been fashionable since the 1990s (Volume 3, Game No.82).}) 15... h6 ({After} 15... Bg7 16. Qd2 {White has unpleasant pressure.} (16. Qc1 c5 17. d5 Nb6 18. Bh6 Nfd7 19. b3 a5 20. a4 bxa4 21. bxa4 Ba6 {Balashov,Y-Spassky,B/Tallin/1973/})) 16. Bd2 Bg7 {The traditional move.} ({But Black's counterplay is much more lively after} 16... exd4 $1 17. cxd4 c5 {.} 18. Z0 ({; the 3rd game went} 18. Bf4 cxd4 19. Nxd4 Ne5 {with equality}) ({, while in the 5th after} 18. d5 $1 Nb6 19. Ba5 (19. Qc1 $5) 19... Nfd7 20. b3 Bg7 21. Rc1 Qf6 $5 {a double-edged position arose and White, after making several weak, impulsive moves, lost in the end.})) 17. a4 $1 {A classic Spanish motif – play against the b5-pawn. Chandler,M: '!?'} ({The alternatives are harmless:} 17. Rc1 {Chandler,M: 'Spassky,B-Unzicker,W/Santa Monika/1966/'} c5 (17... d5 $5) 18. d5 Nb6 19. Bd3 Qc7 20. Nh2 Na4 21. Rb1 c4 22. Bc2 Nc5 {with equality  (Spassky-Unzicker, Santa Monica 1966)}) ({or} 17. Qc1 Kh7 18. h4 ({or} 18. Bb3 Re7 19. dxe5 dxe5 {with equality, Kaplan-Spassky, San Juan 1969}) 18... d5 $1 19. exd5 ({or} 19. h5 $6 dxe4 20. hxg6+ fxg6 21. Nxe4 {, Geller-Rubinetti, Siegen Olympiad 1970,} exd4 $1 22. Nxf6+ Qxf6 23. Nxd4 c5 {with an excellent game for Black}) 19... exd4 20. Nxd4 {(Spassky-Berrios, San Juan 1969)} Ne5 $1 {(Smirin-Belyavsky, 56th USSR Championship, Odessa 1989).}) 17... c5 {This solid defensive set-up is a quite understandable choice for the first game with a dangerous – despite his years of isolation – opponent.} (17... Nb6 {is also possible, as in a training game Karpov-Korchnoi (Leningrad 1971).} 18. axb5 axb5 19. b3 Nfd7 20. Bd3 b4 21. Rxa8 Bxa8 22. dxe5 Nxe5 23. Nxe5 dxe5 24. Qc2 bxc3 25. Bxc3 {Karpov,An-Kortchnoi,V (1)/Lenigrad m/1971/}) ({But} 17... exd4 $6 18. cxd4 c5 {(Kuczynski-Kveinys, Warsaw 1989) is no longer so good in view of} 19. Bf4 $1 (19. d5 Nb6 20. Qc1 Kh7 21. Ba5 Nfd7 $10 {Kuczynski,R-Kveinis,A/Warszawa op/1989/}) 19... Qe7 20. Qd2 Kh7 21. dxc5 dxc5 22. Bd6 Qd8 ({or} 22... Qe6) 23. e5 $1 {and White seizes the initiative.}) 18. d5 c4 {Hoping to establish the knight on c5.} ({In this position} 18... Nb6 {looks less logical (A.Sokolov-Portisch, Madrid rapidplay 1988).}) 19. b4 $1 {A novelty:} ({previously only} 19. Nh2) ({or} 19. Be3 {had occurred.}) ({It was possible not to give Black any choice, by playing} 19. b3 {, but Fischer, knowing his opponent well, forced him to decide on the eternal agonizing question: to take or not to take?}) 19... Nh7 $2 {This move was rightly condemned by many commentators. Chandler,M: '?!'} ({However, grandmaster Razuvaev wrote: "In my view, after} 19... cxb3 {Black also has a difficult position:} 20. Bxb3 Z0 (20... Nc5 21. c4 {. On encountering a new plan at the board, Spassky had to make a difficult choice – and he apparently guessed wrongly." He did indeed guess wrongly. White has the advantage after} Nd3 ({but Black does better to play} 21... bxa4 22. Bxa4 Rf8 $5 (22... Re7 23. Bb4 Rc8 (23... Qc7 24. Bxc5 Qxc5 25. Nd2 h5 26. Nb3 Qxc4 27. Na5 Qc8 28. Nxb7 Rxb7 29. Bc6 $16) 24. Qe2 $14) 23. Be3 Qc7 {followed by ...Rfb8, ...Nfd7 and, at a convenient moment, ...Bc8}) (21... bxc4 22. Bxc4 $14) 22. Rf1 bxc4 23. Bxc4 Nb2 24. Qb3 Nxc4 25. Qxc4 {– a 'bad' bishop at b7 with open b- and c-files.}) ({. In addition,} 20... Qc7 {is possible; for example:} 21. axb5 axb5 22. Qe2 Nc5 23. Bc2 Ba6 24. Be3 Nfd7 {with prospects of equalizing. Now, however, Black remains without his outpost at c5 and ends up in an uncommonly passive position.}) (20... Rc8 21. axb5 axb5 22. Qe2 Qb6 23. Reb1 Ra8 24. Ba4 Ba6 25. c4 $16)) 20. Be3 h5 21. Qd2 Rf8 22. Ra3 $1 {Beginning to prepare an invasion on the a-file.} Ndf6 {An attempt to concentrate his forces on the kingside, while White is occupied with the queenside.} ({The seemingly active} 22... h4 23. Nf1 f5 $6 {would have seriously weakened his king's defences and allowed White to launch a decisive attack over the whole board by} 24. Bh6 $1 {(with the threat of e4xf5):} (24. exf5 gxf5 25. Ng5 Nxg5 26. Bxg5 Bf6 27. Bh6 Bg7 28. Bxg7 Kxg7 29. f4 $16) (24. Bg5 Bf6 (24... Nxg5 25. Qxg5 Qxg5 26. Nxg5 $16) 25. Bh6 Bg7 26. Bxg7 Kxg7 27. exf5 gxf5 28. Ne3 f4 (28... Kg6 29. Nxf5 Rxf5 30. Bxf5+ Kxf5 31. Qc2+ Kf6 32. Qxh7 $18) 29. Nf5+ $16) 24... f4 ({or} 24... fxe4 25. Bxe4 Nhf6 26. Bxg6 Nxd5 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Be4 N7f6 29. Qg5+) 25. Bxg7 Kxg7 26. axb5 axb5 27. Rxa8 Bxa8 28. Ra1 Rh8 (28... Ng5 29. N1h2) 29. Qd1 $1 Nhf6 (29... Bb7 30. Ra5) 30. Ra7 $1 Qb8 31. Qa1 Bb7 32. Ng5 {and wins.}) 23. Rea1 Qd7 ({After} 23... h4 $6 {Chandler,M: '?'} 24. Z0 ({, Chandler recommends} 24. Nxh4 {(?), but after} Nxd5 $1 ({rather than} 24... Nxe4 {(?)} 25. Nxe4 Qxh4 26. Nxd6 {and wins Chandler,M: ''}) 25. exd5 Qxh4 {Black frees himself.}) ({. Therefore} 24. Nf1 $1 {is correct, leaving the opponent with a new weakness.})) 24. R1a2 Rfc8 25. Qc1 Bf8 26. Qa1 Qe8 {Black is prepared for the opening of the a-file and his position seems perfectly defensible. But here the white knight begins a remarkable journey.} 27. Nf1 $1 {Following the route d2-b1, and then, after mass exchanges on the a-file – Na3, and the b5-pawn cannot be defended.} ({In my opinion,} 27. Nd2 $1 {with the same idea was also good.}) 27... Be7 28. N1d2 (28. N3d2 $5 Z0 {with the idea of} (28... Kg7 29. f3 $16) 29. f3 {Soltis.}) 28... Kg7 29. Nb1 $1 {Practically forcing Black to sacrifice a piece.} Nxe4 $1 ({There is nothing else: after} 29... Nd7 $2 30. axb5 axb5 31. Rxa8 Rxa8 32. Rxa8 Qxa8 33. Qxa8 Bxa8 34. Na3 $1 {the game is essentially over. In addition, the sacrifice of a knight for two central pawns, for the sake of activating the bishop at b7, was a patent (although not always successful) Spassky procedure in typical Breyer Variation structures. Chandler,M: '+-'}) 30. Bxe4 f5 $2 {This seems like activity, but it is in fact an irreparable weakening of his own king's defences. Alas, the later Spassky, for all his deep understanding of chess, in tense games would sometimes take impulsive decisions, which he himself was later unable to explain.} ({Thus in the given instance he was surprised why he did not play} 30... Nf6 {, bringing his last reserve into the battle. Although, after Chandler,M: '!?'} 31. Bc2 ({or} 31. Nbd2 Nxe4 32. Nxe4 Bxd5 33. Ned2 {Soltis}) 31... Nxd5 32. Bd2 Nc7 33. Bd1 {White is better, the outcome of the battle is not so obvious. True, here too, especially initially, the material-greedy computer gives a clear preference to White. Incidentally, I should mention that a careful analysis using modern computer programs enables many existing evaluations, not only of individual moves and variations but also entire systems, to be significantly refined or even completely reconsidered. It has also resulted in the discovery of new, clear-cut plans, which earlier, roughly until the mid-1990s, grandmasters developed only on the basis of their experience, understanding and intuition. In our era, variational evidence is becoming increasingly convincing (contrary the old rule, which stated that "in any long variation there are mistakes"), and it is largely on the basis of such evidence that the prospects of various plans or entire set-ups are evaluated.}) 31. Bc2 Bxd5 (31... Nf6 $5) 32. axb5 axb5 33. Ra7 {Chandler,M: '!'} Kf6 $2 {Bringing quite the wrong piece into play: it is not yet the endgame!} (33... Rxa7 34. Rxa7 Ra8 {was more tenacious, and if} 35. Nbd2 {, then} Nf6 {.}) 34. Nbd2 Rxa7 ({With the king at f6 the advance} 34... f4 {is fatal in view of} 35. Ne4+ Ke6 ({or} 35... Bxe4 36. Bxe4 Rxa7 37. Bxa7 {(Chandler) Chandler,M: '+-'} Kg7 38. Bb6 Nf6 39. Bb7 $1) 36. Bb6 Rxa7 (36... Rcb8 37. R2a6) 37. Bxa7 Ra8 38. Ra6 {etc.}) 35. Rxa7 Ra8 {Perhaps Black can nevertheless defend?} 36. g4 $1 {A powerful blow, activating the bishop at c2 and opening a second front – the consequence of the incautious move 30... f5?.} (36. h4 $5) 36... hxg4 37. hxg4 Rxa7 ({It is bad to play} 37... fxg4 $2 38. Nh2 {(Chandler)} Rxa7 39. Nxg4+ Ke6 40. Qxa7 {and wins.}) ({Matanovic suggested} 37... f4 38. Z0 (38. Rxa8 Qxa8 ({but} 38... Bxa8 $1 39. Ba7 Qc8 {is stronger, with counterplay}) 39. Qxa8 Bxa8 40. Bb6 {, when White merely has the better endgame.}) ({. Therefore} 38. Bb6 $1 {is correct, and if} Qc6 ({Black cannot play} 38... Qc8 39. Be4 $1 Qxg4+ 40. Kf1) ({, or} 38... Rxa7 39. Qxa7 Qa8 40. g5+ Kf7 41. Bxg6+ $1 Kxg6 42. Qxe7 Bxf3 43. Qxd6+ {with a decisive attack}) {, then now} 39. Rxa8 Qxa8 40. Qxa8 Bxa8 41. Kf1 {, beginning the calm conversion of his advantage.})) 38. Qxa7 f4 {Black did not have a great deal of choice:} ({he could either suffocate slowly in an ending –} 38... Qa8 39. Qxa8 Bxa8 40. gxf5 gxf5 41. Nh4 $1 e4 42. Nf1 Ke6 43. Ng2 Bf6 (43... Ng5 44. Nf4+ Kd7 45. Bd1) 44. Nf4+ Kf7 45. Ne2 {etc}) ({or perish in the fire of the attack –} 38... fxg4 $2 39. Nh2 Qc8 (39... Be6 40. Ne4+ Kg7 41. Nxd6 $18) 40. Ne4+ Ke6 41. Nxd6 $1 Bxd6 (41... Kxd6 42. Bc5+) 42. Qxh7 $18) ({or} 38... Be6 39. Nh4 $1 Qd7 40. Qa8 Bd8 (40... Qc8 41. Qh1 $1 Qg8 42. Qb7 $1) 41. gxf5 (41. f4 $5) 41... gxf5 42. Qg2 Qf7 43. f4 $1 Qh5 (43... e4 44. Nxf5 $1) 44. Qa8 $1 {etc. All that remains for Spassky is to hope for a miracle, and... it nearly happens!}) (38... Bc6 39. Nh4 Bd7 40. Bh6 $18) 39. Bxf4 $6 {All the commentators gave this move an exclamation mark, Chandler,M: '!'} ({but} 39. Bb6 {would have won more simply; for example:} Z0 (39... Qa8 40. g5+ Kf7 41. Bxg6+ $1 {(as in the note to Black's 37th move)}) (39... Qc6 {(Razuvaev)} 40. g5+ Ke6 (40... Kf7 41. Bd8) 41. Bxg6 Nf8 42. Bf5+ $1) ({, or} 39... Qc8 {(Chandler) Chandler,M: '!'} 40. Be4 $1 (40. Ne4+ $5) 40... Bxe4 (40... Qxg4+ 41. Kf1 Be6 42. Qb8) 41. Nxe4+ Ke6 42. Nxd6 $1 Qc6 43. Nxe5 $1 Qxd6 44. Bd4 $1 Nf6 45. Qb7 {etc.})) 39... exf4 $6 ({Only} 39... Bxf3 {, exchanging one of the formidable white knights, would have left Black with chances of a defence:} 40. Z0 ({. After} 40. Bxe5+ dxe5 41. Nxf3 Ng5 42. Nxg5 Kxg5 43. Qe3+ Kf6 44. Qb6+ ({or} 44. Qh6 Bf8 45. g5+ Ke6) 44... Kg7 45. Qe6 {(Soltis)} Qf7 46. Qxe5+ Qf6 ({or} 46... Bf6 {, he might save the ending with opposite-coloured bishops})) ({, so White should retain all the minor pieces –} 40. Nxf3 $5 exf4 41. Qd4+ Kf7 42. Qxf4+ Kg7 43. g5 Nf8 44. Qd4+ Kh7 45. Qh4+ Kg7 46. Be4 Qf7 47. Nd4 d5 48. Bg2 Nh7 49. f4 Bd8 $1 50. Qe1 Kg8 {, balancing on the edge of the abyss.})) 40. Nh4 $1 {Now White's queen is supported by two mobile knights and his attacking potential rises sharply.} ({He could also have won by} 40. Qd4+ Ke6 41. Qxf4 Kd7 42. Qd4 $1 Qa8 (42... Ke6 43. Bf5+ $1) 43. Bxg6 {etc.}) 40... Bf7 ({There was a slightly more tenacious defence:} 40... Nf8 41. Qd4+ Ke6 42. Nxg6 Nxg6 43. Bf5+ {(Matanovic)} Kf7 44. Qxd5+ $1 ({Chandler's variation} 44. Bxg6+ Kxg6 45. Qxd5 {is unclear in view of Chandler,M: '+-'} Kg7) 44... Kg7 45. Ne4 Nh4 ({or} 45... Ne5 46. Nc5 $1 Nf3+ 47. Qxf3 dxc5 48. bxc5 Bxc5 49. Qxf4) 46. Qd4+ Kh6 47. g5+ $1 Bxg5 48. Qxd6+ Kg7 49. Qd4+ $1 Kg8 (49... Kh6 50. Qd7 $1) 50. Bg4 Bd8 51. Kf1 Kh7 52. Qd5 {and Bd7, winning the b5-pawn and the game.}) 41. Qd4+ Ke6 ({Of course not} 41... Kg5 $2 42. Qg7 $1 {Chandler,M: ''} Kxh4 43. Qh6+ Kxg4 44. f3+ Kg3 45. Qh2# {.}) 42. Nf5 $1 {An elegant leap!} Bf8 ({If} 42... Qc6 {, then} 43. Be4 d5 44. Nf3 $1 Bf6 45. Qa7 $1 {.}) (42... Kd7 43. Qa7+) 43. Qxf4 Kd7 ({If} 43... gxf5 {, the most accurate is} 44. Bxf5+ (44. Qxf5+ Ke7 45. Qxh7 $18) 44... Ke7 45. Bxh7 {and Ne4.}) 44. Nd4 Qe1+ (44... Qe7 45. Nxb5 Ng5 ({or} 45... Nf6) 46. Kf1 {and wins – Soltis.}) 45. Kg2 Bd5+ 46. Be4 Bxe4+ 47. Nxe4 {Now the black queen is cut off from the rest of its army and the game is over.} Be7 48. Nxb5 Nf8 (48... d5 49. Qc7+ Ke6 50. Qc6+ Ke5 51. Ng3 Nf8 52. Nd4 $18) 49. Nbxd6 Ne6 50. Qe5 1-0

