1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Nf3 O-O 6. Be2 e5 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 (8. Be3 {– Game Nos. 13 and 60.}) 8... Ne7 9. Ne1 {A move for all times.} ({White has more rarely played} 9. Nd2) ({or} 9. b4 {(currently the most fashionable)}) ({and for a very short time –} 9. Bd2 {(Game Nos.90 and 92).}) 9... Nd7 10. Nd3 ({In the words of Fischer, "The older} 10. Be3 f5 11. f3 f4 12. Bf2 g5 {has been abandoned. Black's kingside attack has practically been worked out to a forced mate!" But towards the end of the century, mainly through the efforts of Korchnoi, this evaluation changed (cf. Game No.29).}) ({Incidentally, to avoid the attack, back in the 1950s Portisch introduced} 10. f3 f5 11. g4 {, and in the mid-1960s Stein found the worthy reply} Nf6 ({better than} 11... f4 $6 12. h4 $1) ({, or} 11... h5 $6 12. g5 h4 13. Nd3 f4 14. Kh1 Kf7 15. c5 $1 {Larsen-Tal, 1st matchgame, Bled 1965}) 12. Ng2 c6 $1 13. Rb1 cxd5 14. cxd5 Bd7 {with counterplay on both wings.}) 10... f5 11. exf5 {This is also what Reshevsky played in the 11th game of his match with Fischer.} ({The latter seriously thought that "} 11. f3 f4 {followed by ...g6-g5 etc gives Black a strong kingside attack." Apparently, under the influence of games such as Najdorf-Gligoric (cf. the note to White's 10th move in Game No. 29). However, already then exceptions had occurred:} 12. Bd2 g5 13. Rc1 Rf7 14. Nb5 Ng6 $6 (14... a6 $1) 15. c5 $1 Nf6 16. Ba5 $5 b6 17. cxb6 axb6 18. Be1 {and Nb4... 1-0 (Najdorf-Uhlmann, Moscow 1956).}) ({And later a more accurate arrangement of the forces was found for White:} 11. Bd2 $1 Nf6 (11... f4 $2 12. Bg4 $1) (11... c5 $5 {Fischer}) (11... Kh8 $5 {with the idea of ...Ng8 is also played, leaving the other knight at d7 as a defence against c4-c5}) 12. f3 f4 ({if} 12... c5 {, then} 13. g4 {and Black does not have play with ...c7-c6}) 13. c5 $1 g5 14. Rc1 Ng6 15. cxd6 cxd6 16. Nb5 Rf7 17. Qc2 $1 (17. Nf2 {Korchnoi-Gligoric, Leningrad Interzonal 1973}) 17... Z0 ({. Now} 17... g4 18. Nc7 gxf3 19. gxf3 Bh3 {is dubious, however, in view of} 20. Ne6 $1 {with advantage to White} ({but not because of} 20. Nxa8 Nxe4 21. fxe4 Qg5+ {with sharp play – in Larsen-Tal, 5th matchgame, Eersel 1969, Black managed to confuse his opponent and gain a spectacular win.})) ({. Therefore all that remained on the agenda was} 17... Ne8 18. a4 h5 (18... Bd7 $6 19. Qb3 {Larsen-Najdorf, Santa Monica 1966}) 19. Nf2 Bf8 20. h3 Rg7 {, and now White plays} 21. a5 {(Polugayevsky-Tal, 7th matchgame, Alma Ata 1980)} ({or} 21. Qb3 Nh4 22. Rc2 {, Ivanchuk-Timman, 4th matchgame, Hilversum 1991}) ({, since nothing is achieved by} 21. Nxa7 Rc7 $1 22. Ba5 Rxc2 23. Bxd8 Rxe2 24. Nxc8 Rxa4 25. Nd3 g4 {with equality, Averkin-Kasparov, Moscow 1979. In short, since Fischer's day the overall understanding of the chances for both sides in this sharp variation has advanced tremendously, and opinion has changed. All that has remained unchanged is the method of counterattack, devised by Gligoric, and White, in preparing an invasion on the queenside, must be extremely attentive to the opponent's threats on the kingside.}))) 11... Nxf5 {"In this line White gets a grip on e4, Black on d4.} ({"} 11... gxf5 {is more energetic, " wrote Fischer, but he himself took with the knight, preferring a clearer game.}) 12. f3 Nf6 (12... c6 $5 {Kavalek}) ({The aforementioned Reshevsky-Fischer game went} 12... Nd4 13. Ne4 b6 14. Bg5 (14. Bd2 $5 {Fischer}) 14... Qe8 15. Bd2 a5 16. Re1 Nxe2+ 17. Qxe2 h6 18. b3 g5 19. a3 Qg6 20. b4 (20. Ndf2 $1 {is more solid}) ({, but not Geller's recommendation} 20. g4 $6 {in view of} Qf7 $1 21. Kg2 a4 $1 {, and if} 22. b4 {, then} b5 $1 23. cxb5 Nb6) 20... Nf6 21. bxa5 $6 g4 {with excellent play for Black.}) 13. Nf2 Nd4 (13... c6 $5 {Bukic}) 14. Nfe4 Nh5 $6 {An indication of Black's aggressive mood.} ({A simpler alternative was} 14... c5 15. Be3 a6 16. a4 Nh5 {1/2-1/2  (Petrosian-Geller, 12th round)}) ({or} 14... Nxe4 15. Nxe4 Bf5 16. Bd3 c5 {, and "the knight at d4 ensures Black sufficient counter-chances." (Geller)}) 15. Bg5 Qd7 16. g3 $1 {Cutting off the knight at h5.} h6 ({The 11th round game Tal-Gligoric went} 16... c5 17. Nb5 $1 Nxb5 18. cxb5 {with some advantage to White.}) ({In the present game he also made use of the b5-square, and so} 16... a6 $5 {comes into consideration, with the same intention of ...c7-c5.}) 17. Be3 c5 $6 {The prelude to an interesting, but not altogether correct combination. "I was informed that Gligoric thought I had blundered a pawn, but it is a deliberate sac.} ({"On} 17... Nxe2+ 18. Qxe2 g5 19. c5 {White has it all his own way." (Fischer).}) ({However, in the light of what follows,} 17... Qe7 {was safer.}) 18. Bxd4 $1 exd4 19. Nb5 a6 $1 ({Of course not} 19... Be5 $2 20. f4 {.}) 20. Nbxd6 d3 $1 {The key moment of the game.} 21. Qxd3 $6 ({"A double-edged game would result from} 21. Bxd3 Bd4+ 22. Kh1 Nxg3+ 23. Nxg3 Qxd6 24. Z0 (24. Qc2 Bh3 {," writes Fischer}) ({, ignoring the move} 24. Qd2 $1 {, after which it is bad to play} g5 $6 (24... Bh3 $2 {now loses to} 25. Qxh6 Bxf1 26. Bxg6) ({, while after} 24... Kh7 25. Qg2 b5 26. Rae1 {Black does not have sufficient compensation for the pawn}) 25. Qg2 $1 Bd7 26. f4 $1 Rxf4 27. Rxf4 Qxf4 28. Rf1 Qe3 (28... Qg4 29. Be2 $1) (28... Qe5 29. Qf3 $1) 29. Bf5 {with a powerful attack on the light squares.})) (21. Bxd3 Bd4+ 22. Kg2 Nxg3 (22... Qh3+ $2 23. Kh1) 23. Nxg3 Qxd6 24. Qc2 $1 {is also quite good for White}) ({and in addition he has the tempting positional exchange sacrifice} 21. Bxd3 Bd4+ 22. Rf2 $5 {. Hübner gives the variation} Nf6 23. Nxc8 ({possibly he does better to preserve one of his knights from exchange:} 23. Qe2 $5 Nxe4 24. Nxe4 Qg7 25. Kg2 Bxf2 26. Nxf2 Bd7 27. Qc2 {, also with favourable prospects}) 23... Raxc8 24. Kg2 Nxe4 25. Bxe4 Bxf2 26. Kxf2 {with a bishop and two pawns for a rook and "White at any rate has the more comfortable game, even though I don't know how high to estimate his winning chances."}) 21... Bd4+ ({"A mistake would be} 21... Bxb2 22. Nxc8 Bxa1 23. Nb6 {and it's all over (} Bd4+ $2 24. Qxd4 {). The combination requires intricate footwork." (Fischer). Also by White!}) 22. Kg2 $2 ({"After} 22. Kh1 Nxg3+ 23. Nxg3 Qxd6 {White is weak on all the squares and his kingside looks like Swiss cheese. Chances would be even," writes Fischer, and indeed, after} 24. Qd2 Rf4 ({or} 24... Kh7 25. Rad1) 25. Bd3 $1 {the position would have been roughly equal.}) ({However, here too there was the move} 22. Rf2 $5 {, which, in the opinion of Boleslavsky, would have given White an obvious advantage, and in the opinion of Geller – a "decisive" one! Hübner is not so categorical, commenting that after} Nf6 ({if} 22... Bxb2 23. Rb1 Bd4 24. Rb6 {the weakness of the g6-point is perceptible}) 23. Nxf6+ Rxf6 24. Ne4 Z0 (24... Rf8 25. Z0 (25. Raf1 {"it is obvious that White has the more comfortable game." True, after} Qg7 $1 {and ...Bf5(h3) Black is closer to equalizing}) ({, than after} 25. Kg2 $1 Bxf2 26. Kxf2 {.} (26. Z0))) ({. But, on the other hand, a move earlier he too could have played rather more strongly –} 24... Rb6 $1 {; for example:} 25. Rd1 Qg7 26. b3 Bf5 27. Kg2 Bxe4 28. fxe4 Bxf2 29. Kxf2 Qe5 {with an unclear game.})) ({instead, White can play} 22. Rf2 Nf6 23. Nxc8 Raxc8 24. Kg2 Bxf2 25. Nxf2 Kg7 26. Qc3 {with some advantage. But in any case after 21 Qxd3 his queen and bishop are more awkwardly placed that in the variations with 21 Bxd3! analysed above. Thus it was on the 21st move that Gligoric missed his best chance of fighting for an advantage.}) 22... Nxg3 $1 {"This is the resource it was necessary to visualize as far back as move 17." (Fischer). Like, however, both 21 Bxd3! and 22 Rf2!?.} 23. Nxc8 $5 {"Best.} ({"On} 23. Nxg3 {,} Qxd6 {again is good."  (Fischer). The king at g2 is badly placed and after} 24. Qd2 Rf4 $1 {White's position does indeed resemble "Swiss cheese":} 25. Ne4 ({or} 25. Kh1 Rh4 26. f4 Bh3 27. Rf3 Re8) 25... Qe5 26. Bd3 g5 $1 {with excellent compensation for the pawn.}) 23... Nxf1 24. Nb6 $1 Qc7 $1 {"Blow for blow! The threat of mate on h2 keeps the exchange." (Fischer)} 25. Rxf1 Qxb6 26. b4 $1 {The saving move.} Qxb4 ({The last debatable moment. Fischer rightly points out} 26... cxb4 27. c5 $1 Bxc5 28. Nxc5 {etc}) ({but then he writes: "The only other try is} 26... Rf7 27. bxc5 Bxc5 28. Rb1 {followed by d5-d6 with tons of play." Hübner evaluates this position in favour of Black and gives} Qc7 29. d6 (29. Rxb7 $2 Qxb7 30. Nxc5 Qb2 {and wins}) 29... Qc6 30. d7 (30. Kh1 Kh7 $1 31. f4 ({or} 31. Rd1 b6) 31... Raf8 32. Bf3 Qc8 {etc is no better – G.K.}) 30... Rd8 {. However, after} 31. Qd5 $1 Qxd5 32. cxd5 b6 33. Bxa6 Rdxd7 34. a4 {White can hope for a draw.}) 27. Rb1 Qa5 28. Nxc5 $1 (28. Rxb7 $2 Rf7 {Fischer.}) 28... Qxc5 29. Qxg6+ Bg7 30. Rxb7 Qd4 {"The only move. Gligoric was so sure I'd 'find' it that he wrote it down on his score-sheet while I was taking a minute to look for something better." (Fischer)} 31. Bd3 Rf4 32. Qe6+ ({Playing for a win –} 32. d6 {is too dangerous:} Qf6 33. c5 Qxg6+ 34. Bxg6 Rd8 35. Be4 Be5 36. d7 Kf7 37. c6 Ke6 {.}) 32... Kh8 33. Qg6 1/2-1/2

[Event "New York/Los Angeles (match)"]
