1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. e3 O-O 6. Bd3 Nc6 7. a3 ({Reshevsky knew of Fischer's weakness for set-ups with ...Bxc3, otherwise he would have chosen the more logical} 7. O-O $1 {, in order after} dxc4 8. Bxc4 Bd6 {to find a more useful move than} 9. a3 {.}) 7... Bxc3+ ({The standard operation} 7... dxc4 $1 8. Bxc4 Bd6 {and ...e6-e5 (the Ragozin Defence) is undoubtedly better.} 9. Z0 ({. In the Botvinnik-Tal return match, which took place four months before the battle of the American stars, White did not gain anything from the opening after} 9. Nb5 e5 10. Nxd6 Qxd6 11. dxe5 Qxd1+ 12. Kxd1 Ng4 13. Ke2 Ncxe5 14. Bd5 c6 15. Be4 Be6 {(1st game)}) ({, or} 9. b4 e5 10. Bb2 Bg4 11. dxe5 ({or} 11. d5 Ne7 {– 3rd game}) 11... Nxe5 12. Be2 Qe7 13. Nb5 Rfd8 {(5th game).}) (9. e4 e5 10. d5 Ne7 11. O-O Ng6 {is also not dangerous for Black}) ({, while the attempt to prevent ...e6-e5 by} 9. Bb5 {is groundless in view of a well-known pawn sacrifice –} e5 $1 10. Bxc6 exd4 $1 {(a motif from the game Capablanca-Ragozin, Moscow 1936)} 11. Nxd4 ({or} 11. exd4 bxc6 {with equality}) 11... bxc6 12. Nxc6 Qe8 13. Nd4 Rb8 14. f3 Rb6 15. b4 Bd7 {and it is hard for White to halt the attack that is building up against his king.})) 8. bxc3 Na5 9. Nd2 c5 10. O-O {White delays the exchange of pawns, inviting the opponent to declare his intentions.} ({After} 10. cxd5 exd5 11. O-O {the obvious} Be6 ({would be more useful than} 11... b6 {.})) 10... b6 $6 {A second opening error,} ({which is all the more inexplicable, since in a game played the previous year Torgbergsson-Fischer (Reykjavik 1960) Black had already followed the correct course:} 10... dxc4 $1 11. Nxc4 Nxc4 12. Bxc4 Qc7 13. Qe2 e5 14. Bb2 e4 (14... Re8 $1 15. h3 Be6 {is even better}) 15. h3 Bf5 16. Rfd1 Rac8 17. a4 cxd4 18. Rxd4 Rfd8 {with equality.}) 11. cxd5 exd5 12. f3 Re8 13. Re1 Be6 ({In the event of} 13... Bb7 {(recommended by Mednis)} 14. Nf1 Qc7 15. Ra2 c4 ({or} 15... Rad8 16. Ng3 a6 17. Rae2 Nc6 18. Bb2 Ne7 19. e4 $1) 16. Bb1 Nb3 17. Ng3 {the black bishop would have been unable to prevent e3-e4, and would have remained a passive observer of the events taking place on the kingside.}) 14. Ra2 Rc8 ({The immediate} 14... Qd7 {was interesting} 15. Z0 ({, in order to reply to the thematic} 15. g4 $6 {with the stunning} cxd4 16. cxd4 Ne4 $1 {, greatly sharpening the situation:} 17. fxe4 dxe4 18. Bb1 Bxg4 $1 19. Qc2 Rac8 20. Qb2 Nc4 $1 21. Nxc4 Rxc4 {and White's king is in grave danger.}) ({. Therefore he should switch to the queenside –} 15. a4 cxd4 ({if} 15... a6 {, then} 16. Ba3) ({, while after} 15... Bf5 {there is the unpleasant} 16. Bb5 Nc6 17. dxc5 $1 bxc5 18. Nb3) 16. cxd4 Bf5 17. Bb5 Nc6 18. Ba3 {, retaining the initiative.})) 15. Nf1 cxd4 16. cxd4 {The critical position.} h5 $6 {With Roman straightforwardness Fischer prevents g2-g4 and the activation of the white knight via g3. Mednis attaches a question mark to this move and calls it "the losing moment". Certainly, such weakening moves are not usually good, but this evaluation is too severe and it obviously comes from the result of the game.} ({"There was no need for suicidal actions," writes Mednis. "} 16... Qd7 {seems as logical a move as any." Indeed, this is the most natural and, apparently, the best move. Black intends to play 17...Bf5 and restrict the white pawns in the centre. And the planned} 17. g4 {encounters the already familiar tactical thrust} Ne4 $5 {. If White accepts the challenge –} 18. fxe4 dxe4 19. Bb1 {, he encounters the paradoxical} Bxg4 $1 {, creating confusion among his pieces on the queenside:} 20. Qd2 ({after} 20. Rae2 $2 Bf3 $1 21. Ng3 h5 {Black's threats can hardly be parried}) 20... Nb3 21. Qb2 Z0 ({, and here Black can choose between the unexpected return} 21... Be6 $5 22. Ng3 f5 23. Bd2 Nxd2 24. Qxd2 Bxa2 25. Bxa2+ Kh8 26. Rf1 g6 27. Ne2 {with an unusual, double-edged position (I would nevertheless prefer to have White)}) ({, and the even more unexpected move} 21... Bd1 $1 {, leading to a highly picturesque position. After} 22. Rxd1 ({forced, since} 22. Bd2 $2 Qg4+ 23. Ng3 h5 {is bad for White}) 22... Qg4+ 23. Qg2 Qxd1 24. Bc2 {Black has a dazzling array of possibilities:} Z0 ({. The timid} 24... Qf3 $6 {gives White the better endgame –} 25. Qxf3 exf3 26. Bxb3 Rxc1 27. Kf2 {.}) (24... Qxc1 25. Bxb3 Rc3 26. Bd5 Rxe3 {suggests itself, but allows a pretty combinative attack –} 27. Rc2 $1 Qxa3 28. Ra2 $1 Qd3 29. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 30. Rxa7+ Re7 31. Rxe7+ Kxe7 32. Qg5+ Ke6 33. Qxe3 Kd5 34. Qg5+ Kc4 35. d5 {with winning chances.} (35. Z0)) ({. The simple} 24... Rxc2 $1 {leads immediately to a draw –} 25. Qxc2 Qg4+ 26. Kf2 Qf5+ {.}) ({. The subtle} 24... Qh5 {maintains the tension after} 25. Bxb3 Rxc1 26. Rf2 Re7 {.}))) ({Therefore the restrained} 16... Qd7 17. g4 Ne4 18. Rg2 {is preferable:} Nd6 $1 ({after the 'active'} 18... Nc3 $6 {the knight merely gets in the way:} 19. Qc2 h5 20. h3 {etc}) 19. Ng3 {with somewhat the better chances} ({but not immediately} 19. e4 $6 {on account of} dxe4 20. fxe4 Bc4 21. Bc2 Nb5 22. Bb2 Bxf1 23. Kxf1 Nc4 24. Ba4 a6 {.})) 17. h3 h4 {Restricting White's active possibilities to the minimum: now all he can aim for is the standard e3-e4.} 18. Rf2 Qd7 19. e4 $1 {White cannot delay – otherwise 19... Bf5.} dxe4 20. fxe4 Bb3 ({"White's central pawn advance is based on some sharp tactics. If} 20... Qxd4 $2 21. e5 Red8 ({either rook;} 21... Nd5) ({, or} 21... Nd7 {loses the queen after} 22. Bh7+) 22. exf6 Qxd3 23. Rd2 {winning a rook!"  (Mednis)}) ({But the consequences of the resolute} 20... Qxd4 $5 21. Z0 ({. Mednis made a mistake on the very first move:} 21. e5 {is weak on account of} Rxc1 $1 22. Qxc1 (22. Bh7+ $4 Nxh7 23. Qxd4 Rxe1 {and wins}) 22... Ng4 $1 23. hxg4 Qxd3 {and, thanks to the opponent's broken pawns, Black has good chances of saving the game.}) ({. Apparently, in search of a serious advantage White would have had to play for an attack –} 21. Bb2 {, and each of the queen moves has its drawbacks:} Z0 (21... Qc5 22. e5 $1 ({the primitive} 22. Bxf6 $2 gxf6 23. Ne3 Nc6 {allows Black to consolidate}) 22... Nd7 23. Qh5 Nf8 24. Nd2 $1 {and White storms the opponent's kingside unhindered.}) (21... Qd8 {. This forces White to display the utmost ingenuity: the pressurizing} 22. Ne3 {allows} Rc5 $1 {(preventing the dangerous sortie of the white queen to h5)} 23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. Rf4 Qd6 $1 {, and he must play} 25. e5 (25. Rxh4 Qg3) ({, or} 25. Rxf6 Qd4 26. Rh6 Rd8 {is inferior}) 25... Qxe5 26. Rxh4 Qg5 27. Nf5 $1 Rd8 $1 28. Rxe6 $1 Rc1 $1 29. Ne7+ Kf8 30. Rh8+ Kg7 31. Rh7+ Kf8 32. Rh8+ {with an elegant perpetual check.}))) ({Only the sudden exchange sacrifice –} 20... Qxd4 21. Bb2 Qd8 22. Rxf6 $1 gxf6 23. Qh5 {enables White to disclose the main defect of Black's position – the weakness of his king's defences, caused by the absence of the pawn at h7. The main role in the rapidly developing attack is played by the bishop at b2, although White's entire army takes part in the action:} Z0 (23... Bc4 $2 {loses immediately to} 24. Ne3 $3 {(the knight immediately aims for the weak f5-square)} Rc5 (24... Bxd3 25. Nf5 $1) 25. Qg4+ $1 Rg5 26. Qf4 {.}) (23... Nc4 24. Bxc4 Bxc4 {– White's task is somewhat more complicated, but in the end here too Black's downfall is caused by the weakness of f5:} 25. Qg4+ Kf8 26. Qxh4 Re6 27. Ne3 $1 Qd2 28. Bc1 Qc3 29. Qh8+ Ke7 30. Qxc8 Qxe1+ 31. Kh2 Qxc1 32. Nf5# {.}) (23... Rc5 24. Qh6 Re5 {– this attempt to block the diagonal of the rampant bishop at b2 does not halt White's furious onslaught:} 25. Bb5 $1 Rf8 26. Ng3 $3 {(the control of the g3-square proves illusory!)} hxg3 (26... Rxb5 27. e5 $1 Rxb2 28. Nh5 Rxg2+ 29. Kxg2 Qd5+ 30. Kh2 Qa2+ 31. Kg1 {leads to a rapid mate}) (26... Qe7 27. Re3 {does not change anything}) 27. Re3 Rg5 $1 28. Bxf6 Qd1+ 29. Bf1 Qh5 $1 30. Qxg5+ Qxg5 31. Bxg5 {and Black, despite all his trickery, has a lost endgame.}) (23... Nc6 {is the most natural reply, but it runs into} 24. Bb5 $1 {. Now, for even the temporary blocking of the b2-bishop, Black is forced to return the exchange:} Ne5 25. Rd1 Qe7 26. Bxe8 Rxe8 27. Qxh4 {and the win for White is merely a matter of time. Even so, 20...Qxd4 (the capture of a central pawn!) had some practical sense: the probability of the move 21 e5, allowing Black to simplify the game, was very great. Also, the attack involving the exchange sacrifice on f6 was far from obvious and demanded considerable determination on the part of White.})) 21. Qd2 Bc4 ({Now in the event of} 21... Qxd4 22. Z0 ({, the attack with} 22. Bb2 Qc5 23. Bxf6 gxf6 24. e5 {is parried by} Qc3 $1 {.}) ({. On the other hand, after} 22. e5 Rxc1 $1 ({the only move: as pointed out by Mednis,} 22... Red8 $2 23. exf6 Qxd3 24. Re8+ $1 {is bad for Black}) ({, as is} 22... Rxe5 $2 23. Bh7+ Nxh7 24. Qxd4 Rxe1 25. Bh6 $1 gxh6 26. Qg4+ {, when the queen fully reveals its power}) 23. Qxc1 Ng4 24. hxg4 Qxd3 {White, exploiting the unfortunate position of the bishop at b3, quickly launches an offensive –} 25. Nd2 $1 Be6 26. Ne4 {etc.})) ({An imperceptible change in the position (just one move!) brings Black not only new difficulties, but also new hopes –} 21... Nc4 $5 22. Z0 ({. After the exchange of queens –} 22. Qb4 $6 Qa4 23. Qxa4 Bxa4 {he is able to gain strong counterplay:} 24. Bg5 Nxa3 25. Ra1 Rc3 26. Rf3 b5 27. e5 Nd5 $1 {and the black pieces appear to be insecurely placed, but it is not clear how to exploit this (} 28. Be4 Rxf3 29. gxf3 Nc2 $1 {).}) ({. However, after} 22. Qg5 $1 Qxd4 ({or} 22... Nh7 23. Qxh4 Qxd4 24. Qg3 Rc6 25. e5 $1) 23. Bxc4 {White retains the advantage and good attacking chances; for example:} Bxc4 ({or} 23... Rxc4 24. Bb2 Qxf2+ 25. Kxf2 Rc2+ 26. Kg1 Rxb2 27. Qxh4) 24. Bb2 Nh7 25. Qg4 $1 f5 26. Qxh4 Rxe4 27. Rxe4 Qxe4 28. Qg3 Qe7 29. Ne3 $1 Be6 30. Nxf5 Bxf5 31. Rxf5 {etc.})) 22. Bc2 Nb3 ({The exchange of the light-squared bishops by} 22... Bb3 {does not succeed on account of} 23. Bb1 {, when the invasion} Nc4 {is now completely pointless in view of} 24. Qd3 {.}) 23. Bxb3 Bxb3 24. e5 Nd5 25. Qg5 Qe7 $6 {This unnecessary defensive move (all the same the h4-pawn cannot be saved) is the prelude to a blunder.} ({The queen was ideally placed at d7, and Black should have attended to the rapid return of his bishop for the reinforcement of his kingside –} 25... Bc2) ({or first} 25... Rc2 {, provoking an exchange of rooks.}) 26. Qg4 Rc6 ({The alternative} 26... Rc7 {defends against the invasion of the queen, but then the manoeuvre} 27. Nd2 Bc2 28. Nf3 {is very strong, after which White not only wins a pawn, but also increases his attacking potential.}) 27. Bg5 ({In my opinion, here too} 27. Nd2 Bc2 28. Nf3 {was preferable.}) 27... Qxa3 $4 ({The struggle would have lasted much longer if Black had given up the pawn –} 27... Qe6 28. Qxh4 Bc2 29. Nh2 Rc3 30. Nf3 (30. Ng4 Bg6) 30... Qg6 31. Bc1 Bf5 {, relying on the light-squared blockade and the opposite-coloured bishops in a possible endgame. His position would by no means have been hopeless, and one can only guess what it was that caused Bobby to take a truly suicidal decision.}) 28. Qd7 $1 {. Either of the black rooks could easily have defended the king against this attack, but the rooks are forced to defend each other. And now, with the loss of the f7-pawn, the king's fortress is destroyed and resistance becomes hopeless.} 1-0

[Event "Curacao Candidates"]
