[SourceQuality "1"]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 e5 {In the middle of the 20th century this ancient move of La Bourdonnais was considered 'provocative': the weakening of the d5-square!} 5. Nb5 (5. Nxc6 {– Volume 1, Game No.1.}) 5... a6 {A risky attempt by Black to relieve his problems with the help of an early ... d7-d5.} ({Nowadays they play} 5... d6 {, not fearing the weakness at d5 and the fixing of the backward d6-pawn after c2-c4.}) 6. Nd6+ Bxd6 7. Qxd6 Qf6 8. Qd1 {Avoiding the early exchange of queens,} ({Avoiding} 8. Qxf6 Nxf6 9. Nc3) (8. Qc7 Nge7 9. Nc3 Nb4 10. Bd3) ({and} 8. Qa3 Qg6 9. Be3 Qxe4 10. Nc3 Qb4 11. Qxb4 Nxb4 12. O-O-O Ne7 13. Bc5 {(Spassky-Utyatsky, Kislovodsk 1960) are judged by theory to favour White.}) 8... Qg6 9. Nc3 Nge7 ({After} 9... Nf6 $6 {the unexpected return} 10. Qd6 $1 {is strong, practically forcing the now clearly advantageous exchange of queens:} Qg4 11. f3 Qe6 12. Qxe6+ dxe6 13. Be3 {etc.}) 10. h4 (10. Be3 d5 $1) 10... h5 11. Bg5 d5 $1 12. Bxe7 $6 ({As it later transpired, the correct continuation is} 12. exd5 Nb4 {, and now} 13. Bxe7 ({but not} 13. d6 $6 {, A.Nikitin-Gufeld, 26th USSR Championship, Tbilisi 1959,} Nxc2+ 14. Kd2 Bf5 15. dxe7 Qd6+ 16. Ke2 Nd4+ 17. Ke1 Nc2+ {with perpetual check}) 13... Kxe7 14. Bd3 $1 ({if} 14. d6+ Kd8 15. Bd3 Nxd3+ 16. Qxd3 Qxd3 17. cxd3 {, then} Rh6 $1 18. Ne4 Bf5 {with equality}) 14... Nxd3+ 15. Qxd3 Qxd3 16. cxd3 {with somewhat the better ending.}) 12... d4 $1 {A strong intermediate move, that was underestimated by Fischer.} 13. Bg5 $6 ({White can hardly be satisfied with} 13. Ne2) ({but} 13. Bc5 {(preventing castling)} dxc3 14. f3 {is better, although here too Black has a comfortable game.}) 13... dxc3 14. bxc3 ({After} 14. f3 cxb2 15. Rb1 f6 16. Be3 {White has to reckon with} f5 $5 {.}) 14... Qxe4+ 15. Be2 ({If} 15. Qe2 {, then} Qa4 $1 ({although Tal, to judge by his mood, would probably have played} 15... Qxe2+ 16. Bxe2 Be6) 16. Qc4 Qa5 {was good.}) 15... f6 {This and Black's next move indicate that he is striving to obtain 'simply' a favourable endgame.} ({He could have fought for a real advantage with} 15... Bf5 $5 {.}) 16. Be3 Bg4 (16... Qg6 $5) 17. Qd3 $1 {Practically forced.} Qxd3 18. cxd3 Bxe2 19. Kxe2 O-O-O {"Tal's difficult opening experiment has succeeded," writes the master Alexander Chistyakov. Indeed the ending is slightly better for Black. However, for the moment there is one faint consolation for Bobby – White has a bishop against a knight.} 20. Rad1 ({In the light of White's intended plan,} 20. Rhd1 {is more accurate.}) 20... Ne7 21. d4 $5 {Typical of Fischer with his straightforward, aggressive style and dislike of passive defence. He is not afraid of creating new weaknesses in his position – the main thing for him is to open up the game!} Nd5 (21... exd4 {was good}) ({or even} 21... Rhe8 $1 {, since} 22. dxe5 {is poor on account of} Nd5 $1 {.}) 22. Rc1 Rhe8 23. Rhd1 f5 {When playing Fischer it is dangerous to give scope to his bishops!} ({It was simpler to consolidate Black's minimal advantage by} 23... exd4 24. cxd4+ (24. Rxd4 $6 Re5 $1) 24... Kd7 {with a classic knight on the blockading d5-square (} 25. Rc5 Ke6 {, then ...b7-b6, ...Rd7 etc). Karpov would probably have considered that the technical phase had already begun...}) 24. Bg5 $1 {Immediately exploiting the resulting opening.} Rd7 $6 ({"Tal decided not to take a risk, since if} 24... exd4+ {, then} 25. Kd3 $1 dxc3 {(? – G.K.)} 26. Bxd8 Rxd8 27. Kc4 b5+ 28. Kc5 {is very strong." (Chistyakov)}) ({But Tal did not need to sacrifice the exchange –} 24... exd4+ 25. Kd3 Rd6 $1 26. cxd4+ Kd7 {was correct with an excellent game (in analogy with the note to Black's 23rd move).}) 25. dxe5 $1 {Achieving a qualitative change in the position: White gets rid of his blockaded d4-pawn and opens the file for an attack on the knight.} Rxe5+ 26. Kf3 {"Now Black's kingside is weakened and the position of his knight at d5 is not secure. A turning point in the game has occurred: White begins playing for a win." (Chistyakov). However, the safety margin of Black's defences is still very great.} Re4 (26... Nc7 {was also possible, with the idea of ...Ne6}) ({or} 26... Nb6 {intending ...Nc4.}) 27. Rd3 $5 ({But not the insipid} 27. Rd4 Rxd4 28. cxd4+ Rc7 $1 {.}) 27... Rc4 $1 ({Chistyakov condemns this and recommends} 27... Nb6 {with equality, but the computer approves of Tal's move!}) 28. Rcd1 $1 {Again the best chance: by sacrificing a pawn, Bobby begins to confuse the opponent in his time-trouble.} Rxc3 29. Rxc3+ Nxc3 30. Rc1 Rc7 $1 {For the moment Tal is equal to the occasion.} ({In the instructive variation} 30... Rd3+ $2 31. Kf4 Kd7 32. Kxf5 Nxa2 33. Re1 $1 Kc6 34. Kg6 Rd7 35. Re7 $1 {the superiority of the bishop over the knight would tell: White captures on g7 and after g2-g4 he advances his h-pawn unhindered.}) 31. Bf4 Rc6 32. Be5 {Another interesting moment.} Nd5 ({"} 32... Nxa2 {would not have saved Black: after} 33. Rxc6+ bxc6 34. Bxg7 {(? – G.K.)} Z0 (34... c5 {(? – G.K.)} 35. Kf4 c4 36. Kxf5 c3 37. g4 $3 c2 38. Bb2 hxg4 39. h5 {White wins." (Chistyakov)}) ({. But} 34... Kd7 $1 {(to the defence of the f5-pawn!) would have led to a simple draw:} 35. Kf4 Ke6 36. Bd4 $1 ({or} 36. Kg5 c5 37. Kxh5 c4 38. Kg5 c3 39. h5 c2 40. Bb2 c1=Q+ 41. Bxc1 Nxc1 42. h6 Nd3 $1 43. f4 $1 Kf7 44. Kxf5 a5 45. Ke4) 36... c5 $1 37. Bxc5 Kf6 {and ...Kg6.})) ({Therefore the only correct way is} 32... Nxa2 33. Rxc6+ bxc6 34. Kf4 $1 g6 35. Kg5 Kd7 36. Kxg6 Ke6 37. Bd4 $1 {followed by Kxh5 and the advance of the h-pawn.}) 33. Rd1 $1 {In trying to reach the kingside pawns, "Fischer follows the principle 'the threat is stronger than the attack.'} ({"Nothing would be achieved by} 33. Rxc6+ bxc6 34. Bxg7 Kd7 {, when there is nowhere for the white king to break through."  (Chistyakov).} (34... c5 $1 35. Ke2 c4 {is even more obvious.})) 33... Nf6 $6 {The knight should have stayed at d5, not allowing the white king to f4.} ({After} 33... Rc5 34. Bxg7 Kd7 {(Chistyakov)} ({or} 34... b5 {White's activity might have come to nothing.})) ({The commentators also pointed out} 33... Kd7 $1 34. Rxd5+ (34. Bxg7 Kd6) 34... Ke6 35. Ra5 b6 36. Rxa6 Kxe5 {with a draw. However, the knight retreat does not yet lose, but merely testifies (together with Black's next few moves) to Tal's poor form.}) 34. Kf4 $1 g6 $6 {An 'automatic' time-trouble move – the reflex defence of the pawn.} (34... Rc2 $1 {was more energetic. According to Chistyakov, "this is refuted by the subtle} 35. Bd4 $1 {, when the white king destroys Black's kingside pawns." This is how myths are born! After} Rxa2 36. Kxf5 b5 {Black's position is not worse; for example:} 37. Kg6 b4 38. Kxg7 Ng4 39. Kg6 (39. f3 Rxg2 $1) 39... Nxf2 40. Rc1+ Kd7 41. Kxh5 Ra5+ $5 ({or} 41... a5 42. g4 b3 43. g5 Rd2) 42. Kg6 (42. Kh6 Ng4+) 42... Nd3 {etc.}) 35. f3 $1 {Sensing that he has acquired winning chances, Bobby begins playing with the precision of an automaton.} Nd7 36. Bd6 Rc2 $2 {Too late – now this would appear to be the decisive mistake!} ({It was essential for Black to defend the sixth rank and his g6-pawn with all his might –} 36... Nb6 $1 37. Kg5 Nc4 {; for example:} 38. Be7 ({or} 38. Bf4 b5 {followed by ...b5-b4, ...a6-a5 and so on}) 38... Ne5 $1 39. Bf6 Nd7 40. Bg7 b5 {.}) 37. g3 $1 Re2 $6 ({After} 37... Rxa2 $2 38. Kg5 Nb6 39. Kxg6 Nc4 40. Bf4 {and Kxh5 the h4-pawn would have rapidly advanced towards the queening square.}) ({Instead} 37... Nb6 $1 38. Kg5 Rc6 {was more tenacious, but the extra tempi compared with the position in the note to Black's 36th move would have given White serious chances of success; for example:} 39. Be5 Nd7 40. Bf4 $1 Nc5 41. Kh6 Ne6 42. Be5 Nd8 43. Kg7 {etc.}) 38. Kg5 Re6 39. Bf4 $1 {With the threat of Rd6.} Nf8 {A sad necessity;} ({the rook endgame after} 39... Ne5 40. Bxe5 Rxe5 41. Kxg6 Re3 {is lost for Black:} 42. Rf1 Re2 43. Kxf5 Rxa2 44. g4 $1 Rh2 45. g5 {.}) 40. Rd6 (40. Kh6 $5) 40... a5 ({Or} 40... Re7 41. Kf6 Rc7 42. Bh6 Nd7+ 43. Kxg6 Rc3 44. Kxf5 Rxf3+ 45. Bf4 {and wins.}) 41. Kh6 $1 Re2 (41... Re7 42. Rf6) 42. Rd2 $1 Re7 ({Black is obliged to retreat, since} 42... Rxd2 43. Bxd2 a4 44. Kg7 {does not ease his problems.}) (42... Re6 $2 43. Kg7) 43. Bd6 {The sealed move. After the adjournment, exploiting zugzwang motifs, Fischer accurately converted his advantage.} Rh7+ ({If} 43... Rf7 {, then} 44. f4 ({or} 44. Rd3 b5 45. Rd5) 44... a4 45. Rd3 b5 46. Kg5 Kb7 47. Bb4 {is good.}) 44. Kg5 Rf7 ({"Black would have lost by force after} 44... Nd7 45. Kxg6 Rh8 46. Kg7 Re8 47. Kf7 $3 {– a study-like position." (Chistyakov)}) 45. Rb2 $1 {White forces the opponent to return the pawn and then he creates a passed pawn.} f4 46. Bxf4 Rf5+ 47. Kh6 b5 48. Bd6 b4 (48... Rxf3 49. Rxb5) ({or} 48... Ne6 49. f4 {would also not have saved Black.}) 49. g4 {The most energetic.} Rxf3 50. g5 $1 Ne6 ({Or} 50... Kd7 51. Bxf8 Rxf8 52. Kxg6 Rh8 53. Kf7 Rh7+ 54. Kg8 Re7 55. g6 {and wins.}) 51. Kxg6 Rd3 52. Be5 Re3 53. Kf5 ({Or} 53. Bh2 {and Kxh5.}) 53... Nf8 54. Rg2 Rf3+ 55. Bf4 Kd7 56. g6 Ne6 57. g7 Rxf4+ ({If} 57... Nxf4 {, then} 58. Ke4 $1 {is immediately decisive.}) 58. Ke5 Rf8 59. gxf8=Q Nxf8 60. Kd5 a4 61. Rg7+ Ke8 62. Kd6 b3 63. a3 {. Of course, here we saw merely a pale shadow of Tal, but I have given this game in order to demonstrate the fighting spirit and deadly grip of the young Fischer.} 1-0

