1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 Na5 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 ({In the 1962 Curaçao tournament Keres, one of the greatest experts on the Ruy Lopez, introduced the original variation with} 11... Nd7 {(Volume 3, Game No.64).}) 12. Nbd2 cxd4 ({Another interesting idea of Keres –} 12... Rd8 13. Z0 (13. Nf1 d5 $5 {was successfully tried by him against Boleslavsky in Zürich (1953)} ({and later modernized by} 13... cxd4 14. cxd4 exd4 15. Nxd4 d5)) ({, which led to White needing to play} 13. d5 $1 {immediately. This move, which also occurred in the 1950s, gained acceptance in the Karpov era. After all, the rook at d8 takes away this square from the queen's knight, hindering the classical Rubinstein regrouping (...g7-g6, ... Ne8-g7, ...f7-f6 and ...Nb7-d8-f7).})) 13. cxd4 Bb7 ({At the turn of the 20th-21st centuries the most popular move became} 13... Nc6 $1 {, which was also played on several occasions by Keres in his time.}) 14. Nf1 ({Here too} 14. d5 $1 {is the most unpleasant for Black.} Z0 ({. Now after} 14... Bc8 {White has several good alternatives:} 15. Rb1 {(Bronstein-Geller, 19th USSR Championship, Moscow 1951; Gligoric-Reshevsky, 4th and 6th matchgames, New York 1952)} (15. Re3 {, Panno-Eliskases, Mar del Plata 1958}) ({, and} 15. b4 {, Botvinnik-Eliskases, Leipzig Olympiad 1960})) ({. Not} 14... Rfc8 $6 15. Bd3 Nc4 $2 16. Bxc4 bxc4 17. Re3 a5 18. Rc3 {... 1-0 Fischer-Rossetto, Buenos Aires 1970.}) ({. And the sharp variation} 14... Rac8 15. Bd3 Nd7 16. Nf1 f5 {, known from the game Matanovic-Geller (Zagreb 1958) and later employed by Korchnoi, also does not promise Black an easy life.})) 14... Rac8 15. Bd3 ({Other moves were also at the centre of discussion:} 15. Bb1 {(Geller-Keres, 19th USSR Championship, Moscow 1951)}) ({or} 15. Re2 {(Szily-Keres, Budapest 1952; Gligoric-Keres, Helsinki Olympiad 1952; Fischer-Rossetto, Portoroz Interzonal 1958).}) 15... Nc6 {The correct theoretical paths were only just beginning to be discovered and it is possible that the Estonian grandmaster wanted to confuse his still inexperienced opponent.} ({Otherwise he could have played the move} 15... d5 {(Geller-Keres, Amsterdam Candidates 1956; Petrosian-Trifunovic, Leningrad 1957; Fischer-Janosevic, Belgrade 1958)}) ({or} 15... Nd7 {(Smyslov-Keres, Yugoslavia Candidates 1959), although in both cases it is not to easy to achieve complete equality against the best play by White.}) 16. Ne3 $1 Rfe8 $6 ({"Black already has difficulties. On} 16... Nxd4 17. Nxd4 exd4 18. Nf5 Rfe8 19. Bg5 {is strong.}) ({"Not} 16... Nb4 $2 17. Bb1 Bxe4 $4 18. Bxe4 Nxe4 19. a3 Nc6 20. Nd5 {etc (Fischer).}) ({In Hübner's opinion, in the first line} 16... Nxd4 17. Nxd4 exd4 18. Nf5 {it is better to challenge the troublesome knight immediately –} g6 ({instead of} 18... Rfe8) 19. Nxe7+ (19. Nxd4 Rfe8) 19... Qxe7 20. Bg5 Qe5 {. However, in my view, after} 21. Qd2 $1 ({rather than} 21. f4 Qe6 {followed by ...Rfe8 and "I do not see any reason for Black to be concerned." – Hübner}) 21... Z0 (21... Rfe8 22. Bf4 Qe6 23. f3 ({or} 23. a4 {, Black is still a long way from equalizing.})) ({. The alternative} 21... Nxe4 22. Bxe4 Bxe4 23. f3 f5 24. Bf4 $1 Rc2 25. Qxc2 Bxc2 26. Bxe5 dxe5 27. Rxe5 {leads to a difficult endgame for Black:} d3 ({or} 27... Rd8 28. Re2 d3 29. Rd2) 28. Rd5 Re8 29. Kf1 {and Re1.})) (16... exd4 17. Nf5 d5 {is more interesting –} 18. Z0 ({. If} 18. exd5 Nxd5 19. Nxe7+ Ncxe7 ({not} 19... Ndxe7 $2 {on account of another remake of a typical combination –} 20. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 21. Ng5+ Kg6 22. Qg4 f5 23. Qh4 Ne5 24. Ne6 Qd6 25. Rxe5 $1 Qxe5 26. Qxe7 {and wins}) 20. Nxd4 Rfe8 21. Bd2 Qb6 22. Nf5 Nxf5 23. Bxf5 Rcd8 24. Bg5 Nf6 {White's initiative peters out}) ({, but after} 18. e5 $1 Ne4 19. Bf4 Qd7 20. N3xd4 Nxd4 21. Nxd4 Bc5 22. Be2 {he nevertheless retains some advantage.})) 17. Nf5 $6 {"A superficial plan.} ({"Correct is} 17. d5 $1 Nb4 ({if} 17... Nb8 18. a4 $1) 18. Bb1 a5 19. Qe2 $1 Nd7 ({if} 19... Qb6 20. Nf5 $1) 20. Bd2 Qb6 21. a3 Na6 22. b4 {followed by Bd3 with a bind." (Fischer) But in his youth Bobby did not like closing the position and manoeuvring for a long time behind pawn chains, but preferred active piece play, often giving his opponents the opportunity to free themselves.}) 17... Bf8 18. Bg5 Nd7 19. Rc1 Qb8 20. Bb1 Nxd4 21. N3xd4 Rxc1 $2 {Overlooking White's unexpected trick on the 23rd move.} ({After} 21... exd4 22. Rxc8 (22. Qxd4 Rxc1 23. Bxc1 d5 {with equality}) 22... Qxc8 23. Qxd4 Nc5 $1 {(Fischer)} 24. Bd2 (24. Rc1 Qa8) 24... Qe6 25. f3 Qe5 ({but not} 25... d5 $2 26. Nxg7 $1) {, Black would have solved all his problems:} 26. b4 Ne6 27. Qd3 g6 28. Bc3 Qf4 29. Ne3 Qg3 30. Kf1 Rc8 {etc.}) 22. Bxc1 exd4 23. Nh6+ $1 {An elegant combination;} ({instead of the mechanical} 23. Qxd4 d5) ({or} 23. Nxd4 Nf6 24. f3 d5 {with equality.}) 23... gxh6 24. Qg4+ Kh8 25. Qxd7 {"Black's extra pawn is meaningless in view of his mangled pawn formation." (Fischer)} Bd5 $1 {A clever resource: the bishop is ready to come to the defence of the weakened kingside.} 26. Qf5 $2 {An error not noticed by anyone. The queen was not threatened,} ({and after the quiet developing move} 26. Bf4 $1 {White would have retained a clear advantage:} Bc4 (26... Be6 27. Qc6 Qa8 28. Qc1 $1) ({, or} 26... Qa8 27. Qf5 Be6 28. Qh5 {and Bxh6}) 27. b3 Be6 28. Qc6 Qa8 29. Qb6 Bg7 30. Qxd6 {.}) 26... Re5 $1 27. Qf3 f5 $1 {With a series of energetic moves Keres has succeeded in complicating the position.} 28. Bf4 $1 Re8 $6 ({"On} 28... Re7 29. Qd1 Bxe4 30. Bxe4 fxe4 31. Qxd4+ Bg7 32. Qd5 $1 {maintains pressure against the weak pawns. " (Fischer)}) ({However,} 28... Re6 $1 {was a more reliable way of equalizing; for example:} 29. Qd1 (29. Qh5 Bxe4 30. Bxe4 fxe4 31. Qd5 Qe8 {with equality}) 29... Bxe4 30. Bxe4 ({or} 30. Qxd4+ Bg7 31. Qb4 Qe8 32. Bxd6 Bxb1 33. Rxb1 Re2) 30... Qe8 $1 {(an unexpected zwischenzug)} 31. Qxd4+ Bg7 32. Qd5 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Qxe4 34. Qxd6 Qb1+ 35. Kh2 Qxa2 {and a draw is the likely outcome.}) 29. Qh5 {Fischer attaches an exclamation mark to this move,} ({but Hübner gives} 29. Qd1 $5 Bxe4 30. Bxe4 fxe4 31. Qxd4+ Bg7 32. Qb4 {, with which White picks up another of the central pawns and Black is left with the same 'holes'.}) 29... Bxe4 30. f3 Bc6 31. Rc1 $1 (31. Rxe8 $2 Bxe8 32. Qxf5 $2 Bg6 $1 {and Black wins – Fischer.}) 31... Bd7 32. Bxh6 {"A difficult choice.} ({"The alternative was} 32. Bxf5 Bxf5 33. Qxf5 {with possibilities of probing his sick pawns."  (Fischer). At first sight this would have given him even better chances – say, after} Qb7 34. Bxd6 (34. b4 Kg8 {with the idea of ...Qf7}) 34... Bxd6 35. Qf6+ Qg7 36. Qxd6 Z0 (36... d3 $6 37. Rc3 $1 Rg8 38. g4 h5 39. Qxd3 Qe5 40. Kg2 {etc}) ({, but Black does better to play} 36... Qe5 $1 37. Qxh6 (37. Rc6 Qxd6 38. Rxd6 Re2 {with equality}) 37... d3 {and the strong passed pawn compensates for his pawn deficit.})) 32... Re6 $1 33. Bxf8 ({Black's defence is fairly simple after} 33. Bf4 Qe8 {(Fischer)}) ({or} 33. Bd2 Rf6 {.}) 33... Qxf8 34. Qh4 $1 {Already then Bobby was exceptionally inventive in searching for the slightest chance of an advantage!} ({"On} 34. Qxf5 $4 Re1+ {wins!}) ({"Or} 34. Bxf5 Rh6 {wins! The text forces Black into an ending where his weak pawns can't be concealed by tactical tricks," explains Fischer.}) ({"On} 34. Rc7 Qe7 {holds; e.g.} 35. Bxf5 $2 Re1+ 36. Kh2 ({or} 36. Kf2 Qe3+ 37. Kg3 Qe5+) 36... Qe5+ {wins."}) ({The variation in brackets –} 34. Rc7 Z0 (34... Qe7 35. Bxf5 Re1+ {, and now} 36. Kf2 Qe3+ 37. Kg3 Qe5+ {needs refining. As after} 38. Kg4 ({after} 38. Kf2 $2 {Black does indeed win prettily:} Bxf5 39. Qf7 Qe3+ 40. Kg3 Qg5+ 41. Kf2 Qh4+ 42. g3 Rf1+ $1 43. Kxf1 Qxh3+ 44. Ke2 Qg2+) 38... Bxf5+ 39. Qxf5 Qxf5+ 40. Kxf5 d3 41. Kf6 $1 {(a very spectacular move with a threat of mate)} h5 42. Rc8+ Kh7 43. Rc7+ Kh6 44. Rc8 $1 {White nevertheless reaches the haven of a draw.}) ({. In addition, there is} 34... Re1+ 35. Kh2 Qf6 $1 36. f4 (36. Rxd7 $2 Qe5+) 36... Qe6 ({or} 36... Qg6 {with an easy draw.}))) 34... Qf6 ({White has the advantage after} 34... Qg7 $6 35. Rc7 Qe5 (35... Re2 $2 36. Qd8+ Re8 37. Rxd7 $1 Rxd8 38. Rxd8+ {Fischer}) 36. Bxf5 $1 Qxf5 37. Rxd7 Re1+ 38. Qxe1 Qxd7 39. Qb4 {etc.}) 35. Qxf6+ Rxf6 36. Kf2 $6 {"Losing a vital tempo, which gives Black time to rush his king toward the center," writes Fischer,} ({and he recommends} 36. Rc7 Be6 (36... Rf7 $2 37. Ra7 $1 {and Rxa6, winning}) {, and now} 37. b3 $1 {"squelches all counterplay". But it would appear that here too Keres could have saved himself thanks to his strong passed d-pawn:} Bf7 38. Rc6 (38. f4 $6 Bd5 $1) 38... f4 $1 39. Rxa6 Re6 40. Kf2 Bg6 41. Bxg6 hxg6 42. a4 (42. Rb6 d3 $1 43. Rxb5 Re2+ 44. Kf1 Rxa2 {etc}) 42... d3 $1 43. axb5 d2 44. Ra1 Re3 45. b4 Rb3 46. Ke2 Rxb4 47. Ra6 Rxb5 {, reaching a drawn rook endgame.}) 36... Kg7 $1 37. Rc7 Rf7 38. Ke2 (38. Ra7 Bc8 {– Fischer.}) 38... f4 $6 {"A risky selection in time-pressure,} ({"aimed against} 38... f4 $6 39. Kd3 $4 Bf5+ {.}) ({"On} 38... Be6 39. Rc6 {is strong.}) ({"Best is simply} 38... Kf6 39. Ra7 ({not} 39. f4 Be6 40. Rc6 $2 Rg7 41. Kf2 Rxg2+ $1) 39... Bc8 40. Rxf7+ Kxf7 $11 {." (Fischer) After missing a certain draw, Keres is not yet losing the game, but he simply loses a pawn.}) 39. Ra7 Kf6 ({Of course not} 39... Bc8 $2 40. Rxf7+ Kxf7 41. Bxh7 {.}) 40. Rxa6 Re7+ 41. Kf2 (41. Kd2 $2 Rg7 $1) 41... Be6 $2 (41... Ke5 $6 42. Ra7 {was also bad (Fischer).}) ({Black should again base his counterplay on the strength of his passed d-pawn. Fischer attached an exclamation mark to the move in the game, but after it White could have won! Therefore the correct move was} 41... Bf5 $1 42. Bxf5 Kxf5 43. Rxd6 Ke5 44. Rb6 d3 45. Rxb5+ Kd6 46. Rb4 (46. Rb3 Re2+ 47. Kf1 Re3 $1) 46... Re2+ 47. Kf1 Kc5 {(Hübner), when Black saves himself in the rook ending:} 48. Z0 (48. Rb3 Kc4 {with a draw.}) (48. Rb7 Rc2 $1 49. Rc7+ ({the threat was} 49. Z0 Rc1+ 50. Kf2 d2) ({, or} 49. Rd7 Kc4) 49... Kd4 50. Rxh7 Rc1+ 51. Kf2 Rc2+) ({, or} 48. Rxf4 Rxb2 49. Ke1 ({again the threat was} 49. Z0 Rb1+ 50. Kf2 d2) 49... Rxg2 50. a4 Ra2 {and the limit of White's dreams is a theoretically drawn endgame with f- and h-pawns}) ({, whereas Hübner's move} 48. a3 $2 {even loses after} Re3 $1 49. Re4 d2 50. Rxe3 fxe3 $1 51. Ke2 Kc4 {etc.})) 42. Rxd6 Ke5 43. Rc6 $1 ({"On} 43. Rb6 Bc4 44. b3 d3 $1 45. bxc4 bxc4 {and Black's passed pawns should be sufficient to draw." (Fischer). For example:} 46. a4 Kd4 47. a5 Kc3 48. a6 Re2+ 49. Kf1 Re7 50. Rd6 Kd2 51. Ba2 c3 52. Bc4 Re3 $1 {.}) 43... Bd5 {The enemy rook has to be allowed across to the kingside:} ({after} 43... Ra7 44. Rc5+ $1 Kd6 45. Rxb5 Bxa2 46. Bxa2 Rxa2 47. Ke2 Kc6 48. Rb8 {White would have retained his extra pawn and every chance of winning.}) 44. Rh6 $1 ({Of course not} 44. Rc5 Kd6 45. Rxb5 $2 Bc4 {Fischer.}) 44... Rc7 ({Black could hardly have been satisfied with} 44... Bc4 45. Rxh7 ({but not} 45. b3 $6 d3) 45... Rxh7 46. Bxh7 Bxa2 47. g4 {with a won bishop endgame.}) 45. Rh5+ Kd6 46. Rh6+ (46. b3 $1) 46... Ke5 47. Rh5+ ({Not} 47. Rb6 Rc1 48. Bxh7 Bc4 {Fischer.}) 47... Kd6 48. Rf5 $2 ({According to Fischer, "No better is} 48. Rxh7 Rc1 49. Bd3 Bc4 {"}) ({or "if} 48. Bxh7 Rc1 {threatening ...Bc4"}) ({The second line is dubious in view of} 48. Bxh7 Rc1 ({so Black should defend with} 48... Bxa2 49. Be4 Bc4 $1) 49. g4 $1 Bc4 50. b3 {and wins.}) ({But it is something else that is important. "The winning line is} 48. b3 $1 {(to shut him out from c4). If} Rc1 49. Be4 $1 Bxe4 50. fxe4 {etc." Here Fischer is right: White has a great and probably decisive advantage.}) 48... Rc1 49. Bd3 Rd1 ({Not} 49... Bc4 $2 50. Rxf4 $1 Bxd3 51. Rxd4+ {Fischer.}) 50. Ke2 ({After} 50. Bxb5 {Black would have been saved by} Rd2+ 51. Kf1 Bxa2 52. Rxf4 Rxb2 53. Bd3 Kc5 {with the idea of ...Rd2 – in the end White would have had to give up his bishop for the d-pawn.}) 50... Rg1 51. Kf2 Rd1 52. Ke2 Rg1 53. Rg5 Bxa2 ({According to Bobby, after the game Keres claimed that} 53... Ra1 {would have led to an easy draw:} 54. Bxh7 Bc4+ 55. Kd2 Rxa2 56. Kc2 Z0 (56... Ra1 {(?). However, then} 57. Bd3 Rg1 58. h4 Rh1 59. h5 {is strong, with winning chances.}) (56... b4 $1 {is better, although even here after} 57. h4 $1 b3+ 58. Kb1 Ra7 (58... Ra8 59. Bg6 $1) 59. Be4 {Black gains a draw only with difficulty:} d3 $5 60. Kc1 Ra1+ 61. Kd2 Rb1 62. Bxd3 Bxd3 63. Kxd3 Rxb2 64. Kc3 Rb1 65. Rf5 (65. h5 Ke6) 65... Rg1 66. Rxf4 Rxg2 67. Kxb3 Rg3 {.})) 54. Bxb5 Rb1 55. Kd3 h6 $2 {An inexplicable decision, leading to the loss of his pawns  (apparently Keres was again in time-trouble: the second control was on move 56) .} ({He could have drawn by} 55... Rxb2 56. Kxd4 Bb1 $1 {(Fischer)} 57. Bc4 ({or} 57. Bd3 Bxd3 58. Kxd3 Ke6) 57... Rd2+ 58. Kc3 Rc2+ 59. Kb3 Rf2 {.}) 56. Rh5 Rxb2 57. Kxd4 Rxg2 58. Rxh6+ {Here the game was adjourned for the second time.} Ke7 {As Fischer explained, "There are two technical obstacles facing White: 1) He cannot exchange bishops; the ending where he is two pawns ahead remains a theoretical draw with rooks on the board. 2) He cannot exchange rooks so long as Black's king has access to f6; the ending (even with two extra pawns) is still a draw with bishops on the board."} 59. Ke4 Rg5 60. Ba6 $2 (60. Bd3 $2 Bf7 $1 61. Kxf4 Rh5 62. Rxh5 Bxh5 {and ...Bxf3 would also have led to a draw.}) ({In addition, Fischer points out that Keres was afraid of} 60. Bf1 $1 {, since "if} Rg1 61. Ba6 $1 {and Black no longer has the defense mentioned in the next note." In this way White could apparently have won, although not without overcoming considerable technical difficulties.}) 60... Bf7 $2 {"This slip is fatal. Keres told me he had reached this position in his adjournment analysis, but had forgotten his drawing line over-the-board (age nevertheless had its effect! – G.K.).} ({"Right is} 60... Bb1+ $1 61. Kxf4 Rf5+ 62. Kg4 Rf6 63. Rxf6 Kxf6 {with the aforementioned blockade." (Fischer). That is, the white king would not have reached g5.}) 61. Bc8 $1 ({Keres had only reckoned on} 61. Kxf4 $2 Rh5 62. Rxh5 Bxh5 {and ... Bxf3 with a draw.}) 61... Rg6 ({Black also fails to save the game after} 61... Rc5 62. Bg4 Rc4+ 63. Ke5 {(Fischer)}) ({or} 61... Rh5 62. Rb6 $1 {.}) 62. Rh7 (62. Rxg6 $2 Bxg6+ 63. Kxf4 Kf6 {with a draw.}) 62... Kf8 63. Bg4 Rg7 $6 ({Of course not} 63... Rxg4 $2 64. Rxf7+ $1 {.}) ({But} 63... Rb6 64. Kxf4 Kg8 {(Barcza)} 65. Rh4 Kg7 {was objectively better – in view of the awkward position of the white rook, Black would still have retained drawing chances, despite being two pawns down.}) 64. Rh6 $1 Rg6 $2 {The final error, also not mentioned by Fischer.} (64... Rg5 $1 65. Kxf4 Ra5 {would have caused White much greater problems.}) 65. Rxg6 $1 {Now the exchange of rooks leads to a simple win.} Bxg6+ 66. Kxf4 Kg7 67. Kg5 $1 Bd3 68. f4 Be4 69. h4 ({Only not} 69. f5 $2 Bxf5 $1 {with a draw.}) 69... Bd3 70. h5 Be4 71. h6+ Kh8 (71... Kf7 72. Bh5+ Kg8 73. Bg6) 72. Bf5 Bd5 73. Bg6 Be6 74. Kf6 Bc4 75. Kg5 Be6 76. Bh5 $1 Kh7 77. Bg4 $1 Bc4 ({Or} 77... Bxg4 78. Kxg4 Kxh6 79. Kf5 {and wins (Fischer).}) 78. f5 Bf7 79. Bh5 Bc4 80. Bg6+ Kg8 81. f6 ({After} 81. f6 Bb3 82. Kf4 Kh8 83. Ke5 Bc4 84. Kd6 Bb3 85. Ke7 Bc4 86. Bf5 Bf7 87. Bd7 Bh5 88. Be8 {White wins.}) 1-0

