1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 {The Ruy Lopez was always Fischer's favourite weapon,} ({although occasionally he would surprise his opponents with the sharp} 3. Bc4 Bc5 ({or} 3... Nf6 4. Ng5) 4. b4 {, or even the King's Gambit.}) 3... a6 4. Ba4 ({Later the American grandmaster also revived the Exchange Variation –} 4. Bxc6 {(Game No.82).}) 4... Nf6 (4... d6 {– Game Nos. 7 and 66.}) 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 d6 8. c3 O-O 9. h3 Na5 (9... Bb7 {– Game No.75.}) (9... Nb8 {– Game Nos. 103 and 107.}) 10. Bc2 c5 11. d4 Qc7 12. Nbd2 Bd7 {One of the numerous continuations in the classical Chigorin Variation. "I don't think there's any easy way for Black to achieve complete equality – but who knows?" writes Fischer. But in what opening is there an 'easy' way for him to solve all his problems? I think that this indicates Bobby's habit of playing to seize the initiative with Black – the patient strategy of gradually equalizing was alien and unpleasant to him.} (12... cxd4 {– Game No.54.}) 13. Nf1 Rfe8 {"The Yugoslav System, popularized by Gligoric and Matanovic. The idea is to strengthen the position, and transfer the bishop to f8 while awaiting further developments. Black will undertake specific counteraction only after White commits himself." (Fischer)} ({It is curious that in this position Petrosian played only} 13... Nc4 {, ridding himself as soon as possible of his 'bad' knight at a5, and he invariably gained a draw – against Tal (Volume 3, Game No. 3), Korchnoi (Curaçao Candidates 1962), Ljubojevic and Karpov (Milan 1975), where} 14. Ne3 {was played;} ({against me (Banja Luka 1979), where} 14. d5 {was played}) ({, and against Velimirovic (Rio de Janeiro Interzonal 1979), where} 14. b3 {was played.})) 14. Ne3 ({In the 1960s Tal and Geller successfully introduced} 14. b3 {cutting off the knight at a5 and not blocking the path of the c1-bishop: if} g6 {, then} 15. Bg5 $1 {. This plan struck a serious blow at the 'Yugoslav System'.}) 14... g6 15. dxe5 {"This positional approach bares the hole on d5, so that White may gain access to it with his knight," writes Fischer.} ({He also tried the alternatives} 15. Bd2 Bf8 16. Rc1 {(against Rinaldo, Cleveland 1957)} ({and} 16. b4 {(against Matanovic, Bled 1961).})) 15... dxe5 16. Nh2 Rad8 17. Qf3 Be6 18. Nhg4 {A typical idea: after the exchange on g4 the d5-square is weakened and attacking motifs on the h-file arise.} Nxg4 19. hxg4 (19. Nxg4 {is harmless:} Bxg4 20. hxg4 c4 21. g3 Nb7 22. Kg2 Nc5 23. Rh1 f6 {with equality (Fischer-Matanovic, Portoroz Interzonal 1958).}) 19... Qc6 ({"White gets an edge after} 19... Nc4 20. Nd5 Bxd5 21. exd5 {," writes Fischer, evidently relying on his two bishops. It is strange that he ignores} Nb6 $1 {followed by the doubling of rooks on the d-file, as occurred in the games Gligoric-Reshevsky, Zürich Candidates 1953), Smyslov-Gligoric (Zagreb 1955), Keres-Matanovic (Bled 1961) and many others, where Black gained a draw without particular difficulty.}) 20. g5 $5 {Fischer's novelty, which he introduced against Shocron in Mar del Plata (1959).} ({Previously} 20. Qg3 {was played; for example:} f6 (20... Qc7 21. Nf5 $1) ({; in Fischer's opinion,} 20... Bf6 {is better}) 21. g5 Kh8 ({after} 21... fxg5 $5 22. Qxe5 Qd6 $1 {Black is close to equalizing}) 22. b3 Rf8 23. Nd5 $1 Bxd5 24. exd5 Qxd5 25. gxf6 Bxf6 26. Bh6 Rfe8 27. Be4 {with a strong initiative for the pawn (Boleslavsky-Tal, 24th USSR Championship, Moscow 1957).}) 20... Nc4 ({If} 20... Bxg5 {Bobby was intending} 21. Nd5 $1 Bxc1 22. Nf6+ Kh8 $1 23. Raxc1 ({after} 23. Nxe8 Bg5 24. Nf6 Nc4 $1 {Black has good compensation for the exchange}) 23... Rf8 24. Qg3 Qc7 $1 25. Qg5 Kg7 {, "and now White can force a draw with} 26. Nh5+ {etc} ({or try for more with either} 26. f4) ({, or} 26. Re3 {." However, after} h6 $1 {he is unable to achieve any more.})) 21. Ng4 Bxg4 {Forced: the knight cannot be allowed to go to f6.} 22. Qxg4 f6 $2 {Unzicker makes an 'active' move, not sensing how dangerous it is to weaken the light squares and the e5-pawn.} ({Shocron played much better in the aforementioned game:} 22... Nb6 $1 23. g3 c4 $1 ({not immediately} 23... Nd7 $6 24. a4 $1) 24. Kg2 Nd7 $1 25. Rh1 Nf8 {with equality (although later the difference in the class of the two players told). Now, however, there is no question of equality!}) 23. gxf6 Bxf6 24. a4 $1 Nb6 25. axb5 axb5 26. Be3 {"Because both flanks are fluid, White, with his two bishops, is better able to penetrate and exploit the 'loose' pawns." To judge by this and Fischer's later comments, Black is already doomed, although, as we will see, he had opportunities to put up a tenacious resistance.} Ra8 ({"} 26... Nc4 {is met by} 27. Bb3 {instituting an awkward pin." (Fischer). In Hübner's opinion, after} Kh8 28. Bxc4 bxc4 {Black could have held out:} 29. Ra5 (29. Ra7 Ra8 $1) 29... Be7 30. Ra7 Ra8 $1 31. Rea1 Rxa7 32. Rxa7 Bf8 33. Qh4 Bg7 34. Bh6 ({but} 34. f3 $1 {is better; for example:} Kg8 35. Bh6 Bf6 36. Bg5 Bg7 37. Qf2 {with appreciable pressure}) 34... Bf6 35. Bg5 Bg7 {and "no immediate dangers for Black can be seen."}) 27. Red1 Kh8 $6 {"If he swaps, White gains the a-file; if he doesn't, White keeps the d-file." (Fischer). Hübner again objects: "On h8 Black's king is often worse than on g8, because in the endgame it needs more time to reach the center; moreover, the danger to get mated in the middlegame grows if White's bishop appears on h6.} ({"With} 27... Rxa1 28. Rxa1 Ra8 29. Rxa8+ (29. Rd1 Rd8) 29... Nxa8 {Black could have weakened the pressure and gained an acceptable position." Although after} 30. b3 {he would have faced a lengthy and gruelling defence.}) 28. b3 Bg7 ({"Black should ease the pressure by} 28... Rxa1 29. Rxa1 Ra8 30. Rxa8+ Nxa8 {, though} 31. Qd1 $1 {followed by Qa1! forces an invasion on the queenside." (Fischer). According to Hübner, there is nothing terrible about this after} Nc7 $1 32. Qa1 ({however, in my view, there is no point in playing Qa1 immediately –} 32. Bd3 $1 Kg7 33. Bf1 $1 {is stronger, and Black has chronic problems}) 32... Be7 {.}) 29. Qh4 Bf6 30. Bg5 $1 {A concrete approach to the position that is typical of Fischer: he exchanges his 'good' bishop for the opponent's 'bad' bishop, in order to weaken still further the e5-pawn and the defences of the black king.} Bxg5 31. Qxg5 {"Now White must penetrate on either the a- or d-file. Black's e-pawn has clearly been exposed as a weakling." (Fischer)} Rxa1 (31... Z0 32. Rxa8 Nxa8 33. Rd5 {was threatened.}) 32. Rxa1 Nd7 33. Bd1 $1 {A fine move,} ({in contrast to} 33. Ra7 Qd6 $1 {. The awakened 'Fischer bishop' soon begins operating at full power (soon we will also learn of its feats in other games).}) 33... Nf6 ({"Not} 33... Qxe4 34. Bf3 Qf4 ({if} 34... Qc2 35. Ra7 {with a winning attack}) 35. Qxf4 exf4 36. Bc6 Re7 ({or} 36... Rd8 37. Rd1) 37. Ra8+ $1 Kg7 38. Ra7 {wins a piece." (Fischer)}) ({Hübner suggested} 33... b4 {(getting rid of the weakness at b5 and reducing the number of pawns remaining)} 34. cxb4 cxb4 35. Ra7 (35. Be2 Qb6 {and ...Rf8}) 35... Qd6 {and "Black should be able to hold." But, as I see it, after} 36. Be2 Qd4 37. Rb7 Rf8 38. Kh2 $1 {, with the idea of f2-f3 and Bc4, he is too tied up and it is doubtful if he can gain a draw.}) ({Another recommendation of Hübner is better –} 33... Qf6 $5 {(the white queen is driven from its dominating position and the pressure on the e5-pawn is reduced)} 34. Qd2 Nb6 {and "Black obtains counterplay on the d-file." However, here too after} 35. Qe3 $1 Qd6 (35... c4 36. Qc5 $1) 36. Be2 b4 37. cxb4 cxb4 38. Ra7 Ra8 39. Rb7 Nd7 (39... Rb8 $2 40. Rxb8+ Qxb8 41. Qc5) 40. Kh2 Rb8 41. Ra7 Rd8 42. f3 Qf6 43. Qh6 Qf4+ 44. Qxf4 exf4 45. Ra5 {Black's defence is not easy.}) 34. Ra7 Qd6 (34... Nxe4 $2 35. Qh6 {and mate.}) 35. Be2 $1 {"Simply attacking a pawn. Curiously, Black is in too much of a straitjacket to do much about it." (Fischer)} Re7 $2 {Simply giving up the b5-pawn.} ({To demonstrate the hopelessness of Black's position Bobby gives} 35... Nxe4 $2 36. Qh6) (35... b4 $2 36. Ra6 Nxe4 37. Qh4 {etc}) (35... Qb6 36. Rf7 Ng8 37. Qh4 h6 38. Qg4 Rd8 39. Bxb5 $1) ({and also} 35... Rb8 36. Rf7 Ng8 37. Rd7 Qxd7 ({or} 37... Qf6 38. Qe3 Qc6 39. Rd5 {winning a pawn}) 38. Qxe5+ Qg7 39. Qxb8 Qxc3 40. Qxb5 {. According to Hübner, here} Nf6 {is still not altogether clear, but after} 41. Qb8+ Kg7 42. Qc7+ Kh6 43. Kh2 $1 {White should nevertheless convert his extra pawn:} Qd4 (43... Nxe4 $2 44. Qf4+) ({, or} 43... Qd2 44. Qxc5 $1 {with the threat of Qf8+}) 44. f3 Nh5 {(otherwise 45 Bc4)} 45. Bb5 $1 Qf2 46. Bd7 Qh4+ 47. Bh3 {etc.}) ({"However, Fischer fails to take an important defensive resource into account though tactical and positional reasons suggest to play} 35... c4 {," writes Hübner, who gives} 36. bxc4 (36. Rf7 $2 cxb3 $1) 36... Qb6 ({after} 36... bxc4 $2 37. Bxc4 Qd1+ 38. Bf1 Ng8 {, in my opinion,} 39. Qh4 $1 h6 40. Qh3 {and wins is the most accurate}) 37. cxb5 ({if} 37. Rf7 $6 {there is the pretty stroke} Qxf2+ $1) 37... Qxa7 38. Qxf6+ Qg7 39. Qc6 $1 (39. Qxg7+ Kxg7 40. c4 Rc8 $1 {leads to a draw}) 39... Z0 (39... Rf8 {and "it is not clear, whether the position can be considered won for White; but at any rate, tremendous work is necessary before he can secure victory." In my view, after} 40. Qd6 $1 {this task is not so great: tied to the defence of the e5-pawn, Black is unable to halt the victorious march of the b- and c-pawns.}) (39... Qe7 {is more tenacious, but even then after} 40. b6 $1 Kg7 41. b7 (41. Bg4 Rd8 42. Qc7 Kf8 43. c4 h5 44. Bh3 Rd1+ 45. Kh2 h4 $1 {is not so clear}) 41... Rb8 42. Ba6 {with the threat of Qc8 and c4-c5-c6-c7 White should win.})) ({Nevertheless,} 35... c4 $1 36. bxc4 Qb6 $1 {was of course the best practical chance (bearing in mind that after} 37. cxb5 {Black can also consider} Nxe4 {) and in our time, when the level of resistance has increased sharply, any grandmaster would play this without much thought.}) 36. Rxe7 Qxe7 37. Bxb5 Kg7 38. Be2 {"The win is still far from clear. White's major technical problem is creating a passed pawn on the queenside while sheltering his king from a perpetual check." (Fischer)} Qc7 {With the threat of ...Nxe4.} 39. Qe3 Qa5 40. g3 Qa3 $6 ({is more tenacious – Fischer.} 40... Qc7) 41. Kg2 ({As Bobby writes, "Sharper and possibly immediately decisive is} 41. b4 $1 cxb4 42. Qc5 {." He did not risk playing this immediately after the time control, although after} Qxc3 43. Qe7+ Kh6 44. Qxf6 Qe1+ 45. Bf1 Qxe4 46. Qf7 $1 {(halting the b4-pawn) the curtain could indeed have been lowered.}) 41... Qa5 (41... Qxb3 42. Qxc5 Nxe4 ({or} 42... Qe6 43. Bc4 {– Gligoric}) 43. Qxe5+ Nf6 44. c4 {(Fischer) was no better.}) 42. Qd3 Qb6 43. Qc4 Qc6 44. Bd3 ({Not wishing to weaken the king's defences by} 44. f3 {.}) 44... Qb6 45. b4 cxb4 46. cxb4 {"Step one is completed: White has a passed b-pawn." (Fischer)} Ng4 47. Qc5 $1 {Forcing the exchange of queens,} Qxc5 ({since} 47... Qd8 $2 {is bad on account of the reply} 48. Be2 {.}) 48. bxc5 Kf7 49. f4 $1 {Playing for the creation of a second passed pawn.} Ke7 50. Kf3 Nf6 ({Or} 50... h5 51. Bc4 Kd7 $2 52. Bf7 {Fischer.}) 51. Bb5 ({Of course not} 51. fxe5 $2 Nd7 {with a draw.}) 51... Ke6 52. Bc4+ Ke7 53. c6 $1 Ne8 (53... Kd6 $2 54. fxe5+ $1) ({As Fischer pointed out, the more tenacious} 53... exf4 {would also not have helped:} 54. gxf4 Ne8 55. e5 Nc7 56. Ke4 Ne8 (56... h5 57. Kf3) 57. Bg8 Kf8 58. Bxh7 Kg7 59. Bxg6 Kxg6 60. f5+ Kg5 61. f6 Kg6 62. Kd5 Kf7 63. Kc5 Ke6 64. Kb6 {and wins.}) 54. fxe5 $1 h6 55. Ke3 Nc7 56. Kd4 h5 {The last chance.} 57. Ke3 $1 g5 58. Be2 h4 ({Or} 58... Ke6 59. Kd4 $1 {(a flamboyant and, more importantly, effective pendulum-like manoeuvre on the squares d4-e3-f4)} h4 60. gxh4 gxh4 61. Bg4+ {and wins (Gligoric).}) 59. gxh4 gxh4 60. Bc4 Ne8 61. Kf4 Kd8 62. Kg4 Kc7 63. Bf7 Ng7 64. Kxh4 Kxc6 65. Kg5 ({If} 65. Kg5 Kd7 66. Kf6 Ne8+ 67. Bxe8+ {. A brilliant example of Bobby's fine technique, especially taking account of his young age.}) 1-0

[Event "USSR v World, Belgrade"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "1970.??.??"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Petrosian, Tigran V"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B13"]
[Annotator "Garry Kasparov"]
[PlyCount "77"]
[EventDate "1970.??.??"]
[EventType "team-tourn"]
[SourceTitle "My Great Predecessors 4"]
[Source "Everyman Chess"]
[SourceDate "2009.11.01"]
[SourceVersionDate "2009.11.01"]
