1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 {An old, rare and seemingly unpretentious variation, but one that came into fashion after this game. White is trying to play for the restriction of his opponent's forces.} Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 {Developing the bishops in defiance of 'Lasker's rule', according to which one should first develop the knights.} ({These alternatives are inaccurate:} 6. h3 e5 $1) ({or} 6. Bg5 Ne4 $1 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. d5 Ne5 9. Qa4+ Qd7 10. Qxe4 Qf5 {(Fischer).}) 6... Bg4 {The most natural reply, in the spirit of Caro-Kann ideas. After ...e7-e6 a Carlsbad structure with reversed colours will arise. In the 1970s this variation was actively studied in Baku where, under the influence of the master Shakarov, everyone played the Caro-Kann Defence.} 7. Qb3 Na5 $6 ({Later Petrosian lamented that he had not played} 7... Qb6 {, in analogy with an old game against Kasparian (2nd matchgame, Erevan 1946)}) ({or} 7... Qc8 {and ...Bh5-g6 (this idea of Hort was also approved by Fischer).}) ({In later years Black began to prefer} 7... Qd7 8. Nd2 e6 9. Ngf3 Bxf3 10. Nxf3 Bd6 11. Bxd6 Qxd6 {when he has no particular problems:} 12. Qxb7 ({or} 12. O-O O-O {with equality, Lanka-Kasparov, Leningrad 1977}) 12... Rb8 13. Qa6 O-O 14. Bb5 Rb6 15. Qa4 Ne7 16. b3 Ne4 17. Rc1 Rc8 18. Bd3 Ng6 19. Ne5 Nc5 $1 {(Morozevich-Oll, Groningen 1997).}) 8. Qa4+ (8. Qc2 $5) 8... Bd7 $2 {But this timid, passive move followed by the incarceration of the bishop by ... e7-e6 is not at all in the spirit of the Caro-Kann!} (8... Nc6 {(Kholmov), is correct, when the white queen at a4 stands no better than at d1; for example:} 9. Nd2 e6 10. Ngf3 Bd6 11. Ne5 O-O 12. Bg3 Qc7 $1 {with equality (Ka. Müller-Agdestein, German League 1998).}) 9. Qc2 e6 ({Petrosian suggested} 9... a6 {with the idea of ...Bb5, but then} 10. a4 $1 {is good.}) 10. Nf3 Qb6 ({Black is still trying to exchange his light-squared bishop, although the developing} 10... Be7 11. O-O O-O {would have been more useful.}) 11. a4 $1 {A typically Fischer-like, very concrete move: abstract fears about the weakness of the b3-square are unknown to the American! Perhaps it is was this reply that Petrosian underestimated, when he undertook the 'sideways' knight manoeuvre to a5. Here he must have become a little nervous...} Rc8 ({Black faced a difficult choice, since neither of the tempting invasions at b3 would have relieved his difficulties:} 11... Qb3 12. Qe2 $1 Nc4 ({but not} 12... Bxa4 $2 13. Rxa4 Qxa4 14. Bb5+ {Fischer}) 13. Bc1 Rc8 (13... Bd6 $2 14. a5 $1 {, winning a piece}) 14. O-O) ({or} 11... Nb3 $5 {(the exchange of the knight would at least justify the preceding moves)} 12. Ra2 Rc8 13. O-O Be7 14. Be5 (14. Re1 $5) 14... h6 15. Nbd2 Nxd2 16. Nxd2 {etc.}) 12. Nbd2 Nc6 13. Qb1 Nh5 {It is clear that Black has lost the opening battle and the bishop at d7 is no adornment to his position,} ({but he could still have put up a tenacious defence with} 13... g6 {(Fischer)} 14. O-O Nh5 15. Bg5 Bg7 {.}) 14. Be3 h6 $2 {This conceding of the key e5-square is effectively decisive.} (14... f5 $6 {was also bad, allowing White an attack with} 15. g4 $1 fxg4 16. Ng5 {(Fischer).}) ({Many commentators recommended} 14... g6 {, but after} 15. O-O {and Ne5} ({or immediately} 15. Ne5 {, and if} Bd6 {, then} 16. f4 $1 Bxe5 17. a5 Nxa5 18. fxe5 {, White would have retained the initiative.})) ({The immediate} 14... Bd6 {(Suetin) was also suggested} 15. Z0 ({, with the idea of} 15. Bxh7 g6 16. Bxg6 fxg6 17. Qxg6+ Kd8 18. g4 Ne7 {. It is true that after} 19. Qg5 Qxb2 {the play is double-edged.} ({It is bad to play} 19... Kc7 $2 20. c4 $1 {(Kholmov)} dxc4 21. Rc1 $1 Kd8 22. Ne5 Bxe5 23. Qxe5 Rh7 24. gxh5 {etc.})) ({. Therefore Fischer would probably have simply replied} 15. Ne5 $1 {.})) ({If the worst came to the worst, it was possible to sacrifice a pawn by} 14... Qc7 $5 {(not allowing the knight in at e5)} 15. Bxh7 Nf4 16. Bxf4 Qxf4 17. Bd3 Bd6 {, but this would have been in the style of Spassky or Tal, and not at all Petrosian!}) 15. Ne5 $1 Nf6 {"Effectively capitulation," writes Petrosian.} ({"When I played 11...Rc8, I was intending in this position} 15... Nxe5 16. dxe5 Bc5 {with the exchange of bishops and ...Nf4, which in combination with a possible ...f7-f6 or ...d5-d4 would have given Black a good game. And only at the very last moment did I see that the intermediate move} 17. a5 $1 {would disrupt all my plans:} Qc7 18. Z0 (18. g4 $1 {winning a piece, for which after} Bxe3 19. fxe3 Qxe5 20. gxh5 Qxe3+ 21. Be2 Bb5 22. Qd1 {and Nf1 Black has no real compensation."} (22. Z0)) ({. Fischer's suggestion of} 18. Nf3 Bxe3 19. fxe3 {is also good. A strange oversight, testifying to the indifferent competitive mood of the ex-world champion...})) 16. h3 {"To prevent a later ... Ng4 in many variations and maybe g2-g4 after Black castles." (Fischer). White has a solid centre and very powerful pressure – in a word, a strategically won position.} Bd6 17. O-O Kf8 $6 ({"} 17... O-O {is better." (Fischer). "But after} 18. f4 {I did not see what could be done to counter the attack with g2-g4."  (Petrosian). The 'Pillsbury Attack' in the Queen's Gambit involuntarily comes to mind!}) 18. f4 Be8 (18... Nxe5 $2 19. fxe5 Bxe5 20. a5 {and wins – Fischer.}) 19. Bf2 $1 Qc7 ({If} 19... g6 20. Z0 ({, Fischer gave} 20. f5 gxf5 21. Bxf5 exf5 22. Qxf5 {. However, after} Kg7 $1 ({rather than} 22... Qd8 $2 23. Bh4 {and wins – Fischer}) 23. Bh4 (23. Qxc8 Ne7 $1 24. Qa8 Bd7 25. a5 Qa6) ({, or} 23. Ndc4 dxc4 24. Nxc4 Qd8 25. Nxd6 Ne7 $1 {is no better}) 23... Bxe5 24. Bxf6+ Bxf6 25. Qxf6+ Kg8 26. Rf4 Rh7 27. Rg4+ Kf8 28. Qf5 h5 $1 29. Rf4 ({or} 29. Rh4 Qd8 $1) 29... Qc7 $1 {Black repels the attack.}) ({. In my view, a different sacrifice is more accurate –} 20. Bxg6 fxg6 21. Nxg6+ {; for example:} Bxg6 ({or} 21... Kg7 22. Nxh8 Kxh8 23. f5 Bf4 24. Qe1 $1 exf5 ({better than} 24... Qxb2 25. Qxe6 Nd7 26. Nf3) ({, or} 24... Bxd2 25. Qxd2 Kh7 26. fxe6 Ne7 27. Bh4 Ne4 28. Qf4 Ng6 29. Qf5 Nd6 30. Qh5 Qxb2 31. Bg5 $1) 25. Qe6 Nd7 26. Nc4 $3 Qc7 (26... dxc4 27. d5 $1) 27. Ne3 Qd6 28. Qxd5 Qxd5 29. Nxd5 {with a technically won ending}) 22. Qxg6 Z0 (22... Ng8 23. Bh4 $1 Nce7 ({or} 23... Qc7 24. Qxe6 Rh7 25. Qxd5) 24. Bxe7+ Bxe7 25. f5 Nf6 26. Rae1 Rc6 27. a5 $1 {etc}) ({, or} 22... Be7 23. f5 Rg8 (23... exf5 24. Be3 $1) 24. Qxh6+ Ke8 25. Bh4 {with good winning chances.}))) 20. Bh4 Ng8 21. f5 $1 Nxe5 22. dxe5 Bxe5 23. fxe6 Bf6 24. exf7 Bxf7 25. Nf3 $1 Bxh4 ({If} 25... g5 26. Bf2 Kg7 {, then} 27. Bd4 {(Fischer)} ({or} 27. Nd4 $1 Ne7 28. Nb5 {and wins.})) 26. Nxh4 Nf6 27. Ng6+ $1 {A characteristic touch.} (27. Nf5 $1 {suggests itself, but Bobby does not miss a chance to exchange the enemy bishop (he valued this piece very highly) and remain with his favourite light-squared bishop against a knight.}) 27... Bxg6 28. Bxg6 Ke7 $1 {Fischer, who was already expecting resignation, gave this move two exclamation marks! "Black's only chance," writes Petrosian. "His king might just succeed in escaping via the dark squares to the queenside."} 29. Qf5 Kd8 30. Rae1 Qc5+ 31. Kh1 Rf8 ({Both players considered} 31... Rc6 {to be more tenacious, but here too after} 32. Bf7 Rc7 (32... Rd6 33. c4 $1) 33. Be6 {Black would not have lasted long.}) 32. Qe5 $1 {This centralization paralyses Black.} Rc7 (32... Qc7 $2 33. Qxd5+ $1) 33. b4 $1 Qc6 (33... Qe7 34. Qd4) 34. c4 $1 {This is a pretty and energetic conclusion.} dxc4 35. Bf5 Rff7 36. Rd1+ Rfd7 37. Bxd7 Rxd7 38. Qb8+ Ke7 39. Rde1+ 1-0

