[PlyCount "77"]

{This game was from round 2 of the USSR vs The World. The 9th World Champion was known for being very difficult to beat, yet in this match Fischer beat him in consecutive games. When the 11th World Champion uses a setup to soundly defeat the 9th World Champion, it is worth taking note!} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 {In his youth, Fischer had played 3. Nc3 against the Caro-Kann. However, he did not have great results against Petrosian with it. Here, he switches it up and plays the Exchange Variation.} cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Nf6 6. Bf4 {It is worth noting that this same position can be reached in the London System if Black were to trade on d4 and White recaptures with the e-pawn. However, such a trade is not forced in that situation. Here, Black has no good way to avoid it.  6.h3 In his own analysis of this game, Fischer stated that he thought h3 followed by Black immediately striking in the center with e5 equalized. Obviously, when looking to win a game, you do not want to let your opponent equalize so easily. 6...e5= Fischer stopped his analysis here and declared this equality. What is interesting is that if you plug this into modern engines like Stockfish and Leela, they will think White has a strong edge here. However, after you play the moves they recommend, in about 4 moves they realize Fischer was correct and the game is completely equal.  6.Bg5 Another line Fischer analyzed. 6...Ne4 7.Bxe4 dxe4 8.d5?  8.Nd2 Stockfish finds a slightly better line for White, but it still leads to equality. 8...Qd5 9.Be3= 8...Ne5 9.Qa4+ Qd7 10.Qxe4 Qf5⩱ Fischer correctly realized this position was bad for White. Black will end up with the bishop pair and the board will quickly open up.} Bg4 {Here we see Petrosian going into one of our mainlines.  6...g6 In Petrosian's own analysis, he thought g6 was the way to go here. This line is also covered and is shown that White has a nice edge in that line as well.} 7. Qb3 {Sticking to our repertoire.} Na5 {This is one of the ways Black can try to deal with Qb3. Here we see Fischer handle it beautifully! Qc8 and Qd7 are the other 2 ways to deal with it, but each have their own problems as we show in the 4...Nc6 Chapter} 8. Qa4+ Bd7 9. Qc2 e6 {Now the position has transposed into a French-like structure. However, it is one that is very bad for Black! Not only does he have his usual 'bad' bishop, but his development is sloppy and he does not have the typical c-pawn break to help free his position.  9...a6 Petrosian thought this would be better; however, it allows White to continue with his development plans with no pressure.} 10. Nf3 {Now that Bg4 is impossible, White continues developing his kingside.} Qb6 {Petrosian's idea here was that if White castles, Black can play Bb5 and force a trade of light square bishops. Since the light square bishop is the key attacking piece in White's setup, that is the bishop he does not want to trade! However, Petrosian's idea fails to a nice novelty played by Fischer.} 11. a4 {It is not often a simple pawn push gets an exclam. Here, Fischer's idea is that he wants to castle, but doing so immediately would allow the aforementioned exchange of bishops. Thus, he needs to prevent Bb5. How do you do that? Well, control b5! This makes Petrosian's Qb6 just seem silly.} Rc8 {Here Petrosian switches gears, but this maneuver is a bit too slow now. His idea is to play Na5-c6-b4 to force get White's light square bishop another way. Since this takes 2 moves, White has plenty of time to deal with it.  11...Nb3 This is a better way to get the knight active, but it does not do much. 12.Ra2 The rook may look a little strange on a2, but it keeps everything together on the queenside and will eventually go back to a1, or potentially allow for Rfa1 to double on the a-file. 12...Rc8 13.O-O Be7 Black will castle next move, but White is already in good shape and will end up chasing the knight from b3 by developing his own knight.  11...Qb3 If Black tries to trade queens here, it only serves to misplace his own queen. 12.Qe2! Obviously, White is in no hurry to get the queens off the board. 12...Nc4  12...Bxa4? Fischer analyzed this to demonstrate why it does not work. 13.Rxa4 Temporarily sacrificing the exchange. 13...Qxa4 14.Bb5++- And White gets a large return on his investment! 13.Bc1 Ne4 14.O-O ± And while Black's knights look scary, they do not have enough support to do much. The engines like some really complicated lines, but the simple Nbd2 forces all the knights off the board and leaves White with a very comfortable position.} 12. Nbd2 {Developing his last minor piece, supporting the knight on f3, making Nb3 impossible, and a sublty that is only revealed on White's next move.} Nc6 {This is consistent with Petrosian's plan to go after the light square bishop.} 13. Qb1 {The other, perhaps more important, benefit to developing the b1-knight on the previous move. Now, Nb4 is not only useless, it would lose material.} Nh5 {Similar to many London positions, Black would like to trade off that dark square bishop for his knight to aleviate the dark square problems on his queenside.  13...g6 Fischer thought this was a better try. The problem here is Black has already played e6, so this leaves dark square weaknesses all over the kingside where Black is presumably going to castle.} 14. Be3 {White has no problems trading the dark square bishop, but he would like to do it for Black's dark square bishop - not his knight!} h6 {Black has to be careful what he does here as his knight may get trapped. A move like f6 would lose instantly to g4! g6 is another option, but as mentioned, it makes the Swiss Cheese pattern on the kingside.  14...f5 Trying to gain space and prevent White from playing g4; however, this allows White a nice pawn sacrifice. 15.g4! Playing g4 anyway! 15...fxg4 16.Ng5 Now the light squares on the kingside are targets! 16...Bd6 17.Bxh7 White recovers his pawn and is threatening to play Bg6+. The engines evaluate this as equal, but it is very uncomfortable for Black. He must find several difficult defensive moves in a row just to avoid losing material or getting mated.} 15. Ne5 {Despite the fact that this square is attacked by one of Black's own knights, it is very difficult to dislodge.} Nf6 {Petrosian did not want to go into the lines after Nxe5. As the analysis below shows, White is much better in those lines.  15...Nxe5 16.dxe5 Bc5 17.a5 Forcing the Queen to leave the diagonal. 17...Qc7 Trying to remove that pawn so that the h5-knight can retreat back to f6. 18.g4 This is the line Petrosian was worried about.  18.Nf3 Fischer liked Nf3 here, but the problem is that it allows Black the time he needs to give his knight space, albeit at the expense of weakening his kingside. 18...g5 19.Bxc5 Qxc5 20.O-O Nf4 21.Nd4 Nxd3 Trading here simply gives White a better game than he already had. 22.Qxd3± 18...Bxe3 19.fxe3 Qxe5 20.gxh5 Qxe3+ 21.Be2 Bb5 22.Qd1 All very forcing. White has a piece for 2 pawns and will maneuver his knight to f1 and then to g3 to free his queen from the defense of the e2-bishop.} 16. h3 {A subtle move that does multiple things at once. First, if the bishop returns to f4 and Black once again tries to attack it with the knight, it can retreat to h2 and maintain pressure on the b8-h2 diagonal. Second, it prevents Ng4. Third, it will provide luft for the king once White castles.} Bd6 17. O-O {Here we reach the end of our recommended setup. White has completed his development and his pieces are very well coordinated. In just 22 more moves, this game is over!} Kf8 {17...O-O Fischer correctly thought that castling was better for Black. However, The problem Black runs into is that this is a very typical French-structure where White has done everything right, and Black has done almost nothing right! Not many people would want to try to defend this as Black. 18.Ndf3 Qc7 19.Bf4 Nh5 20.Bh2 Ne7 21.Qd1⩲ White has simply improved each of his pieces to mount more and more pressure on the kingside. Meanwhile, Black is struggling to find a plan and is running low on good squares for his pieces.} 18. f4 Be8 {It looks silly, but what else is Black going to do?  18...Nxe5? 19.fxe5+- And Black will give up a piece.  18...Bxe5?! This capture is not much better. 19.fxe5 Ng8+-} 19. Bf2 {With the pawns blocking the bishop's scope, Fischer is now looking to reposition it to make use of it.} Qc7 {19...g6 20.f5 A very Fischer-like pawn sacrifice to open lines towards the enemy king. 20...gxf5 21.Bxf5! In for a penny, in for a pound! 21...exf5 22.Qxf5 22...Qd8 23.Bh4 23...Be7 Black is forced into a passive position in an attempt to survive! 24.Bxf6 Bxf6 25.Qxf6 Qxf6 26.Rxf6+- White has won a pawn and has a strong attack against the Black king.} 20. Bh4 Ng8 {Petrosian was known for his ability to smell out tactics. Here he tries to avoid falling into the sacrificial lines, but it does not help much.} 21. f5 {The same pawn sacrifice we saw in the analysis line. It is just as strong here!} Nxe5 22. dxe5 Bxe5 23. fxe6 Bf6 24. exf7 {Despite Petrosian's attempts to complicate matters, Fischer continues to find the best moves.} Bxf7 25. Nf3 Bxh4 {25...g5 26.Bf2 26...Kg7 27.Bd4+- If Black is forced to give up his dark square bishop and allow White to plant his knight permanently on d4, it is going to be a long ride home.} 26. Nxh4 Nf6 {Trying to untangle his position in an effort to get some activity.} 27. Ng6+ Bxg6 28. Bxg6 Ke7 {Fischer gave this move a double exclam. It is really hard to see why considering White's position is almost crushing here. While Black has been able to activate his pieces, his king is very vunerable and White's pieces are still very active!} 29. Qf5 Kd8 30. Rae1 Qc5+ 31. Kh1 Rf8 {31...Rc6 Fischer and Petrosian both thought this was a better try, but it doesn't matter much. White is already winning due to his safer king and the fact that Black's rook on h8 is still a spectator.} 32. Qe5 {Preventing the king from going c7-b8 to hide in the corner.} Rc7 33. b4 {When you have many ways to win, the game is easy to play. Here almost anything Fischer played was winning, but this move forces some serious problems for Black.} Qc6 34. c4 {With the not so subtle idea of mating the Black king.} dxc4 35. Bf5 {It is important to do this before the check to ensure the Black king cannot run away to the corner.} Rff7 36. Rd1+ Rfd7 37. Bxd7 Rxd7 {37...Nxd7 38.Rfe1+- Where mate on e8 is unavoidable.} 38. Qb8+ Ke7 {38...Qc8 39.Rxd7+ Nxd7 40.Qd6+-} 39. Rde1+ {Petrosian resigned here as there is no way to avoid checkmate.  39.Rde1+ Kf7 40.Qe8} *

