1. e4 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 (2... e6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 3. Nge2 {White's main idea is to meet} d5 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow with} ({Black can also develop his pieces in other ways but White's plan is the same: g2-g3, f1-g2, 0-0; in the event of ...d7-d5 - e4xd5 e6xd5 d2-d4! For instance:} 3... a6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 4. g3 b5 5. Bg2 Bb7 6. O-O Nf6 7. d3 d6 (7... d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. Nxd5 Bxd5 10. Bxd5 Qxd5 11. a4 b4 12. Nf4 Qd7 {1/2 (30) Solodovnichenko,Y (2564)-Ibrayev,N (2406) Voronezh 2009} 13. Nh5 $5 Nc6 14. Re1 $36) 8. f4 {It is time for this advance as the ...d6-d5 counterblow is ineffective due to Black's poor development} Nc6 9. f5 b4 10. Nb1 e5 (10... exf5 11. exf5 {[%cal Ye2f4]}) 11. Nd2 Be7 12. Nc4 {with a pleasant position.}) (3... Nf6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 4. g3 d5 5. exd5 exd5 (5... Nxd5 6. Bg2 Nxc3 7. bxc3 {[%cal Yd2d4] leads to a comfortable position for White}) 6. d4 Bg4 {is not so effective here:} 7. Bg2 cxd4 8. Qxd4 Nc6 9. Qa4 {, and the position of White's queen doesn't give Black sufficient compensation for the weakness of his d5-pawn, e.g.} Bc5 10. Bg5 O-O 11. Qxg4 Nxg4 12. Bxd8 Raxd8 (12... Bxf2+ $2 13. Kf1 Raxd8 14. h3 d4 15. Nb5) 13. O-O Rfe8 14. Nf4 d4 {0-1 (71) Fillon,D (2140)-Gonzales,F (2405) Manresa 1995} 15. Ncd5 $14) 4. exd5 exd5 5. d4 {intending to isolate Black's d5-pawn. Here are some practical examples:} Nc6 6. Be3 c4 (6... Nf6 7. dxc5 {e.g.} Be7 8. Nd4 O-O 9. Be2 Ne5 10. O-O Bxc5 11. Ne6 fxe6 12. Bxc5 Rf7 13. f4 Nc6 14. Bf3 $14 {1-0 (18) Yermolinsky,A (2518)-Shabalov,A (2633) Tulsa 2008}) (6... cxd4 7. Nxd4 Bb4 8. Nxc6 Bxc3+ (8... bxc6 9. Qd4) 9. bxc3 bxc6 10. Bd3 Nf6 11. Bc5 Be6 12. Qe2 Nd7 13. Bd6 Nb6 14. Qe5 Qf6 15. Qxf6 gxf6 16. O-O Na4 17. Rfb1 Nxc3 18. Rb7 $36 {1/2 (40) Spassky,B (2625)-Polugaevsky,L (2600) Bugojno 1982}) 7. g3 Bb4 8. Bg2 h6 (8... Nge7 9. O-O O-O 10. Nf4) 9. O-O Nf6 {0-1 (39) Welling,G (2393)-Sulskis,S (2576) Port Erin 2002} 10. Bf4 O-O 11. Be5 $14 {- in all cases White keeps an initiative due to Black's weak d5-pawn.}) (2... d6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 3. g3 g6 {etc. - 2...c6}) 3. Nge2 e6 (3... e5 4. Nd5 Nce7 $5 $13) 4. g3 d5 ({If Black delays this advance:} 4... Nf6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 5. Bg2 Be7 ({in the event of} 5... d5 {White can already play} 6. exd5 exd5 7. d4) 6. O-O O-O {then White should rather wait with the advance of his d-pawn until Black's d-pawn moves to d6 or d5:} 7. h3 (7. d3 d5 $1) (7. f4 d5 $1) 7... a6 (7... d5 8. exd5 exd5 9. d4 {e.g.} cxd4 10. Nxd4 Qb6 11. Nce2 a5 12. a4 Bc5 13. Nb3 Bf5 14. Nxc5 Qxc5 15. c3 Be4 16. Be3 Qe7 17. Nd4 Ne5 18. Re1 $14 {[%csl Rd5] 1/2 (53) Deviatkin,A (2570)-Grachev,B (2668) Irkutsk 2010}) (7... d6 8. d3 {Now timely!} Rb8 9. f4 {[%cal Yf4f5,Yg3g4]} d5 10. exd5 exd5 11. f5 d4 12. Ne4 Nxe4 13. Bxe4 Bd6 14. Nf4 Ne5 15. Qh5 Re8 16. Ne6 ({too early;} 16. Nd5 $142 $40) 16... g6 $1 17. fxg6 hxg6 18. Rxf7 $1 {1/2 (18) Westerinen,H (2390)-Sakaev,K (2540) Gausdal 1992} gxh5 19. Rg7+ Kh8 20. Rh7+ $11) 8. d3 {After Black's a-pawn advance (not so useful) White can show his hand.} b5 (8... d5 9. exd5 exd5 10. d4 (10. Nf4 $5)) 9. e5 $5 (9. f4 $5) 9... Ne8 10. Bxc6 dxc6 {0-1 (29) Reinderman,D (2435)-Kuporosov,V (2500) Pardubice 1993} 11. Re1 $14 {[%csl Rc8][%cal Ye2f4]}) 5. exd5 exd5 6. Bg2 (6. d4 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow: 'is now not so effective due to'} Bg4 $1 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow: 'It is not the end of the story but I prefer the text move anyway.'}) 6... d4 (6... Nf6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow allows White to implement his main idea:} 7. d4 $1 {The position after the isolation of Black's d-pawn is easy to play for White (although he can't claim a serious advantage yet). Here are some practical examples:} cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bb4 (8... Bg4 9. Qd3 Bc5 10. Qe3+ $5 Ne7 11. O-O O-O 12. Qd3 h6 13. h3 Bh5 14. Be3 Qc8 {1/2 (73) Yu,Y (2585)-Ni,H (2667) Danzhou 2010} 15. Rfe1 $14 {[%cal Yd4b3]}) 9. O-O O-O 10. Nce2 (10. Bg5 {is not too effective:} Bxc3 11. bxc3 h6 12. Bxf6 Qxf6 13. Bxd5 Rd8 14. Bxc6 bxc6 15. Qf3 Qxf3 16. Nxf3 c5 17. Rfe1 Be6 {1/2 (17) Rosenhahn,E (2355)-Polakovic,P (2325) ICCF 1995}) 10... Re8 11. h3 Ne4 12. Nb3 a5 13. c3 Bf8 14. a4 g5 15. Ned4 Bg7 16. Re1 Ne5 17. Nb5 Ra6 18. Be3 Rh6 19. g4 f5 $6 20. f3 Ng3 21. f4 $1 $16 {1-0 (36) Stanojoski,Z (2467)-Bolzoni,V (2252) Kemer 2007}) 7. Nd5 Nf6 (7... Bd6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow leads to a transposition after} 8. d3 Nge7 9. Nef4 O-O 10. O-O Nxd5) ({or} 7... Nge7 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 8. Nef4) 8. Nef4 Nxd5 (8... Bd6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 9. d3 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Bd2 Nxd5) 9. Nxd5 Bd6 (9... Be6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow is senseless due to} 10. Nf4 Bf5 {(Black has just wasted time)} 11. O-O Be7 12. Qh5 $5) 10. O-O (10. d3 $5 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow eliminates the possibility of 10...d3 (see below).}) 10... O-O (10... d3 $5 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow is interesting but still hardly effective:} 11. b4 $5 (11. c3 $5 O-O 12. Qf3 Ne5 13. Qe4 g6 14. Ne3 {[%cal Rf2f4]}) 11... O-O 12. bxc5 Bxc5 13. Bb2 Qg5 14. cxd3 Bd7 15. h4 Qh6 16. Rc1 $36 {1-0 (25) Mariano,N (2416)-Korobov,A (2571) Dubai 2004}) (10... Be6 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 11. d3) 11. d3 Be6 (11... Re8 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow is another (probably more precise) way of development for Black. Here is an example demonstrating White's plans:} 12. Bd2 Bf5 13. Qh5 Bg6 (13... Qd7 14. Rae1 Bg4 15. Qg5 Kh8 16. Qh4) 14. Qg4 Qc8 15. Qxc8 Raxc8 {1/2 (25) Larsson,E (2305)-Mathias,M (2340) ICCF 1996} 16. Bf4 Bxf4 17. Nxf4 Bf5 18. Rac1 {with a complicated endgame.}) 12. Nf4 $146 (12. Qh5 Ne5 $1 13. h3 Ng6 14. f4 f5 $10 {Moehring-Antoschin/Zinnowitz/1966/ Ftacnik/Krasenkow: 'Moehring-Antoshin/Zinnowitz/1966/'}) 12... Bf5 (12... Bxf4 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 13. Bxf4 Bd5 {is more accurate but still White can fight on for an edge:} 14. Qh5 Nb4 15. a3 Bxg2 16. Kxg2 Nd5 (16... Qd5+ 17. Qf3 $1 Qxf3+ 18. Kxf3 Nd5 19. Rfe1 $14) 17. Bd2 Qd6 18. Rfe1 Rae8 {0-1 (40) Blasek,R (2335)-Kharlov,A (2545) Neu Isenburg 1992} 19. Qf3 {[%cal Ye1e4,Yb2b4]}) 13. h3 $1 Rb8 14. Bd2 Re8 15. Re1 Rxe1+ 16. Qxe1 Qd7 $6 17. g4 $1 Re8 18. Qd1 Bxf4 19. Bxf4 Be6 20. Qf3 Nb4 21. Qxb7 (21. a3 $6 Nxc2 22. Rc1 Bb3 23. Qxb7 Qxb7 24. Bxb7 Re1+) 21... Qxb7 22. Bxb7 Nxc2 (22... Nxa2 23. Bd6 $16) 23. Rc1 Nb4 24. Be4 Bxa2 $2 (24... c4 $1 25. dxc4 Bxg4 26. Re1 $1 (26. hxg4 Rxe4) (26. Bxh7+ $2 Kxh7 27. hxg4 Nd3 $19) (26. f3 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow: '!'} Bxh3 (26... f5 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 27. Bb1 Bxf3 28. a3 Nc6 29. Bxf5 $36) (26... Bxf3 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow} 27. Bxf3 Nd3 28. Rd1 Nxf4 29. Rxd4 Nxh3+ 30. Kg2 Ng5 31. Rd7 $44) 27. c5 (27. Bd6 $44 {Ftacnik/Krasenkow}) 27... Rxe4 $1 28. fxe4 Nd3 29. Bg5 Nxc1 30. Bxc1 $17) (26. Bb1 $5 Bxh3 27. a3 Nc6 28. b4 f6 29. b5 Ne5 30. Bxe5 fxe5 31. Be4 $16) 26... Bf5 27. Bd2 Rxe4 28. Rxe4 Bxe4 29. Bxb4 $10) 25. Bd2 $1 (25. Rxc5 Bb1 $1 $132) 25... Bd5 (25... Na6 $2 26. Ra1 $18) (25... Rc8 $2 26. Bxb4 $18) 26. Bxd5 Nxd5 27. Rxc5 Nb6 28. Kf1 $1 f6 29. Ra5 Re7 30. Bb4 Rd7 31. Bc5 Kf7 32. Ke2 g5 $2 (32... Ke6 $16) 33. Kf3 Kg6 34. Ke4 h5 35. Bxd4 Re7+ 36. Kf3 h4 37. Bc5 Re1 38. Rxa7 Nd5 39. Bf8 $1 Re8 (39... Rh1 40. Rg7+ Kh6 41. Rd7+ $18) 40. Bd6 Re6 41. Rd7 Nb6 42. Rd8 Nd5 43. b4 Re1 44. b5 Rb1 45. Rb8 Rb3 46. Ke4 Nc3+ 47. Kd4 Nxb5+ 48. Kc4 Rc3+ 49. Kxb5 Rxd3 50. Kc6 $1 Rxh3 51. Kd5 Rf3 52. Ke6 $1 (52. Bc5 Rf4 53. Rb4 h3 $1) (52. Rb2 f5 53. gxf5+ Kxf5 $132) 52... Rxf2 (52... h3 53. Rg8+ Kh7 54. Kf7 h2 55. Bxh2 $1 (55. Bf8 Rd3 $1 (55... h1=Q $2 56. Bg7 Qh5+ 57. gxh5 $18) 56. Rg7+ Kh8 57. Rg8+ $10) 55... Rxf2 56. Bd6 f5 57. Rg7+ Kh6 58. Rg6+ Kh7 59. Rf6 f4 60. Bf8 Rh2 61. Rf5 $18) 53. Rg8+ Kh7 54. Kf7 Ra2 (54... h3 55. Rg7+ Kh8 (55... Kh6 56. Bf8 h2 57. Rg6+ Kh7 58. Rh6#) 56. Rg6 Kh7 57. Bf8) (54... f5 55. Rg7+ Kh6 (55... Kh8 56. Be5 fxg4+ 57. Kg6 Rf8 58. Rh7+ Kg8 59. Rh8#) 56. Rg6+ Kh7 57. Rf6 Rd2 (57... f4 58. Bf8 $18) 58. gxf5 h3 (58... g4 59. Bf4 Rd7+ 60. Ke6 Rb7 61. Rh6+ Kg8 62. Rxh4 $18) 59. Bf8 Rd7+ 60. Ke6 Rd3 61. Rh6+ Kg8 62. Bd6 Kg7 63. Rh5 $18) 55. Rg7+ Kh6 56. Bf8 Ra7+ 57. Kxf6 Ra6+ (57... Rxg7 58. Bxg7+ Kh7 59. Kf7 h3 60. Be5 $18) 58. Kf7 (58. Kf7 Ra7+ 59. Kg8 Rxg7+ 60. Bxg7+ Kg6 61. Be5 $18) 1-0

