{[%evp 0,47,19,24,48,48,101,86,82,32,43,23,51,19,59,56,86,34,40,-22,31,17,25,32,25,-90,-10,79,84,102,102,114,97,68,58,145,84,242,220,229,211,215,193,262,387,519,519,535,563,654] The following game was shown in a group lesson organised by the Dutch Chess Federation with IM Edwin van Haastert, when I was about 13 years old. At that time Fischer's play made a big impression on me, not particularly because of the sacrifices, but more because of his calm follow-up between moves 16 and 20. Hope you like it too!} 1. e4 {ChessBase} c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. h3 ({In the seventh round of the same event, Fischer played a model game of the Black side of the Najdorf:} 6. Be2 {Pilnik,H-Fischer,R Mar del Plata 1959 Unzicker,W-Fischer,R Varna ol 1962 ChessBase: 'Pilnik,H-Fischer,R Mar del Plata 1959; Unzicker,W-Fischer,R Varna ol 1962'} e5 7. Nb3 Be6 8. O-O Nbd7 9. f4 Qc7 10. f5 Bc4 11. a4 Be7 12. Be3 O-O 13. a5 b5 14. axb6 Nxb6 15. Bxb6 Qxb6+ 16. Kh1 Bb5 17. Bxb5 axb5 18. Nd5 Nxd5 19. Qxd5 Ra4 20. c3 Qa6 21. h3 Rc8 22. Rfe1 h6 23. Kh2 Bg5 24. g3 Qa7 25. Kg2 Ra2 26. Kf1 Rxc3 {0-1 Unzicker.W-Fischer,R Varna 1962. It's this game which influenced the further development and assessment of the weakened dark-square on d5 in the Sicilian Najdorf. Another absolute classic every Najdorf player should know!}) (6. Bg5 {Walther,E-Fischer,R Zürich 1959 Smyslov,V-Fischer,R Beograd ct 1959 ChessBase: 'Walther,E-Fischer,R Zürich 1959; Smyslov,V-Fischer,R Belgrade ct 195'}) (6. Bc4 {Fischer,R-Tal,M Beograd ct 1959 Fischer,R-Bednarski,J Habana ol 1966 Fischer,R-Geller,E Skopje 1967 ChessBase: 'Fischer,R-Tal,M Belgrade ct 1959; Fischer,R-Bednarski,J Havana ol 1966; Fischer,R-Geller,E Skopje 1967'}) 6... b5 $5 (6... g6 {Fischer,R-Reshevsky,S ch-USA 1962} 7. g4 Bg7 8. g5 $6 ({Nowadays people prefer either} 8. Bg2 {or}) (8. Be3) 8... Nh5 9. Be2 e5 10. Nb3 Nf4 11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. Qxd5 Nc6 13. Bg4 Bxg4 14. hxg4 Qc8 15. Qd1 Nd4 $6 ({A technical mistake, after which Black is left with only a poor bishop on g7. The engine mentions} 15... d5 $5 {as a reasonable try for Black.}) 16. c3 Nxb3 17. axb3 Qe6 18. Ra5 $1 f6 19. Qd5 Qxd5 20. Rxd5 Kd7 21. gxf6 Bxf6 22. g5 $1 {Another great move of Fischer in the 6.h3 Najdorf which always springs to mind. Fixing the weakness on h7 is more relevant than placing the g5 pawn on the colour of your own bishop. Fischer demonstrates great technique, developing pressure on all sides of the board:} Be7 23. Ke2 Raf8 24. Be3 Rc8 25. b4 b5 26. Rdd1 Ke6 27. Ra1 Rc6 28. Rh3 Bf8 29. Rah1 Rc7 30. Rh4 d5 31. Ra1 Rc6 32. exd5+ Kxd5 33. Rd1+ Ke6 34. Rd8 Kf5 35. Ra8 Re6 36. Rh3 Bg7 37. Rxh8 Bxh8 38. Rxh7 Re8 39. Rf7+ Kg4 40. f3+ Kg3 41. Kd3 e4+ 42. fxe4 Rd8+ 43. Bd4 Kg4 44. Rf1 Be5 45. Ke3 Bc7 46. Rg1+ Kh4 47. Kf3 Rd7 48. e5 Rf7+ 49. Ke4 Rf5 50. e6 Bd8 51. Bf6 Bxf6 52. gxf6 Rxf6 53. Kd5 Rf2 54. Re1 {1-0 Fischer-Reshevsky, New York 1962.}) (6... Nc6 {Fischer,R-Bolbochan,J Stockholm izt 1962} 7. g4 Nxd4 8. Qxd4 e5 9. Qd3 Be7 10. g5 Nd7 11. Be3 Nc5 $6 {This knight isn't fighting for the d5-square.} 12. Qd2 Be6 13. O-O-O O-O 14. f3 Rc8 15. Kb1 Nd7 16. h4 b5 17. Bh3 $16 {Typical strategy for White to go for the exchange of light-squared bishops in order to gain more control over the d5-square.} Bxh3 18. Rxh3 Nb6 19. Bxb6 $1 {These kinds of move weren't so common still at that time, though nowadays most people would play it without spending a second!} Qxb6 20. Nd5 Qd8 21. f4 exf4 22. Qxf4 Qd7 23. Qf5 Rcd8 24. Ra3 Qa7 25. Rc3 g6 26. Qg4 Qd7 27. Qf3 Qe6 28. Rc7 Rde8 29. Nf4 Qe5 30. Rd5 Qh8 31. a3 h6 32. gxh6 Qxh6 33. h5 Bg5 34. hxg6 fxg6 35. Qb3 Rxf4 36. Re5+ Kf8 37. Rxe8+ {1-0 Fischer-Bolbochan, Stockholm 1962.}) ({The main moves nowadays are} 6... e6 {and}) (6... e5) 7. Nd5 $5 ({Intending to exploit the weakened c6-square. Another approach is} 7. g4) 7... Bb7 $2 (7... Nbd7 $4 8. Nc6 $18 {is a funny way to win the queen.}) (7... Nfd7 8. Bg5 $1 h6 $2 9. Ne6 $1 {is another fun trap.}) ({A safer option is} 7... e6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 {when the queen is bit strangely placed on f6. I think a developing move like 9.d3 makes sense. However, Fischer's recommendation} 9. c4 {can strongly be met by} d5 $1 {and Black seizes the initiative in the centre.} (9... b4 $16)) (7... Nxe4 {'!' ChessBase: '!' is playing with fire, e.g.} 8. Qf3 Nc5 9. b4 $1 (9. Nf6+ $2 gxf6 10. Qxa8 Bb7 11. Qa7 Qc7 {does offer Black fantastic play for the exchange.} (11... e5 12. b4 exd4 13. bxc5 Qe7+ 14. Be2 Nc6 15. Qb6 dxc5 16. O-O $1 $16) 12. b4 Ncd7 $44) 9... Na4 (9... Nb7 $2 10. Qc3 $1) (9... Ncd7 10. Qc3 $1 Ra7 11. Bxb5 $1 (11. Bg5 $5)) (9... e6 10. bxc5 (10. Nf6+ Qxf6 11. Qxa8 Qxd4 12. Qxb8 Qxa1 13. Qxc8+ Ke7) 10... exd5 11. Qxd5 Ra7 {'=' ChessBase: '='} 12. c6 $14) 10. c4 $2 (10. Be3 $1 {is a better way to fight for an advantage.}) 10... e6 11. Nc3 {was seen in Short-Sarana, Moscow 2018 and now again} d5 $1 {would have given Black an excellent position.}) (7... Nxd5 8. exd5 {is positionally ugly.} Bb7 9. c4 $1 {and the bishop is badly placed on b7.}) 8. Nxf6+ gxf6 9. c4 $5 ({An interesting move, attempting to exploit Black's early ...b7-b5. Equally good is a solid move like} 9. Bd3 {which isn't putting Black under pressure right away and probably not seriously considered by Fischer.} Nd7 $14) 9... bxc4 $6 ({Opening the position (with your king in the centre and other pieces on the back rank still) simply feels wrong. A safer option is} 9... b4 { but then ChessBase: ''} 10. Bd3 {and the inclusion of the move c2-c4 is in White's favour. White has a nice central grip, can develop his pieces and ideas to play on both sides as Black's kingside has been weakened and with a2-a3 the queenside can be opened as well.}) (9... Bxe4 $6 10. cxb5 Bg7 11. Qg4 Bg6 {and now instead of} 12. Nf5 {which was mentioned both by Fischer and Kasparov, I think White might also consider} (12. f4 $5 {. In both cases Black remains under serious pressure I would say.}) 12... O-O $13) 10. Bxc4 Bxe4 (10... Qa5+ 11. Bd2 Qe5 12. Qb3 $1 Qxe4+ 13. Kd1 $1 {'' ChessBase: ''} ({I love this move, preparing e1 and is clearly more ambitious than} 13. Be3) 13... e6 14. Nxe6 $1 ({Even stronger than} 14. Re1 {which is Kasparov's recommendation.}) 14... fxe6 15. Bxe6 {and because of Black's coordination problems (king in centre, underdevelopment, b7 which requires protection) White quickly brings in his rook to e1 with a crushing attack. You hardly feel have just sacrificed a piece :)}) 11. O-O $1 {Simple chess: just completing your development!} d5 ({In the event of} 11... e6 {sacrifices on e6 may be considered, though probably many of us would first play} 12. Re1 $5) 12. Re1 $6 ({Probably Fischer's only inaccurate decision, but in this complex position with a wealth of options both players overlooked Black's powerful resource on the next move. After} 12. Bb3 $1 {intending a4 and installing a knight on c6, Black's position will be paralysed.} Bg7 ({Another nice line illustrating the effect of White's 12th move:} 12... e6 13. Re1 Bc5 14. Ba4+ Nd7 15. b4 $1 Bb6 (15... Bxb4 16. Nc6 Qb6 17. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 18. Rb1 {and White is winning.}) 16. Nc6 Qc7 17. Qg4 {and there are no good moves left for Black.}) 13. Ba4+ Nd7 14. Re1 O-O 15. Nc6 Qc7 (15... Qe8 16. f3) 16. Nxe7+ $18 {followed by taking on d5, leading to a position with extra pawn, bishop pair and better structure.}) 12... e5 (12... Rg8 $2 {is met by} 13. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 14. Qh5 Qxd4 (14... Rg7 15. Nf5) (14... Rg6 15. Qxh7 Rg7 16. Qxe4 Ra7 17. Nf5 (17. Bf4)) 15. Qxf7+ Kd8 16. Be3 {with a crushing attack.}) (12... e6 13. Qh5 ({Again} 13. Bb3 $5 {makes sense as well.}) 13... Bg6 14. Qxd5 Qxd5 15. Bxd5 Ra7 16. Bf4 Rd7 17. Nxe6 fxe6 18. Bxe6 Nc6 19. Rac1) (12... h5 13. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 14. Qb3 $1 Qxd4 15. Be3 $18 {followed by taking on f7 and d1.}) (12... Nd7 13. Rxe4 $1 (13. Nc6 Qc7 14. Bxd5) 13... dxe4 (13... dxc4 14. Nc6 Qc7 15. Rxc4 {with a free attack!}) 14. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 15. Qh5+ Kg8 16. Qg4+ Bg7 17. Nf5 {and White wins, as} Qf8 18. Nh6#) (12... dxc4 { ChessBase: ''} 13. Rxe4 Qd5 14. Qf3 $1 e6 15. Bf4 $1 Nd7 (15... f5 16. Nxf5 $1 Qxf5 17. Re5 $18) 16. Nxe6 fxe6 17. Rd1 Qc6 18. Rd6 $1 Bxd6 19. Rxe6+ Kf7 20. Qxc6 Kxe6 21. Qxd6+ {and White wins.}) ({The remarkable} 12... Bxg2 $1 {would have kept Black in the game!} {After} 13. Kxg2 dxc4 14. Qf3 {various annotators have tried to prove something in vain, as with correct play Black should be able to manage his position.} Ra7 (14... Nd7 15. Nf5 Rg8+ (15... e6 16. Rxe6+ $1 fxe6 17. Qh5#) 16. Kh1 e5 (16... e6 17. Qc6 { xe6+ ChessBase: ' xe6+'}) 17. Be3 $18) 15. Be3 Rd7 16. Rad1) 13. Qa4+ $1 ({The immediate} 13. Rxe4 {is also good, though Fischer's idea to start with a check is more logical.} dxe4 14. Qa4+ Qd7 15. Bb5 axb5 16. Qxa8 exd4 17. Qxb8+ Ke7) 13... Nd7 (13... Qd7 14. Bb5 $1 axb5 15. Qxa8 Bd6 16. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 17. Qxe4 $36 { f5 when the knight reaches f5, though after ChessBase: ' f5'} Bc5 18. Nb3 $1 (18. Nf5 $6 Qd1+ 19. Kh2 Bxf2 {is less convincing.}) 18... Qd1+ 19. Kh2 Bxf2 20. Bd2 Qh5 21. Bb4 {with a crushing attack.}) 14. Rxe4 $1 dxe4 (14... dxc4 15. Nf5 {is hopeless.}) 15. Nf5 $1 (15. Qb3 Qb6 16. Bxf7+ Kd8) 15... Bc5 {Particularly this position had made a deep impression on me when I saw this game for the first time. I remember we were asked to look for the best continuation and greedy as we all were, only suggested developing ideas to make further sacrifices possible. I still remember the fun we had, until the games continuation was presented to us and we realised that Fischer's choice is more clever.} 16. Ng7+ $1 Ke7 (16... Kf8 17. Bh6 Kg8 18. Qb3 {'+-' ChessBase: '+-' and the queen joins the attack.} Bxf2+ 19. Kxf2 $1 (19. Kh1 {also wins of course.}) 19... Qb6+ 20. Qxb6 Nxb6 21. Nh5 Nd7 22. Rd1 {and mate is inevitable.}) 17. Nf5+ Ke8 {So we are back with the same position, realising that Black is no longer allowed to castle.} 18. Be3 $1 ({Tal later suggested} 18. Bh6 {Tal,M but the text is much cleaner, depriving Black any hope of counterplay. ChessBase: 'Tal,M'} Ra7 19. Rd1 Qb6) 18... Bxe3 19. fxe3 { d6+ ChessBase: ' d6+' d6 becomes a big threat, although I somehow would feel sad moving the knight away from its amazing outpost on f5! :)} Qb6 20. Rd1 {Including the remaining last piece into the attack.} (20. Bxf7+ Kd8 $1 21. Rd1 Qb5) 20... Ra7 21. Rd6 $1 {'+-!' ChessBase: '+-!'} (21. Bxf7+ Kd8) (21. Nd6+ Ke7) 21... Qd8 { ChessBase: ''} (21... Qc7 22. Rc6 {is convincing, e.g.} (22. Rxf6 $18) 22... Qd8 23. Qb4 {Threatening g7} Rg8 24. Rc8 Qxc8 25. Qe7#) (21... Qxb2 22. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 (22... Kd8 23. Qa5+ Rc7 (23... Kc8 24. Ne7+ Kb8 25. Nc6+ Ka8 26. Nxa7 {and wins.}) 24. Be6 $18) 23. Rxd7+ Rxd7 24. Qxd7+ Kg6 25. Qg7+ Kxf5 26. Qg4#) 22. Qb3 Qc7 (22... Rf8 23. Ng7+ Ke7 24. Qa3 $1 {'+-!' ChessBase: '+-!' Threatening Re6} Qc7 25. Rd5+ $1 Kd8 (25... Nc5 26. Rxc5 Qd6 27. Nf5+ {and wins.}) 26. Qxf8#) 23. Bxf7+ Kd8 (23... Kf8 24. Qe6 (24. Bh5 { f7# ChessBase: ' f7#'}) 24... Qd8 25. Bh5 {and mate follows.}) 24. Be6 $1 (24. Be6 $1 {and Black had seen enough. For example} Rb7 25. Qd5 $1 {is absolutely hopeless for Black, as a loss of material can't be avoided. Judging from these analyses, 1962 wasn't such a bad year for Fischer with the Najdorf!} (25. Qa4 Qc8 26. Qa5+ Ke8 27. Qxa6 Kd8 28. Bxd7 Rxd7 29. Rxd7+ Qxd7 (29... Kxd7 30. Qd6+ Ke8 31. Qe7#) 30. Qxf6+ Kc7 31. Qxe5+ Kb6 32. Qxh8 $18)) 1-0

