{[%evp 0,82,30,26,20,2,-14,-5,21,23,38,65,64,81,115,45,63,77,78,57,57,55,39,10,42,11,17,9,0,1,7,-16,20,20,16,0,0,-27,-44,-11,14,0,-22,-47,-42,-10,-53,-75,-29,-75,-29,-52,-60,-64,-57,-76,-79,-85,-102,-76,-73,-68,-74,-98,-111,-87,-109,-85,-125,-171,-154,-165,-148,-151,-163,-187,-169,-236,-211,-212,-261,-261,-289,-389,-409] In the early '60s, Fischer had publicly challenged the World Champion Botvinnik to a World title match in which he, Fischer, would give him the odds of two points. Of course, nothing came out of it and Fischer needed another decade before working his way up to the supreme match, but in Reykjavik the 0-2 start effectively took place. And I cannot believe it was a coincidence, as in the first game Fischer more or less committed suicide in a dead drawn ending while he did not show up for the second one. The third game, however, was an entirely different business and Fischer started playing at full strength.} 1. d4 {Marin Spassky opened with this move in four out of his first five games with White (meaning until game nine). This policy must have been influenced by the fact that prior to the match he had twice defeated Fischer in the Grünfeld, but the challenger never repeated this opening during the match. After his first and only "genuine" win in Reykjavik against the Najdorf poisoned pawn in game 11, Spassky never returned to 1.d4 until the end of the match.} Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 ({Deviating from game one in which he played} 3. Nc3) 3... c5 $5 {There is a small paradox regarding Fischer's repertoire before Reykjavik. He was known for his stable but narrow opening choice, almost exclusively 1.e4 with White and the Najdorf Sicilian against 1.e4. But against 1.d4 he used several fianchetto openings, the King's Indian, the Grüfeld and the Benoni and starting with the late '60s the Nimzo-Indian. The Benoni is by no means more solid than the Grünfeld, but Fischer must have known well that Spassky would not try something agressive from the early phase.} 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nd2 { c4} Nbd7 8. e4 (8. Nc4 Nb6) 8... Bg7 9. Be2 (9. Bd3 $143 {Schwarz droht gelegentlich: c4, e5 And from time to time Black threatens: c4, e5 Schwarz droht gelegentlich: c4, e5}) (9. Bc4 $143 {Schwarz droht gelegentlich: b5 And from time to time Black threatens: b5 Schwarz droht gelegentlich: b5}) 9... O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 {In those years, this used to be the main line which does make some sense as it is a developing move preparing b2-b3 and b2. But with the passing of the years White started feeling that weakening the control along the d1-h5 diagonal may be premature.} ({This is why} 11. a4 {eventually became the main line, with the typical continuation} Ne5 12. Ra3 g5) (11. f3 {Petrosian Petrosjan}) 11... Nh5 $5 {1-0 Filip,M-Kluger,G Maroczy Memorial 1961 A provocative but entirely sound novelty, taking advantage of the weakened pressure along the light diagonal. Larsen does not agree that this move is new, mentioning a game Timman-Ljubojevic (see the link below). But actually that game only featured a similar idea, while the position was different. And if we talk about similarities, there is an even older example:} ({Two years later, Mecking, who never accepted that he was weaker than Fischer, experimented with what later became Black's main plan.} 11... Ne5 12. b3 {Developing the bishop to b2 offers Black free hands on the kingside.} (12. a4 $5) 12... g5 13. Bb2 g4 14. Rfe1 Nh5 15. Nd1 Nf4 16. Bb5 Rf8 17. Ne3 Qg5 18. Nf5 Bxf5 19. exf5 {Kortschnoj,V-Mecking,H Augusta 1974 (1-0, 42)} Nf3+ $1 20. Nxf3 gxf3 21. g3 Bxb2 22. Qxb2 Qxf5 $15) ({If I am not wrong, the moment when 11.c2 ceased to be the main line was when Mecking played the following game:} 11... Nb6 $5 {An interesting idea by Bilek who had played it three times in the '60s. But of course, Mecking's game's impact was stronger.} 12. Bb5 (12. a4 Nfxd5 $1 13. exd5 Bxc3 14. Bb5 Bxd2 15. Bxe8 Bxc1 16. Bxf7+ Kxf7 17. Qxc1 {Kapu,J-Bilek,I Budapest 1964 (1-0, 31)} Qf6) (12. Re1 {allows} Ng4 {taking full advantage of c2 and ...b6. A much later game revealed Black's idea:} 13. h3 $6 Nxf2 14. Kxf2 Qh4+ 15. Kf1 Bd4 16. Nd1 Qxh3 $1 {0-1 Gurgenidze,B-Tal,M URS-ch24 Final 1957 (18) Coelho,L (2041)-Rocha,W (2361) Brasilia 1999 (0-1, 27)  I am not sure whether Black started the whole attack spontaneously, or was inspired by}) 12... Bd7 13. a4 Bxb5 14. Nxb5 a6 15. Nc3 Nfd7 16. a5 Nc8 17. b3 Na7 18. Bb2 Nb5 19. Nxb5 Bxb2 20. Qxb2 axb5 21. Qc3 b4 22. Qg3 Nf6 $15 {Polugaevsky,L (2620)-Mecking,H (2635) Luzern 1977 (½-½, 42)}) ({Since the program does not allow inserting two game quotes on the same move, here is the link to the game mentioned by Larsen:} 11... Nh5 {0-1 Timman,J-Ljubojevic,L IBM Amsterdam 1972}) 12. Bxh5 {Of course the only reasonable answer. Otherwise the knight would display annoying activity.} (12. g3 Ne5 13. f4 Ng4 14. Nc4 {×  × } Bxc3 { b5 Byrne  b5 Byrne}) (12. f4 Bd4+ 13. Kh1 Ndf6 { 14... g4 Purdy  14... g4 Purdy}) (12. Rd1 Nf4 13. Bf1 Ne5 14. Nc4 g5 $1 {Smyslov}) 12... gxh5 13. Nc4 {Also principled.} (13. a4 Ne5 14. Nd1 $1 {is a bit too slow.} f5 $1 {Marin} (14... Qh4 15. Ne3 Ng4 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Nc4 Qf6 $2 {Nunn} (17... g3 18. fxg3 Qxe4 19. Qxe4 Rxe4 20. Nxd6 Re5 21. Nxf7 $16 {Nunn}) 18. Bd2 Qg6 19. Bc3 Bxc3 20. bxc3 b6 (20... f5 $142 21. f3 Bd7 $13 {Byrne}) 21. Rfe1 Ba6 22. Nd2 Re5 23. f4 $1 $18 {Gligoric-Kavalek/Skopje ol/1972/}) 15. exf5 Rf8 16. Ne3 Ng4 17. Ra3 (17. Ndc4 {Kverndal,L-Wibe,T Norway 1990 (0-1, 36)} Qf6) 17... Qh4 18. h3 (18. Nf3 Qf6) 18... Nxe3 19. Rxe3 (19. fxe3 $6 Bxf5 $1 20. Rxf5 $2 Qe1+ 21. Nf1 Rxf5 22. Qxf5 Rf8 $19) 19... Bxf5 {with comfortable play.}) 13... Ne5 { 14.e5 e5 Purdy  14.e5 e5 Purdy} 14. Ne3 ({In principle, allowing Black to keep his central knight does not look right. Instinctively I felt that} 14. Nxe5 {was the principled continuation, but actually Black's bishops become very active.} Bxe5 15. Be3 {Byrne (Byrne)} (15. f4 Bd4+ 16. Kh1 f5 17. e5 dxe5 18. fxe5 Bxe5 19. Bf4 Bxf4 20. Rxf4 Qd6 21. Rf3 Bd7 22. Raf1 Re5 {ae8 with normal play for Black.}) 15... Qh4 $5 {Making full use of the bishop's strength before exchanging it on c3.} (15... f5 {may still be okay for Black but it is a bit irritating that after} 16. f4 Bxc3 17. Qxc3 Rxe4 {White has} 18. Rf3) 16. f4 (16. h3 Bxh3 $1 17. f4 (17. gxh3 Qxh3 18. f3 Qxf3 $19) 17... Bxc3 18. Qxc3 (18. bxc3 Bf5 19. Bf2 Bxe4 20. Qxe4) 18... Qg3 19. Qd2 Qg7 $11) (16. g3 {Deprives White of a later f3-g3.} Qe7 17. Rfe1 Bd7 18. a4 a6 19. a5 f5 $11) 16... Bxc3 17. Qxc3 (17. bxc3 Bf5 $1 18. Bf2 Bxe4 $1 $15) 17... Rxe4 18. Rf3 Bg4 19. Rg3 f5 20. Bf2 Qe7 21. Re1 (21. h3 h4 $15) 21... Rxe1+ 22. Bxe1 Re8 23. Bf2 Qe2 24. h3 h4 25. Rd3 Bh5 $11) 14... Qh4 {This move eventually turned out well for Black, but only because of White's lack of resolution on the next move.} (14... Ng4 $1 {Marin  Black should make use of this square while still available.} 15. f3 (15. h3 Nxe3 16. Bxe3 Bd7 17. a4 Qh4 $11) (15. Nxg4 hxg4 16. f3 f5 $5 17. fxg4 fxg4 18. Bf4 b6 $1 {Preparing to neutralise b5 with ...a6.} 19. Nb5 Rf8 $1 20. Bg3 Rxf1+ 21. Rxf1 Ba6 $11) 15... Nxe3 16. Bxe3 Bd7 {[%cal Gc3b5]} 17. a4 f5 18. Rae1 Qf6 $132) (14... a6 $5 15. a4 Ng4 {may be even simpler.}) 15. Bd2 (15. f3 $1 {(Byrne) was the way to take advantage of Fischer's move order.} Bd7 16. Ne2 $1 Ng6 17. Ng3 {Black is in strategic trouble and concrete play does not help him.} Bd4 (17... Be5 18. Nc4 Bd4+ 19. Be3 $16) 18. Kh1 Be5 19. f4 Bxf4 20. Nef5 Bxf5 21. Bxf4 Bg4 (21... Bxe4 22. Nxe4 Nxf4 23. g3 $18) 22. Bxd6 $16 {Marin}) (15. Nb5 Qe7 { 16... a6,17... b5    16... a6,17... b5  } 16. a4 a6 17. Nc3 b6 { b8, d7, b5  b8, d7, b5} (17... Rb8 $2 18. a5 $1 $16) 18. a5 $2 b5 {Smith}) (15. Ne2 Ng4 16. Nxg4 Bxg4 $5 (16... Qxg4 17. f3 $1) (16... hxg4 17. Ng3 Be5 18. Bd2 {may offer White some chances to stabilise the position in his favour, even though Black is in no immediate danger.} Bd7 19. Qc1 Qe7 20. Bc3 Bxg3 21. fxg3 Qxe4 22. Rf6 Qe3+ 23. Qxe3 Rxe3 24. Rxd6 $14) 17. f3 (17. Ng3 Qe7 18. f3 h4 19. Ne2 Bd7 $15) 17... Bd7 18. Ng3 Be5 19. Be3 (19. Qf2 f5 20. f4 Bg7 $15) 19... Bxg3 20. hxg3 Qxg3 {White does have compensation for the pawn but with ...f7-f5 in sight Black should be doing fine.}) 15... Ng4 (15... Nf3+ $2 16. gxf3 Be5 17. Rfc1 $18 {Byrne}) 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Bf4 (17. Ne2 $5 f5 (17... Bf5 18. Ng3 (18. exf5 Rxe2 $17) 18... Bg6 19. Rae1 h5 20. Bc3 $10 {Smith}) 18. Ng3 fxe4 $1 (18... Be5 $1 { 19... f4!  19... f4! Actually "?!" Marin} 19. exf5 Rf8 20. Rae1 $1 (20. Bc3 Rf6 $1 21. Bxe5 Rh6 $19) (20. Rfe1 Bxf5 21. Nxf5 $2 Qxh2+ $40 {+- +-}) (20. f4 Bd4+ 21. Kh1 Rf6 { g3  g3} 22. Be1 Rh6 $19 {Purdy}) 20... Bxf5 21. Qc1 Qf6 22. Bg5 Qg6 23. Rxe5 dxe5 24. Be7 Rf7 25. d6 $14 {Marin}) 19. Rfe1 { 20.c3 Smyslov  20.c3 Smyslov} e3 $1 {Causing WHite to waste a tempo.} 20. Bxe3 Qf6 21. Bd2 Bd7 22. Bc3 Qf7 $11 {Marin}) 17... Qf6 18. g3 $6 {'?' This was severely criticised as it "freezes" White's kingside. In principle, it is a concession but White does not have such a great position anyway.} (18. Qd2 {Smith/Smyslov ''} Bd7 (18... Qg6 {''  19... f5  Purdy  19... f5  Purdy} 19. Nb5 $1 Rxe4 20. Rae1 $36 {Marin}) 19. a4 (19. Rae1 b5) 19... Qg6 $132 {Marin}) (18. Bg3 $132 Bd7 $1 {Marin Not only threatening ...b7-b5 but also connecting rooks.} (18... h5 19. f3 $1 {Byrne} (19. Nb5 $1 {Kholmov}) 19... h4 20. Be1 {Purdy Purdy} (20. fxg4 Qe7 $17)) (18... a6 $5 {Marin may be playable, but I prefer the main line.}) 19. f4 (19. f3 b5 20. fxg4 Qg6 21. Rae1 Rac8 {preparing ...b5-b4 followed by ...c5-c4. Black has excellent compensation for the pawn (which is sort of hanging, by the way)}) 19... h5 20. e5 (20. f5 h4 21. Bf4 b5 $11) 20... Qh6 {Threatening ...h5-h4.} 21. Qf2 f5 $1 {Taking advantage of the fact that the pawn on e5 is pinned in view of ...d4.} 22. Rae1 b5 23. Kh1 b4 24. Nd1 h4 25. Bxh4 dxe5 {and Black cannot be worse.}) 18... Bd7 $36 { b5  b5 Marin: I believe it is safe to say that from now until the end of the game there is no way to improve on Fischer's play. With the exception of the move repetition at some later moment, of course.} (18... a6 19. a4 Qg6 20. Qb3 $1 {Marin}) 19. a4 b6 { a6, b5 Smith  a6, b5 Smith} (19... a6 $2 20. a5 $1 $16) 20. Rfe1 (20. a5 b5) 20... a6 $17 {Smith   Black's position is more flexible and clearly preferable. The pawn on e4 is weak and ...b7-b5 is hard to stop. But since Black does not have a knight on e5, he needs to act accurately in order to avoid e4-e5 under favourable circumstances.} 21. Re2 { ad1, e5  ad1, e5} b5 $1 (21... Re7 22. Rae1 Rae8 23. Qd3 $1) 22. Rae1 (22. axb5 axb5 23. Rxa8 Rxa8 24. e5 dxe5 (24... Ra1+ 25. Kg2 dxe5 26. Rxe5 (26. Bxe5 Qh6 $1) 26... b4 27. Ne4 Qh6 $19) 25. Rxe5 b4 26. Ne2 (26. Ne4 Ra1+ 27. Kg2 Qa6 { f1#  f1#}) 26... Qg6 $1 {Byrne Byrne}) 22... Qg6 23. b3 Re7 {After having set his queenside pawns in motion, Fischer starts increasing the pressure on e4 without delay. Since the central pawn break is not annoying (see below) it is safe to say that this is the best move.} (23... Bxc3 24. Qxc3 bxa4 25. bxa4 Bxa4 26. e5 $1 $40 {Byrne}) ({Timman} 23... Rac8 24. axb5 (24. Qd2 c4 $1 25. b4 bxa4 $15) 24... axb5 25. Qd3 (25. Qd2 b4 $1 26. Nd1 Bb5 $19 {Marin}) 25... c4 26. bxc4 Rxc4 $132 {Timman   I assume that Black is slightly better here, but in the game Fischer will get more than that.}) ({If there had been any problem with e4-e5, Black could have considered} 23... Bd4 $5 {before ...e7.}) 24. Qd3 {Trying to slow down Black's regrouping.} (24. e5 $5 {Marin} dxe5 $1 (24... Qxc2 25. Rxc2 dxe5 26. axb5 axb5 27. Be3 c4 28. bxc4 bxc4 29. Ne4 Bf5 30. Rxc4 $11) 25. Bc1 Bf5 $17) (24. axb5 axb5 25. e5 dxe5 26. Qxg6 hxg6 27. Be3 c4 $17) 24... Rb8 { 25... ba  b3 26.ba Byrne   25... ba  b3 26.ba Byrne  On the way to e8, the rook determines some favourable queenside modifications.} 25. axb5 axb5 { 26... b4 27... b5 Smith  26... b4 27... b5 Smith} (25... Bxb5 $2 26. Nxb5 {Byrne}) 26. b4 $8 {Smith Smith} c4 (26... cxb4 27. Na2 $11) 27. Qd2 Rbe8 {Black has reached the desired regrouping after having made some queenside progress, too. The pawn on e4 is doomed.} 28. Re3 (28. Bg5 $4 Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Qxg5 $18) 28... h5 $1 {Fischer does not hurry on his way to win his first game against Spassky ever.} (28... Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Rxe4 30. Rxe4 Rxe4 31. Rxe4 Qxe4 32. Qf6 $132 {Byrne Byrne We can continue the line with} Bf5 {The only chance to maintain a (symbolic) advantage} 33. Qg5+ Kf8 34. Bxd6+ Ke8 {Checks have almost come to an end and Black threatens to start his own decisive attack with ...e1+ and ...e4+.} 35. Qe3 $1 Qxe3 36. fxe3 c3 37. e4 $1 Bxe4 38. Bf4 Bxd5 39. Kf2 Kd7 40. Ke3 c2 41. Kd4 Ke6 42. Bc1 $11 {Black cannot break White's fortress.}) 29. R3e2 Kh7 (29... Bxc3 $142 {Byrne}) 30. Re3 Kg8 (30... Bxc3 31. Qxc3 Rxe4 32. Rxe4 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Qxe4 34. Qf6 $132) 31. R3e2 Bxc3 {'' Byrne} 32. Qxc3 Rxe4 {After checking everything Fischer goes for the decisive operation.} 33. Rxe4 Rxe4 34. Rxe4 {In principle the fewer major pieces on the board, the better saving chances. But now White's back rank will be weak, so keeping a rook on the board deserves examining.} (34. Ra1 {Marin} Re8 $1 35. Qd4 (35. Ra6 Qb1+ 36. Bc1 Bf5 37. Rxd6 Bc2 38. Qd2 c3 39. Qg5+ Bg6 40. Kg2 Qd3 41. Be3 c2 $19 42. Rc6 Rxe3 $19) 35... Bf5 36. Bd2 Be4 37. Bc3 f6 {Black has neutralised White's counterplay and has an extra pawn and the better structure. The threat ...f5-f3 needs also to be permanently reckoned with.} 38. Qxf6 Qxf6 39. Bxf6 Bxd5 {With rooks on the board this should not cause big technical problems.} 40. f4 {The only way to free the king.} (40. Rd1 Bf3) 40... gxf3 41. Rd1 Bb7 42. Rxd6 Re1+ 43. Kf2 Re2+ 44. Kf1 Be4 $19 {[%cal Ge4d3]}) 34... Qxe4 $19 {Smith} 35. Bh6 (35. Bxd6 Qxd5 { 36... c6 +- Byrne  36... c6 +- Byrne}) 35... Qg6 {The queen's and bishop's manoeuvres will gradually neutralise White's counterattacking chances while also creating threats of their own.} 36. Bc1 Qb1 (36... f6 $5 {Marin} 37. Bb2 Kf7 38. Qd4 Qb1+ 39. Kg2 Qf5 40. Kg1 Bc8 41. Kf1 Bb7 42. Bc3 Bxd5 43. Ke1 {This looks less clear than the game as the king has managed to escape.}) 37. Kf1 {With the actual piece placement the attempt to escape with the king does not promise anything good.} (37. Qe1 {Tal} Bf5 38. Bd2 Qc2 39. Bc3 Be4 40. Qd2 Qb1+ 41. Qe1 Qd3 42. Qe3 Qd1+ $1 (42... Qxe3 $2 43. fxe3 Bxd5 44. e4 Bxe4 45. Kf2 f5 46. Ke3 Kf7 47. Kf4 Ke6 48. Ke3 Kd5 49. Kf4 $11 {leads to a different version of a familiar fortress.}) 43. Be1 (43. Qe1 Qf3 44. Kf1 Kh7 $5) 43... Qxd5 $19 {Marin}) (37. Qb2 {Marin} Qe4 38. Qd2 Bf5 39. Qe3 Qb1 40. Qd2 f6 $19 {[%cal Gf5e4]}) 37... Bf5 $1 38. Ke2 Qe4+ $1 {Fischer chooses the only convincing sequence. If allowed to regroup with b2 and d2-c1, White could still hope.} 39. Qe3 (39. Kd2 Qxd5+) 39... Qc2+ $1 (39... Qxd5 40. Qg5+ {Byrne}) 40. Qd2 (40. Ke1 $1 {Byrne   This does not change anything actually.} c3 $1 41. Qg5+ Bg6 42. Qe3 f6 $5 {Just one of the ways to put White in zugzwang.} 43. Qe6+ Bf7 $19 {B} 44. Qe3 Bxd5 45. Qe8+ Kg7 46. Qe7+ Bf7 47. Qe3 Kg8 48. Kf1 (48. Qf4 Bb3 $19) 48... Qd1+ 49. Kg2 (49. Qe1 Bc4+) 49... Bd5+) 40... Qb3 41. Qd4 $2 (41. Ke1 {was relatively better but it would not have saved him at all.} c3 $1 {The most convincing. White loses any chance for setting up a fortress on the dark squares.} (41... Qf3 42. Qg5+ (42. Qe3 Qh1+ 43. Ke2 Bd3+ 44. Kd2 Qxd5 45. Bb2 $17 {Marin}) 42... Bg6 43. Qe3 Qh1+ (43... Qf6 $1 {Marin}) 44. Kd2 (44. Ke2 $1 Qxd5 45. Bb2 Bd3+ 46. Kd2 $17 {Marin}) 44... Qxd5+ 45. Kc3 {Byrne   Actually not that much fun for White:} Qd1 46. Qe8+ Kg7 47. Qe3 Qb3+ 48. Kd2 Qxb4+ {Marin}) 42. Qd4 (42. Qg5+ Bg6 $19) 42... c2 43. Qf4 (43. Kd2 Qf3) 43... Qc3+ 44. Qd2 Qf3 $19 {Marin}) 41... Bd3+ $1 (41... Bd3+ 42. Ke1 (42. Kd2 Qc2+ 43. Ke1 Qxc1#) (42. Ke3 Qd1 $1 43. Qb2 Qf3+ 44. Kd4 Qe4+ 45. Kc3 Qe1+ $1 { 46... e5#  46... e5#}) 42... Qxb4+ $19) 0-1

