{I could not leave out the following famous game, as a fine example.} 1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. a3 {White spends a tempo to force the exchange x.} Bxc3+ ({Deviations: the retreat} 4... Ba5 {must be weaker. White has a pleasant choice between} 5. e5 ({also} 5. b4) ({or, the exchange} 5. exd5 {which is more favourable in this line.}) 5... c5 {the Armenian Variation.}) 5. bxc3 dxe4 $1 {Accepting the consequences is the right way.} ({Deviations: after} 5... Ne7 {White can play} 6. Qg4 $5 ({or, the solid} 6. Bd3 $5 c5 7. exd5 exd5 8. dxc5 $1 $14 {and Black faces a tough struggle for the draw, since Kuzmin-Chernin, USSR Cup 1984.}) ({or, even} 6. exd5 Qxd5 $6 7. Nf3 Bd7 8. c4 $16 {1-0 Miladinovic,I (2559)-Jovic,A (2386)/Kragujevac 2010 (34)}) 6... O-O 7. e5 {similar to the Warsaw Variation.} ({If here} 7. Bd3 $6 {suggested by Shirov, but} e5 $1 8. Qh4 exd4 $132 {seems good for Black.}) 7... f6 $6 ({I think the best try is} 7... f5 $142 $5 {only a few games}) (7... c5 8. Bd3 {the main Warsaw Variation.}) 8. Nf3 c5 9. exf6 Rxf6 10. Bg5 e5 (10... Qa5 11. Bxf6 Qxc3+ 12. Ke2 Ng6 13. Rc1 $1 gxf6 14. h4 $40 {[%cal Gh4h5]}) 11. Qg3 Re6 12. Nxe5 Nbc6 13. Bd3 $1 Qc7 14. O-O Nxe5 15. dxe5 c4 16. Bf6 Ng6 17. Bxg6 hxg6 18. Qxg6 Kh8 19. Rae1 gxf6 20. exf6 {1-0 Vallejo Pons,F (2697)-Dizdarevic,E (2475)/Plovdiv 2010}) 6. Qg4 $1 {This manoeuvre is generally considered to be the most dangerous resource against the Winawer Variation. Black has to face it in many lines.} ({The Winckelmann-Reimer Gambit} 6. f3 {can be met with the straightforward} c5 $1 (6... e5 $5 {is probably a good alternative}) 7. Be3 (7. Be2 {was played by GM Hector:} Nc6 (7... Nf6 $1 8. fxe4 Nxe4 9. Qd3 Qa5 $16) 8. fxe4 cxd4 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Nf3 dxc3 10. Bd3 e5 (10... Qb6 $5) 11. O-O Nge7 12. Qe1 O-O 13. Qxc3 Ng6 14. Bc4 Be6 15. Bxe6 fxe6 16. Be3 Rc8 {and Black is still better, in ½-½ Hector,J (2500)-Brinck Claussen,B (2380)/Copenhagen 1997/ (34)}) 7... Qa5 $1 8. dxc5 ({In the game Simon-De Francesco, Balatonbereny 1995, White tried:} 8. fxe4 Qxc3+ 9. Bd2 Qxd4 10. Nf3 Qxe4+ $17 {, but with the three extra pawns you never lose!}) 8... Nc6 (8... Nf6 $5) 9. Kf2 Nf6 10. Qd6 {As in Velicka-Babula, CZE-chT 1995. And here, instead of 10...exf3, I would recommend:} Nd5 $1 {playing for the initiative} (10... Ne7 $5) 11. Re1 Nxe3 12. Rxe3 e5 $1 $17 {Black intends simply ...e6 and ...d8. For instance,} 13. Bc4 Be6 $1 14. Bxe6 Rd8 $1 {winning the queen.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {The best defence versus g4 in all open lines.} 7. Qxg7 Rg8 8. Qh6 {A key position.} Nbd7 $5 {This has always been considered a good reply.} 9. Ne2 {The most popular (natural) move for White and at least it is not worse than the alternatives.} ({A) The Serbian WEAPON:} 9. a4 $5 {is a modern and dynamic idea, played in many lines (with 4.a3) by Ivanisevic and Miladinovic. White opens up the game to the maximum, harassing the black king which has castled queenside. The queen's bishop may be activated along the diagonal a3-f8:} c5 (9... Nb6 $5 {can be an antidote for Black} 10. a5 Nbd5 11. Ne2 Qd6 $5 $132 {preparing ...d7 and 0-0-0}) ({Or} 9... b6 10. a5 Bb7 $13 {no games with this line}) 10. a5 Qc7 11. Qd2 {It looks as if White is wasting lots of tempi, but the situation is highly unclear. The problem is the unsafe position of the black king.} b6 $5 (11... Rb8 12. Ne2 $14 {Ivanisevic-Psakhis, playchess.com 2006}) 12. Ne2 (12. axb6 Qxb6 13. Ne2 Ba6 $132 {} 14. g3 Bb7 $1) 12... Bb7 13. Qf4 {Miladinovic-Franic, Zadar 2007. Here after:} Qc8 $1 {Black's position is strong enough}) ({B) Ljubojevic's WEAPON:} 9. Nh3 $5 {was a success for White in its time. White prepares g5 or g5 while leaving the bishop on f1, with free development. However, after} b6 $1 {Black can exploit the line 8...b6, see also next game.} ({As usual in this variation} 9... c5 $143 {is very unclear:} 10. Be2 Qa5 11. Bd2 Rxg2 $6 12. Ng5 cxd4 13. Qg7 Rxg5 $8 14. Qxg5 Qxg5 15. Bxg5 dxc3 16. O-O-O $14 {and White's chances are better in the resulting ending, Ljubojevic-Kortschnoj, Tilburg 1986.}) 10. Ng5 {This possibility is one of the differences in comparison with e2, but it doesn't matter!} Rg6 $1 11. Qh4 Bb7 12. Nxh7 Nxh7 13. Qxh7 Qf6 $1 {and Black has obtained fantastic compensation for a pawn – apart from a certain psychological advantage!} 14. Qh3 ({After} 14. h4 O-O-O $1 15. Bg5 Rh8 $1 {Black is OK}) 14... O-O-O 15. Qe3 Qe7 16. Bb2 $6 f5 17. O-O-O Nf6 $132 {With an excellent positional game for Black:} 18. c4 Rf8 (18... Ng4 $5) 19. f3 f4 $1 $36 {in Rombaldoni-Rahal, Verona 2007}) ({C) An amateur WEAPON:} 9. f3 {Black is solid after} b6 $5 (9... c5 $13)) 9... b6 $1 {Originally, recommended by Vukovic in Sahovski Glasnik in 1935. The idea of the fianchetto with ...b7 is a key concept for Black! (see also line-C 8...b6)} ({The sharpest option} 9... c5 {see next game.}) 10. Bg5 $6 {This pseudo-active move leads to disaster for the 11th World Champion.} (10. a4 Ba6 $5 $132 11. Ba3 $6 {H.Nielsen-Rosell, DEN-chT 1976, now:} c5 $1 $15) (10. Ng3 {suggested by Smyslov, but Black has very positive results in this line. For instance:} Rg6 (10... Bb7 $5 {is similar}) 11. Qd2 Bb7 12. Bb2 Qe7 13. O-O-O O-O-O 14. c4 Ng4 $1 15. h3 $2 Nxf2 $1 $17 {Leiros Vila-Garcia Gonzales, Pontevedra 1986}) 10... Qe7 $1 {Casting doubts over White's 10th move. "The key move! White must meet the threat of 11...g4.'' (Kovacevic)} 11. Qh4 $8 ({Tricks:} 11. Ng3 $4 Ng4 $1 12. Bxe7 Nxh6 13. Bh4 Rg4 $19) ({Tricks:} 11. Nf4 $6 Ng4 $1 12. Bxe7 Nxh6 13. Bh4 Nf5 $15) 11... Bb7 12. Ng3 $5 (12. Nf4 h6 (12... O-O-O $5 13. Nh5 Rg6 $132 14. Nf4 Rxg5 $5 15. Qxg5 Rg8 16. Qb5 e5 $1 $44 {C.Garcia-Crotti, Posadas 2005.}) 13. Qxh6 O-O-O 14. Qh4 {Estevez Morales-Vilela de Acuna, Cienfuegos 1991. Here:} Rh8 $5 15. Nh5 Qf8 $1 16. Bxf6 Nxf6 17. Qxf6 Rxh5 $15 {and Black has a clear initiative for the pawn.}) 12... h6 $1 {The deviation theme. Black will be the first to get his desired position.} 13. Bd2 ({Tricks:} 13. Qxh6 $2 {loses the bishop after} Ng4 14. Bxe7 Nxh6 15. Bh4 Rg4 $19) ({Tricks:} 13. Bxh6 Rg4 14. Qh3 O-O-O 15. Be2 Rgg8 (15... Rg6 $5) 16. Qh4 Rh8 $36) 13... O-O-O $1 {This strong and logical move exposes the disadvantages of White's position: a delay in development, a bad pawn structure and a lack of coordination between the pieces.} ({However, the energetic} 13... Rg4 $5 {is a modern alternative:} 14. Qxh6 (14. Qh3 $5 $13 {deserved some - computer - attention}) 14... O-O-O 15. c4 {To prevent ...d5, but:} Ng8 $5 16. Qe3 f5 $1 {Pushing the pawns - on the attack!} 17. Nh5 e5 18. dxe5 Nxe5 19. O-O-O $2 {Missing a nice reply:} Nd3+ $1 {Destroying White's position.} 20. cxd3 Qxa3+ 21. Kc2 exd3+ 22. Bxd3 Qa2+ 23. Kc3 Rxd3+ $1 24. Qxd3 Qa3+ 25. Kc2 Be4 {0-1 Romero Holmes-Matamoros Franco, Elgoibar 1997.}) 14. Be2 {Here Kovacevic introduced his famous knight tango:} Nf8 $5 {I like this manoeuvre, which serves to improve Black's 'worst' piece.} ({The computer's line is:} 14... e3 $5 15. fxe3 Bxg2 16. Rg1 Be4 $132) 15. O-O $6 {After this 'natural' move White faces serious difficulties.} (15. Nh5 $5 {leads to some complications after:} Rxg2 $5 (15... N8d7 $11) 16. Qxf6 Qxf6 17. Nxf6 Rg6 $1 18. Ng4 e3 {but this seems alright for Black.}) 15... Ng6 $1 16. Qxh6 (16. Qh3 Nd5 $36) 16... Rh8 17. Qg5 Rdg8 $1 $40 {Reaching the desired position – the 'French' army is ready to attack.} 18. f3 $5 {A tactical trick in a difficult situation.} ({After} 18. c4 Nh4 19. Qe3 Nf3+ $1 20. gxf3 Nd5 $1 21. Qxe4 (21. cxd5 Qh4 $18) 21... Nc3 $1 {(Psakhis) Black's attack is irresistible.}) 18... e3 $3 {Rona Petrosian! According to an anecdote told by Viktor Korchnoi in his book Memuary Shakhmatista, 2006 in the Russian language, it was Petrosian's wife who helped Kovacevic to find this strong reply. I don't think it was – Vlatko Kovacevic simply played a brilliant game!} ({Tricks:} 18... Nh4 $2 19. fxe4 $1 $13) 19. Bxe3 (19. Qxe3 {is not better, since after} Nd5 {White's position is hopeless} 20. Qg5 (20. Qe4 Qf6 $19) 20... f6 21. Qg4 f5 $1 22. Qg5 Qh7 $19 {wins in the attack.}) 19... Nf8 $1 {In accordance with the 'tango' steps, after five moves the knight heads back to f8. This is stronger than the obvious:} (19... Nh4 20. Rf2 $5 Rxg5 21. Bxg5 Nf5 22. Ne4 {and White has some compensation for the queen.}) 20. Qb5 Nd5 $1 {Now the game is over in view of ...h4} 21. Kf2 (21. Bd2 c6 $1 22. Qd3 Qh4 $19) 21... a6 22. Qd3 Rxh2 23. Rh1 Qh4 24. Rxh2 Qxh2 25. Nf1 Rxg2+ 26. Ke1 Qh4+ 27. Kd2 Ng6 $1 {Another tango step – and one more piece joins in the attack.} 28. Re1 Ngf4 29. Bxf4 Nxf4 30. Qe3 Rf2 {''A superb game, particularly when you consider who was playing the White side'' - Psakhis} 0-1

