{[%evp 0,87,25,18,48,48,48,14,19,12,36,31,66,43,40,-3,2,15,11,3,54,44,51,56,33,32,32,31,23,18,18,12,68,68,72,73,102,51,83,33,61,56,76,63,75,40,41,50,36,31,30,16,36,23,20,20,16,13,16,-77,29,9,9,30,39,41,87,86,78,84,105,108,188,179,223,173,154,171,173,113,134,124,155,193,224,241,271,265,431,497] This game was played between a 17-year-old Bobby Fischer and Hector Rossetto, one of the leading players of Argentina. At that time Fischer was already strong enough to keep up with the best players in the world. But his play was still very unbalanced. The tournament victory was shared by Korchnoi and Reshevsky, against whom Fischer both held a draw. Fischer's disappointing result (he finished 13th out of 20, 4.5 points behind the leaders) was due to his defeats against the supposedly "easier" opponents. For example, he lost to the Argentine players Wexler and Eliskases. Only in passing, the two "German Wolfgangs" Unzicker and Uhlmann finished in 8th and 11th places.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. f3 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ $5 {The Sveshnikov or Chelyabinsk variation was considered inferior at that time. According to the positional theory, the weakness of d5 was dramatic and could not be compensated for even by certain dynamic elements. Rossetto had played this line several times before, so Fischer was probably prepared. Today, the opening is more topical and actually the main weapon of the current world champion.} 6. bxc3 c5 7. cxd5 Nxd5 8. dxc5 $6 {Known today as an inaccurate move order, as Black is given additional options.} Qa5 9. e4 Ne7 $5 {An interesting try, which introduces the typical dynamic counterplay.} 10. Be3 O-O 11. Qb3 Qc7 (11... Nd7 $19 12. Bb5) 12. Ne2 (12. Rb1 $18) (12. a4 $4) 12... Nd7 (12... e5 13. Rb1 (13. Ng3 $2) 13... Nec6 14. Nc1 Na5 $3 {The trick which saves Black.} 15. Qc2 Be6 16. Qa4 Nd7 17. Qb4 Rfc8 18. Nd3 Bc4 $8 19. Kf2 Bxd3 20. Bxd3 Nxc5 $11) 13. Nf4 (13. Qb4 Nc6 14. Qa4 Nce5 15. Nd4 Nxc5 16. Nb5 Ncd3+ (16... Qc6 17. Bxc5 Qxc5 18. Qd4 b6) 17. Bxd3 Nxd3+ 18. Ke2 Qe5 19. Qd4 (19. Kxd3 Bd7 20. c4 a6) 19... Qxb5 20. Qxd3 Qb2+ 21. Qd2 Qxd2+ 22. Kxd2 {It looks like Black loses easily. But he has a nasty trick that's easy to miss:}) 13... Nxc5 14. Qc4 b6 15. Bxc5 {At this point we can draw a conclusion: The material is balanced. White has a nice outpost on d5. Black has dynamic possibilities on the g-file and by means of ...f6-f5. Furthermore, the white pawns on the queenside are in danger of being lost. I would say that the position is in dynamic balance.} bxc5 (15... Qxc5 16. Qxc5 bxc5 17. O-O-O) 16. Nd3 $1 {A really strong move: by White's taking back with the f-pawn, the black counterplay will be limited.} Nc6 17. Qxc5 $1 (17. Nxc5 $1 {Eyeing d6.} Ne5 18. Qd4 Rd8 19. Qe3 Rb8 $1 {It is very difficult for humans to correctly evaluate this type of position. The computer shows a draw as it always does.} 20. Be2 $2 Rb2) 17... Ba6 18. Nb4 {Just consolidating.} Bxf1 19. Nxc6 (19. Rxf1 Rac8 {A well-known method: White's positional advantage cannot be removed. White is therefore able to feint various manoeuvres (here g3-g8) without having to reveal his plans.} 20. Nxc6 Qxc6 21. Qxc6 Rxc6 22. Kd2 Rd8+ $5 23. Kc2 Rdc8) 19... Rfc8 20. Ne7+ Kf8 21. Ng6+ Kg8 22. Ne7+ $14 {Fischer's beloved light-squared bishop secures him a long-lasting advantage. Subsequently, it is interesting to see how Fischer scrutinises the weak pawns of his opponent.} Kf8 23. Ng6+ $1 Kg8 {More or less forced.} 24. Ne7+ {Eyeing the next weakness.} 1-0

