{[%evp 0,109,19,24,7,10,21,28,27,-4,9,0,25,-2,11,12,15,-22,-20,-8,-5,-7,-1,4,25,-1,-21,-17,22,39,39,19,21,32,32,32,63,44,45,49,88,89,74,63,81,42,38,53,53,61,81,41,85,74,81,83,73,52,55,55,75,43,76,6,0,19,32,27,20,29,24,18,25,22,51,47,58,45,50,50,55,40,54,49,72,21,72,113,115,68,171,165,165,134,141,123,76,51,105,136,122,122,126,133,244,197,226,245,520,501,501,530]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. d4 Nxe4 4. Bd3 d5 5. Nxe5 Nd7 6. Qe2 (6. Nxd7 Bxd7 7. O-O Qh4 8. c4 O-O-O 9. c5 $18 {is quite hopeless for Black.} g6 $5 $18 10. Nc3 Bg7 $18 11. g3 $2 (11. Ne2 Rhe8 12. a4 $1 $19 Ng5 13. Ra3 h6 $18 14. Bc2 Rxe2 15. Qxe2 $1 Ne6 16. Qd1 (16. Bxg6 fxg6 17. c6 $18 {[%cal Ya4a8]} Bxc6 (17... bxc6 $1 {[%csl Rc2]} 18. Qa6+ $1 {[%csl Rc2]} Kb8 19. Rb3+) 18. Qxe6+) 16... Nxd4) 11... Qf6 $1 {Black gets unexpected counterplay against the white king and secures the draw.} (11... Qh3 12. Nxd5 Bg4 $11 13. Ne7+ $4 Kb8 $1 14. Nc6+ Kc8 15. Nxa7+ Kb8 16. Nc6+ Kc8 17. f3) 12. Nxe4 dxe4 $1 13. Bxe4 Bh3 14. Qb3 Qa6 15. Re1 Rxd4 16. Bf4) 6... Nxe5 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. Qxe4 Be6 9. Qxe5 Qd7 10. Be3 (10. O-O O-O-O 11. Be3 Bb4 $1 {Black finds the best way to fight and forces Fischer to find the only winning move.} 12. c3 $1 f6 13. Qg3 Be7 14. Nd2 $1 h5 15. f3 g5) 10... Bb4+ 11. c3 Bd6 12. Qa5 (12. Qxg7 O-O-O) 12... Qc6 {This is a line which has practically vanished from modern practice. White should be able to prove an advantage after 13.d5} 13. f3 Bd5 (13... Bc4 {[#]} 14. Nd2 $1 {[%csl Rb4] Another very nice move on the theme of prophylaxis by Fischer. White stops c2 and prepares a4 and c1, highlighting the awkward placement of the black knight on b4. Although it is not the only winning move, it is a very mature move by the 17 year-old Fischer.} Bd3 15. O-O-O b6 16. Qg5 Qxc3+ 17. bxc3 $6 {Here Fischer chooses the most adventurous way to finish the game. It would have been a lot easier to collect the d4 pawn on this or the previous move.} Ba3#) 14. Nd2 O-O 15. O-O Rfe8 16. Rfe1 b6 17. Qa6 Bxh2+ 18. Kxh2 Rxe3 19. Kg1 Qe6 $5 {Black prepares the activation of his dark-squared bishop with d8-b6.} 20. Qf1 Re8 21. Rxe3 $1 {A nice little move which highlights Black's weaknesses on the queenside. White's threat is to play a4.} Qxe3+ $6 {[#]} 22. Qf2 {Fischer goes for his favourite plan of h2-g4. While browsing Fischer's White games in the Ruy Lopez, I stumbled upon h2-g4 at least a dozen times.} Qd3 23. a3 h6 24. Re1 $1 {Of course Fischer declines the exchange of the bishops since this would make Black's life easier} Re6 25. Rxe6 $5 {[%cal Yg3f5] Fischer slowly builds up the pressure on the kingside.} Bxe6 26. Nf1 c6 27. Qd2 $1 Qb1 28. Kf2 a5 29. Ne3 b5 30. Qc2 $6 Qa1 31. Qe2 $2 {The only serious mistake committed by Fischer during the game. My guess is that he underestimated Black's counterplay on the queenside and thought he could force a win by playing g3, g2, h1.} Qa2 $1 32. f4 g6 $6 33. Kg3 a4 34. Ng4 Bxg4 $2 35. Kxg4 Qe6+ {[#]} 36. Kf3 $1 $16 {A very important prophylactic move which stops c3, thus making Black's life harder.} Qd5+ {[#]} 37. Kg3 $1 {Another excellent prophylactic move which stops d4 and forces Black into a more passive defence.} Kf8 38. Kf2 $1 {[%cal Yg1g2,Yd1h1] Finally White is allowed to proceed with his attack on the kingside which is based on the open h-file.} Qf5 39. g3 Qh3 40. Qe5 Qh2+ 41. Kf3 Qxb2 42. d5 cxd5 {Black stops f5 at the cost of giving up the pawn on b5.} 43. Qh8+ 1-0

