{[%evp 0,46,17,16,21,-7,37,46,37,45,55,47,28,28,28,19,31,31,37,18,18,-64,-79,-83,-83,-75,-33,-88,-62,-111,-68,-145,-128,-128,-115,-145,-144,-229,-250,-437,-359,-592,-517,-743,-737,-780,-740,-1197,-1301] I have chosen this game because Fischer was still very young at the time (17 years), but he already played at a high level. My favourite idea in this game is the final queen sacrifice.} 1. Nf3 Nf6 2. g3 g6 3. d4 Bg7 {Fischer is playing his favourite opening, the King's Indian.} 4. c4 O-O $5 {Black wants to provoke e5 and then undermine the center with ..d6. This is an interesting plan but nowadays ...d6 is almost always played.} 5. Bg2 c6 (5... d6 6. O-O Nbd7 (6... Nc6 7. Nc3 a6 (7... Bg4) 8. Qd3)) 6. O-O d6 $1 {This is the essence of this line, with similar ideas the Alekhine Defence where Black is provokes e5 and then undermines the centre with ...d6.} 7. Nc3 Nbd7 $1 {Attacking the centre is natural for this line.} (7... Qa5) (7... Bf5) 8. e4 Qc7 $1 {A very important move. Now Black attacks the e5-pawn with a developing move and is ready to sacrifice a few pawns in order to open up the position to get at the white king.} (8... e5 9. h3 (9. b3) 9... Qb6 10. Re1 Re8 11. d5 Nc5 12. Rb1 a5 13. Be3 Qc7 14. Nd2 Bd7 15. Bf1 Rab8 16. a3 cxd5 17. cxd5 b5 18. b4 Nd3 19. Bxd3 Qxc3 20. Rb3 (20. Nb3 a4 21. Qe2 Bxh3 (21... Qc8 22. Rbc1) 22. Rec1 Bg4 23. Qf1 Bh3 24. Rxc3 Bxf1 25. Kxf1)) 9. h3 e5 10. Be3 $6 (10. Re1) 10... Rd8 $1 {Fisher is trying to provoke White into taking on d6.} (10... Re8 $132 11. Re1 {transposes a normal King's Indian.} (11. Qc2)) 11. Rc1 $6 (11. Qc2) 11... Nf8 12. Qd2 (12. d5 $132 c5 13. a3 $5 {Maybe this was Fischer's intention for the game}) 12... exd4 $5 {Fischer does not want to exchange queens, and still gains an advantage, but 12...dxe5 is objectively best.} (12... Ne6 13. Rfd1 c5 $19 14. dxe5 $1 {would be the best reply, but White was probably scared of the open e-file.} dxe5 15. Nd5 Qb8 $1 $17 {[%cal Ge7g6]} 16. Bxc5 $4 Rxd5 17. cxd5 Nxc5 $19 18. Rxc5 $1 {is also strong, but is not as nice as the game continuation.} Nxe4 19. Qe3 Nxc5 20. Qxc5 e4 21. Ng5 Qe5 22. Nxe4 Bxh3 23. Bxh3 $1 $40 {White's position is collapsing.} Qxe4 $1 {This gives White some counterplay.} 24. d6 Bxb2 25. d7 Rd8 26. Qg5 f6 27. Qa5 Qe7 28. Re1 Be5 29. f4 $15 Rxd7 $1 30. fxe5 Rd3 31. Kh2 b6 $1 32. Qb5 Rd2+ $17 33. Bg2 fxe5 $17 34. Rxe5 {This is best, but Black remains better.} Qf6 35. Qe8+ Qf8 $1 $15 {Surely Black should not exchange the queen. Now the threat is ...Rd8 and ...f6, and Black has a pleasant position.} 36. Qc6 $2 {would open up the position while lagging in development.} Qh6+ $17 37. Kg1 $1 {This is the main move. White should try to finish kingside development.} Rd1+ 38. Bf1 Qd2 $132 39. Qc8+ {This is the main move when} Kf7 40. Qe8+ Kg7 {would be similar to the game.} 41. Re7+ Kh6 42. Qf8+) 13. Nxd4 $6 {Preparing f5 is too slow.} Ne6 14. Nde2 Qe7 $5 {A very nice stylish move. Fisher is trying to open up the position at any cost.} 15. Rfd1 $2 (15. f4) (15. Rcd1) 15... h5 16. f4 Nc7 $1 {Not recapturing the pawn is very nice. Now Black is threatening ...f4 winning a piece, so the next White moves are forced.} 17. e5 {Preventing ...f4, but now the e4 and g4 squares are very weak.} Nfe8 $1 18. Ne4 (18. c5 d5 $1 {Checkmate in the centre of the board. Fischer punished his opponent, who forgot the general principle: Get your king to safety in the opening as quicky as possible.} 19. Nd4) 18... d5 $1 (18... Bf5) 19. Bc5 Qd7 20. N4c3 Ne6 {Material is equal, but the position is completely lost for White. His king is very weak, and the rooks on the open file are very strong. All Black's pieces are participating in the attack.} 21. Qe3 (21. Bf2 $3 {The icing on the cake. I love this move very much. Finishing the game with a queen sacrifice is always a phenomenal feeling.}) 21... N8c7 $1 {A very nice way to finish he game.} (21... Nxc5 22. Qxc5 {[#]}) 22. cxd5 0-1

