{[%evp 0,81,42,13,30,11,13,-14,13,-16,8,-13,20,-47,-12,2,4,-50,-33,-31,-5,-5,-21,11,14,-33,-31,-29,48,39,21,33,28,2,-4,-5,20,20,20,20,137,77,99,78,82,81,181,74,146,39,141,114,173,173,182,206,193,195,195,223,240,237,265,279,279,221,416,174,201,173,171,128,173,173,173,173,169,164,164,182,188,125,136,147] I think everyone has one game that first comes in mind at the simplest mention of a player's name (try it thinking about your favourite players and you will understand what I mean). I like many of Fischer's victories, but this is "that game" for me.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nf3 Bg7 4. g3 c6 5. Bg2 d5 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Ne5 O-O {The good old days. No computers, no Berlin, no deeply-studied Marshall Gambit...} 8. Nc3 e6 9. O-O (9. Bg5) 9... Nfd7 10. f4 Nc6 11. Be3 f6 $5 {This move rightly receives the name of Keres Variation. It failed to impress Fischer in this game, as for his notes he thought the manoeuvre f6-d7-b6 was simply a waste of time and left Black's kingside unprotected. Althought he succesfully proved his points in the present game, we can tell from the future that Keres "won" the theoretical discussion, as his idea survived the test of the time and it is actually a very interesting line, in which we can find many recent games in the database.} (11... Ndxe5) (11... Nb6) 12. Nd3 {'!' Fischer gives an exclamation mark to this capture, probably influenced by how well it worked in the game.} Nb6 13. b3 Qe7 $2 {The start of a wrong plan, which aims to face the manoeuvre f1-e3-d5 by defending that square with as many pieces as possible. Fischer poses the following funny question here: "If such a natural developing move is bad, what should we think about this position for Black?". Although it makes sense, we should not take such evaluation as definitive. The only possible conclusion is that Keres didn't find the best way to deal with White's plan of manoeuvring the knight to d5.} 14. a4 Bd7 15. Bc1 Rfd8 16. e3 Be8 {Black has apparently managed to establish firm control over d5.} 17. Ba3 $1 {But Fischer doesn't care!} Qf7 18. Rc1 Bf8 19. Bxf8 {[%csl Gc2] At first sight, we could think that White didn't accomplish too much with the last operation, as the central pawns were just exchanged and nothing terrible seems to be happening for Black here. But if we examine the position more closely, it will be clear that Black is the side who needs to be careful in his next moves: the c2 became very active, and his king looks dangerously abandoned on g8.} Qxf8 {This move seemed awkward to me, and I can still remember how perplexed I was when seeing Fischer's analysis and being forced to agree with him that there is nothing better for Black. White is threatening to build annoying pressure by simple moves like f4-ad1, and some sacrificial attacking ideas are already in the air. I really like this position, especially because Fischer insists on supporting his evaluation with concrete lines. Let's check them:} 20. g4 {[%cal Ge5g6]} Qe7 21. Qd2 $1 { xd5 Simple and strong, creating the threat of xd5 followed by e4. Instead of looking for forced sequences, Fischer just improves his position.} Rac8 {There is no simple solution for Black. Keres prevents ideas on h7, but on the other hand weakens the dark squares around his king.} 22. Ne2 $1 {Fischer immediately turns his attention to the new weakened squares on the kingside.} Rc7 {Keres struggles to bring his pieces on the queenside to more useful positions.} 23. Rc5 {Another simple, yet strong move.} Nc8 24. f5 $1 {It is very nice how Fischer manages to increase the pressure on Black's camp by means of natural moves.} g5 {[%csl Ge5] × e5 Black is basically forced to keep an eye on e5, as after a possible exchange on c4, an appearance of the White queen on e5 would be devastating.} 25. Ng3 $1 e5 {The lesser evil, but this provides other tactical motifs for White.} 26. Qc1 (26. Bxd5+ Kh8 27. Rfc1 (27. dxe5 Nxe5 28. Rxc7 Qxc7 29. Nxe5 (29. Rc1 Qd6 (29... Rxd5 30. Rxc7 Nf3+ 31. Kg2 Nxd2 32. Rxc8) 30. Nxe5 Qxe5 31. e4 Bc6) 29... Qxe5) 27... Nb6 28. Nb4 (28. e4 Nd7 29. R5c2 Nxd4) (28. Be6 Nd7 29. R5c2 (29. Bxd7 Rcxd7) 29... exd4 30. e4 Nce5 31. Nxe5 Rxc2) 28... exd4 29. exd4 Rcd7 30. Re1 Qf8 31. Nxc6 bxc6 32. Bxc6 Rxd4 33. Qxd4 Rxd4 34. Rxe8) (26. Rfc1 e4 27. Nb4) (26. Rxd5 Bf7 27. Rxd8+ Qxd8) 26... b6 27. Rc2 $1 {Converting the positional advantage into a material one. The back rank becomes the main source of problems for Black now, as Fischer skilfully demonstrates from now.} (27. Bxd5+ Rxd5 28. Rxd5 Nb4) 27... e4 28. Nf2 Nd6 29. Qd2 $1 Rdc8 30. Rfc1 a5 31. Bf1 $1 {[%cal Ge4e5] Back to the old juicy e5-square.} (31. Qc3 Bf7) 31... Nb4 32. Rc3 {Fischer grabs a second pawn, which is enough to win the game.} Qd7 33. Nd1 Rc6 34. Rxc6 Rxc6 35. Rxc6 Qxc6 36. Nc3 (36. Qc3) 36... Kf8 37. Kf2 Ke7 38. Ke1 Kf8 {Keres probably put his hopes on this rook ending in view of his active rook, but Fischer finds a precise idea to clarify things:} 39. Kd1 Qc8 40. Ke1 $1 {[%csl Gh8] Black is a pawn and a king down now, which means he is technically lost.} Kg7 (40... h5 41. h3 (41. gxh5 Nxf5 42. Bh3 Bd7) 41... h4) 41. Na2 {You can argue this is not the most beautiful game of Fischer and I would agree, of course. We didn't see either spectacular sacrifices or unexpected original ideas like his most famous games, but I really like the way he developed a strong initiative right from the opening and kept it until the very end, supporting even the most natural moves by concrete calculation. It's remarkable that on move 19 Black is almost running out of moves, and White's position is so superior that against any move there is a tactical refutation. I believe there is something poetic about such games as well. After all, it is a true masterpiece.} {Keres saw no reason to offer further resistance, as any Soviet master would know this endgame is beyond any chance of salvation. But let's see a possible line to make things clear:} Nxa2 42. Qxa2 Qc7 (42... Qc1+ 43. Kf2) (42... h5 43. Qa3) 43. Kf2 Kf8 44. Qb2 Ke7 45. Be2 Kd8 46. Ke1 Kc8 47. Kd2 Kb7 $18 {and again the king marches to the queenside, with an easy win.} {If} 48. Qc1 Qe7 49. Ke1 Bd7 50. Kf2 Ne8 $1 {White just brings the king to the queenside and wins, as if} 51. Qh1 Qb4 52. h4 Qxb3 (52... gxh4 53. Qxh4 Qxb3 54. Qxh7 Qxa4 55. Qe7 Qc6 56. Nh5) 53. hxg5 fxg5 $18 54. Qxh7 Qxa4 $18 {White also wins after} 55. Qe7 Qc6 56. Qxg5 a4 57. Qe7 Qd6 58. Qxd6 Nxd6 $18 {with an easy endgame.} 59. Bd1 Bb5 $1 $18 {'!'} (59... b5 60. Ne2 b4 61. Nc1 Kb6 (61... Kc7) (61... b3 62. Nxb3 axb3 63. Bxb3)) {It is a little mysterious why Fischer didn't play} 60. Ne2 $1 $18 {here, that basically would force Black to resign.} a3 $18 {was a slower way, also enough to win.} (60... Bxe2 61. Kxe2 b5 62. Kf2) 61. Nc1 $2 Kc7 62. Kg3 Nc4 (62... Kd7 $18 {[%csl Rd5,Rh7]} {In the event of} 63. Kf4 Bc4 64. g5 {Fischer indicates the strong idea} a2 $1 65. Nxa2 Bxa2 $40 {[%cal Ge2e4]  e4 with the queen coming to e4, Black is under serious pressure.} 66. g6 Ke7 (66... Ne8 67. Ba4+) 67. g7 Kf7 $1 68. f6 Bc4 $1 69. Bg4 Kg8 $18 {the attack seems winning for White.} {In his notes, Boleslavsky recommends} 70. Be6+ {as the equalising path} Nf7 71. Kf5 Bb5 72. Kg6 Be8 $1 {Fischer gives this as an improvement} 73. Bxd5 b5 $1 {[%cal Yh4h5,Gd2f3,Gf3h4]  h4-h5, d2-f3-h4 planning to increase the pressure on the kingside by means of h5-f3-h4-g6, when Black does not have a clear way to prevent this plan, for example:} 74. Ba2 b4 75. Bb3) 63. Be2 (63. Kf4 Nb2 64. Bc2 $18 Nd3+ $2) 63... Be8 $1 $18 {'!'} (63... Kd6 64. Kf4 Nb2 65. Bxb5 Nd3+ 66. Bxd3 $18 exd3) 64. Kf4 $4 Nb2 65. Kg5 $2 {would be even worse:} Nd3 66. Nb3 a2 $1 67. Na1 b5 {and Black can't avoid mate in the next moves.} 68. Bd1 b4 $18 69. Bb3 {now loses due to} Nc1 $1 (69... Kd6 70. Kh6 $1 {White invests more material to force the black king to go back to the centre, where it will not survive too long.} Bb5 71. Kg7) 70. Bxd5 Kd6 {[%csl Gb2,Gd5]} (70... Ba4 {loses due to} 71. f6 $1 b3 72. f7 b2 73. f8=Q $18 {winning a piece.} bxa1=Q 74. Qc5+ $1 $16 {[%cal Ge1e8] '!' wins a healthy pawn.} Kb8 {looks a solid retreat, but after} 75. Qb6+ {Black is forced to compromise his kingside structure.} Kc8 76. Qa6+ Kc7 77. Qa5+ $40 {[%cal Yc1g5,Gg4f6,Gg4h6] White has a strong attack.} Kc8 78. Be6+ {[%cal Ge5g6]  xg6} Kb7 79. Qb4+ $40 {[%cal Gc1h6,Yh5f5] with more than one dangerous threat.} Kc7 {allows a beautiful attack:} 80. Qxa4 $1 Qb1 81. Qa7+ Kc6 82. Bd7+ Kd6 83. Ba4) 71. Bc4 Bb5 (71... Ba4 72. f6 $1 {White sacrifices the rook, but in return manages to break through Black's defences} b3 73. f7 Ke7 74. Kh6 b2 75. Kg7 bxa1=Q 76. f8=Q+ Kd7 77. Qf5+ Kc6 78. Qc5+ $18 {and Black loses the queen.} Kb7 79. Qb4+ $18 Kc8 80. Qxa4 $1 {revealing problems on the back rank} Qb1 81. Qa7 Kd8 82. Be6 $18 {[%csl Re6,Re8]} {Not much is changed by} Qb5 83. Kf6 $16) 72. Bg8 Ke7 $1 $18 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Re4d5]  g7# and White wins.} (72... Ba4 73. f6 b3 74. Kh6 $1 ({after} 74. f7 Ke7 $1 $18 {there is no perpetual for Black, but there is mate for White.} 75. Kh6 b2 (75... Kf8 76. g5) 76. Kg7 bxa1=Q $1 $18 {'+-!!'} 77. f8=Q+ Kd7 78. Qf5+ Kc7 79. Qa5+ Kb8 (79... Kc8 80. Be6+ $1 {a strong blow}) 80. Qb4+ Kc7 $18 (80... Kc8 81. Qxa4 $1 {[%cal Ge2e6]} Qc3 (81... Qb1 {Sometimes, if you are going to suffer, it is better to do it by grabbing a pawn. So, had Fischer just ignored or forgot the a2-pawn? Of course not! In that case, Black would fall into a forced sequence, which I have no doubt that Fischer had calculated until the very end:} 82. Qa7) 82. Bxa2 Nxa2 83. Qxa2 $1 Qxe3 84. Qe6+ $18)) 74... b2 75. f7 bxa1=Q 76. f8=Q+ $18) {Fischer mentions that someone suggested the variation} 73. Kh6 Kf8 74. Be6 Bd7 75. g5 {but after} (75. g5 $1 $19 {Black is the side with a winning position.} Bxe6 {is still bad} 76. fxe6 b3 77. g6 $1 {and White has too many threats. For instance:} b2 78. g7+ $1 Ke7 (78... Kg8 79. e7 Kf7 80. g8=Q+ $1 $18 Kxe7 81. Qg5+ $1) 79. g8=Q $1 {a very precise move, Black is simply defenceless against so many threats} bxa1=Q 80. Qf7+ Kd6 81. Qd7# $1) 1-0

