{[%evp 0,53,29,38,48,53,48,30,6,11,38,33,41,32,45,46,43,51,55,35,30,8,23,3,11,-17,-25,-42,-4,32,6,-27,25,24,36,-51,-11,59,52,423,420,435,456,468,471,471,484,473,475,471,483,483,505,508,516,527] A few months after starting to play chess, I received a book for my 8th birthday. It was "My 60 best games" by Bobby Fischer. I read it a couple of times, with my father. Of course none of us were able to understand all the subtleties, but we were already sensible to the beauty of most of the games. I am still a huge fan of his play, and I am glad to comment one of his games now! This game of Bobby Fischer, was played during his first Candidates tournament, in 1959. At only 16, Fischer managed to get very nice wins in this super strong tournament. This game against Pal Benko, shows his natural tactical skills.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Bc4 {Fischer is facing a Classical Sicilian. He always has been fond of the Sozin Attack, that is caracterised by 6.c4 (and can be played against several types of Sicilian. It's not a surprise to see that it was already his favourite here.} e6 $5 7. Bb3 Be7 8. f4 {In the Sozin Attack, White decides to castle to the kingside, and he will try to advance with f2-f4 later.} (8. g4 O-O 9. g5 Nfd7 $18 10. h4 Nc5 11. Be3) 8... O-O 9. Qf3 Qc7 10. O-O {White can't really do without this move.} (10. f5 Nc6 (10... e5 11. Nde2 $18 b5 12. g4) 11. Be3 Na5 12. g4 Nxb3 13. axb3 $18 d5 14. exd5 $1 { f2+} Nxd5 15. Nxd5 $1 exd5 16. O-O-O) 10... b5 $5 {Suppressing this annoying bishop is a very common plan, even if it takes a couple of tempi.} 11. f5 (11. e5 $1 dxe5 12. fxe5 $1 (12. Qxa8 exd4 13. Ne2 Bb7 14. Qa7 Nc6) 12... Bb7 13. exf6 $1 Bxf3 14. fxe7 $18 {Black can't take on f6, so it's completely lost.} Qxe7 15. Rxf3 $1 Nd7) 11... b4 {'!'} 12. Na4 (12. fxe6 bxc3 13. exf7+ Kh8 14. Qxc3 $1 $18 Ra7) 12... e5 13. Ne2 {This is the first really important moment of the game. Black should have gotten rid of the light-squares bishop.} Bb7 $6 {'?'} 14. Ng3 $5 {'!'} Nbd7 15. Be3 {Once again, Black has a vast choice. It wasn't easy, and he couldn't find the best move.} Bc6 $2 {After this move, Fischer won't give another chance.} 16. Bf2 $1 Qb7 $6 (16... Qa5 17. a3 Bxa4 $18 18. axb4 Qxb4 $18 19. Rxa4 {Lombardy,W}) 17. Rfe1 $1 {'+-!' Black is defenceless, but he will try to make Fischer's task as hard as possible.} d5 18. exd5 $1 Nxd5 19. Ne4 Nf4 20. c4 {The last finesse, White shouldn't leave his opponent have a free lunch on a1.} (20. Bg3 $18 Nf6 21. Nxf6+ $1 {A very nice blow.} Bxf6 {I think I had never seen quadruple pawns before.} 22. Qf2 {The point is that Black is unable to properly protect h7.} Nxg2) 20... g6 (20... Nf6 21. Bc2 $18 Kh8) 21. fxg6 {Now, this is over.} (21. f6 Nxf6 22. Bc2 N6h5) 21... f5 22. g7 Kxg7 (22... Rf7 23. c5 $18 Bxe4 24. Rxe4 fxe4 25. Bxf7+ {'+-'} Kxf7 26. Qe3) 23. Qg3+ Kh8 24. Nec5 Nxc5 25. Bxc5 $1 Bxc5+ 26. Nxc5 Qc7 27. Qe3 {A really well organised attack, that made his opponent's position completely fall apart.} Rae8 {It was still possible to misplay, and to lose most of White's advantage.} 28. Re2 (28. g3 $18 {If the queen moves, 23.h5 will mate.} Nh3+ $1 29. Kf1 f4) 28... Nxe2+ 29. Qxe2 Bxg2 30. Nxa6 (30. Kxg2 Qxc5) 30... Qa7+ 31. Kxg2 $14 {White obviously keeps the better chances, but things are complicated!} Rg8+ 32. Kh3 $1 Qg7 33. Bd1 Re6 1-0

