{[%evp 0,67,19,7,21,28,27,6,19,26,26,31,21,19,25,15,70,21,16,23,10,26,22,16,19,19,49,49,59,57,57,57,50,41,63,72,109,99,99,99,108,78,86,97,102,114,90,96,105,96,96,109,88,90,154,154,154,144,143,139,217,245,292,330,427,431,430,428,424,433]} {This was an encounter on first board in one of the key matches between the USA and Hungary.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Nxe4 5. Re1 (5. d4) 5... Nd6 {The Ruy Lopez is one of my main openings and when studying theory of the Exchange Variation from some old Soviet opening encyclopedia, I noticed this game. This was about thirty years ago. Later on I studied a large number of games of Bobby Fischer and he still remains one of my favorite players.} 6. Nxe5 Be7 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Nc3 Nxe5 (8... Ne8 $14 9. Nd5 $6 Bf6 10. Ng4 d6 $16) 9. Rxe5 c6 $6 {This natural move is an inaccuracy.} 10. b3 $1 {This was not a novelty at that time, but it does not make this move less effective. The b7 is under pressure and the knight is ready to go to c4.} Ne8 {Covering the c4 square, but Black's queenside is now seriously weakened.} 11. Bb2 $6 {'!' Not the best way to attack Black's queenside.} d5 12. Qh5 {The c5 is White's main target.} h6 {'!?'} 13. Rae1 Nf6 14. Qh4 $5 {'!'} Bd6 {'?!'} 15. R5e2 Bd7 (15... Bg4 16. Nxd5 $1 cxd5 (16... Nxd5 17. Qxg4) (16... Bxe2 17. Nxf6+ {and Black is only slightly worse - ½-½ (31) Habes,J (1704)-Luers,C (1879) LSS email 2013} gxf6 18. Bxf6) 17. Bxf6 {with a serious advantage.} Qxf6 $6 {is too soft.} 18. Qxg4) (15... Be6 16. Nd1 Nd7 17. Qd4 (17. Qh5 $14 Nf6 $5 {was another interesting idea.} 18. Qh4 Nd7 19. Qh5 Nf6) 17... Qf6 18. Qxf6 Nxf6 19. Ne3 $14 {½-½ (54) Teterev,V (2507)-Novitzkij,D (2373) Minsk 2006} (19. Bxf6 gxf6 20. Ne3 Rfd8 21. Nf5 Bxf5 22. Bxf5 $16 {1-0 (54) Guseinov,G (2654)-Davidov,S (2306) Baku 2019} {Covering the d5-square would be the lesser evil:} Kf8 23. g3 Re8) {Simply} 19... Ne4 {was actually a good alternative.} (19... a5 20. Nf5 Bxf5 21. Bxf5) 20. c4 $6) 16. Nd1 Re8 (16... Ne8 $2 17. Qd4 $18 (17. Qxd8 Rxd8 {with good chances for a draw.} 18. Ne3 g6) 17... Qf6 18. Ne3 (18. Qxf6 {The knight is trapped, so White has to return the exchange.} Nxf6 19. Ne3 Ne4 20. Bxe4 dxe4 21. Nc4) 18... Qxd4 19. Bxd4 g6 {would keep decent hopes for a draw thanks to the presense of opposite colour bishops.} (19... Nc7 20. Nf5 Bxf5 $1 21. Bxf5) 20. h3 (20. c4) 20... h5 $18 21. c4 $1 {was even more precise.} {After} Nc7 (21... dxc4 22. Nxc4 $2 Bc7 23. Bc5) (21... c5 22. Bb2 d4 $11 {½-½ (26) Stets,D (2436)-Koziak,V (2479) Sautron 2016} 23. Nd5 Rd8 24. b4 $14) 22. cxd5 Nxd5 23. Nc4 {the idea ...c7-c5 does not allow the white a-passer to go forward so smoothly.} Bc7 24. Ne5 $1 Bc8 (24... Rfd8 25. Nxd7 $16 Rxd7 26. Re8+) (24... Be6 25. Nxg6) (24... Rad8 25. Bc4 Bf5 26. Bxa7 $16) 25. Bc4 $16) 17. Rxe8+ {Black's main problem is that his king is stuck in the centre and therefore the rooks are disconnected.} (17. Ne3 Ne4 18. Qh5 (18. Qxd8 {was a bit more stubborn, but after} Raxd8 $1 19. c4 Bb4 {White should win anyway.} 20. cxd5 {was also good enough.} Bxd2 {'!?'} 21. Rd1 Bxe3 22. Rxe3 cxd5 {would still force White to work hard in order to convert the extra pawn into a full point.}) 18... Bf4 (18... Qg5 19. Qxg5 hxg5 20. c4 $18) 19. Qf3 Be5 20. Bxe5 Rxe5 21. Qf4 Qf6 (21... Re8 22. c4 {mating.}) 22. Qxf6 Nxf6) 17... Nxe8 $6 18. Qxd8 (18. Qd4 Qf6 {Keeping all the rooks on the board actually makes things tougher for Black, as his king is more vulnerable.} 19. Ne3 $1 Qxd4 20. Bxd4 g6 $6 (20... Nf6 $18 {Glorious career for this knight!} 21. Nf5 Bxf5 22. Bxf5) (20... Nc7 21. Nf5 {The king will come under fire in the centre.} Bf8 22. Be5 Ne6 23. Nd6) 21. c4 {'+-'} Nc7) 18... Rxd8 19. Ne3 $1 $16 {[%csl Rb5] ''} Bf8 (19... Nc7 20. Nf5 Bf8) 20. Nf5 {Black can not save the b5.} Be6 21. Nd4 1-0

