{[%evp 0,55,19,31,47,23,46,24,17,32,16,35,26,31,39,39,50,50,41,10,26,-27,-21,-21,-25,-26,-18,-37,-21,-28,-6,-12,-39,-88,-55,-48,-34,-17,1,0,0,25,182,182,201,243,243,229,229,251,318,314,705,424,29983,29984,29993,29994] I have chosen this Fischer game because it is not as well-known compared to his others. It was played in 1970 when he was already close to becoming the world champion. He put on a great performance in this game and his opening preparation was superb.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 (2. Nc3 d6 3. g3 g6 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. d3 Nf6 6. f4) 2... d6 3. g3 g6 {Fischer once said that he doesn't like to play against the French Winaver. Weird pawn structures are typical for this opening. Still in this game the great Bobby showed one of model plans for White, and many hungreds games were won by White in such a style in the future.} 4. Bg2 Bg7 5. O-O Nf6 (5... Nc6 6. d3 e5) (5... e5 6. d3 Nc6 7. Nc3 Nge7) 6. d3 {Black gave up the very important dark-squared bishop in order to destroy White's queenside pawn structure. This is a very risky strategy and probably the reason the whole system is not very populiar at the top level nowadays.} O-O 7. Nc3 Nc6 8. Nh4 Rb8 {Black is trying to avoid weakening the kingside dark squares.} (8... Ne8 {is the most popular option.} 9. Be3 Nc7 10. Qd2 Nd4 11. Nd1 e5 12. c3 Nde6 13. Bh6 f5 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. exf5 gxf5 16. Re1 f4 17. Qe2 Qf6 18. Nf3 Bd7 19. Nd2 Bc6 20. Qg4+ Kh8 21. Bxc6 bxc6 22. Ne4 Qe7 23. Qh4 Qxh4 24. gxh4 Rad8 25. f3 Rf5 26. Rb1 Rh5 27. b4 cxb4 28. Rxb4 Rxh4 29. Rb7 a6 30. Rb6 d5 31. Nd2 Rg8+ 32. Kh1 Ng5 33. Rxc6 Nh3 34. Rxc7 Rh5) 9. h3 b5 (9... e5 {is also quite playable} 10. Be3 {The alternatives are} b5 {A slightly strange move that provokes Black to play} 11. Qd2 {releasing the pressure on the d4-pawn} {or the direct} b4) 10. a3 {A logical and straightforward approach.} (10. f4 Nd4 11. Ne2 Nxe2+ 12. Qxe2 {and Black's position on the kingside is surprisingly hard to break, while there is counterplay on the queenside.} {Also playable is} Ne8 13. f5 a5 14. Rb1 Ba6 {but here White has a very interesting pawn break:} 15. Be3 $5 b4 16. Rf2 Nf6 17. e5 dxe5 18. Bxc5 Nd7 19. Ba7 $1 Rc8 20. Qe3 $5 Nf6 21. Bb6 Nd5 22. Bxd8 $44 {with good compensation for the material.} Nxe3 23. Bxe7 Rfe8 24. f6 $16 {Also possible is} Bh6 $5 25. Nf3 Bb7 26. Bh1 Nf5 27. Nxe5 Bxh1 28. Kxh1 Nxg3+ $5 {with a complicated position.} {A computer recommendation which looks scary from a human point of view is} 29. Kg2 Nf5 30. Ng4 Ne3+ 31. Nxe3 Bxe3 $5 {A completely different picture of battle arises after} 32. Re2 $5 Bd4 33. Kf3 Rc6 34. Rbe1 Bxf6 35. Bxf6 Rxf6+ 36. Kg3 {or} Rxe2 37. Rxe2 {Strong modern engines are quite happy with} Rc6 $5 38. d4 Rc4 39. Rd2 Kf8) 10... Bd7 11. f4 Qc8 (11... e6 $1 $16 {People quite often forget about such 'antipositional' moves, but a pawn is a pawn after all.} 12. f5 $1 a5) 12. Kh2 $1 {This idea has become standard practice. White has found a weak point in Black's position.} a5 13. f5 e6 $6 {It looks logical not lose a tempo and develop the knight, but actually this is not the best decition. This knight could be more useful on d8 where it defends the f7-pawn. And the other knight might be well placed on e7, where it could go to f5 at a suitable time.} (13... b4) 14. g4 Ne8 $2 {The critical moment of the game. After the text move White's initiative becomes very strong and Fischer conducts it with great power.} 15. Ne2 Nc7 16. f6 $1 {From this moment Fischer shows his best and even the stubborn Stockfish takes a while before evaluating the position as better for White.} Bh8 17. g5 (17. Bh6 Re8 $5 $16 {A better try probably would be} 18. Ng3 b4 19. axb4 axb4 20. Qd2 d5 21. Nxg6 hxg6 $5 {with initiative for White.} {The only way to keep the balance was a very computerish move} 22. Bg7 $3 Bxg7 23. fxg7 Kxg7 24. Qg5 $5 $132 {Hard to believe, but according to engines Black's chances are not worse here! This line perfectly illustrates the complicated character of the French Winawer.}) 17... d5 18. Nf3 e5 19. exd5 Nxd5 20. Nc3 Nc7 21. Ne4 $18 {Now the black position collapses completely.} Ne6 22. Be3 Qc7 23. Kh1 Ncd4 24. c3 Nf5 25. Bg1 Rfd8 (25... Bc6) 26. Qe1 Bc6 27. Rd1 Rxd3 (27... Nf4) 28. Rxd3 {The endgame after} Bxe4 29. Re3 ({After} 29. Qxe4 {White is pushing in style:} Ng3+ $5 $16) 29... Bxf3 30. Rexf3 Nxg5 31. Rxf5 $18 {would be hopeless.} gxf5 32. Qg3 $5 Bxf6 33. Rxf5 Qe7 34. h4 $18 1-0

