1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 g6 {Spassky hopes for a transposition to Queen's Gambit Declined, an opening with which he has a lot of experience} 3. e4 {Fischer readily obliges as he has a novelty in mind.} d6 4. d4 Bg7 5. Nf3 O-O 6. h3 e5 {The Tartakower Variation also carries the names of Soviet masters, Makogonov and Bondarevsky who contributed to it. Under Bondarevsky, his mentor's influence Boris also used it, drawing his games in 1969 World Championship Match.} 7. d5 Nh5 8. Nh2 Bf6 (8... Qe8 $1) 9. Be2 ({On} 9. h4 {Kasparov gives} Bxh4 $1 10. Be2 Nf4 (10... Ng7 {he and other commentators couldn't find a reasonable plan for Black. Decades later in the computer era Kasparov found}) 11. Bf3 Be7 12. g3 Nh3 13. Ng4 Ng5 (13... h5 $18 14. Rxh3 {allows Black counterchances with} hxg4 {Tal came up with the right idea,} 15. Bxg4 {But after}) 14. Nh6+ Kh8 15. Bg2 f6 16. f4 Nf7 $1 17. f5) 9... Ng7 (9... Nf4 $2 {is no worse.} 10. Bf1 (10. Bf3 Na6 $18 {Black could have tried the more active} 11. g3 Nxh3 12. Ng4 {But after} Bxg4 $16 {White should prevail according to Kasparov.} {After} 13. Bxg4 $2 {White wins a pawn with} Ng5 $1 {-Jan Timman})) 10. h4 (10. Ng4 $18 {White also has to be vigilant.} Bg5 $2 (10... Bxg4 11. hxg4 {followed by e5-e6.} {The careless} Bg5 $2 {is met by} 12. Qd3 $1) 11. Bxg5 Qxg5 12. h4 Qe7 13. Nh6+ $19 Kh8 $2 {loses to} 14. g4 $1) 10... Bxh4 (10... h5 11. g4 {would have given Black a breather.} {If} hxg4 $2 12. Nxg4) (10... Nd7) 11. Qd2 h5 (11... f5 12. Nf3 $18 ({Or} 12. Qh6 $2 fxe4 $18 13. Nxe4 $2 Nf5)) 12. Qh6 Be7 13. g4 g5 (13... h4 14. Nf3) 14. gxh5 $5 {Furman's novelty with which he beat Geller in Moscow 1970.The idea is to discourage...Nb8-d7 supporting c5.} f5 $2 15. Bd2 f4 16. O-O-O Nd7 $2 {Now the rook and queen defend each other and Black also threatens 17...axb5, not to mention ...c5-c4. Yet it is inadequate.} (16... Rf6 {only leads to a suffocating end.} 17. Qxg5) 17. Rdg1 {So far the game has followed Furman-Geller, Moscow 1970.} Rf7 18. Nf3 $1 Nf8 $2 {Anxious to get away from the pin, Boris falls back on passive defence.} 19. Nxg5 Rf6 20. Qxg7+ $1 {Probing Black's pawn centre at its most vulnerable point.} Kxg7 $2 {This move, opening the a2-g8 diagonal for the bishop plays into White's hands.} 21. Ne6+ Kh7 22. Nxd8 Bxd8 $6 23. Bg4 Bxg4 24. Rxg4 Rf7 25. f3 Nd7 26. Be1 a6 27. Nd1 Bf6 28. Nf2 Rg8 29. Rxg8 Kxg8 30. Ng4 $1 {After this move neither the Black queen nor the knight can use g5 as a springboard.} Rh7 31. Bf2 Kf7 32. b4 b6 33. Kd2 Be7 34. Kd3 Rh8 35. a4 Ra8 36. Nh6+ Kf8 37. Rg1 $1 Ke8 (37... Nf6 38. Bh4) 38. Rg8+ $1 $18 Bf8 39. Bh4 a5 40. b5 Nc5+ 41. Kc2 Nxa4 $4 {is worse.} 42. Nf5 $1 Kf7 43. Rg1 1-0

