1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 d5 4. Nc3 Bb4 5. e3 O-O 6. Bd3 c5 7. O-O Nc6 8. a3 Ba5 9. Ne2 (9. cxd5 $5 {deserves attention. If} exd5 10. dxc5 $14 {White has more space and better development.} Bxc3 {recommended by Timman and Kasparov is met by} 11. bxc3 Bg4 12. c4) 9... dxc4 {The Breyer Variation, a favourite of Spassky.} 10. Bxc4 Bb6 11. dxc5 Qxd1 12. Rxd1 Bxc5 13. b4 {This plan preventing the freeing advance...c5 was popular in those days.} Be7 14. Bb2 Bd7 (14... b6 $1 15. Nf4 Bb7 16. Ng5 Nd8 17. Rac1 h6 $14 18. Ngxe6 fxe6 19. Nxe6 {Here} Rc8 20. Bb3) 15. Rac1 (15. e4) 15... Rfd8 16. Ned4 Nxd4 {A novelty in this position. Otherwise the idea is known. The move indirectly defends e5 and targets White's b-pawn. However, its drawback is that it shuts out the QR.} 17. Nxd4 {Indirectly defending the b-pawn.} Ba4 18. Bb3 {The battle is for the control of e5 and this move strikes at d6, the heart of its base.} Bxb3 ({If} 18... Rxd4 19. Bxa4) 19. Nxb3 Rxd1+ 20. Rxd1 Rc8 21. Kf1 {Calling into question Black's entire plan initiated with 16...Qb8.} Kf8 22. Ke2 Ne4 23. Rc1 Rxc1 $5 {Spassky offers a pawn for active counterplay.} 24. Bxc1 $1 f6 25. Na5 Nd6 26. Kd3 Bd8 27. Nc4 {The point of White's 24th move.} Bc7 28. Nxd6 Bxd6 29. b5 Bxh2 $2 {anxious to defend the second rank and yet the move turns insufficient} 30. g3 $1 h5 {Forced.} 31. Ke2 h4 32. Kf3 {The point. White exploits the weak second rank.} Ke7 (32... h3 33. Kg4 Bg1 34. Kxh3 Bxf2 35. Bd2 $17) 33. Kg2 hxg3 34. fxg3 Bxg3 35. Kxg3 Kd6 $6 {By now Spassky was in time trouble. Here he tries to activate his rook & bishop and at the same time keep away the White king from his advancing pawns. Unfortunately, it falls short of expectations.} 36. a4 (36. Kg4 Ke5 37. Kh5 Ke4 38. Kg6 (38. a4 $14 f5 39. Kg6 e5 40. Kxg7 f4 $13 {led to a tense draw in Vasiukov-Smejkal, Polanica Zdroj 1972.} 41. exf4 $2 {is tactically flawed on account of} exf4 $1 42. Bd2 Kd3 43. Be1 Kc4 44. Kf8 Kb3 45. a5 b6) 38... e5 39. Kxg7 f5 40. Kf6 f4 41. exf4 $11 {analysis initiated by Olafsson and refined by Timman and Kasparov} exf4 42. Bd2 $11 f3 43. Be1 Kd5 44. Ke7 Kc4 45. a4 b6 46. Kd6 Kb3 $3 47. Kd5 $2 {loses a pawn to} Kxa4 $1 48. Kc4) 36... Kd5 37. Ba3 Ke4 38. Bc5 a6 39. b6 f5 (39... e5 40. Kg4 g6 41. a5 (41. Kg3 Kd3) 41... Kd5 42. Be7 f5+ {White has the upper hand, but no clear win according to Timman and Kasparov.} {If} 43. Kg5 f4 {(Nei) preparing h4 followed by Rbe6 forming a mating net should win.} {If} 44. exf4 $2 exf4 $16 45. Kxf4 $4 {runs into} Ke6 $1 46. Bh4 Kd7 {He had to play} 47. Kg5 $1 Kc8 48. Kxg6 Kd7) 40. Kh4 f4 (40... Kd5 {not} 41. Bb4 Ke4 42. Bd2 Kd3 $18 43. Bc1 Kc2 44. Ba3 Kd3 45. Bc5 Kc4 $18 {Soon Black runs out of moves, either falling into a mating net or losing his queenside pawns.} {not} 46. Bd6 $2 Kd5 47. Bf4 e5 $11 {with a theoretical draw.} {or} 48. Bh2 f4 49. exf4 exf4 50. Kg4 (50. Bxf4 $18 {Larsen offered} Kc5 $1 {and it sets more problems for White. However, after}) 50... Kc5 51. Bg1+ Kb4 {followed by 45.Rd7 wins.} {The suggestion of Krogius and Nei} 52. Kxf4 Kxa4 53. Ke5 {is refuted by Timman.} Kb4 $1 54. Kd6 a5 55. Kc7 a4 $18 {or} 56. Kxb7) 41. exf4 {The sealed move. During the adjournment Spassky and his seconds could find no way of saving the game.} Kxf4 42. Kh5 $1 Kf5 43. Be3 Ke4 44. Bf2 Kf5 {This attempt to prevent g4-g5 does not alter the outcome of the game.} 45. Bh4 e5 46. Bg5 $1 {Pinning the c-pawn so that its companion can be captured.} e4 47. Be3 (47. a5 $1 Ke6 48. Kg6 Kd7 49. Kxg7 e3 50. Bxe3 {If} Kc8 $4) 47... Kf6 48. Kg4 Ke5 49. Kg5 $1 {A little joke.} Kd5 50. Kf5 a5 51. Bf2 g5 52. Kxg5 Kc4 53. Kf5 Kb4 54. Kxe4 Kxa4 55. Kd5 Kb5 56. Kd6 $1 {...and Spassky resigned.} 1-0

