{[%evp 0,41,19,24,90,73,93,84,90,66,34,39,46,40,53,40,23,40,44,48,48,52,38,12,135,98,90,83,117,89,104,79,79,75,82,82,82,311,405,414,394,402,398,397]} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. e4 d6 5. Be2 O-O 6. Nf3 {The most dynamic move.} (6. Be3 e5 7. d5 a5 $10 {Bisguier,A-Benko,P m 1964} 8. g4 (8. h4)) 6... e5 $6 {'?' A dubious reply. At that time the theory of this variation was only just being developed. Nowadays it is clear that the light-squared bishop is important for Black.} (6... c5 $36 {Fischer-Perez Perez, Havana 1965, with advantage to White.} 7. O-O $1 {} Nc6 {; Spassky,B} ({But nowadays} 7... cxd4 $5 {Valvo,M is the most common continuation.} 8. Nxd4 Nc6)) 7. O-O Nc6 8. d5 Ne7 9. b4 Nh5 (9... a5 10. Ba3) 10. g3 f5 11. Ng5 { g2-g4-g5 +- This is the typical attacking reply against ...e5.} Nf6 $6 12. f3 f4 13. b5 (13. c5 Nh5 (13... fxg3 14. hxg3) 14. g4) (13. c5 $36 Nh5 14. g4 $16 Bf6 15. Ne6 Bxe6 16. dxe6 Ng7 17. Qb3 Kh8 18. b5 h5 19. Ba3 hxg4 20. fxg4 Ne8 21. Rad1 Kg7 22. Rd3 Rh8 23. Rfd1 Qc8 24. Bf3 Bg5 25. Kg2 b6 26. cxd6 cxd6 27. Rc1 Qd8 28. Nd5 $18 {Bednarski,J-Kraidman,Y Tel Aviv 1964} {In the event of} Rc8 29. Rdc3 $1 {} Rxc3 30. Rxc3 $16 {and White has a clear advantage.} {Later the plan with} Nxd5 {'!?' came to the fore,} 31. Qxd5 Qe7 32. Rc6 Bf6 33. Rxd6 Nxd6 34. Bxd6 Qd8 35. e7 $1 {'!' Fischer,R-Perez,F Habana 1965} Qc8) 13... fxg3 { d6, f7-f5; c7-c5-c4} 14. hxg3 {Fischer: "I thought White's king would be safer after the text – the drawback is the kingside pawns can no longer safely advance."} Nh5 (14... h6 $5 15. Ne6 $6 {could have been met by} Bxe6 $1 $132 {with a strong counterplay.} 16. dxe6 Qc8 17. Nd5 Qxe6 $1 18. Nxc7 {(Reeve-Schleifer, Canada 1986)} Qh3 $1 {is the most precise, followed by d2-c4.}) 15. Qe1 (15. Kg2 Nf4+ $14 16. gxf4 exf4 17. e5 Bxe5 $16 18. Nge4 $1 {was more precise to prepare against ...f5,} Nf5 19. Rg1 $1 {.} Ng3 20. Bd2 $18 {followed by c4 with a winning position for White.} Bxc3 $2 21. Bxc3 Nxe4 22. fxe4 $18 Qg5+ 23. Kf1 Bh3+ 24. Kf2 $1 Qh4+ 25. Kf3 $17 Qh5+ 26. Kf2 $132 Qh4+ 27. Kf3 $1 $16 {× f5, h5} {An alternative is} g5 {which I prefer,} 28. Bf1 g4+ $1 29. Ke2 f3+ 30. Kd3 g3 $16 {with a serious advantage.} 31. Bxh3 {More active than} Qxh3 32. Qd2 Qg4 33. Qf2 g2 34. Rae1 $16 {and White looks nicely placed.} Rae8 35. Kc2 {followed by ...b5} Rf4 { e2, c2-c3} {According to Fischer, the greedy} 36. Kb3 $6 {'!' is dubious, as} Rexe4 $1 37. Rxe4 Rxe4 38. Qxa7 Qg6 {'' "is hard to crack."} 39. Qf2 Qg4 40. Qa7 Re8 41. Be1 Qe4 $18 42. Bf2 Qd3+ $1 {Better to keep the s.} 43. Kb4 Qd2+ 44. Kb3 {(Robertson-Donnelly, Poland 1991)} b6 $18 {and White has a splendid position.}) 15... c6 16. Be3 $1 {Black already can't play for ...f5 anymore.} Nf4 $6 {Too slow, but Black's situation was already difficult.} 17. gxf4 { xd4, e4-e5 Threatening xd4, followed by e5.} exf4 $2 {Black would like to play for ...f5, but this is losing.} 18. Qh4 {Clearing the way for the e4-.} h6 19. Bxf4 $3 {'!' What a brilliant tactical blow! This game has become evergreen with this move!} (19. Bd2) 19... hxg5 {''} 20. Bxg5 Bxc3 21. Bxe7 $1 {Black resigned in view of} Qb6+ $1 { xd6} 22. Kg2 Rf7 $18 {.} {or} 23. Bxd6 Rh7 $18 24. c5 Rxh4 $1 25. cxb6 Bxa1 $18 {and mate on h7!} 26. Rxa1 cxd5 $18 {The immediate} 27. bxa7 $2 {could have been met by} Rxa7 $1 $132 {'!' and Black is safe.} 28. Bg3 { was more tenacious, although here too after} Rh7 29. exd5 Ra4 30. Kf2 Bf5 31. d6 Kf8 $16 {White has an obvious advantage.} 32. a3 Ke8 33. b6 Bd7 $16 34. Bd3 {} Rg7 {'!' was a better try, threatening ...c4.} 35. Be5 Rg8 36. Be4 Ra5 37. Bc3 $16 {and White is a healthy  up.} Rc5 $2 38. Bd4 $1 {Probably} Rc4 $5 {was giving more practical chances,} 39. Ke3 $1 Bc6 40. Kd3 $16 {and Black is in trouble again.} Ra4 41. Be5 Kd7 42. Rh1 1-0

