{[%evp 0,42,32,27,27,5,43,12,4,4,4,4,4,4,15,-9,-5,-5,1,1,-7,-15,2,1,-3,-5,8,-11,-54,-49,-49,-239,-273,-273,-290,-293,-293,-293,-293,-283,-283,-294,-301,-359,-343] This game was played in the third round of the U.S. championship 1963. Many argue that Fischer was at the peak of his strength during this time. This game and the tournament as a whole are definitely an argument for that statement. Fischer won the U.S. championship with a stunning 11/11, not conceding a single draw. This has never happened before and never been repeated since. Robert Eugene Byrne, playing with the white pieces, was one of the strongest American players at the time and also a participant in the candidates cycle later on. It adds to the incredibility that a player of such calibre loses to Fischer with White in just 21 moves. In the comments I have included Fischer's own account as written in his book "My 60 Memorable Games". Let's begin!} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 (2. Nc3 $36 {(Fischer)} d5 $2 3. Nf3) 2... d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 {Byrne takes a dry approach, aiming for a symmetrical pawn structure at the first opportunity. Fischer points out that 5.b3 would have kept more tension.} (5. Nc5 {soweit Fischer, aber nach} e5 $1 {ist keine Kompensation für Schwarz zu sehen.} (5... b6 6. Na6 Nxa6 $16 7. Bxa6 Qd5) (5... Qc7 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Nxd3 $17 e6) 6. Nxb7 $5 Qxd4 (6... Qb6 7. Nc5 Bxc5 8. dxc5 Qxc5 9. c3) 7. Qxd4 $1 exd4 $1 8. Bd3) 5... Bg6 6. Nf3 Nf6 (6... Nd7) 7. h4 $6 {This is an unusual, and frankly, not great setup for White. By placing the pawn on e3, the dark-squared bishop cannot be developed on the c1-h6 diagonal and the light squares are weakened. The latter will play an important role in the game.} (7. Bd3 e6 8. O-O $1 Be7 9. c4 O-O 10. Bxg6 $1 hxg6) (7. Ne5 Nbd7 8. Nxg6 $11 hxg6) 7... h6 8. Bd3 (8. Ne5 Bh7 9. Bc4 $1 $15 {or the positional exchange sacrifice} e6 {. After} 10. Qe2 Nd5 {White certainly has compensation, but Black should still have the upper-hand after} (10... Qxd4 11. Nxf7)) (8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 $15 Bxd3 $2 {is unsurprisingly no good:} 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Nbd7 12. O-O-O Qc7 13. Ne4 {and too many white pieces are hanging.} (13. Qe2 O-O-O 14. Ne5 Nxe5 {White's position is still holding up. Black has compensation for the sacrificed material, but not more.} 15. dxe5 Nd7 16. f4) 13... O-O-O $5 {was the best move according to Fischer, but modern-day engines think otherwise. As opposed to d8-d7 it allows the defence Das soll laut Fischer der beste Zug sein, was gut möglich ist, aber nicht alle seine Varianten sind korrekt:} 14. g3 $1 {provoking} Nxe4 {. After} (14... Ng4) 15. Qxe4 Bd6) 8... Bxd3 9. Qxd3 e6 10. Bd2 {Fischer comments: "It's hard for either side to introduce an imbalance into this essentially symmetrical variation. Deadeye equality also ensues afer 10. f4 e6 11.b3 a6 12.e1 c8 13.a3 e8 14.c1 (Stahlberg-Flohr, Kemeri 1937)". Indeed, the position is equal. The main difference in the placement of the e-pawn is now emphasised by Fischer with} Nbd7 (10... Qc7 {können auch nicht gefallen.} 11. c4 (11. O-O-O Bd6 12. Ne4 Bf4 13. Nxf6+ {Black once again goes after the weakened light squares in the white camp.} gxf6) 11... Nbd7 12. Bc3 {Stopping g7-c3 and preparing the trade of knights. White is clearly worse, but still fully in the game.} a5 13. O-O $1 {is best, connecting the rooks and planning ventures to the king side via f5 or g4. White has two options:} (13. O-O-O Bb4) 13... Bd6 {Now, White is threatening to take on d5.} 14. Ne4 (14. d5 Bxg3 15. dxe6 $1 (15. fxg3 cxd5 {White has survived the worst and can hope to equalise in this endgame, even though Black is the one pushing.} 16. cxd5 $1 Nxd5 17. Bxg7 Rg8 18. Qh7 N7f6 19. Bxf6 Nxf6 20. Qxh6 Qxg3 21. Qd2 $13) 15... Ne5) 14... Nxe4 15. Qxe4 $1 {With this paradoxical move, Black forces a favourable endgame.} O-O) 11. O-O-O Qc7 {Stepping out of the a3-f8 diagonal and introducing the idea ...e7-e5 in the position.  Die Stellung ist so völlig ausgeglichen, dass Weiß nun leichtsinnig wird.} 12. c4 $6 {A logical move, connecting the rooks. Yet a mistake. White ignores the black idea and misses the chance to move the rooks out of diagonals of the threatening bishops.} O-O-O $1 {Suddenly Fischer is able to introduce the imbalance he was looking for. Fischer says about this moment: "I was a bit worried about weakening my queen's pawn, but felt that the tremendous activity obtained by my minor pieces would permit White no time to exploit it. 12...e6 would probably lead to a draw." Fischer's assessment is spot-on. The black piece activity more than compensates the weakening of the pawn structure.} (12... Bd6 13. Ne4 (13. Ne2 $5 $17 {looks attractive now.} {und} O-O-O {This is relatively best.} 14. Kb1 e5) 13... Bf4 {is certainly not a pleasant position for White, but Black does not break through immediately. Expansion on the queenside with}) 13. Bc3 Qf4+ (13... Bd6 14. Ne4 Bf4+ {would give Black nothing; the position is roughly equal. völlig zufrieden sein, während Programme schwarzen Materialvorteil errechnen.} 15. Kb1 Ne5 $19 {The fork of queen and bishop decides the game right away.} {die Alternativen} 16. Nxe5 Bxe5) (13... c5 14. d5) 14. Kb1 $2 {"Add another to those melancholy case histories entitled: The Wrong Rook." is Fischer's comment. It is understandable that Byrne wanted to break the pin of the e2-knight on the f1-a6 diagonal, but this rook is dearly needed to defend the king side, particularly the point f2. Therefore, the other rook (the right one) should have moved to d1. Der falsche Turm. Dass Weiß diese scheinbar völlig normale und ausgeglichene Stellung in nur 7 Zügen verliert, hat die Partie berühmt gemacht.} Nc5 $1 {Now, the weakening of the light squares with the early e2-e3 becomes an obvious problem. The knight is deeply placed within the white position, controlling many important squares. White tries to get rid of it, but only makes matters worse.} ({On the other hand, Dagegen könnte Weiß nach} 14... Qc7) 15. Qc2 $2 {'?!' Fischer says: "There is hardly any other defense to the threat of . .. e4." It is true that f6-e4 cannot be stopped, yet the game move loses right away. White stays afloat with e2-d4 or e2-f4. Der Zug verliert,} Nce4 $3 {'!' Now it becomes evident why the rook was still needed on f1. The white king is exposed and comes under a furious attack. Interestingly, Byrne and the commentators thought that this move is played out of desperation and White is clearly better! Fischer says: "The complete justification for this sac does not become apparent until White resigns!"  Zwar wird dieser Zug a tempo von Schachprogrammen angezeigt, doch die Begründung ist falsch.} (15... Ncd7 16. Ne5 Nxe5 17. dxe5 $1 $19 {and the mate threat Bc8-h3 means White needs to give material.} {Auf} Nd7 {allows a strong deflection: geht's so:} (17... Ng4 18. Rxd8+ Kxd8 19. Rd1+ $19 {Black is up a pawn and exchange and will have no difficulties to convert.} {or} Kc8 20. Rd4 $19) 18. Rd4 Qxe5 19. Rxd7 $1) 16. Ne5 (16. Ba5) 16... Nxf2 (16... Nxg3 17. fxg3 $1 {Simple, yet powerful. The strongest piece moves for the first time and the game is over - White resigned as the invasion of the queen via h3 is deadly. Fischer writes: "A bitter disappointment. I'd hoped for 22.f2 h3+ 23.g1 e1+!! 24.xe1 xd4 with mate to follow shortly." Let us take a look at this beautiful variation: Quelle: Fischer "Meine 60 denkwürdigen Partien" (dt. Fassung)} Qxg3 {'!'} 18. Rd3 Qf4 19. Rf3 Qe4 $3 20. Nxf7) (16... Nxc3+ {'-+'} 17. bxc3 Rg8 {This indeed would have been a fitting conclusion to this brilliant game by Fischer.} (17... Ng4 $19 18. Nh5 Qf5 $1 {is similar to the game.} 19. Qxf5 {does not save White:} exf5 20. Nxf7) 18. Rd3 h5 19. Rf3 Qh6 20. Nxf7) 17. Rdf1 (17. Rdf1 Qxg3 {After the first intrusion with the knight on d3, now the other one has landed on e3! Later Byrne wrote about this position: "As I sat pondering why Fischer would choose such a line, because it was so obviously lost for Black, there suddenly comes..."} 18. Rxf2 $1 {Fischer is not interested in regaining material by taking the rook on d1. He is going for the throat (of the king)! Once again Byrne: "This dazzling move came as the shocker... the culminating combination is of such depth that, even at the very moment at which I resigned, both grandmasters who were commenting on the play for the spectators in a separate room believed I had a won game!"} Qe3 19. Re2 $1 {This is the punchline of the combination initiated with 15...d3xf2 and likely the move missed by Byrne and commentators alike. Black sacrifices the central pawn to open up the h1-a8 diagonal with devastating effect. Das ist der Clou der mit 15...Sxf2! eingeleiteten Opfervariante.} Qf4 20. Nxf7 {Fischer: "The king is at Black's mercy."}) 0-1

