{[%evp 0,65,16,28,97,43,72,49,19,13,39,2,29,-1,8,2,4,4,34,-3,8,-12,6,-34,14,13,11,11,62,77,82,42,130,84,72,86,107,205,146,146,205,220,205,206,210,206,206,222,222,303,338,295,396,381,435,517,644,716,716,716,730,535,580,575,577,588,744,946] This is the first game of Fischer's book, "My 60 Memorable Games", and I think it is a perfect prelude for a great work. I remember when I first studied this game it made a strong impression on me, because even as young as age 14, we can already see many qualities that would bring great successes for Fischer later: excellent opening knowledge, accurate calculation and very strong logical reasoning behind any move, always supported by energetic, concrete play.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 e6 3. d3 $1 {The KIA was one of Fischer's weapons for many years against 2...e6 in the Sicilian. In his own words, he was convinced that White gets a good KID (also one of Fischer's main lines as Black) with reversed colours, since Black has already "committed" his position with 2...e6.} Nc6 4. g3 Nf6 ({A different set-up was tried by Ivkov:} 4... d5 5. Nbd2 ({A little bit of modern finesse:} 5. Qe2 $5 {is an interesting option, as after} Nf6 6. Bg2 Be7 7. O-O O-O 8. e5 Nd7 {White has now} 9. c4 $1 {[%csl Gb1] when delaying the development of the knight is useful in this position, as White can increase the tension in the centre with c3, or moving the d-pawn would be a serious concession for Black.}) 5... Bd6 6. Bg2 Nge7 7. O-O O-O {[%csl Ye5] Black managed to avoid losing space in the centre with e4-e5, but} 8. Nh4 $5 {[%cal Gf2f4];1-0 Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Piatigorsky-Cup 2nd 1966 (12) '!' Fischer,R-Ivkov,B Santa Monica II. Piatigorsky Cup 1966 preparing the march of the f-pawn, still allowed him to play for the initiative in} ({Also here White has tried other ideas nowadays, for instance:} 8. Re1 Qc7 9. Qe2 f6 10. c3 Bd7 11. a3 $5 {[%cal Gb2b4,Yd3d4];1-0 Kovalev,V (2655)-Kanmazalp,O (2481) TUR-chT Ankara 2018 (12.6)})) 5. Bg2 Be7 6. O-O O-O ({The most played is} 6... d5 {but Black refrains from fighting for the centre in this game. As we will see, not a wise strategy against the young Fischer.}) 7. Nbd2 ({In his notes, Fischer is little impressed by} 7. e5 {, although he thinks White has a small advantage after} Nd5 ({We can find a recent model game on} 7... Ng4 8. Re1 f6 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. c4 $1 {probably aiming c3 and d3-d4, with strong control in centre.} Qb6 11. Nc3 d6 12. a3 $1 {a clever move and the start of a strong plan} e5 {Black had not an easy way to arrange his central pawns, but now he has to be permanently worried about the light squares} 13. Be3 Bg4 14. h3 Bh5 {the next moves by White were really strong:} 15. b4 $1 Qd8 16. g4 $1 Bg6 17. Nh4 Bf7 18. Nf5 $16 {[%csl Gf5,Yg2];1-0 Svidler,P (2745)-Van Foreest,J (2615) Bundesliga 1617 2016 (8.1) White's creative play is crowned with his dominance over the light squares. His position seems full of potential, the Bg2 and the Nf5 in special are very strong and put a lot of pressure on Black's camp. White won convincingly in}) 8. Nbd2 ({White also achieved an advantage after} 8. c4 Nc7 9. Nc3 Rb8 $2 10. Ne4 $1 b6 11. Bf4 {[%csl Gd6]} Bb7 12. h4 $14 {[%cal Yd3d4];1-0 Delchev,A (2648)-Gislason,G (2318) London Classic op 5th 2013 (3) White has successfully prevented Black from getting some air with ...d7-d6 and can continue with d3-d4 next, with a pleasant space advantage.}) 8... f6 (8... d6 {is probably a better choice.}) 9. exf6 Nxf6 10. Re1 $14 {In this position Black's main problem is that there is not an easy way to develop the central pawns, as they could become targets or leave Black with weak squares in his camp, so White's play should be easier.}) ({It should be noted that} 7. Re1 {is probably more precise, as White keeps more flexibility in his next choices.}) 7... Rb8 $6 {This seems too odd for me, but Black can justify that after either ...b7-b6 or ...b7-b5 the rook is better placed out of the diagonal, and he has a point.} (7... d5 {would still transpose to the main line:} 8. Re1 b5 9. e5 Nd7 10. Nf1 b4 ({If I could give advice to someone who is afraid of getting mated as Black in such positions, it would be study Leinier Dominguez' games in this line! Like in this nice example:} 10... a5 11. h4 b4 12. h5 h6 $1 {First: it is very important stop this pawn, as h5-h6 would leave the dark squares very weak on the kingside.} 13. N1h2 Re8 14. Ng4 Bf8 $1 {[%cal Yf8e8,Ye7f8] Second: the regroupment with e8-f8 prevents sacrifices on h6, securing the kingside.} 15. Bf4 a4 {only now, Black can continue his actions on queenside} 16. c4 bxc3 17. bxc3 Ba6 18. Bf1 a3 $132 {0-1 Grischuk,A (2783)-Dominguez Perez,L (2754) Beijing Sportaccord Basque rapid 2013 (1.1)}) 11. h4 a5 12. Bf4 a4 13. a3 $1 $132 {Fischer,R-Miagmasuren,L Sousse izt 1967 An important move for White, as similar to the march of the h-pawn on the kingside, Black was about to weaken many squares on the queenside now with a4-a3. Fischer assessed this position as double-edged in view of Black's counterplay on the queenside, but points out that White is usually faster going for the king. Actually, we can say he was the first player to prove this evaluation in practice, by playing the following beautiful game:} bxa3 14. bxa3 Na5 $2 {Black just tried a bad plan and got no counterplay, while White's attack comes undisturbed.} 15. Ne3 Ba6 16. Bh3 d4 17. Nf1 Nb6 18. Ng5 Nd5 19. Bd2 Bxg5 20. Bxg5 Qd7 21. Qh5 Rfc8 22. Nd2 Nc3 23. Bf6 $1 Qe8 24. Ne4 g6 25. Qg5 Nxe4 26. Rxe4 c4 27. h5 $1 cxd3 28. Rh4 Ra7 29. Bg2 $1 dxc2 30. Qh6 Qf8 31. Qxh7+ $3 {1-0 Fischer,R-Myagmarsuren,L Interzonal-07+ 1967 (3)}) 8. Re1 d6 9. c3 {As Black is not intending to fight for the centre, Fischer proceeds to place another pawn there.} b6 {Not a bad move, according to Fischer.} (9... b5 {was expected} 10. d4 cxd4 $1 (10... b4 {fails tactically due to} 11. e5 $1 bxc3 $2 ({Better is} 11... dxe5 {but after} 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. c4 $16 {Black is deprived of counterplay, while White keeps his positional advantages.}) 12. exf6 cxd2 13. Nxd2 $1 $18 {[%csl Rc6,Re7]}) 11. cxd4 d5 {'='} 12. e5 Nd7 {this position resembles the main lines, but I still prefer White after} 13. Nb3 $14 {[%csl Gc5][%cal Yc1e3,Ya1c1] as besides having more space, he can play for advantage exploiting exposed squares in Black's camp, like c5.}) 10. d4 Qc7 $2 {This mistake is the start of Black's problems.} ({Black should had taken the last chance to fight for the centre with} 10... cxd4 11. cxd4 d5 12. e5 Nd7 {'' and he is solid, although White's play is more pleasant.}) 11. e5 $1 {Fischer finds an energetic way to exploit Black's imprecise play.} Nd5 (11... Nd7 $2 {was bad in view of the nice tactical idea:} 12. exd6 Bxd6 13. Ne4 cxd4 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. Bf4 e5 16. Nxe5 $1 Ndxe5 17. cxd4 $16 {winning a pawn.}) ({Relatively better was} 11... dxe5 {'!'} 12. dxe5 Nd7 13. Qe2 Bb7 14. h4 $16 {[%cal Gd2f1,Gc1f4] When Black's position is defensible, according to Fischer, but it is obvious that White has the advantage.}) 12. exd6 Bxd6 13. Ne4 $1 {The point: White wins the bishop pair.} c4 {Fischer points out that this was a sad choice, as it releases the central tension, thus leaving White's comfortable to look for operations on the kingside.} ({On the other hand, Black did not have better options at his disposal:} 13... Be7 $2 14. c4 Nf6 15. Bf4 $18) (13... cxd4 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 15. c4 $1 Nf6 16. Bf4 $18 {[%csl Rb8] in both cases the b8 is the cause of Black's defeat.}) 14. Nxd6 Qxd6 {The second important moment in the game. One could expect a technical game, with Fischer making use of his bishops to squeeze Black in a positional torture, but...} 15. Ng5 $1 {The young Bobby has different plans! Some defenders are missed on Black's kingside, and White plays for an attack in the KIA, after all.} Nce7 $2 {Sherwin is planning a defensive regroupment, but this plan is too slow.} (15... h6 16. Ne4 Qd8 $16 {was a better try, but Black will always be worried about sacrifices on h6.}) 16. Qc2 $1 {As we will see, the queen is surprisingly aggressive from here.} Ng6 (16... f5 $2 17. Qe2 $18 {[%csl Re6]}) (16... g6 17. Ne4 Qc7 18. Bh6 Rd8 19. Qd2 $40 {with a tragedy on the dark squares soon.}) 17. h4 $1 {Sherwin was probably looking for something like f6-b7. Unfortunately for him, Fischer is not disposed to give him the time needed to accomplish his goal.} Nf6 {Apparently, Black has everything defended.} (17... Rd8 {is not a better try:} 18. Nxh7 $1 Kxh7 19. h5 f5 20. hxg6+ Kxg6 21. Re5 $1 $36 {[%cal Yc2e2] and White should be winning.}) ({Black also has no time to attack the knight:} 17... h6 18. h5 $1 hxg5 19. hxg6 f6 $2 20. Qe2 $18 {[%cal Re2h5]  h5}) 18. Nxh7 $1 {A cold shower for Black. White has advantage in all lines, proving Fischer's point when he says: tactical ideas always arise from better positions.} Nxh7 (18... Kxh7 $2 19. Bf4 $18 {wins on the spot for White, thanks to the well placed Qc2 :)}) 19. h5 Nh4 $1 {Sherwin finds the most fighting continuation.} (19... Ne7 20. Bf4 $18 {is an easy win for White.}) 20. Bf4 Qd8 21. gxh4 ({Of course, not} 21. Bxb8 $2 Nxg2 22. Kxg2 Bb7+ 23. f3 Qxb8 $19) 21... Rb7 $1 {A creative resource.} 22. h6 $1 {Another important and instructive moment. The young Fischer was already aware that initiative matters more than material in such positions.} (22. Bxb7 $2 {would be simply wrong} Bxb7 $13 {[%csl Rg1] '' Although White is an exchange up now, Black is the side playing for advantage with such an exposed king on g1.}) 22... Qxh4 $138 {Accepting the risks.} ({Black could avoid the opening of the kingside with} 22... g6 23. h5 $1 g5 (23... gxh5 24. Qe2 $40) 24. Be5 $16 { but White keeps a clear advantage with a pawn up and the bishop pair.}) 23. hxg7 Kxg7 $2 {After this, Black can't avoid the attack.} ({According to Fischer,} 23... Rd8 {was a better try, but after} 24. Bg3 Qh6 25. Qe2 {Black's position remains very difficult. For example:} b5 26. a4 a6 27. axb5 axb5 28. Ra8 $18 {and Black shouldn't be able to avoid further material losses.}) 24. Re4 $1 {[%cal Gf4e5] With the obvious deadly threat e5+.} Qh5 25. Re3 $1 {[%cal Ge3g3,Ge3h3] The smart manoeuvre e4-e3 is very instructive, as well as the precise conducting of the attack by Fischer.} f5 26. Rh3 Qe8 (26... Qg6 27. Rg3 $18) 27. Be5+ ({White had already the option of grabbing two exchanges with} 27. Bh6+ Kg8 28. Bxf8 Qxf8 29. Bxb7 $18 {but in Fischer's words, he was looking for more at this moment.}) 27... Nf6 (27... Kg8 $2 28. Rg3+ Kf7 29. Rg7#) 28. Qd2 $1 {Don't think about exchanges, look to the king!} Kf7 29. Qg5 Qe7 (29... Ke7 30. Rh7+ $18 {with huge material losses for Black.}) 30. Bxf6 Qxf6 31. Rh7+ Ke8 32. Qxf6 Rxh7 ({In the event of} 32... Rxf6 {only now does Black accept the rook} 33. Bxb7 $18 {indeed, the best moment to take it.}) 33. Bc6+ {A very instructive game, that really let me excited to read the rest of the book! Also a clear example of Fischer's impressive strength at just 14, showing excellent tactical awareness to grab advantages and handle the initiative.} (33. Bc6+ Bd7 34. Qxe6+ $18) 1-0

[Event "Candidates qf Fischer-Taimanov +6-0=0"]
[Site "Vancouver"]
[Date "1971.05.25"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Fischer, Robert James"]
[Black "Taimanov, Mark E"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B47"]
[Annotator "Kuzmin,Alexey"]
[PlyCount "141"]
[EventDate "1971.05.16"]
[EventType "match"]
