{Inf.2/296} 1. e4 {Marin,Mihail} e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d6 9. h3 h6 10. d4 Re8 11. Nbd2 Bf8 12. Nf1 Bd7 13. Ng3 Na5 14. Bc2 c5 15. b3 $1 (15. d5 Nc4 $1 $11) 15... cxd4 $6 (15... Nc6 16. d5 $1 $14 (16. Be3 $2 exd4 17. cxd4 cxd4 18. Nxd4 d5 $1 $11)) 16. cxd4 Nc6 17. Bb2 g6 ({Abstractly speaking,} 17... Rc8 {Marin,Mihail makes more sense at this point in the game, because it develops the last piece, with the exception of the queen.}) 18. Qd2 $16 {Marin,Mihail: 'Commentators of that time were so impressed by the further course of the game that their evaluation after this move varied from  to +- (!) In fact, the truth is somewhere between = and , as usual.'} Bg7 19. Rad1 Qb6 20. Nf1 $1 (20. Bb1 {(Marin,Mihail) Matanovic,A-Liberzon,V/Yerevan 1965/MCL (1/2, 40)} Rac8) 20... Rad8 {Marin,Mihail: 'This is a first sign of failing to understand the position properly.'} (20... Rac8 $5 {Marin,Mihail} 21. Ne3 (21. Bb1 Qb7 $5 22. Ne3 exd4 23. Nxd4 Nxe4) 21... exd4 22. Nxd4 Qc5 23. Nxc6 (23. Nf3 Ne5 $5 $11) (23. f3 Nh5 $1 $36) 23... Bxc6 24. f3 Qg5 $5) 21. Ne3 Qb8 {Marin,Mihail: 'Starting with this move, Spassky simply moves his pieces around. He probably was convinced that his talented, but still very young, opponent, would commit suicide by himself at some point. The score (2-0) from their previous encounters may have caused him to underestimate the player who was to dethrone him, only 6 years later...'} 22. Bb1 Qb7 23. Rc1 Kh7 ({With the black rook on d8 and the white rook on c1,} 23... exd4 {Marin,Mihail} 24. Nxd4 $14 Nxe4 $2 {loses to} 25. Bxe4 Rxe4 26. Nxc6 Bxc6 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. Qc3+ $18) 24. a3 Bc8 25. Bc3 Bd7 26. Qb2 Qb8 27. b4 Kg8 28. Rcd1 Nh7 29. Ba2 Ng5 30. Nxg5 hxg5 31. dxe5 dxe5 32. Nd5 $16 Ne7 33. Nxe7+ Rxe7 34. Qd2 Bf6 35. Qd6 $1 Kg7 {Marin,Mihail: 'So far, this is the closest Fischer ever got to beating Spassky prior to the Reykjavik match, but now he goes astray. '} 36. Qxa6 $4 (36. Bxe5 $1 Rxe5 37. Qxb8 Rxb8 38. Rxd7 $16) 36... Rc8 $1 37. Rd6 $1 $11 Rxc3 38. Rxf6 Be6 39. Rxe6 fxe6 40. Rd1 Qb7 41. Qxb7 Rxb7 42. Bxe6 $44 Rxa3 43. Kh2 Ra4 44. Rb1 Rc7 45. f3 Ra6 46. Bb3 Ra3 47. Rb2 Ra1 48. Kg3 Kf6 49. Kg4 Rc3 50. Bd5 Raa3 51. h4 gxh4 52. Kxh4 Ra1 53. Rd2 Raa3 54. Kg4 Rd3 55. Re2 Rac3 56. Ra2 Ra3 57. Rb2 (57. Rc2 {(Marin,Mihail) Parma,B}) 1/2-1/2

