1. b3 {Bobby used Larsen's opening to defeat Tukmakov at Buenos Aires and Mecking at Palma de Mallorca in 1970.} e5 2. Bb2 d6 3. c4 Nf6 4. g3 g6 5. Bg2 Bg7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. e3 Nbd7 8. Nge2 Re8 9. d3 Nf8 10. O-O c6 11. b4 Qc7 12. a4 Be6 13. Rc1 Qd7 14. Re1 {Bobby often went out of his way to save his Bishops (..Re8 and ...Bh8 as Black and Re1 and Bh1 here).} Bh3 15. Bh1 d5 16. cxd5 cxd5 17. Nb5 Ne6 $2 {Black overeacts to the threat of Nc7/Rc7. Instead of the text which drops a pawn, Black should have played 17...Re7 maintaining the balance.} 18. Bxe5 Rec8 19. Rxc8+ Rxc8 20. Nxa7 Re8 21. Nb5 Ng5 22. Bf4 (22. Bxf6 Bxf6 23. Qb3 Be6 24. Nf4 $18 {is another good continuation for White.}) 22... Ne6 23. Nbc3 d4 {.} 24. Nxd4 Nxd4 ({Black can try to complicate matters with} 24... Nxf4 {but after} 25. gxf4 Nh5 26. Nce2 Bxd4 27. Nxd4 Nxf4 28. Bf3 {White is still winning.}) 25. exd4 Rxe1+ 26. Qxe1 Qxd4 27. Nb5 Qxd3 28. Bxb7 Nd5 29. Bd6 $2 {The right square, but the wrong piece! Correct is} (29. Nd6 Bd7 30. Qd2 Qb1+ 31. Kg2 Nxb4 32. Nxf7 $18 {Black's ...Nd5 is tricky (....Qf3 ideas) and Bobby overlooks threats to his King.}) 29... h6 30. Nc7 $2 {White had to relieve the pressure on the backrank with 30.f4, but Black is already doing well. For example:} (30. f4 Qf3 31. Bxd5 Qxd5 32. Kf2 Bd4+ 33. Nxd4 Qxd4+ 34. Qe3 Qxd6) 30... Bc3 (30... Nc3 31. Bg2 Bxg2 32. Qe8+ Kh7 33. Kxg2 Qxd6 34. Qxf7 {offers White chances to resist.}) 31. Qc1 Bb2 $6 (31... Bd2 $1 {is crushing.} 32. Qa1 Nc3 33. Bc5 Ne2+ 34. Kh1 Bc3 {wins the Queen which cannot abandon the backrank.}) 32. Qe1 Bc3 33. Qc1 {Again 33...Bd2! was a winner.} 1/2-1/2

