1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 ({So for a game appearing on this Dragons site this game does feature a pretty weird move order but it is sort of plausible that the same middlegame position could be reached via slightly normal means e.g.} 2... d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. f3 Nc6 7. Bc4 Bd7 8. Bb3 {and now} Na5 {rather than 8...Bg7. Okay so I could try to justify prioritising some of those moves over others but I'm certainly not going to try to pull the wool over your eyes! What is true is that this strictly speaking 'B57' encounter struggles to fit into a serious opening classification but lacking in slow play games in this current World situation, I thought we'd get retro and maybe occasionally feature the odd Bobby Fischer or other all time greats game.}) 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 {Here then things actually start out as a Sicilian Classical variation.} 6. Bc4 {Fischer's favourite set up was the 'Sozin' in which the light-squared bishop eyes up the f7-square.} ({Most popular here is} 6. Bg5 {which would effectively have put paid to any Black aspirations of transposing back to a Dragon formation (which of course he could have opted for from the start!) as} g6 7. Bxf6 exf6 {isn't great for the pawn structure!}) 6... Bd7 ({Regular subscribers should be very familiar with the trap} 6... g6 $2 7. Nxc6 bxc6 8. e5 $1 {, especially bad in the case of} dxe5 $4 9. Bxf7+ $1) ({Of course} 6... e6 {blunting the bishop is logical and most common but had that have occurred then with this being the Dragons site, I wouldn't be annotating this game now!}) 7. Bb3 ({A useful retreat waiting for Black to commit himself. Although} 7. f3 e6 {is a plausible sequence, certainly} 8. Ndb5 {is well met by} Qb8 $1 {and frequently against such set ups Fisher favoured challenging Black's structure through f4-f5.}) 7... g6 {As we've seen, White has had his chances to deviate this whole opening away from a Dragon but in doggedly sticking to his own preferred system, arguably Black has gained a moral advantage. Indeed via this somewhat convoluted move order White has been deprived of playing the 9 0-0-0 (or for that matter the 9 g4) Yugoslav Attack!} 8. f3 ({Upon the immediate} 8. Be3 {Black could have continued with his kingside fianchetto or dabbled in} Ng4 $5 {Instead White puts paid to that option.}) 8... Na5 (8... Nxd4 $5 9. Qxd4 Bg7 {is a perfectly sensible alternative approach that is reminiscent of all the Accelerated Dragon Maroczy Binds that we've been looking at in recent times. White should park his dark-squared bishop on e3 or g5 before retreating his queen to d2 just when a serious discovered attack becomes a reality. Just one practical example then is} 10. Bg5 O-O 11. Qd2 b5 12. Bh6 a5 13. Bxg7 Kxg7 14. a4 b4 15. Nd5 Nxd5 16. exd5 Rc8 {with chances for both sides in Amonatov,F (2636)-Li,C  (2732) Riadh 2017.}) ({The obvious 8...Bg7 sees us most likely headed for a 9 Bc4 Yugoslav Attack but intriguing is that Gligoric (back then considered one of the strongest non-Soviet Grandmasters) reached this position three years later and on that occasion preferred} 8... Rc8 {when} 9. Nxc6 {(justified as Black usually wants to recapture with the b-pawn but this might render ...Rc8 a waste of time.)} bxc6 10. Be3 Bg7 11. Qd2 O-O 12. O-O-O Qa5 13. Bh6 c5 {(trying to put the c8-rook to good use through the threat of ...c4)} 14. Bxg7 Kxg7 15. Qe2 {was in Ciocaltea,V-Gligoric,S Belgrade 1962 objectively a little better for White given the idea of e4-e5.}) 9. Bg5 {The downside of Black's last move is that it released the pressure on White's centrally posted steed and thus allowed White's dark-squared bishop to venture further afield. On White's part, Fischer seems determined to eek out the ...h6 that creates the odd weakness in the defensive black pawn shield.} Bg7 10. Qd2 h6 {Essentially a fork in the road for Black. The decision made effectively takes Bh6 away from White's options whereas} (10... O-O {wouldn't have incurred the same weaknesses. However regards for example} 11. Bh6 Bxh6 12. Qxh6 {I would add that although an exchange sac on c3 wouldn't be as effective with the knight on a5 rather than e5 as it obstructs the a-pawn (e.g. following 12...Rc8 13 h4 Rxc3 14 bxc3 Qc7) an alternative solution presents itself in the form of} e5 $5 13. Nde2 b5 {when Black definitely has reasonable play to compensate for his backward d6-pawn.}) 11. Be3 Rc8 {Obviously useful although a quick reminder that castling is no longer possible whilst the h6-pawn hangs.} 12. O-O-O Nc4 13. Qe2 {Given my not exactly secretive affiliation to the Dragon bishop (and hence its opposite number too), it won't come as a big surprise that I'm not a fan of this move! Though not yet of course the player he was to become, Fischer was pretty nifty at the age of 16. Apparently Bronstein was so impressed with this concept that he awarded the move with a '!!' but I'm simply going to offer the respect that one of the greatest ever players is due by not awarding it with a '?!'!} ({Keeping things simple is} 13. Bxc4 $1 Rxc4 {and then either} 14. Kb1 {or 14 g4 with the sort of advantage that we've been used to seeing White get in the 'Anti-Soltis' variations.}) 13... Nxe3 14. Qxe3 O-O ({This isn't unreasonable but given Black has delayed castling up to this point, he could hang back further with} 14... Qb6 $5 {This is a very useful move when White's dark-squared bishop has been removed and specifically now ... e5 is a piece winning threat. As it happens that actually occurred the following year with} 15. Qd2 Qc5 {very sensibly seeing Black look to edge forward. However after} 16. f4 {Black should have either finally castled or else continued with some queenside expansion via 16...b5 but instead lost his way through} h5 $6 {when} 17. Nf3 Bh6 18. e5 Ng4 ({Note} 18... dxe5 19. Nxe5 Qxe5 20. fxe5 Bxd2+ 21. Rxd2 Ng4 22. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 23. Rxd7 {is simply a bad ending.}) 19. Rhe1 O-O 20. Ne4 {was all one way traffic in Fischer,R-Marini,L Mar del Plata 1960}) 15. g4 (15. h4 Qb6 16. h5 g5 {seems to offer both sides chances although the Soltis like 15...h5!? stopping White's h-pawn in its tracks looks quite appealing too.}) 15... Qa5 {Unless Black is serious about sacing the exchange on c3, this isn't the right move here. Instead again} (15... Qb6 $5 {looks best and after} 16. Qd2 ({Remember} 16. h4 e5 $1) 16... a5 $5 {brings the concept of ...a4 into play. It still seems fine to me.}) 16. h4 e6 (16... h5 17. g5 Ne8 18. f4 e5 19. fxe5 Qxe5 {at first may seem promising because the Dragon bishop has some life. Unfortunately} 20. Nd5 Nc7 21. Nf6+ $1 Bxf6 22. gxf6 Qxf6 23. Rhf1 $1 Qe5 ({Or} 23... Qxh4 24. Qh6 $1 {bringing into play both Qxg6+ and Nf5 i.e. a massive attack!}) 24. Nf3 {is suddenly a mega initiative for White with the threat to d6 and} Qe7 25. Qh6 {highlighting the danger to Black.}) 17. Nde2 (17. g5 $1 hxg5 18. hxg5 Nh5 19. f4 {is surely most direct. White aims for f4-f5 and if} e5 {then} 20. Nd5 $1 exd4 21. Ne7+ Kh7 22. Qf3 {is cute. Bad things are going to happen on the h-file!}) 17... Rc6 (17... Ne8 $1 {was the best defence both protecting d6 and anticipating g4-g5.}) 18. g5 hxg5 19. hxg5 Nh5 20. f4 Rfc8 21. Kb1 $1 ({With the idea of f4-f5 still on White's mind, this is calm and strong although actually} 21. e5 $5 {blocking out the Dragon bishop, taking advantage of the d-file pin and vacating the e4-square for the knight held a certain amount of appeal too!}) 21... Qb6 (21... Bxc3 22. Nxc3 Rxc3 23. bxc3 Rxc3 24. Qd4 {doesn't remotely cut it and so instead Black tries another approach.}) 22. Qf3 {However White of course isn't interested in a queen swap.} Rc5 23. Qd3 ({We all know it's not always easy to deliver mate when Black has a Dragon bishop and White no equivalent dark-squared bishop to challenge it. Nevertheless I was very surprised not to see the tempting} 23. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 24. Qxh5 {given how strong the threat of f4-f5 is.}) 23... Bxc3 {Black spots an opportunity to part with his pride and joy for something tangible but it's not going to work out! However there is no good way to deal with the threat to the attacked d6-pawn.} ({Indeed} 23... R5c6 24. f5 exf5 25. Rxh5 {might see the neat line} fxe4 (25... gxh5 26. exf5 {is with the likes of Nd5 and f5-f6 in the air, simply grim.}) 26. Qxe4 gxh5 27. Bxf7+ Kxf7 28. g6+ Kg8 29. Qd5+ {and essentially Black is getting mated.}) 24. Nxc3 Nxf4 25. Qf3 Nh5 ({Upon} 25... e5 {simplest is} 26. Ne2 {The black knight is vital to keeping the h-file blocked but it's also currently pinned to the f7-pawn.}) 26. Rxh5 $1 {Just as strong as ever here.} gxh5 27. Qxh5 Be8 ({There can be no running as} 27... Kf8 28. Qh8+ Ke7 29. Qf6+ Ke8 30. Rh1 {demonstrates.}) 28. Qh6 ({Keeping the king trapped is logical although actually} 28. Rh1 Kf8 29. Bxe6 $1 {would have done the business too i. e.} fxe6 ({Or} 29... Rxc3 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Qf6+ Kf8 32. Rh8#) 30. Qh8+ Ke7 31. Rh7+ Bf7 ({Or} 31... Kd8 32. Qf6#) 32. Qf6+ Kd7 33. Qxf7+ Kc6 34. Qd7#) 28... Rxc3 29. bxc3 $1 (29. Rh1 {would have allowed to defend for a bit along the diagonal through} Qd4) 29... Rxc3 30. g6 $1 fxg6 ({The threat was Qh7+ followed by g7+ whilst} 30... Rg3 31. Qh7+ Kf8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qh4+ {simply picks up the rook.}) 31. Rh1 {Now threatening mate in two via Qh8+ and Rh7.} Qd4 32. Qh7+ (32. Bxe6+ Bf7 33. Qh7+ Kf8 34. Qxf7# {equally does the job although Black resigns now anyway as Rf1+ will soon be mating.}) 1-0

