{Sac, Sac, Mate!:- It's probably fair to say that America's (and many believe the World's) greatest ever player didn't have a particularly high opinion of the Dragon. His recipe for extinguishing the fire was rather unsubtle!} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 {As far as I'm concerned, the Danish Grandmaster Bent Larsen has been the World's leading Accelerated Dragon expert for many a year. In the old days he regularly wheeled out the normal Dragon, but significantly I have noted that such outings were extremely few and far between after this particular encounter.} 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 ({It's worth noting that an attempt to hassle the bishop immediately with} 6... Ng4 $4 {loses to} 7. Bb5+ {i.e.} Bd7 8. Qxg4) 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Nxd4 {A once popular variation that is virtually extinct today. There are known alternatives, but nowadays very rarely is anything other than 9...Bd7 seen.} 10. Bxd4 Be6 11. Bb3 $1 {Capturing on e6 may double Black's pawns, but it opens up the f-file and more importantly hands over control of d5.} Qa5 12. O-O-O b5 {In contrast to Black, White hasn't pushed any pawns around his king. Hence an attack with pawns (intended to grant access for the rooks to the enemy king) is always likely to be slower. Note that trading bishops on b3 isn't good for as this time it would be White given control of the c4-square as well as extra cover (after 12 cxb3) for his king. This is an excellent illustrative point. So often White loves to get a knight to d5 and likewise, Black one to c4.} 13. Kb1 b4 14. Nd5 Bxd5 (14... Nxd5 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. exd5 Bd7 {is better for White because of the pressure he can exert on the e7-pawn.}) 15. Bxd5 ({In fact} 15. exd5 $1 Qb5 16. Rhe1 a5 17. Qe2 $1 {is considered to be a better theoretical variation for White and now Black misses his chance.}) 15... Rac8 $2 (15... Nxd5 $1 {was it, with} 16. Bxg7 Nc3+ $1 17. bxc3 ({or} 17. Bxc3 bxc3 18. Qxc3 Qxc3 19. bxc3 Rfc8) 17... Rab8 $1 18. cxb4 Qxb4+ 19. Qxb4 Rxb4+ 20. Bb2 Rfb8 {both likely drawn double rook endings.}) 16. Bb3 $1 Rc7 ({Throughout it is clear that the best way to hassle White's light-squared bishop is with ...a5-a4. However this has not been easy to arrange and even now after} 16... Qb5 {, White can knock this plan on the head with the greedy} 17. Bxa7 $1) 17. h4 $1 {Consistent with Fischer's assessment that beating the Dragon was essentially down to prising open the h-file and then sac, sac mate!} Qb5 18. h5 Rfc8 19. hxg6 hxg6 20. g4 a5 21. g5 Nh5 {Often this succeeds in blocking the h-file and halting White's attack. Alas because Black never got around to exchanging off White's light-squared bishop with the usual ... Ne5-c4 plan, he has lined himself up for some trouble.} 22. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 23. g6 {The f-pawn is pinned.} e5 24. gxf7+ Kf8 25. Be3 {Now the b3-bishop is a monster and if it was required, it has the safe d5 square as a resting place.} d5 {26 Bh6 was threatened and} (25... a4 {fell foul of} 26. Qxd6+ Re7 27. Qd8+ $1 Rxd8 28. Rxd8+ Re8 29. Bc5+ {with mate next go.}) 26. exd5 Rxf7 {Alas on 26. ..a4, White had 27 d6 anyway.} 27. d6 Rf6 28. Bg5 Qb7 {28...Qd7 29 Qd5, threatening 30 Qg8 mate was hopeless.} 29. Bxf6 Bxf6 30. d7 Rd8 31. Qd6+ ({Leading to checkmate although it must be said that} 31. Qh6+ $1 Ke7 32. Qh7+ Kf8 33. Qf7# {would have been quicker!}) 1-0

