{This line was known not to be very good but it has received a certain amount of attention recently and from time to time it occurs even at the very highest level, mainly in Michael Adams' games.} 1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qd8 4. d4 {The initial position of the variation. Now Black has two different plans. One possibility is the fianchetto of his dark-squared Bishop, and another is to play the light-squared Bishop to f5 and the arising position looks like a typical Caro Kann.} g6 5. Bf4 $1 {Although this game was played almost 40 years ago Fischer's plan is considered as the best nowadays. The idea of this move (followed by 6 .Qd2) is to prevent Black's Knight manoeuvre to f5-square via h6.} ({Another possible plan is} 5. Bc4 Bg7 6. Nf3 {aiming for a small but steady advantage, but recent practice proves Black has good chances of equalising:} Nh6 7. Bxh6 $5 ({The alternatives are:} 7. Ne4 Nf5 8. c3 O-O 9. O-O Nd7 10. Bf4 Nd6 11. Bd3 {Kaminski-Kiedrowicz, Bielsko-Biala 1991.}) (7. Bg5 Nf5 8. O-O O-O 9. Re1 h6 10. Bf4 c6 11. Be5 {Arakhamia-Petrovic, Tuzla 1987, with a tiny edge in both cases.}) 7... Bxh6 8. Ne5 O-O 9. O-O Nd7 10. Qe2 Nb6 11. Rad1 Nxc4 12. Qxc4 Bf5 13. Qb3 Rb8 {and Black has little to worry about, Tiviakov-Galliamova, Elista 1998.}) 5... Bg7 ({the immediate} 5... Nh6 {is not good in view of} 6. Be5 $1 f6 7. Bf4 {, weakening Black's K-side.}) 6. Qd2 $1 ({Tempting is} 6. Nb5 {as was played in Bronstein-Kholmov, Tbilisi 1959 is harmless:} Na6 7. Nf3 Nf6 8. c4 c6 9. Nc3 Bg4 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O Nd7 12. Be3 e5 13. d5 c5 14. Ne1 Bxe2 15. Qxe2 f5 {and in this complicated position Black's chances are by no means bad.}) 6... Nf6 ({It's not good for Black to take the d4-pawn:} 6... Qxd4 7. Qxd4 Bxd4 8. Nb5 Bb6 9. Nxc7+ Bxc7 10. Bxc7 {and the endgame clearly favors White}) (6... Bxd4 $2 7. O-O-O Nc6 8. Bb5 Bd7 9. Nd5 $1 e5 10. Nf3 {and Black can't avoid gross material losses.}) 7. O-O-O c6 8. Bh6 O-O $2 {This natural looking move is indeed the decisive mistake!} (8... Bxh6 9. Qxh6 Bf5 {intending to castle Q-side should be played, although in my opinion White is clearly better here.}) 9. h4 $1 {Exploiting Black's mistake White starts a K-side attack without delay.} Qa5 10. h5 $1 gxh5 {The only move.} ({Black can't allow the opening of the h-file:} 10... Rd8 11. hxg6 fxg6 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Qh6+ Kg8 14. Nf3 {[%emt 0:00:15] and Black can't parry the threat of Ng5}) (10... Nxh5 {is also insufficient:} 11. Be2 Nf6 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Qh6+ Kg8 14. g4 $1 {and White wins by force:} Rd8 15. g5 Nh5 16. Bxh5 gxh5 17. Rxh5 Qf5 ({or} 17... Bf5 18. g6 $1) 18. g6 $1 Qxg6 19. Rg5 {winning the Queen.}) 11. Bd3 $1 Nbd7 ({It would be very good for Black to transfer his light-squared Bishop to the g6-square but White's last move made this manoeuvre impossible:} 11... Bf5 $2 12. Qg5) 12. Nge2 Rd8 13. g4 $1 {The decisive breakthrough. Now White's attack becomes unstoppable.} Nf8 ({Black can't accept the sacrifice in view of} 13... Nxg4 14. Rdg1 $1 {with decisive threats.}) 14. gxh5 {Now Black is helpless against threats on the g-file.} Ne6 15. Rdg1 Kh8 ({Or} 15... Kf8 16. Bxg7+ Nxg7 17. Qh6 Ng4 18. Qxh7 {and White wins.}) 16. Bxg7+ Nxg7 17. Qh6 Rg8 (17... Ne6 18. Nf4 $1) 18. Rg5 Qd8 (18... Nf5 {is also bad:} 19. Rxg8+ Nxg8 20. Qf8 {, Rg1 to follow.}) 19. Rhg1 Nf5 $2 {A blunder but Black's position is lost anyway.} 20. Bxf5 {Bobby Fischer at his best!} 1-0

