{Black was accurate in the opening and White was unable to obtain any serious pressure. At some point he could even have faced some problems but Black was rather peaceful and after some manoeuvres the game ended with a most likely result.} 1. c4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. d4 Nf6 4. Nc3 Be7 5. Bg5 h6 6. Bh4 O-O 7. e3 Nbd7 (7... b6 {leads to the Tartakower System.}) 8. Rc1 c6 9. Bd3 dxc4 10. Bxc4 b5 $5 11. Bd3 a6 12. a4 bxa4 13. Nxa4 Qa5+ 14. Nd2 Bb4 15. Nc3 c5 16. Nb3 Qd8 17. O-O cxd4 18. Nxd4 Bb7 19. Be4 Qb8 ({The more risky} 19... Qb6 $5 {is considered in the game Kharitonov - Nenashev/Omsk 1985.}) 20. Bg3 (20. Nc6 $1 {is probably more promising - see the game Kamsky - Salov/Candidates (m/3) 1995.}) 20... Qa7 21. Nc6 Bxc6 22. Bxc6 {White has a pair of bishops but the ending is not yet on the board. Besides, a pair of Q-side pawns might be exchanged.} Rac8 23. Na4 {Otherwise Black was fine after ...Nd7-c5, when he had slightly more active rooks and minor pieces.} Rfd8 24. Bf3 a5 {Securing a solid position - it is very difficult for White to give his opponent any problems.} 25. Rc6 {This is harmless for Black.} ({White could have chosen between} 25. Qe2) ({or} 25. Qb3 {, keeping a symbolic edge after the possible} Nc5 26. Nxc5 Rxc5 27. Rfd1) 25... Rxc6 26. Bxc6 Rc8 (26... Qa6 $5 {was also good.}) 27. Bf3 (27. Bb5 $5 {was worthy of consideration.}) 27... Qa6 28. h3 Qb5 29. Be2 Qc6 30. Bf3 Qb5 {Black is just fine - he has no problems and White's bishops are rather passive.} 31. b3 {White avoids the repetition but he has no real chances to get an advantage and may only bring problems.} Be7 32. Be2 Qb4 33. Ba6 Rc6 34. Bd3 (34. Bc4 {seemed more accurate.}) 34... Nc5 ({After} 34... Bd6 $1 {it was already Black who would be hoping for a slight edge.}) 35. Qf3 $6 ({It was time to play safe chess:} 35. Nxc5 Rxc5 36. Qf3 $5 {although Black still had some pressure after} Nd5) 35... Rc8 $6 {Missing a chance to get the advantage.} (35... Nce4 $1 36. Be5 Rc8 {was stronger with pressure on White's Q-side.}) 36. Nxc5 Bxc5 37. Rc1 {Now White is OK.} Rd8 ({Surely not} 37... Qxb3 $4 38. Rxc5 Rxc5 39. Qa8+ $18) 38. Bc4 Qd2 39. Rf1 Bb4 40. Bc7 Rd7 41. Qc6 (41. Bb6 $5 {was more promising, trying to get the bishop to d4. However, Black kept good control over the position:} Bc3 (41... Nd5 42. Bd4 Bc3 43. Rd1 Qb2 44. Qg3 Bxd4 45. Qb8+ Kh7 46. Rxd4 $14) 42. Qa8+ Kh7 43. Bb5 Rd6 44. Bc7 Rd5 45. Bc6 Rc5 46. Bb6 Rg5 {and so on.}) 41... Qc2 {Preventing White's e-pawn advance.} 42. Be5 Rd2 43. Qa8+ Kh7 44. Bxf6 gxf6 45. Qf3 {White has managed to weaken his opponent's king but he has no chances to exploit it.} f5 46. g4 Qe4 47. Kg2 Kg6 48. Rc1 Ba3 49. Ra1 Bb4 50. Rc1 Be7 51. gxf5+ exf5 52. Re1 {Giving his opponent a chance to get an extra pawn, which cannot change the outcome.} Rxf2+ 53. Kxf2 Bh4+ 54. Ke2 Qxf3+ 55. Kxf3 Bxe1 {Draw agreed.} 1/2-1/2

