1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 3. g3 Bg7 4. Bg2 O-O 5. Nc3 d6 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O e5 8. d5 Ne7 9. c5 $5 {The most direct way of commencing the Queenside attack. 9. e4 followed by Ne1, Bd2 and Nd3 was more patient.} Nd7 10. cxd6 cxd6 11. a4 Nc5 {Another game went} (11... a6 12. a5 Nf5 13. Nd2 b6 14. axb6 Qxb6 15. Nc4 Qc7 16. Na5 $16 {Razuvaev-Agzamov URS 1966}) 12. Nd2 b6 13. b4 Nb7 14. Qb3 $14 {White is slightly better thanks to the awkward Knight on b7.l} Bd7 15. Ba3 a6 16. Nc4 b5 17. Nd2 Qb6 18. Bb2 f5 $1 {Show time! hite must work hard now to keep any advantage because Black has covered all his weaknesses.} 19. Ra3 Bh6 20. e3 Rac8 21. axb5 axb5 22. Qa2 (22. Rfa1 Bxe3 $1) 22... Bg7 23. Ra1 {Very pretty. But exactly how is Korchnoi going to break in?} e4 24. Bf1 Nd8 25. Ra6 $1 Qb8 26. Ra7 $1 {Like this!} Rc7 27. Rxc7 Qxc7 28. Nxb5 Bxb5 29. Bxb5 Nf7 30. Bxg7 Kxg7 31. Bc6 $2 (31. Qb2+ $5 {31 Qb2+ would have given more control.}) 31... Nxc6 32. Rc1 $2 $138 Qa7 $1 33. Qxa7 Nxa7 34. Rc7 Nb5 35. Rb7 Nc3 36. Nc4 Kf6 37. b5 Ne5 38. Nxd6 Rd8 39. Rb6 Kg5 40. Ra6 Nxd5 41. b6 Nb4 42. Ra4 Rxd6 43. Rxb4 Rd1+ 44. Kg2 Nf3 0-1

