{A Classical Classic: A World Championship Benoni which caused shockwaves in the chess world.} 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 c5 4. d5 exd5 5. cxd5 d6 6. Nc3 g6 7. Nd2 Nbd7 8. e4 Bg7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Qc2 {This move has been superseded by 11 a4 (see Portisch-Suba).} Nh5 $5 {This idea caused shockwaves around the world when Fischer unleashed it at Reykjavik. Black allows his kingside pawns to be shattered, hopeful that the activity he gains will compensate for this.} 12. Bxh5 {And why not?} gxh5 13. Nc4 Ne5 14. Ne3 (14. Nxe5 Bxe5 15. Be3 {is another possibility for White.}) 14... Qh4 15. Bd2 $6 {Spassky treats Fischer's idea too passively. Both} (15. f3 $1 {(Byrne) and}) (15. Ne2 Ng4 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Ng3 Be5 18. Bd2 {(Psakhis) promise White some advantage.}) 15... Ng4 $1 {This straightens out the Black pawns.} 16. Nxg4 hxg4 17. Bf4 Qf6 18. g3 $2 {Once again this is too passive. In particular, it leaves a whole on f3 and leaves the e4-pawn very weak. Both} (18. Bg3 {and}) (18. Qd2 {would have been stronger moves.}) 18... Bd7 {Preparing ...b5} 19. a4 b6 20. Rfe1 a6 21. Re2 b5 $1 {Taking the initiative on the queenside. It has to be said that already Black has a very comfortable position.} 22. Rae1 Qg6 23. b3 Re7 $1 {The next stage of the plan: Black's idea is a timely ...Rae8 to add extra pressure to the already weak e4-pawn.} 24. Qd3 Rb8 25. axb5 axb5 26. b4 {Forced, as White has to prevent ...b5-b4. If Black now captures on b4, White will round up the pawn with Na2 and Nxb4.} c4 $1 27. Qd2 Rbe8 {Here comes the pressure on e4!} 28. Re3 (28. Bg5 $4 {loses a piece to} Bxc3 29. Qxc3 Qxg5) 28... h5 29. R3e2 Kh7 30. Re3 Kg8 (30... Bxc3 31. Qxc3 Rxe4 32. Rxe4 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Qxe4 34. Qf6 $132) 31. R3e2 Bxc3 {Finally cashing in his chips to win the e4-pawn.} 32. Qxc3 Rxe4 33. Rxe4 Rxe4 34. Rxe4 Qxe4 35. Bh6 Qg6 {Black is a pawn up, but there is still much work to be done to convert this into the full point, and the presence of opposite coloured bishops also complicates things.} 36. Bc1 Qb1 37. Kf1 Bf5 38. Ke2 Qe4+ 39. Qe3 Qc2+ (39... Qxd5 40. Qg5+ {gives White unwanted counterplay.}) 40. Qd2 (40. Ke1 $1 {would have been more resilient.}) 40... Qb3 41. Qd4 $2 {Again} (41. Ke1 {would have been stronger.}) 41... Bd3+ $1 {and Spassky resigned. White is totally lost, for example} (41... Bd3+ 42. Ke1 (42. Kd2 Qc2+ 43. Ke1 Qxc1#) (42. Ke3 Qd1 43. Bd2 Qf3#) 42... Qxb4+ 43. Kd1 Qb3+ 44. Ke1 b4 {and the pawns charge home.}) 0-1

