{Another interesting element was the caged rook on a7. We can find a similar pattern in the game below, one of the most complicated in the whole chess history. Analysing the whole game would require a volume, so I will focus on the relevant phase.} 1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. Nf3 g6 5. Bc4 (5. Be2 Bg7 6. c4 Nb6 7. exd6 cxd6 8. Nc3 O-O 9. O-O Nc6 10. Be3 Bg4 11. b3 d5 $1) (5. Ng5) 5... Nb6 6. Bb3 Bg7 7. Nbd2 $5 (7. O-O O-O 8. a4 a5 9. h3 Nc6 10. Qe2) 7... O-O 8. h3 $2 a5 $1 9. a4 $2 (9. c3 $15) 9... dxe5 10. dxe5 Na6 $1 11. O-O Nc5 $17 12. Qe2 Qe8 $1 13. Ne4 Nbxa4 14. Bxa4 Nxa4 15. Re1 (15. Qc4 $15) 15... Nb6 16. Bd2 a4 17. Bg5 h6 18. Bh4 Bf5 $5 (18... Bd7 $1) 19. g4 Be6 $2 (19... Bxe4) (19... Bd7) 20. Nd4 $1 Bc4 21. Qd2 Qd7 (21... Bxe5 22. Qxh6 Bg7 23. Qe3 Nd5 24. Qd2) 22. Rad1 Rfe8 23. f4 $1 Bd5 24. Nc5 Qc8 $11 25. Qc3 $5 (25. e6 $1 Nc4 26. Qc1 $11) 25... e6 $1 26. Kh2 Nd7 27. Nd3 $2 (27. Nb5) 27... c5 $1 28. Nb5 Qc6 29. Nd6 Qxd6 (29... Reb8 30. Qd2 b5) 30. exd6 Bxc3 31. bxc3 f6 32. g5 $1 hxg5 33. fxg5 f5 34. Bg3 Kf7 35. Ne5+ Nxe5 36. Bxe5 b5 37. Rf1 Rh8 38. Bf6 $1 a3 39. Rf4 a2 40. c4 (40. Ra1) 40... Bxc4 41. d7 Bd5 42. Kg3 $1 {[ Rh4] [ h4]} Ra3+ 43. c3 (43. Kf2 $2 Raxh3 $1 44. d8=Q Rxd8 45. Bxd8 e5 $1) 43... Rha8 $1 44. Rh4 $1 e5 $1 45. Rh7+ Ke6 46. Re7+ Kd6 47. Rxe5 Rxc3+ 48. Kf2 Rc2+ 49. Ke1 Kxd7 50. Rexd5+ Kc6 51. Rd6+ Kb7 $15 52. Rd7+ (52. Re6 Rc8) 52... Ka6 53. R7d2 Rxd2 54. Kxd2 b4 55. h4 $1 (55. Kc2 $2 Kb5 56. Kb2 c4 57. Ka1 c3 $19) 55... Kb5 {55 moves have passed since the start of the game, many pieces have been exchanged, the kings have moved a lot, but the tension of the fight did not go down for a single bit. Black's four passed pawn are not weaker than the bishop. If White plays passively, he will be crushed by these pawns. Spassky's next move is the only one allowing him to stay in the game. Diagram [#]} 56. h5 $1 ({White does not afford to lose time. In the event of a neutral move such as} 56. Kc2 $2 {the most convincing win is} Ra6 $1 {preventing the game plan. For instance:} 57. h5 gxh5 58. Rg1 f4 59. g6 (59. Ba1 f3 60. g6 f2 61. Rf1 Rxg6 62. Rxf2 h4 $19) 59... Rxf6 60. g7 a1=Q 61. Rxa1 Rg6 $19 {Marin,Mihail}) 56... c4 $1 {[ c3] [ c3]} 57. Ra1 (57. h6 $2 c3+ 58. Kd3 a1=Q 59. Rxa1 Rxa1 60. h7 Rd1+ $1 61. Kc2 Rh1 62. h8=Q Rxh8 63. Bxh8 Kc4 $19) 57... gxh5 58. g6 h4 (58... Ra3 59. g7 Rg3 60. Rxa2 c3+ 61. Ke2 b3 62. Ra8 $11 {Gligoric,Svetozar. In fact, this even wins for White.} c2 (62... b2 63. g8=Q Rxg8 64. Rxg8 b1=Q 65. Rb8+) 63. Rc8 h4 64. Bb2 {winning the rook soon.}) 59. g7 h3 60. Be7 Rg8 61. Bf8 {Diagram [#] Finally, we have reached the same situation with the rook's imprisoning as in the main game. As we will see later, the bishop is alive, while the rook is not.} (61. Bf6) 61... h2 (61... c3+ {is an important alternative, requiring from White very accurate defence.} 62. Kd3 $1 (62. Kc2 $2 Kc4 $19) 62... h2 63. Rh1 f4 64. Ra1 {White can do little else than sit and wait.} f3 65. Rh1 Kc6 (65... Ka4 66. Kc2) {Diagram [#]} 66. Kc4 $1 (66. Kc2 $2 {allows} Kd5 67. Kd3 f2 68. Rf1 Ke6 $5 {Black would also win by advancing one of the queenside pawns and thus clearing the king's path, but he can achieve the same with a small triangle.} (68... Ke5 $2 {runs into} 69. Bxb4 $1 {since the pawn on g7 is taboo. This is a good example for the fact that the bishop not only fulfils a static function. It can also return into play at the right time.} Kf4 70. Bxc3 Kg3 71. Bd4 $11) 69. Kc2 (69. Bxb4 Rxg7 $19) 69... Ke5 70. Kd3 (70. Bxb4 Ke4 71. Bxc3 Kf3 72. Bd4 a1=Q 73. Rxa1 Kg2 $19) 70... Kf4 71. Rxf2+ Kg3 72. Rf1 Kg2 $19) (66. Kd4 {transposes to the main line after} c2 67. Kc4 f2 68. Rf1 $1) 66... c2 67. Ra1 (67. Kxb4 $2 Kd5 $19) (67. Rf1 $2 f2 $19 {puts White in zugzwang. Compare with the main line.}) 67... f2 68. Rf1 $1 {After Black has achieved the most active position, the rook must go here. Previously, it could choose between several good squares.} (68. Rh1 $2 b3 69. Kxb3 Kd5 $19) 68... b3 69. Kxb3 Kd5 70. Kxc2 Ke4 71. Kb2 Kf3 72. Bc5 $1 {This saving resource is available only with the rook on f1.} Rxg7 73. Rxf2+ Kg3 74. Rxh2 Kxh2 75. Kxa2 $11) 62. Kc2 Kc6 63. Rd1 $1 b3+ 64. Kc3 $2 {The question mark attached by Gligoric is pointless, as the game is still drawn after the last move.} (64. Kb2 f4 (64... h1=Q 65. Rxh1 Kd5 66. Rd1+ Ke4 67. Re1+ Kd3 68. Rd1+ Ke2 69. Rc1 $11) 65. Rd6+ Kc7 66. Rd1 $1 f3 67. Kc3 f2 68. Kb2 $1 $11 {In this line we once again notice that the bishop is alive.}) {Diagram [#]} 64... h1=Q $1 {The only chance.} ({Black's king cannot cross the d-file with the help of zugzwang ideas, since after} 64... f4 {White has} 65. Rd6+ $1 {Once again, the bishop is useful.} Kc7 66. Rd1 f3 $11 {(Purdy,Cecil)} 67. Kb2 Kc6 68. Rd6+ (68. Kc3 h1=Q 69. Rxh1 Kd5) 68... Kb5 69. Rd5+ Ka4 70. Rd1 f2 71. Kc3 $11 {Marin, Mihail}) 65. Rxh1 Kd5 66. Kb2 f4 67. Rd1+ Ke4 68. Rc1 Kd3 (68... Kd4 69. Rd1+) {Diagram [#]} 69. Rd1+ $4 {One of the most famous mistakes in chess history. Frankly speaking, I do not find it that obvious under the over the board play circumstances.} (69. Rc3+ $1 Kd4 (69... Ke2 70. Rxc4 f3 71. Rc1 f2 72. Kxb3 f1=Q 73. Rxf1 Kxf1 74. Kxa2) 70. Rf3 c3+ (70... Ke4 71. Rc3 f3 72. Rxc4+ Ke3 73. Rc1 { Kb3= Purdy,Cecil}) 71. Ka1 c2 72. Rxf4+ Kc3 (72... Kd3 73. Rf1 { Kb2+- Purdy,Cecil}) 73. Rf3+ Kd2 74. Ba3 $1 $11 Rxg7 75. Rxb3 Rc7 76. Bb2 $10 (76. Kxa2 $4 Ra7 $1 $19)) 69... Ke2 $1 $19 70. Rc1 f3 71. Bc5 Rxg7 72. Rxc4 Rd7 $1 73. Re4+ Kf1 74. Bd4 f2 {[ Rd4] [ d4]} 0-1

